}, {"name": "with_top.coe_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.lt_iff_exists_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.map_le_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_max.with_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.has_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.unbot'_eq_self_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.trichotomous.gt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.lt_iff_exists_coe_btwn", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.of_dual_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.none_eq_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.coe_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_min.with_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.map_to_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.monotone_map_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.le_to_dual_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.coe_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.has_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.unbot", "description": "Deconstruct a `x : with_bot \u03b1` to the underlying value in `\u03b1`, given a proof that `x \u2260 \u22a5`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.coe_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.not_lt_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.no_max_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.coe_eq_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.to_dual_apply_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.has_repr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.ghost_fun_tac", "description": "An auxiliary tactic for proving that `ghost_fun` respects the ring operations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.ghost_map", "description": " `witt_vector.ghost_map` is a ring homomorphism that maps each Witt vector\nto the sequence of its ghost components.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map_fun.int_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.ghost_equiv_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map_coeff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map_fun.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map_fun.nat_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map_fun.map_fun_tac", "description": "Auxiliary tactic for showing that `map_fun` respects the ring operations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map_fun.injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.constant_coeff_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map_fun", "description": " `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` induces a map from `\ud835\udd4e \u03b1` to `\ud835\udd4e \u03b2` by applying `f` componentwise.\nIf `f` is a ring homomorphism, then so is `f`, see `witt_vector.map f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.ghost_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.comm_ring", "description": "The commutative ring structure on `\ud835\udd4e R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map_fun.one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map_fun.neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map_fun.zsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map", "description": " `witt_vector.map f` is the ring homomorphism `\ud835\udd4e R \u2192+* \ud835\udd4e S` naturally induced\nby a ring homomorphism `f : R \u2192+* S`. It acts coefficientwise.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map_fun.zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.ghost_component", "description": " Evaluates the `n`th Witt polynomial on the first `n` coefficients of `x`,\nproducing a value in `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.ghost_equiv", "description": "`witt_vector.ghost_map` is a ring isomorphism when `p` is invertible in `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.matrix_vec_empty_coeff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map_fun.nsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map_fun.pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.nontrivial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map_fun.surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.ghost_map.bijective_of_invertible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map_fun.sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.ghost_component_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.constant_coeff", "description": "`witt_vector.coeff x 0` as a `ring_hom`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map_fun.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.analytic_set_range_of_polish_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.measurable_comp_iff_restrict", "description": " If `f : X \u2192 Y` is a Borel measurable map from a Polish space to a topological space with second\ncountable topology, then for any measurable space `\u03b2` and `g : Y \u2192 \u03b2`, the composition `g \u2218 f` is\nmeasurable if and only if the restriction of `g` to the range of `f` is measurable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polish_of_countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_measurable_embedding_real", "description": "Any Polish Borel space embeds measurably into the reals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_closed.measurable_set_image_of_continuous_on_inj_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.borel_space_codomain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.analytic_set", "description": " An analytic set is a set which is the continuous image of some Polish space. There are several\nequivalent characterizations of this definition. For the definition, we pick one that avoids\nuniverse issues: a set is analytic if and only if it is a continuous image of `\u2115 \u2192 \u2115` (or if it\nis empty). The above more usual characterization is given\nin `analytic_set_iff_exists_polish_space_range`.\n\nWarning: these are analytic sets in the context of descriptive set theory (which is why they are\nregistered in the namespace `measure_theory`). They have nothing to do with analytic sets in the\ncontext of complex analysis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quotient.borel_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.analytic_set_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_closed.analytic_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.measurable_embedding", "description": "An injective continuous function on a Polish space is a measurable embedding.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set.is_clopenable", "description": " Given a Borel-measurable set in a Polish space, there exists a finer Polish topology making\nit clopen. This is in fact an equivalence, see `is_clopenable_iff_measurable_set`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measurably_separable_range_of_disjoint", "description": " The hard part of the Lusin separation theorem saying that two disjoint analytic sets are\ncontained in disjoint Borel sets (see the full statement in `analytic_set.measurably_separable`).\nHere, we prove this when our analytic sets are the ranges of functions from `\u2115 \u2192 \u2115`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.analytic_set.image_of_continuous_on", "description": "The continuous image of an analytic set is analytic", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measurable_set_exists_tendsto", "description": "The set of points for which a measurable sequence of functions converges is measurable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.map_eq_borel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measurably_separable.Union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.set.univ.polish_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.analytic_set.Inter", "description": "A countable intersection of analytic sets is analytic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set.analytic_set_image", "description": "The image of a measurable set in a Polish space under a measurable map is an analytic set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_open.analytic_set_image", "description": "The image of an open set under a continuous map is analytic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.analytic_set.measurably_separable", "description": " The Lusin separation theorem: if two analytic sets are disjoint, then they are contained in\ndisjoint Borel sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.analytic_set_iff_exists_polish_space_range", "description": "A set is analytic if and only if it is the continuous image of some Polish space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on.measurable_embedding", "description": " If `s` is Borel-measurable in a Polish space and `f` is continuous injective on `s`, then\nthe restriction of `f` to `s` is a measurable embedding.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polish_space.measurable_equiv_of_not_countable", "description": "The **Borel Isomorphism Theorem**: Any two uncountable Polish spaces are Borel isomorphic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.exists_continuous", "description": " Given a Borel-measurable function from a Polish space to a second-countable space, there exists\na finer Polish topology on the source space for which the function is continuous.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.map_borel_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.map_measurable_space_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.map_measurable_space_eq_borel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measurable_equiv_range_coe_nat_of_infinite_of_countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polish_space.borel_schroeder_bernstein", "description": " If two Polish spaces admit Borel measurable injections to one another,\nthen they are Borel isomorphic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polish_space.measurable_equiv_nat_bool_of_not_countable", "description": "Any uncountable Polish space is Borel isomorphic to the Cantor space `\u2115 \u2192 bool`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set.image_of_continuous_on_inj_on", "description": " The Lusin-Souslin theorem: if `s` is Borel-measurable in a Polish space, then its image under\na continuous injective map is also Borel-measurable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.measurable_comp_iff_of_surjective", "description": " If `f : X \u2192 Y` is a surjective Borel measurable map from a Polish space to a topological space\nwith second countable topology, then for any measurable space `\u03b1` and `g : Y \u2192 \u03b1`, the composition\n`g \u2218 f` is measurable if and only if `g` is measurable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.measurable_set_preimage_iff_preimage_coe", "description": " If `f : X \u2192 Y` is a Borel measurable map from a Polish space to a topological space with second\ncountable topology, then the preimage of a set `s` is measurable if and only if the set is\nmeasurable in `set.range f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_clopenable_iff_measurable_set", "description": "In a Polish space, a set is clopenable if and only if it is Borel-measurable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.measurable_set_preimage_iff_inter_range", "description": " If `f : X \u2192 Y` is a Borel measurable map from a Polish space to a topological space with second\ncountable topology and the range of `f` is measurable, then the preimage of a set `s` is measurable\nif and only if the intesection with `set.range f` is measurable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.analytic_set.measurable_set_of_compl", "description": " **Suslin's Theorem**: in a Hausdorff topological space, an analytic set with an analytic\ncomplement is measurable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.analytic_set.image_of_continuous", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measurable_set_range_of_continuous_injective", "description": " The Lusin-Souslin theorem: the range of a continuous injective function defined on a Polish\nspace is Borel-measurable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measurably_separable", "description": " Two sets `u` and `v` in a measurable space are measurably separable if there\nexists a measurable set containing `u` and disjoint from `v`.\nThis is mostly interesting for Borel-separable sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set.analytic_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set.image_of_measurable_inj_on", "description": " The Lusin-Souslin theorem: if `s` is Borel-measurable in a Polish space, then its image under\na measurable injective map taking values in a second-countable topological space\nis also Borel-measurable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.measurable_set_preimage_iff_of_surjective", "description": " If `f : X \u2192 Y` is a surjective Borel measurable map from a Polish space to a topological space\nwith second countable topology, then the preimage of a set `s` is measurable if and only if the set\nis measurable.\nOne implication is the definition of measurability, the other one heavily relies on `X` being a\nPolish space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polish_space.equiv.measurable_equiv", "description": " The **Borel Isomorphism Theorem**: If two Polish spaces have the same cardinality,\nthey are Borel isomorphic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.analytic_set.Union", "description": "A countable union of analytic sets is analytic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quotient_group.borel_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_subset_real_measurable_equiv", "description": "Any Polish Borel space is measurably equivalent to a subset of the reals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_nat_measurable_equiv_range_coe_fin_of_finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quotient_add_group.borel_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.measurable_embedding", "description": " An injective measurable function from a Polish space to a second-countable topological space\nis a measurable embedding.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_add_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_le_four", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_lt_four", "description": "See `zero_lt_four'` for a version with the type explicit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_le_two'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_le_one_class.ne_zero.three", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_lt_three'", "description": "See `zero_lt_three` for a version with the type implicit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_le_three", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_le_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_lt_two", "description": "See `zero_lt_two'` for a version with the type explicit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_lt_four'", "description": "See `zero_lt_four` for a version with the type implicit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_lt_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "two_pos", "description": "**Alias** of `zero_lt_two`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_one_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_lt_three", "description": "See `zero_lt_three'` for a version with the type explicit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_le_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_le_one_class.ne_zero.four", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "three_pos", "description": "**Alias** of `zero_lt_three`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "four_pos", "description": "**Alias** of `zero_lt_four`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_le_one_class.ne_zero.two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_lt_two'", "description": "See `zero_lt_two` for a version with the type implicit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.clm_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.smul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_mul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.const_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_mul_const'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_inv'", "description": " At an invertible element `x` of a normed division algebra `R`, the Fr\u00e9chet derivative of the\ninversion operation is the linear map `\u03bb t, - x\u207b\u00b9 * t * x\u207b\u00b9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_const_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.mul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_clm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.clm_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.mul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.const_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.clm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.smul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.clm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.mul_const'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_const_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.clm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.smul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.mul_const'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.inv'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.mul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.mul'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_clm_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at_inv'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.const_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_inv'", "description": "Non-commutative version of `fderiv_inv`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.smul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_ring_inverse", "description": " At an invertible element `x` of a normed algebra `R`, the Fr\u00e9chet derivative of the inversion\noperation is the linear map `\u03bb t, - x\u207b\u00b9 * t * x\u207b\u00b9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_smul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.const_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.const_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.smul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at_inv'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.mul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.clm_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.mul_const'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.mul'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.clm_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_inv'", "description": "Non-commutative version of `fderiv_within_inv`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.mul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.inv'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_mul'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_clm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.clm_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_smul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.clm_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.mul'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on_inv'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.clm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_mul_const'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.clm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.smul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_mul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.const_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.mul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_mul'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.smul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.const_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.clm_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.inv'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.inv'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.clm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.clm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_clm_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.mul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nndist_triangle", "description": "Triangle inequality for the nonnegative distance", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.diam_univ_of_noncompact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.eventually_prod_nhds_iff", "description": " A version of `filter.eventually_prod_iff` where the second filter consists of neighborhoods\nin a pseudo-metric space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.tendsto_locally_uniformly_iff", "description": "Expressing locally uniform convergence using `dist`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_to_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.dense_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.eball_top_eq_univ", "description": "In a pseudometric space, an open ball of infinite radius is the whole space", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.totally_bounded_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.nonempty_of_unbounded", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.ball", "description": "`ball x \u03b5` is the set of all points `y` with `dist y x < \u03b5`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.nndist_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ball_pi", "description": " An open ball in a product space is a product of open balls. See also `metric.ball_pi'`\nfor a version assuming `nonempty \u03b2` instead of `0 < r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.mem_ball'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_emetric_space.to_pseudo_metric_space", "description": " One gets a pseudometric space from an emetric space if the edistance\nis everywhere finite, by pushing the edistance to reals. We set it up so that the edist and the\nuniformity are defeq in the pseudometric space and the emetric space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "edist_lt_top", "description": "In a pseudometric space, the extended distance is always finite", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.separation_quotient.dist_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric_space.of_dist_topology", "description": " Construct a metric space structure whose underlying topological space structure\n(definitionally) agrees which a pre-existing topology which is compatible with a given distance\nfunction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric_space_pi", "description": "A finite product of metric spaces is a metric space, with the sup distance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cauchy_seq_iff_le_tendsto_0", "description": " Yet another metric characterization of Cauchy sequences on integers. This one is often the\nmost efficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.exists_closed_ball_inter_eq_singleton_of_discrete", "description": " Given a point `x` in a discrete subset `s` of a pseudometric space, there is a closed ball\nof positive radius centered at `x` and intersecting `s` only at `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.to_has_nndist", "description": "Distance as a nonnegative real number.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.Icc_eq_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_triangle_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_le_range_sum_dist", "description": "The triangle (polygon) inequality for sequences of points; `finset.range` version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_continuous.nndist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.ball_eq_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.closed_ball_mem_nhds_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.bounded.ediam_ne_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.nndist_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_le_Ico_sum_of_dist_le", "description": " A version of `dist_le_Ico_sum_dist` with each intermediate distance replaced\nwith an upper estimate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.tendsto_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.eventually_nhds_iff_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.mem_of_closed'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.nhds_basis_ball_inv_nat_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nndist_pi_lt_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.diam_le_of_forall_dist_le", "description": " If the distance between any two points in a set is bounded by some non-negative constant,\nthis constant bounds the diameter.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.uniformity_eq_comap_nhds_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dense.exists_dist_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.bounded_closed_ball", "description": "Closed balls are bounded", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.replace_uniformity", "description": " Build a new pseudometric space from an old one where the bundled uniform structure is provably\n(but typically non-definitionaly) equal to some given uniform structure.\nSee Note [forgetful inheritance].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eventually_closed_ball_subset", "description": " If `u` is a neighborhood of `x`, then for small enough `r`, the closed ball\n`closed_ball x r` is contained in `u`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.ball_half_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.dist_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.continuous_at_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.ball_mem_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_opposite.pseudo_metric_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.sphere_subset_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "edist_le_of_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.bounded_closure_of_bounded", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lebesgue_number_lemma_of_metric_sUnion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.continuous_within_at_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_opposite.nndist_unop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "edist_lt_of_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.uniformity_basis_dist_le_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nndist_pi_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_le_range_sum_of_dist_le", "description": " A version of `dist_le_range_sum_dist` with each intermediate distance replaced\nwith an upper estimate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.ediam_univ_eq_top_iff_noncompact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_triangle_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.closed_ball_disjoint_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nndist_to_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_pi_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "totally_bounded_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "second_countable_of_proper", "description": "A proper pseudo metric space is sigma compact, and therefore second countable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.exists_is_open_bounded_image_inter_of_is_compact_of_forall_continuous_within_at", "description": " If a function is continuous within a set `s` at every point of a compact set `k`, then it is\nbounded on some open neighborhood of `k` in `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.replace_topology_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.bounded_iff_ediam_ne_top", "description": "Characterize the boundedness of a set in terms of the finiteness of its emetric.diameter.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.tendsto_at_top'", "description": "A variant of `tendsto_at_top` that\nuses `\u2203 N, \u2200 n > N, ...` rather than `\u2203 N, \u2200 n \u2265 N, ...`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.bounded.is_compact_closure", "description": "The **Heine\u2013Borel theorem**: In a proper space, the closure of a bounded set is compact.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.diam_closed_ball", "description": "The diameter of a closed ball of radius `r` is at most `2 r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.sphere_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.nhds_within_basis_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.continuous_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_continuous_dist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.nonneg_of_mem_sphere", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "proper_space_of_compact_closed_ball_of_le", "description": " If all closed balls of large enough radius are compact, then the space is proper. Especially\nuseful when the lower bound for the radius is 0.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.mem_uniformity_dist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.uniformity_basis_dist_le", "description": " Contant size closed neighborhoods of the diagonal form a basis\nof the uniformity filter.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.mem_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.replace_bornology", "description": " Build a new pseudometric space from an old one where the bundled bornology structure is provably\n(but typically non-definitionaly) equal to some given bornology structure.\nSee Note [forgetful inheritance].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.bounded_bUnion", "description": "A finite union of bounded sets is bounded", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nndist_triangle_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_opposite.dist_unop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric_space.to_separated", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.replace_topology", "description": " Build a new pseudo metric space from an old one where the bundled topological structure is\nprovably (but typically non-definitionaly) equal to some given topological structure.\nSee Note [forgetful inheritance].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.diam_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_of_proper", "description": "A proper space is complete", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.diam_nonneg", "description": "The diameter of a set is always nonnegative", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nndist_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_triangle4_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric_space.induced", "description": " Metric space structure pulled back by an injective function. Injectivity is necessary to\nensure that `dist x y = 0` only if `x = y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.closure_sphere", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.tendsto_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.mk_uniformity_basis_le", "description": " Given `f : \u03b2 \u2192 \u211d`, if `f` sends `{i | p i}` to a set of positive numbers\naccumulating to zero, then closed neighborhoods of the diagonal of sizes `{f i | p i}`\nform a basis of `\ud835\udce4 \u03b1`.\n\nCurrently we have only one specific basis `uniformity_basis_dist_le` based on this constructor.\nMore can be easily added if needed in the future.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.induced", "description": "Pseudometric space structure pulled back by a function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.closed_ball_diff_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.of_dist", "description": "Construct a bornology from a distance function and metric space axioms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_compact_of_proper", "description": "A proper space is locally compact", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_opposite.dist_unop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.dist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nndist_pi_eq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.positivity_diam", "description": "Extension for the `positivity` tactic: the diameter of a set is always nonnegative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_of_nndist_eq_zero", "description": "Deduce the equality of points with the vanishing of the nonnegative distance", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cauchy_seq_iff_tendsto_dist_at_top_0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.compact_space_iff_bounded_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.closed_ball_zero'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.bounded_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.dist_left_le_of_mem_uIcc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nndist_dist", "description": "Express `nndist` in terms of `dist`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.diam_singleton", "description": "A singleton has zero diameter", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.nndist_insert_nth_insert_nth", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.bounded_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.dist_le_of_mem_uIcc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_of_forall_dist_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nndist_to_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.ne_of_mem_sphere", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.diam", "description": " The diameter of a set in a metric space. To get controllable behavior even when the diameter\nshould be infinite, we express it in terms of the emetric.diameter", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.nndist_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.finite_cover_balls", "description": "**Alias** of `finite_cover_balls_of_compact`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_prod_same_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.dist_lt_add_of_nonempty_ball_inter_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_iff_of_dist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.sphere_disjoint_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.nhds_basis_ball_inv_nat_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.diam_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.bounded_singleton", "description": "A singleton is bounded", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.ball_eq_ball'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.tendsto_uniformly_on_iff", "description": "Expressing uniform convergence on a set using `dist`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abs_dist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closed_ball_pi", "description": " A closed ball in a product space is a product of closed balls. See also `metric.closed_ball_pi'`\nfor a version assuming `nonempty \u03b2` instead of `0 \u2264 r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_le_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype.pseudo_metric_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.uniform_embedding_bot_of_pairwise_le_dist", "description": " If `f : \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b1` sends any two distinct points to points at distance at least `\u03b5 > 0`, then\n`f` is a uniform embedding with respect to the discrete uniformity on `\u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.edist_dist_tac", "description": " This tactic is used to populate `pseudo_metric_space.edist_dist` when the default `edist` is\nused.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_nndist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "squeeze_zero", "description": " Special case of the sandwich theorem; see `tendsto_of_tendsto_of_tendsto_of_le_of_le`\nand  `tendsto_of_tendsto_of_tendsto_of_le_of_le'` for the general case.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.is_open_singleton_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.bounded_closure_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.is_open_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.uniformity_basis_dist_inv_nat_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.bounded.mono", "description": "Subsets of a bounded set are also bounded", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.nndist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.cauchy_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiplicative.pseudo_metric_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inducing.comap_pseudo_metric_space", "description": " Pull back a pseudometric space structure by an inducing map. This is a version of\n`pseudo_metric_space.induced` useful in case if the domain already has a `topological_space`\nstructure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space_pi", "description": "A finite product of pseudometric spaces is a pseudometric space, with the sup distance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.sphere_union_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "comap_dist_left_at_top_eq_cocompact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space", "description": " Pseudo metric and Metric spaces\n\nA pseudo metric space is endowed with a distance for which the requirement `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y` might\nnot hold. A metric space is a pseudo metric space such that `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y`.\nEach pseudo metric space induces a canonical `uniform_space` and hence a canonical\n`topological_space` This is enforced in the type class definition, by extending the `uniform_space`\nstructure. When instantiating a `pseudo_metric_space` structure, the uniformity fields are not\nnecessary, they will be filled in by default. In the same way, each (pseudo) metric space induces a\n(pseudo) emetric space structure. It is included in the structure, but filled in by default.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.to_has_dist", "description": " Pseudo metric and Metric spaces\n\nA pseudo metric space is endowed with a distance for which the requirement `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y` might\nnot hold. A metric space is a pseudo metric space such that `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y`.\nEach pseudo metric space induces a canonical `uniform_space` and hence a canonical\n`topological_space` This is enforced in the type class definition, by extending the `uniform_space`\nstructure. When instantiating a `pseudo_metric_space` structure, the uniformity fields are not\nnecessary, they will be filled in by default. In the same way, each (pseudo) metric space induces a\n(pseudo) emetric space structure. It is included in the structure, but filled in by default.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.dist_self", "description": " Pseudo metric and Metric spaces\n\nA pseudo metric space is endowed with a distance for which the requirement `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y` might\nnot hold. A metric space is a pseudo metric space such that `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y`.\nEach pseudo metric space induces a canonical `uniform_space` and hence a canonical\n`topological_space` This is enforced in the type class definition, by extending the `uniform_space`\nstructure. When instantiating a `pseudo_metric_space` structure, the uniformity fields are not\nnecessary, they will be filled in by default. In the same way, each (pseudo) metric space induces a\n(pseudo) emetric space structure. It is included in the structure, but filled in by default.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.dist_comm", "description": " Pseudo metric and Metric spaces\n\nA pseudo metric space is endowed with a distance for which the requirement `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y` might\nnot hold. A metric space is a pseudo metric space such that `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y`.\nEach pseudo metric space induces a canonical `uniform_space` and hence a canonical\n`topological_space` This is enforced in the type class definition, by extending the `uniform_space`\nstructure. When instantiating a `pseudo_metric_space` structure, the uniformity fields are not\nnecessary, they will be filled in by default. In the same way, each (pseudo) metric space induces a\n(pseudo) emetric space structure. It is included in the structure, but filled in by default.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.dist_triangle", "description": " Pseudo metric and Metric spaces\n\nA pseudo metric space is endowed with a distance for which the requirement `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y` might\nnot hold. A metric space is a pseudo metric space such that `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y`.\nEach pseudo metric space induces a canonical `uniform_space` and hence a canonical\n`topological_space` This is enforced in the type class definition, by extending the `uniform_space`\nstructure. When instantiating a `pseudo_metric_space` structure, the uniformity fields are not\nnecessary, they will be filled in by default. In the same way, each (pseudo) metric space induces a\n(pseudo) emetric space structure. It is included in the structure, but filled in by default.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.edist", "description": " Pseudo metric and Metric spaces\n\nA pseudo metric space is endowed with a distance for which the requirement `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y` might\nnot hold. A metric space is a pseudo metric space such that `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y`.\nEach pseudo metric space induces a canonical `uniform_space` and hence a canonical\n`topological_space` This is enforced in the type class definition, by extending the `uniform_space`\nstructure. When instantiating a `pseudo_metric_space` structure, the uniformity fields are not\nnecessary, they will be filled in by default. In the same way, each (pseudo) metric space induces a\n(pseudo) emetric space structure. It is included in the structure, but filled in by default.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.edist_dist", "description": " Pseudo metric and Metric spaces\n\nA pseudo metric space is endowed with a distance for which the requirement `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y` might\nnot hold. A metric space is a pseudo metric space such that `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y`.\nEach pseudo metric space induces a canonical `uniform_space` and hence a canonical\n`topological_space` This is enforced in the type class definition, by extending the `uniform_space`\nstructure. When instantiating a `pseudo_metric_space` structure, the uniformity fields are not\nnecessary, they will be filled in by default. In the same way, each (pseudo) metric space induces a\n(pseudo) emetric space structure. It is included in the structure, but filled in by default.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.to_uniform_space", "description": " Pseudo metric and Metric spaces\n\nA pseudo metric space is endowed with a distance for which the requirement `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y` might\nnot hold. A metric space is a pseudo metric space such that `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y`.\nEach pseudo metric space induces a canonical `uniform_space` and hence a canonical\n`topological_space` This is enforced in the type class definition, by extending the `uniform_space`\nstructure. When instantiating a `pseudo_metric_space` structure, the uniformity fields are not\nnecessary, they will be filled in by default. In the same way, each (pseudo) metric space induces a\n(pseudo) emetric space structure. It is included in the structure, but filled in by default.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.uniformity_dist", "description": " Pseudo metric and Metric spaces\n\nA pseudo metric space is endowed with a distance for which the requirement `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y` might\nnot hold. A metric space is a pseudo metric space such that `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y`.\nEach pseudo metric space induces a canonical `uniform_space` and hence a canonical\n`topological_space` This is enforced in the type class definition, by extending the `uniform_space`\nstructure. When instantiating a `pseudo_metric_space` structure, the uniformity fields are not\nnecessary, they will be filled in by default. In the same way, each (pseudo) metric space induces a\n(pseudo) emetric space structure. It is included in the structure, but filled in by default.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.to_bornology", "description": " Pseudo metric and Metric spaces\n\nA pseudo metric space is endowed with a distance for which the requirement `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y` might\nnot hold. A metric space is a pseudo metric space such that `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y`.\nEach pseudo metric space induces a canonical `uniform_space` and hence a canonical\n`topological_space` This is enforced in the type class definition, by extending the `uniform_space`\nstructure. When instantiating a `pseudo_metric_space` structure, the uniformity fields are not\nnecessary, they will be filled in by default. In the same way, each (pseudo) metric space induces a\n(pseudo) emetric space structure. It is included in the structure, but filled in by default.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.cobounded_sets", "description": " Pseudo metric and Metric spaces\n\nA pseudo metric space is endowed with a distance for which the requirement `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y` might\nnot hold. A metric space is a pseudo metric space such that `d(x,y)=0 \u2192 x = y`.\nEach pseudo metric space induces a canonical `uniform_space` and hence a canonical\n`topological_space` This is enforced in the type class definition, by extending the `uniform_space`\nstructure. When instantiating a `pseudo_metric_space` structure, the uniformity fields are not\nnecessary, they will be filled in by default. In the same way, each (pseudo) metric space induces a\n(pseudo) emetric space structure. It is included in the structure, but filled in by default.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.bounded_range_of_tendsto_cofinite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric_space.replace_bornology_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric_space.ext", "description": "Two metric space structures with the same distance coincide.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cauchy_seq_of_le_tendsto_0'", "description": " If the distance between `s n` and `s m`, `n \u2264 m` is bounded above by `b n`\nand `b` converges to zero, then `s` is a Cauchy sequence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.frontier_closed_ball_subset_sphere", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.of_dist_topology", "description": " Construct a pseudo-metric space structure whose underlying topological space structure\n(definitionally) agrees which a pre-existing topology which is compatible with a given distance\nfunction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.exists_ball_subset_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "embedding.comap_metric_space", "description": " Pull back a metric space structure by an embedding. This is a version of\n`metric_space.induced` useful in case if the domain already has a `top