e": "matrix.conj_transpose_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.conj_transpose", "description": "The conjugate transpose of a matrix defined in term of `star`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_equiv.map_matrix_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_vec_vec_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.diag_linear_map", "description": "`matrix.diag` as a `linear_map`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transpose_transpose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.conj_transpose_inv_int_cast_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.one_eq_pi_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_equiv.map_matrix_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.diag_conj_transpose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.distrib_mul_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.conj_transpose_reindex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.of", "description": " Cast a function into a matrix.\n\nThe two sides of the equivalence are definitionally equal types. We want to use an explicit cast\nto distinguish the types because `matrix` has different instances to pi types (such as `pi.has_mul`,\nwhich performs elementwise multiplication, vs `matrix.has_mul`).\n\nIf you are defining a matrix, in terms of its entries, use `of (\u03bb i j, _)`. The\npurpose of this approach is to ensure that terms of th\ne form `(\u03bb i j, _) * (\u03bb i j, _)` do not\nappear, as the type of `*` can be misleading.\n\nPorting note: In Lean 3, it is also safe to use pattern matching in a definition as `| i j := _`,\nwhich can only be unfolded when fully-applied. leanprover/lean4#2042 means this does not\n(currently) work in Lean 4.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "alg_hom.map_matrix_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.two_mul_expl", "description": "", "p":