_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.of_real_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.star_ordered_ring", "description": "With `z \u2264 w` iff `w - z` is real and nonnegative, `\u2102` is a star ordered ring.\n(That is, a star ring in which the nonnegative elements are those of the form `star z * z`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.lim_im", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.div_im", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.abs_abs", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.re_sq_le_norm_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.zero_lt_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.mul_im", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.has_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.norm_sq_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.re_le_abs", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.zero_le_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.zero_re", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.norm_sq_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.norm_sq_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.of_real_int_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.abs_re_le_abs", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.has_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.inv_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.re_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.range_re", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.of_real_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.sq_abs", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.star_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.augment_truncate_inv_f_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.to_truncate", "description": "There is a canonical chain map from the truncation of a cochain complex `C` to\nthe \"single object\" cochain complex consisting of the truncated object `C.X 0` in degree 0.\nThe components of this chain map are `C.d 0 1` in degree 0, and zero otherwise.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.chain_complex_d_succ_succ_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.truncate_obj_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.augment_truncate_inv_f_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.truncate_obj_d", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.cochain_complex_d_succ_succ_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.augment_truncate_hom_f_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.truncate_obj_d", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.augment_X_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.truncate_to", "description": "There is a canonical chain map from the truncation of a chain map `C` to\nthe \"single object\" chain complex consisting of the truncated object `C.X 0` in degree 0.\nThe components of this chain map are `C.d 1 0` in degree 0, and zero otherwise.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.truncate_augment_hom_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.augment_truncate", "description": "Augmenting a truncated complex with the original object and morphism is isomorphic\n(with components the identity) to the original complex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.augment_d_zero_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.augment_X_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.augment_X_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.augment_X_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.augment_truncate_hom_f_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.truncate_augment_inv_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.augment_truncate_inv_f_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.truncate", "description": "The truncation of a `\u2115`-indexed cochain complex,\ndeleting the object at `0` and shifting everything else down.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.truncate_map_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.truncate_map_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.augment_truncate_hom_f_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.truncate_augment_inv_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.augment_truncate", "description": "Augmenting a truncated complex with the original object and morphism is isomorphic\n(with components the identity) to the original complex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.augment_d_succ_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.truncate", "description": "The truncation of a `\u2115`-indexed chain complex,\ndeleting the object at `0` and shifting everything else down.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.truncate_augment", "description": "Truncating an augmented chain complex is isomorphic (with components the identity)\nto the original complex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.augment_truncate_hom_f_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.truncate_augment", "description": "Truncating an augmented cochain complex is isomorphic (with components the identity)\nto the original complex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.augment", "description": "We can \"augment\" a cochain complex by inserting an arbitrary object in degree zero\n(shifting everything else up), along with a suitable differential.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.truncate_obj_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.to_single\u2080_as_complex", "description": "A chain map from a chain complex to a single object chain complex in degree zero\ncan be reinterpreted as a chain complex.\n\nThs is the inverse construction of `truncate_to`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.augment_truncate_inv_f_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.truncate_augment_hom_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.from_single\u2080_as_complex", "description": "A chain map from a single object cochain complex in degree zero to a cochain complex\ncan be reinterpreted as a cochain complex.\n\nThs is the inverse construction of `to_truncate`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.augment_d_one_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cochain_complex.augment_d_succ_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "chain_complex.augment", "description": "We can \"augment\" a chain complex by inserting an arbitrary object in degree zero\n(shifting everything else up), along with a suitable differential.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.continuous_linear_map_comp", "description": "Composition by continuous linear maps on the left preserves `C^n` functions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.has_strict_deriv_at", "description": "If a function is `C^n` with `1 \u2264 n`, then the derivative of `f` is also a strict derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_one_iff_deriv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_iterated_fderiv_within_mul_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.comp_continuous_linear_map", "description": "Composition by continuous linear maps on the right preserves `C^n` functions on domains.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.mul", "description": "The product of two `C^n` functions on a domain is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_iterated_fderiv_comp_le", "description": " If the derivatives of `g` at `f x` are bounded by `C`, and the `i`-th derivative\nof `f` at `x` is bounded by `D^i` for all `1 \u2264 i \u2264 n`, then the `n`-th derivative\nof `g \u2218 f` is bounded by `n! * C * D^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.cont_diff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.prod_map'", "description": " The product map of two `C^n` functions within a set at a point is `C^n`\nwithin the product set at the product point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.snd'", "description": "Precomposing `f` with `prod.snd` is `C^n` at `(x, y)`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.iterated_fderiv_comp_left", "description": " The iterated derivative of the composition with a linear map on the left is\nobtained by applying the linear map to the iterated derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.iterated_fderiv_within_comp_left", "description": " The iterated derivative within a set of the composition with a linear map on the left is\nobtained by applying the linear map to the iterated derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.comp\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_id", "description": "The identity is `C^\u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.div_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_prod_assoc_symm", "description": "The natural equivalence `E \u00d7 (F \u00d7 G) \u2243 (E \u00d7 F) \u00d7 G` is smooth.\n\nWarning: see remarks attached to `cont_diff_prod_assoc`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.has_strict_fderiv_at", "description": " If a function is `C^n` with `1 \u2264 n` around a point, then the derivative of `f` at this point\nis also a strict derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.smul", "description": "The scalar multiplication of two `C^n` functions on a domain is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.fst'", "description": "Precomposing `f` with `prod.fst` is `C^n` at `(x, y)`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.sub", "description": "The difference of two `C^n` functions is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.prod", "description": "The cartesian product of `C^n` functions at a point is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on_fst", "description": "The first projection on a domain in a product is `C^\u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_prod_mk_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.cont_diff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.iterate_deriv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.fderiv_right", "description": "`fderiv \ud835\udd5c f` is smooth at `x\u2080`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "iterated_fderiv_within_add_apply'", "description": " The iterated derivative of the sum of two functions is the sum of the iterated derivatives.\nThis is the same as `iterated_fderiv_within_add_apply`, but using the spelling `(\u03bb x, f x + g x)`\ninstead of `f + g`, which can be handy for some rewrites.\nTODO: use one form consistently.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_ftaylor_series_up_to_on.exists_lipschitz_on_with_of_nnnorm_lt", "description": " If `f` has a formal Taylor series `p` up to order `1` on `{x} \u222a s`, where `s` is a convex set,\nand `\u2016p x 1\u2016\u208a < K`, then `f` is `K`-Lipschitz in a neighborhood of `x` within `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_const", "description": "Constants are `C^\u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.mul", "description": "The product of two `C^n` functions at a point is `C^n` at this point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.prod_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.sub", "description": " The difference of two `C^n` functions within a set at a point is `C^n` within this set\nat this point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.const_smul", "description": " The scalar multiplication of a constant and a `C^n` function at a point is `C^n` at this\npoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.continuous_on_deriv_within", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.clm_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.norm_iterated_fderiv_within_le_of_bilinear", "description": " Bounding the norm of the iterated derivative of `B (f x) (g x)` within a set in terms of the\niterated derivatives of `f` and `g` when `B` is bilinear:\n`\u2016D^n (x \u21a6 B (f x) (g x))\u2016 \u2264 \u2016B\u2016 \u2211_{k \u2264 n} n.choose k \u2016D^k f\u2016 \u2016D^{n-k} g\u2016`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.fderiv", "description": "`x \u21a6 fderiv \ud835\udd5c (f x) (g x)` is smooth.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.cont_diff_at_comp_iff", "description": " Composition by continuous linear equivs on the right respects higher differentiability at a\npoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.continuous_linear_map_comp", "description": "Composition by continuous linear maps on the left preserves `C^n` functions on domains.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at_map_inverse", "description": " At a continuous linear equivalence `e : E \u2243L[\ud835\udd5c] F` between Banach spaces, the operation of\ninversion is `C^n`, for all `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.iterated_fderiv_within_comp_right", "description": " The iterated derivative within a set of the composition with a linear map on the right is\nobtained by composing the iterated derivative with the linear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.neg", "description": " The negative of a `C^n` function within a domain at a point is `C^n` within this domain at\nthis point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.iterated_fderiv_within_comp_left", "description": " The iterated derivative within a set of the composition with a linear equiv on the left is\nobtained by applying the linear equiv to the iterated derivative. This is true without\ndifferentiability assumptions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.exists_lipschitz_on_with", "description": " If `f` is `C^1` within a conves set `s` at `x`, then it is Lipschitz on a neighborhood of `x`\nwithin `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at_of_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.iterated_fderiv_comp_right", "description": " The iterated derivative of the composition with a linear map on the right is\nobtained by composing the iterated derivative with the linear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on_snd", "description": "The second projection on a domain in a product is `C^\u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_ftaylor_series_up_to_on.comp_continuous_linear_map", "description": " If `f` admits a Taylor series `p` in a set `s`, and `g` is linear, then `f \u2218 g` admits a Taylor\nseries in `g \u207b\u00b9' s`, whose `k`-th term is given by `p k (g v\u2081, ..., g v\u2096)` .", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_const_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.add", "description": "The sum of two `C^n`functions is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.smul", "description": "The scalar multiplication of two `C^n` functions is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.comp", "description": "The composition of `C^n` functions is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on_top_iff_deriv_of_open", "description": " A function is `C^\u221e` on an open domain if and only if it is differentiable\nthere, and its derivative (formulated with `deriv`) is `C^\u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.div_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.neg", "description": "The negative of a `C^n` function on a domain is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_clm_apply_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.smul", "description": " The scalar multiplication of two `C^n` functions within a set at a point is `C^n` within this\nset at this point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.mul", "description": " The product of two `C^n` functions within a set at a point is `C^n` within this set\nat this point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.norm_iterated_fderiv_comp_left", "description": " Composition with a linear isometry equiv on the left preserves the norm of the iterated\nderivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.div_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.comp'", "description": "The composition of `C^n` functions at points in domains is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.norm_iterated_fderiv_le_of_bilinear", "description": " Bounding the norm of the iterated derivative of `B (f x) (g x)` in terms of the\niterated derivatives of `f` and `g` when `B` is bilinear:\n`\u2016D^n (x \u21a6 B (f x) (g x))\u2016 \u2264 \u2016B\u2016 \u2211_{k \u2264 n} n.choose k \u2016D^k f\u2016 \u2016D^{n-k} g\u2016`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_top_iff_deriv", "description": " A function is `C^\u221e` if and only if it is differentiable,\nand its derivative (formulated in terms of `deriv`) is `C^\u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.comp_continuous_linear_map", "description": "Composition by continuous linear maps on the right preserves `C^n` functions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.clm_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_zero_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.prod_map", "description": " The product map of two `C^n` functions within a set at a point is `C^n`\nwithin the product set at the product point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homeomorph.cont_diff_symm", "description": " If `f` is an `n` times continuously differentiable homeomorphism,\nand if the derivative of `f` at each point is a continuous linear equivalence,\nthen `f.symm` is `n` times continuously differentiable.\n\nThis is one of the easy parts of the inverse function theorem: it assumes that we already have\nan inverse function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.comp", "description": "The composition of `C^n` functions at points is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.fst", "description": "Postcomposing `f` with `prod.fst` is `C^n`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.prod", "description": "The cartesian product of `C^n` functions on domains is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.const_smul", "description": " The scalar multiplication of a constant and a `C^n` function within a set at a point is `C^n`\nwithin this set at this point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.snd", "description": "Postcomposing `f` with `prod.snd` is `C^n`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.has_strict_fderiv_at'", "description": " If a function is `C^n` with `1 \u2264 n` around a point, and its derivative at that point is given to\nus as `f'`, then `f'` is also a strict derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.prod_map'", "description": " The product map of two `C^n` functions within a set at a point is `C^n`\nwithin the product set at the product point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.has_strict_fderiv_at", "description": "If a function is `C^n` with `1 \u2264 n`, then the derivative of `f` is also a strict derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.norm_iterated_fderiv_within_le_of_bilinear_of_le_one", "description": " Bounding the norm of the iterated derivative of `B (f x) (g x)` within a set in terms of the\niterated derivatives of `f` and `g` when `B` is bilinear of norm at most `1`:\n`\u2016D^n (x \u21a6 B (f x) (g x))\u2016 \u2264 \u2211_{k \u2264 n} n.choose k \u2016D^k f\u2016 \u2016D^{n-k} g\u2016`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_iterated_fderiv_mul_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_iterated_fderiv_within_clm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.clm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.comp_continuous_linear_map", "description": " Composition by continuous linear maps on the right preserves `C^n` functions at a point on\na domain.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_apply_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at_fst", "description": "The first projection at a point in a product is `C^\u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.norm_iterated_fderiv_within_le_of_bilinear_aux", "description": " Bounding the norm of the iterated derivative of `B (f x) (g x)` within a set in terms of the\niterated derivatives of `f` and `g` when `B` is bilinear. This lemma is an auxiliary version\nassuming all spaces live in the same universe, to enable an induction. Use instead\n`continuous_linear_map.norm_iterated_fderiv_within_le_of_bilinear` that removes this assumption.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.const_smul", "description": "The scalar multiplication of a constant and a `C^n` function is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "iterated_fderiv_within_const_smul_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on_clm_apply", "description": "A family of continuous linear maps is `C^n` on `s` if all its applications are.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.prod", "description": "The cartesian product of `C^n` functions is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.comp_of_mem", "description": "The composition of `C^n` functions at points in domains is `C^n`,\n with a weaker condition on `s` and `t`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_iterated_fderiv_clm_apply_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.norm_iterated_fderiv_comp_right", "description": " Composition with a linear isometry on the right preserves the norm of the iterated derivative\nwithin a set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homeomorph.cont_diff_symm_deriv", "description": " Let `f` be an `n` times continuously differentiable homeomorphism of a nontrivially normed\nfield.  Suppose that the derivative of `f` is never equal to zero. Then `f.symm` is `n` times\ncontinuously differentiable.\n\nThis is one of the easy parts of the inverse function theorem: it assumes that we already have\nan inverse function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_choose_succ_mul", "description": " The sum of `(n+1).choose i * f i (n+1-i)` can be split into two sums at rank `n`,\nrespectively of `n.choose i * f i (n+1-i)` and `n.choose i * f (i+1) (n-i)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.fderiv_within_apply", "description": "`x \u21a6 fderiv_within \ud835\udd5c (f x) t (g x) (k x)` is smooth at a point within a set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.fst''", "description": "Precomposing `f` with `prod.fst` is `C^n` at `x : E \u00d7 F`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.comp_cont_diff_on\u2083", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at_of_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_prod'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.fderiv_right", "description": "`fderiv \ud835\udd5c f` is smooth.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.fderiv", "description": "`x \u21a6 fderiv \ud835\udd5c (f x) (g x)` is smooth at `x\u2080`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.norm_iterated_fderiv_le_of_bilinear_of_le_one", "description": " Bounding the norm of the iterated derivative of `B (f x) (g x)` in terms of the\niterated derivatives of `f` and `g` when `B` is bilinear of norm at most `1`:\n`\u2016D^n (x \u21a6 B (f x) (g x))\u2016 \u2264 \u2211_{k \u2264 n} n.choose k \u2016D^k f\u2016 \u2016D^{n-k} g\u2016`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on_succ_iff_fderiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.neg", "description": "The negative of a `C^n` function at a point is `C^n` at this point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.fderiv_within_right", "description": "`fderiv_within \ud835\udd5c f s` is smooth at `x\u2080` within `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_ftaylor_series_up_to_on_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.fderiv_within''", "description": " The most general lemma stating that `x \u21a6 fderiv_within \ud835\udd5c (f x) t (g x)` is `C^n`\nat a point within a set.\nTo show that `x \u21a6 D_yf(x,y)g(x)` (taken within `t`) is `C^m` at `x\u2080` within `s`, we require that\n* `f` is `C^n` at `(x\u2080, g(x\u2080))` within `(s \u222a {x\u2080}) \u00d7 t` for `n \u2265 m+1`.\n* `g` is `C^m` at `x\u2080` within `s`;\n* Derivatives are unique at `g(x)` within `t` for `x` sufficiently close to `x\u2080` within `s \u222a {x\u2080}`;\n* `t` is a neighborhood of `g(x\u2080)` within `g '' s`;", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.cont_diff_on_comp_iff", "description": " Composition by continuous linear equivs on the right respects higher differentiability on\ndomains.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_cont_diff_iff", "description": "Composition by continuous linear equivs on the left respects higher differentiability.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at_ring_inverse", "description": " In a complete normed algebra, the operation of inversion is `C^n`, for all `n`, at each\ninvertible element.  The proof is by induction, bootstrapping using an identity expressing the\nderivative of inversion as a bilinear map of inversion itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.smul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.fderiv_within'", "description": "A special case of `cont_diff_within_at.fderiv_within''` where we require that `s \u2286 g\u207b\u00b9(t)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.continuous_on_fderiv_within_apply", "description": " If a function is at least `C^1`, its bundled derivative (mapping `(x, v)` to `Df(x) v`) is\ncontinuous.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_ftaylor_series_up_to_on.restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.norm_iterated_fderiv_within_comp_right", "description": " Composition with a linear isometry on the right preserves the norm of the iterated derivative\nwithin a set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_iterated_fderiv_within_clm_apply_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at_snd", "description": "The second projection within a domain at a point in a product is `C^\u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.iterated_fderiv_within_comp_right", "description": " The iterated derivative within a set of the composition with a linear equiv on the right is\nobtained by composing the iterated derivative with the linear equiv.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.fderiv", "description": "`x \u21a6 fderiv \ud835\udd5c (f x) (g x)` is continuous.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on_succ_iff_deriv_within", "description": " A function is `C^(n + 1)` on a domain with unique derivatives if and only if it is\ndifferentiable there, and its derivative (formulated with `deriv_within`) is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_iterated_fderiv_within_comp_le_aux", "description": " If the derivatives within a set of `g` at `f x` are bounded by `C`, and the `i`-th derivative\nwithin a set of `f` at `x` is bounded by `D^i` for all `1 \u2264 i \u2264 n`, then the `n`-th derivative\nof `g \u2218 f` is bounded by `n! * C * D^n`.\nThis lemma proves this estimate assuming additionally that two of the spaces live in the same\nuniverse, to make an induction possible. Use instead `norm_iterated_fderiv_within_comp_le` that\nremoves this assumption.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.has_strict_deriv_at", "description": " If a function is `C^n` with `1 \u2264 n` around a point, then the derivative of `f` at this point\nis also a strict derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.norm_iterated_fderiv_within_comp_left", "description": " Composition with a linear isometry equiv on the left preserves the norm of the iterated\nderivative within a set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.add", "description": " The sum of two `C^n` functions within a set at a point is `C^n` within this set\nat this point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.deriv_within", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.sub", "description": "The difference of two `C^n` functions on a domain is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.const_smul", "description": "The scalar multiplication of a constant and a `C^n` on a domain is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.comp", "description": "The composition of `C^n` functions at points in domains is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.exists_lipschitz_on_with_of_nnnorm_lt", "description": " If `f` is `C^1` at `x` and `K > \u2016fderiv \ud835\udd42 f x\u2016`, then `f` is `K`-Lipschitz in a neighborhood of\n`x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on_prod'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_ftaylor_series_up_to_on.has_strict_fderiv_at", "description": "If a function has a Taylor series at order at least 1, then at points in the interior of the\n   domain of definition, the term of order 1 of this series is a strict derivative of `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_fst", "description": "The first projection in a product is `C^\u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry.norm_iterated_fderiv_comp_left", "description": " Composition with a linear isometry on the left preserves the norm of the iterated\nderivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.comp_cont_diff_on\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at_prod'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_succ_iff_deriv", "description": "A function is `C^(n + 1)` if and only if it is differentiable,\n and its derivative (formulated in terms of `deriv`) is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.exists_lipschitz_on_with", "description": "If `f` is `C^1` at `x`, then `f` is Lipschitz in a neighborhood of `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on_top_iff_deriv_within", "description": " A function is `C^\u221e` on a domain with unique derivatives if and only if it is differentiable\nthere, and its derivative (formulated with `deriv_within`) is `C^\u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.fderiv_apply", "description": "`x \u21a6 fderiv \ud835\udd5c (f x) (g x) (k x)` is smooth.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.continuous_linear_map_comp", "description": " Composition by continuous linear maps on the left preserves `C^n` functions in a domain\nat a point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.fst'", "description": "Precomposing `f` with `prod.fst` is `C^n`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.has_strict_deriv_at'", "description": " If a function is `C^n` with `1 \u2264 n` around a point, and its derivative at that point is given to\nus as `f'`, then `f'` is also a strict derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.clm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_cont_diff_at_iff", "description": " Composition by continuous linear equivs on the left respects higher differentiability at a\npoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.iterate_deriv'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_ftaylor_series_up_to_on.continuous_linear_map_comp", "description": " If `f` admits a Taylor series `p` in a set `s`, and `g` is linear, then `g \u2218 f` admits a Taylor\nseries whose `k`-th term is given by `g \u2218 (p k)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_of_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.mul", "description": "The product of two `C^n`functions is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_cont_diff_on_iff", "description": " Composition by continuous linear equivs on the left respects higher differentiability on\ndomains.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at_snd", "description": "The second projection at a point in a product is `C^\u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.comp", "description": "The composition of `C^n` functions on domains is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_iterated_fderiv_within_smul_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "iterated_fderiv_zero_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_snd", "description": "The second projection in a product is `C^\u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on_succ_iff_deriv_of_open", "description": " A function is `C^(n + 1)` on an open domain if and only if it is\ndifferentiable there, and its derivative (formulated with `deriv`) is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.div_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.continuous_deriv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.prod_map", "description": "The product map of two `C^n` functions is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.cont_diff_within_at_comp_iff", "description": " Composition by continuous linear equivs on the right respects higher differentiability at a\npoint in a domain.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.continuous_linear_map_comp", "description": " Composition by continuous linear maps on the left preserves `C^n` functions in a domain\nat a point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry.cont_diff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_ftaylor_series_up_to_on.exists_lipschitz_on_with", "description": " If `f` has a formal Taylor series `p` up to order `1` on `{x} \u222a s`, where `s` is a convex set,\nthen `f` is Lipschitz in a neighborhood of `x` within `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry.norm_iterated_fderiv_within_comp_left", "description": " Composition with a linear isometry on the left preserves the norm of the iterated\nderivative within a set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_bounded_linear_map.cont_diff", "description": "Unbundled bounded linear functions are `C^\u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.neg", "description": "The negative of a `C^n`function is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.comp_cont_diff_on", "description": "The composition of a `C^n` function on a domain with a `C^n` function is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_homeomorph.cont_diff_at_symm", "description": " If `f` is a local homeomorphism and the point `a` is in its target,\nand if `f` is `n` times continuously differentiable at `f.symm a`,\nand if the derivative at `f.symm a` is a continuous linear equivalence,\nthen `f.symm` is `n` times continuously differentiable at the point `a`.\n\nThis is one of the easy parts of the inverse function theorem: it assumes that we already have\nan inverse function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_bounded_bilinear_map.cont_diff", "description": "Bilinear functions are `C^\u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.sub", "description": "The difference of two `C^n` functions at a point is `C^n` at this point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.comp'", "description": "The composition of `C^n` functions on domains is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_iterated_fderiv_within_comp_le", "description": " If the derivatives within a set of `g` at `f x` are bounded by `C`, and the `i`-th derivative\nwithin a set of `f` at `x` is bounded by `D^i` for all `1 \u2264 i \u2264 n`, then the `n`-th derivative\nof `g \u2218 f` is bounded by `n! * C * D^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.add", "description": "The sum of two `C^n` functions at a point is `C^n` at this point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.deriv_of_open", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.snd", "description": "Postcomposing `f` with `prod.snd` is `C^n` at `x`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "iterated_fderiv_const_smul_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.cont_diff_comp_iff", "description": "Composition by continuous linear equivs on the right respects higher differentiability.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.comp\u2083", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.add", "description": "The sum of two `C^n` functions on a domain is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on_of_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "iterated_fderiv_const_of_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_ftaylor_series_up_to_on.prod", "description": " If two functions `f` and `g` admit Taylor series `p` and `q` in a set `s`, then the cartesian\nproduct of `f` and `g` admits the cartesian product of `p` and `q` as a Taylor series.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.continuous_on_deriv_of_open", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_succ_iff_fderiv_apply", "description": " This is a useful lemma to prove that a certain operation preserves functions being `C^n`.\nWhen you do induction on `n`, this gives a useful characterization of a function being `C^(n+1)`,\nassuming you have already computed the derivative. The advantage of this version over\n`cont_diff_succ_iff_fderiv` is that both occurences of `cont_diff` are for functions with the same\ndomain and codomain (`E` and `F`). This is not the case for `cont_diff_succ_iff_fderiv`, which\noften requires an inconvenient need to generalize `F`, which results in universe issues\n(see the discussion in the section of `cont_diff.comp`).\n\nThis lemma avoids these universe issues, but only applies for finite dimensional `E`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.has_fderiv_within_at_nhds", "description": "One direction of `cont_diff_within_at_succ_iff_has_fderiv_within_at`, but where all derivatives\nare taken within the same set. Version for partial derivatives / functions with parameters.\nIf `f x` is a `C^n+1` family of functions and `g x` is a `C^n` family of points, then the\n derivative of `f x` at `g x` depends in a `C^n` way on `x`. We give a general version of this fact\n relative to sets which may not have unique derivatives, in the following form.\nIf `f : E \u00d7 F \u2192 G` is `C^n+1` at `(x\u2080, g(x\u2080))` in `(s \u222a {x\u2080}) \u00d7 t \u2286 E \u00d7 F` and `g : E \u2192 F` is\n`C^n` at `x\u2080` within some set `s \u2286 E`, then there is a function `f' : E \u2192 F \u2192L[\ud835\udd5c] G`\nthat is `C^n` at `x\u2080` within `s` such that for all `x` sufficiently close to `x\u2080` within\n`s \u222a {x\u2080}` the function `y \u21a6 f x y` has derivative `f' x` at `g x` within `t \u2286 F`.\nFor convenience, we return an explicit set of `x`'s where this holds that is a subset of\n`s \u222a {x\u2080}`.\nWe need one additional condition, namely that `t` is a neighborhood of `g(x\u2080)` within `g '' s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "iterated_fderiv_add_apply'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "iterated_fderiv_neg_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.fderiv_within", "description": "A special case of `cont_diff_within_at.fderiv_within'` where we require that `x\u2080 \u2208 s` and there\n are unique derivatives everywhere within `t`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "iterated_fderiv_within_add_apply", "description": " The iterated derivative of the sum of two functions is the sum of the iterated derivatives.\nSee also `iterated_fderiv_within_add_apply'`, which uses the spelling `(\u03bb x, f x + g x)`\ninstead of `f + g`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.fst", "description": "Postcomposing `f` with `prod.fst` is `C^n` at `(x, y)`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_iterated_fderiv_smul_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_prod_mk_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.smul", "description": "The scalar multiplication of two `C^n` functions at a point is `C^n` at this point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_ftaylor_series_up_to_on_pi'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.cont_diff_fderiv_apply", "description": "The bundled derivative of a `C^{n+1}` function is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "iterated_fderiv_succ_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_prod_assoc", "description": "The natural equivalence `(E \u00d7 F) \u00d7 G \u2243 E \u00d7 (F \u00d7 G)` is smooth.\n\nWarning: if you think you need this lemma, it is likely that you can simplify your proof by\nreformulating the lemma that you're applying next using the tips in\nNote [continuity lemma statement]", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.prod", "description": "The cartesian product of `C^n` functions at a point in a domain is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.comp_cont_diff_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_iterated_fderiv_clm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.prod_map", "description": "The product map of two `C^n` functions on a set is `C^n` on the product set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.snd'", "description": "Precomposing `f` with `prod.snd` is `C^n`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.comp_cont_diff_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on_fderiv_within_apply", "description": "The bundled derivative of a `C^{n+1}` function is `C^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "iterated_fderiv_add_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at_fst", "description": "The first projection within a domain at a point in a product is `C^\u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.snd''", "description": "Precomposing `f` with `prod.snd` is `C^n` at `x : E \u00d7 F`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_antidiagonal_choose_succ_mul", "description": " The sum along the antidiagonal of `(n+1).choose i * f i j` can be split into two sums along the\nantidiagonal at rank `n`, respectively of `n.choose i * f i (j+1)` and `n.choose j * f (i+1) j`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on_succ_of_fderiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_cont_diff_within_at_iff", "description": " Composition by continuous linear equivs on the left respects higher differentiability at a\npoint in a domain.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.comp_cont_diff_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at_prod'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.cont_diff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_homeomorph.cont_diff_at_symm_deriv", "description": " Let `f` be a local homeomorphism of a nontrivially normed field, let `a` be a point in its\ntarget. if `f` is `n` times continuously differentiable at `f.symm a`, and if the derivative at\n`f.symm a` is nonzero, then `f.symm` is `n` times continuously differentiable at the point `a`.\n\nThis is one of the easy parts of the inverse function theorem: it assumes that we already have\nan inverse function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "iterated_fderiv_within_neg_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.eq_div_of_mul_eq_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd_zero_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.dvd_lcm_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.mod_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.mod_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.mul_div_mul_cancel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.mul_div_cancel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.eq_div_of_mul_eq_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.div_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.zero_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.dvd_mod_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd_mul_lcm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.mul_div_mul_comm_of_dvd_dvd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.dvd_lcm_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.xgcd_aux_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.is_domain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd_eq_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.mul_div_cancel_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.xgcd_aux_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.div_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.mul_div_cancel'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd_eq_gcd_ab", "description": "An explicit version of **B\u00e9zout's lemma** for Euclidean domains.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd_dvd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd_dvd_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.xgcd_aux_P", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.zero_mod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.no_zero_divisors", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.mul_div_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.lcm_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.dvd_div_of_mul_dvd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.lcm_zero_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.mod_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.lcm_zero_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.lcm_dvd_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd_one_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.lcm_dvd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.div_dvd_of_dvd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd_dvd_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.dvd_gcd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_units.is_add_regular", "description": "If `R` is an additive monoid, an element in `add_units R` is add-regular.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_regular_iff_ne_zero", "description": "In a non-trivial integral domain, an element is regular iff it is non-zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_add_right_regular", "description": "An add-right-regular element is an element `c` such that addition on the right by `c`\nis injective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_right_regular", "description": " A right-regular element is an element `c` such that multiplication on the right by `c`\nis injective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_regular_mul_iff", "description": "A product is regular if and only if the factors are.", "p": "mathli