nctor.strong_epi_map_iff_strong_epi_of_is_equivalence", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.split_epi_category_imp_of_is_equivalence", "description": " If `F : C \u2964 D` is an equivalence of categories and `C` is a `split_epi_category`,\nthen `D` also is.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.mono_of_mono_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.reflects_epimorphisms.iso_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.col", "description": "The `j`-th column of a Young diagram consists of the cells whose second coordinate is `j`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.has_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.mem_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.of_row_lens", "description": "Young diagram from a sorted list", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.mem_transpose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.mem_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.exists_not_mem_row", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.equiv_list_row_lens", "description": " Equivalence between Young diagrams and weakly decreasing lists of positive natural numbers.\nA Young diagram `\u03bc` is equivalent to a list of row lengths.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.transpose_eq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.row_eq_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.equiv_list_row_lens_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.transpose_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.order_bot", "description": "The empty Young diagram is (\u22a5 : young_diagram).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.mk_mem_row_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.row_lens", "description": "List of row lengths of a Young diagram", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.coe_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.mem_col_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.transpose_order_iso_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.le_of_transpose_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.not_mem_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.mk_mem_col_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.row_len", "description": "Length of a row of a Young diagram", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.mem_cells", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.col_len", "description": "Length of a column of a Young diagram", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.mem_iff_lt_row_len", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.row_len_transpose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.cells_subset_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.exists_not_mem_col", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.decidable_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.of_row_lens_to_row_lens_eq_self", "description": "The left_inv direction of the equivalence", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.up_left_mem", "description": "In \"English notation\", a Young diagram is drawn so that (i1, j1) \u2264 (i2, j2)\n   means (i1, j1) is weakly up-and-left of (i2, j2).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.mem_cells_of_row_lens", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.mem_row_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.distrib_lattice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.col_len_eq_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.col_len_anti", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.prod.set_like", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.cells_ssubset_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.mem_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.cells_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.transpose_order_iso_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.coe_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.row_len_of_row_lens", "description": "The length of the `i`th row in `of_row_lens w hw` is the `i`th entry of `w`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.card", "description": "Cardinality of a Young diagram", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.mem_of_row_lens", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.row_lens_length_of_row_lens", "description": "The number of rows in `of_row_lens w hw` is the length of `w`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.transpose_transpose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.col_len_transpose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.transpose_le_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.transpose_eq_iff_eq_transpose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.length_row_lens", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.nth_le_row_lens", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.row_lens_of_row_lens_eq_self", "description": "The right_inv direction of the equivalence", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.cells_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.transpose", "description": "The `transpose` of a Young diagram is obtained by swapping i's with j's.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram", "description": " A Young diagram is a finite collection of cells on the `\u2115 \u00d7 \u2115` grid such that whenever\na cell is present, so are all the ones above and to the left of it. Like matrices, an `(i, j)` cell\nis a cell in row `i` and column `j`, where rows are enumerated downward and columns rightward.\n\nYoung diagrams are modeled as finite sets in `\u2115 \u00d7 \u2115` that are lower sets with respect to the\nstandard order on products.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.cells", "description": " A Young diagram is a finite collection of cells on the `\u2115 \u00d7 \u2115` grid such that whenever\na cell is present, so are all the ones above and to the left of it. Like matrices, an `(i, j)` cell\nis a cell in row `i` and column `j`, where rows are enumerated downward and columns rightward.\n\nYoung diagrams are modeled as finite sets in `\u2115 \u00d7 \u2115` that are lower sets with respect to the\nstandard order on products.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.is_lower_set", "description": " A Young diagram is a finite collection of cells on the `\u2115 \u00d7 \u2115` grid such that whenever\na cell is present, so are all the ones above and to the left of it. Like matrices, an `(i, j)` cell\nis a cell in row `i` and column `j`, where rows are enumerated downward and columns rightward.\n\nYoung diagrams are modeled as finite sets in `\u2115 \u00d7 \u2115` that are lower sets with respect to the\nstandard order on products.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.col_eq_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.row", "description": "The `i`-th row of a Young diagram consists of the cells whose first coordinate is `i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.cells_of_row_lens", "description": "The cells making up a `young_diagram` from a list of row lengths", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.cells_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.pos_of_mem_row_lens", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.row_len_eq_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.has_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.row_lens_sorted", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.mem_iff_lt_col_len", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.row_len_anti", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.transpose_order_iso", "description": "Transposing Young diagrams is an `order_iso`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.coe_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "young_diagram.equiv_list_row_lens_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bundled_hom.concrete_category", "description": " A category given by `bundled_hom` is a concrete category.\n\nThis instance generates the type-class problem `bundled_hom ?m` (which is why this is marked as\n`[nolint]`). Currently that is not a problem, as there are almost no instances of `bundled_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bundled_hom.parent_projection", "description": "We use the empty `parent_projection` class to label functions like `comm_monoid.to_monoid`,\nwhich we would like to use to automatically construct `bundled_hom` instances from.\n\nOnce we've set up `Mon` as the category of bundled monoids,\nthis allows us to set up `CommMon` by defining an instance\n```instance : parent_projection (comm_monoid.to_monoid) := \u27e8\u27e9```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bundled_hom.category", "description": " Every `@bundled_hom c _` defines a category with objects in `bundled c`.\n\nThis instance generates the type-class problem `bundled_hom ?m` (which is why this is marked as\n`[nolint]`). Currently that is not a problem, as there are almost no instances of `bundled_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bundled_hom.map_hom", "description": "The `hom` corresponding to first forgetting along `F`, then taking the `hom` associated to `c`.\n\nFor typical usage, see the construction of `CommMon` from `Mon`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bundled_hom.map", "description": "Construct the `bundled_hom` induced by a map between type classes.\nThis is useful for building categories such as `CommMon` from `Mon`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bundled_hom", "description": " Class for bundled homs. Note that the arguments order follows that of lemmas for `monoid_hom`.\nThis way we can use `\u27e8@monoid_hom.to_fun, @monoid_hom.id ...\u27e9` in an instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bundled_hom.to_fun", "description": " Class for bundled homs. Note that the arguments order follows that of lemmas for `monoid_hom`.\nThis way we can use `\u27e8@monoid_hom.to_fun, @monoid_hom.id ...\u27e9` in an instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bundled_hom.id", "description": " Class for bundled homs. Note that the arguments order follows that of lemmas for `monoid_hom`.\nThis way we can use `\u27e8@monoid_hom.to_fun, @monoid_hom.id ...\u27e9` in an instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bundled_hom.comp", "description": " Class for bundled homs. Note that the arguments order follows that of lemmas for `monoid_hom`.\nThis way we can use `\u27e8@monoid_hom.to_fun, @monoid_hom.id ...\u27e9` in an instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bundled_hom.hom_ext", "description": " Class for bundled homs. Note that the arguments order follows that of lemmas for `monoid_hom`.\nThis way we can use `\u27e8@monoid_hom.to_fun, @monoid_hom.id ...\u27e9` in an instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bundled_hom.id_to_fun", "description": " Class for bundled homs. Note that the arguments order follows that of lemmas for `monoid_hom`.\nThis way we can use `\u27e8@monoid_hom.to_fun, @monoid_hom.id ...\u27e9` in an instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bundled_hom.comp_to_fun", "description": " Class for bundled homs. Note that the arguments order follows that of lemmas for `monoid_hom`.\nThis way we 