on": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.interchange", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.interleave_even_odd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.tail_eq_drop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.corec_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.cons_injective_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.mem_of_nth_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.homomorphism", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.even_cons_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.tail_even", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.map_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.head_iterate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.tail_inits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.tail_zip", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.cons_append_stream", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.tail_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.map_eq_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.mem_interleave_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.take_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.length_take", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.drop_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.nth_odd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.map_tail", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.nth_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.nth_nats", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.head_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.zip_inits_tails", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.append_append_stream", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.const_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.corec'_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.map_append_stream", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.nats_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.inits_tail", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.nth_succ_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.cycle_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.corec_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.identity", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.nth_succ_iterate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.mem_of_mem_odd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.tail_iterate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.tails_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.cons_injective_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.enum_eq_zip", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.mem_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.is_pushout", "description": "A type-class stating that the following diagram of scalar towers\nR  \u2192  S\n\u2193     \u2193\nR' \u2192  S'\nis a pushout diagram (i.e. `S' = S \u2297[R] R'`)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.is_pushout.out", "description": "A type-class stating that the following diagram of scalar towers\nR  \u2192  S\n\u2193     \u2193\nR' \u2192  S'\nis a pushout diagram (i.e. `S' = S \u2297[R] R'`)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_tensor_product", "description": "Given a bilinear map `f : M\u2081 \u2192\u2097[R] M\u2082 \u2192\u2097[R] M`, `is_tensor_product f` means that\n`M` is the tensor product of `M\u2081` and `M\u2082` via `f`.\nThis is defined by requiring the lift `M\u2081 \u2297[R] M\u2082 \u2192 M` to be bijective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_base_change.alg_hom_ext'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.is_pushout.comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_base_change.alg_hom_ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_base_change.iff_lift_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.is_pushout_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_base_change.comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_base_change.of_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.is_pushout.symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_tensor_product.map_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_base_change", "description": " Given an `R`-algebra `S` and an `R`-module `M`, an `S`-module `N` together with a map\n`f : M \u2192\u2097[R] N` is the base change of `M` to `S` if the map `S \u00d7 M \u2192 N, (s, m) \u21a6 s \u2022 f m` is the\ntensor product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.is_pushout'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_base_change.equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.lift_alg_hom_comp_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_base_change.lift_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_base_change.lift_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_base_change.of_lift_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_tensor_product.equiv", "description": "If `M` is the tensor product of `M\u2081` and `M\u2082`, it is linearly equivalent to `M\u2081 \u2297[R] M\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_tensor_product.equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_tensor_product.equiv_to_linear_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_tensor_product.lift", "description": " If `M` is the tensor product of `M\u2081` and `M\u2082`, we may lift a bilinear map `M\u2081 \u2192\u2097[R] M\u2082 \u2192\u2097[R] M'`\nto a `M \u2192\u2097[R] M'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.is_pushout.alg_hom_ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.pushout_desc_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_tensor_product.lift_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_tensor_product.equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.is_pushout", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.is_base_change", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_base_change.equiv_tmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.lift_alg_hom_comp_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_base_change.lift", "description": " Suppose `f : M \u2192\u2097[R] N` is the base change of `M` along `R \u2192 S`. Then any `R`-linear map from\n`M` to an `S`-module factors thorugh `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_tensor_product.map", "description": "The tensor product of a pair of linear maps between modules.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_base_change.equiv", "description": "The base change of `M` along `R \u2192 S` is linearly equivalent to `S \u2297[R] M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_base_change.induction_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.pushout_desc", "description": "If `S' = S \u2297[R] R'`, then any pair of `R`-algebra homomorphisms `f : S \u2192 A` and `g : R' \u2192 A`\nsuch that `f x` and `g y` commutes for all `x, y` descends to a (unique) homomoprhism `S' \u2192 A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_tensor_product.induction_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.pushout_desc_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.pushout_desc_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.is_tensor_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "environment", "description": "An __environment__ contains all of the declarations and notation that have been defined so far.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.add_ginductive", "description": "Add a new general inductive declaration to the environment.\n This has the same effect as a `inductive` in the file, including generating\n all the auxiliary definitions, as well as triggering mutual/nested inductive\n compilation, by contrast to `environment.add_inductive` which only adds the\n core axioms supported by the kernel.\n\n The `inds` argument should be a list of inductives in the mutual family.\n The first argument is a pair of the name of the type being constructed\n and the type of this inductive family (not including the params).\n The second argument is a list of intro rules, specified by a name, an\n `implicit_infer_kind` giving the implicitness of the params for this constructor,\n and an expression with the type of the constructor (not including the params).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.decl_noncomputable_reason", "description": " `decl_pos env d` returns the name of a declaration that d inherits\nnoncomputability from, or `none` if it is computable.\n\nNote that this also returns `none` on `axiom`s and `constant`s. These can be detected by using\n`environment.get_decl` and `declaration.is_axiom` and `declaration.is_constant`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.is_projection", "description": "See the docstring for `projection_info`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.is_protected", "description": "check if `n` is the name of a protected declaration", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.get_namespaces", "description": "Retrieve all registered namespaces", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.get_eqn_lemmas_for", "description": "Gets the equation lemmas for the declaration `n`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.fold", "description": "Fold over declarations in the environment.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.inductive_dep_elim", "description": "Return tt iff the inductive datatype recursor supports dependent elimination", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.unfold_all_macros", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.is_definition", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.add_inductive", "description": "Add a new inductive datatype to the environment\n  name, universe parameters, number of parameters, type, constructors (name and type), is_meta", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.get_ext_eqn_lemmas_for", "description": "Gets the equation lemmas for the declaration `n`, including lemmas for match statements, etc.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.is_namespace", "description": "Return tt iff the given name is a namespace", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.is_inductive", "description": "Return tt iff the given name is an inductive datatype", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.add_namespace", "description": "Register the given name as a namespace, making it available to the `open` command", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.inductive_num_params", "description": "Return the number of parameters of the inductive datatype", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.unfold_untrusted_macros", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.has_repr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.inductive_num_indices", "description": "Return the number of indices of the inductive datatype", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.in_current_file", "description": "Return true if 'n' has been declared in the current file", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.relation_info", "description": "`relation_info env n` returns some value if n is marked as a relation in the given environment.\n  the tuple contains: total number of arguments of the relation, lhs position and rhs position.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.is_refl_app", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.trans_for", "description": "`trans_for env R` returns the name of the transitivity theorem for the relation R", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.intro_rule", "description": "One introduction rule in an inductive declaration", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.intro_rule.constr", "description": "One introduction rule in an inductive declaration", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.intro_rule.type", "description": "One introduction rule in an inductive declaration", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.intro_rule.infer", "description": "One introduction rule in an inductive declaration", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.is_ginductive", "description": " Functionally equivalent to `is_inductive`.\n\nTechnically, this works by checking if the name is in the ginductive environment\nextension which is outside the kernel, whereas `is_inductive` works by looking at the kernel extension.\nBut there are no `is_inductive`s which are not `is_ginductive`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.recursor_of", "description": "Return the recursor of the given inductive datatype", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.fingerprint", "description": "The fingerprint of the environment is a hash formed from all of the declarations in the environment.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.decl_olean", "description": "`decl_olean env d` returns the name of the .olean file where d was defined.\n  The result is none if d was not defined in an imported file.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.decl_pos", "description": "`decl_pos env d` returns the source location of d if available.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.contains", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.projection_info", "description": "Consider a type `\u03c8` which is an inductive datatype using a single constructor `mk (a : \u03b1) (b : \u03b2) : \u03c8`.\nLean will automatically make two projection functions `a : \u03c8 \u2192 \u03b1`, `b : \u03c8 \u2192 \u03b2`.\nLean tags these declarations as __projections__.\nThis helps the simplifier / rewriter not have to expand projectors.\nEg `a (mk x y)` will automatically reduce to `x`.\nIf you `extend` a structure, all of the projections on the parent will also be created for the child.\nProjections are also treated differently in the VM for efficiency.\n\nNote that projections have nothing to do with the dot `mylist.map` syntax.\n\nYou can find out if a declaration is a projection using `environment.is_projection` which returns `projection_info`.\n\nData for a projection declaration:\n- `cname`    is the name of the constructor associated with the projection.\n- `nparams`  is the number of constructor parameters. Eg `and.intro` has two type parameters.\n- `idx`      is the parameter being projected by this projection.\n- `is_class` is tt iff this is a typeclass projection.\n\n### Examples:\n\n- `and.right` is a projection with ``{cname := `and.intro, nparams := 2, idx := 1, is_class := ff}``\n- `ordered_ring.neg` is a projection with ``{cname := `ordered_ring.mk, nparams := 1, idx := 5, is_class := tt}``.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.projection_info.cname", "description": "Consider a type `\u03c8` which is an inductive datatype using a single constructor `mk (a : \u03b1) (b : \u03b2) : \u03c8`.\nLean will automatically make two projection functions `a : \u03c8 \u2192 \u03b1`, `b : \u03c8 \u2192 \u03b2`.\nLean tags these declarations as __projections__.\nThis helps the simplifier / rewriter not have to expand projectors.\nEg `a (mk x y)` will automatically reduce to `x`.\nIf you `extend` a structure, all of the projections on the parent will also be created for the child.\nProjections are also treated differently in the VM for efficiency.\n\nNote that projections have nothing to do with the dot `mylist.map` syntax.\n\nYou can find out if a declaration is a projection using `environment.is_projection` which returns `projection_info`.\n\nData for a projection declaration:\n- `cname`    is the name of the constructor associated with the projection.\n- `nparams`  is the number of constructor parameters. Eg `and.intro` has two type parameters.\n- `idx`      is the parameter being projected by this projection.\n- `is_class` is tt iff this is a typeclass projection.\n\n### Examples:\n\n- `and.right` is a projection with ``{cname := `and.intro, nparams := 2, idx := 1, is_class := ff}``\n- `ordered_ring.neg` is a projection with ``{cname := `ordered_ring.mk, nparams := 1, idx := 5, is_class := tt}``.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.projection_info.nparams", "description": "Consider a type `\u03c8` which is an inductive datatype using a single constructor `mk (a : \u03b1) (b : \u03b2) : \u03c8`.\nLean will automatically make two projection functions `a : \u03c8 \u2192 \u03b1`, `b : \u03c8 \u2192 \u03b2`.\nLean tags these declarations as __projections__.\nThis helps the simplifier / rewriter not have to expand projectors.\nEg `a (mk x y)` will automatically reduce to `x`.\nIf you `extend` a structure, all of the projections on the parent will also be created for the child.\nProjections are also treated differently in the VM for efficiency.\n\nNote that projections have nothing to do with the dot `mylist.map` syntax.\n\nYou can find out if a declaration is a projection using `environment.is_projection` which returns `projection_info`.\n\nData for a projection declaration:\n- `cname`    is the name of the constructor associated with the projection.\n- `nparams`  is the number of constructor parameters. Eg `and.intro` has two type parameters.\n- `idx`      is the parameter being projected by this projection.\n- `is_class` is tt iff this is a typeclass projection.\n\n### Examples:\n\n- `and.right` is a projection with ``{cname := `and.intro, nparams := 2, idx := 1, is_class := ff}``\n- `ordered_ring.neg` is a projection with ``{cname := `ordered_ring.mk, nparams := 1, idx := 5, is_class := tt}``.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.projection_info.idx", "description": "Consider a type `\u03c8` which is an inductive datatype using a single constructor `mk (a : \u03b1) (b : \u03b2) : \u03c8`.\nLean will automatically make two projection functions `a : \u03c8 \u2192 \u03b1`, `b : \u03c8 \u2192 \u03b2`.\nLean tags these declarations as __projections__.\nThis helps the simplifier / rewriter not have to expand projectors.\nEg `a (mk x y)` will automatically reduce to `x`.\nIf you `extend` a structure, all of the projections on the parent will also be created for the child.\nProjections are also treated differently in the VM for efficiency.\n\nNote that projections have nothing to do with the dot `mylist.map` syntax.\n\nYou can find out if a declaration is a projection using `environment.is_projection` which returns `projection_info`.\n\nData for a projection declaration:\n- `cname`    is the name of the constructor associated with the projection.\n- `nparams`  is the number of constructor parameters. Eg `and.intro` has two type parameters.\n- `idx`      is the parameter being projected by this projection.\n- `is_class` is tt iff this is a typeclass projection.\n\n### Examples:\n\n- `and.right` is a projection with ``{cname := `and.intro, nparams := 2, idx := 1, is_class := ff}``\n- `ordered_ring.neg` is a projection with ``{cname := `ordered_ring.mk, nparams := 1, idx := 5, is_class := tt}``.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.projection_info.is_class", "description": "Consider a type `\u03c8` which is an inductive datatype using a single constructor `mk (a : \u03b1) (b : \u03b2) : \u03c8`.\nLean will automatically make two projection functions `a : \u03c8 \u2192 \u03b1`, `b : \u03c8 \u2192 \u03b2`.\nLean tags these declarations as __projections__.\nThis helps the simplifier / rewriter not have to expand projectors.\nEg `a (mk x y)` will automatically reduce to `x`.\nIf you `extend` a structure, all of the projections on the parent will also be created for the child.\nProjections are also treated differently in the VM for efficiency.\n\nNote that projections have nothing to do with the dot `mylist.map` syntax.\n\nYou can find out if a declaration is a projection using `environment.is_projection` which returns `projection_info`.\n\nData for a projection declaration:\n- `cname`    is the name of the constructor associated with the projection.\n- `nparams`  is the number of constructor parameters. Eg `and.intro` has two type parameters.\n- `idx`      is the parameter being projected by this projection.\n- `is_class` is tt iff this is a typeclass projection.\n\n### Examples:\n\n- `and.right` is a projection with ``{cname := `and.intro, nparams := 2, idx := 1, is_class := ff}``\n- `ordered_ring.neg` is a projection with ``{cname := `ordered_ring.mk, nparams := 1, idx := 5, is_class := tt}``.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.get", "description": "Retrieve a declaration from the environment", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.refl_for", "description": "`refl_for env R` returns the name of the reflexivity theorem for the relation R", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.is_recursor", "description": "Return tt iff the given name is a recursor", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.mark_namespace_as_open", "description": "Mark a namespace as open", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.get_class_attribute_symbols", "description": "`get_class_attribute_symbols env attr_name` return symbols\n  occurring in instances of type classes tagged with the attribute `attr_name`.\n  Example: [algebra]", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.structure_fields", "description": "Return the fields of the structure with the given name, or `none` if it is not a structure", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.constructors_of", "description": "Return the constructors of the inductive datatype with the given name", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.execute_open", "description": "Modify the environment as if `open %%name` had been parsed", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.add_protected", "description": "add declaration `d` and make it protected", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.add", "description": "Add a new declaration to the environment", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.trust_lvl", "description": "Return the trust level of the given environment", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.implicit_infer_kind", "description": "A marking on the binders of structures and inductives indicating\n  how this constructor should mark its parameters.\n\n      inductive foo\n      | one {} : foo -> foo   -- relaxed_implicit\n      | two ( ) : foo -> foo  -- explicit\n      | two [] : foo -> foo   -- implicit\n      | three : foo -> foo    -- relaxed implicit (default)", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.implicit_infer_kind.implicit", "description": "A marking on the binders of structures and inductives indicating\n  how this constructor should mark its parameters.\n\n      inductive foo\n      | one {} : foo -> foo   -- relaxed_implicit\n      | two ( ) : foo -> foo  -- explicit\n      | two [] : foo -> foo   -- implicit\n      | three : foo -> foo    -- relaxed implicit (default)", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.implicit_infer_kind.relaxed_implicit", "description": "A marking on the binders of structures and inductives indicating\n  how this constructor should mark its parameters.\n\n      inductive foo\n      | one {} : foo -> foo   -- relaxed_implicit\n      | two ( ) : foo -> foo  -- explicit\n      | two [] : foo -> foo   -- implicit\n      | three : foo -> foo    -- relaxed implicit (default)", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.implicit_infer_kind.none", "description": "A marking on the binders of structures and inductives indicating\n  how this constructor should mark its parameters.\n\n      inductive foo\n      | one {} : foo -> foo   -- relaxed_implicit\n      | two ( ) : foo -> foo  -- explicit\n      | two [] : foo -> foo   -- implicit\n      | three : foo -> foo    -- relaxed implicit (default)", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.inductive_type_of", "description": "Return the name of the inductive datatype of the given constructor.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.is_constructor_app", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.is_constructor", "description": "Return tt iff the given name is a constructor", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.mk_std", "description": "Create a standard environment using the given trust level", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.add_defn_eqns", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.implicit_infer_kind.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.symm_for", "description": "`symm_for env R` returns the name of the symmetry theorem for the relation R", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.add_eqn_lemma", "description": "Adds the equation lemma `n`.\nIt is added for the declaration `t.pi_codomain.get_app_fn.const_name` where `t` is the type of the equation lemma.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.is_recursive", "description": "Return tt iff the given name is a recursive inductive datatype", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.mk_protected", "description": "make declaration `n` protected", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_cond_left_inv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_cond_left_inv.left_inv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_total", "description": " `is_total X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is total, that is, that for any\n`x y : X` we have `r x y` or `r y x`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_total.total", "description": " `is_total X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is total, that is, that for any\n`x y : X` we have `r x y` or `r y x`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_strict_weak_order_of_is_total_preorder", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_symm_op_of_is_symm", "description": "The opposite of a symmetric relation is symmetric.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "eqv_lt_iff_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "refl", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_cond_right_inv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_cond_right_inv.right_inv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_symm_op", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_symm_op.symm_op", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "irrefl_of", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_preorder", "description": " `is_preorder X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is a pre-order, that is, reflexive\nand transitive.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_preorder.to_is_refl", "description": " `is_preorder X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is a pre-order, that is, reflexive\nand transitive.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_preorder.to_is_trans", "description": " `is_preorder X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is a pre-order, that is, reflexive\nand transitive.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "antisymm", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_asymm_of_is_trans_of_is_irrefl", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_right_id", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_right_id.right_id", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_distinct", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_distinct.distinct", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_refl", "description": "`is_refl X r` means the binary relation `r` on `X` is reflexive.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_refl.refl", "description": "`is_refl X r` means the binary relation `r` on `X` is reflexive.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_left_cancel", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_left_cancel.left_cancel", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_associative", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_associative.assoc", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "eq_of_incomp", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_trans", "description": "`is_trans X r` means the binary relation `r` on `X` is transitive.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_trans.trans", "description": "`is_trans X r` means the binary relation `r` on `X` is transitive.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "asymm", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_left_null", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_left_null.left_null", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_irrefl", "description": " `is_irrefl X r` means the binary relation `r` on `X` is irreflexive (that is, `r x x` never\nholds).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_irrefl.irrefl", "description": " `is_irrefl X r` means the binary relation `r` on `X` is irreflexive (that is, `r x x` never\nholds).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_idempotent", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_idempotent.idempotent", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "strict_weak_order.etrans", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "lt_of_incomp_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_left_id", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_left_id.left_id", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_strict_weak_order", "description": " `is_strict_weak_order X lt` means that the binary relation `lt` on `X` is a strict weak order,\nthat is, `is_strict_order X lt` and `is_incomp_trans X lt`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_strict_weak_order.to_is_strict_order", "description": " `is_strict_weak_order X lt` means that the binary relation `lt` on `X` is a strict weak order,\nthat is, `is_strict_order X lt` and `is_incomp_trans X lt`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_strict_weak_order.to_is_incomp_trans", "description": " `is_strict_weak_order X lt` means that the binary relation `lt` on `X` is a strict weak order,\nthat is, `is_strict_order X lt` and `is_incomp_trans X lt`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "incomp_trans", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "trans", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "asymm_of", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_right_cancel", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_right_cancel.right_cancel", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_commutative", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_commutative.comm", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_linear_order", "description": " `is_linear_order X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is a linear order, that is,\n`is_partial_order X r` and `is_total X r`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_linear_order.to_is_partial_order", "description": " `is_linear_order X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is a linear order, that is,\n`is_partial_order X r` and `is_total X r`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_linear_order.to_is_total", "description": " `is_linear_order X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is a linear order, that is,\n`is_partial_order X r` and `is_total X r`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_left_inv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_left_inv.left_inv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "strict_weak_order.equiv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "trichotomous_of", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_right_inv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_right_inv.right_inv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_symm", "description": "`is_symm X r` means the binary relation `r` on `X` is symmetric.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_symm.symm", "description": "`is_symm X r` means the binary relation `r` on `X` is symmetric.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "incomp_iff_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_antisymm", "description": "`is_antisymm X r` means the binary relation `r` on `X` is antisymmetric.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_antisymm.antisymm", "description": "`is_antisymm X r` means the binary relation `r` on `X` is antisymmetric.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "symm_of", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "irrefl", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "strict_weak_order.is_equiv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "not_lt_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_per", "description": " `is_per X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is a partial equivalence relation, that\nis, `is_symm X r` and `is_trans X r`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_per.to_is_symm", "description": " `is_per X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is a partial equivalence relation, that\nis, `is_symm X r` and `is_trans X r`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_per.to_is_trans", "description": " `is_per X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is a partial equivalence relation, that\nis, `is_symm X r` and `is_trans X r`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "symm", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "strict_weak_order.esymm", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_strict_order", "description": " `is_strict_order X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is a strict order, that is,\n`is_irrefl X r` and `is_trans X r`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_strict_order.to_is_irrefl", "description": " `is_strict_order X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is a strict order, that is,\n`is_irrefl X r` and `is_trans X r`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_strict_order.to_is_trans", "description": " `is_strict_order X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is a strict order, that is,\n`is_irrefl X r` and `is_trans X r`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "eq_is_equiv", "description": "Equality is an equivalence relation.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_symm_op_of_is_commutative", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "incomp_trans_of", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "strict_weak_order.erefl", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_strict_total_order", "description": " `is_strict_total_order X lt` means that the binary relation `lt` on `X` is a strict total order,\nthat is, `is_trichotomous X lt` and `is_strict_order X lt`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_strict_total_order.to_is_trichotomous", "description": " `is_strict_total_order X lt` means that the binary relation `lt` on `X` is a strict total order,\nthat is, `is_trichotomous X lt` and `is_strict_order X lt`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_strict_total_order.to_is_strict_order", "description": " `is_strict_total_order X lt` means that the binary relation `lt` on `X` is a strict total order,\nthat is, `is_trichotomous X lt` and `is_strict_order X lt`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "total_of", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_total_preorder", "description": "`is_total_preorder X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is total and a preorder.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_total_preorder.to_is_trans", "description": "`is_total_preorder X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is total and a preorder.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_total_preorder.to_is_total", "description": "`is_total_preorder X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is total and a preorder.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_asymm", "description": " `is_asymm X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is asymmetric, that is,\n`r a b \u2192 \u00ac r b a`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_asymm.asymm", "description": " `is_asymm X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is asymmetric, that is,\n`r a b \u2192 \u00ac r b a`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_partial_order", "description": " `is_partial_order X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is a partial order, that is,\n`is_preorder X r` and `is_antisymm X r`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_partial_order.to_is_preorder", "description": " `is_partial_order X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is a partial order, that is,\n`is_preorder X r` and `is_antisymm X r`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_partial_order.to_is_antisymm", "description": " `is_partial_order X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is a partial order, that is,\n`is_preorder X r` and `is_antisymm X r`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "eq_of_eqv_lt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_right_null", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_right_null.right_null", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "lt_of_lt_of_incomp", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "strict_weak_order.not_lt_of_equiv'", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_right_distrib", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_right_distrib.right_distrib", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_left_distrib", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_left_distrib.left_distrib", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "trichotomous", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "strict_weak_order.not_lt_of_equiv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "trans_of", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "refl_of", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_incomp_trans", "description": " `is_incomp_trans X lt` means that for `lt` a binary relation on `X`, the incomparable relation\n`\u03bb a b, \u00ac lt a b \u2227 \u00ac lt b a` is transitive.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_incomp_trans.incomp_trans", "description": " `is_incomp_trans X lt` means that for `lt` a binary relation on `X`, the incomparable relation\n`\u03bb a b, \u00ac lt a b \u2227 \u00ac lt b a` is transitive.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_trichotomous", "description": " `is_trichotomous X lt` means that the binary relation `lt` on `X` is trichotomous, that is,\neither `lt a b` or `a = b` or `lt b a` for any `a` and `b`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_trichotomous.trichotomous", "description": " `is_trichotomous X lt` means that the binary relation `lt` on `X` is trichotomous, that is,\neither `lt a b` or `a = b` or `lt b a` for any `a` and `b`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_equiv", "description": " `is_equiv X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is an equivalence relation, that\nis, `is_preorder X r` and `is_symm X r`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_equiv.to_is_preorder", "description": " `is_equiv X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is an equivalence relation, that\nis, `is_preorder X r` and `is_symm X r`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_equiv.to_is_symm", "description": " `is_equiv X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is an equivalence relation, that\nis, `is_preorder X r` and `is_symm X r`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_total_preorder_is_preorder", "description": "Every total pre-order is a pre-order.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "sum.map_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum.elim_comp_inl_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum.is_left_eq_ff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum.get_right", "description": "Check if a sum is `inr` and if so, retrieve its contents.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum.swap_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.bijective.sum_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum.elim_l