ite_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.compl_univ_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.inclusion_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.disjoint_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.subsingleton_of_univ_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.inf_eq_inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_diff_singleton_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.sep_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_of_mem_insert_of_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.inter_diff_distrib_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.union_diff_cancel'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.has_to_tactic_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_congr_arg", "description": "(mk_congr_arg h\u2081 h\u2082) is a more efficient version of (mk_app `congr_arg [h\u2081, h\u2082])", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.unsafe_change", "description": "Change the target of the main goal.\n  The input expression must be definitionally equal to the current target.\n  The tactic does not check whether `e`\n  is definitionally equal to the current target. The error will only be detected by the kernel type checker.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reflected.has_to_tactic_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.clear_lst", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.trace", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_const", "description": "Return `expr.const c [l_1, ..., l_n]` where l_i's are fresh universe meta-variables.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.first", "description": "`first [t_1, ..., t_n]` applies the first tactic that doesn't fail.\n  The tactic fails if all t_i's fail.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_eq_refl", "description": "(mk_eq_refl h) is a more efficient version of (mk_app `eq.refl [h])", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic_state.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.focus1", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.change", "description": "Change the target of the main goal.\n  The input expression must be definitionally equal to the current target.\n  If `check` is `ff`, then the tactic does not check whether `e`\n  is definitionally equal to the current target. If it is not,\n  then the error will only be detected by the kernel type checker.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.revertible_local_context", "description": " Get the revertible part of the local context. These are the hypotheses that\nappear after the last frozen local instance in the local context. We call them\nrevertible because `revert` can revert them, unlike those hypotheses which occur\nbefore a frozen instance.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.capture", "description": "`capture t` acts like `t`, but succeeds with a result containing either the returned value\nor the exception.\nChanges made by `t` to the `tactic_state` are preserved in both cases.\n\nThe result can be used to inspect the error message, or passed to `unwrap` to rethrow the\nfailure later.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_to_tactic_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.admit", "description": "Closes the main goal using sorry.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.unify", "description": "Succeeds if `t` and `s` can be unified using the given transparency setting.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.copy_attribute", "description": "`copy_attribute attr_name c_name p d_name` copy attribute `attr_name` from\n  `src` to `tgt` if it is defined for `src`; make it persistent if `p` is `tt`;\n  if `p` is `none`, the copied attribute is made persistent iff it is persistent on `src`", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.find_same_type", "description": "`find_same_type t es` tries to find in es an expression with type definitionally equal to t", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_congr_fun", "description": "(mk_congr_fun h\u2081 h\u2082) is a more efficient version of (mk_app `congr_fun [h\u2081, h\u2082])", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.pp", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.rotate", "description": "Rotate the goals to the left by `n`. That is, put the main goal to the back `n` times.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_mapp", "description": "Similar to `mk_app`, but allows to specify which arguments are explicit/implicit.\n  Example, given `(a b : nat)` then\n  ```lean\n  mk_mapp \"ite\" [some (a > b), none, none, some a, some b]\n  ```\n  returns the application\n  ```lean\n  @ite.{1} nat (a > b) (nat.decidable_gt a b) a b\n  ```", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.open_namespaces", "description": "Return list of currently open namespaces", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.target", "description": "Return target type of the main goal. Fail if tactic_state does not have any goal left.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.is_trace_enabled_for", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.save_const_type_info", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.timetac", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.focus", "description": "`focus [t_1, ..., t_n]` applies t_i to the i-th goal. Fails if the number of\ngoals is not n. Returns the results of t_i (one per goal).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option.has_to_tactic_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.find_assumption", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.doc_string", "description": "`doc_string env d k` returns the doc string for `d` (if available)", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.cases", "description": "Similar to `cases_core`, but `e` doesn't need to be a hypothesis.\n   Remark, it reverts dependencies using `revert_kdeps`.\n\n   Two different transparency modes are used `md` and `dmd`.\n   The mode `md` is used with `cases_core` and `dmd` with `generalize` and `revert_kdeps`.\n\n   It returns the constructor names associated with each new goal and the newly\n   introduced hypotheses. Note that while `cases_core` may return \"new\n   hypotheses\" that are not local constants, this tactic only returns local\n   constants.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.local_context", "description": "Return the hypothesis in the main goal. Fail if tactic_state does not have any goal left.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.any_goals'", "description": "Apply the given tactic to any goal where it succeeds. The tactic succeeds only if\n  tac succeeds for at least one goal.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.apply_instance", "description": "Try to solve the main goal using type class resolution.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.whnf_target", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic_state.get_options", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.get_env", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic_state", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.set_basic_attribute", "description": "Set attribute `attr_name` for constant `c_name` with the given priority.\n  If the priority is none, then use default", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.cleanup", "description": "Remark: set_goals will erase any solved goal", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.is_prop", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.kdepends_on", "description": "Return tt iff `t` \"occurs\" in `e`. The occurrence checking is performed using\n   keyed matching with the given transparency setting.\n\n   We say `t` occurs in `e` by keyed matching iff there is a subterm `s`\n   s.t. `t` and `s` have the same head, and `is_def_eq t s md`\n\n   The main idea is to minimize the number of `is_def_eq` checks\n   performed.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.seq_focus", "description": "Applies `tac1` to the main goal, then applies each of the tactics in `tacs2` to\none of the produced subgoals (like `focus'`).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.decl_name", "description": "Name of the declaration currently being elaborated.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.enable_tags", "description": "Enable/disable goal tagging.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.match_or", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.seq_focus'", "description": "Applies `tac1` to the main goal, then applies each of the tactics in `tacs2` to\none of the produced subgoals (like `focus`).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.revert_kdependencies", "description": "Revert all hypotheses that depend on `e`", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.note", "description": "Add `h : t` to the current goal, given a proof `pr : t`", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.is_assigned", "description": "Return true if the given meta-variable is assigned.\n   Fail if argument is not a meta-variable.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interactive.executor", "description": "Typeclass for custom interaction monads, which provides\n   the information required to convert an interactive-mode\n   construction to a `tactic` which can actually be executed.\n\n   Given a `[monad m]`, `execute_with` explains how to turn a `begin ... end`\n   block, or a `by ...` statement into a `tactic \u03b1` which can actually be\n   executed. The `inhabited` first argument facilitates the passing of an\n   optional configuration parameter `config`, using the syntax:\n   ```lean\n   begin [custom_monad] with config,\n       ...\n   end\n   ```", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interactive.executor.config_type", "description": "Typeclass for custom interaction monads, which provides\n   the information required to convert an interactive-mode\n   construction to a `tactic` which can actually be executed.\n\n   Given a `[monad m]`, `execute_with` explains how to turn a `begin ... end`\n   block, or a `by ...` statement into a `tactic \u03b1` which can actually be\n   executed. The `inhabited` first argument facilitates the passing of an\n   optional configuration parameter `config`, using the syntax:\n   ```lean\n   begin [custom_monad] with config,\n       ...\n   end\n   ```", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interactive.executor.inhabited", "description": "Typeclass for custom interaction monads, which provides\n   the information required to convert an interactive-mode\n   construction to a `tactic` which can actually be executed.\n\n   Given a `[monad m]`, `execute_with` explains how to turn a `begin ... end`\n   block, or a `by ...` statement into a `tactic \u03b1` which can actually be\n   executed. The `inhabited` first argument facilitates the passing of an\n   optional configuration parameter `config`, using the syntax:\n   ```lean\n   begin [custom_monad] with config,\n       ...\n   end\n   ```", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interactive.executor.execute_with", "description": "Typeclass for custom interaction monads, which provides\n   the information required to convert an interactive-mode\n   construction to a `tactic` which can actually be executed.\n\n   Given a `[monad m]`, `execute_with` explains how to turn a `begin ... end`\n   block, or a `by ...` statement into a `tactic \u03b1` which can actually be\n   executed. The `inhabited` first argument facilitates the passing of an\n   optional configuration parameter `config`, using the syntax:\n   ```lean\n   begin [custom_monad] with config,\n       ...\n   end\n   ```", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.get_trace_msg_pos", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.save_type_info", "description": "`save_type_info e ref` save (typeof e) at position associated with ref", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_eq_mp", "description": "(mk_eq_mp h\u2081 h\u2082) is a more efficient version of (mk_app `eq.mp [h\u2081, h\u2082])", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.match_app_of", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.head_zeta", "description": "(head) zeta reduction. Reduction of let bindings at the head of the expression. `let x : a := b in c` reduces to `c[x/b]`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic_format_expr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_instance", "description": "Try to create an instance of the given type class.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.intron'", "description": "`intron' n` introduces `n` hypotheses and returns the resulting local\nconstants. Fails if there are not at least `n` arguments to introduce. If you do\nnot need the return value, use `intron`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.result", "description": "Return the partial term/proof constructed so far. Note that the resultant expression\n  may contain variables that are not declarate in the current main goal.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic_state.env", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.add_inductive", "description": "Add a new inductive datatype to the environment\n  name, universe parameters, number of parameters, type, constructors (name and type), is_meta", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_local'", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.exact", "description": "Close the current goal using `e`. Fail if the type of `e` is not definitionally equal to\n   the target type.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.down", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.unwrap", "description": "`unwrap r` unwraps a result previously obtained using `capture`.\n\nIf the previous result was a success, this produces its wrapped value.\nIf the previous result was an exception, this \"rethrows\" the exception as if it came\nfrom where it originated.\n\n`do r \u2190 capture t, unwrap r` is identical to `t`, but allows for intermediate tactics to be inserted.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.revert_all", "description": "Revert \"all\" hypotheses. Actually, the tactic only reverts\n  hypotheses occurring after the last frozen local instance.\n  Recall that frozen local instances cannot be reverted,\n  use `unfreezing revert_all` instead.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.main_goal", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.try_for_time", "description": "Execute `tac` for `max` milliseconds. Useful due to variance\n   in the number of heartbeats taken by various tactics.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.assertv_core", "description": "`assertv_core H T P`, change target to (T -> target) if P has type T.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "task.monad", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.whnf", "description": "Return `e` in weak head normal form with respect to the given transparency setting.\n   If `unfold_ginductive` is `tt`, then nested and/or mutually recursive inductive datatype constructors\n   and types are unfolded. Recall that nested and mutually recursive inductive datatype declarations\n   are compiled into primitive datatypes accepted by the Kernel.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_from_pure_bind", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.try_apply_opt_auto_param_for_apply", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_aux", "description": "`solve_aux type tac` synthesize an element of 'type' using tactic 'tac'", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.new_goals", "description": " How to order the new goals made from an `apply` tactic.\nSupposing we were applying `e : \u2200 (a:\u03b1) (p : P(a)), Q`\n- `non_dep_first` would produce goals `\u22a2 P(?m)`, `\u22a2 \u03b1`. It puts the P goal at the front because none of the arguments after `p` in `e` depend on `p`. It doesn't matter what the result `Q` depends on.\n- `non_dep_only` would produce goal `\u22a2 P(?m)`.\n- `all` would produce goals `\u22a2 \u03b1`, `\u22a2 P(?m)`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.new_goals.non_dep_first", "description": " How to order the new goals made from an `apply` tactic.\nSupposing we were applying `e : \u2200 (a:\u03b1) (p : P(a)), Q`\n- `non_dep_first` would produce goals `\u22a2 P(?m)`, `\u22a2 \u03b1`. It puts the P goal at the front because none of the arguments after `p` in `e` depend on `p`. It doesn't matter what the result `Q` depends on.\n- `non_dep_only` would produce goal `\u22a2 P(?m)`.\n- `all` would produce goals `\u22a2 \u03b1`, `\u22a2 P(?m)`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.new_goals.non_dep_only", "description": " How to order the new goals made from an `apply` tactic.\nSupposing we were applying `e : \u2200 (a:\u03b1) (p : P(a)), Q`\n- `non_dep_first` would produce goals `\u22a2 P(?m)`, `\u22a2 \u03b1`. It puts the P goal at the front because none of the arguments after `p` in `e` depend on `p`. It doesn't matter what the result `Q` depends on.\n- `non_dep_only` would produce goal `\u22a2 P(?m)`.\n- `all` would produce goals `\u22a2 \u03b1`, `\u22a2 P(?m)`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.new_goals.all", "description": " How to order the new goals made from an `apply` tactic.\nSupposing we were applying `e : \u2200 (a:\u03b1) (p : P(a)), Q`\n- `non_dep_first` would produce goals `\u22a2 P(?m)`, `\u22a2 \u03b1`. It puts the P goal at the front because none of the arguments after `p` in `e` depend on `p`. It doesn't matter what the result `Q` depends on.\n- `non_dep_only` would produce goal `\u22a2 P(?m)`.\n- `all` would produce goals `\u22a2 \u03b1`, `\u22a2 P(?m)`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.try_for", "description": "Execute tac for 'max' \"heartbeats\". The heartbeat is approx. the maximum number of\n   memory allocations (in thousands) performed by 'tac'. This is a deterministic way of interrupting\n   long running tactics.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "list.any_of", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.match_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.success_if_fail", "description": "`success_if_fail t` acts like `t`, but succeeds if `t` fails and fails if `t`\nsucceeds. Changes made by `t` to the `tactic_state` are preserved only if `t`\nsucceeds.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.save_info", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.resolve_constant", "description": "Returns n fully qualified if it refers to a constant, or else fails.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.subst_core", "description": "Given a local constant t, if t has type (lhs = rhs) apply substitution.\n  Otherwise, try to find a local constant that has type of the form (t = t') or (t' = t).\n  The tactic fails if the given expression is not a local constant.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.apply_auto_param", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.returnopt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.intros_dep", "description": "Introduces new hypotheses with forward dependencies.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.with_ast", "description": "Run the provided tactic, associating it to the given AST node.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.in_open_namespaces", "description": "Return tt iff 'd' is a declaration in one of the current open namespaces", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.rename_unstable", "description": "Rename a local hypothesis. Unlike `rename` and `rename_many`, this tactic does\nnot preserve the order of hypotheses. Its implementation is simpler (and\ntherefore probably faster) than that of `rename`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.updateex_env", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.has_opt_auto_param", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.trace_result", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.kdependencies", "description": "Return all hypotheses that depends on `e`\n   The dependency test is performed using `kdepends_on` with the given transparency setting.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.sleep", "description": "Blocks the execution of the current thread for at least `msecs` milliseconds.\n   This tactic is used mainly for debugging purposes.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.seq'", "description": "LCF-style AND_THEN tactic. It applies tac1, and if succeed applies tac2 to each subgoal produced by tac1", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.intro1", "description": "Like `intro` except the name is derived from the bound name in the \u03a0.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.resume", "description": "`resume r` continues execution from a result previously obtained using `capture`.\n\nThis is like `unwrap`, but the `tactic_state` is rolled back to point of capture even upon success.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.abstract", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.format_result", "description": "Display the partial term/proof constructed so far. This tactic is *not* equivalent to\n  `do { r \u2190 result, s \u2190 read, return (format_expr s r) }` because this one will format the result with respect\n  to the current goal, and trace_result will do it with respect to the initial goal.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.whnf_no_delta", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.assert_core", "description": "`assert_core H T`, adds a new goal for T, and change target to `T -> target`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.frozen_local_instances", "description": "Return the list of frozen local instances. Return `none` if local instances were not frozen.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.fail_if_success", "description": "`fail_if_success t` acts like `t`, but succeeds if `t` fails and fails if `t`\nsucceeds. Changes made by `t` to the `tactic_state` are preserved only if `t`\nsucceeds.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.generalizes", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.istep", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.fapply", "description": "Same as `apply` but __all__ arguments that weren't inferred are added to goal list.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.assert", "description": "`assert h t`, adds a new goal for t, and the hypothesis `h : t` in the current goal.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.match_heq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.skip", "description": "Does nothing.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.iterate_exactly", "description": "`iterate_exactly n t` iterates `t` `n` times, returning the result of\neach iteration. If any iteration fails, the whole tactic fails.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interactive.executor.execute_with_explicit", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.by_cases", "description": "`by_cases p h` splits the main goal into two cases, assuming `h : p` in the\nfirst branch, and `h : \u00ac p` in the second branch. The expression `p` needs to\nbe a proposition.\n\nThe produced proof term is `dite p ?m_1 ?m_2`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_app", "description": "Helper tactic for creating simple applications where some arguments are inferred using\n  type inference.\n\n  Example, given\n  ```lean\n      rel.{l_1 l_2} : Pi (\u03b1 : Type.{l_1}) (\u03b2 : \u03b1 -> Type.{l_2}), (Pi x : \u03b1, \u03b2 x) -> (Pi x : \u03b1, \u03b2 x) -> , Prop\n      nat     : Type\n      real    : Type\n      vec.{l} : Pi (\u03b1 : Type l) (n : nat), Type.{l1}\n      f g     : Pi (n : nat), vec real n\n  ```\n  then\n  ```lean\n  mk_app_core semireducible \"rel\" [f, g]\n  ```\n  returns the application\n  ```lean\n  rel.{1 2} nat (fun n : nat, vec real n) f g\n  ```\n\n  The unification constraints due to type inference are solved using the transparency `md`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.when_tracing", "description": "Execute tac only if option trace.n is set to true.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.intro", "description": " Pi or elet introduction.\nGiven the tactic state `\u22a2 \u03a0 x : \u03b1, Y`, ``intro `hello`` will produce the state `hello : \u03b1 \u22a2 Y[x/hello]`.\nReturns the new local constant. Similarly for `elet` expressions.\nIf the target is not a Pi or elet it will try to put it in WHNF.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.rename_many", "description": "Rename local hypotheses according to the given `name_map`. The `name_map`\ncontains as keys those hypotheses that should be renamed; the associated values\nare the new names.\n\nThis tactic can only rename hypotheses which occur after the last frozen local\ninstance. If you need to rename earlier hypotheses, try\n`unfreezing (rename_many ...)`.\n\nIf `strict` is true, we fail if `name_map` refers to hypotheses that do not\nappear in the local context or that appear before a frozen local instance.\nConversely, if `strict` is false, some entries of `name_map` may be silently\nignored.\n\nIf `use_unique_names` is true, the keys of `name_map` should be the unique names\nof hypotheses to be renamed. Otherwise, the keys should be display names.\n\nNote that we allow shadowing, so renamed hypotheses may have the same name\nas other hypotheses in the context. If `use_unique_names` is false and there are\nmultiple hypotheses with the same display name in the context, they are all\nrenamed.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.decorate_ex", "description": "Decorate t's exceptions with msg.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.try_apply_opt_auto_param", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.has_opt_auto_param_for_apply", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_tagged_proof", "description": "Convenience function for creating ` for proofs.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.transparency", "description": " A parameter representing how aggressively definitions should be unfolded when trying to decide if two terms match, unify or are definitionally equal.\nBy default, theorem declarations are never unfolded.\n- `all` will unfold everything, including macros and theorems. Except projection macros.\n- `semireducible` will unfold everything except theorems and definitions tagged as irreducible.\n- `instances` will unfold all class instance definitions and definitions tagged with reducible.\n- `reducible` will only unfold definitions tagged with the `reducible` attribute.\n- `none` will never unfold anything.\n[NOTE] You are not allowed to tag a definition with more than one of `reducible`, `irreducible`, `semireducible` attributes.\n[NOTE] there is a config flag `m_unfold_lemmas`that will make it unfold theorems.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.transparency.all", "description": " A parameter representing how aggressively definitions should be unfolded when trying to decide if two terms match, unify or are definitionally equal.\nBy default, theorem declarations are never unfolded.\n- `all` will unfold everything, including macros and theorems. Except projection macros.\n- `semireducible` will unfold everything except theorems and definitions tagged as irreducible.\n- `instances` will unfold all class instance definitions and definitions tagged with reducible.\n- `reducible` will only unfold definitions tagged with the `reducible` attribute.\n- `none` will never unfold anything.\n[NOTE] You are not allowed to tag a definition with more than one of `reducible`, `irreducible`, `semireducible` attributes.\n[NOTE] there is a config flag `m_unfold_lemmas`that will make it unfold theorems.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.transparency.semireducible", "description": " A parameter representing how aggressively definitions should be unfolded when trying to decide if two terms match, unify or are definitionally equal.\nBy default, theorem declarations are never unfolded.\n- `all` will unfold everything, including macros and theorems. Except projection macros.\n- `semireducible` will unfold everything except theorems and definitions tagged as irreducible.\n- `instances` will unfold all class instance definitions and definitions tagged with reducible.\n- `reducible` will only unfold definitions tagged with the `reducible` attribute.\n- `none` will never unfold anything.\n[NOTE] You are not allowed to tag a definition with more than one of `reducible`, `irreducible`, `semireducible` attributes.\n[NOTE] there is a config flag `m_unfold_lemmas`that will make it unfold theorems.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.transparency.instances", "description": " A parameter representing how aggressively definitions should be unfolded when trying to decide if two terms match, unify or are definitionally equal.\nBy default, theorem declarations are never unfolded.\n- `all` will unfold everything, including macros and theorems. Except projection macros.\n- `semireducible` will unfold everything except theorems and definitions tagged as irreducible.\n- `instances` will unfold all class instance definitions and definitions tagged with reducible.\n- `reducible` will only unfold definitions tagged with the `reducible` attribute.\n- `none` will never unfold anything.\n[NOTE] You are not allowed to tag a definition with more than one of `reducible`, `irreducible`, `semireducible` attributes.\n[NOTE] there is a config flag `m_unfold_lemmas`that will make it unfold theorems.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.transparency.reducible", "description": " A parameter representing how aggressively definitions should be unfolded when trying to decide if two terms match, unify or are definitionally equal.\nBy default, theorem declarations are never unfolded.\n- `all` will unfold everything, including macros and theorems. Except projection macros.\n- `semireducible` will unfold everything except theorems and definitions tagged as irreducible.\n- `instances` will unfold all class instance definitions and definitions tagged with reducible.\n- `reducible` will only unfold definitions tagged with the `reducible` attribute.\n- `none` will never unfold anything.\n[NOTE] You are not allowed to tag a definition with more than one of `reducible`, `irreducible`, `semireducible` attributes.\n[NOTE] there is a config flag `m_unfold_lemmas`that will make it unfold theorems.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.transparency.none", "description": " A parameter representing how aggressively definitions should be unfolded when trying to decide if two terms match, unify or are definitionally equal.\nBy default, theorem declarations are never unfolded.\n- `all` will unfold everything, including macros and theorems. Except projection macros.\n- `semireducible` will unfold everything except theorems and definitions tagged as irreducible.\n- `instances` will unfold all class instance definitions and definitions tagged with reducible.\n- `reducible` will only unfold definitions tagged with the `reducible` attribute.\n- `none` will never unfold anything.\n[NOTE] You are not allowed to tag a definition with more than one of `reducible`, `irreducible`, `semireducible` attributes.\n[NOTE] there is a config flag `m_unfold_lemmas`that will make it unfold theorems.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.set_main_tag", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.to_expr_strict", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.read", "description": "Get the tactic_state.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.match_not", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.all_goals'", "description": "Apply the given tactic to all goals.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.opt_to_tac", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.try_core", "description": "`try_core t` acts like `t`, but succeeds even if `t` fails. It returns the\nresult of `t` if `t` succeeded and `none` otherwise.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.any_hyp", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.olean_doc_strings", "description": " Returns a list of all top-level (`/-! ... -/`) docstrings in the active module and imported ones.\nThe returned object is a list of modules, indexed by `(some filename)` for imported modules\nand `none` for the active one, where each module in the list is paired with a list\nof `(position_in_file, docstring)` pairs.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_local_pis", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.write", "description": "Set the tactic_state.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.match_ne", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.introv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.definev_core", "description": "`definev_core H T P`, change target to `let H : T := P in target` if P has type T.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_mvar", "description": "Create a fresh universe `?u`, a metavariable `?T : Type.{?u}`,\n  and return metavariable `?M : ?T`.\n  This action can be used to create a meta-variable when\n  we don't know its type at creation time", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.eval_expr", "description": "(eval_expr \u03b1 e) evaluates 'e' IF 'e' has type '\u03b1'.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.trace_state", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.intro_fresh", "description": "A variant of `intro` which makes sure that the introduced hypothesis's name is\nunique in the context. If there is no hypothesis named `n` in the context yet,\n`intro_fresh n` is the same as `intro n`. If there is already a hypothesis named\n`n`, the new hypothesis is named `n_1` (or `n_2` if `n_1` already exists, etc.).\nIf `offset` is given, the new names are `n_offset`, `n_offset+1` etc.\n\nIf `n` is `_`, `intro_fresh n` is the same as `intro1`. The `offset` is ignored\nin this case.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.is_class", "description": "Return true if the given expression is a type class.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.apply", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.get_main_tag", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.instantiate_mvars", "description": "instantiate assigned metavariables in the given expression", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.try", "description": "`try t` acts like `t`, but succeeds even if `t` fails.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.set_env_core", "description": "Changes the environment to the `new_env`.\nThe new environment does not need to be a descendant of the old one.\nUse with care.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.intron", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.iterate'", "description": "`iterate' t` repeats `t` 100.000 times or until `t` fails.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.all_goals", "description": "Apply the given tactic to all goals. Return one result per goal.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.match_and", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.define_core", "description": "`define_core H T`, adds a new goal for T, and change target to  `let H : T := ?M in target` in the current goal.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.eval_pexpr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.step", "description": "Auxiliary definition used to implement begin ... end blocks", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.has_to_tactic_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.head_beta", "description": "(head) beta reduction. `(\u03bb x, B) c` reduces to `B[x/c]`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.unset_attribute", "description": "`unset_attribute attr_name c_name`", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.head_eta", "description": "(head) eta reduction. `(\u03bb x, f x)` reduces to `f`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.is_protected_decl", "description": "check if `n` is the name of a protected declaration", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.define", "description": "`define h t`, adds a new goal for t, and the hypothesis `h : t := ?M` in the current goal.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.by_contradiction", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.get_arity", "description": "Compute the arity of the given function", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.intros", "description": "Repeatedly apply `intro1` and return the list of new local constants in order of introduction.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.save_info_thunk", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.freeze_local_instances", "description": "Freeze the current set of local instances.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.has_attribute", "description": "`has_attribute attr_name c_name` succeeds if the declaration `decl_name`\n  has the attribute `attr_name`. The result is the priority and whether or not\n  the attribute is persistent.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.match_iff", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.destruct", "description": "Similar to cases tactic, but does not revert/intro/clear hypotheses.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic_state.to_format", "description": "Format the given tactic state. If `target_lhs_only` is true and the target\n   is of the form `lhs ~ rhs`, where `~` is a simplification relation,\n   then only the `lhs` is displayed.\n\n   Remark: the parameter `target_lhs_only` is a temporary hack used to implement\n   the `conv` monad. It will be removed in the future.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.match_refl_app", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.head_eta_expand", "description": "(head) eta expand the given expression. `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` head-eta-expands to `\u03bb a, f a`. If `f` isn't a function then it just returns `f`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.assertv", "description": "`assertv h t v`, adds the hypothesis `h : t` in the current goal if v has type t.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.revert_kdeps", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.type_check", "description": "Type check `e` with respect to the current goal.\n   Fails if `e` is not type correct.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.andthen_seq_focus", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.get_pi_arity", "description": "Compute the arity of the given (Pi-)type", "p": "core"}, {"name": "list.for_each", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.focus'", "description": "`focus' [t_1, ..., t_n]` applies t_i to the i-th goal. Fails if the number of goals is not n.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.resolve_name", "description": "Resolve a name using the current local context, environment, aliases, etc.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.iterate", "description": "`iterate t` repeats `t` 100.000 times or until `t` fails, returning the\nresult of each iteration.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.set_env", "description": "Changes the environment to the `new_env`. `new_env` needs to be a descendant from the current environment.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.revert", "description": "Example: with `x : \u2115, h : P(x) \u22a2 T(x)`, `revert x` returns `2` and produces the state ` \u22a2 \u03a0 x, P(x) \u2192 T(x)`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.add_protected_decl", "description": "add declaration `d` as a protected declaration", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.set_tag", "description": "Tag goal `g` with tag `t`. It does nothing if goal tagging is disabled.\n   Remark: `set_goal g []` removes the tag", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.induction", "description": "Induction on `h` using recursor `rec`, names for the new hypotheses\n  are retrieved from `ns`. If `ns` does not have sufficient names, then use the internal binder names\n  in the recursor.\n  It returns for each new goal the name of the constructor (if `rec_name` is a builtin recursor),\n  a list of new hypotheses, and a list of substitutions for hypotheses\n  depending on `h`. The substitutions map internal names to their replacement terms. If the\n  replacement is again a hypothesis the user name stays the same. The internal names are only valid\n  in the original goal, not in the type context of the new goal.\n  Remark: if `rec_name` is not a builtin recursor, we use parameter names of `rec_name` instead of\n  constructor names.\n\n  If `rec` is none, then the type of `h` is inferred, if it is of the form `C ...`, tactic uses `C.rec`", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.save_options", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.fail_if_no_goals", "description": "Fail if there are no remaining goals.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.rename", "description": "Rename a local hypothesis. This is a special case of `rename_many`;\nsee there for caveats.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic_state.format_expr", "description": "Format expression with respect to the main goal in the tactic state.\n  If the tactic state does not contain any goals, then format expression\n  using an empty local context.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.get_decl", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.any_hyp_aux", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.is_def_eq", "description": "Similar to `unify`, but it treats metavariables as constants.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_eq_mpr", "description": "(mk_eq_mpr h\u2081 h\u2082) is a more efficient version of (mk_app `eq.mpr [h\u2081, h\u2082])", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.zeta", "description": "Zeta reduction. Reduction of let bindings. `let x : a := b in c` reduces to `c[x/b]`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.apply_cfg", "description": " Configuration options for the `apply` tactic.\n- `md` sets how aggressively definitions are unfolded.\n- `new_goals` is the strategy for ordering new goals.\n- `instances` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `[...]` arguments using type class resolution.\n- `auto_param` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `(h : p . tac_id)` arguments using tactic `tac_id`.\n- `opt_param` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `(a : t := v)` arguments by setting them to `v`.\n- `unify` if `tt`, then `apply` is free to assign existing metavariables in the goal when solving unification constraints.\n   For example, in the goal `|- ?x < succ 0`, the tactic `apply succ_lt_succ` succeeds with the default configuration,\n   but `apply_with succ_lt_succ {unify := ff}` doesn't since it would require Lean to assign `?x` to `succ ?y` where\n   `?y` is a fresh metavariable.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.apply_cfg.md", "description": " Configuration options for the `apply` tactic.\n- `md` sets how aggressively definitions are unfolded.\n- `new_goals` is the strategy for ordering new goals.\n- `instances` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `[...]` arguments using type class resolution.\n- `auto_param` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `(h : p . tac_id)` arguments using tactic `tac_id`.\n- `opt_param` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `(a : t := v)` arguments by setting them to `v`.\n- `unify` if `tt`, then `apply` is free to assign existing metavariables in the goal when solving unification constraints.\n   For example, in the goal `|- ?x < succ 0`, the tactic `apply succ_lt_succ` succeeds with the default configuration,\n   but `apply_with succ_lt_succ {unify := ff}` doesn't since it would require Lean to assign `?x` to `succ ?y` where\n   `?y` is a fresh metavariable.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.apply_cfg.approx", "description": " Configuration options for the `apply` tactic.\n- `md` sets how aggressively definitions are unfolded.\n- `new_goals` is the strategy for ordering new goals.\n- `instances` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `[...]` arguments using type class resolution.\n- `auto_param` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `(h : p . tac_id)` arguments using tactic `tac_id`.\n- `opt_param` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `(a : t := v)` arguments by setting them to `v`.\n- `unify` if `tt`, then `apply` is free to assign existing metavariables in the goal when solving unification constraints.\n   For example, in the goal `|- ?x < succ 0`, the tactic `apply succ_lt_succ` succeeds with the default configuration,\n   but `apply_with succ_lt_succ {unify := ff}` doesn't since it would require Lean to assign `?x` to `succ ?y` where\n   `?y` is a fresh metavariable.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.apply_cfg.new_goals", "description": " Configuration options for the `apply` tactic.\n- `md` sets how aggressively definitions are unfolded.\n- `new_goals` is the strategy for ordering new goals.\n- `instances` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `[...]` arguments using type class resolution.\n- `auto_param` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `(h : p . tac_id)` arguments using tactic `tac_id`.\n- `opt_param` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `(a : t := v)` arguments by setting them to `v`.\n- `unify` if `tt`, then `apply` is free to assign existing metavariables in the goal when solving unification constraints.\n   For example, in the goal `|- ?x < succ 0`, the tactic `apply succ_lt_succ` succeeds with the default configuration,\n   but `apply_with succ_lt_succ {unify := ff}` doesn't since it would require Lean to assign `?x` to `succ ?y` where\n   `?y` is a fresh metavariable.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.apply_cfg.instances", "description": " Configuration options for the `apply` tactic.\n- `md` sets how aggressively definitions are unfolded.\n- `new_goals` is the strategy for ordering new goals.\n- `instances` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `[...]` arguments using type class resolution.\n- `auto_param` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `(h : p . tac_id)` arguments using tactic `tac_id`.\n- `opt_param` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `(a : t := v)` arguments by setting them to `v`.\n- `unify` if `tt`, then `apply` is free to assign existing metavariables in the goal when solving unification constraints.\n   For example, in the goal `|- ?x < succ 0`, the tactic `apply succ_lt_succ` succeeds with the default configuration,\n   but `apply_with succ_lt_succ {unify := ff}` doesn't since it would require Lean to assign `?x` to `succ ?y` where\n   `?y` is a fresh metavariable.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.apply_cfg.auto_param", "description": " Configuration options for the `apply` tactic.\n- `md` sets how aggressively definitions are unfolded.\n- `new_goals` is the strategy for ordering new goals.\n- `instances` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `[...]` arguments using type class resolution.\n- `auto_param` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `(h : p . tac_id)` arguments using tactic `tac_id`.\n- `opt_param` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `(a : t := v)` arguments by setting them to `v`.\n- `unify` if `tt`, then `apply` is free to assign existing metavariables in the goal when solving unification constraints.\n   For example, in the goal `|- ?x < succ 0`, the tactic `apply succ_lt_succ` succeeds with the default configuration,\n   but `apply_with succ_lt_succ {unify := ff}` doesn't since it would require Lean to assign `?x` to `succ ?y` where\n   `?y` is a fresh metavariable.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.apply_cfg.opt_param", "description": " Configuration options for the `apply` tactic.\n- `md` sets how aggressively definitions are unfolded.\n- `new_goals` is the strategy for ordering new goals.\n- `instances` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `[...]` arguments using type class resolution.\n- `auto_param` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `(h : p . tac_id)` arguments using tactic `tac_id`.\n- `opt_param` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `(a : t := v)` arguments by setting them to `v`.\n- `unify` if `tt`, then `apply` is free to assign existing metavariables in the goal when solving unification constraints.\n   For example, in the goal `|- ?x < succ 0`, the tactic `apply succ_lt_succ` succeeds with the default configuration,\n   but `apply_with succ_lt_succ {unify := ff}` doesn't since it would require Lean to assign `?x` to `succ ?y` where\n   `?y` is a fresh metavariable.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.apply_cfg.unify", "description": " Configuration options for the `apply` tactic.\n- `md` sets how aggressively definitions are unfolded.\n- `new_goals` is the strategy for ordering new goals.\n- `instances` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `[...]` arguments using type class resolution.\n- `auto_param` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `(h : p . tac_id)` arguments using tactic `tac_id`.\n- `opt_param` if `tt`, then `apply` tries to synthesize unresolved `(a : t := v)` arguments by setting them to `v`.\n- `unify` if `tt`, then `apply` is free to assign existing metavariables in the goal when solving unification constraints.\n   For example, in the goal `|- ?x < succ 0`, the tactic `apply succ_lt_succ` succeeds with the default configuration,\n   but `apply_with succ_lt_succ {unify := ff}` doesn't since it would require Lean to assign `?x` to `succ ?y` where\n   `?y` is a fresh metavariable.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.whnf_ginductive", "description": "Return `e` in weak head normal form with respect to the given transparency setting,\n   or `e` head is a generalized constructor or inductive datatype.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.get_options", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.repeat", "description": "This tactic is applied to each goal. If the application succeeds,\n   the tactic is applied recursively to all the generated subgoals until it eventually fails.\n   The recursion stops in a subgoal when the tactic has failed to make progress.\n   The tactic `repeat` never fails.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_eq_trans", "description": "(mk_eq_trans h\u2081 h\u2082) is a more efficient version of (mk_app `eq.trans [h\u2081, h\u2082])", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.solve1", "description": "Applies the given tactic to the main goal and fails if it is not solved.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.iterate_at_most", "description": "`iterate_at_most n t` iterates `t` `n` times or until `t` fails, returning the\nresult of each successful iteration.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.cases_core", "description": "Apply `cases_on` recursor, names for the new hypotheses are retrieved from `ns`.\n  `h` must be a local constant. It returns for each new goal the name of the constructor, a list of new hypotheses, and a list of\n  substitutions for hypotheses depending on `h`. The number of new goals may be smaller than the\n  number of constructors. Some goals may be discarded when the indices to not match.\n  See `induction` for information on the list of substitutions.\n\n  The `cases` tactic is implemented using this one, and it relaxes the restriction of `h`.\n\n  Note: There is one \"new hypothesis\" for every constructor argument. These are\n  usually local constants, but due to dependent pattern matching, they can also\n  be arbitrary terms.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.get_tag", "description": "Return tag associated with `g`. Return `[]` if there is no tag.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.ex_to_tac", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.rotate_left", "description": "Rotate goals to the left. That is, `rotate_left 1` takes the main goal and puts it to the back of the subgoal list.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.andthen_seq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.with_enable_tags", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_local_def", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "list.has_to_tactic_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.get_assignment", "description": "Return the value assigned to the given meta-variable.\n  Fail if argument is not a meta-variable or if it is not assigned.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.intron_base", "description": "Like `intron'` but the introduced hypotheses' names are derived from `base`,\ni.e. `base`, `base_1` etc. The new names are unique in the context. If `offset`\nis given, the new names will be `base_offset`, `base_offset+1` etc.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.refine", "description": "The same as `exact` except you can add proof holes.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.kabstract", "description": "Abstracts all occurrences of the term `t` in `e` using keyed matching.\n   If `unify` is `ff`, then matching is used instead of unification.\n   That is, metavariables occurring in `e` are not assigned.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.pose", "description": "Add `h : t := pr` to the current goal", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.funext", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.apply_core", "description": "Apply the expression `e` to the main goal, the unification is performed using the transparency mode in `cfg`.\n   Supposing `e : \u03a0 (a\u2081:\u03b1\u2081) ... (a\u2099:\u03b1\u2099), P(a\u2081,...,a\u2099)` and the target is `Q`, `apply` will attempt to unify `Q` with `P(?a\u2081,...?a\u2099)`.\n   All of the metavariables that are not assigned are added as new metavariables.\n   If `cfg.approx` is `tt`, then fallback to first-order unification, and approximate context during unification.\n   `cfg.new_goals` specifies which unassigned metavariables become new goals, and their order.\n   If `cfg.instances` is `tt`, then use type class resolution to instantiate unassigned meta-variables.\n   The fields `cfg.auto_param` and `cfg.opt_param` are ignored by this tactic (See `tactic.apply`).\n   It returns a list of all introduced meta variables and the parameter name associated with them, even the assigned ones.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interactive.executor_tactic", "description": "Default `executor` instance for `tactic`s themselves", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_congr", "description": "(mk_congr h\u2081 h\u2082) is a more efficient version of (mk_app `congr [h\u2081, h\u2082])", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.swap", "description": "Swap first two goals, do nothing if tactic state does not have at least two goals.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.returnex", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic_state.set_options", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_meta_var", "description": "Create a fresh meta-variable with the given type.\n  The scope of the new meta-variable is the local context of the main goal.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.rexact", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.unfreeze_local_instances", "description": "Avoid this function!  Use `unfreezingI`/`resetI`/etc. instead!\n\nUnfreezes the current set of local instances.\nAfter this tactic, the instance cache is disabled.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.up", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_fresh_name", "description": "Make a name that is guaranteed to be unique. Eg `_fresh.1001.4667`. These will be different for each run of the tactic.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.new_aux_decl_name", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.add_decl", "description": "Add the given declaration to the environment", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.iterate_at_most'", "description": "`iterate_at_most' n t` repeats `t` `n` times or until `t` fails.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.add_meta_definition", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.assumption", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.is_proof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.infer_type", "description": "Infer the type of the given expression.\n  Remark: transparency does not affect type inference", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.add_doc_string", "description": "Set the docstring for the given declaration.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.eapplyc", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.replace_target", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.run_simple", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.trace_call_stack", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.intro_core", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_num_meta_univs", "description": "Create a list of universe meta-variables of the given size.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.try_lst", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.clear", "description": "Clear the given local constant. The tactic fails if the given expression is not a local constant.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.get_local", "description": "Get the `local_const` expr for the given `name`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.set_goals", "description": "Replace the current list of goals with the given one. Each expr in the list should be a metavariable. Any assigned metavariables will be ignored.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.alternative", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.seq", "description": "LCF-style AND_THEN tactic. It applies `tac1` to the main goal, then applies\n`tac2` to each goal produced by `tac1`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.definev", "description": "`definev h t v`, adds the hypothesis (h : t := v) in the current goal if v has type t.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_to_tactic_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_to_tactic_format.to_tactic_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.is_prop_decl", "description": "Return true iff n is the name of declaration that is a proposition.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.add_defn_equations", "description": "`add_defn_equations` adds a definition specified by a list of equations.\n\n The arguments:\n   * `lp`: list of universe parameters\n   * `params`: list of parameters (binders before the colon);\n   * `fn`: a local constant giving the name and type of the declaration\n     (with `params` in the local context);\n   * `eqns`: a list of equations, each of which is a list of patterns\n     (constructors applied to new local constants) and the branch\n     expression;\n   * `is_meta`: is the definition meta?\n\n\n `add_defn_equations` can be used as:\n\n     do my_add \u2190 mk_local_def `my_add `(\u2115 \u2192 \u2115),\n         a \u2190 mk_local_def `a \u2115,\n         b \u2190 mk_local_def `b \u2115,\n         add_defn_equations [a] my_add\n             [ ([``(nat.zero)], a),\n               ([``(nat.succ %%b)], my_add b) ])\n             ff -- non-meta\n\n to create the following definition:\n\n     def my_add (a : \u2115) : \u2115 \u2192 \u2115\n     | nat.zero := a\n     | (nat.succ b) := my_add b", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.applyc", "description": "Apply the constant `c`", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.triv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.revert_lst", "description": " `revert_lst : list expr \u2192 tactic nat` is the reverse of `intron`. It takes a local constant `c` and puts it back as bound by a `pi` or `elet` of the main target.\nIf there are other local constants that depend on `c`, these are also reverted. Because of this, the `nat` that is returned is the actual number of reverted local constants.\nExample: with `x : \u2115, h : P(x) \u22a2 T(x)`, `revert_lst [x]` returns `2` and produces the state ` \u22a2 \u03a0 x, P(x) \u2192 T(x)`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.get_univ_assignment", "description": "Return the value assigned to the given universe meta-variable.\n  Fail if argument is not an universe meta-variable or if it is not assigned.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.tag", "description": "A `tag` is a list of `names`. These are attached to goals to help tactics track them.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.funext_lst", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_to_format_to_has_to_tactic_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.funext_core", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.intron_with", "description": "`intron_with i ns base offset` introduces `i` hypotheses using the names from\n`ns`. If `ns` contains less than `i` names, the remaining hypotheses' names are\nderived from `base` and `offset` (as with `intron_base`). If `base` is `_`, the\nnames are derived from the \u03a0 binder names.\n\nReturns the introduced local constants and the remaining names from `ns` (if\n`ns` contains more than `i` names).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.rotate_right", "description": " Rotate the goals to the right by `n`. That is, take the goal at the back and push it to the front `n` times.\n[NOTE] We have to provide the instance argument `[has_mod nat]` because\n   mod for nat was not defined yet", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.replace_hyp", "description": "\"Replace\" hypothesis `h : type` with `h : new_type` where `eq_pr` is a proof\nthat (type = new_type). The tactic actually creates a new hypothesis\nwith the same user facing name, and (tries to) clear `h`.\nThe `clear` step fails if `h` has forward dependencies. In this case, the old `h`\nwill remain in the local context. The tactic returns the new hypothesis.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.set_options", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "order_laws_tac", "description": "Try to prove with `iff.refl`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.induction'", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.any_goals", "description": "Apply `tac` to any goal where it succeeds. The tactic succeeds if `tac`\nsucceeds for at least one goal. The returned list contains the result of `tac`\nfor each goal: `some a` if tac succeeded, or `none` if it did not.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.intro_lst_fresh", "description": "A variant of `intro_lst` which makes sure that the introduced hypotheses' names\nare unique in the context. See `intro_fresh`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.solve", "description": "`solve [t_1, ... t_n]` applies the first tactic that solves the main goal.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.subst", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interactive.executor.execute_explicit", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_eq_symm", "description": "(mk_eq_symm h) is a more efficient version of (mk_app `eq.symm [h])", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.get_unused_name", "description": "Get a fresh name that is guaranteed to not be in use in the local context.\n   If `n` is provided and `n` is not in use, then `n` is returned.\n   Otherwise a number `i` is appended to give `\"n_i\"`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.get_goals", "description": "Gets a list of metavariables, one for each goal.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.iterate_exactly'", "description": "`iterate_exactly' n t` executes `t` `n` times. If any iteration fails, the whole\ntactic fails.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.to_expr", "description": "Elaborate the given quoted expression with respect to the current main goal.\n   Note that this means that any implicit arguments for the given `pexpr` will be applied with fresh metavariables.\n   If `allow_mvars` is tt, then metavariables are tolerated and become new goals if `subgoals` is tt.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.failed", "description": "Cause the tactic to fail with no error message.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.intro_lst", "description": "Same as `intros`, except with the given names for the new hypotheses. Use the name ```_``` to instead use the binder's name.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.done", "description": "Fail if there are unsolved goals.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.num_goals", "description": "Return the number of goals that need to be solved", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic", "description": "`tactic` is the monad for building tactics.\n   You use this to:\n   - View and modify the local goals and hypotheses in the prover's state.\n   - Invoke type checking and elaboration of terms.\n   - View and modify the environment.\n   - Build new tactics out of existing ones such as `simp` and `rewrite`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.apply_opt_param", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.add_aux_decl", "description": "Create an auxiliary definition with name `c` where `type` and `value` may contain local constants and\nmeta-variables. This function collects all dependencies (universe parameters, universe metavariables,\nlocal constants (aka hypotheses) and metavariables).\nIt updates the environment in the tactic_state, and returns an expression of the form\n\n          (c.{l_1 ... l_n} a_1 ... a_m)\n\nwhere l_i's and a_j's are the collected dependencies.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic_state.has_to_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_sorry", "description": "Makes a sorry macro with a meta-variable as its type.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.get_local_type", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic_result", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.tags_enabled", "description": "Return tt iff goal tagging is enabled.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.module_doc_strings", "description": " Returns a list of docstrings in the active module. An entry in the list can be either:\n- a top-level (`/-! ... -/`) docstring, represented as `(none, docstring)`\n- a declaration-specific (`/-- ... -/`) docstring, represented as `(some decl_name, docstring)`", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.eapply", "description": "Same as `apply` but only goals that don't depend on other goals are added to goal list.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_meta_univ", "description": "Create a fresh meta universe variable.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.fail", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.generalize", "description": "Generalizes the target with respect to `e`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "subfield.subfield_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.map_le_iff_le_comap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.list_sum_mem", "description": "Sum of a list of elements in a subfield is in the subfield.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.neg_mem", "description": "A subfield is closed under negation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.copy", "description": " Copy of a subfield with a new `carrier` equal to the old one. Useful to fix definitional\nequalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.comap_comap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.eq_of_eq_on_of_field_closure_eq_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.has_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_comap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.subset_closure", "description": "The subfield generated by a set includes the set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_equiv.subfield_congr", "description": "Makes the identity isomorphism from a proof two subfields of a multiplicative\n   monoid are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.prod_mem", "description": "Product of elements of a subfield indexed by a `finset` is in the subfield.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.eq_on_field_closure", "description": "If two ring homomorphisms are equal on a set, then they are equal on its subfield closure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.closure_Union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.coe_range_restrict_field", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.mem_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.mem_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_copy", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_int_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.inclusion", "description": "The ring homomorphism associated to an inclusion of subfields.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.int.has_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.field_range_eq_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.closure_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.to_field", "description": "A subfield inherits a field structure", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.mem_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.mem_Inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.pow_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield_class.rat_smul_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.mem_to_add_subgroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.to_add_subgroup", "description": "The underlying `add_subgroup` of a subfield.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.mem_to_subring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.Inf_to_subring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_to_submonoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_set_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.mem_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_Inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_Sup_of_directed_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.sum_mem", "description": "Sum of elements in a `subfield` indexed by a `finset` is in the `subfield`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.closure_le", "description": "A subfield `t` includes `closure s` if and only if it includes `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.top_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.to_subring.subtype_eq_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.closure_eq_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.add_mem", "description": "A subfield is closed under addition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield_class.has_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield_class.subfield_class.to_subgroup_class", "description": " A subfield contains `1`, products and inverses.\n\nBe assured that we're not actually proving that subfields are subgroups:\n`subgroup_class` is really an abbreviation of `subgroup_with_or_without_zero_class`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.map_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.gc_map_comap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.top_equiv", "description": "The ring equiv between the top element of `subfield K` and `K`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.complete_lattice", "description": "Subfields of a ring form a complete lattice.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.multiset_prod_mem", "description": "Product of a multiset of elements in a subfield is in the subfield.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.closure_mono", "description": " Subfield closure of a set is monotone in its argument: if `s \u2286 t`,\nthen `closure s \u2264 closure t`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.inv_mem", "description": "A subfield is closed under inverses.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.mem_supr_of_directed", "description": "The underlying set of a non-empty directed Sup of subfields is just a union of the subfields.\n Note that this fails without the directedness assumption (the union of two subfields is\n typically not a subfield)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.closure_sUnion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.map_field_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.comap_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.mem_closure_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.field_range", "description": "The range of a ring homomorphism, as a subfield of the target. See Note [range copy pattern].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.has_inf", "description": "The inf of two subfields is their intersection.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.closure_induction", "description": " An induction principle for closure membership. If `p` holds for `1`, and all elements\nof `s`, and is preserved under addition, negation, and multiplication, then `p` holds for all\nelements of the closure of `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.map_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.mem_carrier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.map", "description": "The image of a subfield along a ring homomorphism is a subfield.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.copy_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.closure_eq", "description": "Closure of a subfield `S` equals `S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.set_like", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield_class.coe_rat_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield_class.to_linear_ordered_field", "description": "A subfield of a `linear_ordered_field` is a `linear_ordered_field`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.to_submonoid", "description": "The underlying submonoid of a subfield.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_to_add_subgroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.div_mem", "description": "A subfield is closed under division.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.range_restrict_field", "description": "Restriction of a ring homomorphism to its range interpreted as a subfield.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.to_linear_ordered_field", "description": "A subfield of a `linear_ordered_field` is a `linear_ordered_field`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.is_glb_Inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.fintype_field_range", "description": " The range of a morphism of fields is a fintype, if the domain is a fintype.\n\nNote that this instance can cause a diamond with `subtype.fintype` if `L` is also a fintype.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.to_subring", "description": "Reinterpret a `subfield` as a `subring`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.closure", "description": "The `subfield` generated by a set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.closure_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_supr_of_directed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.zsmul_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.mem_Sup_of_directed_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.closure_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.to_algebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.mk_le_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.zero_mem", "description": "A subfield contains the field's 0.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.not_mem_of_not_mem_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.map_field_closure", "description": " The image under a ring homomorphism of the subfield generated by a set equals\nthe subfield generated by the image of the set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.eq_locus_field", "description": " The subfield of elements `x : R` such that `f x = g x`, i.e.,\nthe equalizer of f and g as a subfield of R", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.map_supr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.zpow_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield_class.coe_rat_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.field_range_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.gi", "description": "`closure` forms a Galois insertion with the coercion to set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.sub_mem", "description": "A subfield is closed under subtraction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.subring_closure_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield_class.coe_rat_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.closure_preimage_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subring.to_subfield", "description": "A `subring` containing inverses is a `subfield`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.mem_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.eq_of_eq_on_subfield_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.mul_mem", "description": "A subfield is closed under multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.comap", "description": "The preimage of a subfield along a ring homomorphism is a subfield.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.list_prod_mem", "description": "Product of a list of elements in a subfield is in the subfield.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.subtype", "description": "The embedding from a subfield of the field `K` to `K`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.field_closure_preimage_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.mem_comap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield", "description": "`subfield R` is the type of subfields of `R`. A subfield of `R` is a subset `s` that is a\n multiplicative submonoid and an additive subgroup. Note in particular that it shares the\n same 0 and 1 as R.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.carrier", "description": "`subfield R` is the type of subfields of `R`. A subfield of `R` is a subset `s` that is a\n multiplicative submonoid and an additive subgroup. Note in particular that it shares the\n same 0 and 1 as R.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.mul_mem'", "description": "`subfield R` is the type of subfields of `R`. A subfield of `R` is a subset `s` that is a\n multiplicative submonoid and an additive subgroup. Note in particular that it shares the\n same 0 and 1 as R.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.one_mem'", "description": "`subfield R` is the type of subfields of `R`. A subfield of `R` is a subset `s` that is a\n multiplicative submonoid and an additive subgroup. Note in particular that it shares the\n same 0 and 1 as R.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.add_mem'", "description": "`subfield R` is the type of subfields of `R`. A subfield of `R` is a subset `s` that is a\n multiplicative submonoid and an additive subgroup. Note in particular that it shares the\n same 0 and 1 as R.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.zero_mem'", "description": "`subfield R` is the type of subfields of `R`. A subfield of `R` is a subset `s` that is a\n multiplicative submonoid and an additive subgroup. Note in particular that it shares the\n same 0 and 1 as R.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.neg_mem'", "description": "`subfield R` is the type of subfields of `R`. A subfield of `R` is a subset `s` that is a\n multiplicative submonoid and an additive subgroup. Note in particular that it shares the\n same 0 and 1 as R.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.inv_mem'", "description": "`subfield R` is the type of subfields of `R`. A subfield of `R` is a subset `s` that is a\n multiplicative submonoid and an additive subgroup. Note in particular that it shares the\n same 0 and 1 as R.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.has_Inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.has_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.has_top", "description": "The subfield of `K` containing all elements of `K`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.coe_field_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.mem_to_submonoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.coe_to_subring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.map_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.ext", "description": "Two subfields are equal if they have the same elements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.one_mem", "description": "A subfield contains the field's 1.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.comap_infi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield_class.has_rat_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield_class.to_field", "description": "A subfield inherits a field structure", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.mem_field_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.multiset_sum_mem", "description": "Sum of a multiset of elements in a `subfield` is in the `subfield`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.comap_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.top_equiv_symm_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield_class", "description": "`subfield_class S K` states `S` is a type of subsets `s \u2286 K` closed under field operations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield_class.to_subring_class", "description": "`subfield_class S K` states `S` is a type of subsets `s \u2286 K` closed under field operations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield_class.to_inv_mem_class", "description": "`subfield_class S K` states `S` is a type of subsets `s \u2286 K` closed under field operations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.Icc_eq_filter_powerset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.Iic_eq_powerset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.Icc_eq_image_powerset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.Ico_eq