 {"name": "adjoin_root.minpoly_power_basis_gen_of_monic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.polynomial.quot_quot_equiv_comm", "description": "The natural isomorphism `(R/I)[x]/(f mod I) \u2245 (R[x]/I*R[x])/(f mod I[x])` where\n `f : R[X]` and `I : ideal R`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.coe_injective'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.equiv'_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.is_scalar_tower", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.equiv'_symm_to_alg_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.power_basis'_dim", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.smul_comm_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.mk_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.quot_equiv_quot_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.no_zero_smul_divisors_of_prime_of_degree_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.quot_map_of_equiv_quot_map_C_map_span_mk_symm_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.adjoin_root_eq_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.nontrivial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.minpoly_power_basis_gen", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.quot_adjoin_root_equiv_quot_polynomial_quot_mk_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.root_is_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.power_basis'", "description": " The power basis `1, root g, ..., root g ^ (d - 1)` for `adjoin_root g`,\nwhere `g` is a monic polynomial of degree `d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.coe_lift_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.minpoly.to_adjoin_apply'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.mk_ne_zero_of_degree_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.polynomial.quot_quot_equiv_comm_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.equiv'", "description": " If `S` is an extension of `R` with power basis `pb` and `g` is a monic polynomial over `R`\nsuch that `pb.gen` has a minimal polynomial `g`, then `S` is isomorphic to `adjoin_root g`.\n\nCompare `power_basis.equiv_of_root`, which would require\n`h\u2082 : aeval pb.gen (minpoly R (root g)) = 0`; that minimal polynomial is not\nguaranteed to be identical to `g`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.mk_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.finite_presentation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.smul_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_basis.quotient_equiv_quotient_minpoly_map_symm_apply_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.mk_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.lift_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.is_algebraic_root", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.power_basis_gen", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.distrib_mul_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.root", "description": "The adjoined root.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.lift_hom_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.lift", "description": "Lift a ring homomorphism `i : R \u2192+* S` to `adjoin_root f \u2192+* S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.power_basis", "description": " The power basis `1, root f, ..., root f ^ (d - 1)` for `adjoin_root f`,\nwhere `f` is an irreducible polynomial over a field of degree `d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.lift_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.quot_map_C_map_span_mk_equiv_quot_map_C_quot_map_span_mk", "description": "The natural isomorphism `R[\u03b1]/((I[x] \u2294 (f)) / (f)) \u2245 (R[x]/I[x])/((f) \u2294 I[x] / I[x])`\n for `\u03b1` a root of `f : R[X]` and `I : ideal R`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_basis.quotient_equiv_quotient_minpoly_map", "description": " Let `\u03b1` have minimal polynomial `f` over `R` and `I` be an ideal of `R`,\nthen `R[\u03b1] / (I) = (R[x] / (f)) / pS = (R/p)[x] / (f mod p)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_basis.quotient_equiv_quotient_minpoly_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.distrib_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.equiv'_to_alg_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.is_noetherian_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.quot_equiv_quot_map_apply_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.mk_left_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.eval\u2082_root", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.of", "description": "Embedding of the original ring `R` into `adjoin_root f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.smul_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.coe_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.field", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.mk_eq_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.lift_hom_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.quot_map_C_map_span_mk_equiv_quot_map_C_quot_map_span_mk_symm_quot_quot_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_basis.quotient_equiv_quotient_minpoly_map_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.mk_ne_zero_of_nat_degree_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.equiv", "description": " If `L` is a field extension of `F` and `f` is a polynomial over `F` then the set\nof maps from `F[x]/(f)` into `L` is in bijection with the set of roots of `f` in `L`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.mk", "description": "Ring homomorphism from `R[x]` to `adjoin_root f` sending `X` to the `root`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.equiv'_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.is_integral_root'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.is_scalar_tower_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.quot_map_of_equiv_quot_map_C_map_span_mk_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.power_basis_aux", "description": " The elements `1, root f, ..., root f ^ (d - 1)` form a basis for `adjoin_root f`,\nwhere `f` is an irreducible polynomial over a field of degree `d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.finite_type", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.quot_equiv_quot_map_symm_apply_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.algebra_map_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.induction_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.minpoly.to_adjoin_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.alg_hom_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.quot_map_of_equiv_quot_map_C_map_span_mk", "description": " The natural isomorphism `R[\u03b1]/(I[\u03b1]) \u2245 R[\u03b1]/((I[x] \u2294 (f)) / (f))` for `\u03b1` a root of\n`f : R[X]` and `I : ideal R`.\n\nSee `adjoin_root.quot_map_of_equiv` for the isomorphism with `(R/I)[X] / (f mod I)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.lift_comp_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.quot_equiv_quot_map_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.lift_hom_root", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.mul_div_root_cancel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.mod_by_monic_hom", "description": " `adjoin_root.mod_by_monic_hom` sends the equivalence class of `f` mod `g` to `f %\u2098 g`.\n\nThis is a well-defined right inverse to `adjoin_root.mk`, see `adjoin_root.mk_left_inverse`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.lift_hom_eq_alg_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.power_basis_dim", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.is_root_root", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.aeval_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.mod_by_monic_hom_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.minpoly_root", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.algebra_map_eq'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.of.injective_of_degree_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.power_basis_aux'", "description": " The elements `1, root g, ..., root g ^ (d - 1)` form a basis for `adjoin_root g`,\nwhere `g` is a monic polynomial of degree `d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.power_basis_aux'_repr_symm_apply", "description": " This lemma could be autogenerated by `@[simps]` but unfortunately that would require\nunfolding that causes a timeout.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.is_domain_of_prime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.quot_adjoin_root_equiv_quot_polynomial_quot", "description": "The natural isomorphism `R[\u03b1]/I[\u03b1] \u2245 (R/I)[X]/(f mod I)` for `\u03b1` a root of `f : R[X]`\n and `I : ideal R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.power_basis'_gen", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.quot_map_C_map_span_mk_equiv_quot_map_C_quot_map_span_mk_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.minpoly.to_adjoin.surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.has_coe_t", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.power_basis_aux'_repr_apply_to_fun", "description": " This lemma could be autogenerated by `@[simps]` but unfortunately that would require\nunfolding that causes a timeout.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.alg_hom_ext", "description": "Two `R`-`alg_hom` from `adjoin_root f` to the same `R`-algebra are the same iff\n   they agree on `root f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.quot_adjoin_root_equiv_quot_polynomial_quot_symm_mk_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.mk_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.span_maximal_of_irreducible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.minpoly.to_adjoin.apply_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.aeval_alg_hom_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.comm_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.lift_hom", "description": " Produce an algebra homomorphism `adjoin_root f \u2192\u2090[R] S` sending `root f` to\na root of `f` in `S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.minpoly.to_adjoin", "description": " The surjective algebra morphism `R[X]/(minpoly R x) \u2192 R[x]`.\nIf `R` is a GCD domain and `x` is integral, this is an isomorphism,\nsee `adjoin_root.minpoly.equiv_adjoin`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.is_integral_root", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.has_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.quot_equiv_quot_map", "description": "Promote `adjoin_root.quot_adjoin_root_equiv_quot_polynomial_quot` to an alg_equiv.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.algebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root", "description": " Adjoin a root of a polynomial `f` to a commutative ring `R`. We define the new ring\nas the quotient of `R[X]` by the principal ideal generated by `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_root.mk_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coplanar_of_fact_finrank_eq_two", "description": "A set of points in a two-dimensional space is coplanar.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coplanar.finite_dimensional_direction_affine_span", "description": "The direction of the affine span of coplanar points is finite-dimensional.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear_insert_insert_insert_of_mem_affine_span_pair", "description": "If three points lie in the affine span of two points, those five points are collinear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear_iff_of_mem", "description": " Given a point `p\u2080` in a set of points, that set is collinear if and\nonly if the points can all be expressed as multiples of the same\nvector, added to `p\u2080`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.finite_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_independent.affine_span_image_finset_eq_of_le_of_card_eq_finrank_add_one", "description": " If the `affine_span` of a finite subset of an affinely independent\nfamily lies in an affine subspace whose direction has dimension one\nless than its cardinality, it equals that subspace.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear_iff_not_affine_independent_of_ne", "description": "Three points are collinear if and only if they are not affinely independent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_of_fin_dim_affine_independent", "description": "An affine-independent family of points in a finite-dimensional affine space is finite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_independent.vector_span_eq_top_of_card_eq_finrank_add_one", "description": " The `vector_span` of a finite affinely independent family whose\ncardinality is one more than that of the finite-dimensional space is\n`\u22a4`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional_direction_affine_span_image_of_finite", "description": " The direction of the affine span of a subset of a family indexed\nby a `fintype` is finite-dimensional.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.card_eq_finrank_add_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_independent.vector_span_image_finset_eq_of_le_of_card_eq_finrank_add_one", "description": " If the `vector_span` of a finite subset of an affinely independent\nfamily lies in a submodule with dimension one less than its\ncardinality, it equals that submodule.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coplanar.subset", "description": "A subset of a coplanar set is coplanar.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear.coplanar_insert", "description": "Adding a point to a collinear set produces a coplanar set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear_insert_iff_of_mem_affine_span", "description": "Adding a point in the affine span of a set does not change whether that set is collinear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear_iff_not_affine_independent", "description": " Three points are collinear if and only if they are not affinely\nindependent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear_pair", "description": "Two points are collinear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finrank_vector_span_insert_le", "description": "Adding a point to a finite-dimensional subspace increases the dimension by at most one.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coplanar_pair", "description": "Two points are coplanar.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear.coplanar", "description": "Collinear points are coplanar.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear.subset", "description": "A subset of a collinear set is collinear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coplanar_empty", "description": "The empty set is coplanar.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional_vector_span_insert_set", "description": " The `vector_span` of adding a point to a set with a finite-dimensional `vector_span` is\nfinite-dimensional.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coplanar.finrank_le_two", "description": "**Alias** of the forward direction of `coplanar_iff_finrank_le_two`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_independent.affine_span_eq_of_le_of_card_eq_finrank_add_one", "description": " If the `affine_span` of a finite affinely independent family lies\nin an affine subspace whose direction has dimension one less than its\ncardinality, it equals that subspace.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.finite_dimensional", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coplanar.finite_dimensional_vector_span", "description": "The `vector_span` of coplanar points is finite-dimensional.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear_iff_rank_le_one", "description": "The definition of `collinear`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear", "description": " A set of points is collinear if their `vector_span` has dimension\nat most `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.exists_affine_basis_of_finite_dimensional", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear_triple_of_mem_affine_span_pair", "description": "If three points lie in the affine span of two points, the first three points are collinear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coplanar_triple", "description": "Three points are coplanar.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional_direction_affine_span_of_finite", "description": " The direction of the affine span of a finite set is\nfinite-dimensional.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finrank_vector_span_insert_le_set", "description": " Adding a point to a set with a finite-dimensional span increases the dimension by at most\none.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine.simplex.span_eq_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coplanar_singleton", "description": "A single point is coplanar.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear_iff_finrank_le_one", "description": " A set of points, whose `vector_span` is finite-dimensional, is\ncollinear if and only if their `vector_span` has dimension at most\n`1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_independent_iff_finrank_vector_span_eq", "description": " `n + 1` points are affinely independent if and only if their\n`vector_span` has dimension `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional_direction_affine_span_range", "description": " The direction of the affine span of a family indexed by a\n`fintype` is finite-dimensional.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear.finrank_le_one", "description": "**Alias** of the forward direction of `collinear_iff_finrank_le_one`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear_singleton", "description": "A single point is collinear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear_iff_exists_forall_eq_smul_vadd", "description": " A set of points is collinear if and only if they can all be\nexpressed as multiples of the same vector, added to the same base\npoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear.finite_dimensional_direction_affine_span", "description": "The direction of the affine span of collinear points is finite-dimensional.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coplanar_of_finrank_eq_two", "description": "A set of points in a two-dimensional space is coplanar.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_independent.vector_span_eq_of_le_of_card_eq_finrank_add_one", "description": " If the `vector_span` of a finite affinely independent\nfamily lies in a submodule with dimension one less than its\ncardinality, it equals that submodule.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear_iff_not_affine_independent_set", "description": "Three points are collinear if and only if they are not affinely independent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional_vector_span_image_of_finite", "description": " The `vector_span` of a subset of a family indexed by a `fintype`\nis finite-dimensional.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_independent_iff_not_collinear_set", "description": "Three points are affinely independent if and only if they are not collinear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional_direction_affine_span_insert", "description": " The direction of the affine span of adding a point to a finite-dimensional subspace is\nfinite-dimensional.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear.affine_span_eq_of_ne", "description": " The affine span of any two distinct points of a collinear set of points equals the affine\nspan of the whole set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear.collinear_insert_iff_of_ne", "description": " Given a collinear set of points, and two distinct points `p\u2082` and `p\u2083` in it, a point `p\u2081` is\ncollinear with the set if and only if it is collinear with `p\u2082` and `p\u2083`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_independent_iff_not_collinear_of_ne", "description": "Three points are affinely independent if and only if they are not collinear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear_insert_insert_of_mem_affine_span_pair", "description": "If two points lie in the affine span of two points, those four points are collinear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coplanar_insert_iff_of_mem_affine_span", "description": "Adding a point in the affine span of a set does not change whether that set is coplanar.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finrank_vector_span_range_le", "description": " The `vector_span` of an indexed family of `n + 1` points has\ndimension at most `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finrank_vector_span_le_iff_not_affine_independent", "description": " `n + 2` points have a `vector_span` with dimension at most `n` if\nand only if they are not affinely independent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear_insert_insert_insert_left_of_mem_affine_span_pair", "description": "If three points lie in the affine span of two points, the first four points are collinear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear.finite_dimensional_vector_span", "description": "The `vector_span` of collinear points is finite-dimensional.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finrank_vector_span_image_finset_le", "description": " The `vector_span` of `n + 1` points in an indexed family has\ndimension at most `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_set_of_fin_dim_affine_independent", "description": "An affine-independent subset of a finite-dimensional affine space is finite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_independent_iff_not_collinear", "description": " Three points are affinely independent if and only if they are not\ncollinear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_independent_iff_not_finrank_vector_span_le", "description": " `n + 2` points are affinely independent if and only if their\n`vector_span` does not have dimension at most `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear.mem_affine_span_of_mem_of_ne", "description": " A point in a collinear set of points lies in the affine span of any two distinct points of\nthat set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coplanar", "description": "A set of points is coplanar if their `vector_span` has dimension at most `2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coplanar_iff_finrank_le_two", "description": " A set of points, whose `vector_span` is finite-dimensional, is coplanar if and only if their\n`vector_span` has dimension at most `2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ne\u2082\u2083_of_not_collinear", "description": "If three points are not collinear, the second and third are different.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_independent.finrank_vector_span_image_finset", "description": " The `vector_span` of a finite subset of an affinely independent\nfamily has dimension one less than its cardinality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_independent.affine_span_eq_top_iff_card_eq_finrank_add_one", "description": " The `affine_span` of a finite affinely independent family is `\u22a4` iff the\nfamily's cardinality is one more than that of the finite-dimensional space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ne\u2081\u2083_of_not_collinear", "description": "If three points are not collinear, the first and third are different.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional_vector_span_of_finite", "description": "The `vector_span` of a finite set is finite-dimensional.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_independent_iff_le_finrank_vector_span", "description": " `n + 1` points are affinely independent if and only if their\n`vector_span` has dimension at least `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear_empty", "description": "The empty set is collinear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_independent.finrank_vector_span", "description": " The `vector_span` of a finite affinely independent family has\ndimension one less than its cardinality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional_vector_span_insert", "description": "The `vector_span` of adding a point to a finite-dimensional subspace is finite-dimensional.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ne\u2081\u2082_of_not_collinear", "description": "If three points are not collinear, the first and second are different.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional_vector_span_range", "description": " The `vector_span` of a family indexed by a `fintype` is\nfinite-dimensional.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear_insert_of_mem_affine_span_pair", "description": "If a point lies in the affine span of two points, those three points are collinear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.to_eventual_ranges_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.eventual_range_eq_range_precomp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.to_preimages", "description": "The subfunctor of `F` obtained by restricting to the preimages of a set `s \u2208 F.obj i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.to_eventual_ranges_sections_equiv", "description": "The sections of the functor `F : J \u2964 Type v` are in bijection with the sections of\n`F.eventual_ranges`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.to_preimages_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.is_mittag_leffler_of_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.to_eventual_ranges_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.eventual_range", "description": "The eventual range of the functor `F : J \u2964 Type v` at index `j : J` is the intersection\nof the ranges of all maps `F.map f` with `i : J` and `f : i \u27f6 j`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.eval_section_surjective_of_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.to_eventual_ranges_finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonempty_sections_of_finite_cofiltered_system", "description": " The cofiltered limit of nonempty finite types is nonempty.\n\nSee `nonempty_sections_of_finite_inverse_system` for a specialization to inverse limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.eval_section_injective_of_eventually_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonempty_sections_of_finite_inverse_system", "description": " The inverse limit of nonempty finite types is nonempty.\n\nSee `nonempty_sections_of_finite_cofiltered_system` for a generalization to cofiltered limits.\nThat version applies in almost all cases, and the only difference is that this version\nallows `J` to be empty.\n\nThis may be regarded as a generalization of K\u0151nig's lemma.\nTo specialize: given a locally finite connected graph, take `J\u1d52\u1d56` to be `\u2115` and\n`F j` to be length-`j` paths that start from an arbitrary fixed vertex.\nElements of `F.sections` can be read off as infinite rays in the graph.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.eventually_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.to_preimages_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.is_mittag_leffler", "description": "The functor `F : J \u2964 Type v` satisfies the Mittag-Leffler condition if for all `j : J`,\nthere exists some `i : J` and `f : i \u27f6 j` such that for all `k : J` and `g : k \u27f6 j`, the range\nof `F.map f` is contained in that of `F.map g`;\nin other words (see `is_mittag_leffler_iff_eventual_range`), the eventual range at `j` is attained\nby some `f : i \u27f6 j`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.thin_diagram_of_surjective", "description": "If `F` has all arrows surjective, then it \"factors through a poset\".", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.eventual_range_maps_to", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.is_mittag_leffler.subset_image_eventual_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonempty_sections_of_finite_cofiltered_system.init", "description": " This bootstraps `nonempty_sections_of_finite_inverse_system`. In this version,\nthe `F` functor is between categories of the same universe, and it is an easy\ncorollary to `Top.nonempty_limit_cone_of_compact_t2_inverse_system`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.is_mittag_leffler_of_exists_finite_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.is_mittag_leffler_iff_eventual_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.mem_eventual_range_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.to_eventual_ranges", "description": "The subfunctor of `F` obtained by restricting to the eventual range at each index.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.is_mittag_leffler.eq_image_eventual_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.to_preimages_finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.is_mittag_leffler.to_preimages", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.is_mittag_leffler_iff_subset_range_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.surjective_to_eventual_ranges", "description": "If `F` satisfies the Mittag-Leffler condition, its restriction to eventual ranges is a surjective\nfunctor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.to_eventual_ranges_nonempty", "description": "If `F` is nonempty at each index and Mittag-Leffler, then so is `F.to_eventual_ranges`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.to_preimages_nonempty_of_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.eventual_range_eq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.ih_name", "description": "`ih_name arg_name` is the name `ih_<arg_name>`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.get_inductive_info", "description": "Get information about an inductive type `I`, given `I`'s name.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.inductive_info", "description": "Information about an inductive type. Contains:\n\n- `iname`: the type's name.\n- `constructors`: information about the type's constructors.\n- `num_constructors`: the length of `constructors`.\n- `type`: the type's type.\n- `num_param`: the type's number of parameters.\n- `num_indices`: the type's number of indices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.inductive_info.iname", "description": "Information about an inductive type. Contains:\n\n- `iname`: the type's name.\n- `constructors`: information about the type's constructors.\n- `num_constructors`: the length of `constructors`.\n- `type`: the type's type.\n- `num_param`: the type's number of parameters.\n- `num_indices`: the type's number of indices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.inductive_info.constructors", "description": "Information about an inductive type. Contains:\n\n- `iname`: the type's name.\n- `constructors`: information about the type's constructors.\n- `num_constructors`: the length of `constructors`.\n- `type`: the type's type.\n- `num_param`: the type's number of parameters.\n- `num_indices`: the type's number of indices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.inductive_info.num_constructors", "description": "Information about an inductive type. Contains:\n\n- `iname`: the type's name.\n- `constructors`: information about the type's constructors.\n- `num_constructors`: the length of `constructors`.\n- `type`: the type's type.\n- `num_param`: the type's number of parameters.\n- `num_indices`: the type's number of indices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.inductive_info.type", "description": "Information about an inductive type. Contains:\n\n- `iname`: the type's name.\n- `constructors`: information about the type's constructors.\n- `num_constructors`: the length of `constructors`.\n- `type`: the type's type.\n- `num_param`: the type's number of parameters.\n- `num_indices`: the type's number of indices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.inductive_info.num_params", "description": "Information about an inductive type. Contains:\n\n- `iname`: the type's name.\n- `constructors`: information about the type's constructors.\n- `num_constructors`: the length of `constructors`.\n- `type`: the type's type.\n- `num_param`: the type's number of parameters.\n- `num_indices`: the type's number of indices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.inductive_info.num_indices", "description": "Information about an inductive type. Contains:\n\n- `iname`: the type's name.\n- `constructors`: information about the type's constructors.\n- `num_constructors`: the length of `constructors`.\n- `type`: the type's type.\n- `num_param`: the type's number of parameters.\n- `num_indices`: the type's number of indices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.induction'", "description": "A variant of `tactic.interactive.induction`, with the following differences:\n\n- If the major premise (the hypothesis we are performing induction on) has\n  complex indices, `induction'` 'remembers' them. A complex expression is any\n  expression that is not merely a local hypothesis. A major premise\n  `h : I p\u2081 ... p\u2099 j\u2081 ... j\u2098`, where `I` is an inductive type with `n`\n  parameters and `m` indices, has a complex index if any of the `j\u1d62` are\n  complex. In this situation, standard `induction` effectively forgets the exact\n  values of the complex indices, which often leads to unprovable goals.\n  `induction'` 'remembers' them by adding propositional equalities. As a\n  result, you may find equalities named `induction_eq` in your goal, and the\n  induction hypotheses may also quantify over additional equalities.\n- `induction'` generalises induction hypotheses as much as possible by default.\n  This means that if you eliminate `n` in the goal\n  ```lean\n  n m : \u2115\n  \u22a2 P n m\n  ```\n  the induction hypothesis is `\u2200 m, P n m` instead of `P n m`.\n- `induction'` generates much more human-friendly names than `induction`. It\n  also clears redundant hypotheses more aggressively.\n- `induction'` currently does not support custom induction principles a la\n  `induction using`.\n\nLike `induction`, `induction'` supports some modifiers:\n\n`induction' e with n\u2081 ... n\u2098` uses the names `n\u1d62` for the new hypotheses.\nInstead of a name, you can also give an underscore (`_`) to have `induction'`\ngenerate a name for you, or a hyphen (`-`) to clear the hypothesis and any\nhypotheses that depend on it.\n\n`induction' e fixing h\u2081 ... h\u2099` fixes the hypotheses `h\u1d62`, so the induction\nhypothesis is not generalised over these hypotheses.\n\n`induction' e fixing *` fixes all hypotheses. This disables the generalisation\nfunctionality, so this mode behaves like standard `induction`.\n\n`induction' e generalizing h\u2081 ... h\u2099` generalises only the hypotheses `h\u1d62`. This\nmode behaves like `induction e generalizing h\u2081 ... h\u2099`.\n\n`induction' t`, where `t` is an arbitrary term (rather than a hypothesis),\ngeneralises the goal over `t`, then performs induction on the generalised goal.\n\n`induction' h : t = x` is similar, but also adds an equation `h : t = x` to\nremember the value of `t`.\n\nTo debug this tactic, use\n\n```lean\nset_option trace.eliminate_hyp true\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate_expr", "description": "A variant of `tactic.eliminate_hyp` which performs induction or case analysis on\nan arbitrary expression. `eliminate_hyp` requires that the major premise is a\nhypothesis. `eliminate_expr` lifts this restriction by generalising the goal\nover the major premise before calling `eliminate_hyp`. The generalisation\nreplaces the major premise with a new hypothesis `x` everywhere in the goal.\nIf `eq_name` is `some h`, an equation `h : major_premise = x` is added to\nremember the value of the major premise.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_naming_info", "description": "Information used when naming a constructor argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_naming_info.mpinfo", "description": "Information used when naming a constructor argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_naming_info.iinfo", "description": "Information used when naming a constructor argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_naming_info.cinfo", "description": "Information used when naming a constructor argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_naming_info.ainfo", "description": "Information used when naming a constructor argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.get_index_occurrences", "description": "From the return type of a constructor `C` of an inductive type `I`, determine\nthe index occurrences of the constructor arguments of `C`.\n\nInput:\n\n- `num_params:` the number of parameters of `I`.\n- `ret_type`: the return type of `C`. `e` must be of the form `I x\u2081 ... x\u2099`.\n\nOutput: A map associating each local constant `c` that appears in any of the `x\u1d62`\nwith the set of indexes `j` such that `c` appears in `x\u2c7c` and `x\u2c7c`'s type\nmatches that of `c` according to `tactic.index_occurrence_type_match`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_intros", "description": "Introduce the new hypotheses generated by the minor premise for a given\nconstructor. The new hypotheses are given fresh (unique, non-human-friendly)\nnames. They are later renamed by `constructor_renames`. We delay the generation\nof the human-friendly names because when `constructor_renames` is called, more\nnames may have become unused.\n\nInput:\n\n- `generate_induction_hyps`: whether we generate induction hypotheses (i.e.\n  whether `eliminate_hyp` is in `induction` or `cases` mode).\n- `cinfo`: information about the constructor.\n\nOutput:\n\n- For each constructor argument: (1) the pretty name of the newly introduced\n  hypothesis corresponding to the argument; (2) the argument's\n  `constructor_argument_info`.\n- For each newly introduced induction hypothesis: (1) its pretty name; (2) the\n  pretty name of the hypothesis corresponding to the constructor argument from\n  which this induction hypothesis was derived; (3) that constructor argument's\n  `constructor_argument_info`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.generalize_hyps", "description": "Generalize hypotheses for the given major premise and generalization mode. See\n`generalization_mode` and `to_generalize`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_naming_rule", "description": "A constructor argument naming rule takes a `constructor_argument_naming_info`\nstructure and returns a list of suitable names for the argument. If the rule is\nnot applicable to the given constructor argument, the returned list is empty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_naming_rule_rec", "description": "Naming rule for recursive constructor arguments.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_naming_rule_type", "description": "Naming rule for constructor arguments whose type is associated with a list of\ntypical variable names. See `tactic.typical_variable_names`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.clear_dependent_if_exists", "description": "If `h` refers to a hypothesis, `clear_dependent_if_exists h` clears `h` and any\nhypotheses which depend on it. Otherwise, the tactic does nothing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_naming_rule_named", "description": "Naming rule for constructor arguments which are named in the constructor\ndeclaration.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_info.num_nameable_hypotheses", "description": "When we construct the goal for the minor premise of a given constructor, this is\nthe number of hypotheses we must name.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.assign_locals_to_unassigned_mvars", "description": "Apply `assign_local_to_unassigned_mvar` to a list of metavariables. Returns the\nnewly created local constants.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_naming_rule_fallback", "description": "Fallback constructor argument naming rule. This rule never fails.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.simplify_ih", "description": "Simplify an induction hypothesis.\n\nInput: a local constant\n```lean\nih : \u2200 (a\u2081 : A\u2081) ... (a\u2099 : A\u2099) (b\u2081 : B\u2081) ... (b\u2098 : B\u2098)\n       (eq\u2081 : y\u2081 = z\u2081) ... (eq\u2096 : y\u2092 = z\u2092), P\n```\nwhere `n = num_leading_pis`, `m = num_generalized` and `o = num_index_vars`.\nThe `a\u1d62` are arguments of the type of the constructor argument to which this\ninduction hypothesis belongs (usually zero). The `x\u1d62` are hypotheses that we\ngeneralised over before performing induction. The `eq\u1d62` are index equations.\n\nOutput: a new local constant\n```lean\nih' : \u2200 (a'\u2081 : A'\u2081) ... (b'\u2096 : B'\u2096) (eq'\u2081 : y'\u2081 = z'\u2081) ... (eq'\u2097 : y'\u2097 = z'\u2097), P'\n```\nThis new induction hypothesis is derived from `ih` by removing those `eq\u1d62` whose\nleft- and right-hand sides can be unified. This unification may also determine\nsome of the `a\u1d62` and `b\u1d62`. The `a'\u1d62`, `b'\u1d62` and `eq'\u1d62` are those `a\u1d62`, `b\u1d62` and\n`eq\u1d62` that were not removed by this process.\n\nSome of the `eq\u1d62` may be heterogeneous: `eq\u1d62 : y\u1d62 == z\u1d62`. In this case, we\nproceed as follows:\n\n- If `y\u1d62` and `z\u1d62` are defeq, then `eq\u1d62` is removed.\n- If `y\u1d62` and `z\u1d62` are not defeq but their types are, then `eq\u1d62` is replaced by\n  `eq'\u1d62 : x = y`.\n- Otherwise `eq\u1d62` remains unchanged.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.process_index_equation", "description": "Process one index equation for `simplify_ih`.\n\nInput: a local constant `h : x = y` or `h : x == y`.\n\nOutput: A proof of `x = y` or `x == y` and possibly a local constant of type\n`x = y` or `x == y` used in the proof. More specifically:\n\n- For `h : x = y` and `x` defeq `y`, we return the proof of `x = y` by\n  reflexivity and `none`.\n- For `h : x = y` and `x` not defeq `y`, we return `h` and `h`.\n- For `h : x == y` where `x` and `y` have defeq types:\n  - If `x` defeq `y`, we return the proof of `x == y` by reflexivity and `none`.\n  - If `x` not defeq `y`, we return `heq_of_eq h'` and a fresh local constant\n    `h' : x = y`.\n- For `h : x == y` where `x` and `y` do not have defeq types, we return\n  `h` and `h`.\n\nChecking for definitional equality of the left- and right-hand sides may assign\nmetavariables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.generalisation_mode_parser", "description": "Parse a `fixing` or `generalizing` clause for `induction'` or `cases'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.generalization_mode.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_naming_rule_prop", "description": "Naming rule for constructor arguments whose type is in `Prop`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.apply_constructor_argument_naming_rules", "description": "`apply_constructor_argument_naming_rules info rules` applies the constructor\nargument naming rules in `rules` to the constructor argument given by `info`.\nReturns the result of the first applicable rule. Fails if no rule is applicable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_info.has_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.major_premise_info", "description": "Information about a major premise (i.e. the hypothesis on which we are\nperforming induction). Contains:\n\n- `mpname`: the major premise's name.\n- `mpexpr`: the major premise itself.\n- `type`: the type of `mpexpr`.\n- `args`: the arguments of the major premise. The major premise has type\n  `I x\u2080 ... x\u2099`, where `I` is an inductive type. `args` is the map\n  `[0 \u2192 x\u2080, ..., n \u2192 x\u2099]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.major_premise_info.mpname", "description": "Information about a major premise (i.e. the hypothesis on which we are\nperforming induction). Contains:\n\n- `mpname`: the major premise's name.\n- `mpexpr`: the major premise itself.\n- `type`: the type of `mpexpr`.\n- `args`: the arguments of the major premise. The major premise has type\n  `I x\u2080 ... x\u2099`, where `I` is an inductive type. `args` is the map\n  `[0 \u2192 x\u2080, ..., n \u2192 x\u2099]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.major_premise_info.mpexpr", "description": "Information about a major premise (i.e. the hypothesis on which we are\nperforming induction). Contains:\n\n- `mpname`: the major premise's name.\n- `mpexpr`: the major premise itself.\n- `type`: the type of `mpexpr`.\n- `args`: the arguments of the major premise. The major premise has type\n  `I x\u2080 ... x\u2099`, where `I` is an inductive type. `args` is the map\n  `[0 \u2192 x\u2080, ..., n \u2192 x\u2099]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.major_premise_info.type", "description": "Information about a major premise (i.e. the hypothesis on which we are\nperforming induction). Contains:\n\n- `mpname`: the major premise's name.\n- `mpexpr`: the major premise itself.\n- `type`: the type of `mpexpr`.\n- `args`: the arguments of the major premise. The major premise has type\n  `I x\u2080 ... x\u2099`, where `I` is an inductive type. `args` is the map\n  `[0 \u2192 x\u2080, ..., n \u2192 x\u2099]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.major_premise_info.args", "description": "Information about a major premise (i.e. the hypothesis on which we are\nperforming induction). Contains:\n\n- `mpname`: the major premise's name.\n- `mpexpr`: the major premise itself.\n- `type`: the type of `mpexpr`.\n- `args`: the arguments of the major premise. The major premise has type\n  `I x\u2080 ... x\u2099`, where `I` is an inductive type. `args` is the map\n  `[0 \u2192 x\u2080, ..., n \u2192 x\u2099]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.intron_fresh", "description": "`intron_fresh n` introduces `n` hypotheses with names generated by\n`tactic.mk_fresh_name`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_renames", "description": "Rename the new hypotheses in the goal for a minor premise.\n\nInput:\n\n- `generate_induction_hyps`: whether we generate induction hypotheses (i.e.\n  whether `eliminate_hyp` is in `induction` or `cases` mode).\n- `mpinfo`: information about the major premise.\n- `iinfo`: information about the inductive type.\n- `cinfo`: information about the constructor whose minor premise we are\n  processing.\n- `with_patterns`: a list of `with` patterns given by the user. These are used\n  to name constructor arguments and induction hypotheses. If the list does not\n  contain enough patterns for all introduced hypotheses, the remaining ones are\n  treated as if the user had given `with_pattern.auto` (`_`).\n- `args` and `ihs`: the output of `constructor_intros`.\n\nOutput:\n\n- The newly introduced hypotheses corresponding to constructor arguments.\n- The newly introduced induction hypotheses.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_info", "description": "Information about a constructor argument. E.g. given the declaration\n\n```lean\ninduction \u2115 : Type\n| zero : \u2115\n| suc (n : \u2115) : \u2115\n```\n\nthe `zero` constructor has no arguments and the `suc` constructor has one\nargument, `n`.\n\nWe record the following information:\n\n- `aname`: the argument's name. If the argument was not explicitly named in the\n  declaration, the elaborator generates a name for it.\n- `type` : the argument's type.\n- `dependent`: whether the argument is dependent, i.e. whether it occurs in the\n  remainder of the constructor type.\n- `index_occurrences`: the index arguments of the constructor's return type\n  in which this argument occurs. If the constructor return type is\n  `I i\u2080 ... i\u2099` and the argument under consideration is `a`, and `a` occurs in\n  `i\u2081` and `i\u2082`, then the `index_occurrences` are `1, 2`. As an additional\n  requirement, for `i\u2c7c` to be considered an index occurrences,\n  the type of `i\u2c7c` must match that of `a` according to\n  `index_occurrence_type_match`.\n- `recursive_leading_pis`: `none` if this constructor is not recursive.\n  Otherwise, the argument has type `\u03a0 (x\u2081 : T\u2081) ... (x\u2099 : T\u2099), I ...`\n  where `I` is the inductive type to which this constructor belongs. In this\n  case, `recursive_leading_pis` is `some n` with `n` the number of leading \u03a0\n  binders in the argument's type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_info.aname", "description": "Information about a constructor argument. E.g. given the declaration\n\n```lean\ninduction \u2115 : Type\n| zero : \u2115\n| suc (n : \u2115) : \u2115\n```\n\nthe `zero` constructor has no arguments and the `suc` constructor has one\nargument, `n`.\n\nWe record the following information:\n\n- `aname`: the argument's name. If the argument was not explicitly named in the\n  declaration, the elaborator generates a name for it.\n- `type` : the argument's type.\n- `dependent`: whether the argument is dependent, i.e. whether it occurs in the\n  remainder of the constructor type.\n- `index_occurrences`: the index arguments of the constructor's return type\n  in which this argument occurs. If the constructor return type is\n  `I i\u2080 ... i\u2099` and the argument under consideration is `a`, and `a` occurs in\n  `i\u2081` and `i\u2082`, then the `index_occurrences` are `1, 2`. As an additional\n  requirement, for `i\u2c7c` to be considered an index occurrences,\n  the type of `i\u2c7c` must match that of `a` according to\n  `index_occurrence_type_match`.\n- `recursive_leading_pis`: `none` if this constructor is not recursive.\n  Otherwise, the argument has type `\u03a0 (x\u2081 : T\u2081) ... (x\u2099 : T\u2099), I ...`\n  where `I` is the inductive type to which this constructor belongs. In this\n  case, `recursive_leading_pis` is `some n` with `n` the number of leading \u03a0\n  binders in the argument's type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_info.type", "description": "Information about a constructor argument. E.g. given the declaration\n\n```lean\ninduction \u2115 : Type\n| zero : \u2115\n| suc (n : \u2115) : \u2115\n```\n\nthe `zero` constructor has no arguments and the `suc` constructor has one\nargument, `n`.\n\nWe record the following information:\n\n- `aname`: the argument's name. If the argument was not explicitly named in the\n  declaration, the elaborator generates a name for it.\n- `type` : the argument's type.\n- `dependent`: whether the argument is dependent, i.e. whether it occurs in the\n  remainder of the constructor type.\n- `index_occurrences`: the index arguments of the constructor's return type\n  in which this argument occurs. If the constructor return type is\n  `I i\u2080 ... i\u2099` and the argument under consideration is `a`, and `a` occurs in\n  `i\u2081` and `i\u2082`, then the `index_occurrences` are `1, 2`. As an additional\n  requirement, for `i\u2c7c` to be considered an index occurrences,\n  the type of `i\u2c7c` must match that of `a` according to\n  `index_occurrence_type_match`.\n- `recursive_leading_pis`: `none` if this constructor is not recursive.\n  Otherwise, the argument has type `\u03a0 (x\u2081 : T\u2081) ... (x\u2099 : T\u2099), I ...`\n  where `I` is the inductive type to which this constructor belongs. In this\n  case, `recursive_leading_pis` is `some n` with `n` the number of leading \u03a0\n  binders in the argument's type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_info.dependent", "description": "Information about a constructor argument. E.g. given the declaration\n\n```lean\ninduction \u2115 : Type\n| zero : \u2115\n| suc (n : \u2115) : \u2115\n```\n\nthe `zero` constructor has no arguments and the `suc` constructor has one\nargument, `n`.\n\nWe record the following information:\n\n- `aname`: the argument's name. If the argument was not explicitly named in the\n  declaration, the elaborator generates a name for it.\n- `type` : the argument's type.\n- `dependent`: whether the argument is dependent, i.e. whether it occurs in the\n  remainder of the constructor type.\n- `index_occurrences`: the index arguments of the constructor's return type\n  in which this argument occurs. If the constructor return type is\n  `I i\u2080 ... i\u2099` and the argument under consideration is `a`, and `a` occurs in\n  `i\u2081` and `i\u2082`, then the `index_occurrences` are `1, 2`. As an additional\n  requirement, for `i\u2c7c` to be considered an index occurrences,\n  the type of `i\u2c7c` must match that of `a` according to\n  `index_occurrence_type_match`.\n- `recursive_leading_pis`: `none` if this constructor is not recursive.\n  Otherwise, the argument has type `\u03a0 (x\u2081 : T\u2081) ... (x\u2099 : T\u2099), I ...`\n  where `I` is the inductive type to which this constructor belongs. In this\n  case, `recursive_leading_pis` is `some n` with `n` the number of leading \u03a0\n  binders in the argument's type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_info.index_occurrences", "description": "Information about a constructor argument. E.g. given the declaration\n\n```lean\ninduction \u2115 : Type\n| zero : \u2115\n| suc (n : \u2115) : \u2115\n```\n\nthe `zero` constructor has no arguments and the `suc` constructor has one\nargument, `n`.\n\nWe record the following information:\n\n- `aname`: the argument's name. If the argument was not explicitly named in the\n  declaration, the elaborator generates a name for it.\n- `type` : the argument's type.\n- `dependent`: whether the argument is dependent, i.e. whether it occurs in the\n  remainder of the constructor type.\n- `index_occurrences`: the index arguments of the constructor's return type\n  in which this argument occurs. If the constructor return type is\n  `I i\u2080 ... i\u2099` and the argument under consideration is `a`, and `a` occurs in\n  `i\u2081` and `i\u2082`, then the `index_occurrences` are `1, 2`. As an additional\n  requirement, for `i\u2c7c` to be considered an index occurrences,\n  the type of `i\u2c7c` must match that of `a` according to\n  `index_occurrence_type_match`.\n- `recursive_leading_pis`: `none` if this constructor is not recursive.\n  Otherwise, the argument has type `\u03a0 (x\u2081 : T\u2081) ... (x\u2099 : T\u2099), I ...`\n  where `I` is the inductive type to which this constructor belongs. In this\n  case, `recursive_leading_pis` is `some n` with `n` the number of leading \u03a0\n  binders in the argument's type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_info.recursive_leading_pis", "description": "Information about a constructor argument. E.g. given the declaration\n\n```lean\ninduction \u2115 : Type\n| zero : \u2115\n| suc (n : \u2115) : \u2115\n```\n\nthe `zero` constructor has no arguments and the `suc` constructor has one\nargument, `n`.\n\nWe record the following information:\n\n- `aname`: the argument's name. If the argument was not explicitly named in the\n  declaration, the elaborator generates a name for it.\n- `type` : the argument's type.\n- `dependent`: whether the argument is dependent, i.e. whether it occurs in the\n  remainder of the constructor type.\n- `index_occurrences`: the index arguments of the constructor's return type\n  in which this argument occurs. If the constructor return type is\n  `I i\u2080 ... i\u2099` and the argument under consideration is `a`, and `a` occurs in\n  `i\u2081` and `i\u2082`, then the `index_occurrences` are `1, 2`. As an additional\n  requirement, for `i\u2c7c` to be considered an index occurrences,\n  the type of `i\u2c7c` must match that of `a` according to\n  `index_occurrence_type_match`.\n- `recursive_leading_pis`: `none` if this constructor is not recursive.\n  Otherwise, the argument has type `\u03a0 (x\u2081 : T\u2081) ... (x\u2099 : T\u2099), I ...`\n  where `I` is the inductive type to which this constructor belongs. In this\n  case, `recursive_leading_pis` is `some n` with `n` the number of leading \u03a0\n  binders in the argument's type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.with_pattern.parser", "description": "Parser for a `with_pattern`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.get_constructor_info", "description": "Get information about a constructor `C` of an inductive type `I`.\n\nInput:\n\n- `iname`: the name of `I`.\n- `num_params`: the number of parameters of `I`.\n- `c` : the name of `C`.\n\nOutput:\n\nA `constructor_info` structure for `C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.assign_local_to_unassigned_mvar", "description": "`assign_local_to_unassigned_mvar mv pp_name binfo`, where `mv` is a\nmetavariable, acts as follows:\n\n- If `mv` is assigned, it is not changed and the tactic returns `none`.\n- If `mv` is not assigned, it is assigned a fresh local constant with\n  the type of `mv`, pretty name `pp_name` and binder info `binfo`. This local\n  constant is returned.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.generalization_mode", "description": "A value of `generalization_mode` describes the behaviour of the\nauto-generalisation functionality:\n\n- `generalize_all_except hs` means that the `hs` remain fixed and all other\n  hypotheses are generalised. However, there are three exceptions:\n\n  * Hypotheses depending on any `h` in `hs` also remain fixed. If we were to\n    generalise them, we would have to generalise `h` as well.\n  * Hypotheses which do not occur in the target and which do not mention the\n    major premise or its dependencies are never generalised. Generalising them\n    would not lead to a more general induction hypothesis.\n  * Local definitions (hypotheses of the form `h : T := t`) and their\n    dependencies are not generalised. This is due to limitations of the\n    implementation; local definitions could in principle be generalised.\n\n- `generalize_only hs` means that only the `hs` are generalised. Exception:\n  hypotheses which depend on the major premise are generalised even if they do\n  not appear in `hs`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.generalization_mode.generalize_all_except", "description": "A value of `generalization_mode` describes the behaviour of the\nauto-generalisation functionality:\n\n- `generalize_all_except hs` means that the `hs` remain fixed and all other\n  hypotheses are generalised. However, there are three exceptions:\n\n  * Hypotheses depending on any `h` in `hs` also remain fixed. If we were to\n    generalise them, we would have to generalise `h` as well.\n  * Hypotheses which do not occur in the target and which do not mention the\n    major premise or its dependencies are never generalised. Generalising them\n    would not lead to a more general induction hypothesis.\n  * Local definitions (hypotheses of the form `h : T := t`) and their\n    dependencies are not generalised. This is due to limitations of the\n    implementation; local definitions could in principle be generalised.\n\n- `generalize_only hs` means that only the `hs` are generalised. Exception:\n  hypotheses which depend on the major premise are generalised even if they do\n  not appear in `hs`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.generalization_mode.generalize_only", "description": "A value of `generalization_mode` describes the behaviour of the\nauto-generalisation functionality:\n\n- `generalize_all_except hs` means that the `hs` remain fixed and all other\n  hypotheses are generalised. However, there are three exceptions:\n\n  * Hypotheses depending on any `h` in `hs` also remain fixed. If we were to\n    generalise them, we would have to generalise `h` as well.\n  * Hypotheses which do not occur in the target and which do not mention the\n    major premise or its dependencies are never generalised. Generalising them\n    would not lead to a more general induction hypothesis.\n  * Local definitions (hypotheses of the form `h : T := t`) and their\n    dependencies are not generalised. This is due to limitations of the\n    implementation; local definitions could in principle be generalised.\n\n- `generalize_only hs` means that only the `hs` are generalised. Exception:\n  hypotheses which depend on the major premise are generalised even if they do\n  not appear in `hs`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.trace_state_eliminate_hyp", "description": "`trace_state_eliminate_hyp msg` traces `msg` followed by the tactic state if the\noption `trace.eliminate_hyp` is `true`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.with_pattern.to_name_spec", "description": "`to_name_spec auto_candidates p` returns a description of how the hypothesis to\nwhich the `with_pattern` `p` applies should be named. If this function returns\n`none`, the hypothesis should be cleared. If it returns `some (inl n)`, it\nshould receive exactly the name `n`, even if this shadows other hypotheses. If\nit returns `some (inr ns)`, it should receive the first unused name from `ns`.\n\nIf `p = auto`, the `auto_candidates` tactic is run to determine candidate names\nfor the hypothesis (from which the first fresh one is later chosen).\n`auto_candidates` must return a nonempty list.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.get_constructor_argument_info", "description": "Get information about the arguments of a constructor `C` of an inductive type\n`I`.\n\nInput:\n\n- `inductive_name`: the name of `I`.\n- `num_params`: the number of parameters of `I`.\n- `T`: the type of `C`.\n\nOutput: a `constructor_argument_info` structure for each argument of `C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.with_pattern", "description": "Representation of a pattern in the `with n ...` syntax supported by\n`induction'` and `cases'`. A `with_pattern` can be:\n\n- `with_pattern.auto` (`with _` or no `with` clause): use the name generated by the tactic.\n- `with_pattern.clear` (`with -`): clear this hypothesis and any hypotheses depending on it.\n- `with_pattern.exact n` (`with n`): use the name `n` for this hypothesis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.with_pattern.auto", "description": "Representation of a pattern in the `with n ...` syntax supported by\n`induction'` and `cases'`. A `with_pattern` can be:\n\n- `with_pattern.auto` (`with _` or no `with` clause): use the name generated by the tactic.\n- `with_pattern.clear` (`with -`): clear this hypothesis and any hypotheses depending on it.\n- `with_pattern.exact n` (`with n`): use the name `n` for this hypothesis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.with_pattern.clear", "description": "Representation of a pattern in the `with n ...` syntax supported by\n`induction'` and `cases'`. A `with_pattern` can be:\n\n- `with_pattern.auto` (`with _` or no `with` clause): use the name generated by the tactic.\n- `with_pattern.clear` (`with -`): clear this hypothesis and any hypotheses depending on it.\n- `with_pattern.exact n` (`with n`): use the name `n` for this hypothesis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.with_pattern.exact", "description": "Representation of a pattern in the `with n ...` syntax supported by\n`induction'` and `cases'`. A `with_pattern` can be:\n\n- `with_pattern.auto` (`with _` or no `with` clause): use the name generated by the tactic.\n- `with_pattern.clear` (`with -`): clear this hypothesis and any hypotheses depending on it.\n- `with_pattern.exact n` (`with n`): use the name `n` for this hypothesis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.generalization_mode.to_generalize", "description": "Given the major premise and a generalization_mode, this function returns the\nunique names of the hypotheses that should be generalized. See\n`generalization_mode` for what these are.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_info.is_recursive", "description": "`is_recursive c` is true iff the constructor argument described by `c` is\nrecursive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_info.has_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_info", "description": "Information about a constructor. Contains:\n\n- `cname`: the constructor's name.\n- `non_param_args`: information about the arguments of the constructor,\n  excluding the arguments induced by the parameters of the inductive type.\n- `num_non_param_args`: the length of `non_param_args`.\n- `rec_args`: the subset of `non_param_args` which are recursive constructor\n  arguments.\n- `num_rec_args`: the length of `rec_args`.\n\nFor example, take the constructor\n```lean\nlist.cons : \u2200 {\u03b1} (x : \u03b1) (xs : list \u03b1), list \u03b1\n```\n`\u03b1` is a parameter of `list`, so `non_param_args` contains information about `x`\nand `xs`. `rec_args` contains information about `xs`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_info.cname", "description": "Information about a constructor. Contains:\n\n- `cname`: the constructor's name.\n- `non_param_args`: information about the arguments of the constructor,\n  excluding the arguments induced by the parameters of the inductive type.\n- `num_non_param_args`: the length of `non_param_args`.\n- `rec_args`: the subset of `non_param_args` which are recursive constructor\n  arguments.\n- `num_rec_args`: the length of `rec_args`.\n\nFor example, take the constructor\n```lean\nlist.cons : \u2200 {\u03b1} (x : \u03b1) (xs : list \u03b1), list \u03b1\n```\n`\u03b1` is a parameter of `list`, so `non_param_args` contains information about `x`\nand `xs`. `rec_args` contains information about `xs`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_info.non_param_args", "description": "Information about a constructor. Contains:\n\n- `cname`: the constructor's name.\n- `non_param_args`: information about the arguments of the constructor,\n  excluding the arguments induced by the parameters of the inductive type.\n- `num_non_param_args`: the length of `non_param_args`.\n- `rec_args`: the subset of `non_param_args` which are recursive constructor\n  arguments.\n- `num_rec_args`: the length of `rec_args`.\n\nFor example, take the constructor\n```lean\nlist.cons : \u2200 {\u03b1} (x : \u03b1) (xs : list \u03b1), list \u03b1\n```\n`\u03b1` is a parameter of `list`, so `non_param_args` contains information about `x`\nand `xs`. `rec_args` contains information about `xs`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_info.num_non_param_args", "description": "Information about a constructor. Contains:\n\n- `cname`: the constructor's name.\n- `non_param_args`: information about the arguments of the constructor,\n  excluding the arguments induced by the parameters of the inductive type.\n- `num_non_param_args`: the length of `non_param_args`.\n- `rec_args`: the subset of `non_param_args` which are recursive constructor\n  arguments.\n- `num_rec_args`: the length of `rec_args`.\n\nFor example, take the constructor\n```lean\nlist.cons : \u2200 {\u03b1} (x : \u03b1) (xs : list \u03b1), list \u03b1\n```\n`\u03b1` is a parameter of `list`, so `non_param_args` contains information about `x`\nand `xs`. `rec_args` contains information about `xs`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_info.rec_args", "description": "Information about a constructor. Contains:\n\n- `cname`: the constructor's name.\n- `non_param_args`: information about the arguments of the constructor,\n  excluding the arguments induced by the parameters of the inductive type.\n- `num_non_param_args`: the length of `non_param_args`.\n- `rec_args`: the subset of `non_param_args` which are recursive constructor\n  arguments.\n- `num_rec_args`: the length of `rec_args`.\n\nFor example, take the constructor\n```lean\nlist.cons : \u2200 {\u03b1} (x : \u03b1) (xs : list \u03b1), list \u03b1\n```\n`\u03b1` is a parameter of `list`, so `non_param_args` contains information about `x`\nand `xs`. `rec_args` contains information about `xs`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_info.num_rec_args", "description": "Information about a constructor. Contains:\n\n- `cname`: the constructor's name.\n- `non_param_args`: information about the arguments of the constructor,\n  excluding the arguments induced by the parameters of the inductive type.\n- `num_non_param_args`: the length of `non_param_args`.\n- `rec_args`: the subset of `non_param_args` which are recursive constructor\n  arguments.\n- `num_rec_args`: the length of `rec_args`.\n\nFor example, take the constructor\n```lean\nlist.cons : \u2200 {\u03b1} (x : \u03b1) (xs : list \u03b1), list \u03b1\n```\n`\u03b1` is a parameter of `list`, so `non_param_args` contains information about `x`\nand `xs`. `rec_args` contains information about `xs`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.generalize_complex_index_args", "description": "Generalise the complex index arguments.\n\nInput:\n\n- `major premise`: the major premise.\n- `num_params`: the number of parameters of the inductive type.\n- `generate_induction_hyps`: whether we generate induction hypotheses (i.e.\n  whether `eliminate_hyp` is in `induction` or `cases` mode).\n\nOutput:\n\n- The new major premise. This procedure may change the major premise's type\n  signature, so the old major premise hypothesis is invalidated.\n- The number of index placeholder hypotheses we introduced.\n- The index placeholder hypotheses we introduced.\n- The number of hypotheses which were reverted because they contain complex\n  indices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate_hyp", "description": "`eliminate_hyp generate_ihs h gm with_patterns` performs induction or case\nanalysis on the hypothesis `h`. If `generate_ihs` is true, the tactic performs\ninduction, otherwise case analysis.\n\nIn case analysis mode, `eliminate_hyp` is very similar to `tactic.cases`. The\nonly differences (assuming no bugs in `eliminate_hyp`) are that `eliminate_hyp`\ncan do case analysis on a slightly larger class of hypotheses and that it\ngenerates more human-friendly names.\n\nIn induction mode, `eliminate_hyp` is similar to `tactic.induction`, but with\nmore significant differences:\n\n- If `h` (the hypothesis we are performing induction on) has complex indices,\n  `eliminate_hyp` 'remembers' them. A complex expression is any expression that\n  is not merely a local hypothesis. A hypothesis `h : I p\u2081 ... p\u2099 j\u2081 ... j\u2098`,\n  where `I` is an inductive type with `n` parameters and `m` indices, has a\n  complex index if any of the `j\u1d62` are complex. In this situation, standard\n  `induction` effectively forgets the exact values of the complex indices,\n  which often leads to unprovable goals. `eliminate_hyp` 'remembers' them by\n  adding propositional equalities. As a result, you may find equalities named\n  `induction_eq` in your goal, and the induction hypotheses may also quantify\n  over additional equalities.\n- `eliminate_hyp` generalises induction hypotheses as much as possible by\n  default. This means that if you eliminate `n` in the goal\n  ```lean\n  n m : \u2115\n  \u22a2 P n m\n  ```\n  the induction hypothesis is `\u2200 m, P n m` instead of `P n m`.\n\n  You can modify this behaviour by giving a different generalisation mode `gm`;\n  see `tactic.eliminate.generalization_mode`.\n- `eliminate_hyp` generates much more human-friendly names than `induction`. It\n  also clears more redundant hypotheses.\n- `eliminate_hyp` currently does not support custom induction principles a la\n  `induction using`.\n\nThe `with_patterns` can be used to give names for the hypotheses introduced by\n`eliminate_hyp`. See `tactic.eliminate.with_pattern` for details.\n\nTo debug this tactic, use\n\n```lean\nset_option trace.eliminate_hyp true\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.inductive_info.has_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.set_cases_tags", "description": "Updates the tags of new subgoals produced by `cases` or `induction`. `in_tag`\nis the initial tag, i.e. the tag of the goal on which `cases`/`induction` was\napplied. `rs` should contain, for each subgoal, the constructor name\nassociated with that goal and the hypotheses that were introduced.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.cases'", "description": "A variant of `tactic.interactive.cases`, with minor changes:\n\n- `cases'` can perform case analysis on some (rare) goals that `cases` does not\n  support.\n- `cases'` generates much more human-friendly names for the new hypotheses it\n  introduces.\n\nThis tactic supports the same modifiers as `cases`, e.g.\n\n```lean\ncases' H : e = x with n _ o\n```\n\nThis is almost exactly the same as `tactic.interactive.induction'`, only that no\ninduction hypotheses are generated.\n\nTo debug this tactic, use\n\n```lean\nset_option trace.eliminate_hyp true\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.trace_eliminate_hyp", "description": "`trace_eliminate_hyp msg` traces `msg` if the option `trace.eliminate_hyp` is\n`true`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.generalization_mode.has_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.with_pattern.has_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.get_major_premise_info", "description": "Get information about a major premise. The given `expr` must be a local\nhypothesis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_naming_rule_index", "description": "Naming rule for constructor arguments associated with an index.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.match_recursive_constructor_arg", "description": "`match_recursive_constructor_arg I T`, given `I` the name of an inductive type\nand `T` the type of an argument of a constructor of `I`, returns `none` if the\nargument is non-recursive (i.e. `I` does not appear in `T`). If the argument is\nrecursive, `T` is of the form `\u03a0 (x\u2081 : T\u2081) ... (x\u2099 : T\u2099), I ...`, in which case\n`match_recursive_constructor_arg` returns `some n`. Matching is performed up to\nWHNF with semireducible transparency.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.with_pattern.clause_parser", "description": "Parser for a `with` clause.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.index_occurrence_type_match", "description": "`index_occurrence_type_match t s` is true iff `t` and `s` are definitionally\nequal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eliminate.constructor_argument_names", "description": "Get possible names for a constructor argument. This tactic applies all the\npreviously defined rules in order. It cannot fail and always returns a nonempty\nlist.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_inv_smul_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "arrow_add_action_to_has_vadd_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vadd_left_cancel_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_action.to_perm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "arrow_action_to_has_smul_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_action.to_perm_injective", "description": "`add_action.to_perm` is injective on faithful actions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_distrib_mul_action.to_mul_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_ne_zero_iff_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.smul_right_iff\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "distrib_mul_action.to_add_equiv", "description": " Each element of the group defines an additive monoid isomorphism.\n\nThis is a stronger version of `mul_action.to_perm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_action.surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.smul_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.to_perm_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.to_perm", "description": "Given an action of a group `\u03b1` on `\u03b2`, each `g : \u03b1` defines a permutation of `\u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.apply_has_faithful_smul", "description": "`equiv.perm.apply_mul_action` is faithful.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_distrib_mul_action.to_mul_aut", "description": " Each element of the group defines an multiplicative monoid isomorphism.\n\nThis is a stronger version of `mul_action.to_perm_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_unit.smul_sub_iff_sub_inv_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_action.to_perm", "description": "Given an action of an additive group `\u03b1` on `\u03b2`, each `g : \u03b1` defines a permutation of `\u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.bijective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "distrib_mul_action.to_add_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "arrow_action", "description": "If `G` acts on `A`, then it acts also on `A \u2192 B`, by `(g \u2022 F) a = F (g\u207b\u00b9 \u2022 a)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut_arrow_apply_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vadd_neg_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.bijective\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "distrib_mul_action.to_add_equiv\u2080", "description": " Each non-zero element of a `group_with_zero` defines an additive monoid isomorphism of an\n`add_monoid` on which it acts distributively.\n\nThis is a stronger version of `distrib_mul_action.to_add_monoid_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_eq_iff_eq_inv_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "right_cancel_monoid.to_has_faithful_smul", "description": "`monoid.to_mul_action` is faithful on cancellative monoids.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "distrib_mul_action.to_add_aut", "description": " Each element of the group defines an additive monoid isomorphism.\n\nThis is a stronger version of `mul_action.to_perm_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.surjective\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.smul_left_iff\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut_arrow_apply_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_inv_smul_iff\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inv_smul_eq_iff\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.to_perm_hom", "description": "Given an action of a group `\u03b1` on a set `\u03b2`, each `g : \u03b1` defines a permutation of `\u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_eq_zero_iff_eq'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.smul_right_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "distrib_mul_action.to_add_aut_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_zpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_eq_zero_iff_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cancel_monoid_with_zero.to_has_faithful_smul", "description": "`monoid.to_mul_action` is faithful on nontrivial cancellative monoids with zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_unit_smul_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.apply_mul_action", "description": " The tautological action by `equiv.perm \u03b1` on `\u03b1`.\n\nThis generalizes `function.End.apply_mul_action`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vadd_eq_iff_eq_neg_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_action.to_perm_hom", "description": " Given an action of a additive group `\u03b1` on a set `\u03b2`, each `g : \u03b1` defines a permutation of\n`\u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vadd_left_cancel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_ne_zero_iff_ne'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_left_cancel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inv_smul_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_distrib_mul_action.to_mul_aut_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut_arrow", "description": "Given groups `G H` with `G` acting on `A`, `G` acts by\n multiplicative automorphisms on `A \u2192 H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_action.to_perm_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "neg_vadd_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "neg_vadd_eq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_inv_smul\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.to_perm_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_left_cancel_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.to_perm_injective", "description": "`mul_action.to_perm` is injective on faithful actions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "arrow_mul_distrib_mul_action", "description": "When `B` is a monoid, `arrow_action` is additionally a `mul_distrib_mul_action`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_action.to_perm_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_add_unit.vadd_left_cancel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_unit.smul_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_right_cancel_monoid.to_has_faithful_vadd", "description": "`add_monoid.to_add_action` is faithful on additive cancellative monoids.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inv_smul_smul\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_distrib_mul_action.to_mul_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_action.injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "arrow_add_action", "description": "If `G` acts on `A`, then it acts also on `A \u2192 B`, by\n`(g +\u1d65 F) a = F (g\u207b\u00b9 +\u1d65 a)`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "distrib_mul_action.to_add_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_distrib_mul_action.to_mul_equiv", "description": " Each element of the group defines a multiplicative monoid isomorphism.\n\nThis is a stronger version of `mul_action.to_perm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_inv_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_action.bijective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inv_smul_eq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_unit.smul_left_cancel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_neg_vadd_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.smul_left_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.to_perm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.injective\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_self_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.eq_tsub_iff_add_eq_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.tsub_lt_iff_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.lt_tsub_iff_left_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.tsub_eq_iff_eq_add_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_le_tsub_iff_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_of_le_tsub_right_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_lt_iff_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_tsub_tsub_cancel_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_lt_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_right_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_lt_iff_tsub_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.tsub_tsub_cancel_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.tsub_lt_tsub_iff_left_of_le", "description": "See `lt_tsub_iff_left_of_le_of_le` for a weaker statement in a partial order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.tsub_add_eq_add_tsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_tsub_iff_right_of_le", "description": "See `lt_tsub_iff_right` for a stronger statement in a linear order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_eq_iff_eq_add_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_lt_tsub_iff_right", "description": "This lemma also holds for `ennreal`, but we need a different proof for that.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_add_tsub_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_tsub_cancel_of_le", "description": "See `tsub_tsub_le` for an inequality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_le_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_tsub_iff_add_eq_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_add_min", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.tsub_lt_iff_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_tsub_cancel_iff_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.le_tsub_iff_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.tsub_lt_self_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.tsub_tsub_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_eq_zero_iff_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_add_eq_max", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.tsub_add_tsub_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_add_eq_add_tsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.tsub_lt_tsub_iff_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_tsub_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_tsub_cancel_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.le_tsub_iff_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_pos_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_add_tsub_cancel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_tsub_assoc_of_le", "description": "See `add_tsub_le_assoc` for an inequality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_tsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "canonically_ordered_add_monoid.to_add_cancel_comm_monoid", "description": " A `canonically_ordered_add_monoid` with ordered subtraction and order-reflecting addition is\ncancellative. This is not an instance at it would form a typeclass loop.\n\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_tsub_tsub_cancel_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_tsub_tsub_cancel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_lt_self_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_le_tsub_iff_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_inj_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_add_cancel_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.lt_of_tsub_lt_tsub_left_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_left_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_eq_tsub_min", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_of_le_tsub_left_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.lt_tsub_iff_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_tsub_iff_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_pos_iff_not_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_eq_zero_of_le", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `tsub_eq_zero_iff_le`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_of_tsub_lt_tsub_left_of_le", "description": "See `lt_of_tsub_lt_tsub_left` for a stronger statement in a linear order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"