cription": "The functor associated to the embedding of subgroupoids", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subgroupoid.comap_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subgroupoid.mem_im_objs_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subgroupoid.top_is_normal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subgroupoid.is_wide_iff_objs_eq_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subgroupoid.hom.inj_on_objects", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subgroupoid.is_thin_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subgroupoid.mem_full_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subgroupoid.le_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subgroupoid.sigma.set_like", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subgroupoid.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subgroupoid.hom", "description": "The embedding of the coerced subgroupoid to its parent", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_rotate", "description": "Rotate `fin n` one step to the right.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.preimage_apply_01_prod'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.pi_fin_succ_above_iso", "description": " Order isomorphism between `\u03a0 j : fin (n + 1), \u03b1 j` and\n`\u03b1 i \u00d7 \u03a0 j : fin n, \u03b1 (fin.succ_above i j)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_fin_two_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_fin_two_equiv", "description": " `\u03a0 i : fin 2, \u03b1 i` is equivalent to `\u03b1 0 \u00d7 \u03b1 1`. See also `fin_two_arrow_equiv` for a\nnon-dependent version and `prod_equiv_pi_fin_two` for a version with inputs `\u03b1 \u03b2 : Type u`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_rotate_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.pi_fin_succ", "description": "Equivalence between `fin (n + 1) \u2192 \u03b2` and `\u03b2 \u00d7 (fin n \u2192 \u03b2)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.cast_le_order_iso_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod_equiv_pi_fin_two", "description": " A product space `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2` is equivalent to the space `\u03a0 i : fin 2, \u03b3 i`, where\n`\u03b3 = fin.cons \u03b1 (fin.cons \u03b2 fin_zero_elim)`. See also `pi_fin_two_equiv` and\n`fin_two_arrow_equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.cast_le_order_iso", "description": " Promote a `fin n` into a larger `fin m`, as a subtype where the underlying\nvalues are retained. This is the `order_iso` version of `fin.cast_le`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.div_mod_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_two_equiv", "description": "Equivalence between `fin 2` and `bool`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.div_mod_equiv", "description": " The equivalence induced by `a \u21a6 (a / n, a % n)` for nonzero `n`.\n\nSee `int.div_mod_unique` for a similar propositional statement.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_add_flip_apply_mk_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_add_flip_apply_mk_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_sum_fin_equiv_symm_apply_cast_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv_last", "description": "`equiv` between `fin (n + 1)` and `option (fin n)` sending `fin.last n` to `none`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod_equiv_pi_fin_two_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv'_symm_some", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod_equiv_pi_fin_two_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsingleton_fin_one", "description": "`fin 1` is a subsingleton.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_rotate_last'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.preimage_apply_01_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_zero_equiv'", "description": "Equivalence between `fin 0` and `pempty`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_sum_fin_equiv", "description": "Equivalence between `fin m \u2295 fin n` and `fin (m + n)`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv'_symm_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_above_equiv_symm_apply_last", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.pi_fin_succ_above_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_add_flip_apply_cast_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv'_above", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.pi_fin_succ_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_fin_rotate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_sum_fin_equiv_apply_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv_symm_some", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_fin_rotate_of_ne_last", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv_last_symm_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.pi_fin_succ_above_equiv", "description": "Equivalence between `\u03a0 j : fin (n + 1), \u03b1 j` and `\u03b1 i \u00d7 \u03a0 j : fin n, \u03b1 (fin.succ_above i j)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_prod_fin_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.fin_two_arrow_iso", "description": " The space of functions `fin 2 \u2192 \u03b1` is order equivalent to `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b1`. See also\n`order_iso.pi_fin_two_iso`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_rotate_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_above_equiv_symm_apply_ne_last", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_sum_fin_equiv_symm_apply_nat_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv_symm_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_sum_fin_equiv_symm_last", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.div_mod_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_add_flip_apply_nat_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv'_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_one_equiv", "description": "Equivalence between `fin 1` and `unit`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv_last_last", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_two_arrow_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.pi_fin_two_iso", "description": " `\u03a0 i : fin 2, \u03b1 i` is order equivalent to `\u03b1 0 \u00d7 \u03b1 1`. See also `order_iso.fin_two_arrow_equiv`\nfor a non-dependent version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.div_mod_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.snoc_eq_cons_rotate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_congr", "description": "The 'identity' equivalence between `fin n` and `fin m` when `n = m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_rotate_succ_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_two_arrow_equiv", "description": " The space of functions `fin 2 \u2192 \u03b1` is equivalent to `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b1`. See also `pi_fin_two_equiv` and\n`prod_equiv_pi_fin_two`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_zero_equiv", "description": "Equivalence between `fin 0` and `empty`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.pi_fin_succ_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv'_symm_some_below", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_fin_two_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsingleton_fin_zero", "description": "`fin 0` is a subsingleton.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv'_succ_above", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_add_flip", "description": "The equivalence between `fin (m + n)` and `fin (n + m)` which rotates by `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv'_symm_coe_below", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_congr_apply_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_two_arrow_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv'_last_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv'_ne_last_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_prod_fin_equiv", "description": "Equivalence between `fin m \u00d7 fin n` and `fin (m * n)`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_congr_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv'_below", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv'_symm_coe_above", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_congr_symm_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_rotate_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_above_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_rotate_last", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.pi_fin_succ_above_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_rotate_apply_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv'_symm_some_above", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_above_equiv", "description": "`succ_above` as an order isomorphism between `fin n` and `{x : fin (n + 1) // x \u2260 p}`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv'", "description": " An equivalence that removes `i` and maps it to `none`.\nThis is a version of `fin.pred_above` that produces `option (fin n)` instead of\nmapping both `i.cast_succ` and `i.succ` to `i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_prod_fin_equiv_apply_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.cast_le_order_iso_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.div_mod_equiv", "description": " The equivalence induced by `a \u21a6 (a / n, a % n)` for nonzero `n`.\n\nThis is like `fin_prod_fin_equiv.symm` but with `m` infinite.\nSee `nat.div_mod_unique` for a similar propositional statement.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_sum_fin_equiv_apply_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv'_zero", "description": "The equiv version of `fin.pred_above_zero`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv_last_symm_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv", "description": " Equivalence between `fin (n + 1)` and `option (fin n)`.\nThis is a version of `fin.pred` that produces `option (fin n)` instead of\nrequiring a proof that the input is not `0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv_symm_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.div_mod_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv_last_cast_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_congr_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_succ_equiv_last_symm_some", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "card_comm_eq_card_conj_classes_mul_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "comm_prob_eq_one_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "comm_prob_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "comm_prob_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "comm_prob_def'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "comm_prob_le_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.comm_prob_subgroup_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "comm_prob", "description": "The commuting probability of a finite type with a multiplication operation", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inv_card_commutator_le_comm_prob", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.comm_prob_quotient_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsupport_smul_subset_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support_iff_eventually_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_mem_tsupport_iff_eventually_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support_comp_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support.comp\u2082_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_of_tsupport", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_mul_support.comp\u2082_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "range_subset_insert_image_tsupport", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support.comp_closed_embedding", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsupport", "description": "The topological support of a function is the closure of its support. i.e. the closure of the\n set of all elements where the function is nonzero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subset_mul_tsupport", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support.smul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_compact_iff_has_compact_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite.exists_finset_nhd_mul_support_subset", "description": " If a family of functions `f` has locally-finite multiplicative support, subordinate to a family\nof open sets, then for any point we can find a neighbourhood on which only finitely-many members of\n`f` are not equal to 1.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "image_eq_one_of_nmem_mul_tsupport", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support.mul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support.mono'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support.is_compact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsupport_mul_subset_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_mul_support_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support.intro", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_mul_support.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support.mul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_closed_mul_tsupport", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_tsupport_eq_empty_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_tsupport", "description": "The topological support of a function is the closure of its support, i.e. the closure of the\n set of all elements where the function is not equal to 1.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support", "description": " A function `f` *has compact support* or is *compactly supported* if the closure of the support\nof `f` is compact. In a T\u2082 space this is equivalent to `f` being equal to `0` outside a compact\nset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "range_subset_insert_image_mul_tsupport", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite.exists_finset_nhd_support_subset", "description": " If a family of functions `f` has locally-finite support, subordinate to a family of open sets,\nthen for any point we can find a neighbourhood on which only finitely-many members of `f` are\nnon-zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_mul_support.intro", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_mem_mul_tsupport_iff_eventually_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsupport_mul_subset_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_mul_support.mono'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subset_tsupport", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "image_eq_zero_of_nmem_tsupport", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_mul_support.is_compact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support.smul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_mul_support.is_compact_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_of_mul_tsupport", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_mul_support.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_mul_support_comp_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_compact_iff_has_compact_mul_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "range_eq_image_tsupport_or", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsupport_eq_empty_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "range_eq_image_mul_tsupport_or", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_mul_support", "description": " A function `f` *has compact multiplicative support* or is *compactly supported* if the closure\nof the multiplicative support of `f` is compact. In a T\u2082 space this is equivalent to `f` being equal\nto `1` outside a compact set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_mul_support.comp_closed_embedding", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_closed_tsupport", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_mul_support.comp_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support.is_compact_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_mul_support_iff_eventually_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support.comp_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support.smul_left'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.sum_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.distrib_mul_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.curry_sum", "description": " A multilinear map on `\u03a0 i : \u03b9 \u2295 \u03b9', M'` defines a multilinear map on `\u03a0 i : \u03b9, M'`\ntaking values in the space of multilinear maps on `\u03a0 i : \u03b9', M'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.mk_pi_algebra_fin_apply_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.dom_dom_congr_linear_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.snoc_add", "description": " In the specific case of multilinear maps on spaces indexed by `fin (n+1)`, where one can build\nan element of `\u03a0(i : fin (n+1)), M i` using `snoc`, one can express directly the additivity of a\nmultilinear map along the first variable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.has_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.smul_right", "description": " Given an `R`-multilinear map `f` taking values in `R`, `f.smul_right z` is the map\nsending `m` to `f m \u2022 z`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.curry_right", "description": " Given a multilinear map `f` in `n+1` variables, split the last variable to obtain\na multilinear map in `n` variables taking values in linear maps from `M (last n)` to `M\u2082`, given by\n`m \u21a6 (x \u21a6 f (snoc m x))`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.dom_dom_congr_linear_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.dom_dom_congr_eq_iff", "description": " The results of applying `dom_dom_congr` to two maps are equal if\nand only if those maps are.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.mk_pi_ring", "description": " The canonical multilinear map on `R^\u03b9` when `\u03b9` is finite, associating to `m` the product of\nall the `m i` (multiplied by a fixed reference element `z` in the target module). See also\n`mk_pi_algebra` for a more general version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map", "description": " Multilinear maps over the ring `R`, from `\u03a0i, M\u2081 i` to `M\u2082` where `M\u2081 i` and `M\u2082` are modules\nover `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.to_fun", "description": " Multilinear maps over the ring `R`, from `\u03a0i, M\u2081 i` to `M\u2082` where `M\u2081 i` and `M\u2082` are modules\nover `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map_add'", "description": " Multilinear maps over the ring `R`, from `\u03a0i, M\u2081 i` to `M\u2082` where `M\u2081 i` and `M\u2082` are modules\nover `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map_smul'", "description": " Multilinear maps over the ring `R`, from `\u03a0i, M\u2081 i` to `M\u2082` where `M\u2081 i` and `M\u2082` are modules\nover `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.cons_add", "description": " In the specific case of multilinear maps on spaces indexed by `fin (n+1)`, where one can build\nan element of `\u03a0(i : fin (n+1)), M i` using `cons`, one can express directly the additivity of a\nmultilinear map along the first variable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.uncurry_left", "description": " Given a linear map `f` from `M 0` to multilinear maps on `n` variables,\nconstruct the corresponding multilinear map on `n+1` variables obtained by concatenating\nthe variables, given by `m \u21a6 f (m 0) (tail m)`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.comp_linear_map_injective", "description": "Composing with a family of surjective linear maps is injective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.mk_pi_algebra", "description": " Given an `R`-algebra `A`, `mk_pi_algebra` is the multilinear map on `A^\u03b9` associating\nto `m` the product of all the `m i`.\n\nSee also `multilinear_map.mk_pi_algebra_fin` for a version that works with a non-commutative\nalgebra `A` but requires `\u03b9 = fin n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_curry_left_equiv", "description": " The space of multilinear maps on `\u03a0(i : fin (n+1)), M i` is canonically isomorphic to\nthe space of linear maps from `M 0` to the space of multilinear maps on\n`\u03a0(i : fin n), M i.succ `, by separating the first variable. We register this isomorphism as a\nlinear isomorphism in `multilinear_curry_left_equiv R M M\u2082`.\n\nThe direct and inverse maps are given by `f.uncurry_left` and `f.curry_left`. Use these\nunless you need the full framework of linear equivs.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.add_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.comp_linear_map_id", "description": "Composing a multilinear map with the identity linear map in each argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.range", "description": "The range of a multilinear map, closed under scalar multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_curry_right_equiv", "description": " The space of multilinear maps on `\u03a0(i : fin (n+1)), M i` is canonically isomorphic to\nthe space of linear maps from the space of multilinear maps on `\u03a0(i : fin n), M i.cast_succ` to the\nspace of linear maps on `M (last n)`, by separating the last variable. We register this isomorphism\nas a linear isomorphism in `multilinear_curry_right_equiv R M M\u2082`.\n\nThe direct and inverse maps are given by `f.uncurry_right` and `f.curry_right`. Use these\nunless you need the full framework of linear equivs.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map_update_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.comp_linear_map", "description": " If `g` is a multilinear map and `f` is a collection of linear maps,\nthen `g (f\u2081 m\u2081, ..., f\u2099 m\u2099)` is again a multilinear map, that we call\n`g.comp_linear_map f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.const_linear_equiv_of_is_empty", "description": " The space of constant maps is equivalent to the space of maps that are multilinear with respect\nto an empty family.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.mk_pi_ring_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map_coord_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.zero_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.mk_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.coe_restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map_update_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.sub_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map_nonempty", "description": "The map is always nonempty. This lemma is needed to apply `sub_mul_action.zero_mem`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.comp_linear_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.to_linear_map", "description": " If `f` is a multilinear map, then `f.to_linear_map m i` is the linear map obtained by fixing all\ncoordinates but `i` equal to those of `m`, and varying the `i`-th coordinate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.const_linear_equiv_of_is_empty_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.uncurry_curry_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.curry_fin_finset_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map_sum", "description": " If `f` is multilinear, then `f (\u03a3_{j\u2081} g\u2081 j\u2081, ..., \u03a3_{j\u2099} g\u2099 j\u2099)` is the sum of\n`f (g\u2081 (r 1), ..., g\u2099 (r n))` where `r` ranges over all functions `r`. This follows from\nmultilinearity by expanding successively with respect to each coordinate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.comp_multilinear_map", "description": "Composing a multilinear map with a linear map gives again a multilinear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.smul_right_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.of_subsingleton_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.curry_right_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.mk_pi_algebra_fin_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.curry_left", "description": " Given a multilinear map `f` in `n+1` variables, split the first variable to obtain\na linear map into multilinear maps in `n` variables, given by `x \u21a6 (m \u21a6 f (cons x m))`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.pi_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.coe_comp_multilinear_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.prod", "description": "The cartesian product of two multilinear maps, as a multilinear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.mk_pi_algebra_fin", "description": " Given an `R`-algebra `A`, `mk_pi_algebra_fin` is the multilinear map on `A^n` associating\nto `m` the product of all the `m i`.\n\nSee also `multilinear_map.mk_pi_algebra` for a version that assumes `[comm_semiring A]` but works\nfor `A^\u03b9` with any finite type `\u03b9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.module", "description": " The space of multilinear maps over an algebra over `R` is a module over `R`, for the pointwise\naddition and scalar multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.curry_fin_finset_symm_apply_piecewise_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.has_coe_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.comp_multilinear_map_dom_dom_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.uncurry_right", "description": " Given a multilinear map `f` in `n` variables to the space of linear maps from `M (last n)` to\n`M\u2082`, construct the corresponding multilinear map on `n+1` variables obtained by concatenating\nthe variables, given by `m \u21a6 f (init m) (m (last n))`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map_update_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.has_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.coe_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map_add_univ", "description": " Additivity of a multilinear map along all coordinates at the same time,\nwriting `f (m + m')` as the sum  of `f (s.piecewise m m')` over all sets `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.snoc_smul", "description": " In the specific case of multilinear maps on spaces indexed by `fin (n+1)`, where one can build\nan element of `\u03a0(i : fin (n+1)), M i` using `cons`, one can express directly the multiplicativity\nof a multilinear map along the first variable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.mk_pi_ring_eq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.dom_dom_congr_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.dom_dom_congr_linear_equiv'_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.comp_linear_equiv_eq_zero_iff", "description": " Composing a multilinear map with a linear equiv on each argument gives the zero map\nif and only if the multilinear map is the zero map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.dom_dom_congr", "description": " Transfer the arguments to a map along an equivalence between argument indices.\n\nThe naming is derived from `finsupp.dom_congr`, noting that here the permutation applies to the\ndomain of the domain.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.const_of_is_empty", "description": "The constant map is multilinear when `\u03b9` is empty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.cod_restrict_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.curry_uncurry_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.dom_dom_congr_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.comp_linear_map_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.dom_dom_congr_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.comp_linear_map_assoc", "description": " Composing a multilinear map twice with a linear map in each argument is\nthe same as composing with their composition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.to_linear_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.curry_sum_equiv", "description": " Linear equivalence between the space of multilinear maps on `\u03a0 i : \u03b9 \u2295 \u03b9', M'` and the space\nof multilinear maps on `\u03a0 i : \u03b9, M'` taking values in the space of multilinear maps\non `\u03a0 i : \u03b9', M'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.pi", "description": " Combine a family of multilinear maps with the same domain and codomains `M' i` into a\nmultilinear map taking values in the space of functions `\u03a0 i, M' i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.to_fun_eq_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.dom_dom_congr_linear_equiv'_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.dom_dom_congr_linear_equiv'", "description": "The dependent version of `multilinear_map.dom_dom_congr_linear_equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.dom_dom_congr_trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.coe_curry_sum_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.dom_dom_congr_linear_equiv", "description": "`multilinear_map.dom_dom_congr` as a `linear_equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.uncurry_right_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.mk_pi_ring_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.curry_fin_finset", "description": " If `s : finset (fin n)` is a finite set of cardinality `k` and its complement has cardinality\n`l`, then the space of multilinear maps on `\u03bb i : fin n, M'` is isomorphic to the space of\nmultilinear maps on `\u03bb i : fin k, M'` taking values in the space of multilinear maps\non `\u03bb i : fin l, M'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.curry_fin_finset_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.curry_sum_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.subtype_comp_multilinear_map_cod_restrict", "description": "The multilinear version of `linear_map.subtype_comp_cod_restrict`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map_smul_univ", "description": " Multiplicativity of a multilinear map along all coordinates at the same time,\nwriting `f (\u03bbi, c i \u2022 m i)` as `(\u220f i, c i) \u2022 f m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.curry_left_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.restr", "description": " Given a multilinear map `f` on `n` variables (parameterized by `fin n`) and a subset `s` of `k`\nof these variables, one gets a new multilinear map on `fin k` by varying these variables, and fixing\nthe other ones equal to a given value `z`. It is denoted by `f.restr s hk z`, where `hk` is a\nproof that the cardinality of `s` is `k`. The implicit identification between `fin k` and `s` that\nwe use is the canonical (increasing) bijection.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.uncurry_left_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.const_linear_equiv_of_is_empty_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.coe_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.cod_restrict", "description": " Restrict the codomain of a multilinear map to a submodule.\n\nThis is the multilinear version of `linear_map.cod_restrict`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.no_zero_smul_divisors", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.uncurry_curry_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.coe_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.prod_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.congr_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map_sum_finset", "description": " If `f` is multilinear, then `f (\u03a3_{j\u2081 \u2208 A\u2081} g\u2081 j\u2081, ..., \u03a3_{j\u2099 \u2208 A\u2099} g\u2099 j\u2099)` is the sum of\n`f (g\u2081 (r 1), ..., g\u2099 (r n))` where `r` ranges over all functions with `r 1 \u2208 A\u2081`, ...,\n`r n \u2208 A\u2099`. This follows from multilinearity by expanding successively with respect to each\ncoordinate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.zero_comp_linear_map", "description": "Composing the zero multilinear map with a linear map in each argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.cons_smul", "description": " In the specific case of multilinear maps on spaces indexed by `fin (n+1)`, where one can build\nan element of `\u03a0(i : fin (n+1)), M i` using `cons`, one can express directly the multiplicativity\nof a multilinear map along the first variable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.comp_multilinear_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.mk_pi_ring_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.smul_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.congr_arg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.curry_fin_finset_symm_apply_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.uncurry_sum", "description": " A multilinear map on `\u03a0 i : \u03b9, M'` taking values in the space of multilinear maps\non `\u03a0 i : \u03b9', M'` defines a multilinear map on `\u03a0 i : \u03b9 \u2295 \u03b9', M'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.neg_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.uncurry_sum_aux_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.curry_fin_finset_apply_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map_piecewise_smul", "description": " If one multiplies by `c i` the coordinates in a finset `s`, then the image under a multilinear\nmap is multiplied by `\u220f i in s, c i`. This is mainly an auxiliary statement to prove the result when\n`s = univ`, given in `map_smul_univ`, although it can be useful in its own right as it does not\nrequire the index set `\u03b9` to be finite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.has_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.mk_pi_algebra_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.const_of_is_empty_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.dom_dom_congr_equiv", "description": " `multilinear_map.dom_dom_congr` as an equivalence.\n\nThis is declared separately because it does not work with dot notation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.comp_multilinear_map_cod_restrict", "description": "The multilinear version of `linear_map.comp_cod_restrict`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.pi_ring_equiv", "description": " When `\u03b9` is finite, multilinear maps on `R^\u03b9` with values in `M\u2082` are in bijection with `M\u2082`,\nas such a multilinear map is completely determined by its value on the constant vector made of ones.\nWe register this bijection as a linear equivalence in `multilinear_map.pi_ring_equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.coe_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.coe_curr_sum_equiv_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.restrict_scalars", "description": " Reinterpret an `A`-multilinear map as an `R`-multilinear map, if `A` is an algebra over `R`\nand their actions on all involved modules agree with the action of `R` on `A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.mk_pi_ring_apply_one_eq_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map", "description": " The pushforward of an indexed collection of submodule `p i \u2286 M\u2081 i` by `f : M\u2081 \u2192 M\u2082`.\n\nNote that this is not a submodule - it is not closed under addition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map_sum_finset_aux", "description": " If `f` is multilinear, then `f (\u03a3_{j\u2081 \u2208 A\u2081} g\u2081 j\u2081, ..., \u03a3_{j\u2099 \u2208 A\u2099} g\u2099 j\u2099)` is the sum of\n`f (g\u2081 (r 1), ..., g\u2099 (r n))` where `r` ranges over all functions with `r 1 \u2208 A\u2081`, ...,\n`r n \u2208 A\u2099`. This follows from multilinearity by expanding successively with respect to each\ncoordinate. Here, we give an auxiliary statement tailored for an inductive proof. Use instead\n`map_sum_finset`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.has_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.map_piecewise_add", "description": " If one adds to a vector `m'` another vector `m`, but only for coordinates in a finset `t`, then\nthe image under a multilinear map `f` is the sum of `f (s.piecewise m m')` along all subsets `s` of\n`t`. This is mainly an auxiliary statement to prove the result when `t = univ`, given in\n`map_add_univ`, although it can be useful in its own right as it does not require the index set `\u03b9`\nto be finite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.dom_dom_congr_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.has_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.curry_uncurry_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.add_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multilinear_map.of_subsingleton", "description": "The evaluation map from `\u03b9 \u2192 M\u2082` to `M\u2082` is multilinear at a given `i` when `\u03b9` is subsingleton.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_empty'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_le'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_mul_eq_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_self_mul_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_compl_mul_self_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_le_mul_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.eq_on_mul_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.comp_mul_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_mul_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_const_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_zero'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_Union_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_union_of_not_mem_inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_zero_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_compl_mul_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.eq_on_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_indicator_eq_sum_filter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator", "description": "`mul_indicator s f a` is `f a` if `a \u2208 s`, `1` otherwise.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_finset_bUnion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_eq_one_iff_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_apply_le'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_hom", "description": "`set.mul_indicator` as a `monoid_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_union_of_disjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_diff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_inter_mul_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_of_not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_range_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_mul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.comp_indicator_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_comp_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_mul_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_comp_of_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.prod_mul_indicator_subset_of_eq_one", "description": " Consider a product of `g i (f i)` over a `finset`.  Suppose `g` is a\nfunction such as `pow`, which maps a second argument of `1` to\n`1`. Then if `f` is replaced by the corresponding multiplicative indicator\nfunction, the `finset` may be replaced by a possibly larger `finset`\nwithout changing the value of the sum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.prod_mul_indicator_subset", "description": " Taking the product of an indicator function over a possibly larger `finset` is the same as\ntaking the original function over the original `finset`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.le_mul_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_comp_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.inter_indicator_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_compl_add_self_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_compl'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_le_indicator_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_Union_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_range_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_le_self'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_mul_compl_eq_piecewise", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_range_mul_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_apply_le'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_apply_eq_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_self_mul_compl_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_le'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_union_of_disjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_nonpos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_preimage_of_not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_one_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_eq_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_apply_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_nonpos_le_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.sum_indicator_subset_of_eq_zero", "description": " Consider a sum of `g i (f i)` over a `finset`. Suppose `g` is a\nfunction such as multiplication, which maps a second argument of 0 to\n0.  (A typical use case would be a weighted sum of `f i * h i` or `f i\n\u2022 h i`, where `f` gives the weights that are multiplied by some other\nfunction `h`.)  Then if `f` is replaced by the corresponding indicator\nfunction, the `finset` may be replaced by a possibly larger `finset`\nwithout changing the value of the sum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_finset_bUnion_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_div'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_compl_add_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_eq_zero'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_eq_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.le_indicator_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_le_self'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.le_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_le_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_finset_bUnion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator", "description": "`indicator s f a` is `f a` if `a \u2208 s`, `0` otherwise.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.inter_indicator_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_add_compl_eq_piecewise", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_union_add_inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_one_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_of_indicator_ne_zero", "description": "If an additive indicator function is not equal to `0` at a point, then that point is\nin the set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.support_indicator_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_support_mul_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_mul_indicator_eq_prod_filter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_add_eq_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_self_add_compl_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.comp_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.le_mul_indicator_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_add_eq_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_apply_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_mul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_apply_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_finset_bUnion_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_union_of_not_mem_inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_range_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_of_mul_indicator_ne_one", "description": " If a multiplicative indicator function is not equal to `1` at a point, then that point is in the\nset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_diff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_inter_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_finset_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_empty'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_add'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_le_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.piecewise_eq_mul_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_eq_one'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_const_preimage_eq_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.one_le_mul_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_neg'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.sum_indicator_subset", "description": "Summing an indicator function over a possibly larger `finset` is the same as summing\nthe original function over the original `finset`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_one_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_mul'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_one'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_apply_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_hom", "description": "`set.indicator` as an `add_monoid_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_mul_eq_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.support_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_smul_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_const_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_const_smul_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_apply_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_const_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_support_mul_indicator_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_comp_of_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_hom.map_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_preimage_of_not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_self_add_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_union_mul_inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_apply_ne_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_le_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_finset_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_rel_mul_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_inv'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_diff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_sub'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_apply_le_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_apply_eq_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_le_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_eq_zero_iff_not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_const_preimage_eq_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_eq_zero_or_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_apply_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_union_mul_inter_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_of_not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_apply_nonpos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monoid_hom.map_mul_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_eq_self_of_superset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.piecewise_eq_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_rel_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.one_le_mul_indicator_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_eq_one_or_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_union_add_inter_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.comp_mul_indicator_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_eq_self_of_superset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_le_indicator_of_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_indicator_le_mul_indicator_of_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.indicator_prod_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.by_proof_irrel", "description": " Attempts to prove the goal by proof irrelevance, but avoids unifying universe metavariables\nto do so.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.congr'", "description": "Same as the `congr` tactic, but takes an optional argument which gives\nthe depth of recursive applications.\n* This is useful when `congr` is too aggressive in breaking down the goal.\n* For example, given `\u22a2 f (g (x + y)) = f (g (y + x))`, `congr'` produces the goals `\u22a2 x = y`\n  and `\u22a2 y = x`, while `congr' 2` produces the intended `\u22a2 x + y = y + x`.\n* If, at any point, a subgoal matches a hypothesis then the subgoal will be closed.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.convert", "description": "The `exact e` and `refine e` tactics require a term `e` whose type is\ndefinitionally equal to the goal. `convert e` is similar to `refine e`,\nbut the type of `e` is not required to exactly match the\ngoal. Instead, new goals are created for differences between the type\nof `e` and the goal. For example, in the proof state\n\n```lean\nn : \u2115,\ne : prime (2 * n + 1)\n\u22a2 prime (n + n + 1)\n```\n\nthe tactic `convert e` will change the goal to\n\n```lean\n\u22a2 n + n = 2 * n\n```\n\nIn this example, the new goal can be solved using `ring`.\n\nThe `convert` tactic applies congruence lemmas eagerly before reducing,\ntherefore it can fail in cases where `exact` succeeds:\n```lean\ndef p (n : \u2115) := true\nexample (h : p 0) : p 1 := by exact h -- succeeds\nexample (h : p 0) : p 1 := by convert h -- fails, with leftover goal `1 = 0`\n```\n\nIf `x y : t`, and an instance `subsingleton t` is in scope, then any goals of the form\n`x = y` are solved automatically.\n\nThe syntax `convert \u2190 e` will reverse the direction of the new goals\n(producing `\u22a2 2 * n = n + n` in this example).\n\nInternally, `convert e` works by creating a new goal asserting that\nthe goal equals the type of `e`, then simplifying it using\n`congr'`. The syntax `convert e using n` can be used to control the\ndepth of matching (like `congr' n`). In the example, `convert e using\n1` would produce a new goal `\u22a2 n + n + 1 = 2 * n + 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.rcongr", "description": "Repeatedly and apply `congr'` and `ext`, using the given patterns as arguments for `ext`.\n\nThere are two ways this tactic stops:\n* `congr'` fails (makes no progress), after having already applied `ext`.\n* `congr'` canceled out the last usage of `ext`. In this case, the state is reverted to before\n  the `congr'` was applied.\n\nFor example, when the goal is\n```lean\n\u22a2 (\u03bb x, f x + 3) '' s = (\u03bb x, g x + 3) '' s\n```\nthen `rcongr x` produces the goal\n```lean\nx : \u03b1 \u22a2 f x = g x\n```\nThis gives the same result as `congr', ext x, congr'`.\n\nIn contrast, `congr'` would produce\n```lean\n\u22a2 (\u03bb x, f x + 3) = (\u03bb x, g x + 3)\n```\nand `congr' with x` (or `congr', ext x`) would produce\n```lean\nx : \u03b1 \u22a2 f x + 3 = g x + 3\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.convert_to_core", "description": "The main function in `convert_to`. Changes the goal to `r` and a proof obligation that the goal\n is equal to `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.congr'", "description": "Same as the `congr` tactic, but takes an optional argument which gives\nthe depth of recursive applications.\n* This is useful when `congr` is too aggressive in breaking down the goal.\n* For example, given `\u22a2 f (g (x + y)) = f (g (y + x))`, `congr'` produces the goals `\u22a2 x = y`\n  and `\u22a2 y = x`, while `congr' 2` produces the intended `\u22a2 x + y = y + x`.\n* If, at any point, a subgoal matches a hypothesis then the subgoal will be closed.\n* You can use `congr' with p (: n)?` to call `ext p (: n)?` to all subgoals generated by `congr'`.\n  For example, if the goal is `\u22a2 f '' s = g '' s` then `congr' with x` generates the goal\n  `x : \u03b1 \u22a2 f x = g x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.congr_core'", "description": "The main part of the body for the loop in `congr'`. This will try to replace a goal `f x = f y`\nwith `x = y`. Also has support for `==` and `\u2194`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.convert_to", "description": "`convert_to g using n` attempts to change the current goal to `g`, but unlike `change`,\nit will generate equality proof obligations using `congr' n` to resolve discrepancies.\n`convert_to g` defaults to using `congr' 1`.\n\n`convert_to` is similar to `convert`, but `convert_to` takes a type (the desired subgoal) while\n`convert` takes a proof term.\nThat is, `convert_to g using n` is equivalent to `convert (_ : g) using n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.congr'", "description": "Same as the `congr` tactic, but takes an optional argument which gives\nthe depth of recursive applications.\n* This is useful when `congr` is too aggressive in breaking down the goal.\n* For example, given `\u22a2 f (g (x + y)) = f (g (y + x))`, `congr'` produces the goals `\u22a2 x = y`\n  and `\u22a2 y = x`, while `congr' 2` produces the intended `\u22a2 x + y = y + x`.\n* If, at any point, a subgoal matches a hypothesis then the subgoal will be closed.\n* You can use `congr' with p (: n)?` to call `ext p (: n)?` to all subgoals generated by `congr'`.\n  For example, if the goal is `\u22a2 f '' s = g '' s` then `congr' with x` generates the goal\n  `x : \u03b1 \u22a2 f x = g x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.ac_change", "description": "`ac_change g using n` is `convert_to g using n` followed by `ac_refl`. It is useful for\nrearranging/reassociating e.g. sums:\n```lean\nexample (a b c d e f g N : \u2115) : (a + b) + (c + d) + (e + f) + g \u2264 N :=\nbegin\n  ac_change a + d + e + f + c + g + b \u2264 _,\n-- \u22a2 a + d + e + f + c + g + b \u2264 N\nend\n```\n\n##  Related tactic: `move_add`\nIn the case in which the expression to be changed is a sum of terms, tactic\n`tactive.interactive.move_add` can also be useful.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.convert_to", "description": "`convert_to g using n` attempts to change the current goal to `g`, but unlike `change`,\nit will generate equality proof obligations using `congr' n` to resolve discrepancies.\n`convert_to g` defaults to using `congr' 1`.\n\n`convert_to` is similar to `convert`, but `convert_to` takes a type (the desired subgoal) while\n`convert` takes a proof term.\nThat is, `convert_to g using n` is equivalent to `convert (_ : g) using n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.convert", "description": "The `exact e` and `refine e` tactics require a term `e` whose type is\ndefinitionally equal to the goal. `convert e` is similar to `refine e`,\nbut the type of `e` is not required to exactly match the\ngoal. Instead, new goals are created for differences between the type\nof `e` and the goal. For example, in the proof state\n\n```lean\nn : \u2115,\ne : prime (2 * n + 1)\n\u22a2 prime (n + n + 1)\n```\n\nthe tactic `convert e` will change the goal to\n\n```lean\n\u22a2 n + n = 2 * n\n```\n\nIn this example, the new goal can be solved using `ring`.\n\nThe `convert` tactic applies congruence lemmas eagerly before reducing,\ntherefore it can fail in cases where `exact` succeeds:\n```lean\ndef p (n : \u2115) := true\nexample (h : p 0) : p 1 := by exact h -- succeeds\nexample (h : p 0) : p 1 := by convert h -- fails, with leftover goal `1 = 0`\n```\n\nIf `x y : t`, and an instance `subsingleton t` is in scope, then any goals of the form\n`x = y` are solved automatically.\n\nThe syntax `convert \u2190 e` will reverse the direction of the new goals\n(producing `\u22a2 2 * n = n + n` in this example).\n\nInternally, `convert e` works by creating a new goal asserting that\nthe goal equals the type of `e`, then simplifying it using\n`congr'`. The syntax `convert e using n` can be used to control the\ndepth of matching (like `congr' n`). In the example, `convert e using\n1` would produce a new goal `\u22a2 n + n + 1 = 2 * n + 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.apply_iff_congr_core", "description": "Apply the constant `iff_of_eq` to the goal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.Ioc_coe_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.Icc_coe_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order_bot.of_Iic'", "description": " A constructor from a definition of `finset.Iic` alone, the other ones being derived by removing\nthe ends. As opposed to `locally_finite_order.of_Icc`, this one requires `decidable_rel (\u2264)` but\nonly `preorder`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.Ioo", "description": " The multiset of elements `x` such that `a < x` and `x < b`. Basically `set.Ioo a b` as a\nmultiset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ici_to_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.Icc_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order.of_finite_Icc", "description": "A noncomputable constructor from the finiteness of all closed intervals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ioi_to_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.coe_Ici", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.to_locally_finite_order", "description": " A fintype is a locally finite order.\n\nThis is not an instance as it would not be defeq to better instances such as\n`fin.locally_finite_order`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.Ioo_coe_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order_top.of_Ici", "description": " A constructor from a definition of `finset.Iic` alone, the other ones being derived by removing\nthe ends. As opposed to `locally_finite_order_top.of_Ici'`, this one requires `partial_order` but\nonly `decidable_eq`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.uIcc_mk_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.fintype_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.fintype_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.Ico", "description": " The multiset of elements `x` such that `a \u2264 x` and `x < b`. Basically `set.Ico a b` as a\nmultiset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.Icc", "description": "The finset of elements `x` such that `a \u2264 x` and `x \u2264 b`. Basically `set.Icc a b` as a finset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Iic_to_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.map_subtype_embedding_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.Ioc", "description": "The finset of elements `x` such that `a < x` and `x \u2264 b`. Basically `set.Ioc a b` as a finset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.coe_uIcc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Icc_of_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_dual.locally_finite_order_bot", "description": " Note we define `Iic (to_dual a)` as `Ici a` (which has type `finset \u03b1` not `finset \u03b1\u1d52\u1d48`!)\ninstead of `(Ici a).map to_dual.to_embedding` as this means the following is defeq:\n```lean\nlemma this : (Iic (to_dual (to_dual a)) : _) = (Iic a : _) := rfl\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.Ioc_bot_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.uIcc_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.subtype_Iic_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.locally_finite_order.to_locally_finite_order_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.mem_Ici", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.coe_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.subtype_Ioc_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.fintype_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ico_of_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.map_subtype_embedding_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_Iio", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.fintype_Iic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.coe_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.map_subtype_embedding_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype.locally_finite_order_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.Ico_coe_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.subtype_Ici_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.card_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.Icc_coe_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ioo_to_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order.subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.locally_finite_order_bot", "description": "Transfer `locally_finite_order_bot` across an `order_iso`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.Icc", "description": " The multiset of elements `x` such that `a \u2264 x` and `x \u2264 b`. Basically `set.Icc a b` as a\nmultiset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.fintype_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype.locally_finite_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Iic_of_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.subtype_Ico_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.Iic", "description": "The finset of elements `x` such that `a \u2264 x`. Basically `set.Iic a` as a finset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.Ico_bot_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.Ioi", "description": "The multiset of elements `x` such that `a < x`. Basically `set.Ioi a` as a multiset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype.locally_finite_order_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.locally_finite_order_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.map_subtype_embedding_Ici", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.Ioc_coe_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.mem_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.map_subtype_embedding_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.fintype_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.locally_finite_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Icc_to_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.locally_finite_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_embedding.locally_finite_order", "description": "Given an order embedding `\u03b1 \u21aao \u03b2`, pulls back the `locally_finite_order` on `\u03b2` to `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order_bot", "description": " A locally finite order bot is an order where all intervals bounded below are finite. This is\nslightly weaker than `locally_finite_order` + `order_bot` as it allows empty types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order_bot.finset_Iio", "description": " A locally finite order bot is an order where all intervals bounded below are finite. This is\nslightly weaker than `locally_finite_order` + `order_bot` as it allows empty types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order_bot.finset_Iic", "description": " A locally finite order bot is an order where all intervals bounded below are finite. This is\nslightly weaker than `locally_finite_order` + `order_bot` as it allows empty types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order_bot.finset_mem_Iic", "description": " A locally finite order bot is an order where all intervals bounded below are finite. This is\nslightly weaker than `locally_finite_order` + `order_bot` as it allows empty types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order_bot.finset_mem_Iio", "description": " A locally finite order bot is an order where all intervals bounded below are finite. This is\nslightly weaker than `locally_finite_order` + `order_bot` as it allows empty types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order.of_Icc", "description": " A constructor from a definition of `finset.Icc` alone, the other ones being derived by removing\nthe ends. As opposed to `locally_finite_order.of_Icc`, this one requires `partial_order` but only\n`decidable_eq`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.locally_finite_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.Icc_mk_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.coe_Iio", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.Iio", "description": "The finset of elements `x` such that `a < x`. Basically `set.Iio a` as a finset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.Ico_coe_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Iio_to_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.fintype_uIcc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.mem_Iic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.subtype_Ioi_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.Ioi", "description": "The finset of elements `x` such that `a < x`. Basically `set.Ioi a` as a finset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order.of_Icc'", "description": " A constructor from a definition of `finset.Icc` alone, the other ones being derived by removing\nthe ends. As opposed to `locally_finite_order.of_Icc`, this one requires `decidable_rel (\u2264)` but\nonly `preorder`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.fintype_Ici", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ioo_of_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.mem_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.Ioc", "description": " The multiset of elements `x` such that `a < x` and `x \u2264 b`. Basically `set.Ioc a b` as a\nmultiset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order", "description": " A locally finite order is an order where bounded intervals are finite. When you don't care too\nmuch about definitional equality, you can use `locally_finite_order.of_Icc` or\n`locally_finite_order.of_finite_Icc` to build a locally finite order from just `finset.Icc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order.finset_Icc", "description": " A locally finite order is an order where bounded intervals are finite. When you don't care too\nmuch about definitional equality, you can use `locally_finite_order.of_Icc` or\n`locally_finite_order.of_finite_Icc` to build a locally finite order from just `finset.Icc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order.finset_Ico", "description": " A locally finite order is an order where bounded intervals are finite. When you don't care too\nmuch about definitional equality, you can use `locally_finite_order.of_Icc` or\n`locally_finite_order.of_finite_Icc` to build a locally finite order from just `finset.Icc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order.finset_Ioc", "description": " A locally finite order is an order where bounded intervals are finite. When you don't care too\nmuch about definitional equality, you can use `locally_finite_order.of_Icc` or\n`locally_finite_order.of_finite_Icc` to build a locally finite order from just `finset.Icc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order.finset_Ioo", "description": " A locally finite order is an order where bounded intervals are finite. When you don't care too\nmuch about definitional equality, you can use `locally_finite_order.of_Icc` or\n`locally_finite_order.of_finite_Icc` to build a locally finite order from just `finset.Icc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order.finset_mem_Icc", "description": " A locally finite order is an order where bounded intervals are finite. When you don't care too\nmuch about definitional equality, you can use `locally_finite_order.of_Icc` or\n`locally_finite_order.of_finite_Icc` to build a locally finite order from just `finset.Icc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order.finset_mem_Ico", "description": " A locally finite order is an order where bounded intervals are finite. When you don't care too\nmuch about definitional equality, you can use `locally_finite_order.of_Icc` or\n`locally_finite_order.of_finite_Icc` to build a locally finite order from just `finset.Icc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order.finset_mem_Ioc", "description": " A locally finite order is an order where bounded intervals are finite. When you don't care too\nmuch about definitional equality, you can use `locally_finite_order.of_Icc` or\n`locally_finite_order.of_finite_Icc` to build a locally finite order from just `finset.Icc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order.finset_mem_Ioo", "description": " A locally finite order is an order where bounded intervals are finite. When you don't care too\nmuch about definitional equality, you can use `locally_finite_order.of_Icc` or\n`locally_finite_order.of_finite_Icc` to build a locally finite order from just `finset.Icc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.coe_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_uIcc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.fintype_Iio", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.map_subtype_embedding_Iio", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.card_uIcc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.Iic", "description": "The multiset of elements `x` such that `x \u2264 b`. Basically `set.Iic b` as a multiset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite_Ici", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.Icc_coe_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.map_subtype_embedding_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.locally_finite_order", "description": "Transfer `locally_finite_order` across an `order_iso`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ioc_of_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.Icc_bot_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.subtype_Ioo_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.subtype_Iio_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.Ioo_bot_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_Ici", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_dual.locally_finite_order", "description": " Note we define `Icc (to_dual a) (to_dual b)` as `Icc \u03b1 _ _ b a` (which has type `finset \u03b1` not\n`finset \u03b1\u1d52\u1d48`!) instead of `(Icc b a).map to_dual.to_embedding` as this means the\nfollowing is defeq:\n```lean\nlemma this : (Icc (to_dual (to_dual a)) (to_dual (to_dual b)) : _) = (Icc a b : _) := rfl\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.coe_Iic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.Ioo_coe_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ioc_to_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order_top", "description": " A locally finite order top is an order where all intervals bounded above are finite. This is\nslightly weaker than `locally_finite_order` + `order_top` as it allows empty types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order_top.finset_Ioi", "description": " A locally finite order top is an order where all intervals bounded above are finite. This is\nslightly weaker than `locally_finite_order` + `order_top` as it allows empty types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order_top.finset_Ici", "description": " A locally finite order top is an order where all intervals bounded above are finite. This is\nslightly weaker than `locally_finite_order` + `order_top` as it allows empty types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order_top.finset_mem_Ici", "description": " A locally finite order top is an order where all intervals bounded above are finite. This is\nslightly weaker than `locally_finite_order` + `order_top` as it allows empty types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order_top.finset_mem_Ioi", "description": " A locally finite order top is an order where all intervals bounded above are finite. This is\nslightly weaker than `locally_finite_order` + `order_top` as it allows empty types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ici_of_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Iio_of_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ico_to_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.Ioo", "description": "The finset of elements `x` such that `a < x` and `x < b`. Basically `set.Ioo a b` as a finset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.Iio_eq_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty.to_locally_finite_order", "description": " An empty type is locally finite.\n\nThis is not an instance as it would not be defeq to more specific instances.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_Iic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.coe_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order_bot.subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.Ioc_coe_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ioi_of_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.Ioi_eq_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.Ici", "description": "The finset of elements `x` such that `a \u2264 x`. Basically `set.Ici a` as a finset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.map_subtype_embedding_Iic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.mem_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.Ici", "description": "The multiset of elements `x` such that `a \u2264 x`. Basically `set.Ici a` as a multiset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.locally_finite_order_top", "description": "Transfer `locally_finite_order_top` across an `order_iso`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.Ici_eq_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite_Iic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty.to_locally_finite_order_top", "description": " An empty type is locally finite.\n\nThis is not an instance as it would not be defeq to more specific instances.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite_Iio", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.locally_finite_order_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite_interval", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.mem_Iio", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order_top.subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order.to_locally_finite_order_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.subtype_Icc_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order_top.of_Iic", "description": " A constructor from a definition of `finset.Iic` alone, the other ones being derived by removing\nthe ends. As opposed to `locally_finite_order_top.of_Ici'`, this one requires `partial_order` but\nonly `decidable_eq`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.Ico_coe_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.Ioo_coe_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.mem_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.coe_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.Iio", "description": "The multiset of elements `x` such that `x < b`. Basically `set.Iio b` as a multiset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty.to_locally_finite_order_bot", "description": " An empty type is locally finite.\n\nThis is not an instance as it would not be defeq to more specific instances.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_order_top.of_Ici'", "description": " A constructor from a definition of `finset.Iic` alone, the other ones being derived by removing\nthe ends. As opposed to `locally_finite_order_top.of_Ici`, this one requires `decidable_rel (\u2264)` but\nonly `preorder`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.uIcc", "description": " `finset.uIcc a b` is the set of elements lying between `a` and `b`, with `a` and `b` included.\nNote that we define it more generally in a lattice as `finset.Icc (a \u2293 b) (a \u2294 b)`. In a\nproduct type, `finset.uIcc` corresponds to the bounding box of the two elements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.mem_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.Iic_eq_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_dual.locally_finite_order_top", "description": " Note we define `Ici (to_dual a)` as `Iic a` (which has type `finset \u03b1` not `finset \u03b1\u1d52\u1d48`!)\ninstead of `(Iic a).map to_dual.to_embedding` as this means the following is defeq:\n```lean\nlemma this : (Ici (to_dual (to_dual a)) : _) = (Ici a : _) := rfl\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.Ico", "description": "The finset of elements `x` such that `a \u2264 x` and `x < b`. Basically `set.Ico a b` as a finset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.tsub_eq_of_eq_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.add_tsub_cancel_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_tsub_add_eq_tsub_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone_const_tsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_tsub_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_add_tsub'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_tsub_le_left", "description": "See `add_tsub_cancel_left` for the equality if `contravariant_class \u03b1 \u03b1 (+) (\u2264)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_le_tsub_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_tsub_cancel_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_add_of_tsub_lt_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.lt_tsub_of_add_lt_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_tsub_of_add_le_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_tsub_of_add_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_of_tsub_lt_tsub_left", "description": "See `lt_of_tsub_lt_tsub_left_of_le` for a weaker statement in a partial order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_eq_of_eq_add_rev", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_add_add_tsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_tsub_of_add_lt_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_tsub_add_le_tsub_right", "description": "See `add_tsub_add_eq_tsub_right` for the equality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_add_of_tsub_lt_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_eq_of_eq_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_tsub_add_eq_tsub_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_le_iff_tsub_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_tsub_of_add_lt_left", "description": " This lemma (and some of its corollaries) also holds for `ennreal`, but this proof doesn't work\nfor it. Maybe we should add this lemma as field to `has_ordered_sub`?", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_tsub_tsub_le_tsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_tsub_le", "description": "See `tsub_tsub_cancel_of_le` for the equality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_le_iff_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.eq_tsub_of_add_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_tsub_add_le_tsub_add_tsub", "description": "See `tsub_add_tsub_comm` for the equality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_tsub_iff_left", "description": "See `lt_tsub_iff_left_of_le` for a weaker statement in a partial order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_tsub_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_ordered_sub", "description": " `has_ordered_sub \u03b1` means that `\u03b1` has a subtraction characterized by `a - b \u2264 c \u2194 a \u2264 c + b`.\nIn other words, `a - b` is the least `c` such that `a \u2264 b + c`.\n\nThis is satisfied both by the subtraction in additive ordered groups and by truncated subtraction\nin canonically ordered monoids on many specific types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_ordered_sub.tsub_le_iff_right", "description": " `has_ordered_sub \u03b1` means that `\u03b1` has a subtraction characterized by `a - b \u2264 c \u2194 a \u2264 c + b`.\nIn other words, `a - b` is the least `c` such that `a \u2264 b + c`.\n\nThis is satisfied both by the subtraction in additive ordered groups and by truncated subtraction\nin canonically ordered monoids on many specific types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.le_tsub_of_add_le_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_add_eq_tsub_tsub_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_of_tsub_lt_tsub_right", "description": "See `lt_of_tsub_lt_tsub_right_of_le` for a weaker statement in a partial order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_tsub_le_tsub_add", "description": "See `tsub_add_eq_add_tsub` for the equality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_nonpos_of_le", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `tsub_nonpos`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_le_tsub_add_tsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.lt_add_of_tsub_lt_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.le_tsub_of_add_le_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_tsub_add_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_le_iff_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.le_add_tsub_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_tsub_of_add_le_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_tsub_le_right", "description": "See `add_tsub_cancel_right` for the equality if `contravariant_class \u03b1 \u03b1 (+) (\u2264)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_nonpos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.lt_tsub_of_add_lt_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_right_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_tsub_cancel_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_tsub_le_assoc", "description": "See `add_tsub_assoc_of_le` for the equality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_le_tsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_add_eq_tsub_tsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_tsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.tsub_eq_of_eq_add_rev", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_le_tsub_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_tsub_add_le_tsub_left", "description": "See `add_tsub_add_eq_tsub_left` for the equality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_add_tsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_tsub_le_tsub_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.le_add_tsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.add_tsub_cancel_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_tsub_iff_right", "description": "See `lt_tsub_iff_right_of_le` for a weaker statement in a partial order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_add_tsub_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_le_cancellable.lt_add_of_tsub_lt_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.to_box_additive_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.to_box_additive", "description": " If `\u03bc` is a locally finite measure on `\u211d\u207f`, then `\u03bb J, (\u03bc J).to_real` is a box-additive\nfunction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.coe_ae_eq_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.measurable_set_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.measure_coe_lt_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box_additive_map.volume_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.volume_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.measurable_set_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.prepartition.measure_Union_to_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.measurable_set_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box_additive_map.volume", "description": " Box-additive map sending each box `I` to the continuous linear endomorphism\n`x \u21a6 (volume I).to_real \u2022 x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.volume_face_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.Ioo_ae_eq_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.measure_Icc_lt_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases.process_constructors", "description": " Takes a list of constructor names, and an (alternation) list of patterns, and matches each\npattern against its constructor. It returns the list of names that will be passed to `cases`,\nand the list of `(constructor name, patterns)` for each constructor, where `patterns` is the\n(conjunctive) list of patterns to apply to each constructor argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.rsufficesI", "description": "The `rsufficesI` tactic is an instance-cache aware version of `rsuffices`; it resets the instance\ncache on the resulting goals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rintro_hint", "description": "* `rintro?` is like `rintro ...`, except it generates `...` by introducing and matching on\neverything it can, and it outputs an `rintro` invocation that should have the same effect.\n* `rintro? : n` can be used to control the depth of case splits (especially important for\nrecursive types like `nat`, which can be cased as many times as you like).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.tuple\u2081_core", "description": " This function is used for producing rcases patterns based on a case tree. Suppose that we have\na list of patterns `ps` that will match correctly against the branches of the case tree for one\nconstructor. This function will merge tuples at the end of the list, so that `[a, b, \u27e8c, d\u27e9]`\nbecomes `\u27e8a, b, c, d\u27e9` instead of `\u27e8a, b, \u27e8c, d\u27e9\u27e9`.\n\nWe must be careful to turn `[a, \u27e8\u27e9]` into `\u27e8a, \u27e8\u27e9\u27e9` instead of `\u27e8a\u27e9` (which will not perform the\nnested match).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_hint.process_constructors", "description": "* `rcases_hint_core depth e` does the same as `rcases p e`, except the pattern `p` is an output\n  instead of an input, controlled only by the case depth argument `depth`. We use `cases` to depth\n  `depth` and then reconstruct an `rcases` pattern `p` that would, if passed to `rcases`, perform\n  the same thing as the case tree we just constructed (or at least, the nearest expressible\n  approximation to this.)\n* `rcases_hint.process_constructors depth cs l` takes a list of constructor names `cs` and a\n  matching list `l` of elements `(g, c', hs, _)` where  `c'` is a constructor name (used for\n  alignment with `cs`), `g` is the subgoal, and `hs` is the list of local hypotheses created by\n  `cases` in that subgoal. It matches on all of them, and then produces a `\u03a3\u03a0`-list of `rcases`\n  patterns describing the result, and the list of generated subgoals.\n* `rcases_hint.continue depth es` does the same as `rcases.continue (ps.zip es)`, except the\n  patterns `ps` are an output instead of an input, created by matching on everything to depth\n  `depth` and recording the successful cases. It returns `ps`, and the list of generated subgoals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases.process_constructor", "description": " Takes the number of fields of a single constructor and patterns to match its fields against\n(not necessarily the same number). The returned lists each contain one element per field of the\nconstructor. The `name` is the name which will be used in the top-level `cases` tactic, and the\n`rcases_patt` is the pattern which the field will be matched against by subsequent `cases`\ntactics.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt_parse_hi", "description": " `rcases_patt_parse_hi` will parse a high precedence `rcases` pattern, `patt_hi`.\nThis means only tuples and identifiers are allowed; alternations and type ascriptions\nrequire `(...)` instead, which switches to `patt`.\n```lean\npatt_hi ::= id | \"rfl\" | \"_\" | \"@\" patt_hi | \"\u27e8\" (patt \",\")* patt \"\u27e9\" | \"(\" patt \")\"\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_args", "description": " The arguments to `rcases`, which in fact dispatch to several other tactics.\n* `rcases? expr (: n)?` or `rcases? \u27e8expr, ...\u27e9 (: n)?` calls `rcases_hint`\n* `rcases? \u27e8expr, ...\u27e9 (: n)?` calls `rcases_hint_many`\n* `rcases (h :)? expr (with patt)?` calls `rcases`\n* `rcases \u27e8expr, ...\u27e9 (with patt)?` calls `rcases_many`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_args.hint", "description": " The arguments to `rcases`, which in fact dispatch to several other tactics.\n* `rcases? expr (: n)?` or `rcases? \u27e8expr, ...\u27e9 (: n)?` calls `rcases_hint`\n* `rcases? \u27e8expr, ...\u27e9 (: n)?` calls `rcases_hint_many`\n* `rcases (h :)? expr (with patt)?` calls `rcases`\n* `rcases \u27e8expr, ...\u27e9 (with patt)?` calls `rcases_many`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_args.rcases", "description": " The arguments to `rcases`, which in fact dispatch to several other tactics.\n* `rcases? expr (: n)?` or `rcases? \u27e8expr, ...\u27e9 (: n)?` calls `rcases_hint`\n* `rcases? \u27e8expr, ...\u27e9 (: n)?` calls `rcases_hint_many`\n* `rcases (h :)? expr (with patt)?` calls `rcases`\n* `rcases \u27e8expr, ...\u27e9 (with patt)?` calls `rcases_many`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_args.rcases_many", "description": " The arguments to `rcases`, which in fact dispatch to several other tactics.\n* `rcases? expr (: n)?` or `rcases? \u27e8expr, ...\u27e9 (: n)?` calls `rcases_hint`\n* `rcases? \u27e8expr, ...\u27e9 (: n)?` calls `rcases_hint_many`\n* `rcases (h :)? expr (with patt)?` calls `rcases`\n* `rcases \u27e8expr, ...\u27e9 (with patt)?` calls `rcases_many`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_hint", "description": "* `rcases? e` is like `rcases e with ...`, except it generates `...` by matching on everything it\ncan, and it outputs an `rcases` invocation that should have the same effect.\n* `rcases? e : n` can be used to control the depth of case splits (especially important for\nrecursive types like `nat`, which can be cased as many times as you like).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rintro_patt_parse_low", "description": "`rintro_patt_parse_low` parses `rintro_patt_low`, which is the same as `rintro_patt_parse tt` but\nit does not permit an unparenthesized alternation list, it must have the form `p1 p2 p3 (: ty)?`.\n```lean\nrintro_patt_low ::= rintro_patt_hi* (\":\" expr)?\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.obtain", "description": "The `obtain` tactic is a combination of `have` and `rcases`. See `rcases` for\na description of supported patterns.\n\n```lean\nobtain \u27e8patt\u27e9 : type,\n{ ... }\n```\nis equivalent to\n```lean\nhave h : type,\n{ ... },\nrcases h with \u27e8patt\u27e9\n```\n\nThe syntax `obtain \u27e8patt\u27e9 : type := proof` is also supported.\n\nIf `\u27e8patt\u27e9` is omitted, `rcases` will try to infer the pattern.\n\nIf `type` is omitted, `:= proof` is required.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.format", "description": " Formats an `rcases` pattern. If the `bracket` argument is true, then it will be\nprinted at high precedence, i.e. it will have parentheses around it if it is not already a tuple\nor atomic name.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.rcases", "description": "`rcases` is a tactic that will perform `cases` recursively, according to a pattern. It is used to\ndestructure hypotheses or expressions composed of inductive types like `h1 : a \u2227 b \u2227 c \u2228 d` or\n`h2 : \u2203 x y, trans_rel R x y`. Usual usage might be `rcases h1 with \u27e8ha, hb, hc\u27e9 | hd` or\n`rcases h2 with \u27e8x, y, _ | \u27e8z, hxz, hzy\u27e9\u27e9` for these examples.\n\nEach element of an `rcases` pattern is matched against a particular local hypothesis (most of which\nare generated during the execution of `rcases` and represent individual elements destructured from\nthe input expression). An `rcases` pattern has the following grammar:\n\n* A name like `x`, which names the active hypothesis as `x`.\n* A blank `_`, which does nothing (letting the automatic naming system used by `cases` name the\n  hypothesis).\n* A hyphen `-`, which clears the active hypothesis and any dependents.\n* The keyword `rfl`, which expects the hypothesis to be `h : a = b`, and calls `subst` on the\n  hypothesis (which has the effect of replacing `b` with `a` everywhere or vice versa).\n* A type ascription `p : ty`, which sets the type of the hypothesis to `ty` and then matches it\n  against `p`. (Of course, `ty` must unify with the actual type of `h` for this to work.)\n* A tuple pattern `\u27e8p1, p2, p3\u27e9`, which matches a constructor with many arguments, or a series\n  of nested conjunctions or existentials. For example if the active hypothesis is `a \u2227 b \u2227 c`,\n  then the conjunction will be destructured, and `p1` will be matched against `a`, `p2` against `b`\n  and so on.\n* A `@` before a tuple pattern as in `@\u27e8p1, p2, p3\u27e9` will bind all arguments in the constructor,\n  while leaving the `@` off will only use the patterns on the explicit arguments.\n* An alteration pattern `p1 | p2 | p3`, which matches an inductive type with multiple constructors,\n  or a nested disjunction like `a \u2228 b \u2228 c`.\n\nA pattern like `\u27e8a, b, c\u27e9 | \u27e8d, e\u27e9` will do a split over the inductive datatype,\nnaming the first three parameters of the first constructor as `a,b,c` and the\nfirst two of the second constructor `d,e`. If the list is not as long as the\nnumber of arguments to the constructor or the number of constructors, the\nremaining variables will be automatically named. If there are nested brackets\nsuch as `\u27e8\u27e8a\u27e9, b | c\u27e9 | d` then these will cause more case splits as necessary.\nIf there are too many arguments, such as `\u27e8a, b, c\u27e9` for splitting on\n`\u2203 x, \u2203 y, p x`, then it will be treated as `\u27e8a, \u27e8b, c\u27e9\u27e9`, splitting the last\nparameter as necessary.\n\n`rcases` also has special support for quotient types: quotient induction into Prop works like\nmatching on the constructor `quot.mk`.\n\n`rcases h : e with PAT` will do the same as `rcases e with PAT` with the exception that an\nassumption `h : e = PAT` will be added to the context.\n\n`rcases? e` will perform case splits on `e` in the same way as `rcases e`,\nbut rather than accepting a pattern, it does a maximal cases and prints the\npattern that would produce this case splitting. The default maximum depth is 5,\nbut this can be modified with `rcases? e : n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.uncleared_goal", "description": "A metavariable representing a subgoal, together with a list of local constants to clear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.merge", "description": " Merge two `rcases` patterns. This is used to underapproximate a case tree by an `rcases`\npattern. The two patterns come from cases in two branches, that due to the syntax of `rcases`\npatterns are forced to overlap. The rule here is that we take only the case splits that are in\ncommon between both branches. For example if one branch does `\u27e8a, b\u27e9` and the other does `c`,\nthen we return `c` because we don't know that a case on `c` would be safe to do.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.name", "description": "Get the name from a pattern, if provided", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt", "description": "An `rcases` pattern can be one of the following, in a nested combination:\n\n* A name like `foo`\n* The special keyword `rfl` (for pattern matching on equality using `subst`)\n* A hyphen `-`, which clears the active hypothesis and any dependents.\n* A type ascription like `pat : ty` (parentheses are optional)\n* A tuple constructor like `\u27e8p1, p2, p3\u27e9`\n* An alternation / variant pattern `p1 | p2 | p3`\n\nParentheses can be used for grouping; alternation is higher precedence than type ascription, so\n`p1 | p2 | p3 : ty` means `(p1 | p2 | p3) : ty`.\n\nN-ary alternations are treated as a group, so `p1 | p2 | p3` is not the same as `p1 | (p2 | p3)`,\nand similarly for tuples. However, note that an n-ary alternation or tuple can match an n-ary\nconjunction or disjunction, because if the number of patterns exceeds the number of constructors in\nthe type being destructed, the extra patterns will match on the last element, meaning that\n`p1 | p2 | p3` will act like `p1 | (p2 | p3)` when matching `a1 \u2228 a2 \u2228 a3`. If matching against a\ntype with 3 constructors,  `p1 | (p2 | p3)` will act like `p1 | (p2 | p3) | _` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.one", "description": "An `rcases` pattern can be one of the following, in a nested combination:\n\n* A name like `foo`\n* The special keyword `rfl` (for pattern matching on equality using `subst`)\n* A hyphen `-`, which clears the active hypothesis and any dependents.\n* A type ascription like `pat : ty` (parentheses are optional)\n* A tuple constructor like `\u27e8p1, p2, p3\u27e9`\n* An alternation / variant pattern `p1 | p2 | p3`\n\nParentheses can be used for grouping; alternation is higher precedence than type ascription, so\n`p1 | p2 | p3 : ty` means `(p1 | p2 | p3) : ty`.\n\nN-ary alternations are treated as a group, so `p1 | p2 | p3` is not the same as `p1 | (p2 | p3)`,\nand similarly for tuples. However, note that an n-ary alternation or tuple can match an n-ary\nconjunction or disjunction, because if the number of patterns exceeds the number of constructors in\nthe type being destructed, the extra patterns will match on the last element, meaning that\n`p1 | p2 | p3` will act like `p1 | (p2 | p3)` when matching `a1 \u2228 a2 \u2228 a3`. If matching against a\ntype with 3 constructors,  `p1 | (p2 | p3)` will act like `p1 | (p2 | p3) | _` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.clear", "description": "An `rcases` pattern can be one of the following, in a nested combination:\n\n* A name like `foo`\n* The special keyword `rfl` (for pattern matching on equality using `subst`)\n* A hyphen `-`, which clears the active hypothesis and any dependents.\n* A type ascription like `pat : ty` (parentheses are optional)\n* A tuple constructor like `\u27e8p1, p2, p3\u27e9`\n* An alternation / variant pattern `p1 | p2 | p3`\n\nParentheses can be used for grouping; alternation is higher precedence than type ascription, so\n`p1 | p2 | p3 : ty` means `(p1 | p2 | p3) : ty`.\n\nN-ary alternations are treated as a group, so `p1 | p2 | p3` is not the same as `p1 | (p2 | p3)`,\nand similarly for tuples. However, note that an n-ary alternation or tuple can match an n-ary\nconjunction or disjunction, because if the number of patterns exceeds the number of constructors in\nthe type being destructed, the extra patterns will match on the last element, meaning that\n`p1 | p2 | p3` will act like `p1 | (p2 | p3)` when matching `a1 \u2228 a2 \u2228 a3`. If matching against a\ntype with 3 constructors,  `p1 | (p2 | p3)` will act like `p1 | (p2 | p3) | _` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.explicit", "description": "An `rcases` pattern can be one of the following, in a nested combination:\n\n* A name like `foo`\n* The special keyword `rfl` (for pattern matching on equality using `subst`)\n* A hyphen `-`, which clears the active hypothesis and any dependents.\n* A type ascription like `pat : ty` (parentheses are optional)\n* A tuple constructor like `\u27e8p1, p2, p3\u27e9`\n* An alternation / variant pattern `p1 | p2 | p3`\n\nParentheses can be used for grouping; alternation is higher precedence than type ascription, so\n`p1 | p2 | p3 : ty` means `(p1 | p2 | p3) : ty`.\n\nN-ary alternations are treated as a group, so `p1 | p2 | p3` is not the same as `p1 | (p2 | p3)`,\nand similarly for tuples. However, note that an n-ary alternation or tuple can match an n-ary\nconjunction or disjunction, because if the number of patterns exceeds the number of constructors in\nthe type being destructed, the extra patterns will match on the last element, meaning that\n`p1 | p2 | p3` will act like `p1 | (p2 | p3)` when matching `a1 \u2228 a2 \u2228 a3`. If matching against a\ntype with 3 constructors,  `p1 | (p2 | p3)` will act like `p1 | (p2 | p3) | _` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.typed", "description": "An `rcases` pattern can be one of the following, in a nested combination:\n\n* A name like `foo`\n* The special keyword `rfl` (for pattern matching on equality using `subst`)\n* A hyphen `-`, which clears the active hypothesis and any dependents.\n* A type ascription like `pat : ty` (parentheses are optional)\n* A tuple constructor like `\u27e8p1, p2, p3\u27e9`\n* An alternation / variant pattern `p1 | p2 | p3`\n\nParentheses can be used for grouping; alternation is higher precedence than type ascription, so\n`p1 | p2 | p3 : ty` means `(p1 | p2 | p3) : ty`.\n\nN-ary alternations are treated as a group, so `p1 | p2 | p3` is not the same as `p1 | (p2 | p3)`,\nand similarly for tuples. However, note that an n-ary alternation or tuple can match an n-ary\nconjunction or disjunction, because if the number of patterns exceeds the number of constructors in\nthe type being destructed, the extra patterns will match on the last element, meaning that\n`p1 | p2 | p3` will act like `p1 | (p2 | p3)` when matching `a1 \u2228 a2 \u2228 a3`. If matching against a\ntype with 3 constructors,  `p1 | (p2 | p3)` will act like `p1 | (p2 | p3) | _` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.tuple", "description": "An `rcases` pattern can be one of the following, in a nested combination:\n\n* A name like `foo`\n* The special keyword `rfl` (for pattern matching on equality using `subst`)\n* A hyphen `-`, which clears the active hypothesis and any dependents.\n* A type ascription like `pat : ty` (parentheses are optional)\n* A tuple constructor like `\u27e8p1, p2, p3\u27e9`\n* An alternation / variant pattern `p1 | p2 | p3`\n\nParentheses can be used for grouping; alternation is higher precedence than type ascription, so\n`p1 | p2 | p3 : ty` means `(p1 | p2 | p3) : ty`.\n\nN-ary alternations are treated as a group, so `p1 | p2 | p3` is not the same as `p1 | (p2 | p3)`,\nand similarly for tuples. However, note that an n-ary alternation or tuple can match an n-ary\nconjunction or disjunction, because if the number of patterns exceeds the number of constructors in\nthe type being destructed, the extra patterns will match on the last element, meaning that\n`p1 | p2 | p3` will act like `p1 | (p2 | p3)` when matching `a1 \u2228 a2 \u2228 a3`. If matching against a\ntype with 3 constructors,  `p1 | (p2 | p3)` will act like `p1 | (p2 | p3) | _` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.alts", "description": "An `rcases` pattern can be one of the following, in a nested combination:\n\n* A name like `foo`\n* The special keyword `rfl` (for pattern matching on equality using `subst`)\n* A hyphen `-`, which clears the active hypothesis and any dependents.\n* A type ascription like `pat : ty` (parentheses are optional)\n* A tuple constructor like `\u27e8p1, p2, p3\u27e9`\n* An alternation / variant pattern `p1 | p2 | p3`\n\nParentheses can be used for grouping; alternation is higher precedence than type ascription, so\n`p1 | p2 | p3 : ty` means `(p1 | p2 | p3) : ty`.\n\nN-ary alternations are treated as a group, so `p1 | p2 | p3` is not the same as `p1 | (p2 | p3)`,\nand similarly for tuples. However, note that an n-ary alternation or tuple can match an n-ary\nconjunction or disjunction, because if the number of patterns exceeds the number of constructors in\nthe type being destructed, the extra patterns will match on the last element, meaning that\n`p1 | p2 | p3` will act like `p1 | (p2 | p3)` when matching `a1 \u2228 a2 \u2228 a3`. If matching against a\ntype with 3 constructors,  `p1 | (p2 | p3)` will act like `p1 | (p2 | p3) | _` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.tuple\u2081", "description": " This function is used for producing rcases patterns based on a case tree. This is like\n`tuple\u2081_core` but it produces a pattern instead of a tuple pattern list, converting `[n]` to `n`\ninstead of `\u27e8n\u27e9` and `[]` to `_`, and otherwise just converting `[a, b, c]` to `\u27e8a, b, c\u27e9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rintro_parse", "description": "Syntax for a `rintro` pattern: `('?' (: n)?) | rintro_patt`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.alts'", "description": " Convert a list of patterns to an alternation pattern, but mapping `[p]` to `p` instead of\na unary alternation `|p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rintro", "description": " `rintro pat\u2081 pat\u2082 ... pat\u2099` introduces `n` arguments, then pattern matches on the `pat\u1d62` using\nthe same syntax as `rcases`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt_parse_list_rest", "description": "* `rcases_patt_parse_hi` will parse a high precedence `rcases` pattern, `patt_hi`.\n  This means only tuples and identifiers are allowed; alternations and type ascriptions\n  require `(...)` instead, which switches to `patt`.\n* `rcases_patt_parse` will parse a low precedence `rcases` pattern, `patt`. This consists of a\n  `patt_med` (which deals with alternations), optionally followed by a `: ty` type ascription. The\n  expression `ty` is at `texpr` precedence because it can appear at the end of a tactic, for\n  example in `rcases e with x : ty <|> skip`.\n* `rcases_patt_parse_list` will parse an alternation list, `patt_med`, one or more `patt`\n  patterns separated by `|`. It does not parse a `:` at the end, so that `a | b : ty` parses as\n  `(a | b) : ty` where `a | b` is the `patt_med` part.\n* `rcases_patt_parse_list_rest a` parses an alternation list after the initial pattern, `| b | c`.\n\n```lean\npatt ::= patt_med (\":\" expr)?\npatt_med ::= (patt_hi \"|\")* patt_hi\npatt_hi ::= id | \"rfl\" | \"_\" | \"@\" patt_hi | \"\u27e8\" (patt \",\")* patt \"\u27e9\" | \"(\" patt \")\"\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.obtain_parse", "description": " Parses `patt? (: expr)? (:= expr)?`, the arguments for `obtain`.\n (This is almost the same as `rcases_patt_parse`,\nbut it allows the pattern part to be empty.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.rintros", "description": "Alias for `rintro`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_hint_core", "description": "* `rcases_hint_core depth e` does the same as `rcases p e`, except the pattern `p` is an output\n  instead of an input, controlled only by the case depth argument `depth`. We use `cases` to depth\n  `depth` and then reconstruct an `rcases` pattern `p` that would, if passed to `rcases`, perform\n  the same thing as the case tree we just constructed (or at least, the nearest expressible\n  approximation to this.)\n* `rcases_hint.process_constructors depth cs l` takes a list of constructor names `cs` and a\n  matching list `l` of elements `(g, c', hs, _)` where  `c'` is a constructor name (used for\n  alignment with `cs`), `g` is the subgoal, and `hs` is the list of local hypotheses created by\n  `cases` in that subgoal. It matches on all of them, and then produces a `\u03a3\u03a0`-list of `rcases`\n  patterns describing the result, and the list of generated subgoals.\n* `rcases_hint.continue depth es` does the same as `rcases.continue (ps.zip es)`, except the\n  patterns `ps` are an output instead of an input, created by matching on everything to depth\n  `depth` and recording the successful cases. It returns `ps`, and the list of generated subgoals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt_parse_list'", "description": "* `rcases_patt_parse_hi` will parse a high precedence `rcases` pattern, `patt_hi`.\n  This means only tuples and identifiers are allowed; alternations and type ascriptions\n  require `(...)` instead, which switches to `patt`.\n* `rcases_patt_parse` will parse a low precedence `rcases` pattern, `patt`. This consists of a\n  `patt_med` (which deals with alternations), optionally followed by a `: ty` type ascription. The\n  expression `ty` is at `texpr` precedence because it can appear at the end of a tactic, for\n  example in `rcases e with x : ty <|> skip`.\n* `rcases_patt_parse_list` will parse an alternation list, `patt_med`, one or more `patt`\n  patterns separated by `|`. It does not parse a `:` at the end, so that `a | b : ty` parses as\n  `(a | b) : ty` where `a | b` is the `patt_med` part.\n* `rcases_patt_parse_list_rest a` parses an alternation list after the initial pattern, `| b | c`.\n\n```lean\npatt ::= patt_med (\":\" expr)?\npatt_med ::= (patt_hi \"|\")* patt_hi\npatt_hi ::= id | \"rfl\" | \"_\" | \"@\" patt_hi | \"\u27e8\" (patt \",\")* patt \"\u27e9\" | \"(\" patt \")\"\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_parse_depth", "description": "Parse the optional depth argument `(: n)?` of `rcases?` and `rintro?`, with default depth 5.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt_parse'", "description": "* `rcases_patt_parse_hi` will parse a high precedence `rcases` pattern, `patt_hi`.\n  This means only tuples and identifiers are allowed; alternations and type ascriptions\n  require `(...)` instead, which switches to `patt`.\n* `rcases_patt_parse` will parse a low precedence `rcases` pattern, `patt`. This consists of a\n  `patt_med` (which deals with alternations), optionally followed by a `: ty` type ascription. The\n  expression `ty` is at `texpr` precedence because it can appear at the end of a tactic, for\n  example in `rcases e with x : ty <|> skip`.\n* `rcases_patt_parse_list` will parse an alternation list, `patt_med`, one or more `patt`\n  patterns separated by `|`. It does not parse a `:` at the end, so that `a | b : ty` parses as\n  `(a | b) : ty` where `a | b` is the `patt_med` part.\n* `rcases_patt_parse_list_rest a` parses an alternation list after the initial pattern, `| b | c`.\n\n```lean\npatt ::= patt_med (\":\" expr)?\npatt_med ::= (patt_hi \"|\")* patt_hi\npatt_hi ::= id | \"rfl\" | \"_\" | \"@\" patt_hi | \"\u27e8\" (patt \",\")* patt \"\u27e9\" | \"(\" patt \")\"\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_parse", "description": " Syntax for a `rcases` pattern:\n* `rcases? expr (: n)?`\n* `rcases (h :)? expr (with patt_list (: expr)?)?`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.tuple'", "description": "Convert a list of patterns to a tuple pattern, but mapping `[p]` to `p` instead of `\u27e8p\u27e9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.merge_list", "description": " Like `zip_with`, but if the lists don't match in length, the excess elements will be put at the\nend of the result.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.alts\u2081_core", "description": " This function is used for producing rcases patterns based on a case tree. Here we are given\nthe list of patterns to apply to each argument of each constructor after the main case, and must\nproduce a list of alternatives with the same effect. This function calls `tuple\u2081` to make the\nindividual alternatives, and handles merging `[a, b, c | d]` to `a | b | c | d` instead of\n`a | b | (c | d)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_hint.continue", "description": "* `rcases_hint_core depth e` does the same as `rcases p e`, except the pattern `p` is an output\n  instead of an input, controlled only by the case depth argument `depth`. We use `cases` to depth\n  `depth` and then reconstruct an `rcases` pattern `p` that would, if passed to `rcases`, perform\n  the same thing as the case tree we just constructed (or at least, the nearest expressible\n  approximation to this.)\n* `rcases_hint.process_constructors depth cs l` takes a list of constructor names `cs` and a\n  matching list `l` of elements `(g, c', hs, _)` where  `c'` is a constructor name (used for\n  alignment with `cs`), `g` is the subgoal, and `hs` is the list of local hypotheses created by\n  `cases` in that subgoal. It matches on all of them, and then produces a `\u03a3\u03a0`-list of `rcases`\n  patterns describing the result, and the list of generated subgoals.\n* `rcases_hint.continue depth es` does the same as `rcases.continue (ps.zip es)`, except the\n  patterns `ps` are an output instead of an input, created by matching on everything to depth\n  `depth` and recording the successful cases. It returns `ps`, and the list of generated subgoals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_many", "description": " `rcases_many es pats` performs case distinction on the `es` using `pat` to\nname the arising new variables and assumptions.\nSee the module comment for the syntax of `pat`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.obtain", "description": "The `obtain` tactic is a combination of `have` and `rcases`. See `rcases` for\na description of supported patterns.\n\n```lean\nobtain \u27e8patt\u27e9 : type,\n{ ... }\n```\nis equivalent to\n```lean\nhave h : type,\n{ ... },\nrcases h with \u27e8patt\u27e9\n```\n\nThe syntax `obtain \u27e8patt\u27e9 : type := proof` is also supported.\n\nIf `\u27e8patt\u27e9` is omitted, `rcases` will try to infer the pattern.\n\nIf `type` is omitted, `:= proof` is required.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt_parse", "description": " `rcases_patt_parse` will parse a low precedence `rcases` pattern, `patt`. This consists of a\n`patt_med` (which deals with alternations), optionally followed by a `: ty` type ascription. The\nexpression `ty` is at `texpr` precedence because it can appear at the end of a tactic, for\nexample in `rcases e with x : ty <|> skip`.\n```lean\npatt ::= patt_med (\":\" expr)?\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.as_alts", "description": " Interpret an rcases pattern as an alternation, where non-alternations are treated as one\nalternative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.clear_goals", "description": " Given a list of `uncleared_goal`s, each of which is a goal metavariable and\na list of variables to clear, actually perform the clear and set the goals with the result.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.alts\u2081", "description": " This function is used for producing rcases patterns based on a case tree. This is like\n`alts\u2081_core`, but it produces a cases pattern directly instead of a list of alternatives. We\nspecially translate the empty alternation to `\u27e8\u27e9`, and translate `|(a | b)` to `\u27e8a | b\u27e9` (because we\ndon't have any syntax for unary alternation). Otherwise we can use the regular merging of\nalternations at the last argument so that `a | b | (c | d)` becomes `a | b | c | d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.rintro", "description": "The `rintro` tactic is a combination of the `intros` tactic with `rcases` to\nallow for destructuring patterns while introducing variables. See `rcases` for\na description of supported patterns. For example, `rintro (a | \u27e8b, c\u27e9) \u27e8d, e\u27e9`\nwill introduce two variables, and then do case splits on both of them producing\ntwo subgoals, one with variables `a d e` and the other with `b c d e`.\n\n`rintro`, unlike `rcases`, also supports the form `(x y : ty)` for introducing\nand type-ascripting multiple variables at once, similar to binders.\n\n`rintro?` will introduce and case split on variables in the same way as\n`rintro`, but will also print the `rintro` invocation that would have the same\nresult. Like `rcases?`, `rintro? : n` allows for modifying the\ndepth of splitting; the default is 5.\n\n`rintros` is an alias for `rintro`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases.continue", "description": "* `rcases_core p e` will match a pattern `p` against a local hypothesis `e`.\n  It returns the list of subgoals that were produced.\n* `rcases.continue pes` will match a (conjunctive) list of `(p, e)` pairs which refer to\n  patterns and local hypotheses to match against, and applies all of them. Note that this can\n  involve matching later arguments multiple times given earlier arguments, for example\n  `\u27e8a | b, \u27e8c, d\u27e9\u27e9` performs the `\u27e8c, d\u27e9` match twice, once on the `a` branch and once on `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases", "description": " `rcases h e pat` performs case distinction on `e` using `pat` to\nname the arising new variables and assumptions. If `h` is `some` name,\na new assumption `h : e = pat` will relate the expression `e` with the\ncurrent pattern. See the module comment for the syntax of `pat`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.rsuffices", "description": "The `rsuffices` tactic is an alternative version of `suffices`, that allows the usage\nof any syntax that would be valid in an `obtain` block. This tactic just calls `obtain`\non the expression, and then `rotate 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt_parse_hi'", "description": "* `rcases_patt_parse_hi` will parse a high precedence `rcases` pattern, `patt_hi`.\n  This means only tuples and identifiers are allowed; alternations and type ascriptions\n  require `(...)` instead, which switches to `patt`.\n* `rcases_patt_parse` will parse a low precedence `rcases` pattern, `patt`. This consists of a\n  `patt_med` (which deals with alternations), optionally followed by a `: ty` type ascription. The\n  expression `ty` is at `texpr` precedence because it can appear at the end of a tactic, for\n  example in `rcases e with x : ty <|> skip`.\n* `rcases_patt_parse_list` will parse an alternation list, `patt_med`, one or more `patt`\n  patterns separated by `|`. It does not parse a `:` at the end, so that `a | b : ty` parses as\n  `(a | b) : ty` where `a | b` is the `patt_med` part.\n* `rcases_patt_parse_list_rest a` parses an alternation list after the initial pattern, `| b | c`.\n\n```lean\npatt ::= patt_med (\":\" expr)?\npatt_med ::= (patt_hi \"|\")* patt_hi\npatt_hi ::= id | \"rfl\" | \"_\" | \"@\" patt_hi | \"\u27e8\" (patt \",\")* patt \"\u27e9\" | \"(\" patt \")\"\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_hint_many", "description": "* `rcases? \u27e8e1, e2, e3\u27e9` is like `rcases \u27e8e1, e2, e3\u27e9 with ...`, except it\n  generates `...` by matching on everything it can, and it outputs an `rcases`\n  invocation that should have the same effect.\n* `rcases? \u27e8e1, e2, e3\u27e9 : n` can be used to control the depth of case splits\n  (especially important for recursive types like `nat`, which can be cased as many\n  times as you like).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rintro_patt_parse", "description": "`rintro_patt_parse` will parse a low precedence `rcases` pattern, `rintro_patt` below.\nThis consists of either a sequence of patterns `p1 p2 p3` or an alternation list `p1 | p2 | p3`\ntreated as a single pattern, optionally followed by a `: ty` type ascription, which applies to\nevery pattern in the list.\n```lean\nrintro_patt ::= (rintro_patt_hi+ | patt_med) (\":\" expr)?\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.get_pi_arity_list", "description": "Compute the arity of the given (Pi-)type", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.list_Sigma", "description": "A list, with a disjunctive meaning (like a list of inductive constructors, or subgoals)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.as_tuple", "description": " Interpret an rcases pattern as a tuple, where `p` becomes `\u27e8p\u27e9`\nif `p` is not already a tuple.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.rsufficesI", "description": "The `rsufficesI` tactic is an instance-cache aware version of `rsuffices`; it resets the instance\ncache on the resulting goals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rintro_patt_parse'", "description": "`rintro_patt_parse_hi` and `rintro_patt_parse` are like `rcases_patt_parse`, but is used for\nparsing top level `rintro` patterns, which allow sequences like `(x y : t)` in addition to simple\n`rcases` patterns.\n\n* `rintro_patt_parse_hi` will parse a high precedence `rcases` pattern, `rintro_patt_hi` below.\n  This means only tuples and identifiers are allowed; alternations and type ascriptions\n  require `(...)` instead, which switches to `patt`.\n* `rintro_patt_parse` will parse a low precedence `rcases` pattern, `rintro_patt` below.\n  This consists of either a sequence of patterns `p1 p2 p3` or an alternation list `p1 | p2 | p3`\n  treated as a single pattern, optionally followed by a `: ty` type ascription, which applies to\n  every pattern in the list.\n* `rintro_patt_parse_low` parses `rintro_patt_low`, which is the same as `rintro_patt_parse tt` but\n  it does not permit an unparenthesized alternation list, it must have the form `p1 p2 p3 (: ty)?`.\n\n```lean\nrintro_patt ::= (rintro_patt_hi+ | patt_med) (\":\" expr)?\nrintro_patt_low ::= rintro_patt_hi* (\":\" expr)?\nrintro_patt_hi ::= patt_hi | \"(\" rintro_patt \")\"\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rintro_patt_parse_hi'", "description": "`rintro_patt_parse_hi` and `rintro_patt_parse` are like `rcases_patt_parse`, but is used for\nparsing top level `rintro` patterns, which allow sequences like `(x y : t)` in addition to simple\n`rcases` patterns.\n\n* `rintro_patt_parse_hi` will parse a high precedence `rcases` pattern, `rintro_patt_hi` below.\n  This means only tuples and identifiers are allowed; alternations and type ascriptions\n  require `(...)` instead, which switches to `patt`.\n* `rintro_patt_parse` will parse a low precedence `rcases` pattern, `rintro_patt` below.\n  This consists of either a sequence of patterns `p1 p2 p3` or an alternation list `p1 | p2 | p3`\n  treated as a single pattern, optionally followed by a `: ty` type ascription, which applies to\n  every pattern in the list.\n* `rintro_patt_parse_low` parses `rintro_patt_low`, which is the same as `rintro_patt_parse tt` but\n  it does not permit an unparenthesized alternation list, it must have the form `p1 p2 p3 (: ty)?`.\n\n```lean\nrintro_patt ::= (rintro_patt_hi+ | patt_med) (\":\" expr)?\nrintro_patt_low ::= rintro_patt_hi* (\":\" expr)?\nrintro_patt_hi ::= patt_hi | \"(\" rintro_patt \")\"\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.rintro", "description": "The `rintro` tactic is a combination of the `intros` tactic with `rcases` to\nallow for destructuring patterns while introducing variables. See `rcases` for\na description of supported patterns. For example, `rintro (a | \u27e8b, c\u27e9) \u27e8d, e\u27e9`\nwill introduce two variables, and then do case splits on both of them producing\ntwo subgoals, one with variables `a d e` and the other with `b c d e`.\n\n`rintro`, unlike `rcases`, also supports the form `(x y : ty)` for introducing\nand type-ascripting multiple variables at once, similar to binders.\n\n`rintro?` will introduce and case split on variables in the same way as\n`rintro`, but will also print the `rintro` invocation that would have the same\nresult. Like `rcases?`, `rintro? : n` allows for modifying the\ndepth of splitting; the default is 5.\n\n`rintros` is an alias for `rintro`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.get_arity_list", "description": "Compute the arity of the given function", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_core", "description": "* `rcases_core p e` will match a pattern `p` against a local hypothesis `e`.\n  It returns the list of subgoals that were produced.\n* `rcases.continue pes` will match a (conjunctive) list of `(p, e)` pairs which refer to\n  patterns and local hypotheses to match against, and applies all of them. Note that this can\n  involve matching later arguments multiple times given earlier arguments, for example\n  `\u27e8a | b, \u27e8c, d\u27e9\u27e9` performs the `\u27e8c, d\u27e9` match twice, once on the `a` branch and once on `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.list_Pi", "description": "A list, with a conjunctive meaning (like a list of constructor arguments, or hypotheses)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.rsuffices", "description": "The `rsuffices` tactic is an alternative version of `suffices`, that allows the usage\nof any syntax that would be valid in an `obtain` block. This tactic just calls `obtain`\non the expression, and then `rotate 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.has_to_tactic_format", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_args.has_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt_parse_list", "description": " `rcases_patt_parse_list` will parse an alternation list, `patt_med`, one or more `patt`\npatterns separated by `|`. It does not parse a `:` at the end, so that `a | b : ty` parses as\n`(a | b) : ty` where `a | b` is the `patt_med` part.\n```lean\npatt_med ::= (patt_hi \"|\")* patt_hi\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.rcases", "description": "`rcases` is a tactic that will perform `cases` recursively, according to a pattern. It is used to\ndestructure hypotheses or expressions composed of inductive types like `h1 : a \u2227 b \u2227 c \u2228 d` or\n`h2 : \u2203 x y, trans_rel R x y`. Usual usage might be `rcases h1 with \u27e8ha, hb, hc\u27e9 | hd` or\n`rcases h2 with \u27e8x, y, _ | \u27e8z, hxz, hzy\u27e9\u27e9` for these examples.\n\nEach element of an `rcases` pattern is matched against a particular local hypothesis (most of which\nare generated during the execution of `rcases` and represent individual elements destructured from\nthe input expression). An `rcases` pattern has the following grammar:\n\n* A name like `x`, which names the active hypothesis as `x`.\n* A blank `_`, which does nothing (letting the automatic naming system used by `cases` name the\n  hypothesis).\n* A hyphen `-`, which clears the active hypothesis and any dependents.\n* The keyword `rfl`, which expects the hypothesis to be `h : a = b`, and calls `subst` on the\n  hypothesis (which has the effect of replacing `b` with `a` everywhere or vice versa).\n* A type ascription `p : ty`, which sets the type of the hypothesis to `ty` and then matches it\n  against `p`. (Of course, `ty` must unify with the actual type of `h` for this to work.)\n* A tuple pattern `\u27e8p1, p2, p3\u27e9`, which matches a constructor with many arguments, or a series\n  of nested conjunctions or existentials. For example if the active hypothesis is `a \u2227 b \u2227 c`,\n  then the conjunction will be destructured, and `p1` will be matched against `a`, `p2` against `b`\n  and so on.\n* A `@` before a tuple pattern as in `@\u27e8p1, p2, p3\u27e9` will bind all arguments in the constructor,\n  while leaving the `@` off will only use the patterns on the explicit arguments.\n* An alteration pattern `p1 | p2 | p3`, which matches an inductive type with multiple constructors,\n  or a nested disjunction like `a \u2228 b \u2228 c`.\n\nA pattern like `\u27e8a, b, c\u27e9 | \u27e8d, e\u27e9` will do a split over the inductive datatype,\nnaming the first three parameters of the first constructor as `a,b,c` and the\nfirst two of the second constructor `d,e`. If the list is not as long as the\nnumber of arguments to the constructor or the number of constructors, the\nremaining variables will be automatically named. If there are nested brackets\nsuch as `\u27e8\u27e8a\u27e9, b | c\u27e9 | d` then these will cause more case splits as necessary.\nIf there are too many arguments, such as `\u27e8a, b, c\u27e9` for splitting on\n`\u2203 x, \u2203 y, p x`, then it will be treated as `\u27e8a, \u27e8b, c\u27e9\u27e9`, splitting the last\nparameter as necessary.\n\n`rcases` also has special support for quotient types: quotient induction into Prop works like\nmatching on the constructor `quot.mk`.\n\n`rcases h : e with PAT` will do the same as `rcases e with PAT` with the exception that an\nassumption `h : e = PAT` will be added to the context.\n\n`rcases? e` will perform case splits on `e` in the same way as `rcases e`,\nbut rather than accepting a pattern, it does a maximal cases and prints the\npattern that would produce this case splitting. The default maximum depth is 5,\nbut this can be modified with `rcases? e : n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rintro_patt_parse_hi", "description": "`rintro_patt_parse_hi` will parse a high precedence `rcases` pattern, `rintro_patt_hi` below.\nThis means only tuples and identifiers are allowed; alternations and type ascriptions\nrequire `(...)` instead, which switches to `patt`.\n```lean\nrintro_patt_hi ::= patt_hi | \"(\" rintro_patt \")\"\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rcases_patt.has_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_colimits_of_finite_and_filtered", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.coproducts_from_finite_filtered.lift_to_finset_colimit_cocone_cocone_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_products_of_finite_and_cofiltered", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_limits_of_finite_and_cofiltered", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.coproducts_from_finite_filtered.lift_to_finset_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_coproducts_of_finite_and_filtered", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.coproducts_from_finite_filtered.lift_to_finset_colimit_cocone", "description": "If `C` has finite coproducts and filtered colimits, we can construct arbitrary coproducts by\n   taking the colimit of the diagram formed by the coproducts of finite sets over the indexing\n   type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.coproducts_from_finite_filtered.lift_to_finset", "description": "If `C` has finite coproducts, a functor `discrete \u03b1 \u2964 C` lifts to a functor\n   `finset (discrete \u03b1) \u2964 C` by taking coproducts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.coproducts_from_finite_filtered.lift_to_finset_colimit_cocone_cocone_\u03b9", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.coproducts_from_finite_filtered.lift_to_finset_colimit_cocone_is_colimit_desc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.coproducts_from_finite_filtered.lift_to_finset_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.is_closed_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.nhds_F", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.mem_interior_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.ext'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite.realizer.to_locally_finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.to_topsp_is_topological_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.of_equiv_\u03c3", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite.realizer", "description": " A `locally_finite.realizer F f` is a realization that `f` is locally finite, namely it is a\nchoice of open sets from the basis of `F` such that they intersect only finitely many of the values\nof `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite.realizer.bas", "description": " A `locally_finite.realizer F f` is a realization that `f` is locally finite, namely it is a\nchoice of open sets from the basis of `F` such that they intersect only finitely many of the values\nof `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite.realizer.sets", "description": " A `locally_finite.realizer F f` is a realization that `f` is locally finite, namely it is a\nchoice of open sets from the basis of `F` such that they intersect only finitely many of the values\nof `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.mem_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.of_equiv_F", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.of_equiv_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.of_equiv", "description": "Replace the representation type of a `ctop` realizer.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compact.realizer.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.to_realizer", "description": "A `ctop` realizes the topological space it generates.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.nhds_\u03c3", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite.realizer.nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.id", "description": "The topological space realizer made of the open sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.is_open", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.mem_nhds_to_topsp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.has_coe_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.nhds", "description": "A realizer of the neighborhood of a point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.to_topsp", "description": "Every `ctop` is a topological space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.is_open_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.tendsto_nhds_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer", "description": "A `ctop` realizer for the topological space `T` is a `ctop`\n which generates `T`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.\u03c3", "description": "A `ctop` realizer for the topological space `T` is a `ctop`\n which generates `T`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.F", "description": "A `ctop` realizer for the topological space `T` is a `ctop`\n which generates `T`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.eq", "description": "A `ctop` realizer for the topological space `T` is a `ctop`\n which generates `T`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compact.realizer", "description": " A `compact.realizer s` is a realization that `s` is compact, namely it is a\nchoice of finite open covers for each set family covering `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_finite_iff_exists_realizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.is_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.coe_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.of_equiv", "description": "Map a ctop to an equivalent representation type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop", "description": "A `ctop \u03b1 \u03c3` is a realization of a topology (basis) on `\u03b1`,\n represented by a type `\u03c3` together with operations for the top element and\n the intersection operation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.f", "description": "A `ctop \u03b1 \u03c3` is a realization of a topology (basis) on `\u03b1`,\n represented by a type `\u03c3` together with operations for the top element and\n the intersection operation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.top", "description": "A `ctop \u03b1 \u03c3` is a realization of a topology (basis) on `\u03b1`,\n represented by a type `\u03c3` together with operations for the top element and\n the intersection operation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.top_mem", "description": "A `ctop \u03b1 \u03c3` is a realization of a topology (basis) on `\u03b1`,\n represented by a type `\u03c3` together with operations for the top element and\n the intersection operation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.inter", "description": "A `ctop \u03b1 \u03c3` is a realization of a topology (basis) on `\u03b1`,\n represented by a type `\u03c3` together with operations for the top element and\n the intersection operation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.inter_mem", "description": "A `ctop \u03b1 \u03c3` is a realization of a topology (basis) on `\u03b1`,\n represented by a type `\u03c3` together with operations for the top element and\n the intersection operation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.inter_sub", "description": "A `ctop \u03b1 \u03c3` is a realization of a topology (basis) on `\u03b1`,\n represented by a type `\u03c3` together with operations for the top element and\n the intersection operation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ctop.realizer.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.nonempty.convex_hull", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `convex_hull_nonempty_iff`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subset_convex_hull", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_subset_affine_span", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_min", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_eq_Inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.convex_hull_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_convex_hull_union_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "segment_subset_convex_hull", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_convex_hull_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_nonempty_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.convex_hull_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_pair", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull", "description": "The convex hull of a set `s` is the minimal convex set that includes `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_linear_map.convex_hull_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_convex_hull_union_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_map.image_convex_hull", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_convex_hull", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_span_convex_hull", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_empty_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.convex_remove_iff_not_mem_convex_hull_remove", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.convex_hull_subset_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.has_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_adjoint_pair", "description": " Given a pair of modules equipped with bilinear forms, this is the condition for a pair of\nmaps between them to be mutually adjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.distrib_mul_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.comp_id_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.neg_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_ortho_smul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.restrict", "description": "The restriction of a bilinear form on a submodule.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.mem_skew_adjoint_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_ortho", "description": " The proposition that two elements of a bilinear form space are orthogonal. For orthogonality\nof an indexed set of elements, use `bilin_form.is_Ortho`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_symm.sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.restrict_orthogonal_span_singleton_nondegenerate", "description": " The restriction of a reflexive, non-degenerate bilinear form on the orthogonal complement of\nthe span of a singleton is also non-degenerate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.congr", "description": "Apply a linear equivalence on the arguments of a bilinear form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.lin_mul_lin", "description": "`lin_mul_lin f g` is the bilinear form mapping `x` and `y` to `f x * g y`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_ortho_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.coe_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.mem_orthogonal_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_pair_self_adjoint", "description": " The condition for an endomorphism to be \"self-adjoint\" with respect to a pair of bilinear forms\non the underlying module. In the case that these two forms are identical, this is the usual concept\nof self adjointness. In the case that one of the forms is the negation of the other, this is the\nusual concept of skew adjointness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.zero_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.comp_symm_comp_of_nondegenerate_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.symm_comp_of_nondegenerate_left_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.dual_basis_repr_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.comp_right", "description": "Apply a linear map to the right argument of a bilinear form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.nondegenerate_restrict_of_disjoint_orthogonal", "description": " The restriction of a reflexive bilinear form `B` onto a submodule `W` is\nnondegenerate if `disjoint W (B.orthogonal W)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_adjoint_pair_iff_comp_left_eq_comp_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.to_lin_hom_flip", "description": " The linear map obtained from a `bilin_form` by fixing the right co-ordinate and evaluating in\nthe left.\nThis is the most general version of the construction; it is `R\u2082`-linear for some distinguished\ncommutative subsemiring `R\u2082` of the scalar ring.  Over semiring with no particular distinguished\nsuch subsemiring, use `to_lin'_flip`, which is `\u2115`-linear.  Over a commutative semiring, use\n`to_lin_flip`, which is linear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.zero_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.nondegenerate.ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.sum_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.lin_mul_lin_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_symm.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.flip", "description": " The `flip` of a bilinear form over a commutative ring, obtained by exchanging the left and\nright arguments.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.to_bilin_aux_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.finrank_add_finrank_orthogonal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_ortho_zero_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_refl.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.to_dual_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.to_lin_hom", "description": " The linear map obtained from a `bilin_form` by fixing the left co-ordinate and evaluating in\nthe right.\nThis is the most general version of the construction; it is `R\u2082`-linear for some distinguished\ncommutative subsemiring `R\u2082` of the scalar ring.  Over a semiring with no particular distinguished\nsuch subsemiring, use `to_lin'`, which is `\u2115`-linear.  Over a commutative semiring, use `to_lin`,\nwhich is linear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.sum_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.to_lin'_flip_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.comp_bilin_form", "description": "Apply a linear map on the output of a bilinear form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.orthogonal_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_symm_iff_flip'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.congr_trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.coe_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.orthogonal", "description": " The orthogonal complement of a submodule `N` with respect to some bilinear form is the set of\nelements `x` which are orthogonal to all elements of `N`; i.e., for all `y` in `N`, `B x y = 0`.\n\nNote that for general (neither symmetric nor antisymmetric) bilinear forms this definition has a\nchirality; in addition to this \"left\" orthogonal complement one could define a \"right\" orthogonal\ncomplement for which, for all `y` in `N`, `B y x = 0`.  This variant definition is not currently\nprovided in mathlib.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.skew_adjoint_submodule", "description": " The set of skew-adjoint endomorphisms of a module with bilinear form is a submodule. (In fact\nit is a Lie subalgebra.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_symm_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_refl.neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_alt.self_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.add_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.restrict_nondegenerate_of_is_compl_orthogonal", "description": " A subspace is complement to its orthogonal complement with respect to some\nreflexive bilinear form if that bilinear form restricted on to the subspace is nondegenerate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.to_lin_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.nondegenerate.ker_eq_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.orthogonal_span_singleton_eq_to_lin_ker", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_refl.eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.has_coe_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_adjoint_pair_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.coe_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.to_lin_restrict_range_dual_coannihilator_eq_orthogonal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.comp_id_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_self_adjoint", "description": " An endomorphism of a module is self-adjoint with respect to a bilinear form if it serves as an\nadjoint for itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_adjoint_pair.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_pair_self_adjoint_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.to_lin'_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.comp", "description": "Apply a linear map on the left and right argument of a bilinear form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.neg_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.restrict_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.to_lin'_flip", "description": " The linear map obtained from a `bilin_form` by fixing the right co-ordinate and evaluating in\nthe left.\nOver a commutative semiring, use `to_lin_flip`, which is linear rather than `\u2115`-linear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_alt.ortho_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.has_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.le_orthogonal_orthogonal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_refl.group_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.add_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.nondegenerate_iff_ker_eq_bot", "description": "A bilinear form is nondegenerate if and only if it has a trivial kernel.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_refl", "description": "The proposition that a bilinear form is reflexive", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_adjoint_pair.sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.comp_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.lin_mul_lin_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.mem_self_adjoint_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_symm.neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.lin_mul_lin_comp_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.coe_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.comp_right_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.restrict_symm", "description": "The restriction of a symmetric bilinear form on a submodule is also symmetric.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.coe_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.coe_fn_add_monoid_hom", "description": "`coe_fn` as an `add_monoid_hom`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.comp_right_comp_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_adjoint_pair_unique_of_nondegenerate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_Ortho.nondegenerate_iff_not_is_ortho_basis_self", "description": " Given an orthogonal basis with respect to a bilinear form, the bilinear form is nondegenerate\niff the basis has no elements which are self-orthogonal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_alt_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.nondegenerate_congr_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.sub_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_compl_span_singleton_orthogonal", "description": "Given a bilinear form `B` and some `x` such that `B x x \u2260 0`, the span of the singleton of `x`\n is complement to its orthogonal complement.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.coe_fn_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_Ortho_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_alt.neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.to_lin_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.congr_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_ortho_smul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.congr_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.sub_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_alt_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.zero_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.to_lin_hom_aux\u2082", "description": "Auxiliary definition to define `to_lin_hom`; see below.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.flip_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.comp_left_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.to_lin_hom_aux\u2081", "description": "Auxiliary definition to define `to_lin_hom`; see below.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.apply_dual_basis_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.sum_repr_mul_repr_mul", "description": "Write out `B x y` as a sum over `B (b i) (b j)` if `b` is a basis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_adjoint_pair.comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.comp_id_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_symm.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.neg_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.comp_bilin_form_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.self_adjoint_submodule", "description": " The set of self-adjoint endomorphisms of a module with bilinear form is a submodule. (In fact\nit is a Jordan subalgebra.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.comp_left_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_symm_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_adjoint_pair.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_adjoint_pair.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.has_smul", "description": " `bilin_form R M` inherits the scalar action by `\u03b1` on `R` if this is compatible with\nmultiplication.\n\nWhen `R` itself is commutative, this provides an `R`-action via `algebra.id`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.span_singleton_sup_orthogonal_eq_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_alt.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_adjoint_pair_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_symm.is_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.congr_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_ortho_zero_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_alt.sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.to_lin", "description": "Bilinear forms are linearly equivalent to maps with two arguments that are linear in both.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.smul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.comp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_adjoint_pair.eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.linear_independent_of_is_Ortho", "description": "A set of orthogonal vectors `v` with respect to some bilinear form `B` is linearly independent\n if for all `i`, `B (v i) (v i) \u2260 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.not_nondegenerate_zero", "description": "In a non-trivial module, zero is not non-degenerate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_adjoint_pair_iff_eq_of_nondegenerate", "description": " Given the nondegenerate bilinear form `B`, the linear map `\u03c6` has a unique left adjoint given by\n`bilin_form.left_adjoint_of_nondegenerate`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.mem_is_pair_self_adjoint_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.lin_mul_lin_comp_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_refl.ortho_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.flip_flip_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.to_bilin_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.congr_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.left_adjoint_of_nondegenerate", "description": " Given the nondegenerate bilinear form `B` and the linear map `\u03c6`,\n`left_adjoint_of_nondegenerate` provides the left adjoint of `\u03c6` with respect to `B`.\nThe lemma proving this property is `bilin_form.is_adjoint_pair_left_adjoint_of_nondegenerate`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.comp_left_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_symm", "description": "The proposition that a bilinear form is symmetric", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.congr_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.coe_fn_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.sub_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.has_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.comp_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.congr_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_refl_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.smul_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.to_lin'", "description": " The linear map obtained from a `bilin_form` by fixing the left co-ordinate and evaluating in\nthe right.\nOver a commutative semiring, use `to_lin`, which is linear rather than `\u2115`-linear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_Ortho", "description": " A set of vectors `v` is orthogonal with respect to some bilinear form `B` if and only\nif for all `i \u2260 j`, `B (v i) (v j) = 0`. For orthogonality between two elements, use\n`bilin_form.is_ortho`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_symm.ortho_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.dual_basis", "description": " The `B`-dual basis `B.dual_basis hB b` to a finite basis `b` satisfies\n`B (B.dual_basis hB b i) (b j) = B (b i) (B.dual_basis hB b j) = if i = j then 1 else 0`,\nwhere `B` is a nondegenerate (symmetric) bilinear form and `b` is a finite basis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.to_dual", "description": " Given a nondegenerate bilinear form `B` on a finite-dimensional vector space, `B.to_dual` is\nthe linear equivalence between a vector space and its dual with the underlying linear map\n`B.to_lin`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_symm.eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.ext_basis", "description": "Two bilinear forms are equal when they are equal on all basis vectors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.to_bilin_aux", "description": " A map with two arguments that is linear in both is a bilinear form.\n\nThis is an auxiliary definition for the full linear equivalence `linear_map.to_bilin`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.restrict_nondegenerate_iff_is_compl_orthogonal", "description": " A subspace is complement to its orthogonal complement with respect to some reflexive bilinear\nform if and only if that bilinear form restricted on to the subspace is nondegenerate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_skew_adjoint_iff_neg_self_adjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_adjoint_pair_left_adjoint_of_nondegenerate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form", "description": "`bilin_form R M` is the type of `R`-bilinear functions `M \u2192 M \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.bilin", "description": "`bilin_form R M` is the type of `R`-bilinear functions `M \u2192 M \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.bilin_add_left", "description": "`bilin_form R M` is the type of `R`-bilinear functions `M \u2192 M \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.bilin_smul_left", "description": "`bilin_form R M` is the type of `R`-bilinear functions `M \u2192 M \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.bilin_add_right", "description": "`bilin_form R M` is the type of `R`-bilinear functions `M \u2192 M \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.bilin_smul_right", "description": "`bilin_form R M` is the type of `R`-bilinear functions `M \u2192 M \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_skew_adjoint", "description": " An endomorphism of a module is skew-adjoint with respect to a bilinear form if its negation\nserves as an adjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_alt.neg_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.flip_hom", "description": " The flip of a bilinear form, obtained by exchanging the left and right arguments. This is a\nless structured version of the equiv which applies to general (noncommutative) rings `R` with a\ndistinguished commutative subring `R\u2082`; over a commutative ring use `flip`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.add_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_pair_self_adjoint_submodule", "description": "The set of pair-self-adjoint endomorphisms are a submodule of the type of all endomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.to_lin_restrict_ker_eq_inf_orthogonal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.flip_hom_aux", "description": " Auxiliary construction for the flip of a bilinear form, obtained by exchanging the left and\nright arguments. This version is a `linear_map`; it is later upgraded to a `linear_equiv`\nin `flip_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.apply_dual_basis_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.has_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_alt.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.add_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.to_bilin", "description": "A map with two arguments that is linear in both is linearly equivalent to bilinear form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.nondegenerate.congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.smul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.module", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.nondegenerate", "description": " A nondegenerate bilinear form is a bilinear form such that the only element that is orthogonal\nto every other element is `0`; i.e., for all nonzero `m` in `M`, there exists `n` in `M` with\n`B m n \u2260 0`.\n\nNote that for general (neither symmetric nor antisymmetric) bilinear forms this definition has a\nchirality; in addition to this \"left\" nondegeneracy condition one could define a \"right\"\nnondegeneracy condition that in the situation described, `B n m \u2260 0`.  This variant definition is\nnot currently provided in mathlib. In finite dimension either definition implies the other.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.comp_right_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.symm_comp_of_nondegenerate", "description": " Given bilinear forms `B\u2081, B\u2082` where `B\u2082` is nondegenerate, `symm_comp_of_nondegenerate`\nis the linear map `B\u2082.to_lin\u207b\u00b9 \u2218 B\u2081.to_lin`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.ne_zero_of_not_is_ortho_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.comp_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.span_singleton_inf_orthogonal_eq_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_alt", "description": "The proposition that a bilinear form is alternating", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.comp_left_comp_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_Ortho.not_is_ortho_basis_self_of_nondegenerate", "description": " An orthogonal basis with respect to a nondegenerate bilinear form has no self-orthogonal\nelements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.comp_left", "description": "Apply a linear map to the left argument of a bilinear form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_refl_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.coe_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.flip_flip", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_alt.is_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.flip'", "description": " The flip of a bilinear form over a ring, obtained by exchanging the left and right arguments,\nhere considered as an `\u2115`-linear equivalence, i.e. an additive equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_prime_add_order_of_dvd_card", "description": " For every prime `p` dividing the order of a finite additive group `G` there exists an element of\norder `p` in `G`. This is the additive version of Cauchy's theorem.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.vectors_prod_eq_one.mem_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.vectors_prod_eq_one.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.is_conj_of_cycle_type_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.vectors_prod_eq_one.rotate_rotate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.sign_of_cycle_type", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.is_cycle_of_prime_order'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.sign_of_cycle_type'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.is_cycle.cycle_type", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.two_le_of_mem_cycle_type", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.vectors_prod_eq_one.card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.vectors_prod_eq_one.zero_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.card_cycle_type_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.order_of_cycle_of_dvd_order_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.filter_parts_partition_eq_cycle_type", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.cycle_type_of_card_le_mem_cycle_type_add_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.cycle_type_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.vectors_prod_eq_one", "description": "The type of vectors with terms from `G`, length `n`, and product equal to `1:G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.cycle_type_extend_domain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.vectors_prod_eq_one.equiv_vector", "description": " Given a vector `v` of length `n` whose product is 1, make a vector of length `n - 1`,\nby deleting the last entry of `v`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.cycle_type_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.cycle_type_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.cycle_type_of_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.partition_eq_of_is_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.swap_mul_swap_same_mem_closure_three_cycles", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.one_lt_of_mem_cycle_type", "description": "", "p