name": "unitary.coe_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.mem_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.to_units_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.mem_iff_self_mul_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.star_mul_self_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.coe_to_units_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.coe_zpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.star_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.coe_mul_star_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary", "description": "In a *-monoid, `unitary R` is the submonoid consisting of all the elements `U` of\n`R` such that `star U * U = 1` and `U * star U = 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.star_mem_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.star_mul_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.star_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.coe_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.has_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.star_eq_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.coe_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.coe_star_mul_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.star_eq_inv'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.coe_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.mul_star_self_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.has_distrib_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.to_units", "description": "The unitary elements embed into the units.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.mem_iff_star_mul_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.mul_star_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.has_involutive_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.coe_inv_to_units_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unitary.has_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_eq_one_iff_of_norm_one", "description": " If the inner product of two unit vectors is `1`, then the two vectors are equal. One form of\nthe equality case for Cauchy-Schwarz.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_mul_symm_re_eq_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_self_eq_norm_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.map_linear_isometry_equiv", "description": " A linear isometric equivalence, applied with `basis.map`, preserves the property of being\northonormal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.complex_to_real", "description": "A complex inner product implies a real inner product", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_sub_sub_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_add_sq_eq_norm_sq_add_norm_sq_real", "description": "Pythagorean theorem, vector inner product form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.completion.inner_product_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real_inner_le_norm", "description": "Cauchy\u2013Schwarz inequality with norm", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_R_or_C.inner_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.equiv", "description": "A linear isometric equivalence that sends an orthonormal basis to a given orthonormal basis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.inner_left_finsupp", "description": " The inner product of a linear combination of a set of orthonormal vectors with one of those\nvectors picks out the coefficient of that vector.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_inner_div_norm_mul_norm_eq_one_of_ne_zero_of_ne_zero_mul", "description": " The inner product of a nonzero vector with a nonzero multiple of\nitself, divided by the product of their norms, has absolute value\n1.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ext_inner_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_add_add_self", "description": "Expand `inner (x + y) (x + y)`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.inner_sum", "description": " The inner product of two linear combinations of a set of orthonormal vectors, expressed as\na sum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real_inner_smul_self_right", "description": "The inner product of a vector with a multiple of itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis_of_orthonormal_of_card_eq_finrank", "description": "A family of orthonormal vectors with the correct cardinality forms a basis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_bounded_bilinear_map_inner", "description": " When an inner product space `E` over `\ud835\udd5c` is considered as a real normed space, its inner\nproduct satisfies `is_bounded_bilinear_map`.\n\nIn order to state these results, we need a `normed_space \u211d E` instance. We will later establish\nsuch an instance by restriction-of-scalars, `inner_product_space.is_R_or_C_to_real \ud835\udd5c E`, but this\ninstance may be not definitionally equal to some other \u201cnatural\u201d instance. So, we assume\n`[normed_space \u211d E]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.to_has_norm", "description": " Norm constructed from a `inner_product_space.core` structure, defined to be the square root\nof the scalar product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_neg_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.inner_left_sum", "description": " The inner product of a linear combination of a set of orthonormal vectors with one of those\nvectors picks out the coefficient of that vector.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_self_re_to_K", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abs_real_inner_div_norm_mul_norm_eq_one_iff", "description": " The inner product of two vectors, divided by the product of their\nnorms, has absolute value 1 if and only if they are nonzero and one is\na multiple of the other. One form of equality case for Cauchy-Schwarz.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_zero_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal_subtype_iff_ite", "description": " `if ... then ... else` characterization of a set of vectors being orthonormal.  (Inner product\nequals Kronecker delta.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real_inner_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real_inner_smul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_maximal_orthonormal", "description": " Given an orthonormal set `v` of vectors in `E`, there exists a maximal orthonormal set\ncontaining it.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_equiv.coe_isometry_of_inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_sub_mul_self_real", "description": "Expand the square", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.equiv_to_linear_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abs_real_inner_div_norm_mul_norm_eq_one_of_ne_zero_of_ne_zero_mul", "description": " The inner product of a nonzero vector with a nonzero multiple of\nitself, divided by the product of their norms, has absolute value\n1.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real_inner_div_norm_mul_norm_eq_neg_one_of_ne_zero_of_neg_mul", "description": " The inner product of a nonzero vector with a negative multiple of\nitself, divided by the product of their norms, has value -1.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_equiv.isometry_of_inner", "description": "A linear equivalence that preserves the inner product is a linear isometric equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "re_inner_eq_norm_add_mul_self_sub_norm_sub_mul_self_div_four", "description": "Polarization identity: The real part of the  inner product, in terms of the norm.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.sum_inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_re_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_family.inner_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.re_apply_inner_self", "description": "Extract a real bilinear form from an operator `T`, by taking the pairing `\u03bb x, re \u27eaT x, x\u27eb`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "innerSL_flip", "description": "The inner product as a continuous sesquilinear map, with the two arguments flipped.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.is_R_or_C_to_real", "description": " A general inner product space structure implies a real inner product structure. This is not\nregistered as an instance since it creates problems with the case `\ud835\udd5c = \u211d`, but in can be used in a\nproof to obtain a real inner product space structure from a given `\ud835\udd5c`-inner product space\nstructure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.orthonormal_of_forall_eq_or_eq_neg", "description": " Given an orthonormal family, a second family of vectors is orthonormal if every vector equals\nthe corresponding vector in the original family or its negation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.to_has_inner'", "description": " Inner product defined by the `inner_product_space.core` structure. We can't reuse\n`inner_product_space.core.to_has_inner` because it takes `inner_product_space.core` as an explicit\nargument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real_inner_eq_re_inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_add_sq", "description": "Expand the square", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_smul_real_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.map_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on.inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_self_im", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real_inner_self_abs", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.to_sesq_form_apply_norm_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_self_nonpos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.inner_left_right_finset", "description": "The double sum of weighted inner products of pairs of vectors from an orthonormal sequence is the\nsum of the weights.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real_inner_add_add_self", "description": "Expand `\u27eax + y, x + y\u27eb_\u211d`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_map_polarization", "description": "A complex polarization identity, with a linear map", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_eq_sqrt_inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_self_norm_to_K", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_self_eq_norm_mul_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.isometry_of_orthonormal_to_linear_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.inner_map_map", "description": "A linear isometric equivalence preserves the inner product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.comp_linear_isometry", "description": "A linear isometry preserves the property of being orthonormal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_self_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abs_real_inner_div_norm_mul_norm_le_one", "description": " The real inner product of two vectors, divided by the product of their\nnorms, has absolute value at most 1.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry.orthonormal_comp_iff", "description": "A linear isometry preserves the property of being orthonormal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space", "description": "An inner product space is a vector space with an additional operation called inner product.\nThe norm could be derived from the inner product, instead we require the existence of a norm and\nthe fact that `\u2016x\u2016^2 = re \u27eax, x\u27eb` to be able to put instances on `\ud835\udd42` or product\nspaces.\n\nTo construct a norm from an inner product, see `inner_product_space.of_core`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.to_normed_space", "description": "An inner product space is a vector space with an additional operation called inner product.\nThe norm could be derived from the inner product, instead we require the existence of a norm and\nthe fact that `\u2016x\u2016^2 = re \u27eax, x\u27eb` to be able to put instances on `\ud835\udd42` or product\nspaces.\n\nTo construct a norm from an inner product, see `inner_product_space.of_core`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.to_has_inner", "description": "An inner product space is a vector space with an additional operation called inner product.\nThe norm could be derived from the inner product, instead we require the existence of a norm and\nthe fact that `\u2016x\u2016^2 = re \u27eax, x\u27eb` to be able to put instances on `\ud835\udd42` or product\nspaces.\n\nTo construct a norm from an inner product, see `inner_product_space.of_core`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.norm_sq_eq_inner", "description": "An inner product space is a vector space with an additional operation called inner product.\nThe norm could be derived from the inner product, instead we require the existence of a norm and\nthe fact that `\u2016x\u2016^2 = re \u27eax, x\u27eb` to be able to put instances on `\ud835\udd42` or product\nspaces.\n\nTo construct a norm from an inner product, see `inner_product_space.of_core`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.conj_symm", "description": "An inner product space is a vector space with an additional operation called inner product.\nThe norm could be derived from the inner product, instead we require the existence of a norm and\nthe fact that `\u2016x\u2016^2 = re \u27eax, x\u27eb` to be able to put instances on `\ud835\udd42` or product\nspaces.\n\nTo construct a norm from an inner product, see `inner_product_space.of_core`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.add_left", "description": "An inner product space is a vector space with an additional operation called inner product.\nThe norm could be derived from the inner product, instead we require the existence of a norm and\nthe fact that `\u2016x\u2016^2 = re \u27eax, x\u27eb` to be able to put instances on `\ud835\udd42` or product\nspaces.\n\nTo construct a norm from an inner product, see `inner_product_space.of_core`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.smul_left", "description": "An inner product space is a vector space with an additional operation called inner product.\nThe norm could be derived from the inner product, instead we require the existence of a norm and\nthe fact that `\u2016x\u2016^2 = re \u27eax, x\u27eb` to be able to put instances on `\ud835\udd42` or product\nspaces.\n\nTo construct a norm from an inner product, see `inner_product_space.of_core`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.re_apply_inner_self_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.of_core", "description": " Given a `inner_product_space.core` structure on a space, one can use it to turn\nthe space into an inner product space. The `normed_add_comm_group` structure is expected\nto already be defined with `inner_product_space.of_core.to_normed_add_comm_group`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_family.comp", "description": " The composition of an orthogonal family of subspaces with an injective function is also an\northogonal family.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_eq_sum_norm_sq_div_four", "description": "Polarization identity: The inner product, in terms of the norm.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real_inner_self_eq_norm_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_sub_sq_eq_norm_sq_add_norm_sq_iff_real_inner_eq_zero", "description": " Pythagorean theorem, subtracting vectors, if-and-only-if vector\ninner product form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "innerSL_flip_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.to_uniform_convex_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real_inner_add_sub_eq_zero_iff", "description": " The sum and difference of two vectors are orthogonal if and only\nif they have the same norm.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_self_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.cauchy_schwarz_aux", "description": " An auxiliary equality useful to prove the **Cauchy\u2013Schwarz inequality**: the square of the norm\nof `\u27eax, y\u27eb \u2022 x - \u27eax, x\u27eb \u2022 y` is equal to `\u2016x\u2016 ^ 2 * (\u2016x\u2016 ^ 2 * \u2016y\u2016 ^ 2 - \u2016\u27eax, y\u27eb\u2016 ^ 2)`. We use\n`inner_product_space.of_core.norm_sq x` etc (defeq to `is_R_or_C.re \u27eax, x\u27eb`) instead of `\u2016x\u2016 ^ 2`\netc to avoid extra rewrites when applying it to an `inner_product_space`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_add_add_self", "description": "Expand `\u27eax + y, x + y\u27eb`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.to_sesq_form_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_self_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.to_sesq_form", "description": " Given `f : E \u2192L[\ud835\udd5c] E'`, construct the continuous sesquilinear form `\u03bb x y, \u27eax, A y\u27eb`, given\nas a continuous linear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_eq_zero_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_inner_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.re_apply_inner_self_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_family.summable_iff_norm_sq_summable", "description": " A family `f` of mutually-orthogonal elements of `E` is summable, if and only if\n`(\u03bb i, \u2016f i\u2016 ^ 2)` is summable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_add_sq_eq_norm_sq_add_norm_sq_of_inner_eq_zero", "description": "Pythagorean theorem, vector inner product form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_neg_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_im_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_smul_real_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "parallelogram_law_with_nnnorm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.norm_eq_sqrt_inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.inner_finsupp_eq_sum_left", "description": " The inner product of two linear combinations of a set of orthonormal vectors, expressed as\na sum over the first `finsupp`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.inner_left_fintype", "description": " The inner product of a linear combination of a set of orthonormal vectors with one of those\nvectors picks out the coefficient of that vector.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry.inner_map_map", "description": "A linear isometry preserves the inner product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_family", "description": " An indexed family of mutually-orthogonal subspaces of an inner product space `E`.\n\nThe simple way to express this concept would be as a condition on `V : \u03b9 \u2192 submodule \ud835\udd5c E`.  We\nWe instead implement it as a condition on a family of inner product spaces each equipped with an\nisometric embedding into `E`, thus making it a property of morphisms rather than subobjects.\nThe connection to the subobject spelling is shown in `orthogonal_family_iff_pairwise`.\n\nThis definition is less lightweight, but allows for better definitional properties when the inner\nproduct space structure on each of the submodules is important -- for example, when considering\ntheir Hilbert sum (`pi_lp V 2`).  For example, given an orthonormal set of vectors `v : \u03b9 \u2192 E`,\nwe have an associated orthogonal family of one-dimensional subspaces of `E`, which it is convenient\nto be able to discuss using `\u03b9 \u2192 \ud835\udd5c` rather than `\u03a0 i : \u03b9, span \ud835\udd5c (v i)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.coe_inner", "description": "The inner product on submodules is the same as on the ambient space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_eq_norm_mul_iff_real", "description": " If the inner product of two vectors is equal to the product of their norms (i.e.,\n`\u27eax, y\u27eb = \u2016x\u2016 * \u2016y\u2016`), then the two vectors are nonnegative real multiples of each other. One form\nof the equality case for Cauchy-Schwarz.\nCompare `norm_inner_eq_norm_iff`, which takes the weaker hypothesis `abs \u27eax, y\u27eb = \u2016x\u2016 * \u2016y\u2016`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_re_zero_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.sqrt_norm_sq_eq_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_zero_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_sub_sq_real", "description": "Expand the square", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.isometry_of_inner_to_linear_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_inner_eq_norm_tfae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_conj_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_sum", "description": "An inner product with a sum on the right.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_neg_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_family.norm_sq_diff_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner\u209b\u2097_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_equiv.isometry_of_inner_to_linear_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.norm_inner_le_norm", "description": "Cauchy\u2013Schwarz inequality with norm", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_add_sq_real", "description": "Expand the square", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real_inner_eq_norm_add_mul_self_sub_norm_mul_self_sub_norm_mul_self_div_two", "description": "Polarization identity: The real inner product, in terms of the norm.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "re_inner_eq_norm_mul_self_add_norm_mul_self_sub_norm_sub_mul_self_div_two", "description": "Polarization identity: The real part of the  inner product, in terms of the norm.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_self_im", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.equiv_trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_map_self_eq_zero", "description": "A linear map `T` is zero, if and only if the identity `\u27eaT x, x\u27eb_\u2102 = 0` holds for all `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal_Union_of_directed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_add_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.re_apply_inner_self_continuous", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_add_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_sub_sub_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "parallelogram_law_with_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.comp_linear_isometry_equiv", "description": "A linear isometric equivalence preserves the property of being orthonormal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_inner.is_R_or_C_to_real", "description": " A general inner product implies a real inner product. This is not registered as an instance\nsince it creates problems with the case `\ud835\udd5c = \u211d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_eq_sqrt_real_inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.comp", "description": " A subfamily of an orthonormal family (i.e., a composition with an injective map) is an\northonormal family.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sesq_form_of_inner_apply_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_smul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_self_eq_norm_mul_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.isometry_of_inner", "description": "A linear map that preserves the inner product is a linear isometry.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.norm_sq", "description": "The norm squared function for `inner_product_space.core` structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ext_inner_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_family.inner_right_dfinsupp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal_subtype_range", "description": " An injective family `v : \u03b9 \u2192 E` is orthonormal if and only if `coe : (range v) \u2192 E` is\northonormal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_add_sq_eq_norm_sq_add_norm_sq_iff_real_inner_eq_zero", "description": "Pythagorean theorem, if-and-only-if vector inner product form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_re_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_family.norm_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_lt_one_iff_real_of_norm_one", "description": " If the inner product of two unit vectors is strictly less than `1`, then the two vectors are\ndistinct. One form of the equality case for Cauchy-Schwarz.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_sub_eq_norm_add", "description": "Given two orthogonal vectors, their sum and difference have equal norms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.isometry_of_orthonormal", "description": "A linear map that sends an orthonormal basis to orthonormal vectors is a linear isometry.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_inner_eq_norm_iff", "description": "If the inner product of two vectors is equal to the product of their norms, then the two vectors\nare multiples of each other. One form of the equality case for Cauchy-Schwarz.\nCompare `inner_eq_norm_mul_iff`, which takes the stronger hypothesis `\u27eax, y\u27eb = \u2016x\u2016 * \u2016y\u2016`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real_inner_mul_inner_self_le", "description": "Cauchy\u2013Schwarz inequality for real inner products.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_self_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_smul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_sub_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_family.inner_right_fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.tsum_inner_products_le", "description": "Bessel's inequality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_sub_sq_eq_norm_sq_add_norm_sq_real", "description": " Pythagorean theorem, subtracting vectors, vector inner product\nform.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.coe_norm_sq_eq_inner_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real_inner_div_norm_mul_norm_eq_one_of_ne_zero_of_pos_mul", "description": " The inner product of a nonzero vector with a positive multiple of\nitself, divided by the product of their norms, has value 1.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_eq_norm_mul_iff", "description": " If the inner product of two vectors is equal to the product of their norms (i.e.,\n`\u27eax, y\u27eb = \u2016x\u2016 * \u2016y\u2016`), then the two vectors are nonnegative real multiples of each other. One form\nof the equality case for Cauchy-Schwarz.\nCompare `norm_inner_eq_norm_iff`, which takes the weaker hypothesis `abs \u27eax, y\u27eb = \u2016x\u2016 * \u2016y\u2016`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal_span", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_basis_of_orthonormal_of_card_eq_finrank", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal_sUnion_of_directed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_independent_of_ne_zero_of_inner_eq_zero", "description": " A family of vectors is linearly independent if they are nonzero\nand orthogonal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real_inner_self_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.sum_inner", "description": "An inner product with a sum on the left, `finsupp` version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "im_inner_eq_norm_sub_I_smul_mul_self_sub_norm_add_I_smul_mul_self_div_four", "description": "Polarization identity: The imaginary part of the inner product, in terms of the norm.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_sub_sq", "description": "Expand the square", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_add_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_self_eq_norm_sq_to_K", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.inner_right_fintype", "description": " The inner product of a linear combination of a set of orthonormal vectors with one of those\nvectors picks out the coefficient of that vector.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_map_complex", "description": " The inner product on an inner product space of dimension 2 can be evaluated in terms\nof a complex-number representation of the space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.orthogonal_family", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_inner_le_norm", "description": "Cauchy\u2013Schwarz inequality with norm", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_self_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_sum_smul_sum_smul_of_sum_eq_zero", "description": " The inner product of two weighted sums, where the weights in each\nsum add to 0, in terms of the norms of pairwise differences.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "parallelogram_law", "description": "Parallelogram law", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_lt_norm_mul_iff_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_zero_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.equiv_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "innerSL", "description": " The inner product as a continuous sesquilinear map. Note that `to_dual_map` (resp. `to_dual`)\nin `inner_product_space.dual` is a version of this given as a linear isometry (resp. linear\nisometric equivalence).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_neg_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real_inner_smul_self_left", "description": "The inner product of a vector with a multiple of itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_sub_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_self_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_div_norm_sq_smul", "description": " Formula for the distance between the images of two nonzero points under an inversion with center\nzero. See also `euclidean_geometry.dist_inversion_inversion` for inversions around a general\npoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_family.eq_ite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_inner_div_norm_mul_norm_eq_one_iff", "description": " The inner product of two vectors, divided by the product of their\nnorms, has absolute value 1 if and only if they are nonzero and one is\na multiple of the other. One form of equality case for Cauchy-Schwarz.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.norm_inner_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_at.inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal", "description": "An orthonormal set of vectors in an `inner_product_space`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_mul_inner_self_le", "description": "**Cauchy\u2013Schwarz inequality**.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.to_core", "description": " Define `inner_product_space.core` from `inner_product_space`. Defined to reuse lemmas about\n`inner_product_space.core` for `inner_product_space`s. Note that the `has_norm` instance provided by\n`inner_product_space.core.has_norm` is propositionally but not definitionally equal to the original\nnorm.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form_of_real_inner", "description": "The real inner product as a bilinear form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner\u209b\u2097", "description": "The inner product as a sesquilinear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.linear_independent", "description": "An orthonormal set is linearly independent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_R_or_C.inner_product_space", "description": "A field `\ud835\udd5c` satisfying `is_R_or_C` is itself a `\ud835\udd5c`-inner product space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_smul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.sum_inner_products_le", "description": "Bessel's inequality for finite sums.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form_of_real_inner_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.inner_finsupp_eq_zero", "description": " A linear combination of some subset of an orthonormal set is orthogonal to other members of the\nset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_add_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_equiv.coe_isometry_of_orthonormal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.cod_restrict", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.completion.has_inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner\u209b\u2097_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real_inner_eq_norm_mul_self_add_norm_mul_self_sub_norm_sub_mul_self_div_two", "description": "Polarization identity: The real inner product, in terms of the norm.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_sub_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.inner_right_sum", "description": " The inner product of a linear combination of a set of orthonormal vectors with one of those\nvectors picks out the coefficient of that vector.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_re_zero_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_zero_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.is_internal.collected_basis_orthonormal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "innerSL_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_sub_mul_self", "description": "Expand the square", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.equiv_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.coe_isometry_of_orthonormal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_im_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.inner_sum", "description": "An inner product with a sum on the right, `finsupp` version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_add_mul_self", "description": "Expand the square", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real_inner_smul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "innerSL_apply_norm", "description": " `innerSL` is an isometry. Note that the associated `linear_isometry` is defined in\n`inner_product_space.dual` as `to_dual_map`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "re_inner_eq_norm_add_mul_self_sub_norm_mul_self_sub_norm_mul_self_div_two", "description": "Polarization identity: The real part of the  inner product, in terms of the norm.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal.inner_finsupp_eq_sum_right", "description": " The inner product of two linear combinations of a set of orthonormal vectors, expressed as\na sum over the second `finsupp`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_self_re_eq_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_eq_norm_mul_iff_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_sub_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_family.orthonormal_sigma_orthonormal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_equiv.isometry_of_orthonormal_to_linear_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core", "description": " A structure requiring that a scalar product is positive definite and symmetric, from which one\ncan construct an `inner_product_space` instance in `inner_product_space.of_core`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.to_has_inner", "description": " A structure requiring that a scalar product is positive definite and symmetric, from which one\ncan construct an `inner_product_space` instance in `inner_product_space.of_core`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.conj_symm", "description": " A structure requiring that a scalar product is positive definite and symmetric, from which one\ncan construct an `inner_product_space` instance in `inner_product_space.of_core`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.nonneg_re", "description": " A structure requiring that a scalar product is positive definite and symmetric, from which one\ncan construct an `inner_product_space` instance in `inner_product_space.of_core`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.definite", "description": " A structure requiring that a scalar product is positive definite and symmetric, from which one\ncan construct an `inner_product_space` instance in `inner_product_space.of_core`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.add_left", "description": " A structure requiring that a scalar product is positive definite and symmetric, from which one\ncan construct an `inner_product_space` instance in `inner_product_space.of_core`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.smul_left", "description": " A structure requiring that a scalar product is positive definite and symmetric, from which one\ncan construct an `inner_product_space` instance in `inner_product_space.of_core`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_add_pow_two", "description": "**Alias** of `norm_add_sq`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real_inner_div_norm_mul_norm_eq_one_iff", "description": " The inner product of two vectors, divided by the product of their\nnorms, has value 1 if and only if they are nonzero and one is\na positive multiple of the other.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "re_inner_le_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.core.inner_conj_symm", "d