irectly if possible,\nsince this produces equation lemmas.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.le_of_pred_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.lt_succ_iff_lt_or_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.mod_succ_eq_iff_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.div_eq_iff_eq_mul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.mul_mod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.le_rec_on_succ_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.le_rec_on", "description": " Recursion starting at a non-zero number: given a map `C k \u2192 C (k+1)` for each `k`,\nthere is a map from `C n` to each `C m`, `n \u2264 m`. For a version where the assumption is only made\nwhen `k \u2265 n`, see `le_rec_on'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.div_add_mod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.pincer_recursion", "description": " Given `P : \u2115 \u2192 \u2115 \u2192 Sort*`, if we have `P i 0` and `P 0 i` for all `i : \u2115`,\nand for any `x y : \u2115` we can extend `P` from `(x,y+1)` and `(x+1,y)` to `(x+1,y+1)`\nthen we have `P n m` for all `n m : \u2115`.\nNote that for non-`Prop` output it is preferable to use the equation compiler directly if possible,\nsince this produces equation lemmas.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.le_rec_on_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.add_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.find_le_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.succ_le_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.add_mod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.decreasing_induction'", "description": " Decreasing induction: if `P (k+1)` implies `P k` for all `m \u2264 k < n`, then `P n` implies `P m`.\nAlso works for functions to `Sort*`. Weakens the assumptions of `decreasing_induction`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.add_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, 