 "subtype.property", "description": "Remark: subtype must take a Sort instead of Type because of the axiom strong_indefinite_description.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "eq.symm", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "id_rhs", "description": "id_rhs is an auxiliary declaration used in the equation compiler to address performance\nissues when proving equational lemmas. The equation compiler uses it as a marker.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "heq", "description": "Heterogeneous equality.\n\nIts purpose is to write down equalities between terms whose types are not definitionally equal.\nFor example, given `x : vector \u03b1 n` and `y : vector \u03b1 (0+n)`, `x = y` doesn't typecheck but `x == y` does.\n\nIf you have a goal `\u22a2 x == y`,\nyour first instinct should be to ask (either yourself, or on [zulip](https://leanprover.zulipchat.com/))\nif something has gone wrong already.\nIf you really do need to follow this route,\nyou may find the lemmas `eq_rec_heq` and `eq_mpr_heq` useful.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "heq.refl", "description": "Heterogeneous equality.\n\nIts purpose is to write down equalities between terms whose types are not definitionally equal.\nFor example, given `x : vector \u03b1 n` and `y : vector \u03b1 (0+n)`, `x = y` doesn't typecheck but `x == y` does.\n\nIf you have a goal `\u22a2 x == y`,\nyour first instinct should be to ask (either yourself, or on [zulip](https://leanprover.zulipchat.com/))\nif something has gone wrong already.\nIf you really do need to follow this route,\nyou may find the lemmas `eq_rec_heq` and `eq_mpr_heq` useful.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "id_delta", "description": "The kernel definitional equality test (t =?= s) has special support for id_delta applications.\nIt implements the following rules\n\n   1)   (id_delta t) =?= t\n   2)   t =?= (id_delta t)\n   3)   (id_delta t) =?= s  IF (unfold_of t) =?= s\n   4)   t =?= id_delta s    IF t =?= (unfold_of s)\n\nThis is mechanism for controlling the delta reduction (aka unfolding) used in the kernel.\n\nWe use id_delta applications to address performance problems when type checking\nlemmas generated by the equation compiler.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unification_constraint", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unification_constraint.\u03b1", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unification_constraint.lhs", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unification_constraint.rhs", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "nat_add_zero", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "list.has_sizeof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_pow", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_pow.pow", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "sizeof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_div", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_div.div", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_mul", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_mul.mul", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "punit.has_sizeof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "std.priority.default", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "bin_tree", "description": "Auxiliary datatype for #[ ... ] notation.\n   #[1, 2, 3, 4] is notation for\n\n   bin_tree.node\n     (bin_tree.node (bin_tree.leaf 1) (bin_tree.leaf 2))\n     (bin_tree.node (bin_tree.leaf 3) (bin_tree.leaf 4))\n\n   We use this notation to input long sequences without exhausting the system stack space.\n   Later, we define a coercion from `bin_tree` into `list`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "bin_tree.empty", "description": "Auxiliary datatype for #[ ... ] notation.\n   #[1, 2, 3, 4] is notation for\n\n   bin_tree.node\n     (bin_tree.node (bin_tree.leaf 1) (bin_tree.leaf 2))\n     (bin_tree.node (bin_tree.leaf 3) (bin_tree.leaf 4))\n\n   We use this notation to input long sequences without exhausting the system stack space.\n   Later, we define a coercion from `bin_tree` into `list`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "bin_tree.leaf", "description": "Auxiliary datatype for #[ ... ] notation.\n   #[1, 2, 3, 4] is notation for\n\n   bin_tree.node\n     (bin_tree.node (bin_tree.leaf 1) (bin_tree.leaf 2))\n     (bin_tree.node (bin_tree.leaf 3) (bin_tree.leaf 4))\n\n   We use this notation to input long sequences without exhausting the system stack space.\n   Later, we define a coercion from `bin_tree` into `list`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "bin_tree.node", "description": "Auxiliary datatype for #[ ... ] notation.\n   #[1, 2, 3, 4] is notation for\n\n   bin_tree.node\n     (bin_tree.node (bin_tree.leaf 1) (bin_tree.leaf 2))\n     (bin_tree.node (bin_tree.leaf 3) (bin_tree.leaf 4))\n\n   We use this notation to input long sequences without exhausting the system stack space.\n   Later, we define a coercion from `bin_tree` into `list`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_dvd", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_dvd.dvd", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_sep", "description": "Type class used to implement the notation { a \u2208 c | p a }", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_sep.sep", "description": "Type class used to implement the notation { a \u2208 c | p a }", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_union", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_union.union", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_sdiff", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_sdiff.sdiff", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_subset", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_subset.subset", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "nat.has_zero", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "sigma.has_sizeof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_append", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_append.append", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "eq_of_heq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_singleton", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_singleton.singleton", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_add", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_add.add", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "nat.has_add", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_singleton", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_singleton.insert_emptyc_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "bool", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "bool.ff", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "bool.tt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "std.prec.arrow", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "ge", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_neg", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_neg.neg", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_insert", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_insert.insert", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "std.prec.max", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "or", "description": "Logical or.\n\n`or P Q`, with notation `P \u2228 Q`, is the proposition which is true if and only if `P` or `Q` is\ntrue.\n\nTo prove a goal `\u22a2 P \u2228 Q`, if you know which alternative you want to prove,\nyou can use the tactics `left` (which gives the goal `\u22a2 P`)\nor `right` (which gives the goal `\u22a2 Q`).\n\nGiven a hypothesis `h : P \u2228 Q` and goal `\u22a2 R`,\nthe tactic `cases h` will give you two copies of the goal `\u22a2 R`,\nwith the hypothesis `h : P` in the first, and the hypothesis `h : Q` in the second.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "or.inl", "description": "Logical or.\n\n`or P Q`, with notation `P \u2228 Q`, is the proposition which is true if and only if `P` or `Q` is\ntrue.\n\nTo prove a goal `\u22a2 P \u2228 Q`, if you know which alternative you want to prove,\nyou can use the tactics `left` (which gives the goal `\u22a2 P`)\nor `right` (which gives the goal `\u22a2 Q`).\n\nGiven a hypothesis `h : P \u2228 Q` and goal `\u22a2 R`,\nthe tactic `cases h` will give you two copies of the goal `\u22a2 R`,\nwith the hypothesis `h : P` in the first, and the hypothesis `h : Q` in the second.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "or.inr", "description": "Logical or.\n\n`or P Q`, with notation `P \u2228 Q`, is the proposition which is true if and only if `P` or `Q` is\ntrue.\n\nTo prove a goal `\u22a2 P \u2228 Q`, if you know which alternative you want to prove,\nyou can use the tactics `left` (which gives the goal `\u22a2 P`)\nor `right` (which gives the goal `\u22a2 Q`).\n\nGiven a hypothesis `h : P \u2228 Q` and goal `\u22a2 R`,\nthe tactic `cases h` will give you two copies of the goal `\u22a2 R`,\nwith the hypothesis `h : P` in the first, and the hypothesis `h : Q` in the second.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "eq.subst", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "decidable_rel", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "gt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "ssuperset", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma.fst", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma.snd", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "nat.has_sizeof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "prod.has_sizeof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "bit0", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "std.prec.max_plus", "description": "This def is \"max + 10\". It can be used e.g. for postfix operations that should\nbe stronger than application.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unit", "description": "An abbreviation for `punit.{0}`, its most common instantiation.\n   This type should be preferred over `punit` where possible to avoid\n   unnecessary universe parameters.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "or.intro_left", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_inter", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_inter.inter", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "subtype.has_sizeof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "out_param", "description": "Gadget for marking output parameters in type classes.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_le", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_le.le", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "heq.rfl", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "and.elim_left", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "infer_instance", "description": "Like `by apply_instance`, but not dependent on the tactic framework.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "nat", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "nat.zero", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "nat.succ", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "rfl", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psum", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psum.inl", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psum.inr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_sizeof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_sizeof.sizeof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "false", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_mod", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_mod.mod", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "pprod", "description": "Similar to `prod`, but \u03b1 and \u03b2 can be propositions.\n  We use this type internally to automatically generate the brec_on recursor.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "pprod.fst", "description": "Similar to `prod`, but \u03b1 and \u03b2 can be propositions.\n  We use this type internally to automatically generate the brec_on recursor.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "pprod.snd", "description": "Similar to `prod`, but \u03b1 and \u03b2 can be propositions.\n  We use this type internally to automatically generate the brec_on recursor.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "nat.has_one", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_zero", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_zero.zero", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "sigma", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "sigma.fst", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "sigma.snd", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psum.has_sizeof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "punit", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "not", "description": "Logical not.\n\n`not P`, with notation `\u00ac P`, is the `Prop` which is true if and only if `P` is false. It is\ninternally represented as `P \u2192 false`, so one way to prove a goal `\u22a2 \u00ac P` is to use `intro h`,\nwhich gives you a new hypothesis `h : P` and the goal `\u22a2 false`.\n\nA hypothesis `h : \u00ac P` can be used in term mode as a function, so if `w : P` then `h w : false`.\n\nRelated mathlib tactic: `contrapose`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "nat.prio", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "opt_param", "description": "Gadget for optional parameter support.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "list", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "list.nil", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "list.cons", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_emptyc", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_emptyc.emptyc", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_one", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_one.one", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "std.priority.max", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "nat.add", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "decidable", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "decidable.is_false", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "decidable.is_true", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "and", "description": "Logical and.\n\n`and P Q`, with notation `P \u2227 Q`, is the `Prop` which is true precisely when `P` and `Q` are\nboth true.\n\nTo prove a goal `\u22a2 P \u2227 Q`, you can use the tactic `split`,\nwhich gives two separate goals `\u22a2 P` and `\u22a2 Q`.\n\nGiven a hypothesis `h : P \u2227 Q`, you can use the tactic `cases h with hP hQ`\nto obtain two new hypotheses `hP : P` and `hQ : Q`. See also the `obtain` or `rcases` tactics in\nmathlib.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "and.left", "description": "Logical and.\n\n`and P Q`, with notation `P \u2227 Q`, is the `Prop` which is true precisely when `P` and `Q` are\nboth true.\n\nTo prove a goal `\u22a2 P \u2227 Q`, you can use the tactic `split`,\nwhich gives two separate goals `\u22a2 P` and `\u22a2 Q`.\n\nGiven a hypothesis `h : P \u2227 Q`, you can use the tactic `cases h with hP hQ`\nto obtain two new hypotheses `hP : P` and `hQ : Q`. See also the `obtain` or `rcases` tactics in\nmathlib.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "and.right", "description": "Logical and.\n\n`and P Q`, with notation `P \u2227 Q`, is the `Prop` which is true precisely when `P` and `Q` are\nboth true.\n\nTo prove a goal `\u22a2 P \u2227 Q`, you can use the tactic `split`,\nwhich gives two separate goals `\u22a2 P` and `\u22a2 Q`.\n\nGiven a hypothesis `h : P \u2227 Q`, you can use the tactic `cases h with hP hQ`\nto obtain two new hypotheses `hP : P` and `hQ : Q`. See also the `obtain` or `rcases` tactics in\nmathlib.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "default_has_sizeof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "sum.has_sizeof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "true", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_inv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_inv.inv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_lt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_lt.lt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "bit1", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "or.intro_right", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "empty", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unit.star", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "prod", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "prod.fst", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "prod.snd", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option.none", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option.some", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "eq.trans", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_mem", "description": "Type class for set-like membership", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_mem.mem", "description": "Type class for set-like membership", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_equiv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_equiv.equiv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma.has_sizeof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "eq.refl", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_sub", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_sub.sub", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "bool.has_sizeof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "sum", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "sum.inl", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "sum.inr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unification_hint", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unification_hint.pattern", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unification_hint.constraints", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.right_unitor_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.associator_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_left_twice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso_whisker_right_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_right_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_right_twice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.left_unitor_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_left_id'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso_whisker_right", "description": "If `\u03b1 : G \u2245 H` then\n`iso_whisker_right \u03b1 F : (G \u22d9 F) \u2245 (H \u22d9 F)` has components `F.map_iso (\u03b1.app X)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_right_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.left_unitor_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_right", "description": "If `\u03b1 : G \u27f6 H` then\n`whisker_right \u03b1 F : (G \u22d9 F) \u27f6 (G \u22d9 F)` has components `F.map (\u03b1.app X)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.left_unitor", "description": "The left unitor, a natural isomorphism `((\ud835\udfed _) \u22d9 F) \u2245 F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_right_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_left_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso_whisker_right_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whiskering_right_obj_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso_whisker_left", "description": "If `\u03b1 : G \u2245 H` is a natural isomorphism then\n`iso_whisker_left F \u03b1 : (F \u22d9 G) \u2245 (F \u22d9 H)` has components `\u03b1.app (F.obj X)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whiskering_left_obj_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.associator", "description": "The associator for functors, a natural isomorphism `((F \u22d9 G) \u22d9 H) \u2245 (F \u22d9 (G \u22d9 H))`.\n\n(In fact, `iso.refl _` will work here, but it tends to make Lean slow later,\nand it's usually best to insert explicit associators.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.right_unitor", "description": "The right unitor, a natural isomorphism `(F \u22d9 (\ud835\udfed B)) \u2245 F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.right_unitor_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.associator_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso_whisker_left_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_right_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso_whisker_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whiskering_left_obj_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso_whisker_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whiskering_left_map_app_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whiskering_left", "description": "Left-composition gives a functor `(C \u2964 D) \u2964 ((D \u2964 E) \u2964 (C \u2964 E))`.\n\n`(whiskering_left.obj F).obj G` is `F \u22d9 G`, and\n`(whiskering_left.obj F).map \u03b1` is `whisker_left F \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso_whisker_left_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.pentagon", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whiskering_right", "description": "Right-composition gives a functor `(D \u2964 E) \u2964 ((C \u2964 D) \u2964 (C \u2964 E))`.\n\n`(whiskering_right.obj H).obj F` is `F \u22d9 H`, and\n`(whiskering_right.obj H).map \u03b1` is `whisker_right \u03b1 H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_left_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_left_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.faithful_whiskering_right_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whiskering_right_obj_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_left", "description": "If `\u03b1 : G \u27f6 H` then\n`whisker_left F \u03b1 : (F \u22d9 G) \u27f6 (F \u22d9 H)` has components `\u03b1.app (F.obj X)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_right_id'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.triangle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whiskering_right_map_app_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.concrete.multiequalizer_equiv", "description": " The equivalence between the noncomputable multiequalizer and\nand the concrete multiequalizer.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.concrete.multiequalizer_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.concrete.to_product_injectiv