mul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_left_cancel_monoid", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is left-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_left_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_left_cancel_monoid.add", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is left-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_left_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_left_cancel_monoid.add_assoc", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is left-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_left_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_left_cancel_monoid.add_left_cancel", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is left-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_left_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_left_cancel_monoid.zero", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is left-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_left_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_left_cancel_monoid.zero_add", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is left-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_left_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_left_cancel_monoid.add_zero", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is left-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_left_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_left_cancel_monoid.nsmul", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is left-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_left_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_left_cancel_monoid.nsmul_zero'", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is left-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_left_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_left_cancel_monoid.nsmul_succ'", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is left-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_left_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "neg_eq_of_add_eq_zero_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_left_cancel_mul", "description": "A mixin for left cancellative multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_left_cancel_mul.mul_left_cancel", "description": "A mixin for left cancellative multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_left_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_vadd", "description": "Type class for the `+\u1d65` notation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_vadd.vadd", "description": "Type class for the `+\u1d65` notation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "comm_monoid.to_comm_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_monoid.to_add_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_group.to_add_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_zero_class.to_has_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inv_eq_of_mul_eq_one_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zsmul_rec", "description": " The fundamental scalar multiplication in an additive group. `zsmul_rec n a = a+a+...+a` n\ntimes, for integer `n`. Use instead `n \u2022 a`, which has better definitional behavior.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semigroup.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_left_cancel_semigroup.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtraction_comm_monoid.to_subtraction_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid.has_smul_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inv_one_class", "description": "Typeclass for expressing that `1\u207b\u00b9 = 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inv_one_class.one", "description": "Typeclass for expressing that `1\u207b\u00b9 = 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inv_one_class.inv", "description": "Typeclass for expressing that `1\u207b\u00b9 = 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inv_one_class.inv_one", "description": "Typeclass for expressing that `1\u207b\u00b9 = 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_involutive_inv", "description": "Auxiliary typeclass for types with an involutive `has_inv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_involutive_inv.inv", "description": "Auxiliary typeclass for types with an involutive `has_inv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_involutive_inv.inv_inv", "description": "Auxiliary typeclass for types with an involutive `has_inv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_zsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_left_cancel_add", "description": "A mixin for left cancellative addition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_left_cancel_add.add_left_cancel", "description": "A mixin for left cancellative addition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_semigroup.to_is_associative", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_group.to_division_add_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_right_cancel_semigroup.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtraction_monoid.to_has_involutive_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_semigroup.to_add_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semigroup.to_has_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_cancel_mul", "description": "A mixin for cancellative multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_cancel_mul.mul_left_cancel", "description": "A mixin for cancellative multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_cancel_mul.mul_right_cancel", "description": "A mixin for cancellative multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "neg_add_cancel_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "left_cancel_monoid.to_left_cancel_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monoid.has_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_right_cancel_add", "description": "A mixin for right cancellative addition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_right_cancel_add.add_right_cancel", "description": "A mixin for right cancellative addition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_semigroup.is_left_cancel_add.to_is_cancel_add", "description": "Any `add_comm_semigroup G`\nthat satisfies `is_left_cancel_add G` also satisfies `is_cancel_add G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_left_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "comm_semigroup.is_right_cancel_mul.to_is_cancel_mul", "description": " Any `comm_semigroup G` that satisfies `is_right_cancel_mul G` also satisfies\n`is_cancel_mul G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtraction_comm_monoid.to_add_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "neg_zero_class", "description": "Typeclass for expressing that `-0 = 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "neg_zero_class.zero", "description": "Typeclass for expressing that `-0 = 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "neg_zero_class.neg", "description": "Typeclass for expressing that `-0 = 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "neg_zero_class.neg_zero", "description": "Typeclass for expressing that `-0 = 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_one_class.to_is_right_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "comm_semigroup.is_left_cancel_mul.to_is_right_cancel_mul", "description": " Any `comm_semigroup G` that satisfies `is_left_cancel_mul G` also satisfies\n`is_right_cancel_mul G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "div_inv_monoid.to_has_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "npow_eq_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "right_cancel_monoid.to_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zpow_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_semigroup.to_has_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "div_inv_monoid", "description": " A `div_inv_monoid` is a `monoid` with operations `/` and `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying\n`div_eq_mul_inv : \u2200 a b, a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`.\n\nThis deduplicates the name `div_eq_mul_inv`.\nThe default for `div` is such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.\n\nAdding `div` as a field rather than defining `a / b := a * b\u207b\u00b9` allows us to\navoid certain classes of unification failures, for example:\nLet `foo X` be a type with a `\u2200 X, has_div (foo X)` instance but no\n`\u2200 X, has_inv (foo X)`, e.g. when `foo X` is a `euclidean_domain`. Suppose we\nalso have an instance `\u2200 X [cromulent X], group_with_zero (foo X)`. Then the\n`(/)` coming from `group_with_zero_has_div` cannot be definitionally equal to\nthe `(/)` coming from `foo.has_div`.\n\nIn the same way, adding a `zpow` field makes it possible to avoid definitional failures\nin diamonds. See the definition of `monoid` and Note [forgetful inheritance] for more\nexplanations on this.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "div_inv_monoid.mul", "description": " A `div_inv_monoid` is a `monoid` with operations `/` and `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying\n`div_eq_mul_inv : \u2200 a b, a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`.\n\nThis deduplicates the name `div_eq_mul_inv`.\nThe default for `div` is such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.\n\nAdding `div` as a field rather than defining `a / b := a * b\u207b\u00b9` allows us to\navoid certain classes of unification failures, for example:\nLet `foo X` be a type with a `\u2200 X, has_div (foo X)` instance but no\n`\u2200 X, has_inv (foo X)`, e.g. when `foo X` is a `euclidean_domain`. Suppose we\nalso have an instance `\u2200 X [cromulent X], group_with_zero (foo X)`. Then the\n`(/)` coming from `group_with_zero_has_div` cannot be definitionally equal to\nthe `(/)` coming from `foo.has_div`.\n\nIn the same way, adding a `zpow` field makes it possible to avoid definitional failures\nin diamonds. See the definition of `monoid` and Note [forgetful inheritance] for more\nexplanations on this.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "div_inv_monoid.mul_assoc", "description": " A `div_inv_monoid` is a `monoid` with operations `/` and `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying\n`div_eq_mul_inv : \u2200 a b, a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`.\n\nThis deduplicates the name `div_eq_mul_inv`.\nThe default for `div` is such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.\n\nAdding `div` as a field rather than defining `a / b := a * b\u207b\u00b9` allows us to\navoid certain classes of unification failures, for example:\nLet `foo X` be a type with a `\u2200 X, has_div (foo X)` instance but no\n`\u2200 X, has_inv (foo X)`, e.g. when `foo X` is a `euclidean_domain`. Suppose we\nalso have an instance `\u2200 X [cromulent X], group_with_zero (foo X)`. Then the\n`(/)` coming from `group_with_zero_has_div` cannot be definitionally equal to\nthe `(/)` coming from `foo.has_div`.\n\nIn the same way, adding a `zpow` field makes it possible to avoid definitional failures\nin diamonds. See the definition of `monoid` and Note [forgetful inheritance] for more\nexplanations on this.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "div_inv_monoid.one", "description": " A `div_inv_monoid` is a `monoid` with operations `/` and `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying\n`div_eq_mul_inv : \u2200 a b, a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`.\n\nThis deduplicates the name `div_eq_mul_inv`.\nThe default for `div` is such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.\n\nAdding `div` as a field rather than defining `a / b := a * b\u207b\u00b9` allows us to\navoid certain classes of unification failures, for example:\nLet `foo X` be a type with a `\u2200 X, has_div (foo X)` instance but no\n`\u2200 X, has_inv (foo X)`, e.g. when `foo X` is a `euclidean_domain`. Suppose we\nalso have an instance `\u2200 X [cromulent X], group_with_zero (foo X)`. Then the\n`(/)` coming from `group_with_zero_has_div` cannot be definitionally equal to\nthe `(/)` coming from `foo.has_div`.\n\nIn the same way, adding a `zpow` field makes it possible to avoid definitional failures\nin diamonds. See the definition of `monoid` and Note [forgetful inheritance] for more\nexplanations on this.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "div_inv_monoid.one_mul", "description": " A `div_inv_monoid` is a `monoid` with operations `/` and `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying\n`div_eq_mul_inv : \u2200 a b, a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`.\n\nThis deduplicates the name `div_eq_mul_inv`.\nThe default for `div` is such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.\n\nAdding `div` as a field rather than defining `a / b := a * b\u207b\u00b9` allows us to\navoid certain classes of unification failures, for example:\nLet `foo X` be a type with a `\u2200 X, has_div (foo X)` instance but no\n`\u2200 X, has_inv (foo X)`, e.g. when `foo X` is a `euclidean_domain`. Suppose we\nalso have an instance `\u2200 X [cromulent X], group_with_zero (foo X)`. Then the\n`(/)` coming from `group_with_zero_has_div` cannot be definitionally equal to\nthe `(/)` coming from `foo.has_div`.\n\nIn the same way, adding a `zpow` field makes it possible to avoid definitional failures\nin diamonds. See the definition of `monoid` and Note [forgetful inheritance] for more\nexplanations on this.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "div_inv_monoid.mul_one", "description": " A `div_inv_monoid` is a `monoid` with operations `/` and `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying\n`div_eq_mul_inv : \u2200 a b, a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`.\n\nThis deduplicates the name `div_eq_mul_inv`.\nThe default for `div` is such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.\n\nAdding `div` as a field rather than defining `a / b := a * b\u207b\u00b9` allows us to\navoid certain classes of unification failures, for example:\nLet `foo X` be a type with a `\u2200 X, has_div (foo X)` instance but no\n`\u2200 X, has_inv (foo X)`, e.g. when `foo X` is a `euclidean_domain`. Suppose we\nalso have an instance `\u2200 X [cromulent X], group_with_zero (foo X)`. Then the\n`(/)` coming from `group_with_zero_has_div` cannot be definitionally equal to\nthe `(/)` coming from `foo.has_div`.\n\nIn the same way, adding a `zpow` field makes it possible to avoid definitional failures\nin diamonds. See the definition of `monoid` and Note [forgetful inheritance] for more\nexplanations on this.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "div_inv_monoid.npow", "description": " A `div_inv_monoid` is a `monoid` with operations `/` and `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying\n`div_eq_mul_inv : \u2200 a b, a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`.\n\nThis deduplicates the name `div_eq_mul_inv`.\nThe default for `div` is such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.\n\nAdding `div` as a field rather than defining `a / b := a * b\u207b\u00b9` allows us to\navoid certain classes of unification failures, for example:\nLet `foo X` be a type with a `\u2200 X, has_div (foo X)` instance but no\n`\u2200 X, has_inv (foo X)`, e.g. when `foo X` is a `euclidean_domain`. Suppose we\nalso have an instance `\u2200 X [cromulent X], group_with_zero (foo X)`. Then the\n`(/)` coming from `group_with_zero_has_div` cannot be definitionally equal to\nthe `(/)` coming from `foo.has_div`.\n\nIn the same way, adding a `zpow` field makes it possible to avoid definitional failures\nin diamonds. See the definition of `monoid` and Note [forgetful inheritance] for more\nexplanations on this.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "div_inv_monoid.npow_zero'", "description": " A `div_inv_monoid` is a `monoid` with operations `/` and `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying\n`div_eq_mul_inv : \u2200 a b, a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`.\n\nThis deduplicates the name `div_eq_mul_inv`.\nThe default for `div` is such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.\n\nAdding `div` as a field rather than defining `a / b := a * b\u207b\u00b9` allows us to\navoid certain classes of unification failures, for example:\nLet `foo X` be a type with a `\u2200 X, has_div (foo X)` instance but no\n`\u2200 X, has_inv (foo X)`, e.g. when `foo X` is a `euclidean_domain`. Suppose we\nalso have an instance `\u2200 X [cromulent X], group_with_zero (foo X)`. Then the\n`(/)` coming from `group_with_zero_has_div` cannot be definitionally equal to\nthe `(/)` coming from `foo.has_div`.\n\nIn the same way, adding a `zpow` field makes it possible to avoid definitional failures\nin diamonds. See the definition of `monoid` and Note [forgetful inheritance] for more\nexplanations on this.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "div_inv_monoid.npow_succ'", "description": " A `div_inv_monoid` is a `monoid` with operations `/` and `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying\n`div_eq_mul_inv : \u2200 a b, a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`.\n\nThis deduplicates the name `div_eq_mul_inv`.\nThe default for `div` is such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.\n\nAdding `div` as a field rather than defining `a / b := a * b\u207b\u00b9` allows us to\navoid certain classes of unification failures, for example:\nLet `foo X` be a type with a `\u2200 X, has_div (foo X)` instance but no\n`\u2200 X, has_inv (foo 