* b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group.mul_one", "description": " A `group` is a `monoid` with an operation `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying `a\u207b\u00b9 * a = 1`.\n\nThere is also a division operation `/` such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`,\nwith a default so that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group.npow", "description": " A `group` is a `monoid` with an operation `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying `a\u207b\u00b9 * a = 1`.\n\nThere is also a division operation `/` such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`,\nwith a default so that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group.npow_zero'", "description": " A `group` is a `monoid` with an operation `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying `a\u207b\u00b9 * a = 1`.\n\nThere is also a division operation `/` such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`,\nwith a default so that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group.npow_succ'", "description": " A `group` is a `monoid` with an operation `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying `a\u207b\u00b9 * a = 1`.\n\nThere is also a division operation `/` such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`,\nwith a default so that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group.inv", "description": " A `group` is a `monoid` with an operation `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying `a\u207b\u00b9 * a = 1`.\n\nThere is also a division operation `/` such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`,\nwith a default so that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group.div", "description": " A `group` is a `monoid` with an operation `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying `a\u207b\u00b9 * a = 1`.\n\nThere is also a division operation `/` such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`,\nwith a default so that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group.div_eq_mul_inv", "description": " A `group` is a `monoid` with an operation `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying `a\u207b\u00b9 * a = 1`.\n\nThere is also a division operation `/` such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`,\nwith a default so that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group.zpow", "description": " A `group` is a `monoid` with an operation `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying `a\u207b\u00b9 * a = 1`.\n\nThere is also a division operation `/` such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`,\nwith a default so that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group.zpow_zero'", "description": " A `group` is a `monoid` with an operation `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying `a\u207b\u00b9 * a = 1`.\n\nThere is also a division operation `/` such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`,\nwith a default so that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group.zpow_succ'", "description": " A `group` is a `monoid` with an operation `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying `a\u207b\u00b9 * a = 1`.\n\nThere is also a division operation `/` such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`,\nwith a default so that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group.zpow_neg'", "description": " A `group` is a `monoid` with an operation `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying `a\u207b\u00b9 * a = 1`.\n\nThere is also a division operation `/` such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`,\nwith a default so that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group.mul_left_inv", "description": " A `group` is a `monoid` with an operation `\u207b\u00b9` satisfying `a\u207b\u00b9 * a = 1`.\n\nThere is also a division operation `/` such that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9`,\nwith a default so that `a / b = a * b\u207b\u00b9` holds by definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_neg_cancel_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sub_neg_monoid.to_add_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_right_cancel_mul", "description": "A mixin for right cancellative multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_right_cancel_mul.mul_right_cancel", "description": "A mixin for right cancellative multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "division_comm_monoid.to_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_ne_mul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_nsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_right_cancel_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cancel_comm_monoid", "description": "Commutative version of `cancel_monoid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cancel_comm_monoid.mul", "description": "Commutative version of `cancel_monoid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cancel_comm_monoid.mul_assoc", "description": "Commutative version of `cancel_monoid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cancel_comm_monoid.mul_left_cancel", "description": "Commutative version of `cancel_monoid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cancel_comm_monoid.one", "description": "Commutative version of `cancel_monoid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cancel_comm_monoid.one_mul", "description": "Commutative version of `cancel_monoid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cancel_comm_monoid.mul_one", "description": "Commutative version of `cancel_monoid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cancel_comm_monoid.npow", "description": "Commutative version of `cancel_monoid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cancel_comm_monoid.npow_zero'", "description": "Commutative version of `cancel_monoid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cancel_comm_monoid.npow_succ'", "description": "Commutative version of `cancel_monoid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cancel_comm_monoid.mul_comm", "description": "Commutative version of `cancel_monoid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "comm_group.to_division_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_cancel_add.to_is_left_cancel_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sub_neg_monoid", "description": " A `sub_neg_monoid` is an `add_monoid` with unary `-` and binary `-` operations\nsatisfying `sub_eq_add_neg : \u2200 a b, a - b = a + -b`.\n\nThe default for `sub` is such that `a - b = a + -b` holds by definition.\n\nAdding `sub` as a field rather than defining `a - b := a + -b` allows us to\navoid certain classes of unification failures, for example:\nLet `foo X` be a type with a `\u2200 X, has_sub (foo X)` instance but no\n`\u2200 X, has_neg (foo X)`. Suppose we also have an instance\n`\u2200 X [cromulent X], add_group (foo X)`. Then the `(-)` coming from\n`add_group.has_sub` cannot be definitionally equal to the `(-)` coming from\n`foo.has_sub`.\n\nIn the same way, adding a `zsmul` field makes it possible to avoid definitional failures\nin diamonds. See the definition of `add_monoid` and Note [forgetful inheritance] for more\nexplanations on this.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sub_neg_monoid.add", "description": " A `sub_neg_monoid` is an `add_monoid` with unary `-` and binary `-` operations\nsatisfying `sub_eq_add_neg : \u2200 a b, a - b = a + -b`.\n\nThe default for `sub` is such that `a - b = a + -b` holds by definition.\n\nAdding `sub` as a field rather than defining `a - b := a + -b` allows us to\navoid certain classes of unification failures, for example:\nLet `foo X` be a type with a `\u2200 X, has_sub (foo X)` instance but no\n`\u2200 X, has_neg (foo X)`. Suppose we also have an instance\n`\u2200 X [cromulent X], add_group (foo X)`. Then the `(-)` coming from\n`add_group.has_sub` cannot be definitionally equal to the `(-)` coming from\n`foo.has_sub`.\n\nIn the same way, adding a `zsmul` field makes it possible to avoid definitional failures\nin diamonds. See the definition of `add_monoid` and Note [forgetful inheritance] for more\nexplanations on this.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sub_neg_monoid.add_assoc", "description": " A `sub_neg_monoid` is an `add_monoid` with unary `-` and binary `-` operations\nsatisfying `sub_eq_add_neg : \u2200 a b, a - b = a + -b`.\n\nThe default for `sub` is such that `a - b = a + -b` holds by definition.\n\nAdding `sub` as a field rather than defining `a - b := a + -b` allows us to\navoid certain classes of unification failures, for example:\nLet `foo X` be a type with a `\u2200 X, has_sub (foo X)` instance but no\n`\u2200 X, has_neg (foo X)`. Suppose we also have an instance\n`\u2200 X [cromulent X], add_group (foo X)`. Then the `(-)` coming from\n`add_group.has_sub` cannot be definitionally equal to the `(-)` coming from\n`foo.has_sub`.\n\nIn the same way, adding a `zsmul` field makes it possible to avoid definitional failures\nin diamonds. See the definition of `add_monoid` and Note [forgetful inheritance] for more\nexplanations on this.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sub_neg_monoid.zero", "description": " A `sub_neg_monoid` is an `add_monoid` with unary `-` and binary `-` operations\nsatisfying `sub_eq_add_neg : \u2200 a b, a - b = a + -b`.\n\nThe default for `sub` is such that `a - b = a + -b` holds by definition.\n\nAdding `sub` as a field rather than defining `a - b := a + -b` allows us to\navoid certain classes of unification failures, for example:\nLet `foo X` be a type with a `\u2200 X, has_sub (foo X)` instance but no\n`\u2200 X, has_neg (foo X)`. Suppose we also have an instance\n`\u2200 X [cromulent X], add_group (foo X)`. Then the `(-)` coming from\n`add_group.has_sub` cannot be definitionally equal to the `(-)` coming from\n`foo.has_sub`.\n\nIn the same way, adding a `zsmul` field makes it possible to avoid definitional failures\nin diamonds. See the definition of `add_monoid` and Note [forgetful inheritance] for more\nexplanations on this.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sub_neg_monoid.zero_add", "description": " A `sub_neg_monoid` is an `add_monoid` with unary `-` and binary `-` operations\nsatisfying `sub_eq_add_neg : \u2200 a b, a - b = a + -b`.\n\nThe default for `sub` is such that `a - b = a + -b` holds by definition.\n\nAdding `sub` as a field rather than defining `a - b := a + -b` allows us to\navoid certain classes of unification failures, for example:\nLet `foo X` be a type with a `\u2200 X, has_sub (foo X)` instance but no\n`\u2200 X, has_neg (foo X)`. Suppose we also have an instance\n`\u2200 X [cromulent X], add_group (foo X)`. Then the `(-)` coming from\n`add_group.has_sub` cannot be definitionally equal to the `(-)` coming from\n`foo.has_sub`.\n\nIn the same way, adding a `zsmul` field makes it possible to avoid definitional failures\nin diamonds. See the definition of `add_monoid` and Note [forgetful inheritance] for more\nexplanations on this.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sub_neg_monoid.add_zero", "description": " A `sub_neg_monoid` is an `add_monoid` with unary `-` and binary `-` operations\nsatisfying `sub_eq_add_neg : \u2200 a b, a - b = a + -b`.\n\nThe default for `sub` is such that `a - b = a + -b` holds by definition.\n\nAdding `sub` as a field rather than defining `a - b := a + -b` allows us to\navoid certain classes of unification failures, for example:\nLet `foo X` be a type with a `\u2200 X, has_sub (foo X)` instance but no\n`\u2200 X, has_neg (foo X)`. Suppose we also have an instance\n`\u2200 X [cromulent X], add_group (foo X)`. Then the `(-)` coming from\n`add_group.has_sub` cannot be definitionally equal to the `(-)` coming from\n`foo.has_sub`.\n\nIn the same way, adding a `zsmul` field makes it possible to avoid definitional failures\nin diamonds. See the definition of `add_monoid` and Note [forgetful inheritance] for more\nexplanations on this.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sub_neg_monoid.nsmul", "description": " A `sub_neg_monoid` is an `add_monoid` with unary `-` and binary `-` operations\nsatisfying `sub_eq_add_neg : \u2200 a b, a - b = a + -b`.\n\nThe default for `sub` is such that `a - b = a + -b` holds by definition.\n\nAdding `sub` as a field rather than defining `a - b := a + -b` allows us to\navoid certain classes of unification failures, for example:\nLet `foo X` be a type with a `\u2200 X, has_sub (foo X)` instance but no\n`\u2200 X, has_neg (foo X)`. Suppose we also have an instance\n`