ion": " A `subtraction_monoid` is a `sub_neg_monoid` with involutive negation and such that\n`-(a + b) = -b + -a` and `a + b = 0 \u2192 -a = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtraction_monoid.zero", "description": " A `subtraction_monoid` is a `sub_neg_monoid` with involutive negation and such that\n`-(a + b) = -b + -a` and `a + b = 0 \u2192 -a = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtraction_monoid.zero_add", "description": " A `subtraction_monoid` is a `sub_neg_monoid` with involutive negation and such that\n`-(a + b) = -b + -a` and `a + b = 0 \u2192 -a = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtraction_monoid.add_zero", "description": " A `subtraction_monoid` is a `sub_neg_monoid` with involutive negation and such that\n`-(a + b) = -b + -a` and `a + b = 0 \u2192 -a = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtraction_monoid.nsmul", "description": " A `subtraction_monoid` is a `sub_neg_monoid` with involutive negation and such that\n`-(a + b) = -b + -a` and `a + b = 0 \u2192 -a = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtraction_monoid.nsmul_zero'", "description": " A `subtraction_monoid` is a `sub_neg_monoid` with involutive negation and such that\n`-(a + b) = -b + -a` and `a + b = 0 \u2192 -a = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtraction_monoid.nsmul_succ'", "description": " A `subtraction_monoid` is a `sub_neg_monoid` with involutive negation and such that\n`-(a + b) = -b + -a` and `a + b = 0 \u2192 -a = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtraction_monoid.neg", "description": " A `subtraction_monoid` is a `sub_neg_monoid` with involutive negation and such that\n`-(a + b) = -b + -a` and `a + b = 0 \u2192 -a = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtraction_monoid.sub", "description": " A `subtraction_monoid` is a `sub_neg_monoid` with involutive negation and such that\n`-(a + b) = -b + -a` and `a + b = 0 \u2192 -a = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtraction_monoid.sub_eq_add_neg", "description": " A `subtraction_monoid` is a `sub_neg_monoid` with involutive negation and such that\n`-(a + b) = -b + -a` and `a + b = 0 \u2192 -a = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtraction_monoid.zsmul", "description": " A `subtraction_monoid` is a `sub_neg_monoid` with involutive negation and such that\n`-(a + b) = -b + -a` and `a + b = 0 \u2192 -a = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtraction_monoid.zsmul_zero'", "description": " A `subtraction_monoid` is a `sub_neg_monoid` with involutive negation and such that\n`-(a + b) = -b + -a` and `a + b = 0 \u2192 -a = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtraction_monoid.zsmul_succ'", "description": " A `subtraction_monoid` is a `sub_neg_monoid` with involutive negation and such that\n`-(a + b) = -b + -a` and `a + b = 0 \u2192 -a = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtraction_monoid.zsmul_neg'", "description": " A `subtraction_monoid` is a `sub_neg_monoid` with involutive negation and such that\n`-(a + b) = -b + -a` and `a + b = 0 \u2192 -a = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtraction_monoid.neg_neg", "description": " A `subtraction_monoid` is a `sub_neg_monoid` with involutive negation and such that\n`-(a + b) = -b + -a` and `a + b = 0 \u2192 -a = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtraction_monoid.neg_add_rev", "description": " A `subtraction_monoid` is a `sub_neg_monoid` with involutive negation and such that\n`-(a + b) = -b + -a` and `a + b = 0 \u2192 -a = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtraction_monoid.neg_eq_of_add", "description": " A `subtraction_monoid` is a `sub_neg_monoid` with involutive negation and such that\n`-(a + b) = -b + -a` and `a + b = 0 \u2192 -a = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "right_cancel_monoid.to_right_cancel_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cancel_comm_monoid.to_left_cancel_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nsmul_eq_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "comm_semigroup.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "left_neg_eq_right_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_right_cancel_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_semigroup.to_is_commutative", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group.to_div_inv_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "left_cancel_semigroup.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "div_inv_monoid.to_has_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "neg_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_right_cancel_monoid", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is right-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_right_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_right_cancel_monoid.add", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is right-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_right_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_right_cancel_monoid.add_assoc", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is right-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_right_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_right_cancel_monoid.add_right_cancel", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is right-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_right_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_right_cancel_monoid.zero", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is right-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_right_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_right_cancel_monoid.zero_add", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is right-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_right_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_right_cancel_monoid.add_zero", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is right-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_right_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_right_cancel_monoid.nsmul", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is right-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_right_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_right_cancel_monoid.nsmul_zero'", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is right-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_right_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_right_cancel_monoid.nsmul_succ'", "description": " An additive monoid in which addition is right-cancellative.\nMain examples are `\u2115` and groups. This is the right typeclass for many sum lemmas, as having a zero\nis useful to define the sum over the empty set, so `add_right_cancel_semigroup` is not enough.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_zero_class.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zpow_eq_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "library_note.forgetful inheritance", "description": " Suppose that one can put two mathematical structures on a type, a rich one `R` and a poor one\n`P`, and that one can deduce the poor structure from the rich structure through a map `F` (called a\nforgetful functor) (think `R = metric_space` and `P = topological_space`). A possible\nimplementation would be to have a type class `rich` containing a field `R`, a type class `poor`\ncontaining a field `P`, and an instance from `rich` to `poor`. However, this creates diamond\nproblems, and a better approach is to let `rich` extend `poor` and have a field saying that\n`F R = P`.\n\nTo illustrate this, consider the pair `metric_space` / `topological_space`. Consider the topology\non a product of two metric spaces. With the first approach, it could be obtained by going first from\neach metric space to its topology, and then taking the product topology. But it could also be\nobtained by considering the product metric space (with its sup distance) and then the topology\ncoming from this distance. These would be the same topology, but not definitionally, which means\nthat from the point of view of Lean's kernel, there would be two different `topological_space`\ninstances on the product. This is not compatible with the way instances are designed and used:\nthere should be at most one instance of a kind on each type. This approach has created an instance\ndiamond that does not commute definitionally.\n\nThe second approach solves this issue. Now, a metric space contains both a distance, a topology, and\na proof that the topology coincides with the one coming from the distance. When one defines the\nproduct of two metric spaces, one uses the sup distance and the product topology, and one has to\ngive the proof that the sup distance induces the product topology. Following both sides of the\ninstance diamond then gives rise (definitionally) to the product topology on the product space.\n\nAnother approach would be to have the rich type class take the poor type class as an instance\nparameter. It would solve the diamond problem, but it would lead to a blow up of the number\nof type classes one would need to declare to work with complicated classes, say a real inner\nproduct space, and would create exponential complexity when working with products of\nsuch complicated spaces, that are avoided by bundling things carefully as above.\n\nNote that this description of this specific case of the product of metric spaces is oversimplified\ncompared to mathlib, as there is an intermediate typeclass between `metric_space` and\n`topological_space` called `uniform_space`. The above scheme is used at both levels, embedding a\ntopology in the uniform space structure, and a uniform structure in the metric space structure.\n\nNote also that, when `P` is a proposition, there is no such issue as any two proofs of `P` are\ndefinitionally equivalent in Lean.\n\nTo avoid boilerplate, there are some designs that can automatically fill the poor fields when\ncreating a rich structure if one doesn't want to do something special about them. For instance,\nin the definition of metric spaces, default tactics fill the uniform space fields if they are\nnot given explicitly. One can also have a helper function creating the rich structure from a\nstructure with fewer fields, where the helper function fills the remaining fields. See for instance\n`uniform_space.of_core` or `real_inner_product.of_core`.\n\nFor more details on this question, called the forgetful inheritance pattern, see [Competing\ninheritance paths in dependent type theory: a case study in functional\nanalysis](https://hal.inria.fr/hal-02463336).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_right_cancel_monoid.to_add_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "right_cancel_semigroup.to_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cancel_monoid.to_left_cancel_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "left_cancel_monoid.to_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_cancel_monoid.to_is_cancel_add", "description": "Any `add_cancel_monoid M` satisfies `is_cancel_add M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inv_mul_cancel_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_ne_add_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zpow_coe_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_semigroup", "description": "An additive semigroup is a type with an associative `(+)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_semigroup.add", "description": "An additive semigroup is a type with an associative `(+)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_semigroup.add_assoc", "description": "An additive semigroup is a type with an associative `(+)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "name_set.to_list", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.of_list", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "name_set.size", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_set", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "name_set.mfold", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_set.erase", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "name_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_set.size", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "mk_name_set", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_lmap.find", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "name_set.of_list", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "name_set.has_to_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "mk_name_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "name_set.contains", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "name_set.union", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.mk", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_set.fold", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_set.contains", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.insert", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_lmap.contains", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "name_set.insert", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.max", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_lmap.mk", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_set.mfold", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.values", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.for", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.keys", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "name_set.erase", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_set.has_to_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.nat_map.mk", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "name_set", "description": "An rb_map of `name`s.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.mfold", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.empty", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.set_of_list", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_lmap.insert", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_lmap.erase", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.size", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.find", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "name_set.empty", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.mk_rb_set", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.nat_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.contains", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_set.insert", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "name_set.fold", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.mk_core", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.min", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_lmap", "description": "a variant of rb_maps that stores a list of elements for each key.\n  `find` returns the list of elements in the opposite order that they were inserted.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.filter", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.erase", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.mk_nat_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.mk_rb_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_set.to_list", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.fold", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "name_map.mk", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.to_list", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_set.empty", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "native.rb_map.has_to_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "mat_poly_equiv", "description": "The algebra isomorphism stating \"matrices of polynomials are the same as polynomials of matrices\".\n\n(You probably shouldn't attempt to use this underlying definition ---\nit's an algebra equivalence, and characterised extensionally by the lemma\n`mat_poly_equiv_coeff_apply` below.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor.to_fun_linear_mul_tmul_mul_aux_2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor_symm_apply_tmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor.right_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor.to_fun_alg_hom", "description": "(Implementation detail).\nThe algebra homomorphism `A \u2297[R] R[X] \u2192\u2090[R] A[X]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor.inv_fun_monomial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor", "description": "The `R`-algebra isomorphism `A[X] \u2243\u2090[R] (A \u2297[R] R[X])`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor.to_fun_alg_hom_apply_tmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor.to_fun_bilinear_apply_eq_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "support_subset_support_mat_poly_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mat_poly_equiv_coeff_apply_aux_2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor.left_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mat_poly_equiv_symm_apply_coeff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor.to_fun_bilinear", "description": "(Implementation detail).\nThe function underlying `A \u2297[R] R[X] \u2192\u2090[R] A[X]`,\nas a bilinear function of two arguments.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor.to_fun_linear_mul_tmul_mul_aux_1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor.to_fun_bilinear_apply_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mat_poly_equiv_coeff_apply_aux_1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mat_poly_equiv_coeff_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor.to_fun_linear_mul_tmul_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mat_poly_equiv_smul_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor.to_fun_linear_tmul_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor.to_fun_linear_algebra_map_tmul_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor.inv_fun", "description": "(Implementation detail.)\n\nThe bare function `A[X] \u2192 A \u2297[R] R[X]`.\n(We don't need to show that it's an algebra map, thankfully --- just that it's an inverse.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor.to_fun_linear", "description": "(Implementation detail).\nThe function underlying `A \u2297[R] R[X] \u2192\u2090[R] A[X]`,\nas a linear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor.inv_fun_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "poly_equiv_tensor.equiv", "description": "(Implementation detail)\n\nThe equivalence, ignoring the algebra structure, `(A \u2297[R] R[X]) \u2243 A[X]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decomposition.decompose'_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_symm_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_symm_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_linear_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decomposition.subsingleton", "description": "`direct_sum.decomposition` instances, while carrying data, are always equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.sum_support_decompose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decomposition.induction_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_linear_equiv", "description": " If `M` is graded by `\u03b9` with degree `i` component `\u2133 i`, then it is isomorphic as\na module to a direct sum of components.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.add_comm_group_set_like", "description": " The `-` in the statements below doesn't resolve without this line.\n\nThis seems to a be a problem of synthesized vs inferred typeclasses disagreeing. If we replace\nthe statement of `decompose_neg` with `@eq (\u2a01 i, \u2133 i) (decompose \u2133 (-x)) (-decompose \u2133 x)`\ninstead of `decompose \u2133 (-x) = -decompose \u2133 x`, which forces the typeclasses needed by `\u2a01 i, \u2133 i` to\nbe found by unification rather than synthesis, then everything works fine without this instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_symm_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_symm_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_symm_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_add_equiv", "description": " If `M` is graded by `\u03b9` with degree `i` component `\u2133 i`, then it is isomorphic as\nan additive monoid to a direct sum of components.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_add_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_of_mem_same", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decomposition.is_internal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_add_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose", "description": " If `M` is graded by `\u03b9` with degree `i` component `\u2133 i`, then it is isomorphic as\nto a direct sum of components. This is the canonical spelling of the `decompose'` field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_linear_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decomposition", "description": " A decomposition is an equivalence between an additive monoid `M` and a direct sum of additive\nsubmonoids `\u2133 i` of that `M`, such that the \"recomposition\" is canonical. This definition also\nworks for additive groups and modules.\n\nThis is a version of `direct_sum.is_internal` which comes with a constructive inverse to the\ncanonical \"recomposition\" rather than just a proof that the \"recomposition\" is bijective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decomposition.decompose'", "description": " A decomposition is an equivalence between an additive monoid `M` and a direct sum of additive\nsubmonoids `\u2133 i` of that `M`, such that the \"recomposition\" is canonical. This definition also\nworks for additive groups and modules.\n\nThis is a version of `direct_sum.is_internal` which comes with a constructive inverse to the\ncanonical \"recomposition\" rather than just a proof that the \"recomposition\" is bijective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decomposition.left_inv", "description": " A decomposition is an equivalence between an additive monoid `M` and a direct sum of additive\nsubmonoids `\u2133 i` of that `M`, such that the \"recomposition\" is canonical. This definition also\nworks for additive groups and modules.\n\nThis is a version of `direct_sum.is_internal` which comes with a constructive inverse to the\ncanonical \"recomposition\" rather than just a proof that the \"recomposition\" is bijective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decomposition.right_inv", "description": " A decomposition is an equivalence between an additive monoid `M` and a direct sum of additive\nsubmonoids `\u2133 i` of that `M`, such that the \"recomposition\" is canonical. This definition also\nworks for additive groups and modules.\n\nThis is a version of `direct_sum.is_internal` which comes with a constructive inverse to the\ncanonical \"recomposition\" rather than just a proof that the \"recomposition\" is bijective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_symm_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.decompose_of_mem_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hitting_of_lt", "description": "This lemma is strictly weaker than `hitting_of_le`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.le_hitting_of_exists", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_stopping_time_hitting_is_stopping_time", "description": " The hitting time of a discrete process with the starting time indexed by a stopping time\nis a stopping time.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hitting_le_iff_of_exists", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.not_mem_of_lt_hitting", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hitting_mem_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hitting_lt_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hitting_bot_le_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hitting_le_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hitting_eq_hitting_of_exists", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.le_hitting", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hitting", "description": " Hitting time: given a stochastic process `u` and a set `s`, `hitting u s n m` is the first time\n`u` is in `s` after time `n` and before time `m` (if `u` does not hit `s` after time `n` and\nbefore `m` then the hitting time is simply `m`).\n\nThe hitting time is a stopping time if the process is adapted and discrete.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hitting_is_stopping_time", "description": "A discrete hitting time is a stopping time.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hitting_mem_set_of_hitting_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hitting_mem_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hitting_eq_Inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hitting_eq_end_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hitting_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hitting_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hitting_le_iff_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hitting_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.stopped_value_hitting_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.image_of_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.measure_fundamental_interior", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.fundamental_interior", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.smul_of_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.measure_eq_tsum_of_ac", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.set_lintegral_eq_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.add_fundamental_interior_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.fundamental_frontier_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.restrict_restrict", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain", "description": " A measurable set `s` is a *fundamental domain* for an action of a group `G` on a measurable\nspace `\u03b1` with respect to a measure `\u03b1` if the sets `g \u2022 s`, `g : G`, are pairwise a.e. disjoint and\ncover the whole space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.null_measurable_set", "description": " A measurable set `s` is a *fundamental domain* for an action of a group `G` on a measurable\nspace `\u03b1` with respect to a measure `\u03b1` if the sets `g \u2022 s`, `g : G`, are pairwise a.e. disjoint and\ncover the whole space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.ae_covers", "description": " A measurable set `s` is a *fundamental domain* for an action of a group `G` on a measurable\nspace `\u03b1` with respect to a measure `\u03b1` if the sets `g \u2022 s`, `g : G`, are pairwise a.e. disjoint and\ncover the whole space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.ae_disjoint", "description": " A measurable set `s` is a *fundamental domain* for an action of a group `G` on a measurable\nspace `\u03b1` with respect to a measure `\u03b1` if the sets `g \u2022 s`, `g : G`, are pairwise a.e. disjoint and\ncover the whole space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.integrable_on_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.set_lintegral_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.fundamental_interior_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.integral_eq_tsum_of_ac", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.add_fundamental_frontier", "description": "The boundary of a fundamental domain, those\npoints of the domain that also lie in a nontrivial translate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_add_fundamental_frontier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.add_fundamental_interior_union_add_fundamental_frontier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.set_integral_eq_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.preimage_of_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.null_measurable_set_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.sdiff_fundamental_interior", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.pairwise_ae_disjoint_of_ac", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.set_lintegral_eq_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.pairwise_ae_disjoint_of_ac", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.set_lintegral_eq_tsum'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.measure_eq", "description": "If `s` and `t` are two fundamental domains of the same action, then their measures are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.sum_restrict", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.measure_le_of_pairwise_disjoint", "description": "If the additive action of a countable group `G` admits an invariant measure `\u03bc` with\na fundamental domain `s`, then every null-measurable set `t` such that the sets `g +\u1d65 t \u2229 s` are\npairwise a.e.-disjoint has measure at most `\u03bc s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.measure_add_fundamental_interior", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.pairwise_disjoint_add_fundamental_interior", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.pairwise_disjoint_fundamental_interior", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.exists_ne_one_smul_eq", "description": " If the action of a countable group `G` admits an invariant measure `\u03bc` with a fundamental domain\n`s`, then every null-measurable set `t` of measure strictly greater than `\u03bc s` contains two\npoints `x y` such that `g \u2022 x = y` for some `g \u2260 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.image_of_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.fundamental_frontier_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.fundamental_interior", "description": "The interior of a fundamental domain, those points of the domain not lying in any translate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.fundamental_frontier_union_fundamental_interior", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.measure_eq_card_smul_of_smul_ae_eq_self", "description": " Given a measure space with an action of a finite group `G`, the measure of any `G`-invariant set\nis determined by the measure of its intersection with a fundamental domain for the action of `G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.set_lintegral_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.fundamental_interior_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.fundamental_frontier", "description": " The boundary of a fundamental domain, those points of the domain that also lie in a nontrivial\ntranslate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.null_measurable_set.fundamental_interior", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.measure_set_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.disjoint_fundamental_interior_fundamental_frontier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.Union_vadd_ae_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_fundamental_interior", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.add_fundamental_interior_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.ae_strongly_measurable_on_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.Union_smul_ae_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.sdiff_fundamental_frontier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.integral_eq_tsum''", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.measure_fundamental_frontier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.mk_of_measure_univ_le", "description": "If a measurable space has a finite measure `\u03bc` and a countable additive group `G` acts\nquasi-measure-preservingly, then to show that a set `s` is a fundamental domain, it is sufficient\nto check that its translates `g +\u1d65 s` are (almost) disjoint and that the sum `\u2211' g, \u03bc (g +\u1d65 s)` is\nsufficiently large.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.sdiff_add_fundamental_frontier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.measure_eq_tsum'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.measure_set_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.measure_zero_of_invariant", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.has_finite_integral_on_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.null_measurable_set.add_fundamental_interior", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.set_integral_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.lintegral_eq_tsum_of_ac", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.lintegral_eq_tsum'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.fundamental_interior_union_fundamental_frontier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.lintegral_eq_tsum'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.vadd_of_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.ae_strongly_measurable_on_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.exists_ne_zero_vadd_eq", "description": "If the additive action of a countable group `G` admits an invariant measure `\u03bc` with\na fundamental domain `s`, then every null-measurable set `t` of measure strictly greater than `\u03bc s`\ncontains two points `x y` such that `g +\u1d65 x = y` for some `g \u2260 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.lintegral_eq_tsum''", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.restrict_restrict", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.mk'", "description": " If for each `x : \u03b1`, exactly one of `g \u2022 x`, `g : G`, belongs to a measurable set `s`, then `s`\nis a fundamental domain for the action of `G` on `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.add_fundamental_interior", "description": "The interior of a fundamental domain, those\npoints of the domain not lying in any translate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.set_integral_eq_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.measure_eq_tsum'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.lintegral_eq_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.mk'", "description": "If for each `x : \u03b1`, exactly one of `g +\u1d65 x`, `g : G`, belongs to a measurable set\n`s`, then `s` is a fundamental domain for the additive action of `G` on `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.lintegral_eq_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.has_finite_integral_on_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_fundamental_frontier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.measure_add_fundamental_frontier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.sum_restrict_of_ac", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.null_measurable_set_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.integral_eq_tsum_of_ac", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.add_fundamental_frontier_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.set_lintegral_eq_tsum'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.null_measurable_set.add_fundamental_frontier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.measure_eq_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.sum_restrict", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.mk''", "description": "For `s` to be a fundamental domain, it's enough to check `ae_disjoint (g \u2022 s) s` for `g \u2260 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.ess_sup_measure_restrict", "description": "If `f` is invariant under the action of a countable group `G`, and `\u03bc` is a `G`-invariant\n measure with a fundamental domain `s`, then the `ess_sup` of `f` restricted to `s` is the same as\n that of `f` on all of its domain.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.mk''", "description": "For `s` to be a fundamental domain, it's enough to check `ae_disjoint (g +\u1d65 s) s` for\n`g \u2260 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.sdiff_add_fundamental_interior", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.measure_eq_card_smul_of_vadd_ae_eq_self", "description": "Given a measure space with an action of a\nfinite additive group `G`, the measure of any `G`-invariant set is determined by the measure of its\nintersection with a fundamental domain for the action of `G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain", "description": " A measurable set `s` is a *fundamental domain* for an additive action of an additive group `G`\non a measurable space `\u03b1` with respect to a measure `\u03b1` if the sets `g +\u1d65 s`, `g : G`, are pairwise\na.e. disjoint and cover the whole space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.null_measurable_set", "description": " A measurable set `s` is a *fundamental domain* for an additive action of an additive group `G`\non a measurable space `\u03b1` with respect to a measure `\u03b1` if the sets `g +\u1d65 s`, `g : G`, are pairwise\na.e. disjoint and cover the whole space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.ae_covers", "description": " A measurable set `s` is a *fundamental domain* for an additive action of an additive group `G`\non a measurable space `\u03b1` with respect to a measure `\u03b1` if the sets `g +\u1d65 s`, `g : G`, are pairwise\na.e. disjoint and cover the whole space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.ae_disjoint", "description": " A measurable set `s` is a *fundamental domain* for an additive action of an additive group `G`\non a measurable space `\u03b1` with respect to a measure `\u03b1` if the sets `g +\u1d65 s`, `g : G`, are pairwise\na.e. disjoint and cover the whole space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.integral_eq_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.preimage_of_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.integrable_on_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.measure_zero_of_invariant", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.measure_le_of_pairwise_disjoint", "description": " If the action of a countable group `G` admits an invariant measure `\u03bc` with a fundamental domain\n`s`, then every null-measurable set `t` such that the sets `g \u2022 t \u2229 s` are pairwise a.e.-disjoint\nhas measure at most `\u03bc s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.lintegral_eq_tsum_of_ac", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.sum_restrict_of_ac", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.measure_eq", "description": "If `s` and `t` are two fundamental domains of the same action, then their measures\nare equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.lintegral_eq_tsum''", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.set_integral_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.integral_eq_tsum'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.set_integral_eq_tsum'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.integral_eq_tsum'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.disjoint_add_fundamental_interior_add_fundamental_frontier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.ess_sup_measure_restrict", "description": "If `f` is invariant under the action of a countable additive group `G`, and `\u03bc` is a\n`G`-invariant measure with a fundamental domain `s`, then the `ess_sup` of `f` restricted to `s` is\nthe same as that of `f` on all of its domain.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.set_integral_eq_tsum'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.integral_eq_tsum''", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.add_fundamental_frontier_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.null_measurable_set.fundamental_frontier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.integral_eq_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_add_fundamental_interior", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.mk_of_measure_univ_le", "description": " If a measurable space has a finite measure `\u03bc` and a countable group `G` acts\nquasi-measure-preservingly, then to show that a set `s` is a fundamental domain, it is sufficient\nto check that its translates `g \u2022 s` are (almost) disjoint and that the sum `\u2211' g, \u03bc (g \u2022 s)` is\nsufficiently large.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.measure_eq_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.measure_eq_tsum_of_ac", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.additive_energy_empty_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.multiplicative_energy_mono_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.additive_energy_mono_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.le_multiplicative_energy", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.multiplicative_energy_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.le_additive_energy", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.additive_energy_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.multiplicative_energy_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.multiplicative_energy_empty_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.additive_energy_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.multiplicative_energy_univ_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.additive_energy_pos_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.multiplicative_energy_pos_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.additive_energy", "description": "The additive energy of two finsets `s` and `t` in a group is the\nnumber of quadruples `(a\u2081, a\u2082, b\u2081, b\u2082) \u2208 s \u00d7 s \u00d7 t \u00d7 t` such that `a\u2081 + b\u2081 = a\u2082 + b\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.additive_energy_mono_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.additive_energy_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.multiplicative_energy", "description": " The multiplicative energy of two finsets `s` and `t` in a group is the number of quadruples\n`(a\u2081, a\u2082, b\u2081, b\u2082) \u2208 s \u00d7 s \u00d7 t \u00d7 t` such that `a\u2081 * b\u2081 = a\u2082 * b\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.multiplicative_energy_univ_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.multiplicative_energy_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.additive_energy_univ_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.additive_energy_univ_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.multiplicative_energy_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.additive_energy_empty_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.multiplicative_energy_empty_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.additive_energy_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.multiplicative_energy_mono_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_lt_ack_succ_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_mono_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_ack_lt_ack_max_add_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_le_iff_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_lt_ack_succ_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_le_iff_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_strict_mono_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack", "description": " The two-argument Ackermann function, defined so that\n\n- `ack 0 n = n + 1`\n- `ack (m + 1) 0 = ack m 1`\n- `ack (m + 1) (n + 1) = ack m (ack (m + 1) n)`.\n\nThis is of interest as both a fast-growing function, and as an example of a recursive function that\nisn't primitive recursive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_primrec\u2082_ack", "description": "The Ackermann function is not primitive recursive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_lt_iff_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_succ_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_le_ack", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "max_ack_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_ack_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_succ_right_le_ack_succ_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_primrec_ack_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_succ_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_add_one_sq_lt_ack_add_four", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_mono_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_injective_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_strict_mono_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_mkpair_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_ack_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_nat_primrec_ack_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_lt_ack_of_nat_primrec", "description": "If `f` is primitive recursive, there exists `m` such that `f n < ack m n` for all `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "max_ack_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_inj_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_lt_ack", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_add_one_sq_lt_ack_add_three", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_lt_iff_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_injective_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_three", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_add_one_le_ack", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ack_inj_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2082_unit_iso", "description": "The unit isomorphism of the equivalence `equivalence\u2082` between `A` and `B`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2081", "description": " An intermediate equivalence `A \u2245 B'` whose functor is `F` and whose inverse is\n`e'.inverse \u22d9 eA.inverse`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2081_unit_iso_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2081_unit_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence_counit_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.\u03c4\u2080_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence_unit_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2081_counit_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2082_unit_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.\u03c5_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.\u03c4\u2081_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2081_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.\u03c5", "description": " The isomorphism `eA.functor \u2245 F \u22d9 e'.inverse` deduced from the\nunit isomorphism of `e'` and the isomorphism `hF : eA.functor \u22d9 e'.functor \u2245 F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2082_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2081_counit_iso_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2082_counit_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2082_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2082_counit_iso_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2081_counit_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2080_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2082_unit_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2080_unit_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2081_unit_iso", "description": "The unit isomorphism of the equivalence `equivalence\u2081` between `A` and `B'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence", "description": " The equivalence `A \u2245 B` whose functor is `F \u22d9 eB.inverse` and\nwhose inverse is `G : B \u2245 A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.\u03c5_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2082_unit_iso_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2082", "description": " An intermediate equivalence `A \u2245 B` obtained as the composition of `equivalence\u2081` and\nthe inverse of `eB : B \u224c B'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2081_counit_iso", "description": "The counit isomorphism of the equivalence `equivalence\u2081` between `A` and `B'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence_unit_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence_unit_iso", "description": "The unit isomorphism of `equivalence`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.\u03c4\u2080", "description": " The isomorphism `eB.functor \u22d9 e'.inverse \u22d9 e'.functor \u2245 eB.functor` deduced\nfrom the counit isomorphism of `e'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.\u03c4\u2081", "description": " The isomorphism `eB.functor \u22d9 e'.inverse \u22d9 e'.functor \u2245 eB.functor` deduced\nfrom the isomorphisms `hF : eA.functor \u22d9 e'.functor \u2245 F`,\n`hG : eB.functor \u22d9 e'.inverse \u2245 G \u22d9 eA.functor` and the datum of\nan isomorphism `\u03b7 : G \u22d9 F \u2245 eB.functor`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2080_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2081_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence_counit_iso_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence_counit_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2081_unit_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2082_counit_iso", "description": "The counit isomorphism of the equivalence `equivalence\u2082` between `A` and `B`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence_unit_iso_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence_counit_iso", "description": "The counit isomorphism of `equivalence`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2080", "description": "A basic equivalence `A \u2245 B'` obtained by composing `eA : A \u2245 A'` and `e' : A' \u2245 B'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility.equivalence\u2082_counit_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.mk_cache", "description": "`mk_cache names` creates a `norm_cast_cache`. It infers the proper `norm_cast` attributes\nfor names in `names`, and collects the lemmas attributed with specific `norm_cast` attributes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.derive_push_cast", "description": "A small variant of `push_cast` suited for non-interactive use.\n\n`derive_push_cast extra_lems e` returns an expression `e'` and a proof that `e = e'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.exact_mod_cast", "description": "Normalize the goal and the given expression, then close the goal with exact.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.rw_mod_cast", "description": "Rewrite with the given rules and normalize casts between steps.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.add_lemma", "description": "`add_lemma cache decl` infers the proper `norm_cast` attribute for `decl` and adds it to `cache`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.label.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_instance_fast", "description": "Runs `mk_instance` with a time limit.\n\nThis is a work around to the fact that in some cases\nmk_instance times out instead of failing,\nfor example: `has_lift_t \u2124 \u2115`\n\n`mk_instance_fast` is used when we assume the type class search\nshould end instantly.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.label.has_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.norm_cast", "description": "The `norm_cast` family of tactics is used to normalize casts inside expressions.\nIt is basically a simp tactic with a specific set of lemmas to move casts\nupwards in the expression.\nTherefore it can be used more safely as a non-terminating tactic.\nIt also has special handling of numerals.\n\nFor instance, given an assumption\n```lean\na b : \u2124\nh : \u2191a + \u2191b < (10 : \u211a)\n```\n\nwriting `norm_cast at h` will turn `h` into\n```lean\nh : a + b < 10\n```\n\nYou can also use `exact_mod_cast`, `apply_mod_cast`, `rw_mod_cast`\nor `assumption_mod_cast`.\nWriting `exact_mod_cast h` and `apply_mod_cast h` will normalize the goal and\n`h` before using `exact h` or `apply h`.\nWriting `assumption_mod_cast` will normalize the goal and for every\nexpression `h` in the context it will try to normalize `h` and use\n`exact h`.\n`rw_mod_cast` acts like the `rw` tactic but it applies `norm_cast` between steps.\n\n`push_cast` rewrites the expression to move casts toward the leaf nodes.\nThis uses `norm_cast` lemmas in the forward direction.\nFor example, `\u2191(a + b)` will be written to `\u2191a + \u2191b`.\nIt is equivalent to `simp only with push_cast`.\nIt can also be used at hypotheses with `push_cast at h`\nand with extra simp lemmas with `push_cast [int.add_zero]`.\n\n```lean\nexample (a b : \u2115) (h1 : ((a + b : \u2115) : \u2124) = 10) (h2 : ((a + b + 0 : \u2115) : \u2124) = 10) :\n  ((a + b : \u2115) : \u2124) = 10 :=\nbegin\n  push_cast,\n  push_cast at h1,\n  push_cast [int.add_zero] at h2,\nend\n```\n\nThe implementation and behavior of the `norm_cast` family is described in detail at\n<https://lean-forward.github.io/norm_cast/norm_cast.pdf>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.push_cast", "description": "`push_cast` rewrites the expression to move casts toward the leaf nodes.\nFor example, `\u2191(a + b)` will be written to `\u2191a + \u2191b`.\nEquivalent to `simp only with push_cast`.\nCan also be used at hypotheses.\n\n`push_cast` can also be used at hypotheses and with extra simp rules.\n\n```lean\nexample (a b : \u2115) (h1 : ((a + b : \u2115) : \u2124) = 10) (h2 : ((a + b + 0 : \u2115) : \u2124) = 10) :\n  ((a + b : \u2115) : \u2124) = 10 :=\nbegin\n  push_cast,\n  push_cast at h1,\n  push_cast [int.add_zero] at h2,\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.label.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.splitting_procedure", "description": "This is the main heuristic used alongside the elim and move lemmas.\nThe goal is to help casts move past operators by adding intermediate casts.\nAn expression of the shape: op (\u2191(x : \u03b1) : \u03b3) (\u2191(y : \u03b2) : \u03b3)\nis rewritten to:            op (\u2191(\u2191(x : \u03b1) : \u03b2) : \u03b3) (\u2191(y : \u03b2) : \u03b3)\nwhen (\u2191(\u2191(x : \u03b1) : \u03b2) : \u03b3) = (\u2191(x : \u03b1) : \u03b3) can be proven with a squash lemma", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.norm_cast_attr_ty", "description": "The type of the `norm_cast` attribute.\nThe optional label is used to overwrite the classifier.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.empty_cache", "description": "Empty `norm_cast_cache`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.norm_cast", "description": "Normalize casts at the given locations by moving them \"upwards\".\nAs opposed to simp, norm_cast can be used without necessarily closing the goal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.label.has_to_format", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.make_guess", "description": "Classify a declaration as a `norm_cast` rule.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.assumption_mod_cast", "description": "Normalize the goal and every expression in the local context, then close the goal with assumption.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.prove_eq_using_down", "description": "Prove `a = b` by simplifying using move and squash lemmas.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.assumption_mod_cast", "description": " `assumption_mod_cast` runs `norm_cast` on the goal. For each local hypothesis `h`, it also\nnormalizes `h` and tries to use that to close the goal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.count_head_coes", "description": "Count how many coercions are at the top of the expression.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.apply_mod_cast", "description": "Normalize the goal and the given expression, then apply the expression to the goal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.exact_mod_cast", "description": " `exact_mod_cast e` runs `norm_cast` on the goal and `e`, and tries to use `e` to close the\ngoal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.trace_norm_cast", "description": "Output a trace message if `trace.norm_cast` is enabled.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.count_coes", "description": "Count how many coercions are inside the expression, including the top ones.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.apply_mod_cast", "description": "`apply_mod_cast e` runs `norm_cast` on the goal and `e`, and tries to apply `e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dite_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.upward_and_elim", "description": "Core rewriting function used in the \"squash\" step, which moves casts upwards\nand eliminates them.\n\nIt tries to rewrite an expression using the elim and move lemmas.\nOn failure, it calls the splitting procedure heuristic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.label.of_string", "description": "Convert `string` into `label`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.classify_type", "description": "Classifies a declaration of type `ty` as a `norm_cast` rule.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.get_label", "description": "Gets the `norm_cast` classification label for a declaration. Applies the\noverride specified on the attribute, if necessary.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.attribute.norm_cast attributes", "description": "The `norm_cast` attribute should be given to lemmas that describe the\nbehaviour of a coercion in regard to an operator, a relation, or a particular\nfunction.\n\nIt only concerns equality or iff lemmas involving `\u2191`, `\u21d1` and `\u21a5`, describing the behavior of\nthe coercion functions.\nIt does not apply to the explicit functions that define the coercions.\n\nExamples:\n```lean\n@[norm_cast] theorem coe_nat_inj' {m n : \u2115} : (\u2191m : \u2124) = \u2191n \u2194 m = n\n\n@[norm_cast] theorem coe_int_denom (n : \u2124) : (n : \u211a).denom = 1\n\n@[norm_cast] theorem cast_id : \u2200 n : \u211a, \u2191n = n\n\n@[norm_cast] theorem coe_nat_add (m n : \u2115) : (\u2191(m + n) : \u2124) = \u2191m + \u2191n\n\n@[norm_cast] theorem cast_sub [add_group \u03b1] [has_one \u03b1] {m n} (h : m \u2264 n) :\n  ((n - m : \u2115) : \u03b1) = n - m\n\n@[norm_cast] theorem coe_nat_bit0 (n : \u2115) : (\u2191(bit0 n) : \u2124) = bit0 \u2191n\n\n@[norm_cast] theorem cast_coe_nat (n : \u2115) : ((n : \u2124) : \u03b1) = n\n\n@[norm_cast] theorem cast_one : ((1 : \u211a) : \u03b1) = 1\n```\n\nLemmas tagged with `@[norm_cast]` are classified into three categories: `move`, `elim`, and\n`squash`. They are classified roughly as follows:\n\n* elim lemma:   LHS has 0 head coes and \u2265 1 internal coe\n* move lemma:   LHS has 1 head coe and 0 internal coes,    RHS has 0 head coes and \u2265 1 internal coes\n* squash lemma: LHS has \u2265 1 head coes and 0 internal coes, RHS has fewer head coes\n\n`norm_cast` uses `move` and `elim` lemmas to factor coercions toward the root of an expression\nand to cancel them from both sides of an equation or relation. It uses `squash` lemmas to clean\nup the result.\n\nOccasionally you may want to override the automatic classification.\nYou can do this by giving an optional `elim`, `move`, or `squash` parameter to the attribute.\n\n```lean\n@[simp, norm_cast elim] lemma nat_cast_re (n : \u2115) : (n : \u2102).re = n :=\nby rw [\u2190 of_real_nat_cast, of_real_re]\n```\n\nDon't do this unless you understand what you are doing.\n\nA full description of the tactic, and the use of each lemma category, can be found at\n<https://lean-forward.github.io/norm_cast/norm_cast.pdf>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.add_move", "description": "`add_move cache e` adds `e` as a `move` lemma to `cache`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.add_elim", "description": "`add_elim cache e` adds `e` as an `elim` lemma to `cache`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.derive", "description": "The core simplification routine of `norm_cast`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.prove_eq_using", "description": "Prove `a = b` using the given simp set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.label", "description": "`label` is a type used to classify `norm_cast` lemmas.\n* elim lemma:   LHS has 0 head coes and \u2265 1 internal coe\n* move lemma:   LHS has 1 head coe and 0 internal coes,    RHS has 0 head coes and \u2265 1 internal coes\n* squash lemma: LHS has \u2265 1 head coes and 0 internal coes, RHS has fewer head coes", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.label.elim", "description": "`label` is a type used to classify `norm_cast` lemmas.\n* elim lemma:   LHS has 0 head coes and \u2265 1 internal coe\n* move lemma:   LHS has 1 head coe and 0 internal coes,    RHS has 0 head coes and \u2265 1 internal coes\n* squash lemma: LHS has \u2265 1 head coes and 0 internal coes, RHS has fewer head coes", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.label.move", "description": "`label` is a type used to classify `norm_cast` lemmas.\n* elim lemma:   LHS has 0 head coes and \u2265 1 internal coe\n* move lemma:   LHS has 1 head coe and 0 internal coes,    RHS has 0 head coes and \u2265 1 internal coes\n* squash lemma: LHS has \u2265 1 head coes and 0 internal coes, RHS has fewer head coes", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.label.squash", "description": "`label` is a type used to classify `norm_cast` lemmas.\n* elim lemma:   LHS has 0 head coes and \u2265 1 internal coe\n* move lemma:   LHS has 1 head coe and 0 internal coes,    RHS has 0 head coes and \u2265 1 internal coes\n* squash lemma: LHS has \u2265 1 head coes and 0 internal coes, RHS has fewer head coes", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conv.interactive.norm_cast", "description": "the converter version of `norm_cast'", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.aux_mod_cast", "description": " `aux_mod_cast e` runs `norm_cast` on `e` and returns the result. If `include_goal` is true, it\nalso normalizes the goal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.norm_cast_cache", "description": "The cache for `norm_cast` attribute stores three `simp_lemma` objects.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.norm_cast_cache.up", "description": "The cache for `norm_cast` attribute stores three `simp_lemma` objects.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.norm_cast_cache.down", "description": "The cache for `norm_cast` attribute stores three `simp_lemma` objects.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.norm_cast_cache.squash", "description": "The cache for `norm_cast` attribute stores three `simp_lemma` objects.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.numeral_to_coe", "description": "If possible, rewrite `(n : \u03b1)` to `((n : \u2115) : \u03b1)` where `n` is a numeral and `\u03b1 \u2260 \u2115`.\nReturns a pair of the new expression and proof that they are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.norm_cast_cache.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.get_label_param", "description": "Efficient getter for the `@[norm_cast]` attribute parameter that does not call `eval_expr`.\n\nSee Note [user attribute parameters].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.label.to_string", "description": "Convert `label` into `string`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.add_squash", "description": "`add_squash cache e` adds `e` as an `squash` lemma to `cache`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.label.has_repr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ite_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.label.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.coe_to_numeral", "description": "If possible, rewrite `((n : \u2115) : \u03b1)` to `(n : \u03b1)` where `n` is a numeral.\nReturns a pair of the new expression and proof that they are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cast.norm_cast_attr", "description": "The `norm_cast` attribute.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closed_eq_range_close", "description": "The set of closed elements for `l` is the range of `u \u2218 l`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.simps.apply", "description": "See Note [custom simps projection]", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.id", "description": "The identity function as a lower adjoint to itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.monotone", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.idempotent", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.mem_closed_iff_closure_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.mk\u2083_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.mk'_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.gi", "description": "The set of closed elements has a Galois insertion to the underlying type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.monotone", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closure_Union_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.le_closure", "description": " Every element is less than its closure. This property is sometimes referred to as extensivity or\ninflationarity.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closure_sup_closure_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.mem_closed_iff_closure_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.le_closure_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.closure_sup_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.not_mem_of_not_mem_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.mem_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.id_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.le_closure_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closure_union_closure_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.le_closure", "description": " Every element is less than its closure. This property is sometimes referred to as extensivity or\ninflationarity.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "galois_connection.lower_adjoint", "description": "Every Galois connection induces a lower adjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "galois_connection.closure_operator_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.closed_eq_range_close", "description": "The set of closed elements for `c` is exactly its range.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closure_union_closure_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closure_le_closed_iff_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.mk\u2083", "description": " Expanded out version of `mk\u2082`. `p` implies being closed. This constructor should be used when\nyou already know a sufficient condition for being closed and using `mem_mk\u2083_closed` will avoid you\nthe (slight) hassle of having to prove it both inside and outside the constructor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.closure_supr_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closure_supr\u2082_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.mem_mk\u2083_closed", "description": "The property `p` fed into the `mk\u2083` constructor implies being closed.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator", "description": " A closure operator on the preorder `\u03b1` is a monotone function which is extensive (every `x`\nis less than its closure) and idempotent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.to_order_hom", "description": " A closure operator on the preorder `\u03b1` is a monotone function which is extensive (every `x`\nis less than its closure) and idempotent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.le_closure'", "description": " A closure operator on the preorder `\u03b1` is a monotone function which is extensive (every `x`\nis less than its closure) and idempotent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.idempotent'", "description": " A closure operator on the preorder `\u03b1` is a monotone function which is extensive (every `x`\nis less than its closure) and idempotent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.to_closed", "description": "Send an `x` to an element of the set of closed elements (by taking the closure).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.le_iff_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.mk\u2082_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.subset_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closure_sup_closure_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closed", "description": "An element `x` is closed for `l : lower_adjoint u` if it is a fixed point: `u (l x) = x`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.closure_sup_closure_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.closure_le_mk\u2083_iff", "description": "Analogue of `closure_le_closed_iff_le` but with the `p` that was fed into the `mk\u2083` constructor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.closure_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.idempotent", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closure_Union\u2082_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.mk'", "description": "Constructor for a closure operator using the weaker idempotency axiom: `f (f x) \u2264 f x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.mk\u2082", "description": " Convenience constructor for a closure operator using the weaker minimality axiom:\n`x \u2264 f y \u2192 f x \u2264 f y`, which is sometimes easier to prove in practice.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closure_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closure_operator", "description": " Every lower adjoint induces a closure operator given by the composition. This is the partial\norder version of the statement that every adjunction induces a monad.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.closure_eq_self_of_mem_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint", "description": " A lower adjoint of `u` on the preorder `\u03b1` is a function `l` such that `l` and `u` form a Galois\nconnection. It allows us to define closure operators whose output does not match the input. In\npractice, `u` is often `coe : \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.to_fun", "description": " A lower adjoint of `u` on the preorder `\u03b1` is a function `l` such that `l` and `u` form a Galois\nconnection. It allows us to define closure operators whose output does not match the input. In\npractice, `u` is often `coe : \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.gc'", "description": " A lower adjoint of `u` on the preorder `\u03b1` is a function `l` such that `l` and `u` form a Galois\nconnection. It allows us to define closure operators whose output does not match the input. In\npractice, `u` is often `coe : \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.closure_sup_closure_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.gc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.has_coe_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closure_union_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.has_coe_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.id", "description": "The identity function as a closure operator.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.closure_is_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closure_supr_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.mem_closed_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.id_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "galois_connection.lower_adjoint_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closure_operator_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closure_union_closure_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.closure_inf_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.closure_le_closed_iff_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "galois_connection.closure_operator", "description": " Every Galois connection induces a closure operator given by the composition. This is the partial\norder version of the statement that every adjunction induces a monad.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.simps.apply", "description": "See Note [custom simps projection]", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.closure_sup_closure_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closure_inf_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closure_sup_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.closure_supr\u2082_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.closure_mem_mk\u2083", "description": " This lemma shows that the image of `x` of a closure operator built from the `mk\u2083` constructor\nrespects `p`, the property that was fed into it.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.top_mem_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.mem_closed_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closure_eq_self_of_mem_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.eq_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.closed", "description": "An element `x` is closed for the closure operator `c` if it is a fixed point for it.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closure_sup_closure_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator_gi_self", "description": " The Galois insertion associated to a closure operator can be used to reconstruct the closure\noperator.\nNote that the inverse in the opposite direction does not hold in general.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.to_closed", "description": "Send an `x` to an element of the set of closed elements (by taking the closure).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.eq_mk\u2083_closed", "description": " A closure operator is equal to the closure operator obtained by feeding `c.closed` into the\n`mk\u2083` constructor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lower_adjoint.closure_is_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_operator.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biprod.lift_desc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone.of_limit_cone_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biproduct.total", "description": "In any preadditive category, any biproduct satsifies\n`\u2211 j : J, biproduct.\u03c0 f j \u226b biproduct.\u03b9 f j = \ud835\udfd9 (\u2a01 f)`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biprod.gaussian'", "description": "If `f` is a morphism `X\u2081 \u229e X\u2082 \u27f6 Y\u2081 \u229e Y\u2082` whose `X\u2081 \u27f6 Y\u2081` entry is an isomorphism,\nthen we can construct isomorphisms `L : X\u2081 \u229e X\u2082 \u2245 X\u2081 \u229e X\u2082` and `R : Y\u2081 \u229e Y\u2082 \u2245 Y\u2081 \u229e Y\u2082`\nso that `L.hom \u226b g \u226b R.hom` is diagonal (with `X\u2081 \u27f6 Y\u2081` component still `f`),\nvia Gaussian elimination.\n\n(This is the version of `biprod.gaussian` written in terms of components.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_biproduct.of_has_product", "description": "In a preadditive category, if the product over `f : J \u2192 C` exists,\n   then the biproduct over `f` exists.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biprod.add_eq_lift_id_desc", "description": "The existence of binary biproducts implies that there is at most one preadditive structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biprod.inr_of_components", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biprod.column_nonzero_of_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_binary_coproducts_of_preserves_binary_biproducts", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories that preserves (zero morphisms and) binary biproducts\n   preserves binary coproducts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone_of_is_split_epi_of_kernel_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biproduct.column_nonzero_of_iso", "description": "If `f : \u2a01 S \u27f6 \u2a01 T` is an isomorphism, and `s` is a non-trivial summand of the source,\nthen there is some `t` in the target so that the `s, t` matrix entry of `f` is nonzero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.fst_of_is_colimit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biproduct.matrix_desc_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone.of_limit_cone_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biproduct.matrix_map_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone_is_bilimit_of_limit_cone_of_is_limit", "description": "We can turn any limit cone over a pair into a bilimit bicone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_bilimit_binary_bicone_of_is_split_epi_of_kernel", "description": " The bicone constructed in `binary_bicone_of_is_split_epi_of_kernel` is a bilimit.\nThis is a version of the splitting lemma that holds in all preadditive categories.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_binary_coproduct_of_preserves_binary_biproduct", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories that preserves (zero morphisms and) binary biproducts\n   preserves binary coproducts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_bilimit_of_total", "description": "In a preadditive category, we can construct a biproduct for `f : J \u2192 C` from\nany bicone `b` for `f` satisfying `total : \u2211 j : J, b.\u03c0 j \u226b b.\u03b9 j = \ud835\udfd9 b.X`.\n\n(That is, such a bicone is a limit cone and a colimit cocone.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biprod.unipotent_lower_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biproduct.reindex_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.bicone_is_bilimit_of_limit_cone_of_is_limit", "description": "We can turn any limit cone over a pair into a bilimit bicone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_binary_bilimit_of_is_colimit", "description": "In a preadditive category, any binary bicone which is a colimit cocone is in fact a\n   bilimit bicone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biprod.total", "description": "In any preadditive category, any binary biproduct satsifies\n`biprod.fst \u226b biprod.inl + biprod.snd \u226b biprod.inr = \ud835\udfd9 (X \u229e Y)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biproduct.matrix_desc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.bicone_is_bilimit_of_colimit_cocone_of_is_colimit", "description": "We can turn any limit cone over a pair into a bilimit bicone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biprod.gaussian", "description": "If `f` is a morphism `X\u2081 \u229e X\u2082 \u27f6 Y\u2081 \u229e Y\u2082` whose `X\u2081 \u27f6 Y\u2081` entry is an isomorphism,\nthen we can construct isomorphisms `L : X\u2081 \u229e X\u2082 \u2245 X\u2081 \u229e X\u2082` and `R : Y\u2081 \u229e Y\u2082 \u2245 Y\u2081 \u229e Y\u2082`\nso that `L.hom \u226b g \u226b R.hom` is diagonal (with `X\u2081 \u27f6 Y\u2081` component still `f`),\nvia Gaussian elimination.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone.of_colimit_cocone_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biprod.add_eq_lift_desc_id", "description": "The existence of binary biproducts implies that there is at most one preadditive structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_biproduct_of_total", "description": "In a preadditive category, we can construct a biproduct for `f : J \u2192 C` from\nany bicone `b` for `f` satisfying `total : \u2211 j : J, b.\u03c0 j \u226b b.\u03b9 j = \ud835\udfd9 b.X`.\n\n(That is, such a bicone is a limit cone and a colimit cocone.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone_of_is_split_epi_of_kernel", "description": "Every split epi `f` with a kernel induces a binary bicone with `f` as its `snd` and\nthe kernel map as its `inl`.\nWe will show in `binary_bicone_of_is_split_mono_of_cokernel` that this binary bicone is in fact\nalready a biproduct.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_biproduct.of_has_coproduct", "description": "In a preadditive category, if the coproduct over `f : J \u2192 C` exists,\n   then the biproduct over `f` exists.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone_is_bilimit_of_colimit_cocone_of_is_colimit", "description": "We can turn any colimit cocone over a pair into a bilimit bicone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone_of_is_split_mono_of_cokernel", "description": "Every split mono `f` with a cokernel induces a binary bicone with `f` as its `inl` and\nthe cokernel map as its `snd`.\nWe will show in `is_bilimit_binary_bicone_of_split_mono_of_cokernel` that this binary bicone is in\nfact already a biproduct.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biprod.unipotent_upper", "description": "The unipotent upper triangular matrix\n```lean\n(1 r)\n(0 1)\n```\nas an isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biproduct.map_matrix", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone_of_is_split_mono_of_cokernel_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_binary_products_of_preserves_binary_biproducts", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories that preserves (zero morphisms and) binary biproducts\n   preserves binary products.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_binary_biproducts_of_preserves_binary_coproducts", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories that preserves (zero morphisms and) binary coproducts\n   preserves binary biproducts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.inr_of_is_limit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biproduct.lift_desc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone.of_limit_cone_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone_of_is_split_mono_of_cokernel_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biprod.desc_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biproduct.lift_matrix", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_biproduct_of_mono_biproduct_comparison", "description": "If the (product-like) biproduct comparison for `F` and `f` is a monomorphism, then `F`\n   preserves the biproduct of `f`. For the converse, see `map_biproduct`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biprod.inl_of_components", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_finite_biproducts.of_has_finite_coproducts", "description": "A preadditive category with finite coproducts has finite biproducts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_binary_biproduct_of_mono_biprod_comparison", "description": "If the (product-like) biproduct comparison for `F`, `X` and `Y` is a monomorphism, then\n   `F` preserves the biproduct of `X` and `Y`. For the converse, see `map_biprod`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone_of_is_split_mono_of_cokernel_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biprod.of_components_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_coproducts_of_shape_of_preserves_biproducts_of_shape", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories that preserves (zero morphisms and) finite biproducts\n   preserves finite coproducts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_binary_biproducts.of_has_binary_products", "description": "In a preadditive category, if all binary products exist, then all binary biproducts exist.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_binary_product_of_preserves_binary_biproduct", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories that preserves (zero morphisms and) binary biproducts\n   preserves binary products.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone.is_bilimit_of_kernel_inl", "description": "If `b` is a binary bicone such that `b.inl` is a kernel of `b.snd`, then `b` is a bilimit\n   bicone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone_of_is_split_epi_of_kernel_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_binary_biproduct_of_total", "description": "In a preadditive category, we can construct a binary biproduct for `X Y : C` from\nany binary bicone `b` satisfying `total : b.fst \u226b b.inl + b.snd \u226b b.inr = \ud835\udfd9 b.X`.\n\n(That is, such a bicone is a limit cone and a colimit cocone.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biproduct.lift_matrix_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biprod.unipotent_upper_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_bilimit_of_is_colimit", "description": "In a preadditive category, any finite bicone which is a colimit cocone is in fact a bilimit\n   bicone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_binary_biproduct_of_preserves_binary_product", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories that preserves (zero morphisms and) binary products\n   preserves binary biproducts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_bilimit_binary_bicone_of_is_split_mono_of_cokernel", "description": " The bicone constructed in `binary_bicone_of_split_mono_of_cokernel` is a bilimit.\nThis is a version of the splitting lemma that holds in all preadditive categories.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biprod.unipotent_upper_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone_of_is_split_mono_of_cokernel_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biprod.unipotent_lower_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biproduct.column_nonzero_of_iso'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preadditive.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_binary_biproduct.of_has_binary_product", "description": "In a preadditive category, if the product of `X` and `Y` exists, then the\n   binary biproduct of `X` and `Y` exists.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biprod.iso_elim'", "description": "If `X\u2081 \u229e X\u2082 \u2245 Y\u2081 \u229e Y\u2082` via a two-by-two matrix whose `X\u2081 \u27f6 Y\u2081` entry is an isomorphism,\nthen we can construct an isomorphism `X\u2082 \u2245 Y\u2082`, via Gaussian elimination.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preadditive.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biprod.of_components_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_bilimit_of_is_limit", "description": "In a preadditive category, any finite bicone which is a limit cone is in fact a bilimit\n   bicone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone.of_colimit_cocone_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone_of_is_split_epi_of_kernel_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biprod.map_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_biproduct_of_epi_biproduct_comparison'", "description": "If the (coproduct-like) biproduct comparison for `F` and `f` is an epimorphism, then `F`\n   preserves the biproduct of `F` and `f`. For the converse, see `map_biproduct`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone.of_limit_cone", "description": "We can turn any limit cone over a pair into a bicone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biprod.lift_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biprod.iso_elim", "description": "If `f` is an isomorphism `X\u2081 \u229e X\u2082 \u2245 Y\u2081 \u229e Y\u2082` whose `X\u2081 \u27f6 Y\u2081` entry is an isomorphism,\nthen we can construct an isomorphism `X\u2082 \u2245 Y\u2082`, via Gaussian elimination.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_binary_biproduct_of_epi_biprod_comparison'", "description": "If the (coproduct-like) biproduct comparison for `F`, `X` and `Y` is an epimorphism, then\n   `F` preserves the biproduct of `X` and `Y`. For the converse, see `map_biprod`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_binary_bilimit_of_is_limit", "description": "In a preadditive category, any binary bicone which is a limit cone is in fact a bilimit\n   bicone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_biproduct_of_preserves_coproduct", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories that preserves (zero morphisms and) finite coproducts\n   preserves finite biproducts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone_of_is_split_epi_of_kernel_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_biproducts_of_shape_of_preserves_coproducts_of_shape", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories that preserves (zero morphisms and) finite coproducts\n   preserves finite biproducts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone.of_colimit_cocone_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_products_of_shape_of_preserves_biproducts_of_shape", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories that preserves (zero morphisms and) finite biproducts\n   preserves finite products.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_biproducts_of_shape_of_preserves_products_of_shape", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories that preserves (zero morphisms and) finite products\n   preserves finite biproducts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subsingleton_preadditive_of_has_binary_biproducts", "description": "The existence of binary biproducts implies that there is at most one preadditive structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_binary_biproducts_of_preserves_binary_products", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories that preserves (zero morphisms and) binary products\n   preserves binary biproducts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biproduct.matrix_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biproduct.lift_desc_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone_of_is_split_epi_of_kernel_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone.of_limit_cone_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biprod.unipotent_lower", "description": "The unipotent lower triangular matrix\n```lean\n(1 0)\n(r 1)\n```\nas an isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biprod.lift_desc_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_binary_biproduct_of_preserves_binary_coproduct", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories that preserves (zero morphisms and) binary coproducts\n   preserves binary biproducts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_product_of_preserves_biproduct", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories that preserves (zero morphisms and) finite biproducts\n   preserves finite products.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_binary_biproduct.of_has_binary_coproduct", "description": "In a preadditive category, if the coproduct of `X` and `Y` exists, then the\n   binary biproduct of `X` and `Y` exists.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.inl_of_is_limit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biproduct.map_matrix_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_biproduct_of_preserves_product", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories that preserves (zero morphisms and) finite products\n   preserves finite biproducts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone.is_bilimit_of_cokernel_fst", "description": "If `b` is a binary bicone such that `b.fst` is a cokernel of `b.inr`, then `b` is a bilimit\n   bicone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone.is_bilimit_of_kernel_inr", "description": "If `b` is a binary bicone such that `b.inr` is a kernel of `b.fst`, then `b` is a bilimit\n   bicone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biproduct.desc_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biprod.of_components", "description": "The \"matrix\" morphism `X\u2081 \u229e X\u2082 \u27f6 Y\u2081 \u229e Y\u2082` with specified components.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone.of_colimit_cocone", "description": "We can turn any colimit cocone over a pair into a bicone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.snd_of_is_colimit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biproduct.reindex_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone.is_bilimit_of_cokernel_snd", "description": "If `b` is a binary bicone such that `b.snd` is a cokernel of `b.inl`, then `b` is a bilimit\n   bicone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biproduct.reindex", "description": "Reindex a categorical biproduct via an equivalence of the index types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_bilimit.binary_total", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_finite_biproducts.of_has_finite_products", "description": "A preadditive category with finite products has finite biproducts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_coproduct_of_preserves_biproduct", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories that preserves (zero morphisms and) finite biproducts\n   preserves finite coproducts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_bilimit.total", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biprod.of_components_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biprod.of_components_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone.of_colimit_cocone_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biproduct.lift_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.biproduct.map_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone.of_limit_cone_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_binary_bilimit_of_total", "description": "In a preadditive category, we can construct a binary biproduct for `X Y : C` from\nany binary bicone `b` satisfying `total : b.fst \u226b b.inl + b.snd \u226b b.inr = \ud835\udfd9 b.X`.\n\n(That is, such a bicone is a limit cone and a colimit cocone.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_binary_biproducts.of_has_binary_coproducts", "description": "In a preadditive category, if all binary coproducts exist, then all binary biproducts exist.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone_of_is_split_mono_of_cokernel_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.binary_bicone.of_colimit_cocone_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.obj", "description": "Applying `P` to an object of `Type`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.append_contents", "description": "append arrows of a polynomial functor application", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.comp.mk_get", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.map_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.obj.is_lawful_mvfunctor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.const.get", "description": "Destructor for the constant functor", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.supp_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor", "description": "multivariate polynomial functors", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.A", "description": "multivariate polynomial functors", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.B", "description": "multivariate polynomial functors", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.last", "description": " Split polynomial functor, get a univariate functor\nfrom a `n+1`-ary functor", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.const.get_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.liftr_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.comp", "description": "Functor composition on polynomial functors", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.comp.mk", "description": "Constructor for functor composition", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.obj.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.drop", "description": " Split polynomial functor, get a n-ary functor\nfrom a `n+1`-ary functor", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.id_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.liftp_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.const.mk_get", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.comp.get_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.comp.get", "description": "Destructor for functor composition", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.comp_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.liftp_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.map", "description": "Applying `P` to a morphism of `Type`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.const.get_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.const.mk", "description": "Constructor for the constant functor", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.const", "description": "Constant functor where the input object does not affect the output", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.comp.get_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvpfunctor.obj.mvfunctor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.full", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.lift", "description": "A functor which maps objects to objects satisfying a certain property induces a lift through\n   the full subcategory of objects satisfying that property.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.map.faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.induced_category.faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.lift.faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.lift_obj_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.inclusion_obj_lift_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.map", "description": "An implication of predicates `Z \u2192 Z'` induces a functor between full subcategories.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.induced_functor", "description": "The forgetful functor from an induced category to the original category,\nforgetting the extra data.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.induced_category", "description": "`induced_category D F`, where `F : C \u2192 D`, is a typeclass synonym for `C`,\nwhich provides a category structure so that the morphisms `X \u27f6 Y` are the morphisms\nin `D` from `F X` to `F Y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory", "description": "A subtype-like structure for full subcategories. Morphisms just ignore the property. We don't use\nactual subtypes since the simp-normal form `\u2191X` of `X.val` does not work well for full\nsubcategories.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001D>. We do not define 'strictly full' subcategories.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.obj", "description": "A subtype-like structure for full subcategories. Morphisms just ignore the property. We don't use\nactual subtypes since the simp-normal form `\u2191X` of `X.val` does not work well for full\nsubcategories.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001D>. We do not define 'strictly full' subcategories.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.property", "description": "A subtype-like structure for full subcategories. Morphisms just ignore the property. We don't use\nactual subtypes since the simp-normal form `\u2191X` of `X.val` does not work well for full\nsubcategories.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001D>. We do not define 'strictly full' subcategories.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.induced_category.has_coe_to_sort", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.induced_functor_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory_inclusion.map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.map_obj_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.induced_category.category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.inclusion_map_lift_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory_inclusion", "description": "The forgetful functor from a full subcategory into the original category\n(\"forgetting\" the condition).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.lift.full", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.lift_comp_inclusion", "description": "Composing the lift of a functor through a full subcategory with the inclusion yields the\n   original functor. Unfortunately, this is not true by definition, so we only get a natural\n   isomorphism, but it is pointwise definitionally true, see\n   `full_subcategory.inclusion_obj_lift_obj` and `full_subcategory.inclusion_map_lift_map`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.map.full", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.lift_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.map_inclusion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.induced_category.full", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.induced_functor_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.lift_comp_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.map_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory_inclusion.obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite_pure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.fintype_inter_of_right", "description": "A `fintype` instance for set intersection where the right set has a `fintype` instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.infinite_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.card_fintype_insert_of_not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite.image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.seq_of_forall_finite_exists", "description": "If `P` is some relation between terms of `\u03b3` and sets in `\u03b3`,\nsuch that every finite set `t : set \u03b3` has some `c : \u03b3` related to it,\nthen there is a recursively defined sequence `u` in `\u03b3`\nso `u n` is related to the image of `{0, 1, ..., n-1}` under `u`.\n\n(We use this later to show sequentially compact sets\nare totally bounded.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite.to_finset_eq_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite.bdd_below_bUnion", "description": "A finite union of sets which are all bounded below is still bounded below.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite_diff_Union_Ioo'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite.sInter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.infinite.of_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite_or_infinite", "descrip