ive"}, {"name": "imo1998_q2.agreed_triple.contestant", "description": "The contestant from the triple consisting of a contestant and an ordered pair of judges.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "is_coprime.of_mul_right_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.mul_add_left_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.mul_add_right_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.add_mul_left_right_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.of_mul_left_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.mul_right_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.of_add_mul_left_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.mul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.neg_right_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.of_add_mul_right_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime_one_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.of_add_mul_left_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.add_mul_right_right_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.of_mul_add_right_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.neg_left_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.of_coprime_of_dvd_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime_mul_unit_right_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime_mul_unit_left_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.mul_dvd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.of_mul_right_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.mul_add_right_left_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime_mul_unit_left_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.mul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.neg_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime_group_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.mul_add_left_right_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime", "description": " The proposition that `x` and `y` are coprime, defined to be the existence of `a` and `b` such\nthat `a * x + b * y = 1`. Note that elements with no common divisors are not necessarily coprime,\ne.g., the multivariate polynomials `x\u2081` and `x\u2082` are not coprime.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.of_mul_left_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.ne_zero", "description": "If a 2-vector `p` satisfies `is_coprime (p 0) (p 1)`, then `p \u2260 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime_mul_unit_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.add_mul_right_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_coprime_zero_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.add_mul_left_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.of_mul_add_left_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.dvd_of_dvd_mul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.of_mul_add_left_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.of_coprime_of_dvd_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime_one_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime_zero_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.add_mul_right_left_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.neg_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.add_mul_left_left_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.neg_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime_group_smul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.sq_add_sq_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.of_add_mul_right_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.is_unit_of_dvd'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime_zero_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime_mul_unit_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.mul_add_left_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.mul_add_left_left_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.mul_add_right_right_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime_group_smul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.of_mul_add_right_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.is_unit_of_dvd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.add_mul_left_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.dvd_of_dvd_mul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.mul_add_right_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.add_mul_right_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime_mul_unit_right_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.neg_neg_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime.mul_left_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.irreducible_of_eisenstein_criterion", "description": " If `f` is a non constant polynomial with coefficients in `R`, and `P` is a prime ideal in `R`,\nthen if every coefficient in `R` except the leading coefficient is in `P`, and\nthe trailing coefficient is not in `P^2` and no non units in `R` divide `f`, then `f` is\nirreducible.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.eisenstein_criterion_aux.le_nat_degree_of_map_eq_mul_X_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.eisenstein_criterion_aux.eval_zero_mem_ideal_of_eq_mul_X_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.eisenstein_criterion_aux.map_eq_C_mul_X_pow_of_forall_coeff_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.eisenstein_criterion_aux.is_unit_of_nat_degree_eq_zero_of_forall_dvd_is_unit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2081_map_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2082_obj_X_d", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2081_obj_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2081_obj_p", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2082_map_f_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2082_obj_X_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2082", "description": "The extension of `N\u2081` to the Karoubi envelope of `simplicial_object C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2082_obj_p_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2081", "description": " The functor `simplicial_object C \u2964 karoubi (chain_complex C \u2115)` which maps\n`X` to the formal direct factor of `K[X]` defined by `P_infty`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.mem_base_set.exists_common_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.Riemann", "description": " The `box_integral.integration_params` corresponding to the Riemann integral. In the\ncorresponding filter, we require that the diameters of all boxes `J` of a tagged partition are\nbounded from above by a constant upper estimate that may not depend on the geometry of `J`, and each\ntag belongs to the corresponding closed box.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.partial_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.to_filter_Union_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.Henstock_le_Riemann", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.mem_base_set.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.to_filter_distortion_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.iso_prod", "description": "Auxiliary `order_iso` with a product type used to lift a `bounded_order` structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params", "description": " An `integration_params` is a structure holding 3 boolean values used to define a filter to be\nused in the definition of a box-integrable function.\n\n* `bRiemann`: the value `tt` means that the filter corresponds to a Riemann-style integral, i.e. in\n  the definition of integrability we require a constant upper estimate `r` on the size of boxes of a\n  tagged partition; the value `ff` means that the estimate may depend on the position of the tag.\n\n* `bHenstock`: the value `tt` means that we require that each tag belongs to its own closed box; the\n  value `ff` means that we only require that tags belong to the ambient box.\n\n* `bDistortion`: the value `tt` means that `r` can depend on the maximal ratio of sides of the same\n  box of a partition. Presence of this case makes quite a few proofs harder but we can prove the\n  divergence theorem only for the filter\n  `box_integral.integration_params.GP = \u22a5 = {bRiemann := ff, bHenstock := tt, bDistortion := tt}`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.bRiemann", "description": " An `integration_params` is a structure holding 3 boolean values used to define a filter to be\nused in the definition of a box-integrable function.\n\n* `bRiemann`: the value `tt` means that the filter corresponds to a Riemann-style integral, i.e. in\n  the definition of integrability we require a constant upper estimate `r` on the size of boxes of a\n  tagged partition; the value `ff` means that the estimate may depend on the position of the tag.\n\n* `bHenstock`: the value `tt` means that we require that each tag belongs to its own closed box; the\n  value `ff` means that we only require that tags belong to the ambient box.\n\n* `bDistortion`: the value `tt` means that `r` can depend on the maximal ratio of sides of the same\n  box of a partition. Presence of this case makes quite a few proofs harder but we can prove the\n  divergence theorem only for the filter\n  `box_integral.integration_params.GP = \u22a5 = {bRiemann := ff, bHenstock := tt, bDistortion := tt}`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.bHenstock", "description": " An `integration_params` is a structure holding 3 boolean values used to define a filter to be\nused in the definition of a box-integrable function.\n\n* `bRiemann`: the value `tt` means that the filter corresponds to a Riemann-style integral, i.e. in\n  the definition of integrability we require a constant upper estimate `r` on the size of boxes of a\n  tagged partition; the value `ff` means that the estimate may depend on the position of the tag.\n\n* `bHenstock`: the value `tt` means that we require that each tag belongs to its own closed box; the\n  value `ff` means that we only require that tags belong to the ambient box.\n\n* `bDistortion`: the value `tt` means that `r` can depend on the maximal ratio of sides of the same\n  box of a partition. Presence of this case makes quite a few proofs harder but we can prove the\n  divergence theorem only for the filter\n  `box_integral.integration_params.GP = \u22a5 = {bRiemann := ff, bHenstock := tt, bDistortion := tt}`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.bDistortion", "description": " An `integration_params` is a structure holding 3 boolean values used to define a filter to be\nused in the definition of a box-integrable function.\n\n* `bRiemann`: the value `tt` means that the filter corresponds to a Riemann-style integral, i.e. in\n  the definition of integrability we require a constant upper estimate `r` on the size of boxes of a\n  tagged partition; the value `ff` means that the estimate may depend on the position of the tag.\n\n* `bHenstock`: the value `tt` means that we require that each tag belongs to its own closed box; the\n  value `ff` means that we only require that tags belong to the ambient box.\n\n* `bDistortion`: the value `tt` means that `r` can depend on the maximal ratio of sides of the same\n  box of a partition. Presence of this case makes quite a few proofs harder but we can prove the\n  divergence theorem only for the filter\n  `box_integral.integration_params.GP = \u22a5 = {bRiemann := ff, bHenstock := tt, bDistortion := tt}`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.mem_base_set.mono'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.to_filter_distortion_Union_ne_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.has_basis_to_filter_distortion_Union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.has_basis_to_filter_distortion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.to_filter_Union_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.to_filter_distortion_Union_ne_bot'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.r_cond.min", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.mem_base_set.filter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.to_filter_inf_Union_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.has_le.le.decidable_rel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.tendsto_embed_box_to_filter_Union_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.has_basis_to_filter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.bUnion_tagged_mem_base_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.r_cond", "description": "A predicate saying that in case `l.bRiemann = tt`, the function `r` is a constant.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.to_filter_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.eventually_is_partition", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.McShane", "description": " The `box_integral.integration_params` corresponding to the McShane integral. In the\ncorresponding filter, we require that the tagged partition is subordinate to a (possibly,\ndiscontinuous) positive function `r`; the tags may be outside of the corresponding closed box\n(but still inside the ambient closed box `I.Icc`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.Henstock_le_McShane", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.has_basis_to_filter_Union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.GP", "description": " The `box_integral.integration_params` corresponding to the generalized Perron integral. In the\ncorresponding filter, we require that the tagged partition is subordinate to a (possibly,\ndiscontinuous) positive function `r` and each tag belongs to the corresponding closed box. We also\nrequire an upper estimate on the distortion of all boxes of the partition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.r_cond_of_bRiemann_eq_ff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.Henstock", "description": " The `box_integral.integration_params` corresponding to the Henstock-Kurzweil integral. In the\ncorresponding filter, we require that the tagged partition is subordinate to a (possibly,\ndiscontinuous) positive function `r` and each tag belongs to the corresponding closed box.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.to_filter_distortion_Union", "description": " A set `s : set (tagged_prepartition I)` belongs to `l.to_filter_distortion_Union I c \u03c0\u2080` if\nthere exists a function `r : \u211d\u207f \u2192 (0, \u221e)` (or a constant `r` if `l.bRiemann = tt`) such that `s`\ncontains each prepartition `\u03c0` such that `l.mem_base_set I c r \u03c0` and `\u03c0.Union = \u03c0\u2080.Union`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.mem_base_set", "description": " The predicate corresponding to a base set of the filter defined by an\n`integration_params`. It says that\n\n* if `l.bHenstock`, then `\u03c0` is a Henstock prepartition, i.e. each tag belongs to the corresponding\n  closed box;\n* `\u03c0` is subordinate to `r`;\n* if `l.bDistortion`, then the distortion of each box in `\u03c0` is less than or equal to `c`;\n* if `l.bDistortion`, then there exists a prepartition `\u03c0'` with distortion `\u2264 c` that covers\n  exactly `I \\ \u03c0.Union`.\n\nThe last condition is automatically verified for partitions, and is used in the proof of the\nSacks-Henstock inequality to compare two prepartitions covering the same part of the box.\n\nIt is also automatically satisfied for any `c > 1`, see TODO section of the module docstring for\ndetails.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.mem_base_set.is_subordinate", "description": " The predicate corresponding to a base set of the filter defined by an\n`integration_params`. It says that\n\n* if `l.bHenstock`, then `\u03c0` is a Henstock prepartition, i.e. each tag belongs to the corresponding\n  closed box;\n* `\u03c0` is subordinate to `r`;\n* if `l.bDistortion`, then the distortion of each box in `\u03c0` is less than or equal to `c`;\n* if `l.bDistortion`, then there exists a prepartition `\u03c0'` with distortion `\u2264 c` that covers\n  exactly `I \\ \u03c0.Union`.\n\nThe last condition is automatically verified for partitions, and is used in the proof of the\nSacks-Henstock inequality to compare two prepartitions covering the same part of the box.\n\nIt is also automatically satisfied for any `c > 1`, see TODO section of the module docstring for\ndetails.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.mem_base_set.is_Henstock", "description": " The predicate corresponding to a base set of the filter defined by an\n`integration_params`. It says that\n\n* if `l.bHenstock`, then `\u03c0` is a Henstock prepartition, i.e. each tag belongs to the corresponding\n  closed box;\n* `\u03c0` is subordinate to `r`;\n* if `l.bDistortion`, then the distortion of each box in `\u03c0` is less than or equal to `c`;\n* if `l.bDistortion`, then there exists a prepartition `\u03c0'` with distortion `\u2264 c` that covers\n  exactly `I \\ \u03c0.Union`.\n\nThe last condition is automatically verified for partitions, and is used in the proof of the\nSacks-Henstock inequality to compare two prepartitions covering the same part of the box.\n\nIt is also automatically satisfied for any `c > 1`, see TODO section of the module docstring for\ndetails.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.mem_base_set.distortion_le", "description": " The predicate corresponding to a base set of the filter defined by an\n`integration_params`. It says that\n\n* if `l.bHenstock`, then `\u03c0` is a Henstock prepartition, i.e. each tag belongs to the corresponding\n  closed box;\n* `\u03c0` is subordinate to `r`;\n* if `l.bDistortion`, then the distortion of each box in `\u03c0` is less than or equal to `c`;\n* if `l.bDistortion`, then there exists a prepartition `\u03c0'` with distortion `\u2264 c` that covers\n  exactly `I \\ \u03c0.Union`.\n\nThe last condition is automatically verified for partitions, and is used in the proof of the\nSacks-Henstock inequality to compare two prepartitions covering the same part of the box.\n\nIt is also automatically satisfied for any `c > 1`, see TODO section of the module docstring for\ndetails.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.mem_base_set.exists_compl", "description": " The predicate corresponding to a base set of the filter defined by an\n`integration_params`. It says that\n\n* if `l.bHenstock`, then `\u03c0` is a Henstock prepartition, i.e. each tag belongs to the corresponding\n  closed box;\n* `\u03c0` is subordinate to `r`;\n* if `l.bDistortion`, then the distortion of each box in `\u03c0` is less than or equal to `c`;\n* if `l.bDistortion`, then there exists a prepartition `\u03c0'` with distortion `\u2264 c` that covers\n  exactly `I \\ \u03c0.Union`.\n\nThe last condition is automatically verified for partitions, and is used in the proof of the\nSacks-Henstock inequality to compare two prepartitions covering the same part of the box.\n\nIt is also automatically satisfied for any `c > 1`, see TODO section of the module docstring for\ndetails.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.mem_base_set.union_compl_to_subordinate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.to_filter_Union_ne_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.has_basis_to_filter_Union_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.r_cond.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.to_filter_distortion_ne_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.to_filter_distortion", "description": " A set `s : set (tagged_prepartition I)` belongs to `l.to_filter_distortion I c` if there exists\na function `r : \u211d\u207f \u2192 (0, \u221e)` (or a constant `r` if `l.bRiemann = tt`) such that `s` contains each\nprepartition `\u03c0` such that `l.mem_base_set I c r \u03c0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.GP_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.to_filter_Union", "description": " A set `s : set (tagged_prepartition I)` belongs to `l.to_filter_Union I \u03c0\u2080` if for any `c : \u211d\u22650`\nthere exists a function `r : \u211d\u207f \u2192 (0, \u221e)` (or a constant `r` if `l.bRiemann = tt`) such that `s`\ncontains each prepartition `\u03c0` such that `l.mem_base_set I c r \u03c0` and `\u03c0.Union = \u03c0\u2080.Union`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.bounded_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.exists_mem_base_set_le_Union_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.to_filter_ne_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.exists_mem_base_set_is_partition", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.equiv_prod", "description": "Auxiliary equivalence with a product type used to lift an order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.inhabited", "description": " The value\n`box_integral.integration_params.GP = \u22a5` (`bRiemann = ff`, `bHenstock = tt`, `bDistortion = tt`)\ncorresponds to a generalization of the Henstock integral such that the Divergence theorem holds true\nwithout additional integrability assumptions, see the module docstring for details.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.integration_params.to_filter", "description": " A set `s : set (tagged_prepartition I)` belongs to `l.to_filter I` if for any `c : \u211d\u22650` there\nexists a function `r : \u211d\u207f \u2192 (0, \u221e)` (or a constant `r` if `l.bRiemann = tt`) such that\n`s` contains each prepartition `\u03c0` such that `l.mem_base_set I c r \u03c0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.iterate_verschiebung_mul_coeff", "description": "This is a slightly specialized form of [Hazewinkel, *Witt Vectors*][Haze09] 6.2 equation 5.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.iterate_verschiebung_coeff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.coeff_p_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.verschiebung_mul_frobenius", "description": "The \u201cprojection formula\u201d for Frobenius and Verschiebung.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.iterate_verschiebung_mul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.fraction_ring.p_nonzero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.iterate_frobenius_coeff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.coeff_p_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.p_nonzero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.coeff_p_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.verschiebung_frobenius_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.coeff_p_pow_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.verschiebung_zmod", "description": "Verschiebung is the same as multiplication by `p` on the ring of Witt vectors of `zmod p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.iterate_verschiebung_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.frobenius_verschiebung", "description": "The composition of Frobenius and Verschiebung is multiplication by `p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.coeff_p", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.mul_char_p_coeff_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.mul_char_p_coeff_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.verschiebung_frobenius", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.Q_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.Q", "description": "`Q q` is the complement projection associated to `P q`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.Q_f_0_eq", "description": "All the `Q q` coincide with `0` in degree 0.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.higher_faces_vanish.of_P", "description": " This lemma expresses the vanishing of\n`(P q).f (n+1) \u226b X.\u03b4 k : X _[n+1] \u27f6 X _[n]` when `k\u22600` and `k\u2265n-q+2`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.nat_trans_Q_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.P_add_Q_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.higher_faces_vanish.comp_P_eq_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.map_P", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.nat_trans_P", "description": "For each `q`, `P q` is a natural transformation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.comp_P_eq_self_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.higher_faces_vanish.comp_P_eq_self_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.Q_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.Q_f_naturality_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.P_f_0_eq", "description": "All the `P q` coincide with `\ud835\udfd9 _` in degree 0.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.Q_f_idem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.nat_trans_P_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.Q_idem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.Q_idem_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.P_f_idem_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.P_idem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.P_add_Q", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.map_Q", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.Q_f_idem_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.P_idem_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.P_f_idem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.nat_trans_Q", "description": "For each `q`, `Q q` is a natural transformation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.P_f_naturality_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.P_f_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.P", "description": " This is the inductive definition of the projections `P q : K[X] \u27f6 K[X]`,\nwith `P 0 := \ud835\udfd9 _` and `P (q+1) := P q \u226b (\ud835\udfd9 _ + H\u03c3 q)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.Q_f_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.coimage_image_comparison_eq_coimage_image_comparison'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.factor_thru_coimage", "description": "There is a canonical monomorphism `i : coimage f \u27f6 Q`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.mono_factor_thru_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.coimage", "description": "The cokernel of the kernel of `f` is called the (abelian) coimage of `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.image.fac", "description": "`f` factors through its image via the canonical morphism `p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.image.fac_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.factor_thru_image", "description": "There is a canonical epimorphism `p : P \u27f6 image f` for every `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.coimage_image_factorisation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.coimage_image_comparison", "description": "The canonical map from the abelian coimage to the abelian image.\nIn any abelian category this is an isomorphism.\n\nConversely, any additive category with kernels and cokernels and\nin which this is always an isomorphism, is abelian.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0107>", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.image", "description": "The kernel of the cokernel of `f` is called the (abelian) image of `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.coimage_image_factorisation_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.coimage_image_comparison'", "description": "An alternative formulation of the canonical map from the abelian coimage to the abelian image.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.epi_factor_thru_coimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.coimage.\u03c0", "description": "The projection onto the coimage.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.image.\u03b9", "description": "The inclusion of the image into the codomain.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.coimage.fac", "description": "`f` factors through its coimage via the canonical morphism `p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.single_le_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.lattice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.disjoint_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.inf_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.tsub", "description": " This is called `tsub` for truncated subtraction, to distinguish it with subtraction in an\nadditive group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.preorder", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.ordered_cancel_add_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.coe_fn_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.support_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.le_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.bot_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.has_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.support_sup_union_support_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.sup_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.canonically_ordered_add_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.decidable_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.le_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.support_inf_union_support_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.semilattice_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.order_embedding_to_fun_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.subset_support_tsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.ordered_add_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.support_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.order_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.has_ordered_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.order_embedding_to_fun", "description": "The order on `dfinsupp`s over a partial order embeds into the order on functions", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.coe_tsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.le_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.add_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.has_le.le.contravariant_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.partial_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.single_tsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.semilattice_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.support_tsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.tsub_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.core.inclusion", "description": "The core of a category is naturally included in the category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.of_equiv_functor", "description": "`of_equiv_functor m` lifts a type-level `equiv_functor`\nto a categorical functor `core (Type u\u2081) \u2964 core (Type u\u2082)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.core.id_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.core", "description": " The core of a category C is the groupoid whose morphisms are all the\nisomorphisms of C.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.core_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.core.functor_to_core", "description": "A functor from a groupoid to a category C factors through the core of C.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.core.forget_functor_to_core", "description": "We can functorially associate to any functor from a groupoid to the core of a category `C`,\na functor from the groupoid to `C`, simply by composing with the embedding `core C \u2964 C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.core.comp_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.core.inclusion.category_theory.faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.aux_uniformity_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.basis_fun_repr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.basis_fun_equiv_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.lipschitz_with_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.pi_Lp_congr_left_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.norm_eq_csupr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.has_edist", "description": " Endowing the space `pi_Lp p \u03b2` with the `L^p` edistance. We register this instance\nseparate from `pi_Lp.pseudo_emetric` since the latter requires the type class hypothesis\n`[fact (1 \u2264 p)]` in order to prove the triangle inequality.\n\nRegistering this separately allows for a future emetric-like structure on `pi_Lp p \u03b2` for `p < 1`\nsatisfying a relaxed triangle inequality. The terminology for this varies throughout the\nliterature, but it is sometimes called a *quasi-metric* or *semi-metric*.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.norm_eq_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.linear_equiv", "description": "`pi_Lp.equiv` as a linear equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.dist_eq_of_L2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.seminormed_add_comm_group", "description": " seminormed group instance on the product of finitely many normed groups, using the `L^p`\nnorm.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.add_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.pi_Lp_congr_left_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.linear_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.basis_fun_eq_pi_basis_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.nndist_eq_supr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.pseudo_emetric_space", "description": " pseudoemetric space instance on the product of finitely many pseudoemetric spaces, using the\n`L^p` pseudoedistance, and having as uniformity the product uniformity.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.has_dist", "description": " Endowing the space `pi_Lp p \u03b2` with the `L^p` distance. We register this instance\nseparate from `pi_Lp.pseudo_metric` since the latter requires the type class hypothesis\n`[fact (1 \u2264 p)]` in order to prove the triangle inequality.\n\nRegistering this separately allows for a future metric-like structure on `pi_Lp p \u03b2` for `p < 1`\nsatisfying a relaxed triangle inequality. The terminology for this varies throughout the\nliterature, but it is sometimes called a *quasi-metric* or *semi-metric*.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.nnnorm_equiv_symm_const'", "description": " When `is_empty \u03b9`, this lemma does not hold without the additional assumption `p \u2260 \u221e` because\nthe left-hand side simplifies to `0`, while the right-hand side simplifies to `\u2016b\u2016\u208a`. See\n`pi_Lp.nnnorm_equiv_symm_const` for a version which exchanges the hypothesis `nonempty \u03b9`.\nfor `p \u2260 \u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.smul_comm_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.equiv", "description": " Canonical bijection between `pi_Lp p \u03b1` and the original Pi type. We introduce it to be able\nto compare the `L^p` and `L^\u221e` distances through it.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.pseudo_metric_aux", "description": " Endowing the space `pi_Lp p \u03b1` with the `L^p` pseudometric structure. This definition is not\nsatisfactory, as it does not register the fact that the topology, the uniform structure, and the\nbornology coincide with the product ones. Therefore, we do not register it as an instance. Using\nthis as a temporary pseudoemetric space instance, we will show that the uniform structure is equal\n(but not defeq) to the product one, and then register an instance in which we replace the uniform\nstructure and the bornology by the product ones using this pseudometric space,\n`pseudo_metric_space.replace_uniformity`, and `pseudo_metric_space.replace_bornology`.\n\nSee note [reducible non-instances]", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.antilipschitz_with_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.basis_to_matrix_basis_fun_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.pi_Lp_congr_left_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.norm_equiv_symm_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.dist_eq_csupr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.nnnorm_eq_csupr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.smul_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.pseudo_metric_space", "description": " pseudometric space instance on the product of finitely many psuedometric spaces, using the\n`L^p` distance, and having as uniformity the product uniformity.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.emetric_space", "description": " emetric space instance on the product of finitely many emetric spaces, using the `L^p`\nedistance, and having as uniformity the product uniformity.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.antilipschitz_with_equiv_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.edist_eq_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp", "description": " A copy of a Pi type, on which we will put the `L^p` distance. Since the Pi type itself is\nalready endowed with the `L^\u221e` distance, we need the type synonym to avoid confusing typeclass\nresolution. Also, we let it depend on `p`, to get a whole family of type on which we can put\ndifferent distances.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.sub_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.equiv_symm_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.uniform_continuous_equiv_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.edist_comm", "description": " This holds independent of `p` and does not require `[fact (1 \u2264 p)]`. We keep it separate\nfrom `pi_Lp.pseudo_emetric_space` so it can be used also for `p < 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.dist_eq_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.equiv\u2097\u1d62", "description": " The canonical map `pi_Lp.equiv` between `pi_Lp \u221e \u03b2` and `\u03a0 i, \u03b2 i` as a linear isometric\nequivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.edist_self", "description": " This holds independent of `p` and does not require `[fact (1 \u2264 p)]`. We keep it separate\nfrom `pi_Lp.pseudo_emetric_space` so it can be used also for `p < 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.equiv_symm_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.nnnorm_equiv_symm_const", "description": " When `p = \u221e`, this lemma does not hold without the additional assumption `nonempty \u03b9` because\nthe left-hand side simplifies to `0`, while the right-hand side simplifies to `\u2016b\u2016\u208a`. See\n`pi_Lp.nnnorm_equiv_symm_const'` for a version which exchanges the hypothesis `p \u2260 \u221e` for\n`nonempty \u03b9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.uniform_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.norm_eq_of_L2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.norm_equiv_symm_const'", "description": " When `is_empty \u03b9`, this lemma does not hold without the additional assumption `p \u2260 \u221e` because\nthe left-hand side simplifies to `0`, while the right-hand side simplifies to `\u2016b\u2016\u208a`. See\n`pi_Lp.norm_equiv_symm_const` for a version which exchanges the hypothesis `nonempty \u03b9`.\nfor `p \u2260 \u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.continuous_linear_equiv", "description": "`pi_Lp.equiv` as a continuous linear equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.edist_eq_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.equiv_symm_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.continuous_linear_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.continuous_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.lipschitz_with_equiv_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.equiv_symm_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.equiv_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.dist_eq_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.nnnorm_equiv_symm_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.aux_cobounded_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.normed_space", "description": "The product of finitely many normed spaces is a normed space, with the `L^p` norm.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.nnnorm_eq_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.continuous_equiv_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.uniform_continuous_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.edist_equiv_symm_single_same", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.bornology", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.finite_dimensional", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.has_norm", "description": " Endowing the space `pi_Lp p \u03b2` with the `L^p` norm. We register this instance\nseparate from `pi_Lp.seminormed_add_comm_group` since the latter requires the type class hypothesis\n`[fact (1 \u2264 p)]` in order to prove the triangle inequality.\n\nRegistering this separately allows for a future norm-like structure on `pi_Lp p \u03b2` for `p < 1`\nsatisfying a relaxed triangle inequality. These are called *quasi-norms*.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.nnnorm_eq_of_L2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.neg_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.equiv_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.basis_fun_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.equiv_symm_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.normed_add_comm_group", "description": "normed group instance on the product of finitely many normed groups, using the `L^p` norm.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.norm_eq_of_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.continuous_linear_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.nndist_equiv_symm_single_same", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.equiv_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.is_scalar_tower", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.continuous_linear_equiv_to_linear_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.norm_equiv_symm_const", "description": " When `p = \u221e`, this lemma does not hold without the additional assumption `nonempty \u03b9` because\nthe left-hand side simplifies to `0`, while the right-hand side simplifies to `\u2016b\u2016\u208a`. See\n`pi_Lp.norm_equiv_symm_const'` for a version which exchanges the hypothesis `p \u2260 \u221e` for\n`nonempty \u03b9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.basis_fun", "description": "A version of `pi.basis_fun` for `pi_Lp`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.equiv_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.nnnorm_equiv_symm_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.infty_equiv_isometry", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.zero_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.norm_eq_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.metric_space", "description": " metric space instance on the product of finitely many metric spaces, using the `L^p` distance,\nand having as uniformity the product uniformity.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.pseudo_emetric_aux", "description": " Endowing the space `pi_Lp p \u03b2` with the `L^p` pseudoemetric structure. This definition is not\nsatisfactory, as it does not register the fact that the topology and the uniform structure coincide\nwith the product one. Therefore, we do not register it as an instance. Using this as a temporary\npseudoemetric space instance, we will show that the uniform structure is equal (but not defeq) to\nthe product one, and then register an instance in which we replace the uniform structure by the\nproduct one using this pseudoemetric space and `pseudo_emetric_space.replace_uniformity`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.dist_equiv_symm_single_same", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.basis_fun_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.norm_equiv_symm_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.nndist_eq_of_L2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.nndist_eq_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.supr_edist_ne_top_aux", "description": " An auxiliary lemma used twice in the proof of `pi_Lp.pseudo_metric_aux` below. Not intended for\nuse outside this file.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.linear_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.edist_eq_of_L2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.norm_sq_eq_of_L2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.equiv_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_Lp.edist_eq_supr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.pi_Lp_congr_left", "description": " An equivalence of finite domains induces a linearly isometric equivalence of finitely supported\nfunctions", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.prove_eq_fin", "description": "If `a b : fin n`, then `prove_eq_fin a b` proves `a = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.reduce_fin", "description": " Given `a : fin n`, `eval_fin ff n a` returns `(b, p)` where `p : normalize_fin n a b`, and\n`eval_fin tt n a` returns `p : normalize_fin_lt n a b`. Unlike `eval_fin`, this also does reduction\nof the numeral `b`; for example `reduce_fin ff 6 (5 + 5 : fin 6)` returns `4`. As a result, it\nfails if `n` is a variable, for example `reduce_fin ff (n+1) (5 + 5 : fin (n+1))` fails.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.prove_le_fin'", "description": " If `a b : fin n` and `a'` and `b'` are as returned by `eval_fin`,\nthen `prove_le_fin' n a b a' b'` proves `a \u2264 b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin_lt", "description": " `normalize_fin_lt n a b` means that `a : fin n` is equivalent to `b : \u2115` in the embedding\nsense - that is, `\u2191a = b`. This is used for operations that treat `fin n` as the subset\n`{0, ..., n-1}` of `\u2115`. For example, `fin.succ : fin n \u2192 fin (n+1)` is thought of as the successor\nfunction, but it does not lift to a map `zmod n \u2192 zmod (n+1)`; this addition only makes sense if\nthe input is strictly less than `n`.\n\n`normalize_fin_lt n a b` is equivalent to `normalize_fin n a b \u2227 b < n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.prove_eq_fin'", "description": " If `a b : fin n` and `a'` and `b'` are as returned by `eval_fin`,\nthen `prove_eq_fin' n a b a' b'` proves `a = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.eval_ineq", "description": " A `norm_num` extension that evaluates equalities and inequalities on the type `fin n`.\n\n```lean\nexample : (5 : fin 7) = fin.succ (fin.succ 3) := by norm_num\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin.one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin_lt.cast_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin.eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin.zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.get_fin_type", "description": "Get `n` such that `a : fin n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin_lt.nat_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin_lt.lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.eval_rel", "description": " The common prep work for the cases in `eval_ineq`. Given inputs `a b : fin n`, it calls\n`f n a' b' na nb` where `a'` and `b'` are the result of `eval_fin` and `na` and `nb` are\n`a' % n` and `b' % n` as natural numbers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.as_numeral", "description": " Evaluates `e : fin n` to a natural number less than `n`. Returns `none` if it is not a natural\nnumber or greater than `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin_lt.zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin.cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.eval_fin_m.reset", "description": " Evaluates a monadic action with a fresh `n` cache, and restore the old cache on completion of\nthe action. This is used when evaluating a tactic in the context of a different `n` than the parent\ncontext. For example if we are evaluating `fin.succ a`, then `a : fin n` and\n`fin.succ a : fin (n+1)`, so the parent cache will be about `n+1` and we need a separate cache for\n`n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin_lt.of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.eval_prove_fin", "description": "Given a function with the type of `prove_eq_fin'`, evaluates it with the given `a` and `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.eval_fin_m.run", "description": "Run an `eval_fin_m` action with a new cache and discard the cache after evaluation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.match_fin_result", "description": " The expression constructors recognized by the `eval_fin` evaluator. This is used instead of a\ndirect expr pattern match because expr pattern matches generate very large terms under the\nhood so going via an intermediate inductive type like this is more efficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.match_fin_result.zero", "description": " The expression constructors recognized by the `eval_fin` evaluator. This is used instead of a\ndirect expr pattern match because expr pattern matches generate very large terms under the\nhood so going via an intermediate inductive type like this is more efficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.match_fin_result.one", "description": " The expression constructors recognized by the `eval_fin` evaluator. This is used instead of a\ndirect expr pattern match because expr pattern matches generate very large terms under the\nhood so going via an intermediate inductive type like this is more efficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.match_fin_result.add", "description": " The expression constructors recognized by the `eval_fin` evaluator. This is used instead of a\ndirect expr pattern match because expr pattern matches generate very large terms under the\nhood so going via an intermediate inductive type like this is more efficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.match_fin_result.mul", "description": " The expression constructors recognized by the `eval_fin` evaluator. This is used instead of a\ndirect expr pattern match because expr pattern matches generate very large terms under the\nhood so going via an intermediate inductive type like this is more efficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.match_fin_result.bit0", "description": " The expression constructors recognized by the `eval_fin` evaluator. This is used instead of a\ndirect expr pattern match because expr pattern matches generate very large terms under the\nhood so going via an intermediate inductive type like this is more efficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.match_fin_result.bit1", "description": " The expression constructors recognized by the `eval_fin` evaluator. This is used instead of a\ndirect expr pattern match because expr pattern matches generate very large terms under the\nhood so going via an intermediate inductive type like this is more efficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.match_fin_result.succ", "description": " The expression constructors recognized by the `eval_fin` evaluator. This is used instead of a\ndirect expr pattern match because expr pattern matches generate very large terms under the\nhood so going via an intermediate inductive type like this is more efficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.match_fin_result.cast_lt", "description": " The expression constructors recognized by the `eval_fin` evaluator. This is used instead of a\ndirect expr pattern match because expr pattern matches generate very large terms under the\nhood so going via an intermediate inductive type like this is more efficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.match_fin_result.cast_le", "description": " The expression constructors recognized by the `eval_fin` evaluator. This is used instead of a\ndirect expr pattern match because expr pattern matches generate very large terms under the\nhood so going via an intermediate inductive type like this is more efficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.match_fin_result.cast", "description": " The expression constructors recognized by the `eval_fin` evaluator. This is used instead of a\ndirect expr pattern match because expr pattern matches generate very large terms under the\nhood so going via an intermediate inductive type like this is more efficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.match_fin_result.cast_add", "description": " The expression constructors recognized by the `eval_fin` evaluator. This is used instead of a\ndirect expr pattern match because expr pattern matches generate very large terms under the\nhood so going via an intermediate inductive type like this is more efficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.match_fin_result.cast_succ", "description": " The expression constructors recognized by the `eval_fin` evaluator. This is used instead of a\ndirect expr pattern match because expr pattern matches generate very large terms under the\nhood so going via an intermediate inductive type like this is more efficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.match_fin_result.add_nat", "description": " The expression constructors recognized by the `eval_fin` evaluator. This is used instead of a\ndirect expr pattern match because expr pattern matches generate very large terms under the\nhood so going via an intermediate inductive type like this is more efficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.match_fin_result.nat_add", "description": " The expression constructors recognized by the `eval_fin` evaluator. This is used instead of a\ndirect expr pattern match because expr pattern matches generate very large terms under the\nhood so going via an intermediate inductive type like this is more efficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin_lt.cast_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.eval_fin_lt", "description": " `eval_fin_lt n a` expects that `a : fin n`, and produces `(b, p)` where\n`p : normalize_fin_lt n a b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin_lt.reduce", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin.reduce", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin.bit1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin_lt.cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.reduce_fin'", "description": " `reduce_fin lt n a (a', pa)` expects that `pa : normalize_fin n a a'` where `a'`\nis a natural numeral, and produces `(b, pb)` where `pb : normalize_fin n a b` if `lt` is false, or\n`pb : normalize_fin_lt n a b` if `lt` is true. In either case, `b` will be chosen to be less than\n`n`, but if `lt` is true then we also prove it. This requires that `n` can be evaluated to a\nnumeral.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.eval_fin_num", "description": " Given `a : fin n`, returns `(b, \u22a2 a = b)` where `b` is a normalized fin numeral. Fails if `a`\nis already normalized.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.eval_fin_m.has_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.prove_le_fin", "description": "If `a b : fin n`, then `prove_le_fin a b` proves `a \u2264 b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin.le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.eval_fin_m.alternative", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin_lt.add_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.eval_fin", "description": " Given `a : fin n`, `eval_fin a` returns `(b, p)` where `p : normalize_fin n a b`. This function\ndoes no reduction of the numeral `b`; for example `eval_fin (5 + 5 : fin 6)` returns `10`. It works\neven if `n` is a variable, for example `eval_fin (5 + 5 : fin (n+1))` also returns `10`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.match_fin", "description": " Match a fin expression to a `match_fin_result`, for easier pattern matching in the\nevaluator.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.prove_lt_fin'", "description": " If `a b : fin n` and `a'` and `b'` are as returned by `eval_fin`,\nthen `prove_lt_fin' n a b a' b'` proves `a < b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.eval_fin_m.monad", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.eval_fin_m.eval_n", "description": " Given `n`, returns a tuple `(nn, n', p)` where `p` is a proof of `n = n'` and `nn` is `n`\nevaluated to a natural number. The result of the evaluation is cached for future references.\nFuture calls to this function must use the same value of `n`, unless it is in a sub-context\ncreated by `eval_fin_m.reset`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin_lt.coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.prove_lt_fin", "description": "If `a b : fin n`, then `prove_lt_fin a b` proves `a < b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.eval_fin_m.lift_ic", "description": "Lifts an `instance_cache` tactic into the `eval_fin_m` monad.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin.lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin_lt.succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.eval_fin_m", "description": " The monad for the `norm_fin` internal tactics. The state consists of an instance cache for `\u2115`,\nand a tuple `(nn, n', p)` where `p` is a proof of `n = n'` and `nn` is `n` evaluated to a natural\nnumber. (`n` itself is implicit.)  It is in an `option` because it is lazily initialized - for many\n`n` we will never need this information, and indeed eagerly computing it would make some reductions\nfail spuriously if `n` is not a numeral.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.match_fin_coe_fn", "description": " Match a fin expression of the form `(coe_fn f a)` where `f` is some fin function. Several fin\nfunctions are written this way: for example `cast_le : n \u2264 m \u2192 fin n \u21aao fin m` is not actually a\nfunction but rather an order embedding with a coercion to a function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin_lt.cast_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin", "description": " `normalize_fin n a b` means that `a : fin n` is equivalent to `b : \u2115` in the modular sense -\nthat is, `\u2191a \u2261 b (mod n)`. This is used for translating the algebraic operations: addition,\nmultiplication, zero and one, which use modulo for reduction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin.bit0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.prove_lt_ge_fin", "description": " Given `a b : fin n`, proves either `(n, tt, p)` where `p : a < b` or\n`(n, ff, p)` where `p : b \u2264 a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin_lt.cast_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.norm_fin", "description": "Rewrites occurrences of fin expressions to normal form anywhere in the goal.\nThe `norm_num` extension will only rewrite fin expressions if they appear in equalities and\ninequalities. For example if the goal is `P (2 + 2 : fin 3)` then `norm_num` will not do anything\nbut `norm_fin` will reduce the goal to `P 1`.\n\n```lean\nexample : (5 : fin 7) = fin.succ (fin.succ 3) := by norm_num\nexample (P : fin 7 \u2192 Prop) (h : P 5) : P (fin.succ (fin.succ 3)) := by norm_fin; exact h\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.eval_fin_m.lift", "description": "Lifts a tactic into the `eval_fin_m` monad.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.norm_fin", "description": " Rewrites occurrences of fin expressions to normal form anywhere in the goal.\nThe `norm_num` extension will only rewrite fin expressions if they appear in equalities and\ninequalities. For example if the goal is `P (2 + 2 : fin 3)` then `norm_num` will not do anything\nbut `norm_fin` will reduce the goal to `P 1`.\n\n(The reason this is not part of `norm_num` is because evaluation of fin numerals uses a top down\nevaluation strategy while `norm_num` works bottom up; also determining whether a normalization\nwill work is expensive, meaning that unrelated uses of `norm_num` would be slowed down with this\nas a plugin.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.normalize_fin_lt.mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.eval_fin_lt'", "description": " `eval_fin_lt' eval_fin n a` expects that `a : fin n`, and produces `(b, p)` where\n`p : normalize_fin_lt n a b`. (It is mutually recursive with `eval_fin` which is why it takes the\nfunction as an argument.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.mk_fin_numeral", "description": " Given expressions `n` and `m` such that `n` is definitionally equal to `m.succ`, and\na natural numeral `a`, proves `(b, \u22a2 normalize_fin n b a)`, where `n` and `m` are both used\nin the construction of the numeral `b : fin n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_fin.prove_eq_ne_fin", "description": " Given `a b : fin n`, proves either `(n, tt, p)` where `p : a = b` or\n`(n, ff, p)` where `p : a \u2260 b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sin_eq_iff_eq_or_add_eq_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.tan_coe_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sign_two_zsmul_eq_sign_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.cos_sin_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.continuous_cos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.tan_eq_of_two_nsmul_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.two_nsmul_eq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_coe_eq_self_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.two_nsmul_coe_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.two_nsmul_to_real_eq_two_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_neg_pi_div_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.nsmul_eq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.circular_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_coe_eq_self_add_two_pi_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sign_pi_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.neg_eq_self_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.tan_eq_of_two_zsmul_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.cos_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.neg_pi_div_two_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.continuous_sin", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sin_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.two_zsmul_to_real_eq_two_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sign_neg_coe_nonpos_of_nonneg_of_le_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.coe_to_Ico_mod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.ne_neg_self_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.coe_to_Ioc_mod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sin_pi_div_two_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sin_ne_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sign_coe_pi_div_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sin_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.coe_coe_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.continuous_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.cos_eq_iff_coe_eq_or_eq_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.cos_sq_add_sin_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sign_pi_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.cos_antiperiodic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sign", "description": " The sign of a `real.angle` is `0` if the angle is `0` or `\u03c0`, `1` if the angle is strictly\nbetween `0` and `\u03c0` and `-1` is the angle is strictly between `-\u03c0` and `0`. It is defined as the\nsign of the sine of the angle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_eq_pi_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sign_antiperiodic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.cos_eq_iff_eq_or_eq_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.has_coe_t", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.angle_eq_iff_two_pi_dvd_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sign_ne_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sign_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.two_zsmul_coe_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.pi_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sign_add_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.two_nsmul_to_real_eq_two_mul_sub_two_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_neg_iff_sign_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sin_coe_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.two_zsmul_ne_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.abs_cos_eq_of_two_zsmul_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real", "description": "Convert a `real.angle` to a real number in the interval `Ioc (-\u03c0) \u03c0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.coe_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_coe_eq_self_iff_mem_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.abs_sin_eq_of_two_nsmul_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sin_sub_pi_div_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.eq_neg_self_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.cos_to_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.tan_eq_inv_of_two_nsmul_add_two_nsmul_eq_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on.angle_sign_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sign_two_nsmul_eq_sign_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.cos_pos_iff_abs_to_real_lt_pi_div_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sin_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.abs_sin_eq_of_two_zsmul_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.tan_eq_inv_of_two_zsmul_add_two_zsmul_eq_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.abs_cos_eq_abs_sin_of_two_zsmul_add_two_zsmul_eq_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.cos_eq_real_cos_iff_eq_or_eq_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.coe_int_mul_eq_zsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.nsmul_to_real_eq_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sign_coe_nonneg_of_nonneg_of_le_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_pi_div_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.two_zsmul_to_real_eq_two_mul_add_two_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.coe_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.neg_coe_abs_to_real_of_sign_nonpos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.cos_neg_iff_pi_div_two_lt_abs_to_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_eq_neg_pi_div_two_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.continuous_at_sign", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.eq_iff_abs_to_real_eq_of_sign_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_mem_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_le_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.cos_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sign_sub_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.neg_ne_self_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sin_sub_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sub_coe_pi_eq_add_coe_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_coe_eq_self_sub_two_mul_int_mul_pi_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.tan_add_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sin_antiperiodic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.cos_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sign_coe_neg_pi_div_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.two_nsmul_to_real_eq_two_mul_add_two_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_eq_pi_div_two_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.two_nsmul_ne_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.cos_pi_div_two_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.coe_pi_add_coe_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_coe_eq_self_sub_two_pi_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.coe_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.cos_add_pi_div_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sin_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.cos_add_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.coe_nat_mul_eq_nsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.cos_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.coe_to_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_nonneg_iff_sign_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.induction_on", "description": " An induction principle to deduce results for `angle` from those for `\u211d`, used with\n`induction \u03b8 using real.angle.induction_on`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sign_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sin_to_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.tan_sub_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.two_zsmul_coe_div_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sign_eq_of_continuous_on", "description": " Suppose a function to angles is continuous on a connected set and never takes the values `0`\nor `\u03c0` on that set. Then the values of the function on that set all have the same sign.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.abs_to_real_le_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.cos_sub_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.coe_abs_to_real_of_sign_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.tan_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.two_nsmul_coe_div_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sign_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_Ioc_mod_to_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.normed_add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.two_zsmul_eq_pi_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sign_coe_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.to_real_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.abs_to_real_coe_eq_self_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sin_add_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.coe_nsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.tan", "description": "The tangent of a `real.angle`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.tan_to_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sin_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sin_add_pi_div_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.sin_eq_iff_coe_eq_or_add_eq_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.two_zsmul_to_real_eq_two_mul_sub_two_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.cos_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.abs_to_real_neg_coe_eq_self_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.abs_cos_eq_abs_sin_of_two_nsmul_add_two_nsmul_eq_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.two_zsmul_neg_pi_div_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.two_nsmul_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.coe_two_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.angle.pi_div_two_ne_