, "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gal_mul_is_solvable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gal_X_pow_sub_C_is_solvable_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "solvable_by_rad.is_solvable'", "description": " **Abel-Ruffini Theorem** (one direction): An irreducible polynomial with an\n`is_solvable_by_rad` root has solvable Galois group", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "splits_X_pow_sub_one_of_X_pow_sub_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gal_X_sub_C_is_solvable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "solvable_by_rad.induction3", "description": "An auxiliary induction lemma, which is generalized by `solvable_by_rad.is_solvable`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gal_X_is_solvable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "solvable_by_rad.is_solvable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gal_X_pow_sub_C_is_solvable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "solvable_by_rad.is_integral", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gal_X_pow_sub_one_is_solvable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space", "description": " The character space of a topological algebra is the subset of elements of the weak dual that\nare also algebra homomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.continuous_linear_map_class", "description": "Elements of the character space are continuous linear maps.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.coe_to_clm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.eq_set_map_one_map_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.alg_hom_class", "description": "In a unital algebra, elements of the character space are algebra homomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.to_clm", "description": "An element of the character space, as a continuous linear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.to_alg_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.ker_is_maximal", "description": "The `ring_hom.ker` of `\u03c6 : character_space \ud835\udd5c A` is maximal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.gelfand_transform", "description": " The **Gelfand transform** is an algebra homomorphism (over `\ud835\udd5c`) from a topological `\ud835\udd5c`-algebra\n`A` into the `\ud835\udd5c`-algebra of continuous `\ud835\udd5c`-valued functions on the `character_space \ud835\udd5c A`.\nThe character space itself consists of all algebra homomorphisms from `A` to `\ud835\udd5c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.ext_ker", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.to_alg_hom", "description": "An element of the character space of a unital algebra, as an algebra homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.non_unital_alg_hom_class", "description": "Elements of the character space are non-unital algebra homomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.apply_mem_spectrum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.is_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.union_zero_is_closed", "description": "The `character_space \ud835\udd5c A` along with `0` is always a closed set in `weak_dual \ud835\udd5c A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.to_non_unital_alg_hom", "description": "An element of the character space, as an non-unital algebra homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.coe_to_non_unital_alg_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.coe_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.gelfand_transform_apply_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.is_closed", "description": " under suitable mild assumptions on `\ud835\udd5c`, the character space is a closed set in\n`weak_dual \ud835\udd5c A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.union_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiRing.forget\u2082_AddCommMon_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ring.forget\u2082_SemiRing_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": "The forgetful functor from rings to semirings preserves all limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommSemiRing.limit_comm_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ring.forget\u2082_AddCommGroup_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": "The forgetful functor from rings to additive commutative groups preserves all limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiRing.forget_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": "The forgetful functor from semirings to types preserves all limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiRing.sections_subsemiring", "description": "The flat sections of a functor into `SemiRing` form a subsemiring of all sections.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.comm_ring_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ring.has_limits_of_size", "description": "The category of rings has all limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ring.forget\u2082_AddCommGroup_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.forget\u2082_Ring_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ring.forget\u2082_SemiRing_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.forget\u2082_CommSemiRing_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": "The forgetful functor from commutative rings to commutative semirings preserves all limits.\n(That is, the underlying commutative semirings could have been computed instead as limits\nin the category of commutative semirings.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiRing.forget\u2082_Mon_preserves_limits_aux", "description": "An auxiliary declaration to speed up typechecking.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiRing.has_limits.limit_cone_is_limit", "description": "Witness that the limit cone in `SemiRing` is a limit cone.\n(Internal use only; use the limits API.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiRing.forget\u2082_Mon_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": "The forgetful functor from semirings to monoids preserves all limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.limit_cone_is_limit", "description": "The chosen cone is a limit cone.\n(Generally, you'll just want to use `limit.cone F`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ring.sections_subring", "description": "The flat sections of a functor into `Ring` form a subring of all sections.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiRing.has_limits.limit_cone", "description": "Construction of a limit cone in `SemiRing`.\n(Internal use only; use the limits API.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommSemiRing.forget_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": "The forgetful functor from rings to types preserves all limits. (That is, the underlying\ntypes could have been computed instead as limits in the category of types.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiRing.has_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ring.has_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.forget\u2082_Ring_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": "The forgetful functor from commutative rings to rings preserves all limits.\n(That is, the underlying rings could have been computed instead as limits in the category of rings.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.has_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.category_theory.forget\u2082.category_theory.creates_limit", "description": "We show that the forgetful functor `CommRing \u2964 Ring` creates limits.\n\nAll we need to do is notice that the limit point has a `comm_ring` instance available,\nand then reuse the existing limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ring.forget_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": "The forgetful functor from rings to types preserves all limits. (That is, the underlying\ntypes could have been computed instead as limits in the category of types.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiRing.has_limits_of_size", "description": "The category of rings has all limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.has_limits_of_size", "description": "The category of commutative rings has all limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommSemiRing.comm_semiring_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommSemiRing.forget\u2082_SemiRing_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommSemiRing.has_limits_of_size", "description": "The category of rings has all limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiRing.forget\u2082_Mon_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiRing.forget\u2082_AddCommMon_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": "The forgetful functor from semirings to additive commutative monoids preserves all limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommSemiRing.limit_cone", "description": "A choice of limit cone for a functor into `CommSemiRing`.\n(Generally, you'll just want to use `limit F`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommSemiRing.has_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommSemiRing.forget\u2082_SemiRing_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": "The forgetful functor from rings to semirings preserves all limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommSemiRing.category_theory.forget\u2082.category_theory.creates_limit", "description": "We show that the forgetful functor `CommSemiRing \u2964 SemiRing` creates limits.\n\nAll we need to do is notice that the limit point has a `comm_semiring` instance available,\nand then reuse the existing limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ring.forget\u2082_AddCommGroup_preserves_limits_aux", "description": "An auxiliary declaration to speed up typechecking.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommSemiRing.limit_cone_is_limit", "description": "The chosen cone is a limit cone.\n(Generally, you'll just want to use `limit.cone F`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ring.ring_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.forget\u2082_CommSemiRing_preserves_limits_aux", "description": "An auxiliary declaration to speed up typechecking.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.forget_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": "The forgetful functor from commutative rings to types preserves all limits.\n(That is, the underlying types could have been computed instead as limits in the category of types.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiRing.forget_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiRing.forget\u2082_AddCommMon_preserves_limits_aux", "description": "An auxiliary declaration to speed up typechecking.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.forget\u2082_CommSemiRing_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiRing.limit_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.limit_cone", "description": "A choice of limit cone for a functor into `CommRing`.\n(Generally, you'll just want to use `limit F`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiRing.semiring_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ring.limit_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.forget_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ring.limit_cone", "description": "A choice of limit cone for a functor into `Ring`.\n(Generally, you'll just want to use `limit F`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiRing.limit_\u03c0_ring_hom", "description": "`limit.\u03c0 (F \u22d9 forget SemiRing) j` as a `ring_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.limit_comm_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ring.limit_cone_is_limit", "description": "The chosen cone is a limit cone.\n(Generally, you'll just want to use `limit.cone F`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "library_note.change elaboration strategy with `by apply`", "description": "Some definitions may be extremely slow to elaborate, when the target type to be constructed\nis complicated and when the type of the term given in the definition is also complicated and does\nnot obviously match the target type. In this case, instead of just giving the term, prefixing it\nwith `by apply` may speed up things considerably as the types are not elaborated in the same order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommSemiRing.forget_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ring.category_theory.forget\u2082.category_theory.creates_limit", "description": "We show that the forgetful functor `CommRing \u2964 Ring` creates limits.\n\nAll we need to do is notice that the limit point has a `ring` instance available,\nand then reuse the existing limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ring.forget_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor", "description": "The tensor product expressed as a functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.prod_monoidal_left_unitor_inv_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_is_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.right_assoc_tensor_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.triangle_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.right_unitor_tensor_inv_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_right_tensor_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_left_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.hom_inv_id_tensor_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.id_tensor_comp_tensor_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_dite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.associator_nat_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.dite_tensor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.associator_inv_conjugation_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensoring_right_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_inv_hom_id_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.right_unitor_inv_naturality_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.hom_inv_id_tensor'_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.hom_inv_id_tensor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_left_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.prod_monoidal_tensor_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.right_assoc_tensor_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.inv_hom_id_tensor'_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.associator_naturality_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.left_unitor_nat_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.id_tensor_associator_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.triangle_assoc_comp_left_inv_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensoring_right_map_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_inv_hom_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.prod_monoidal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensoring_left", "description": "Tensoring on the left, as a functor from `C` into endofunctors of `C`.\n\nTODO: show this is a op-monoidal functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.prod_monoidal_left_unitor_hom_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.prod_monoidal_right_unitor_hom_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_inv_hom_id'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.triangle_assoc_comp_left_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.right_unitor_tensor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.hom_inv_id_tensor'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_comp_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.inv_hom_id_tensor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.prod_monoidal_right_unitor_hom_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.comp_tensor_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.associator_inv_naturality_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.right_unitor_inv_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.left_unitor_inv_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensoring_left_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_unit_right", "description": "The functor `\u03bb X, X \u2297 \ud835\udfd9_ C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.associator_conjugation_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.associator_inv_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.triangle_assoc_comp_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.right_unitor_tensor_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_left_tensor_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_right_tensor", "description": "Tensoring on the right with `X \u2297 Y` is naturally isomorphic to\ntensoring on the right with `X`, and then again with `Y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.triangle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_left_tensor", "description": "Tensoring on the left with `X \u2297 Y` is naturally isomorphic to\ntensoring on the left with `Y`, and then again with `X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.id_tensor_associator_inv_naturality_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.right_unitor_nat_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.inv_hom_id_tensor_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_hom_inv_id_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_hom_inv_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.comp_tensor_id_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_id_comp_id_tensor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.pentagon_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_inv_hom_id'_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.prod_monoidal_left_unitor_inv_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.id_tensor_comp_tensor_id_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.left_unitor_naturality_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.id_tensor_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_iso_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensoring_right", "description": "Tensoring on the right, as a functor from `C` into endofunctors of `C`.\n\nWe later show this is a monoidal functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensoring_left_map_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.prod_monoidal_right_unitor_inv_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.right_unitor_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.associator_conjugation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.associator_nat_iso", "description": "The associator as a natural isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_hom_inv_id'_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_id_comp_id_tensor_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_left", "description": "Tensoring on the left with a fixed object, as a functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_right_tensor_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.right_unitor_nat_iso", "description": "The right unitor as a natural isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_right_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.inv_tensor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.right_unitor_tensor_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.id_tensor_associator_inv_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.left_unitor_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.left_unitor_nat_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.prod_monoidal_associator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.id_tensor_comp_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category", "description": "In a monoidal category, we can take the tensor product of objects, `X \u2297 Y` and of morphisms `f \u2297 g`.\nTensor product does not need to be strictly associative on objects, but there is a\nspecified associator, `\u03b1_ X Y Z : (X \u2297 Y) \u2297 Z \u2245 X \u2297 (Y \u2297 Z)`. There is a tensor unit `\ud835\udfd9_ C`,\nwith specified left and right unitor isomorphisms `\u03bb_ X : \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2297 X \u2245 X` and `\u03c1_ X : X \u2297 \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2245 X`.\nThese associators and unitors satisfy the pentagon and triangle equations.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FFK>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_obj", "description": "In a monoidal category, we can take the tensor product of objects, `X \u2297 Y` and of morphisms `f \u2297 g`.\nTensor product does not need to be strictly associative on objects, but there is a\nspecified associator, `\u03b1_ X Y Z : (X \u2297 Y) \u2297 Z \u2245 X \u2297 (Y \u2297 Z)`. There is a tensor unit `\ud835\udfd9_ C`,\nwith specified left and right unitor isomorphisms `\u03bb_ X : \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2297 X \u2245 X` and `\u03c1_ X : X \u2297 \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2245 X`.\nThese associators and unitors satisfy the pentagon and triangle equations.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FFK>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_hom", "description": "In a monoidal category, we can take the tensor product of objects, `X \u2297 Y` and of morphisms `f \u2297 g`.\nTensor product does not need to be strictly associative on objects, but there is a\nspecified associator, `\u03b1_ X Y Z : (X \u2297 Y) \u2297 Z \u2245 X \u2297 (Y \u2297 Z)`. There is a tensor unit `\ud835\udfd9_ C`,\nwith specified left and right unitor isomorphisms `\u03bb_ X : \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2297 X \u2245 X` and `\u03c1_ X : X \u2297 \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2245 X`.\nThese associators and unitors satisfy the pentagon and triangle equations.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FFK>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_id'", "description": "In a monoidal category, we can take the tensor product of objects, `X \u2297 Y` and of morphisms `f \u2297 g`.\nTensor product does not need to be strictly associative on objects, but there is a\nspecified associator, `\u03b1_ X Y Z : (X \u2297 Y) \u2297 Z \u2245 X \u2297 (Y \u2297 Z)`. There is a tensor unit `\ud835\udfd9_ C`,\nwith specified left and right unitor isomorphisms `\u03bb_ X : \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2297 X \u2245 X` and `\u03c1_ X : X \u2297 \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2245 X`.\nThese associators and unitors satisfy the pentagon and triangle equations.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FFK>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_comp'", "description": "In a monoidal category, we can take the tensor product of objects, `X \u2297 Y` and of morphisms `f \u2297 g`.\nTensor product does not need to be strictly associative on objects, but there is a\nspecified associator, `\u03b1_ X Y Z : (X \u2297 Y) \u2297 Z \u2245 X \u2297 (Y \u2297 Z)`. There is a tensor unit `\ud835\udfd9_ C`,\nwith specified left and right unitor isomorphisms `\u03bb_ X : \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2297 X \u2245 X` and `\u03c1_ X : X \u2297 \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2245 X`.\nThese associators and unitors satisfy the pentagon and triangle equations.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FFK>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_unit", "description": "In a monoidal category, we can take the tensor product of objects, `X \u2297 Y` and of morphisms `f \u2297 g`.\nTensor product does not need to be strictly associative on objects, but there is a\nspecified associator, `\u03b1_ X Y Z : (X \u2297 Y) \u2297 Z \u2245 X \u2297 (Y \u2297 Z)`. There is a tensor unit `\ud835\udfd9_ C`,\nwith specified left and right unitor isomorphisms `\u03bb_ X : \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2297 X \u2245 X` and `\u03c1_ X : X \u2297 \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2245 X`.\nThese associators and unitors satisfy the pentagon and triangle equations.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FFK>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.associator", "description": "In a monoidal category, we can take the tensor product of objects, `X \u2297 Y` and of morphisms `f \u2297 g`.\nTensor product does not need to be strictly associative on objects, but there is a\nspecified associator, `\u03b1_ X Y Z : (X \u2297 Y) \u2297 Z \u2245 X \u2297 (Y \u2297 Z)`. There is a tensor unit `\ud835\udfd9_ C`,\nwith specified left and right unitor isomorphisms `\u03bb_ X : \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2297 X \u2245 X` and `\u03c1_ X : X \u2297 \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2245 X`.\nThese associators and unitors satisfy the pentagon and triangle equations.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FFK>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.associator_naturality'", "description": "In a monoidal category, we can take the tensor product of objects, `X \u2297 Y` and of morphisms `f \u2297 g`.\nTensor product does not need to be strictly associative on objects, but there is a\nspecified associator, `\u03b1_ X Y Z : (X \u2297 Y) \u2297 Z \u2245 X \u2297 (Y \u2297 Z)`. There is a tensor unit `\ud835\udfd9_ C`,\nwith specified left and right unitor isomorphisms `\u03bb_ X : \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2297 X \u2245 X` and `\u03c1_ X : X \u2297 \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2245 X`.\nThese associators and unitors satisfy the pentagon and triangle equations.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FFK>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.left_unitor", "description": "In a monoidal category, we can take the tensor product of objects, `X \u2297 Y` and of morphisms `f \u2297 g`.\nTensor product does not need to be strictly associative on objects, but there is a\nspecified associator, `\u03b1_ X Y Z : (X \u2297 Y) \u2297 Z \u2245 X \u2297 (Y \u2297 Z)`. There is a tensor unit `\ud835\udfd9_ C`,\nwith specified left and right unitor isomorphisms `\u03bb_ X : \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2297 X \u2245 X` and `\u03c1_ X : X \u2297 \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2245 X`.\nThese associators and unitors satisfy the pentagon and triangle equations.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FFK>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.left_unitor_naturality'", "description": "In a monoidal category, we can take the tensor product of objects, `X \u2297 Y` and of morphisms `f \u2297 g`.\nTensor product does not need to be strictly associative on objects, but there is a\nspecified associator, `\u03b1_ X Y Z : (X \u2297 Y) \u2297 Z \u2245 X \u2297 (Y \u2297 Z)`. There is a tensor unit `\ud835\udfd9_ C`,\nwith specified left and right unitor isomorphisms `\u03bb_ X : \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2297 X \u2245 X` and `\u03c1_ X : X \u2297 \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2245 X`.\nThese associators and unitors satisfy the pentagon and triangle equations.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FFK>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.right_unitor", "description": "In a monoidal category, we can take the tensor product of objects, `X \u2297 Y` and of morphisms `f \u2297 g`.\nTensor product does not need to be strictly associative on objects, but there is a\nspecified associator, `\u03b1_ X Y Z : (X \u2297 Y) \u2297 Z \u2245 X \u2297 (Y \u2297 Z)`. There is a tensor unit `\ud835\udfd9_ C`,\nwith specified left and right unitor isomorphisms `\u03bb_ X : \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2297 X \u2245 X` and `\u03c1_ X : X \u2297 \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2245 X`.\nThese associators and unitors satisfy the pentagon and triangle equations.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FFK>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.right_unitor_naturality'", "description": "In a monoidal category, we can take the tensor product of objects, `X \u2297 Y` and of morphisms `f \u2297 g`.\nTensor product does not need to be strictly associative on objects, but there is a\nspecified associator, `\u03b1_ X Y Z : (X \u2297 Y) \u2297 Z \u2245 X \u2297 (Y \u2297 Z)`. There is a tensor unit `\ud835\udfd9_ C`,\nwith specified left and right unitor isomorphisms `\u03bb_ X : \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2297 X \u2245 X` and `\u03c1_ X : X \u2297 \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2245 X`.\nThese associators and unitors satisfy the pentagon and triangle equations.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FFK>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.pentagon'", "description": "In a monoidal category, we can take the tensor product of objects, `X \u2297 Y` and of morphisms `f \u2297 g`.\nTensor product does not need to be strictly associative on objects, but there is a\nspecified associator, `\u03b1_ X Y Z : (X \u2297 Y) \u2297 Z \u2245 X \u2297 (Y \u2297 Z)`. There is a tensor unit `\ud835\udfd9_ C`,\nwith specified left and right unitor isomorphisms `\u03bb_ X : \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2297 X \u2245 X` and `\u03c1_ X : X \u2297 \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2245 X`.\nThese associators and unitors satisfy the pentagon and triangle equations.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FFK>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.triangle'", "description": "In a monoidal category, we can take the tensor product of objects, `X \u2297 Y` and of morphisms `f \u2297 g`.\nTensor product does not need to be strictly associative on objects, but there is a\nspecified associator, `\u03b1_ X Y Z : (X \u2297 Y) \u2297 Z \u2245 X \u2297 (Y \u2297 Z)`. There is a tensor unit `\ud835\udfd9_ C`,\nwith specified left and right unitor isomorphisms `\u03bb_ X : \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2297 X \u2245 X` and `\u03c1_ X : X \u2297 \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2245 X`.\nThese associators and unitors satisfy the pentagon and triangle equations.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FFK>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_left_tensor_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.pentagon", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_right_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.right_unitor_conjugation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.associator_nat_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.prod_monoidal_tensor_unit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.prod_monoidal_tensor_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.right_unitor_nat_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.id_tensor_associator_naturality_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.associator_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_unit_left", "description": "The functor `\u03bb X, \ud835\udfd9_ C \u2297 X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_hom_inv_id'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensoring_right.category_theory.faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.prod_monoidal_right_unitor_inv_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.left_unitor_inv_naturality_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_iso_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.right_assoc_tensor", "description": "The right-associated triple tensor product as a functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.pentagon_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_right", "description": "Tensoring on the right with a fixed object, as a functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.associator_inv_conjugation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.left_unitor_conjugation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.left_assoc_tensor", "description": "The left-associated triple tensor product as a functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_right_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_left_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.left_assoc_tensor_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.left_assoc_tensor_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensoring_left.category_theory.faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.left_unitor_nat_iso", "description": "The left unitor as a natural isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.triangle_assoc_comp_right_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.prod_monoidal_left_unitor_hom_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.right_unitor_naturality_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.pentagon_inv_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_obj_2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_iso", "description": "The tensor product of two isomorphisms is an isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.inv_hom_id_tensor'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.library_search_hole_cmd", "description": " Invoking the hole command `library_search` (\"Use `library_search` to complete the goal\") calls\nthe tactic `library_search` to produce a proof term with the type of the hole.\n\nRunning it on\n\n```lean\nexample : 0 < 1 :=\n{!!}\n```\n\nproduces\n\n```lean\nexample : 0 < 1 :=\nnat.one_pos\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.unpack_iff_both", "description": "We unpack any element of a list of `decl_data` corresponding to an `\u2194` statement that could apply\nin both directions into two separate elements.\n\nThis ensures that both directions can be independently returned by `suggest`,\nand avoids a problem where the application of one direction prevents\nthe application of the other direction. (See `exp_le_exp` in the tests.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.head_symbol_match.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.head_symbol_match.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.suggest_opt.mk_accept", "description": "Convert a `suggest_opt` structure to a `opt` structure suitable for `solve_by_elim`,\nby setting the `accept` parameter to require that all complete solutions\nuse everything in `compulsory_hyps`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest_scripts", "description": "Returns a list of at most `limit` strings, of the form `Try this: exact ...` or\n`Try this: refine ...`, which make progress on the current goal using a declaration\nfrom the library.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.library_search", "description": "Returns a string of the form `Try this: exact ...`, which closes the current goal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.head_symbol_match.to_string", "description": "a textual representation of a `head_symbol_match`, for trace debugging.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.hole_command.library_search", "description": " Invoking the hole command `library_search` (\"Use `library_search` to complete the goal\") calls\nthe tactic `library_search` to produce a proof term with the type of the hole.\n\nRunning it on\n\n```lean\nexample : 0 < 1 :=\n{!!}\n```\n\nproduces\n\n```lean\nexample : 0 < 1 :=\nnat.one_pos\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.normalize_synonym", "description": " Map a name (typically a head symbol) to a \"canonical\" definitional synonym.\nGiven a name `n`, we want a name `n'` such that a sufficiently applied\nexpression with head symbol `n` is always definitionally equal to an expression\nwith head symbol `n'`.\nThus, we can search through all lemmas with a result type of `n'`\nto solve a goal with head symbol `n`.\n\nFor example, `>` is mapped to `<` because `a > b` is definitionally equal to `b < a`,\nand `not` is mapped to `false` because `\u00ac a` is definitionally equal to `p \u2192 false`\nThe default is that the original argument is returned, so `<` is just mapped to `<`.\n\n`normalize_synonym` is called for every lemma in the library, so it needs to be fast.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.match_head_symbol", "description": "Determine if, and in which way, a given expression matches the specified head symbol.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.library_search", "description": "`library_search` is a tactic to identify existing lemmas in the library. It tries to close the\ncurrent goal by applying a lemma from the library, then discharging any new goals using\n`solve_by_elim`.\n\nIf it succeeds, it prints a trace message `exact ...` which can replace the invocation\nof `library_search`.\n\nTypical usage is:\n```lean\nexample (n m k : \u2115) : n * (m - k) = n * m - n * k :=\nby library_search -- Try this: exact mul_tsub n m k\n```\n\n`library_search using h\u2081 h\u2082` will only show solutions\nthat make use of the local hypotheses `h\u2081` and `h\u2082`.\n\nBy default `library_search` only unfolds `reducible` definitions\nwhen attempting to match lemmas against the goal.\nPreviously, it would unfold most definitions, sometimes giving surprising answers, or slow answers.\nThe old behaviour is still available via `library_search!`.\n\nYou can add additional lemmas to be used along with local hypotheses\nafter the application of a library lemma,\nusing the same syntax as for `solve_by_elim`, e.g.\n```lean\nexample {a b c d: nat} (h\u2081 : a < c) (h\u2082 : b < d) : max (c + d) (a + b) = (c + d) :=\nbegin\n  library_search [add_lt_add], -- Says: `Try this: exact max_eq_left_of_lt (add_lt_add h\u2081 h\u2082)`\nend\n```\nYou can also use `library_search with attr` to include all lemmas with the attribute `attr`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.apply_and_solve", "description": "Apply the lemma `e`, then attempt to close all goals using\n`solve_by_elim opt`, failing if `close_goals = tt`\nand there are any goals remaining.\n\nReturns the number of subgoals which were closed using `solve_by_elim`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest_core", "description": "The core `suggest` tactic.\nIt attempts to apply a declaration from the library,\nthen solve new goals using `solve_by_elim`.\n\nIt returns a list of `application`s consisting of fields:\n* `state`, a tactic state resulting from the successful application of a declaration from\n  the library,\n* `script`, a string of the form `Try this: refine ...` or `Try this: exact ...` which will\n  reproduce that tactic state,\n* `decl`, an `option declaration` indicating the declaration that was applied\n  (or none, if `solve_by_elim` succeeded),\n* `num_goals`, the number of remaining goals, and\n* `hyps_used`, the number of local hypotheses used in the solution.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.head_symbol_match", "description": "A declaration can match the head symbol of the current goal in four possible ways:\n* `ex`  : an exact match\n* `mp`  : the declaration returns an `iff`, and the right hand side matches the goal\n* `mpr` : the declaration returns an `iff`, and the left hand side matches the goal\n* `both`: the declaration returns an `iff`, and the both sides match the goal", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.head_symbol_match.ex", "description": "A declaration can match the head symbol of the current goal in four possible ways:\n* `ex`  : an exact match\n* `mp`  : the declaration returns an `iff`, and the right hand side matches the goal\n* `mpr` : the declaration returns an `iff`, and the left hand side matches the goal\n* `both`: the declaration returns an `iff`, and the both sides match the goal", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.head_symbol_match.mp", "description": "A declaration can match the head symbol of the current goal in four possible ways:\n* `ex`  : an exact match\n* `mp`  : the declaration returns an `iff`, and the right hand side matches the goal\n* `mpr` : the declaration returns an `iff`, and the left hand side matches the goal\n* `both`: the declaration returns an `iff`, and the both sides match the goal", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.head_symbol_match.mpr", "description": "A declaration can match the head symbol of the current goal in four possible ways:\n* `ex`  : an exact match\n* `mp`  : the declaration returns an `iff`, and the right hand side matches the goal\n* `mpr` : the declaration returns an `iff`, and the left hand side matches the goal\n* `both`: the declaration returns an `iff`, and the both sides match the goal", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.head_symbol_match.both", "description": "A declaration can match the head symbol of the current goal in four possible ways:\n* `ex`  : an exact match\n* `mp`  : the declaration returns an `iff`, and the right hand side matches the goal\n* `mpr` : the declaration returns an `iff`, and the left hand side matches the goal\n* `both`: the declaration returns an `iff`, and the both sides match the goal", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.process_declaration", "description": "Generate a `decl_data` from the given declaration if\nit matches the head symbol `hs` for the current goal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.allowed_head_symbols", "description": "Compute the head symbol of an expression, then normalise synonyms.\n\nThis is only used when analysing the goal, so it is okay to do more expensive analysis here.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.decl_data", "description": " A package of `declaration` metadata, including the way in which its type matches the head symbol\nwhich we are searching for.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.decl_data.d", "description": " A package of `declaration` metadata, including the way in which its type matches the head symbol\nwhich we are searching for.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.decl_data.n", "description": " A package of `declaration` metadata, including the way in which its type matches the head symbol\nwhich we are searching for.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.decl_data.m", "description": " A package of `declaration` metadata, including the way in which its type matches the head symbol\nwhich we are searching for.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.decl_data.l", "description": " A package of `declaration` metadata, including the way in which its type matches the head symbol\nwhich we are searching for.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest", "description": "See `suggest_core`.\n\nReturns a list of at most `limit` `application`s,\nsorted by number of goals, and then (reverse) number of hypotheses used.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.application", "description": "An `application` records the result of a successful application of a library lemma.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.application.state", "description": "An `application` records the result of a successful application of a library lemma.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.application.script", "description": "An `application` records the result of a successful application of a library lemma.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.application.decl", "description": "An `application` records the result of a successful application of a library lemma.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.application.num_goals", "description": "An `application` records the result of a successful application of a library lemma.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.application.hyps_used", "description": "An `application` records the result of a successful application of a library lemma.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.suggest_opt", "description": " An extension to the option structure for `solve_by_elim`.\n* `compulsory_hyps` specifies a list of local hypotheses which must appear in any solution.\n  These are useful for constraining the results from `library_search` and `suggest`.\n* `try_this` is a flag (default: `tt`) that controls whether a \"Try this:\"-line should be traced.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.suggest_opt.to_opt", "description": " An extension to the option structure for `solve_by_elim`.\n* `compulsory_hyps` specifies a list of local hypotheses which must appear in any solution.\n  These are useful for constraining the results from `library_search` and `suggest`.\n* `try_this` is a flag (default: `tt`) that controls whether a \"Try this:\"-line should be traced.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.suggest_opt.compulsory_hyps", "description": " An extension to the option structure for `solve_by_elim`.\n* `compulsory_hyps` specifies a list of local hypotheses which must appear in any solution.\n  These are useful for constraining the results from `library_search` and `suggest`.\n* `try_this` is a flag (default: `tt`) that controls whether a \"Try this:\"-line should be traced.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.suggest_opt.try_this", "description": " An extension to the option structure for `solve_by_elim`.\n* `compulsory_hyps` specifies a list of local hypotheses which must appear in any solution.\n  These are useful for constraining the results from `library_search` and `suggest`.\n* `try_this` is a flag (default: `tt`) that controls whether a \"Try this:\"-line should be traced.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.suggest", "description": "`suggest` tries to apply suitable theorems/defs from the library, and generates\na list of `exact ...` or `refine ...` scripts that could be used at this step.\nIt leaves the tactic state unchanged. It is intended as a complement of the search\nfunction in your editor, the `#find` tactic, and `library_search`.\n\n`suggest` takes an optional natural number `num` as input and returns the first `num`\n(or less, if all possibilities are exhausted) possibilities ordered by length of lemma names.\nThe default for `num` is `50`.\nFor performance reasons `suggest` uses monadic lazy lists (`mllist`). This means that\n`suggest` might miss some results if `num` is not large enough. However, because\n`suggest` uses monadic lazy lists, smaller values of `num` run faster than larger values.\n\nYou can add additional lemmas to be used along with local hypotheses\nafter the application of a library lemma,\nusing the same syntax as for `solve_by_elim`, e.g.\n```lean\nexample {a b c d: nat} (h\u2081 : a < c) (h\u2082 : b < d) : max (c + d) (a + b) = (c + d) :=\nbegin\n  suggest [add_lt_add], -- Says: `Try this: exact max_eq_left_of_lt (add_lt_add h\u2081 h\u2082)`\nend\n```\nYou can also use `suggest with attr` to include all lemmas with the attribute `attr`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.library_search", "description": "`library_search` is a tactic to identify existing lemmas in the library. It tries to close the\ncurrent goal by applying a lemma from the library, then discharging any new goals using\n`solve_by_elim`.\n\nIf it succeeds, it prints a trace message `exact ...` which can replace the invocation\nof `library_search`.\n\nTypical usage is:\n```lean\nexample (n m k : \u2115) : n * (m - k) = n * m - n * k :=\nby library_search -- Try this: exact mul_tsub n m k\n```\n\n`library_search using h\u2081 h\u2082` will only show solutions\nthat make use of the local hypotheses `h\u2081` and `h\u2082`.\n\nBy default `library_search` only unfolds `reducible` definitions\nwhen attempting to match lemmas against the goal.\nPreviously, it would unfold most definitions, sometimes giving surprising answers, or slow answers.\nThe old behaviour is still available via `library_search!`.\n\nYou can add additional lemmas to be used along with local hypotheses\nafter the application of a library lemma,\nusing the same syntax as for `solve_by_elim`, e.g.\n```lean\nexample {a b c d: nat} (h\u2081 : a < c) (h\u2082 : b < d) : max (c + d) (a + b) = (c + d) :=\nbegin\n  library_search [add_lt_add], -- Says: `Try this: exact max_eq_left_of_lt (add_lt_add h\u2081 h\u2082)`\nend\n```\nYou can also use `library_search with attr` to include all lemmas with the attribute `attr`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.library_defs", "description": "Retrieve all library definitions with a given head symbol.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.suggest", "description": "`suggest` lists possible usages of the `refine` tactic and leaves the tactic state unchanged.\nIt is intended as a complement of the search function in your editor, the `#find` tactic, and\n`library_search`.\n\n`suggest` takes an optional natural number `num` as input and returns the first `num` (or less, if\nall possibilities are exhausted) possibilities ordered by length of lemma names.\nThe default for `num` is `50`.\n\n`suggest using h\u2081 h\u2082` will only show solutions that make use of the local hypotheses `h\u2081` and `h\u2082`.\n\nFor performance reasons `suggest` uses monadic lazy lists (`mllist`). This means that `suggest`\nmight miss some results if `num` is not large enough. However, because `suggest` uses monadic\nlazy lists, smaller values of `num` run faster than larger values.\n\nAn example of `suggest` in action,\n\n```lean\nexample (n : nat) : n < n + 1 :=\nbegin suggest, sorry end\n```\n\nprints the list,\n\n```lean\nTry this: exact nat.lt.base n\nTry this: exact nat.lt_succ_self n\nTry this: refine not_le.mp _\nTry this: refine gt_iff_lt.mp _\nTry this: refine nat.lt.step _\nTry this: refine lt_of_not_ge _\n...\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.suggest.apply_declaration", "description": "Apply the declaration `d` (or the forward and backward implications separately, if it is an `iff`),\nand then attempt to solve the subgoal using `apply_and_solve`.\n\nReturns the number of subgoals successfully closed.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.has_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_filter_basis.mul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module_filter_basis.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.N", "description": "The neighborhood function of a `add_group_filter_basis`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.prod_subset_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.has_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module_filter_basis.inhabited", "description": " If `R` is discrete then the trivial additive group filter basis on any `R`-module is a\nmodule filter basis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.topology", "description": "The topological space structure coming from a group filter basis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_filter_basis.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module_filter_basis.smul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_filter_basis.is_topological_ring", "description": " If a ring is endowed with a topological structure coming from\na ring filter basis then it's a topological ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_filter_basis.topology", "description": " The topology associated to a ring filter basis.\nIt has the given basis as a basis of neighborhoods of zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_filter_basis", "description": "A `ring_filter_basis` on a ring is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional axioms.\n Example : if `R` is a topological ring then the neighbourhoods of the identity are a\n `ring_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `ring_filter_basis` on a ring `R`, one can define a\n topology on `R` which is compatible with the ring structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_filter_basis.to_add_group_filter_basis", "description": "A `ring_filter_basis` on a ring is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional axioms.\n Example : if `R` is a topological ring then the neighbourhoods of the identity are a\n `ring_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `ring_filter_basis` on a ring `R`, one can define a\n topology on `R` which is compatible with the ring structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_filter_basis.mul'", "description": "A `ring_filter_basis` on a ring is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional axioms.\n Example : if `R` is a topological ring then the neighbourhoods of the identity are a\n `ring_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `ring_filter_basis` on a ring `R`, one can define a\n topology on `R` which is compatible with the ring structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_filter_basis.mul_left'", "description": "A `ring_filter_basis` on a ring is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional axioms.\n Example : if `R` is a topological ring then the neighbourhoods of the identity are a\n `ring_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `ring_filter_basis` on a ring `R`, one can define a\n topology on `R` which is compatible with the ring structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_filter_basis.mul_right'", "description": "A `ring_filter_basis` on a ring is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional axioms.\n Example : if `R` is a topological ring then the neighbourhoods of the identity are a\n `ring_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `ring_filter_basis` on a ring `R`, one can define a\n topology on `R` which is compatible with the ring structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.N_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module_filter_basis.has_continuous_smul", "description": " If a module is endowed with a topological structure coming from\na module filter basis then it's a topological module.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.inhabited", "description": " The trivial group filter basis consists of `{1}` only. The associated topology\nis discrete.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.nhds_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module_filter_basis", "description": "A `module_filter_basis` on a module is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional axioms.\n Example : if `M` is a topological module then the neighbourhoods of zero are a\n `module_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `module_filter_basis` one can define a topology\n compatible with the module structure on `M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module_filter_basis.to_add_group_filter_basis", "description": "A `module_filter_basis` on a module is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional axioms.\n Example : if `M` is a topological module then the neighbourhoods of zero are a\n `module_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `module_filter_basis` one can define a topology\n compatible with the module structure on `M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module_filter_basis.smul'", "description": "A `module_filter_basis` on a module is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional axioms.\n Example : if `M` is a topological module then the neighbourhoods of zero are a\n `module_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `module_filter_basis` one can define a topology\n compatible with the module structure on `M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module_filter_basis.smul_left'", "description": "A `module_filter_basis` on a module is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional axioms.\n Example : if `M` is a topological module then the neighbourhoods of zero are a\n `module_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `module_filter_basis` one can define a topology\n compatible with the module structure on `M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module_filter_basis.smul_right'", "description": "A `module_filter_basis` on a module is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional axioms.\n Example : if `M` is a topological module then the neighbourhoods of zero are a\n `module_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `module_filter_basis` one can define a topology\n compatible with the module structure on `M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.mem_nhds_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.topology", "description": "The topological space structure coming from an additive group filter basis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.nhds_has_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis", "description": "A `add_group_filter_basis` on an additive group is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional\n axioms. Example : if `G` is a topological group then the neighbourhoods of the identity are a\n `add_group_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `add_group_filter_basis` one can define a topology\n compatible with the group structure on `G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.to_filter_basis", "description": "A `add_group_filter_basis` on an additive group is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional\n axioms. Example : if `G` is a topological group then the neighbourhoods of the identity are a\n `add_group_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `add_group_filter_basis` one can define a topology\n compatible with the group structure on `G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.zero'", "description": "A `add_group_filter_basis` on an additive group is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional\n axioms. Example : if `G` is a topological group then the neighbourhoods of the identity are a\n `add_group_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `add_group_filter_basis` one can define a topology\n compatible with the group structure on `G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.add'", "description": "A `add_group_filter_basis` on an additive group is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional\n axioms. Example : if `G` is a topological group then the neighbourhoods of the identity are a\n `add_group_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `add_group_filter_basis` one can define a topology\n compatible with the group structure on `G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.neg'", "description": "A `add_group_filter_basis` on an additive group is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional\n axioms. Example : if `G` is a topological group then the neighbourhoods of the identity are a\n `add_group_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `add_group_filter_basis` one can define a topology\n compatible with the group structure on `G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.conj'", "description": "A `add_group_filter_basis` on an additive group is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional\n axioms. Example : if `G` is a topological group then the neighbourhoods of the identity are a\n `add_group_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `add_group_filter_basis` one can define a topology\n compatible with the group structure on `G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.has_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.has_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.is_topological_add_group", "description": "If a group is endowed with a topological structure coming from a group filter basis\nthen it's a topological group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module_filter_basis.topology'", "description": " The topology associated to a module filter basis on a module over a topological ring.\nIt has the given basis as a basis of neighborhoods of zero. This version gets the ring\ntopology by unification instead of type class inference.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.nhds_one_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_filter_basis.mul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module_filter_basis.of_bases", "description": "Build a module filter basis from compatible ring and additive group filter bases.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module_filter_basis.smul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.nhds_has_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.nhds_zero_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis_of_comm", "description": "`add_group_filter_basis` constructor in the additive commutative group case.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis_of_comm", "description": "`group_filter_basis` constructor in the commutative group case.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.mem_nhds_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module_filter_basis.group_filter_basis.has_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module_filter_basis.topology", "description": " The topology associated to a module filter basis on a module over a topological ring.\nIt has the given basis as a basis of neighborhoods of zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_filter_basis.has_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.inhabited", "description": "The trivial additive group filter basis consists of `{0}` only. The associated\ntopology is discrete.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_continuous_smul.of_basis_zero", "description": " A topological add group whith a basis of `\ud835\udcdd 0` satisfying the axioms of `module_filter_basis`\nis a topological module.\n\nThis lemma is mathematically useless because one could obtain such a result by applying\n`module_filter_basis.has_continuous_smul` and use the fact that group topologies are characterized\nby their neighborhoods of 0 to obtain the `has_continuous_smul` on the pre-existing topology.\n\nBut it turns out it's just easier to get it as a biproduct of the proof, so this is just a free\nquality-of-life improvement.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.N", "description": "The neighborhood function of a `group_filter_basis`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.nhds_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.is_topological_group", "description": " If a group is endowed with a topological structure coming from a group filter basis then it's a\ntopological group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.nhds_zero_has_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis", "description": "A `group_filter_basis` on a group is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional axioms.\n Example : if `G` is a topological group then the neighbourhoods of the identity are a\n `group_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `group_filter_basis` one can define a topology\n compatible with the group structure on `G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.to_filter_basis", "description": "A `group_filter_basis` on a group is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional axioms.\n Example : if `G` is a topological group then the neighbourhoods of the identity are a\n `group_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `group_filter_basis` one can define a topology\n compatible with the group structure on `G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.one'", "description": "A `group_filter_basis` on a group is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional axioms.\n Example : if `G` is a topological group then the neighbourhoods of the identity are a\n `group_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `group_filter_basis` one can define a topology\n compatible with the group structure on `G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.mul'", "description": "A `group_filter_basis` on a group is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional axioms.\n Example : if `G` is a topological group then the neighbourhoods of the identity are a\n `group_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `group_filter_basis` one can define a topology\n compatible with the group structure on `G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.inv'", "description": "A `group_filter_basis` on a group is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional axioms.\n Example : if `G` is a topological group then the neighbourhoods of the identity are a\n `group_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `group_filter_basis` one can define a topology\n compatible with the group structure on `G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.conj'", "description": "A `group_filter_basis` on a group is a `filter_basis` satisfying some additional axioms.\n Example : if `G` is a topological group then the neighbourhoods of the identity are a\n `group_filter_basis`. Conversely given a `group_filter_basis` one can define a topology\n compatible with the group structure on `G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.N_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.sum_subset_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_filter_basis.nhds_one_has_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_algebra.star_\u03b9", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_algebra.star_algebra_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_monoid.star_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_monoid.star_one", "description": "Note that `star_one` is already a global simp lemma, but this one works with dsimp too", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_monoid.star_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_algebra.star_hom", "description": "`star` as an `alg_equiv`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_algebra.star_ring", "description": "The star ring formed by reversing the elements of products", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.normalize_mode", "description": " If `ring` fails to close the goal, it falls back on normalizing the expression to a \"pretty\"\nform so that you can see why it failed. This setting adjusts the resulting form:\n\n  * `raw` is the form that `ring` actually uses internally, with iterated applications of `horner`.\n    Not very readable but useful if you don't want any postprocessing.\n    This results in terms like `horner (horner (horner 3 y 1 0) x 2 1) x 1 (horner 1 y 1 0)`.\n  * `horner` maintains the Horner form structure, but it unfolds the `horner` definition itself,\n    and tries to otherwise minimize parentheses.\n    This results in terms like `(3 * x ^ 2 * y + 1) * x + y`.\n  * `SOP` means sum of products form, expanding everything to monomials.\n    This results in terms like `3 * x ^ 3 * y + x + y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.normalize_mode.raw", "description": " If `ring` fails to close the goal, it falls back on normalizing the expression to a \"pretty\"\nform so that you can see why it failed. This setting adjusts the resulting form:\n\n  * `raw` is the form that `ring` actually uses internally, with iterated applications of `horner`.\n    Not very readable but useful if you don't want any postprocessing.\n    This results in terms like `horner (horner (horner 3 y 1 0) x 2 1) x 1 (horner 1 y 1 0)`.\n  * `horner` maintains the Horner form structure, but it unfolds the `horner` definition itself,\n    and tries to otherwise minimize parentheses.\n    This results in terms like `(3 * x ^ 2 * y + 1) * x + y`.\n  * `SOP` means sum of products form, expanding everything to monomials.\n    This results in terms like `3 * x ^ 3 * y + x + y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.normalize_mode.SOP", "description": " If `ring` fails to close the goal, it falls back on normalizing the expression to a \"pretty\"\nform so that you can see why it failed. This setting adjusts the resulting form:\n\n  * `raw` is the form that `ring` actually uses internally, with iterated applications of `horner`.\n    Not very readable but useful if you don't want any postprocessing.\n    This results in terms like `horner (horner (horner 3 y 1 0) x 2 1) x 1 (horner 1 y 1 0)`.\n  * `horner` maintains the Horner form structure, but it unfolds the `horner` definition itself,\n    and tries to otherwise minimize parentheses.\n    This results in terms like `(3 * x ^ 2 * y + 1) * x + y`.\n  * `SOP` means sum of products form, expanding everything to monomials.\n    This results in terms like `3 * x ^ 3 * y + x + y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.normalize_mode.horner", "description": " If `ring` fails to close the goal, it falls back on normalizing the expression to a \"pretty\"\nform so that you can see why it failed. This setting adjusts the resulting form:\n\n  * `raw` is the form that `ring` actually uses internally, with iterated applications of `horner`.\n    Not very readable but useful if you don't want any postprocessing.\n    This results in terms like `horner (horner (horner 3 y 1 0) x 2 1) x 1 (horner 1 y 1 0)`.\n  * `horner` maintains the Horner form structure, but it unfolds the `horner` definition itself,\n    and tries to otherwise minimize parentheses.\n    This results in terms like `(3 * x ^ 2 * y + 1) * x + y`.\n  * `SOP` means sum of products form, expanding everything to monomials.\n    This results in terms like `3 * x ^ 3 * y + x + y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.nc_lift", "description": " Lift an instance cache tactic (probably from `norm_num`) to the `ring_m` monad. This uses\nthe instance cache corresponding to `\u2115`, which is used for computations in the exponent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.eval_mul", "description": "Evaluate `a * b` where `a` and `b` are in normal form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_mul_horner_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_add_horner_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.add_atom", "description": " Get the index corresponding to an atomic expression, if it has already been encountered, or\nput it in the list of atoms and return the new index, otherwise.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.normalize_mode.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner", "description": " The normal form that `ring` uses is mediated by the function `horner a x n b := a * x ^ n + b`.\nThe reason we use a definition rather than the (more readable) expression on the right is because\nthis expression contains a number of typeclass arguments in different positions, while `horner`\ncontains only one `comm_semiring` instance at the top level. See also `horner_expr` for a\ndescription of normal form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.ring_nf", "description": " Simplification tactic for expressions in the language of commutative (semi)rings,\nwhich rewrites all ring expressions into a normal form. When writing a normal form,\n`ring_nf SOP` will use sum-of-products form instead of horner form.\n`ring_nf!` will use a more aggressive reducibility setting to identify atoms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_expr.refl_conv", "description": "Reflexivity conversion for a `horner_expr`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_expr.is_zero", "description": "Is this expr the constant `0`?", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.unfold_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_expr.to_string", "description": "Pretty printer for `horner_expr`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.zero_horner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.eval_pow", "description": "Evaluate `a ^ n` where `a` is in normal form and `n` is a natural numeral.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.ring1", "description": "Tactic for solving equations in the language of *commutative* (semi)rings.\n This version of `ring` fails if the target is not an equality\n that is provable by the axioms of commutative (semi)rings.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.ring.mode", "description": " Parser for `ring_nf`'s `mode` argument, which can only be the \"keywords\" `raw`, `horner` or\n`SOP`. (Because these are not actually keywords we use a name parser and postprocess the result.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.ring", "description": " Tactic for solving equations in the language of *commutative* (semi)rings.\n`ring!` will use a more aggressive reducibility setting to identify atoms.\n\nIf the goal is not solvable, it falls back to rewriting all ring expressions\ninto a normal form, with a suggestion to use `ring_nf` instead, if this is the intent.\nSee also `ring1`, which is the same as `ring` but without the fallback behavior.\n\nBased on [Proving Equalities in a Commutative Ring Done Right\nin Coq](http://www.cs.ru.nl/~freek/courses/tt-2014/read/10.1.1.61.3041.pdf) by Benjamin Gr\u00e9goire\nand Assia Mahboubi.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_mul_horner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_atom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.ring_m.run'", "description": " Run a `ring_m` tactic in the tactic monad. This version of `ring_m.run` uses an external\natoms ref, so that subexpressions can be named across multiple `ring_m` calls.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.eval_const_mul", "description": "Evaluate `k * a` where `k` is a rational numeral and `a` is in normal form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.normalize_mode.has_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.normalize", "description": "A `ring`-based normalization simplifier that rewrites ring expressions into the specified mode.\n\n * `raw` is the form that `ring` actually uses internally, with iterated applications of `horner`.\n   Not very readable but useful if you don't want any postprocessing.\n   This results in terms like `horner (horner (horner 3 y 1 0) x 2 1) x 1 (horner 1 y 1 0)`.\n * `horner` maintains the Horner form structure, but it unfolds the `horner` definition itself,\n   and tries to otherwise minimize parentheses.\n   This results in terms like `(3 * x ^ 2 * y + 1) * x + y`.\n * `SOP` means sum of products form, expanding everything to monomials.\n   This results in terms like `3 * x ^ 3 * y + x + y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.pow_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.ring_m.alternative", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.unfold_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_expr.has_coe_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.ring_nf_cfg", "description": "Configuration for `ring_nf`.\n\n * `recursive`: if true, atoms inside ring expressions will be reduced recursively", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.ring_nf_cfg.recursive", "description": "Configuration for `ring_nf`.\n\n * `recursive`: if true, atoms inside ring expressions will be reduced recursively", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.mul_assoc_rev", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_const_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_add_horner_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.const_add_horner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.cache", "description": "This cache contains data required by the `ring` tactic during execution.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.cache.\u03b1", "description": "This cache contains data required by the `ring` tactic during execution.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.cache.univ", "description": "This cache contains data required by the `ring` tactic during execution.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.cache.comm_semiring_inst", "description": "This cache contains data required by the `ring` tactic during execution.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.cache.red", "description": "This cache contains data required by the `ring` tactic during execution.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.cache.ic", "description": "This cache contains data required by the `ring` tactic during execution.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.cache.nc", "description": "This cache contains data required by the `ring` tactic during execution.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.cache.atoms", "description": "This cache contains data required by the `ring` tactic during execution.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_mul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.expr.has_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.eval", "description": " Evaluate a ring expression `e` recursively to normal form, together with a proof of\nequality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.eval_add", "description": "Evaluate `a + b` where `a` and `b` are already in normal form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.pow_add_rev_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.ring_m.run", "description": "Run a `ring_m` tactic in the tactic monad.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.eval_norm_atom", "description": "Evaluate `a` where `a` is an atom.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.ring_nf_cfg.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_add_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.normalize_mode.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.ic_lift", "description": " Lift an instance cache tactic (probably from `norm_num`) to the `ring_m` monad. This uses\nthe instance cache corresponding to the ring `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_expr", "description": " Every expression in the language of commutative semirings can be viewed as a sum of monomials,\nwhere each monomial is a product of powers of atoms. We fix a global order on atoms (up to\ndefinitional equality), and then separate the terms according to their smallest atom. So the top\nlevel expression is `a * x^n + b` where `x` is the smallest atom and `n > 0` is a numeral, and\n`n` is maximal (so `a` contains at least one monomial not containing an `x`), and `b` contains no\nmonomials with an `x` (hence all atoms in `b` are larger than `x`).\n\nIf there is no `x` satisfying these constraints, then the expression must be a numeral. Even though\nwe are working over rings, we allow rational constants when these can be interpreted in the ring,\nso we can solve problems like `x / 3 = 1 / 3 * x` even though these are not technically in the\nlanguage of rings.\n\nThese constraints ensure that there is a unique normal form for each ring expression, and so the\nalgorithm is simply to calculate the normal form of each side and compare for equality.\n\nTo allow us to efficiently pattern match on normal forms, we maintain this inductive type that\nholds a normalized expression together with its structure. All the `expr`s in this type could be\nremoved without loss of information, and conversely the `horner_expr` structure and the `\u2115` and\n`\u211a` values can be recovered from the top level `expr`, but we keep both in order to keep proof\n producing normalization functions efficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_expr.const", "description": " Every expression in the language of commutative semirings can be viewed as a sum of monomials,\nwhere each monomial is a product of powers of atoms. We fix a global order on atoms (up to\ndefinitional equality), and then separate the terms according to their smallest atom. So the top\nlevel expression is `a * x^n + b` where `x` is the smallest atom and `n > 0` is a numeral, and\n`n` is maximal (so `a` contains at least one monomial not containing an `x`), and `b` contains no\nmonomials with an `x` (hence all atoms in `b` are larger than `x`).\n\nIf there is no `x` satisfying these constraints, then the expression must be a numeral. Even though\nwe are working over rings, we allow rational constants when these can be interpreted in the ring,\nso we can solve problems like `x / 3 = 1 / 3 * x` even though these are not technically in the\nlanguage of rings.\n\nThese constraints ensure that there is a unique normal form for each ring expression, and so the\nalgorithm is simply to calculate the normal form of each side and compare for equality.\n\nTo allow us to efficiently pattern match on normal forms, we maintain this inductive type that\nholds a normalized expression together with its structure. All the `expr`s in this type could be\nremoved without loss of information, and conversely the `horner_expr` structure and the `\u2115` and\n`\u211a` values can be recovered from the top level `expr`, but we keep both in order to keep proof\n producing normalization functions efficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_expr.xadd", "description": " Every expression in the language of commutative semirings can be viewed as a sum of monomials,\nwhere each monomial is a product of powers of atoms. We fix a global order on atoms (up to\ndefinitional equality), and then separate the terms according to their smallest atom. So the top\nlevel expression is `a * x^n + b` where `x` is the smallest atom and `n > 0` is a numeral, and\n`n` is maximal (so `a` contains at least one monomial not containing an `x`), and `b` contains no\nmonomials with an `x` (hence all atoms in `b` are larger than `x`).\n\nIf there is no `x` satisfying these constraints, then the expression must be a numeral. Even though\nwe are working over rings, we allow rational constants when these can be interpreted in the ring,\nso we can solve problems like `x / 3 = 1 / 3 * x` even though these are not technically in the\nlanguage of rings.\n\nThese constraints ensure that there is a unique normal form for each ring expression, and so the\nalgorithm is simply to calculate the normal form of each side and compare for equality.\n\nTo allow us to efficiently pattern match on normal forms, we maintain this inductive type that\nholds a normalized expression together with its structure. All the `expr`s in this type could be\nremoved without loss of information, and conversely the `horner_expr` structure and the `\u2115` and\n`\u211a` values can be recovered from the top level `expr`, but we keep both in order to keep proof\n producing normalization functions efficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.get_atom", "description": "Get an already encountered atom by its index.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_def'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.cache.cs_app", "description": " Apply a theorem that expects a `comm_semiring` instance. This is a special case of\n`ic_lift mk_app`, but it comes up often because `horner` and all its theorems have this assumption;\nit also does not require the tactic monad which improves access speed a bit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.add_neg_eq_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.get_cache", "description": "Get the `ring` data from the monad.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.ring_m", "description": " The monad that `ring` works in. This is a reader monad containing a mutable cache (using `ref`\nfor mutability), as well as the list of atoms-up-to-defeq encountered thus far, used for atom\nsorting.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_expr.pp", "description": "Pretty printer for `horner_expr`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_expr.e", "description": " Get the expression corresponding to a `horner_expr`. This can be calculated recursively from\nthe structure, but we cache the exprs in all subterms so that this function can be computed in\nconstant time.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.eval'", "description": " Evaluate a ring expression `e` recursively to normal form, together with a proof of\nequality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_expr.has_to_tactic_format", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.lift", "description": "Lift a tactic into the `ring_m` monad.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.eval_neg", "description": "Evaluate `-a` where `a` is already in normal form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conv.interactive.ring", "description": "Normalises expressions in commutative (semi-)rings inside of a `conv` block using the tactic `ring`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_horner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.eval_horner", "description": "Evaluate `horner a n x b` where `a` and `b` are already in normal form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.ring_m.monad", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.normalize'", "description": " A `ring`-based normalization simplifier that rewrites ring expressions into the specified mode.\nSee `normalize`. This version takes a list of atoms to persist across multiple calls.\n\n* `atoms`: a mutable reference containing the atom set from the previous call\n* `red`: the reducibility setting to use when comparing atoms for defeq\n* `mode`: the normalization style (see `normalize_mode`)\n* `recursive`: if true, atoms will be reduced recursively using `normalize'`\n* `e`: the expression to normalize\n* `inner`: This should be set to `ff`. It is used internally to disable normalization\n  at the top level when called from `eval` in order to prevent an infinite loop\n  `eval' -> eval_atom -> normalize' -> eval'` when called on something that can't\n  be simplified like `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_expr.xadd'", "description": "Construct a `xadd` node, generating the cached expr using the input cache.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.eval_atom", "description": "Evaluate `a` where `a` is an atom.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.pow_add_rev", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.ic_lift'", "description": " Lift an instance cache tactic (probably from `norm_num`) to the `ring_m` monad. This version\nis abstract over the instance cache in question (either the ring `\u03b1`, or `\u2115` for exponents).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.horner_add_horner_gt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.ring", "description": " Tactic for solving equations in the language of *commutative* (semi)rings.\n`ring!` will use a more aggressive reducibility setting to identify atoms.\n\nIf the goal is not solvable, it falls back to rewriting all ring expressions\ninto a normal form, with a suggestion to use `ring_nf` instead, if this is the intent.\nSee also `ring1`, which is the same as `ring` but without the fallback behavior.\n\nBased on [Proving Equalities in a Commutative Ring Done Right\nin Coq](http://www.cs.ru.nl/~freek/courses/tt-2014/read/10.1.1.61.3041.pdf) by Benjamin Gr\u00e9goire\nand Assia Mahboubi.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ring.subst_into_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conv.interactive.ring_nf", "description": "Normalises expressions in commutative (semi-)rings inside of a `conv` block using the tactic `ring`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.add_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.mul_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_fn_alg_hom", "description": "Coercion to a function as an `alg_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.pow_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_hom.comp_left_continuous_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_nat_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.non_assoc_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.sum_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_fn_monoid_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.mul_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.subsingleton_subalgebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.non_unital_comm_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.zsmul_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_hom.comp_left_continuous", "description": "Composition on the left by a (continuous) homomorphism of topological `add_monoid`s,\nas an `add_monoid_hom`. Similar to `add_monoid_hom.comp_left`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.add_comm_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.comp_left_continuous", "description": " Composition on the left by a (continuous) homomorphism of topological semirings, as a\n`ring_hom`.  Similar to `ring_hom.comp_left`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_functions.set_of.has_coe_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_subalgebra", "description": "The `R`-subalgebra of continuous maps `\u03b1 \u2192 A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.one_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.comp_right_alg_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_int_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.mul_zero_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.comp_monoid_hom'", "description": "Composition on the right as a `monoid_hom`. Similar to `monoid_hom.comp_hom'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.comp_left_continuous_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_submonoid", "description": "The `submonoid` of continuous maps `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.vadd_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subalgebra.separates_points.strongly", "description": "Working in continuous functions into a topological field,\na subalgebra of functions that separates points also separates points strongly.\n\nBy the hypothesis, we can find a function `f` so `f x \u2260 f y`.\nBy an affine transformation in the field we can arrange so that `f x = a` and `f x = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.is_central_scalar", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.sub_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.neg_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_fn_monoid_hom", "description": "Coercion to a function as an `monoid_hom`. Similar to `monoid_hom.coe_fn`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.semigroup_with_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_continuous_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.zsmul_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.comp_monoid_hom'_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.comm_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.nat_cast_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_subgroup", "description": "The subgroup of continuous maps `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_subring", "description": "The subring of continuous maps `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.module", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.star_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_continuous_const_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homeomorph.comp_star_alg_equiv'", "description": " `continuous_map.comp_star_alg_hom'` as a `star_alg_equiv` when the continuous map `f` is\nactually a homeomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_continuous_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_int_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.add_monoid_with_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.add_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_fn_ring_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.star_add_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.comp_left_continuous", "description": " Composition on the left by a continuous linear map, as a `linear_map`.\nSimilar to `linear_map.comp_left`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_involutive_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.neg_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.separates_points_strongly", "description": "A set of continuous maps \"separates points strongly\"\nif for each pair of distinct points there is a function with specified values on them.\n\nWe give a slightly unusual formulation, where the specified values are given by some\nfunction `v`, and we ask `f x = v x \u2227 f y = v y`. This avoids needing a hypothesis `x \u2260 y`.\n\nIn fact, this definition would work perfectly well for a set of non-continuous functions,\nbut as the only current use case is in the Stone-Weierstrass theorem,\nwriting it this way avoids having to deal with casts inside the set.\n(This may need to change if we do Stone-Weierstrass on non-compact spaces,\nwhere the functions would be continuous functions vanishing at infinity.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.div_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "min_eq_half_add_sub_abs_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_add_submonoid", "description": "The `add_submonoid` of continuous maps `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.sub_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.mul_one_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.distrib_mul_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_fn_ring_hom", "description": "Coercion to a function as a `ring_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.add_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subalgebra.separates_points", "description": "A version of `separates_points` for subalgebras of the continuous functions,\nused for stating the Stone-Weierstrass theorem.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.non_unital_comm_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.comp_star_alg_hom'_comp", "description": "`continuous_map.comp_star_alg_hom` is functorial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monoid_hom.comp_left_continuous", "description": " Composition on the left by a (continuous) homomorphism of topological monoids, as a\n`monoid_hom`. Similar to `monoid_hom.comp_left`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.comm_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.vadd_comm_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.nsmul_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.star_module", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_nsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.sup_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.zero_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.inf_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_continuous_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_smul'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.topological_add_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_add_subgroup", "description": "The `add_subgroup` of continuous maps `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.tsum_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_continuous_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.int_cast_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.summable_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.comp_star_alg_hom'", "description": " The functorial map taking `f : C(X, Y)` to `C(Y, A) \u2192\u22c6\u2090[\ud835\udd5c] C(X, A)` given by pre-composition\nwith the continuous function `f`. See `continuous_map.comp_monoid_hom'` and\n`continuous_map.comp_add_monoid_hom'`, `continuous_map.comp_right_alg_hom` for bundlings of\npre-composition into a `monoid_hom`, an `add_monoid_hom` and an `alg_hom`, respectively, under\nsuitable assumptions on `A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.add_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_fn_add_monoid_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.pow_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.mul_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.comp_add_monoid_hom'", "description": "Composition on the right as an `add_monoid_hom`. Similar to\n`add_monoid_hom.comp_hom'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.topological_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.add_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.non_unital_non_assoc_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.comp_star_alg_hom'_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "alg_hom.comp_left_continuous", "description": " Composition on the left by a (continuous) homomorphism of topological `R`-algebras, as an\n`alg_hom`. Similar to `alg_hom.comp_left`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.non_unital_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.comp_add_monoid_hom'_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.inv_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.inv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.comp_left_continuous_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.comp_right_alg_hom", "description": "Precomposition of functions into a normed ring by a continuous map is an algebra homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.algebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_fn_add_monoid_hom", "description": "Coercion to a function as an `add_monoid_hom`. Similar to `add_monoid_hom.coe_fn`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_nsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monoid_hom.comp_left_continuous_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_zsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_fn_linear_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homeomorph.comp_star_alg_equiv'_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.comm_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_zpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.C_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "max_eq_half_add_add_abs_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_fn_alg_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_zpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_zsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subalgebra.separates_points_monotone", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.add_zero_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.module'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_continuous_const_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.nsmul_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.smul_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.zero_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.add_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.one_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.non_assoc_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.div_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.star_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.zpow_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_nat_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.smul_comm_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_subsemiring", "description": "The subsemiring of continuous maps `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homeomorph.comp_star_alg_equiv'_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.is_scalar_tower", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.non_unital_non_assoc_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.non_unital_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.vadd_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.C", "description": "Continuous constant functions as a `ring_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_submodule", "description": "The `R`-submodule of continuous maps `\u03b1 \u2192 M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.prod_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.comm_monoid_with_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.star_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.comp_star_alg_hom'_id", "description": " `continuous_map.comp_star_alg_hom'` sends the identity continuous map to the identity\n`star_alg_hom`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.monoid_with_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.has_sum_apply", "description": " If `\u03b1` is locally compact, and an infinite sum of functions in `C(\u03b1, \u03b2)`\nconverges to `g` (for the compact-open topology), then the pointwise sum converges to `g x` for\nall `x \u2208 \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "alg_hom.comp_left_continuous_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.smul_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.zpow_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.periodic_tsum_comp_add_zsmul", "description": " Summing the translates of `f` by `\u2124 \u2022 p` gives a map which is periodic with period `p`.\n(This is true without any convergence conditions, since if the sum doesn't converge it is taken to\nbe the zero map, which is periodic.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_fn_linear_map", "description": "Coercion to a function as a `linear_map`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module_info.has_repr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "module_info.of_module_name", "description": "Retrieves the module with the given `module_name`.\n\n**ONLY USE THIS FUNCTION IN (CI) SCRIPTS!**\n\nThis function is constant-time if the module is already a dependency.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "module_info.of_module_id", "description": "Retrieves the module with the given `module_id`.\n\n**ONLY USE THIS FUNCTION IN (CI) SCRIPTS!**\n\nThis function is constant-time if the module is already a dependency.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.for_decl_of_imported_module", "description": "Creates an environment containing the module `id` until `decl_name` including dependencies.\n\n**ONLY USE THIS FUNCTION IN (CI) SCRIPTS!**", "p": "core"}, {"name": "module_info.id", "description": "Returns the `module_id` of the module.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.from_imported_module_name", "description": "Creates an environment containing the module `name` including dependencies.\n\n**ONLY USE THIS FUNCTION IN (CI) SCRIPTS!**", "p": "core"}, {"name": "module_info.has_to_tactic_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "module_info.module_id", "description": "The absolute path to the `.lean` file containing the module (e.g. `\".../data/dlist.lean\"`).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.import_only", "description": "Imports only the module (without the dependencies) into an environment.\n\n**ONLY USE THIS FUNCTION IN (CI) SCRIPTS!**", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.import_dependencies", "description": "Imports the dependencies of a module into an environment.\n\n**ONLY USE THIS FUNCTION IN (CI) SCRIPTS!**\n\nAlready imported dependencies will not be imported twice.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "module_info", "description": "Information about a currently loaded module (such as `data.dlist`).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "module_info.resolve_module_name", "description": "Resolves a `module_name` to `module_id`, using the global search path.\n\n**ONLY USE THIS FUNCTION IN (CI) SCRIPTS!**", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.for_decl_of_imported_module_name", "description": "Creates an environment containing the module `name` until declaration `decl_name`\nincluding dependencies.\n\n**ONLY USE THIS FUNCTION IN (CI) SCRIPTS!**", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.import_only_until_decl", "description": "Imports all declarations until `decl_name` of the module (without the dependencies) into an environment.\n\n**ONLY USE THIS FUNCTION IN (CI) SCRIPTS!**", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.from_imported_module", "description": "Creates an environment containing the module `id` including dependencies.\n\n**ONLY USE THIS FUNCTION IN (CI) SCRIPTS!**\n\nThe environment `from_imported_module \".../data/dlist.lean\"` is roughly equivalent to\nthe environment at the end of a file containing just `import data.dlist`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "module_info.module_name", "description": "The name of the module, as used in an import command (e.g. `data.dlist`).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.import'", "description": "Imports a module including dependencies into an environment.\n\n**ONLY USE THIS FUNCTION IN (CI) SCRIPTS!**", "p": "core"}, {"name": "environment.import_until_decl", "description": "Imports a module until `decl_name` including dependencies into an environment.\n\n**ONLY USE THIS FUNCTION IN (CI) SCRIPTS!**", "p": "core"}, {"name": "module_info.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "module_info.has_to_format", "descri