e element of the singleton", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cInf_Ici", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.to_has_Sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.conditionally_complete_lattice", "description": " Adding a top element to a conditionally complete lattice\ngives a conditionally complete lattice", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_cInf_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_lub.csupr_set_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice_of_Inf", "description": " Create a `conditionally_complete_lattice_of_Inf` from a `partial_order` and `Inf` function\nthat returns the greatest lower bound of a nonempty set which is bounded below. Usually this\nconstructor provides poor definitional equalities.  If other fields are known explicitly, they\nshould be provided; for example, if `inf` is known explicitly, construct the\n`conditionally_complete_lattice` instance as\n```lean\ninstance : conditionally_complete_lattice my_T :=\n{ inf := better_inf,\n  le_inf := ...,\n  inf_le_right := ...,\n  inf_le_left := ...\n  -- don't care to fix sup, Sup\n  ..conditionally_complete_lattice_of_Inf my_T _ }\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_cSup_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "infi_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cInf_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cinfi_set_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "galois_connection.l_csupr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.has_Inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.map_csupr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.is_lub_Sup'", "description": " The Sup of a non-empty set is its least upper bound for a conditionally\ncomplete lattice with a top.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cSup_union", "description": "The sup of a union of two sets is the max of the suprema of each subset, under the assumptions\nthat all sets are bounded above and nonempty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cInf_le_cInf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.map_cInf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cSup_image2_eq_cSup_cSup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cSup_pair", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone.cInf_image_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice_of_lattice_of_Sup", "description": "A version of `conditionally_complete_lattice_of_Sup` when we already know that `\u03b1` is a lattice.\n\nThis should only be used when it is both hard and unnecessary to provide `Inf` explicitly.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.map_cinfi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_order.to_linear_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_cInf_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "csupr_of_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.Inf_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.cSup_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cSup_image2_eq_cSup_cInf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.with_bot.complete_linear_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cInf_insert", "description": " The infimum of insert a s is the minimum of a and the infimum of s, if s is\nnonempty and bounded below.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "supr_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.Sup_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "csupr_false", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_lt_of_lt_csupr", "description": "Indexed version of the above lemma `exists_lt_of_lt_cSup`.\nWhen `b < supr f`, there is an element `i` such that `b < f i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_greatest.cSup_eq", "description": "A greatest element of a set is the supremum of this set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cInf_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_csupr_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone_on.map_Inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.is_lub_Sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.supr_coe_lt_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cSup_Iio", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.map_cSup'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "csupr_le_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cInf_le'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_cInf_inter", "description": "The infimum of an intersection of two sets is bounded below by the maximum of the\ninfima of each set, if all sets are bounded below and nonempty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.coe_Inf'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.complete_linear_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cSup_inter_le", "description": "The supremum of an intersection of two sets is bounded by the minimum of the suprema of each\nset, if all sets are bounded above and nonempty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.has_Inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.cInf_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cInf_eq_of_forall_ge_of_forall_gt_exists_lt", "description": "Introduction rule to prove that `b` is the infimum of `s`: it suffices to check that `b`\nis smaller than all elements of `s`, and that this is not the case of any `w>b`.\nSee `Inf_eq_of_forall_ge_of_forall_gt_exists_lt` for a version in complete lattices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cSup_le_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.csupr_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_csupr_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_glb_cinfi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cInf_image2_eq_cInf_cSup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_cinfi_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_cinfi", "description": "The indexed minimum of a function is bounded below by a uniform lower bound", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cSup_image2_eq_cInf_cInf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cInf_image2_eq_cSup_cSup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.with_top.complete_lattice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.coe_Sup'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_cinfi_set_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cInf_image2_eq_cInf_cInf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "csupr_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone.le_cInf_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cinfi_le", "description": "The indexed infimum of a function is bounded above by the value taken at one point", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_dual.conditionally_complete_lattice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "galois_connection.u_cInf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone.cSup_image_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cInf_upper_bounds_eq_cSup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.map_cinfi_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_least.Inf_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.cinfi_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "infi_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_cInf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.coe_Inf'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_lt_of_cinfi_lt", "description": "Indexed version of the above lemma `exists_lt_of_cInf_lt`\nWhen `infi f < a`, there is an element `i` such that `f i < a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_least_Inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.with_top.complete_linear_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_lub_csupr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_mem_of_lt_cInf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_lub_cSup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_glb.cinfi_set_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cSup_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_cSup_of_lt", "description": "b < Sup s when there is an element a in s with b < a, when s is bounded above.\nThis is essentially an iff, except that the assumptions for the two implications are\nslightly different (one needs boundedness above for one direction, nonemptiness and linear\norder for the other one), so we formulate separately the two implications, contrary to\nthe complete_lattice case.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.to_lattice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_linear_order.to_conditionally_complete_linear_order_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_glb.cinfi_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_lt_of_cInf_lt", "description": "When Inf s < b, there is an element a in s with a < b, if s is nonempty and the order is\na linear order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cinfi_eq_of_forall_ge_of_forall_gt_exists_lt", "description": "Introduction rule to prove that `b` is the infimum of `f`: it suffices to check that `b`\nis smaller than `f i` for all `i`, and that this is not the case of any `w>b`.\nSee `infi_eq_of_forall_ge_of_forall_gt_exists_lt` for a version in complete lattices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cSup_lower_bounds_eq_cInf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cSup_le'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cInf_le_cSup", "description": "If a set is bounded below and above, and nonempty, its infimum is less than or equal to\nits supremum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cInf_le_cInf'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.coe_supr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_cinfi_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cSup_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.is_glb_Inf'", "description": " The Inf of a bounded-below set is its greatest lower bound for a conditionally\ncomplete lattice with a top.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "csupr_eq_of_forall_le_of_forall_lt_exists_gt", "description": "Introduction rule to prove that `b` is the supremum of `f`: it suffices to check that `b`\nis larger than `f i` for all `i`, and that this is not the case of any `w<b`.\nSee `supr_eq_of_forall_le_of_forall_lt_exists_gt` for a version in complete lattices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "csupr_set_le_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.sup", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.le", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.lt", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.le_refl", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.le_trans", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.lt_iff_le_not_le", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.le_antisymm", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.le_sup_left", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.le_sup_right", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.sup_le", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.inf", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.inf_le_left", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.inf_le_right", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.le_inf", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.Sup", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.Inf", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.le_cSup", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.cSup_le", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.cInf_le", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice.le_cInf", "description": " A conditionally complete lattice is a lattice in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded below has an infimum.\nTypical examples are real numbers or natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete lattices, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cInf_union", "description": "The inf of a union of two sets is the min of the infima of each subset, under the assumptions\nthat all sets are bounded below and nonempty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "csupr_le_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_lub.csupr_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "galois_connection.l_cSup'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cSup_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "galois_connection.u_cinfi_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_lattice_of_Sup", "description": " Create a `conditionally_complete_lattice` from a `partial_order` and `Sup` function\nthat returns the least upper bound of a nonempty set which is bounded above. Usually this\nconstructor provides poor definitional equalities.  If other fields are known explicitly, they\nshould be provided; for example, if `inf` is known explicitly, construct the\n`conditionally_complete_lattice` instance as\n```lean\ninstance : conditionally_complete_lattice my_T :=\n{ inf := better_inf,\n  le_inf := ...,\n  inf_le_right := ...,\n  inf_le_left := ...\n  -- don't care to fix sup, Inf\n  ..conditionally_complete_lattice_of_Sup my_T _ }\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cinfi_le'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.Sup_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_lt_of_lt_cSup", "description": " When b < Sup s, there is an element a in s with b < a, if s is nonempty and the order is\na linear order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cInf_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.map_csupr_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_order_bot.to_order_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cSup_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "galois_connection.u_cinfi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "galois_connection.l_cSup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.to_conditionally_complete_lattice", "description": " A complete lattice is a conditionally complete lattice, as there are no restrictions\non the properties of Inf and Sup in a complete lattice.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_greatest.Sup_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cinfi_mono", "description": "The indexed infimum of two functions are comparable if the functions are pointwise comparable", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cSup_singleton", "description": "The supremum of a singleton is the element of the singleton", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_lt_of_lt_csupr'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_cSup_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.coe_Inf", "description": "A version of `with_top.coe_Inf'` with a more convenient but less general statement.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cInf_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cSup_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone.le_cSup_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cinfi_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_order_bot", "description": " A conditionally complete linear order with `bot` is a linear order with least element, in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and every nonempty subset (necessarily\nbounded below) has an infimum.  A typical example is the natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete linear orders, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_order_bot.sup", "description": " A conditionally complete linear order with `bot` is a linear order with least element, in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and every nonempty subset (necessarily\nbounded below) has an infimum.  A typical example is the natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete linear orders, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_order_bot.le", "description": " A conditionally complete linear order with `bot` is a linear order with least element, in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and every nonempty subset (necessarily\nbounded below) has an infimum.  A typical example is the natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete linear orders, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_order_bot.lt", "description": " A cond