ame statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_order_bot.decidable_le", "description": " A conditionally complete linear order with `bot` is a linear order with least element, in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and every nonempty subset (necessarily\nbounded below) has an infimum.  A typical example is the natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete linear orders, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_order_bot.decidable_eq", "description": " A conditionally complete linear order with `bot` is a linear order with least element, in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and every nonempty subset (necessarily\nbounded below) has an infimum.  A typical example is the natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete linear orders, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_order_bot.decidable_lt", "description": " A conditionally complete linear order with `bot` is a linear order with least element, in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and every nonempty subset (necessarily\nbounded below) has an infimum.  A typical example is the natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete linear orders, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_order_bot.max_def", "description": " A conditionally complete linear order with `bot` is a linear order with least element, in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and every nonempty subset (necessarily\nbounded below) has an infimum.  A typical example is the natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete linear orders, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_order_bot.min_def", "description": " A conditionally complete linear order with `bot` is a linear order with least element, in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and every nonempty subset (necessarily\nbounded below) has an infimum.  A typical example is the natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete linear orders, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_order_bot.bot", "description": " A conditionally complete linear order with `bot` is a linear order with least element, in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and every nonempty subset (necessarily\nbounded below) has an infimum.  A typical example is the natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete linear orders, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_order_bot.bot_le", "description": " A conditionally complete linear order with `bot` is a linear order with least element, in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and every nonempty subset (necessarily\nbounded below) has an infimum.  A typical example is the natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete linear orders, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_order_bot.cSup_empty", "description": " A conditionally complete linear order with `bot` is a linear order with least element, in which\nevery nonempty subset which is bounded above has a supremum, and every nonempty subset (necessarily\nbounded below) has an infimum.  A typical example is the natural numbers.\n\nTo differentiate the statements from the corresponding statements in (unconditional)\ncomplete linear orders, we prefix Inf and Sup by a c everywhere. The same statements should\nhold in both worlds, sometimes with additional assumptions of nonemptiness or\nboundedness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "csupr_mono", "description": "The indexed supremum of two functions are comparable if the functions are pointwise comparable", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cInf_lt_of_lt", "description": "Inf s < b when there is an element a in s with a < b, when s is bounded below.\nThis is essentially an iff, except that the assumptions for the two implications are\nslightly different (one needs boundedness below for one direction, nonemptiness and linear\norder for the other one), so we formulate separately the two implications, contrary to\nthe complete_lattice case.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_cInf_iff''", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cInf_le_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.ghost_simp", "description": "A macro for a common simplification when rewriting with ghost component equations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.add_is_poly\u2082.comp\u2082_i", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.is_poly\u2082.map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.mul_is_poly\u2082", "description": "Multiplication of Witt vectors is a polynomial function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.is_poly\u2082.comp_left", "description": "The composition of a binary polynomial function\nwith a unary polynomial function in the first argument is polynomial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.mul_is_poly\u2082.comp_diag", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.is_poly\u2082.comp_right", "description": "The composition of a binary polynomial function\nwith a unary polynomial function in the second argument is polynomial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.mul_is_poly\u2082.comp\u2082_i", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.tactic.mk_poly\u2082_comp_lemmas", "description": "If `n` is the name of a lemma with opened type `\u2200 vars, is_poly\u2082 p _`,\n`mk_poly\u2082_comp_lemmas n vars p` adds composition instances to the environment\n`n.comp\u2082_i` and `n.comp_diag`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.is_poly\u2082.comp", "description": "The composition of polynomial functions is polynomial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.is_poly.comp", "description": "The composition of polynomial functions is polynomial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.tactic.mk_poly_comp_lemmas", "description": "If `n` is the name of a lemma with opened type `\u2200 vars, is_poly p _`,\n`mk_poly_comp_lemmas n vars p` adds composition instances to the environment\n`n.comp_i` and `n.comp\u2082_i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.ghost_calc", "description": "`ghost_calc` is a tactic for proving identities between polynomial functions.\nTypically, when faced with a goal like\n```lean\n\u2200 (x y : \ud835\udd4e R), verschiebung (x * frobenius y) = verschiebung x * y\n```\nyou can\n1. call `ghost_calc`\n2. do a small amount of manual work -- maybe nothing, maybe `rintro`, etc\n3. call `ghost_simp`\n\nand this will close the goal.\n\n`ghost_calc` cannot detect whether you are dealing with unary or binary polynomial functions.\nYou must give it arguments to determine this.\nIf you are proving a universally quantified goal like the above,\ncall `ghost_calc _ _`.\nIf the variables are introduced already, call `ghost_calc x y`.\nIn the unary case, use `ghost_calc _` or `ghost_calc x`.\n\n`ghost_calc` is a light wrapper around type class inference.\nAll it does is apply the appropriate extensionality lemma and try to infer the resulting goals.\nThis is subtle and Lean's elaborator doesn't like it because of the HO unification involved,\nso it is easier (and prettier) to put it in a tactic script.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.id_is_poly_i'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.one_is_poly", "description": "The function that is constantly one on Witt vectors is a polynomial function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.neg_is_poly.comp\u2082_i", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.neg_is_poly.comp_i", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.is_poly.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.is_poly\u2082.diag", "description": "The diagonal `\u03bb x, f x x` of a polynomial function `f` is polynomial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.bind\u2081_one_poly_witt_polynomial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.is_poly\u2082.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.is_poly.comp\u2082", "description": "The composition of a polynomial function with a binary polynomial function is polynomial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.neg_is_poly", "description": "The additive negation is a polynomial function on Witt vectors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.tactic.is_poly_attr", "description": "`@[is_poly]` is applied to lemmas of the form `is_poly f \u03c6` or `is_poly\u2082 f \u03c6`.\nThese lemmas should *not* be tagged as instances, and only atomic `is_poly` defs should be tagged:\ncomposition lemmas should not. Roughly speaking, lemmas that take `is_poly` proofs as arguments\nshould not be tagged.\n\nType class inference struggles with function composition, and the higher order unification problems\ninvolved in inferring `is_poly` proofs are complex. The standard style writing these proofs by hand\ndoesn't work very well. Instead, we construct the type class hierarchy \"under the hood\", with\nlimited forms of composition.\n\nApplying `@[is_poly]` to a lemma creates a number of instances. Roughly, if the tagged lemma is a\nproof of `is_poly f \u03c6`, the instances added have the form\n```lean\n\u2200 g \u03c8, [is_poly g \u03c8] \u2192 is_poly (f \u2218 g) _\n```\nSince `f` is fixed in this instance, it restricts the HO unification needed when the instance is\napplied. Composition lemmas relating `is_poly` with `is_poly\u2082` are also added.\n`id_is_poly` is an atomic instance.\n\nThe user-written lemmas are not instances. Users should be able to assemble `is_poly` proofs by hand\n\"as normal\" if the tactic fails.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.one_poly", "description": "The coefficients of `1 : \ud835\udd4e R` as polynomials.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.add_is_poly\u2082.comp_diag", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.is_poly\u2082.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.bind\u2081_zero_witt_polynomial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.is_poly.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.add_is_poly\u2082", "description": "Addition of Witt vectors is a polynomial function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.tactic.mk_comp_lemmas", "description": "The `after_set` function for `@[is_poly]`. Calls `mk_poly(\u2082)_comp_lemmas`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.is_poly.map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.is_poly", "description": "A function `f : \u03a0 R, \ud835\udd4e R \u2192 \ud835\udd4e R` that maps Witt vectors to Witt vectors over arbitrary base rings\nis said to be *polynomial* if there is a family of polynomials `\u03c6\u2099` over `\u2124` such that the `n`th\ncoefficient of `f x` is given by evaluating `\u03c6\u2099` at the coefficients of `x`.\n\nSee also `witt_vector.is_poly\u2082` for the binary variant.\n\nThe `ghost_calc` tactic treats `is_poly` as a type class,\nand the `@[is_poly]` attribute derives certain specialized composition instances\nfor declarations of type `is_poly f`.\nFor the most part, users are not expected to treat `is_poly` as a class.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.is_poly.poly", "description": "A function `f : \u03a0 R, \ud835\udd4e R \u2192 \ud835\udd4e R` that maps Witt vectors to Witt vectors over arbitrary base rings\nis said to be *polynomial* if there is a family of polynomials `\u03c6\u2099` over `\u2124` such that the `n`th\ncoefficient of `f x` is given by evaluating `\u03c6\u2099` at the coefficients of `x`.\n\nSee also `witt_vector.is_poly\u2082` for the binary variant.\n\nThe `ghost_calc` tactic treats `is_poly` as a type class,\nand the `@[is_poly]` attribute derives certain specialized composition instances\nfor declarations of type `is_poly f`.\nFor the most part, users are not expected to treat `is_poly` as a class.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.zero_is_poly", "description": "The function that is constantly zero on Witt vectors is a polynomial function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.is_poly\u2082", "description": "A binary function `f : \u03a0 R, \ud835\udd4e R \u2192 \ud835\udd4e R \u2192 \ud835\udd4e R` on Witt vectors\nis said to be *polynomial* if there is a family of polynomials `\u03c6\u2099` over `\u2124` such that the `n`th\ncoefficient of `f x y` is given by evaluating `\u03c6\u2099` at the coefficients of `x` and `y`.\n\nSee also `witt_vector.is_poly` for the unary variant.\n\nThe `ghost_calc` tactic treats `is_poly\u2082` as a type class,\nand the `@[is_poly]` attribute derives certain specialized composition instances\nfor declarations of type `is_poly\u2082 f`.\nFor the most part, users are not expected to treat `is_poly\u2082` as a class.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.is_poly\u2082.poly", "description": "A binary function `f : \u03a0 R, \ud835\udd4e R \u2192 \ud835\udd4e R \u2192 \ud835\udd4e R` on Witt vectors\nis said to be *polynomial* if there is a family of polynomials `\u03c6\u2099` over `\u2124` such that the `n`th\ncoefficient of `f x y` is given by evaluating `\u03c6\u2099` at the coefficients of `x` and `y`.\n\nSee also `witt_vector.is_poly` for the unary variant.\n\nThe `ghost_calc` tactic treats `is_poly\u2082` as a type class,\nand the `@[is_poly]` attribute derives certain specialized composition instances\nfor declarations of type `is_poly\u2082 f`.\nFor the most part, users are not expected to treat `is_poly\u2082` as a class.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.poly_eq_of_witt_polynomial_bind_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.id_is_poly", "description": "The identity function on Witt vectors is a polynomial function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.poly_eq_of_witt_polynomial_bind_eq'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Rep.diagonal_hom_equiv_symm_apply", "description": " Given a `k`-linear `G`-representation `A`, `diagonal_hom_equiv` is a `k`-linear isomorphism of\nthe set of representation morphisms `Hom(k[G\u207f\u207a\u00b9], A)` with `Fun(G\u207f, A)`. This lemma says that the\ninverse map sends a function `f : G\u207f \u2192 A` to the representation morphism sending\n`(g\u2080, ... g\u2099) \u21a6 \u03c1(g\u2080)(f(g\u2080\u207b\u00b9g\u2081, g\u2081\u207b\u00b9g\u2082, ..., g\u2099\u208b\u2081\u207b\u00b9g\u2099))`, where `\u03c1` is the representation attached\nto `A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.diagonal_succ_inv_single_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Rep.diagonal_hom_equiv_symm_partial_prod_succ", "description": " Auxiliary lemma for defining group cohomology, used to show that the isomorphism\n`diagonal_hom_equiv` commutes with the differentials in two complexes which compute\ngroup cohomology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.diagonal_succ_hom_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.Action_diagonal_succ_inv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.ProjectiveResolution", "description": "The standard projective resolution of `k` as a trivial `k`-linear `G`-representation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.quasi_iso_of_forget\u2082_\u03b5_to_single\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.d_eq", "description": " Simpler expression for the differential in the standard resolution of `k` as a\n`G`-representation. It sends `(g\u2080, ..., g\u2099\u208a\u2081) \u21a6 \u2211 (-1)\u2071 \u2022 (g\u2080, ..., \u011d\u1d62, ..., g\u2099\u208a\u2081)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Rep.category_theory.enough_projectives", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Rep.diagonal_hom_equiv_apply", "description": " Given a `k`-linear `G`-representation `A`, `diagonal_hom_equiv` is a `k`-linear isomorphism of\nthe set of representation morphisms `Hom(k[G\u207f\u207a\u00b9], A)` with `Fun(G\u207f, A)`. This lemma says that this\nsends a morphism of representations `f : k[G\u207f\u207a\u00b9] \u27f6 A` to the function\n`(g\u2081, ..., g\u2099) \u21a6 f(1, g\u2081, g\u2081g\u2082, ..., g\u2081g\u2082...g\u2099).`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.of_mul_action_basis", "description": " A `k[G]`-basis of `k[G\u207f\u207a\u00b9]`, coming from the `k[G]`-linear isomorphism\n`k[G] \u2297\u2096 k[G\u207f] \u2243 k[G\u207f\u207a\u00b9].`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.d_comp_\u03b5", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "classifying_space_universal_cover.extra_degeneracy_augmented_cech_nerve", "description": " The augmented \u010cech nerve of the map from `fin 1 \u2192 G` to the terminal object in `Type u` has an\nextra degeneracy.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.\u03b5_to_single\u2080.quasi_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.d_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.\u03b5", "description": "The hom of `k`-linear `G`-representations `k[G\u00b9] \u2192 k` sending `\u2211 n\u1d62g\u1d62 \u21a6 \u2211 n\u1d62`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "classifying_space_universal_cover.cech_nerve_terminal_from_iso", "description": " When the category is `G`-Set, `cech_nerve_terminal_from` of `G` with the left regular action is\nisomorphic to `EG`, the universal cover of the classifying space of `G` as a simplicial `G`-set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "classifying_space_universal_cover.comp_forget_augmented.to_Module", "description": " The free functor `Type u \u2964 Module.{u} k` applied to the universal cover of the classifying\nspace of `G` as a simplicial set, augmented by the map from `fin 1 \u2192 G` to the terminal object\nin `Type u`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Rep.diagonal_hom_equiv", "description": " Given a `k`-linear `G`-representation `A`, the set of representation morphisms\n`Hom(k[G\u207f\u207a\u00b9], A)` is `k`-linearly isomorphic to the set of functions `G\u207f \u2192 A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.forget\u2082_to_Module_homotopy_equiv_f_0_eq", "description": " The homotopy equivalence of complexes of `k`-modules between the standard resolution of `k` as\na trivial `G`-representation, and the complex which is `k` at 0 and 0 everywhere else, acts as\n`\u2211 n\u1d62g\u1d62 \u21a6 \u2211 n\u1d62 : k[G\u00b9] \u2192 k` at 0.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "classifying_space_universal_cover.cech_nerve_terminal_from_iso_comp_forget", "description": " As a simplicial set, `cech_nerve_terminal_from` of a monoid `G` is isomorphic to the universal\ncover of the classifying space of `G` as a simplicial set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.X_iso", "description": " The `n`th object of the standard resolution of `k` is definitionally isomorphic to `k[G\u207f\u207a\u00b9]`\nequipped with the representation induced by the diagonal action of `G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.comp_forget_augmented_iso", "description": " If we apply the free functor `Type u \u2964 Module.{u} k` to the universal cover of the classifying\nspace of `G` as a simplicial set, then take the alternating face map complex, the result is\nisomorphic to the standard resolution of the trivial `G`-representation `k` as a complex of\n`k`-modules.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.\u03b5_to_single\u2080", "description": " The chain map from the standard resolution of `k` to `k[0]` given by `\u2211 n\u1d62g\u1d62 \u21a6 \u2211 n\u1d62` in\ndegree zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.\u03b5_to_single\u2080_comp_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "classifying_space_universal_cover.extra_degeneracy_comp_forget_augmented_to_Module", "description": " If we augment the universal cover of the classifying space of `G` as a simplicial set by the\nmap from `fin 1 \u2192 G` to the terminal object in `Type u`, then apply the free functor\n`Type u \u2964 Module.{u} k`, the resulting augmented simplicial `k`-module has an extra degeneracy.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "classifying_space_universal_cover.extra_degeneracy_comp_forget_augmented", "description": " The universal cover of the classifying space of `G` as a simplicial set, augmented by the map\nfrom `fin 1 \u2192 G` to the terminal object in `Type u`, has an extra degeneracy.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.diagonal_succ_inv_single_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution", "description": " The standard resolution of `k` as a trivial representation, defined as the alternating\nface map complex of a simplicial `k`-linear `G`-representation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.Action_diagonal_succ_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.diagonal_succ_inv_single_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.of_mul_action_basis_aux", "description": " The `k[G]`-linear isomorphism `k[G] \u2297\u2096 k[G\u207f] \u2243 k[G\u207f\u207a\u00b9]`, where the `k[G]`-module structure on\nthe lefthand side is `tensor_product.left_module`, whilst that of the righthand side comes from\n`representation.as_module`. Allows us to use `basis.algebra_tensor_product` to get a `k[G]`-basis\nof the righthand side.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.diagonal_succ", "description": " An isomorphism of `k`-linear representations of `G` from `k[G\u207f\u207a\u00b9]` to `k[G] \u2297\u2096 k[G\u207f]` (on\nwhich `G` acts by `\u03c1(g\u2081)(g\u2082 \u2297 x) = (g\u2081 * g\u2082) \u2297 x`) sending `(g\u2080, ..., g\u2099)` to\n`g\u2080 \u2297 (g\u2080\u207b\u00b9g\u2081, g\u2081\u207b\u00b9g\u2082, ..., g\u2099\u208b\u2081\u207b\u00b9g\u2099)`. The inverse sends `g\u2080 \u2297 (g\u2081, ..., g\u2099)` to\n`(g\u2080, g\u2080g\u2081, ..., g\u2080g\u2081...g\u2099)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.X_projective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "classifying_space_universal_cover", "description": "The simplicial `G`-set sending `[n]` to `G\u207f\u207a\u00b9` equipped with the diagonal action of `G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.Action_diagonal_succ", "description": " An isomorphism of `G`-sets `G\u207f\u207a\u00b9 \u2245 G \u00d7 G\u207f`, where `G` acts by left multiplication on `G\u207f\u207a\u00b9` and\n`G` but trivially on `G\u207f`. The map sends `(g\u2080, ..., g\u2099) \u21a6 (g\u2080, (g\u2080\u207b\u00b9g\u2081, g\u2081\u207b\u00b9g\u2082, ..., g\u2099\u208b\u2081\u207b\u00b9g\u2099))`,\nand the inverse is `(g\u2080, (g\u2081, ..., g\u2099)) \u21a6 (g\u2080, g\u2080g\u2081, g\u2080g\u2081g\u2082, ..., g\u2080g\u2081...g\u2099).`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.forget\u2082_to_Module_homotopy_equiv", "description": " As a complex of `k`-modules, the standard resolution of the trivial `G`-representation `k` is\nhomotopy equivalent to the complex which is `k` at 0 and 0 elsewhere.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.Ext_iso", "description": " Given a `k`-linear `G`-representation `V`, `Ext\u207f(k, V)` (where `k` is a trivial `k`-linear\n`G`-representation) is isomorphic to the `n`th cohomology group of `Hom(P, V)`, where `P` is the\nstandard resolution of `k` called `group_cohomology.resolution k G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "classifying_space_universal_cover.comp_forget_augmented", "description": " The universal cover of the classifying space of `G` as a simplicial set, augmented by the map\nfrom `fin 1 \u2192 G` to the terminal object in `Type u`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.d", "description": " The `k`-linear map underlying the differential in the standard resolution of `k` as a trivial\n`k`-linear `G`-representation. It sends `(g\u2080, ..., g\u2099) \u21a6 \u2211 (-1)\u2071 \u2022 (g\u2080, ..., \u011d\u1d62, ..., g\u2099)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.of_mul_action_free", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_cohomology.resolution.forget\u2082_to_Module", "description": "The standard resolution of `k` as a trivial representation as a complex of `k`-modules.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_add_swap", "description": " Given three points not equal to `p`, the angle between the second and the third at `p` plus\nthe angle between the first and the second equals the angle between the first and the third.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_rotate_sign", "description": "Rotating the points in an oriented angle does not change the sign of that angle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_eq_pi_iff_sbtw", "description": " The oriented angle between three points is \u03c0 if and only if the second point is strictly\nbetween the other two.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.collinear_iff_of_two_zsmul_oangle_eq", "description": " If twice the oriented angles between two triples of points are equal, one triple is collinear\nif and only if the other is.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.eq_zero_or_angle_eq_zero_or_pi_of_sign_oangle_eq_zero", "description": " If the sign of the oriented angle at `p` between two points is zero, either one of the points\nequals `p` or the unoriented angle is 0 or \u03c0.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.right_ne_of_oangle_sign_ne_zero", "description": " If the sign of the angle between three points is nonzero, the last two points are not\nequal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.dist_eq_iff_eq_smul_rotation_pi_div_two_vadd_midpoint", "description": " Two different points are equidistant from a third point if and only if that third point\nequals some multiple of a `\u03c0 / 2` rotation of the vector between those points, plus the midpoint\nof those points.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_eq_zero_iff_oangle_rev_eq_zero", "description": " An oriented angle is zero if and only if the angle with the order of the points reversed is\nzero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.angle_rev_eq_pi_div_two_of_oangle_eq_neg_pi_div_two", "description": " If the oriented angle between three points is `-\u03c0 / 2`, the unoriented angle (reversed) is\n`\u03c0 / 2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.right_ne_of_oangle_eq_pi_div_two", "description": "If the angle between three points is `\u03c0 / 2`, the last two points are not equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear.oangle_sign_of_same_ray_vsub", "description": " Given two pairs of distinct points on the same line, such that the vectors between those\npairs of points are on the same ray (oriented in the same direction on that line), and a fifth\npoint, the angles at the fifth point between each of those two pairs of points have the same\nsign.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_add_cyc3", "description": " Given three points not equal to `p`, adding the angles between them at `p` in cyclic order\nresults in 0.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_midpoint_rev_right", "description": " An oriented angle is unchanged by replacing the third point with the midpoint of the segment\nbetween the second point and that point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.right_ne_of_oangle_eq_pi", "description": "If the angle between three points is `\u03c0`, the last two points are not equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.angle_eq_pi_div_two_of_oangle_eq_neg_pi_div_two", "description": "If the oriented angle between three points is `-\u03c0 / 2`, the unoriented angle is `\u03c0 / 2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.abs_oangle_right_to_real_lt_pi_div_two_of_dist_eq", "description": "A base angle of an isosceles triangle is acute, oriented angle-at-point form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_add_oangle_rev", "description": "Adding an angle to that with the order of the points reversed results in 0.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_add", "description": " Given three points not equal to `p`, the angle between the first and the second at `p` plus\nthe angle between the second and the third equals the angle between the first and the third.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_self_right", "description": "The angle \u2221ABB at a point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.left_ne_of_oangle_sign_ne_zero", "description": " If the sign of the angle between three points is nonzero, the first two points are not\nequal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.cos_oangle_eq_cos_angle", "description": " The cosine of the oriented angle at `p` between two points not equal to `p` equals that of the\nunoriented angle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_eq_pi_iff_oangle_rev_eq_pi", "description": " An oriented angle is `\u03c0` if and only if the angle with the order of the points reversed is\n`\u03c0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.oangle_sign_eq_left", "description": " Given three points in strict order on the same line, and a fourth point, the angles at the\nfourth point between the first and second or first and third points have the same sign.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.affine_independent_iff_of_two_zsmul_oangle_eq", "description": " If twice the oriented angles between two triples of points are equal, one triple is affinely\nindependent if and only if the other is.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.left_ne_of_oangle_sign_eq_neg_one", "description": " If the sign of the angle between three points is negative, the first two points are not\nequal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.left_ne_right_of_oangle_eq_pi", "description": "If the angle between three points is `\u03c0`, the first and third points are not equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.oangle_sign_eq_right", "description": " Given three points in strict order on the same line, and a fourth point, the angles at the\nfourth point between the second and third or first and third points have the same sign.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_eq_oangle_of_dist_eq", "description": "Pons asinorum, oriented angle-at-point form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.left_ne_of_oangle_ne_zero", "description": "If the angle between three points is nonzero, the first two points are not equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.oangle\u2083\u2081\u2082_eq_zero", "description": " If the second of three points is strictly between the other two, the oriented angle at the\nfirst point (reversed) is zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "wbtw.oangle\u2082\u2083\u2081_eq_zero", "description": " If the second of three points is weakly between the other two, the oriented angle at the\nthird point is zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "wbtw.oangle\u2083\u2081\u2082_eq_zero", "description": " If the second of three points is weakly between the other two, the oriented angle at the\nfirst point (reversed) is zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_eq_angle_of_sign_eq_one", "description": " The oriented angle between three points equals the unoriented angle if the sign is\npositive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.two_zsmul_oangle_of_parallel", "description": " If the lines determined by corresponding pairs of points in two angles are parallel, twice\nthose angles are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_eq_zero_iff_wbtw", "description": " The oriented angle between three points is zero if and only if one of the first and third\npoints is weakly between the other two.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_eq_pi_iff_angle_eq_pi", "description": "The oriented angle between three points is `\u03c0` if and only if the unoriented angle is `\u03c0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.oangle_eq_right", "description": " An oriented angle is unchanged by replacing the third point by one strictly further away on\nthe same ray.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.right_ne_of_oangle_sign_eq_one", "description": " If the sign of the angle between three points is positive, the last two points are not\nequal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_midpoint_right", "description": " An oriented angle is unchanged by replacing the third point with the midpoint of the segment\nbetween it and the second point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.oangle\u2082\u2081\u2083_eq_zero", "description": " If the second of three points is strictly between the other two, the oriented angle at the\nfirst point is zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.angle_eq_iff_oangle_eq_of_sign_eq", "description": " If the signs of two nondegenerate oriented angles between points are equal, the oriented\nangles are equal if and only if the unoriented angles are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_eq_angle_or_eq_neg_angle", "description": " The oriented angle at `p` between two points not equal to `p` is plus or minus the unoriented\nangle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_midpoint_left", "description": " An oriented angle is unchanged by replacing the first point with the midpoint of the segment\nbetween it and the second point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.oangle_eq_left", "description": " An oriented angle is unchanged by replacing the first point by one strictly further away on\nthe same ray.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.oangle_sign_eq", "description": " Given three points in strict order on the same line, and a fourth point, the angles at the\nfourth point between the first and second or second and third points have the same sign.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.left_ne_right_of_oangle_ne_zero", "description": "If the angle between three points is nonzero, the first and third points are not equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.angle_eq_pi_div_two_of_oangle_eq_pi_div_two", "description": "If the oriented angle between three points is `\u03c0 / 2`, so is the unoriented angle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.right_ne_of_oangle_sign_eq_neg_one", "description": "If the sign of the angle between three points is negative, the last two points are not equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle", "description": " The oriented angle at `p\u2082` between the line segments to `p\u2081` and `p\u2083`, modulo `2 * \u03c0`. If\neither of those points equals `p\u2082`, this is 0. See `euclidean_geometry.angle` for the\ncorresponding unoriented angle definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_midpoint_rev_left", "description": " An oriented angle is unchanged by replacing the first point with the midpoint of the segment\nbetween the second point and that point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "wbtw.oangle_sign_eq_of_ne_left", "description": " Given three points in weak order on the same line, with the first not equal to the second,\nand a fourth point, the angles at the fourth point between the first and second or first and\nthird points have the same sign.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.oangle\u2081\u2083\u2082_eq_zero", "description": " If the second of three points is strictly between the other two, the oriented angle at the\nthird point (reversed) is zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.left_ne_right_of_oangle_sign_eq_neg_one", "description": " If the sign of the angle between three points is negative, the first and third points are not\nequal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.oangle_eq_left_right", "description": " Replacing both the first and third points by ones on the same lines but the opposite rays\ndoes not change the oriented angle (vertically opposite angles).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "wbtw.oangle\u2081\u2083\u2082_eq_zero", "description": " If the second of three points is weakly between the other two, the oriented angle at the\nthird point (reversed) is zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.two_zsmul_oangle_of_vector_span_eq", "description": " If corresponding pairs of points in two angles have the same vector span, twice those angles\nare equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "wbtw.oangle\u2082\u2081\u2083_eq_zero", "description": " If the second of three points is weakly between the other two, the oriented angle at the\nfirst point is zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_eq_zero_iff_angle_eq_zero", "description": " The unoriented angle at `p` between two points not equal to `p` is zero if and only if the\nunoriented angle is zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.oangle\u2081\u2082\u2083_eq_pi", "description": " If the second of three points is strictly between the other two, the oriented angle at that\npoint is \u03c0.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear.two_zsmul_oangle_eq_left", "description": "Replacing the first point by one on the same line does not change twice the oriented angle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear.two_zsmul_oangle_eq_right", "description": "Replacing the third point by one on the same line does not change twice the oriented angle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_rev", "description": "Reversing the order of the points passed to `oangle` negates the angle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.oangle_eq_add_pi_right", "description": " Replacing the third point by one on the same line but the opposite ray adds \u03c0 to the oriented\nangle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.right_ne_of_oangle_ne_zero", "description": "If the angle between three points is nonzero, the last two points are not equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.left_ne_right_of_oangle_sign_eq_one", "description": " If the sign of the angle between three points is positive, the first and third points are not\nequal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.angle_eq_abs_oangle_to_real", "description": " The unoriented angle at `p` between two points not equal to `p` is the absolute value of the\noriented angle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.left_ne_of_oangle_eq_pi", "description": "If the angle between three points is `\u03c0`, the first two points are not equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.continuous_at_oangle", "description": "Oriented angles are continuous when neither end point equals the middle point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_eq_neg_angle_of_sign_eq_neg_one", "description": " The oriented angle between three points equals minus the unoriented angle if the sign is\nnegative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.oangle\u2083\u2082\u2081_eq_pi", "description": " If the second of three points is strictly between the other two, the oriented angle at that\npoint (reversed) is \u03c0.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_eq_of_angle_eq_of_sign_eq", "description": " If two unoriented angles are equal, and the signs of the corresponding oriented angles are\nequal, then the oriented angles are equal (even in degenerate cases).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_swap\u2082\u2083_sign", "description": "Swapping the second and third points in an oriented angle negates the sign of that angle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.left_ne_right_of_oangle_sign_ne_zero", "description": " If the sign of the angle between three points is nonzero, the first and third points are not\nequal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_subspace.s_same_side.oangle_sign_eq", "description": " Given two points in an affine subspace, the angles between those two points at two other\npoints on the same side of that subspace have the same sign.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_ne_zero_and_ne_pi_iff_affine_independent", "description": " An oriented angle is not zero or `\u03c0` if and only if the three points are affinely\nindependent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "wbtw.oangle_eq_right", "description": " An oriented angle is unchanged by replacing the third point by one weakly further away on the\nsame ray.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_eq_zero_or_eq_pi_iff_collinear", "description": "An oriented angle is zero or `\u03c0` if and only if the three points are collinear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.right_ne_of_oangle_eq_neg_pi_div_two", "description": "If the angle between three points is `-\u03c0 / 2`, the last two points are not equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_eq_pi_sub_two_zsmul_oangle_of_dist_eq", "description": " The angle at the apex of an isosceles triangle is `\u03c0` minus twice a base angle, oriented\nangle-at-point form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.left_ne_right_of_oangle_eq_pi_div_two", "description": "If the angle between three points is `\u03c0 / 2`, the first and third points are not equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_sub_left", "description": " Given three points not equal to `p`, the angle between the first and the third at `p` minus\nthe angle between the first and the second equals the angle between the second and the third.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.left_ne_of_oangle_eq_neg_pi_div_two", "description": "If the angle between three points is `-\u03c0 / 2`, the first two points are not equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.left_ne_of_oangle_sign_eq_one", "description": " If the sign of the angle between three points is positive, the first two points are not\nequal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.oangle_eq_add_pi_left", "description": " Replacing the first point by one on the same line but the opposite ray adds \u03c0 to the oriented\nangle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_swap\u2081\u2083_sign", "description": "Swapping the first and third points in an oriented angle negates the sign of that angle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_swap\u2081\u2082_sign", "description": "Swapping the first and second points in an oriented angle negates the sign of that angle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_self_left", "description": "The angle \u2221AAB at a point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.oangle\u2082\u2083\u2081_eq_zero", "description": " If the second of three points is strictly between the other two, the oriented angle at the\nthird point is zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.angle_rev_eq_pi_div_two_of_oangle_eq_pi_div_two", "description": " If the oriented angle between three points is `\u03c0 / 2`, so is the unoriented angle\n(reversed).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "wbtw.oangle_sign_eq_of_ne_right", "description": " Given three points in weak order on the same line, with the second not equal to the third,\nand a fourth point, the angles at the fourth point between the second and third or first and\nthird points have the same sign.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.left_ne_right_of_oangle_eq_neg_pi_div_two", "description": "If the angle between three points is `-\u03c0 / 2`, the first and third points are not equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.abs_oangle_left_to_real_lt_pi_div_two_of_dist_eq", "description": "A base angle of an isosceles triangle is acute, oriented angle-at-point form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_subspace.s_opp_side.oangle_sign_eq_neg", "description": " Given two points in an affine subspace, the angles between those two points at two other\npoints on opposite sides of that subspace have opposite signs.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_self_left_right", "description": "The angle \u2221ABA at a point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.oangle_sub_right", "description": " Given three points not equal to `p`, the angle between the first and the third at `p` minus\nthe angle between the second and the third equals the angle between the first and the second.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.left_ne_of_oangle_eq_pi_div_two", "description": "If the angle between three points is `\u03c0 / 2`, the first two points are not equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "wbtw.oangle_eq_left", "description": " An oriented angle is unchanged by replacing the first point by one weakly further away on the\nsame ray.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.split", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_split.short_exact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.iso_comp_snd_eq_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.inl_comp_iso_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.iso_hom_fst_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact_of_split", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.inr_iso_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.split_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.short_exact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.section_retraction_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.section", "description": " If `h` is a splitting of `A -f\u27f6 B -g\u27f6 C`,\nthen `h.section : C \u27f6 B` is the morphism satisfying `h.section \u226b g = \ud835\udfd9 C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.split.left_split", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact_inl_snd", "description": "The sequence `A \u27f6 A \u229e B \u27f6 B` is exact.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.retraction.category_theory.epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_split", "description": " An exact sequence `A -f\u27f6 B -g\u27f6 C` is *right split*\nif there exists a morphism `\u03c6 : C \u27f6 B` such that `f \u226b \u03c6 = \ud835\udfd9 A` and `f` is mono.\n\nSuch a sequence is automatically short exact (i.e., `g` is epi).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_split.right_split", "description": " An exact sequence `A -f\u27f6 B -g\u27f6 C` is *right split*\nif there exists a morphism `\u03c6 : C \u27f6 B` such that `f \u226b \u03c6 = \ud835\udfd9 A` and `f` is mono.\n\nSuch a sequence is automatically short exact (i.e., `g` is epi).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_split.mono", "description": " An exact sequence `A -f\u27f6 B -g\u27f6 C` is *right split*\nif there exists a morphism `\u03c6 : C \u27f6 B` such that `f \u226b \u03c6 = \ud835\udfd9 A` and `f` is mono.\n\nSuch a sequence is automatically short exact (i.e., `g` is epi).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_split.exact", "description": " An exact sequence `A -f\u27f6 B -g\u27f6 C` is *right split*\nif there exists a morphism `\u03c6 : C \u27f6 B` such that `f \u226b \u03c6 = \ud835\udfd9 A` and `f` is mono.\n\nSuch a sequence is automatically short exact (i.e., `g` is epi).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.section.category_theory.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.exact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact_inr_fst", "description": "The sequence `B \u27f6 A \u229e B \u27f6 A` is exact.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.split", "description": " An exact sequence `A -f\u27f6 B -g\u27f6 C` is *split* if there exist\n`\u03c6 : B \u27f6 A` and `\u03c7 : C \u27f6 B` such that:\n* `f \u226b \u03c6 = \ud835\udfd9 A`\n* `\u03c7 \u226b g = \ud835\udfd9 C`\n* `f \u226b g = 0`\n* `\u03c7 \u226b \u03c6 = 0`\n* `\u03c6 \u226b f + g \u226b \u03c7 = \ud835\udfd9 B`\n\nSuch a sequence is automatically short exact (i.e., `f` is mono and `g` is epi).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.split.split", "description": " An exact sequence `A -f\u27f6 B -g\u27f6 C` is *split* if there exist\n`\u03c6 : B \u27f6 A` and `\u03c7 : C \u27f6 B` such that:\n* `f \u226b \u03c6 = \ud835\udfd9 A`\n* `\u03c7 \u226b g = \ud835\udfd9 C`\n* `f \u226b g = 0`\n* `\u03c7 \u226b \u03c6 = 0`\n* `\u03c6 \u226b f + g \u226b \u03c7 = \ud835\udfd9 B`\n\nSuch a sequence is automatically short exact (i.e., `f` is mono and `g` is epi).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.comp_iso_eq_inl_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.\u03c0_section_eq_id_sub_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.inr_iso_inv_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_split", "description": " An exact sequence `A -f\u27f6 B -g\u27f6 C` is *left split*\nif there exists a morphism `\u03c6 : B \u27f6 A` such that `f \u226b \u03c6 = \ud835\udfd9 A` and `g` is epi.\n\nSuch a sequence is automatically short exact (i.e., `f` is mono).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_split.left_split", "description": " An exact sequence `A -f\u27f6 B -g\u27f6 C` is *left split*\nif there exists a morphism `\u03c6 : B \u27f6 A` such that `f \u226b \u03c6 = \ud835\udfd9 A` and `g` is epi.\n\nSuch a sequence is automatically short exact (i.e., `f` is mono).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_split.epi", "description": " An exact sequence `A -f\u27f6 B -g\u27f6 C` is *left split*\nif there exists a morphism `\u03c6 : B \u27f6 A` such that `f \u226b \u03c6 = \ud835\udfd9 A` and `g` is epi.\n\nSuch a sequence is automatically short exact (i.e., `f` is mono).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_split.exact", "description": " An exact sequence `A -f\u27f6 B -g\u27f6 C` is *left split*\nif there exists a morphism `\u03c6 : B \u27f6 A` such that `f \u226b \u03c6 = \ud835\udfd9 A` and `g` is epi.\n\nSuch a sequence is automatically short exact (i.e., `f` is mono).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.comp_eq_zero_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.split.map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.split.exact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.split_mono", "description": "The retraction in a splitting is a split mono.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.split.short_exact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.iso_comp_eq_snd_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting", "description": " A *splitting* of a sequence `A -f\u27f6 B -g\u27f6 C` is an isomorphism\nto the short exact sequence `0 \u27f6 A \u27f6 A \u229e C \u27f6 C \u27f6 0` such that\nthe vertical maps on the left and the right are the identity.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.iso", "description": " A *splitting* of a sequence `A -f\u27f6 B -g\u27f6 C` is an isomorphism\nto the short exact sequence `0 \u27f6 A \u27f6 A \u229e C \u27f6 C \u27f6 0` such that\nthe vertical maps on the left and the right are the identity.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.comp_iso_eq_inl", "description": " A *splitting* of a sequence `A -f\u27f6 B -g\u27f6 C` is an isomorphism\nto the short exact sequence `0 \u27f6 A \u27f6 A \u229e C \u27f6 C \u27f6 0` such that\nthe vertical maps on the left and the right are the identity.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.iso_comp_snd_eq", "description": " A *splitting* of a sequence `A -f\u27f6 B -g\u27f6 C` is an isomorphism\nto the short exact sequence `0 \u27f6 A \u27f6 A \u229e C \u27f6 C \u27f6 0` such that\nthe vertical maps on the left and the right are the identity.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.\u03c0_section_eq_id_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.short_exact", "description": "If `f : A \u27f6 B` and `g : B \u27f6 C` then `short_exact f g` is the proposition saying\n the resulting diagram `0 \u27f6 A \u27f6 B \u27f6 C \u27f6 0` is an exact sequence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.short_exact.mono", "description": "If `f : A \u27f6 B` and `g : B \u27f6 C` then `short_exact f g` is the proposition saying\n the resulting diagram `0 \u27f6 A \u27f6 B \u27f6 C \u27f6 0` is an exact sequence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.short_exact.epi", "description": "If `f : A \u27f6 B` and `g : B \u27f6 C` then `short_exact f g` is the proposition saying\n the resulting diagram `0 \u27f6 A \u27f6 B \u27f6 C \u27f6 0` is an exact sequence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.short_exact.exact", "description": "If `f : A \u27f6 B` and `g : B \u27f6 C` then `short_exact f g` is the proposition saying\n the resulting diagram `0 \u27f6 A \u27f6 B \u27f6 C \u27f6 0` is an exact sequence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.\u03b9_retraction_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.comp_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.iso_hom_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.retraction_\u03b9_eq_id_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.retraction", "description": " If `h` is a splitting of `A -f\u27f6 B -g\u27f6 C`,\nthen `h.retraction : B \u27f6 A` is the morphism satisfying `f \u226b h.retraction = \ud835\udfd9 A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.split_epi", "description": "The section in a splitting is a split epi.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_split.short_exact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.split.right_split", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.inl_comp_iso_eq_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.section_\u03c0_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.splitting_of_is_iso_zero", "description": " A short exact sequence of the form `X -f\u27f6 Y -0\u27f6 Z` where `f` is an iso and `Z` is zero\nhas a splitting.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.splittings_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.section_\u03c0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.section_retraction", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.retraction_\u03b9_eq_id_sub_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.iso_comp_eq_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.\u03b9_retraction", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdifferential", "description": "The homogeneous differential as a linear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pointed_smooth_map.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_functions_tower", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_functions_algebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pointed_smooth_map.fun_like", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pointed_smooth_map.eval_algebra", "description": "`smooth_map.eval_ring_hom` gives rise to an algebra structure of `C^\u221e\u27eeI, M; \ud835\udd5c\u27ef` on `\ud835\udd5c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdifferential_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_function.eval_at", "description": "Evaluation at a point gives rise to a `C^\u221e\u27eeI, M; \ud835\udd5c\u27ef`-linear map between `C^\u221e\u27eeI, M; \ud835\udd5c\u27ef` and `\ud835\udd5c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pointed_smooth_map.algebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pointed_smooth_map.cont_mdiff_map.is_scalar_tower", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hfdifferential", "description": " The heterogeneous differential as a linear map. Instead of taking a function as an argument this\ndifferential takes `h : f x = y`. It is particularly handy to deal with situations where the points\non where it has to be evaluated are equal but not definitionally equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pointed_smooth_map.eval", "description": "With the `eval_algebra` algebra structure evaluation is actually an algebra morphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "apply_hfdifferential", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "derivation.eval_at", "description": "The evaluation at a point as a linear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "point_derivation", "description": " The derivations at a point of a manifold. Some regard this as a possible definition of the\ntangent space", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "apply_fdifferential", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "derivation.eval_at_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pointed_smooth_map.smul_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pointed_smooth_map", "description": " Type synonym, introduced to put a different `has_smul` action on `C^n\u27eeI, M; \ud835\udd5c\u27ef`\nwhich is defined as `f \u2022 r = f(x) * r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pointed_smooth_map.comm_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pointed_smooth_map.is_scalar_tower", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pointed_smooth_map.cont_mdiff_map.algebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.bound_of_antilipschitz", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.mk_continuous_of_exists_bound_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.coe_to_span_nonzero_singleton_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.to_continuous_linear_map\u2081", "description": " Reinterpret a linear map `\ud835\udd5c \u2192\u2097[\ud835\udd5c] E` as a continuous linear map. This construction\nis generalized to the case of any finite dimensional domain\nin `linear_map.to_continuous_linear_map`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.coord", "description": "Given a nonzero element `x` of a normed space `E\u2081` over a field `\ud835\udd5c`, the natural continuous\n   linear map from the span of `x` to `\ud835\udd5c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.to_span_nonzero_singleton", "description": "Given a nonzero element `x` of a normed space `E\u2081` over a field `\ud835\udd5c`, the natural\n   continuous linear equivalence from `E\u2081` to the span of `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_of_linear_of_bound", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.to_continuous_linear_map\u2081_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.coord_to_span_nonzero_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.to_span_nonzero_singleton_coord", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.to_span_singleton_homothety", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.mk_continuous_of_exists_bound", "description": " Construct a continuous linear map from a linear map and the existence of a bound on this linear\nmap. If you have an explicit bound, use `linear_map.mk_continuous` instead, as a norm estimate will\nfollow automatically in `linear_map.mk_continuous_norm_le`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.uniform_embedding_of_bound", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.mk_continuous_of_exists_bound_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.to_span_nonzero_singleton_homothety", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.of_homothety", "description": "A linear equivalence which is a homothety is a continuous linear equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.mk_continuous_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.mk_continuous", "description": " Construct a continuous linear map from a linear map and a bound on this linear map.\nThe fact that the norm of the continuous linear map is then controlled is given in\n`linear_map.mk_continuous_norm_le`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.antilipschitz_of_bound", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.to_continuous_linear_map\u2081_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.of_homothety", "description": "A (semi-)linear map which is a homothety is a continuous linear map.\n   Since the field `\ud835\udd5c` need not have `\u211d` as a subfield, this theorem is not directly deducible from\n   the corresponding theorem about isometries plus a theorem about scalar multiplication.  Likewise\n   for the other theorems about homotheties in this file.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.mk_continuous_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.homothety_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_equiv.to_continuous_linear_equiv_of_bounds", "description": " Construct a continuous linear equivalence from a linear equivalence together with\nbounds in both directions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.coord_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_of_linear_of_bound\u209b\u2097", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "punit.vadd_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "punit.is_central_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "punit.has_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "punit.smul_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "punit.zero_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "punit.normalized_gcd_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "punit.smul_with_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "punit.mul_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "punit.linear_ordered_cancel_add_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "punit.canonically_ordered_add_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": 