hlib"}, {"name": "lie_module_hom.ker", "description": "The kernel of a morphism of Lie algebras, as an ideal in the domain.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.comap_map_le", "description": "See also `lie_ideal.map_comap_eq`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.hom_of_le_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.has_zero", "description": "The zero module is a Lie submodule of any Lie module.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_mem_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module_hom.ker_eq_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.mem_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.coe_to_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.coe_map_of_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.has_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module_hom.comp_ker_incl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.span_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.add_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module_hom.range", "description": " The range of a morphism of Lie modules `f : M \u2192 N` is a Lie submodule of `N`.\nSee Note [range copy pattern].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.map_le_iff_le_comap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.mem_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.eq_bot_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_hom.ideal_range_eq_lie_span_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.span_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_hom.range_subset_ideal_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.sup_coe_to_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.nontrivial_iff_ne_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.comap_coe_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.map_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.subsingleton_of_bot", "description": " Note that this is not a special case of `lie_submodule.subsingleton_of_bot`. Indeed, given\n`I : lie_ideal R L`, in general the two lattices `lie_ideal R I` and `lie_submodule R L I` are\ndifferent (though the latter does naturally inject into the former).\n\nIn other words, in general, ideals of `I`, regarded as a Lie algebra in its own right, are not the\nsame as ideals of `L` contained in `I`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.incl_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.incl", "description": "The inclusion of a Lie submodule into its ambient space is a morphism of Lie modules.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.mem_coe_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.has_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.mk_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.coe_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_hom.le_ker_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.coe_submodule_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.coe_bracket", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.top_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.mem_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module_hom.ker_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.ker_incl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.Inf_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.hom_of_le_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.lie_algebra", "description": "Transfer the `lie_algebra` instance from the coercion `lie_ideal \u2192 lie_subalgebra`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.coe_to_subalgebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.nontrivial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.coe_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.coe_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.module", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.coe_submodule_le_coe_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.coe_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.mem_to_lie_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.map", "description": " A morphism of Lie algebras `f : L \u2192 L'` pushes forward Lie ideals of `L` to Lie ideals of `L'`.\n\nNote that unlike `lie_submodule.map`, we must take the `lie_span` of the image. Mathematically\nthis is because although `f` makes `L'` into a Lie module over `L`, in general the `L` submodules of\n`L'` are not the same as the ideals of `L'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_hom.ker_coe_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.bot_coe_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.coe_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.coe_to_submodule_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.mem_lie_span", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.map_comap_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.hom_of_le", "description": "Given two nested Lie ideals `I\u2081 \u2286 I\u2082`, the inclusion `I\u2081 \u21aa I\u2082` is a morphism of Lie algebras.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.top_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.span_Union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_hom.ideal_range_eq_top_of_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_hom.is_ideal_morphism_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.lie_ring", "description": "An ideal of `L` is a Lie subalgebra of `L`, so it is a Lie ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.lie_span_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.Inf_coe_to_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.map_le_iff_le_comap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.mem_carrier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.inf_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.comap_incl_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.incl_is_ideal_morphism", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.coe_to_lie_subalgebra_to_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.comap", "description": " A morphism of Lie modules `f : M \u2192 M'` pulls back Lie submodules of `M'` to Lie submodules of\n`M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.map_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_hom.range_eq_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.coe_to_lie_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.incl_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module_hom.map_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.add_eq_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_hom.ker_eq_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.lie_module", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.set_like", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_hom.map_le_ideal_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module_hom.mem_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.submodule_span_le_lie_span", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.copy", "description": " Copy of a lie_submodule with a new `carrier` equal to the old one. Useful to fix definitional\nequalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module_hom.coe_submodule_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal", "description": "An ideal of a Lie algebra is a Lie submodule of the Lie algebra as a Lie module over itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.incl", "description": " Regarding an ideal `I` as a subalgebra, the inclusion map into its ambient space is a morphism\nof Lie algebras.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.coe_submodule_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.gc_map_comap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.coe_hom_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.to_submodule_eq_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.has_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_hom.is_ideal_morphism_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "div_sq_cancel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.zpow_left\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.self_zpow\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semiconj_by.zpow_right\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zpow_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.zpow_zpow\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zpow_add_one\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pow_inv_comm\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zpow_ne_zero_of_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inv_pow_sub_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zpow_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.zpow_self\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zpow_bit1'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zpow_neg_mul_zpow_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pow_sub_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_zpow_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zpow_sub\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zpow_add'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_zpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zpow_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pow_sub\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zpow_bit1\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.zpow_zpow_self\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zpow_sub_one\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "map_zpow\u2080", "description": " If a monoid homomorphism `f` between two `group_with_zero`s maps `0` to `0`, then it maps `x^n`,\n`n : \u2124`, to `(f x)^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.zpow_right\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zpow_add\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inv_pow_sub\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zpow_one_add\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.mod_right", "description": "The Jacobi symbol `J(a | b)` depends only on `b` mod `4*a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "qr_sign.symm", "description": "`qr_sign` is symmetric when both arguments are odd.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.mod_left'", "description": "The symbol `J(a | b)` depends only on `a` mod `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "qr_sign.mul_left", "description": "`qr_sign` is multiplicative in the first argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.sq_one", "description": "The square of `J(a | b)` is `1` when `a` and `b` are coprime.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.list_prod_right", "description": "We can pull out a product over a list in the second argument of the Jacobi symbol.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.quadratic_reciprocity_three_mod_four", "description": " The Law of Quadratic Reciprocityfor the Jacobi symbol: if `a` and `b` are natural numbers\nboth congruent to `3` mod `4`, then `J(a | b) = -J(b | a)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.mod_left", "description": "The symbol `J(a | b)` depends only on `a` mod `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.mod_right'", "description": "The Jacobi symbol `J(a | b)` depends only on `b` mod `4*a` (version for `a : \u2115`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "qr_sign.sq_eq_one", "description": "When `m` and `n` are odd, then the square of `qr_sign m n` is `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.list_prod_left", "description": "We can pull out a product over a list in the first argument of the Jacobi symbol.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym", "description": "The Jacobi symbol of `a` and `b`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.quadratic_reciprocity", "description": "The Law of Quadratic Reciprocity for the Jacobi symbol", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.is_square_of_jacobi_sym_eq_one", "description": "If `p` is prime and `J(a | p) = 1`, then `a` is q square mod `p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.pow_left", "description": "We have that `J(a^e | b) = J(a | b)^e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "legendre_sym.to_jacobi_sym", "description": " The Legendre symbol `legendre_sym p a` with an integer `a` and a prime number `p`\nis the same as the Jacobi symbol `J(a | p)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "qr_sign.neg_one_pow", "description": "We can express `qr_sign m n` as a power of `-1` when `m` and `n` are odd.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.prime_dvd_of_eq_neg_one", "description": " If `p` is prime, `J(a | p) = -1` and `p` divides `x^2 - a*y^2`, then `p` must divide\n`x` and `y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.trichotomy", "description": "The Jacobi symbol takes only the values `0`, `1` and `-1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.at_two", "description": "If `b` is odd, then `J(2 | b)` is given by `\u03c7\u2088 b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.value_at", "description": " If `\u03c7` is a multiplicative function such that `J(a | p) = \u03c7 p` for all odd primes `p`,\nthen `J(a | b)` equals `\u03c7 b` for all odd natural numbers `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.quadratic_reciprocity'", "description": "The Law of Quadratic Reciprocity for the Jacobi symbol, version with `qr_sign`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.at_neg_one", "description": "If `b` is odd, then `J(-1 | b)` is given by `\u03c7\u2084 b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.zero_left", "description": "The symbol `J(0 | b)` vanishes when `b > 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "qr_sign", "description": "The bi-multiplicative map giving the sign in the Law of Quadratic Reciprocity", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.mul_right'", "description": "The Jacobi symbol is multiplicative in its second argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.eq_zero_iff_not_coprime", "description": "The symbol `J(a | b)` vanishes iff `a` and `b` are not coprime (assuming `b \u2260 0`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.mul_right", "description": "The Jacobi symbol is multiplicative in its second argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.mul_left", "description": "The Jacobi symbol is multiplicative in its first argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.eq_zero_iff", "description": "The symbol `J(a | b)` vanishes if and only if `b \u2260 0` and `a` and `b` are not coprime.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.neg", "description": "If `b` is odd, then `J(-a | b) = \u03c7\u2084 b * J(a | b)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.sq_one'", "description": "The symbol `J(a^2 | b)` is `1` when `a` and `b` are coprime.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.zero_right", "description": "The symbol `J(a | 0)` has the value `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.pow_right", "description": "We have that `J(a | b^e) = J(a | b)^e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.one_right", "description": "The symbol `J(a | 1)` has the value `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.quadratic_reciprocity_one_mod_four'", "description": " The Law of Quadratic Reciprocity for the Jacobi symbol: if `a` and `b` are natural numbers\nwith `a` odd and `b % 4 = 1`, then `J(a | b) = J(b | a)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.one_left", "description": "The symbol `J(1 | b)` has the value `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.at_neg_two", "description": "If `b` is odd, then `J(-2 | b)` is given by `\u03c7\u2088' b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.eq_one_or_neg_one", "description": "The symbol `J(a | b)` takes the value `1` or `-1` if `a` and `b` are coprime.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.nonsquare_of_jacobi_sym_eq_neg_one", "description": "If `J(a | b)` is `-1`, then `a` is not a square modulo `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.quadratic_reciprocity_one_mod_four", "description": " The Law of Quadratic Reciprocity for the Jacobi symbol: if `a` and `b` are natural numbers\nwith `a % 4 = 1` and `b` odd, then `J(a | b) = J(b | a)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.nonsquare_iff_jacobi_sym_eq_neg_one", "description": "If `p` is prime, then `J(a | p)` is `-1` iff `a` is not a square modulo `p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "qr_sign.mul_right", "description": "`qr_sign` is multiplicative in the second argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "qr_sign.eq_iff_eq", "description": "We can move `qr_sign m n` from one side of an equality to the other when `m` and `n` are odd.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.eq_neg_one_at_prime_divisor_of_eq_neg_one", "description": "If `J(a | n) = -1`, then `n` has a prime divisor `p` such that `J(a | p) = -1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jacobi_sym.ne_zero", "description": "The symbol `J(a | b)` is nonzero when `a` and `b` are coprime.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "irrational.subtype.order_topology", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eventually_residual_irrational", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "irrational.eventually_forall_le_dist_cast_div_of_denom_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "irrational.eventually_forall_le_dist_cast_rat_of_denom_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "irrational.subtype.densely_ordered", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_G\u03b4_irrational", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "irrational.eventually_forall_le_dist_cast_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dense_irrational", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "irrational.subtype.no_max_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "irrational.subtype.no_min_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_eq.differentiable_within_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_zero_of_eventually_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_filter_iff_tendsto", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_eq.fderiv_within", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_eq.has_strict_fderiv_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "asymptotics.is_O.has_fderiv_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_filter_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.fderiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.differentiable_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.insert'", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `has_fderiv_within_at_insert`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.exists_lipschitz_on_with_of_nnnorm_lt", "description": " If `f` is strictly differentiable at `x` with derivative `f'` and `K > \u2016f'\u2016\u208a`, then `f` is\n`K`-Lipschitz in a neighborhood of `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at_congr_set'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.is_O", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.differentiable_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_filter.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_filter", "description": " A function `f` has the continuous linear map `f'` as derivative along the filter `L` if\n`f x' = f x + f' (x' - x) + o (x' - x)` when `x'` converges along the filter `L`. This definition\nis designed to be specialized for `L = \ud835\udcdd x` (in `has_fderiv_at`), giving rise to the usual notion\nof Fr\u00e9chet derivative, and for `L = \ud835\udcdd[s] x` (in `has_fderiv_within_at`), giving rise to\nthe notion of Fr\u00e9chet derivative along the set `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.le_of_lip", "description": " Converse to the mean value inequality: if `f` is differentiable at `x\u2080` and `C`-lipschitz\non a neighborhood of `x\u2080` then it its derivative at `x\u2080` has norm bounded by `C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_eq.has_fderiv_within_at_iff_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at_iff_tendsto", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at.eq", "description": "`unique_diff_within_at` achieves its goal: it implies the uniqueness of the derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.continuous_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_at.le_of_lip", "description": " Converse to the mean value inequality: if `f` is differentiable at `x\u2080` and `C`-lipschitz\non a neighborhood of `x\u2080` then it its derivative at `x\u2080` has norm bounded by `C`.\nVersion using `fderiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_const_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.subsingleton.differentiable_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.continuous_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at_congr_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at", "description": " A function `f` is differentiable at a point `x` within a set `s` if it admits a derivative\nthere (possibly non-unique).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_filter.is_O_sub_rev", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.exists_lipschitz_on_with", "description": " If `f` is strictly differentiable at `x` with derivative `f'`, then `f` is Lipschitz in a\nneighborhood of `x`. See also `has_strict_fderiv_at.exists_lipschitz_on_with_of_nnnorm_lt` for a\nmore precise statement.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_eq_fderiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.has_fderiv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.differentiable_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.has_fderiv_at_of_univ", "description": "**Alias** of the forward direction of `has_fderiv_within_at_univ`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.fderiv_within_congr_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_iff_is_o_nhds_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_id'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.congr_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_eq.has_fderiv_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at_of_not_mem_closure", "description": " If `x` is not in the closure of `s`, then `f` has any derivative at `x` within `s`,\nas this statement is empty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_eq.fderiv_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on_of_locally_differentiable_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on.eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.has_fderiv_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.fderiv_within", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.congr_of_eventually_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at_id'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "asymptotics.is_O.has_fderiv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_eq.has_fderiv_within_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_congr_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.lim", "description": " If a function f has a derivative f' at x, a rescaled version of f around x converges to f',\ni.e., `n (f (x + (1/n) v) - f x)` converges to `f' v`. More generally, if `c n` tends to infinity\nand `c n * d n` tends to `v`, then `c n * (f (x + d n) - f x)` tends to `f' v`. This lemma expresses\nthis fact, for functions having a derivative within a set. Its specific formulation is useful for\ntangent cone related discussions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_eq.fderiv_within_eq_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_filter.tendsto_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.continuous_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_congr'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at_congr_set'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at_inter'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_eventually_congr_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.mono_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.is_O_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_eventually_congr_set'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.insert", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.congr_of_eventually_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_mem_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at_diff_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at_insert", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.has_fderiv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at_inter'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "support_fderiv_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.continuous_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.differentiable_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.congr_of_eventually_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_eq.fderiv_within'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.has_fderiv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.lim", "description": "Directional derivative agrees with `has_fderiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.is_O", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_filter_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at", "description": " A function `f` has derivative `f'` at `a` in the sense of *strict differentiability*\nif `f x - f y - f' (x - y) = o(x - y)` as `x, y \u2192 a`. This form of differentiability is required,\ne.g., by the inverse function theorem. Any `C^1` function on a vector space over `\u211d` is strictly\ndifferentiable but this definition works, e.g., for vector spaces over `p`-adic numbers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.unique_on", "description": " If `f'` and `f\u2081'` are two derivatives of `f` within `s` at `x`, then they are equal on the\ntangent cone to `s` at `x`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_id'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support.fderiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_eq.fderiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.continuous", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_id'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.of_insert", "description": "**Alias** of the forward direction of `has_fderiv_within_at_insert`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.congr'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.le_of_lip'", "description": " Converse to the mean value inequality: if `f` is differentiable at `x\u2080` and `C`-lipschitz\non a neighborhood of `x\u2080` then it its derivative at `x\u2080` has norm bounded by `C`. This version\nonly assumes that `\u2016f x - f x\u2080\u2016 \u2264 C * \u2016x - x\u2080\u2016` in a neighborhood of `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.differentiable_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.differentiable_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.has_fderiv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_filter.congr_of_eventually_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at", "description": " A function `f` has the continuous linear map `f'` as derivative at `x` if\n`f x' = f x + f' (x' - x) + o (x' - x)` when `x'` tends to `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_const_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within", "description": " If `f` has a derivative at `x` within `s`, then `fderiv_within \ud835\udd5c f s x` is such a derivative.\nOtherwise, it is set to `0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.fderiv_within", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.eventually_differentiable_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_of_mem_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.is_O_sub_rev", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_eq.differentiable_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at", "description": " A function `f` is differentiable at a point `x` if it admits a derivative there (possibly\nnon-unique).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.continuous_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_congr_set'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.nhds_within", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on", "description": "`differentiable_on \ud835\udd5c f s` means that `f` is differentiable within `s` at any point of `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.has_fderiv_at_filter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.has_fderiv_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_eq.has_fderiv_at_filter_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.differentiable_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.mono_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at", "description": " A function `f` has the continuous linear map `f'` as derivative at `x` within a set `s` if\n`f x' = f x + f' (x' - x) + o (x' - x)` when `x'` tends to `x` inside `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_eq.differentiable_within_at_iff_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at_congr_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_iff_tendsto", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.congr_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_zero_of_not_differentiable_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.continuous_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_of_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_of_open", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.differentiable_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_mem_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at_inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_zero_of_not_differentiable_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.congr_of_eventually_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsupport_fderiv_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_filter.is_O_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable", "description": "`differentiable \ud835\udd5c f` means that `f` is differentiable at any point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_eq.fderiv_within_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.congr_of_eventually_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at_inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.congr_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv", "description": " If `f` has a derivative at `x`, then `fderiv \ud835\udd5c f x` is such a derivative. Otherwise, it is\nset to `0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.walk.is_eulerian.card_filter_odd_degree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.walk.is_eulerian.fintype_edge_set", "description": "The edge set of an Eulerian graph is finite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.walk.is_eulerian", "description": " An *Eulerian trail* (also known as an \"Eulerian path\") is a walk\n`p` that visits every edge exactly once.  The lemma `simple_graph.walk.is_eulerian.is_trail` shows\nthat these are trails.\n\nCombine with `p.is_circuit` to get an Eulerian circuit (also known as an \"Eulerian cycle\").", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.walk.is_eulerian.even_degree_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.walk.is_eulerian_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.walk.is_trail.even_countp_edges_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.walk.is_eulerian.card_odd_degree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.walk.is_trail.edges_finset", "description": "The edges of a trail as a finset, since each edge in a trail appears exactly once.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.walk.is_eulerian.edges_finset_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.walk.is_eulerian.mem_edges_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.walk.is_trail.is_eulerian_of_forall_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.walk.is_eulerian.is_trail", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.periodic.map_mod_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.periodic_coprime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.periodic_mod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.filter_multiset_Ico_card_eq_of_periodic", "description": " An interval of length `a` filtered over a periodic predicate of period `a` has cardinality\nequal to the number naturals below `a` for which `p a` is true.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.filter_Ico_card_eq_of_periodic", "description": " An interval of length `a` filtered over a periodic predicate of period `a` has cardinality\nequal to the number naturals below `a` for which `p a` is true.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.periodic_gcd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euler_sine.antideriv_sin_comp_const_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euler_sine.integral_sin_mul_sin_mul_cos_pow_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euler_sine.integral_cos_mul_cos_pow_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euler_sine.integral_cos_mul_cos_pow", "description": "Note this also holds for `z = 0`, but we do not need this case for `sin_pi_mul_eq`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.tendsto_euler_sin_prod", "description": "Euler's infinite product formula for the real sine function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euler_sine.integral_cos_pow_eq", "description": " Relate the integral `cos x ^ n` over `[0, \u03c0/2]` to the integral of `sin x ^ n` over `[0, \u03c0]`,\nwhich is studied in `data.real.pi.wallis` and other places.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euler_sine.integral_cos_mul_cos_pow_even", "description": "Note this also holds for `z = 0`, but we do not need this case for `sin_pi_mul_eq`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euler_sine.tendsto_integral_cos_pow_mul_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.tendsto_euler_sin_prod", "description": "Euler's infinite product formula for the complex sine function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euler_sine.sin_pi_mul_eq", "description": " Finite form of Euler's sine product, with remainder term expressed as a ratio of cosine\nintegrals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euler_sine.integral_cos_pow_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euler_sine.antideriv_cos_comp_const_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at.pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on_convex", "description": " In a real vector space, a convex set with nonempty interior is a set of unique\ndifferentiability.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at.mono_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at_of_mem_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on_Ici", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on", "description": " A property ensuring that the tangent cone to `s` at any of its points spans a dense subset of\nthe whole space.  The main role of this property is to ensure that the differential along `s` is\nunique, hence this name. The uniqueness it asserts is proved in `unique_diff_on.eq` in\n`fderiv.lean`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at_inter'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tangent_cone_mono_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on.univ_pi", "description": " The finite product of a family of sets of unique differentiability is a set of unique\ndifferentiability.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tangent_cone_congr", "description": "Tangent cone of `s` at `x` depends only on `\ud835\udcdd[s] x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on.prod", "description": "The product of two sets of unique differentiability is a set of unique differentiability.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at_Iio", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tangent_cone_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_tangent_cone_of_open_segment_subset", "description": " If a subset of a real vector space contains an open segment, then the direction of this\nsegment belongs to the tangent cone at its endpoints.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at.inter'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on_Iio", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at_inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at.inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on_Icc_zero_one", "description": "The real interval `[0, 1]` is a set of unique differentiability.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subset_tangent_cone_prod_right", "description": "The tangent cone of a product contains the tangent cone of its right factor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_tangent_cone_of_segment_subset", "description": " If a subset of a real vector space contains a segment, then the direction of this\nsegment belongs to the tangent cone at its endpoints.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tangent_cone_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_open.unique_diff_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on.unique_diff_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on_Iic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at.prod", "description": " The product of two sets of unique differentiability at points `x` and `y` has unique\ndifferentiability at `(x, y)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at.univ_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subset_tangent_cone_prod_left", "description": "The tangent cone of a product contains the tangent cone of its left factor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tangent_cone_at", "description": "The set of all tangent directions to the set `s` at the point `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tangent_cone_at.lim_zero", "description": " Auxiliary lemma ensuring that, under the assumptions defining the tangent cone,\nthe sequence `d` tends to 0 at infinity.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tangent_cone_inter_nhds", "description": "Intersecting with a neighborhood of the point does not change the tangent cone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at", "description": " A property ensuring that the tangent cone to `s` at `x` spans a dense subset of the whole space.\nThe main role of this property is to ensure that the differential within `s` at `x` is unique,\nhence this name. The uniqueness it asserts is proved in `unique_diff_within_at.eq` in `fderiv.lean`.\nTo avoid pathologies in dimension 0, we also require that `x` belongs to the closure of `s` (which\nis automatic when `E` is not `0`-dimensional).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at.dense_tangent_cone", "description": " A property ensuring that the tangent cone to `s` at `x` spans a dense subset of the whole space.\nThe main role of this property is to ensure that the differential within `s` at `x` is unique,\nhence this name. The uniqueness it asserts is proved in `unique_diff_within_at.eq` in `fderiv.lean`.\nTo avoid pathologies in dimension 0, we also require that `x` belongs to the closure of `s` (which\nis automatic when `E` is not `0`-dimensional).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at.mem_closure", "description": " A property ensuring that the tangent cone to `s` at `x` spans a dense subset of the whole space.\nThe main role of this property is to ensure that the differential within `s` at `x` is unique,\nhence this name. The uniqueness it asserts is proved in `unique_diff_within_at.eq` in `fderiv.lean`.\nTo avoid pathologies in dimension 0, we also require that `x` belongs to the closure of `s` (which\nis automatic when `E` is not `0`-dimensional).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maps_to_tangent_cone_pi", "description": "The tangent cone of a product contains the tangent cone of each factor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on.pi", "description": " The finite product of a family of sets of unique differentiability is a set of unique\ndifferentiability.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_open.unique_diff_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on.inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_within_at_convex", "description": " In a real vector space, a convex set with nonempty interior is a set of unique\ndifferentiability at every point of its closure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_two.sub_eq_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_two.multiset_sum_mul_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_two.bit1_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_two.add_self_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_two.sum_mul_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_two.multiset_sum_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_two.sub_eq_add'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_two.neg_eq'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_two.bit1_apply_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_two.neg_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_two.bit0_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_of_neg_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "neg_one_eq_one_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_two.list_sum_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_two.add_mul_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_two.add_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_two.bit0_apply_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_two.sum_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_two.list_sum_mul_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_two.two_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry.map_orthogonal_projection'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflection_involutive", "description": "Reflection is involutive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_orthogonal_projection_of_le", "description": "If `U \u2264 V`, then projecting on `V` and then on `U` is the same as projecting on `U`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.orthogonal_orthogonal_eq_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_family.is_internal_iff", "description": " An orthogonal family of subspaces of `E` satisfies `direct_sum.is_internal` (that is,\nthey provide an internal direct sum decomposition of `E`) if and only if their span has trivial\northogonal complement.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_orthogonal_projection_eq_of_mem_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.sup_orthogonal_of_complete_space", "description": "If `K` is complete, `K` and `K\u15ee` span the whole space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflection", "description": " Reflection in a complete subspace of an inner product space.  The word \"reflection\" is\nsometimes understood to mean specifically reflection in a codimension-one subspace, and sometimes\nmore generally to cover operations such as reflection in a point.  The definition here, of\nreflection in a subspace, is a more general sense of the word that includes both those common\ncases.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_eq_infi_iff_real_inner_eq_zero", "description": "Characterization of minimizers in the projection on a subspace, in the real case.\nLet `u` be a point in a real inner product space, and let `K` be a nonempty subspace.\nThen point `v` minimizes the distance `\u2016u - v\u2016` over points in `K` if and only if\nfor all `w \u2208 K`, `\u27eau - v, w\u27eb = 0` (i.e., `u - v` is orthogonal to the subspace `K`).\nThis is superceded by `norm_eq_infi_iff_inner_eq_zero` that gives the same conclusion over\nany `is_R_or_C` field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_minimal", "description": "The orthogonal projection of `y` on `U` minimizes the distance `\u2016y - x\u2016` for `x \u2208 U`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_family.sum_projection_of_mem_supr", "description": "If `x` lies within an orthogonal family `v`, it can be expressed as a sum of projections.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_singleton", "description": "Formula for orthogonal projection onto a single vector.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflection_mem_subspace_eq_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sub_orthogonal_projection_mem_orthogonal", "description": "The difference of `v` from its orthogonal projection onto `K` is in `K\u15ee`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_eq_infi_iff_inner_eq_zero", "description": "Characterization of minimizers in the projection on a subspace.\nLet `u` be a point in an inner product space, and let `K` be a nonempty subspace.\nThen point `v` minimizes the distance `\u2016u - v\u2016` over points in `K` if and only if\nfor all `w \u2208 K`, `\u27eau - v, w\u27eb = 0` (i.e., `u - v` is orthogonal to the subspace `K`)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_norm_le", "description": "The orthogonal projection has norm `\u2264 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_bot", "description": "The orthogonal projection onto the trivial submodule is the zero map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_unit_singleton", "description": "Formula for orthogonal projection onto a single unit vector.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.sup_orthogonal_inf_of_complete_space", "description": "If `K\u2081` is complete and contained in `K\u2082`, `K\u2081` and `K\u2081\u15ee \u2293 K\u2082` span `K\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_orthogonal_projection_fn_of_mem_of_inner_eq_zero", "description": " The unbundled orthogonal projection is the unique point in `K`\nwith the orthogonality property.  This lemma is only intended for use\nin setting up the bundled version and should not be used once that is\ndefined.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_is_symmetric", "description": "The orthogonal projection is symmetric.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_comp_subtypeL_eq_zero_iff", "description": "The projection into `U` from `V` is the zero map if and only if `U` and `V` are orthogonal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_coe_linear_map_eq_linear_proj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.triorthogonal_eq_orthogonal", "description": "The orthogonal complement satisfies `K\u15ee\u15ee\u15ee = K\u15ee`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflection_mul_reflection", "description": "Reflection is involutive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_fn_inner_eq_zero", "description": " The characterization of the unbundled orthogonal projection.  This\nlemma is only intended for use in setting up the bundled version\nand should not be used once that is defined.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finrank_orthogonal_span_singleton", "description": " In a finite-dimensional inner product space, the dimension of the orthogonal complement of the\nspan of a nonzero vector is one less than the dimension of the space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_map_apply", "description": "Orthogonal projection onto the `submodule.map` of a subspace.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflection_orthogonal_complement_singleton_eq_neg", "description": "The reflection in `(\ud835\udd5c \u2219 v)\u15ee` of `v` is `-v`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dense.eq_zero_of_mem_orthogonal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_family.projection_direct_sum_coe_add_hom", "description": " If a family of submodules is orthogonal, then the `orthogonal_projection` on a direct sum\nis just the coefficient of that direct sum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dense.eq_of_inner_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.orthogonal_orthogonal", "description": "If `K` is complete, then the orthogonal complement of its orthogonal complement is itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflection_inv", "description": "Reflection is its own inverse.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_eq_linear_proj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_tendsto_closure_supr", "description": " Given a monotone family `U` of complete submodules of `E` and a fixed `x : E`,\nthe orthogonal projection of `x` on `U i` tends to the orthogonal projection of `x` on\n`(\u2a06 i, U i).topological_closure` along `at_top`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_fn_norm_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflection_map_apply", "description": "Reflection in the `submodule.map` of a subspace.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflection_mem_subspace_orthogonal_precomplement_eq_neg", "description": "The reflection in `K\u15ee` of an element of `K` is its negation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_inner_eq_zero", "description": "The characterization of the orthogonal projection.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.finrank_add_inf_finrank_orthogonal'", "description": " Given a finite-dimensional subspace `K\u2082`, and a subspace `K\u2081`\ncontainined in it, the dimensions of `K\u2081` and the intersection of its\northogonal subspace with `K\u2082` add to that of `K\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_fn_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_orthogonal_complement_singleton_eq_zero", "description": "The orthogonal projection onto `(\ud835\udd5c \u2219 v)\u15ee` of `v` is zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflection_mem_subspace_orthogonal_complement_eq_neg", "description": "The reflection in `K` of an element of `K\u15ee` is its negation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_compl_orthogonal_of_complete_space", "description": "If `K` is complete, `K` and `K\u15ee` are complements of each other.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_ortho.orthogonal_projection_comp_subtypeL", "description": "The projection into `U` from an orthogonal submodule `V` is the zero map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dense.eq_zero_of_inner_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_fn_mem", "description": " The unbundled orthogonal projection is in the given subspace.\nThis lemma is only intended for use in setting up the bundled version\nand should not be used once that is defined.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_sq_eq_add_norm_sq_projection", "description": "The Pythagorean theorem, for an orthogonal projection.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection", "description": "The orthogonal projection onto a complete subspace.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_eq_self_iff", "description": "A point equals its orthogonal projection if and only if it lies in the subspace.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_fn", "description": " The orthogonal projection onto a complete subspace, as an\nunbundled function.  This definition is only intended for use in\nsetting up the bundled version `orthogonal_projection` and should not\nbe used once that is defined.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflection_bot", "description": "Reflection through the trivial subspace {0} is just negation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_norm_eq_infi_of_complete_convex", "description": "Existence of minimizers\nLet `u` be a point in a real inner product space, and let `K` be a nonempty complete convex subset.\nThen there exists a (unique) `v` in `K` that minimizes the distance `\u2016u - v\u2016` to `u`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.finrank_add_inf_finrank_orthogonal", "description": " Given a finite-dimensional subspace `K\u2082`, and a subspace `K\u2081`\ncontainined in it, the dimensions of `K\u2081` and the intersection of its\northogonal subspace with `K\u2082` add to that of `K\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.finrank_add_finrank_orthogonal", "description": " Given a finite-dimensional space `E` and subspace `K`, the dimensions of `K` and `K\u15ee` add to\nthat of `E`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflection_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_orthogonal_projection_of_mem_of_inner_eq_zero", "description": " The orthogonal projection is the unique point in `K` with the\northogonality property.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.topological_closure_eq_top_iff", "description": "The closure of `K` is the full space iff `K\u15ee` is trivial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflection_linear_equiv", "description": "Auxiliary definition for `reflection`: the reflection as a linear equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_mem_subspace_eq_self", "description": "The orthogonal projection sends elements of `K` to themselves.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflection_symm", "description": "Reflection is its own inverse.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.finrank_add_finrank_orthogonal'", "description": " Given a finite-dimensional space `E` and subspace `K`, the dimensions of `K` and `K\u15ee` add to\nthat of `E`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflection_trans_reflection", "description": "Reflection is involutive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.reflections_generate_dim_aux", "description": " An element `\u03c6` of the orthogonal group of `F` can be factored as a product of reflections, and\nspecifically at most as many reflections as the dimension of the complement of the fixed subspace\nof `\u03c6`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_orthogonal_projection_left_eq_right", "description": "The orthogonal projection is self-adjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dense.eq_of_inner_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_family.is_internal_iff_of_is_complete", "description": " An orthogonal family of subspaces of `E` satisfies `direct_sum.is_internal` (that is,\nthey provide an internal direct sum decomposition of `E`) if and only if their span has trivial\northogonal complement.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_eq_infi_iff_real_inner_le_zero", "description": " Characterization of minimizers for the projection on a convex set in a real inner product\nspace.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_mem_subspace_orthogonal_precomplement_eq_zero", "description": "The orthogonal projection onto `K\u15ee` of an element of `K` is zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_orthogonal_projection_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry.map_orthogonal_projection", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_mem_subspace_orthogonal_complement_eq_zero", "description": "The orthogonal projection onto `K` of an element of `K\u15ee` is zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflection_map", "description": "Reflection in the `submodule.map` of a subspace.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_orthogonal_projection_eq_of_mem_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflection_eq_self_iff", "description": "A point is its own reflection if and only if it is in the subspace.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "id_eq_sum_orthogonal_projection_self_orthogonal_complement", "description": " In a complete space `E`, the projection maps onto a complete subspace `K` and its orthogonal\ncomplement sum to the identity.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.reflections_generate_dim", "description": " The orthogonal group of `F` is generated by reflections; specifically each element `\u03c6` of the\northogonal group is a product of at most as many reflections as the dimension of `F`.\n\nSpecial case of the **Cartan\u2013Dieudonn\u00e9 theorem**.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.orthogonal_eq_bot_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflection_apply", "description": "The result of reflecting.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximal_orthonormal_iff_basis_of_finite_dimensional", "description": " An orthonormal set in a finite-dimensional `inner_product_space` is maximal, if and only if it\nis a basis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dense.eq_of_sub_mem_orthogonal", "description": "If `S` is dense and `x - y \u2208 K\u15ee`, then `x = y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflection_reflection", "description": "Reflecting twice in the same subspace.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximal_orthonormal_iff_orthogonal_complement_eq_bot", "description": " An orthonormal set in an `inner_product_space` is maximal, if and only if the orthogonal\ncomplement of its span is empty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_family.decomposition", "description": " If a family of submodules is orthogonal and they span the whole space, then the orthogonal\nprojection provides a means to decompose the space into its submodules.\n\nThe projection function is `decompose V x i = orthogonal_projection (V i) x`.\n\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dense.eq_zero_of_inner_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_orthogonal_projection_of_eq_submodule", "description": "The orthogonal projections onto equal subspaces are coerced back to the same point in `E`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_orthogonal_projection_of_mem_orthogonal", "description": " A point in `K` with the orthogonality property (here characterized in terms of `K\u15ee`) must be the\northogonal projection.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_orthogonal_projection_of_mem_orthogonal'", "description": " A point in `K` with the orthogonality property (here characterized in terms of `K\u15ee`) must be the\northogonal projection.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_sum_orthogonal_projection_self_orthogonal_complement", "description": " In a complete space `E`, a vector splits as the sum of its orthogonal projections onto a\ncomplete submodule `K` and onto the orthogonal complement of `K`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.exists_sum_mem_mem_orthogonal", "description": "If `K` is complete, any `v` in `E` can be expressed as a sum of elements of `K` and `K\u15ee`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.reflections_generate", "description": "The orthogonal group of `F` is generated by reflections.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthogonal_projection_tendsto_self", "description": " Given a monotone family `U` of complete submodules of `E` with dense span supremum,\nand a fixed `x : E`, the orthogonal projection of `x` on `U i` tends to `x` along `at_top`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_norm_eq_infi_of_complete_subspace", "description": "Existence of projections on complete subspaces.\nLet `u` be a point in an inner product space, and let `K` be a nonempty complete subspace.\nThen there exists a (unique) `v` in `K` that minimizes the distance `\u2016u - v\u2016` to `u`.\nThis point `v` is usually called the orthogonal projection of `u` onto `K`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda_sections_hom_down", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda.yoneda_faithful", "description": "The Yoneda embedding is faithful.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001P>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coyoneda_obj_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.repr_f.category_theory.is_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda.naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda_sections_small", "description": "When `C` is a small category, we can restate the isomorphism from `yoneda_sections`\nwithout having to change universes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coyoneda.naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.corepr_w", "description": "An isomorphism between `F` and a functor of the form `C(F.corepr X, -)`. Note the components\n`F.corepr_w.app X` definitionally have type `F.corepr_X \u27f6 X \u2245 F.obj X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.corepr_f", "description": "The (forward direction of the) isomorphism witnessing `F` is corepresentable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.repr_w_app_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda_map_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda_equiv_symm_app_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coyoneda.coyoneda_faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda.ext", "description": " Extensionality via Yoneda. The typical usage would be\n```lean\n-- Goal is `X \u2245 Y`\napply yoneda.ext,\n-- Goals are now functions `(Z \u27f6 X) \u2192 (Z \u27f6 Y)`, `(Z \u27f6 Y) \u2192 (Z \u27f6 X)`, and the fact that these\nfunctions are inverses and natural in `Z`.\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.repr_x", "description": "The representing element for the representable functor `F`, sometimes called the universal\nelement of the functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.representable_of_nat_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coyoneda", "description": "The co-Yoneda embedding, as a functor from `C\u1d52\u1d56` into co-presheaves on `C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.id.functor.corepresentable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.curried_yoneda_lemma'", "description": "The curried version of yoneda lemma when `C` is small.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda_sections_small_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda_pairing_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda_equiv_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coyoneda.obj_op_op_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.obj.corepresentable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.corepresentable_of_nat_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda_sections_small_inv_app_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.prod_category_instance_1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda_equiv", "description": "We have a type-level equivalence between natural transformations from the yoneda embedding\nand elements of `F.obj X`, without any universe switching.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coyoneda.obj_op_op", "description": "Taking the `unop` of morphisms is a natural isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coyoneda.is_iso", "description": "If `coyoneda.map f` is an isomorphism, so was `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coyoneda_map_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda_evaluation_map_down", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda", "description": "The Yoneda embedding, as a functor from `C` into presheaves on `C`.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001O>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coyoneda_obj_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.representable", "description": "A functor `F : C\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 Type v\u2081` is representable if there is object `X` so `F \u2245 yoneda.obj X`.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001Q>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.representable.has_representation", "description": "A functor `F : C\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 Type v\u2081` is representable if there is object `X` so `F \u2245 yoneda.obj X`.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001Q>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda_obj_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.repr_X", "description": "The representing object for the representable functor `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coyoneda.obj_op_op_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda.obj_map_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.corepr_w_app_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda_pairing", "description": "The \"Yoneda pairing\" functor, which sends `X : C\u1d52\u1d56` and `F : C\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 Type`\nto `yoneda.op.obj X \u27f6 F`, functorially in both `X` and `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.repr_w_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda_sections_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.repr_w", "description": "An isomorphism between `F` and a functor of the form `C(-, F.repr_X)`.  Note the components\n`F.repr_w.app X` definitionally have type `(X.unop \u27f6 F.repr_X) \u2245 F.obj X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.prod_category_instance_2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.repr_f", "description": "The (forward direction of the) isomorphism witnessing `F` is representable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.obj.representable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda_sections", "description": "The isomorphism between `yoneda.obj X \u27f6 F` and `F.obj (op X)`\n(we need to insert a `ulift` to get the universes right!)\ngiven by the Yoneda lemma.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda.yoneda_full", "description": "The Yoneda embedding is full.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001P>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda_evaluation", "description": "The \"Yoneda evaluation\" functor, which sends `X : C\u1d52\u1d56` and `F : C\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 Type`\nto `F.obj X`, functorially in both `X` and `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.corepr_f.category_theory.is_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda_lemma", "description": "The Yoneda lemma asserts that that the Yoneda pairing\n`(X : C\u1d52\u1d56, F : C\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 Type) \u21a6 (yoneda.obj (unop X) \u27f6 F)`\nis naturally isomorphic to the evaluation `(X, F) \u21a6 F.obj X`.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001P>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.corepr_X", "description": "The representing object for the corepresentable functor `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coyoneda.punit_iso", "description": "The identity functor on `Type` is isomorphic to the coyoneda functor coming from `punit`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.curried_yoneda_lemma", "description": "The curried version of yoneda lemma when `C` is small.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.corepresentable", "description": "A functor `F : C \u2964 Type v\u2081` is corepresentable if there is object `X` so `F \u2245 coyoneda.obj X`.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001Q>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.corepresentable.has_corepresentation", "description": "A functor `F : C \u2964 Type v\u2081` is corepresentable if there is object `X` so `F \u2245 coyoneda.obj X`.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001Q>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda.is_iso", "description": "If `yoneda.map f` is an isomorphism, so was `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coyoneda.coyoneda_full", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.yoneda_obj_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.corepr_x", "description": "The representing element for the corepresentable functor `F`, sometimes called the universal\nelement of the functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.exists_forall_ge_of_has_compact_mul_support", "description": "A continuous function with compact support has a global maximum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.exists_Inf_image_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.exists_forall_le_of_has_compact_support", "description": "A continuous function with compact support has a global minimum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.bdd_below_range_of_has_compact_mul_support", "description": "A continuous function with compact support is bounded below.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.compact_Icc_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.bdd_below", "description": "A compact set is bounded below", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.is_greatest_Sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.lt_Inf_iff_of_continuous", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.is_glb_Inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact_uIcc", "description": "An unordered closed interval is compact.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.exists_Sup_image_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.is_lub_Sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.exists_forall_ge", "description": " The **extreme value theorem**: if a continuous function `f` tends to negative infinity away from\ncompact sets, then it has a global maximum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.compact_Icc_space'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on.exists_forall_ge'", "description": " The **extreme value theorem**: if a function `f` is continuous on a closed set `s` and it is\nsmaller than a value in its image away from compact sets, then it has a maximum on this set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.exists_is_least", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.exists_is_glb", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on.Inf_image_Icc_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_order.to_compact_Icc_space", "description": "A closed interval in a conditionally complete linear order is compact.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on.le_Sup_image_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compact_space_of_complete_linear_order", "description": " A complete linear order is a compact space.\n\nWe do not register an instance for a `[compact_Icc_space \u03b1]` because this would only add instances\nfor products (indexed or not) of complete linear orders, and we have instances with higher priority\nthat cover these cases.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.compact_Icc_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.exists_local_min_mem_open", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on.image_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.bdd_above_range_of_has_compact_support", "description": "A continuous function with compact support is bounded above.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.exists_Sup_image_eq_and_ge", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.bdd_above", "description": "A compact set is bounded above", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on.image_uIcc_eq_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.exists_forall_le", "description": " The **extreme value theorem**: if a continuous function `f` tends to infinity away from compact\nsets, then it has a global minimum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on.image_uIcc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.exists_forall_le_of_has_compact_mul_support", "description": "A continuous function with compact support has a global minimum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.bdd_above_image", "description": "A continuous function is bounded above on a compact set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.continuous_Inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compact_Icc_space", "description": " This typeclass says that all closed intervals in `\u03b1` are compact. This is true for all\nconditionally complete linear orders with order topology and products (finite or infinite)\nof such spaces.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compact_Icc_space.is_compact_Icc", "description": " This typeclass says that all closed intervals in `\u03b1` are compact. This is true for all\nconditionally complete linear orders with order topology and products (finite or infinite)\nof such spaces.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.exists_forall_ge'", "description": " The **extreme value theorem**: if a continuous function `f` is smaller than a value in its range\naway from compact sets, then it has a global maximum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.exists_local_min_on_mem_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_Icc_of_connected_compact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.exists_is_lub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.Inf_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.exists_Inf_image_eq_and_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.exists_forall_ge", "description": "The **extreme value theorem**: a continuous function realizes its maximum on a compact set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.is_least_Inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.exists_forall_le", "description": "The **extreme value theorem**: a continuous function realizes its minimum on a compact set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.exists_forall_le'", "description": " The **extreme value theorem**: if a continuous function `f` is larger than a value in its range\naway from compact sets, then it has a global minimum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.bdd_below_image", "description": "A continuous function is bounded below on a compact set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.exists_is_greatest", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.Sup_lt_iff_of_continuous", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.Sup_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on.exists_forall_le'", "description": " The **extreme value theorem**: if a function `f` is continuous on a closed set `s` and it is\nlarger than a value in its image away from compact sets, then it has a minimum on this set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compact_space_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.exists_forall_ge_of_has_compact_support", "description": "A continuous function with compact support has a global maximum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.continuous_Sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"n