d to come with an \u201cinverse\u201d function, and the inverse of `0` must be `0`.\n\nExamples include division rings and the ordered monoids that are the\ntarget of valuations in general valuation theory.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_with_zero.zpow_zero'", "description": " A type `G\u2080` is a \u201cgroup with zero\u201d if it is a monoid with zero element (distinct from `1`)\nsuch that every nonzero element is invertible.\nThe type is required to come with an \u201cinverse\u201d function, and the inverse of `0` must be `0`.\n\nExamples include division rings and the ordered monoids that are the\ntarget of valuations in general valuation theory.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_with_zero.zpow_succ'", "description": " A type `G\u2080` is a \u201cgroup with zero\u201d if it is a monoid with zero element (distinct from `1`)\nsuch that every nonzero element is invertible.\nThe type is required to come with an \u201cinverse\u201d function, and the inverse of `0` must be `0`.\n\nExamples include division rings and the ordered monoids that are the\ntarget of valuations in general valuation theory.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_with_zero.zpow_neg'", "description": " A type `G\u2080` is a \u201cgroup with zero\u201d if it is a monoid with zero element (distinct from `1`)\nsuch that every nonzero element is invertible.\nThe type is required to come with an \u201cinverse\u201d function, and the inverse of `0` must be `0`.\n\nExamples include division rings and the ordered monoids that are the\ntarget of valuations in general valuation theory.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_with_zero.exists_pair_ne", "description": " A type `G\u2080` is a \u201cgroup with zero\u201d if it is a monoid with zero element (distinct from `1`)\nsuch that every nonzero element is invertible.\nThe type is required to come with an \u201cinverse\u201d function, and the inverse of `0` must be `0`.\n\nExamples include division rings and the ordered monoids that are the\ntarget of valuations in general valuation theory.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_with_zero.inv_zero", "description": " A type `G\u2080` is a \u201cgroup with zero\u201d if it is a monoid with zero element (distinct from `1`)\nsuch that every nonzero element is invertible.\nThe type is required to come with an \u201cinverse\u201d function, and the inverse of `0` must be `0`.\n\nExamples include division rings and the ordered monoids that are the\ntarget of valuations in general valuation theory.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_with_zero.mul_inv_cancel", "description": " A type `G\u2080` is a \u201cgroup with zero\u201d if it is a monoid with zero element (distinct from `1`)\nsuch that every nonzero element is invertible.\nThe type is required to come with an \u201cinverse\u201d function, and the inverse of `0` must be `0`.\n\nExamples include division rings and the ordered monoids that are the\ntarget of valuations in general valuation theory.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_eq_zero_comm", "description": " If `\u03b1` has no zero divisors, then for elements `a, b : \u03b1`, `a * b` equals zero iff so is\n`b * a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monoid_with_zero", "description": " A type `M\u2080` is a \u201cmonoid with zero\u201d if it is a monoid with zero element, and `0` is left\nand right absorbing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monoid_with_zero.mul", "description": " A type `M\u2080` is a \u201cmonoid with zero\u201d if it is a monoid with zero element, and `0` is left\nand right absorbing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monoid_with_zero.mul_assoc", "description": " A type `M\u2080` is a \u201cmonoid with zero\u201d if it is a monoid with zero element, and `0` is left\nand right absorbing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monoid_with_zero.one", "description": " A type `M\u2080` is a \u201cmonoid with zero\u201d if it is a monoid with zero element, and `0` is left\nand right absorbing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monoid_with_zero.one_mul", "description": " A type `M\u2080` is a \u201cmonoid with zero\u201d if it is a monoid with zero element, and `0` is left\nand right absorbing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monoid_with_zero.mul_one", "description": " A type `M\u2080` is a \u201cmonoid with zero\u201d if it is a monoid with zero element, and `0` is left\nand right absorbing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monoid_with_zero.npow", "description": " A type `M\u2080` is a \u201cmonoid with zero\u201d if it is a monoid with zero element, and `0` is left\nand right absorbing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monoid_with_zero.npow_zero'", "description": " A type `M\u2080` is a \u201cmonoid with zero\u201d if it is a monoid with zero element, and `0` is left\nand right absorbing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monoid_with_zero.npow_succ'", "description": " A type `M\u2080` is a \u201cmonoid with zero\u201d if it is a monoid with zero element, and `0` is left\nand right absorbing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monoid_with_zero.zero", "description": " A type `M\u2080` is a \u201cmonoid with zero\u201d if it is a monoid with zero element, and `0` is left\nand right absorbing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monoid_with_zero.zero_mul", "description": " A type `M\u2080` is a \u201cmonoid with zero\u201d if it is a monoid with zero element, and `0` is left\nand right absorbing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monoid_with_zero.mul_zero", "description": " A type `M\u2080` is a \u201cmonoid with zero\u201d if it is a monoid with zero element, and `0` is left\nand right absorbing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "group_with_zero.to_div_inv_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cancel_comm_monoid_with_zero.to_cancel_monoid_with_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_eq_zero", "description": " If `\u03b1` has no zero divisors, then the product of two elements equals zero iff one of them\nequals zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conj.symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conj_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conj.conjugates_of_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.one_eq_mk_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.mk_bijective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conj_iff_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conjugates_of", "description": "Given an element `a`, `conjugates a` is the set of conjugates.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.mk_equiv", "description": "The bijection between a `comm_group` and its `conj_classes`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "library_note.slow-failing instance priority", "description": "Certain instances trigger further searches when they are considered as candidate instances;\nthese instances should be assigned a priority lower than the default of 1000 (for example, 900).\n\nThe conditions for this rule are as follows:\n * a class `C` has instances `instT : C T` and `instT' : C T'`\n * types `T` and `T'` are both specializations of another type `S`\n * the parameters supplied to `S` to produce `T` are not (fully) determined by `instT`,\n   instead they have to be found by instance search\nIf those conditions hold, the instance `instT` should be assigned lower priority.\n\nFor example, suppose the search for an instance of `decidable_eq (multiset \u03b1)` tries the\ncandidate instance `con.quotient.decidable_eq (c : con M) : decidable_eq c.quotient`.\nSince `multiset` and `con.quotient` are both quotient types, unification will check\nthat the relations `list.perm` and `c.to_setoid.r` unify. However, `c.to_setoid` depends on\na `has_mul M` instance, so this unification triggers a search for `has_mul (list \u03b1)`;\nthis will traverse all subclasses of `has_mul` before failing.\nOn the other hand, the search for an instance of `decidable_eq (con.quotient c)` for `c : con M`\ncan quickly reject the candidate instance `multiset.has_decidable_eq` because the type of\n`list.perm : list ?m_1 \u2192 list ?m_1 \u2192 Prop` does not unify with `M \u2192 M \u2192 Prop`.\nTherefore, we should assign `con.quotient.decidable_eq` a lower priority because it fails slowly.\n(In terms of the rules above, `C := decidable_eq`, `T := con.quotient`,\n`instT := con.quotient.decidable_eq`, `T' := multiset`, `instT' := multiset.has_decidable_eq`,\nand `S := quot`.)\n\nIf the type involved is a free variable (rather than an instantiation of some type `S`),\nthe instance priority should be even lower, see Note [lower instance priority].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conj.trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conj_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.quotient_mk_eq_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conj", "description": "We say that `a` is conjugate to `b` if for some unit `c` we have `c * a * c\u207b\u00b9 = b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.mem_carrier_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes", "description": "The quotient type of conjugacy classes of a group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conj.refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.mk_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.map_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.quot_mk_eq_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conj.setoid", "description": "The setoid of the relation `is_conj` iff there is a unit `u` such that `u * x = y * u`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.map", "description": "A `monoid_hom` maps conjugacy classes of one group to conjugacy classes of another.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.carrier_eq_preimage_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.carrier", "description": "Given a conjugacy class `a`, `carrier a` is the set it represents.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_zpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.mk_eq_mk_iff_is_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.mk_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conj_one_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.forall_is_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conj_one_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conj_iff_conjugates_of_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.exists_rep", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.mk", "description": "The canonical quotient map from a monoid `\u03b1` into the `conj_classes` of `\u03b1`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.has_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.mem_carrier_iff_mk_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_conjugates_of_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conj_iff\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monoid_hom.map_is_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.strict_mono_on_logb_of_base_lt_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.logb_lt_logb_iff_of_base_lt_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.logb_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.logb_le_iff_le_rpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.logb_le_logb_of_base_lt_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.tendsto_logb_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.logb_lt_logb", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.le_logb_iff_rpow_le_of_base_lt_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.logb_lt_iff_lt_rpow_of_base_