miRing`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommSemiRing.of", "description": "Construct a bundled CommSemiRing from the underlying type and typeclass.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiRing.of_hom", "description": "Typecheck a `ring_hom` as a morphism in `SemiRing`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.CommRing_iso_to_ring_equiv_to_ring_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.comm_ring.to_ring.category_theory.bundled_hom.parent_projection", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommSemiRing.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiRing.has_coe_to_sort", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ring.ring.to_semiring.category_theory.bundled_hom.parent_projection", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.concrete_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_equiv.to_CommRing_iso_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ring.ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ring.coe_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommSemiRing.has_forget_to_CommMon", "description": "The forgetful functor from commutative rings to (multiplicative) commutative monoids.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective_presentation", "description": "A projective presentation of an object `X` consists of an epimorphism `f : P \u27f6 X`\nfrom some projective object `P`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective_presentation.P", "description": "A projective presentation of an object `X` consists of an epimorphism `f : P \u27f6 X`\nfrom some projective object `P`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective_presentation.projective", "description": "A projective presentation of an object `X` consists of an epimorphism `f : P \u27f6 X`\nfrom some projective object `P`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective_presentation.f", "description": "A projective presentation of an object `X` consists of an epimorphism `f : P \u27f6 X`\nfrom some projective object `P`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective_presentation.epi", "description": "A projective presentation of an object `X` consists of an epimorphism `f : P \u27f6 X`\nfrom some projective object `P`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adjunction.map_projective_presentation", "description": " Given an adjunction `F \u22a3 G` such that `G` preserves epis, `F` maps a projective presentation of\n`X` to a projective presentation of `F(X)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.\u03c0_epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.syzygies.projective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.category_theory.limits.biprod.projective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adjunction.projective_of_map_projective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adjunction.map_projective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.\u03c0", "description": "The epimorphism `projective.\u03c0 : projective.over X \u27f6 X`\nfrom the arbitrarily chosen projective object over `X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.syzygies", "description": "When `C` has enough projectives, the object `projective.syzygies f` is\nan arbitrarily chosen projective object over `kernel f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.projective_over", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.projective_iff_preserves_epimorphisms_coyoneda_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.factor_thru", "description": "An arbitrarily chosen factorisation of a morphism out of a projective object through an epimorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.projective", "description": "The axiom of choice says that every type is a projective object in `Type`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.zero_projective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.equivalence.projective_presentation_of_map_projective_presentation", "description": " Given an equivalence of categories `F`, a projective presentation of `F(X)` induces a\nprojective presentation of `X.`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.factor_thru_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.over", "description": "`projective.over X` provides an arbitrarily chosen projective object equipped with\nan epimorphism `projective.\u03c0 : projective.over X \u27f6 X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact.lift", "description": "Given a projective object `P` mapping via `h` into\nthe middle object `R` of a pair of exact morphisms `f : Q \u27f6 R` and `g : R \u27f6 S`,\nsuch that `h \u226b g = 0`, there is a lift of `h` to `Q`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.of_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact.lift_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.category_theory.limits.sigma_obj.projective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.iso_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.category_theory.limits.biproduct.projective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.d", "description": "When `C` has enough projectives,\n`projective.d f : projective.syzygies f \u27f6 X` is the composition\n`\u03c0 (kernel f) \u226b kernel.\u03b9 f`.\n\n(When `C` is abelian, we have `exact (projective.d f) f`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective", "description": "An object `P` is called projective if every morphism out of `P` factors through every epimorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.factors", "description": "An object `P` is called projective if every morphism out of `P` factors through every epimorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.Type.enough_projectives", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.category_theory.limits.coprod.projective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.enough_projectives", "description": "A category \"has enough projectives\" if for every object `X` there is a projective object `P` and\n   an epimorphism `P \u21a0 X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.enough_projectives.presentation", "description": "A category \"has enough projectives\" if for every object `X` there is a projective object `P` and\n   an epimorphism `P \u21a0 X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.equivalence.enough_projectives_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.prod_X_sub_C_eq_sum_esymm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.prod_X_sub_C_coeff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_X_add_C_coeff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.prod_X_add_C_coeff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.prod_C_add_X_eq_sum_esymm", "description": " A sum version of Vieta's formula for `mv_polynomial`: viewing `X i` as variables,\nthe product of linear terms `\u03bb + X i` is equal to a linear combination of\nthe symmetric polynomials `esymm \u03c3 R j`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.esymm_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.prod_X_add_C_coeff", "description": " Vieta's formula for the coefficients of the product of linear terms `X + \u03bb` where `\u03bb` runs\nthrough a multiset `s` : the `k`th coefficient is the symmetric function `esymm (card s - k) s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.prod_X_add_C_eq_sum_esymm", "description": " A sum version of Vieta's formula for `multiset`: the product of the linear terms `X + \u03bb` where\n`\u03bb` runs through a multiset `s` is equal to a linear combination of the symmetric functions\n`esymm s` of the `\u03bb`'s .", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_eq_esymm_roots_of_card", "description": "Vieta's formula for the coefficients and the roots of a polynomial over an integral domain\n with as many roots as its degree.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_eq_esymm_roots_of_splits", "description": "Vieta's formula for split polynomials over a field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.prod_X_add_C_coeff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nhds_left'_le_nhds_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nhds_left_sup_nhds_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_at_iff_continuous_left_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_at_iff_continuous_left'_right'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nhds_right'_le_nhds_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_within_at_Iio_iff_Iic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nhds_left_sup_nhds_right'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_within_at_Ioi_iff_Ici", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nhds_left'_sup_nhds_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nhds_left'_sup_nhds_right'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "theorems_100.heron", "description": "**Heron's formula**: The area of a triangle with side lengths `a`, `b`, and `c` is\n `\u221a(s * (s - a) * (s - b) * (s - c))` where `s = (a + b + c) / 2` is the semiperimeter.\n We show this by equating this formula to `a * b * sin \u03b3`, where `\u03b3` is the angle opposite\n the side `c`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "multiset.to_dfinsupp_le_to_dfinsupp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.to_dfinsupp", "description": "A computable version of `multiset.to_finsupp`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.add_zero_class'", "description": "Non-dependent special case of `dfinsupp.add_zero_class` to help typeclass search.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.equiv_dfinsupp", "description": "`multiset.to_dfinsupp` as an `add_equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.to_dfinsupp_replicate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.to_multiset_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.to_multiset", "description": "A computable version of `finsupp.to_multiset`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.to_dfinsupp_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.to_dfinsupp_lt_to_dfinsupp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.to_dfinsupp_inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.to_dfinsupp_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.to_multiset_lt_to_multiset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.to_multiset_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.to_dfinsupp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.to_dfinsupp_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.to_multiset_le_to_multiset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.to_dfinsupp_to_multiset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.to_multiset_to_dfinsupp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.to_dfinsupp_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.to_multiset_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.to_dfinsupp_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.to_multiset_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.equiv_dfinsupp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.equiv_dfinsupp_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.to_multiset_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.simple.iff_of_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.biprod.is_iso_inl_iff_is_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.simple_iff_subobject_is_simple_order", "description": "`X` is simple iff it has subobject lattice `{\u22a5, \u22a4}`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.simple.of_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.End.nontrivial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject_simple_iff_is_atom", "description": "A subobject is simple iff it is an atom in the subobject lattice.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.simple.not_is_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso_of_epi_of_nonzero", "description": "A nonzero epimorphism from a simple object is an isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.epi_from_simple_zero_of_not_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.is_simple_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.simple_of_cosimple", "description": "In an abelian category, an object satisfying the dual of the definition of a simple object is\n   simple.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.kernel_zero_of_nonzero_from_simple", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.mono_to_simple_zero_of_not_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.simple_of_is_simple_order_subobject", "description": "If `X` has subobject lattice `{\u22a5, \u22a4}`, then `X` is simple.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso_of_mono_of_nonzero", "description": "A nonzero monomorphism to a simple object is an isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.simple", "description": "An object is simple if monomorphisms into it are (exclusively) either isomorphisms or zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.simple.mono_is_iso_iff_nonzero", "description": "An object is simple if monomorphisms into it are (exclusively) either isomorphisms or zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cokernel_zero_of_nonzero_to_simple", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.zero_not_simple", "description": "We don't want the definition of 'simple' to include the zero object, so we check that here.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.epi_of_nonzero_to_simple", "description": "A nonzero morphism `f` to a simple object is an epimorphism\n(assuming `f` has an image, and `C` has equalizers).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.nontrivial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.id_nonzero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.indecomposable_of_simple", "description": "Any simple object in a preadditive category is indecomposable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.length_sort", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.has_repr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.sort_sorted", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.sort_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.sort_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.sort", "description": "`sort s` constructs a sorted list from the multiset `s`.\n (Uses merge sort algorithm.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.sort_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.mem_sort", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.coe_sort", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "balanced_core_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "balanced_core_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_balanced_core_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "balanced_core_aux_balanced", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "balanced_core_aux_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "balanced_core_subset_balanced_core_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "balanced_core_aux_maximal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "balanced.subset_core_of_subset", "description": " The balanced core of `t` is maximal in the sense that it contains any balanced subset\n`s` of `t`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "balanced_core_nonempty_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nhds_basis_closed_balanced", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subset_balanced_hull", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "balanced.hull_subset_of_subset", "description": " The balanced hull of `s` is minimal in the sense that it is contained in any balanced superset\n`t` of `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "balanced_hull", "description": "The smallest balanced superset of `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "balanced_core_zero_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_balanced_core_aux_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "balanced_core_aux_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "balanced_core_eq_Inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "balanced_core_mem_nhds_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nhds_basis_balanced", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_balanced_hull_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subset_balanced_core", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "balanced_core_balanced", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_closed.balanced_core", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_balanced_core_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "balanced_hull.balanced", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "balanced_core", "description": "The largest balanced subset of `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "balanced_core_aux", "description": "Helper definition to prove `balanced_core_eq_Inter`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.disjoint_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.product_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.off_diag_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.product_sdiff_diag", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.subset_product_image_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.filter_product_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.coe_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.product_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.product_eq_bUnion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.nonempty_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.filter_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.singleton_product_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.nonempty.product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.union_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.product_image_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.diag_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.off_diag_inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.diag_insert", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.product_subset_product_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_off_diag", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.diag_union_off_diag", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.off_diag_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.off_diag_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.product", "description": "`product s t` is the set of pairs `(a, b)` such that `a \u2208 s` and `b \u2208 t`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.product_subset_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.diag_inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.diag", "description": " Given a finite set `s`, the diagonal, `s.diag` is the set of pairs of the form `(a, a)` for\n`a \u2208 s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.product_bUnion", "description": "See also `finset.sup_product_left`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.product_image_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.nonempty.snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.product_subset_product_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.product_disj_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.subset_product_image_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.diag_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.product_inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_diag", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.disj_union_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.subset_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.disjoint_diag_off_diag", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.card_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.product_eq_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.coe_off_diag", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.diag_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.singleton_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.empty_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.inter_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.filter_product_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.diag_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.product_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mk_mem_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.product_inter_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.off_diag_insert", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.product_sdiff_off_diag", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.off_diag_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.off_diag", "description": " Given a finite set `s`, the off-diagonal, `s.off_diag` is the set of pairs `(a, b)` with `a \u2260 b`\nfor `a, b \u2208 s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.nonempty.fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.product_eq_bUnion_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.diag_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.filter_product_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.map_swap_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.product_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.off_diag_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.image_swap_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.mem_prod_same_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.map_lift_eq2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_principal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.prod_lift_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_infi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.prod_same_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_lift'_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift'_ne_bot_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.mem_lift'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.sInter_lift'_sets", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift'_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift'_infi_of_map_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.monotone_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.mem_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift'_inf_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift'_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_lift'_same_le_lift'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift'_infi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift'_cong", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.monotone_lift'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.sInter_lift_sets", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.prod_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto_lift'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.comap_lift_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.le_lift'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_principal2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift'_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_infi_of_directed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.prod_lift'_lift'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_lift'_same_eq_lift'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift'", "description": " Specialize `lift` to functions `set \u03b1 \u2192 set \u03b2`. This can be viewed as a generalization of `map`.\nThis is essentially a push-forward along a function mapping each set to a set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.mem_lift_sets", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_mono'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_lift'_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.mem_lift'_sets", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.comap_lift'_eq2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift'_lift'_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.principal_le_lift'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.map_lift_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.has_basis.lift'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.has_basis.lift", "description": " If `(p : \u03b9 \u2192 Prop, s : \u03b9 \u2192 set \u03b1)` is a basis of a filter `f`, `g` is a monotone function\n`set \u03b1 \u2192 filter \u03b3`, and for each `i`, `(pg : \u03b2 i \u2192 Prop, sg : \u03b2 i \u2192 set \u03b1)` is a basis\nof the filter `g (s i)`, then `(\u03bb (i : \u03b9) (x : \u03b2 i), p i \u2227 pg i x, \u03bb (i : \u03b9) (x : \u03b2 i), sg i x)`\nis a basis of the filter `f.lift g`.\n\nThis basis is parametrized by `i : \u03b9` and `x : \u03b2 i`, so in order to formulate this fact using\n`has_basis` one has to use `\u03a3 i, \u03b2 i` as the index type. See also `filter.has_basis.mem_lift_iff`\nfor the corresponding `mem_iff` statement formulated without using a sigma type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift'_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift'_pure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_infi_of_map_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.comap_lift_eq2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift'_principal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.map_lift'_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_lift_same_eq_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.comap_lift'_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.map_lift'_eq2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_infi_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.le_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift", "description": " A variant on `bind` using a function `g` taking a set instead of a member of `\u03b1`.\nThis is essentially a push-forward along a function mapping each set to a filter.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift'_inf_principal_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_lift_same_le_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift'_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift'_map_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_map_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.comap_eq_lift'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto_prod_self_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift'_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift'_lift_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_ne_bot_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.lift'_mono'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.has_basis.mem_lift_iff", "description": " If `(p : \u03b9 \u2192 Prop, s : \u03b9 \u2192 set \u03b1)` is a basis of a filter `f`, `g` is a monotone function\n`set \u03b1 \u2192 filter \u03b3`, and for each `i`, `(pg : \u03b2 i \u2192 Prop, sg : \u03b2 i \u2192 set \u03b1)` is a basis\nof the filter `g (s i)`, then `(\u03bb (i : \u03b9) (x : \u03b2 i), p i \u2227 pg i x, \u03bb (i : \u03b9) (x : \u03b2 i), sg i x)`\nis a basis of the filter `f.lift g`.\n\nThis basis is parametrized by `i : \u03b9` and `x : \u03b2 i`, so in order to formulate this fact using\n`has_basis` one has to use `\u03a3 i, \u03b2 i` as the index type, see `filter.has_basis.lift`.\nThis lemma states the corresponding `mem_iff` statement without using a sigma type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.revert_target_deps", "description": "Reverts all local constants on which the target depends (recursively).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.format_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.guard_proof_term", "description": "`guard_proof_term { t } e` applies tactic `t` and tests whether the resulting proof term\n unifies with `p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.recover", "description": " For debugging only. This tactic checks the current state for any\nmissing dropped goals and restores them. Useful when there are no\ngoals to solve but \"result contains meta-variables\".", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.generalize'", "description": "`generalize' : e = x` replaces all occurrences of `e` in the target with a new hypothesis `x` of\nthe same type.\n\n`generalize' h : e = x` in addition registers the hypothesis `h : e = x`.\n\n`generalize'` is similar to `generalize`. The difference is that `generalize' : e = x` also\nsucceeds when `e` does not occur in the goal. It is similar to `set`, but the resulting hypothesis\n`x` is not a local definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.loc.get_local_pp_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.generalize_hyp", "description": "Like `generalize` but also considers assumptions\nspecified by the user. The user can also specify to\nomit the goal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.guard_tags", "description": "Test that `t` is the tag of the main goal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.guard_target_strict", "description": "`guard_target_strict t` fails if the target of the main goal is not syntactically `t`.\nWe use this tactic for writing tests.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.trivial'", "description": "A weaker version of `trivial` that tries to solve the goal using a canonical proof of `true` or the\n`reflexivity` tactic (unfolding only `reducible` constants, so can fail faster than `trivial`),\nand otherwise tries the `contradiction` tactic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.guard_target'", "description": "`guard_target' t` fails if the target of the main goal is not definitionally equal to `t`.\nWe use this tactic for writing tests.\nThe difference with `guard_target` is that this uses definitional equality instead of\nalpha-equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.unfold_coes", "description": "Unfold coercion-related definitions", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.fconstructor", "description": "Similar to `constructor`, but does not reorder goals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.classical", "description": " Make every proposition in the context decidable.\n\n`classical!` does this more aggressively, such that even if a decidable instance is already\navailable for a specific proposition, the noncomputable one will be used instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.rotate", "description": "`rotate` moves the first goal to the back. `rotate n` will do this `n` times.\n\nSee also `tactic.interactive.swap`, which moves the `n`th goal to the front.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.triv", "description": "Tries to solve the goal using a canonical proof of `true` or the `reflexivity` tactic.\nUnlike `trivial` or `trivial'`, does not the `contradiction` tactic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.have_field", "description": "`have_field`, used after `refine_struct _` poses `field` as a local constant\nwith the type of the field of the current goal:\n\n```lean\nrefine_struct ({ .. } : semigroup \u03b1),\n{ have_field, ... },\n{ have_field, ... },\n```\nbehaves like\n```lean\nrefine_struct ({ .. } : semigroup \u03b1),\n{ have field := @semigroup.mul, ... },\n{ have field := @semigroup.mul_assoc, ... },\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.h_generalize", "description": "`h_generalize Hx : e == x` matches on `cast _ e` in the goal and replaces it with\n`x`. It also adds `Hx : e == x` as an assumption. If `cast _ e` appears multiple\ntimes (not necessarily with the same proof), they are all replaced by `x`. `cast`\n`eq.mp`, `eq.mpr`, `eq.subst`, `eq.substr`, `eq.rec` and `eq.rec_on` are all treated\nas casts.\n\n- `h_generalize Hx : e == x with h` adds hypothesis `\u03b1 = \u03b2` with `e : \u03b1, x : \u03b2`;\n- `h_generalize Hx : e == x with _` chooses automatically chooses the name of\n  assumption `\u03b1 = \u03b2`;\n- `h_generalize! Hx : e == x` reverts `Hx`;\n- when `Hx` is omitted, assumption `Hx : e == x` is not added.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.clear_aux_decl", "description": "`clear_aux_decl` clears every `aux_decl` in the local context for the current goal.\nThis includes the induction hypothesis when using the equation compiler and\n`_let_match` and `_fun_match`.\n\nIt is useful when using a tactic such as `finish`, `simp *` or `subst` that may use these\nauxiliary declarations, and produce an error saying the recursion is not well founded.\n\n```lean\nexample (n m : \u2115) (h\u2081 : n = m) (h\u2082 : \u2203 a : \u2115, a = n \u2227 a = m) : 2 * m = 2 * n :=\nlet \u27e8a, ha\u27e9 := h\u2082 in\nbegin\n  clear_aux_decl, -- subst will fail without this line\n  subst h\u2081\nend\n\nexample (x y : \u2115) (h\u2081 : \u2203 n : \u2115, n * 1 = 2) (h\u2082 : 1 + 1 = 2 \u2192 x * 1 = y) : x = y :=\nlet \u27e8n, hn\u27e9 := h\u2081 in\nbegin\n  clear_aux_decl, -- finish produces an error without this line\n  finish\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.generalize_hyp", "description": "Like `generalize` but also considers assumptions\nspecified by the user. The user can also specify to\nomit the goal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.continue", "description": " Like `try { tac }`, but in the case of failure it continues\nfrom the failure state instead of reverting to the original state.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.substs", "description": "Multiple `subst`. `substs x y z` is the same as `subst x, subst y, subst z`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.apply_field", "description": "`apply_field` functions as `have_field, apply field, clear field`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.generalize'", "description": "`generalize' : e = x` replaces all occurrences of `e` in the target with a new hypothesis `x` of\nthe same type.\n\n`generalize' h : e = x` in addition registers the hypothesis `h : e = x`.\n\n`generalize'` is similar to `generalize`. The difference is that `generalize' : e = x` also\nsucceeds when `e` does not occur in the goal. It is similar to `set`, but the resulting hypothesis\n`x` is not a local definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.subst'", "description": "If the expression `q` is a local variable with type `x = t` or `t = x`, where `x` is a local\nconstant, `tactic.interactive.subst' q` substitutes `x` by `t` everywhere in the main goal and\nthen clears `q`.\nIf `q` is another local variable, then we find a local constant with type `q = t` or `t = q` and\nsubstitute `t` for `q`.\n\nLike `tactic.interactive.subst`, but fails with a nicer error message if the substituted variable is\na local definition. It is trickier to fix this in core, since `tactic.is_local_def` is in mathlib.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.set", "description": "`set a := t with h` is a variant of `let a := t`. It adds the hypothesis `h : a = t` to\nthe local context and replaces `t` with `a` everywhere it can.\n\n`set a := t with \u2190h` will add `h : t = a` instead.\n\n`set! a := t with h` does not do any replacing.\n\n```lean\nexample (x : \u2115) (h : x = 3)  : x + x + x = 9 :=\nbegin\n  set y := x with \u2190h_xy,\n/-\nx : \u2115,\ny : \u2115 := x,\nh_xy : x = y,\nh : y = 3\n\u22a2 y + y + y = 9\n-/\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.guard_target_mod_implicit", "description": "`guard_target_mod_implicit t` fails if the target of the main goal\nis not definitionally equal to `t` modulo none transparency\n(i.e., unifying the implicit arguments modulo semireducible transparency).\nWe use this tactic for writing tests.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.change'", "description": "The logic of `change x with y at l` fails when there are dependencies.\n`change'` mimics the behavior of `change`, except in the case of `change x with y at l`.\nIn this case, it will correctly replace occurences of `x` with `y` at all possible hypotheses\nin `l`. As long as `x` and `y` are defeq, it should never fail.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.id", "description": " `id { tac }` is the same as `tac`, but it is useful for creating a block scope without\nrequiring the goal to be solved at the end like `{ tac }`. It can also be used to enclose a\nnon-interactive tactic for patterns like `tac1; id {tac2}` where `tac2` is non-interactive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.inhabit", "description": "`inhabit \u03b1` tries to derive a `nonempty \u03b1` instance and then upgrades this\nto an `inhabited \u03b1` instance.\nIf the target is a `Prop`, this is done constructively;\notherwise, it uses `classical.choice`.\n\n```lean\nexample (\u03b1) [nonempty \u03b1] : \u2203 a : \u03b1, true :=\nbegin\n  inhabit \u03b1,\n  existsi default,\n  trivial\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.generalize_a_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.refine_struct", "description": "`refine_struct { .. }` acts like `refine` but works only with structure instance\nliterals. It creates a goal for each missing field and tags it with the name of the\nfield so that `have_field` can be used to generically refer to the field currently\nbeing refined.\n\nAs an example, we can use `refine_struct` to automate the construction of semigroup\ninstances:\n\n```lean\nrefine_struct ( { .. } : semigroup \u03b1 ),\n-- case semigroup, mul\n-- \u03b1 : Type u,\n-- \u22a2 \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1\n\n-- case semigroup, mul_assoc\n-- \u03b1 : Type u,\n-- \u22a2 \u2200 (a b c : \u03b1), a * b * c = a * (b * c)\n```\n\n`have_field`, used after `refine_struct _`, poses `field` as a local constant\nwith the type of the field of the current goal:\n\n```lean\nrefine_struct ({ .. } : semigroup \u03b1),\n{ have_field, ... },\n{ have_field, ... },\n```\nbehaves like\n```lean\nrefine_struct ({ .. } : semigroup \u03b1),\n{ have field := @semigroup.mul, ... },\n{ have field := @semigroup.mul_assoc, ... },\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.guard_hyp_strict", "description": "`guard_hyp_strict h : t` fails if the hypothesis `h` does not have type syntactically equal\nto `t`.\nWe use this tactic for writing tests.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.unfold_aux", "description": "Unfold auxiliary definitions associated with the current declaration.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.revert_target_deps", "description": "Reverts all local constants on which the target depends (recursively).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.guard_hyp_nums", "description": "Tests that there are `n` hypotheses in the current context.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.try_for", "description": " `try_for n { tac }` executes `tac` for `n` ticks, otherwise uses `sorry` to close the goal.\nNever fails. Useful for debugging.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.clear_", "description": "Clear all hypotheses starting with `_`, like `_match` and `_let_match`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.work_on_goal", "description": "`work_on_goal n { tac }` creates a block scope for the `n`-goal,\nand does not require that the goal be solved at the end\n(any remaining subgoals are inserted back into the list of goals).\n\nTypically usage might look like:\n````\nintros,\nsimp,\napply lemma_1,\nwork_on_goal 3\n{ dsimp,\n  simp },\nrefl\n````\n\nSee also `id { tac }`, which is equivalent to `work_on_goal 1 { tac }`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.replace", "description": "Acts like `have`, but removes a hypothesis with the same name as\nthis one. For example if the state is `h : p \u22a2 goal` and `f : p \u2192 q`,\nthen after `replace h := f h` the goal will be `h : q \u22a2 goal`,\nwhere `have h := f h` would result in the state `h : p, h : q \u22a2 goal`.\nThis can be used to simulate the `specialize` and `apply at` tactics\nof Coq.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.subst'", "description": "If the expression `q` is a local variable with type `x = t` or `t = x`, where `x` is a local\nconstant, `tactic.interactive.subst' q` substitutes `x` by `t` everywhere in the main goal and\nthen clears `q`.\nIf `q` is another local variable, then we find a local constant with type `q = t` or `t = q` and\nsubstitute `t` for `q`.\n\nLike `tactic.interactive.subst`, but fails with a nicer error message if the substituted variable is\na local definition. It is trickier to fix this in core, since `tactic.is_local_def` is in mathlib.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.substs", "description": "Multiple `subst`. `substs x y z` is the same as `subst x, subst y, subst z`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.clear_except", "description": "`clear_except h\u2080 h\u2081` deletes all the assumptions it can except for `h\u2080` and `h\u2081`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.collect_struct'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.rotate", "description": "`rotate` moves the first goal to the back. `rotate n` will do this `n` times.\n\nSee also `tactic.interactive.swap`, which moves the `n`th goal to the front.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.compact_decl_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.loc.get_local_uniq_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.inhabit", "description": "`inhabit \u03b1` tries to derive a `nonempty \u03b1` instance and then upgrades this\nto an `inhabited \u03b1` instance.\nIf the target is a `Prop`, this is done constructively;\notherwise, it uses `classical.choice`.\n\n```lean\nexample (\u03b1) [nonempty \u03b1] : \u2203 a : \u03b1, true :=\nbegin\n  inhabit \u03b1,\n  existsi default,\n  trivial\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.refine_struct", "description": "`refine_struct { .. }` acts like `refine` but works only with structure instance\nliterals. It creates a goal for each missing field and tags it with the name of the\nfield so that `have_field` can be used to generically refer to the field currently\nbeing refined.\n\nAs an example, we can use `refine_struct` to automate the construction of semigroup\ninstances:\n\n```lean\nrefine_struct ( { .. } : semigroup \u03b1 ),\n-- case semigroup, mul\n-- \u03b1 : Type u,\n-- \u22a2 \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1\n\n-- case semigroup, mul_assoc\n-- \u03b1 : Type u,\n-- \u22a2 \u2200 (a b c : \u03b1), a * b * c = a * (b * c)\n```\n\n`have_field`, used after `refine_struct _`, poses `field` as a local constant\nwith the type of the field of the current goal:\n\n```lean\nrefine_struct ({ .. } : semigroup \u03b1),\n{ have_field, ... },\n{ have_field, ... },\n```\nbehaves like\n```lean\nrefine_struct ({ .. } : semigroup \u03b1),\n{ have field := @semigroup.mul, ... },\n{ have field := @semigroup.mul_assoc, ... },\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.use", "description": "Similar to `existsi`. `use x` will instantiate the first term of an `\u2203` or `\u03a3` goal with `x`. It\nwill then try to close the new goal using `trivial'`, or try to simplify it by applying\n`exists_prop`. Unlike `existsi`, `x` is elaborated with respect to the expected type.\n`use` will alternatively take a list of terms `[x0, ..., xn]`.\n\n`use` will work with constructors of arbitrary inductive types.\n\nExamples:\n```lean\nexample (\u03b1 : Type) : \u2203 S : set \u03b1, S = S :=\nby use \u2205\n\nexample : \u2203 x : \u2124, x = x :=\nby use 42\n\nexample : \u2203 n > 0, n = n :=\nbegin\n  use 1,\n  -- goal is now 1 > 0 \u2227 1 = 1, whereas it would be \u2203 (H : 1 > 0), 1 = 1 after existsi 1.\n  exact \u27e8zero_lt_one, rfl\u27e9,\nend\n\nexample : \u2203 a b c : \u2124, a + b + c = 6 :=\nby use [1, 2, 3]\n\nexample : \u2203 p : \u2124 \u00d7 \u2124, p.1 = 1 :=\nby use \u27e81, 42\u27e9\n\nexample : \u03a3 x y : \u2124, (\u2124 \u00d7 \u2124) \u00d7 \u2124 :=\nby use [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n\ninductive foo\n| mk : \u2115 \u2192 bool \u00d7 \u2115 \u2192 \u2115 \u2192 foo\n\nexample : foo :=\nby use [100, tt, 4, 3]\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.triv", "description": "Tries to solve the goal using a canonical proof of `true` or the `reflexivity` tactic.\nUnlike `trivial` or `trivial'`, does not the `contradiction` tactic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.success_if_fail_with_msg", "description": " `success_if_fail_with_msg { tac } msg` succeeds if the interactive tactic `tac` fails with\nerror message `msg` (for test writing purposes).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.use", "description": "Similar to `existsi`. `use x` will instantiate the first term of an `\u2203` or `\u03a3` goal with `x`. It\nwill then try to close the new goal using `trivial'`, or try to simplify it by applying\n`exists_prop`. Unlike `existsi`, `x` is elaborated with respect to the expected type.\n`use` will alternatively take a list of terms `[x0, ..., xn]`.\n\n`use` will work with constructors of arbitrary inductive types.\n\nExamples:\n```lean\nexample (\u03b1 : Type) : \u2203 S : set \u03b1, S = S :=\nby use \u2205\n\nexample : \u2203 x : \u2124, x = x :=\nby use 42\n\nexample : \u2203 n > 0, n = n :=\nbegin\n  use 1,\n  -- goal is now 1 > 0 \u2227 1 = 1, whereas it would be \u2203 (H : 1 > 0), 1 = 1 after existsi 1.\n  exact \u27e8zero_lt_one, rfl\u27e9,\nend\n\nexample : \u2203 a b c : \u2124, a + b + c = 6 :=\nby use [1, 2, 3]\n\nexample : \u2203 p : \u2124 \u00d7 \u2124, p.1 = 1 :=\nby use \u27e81, 42\u27e9\n\nexample : \u03a3 x y : \u2124, (\u2124 \u00d7 \u2124) \u00d7 \u2124 :=\nby use [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n\ninductive foo\n| mk : \u2115 \u2192 bool \u00d7 \u2115 \u2192 \u2115 \u2192 foo\n\nexample : foo :=\nby use [100, tt, 4, 3]\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.trivial'", "description": "A weaker version of `trivial` that tries to solve the goal using a canonical proof of `true` or the\n`reflexivity` tactic (unfolding only `reducible` constants, so can fail faster than `trivial`),\nand otherwise tries the `contradiction` tactic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.guard_target'", "description": "`guard_target' t` fails if the target of the main goal is not definitionally equal to `t`.\nWe use this tactic for writing tests.\nThe difference with `guard_target` is that this uses definitional equality instead of\nalpha-equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.replace", "description": "Acts like `have`, but removes a hypothesis with the same name as\nthis one. For example if the state is `h : p \u22a2 goal` and `f : p \u2192 q`,\nthen after `replace h := f h` the goal will be `h : q \u22a2 goal`,\nwhere `have h := f h` would result in the state `h : p, h : q \u22a2 goal`.\nThis can be used to simulate the `specialize` and `apply at` tactics\nof Coq.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.clear_", "description": "Clear all hypotheses starting with `_`, like `_match` and `_let_match`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.return_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.clear_aux_decl", "description": "`clear_aux_decl` clears every `aux_decl` in the local context for the current goal.\nThis includes the induction hypothesis when using the equation compiler and\n`_let_match` and `_fun_match`.\n\nIt is useful when using a tactic such as `finish`, `simp *` or `subst` that may use these\nauxiliary declarations, and produce an error saying the recursion is not well founded.\n\n```lean\nexample (n m : \u2115) (h\u2081 : n = m) (h\u2082 : \u2203 a : \u2115, a = n \u2227 a = m) : 2 * m = 2 * n :=\nlet \u27e8a, ha\u27e9 := h\u2082 in\nbegin\n  clear_aux_decl, -- subst will fail without this line\n  subst h\u2081\nend\n\nexample (x y : \u2115) (h\u2081 : \u2203 n : \u2115, n * 1 = 2) (h\u2082 : 1 + 1 = 2 \u2192 x * 1 = y) : x = y :=\nlet \u27e8n, hn\u27e9 := h\u2081 in\nbegin\n  clear_aux_decl, -- finish produces an error without this line\n  finish\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.guard_expr_strict", "description": "`guard_expr_strict t := e` fails if the expr `t` is not equal to `e`. By contrast\nto `guard_expr`, this tests strict (syntactic) equality.\nWe use this tactic for writing tests.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.refine_recursively", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.list_cast_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.revert_deps", "description": " `revert_deps n\u2081 n\u2082 ...` reverts all the hypotheses that depend on one of `n\u2081, n\u2082, ...`\nIt does not revert `n\u2081, n\u2082, ...` themselves (unless they depend on another `n\u1d62`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.refine_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.swap", "description": "`swap n` will move the `n`th goal to the front.\n`swap` defaults to `swap 2`, and so interchanges the first and second goals.\n\nSee also `tactic.interactive.rotate`, which moves the first `n` goals to the back.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.revert_deps", "description": " `revert_deps n\u2081 n\u2082 ...` reverts all the hypotheses that depend on one of `n\u2081, n\u2082, ...`\nIt does not revert `n\u2081, n\u2082, ...` themselves (unless they depend on another `n\u1d62`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.clean", "description": "Remove identity functions from a term. These are normally\nautomatically generated with terms like `show t, from p` or\n`(p : t)` which translate to some variant on `@id t p` in\norder to retain the type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.swap", "description": "`swap n` will move the `n`th goal to the front.\n`swap` defaults to `swap 2`, and so interchanges the first and second goals.\n\nSee also `tactic.interactive.rotate`, which moves the first `n` goals to the back.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.guard_expr_eq'", "description": "Tests whether `t` is definitionally equal to `p`. The difference with `guard_expr_eq` is that\n this uses definitional equality instead of alpha-equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.set", "description": "`set a := t with h` is a variant of `let a := t`. It adds the hypothesis `h : a = t` to\nthe local context and replaces `t` with `a` everywhere it can.\n\n`set a := t with \u2190h` will add `h : t = a` instead.\n\n`set! a := t with h` does not do any replacing.\n\n```lean\nexample (x : \u2115) (h : x = 3)  : x + x + x = 9 :=\nbegin\n  set y := x with \u2190h_xy,\n/-\nx : \u2115,\ny : \u2115 := x,\nh_xy : x = y,\nh : y = 3\n\u22a2 y + y + y = 9\n-/\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.apply_rules", "description": "`apply_rules hs with attrs n` applies the list of lemmas `hs` and all lemmas tagged with an\nattribute from the list `attrs`, as well as the `assumption` tactic on the\nfirst goal and the resulting subgoals, iteratively, at most `n` times.\n`n` is optional, equal to 50 by default.\nYou can pass an `apply_cfg` option argument as `apply_rules hs n opt`.\n(A typical usage would be with `apply_rules hs n { md := reducible }`,\nwhich asks `apply_rules` to not unfold `semireducible` definitions (i.e. most)\nwhen checking if a lemma matches the goal.)\n\nFor instance:\n\n```lean\n@[user_attribute]\nmeta def mono_rules : user_attribute :=\n{ name := `mono_rules,\n  descr := \"lemmas usable to prove monotonicity\" }\n\nattribute [mono_rules] add_le_add mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_right\n\nlemma my_test {a b c d e : real} (h1 : a \u2264 b) (h2 : c \u2264 d) (h3 : 0 \u2264 e) :\na + c * e + a + c + 0 \u2264 b + d * e + b + d + e :=\n-- any of the following lines solve the goal:\nadd_le_add (add_le_add (add_le_add (add_le_add h1 (mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_right h2 h3)) h1 ) h2) h3\nby apply_rules [add_le_add, mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_right]\nby apply_rules with mono_rules\nby apply_rules [add_le_add] with mono_rules\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.match_hyp", "description": "`match_hyp h : t` fails if the hypothesis `h` does not match the type `t` (which may be a pattern).\nWe use this tactic for writing tests.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.extract_goal", "description": "Format the current goal as a stand-alone example. Useful for testing tactics\nor creating [minimal working examples](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mwe.html).\n\n* `extract_goal`: formats the statement as an `example` declaration\n* `extract_goal my_decl`: formats the statement as a `lemma` or `def` declaration\n  called `my_decl`\n* `extract_goal with i j k:` only use local constants `i`, `j`, `k` in the declaration\n\nExamples:\n\n```lean\nexample (i j k : \u2115) (h\u2080 : i \u2264 j) (h\u2081 : j \u2264 k) : i \u2264 k :=\nbegin\n  extract_goal,\n     -- prints:\n     -- example (i j k : \u2115) (h\u2080 : i \u2264 j) (h\u2081 : j \u2264 k) : i \u2264 k :=\n     -- begin\n     --   admit,\n     -- end\n  extract_goal my_lemma\n     -- prints:\n     -- lemma my_lemma (i j k : \u2115) (h\u2080 : i \u2264 j) (h\u2081 : j \u2264 k) : i \u2264 k :=\n     -- begin\n     --   admit,\n     -- end\nend\n\nexample {i j k x y z w p q r m n : \u2115} (h\u2080 : i \u2264 j) (h\u2081 : j \u2264 k) (h\u2081 : k \u2264 p) (h\u2081 : p \u2264 q) : i \u2264 k :=\nbegin\n  extract_goal my_lemma,\n    -- prints:\n    -- lemma my_lemma {i j k x y z w p q r m n : \u2115}\n    --   (h\u2080 : i \u2264 j)\n    --   (h\u2081 : j \u2264 k)\n    --   (h\u2081 : k \u2264 p)\n    --   (h\u2081 : p \u2264 q) :\n    --   i \u2264 k :=\n    -- begin\n    --   admit,\n    -- end\n\n  extract_goal my_lemma with i j k\n    -- prints:\n    -- lemma my_lemma {p i j k : \u2115}\n    --   (h\u2080 : i \u2264 j)\n    --   (h\u2081 : j \u2264 k)\n    --   (h\u2081 : k \u2264 p) :\n    --   i \u2264 k :=\n    -- begin\n    --   admit,\n    -- end\nend\n\nexample : true :=\nbegin\n  let n := 0,\n  have m : \u2115, admit,\n  have k : fin n, admit,\n  have : n + m + k.1 = 0, extract_goal,\n    -- prints:\n    -- example (m : \u2115)  : let n : \u2115 := 0 in \u2200 (k : fin n), n + m + k.val = 0 :=\n    -- begin\n    --   intros n k,\n    --   admit,\n    -- end\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.clear_value", "description": " `clear_value n\u2081 n\u2082 ...` clears the bodies of the local definitions `n\u2081, n\u2082 ...`, changing them\ninto regular hypotheses. A hypothesis `n : \u03b1 := t` is changed to `n : \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.field", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.revert_after", "description": "`revert_after n` reverts all the hypotheses after `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.clear_except", "description": "`clear_except h\u2080 h\u2081` deletes all the assumptions it can except for `h\u2080` and `h\u2081`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.guard_hyp_mod_implicit", "description": "`guard_hyp_mod_implicit h : t` fails if the type of the hypothesis `h`\nis not definitionally equal to `t` modulo none transparency\n(i.e., unifying the implicit arguments modulo semireducible transparency).\nWe use this tactic for writing tests.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.guard_hyp'", "description": "`guard_hyp' h : t` fails if the hypothesis `h` does not have type `t`.\nWe use this tactic for writing tests.\nFixes `guard_hyp` by instantiating meta variables", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.list_cast_of_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.get_current_field", "description": "Get the field of the current goal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.apply_rules", "description": "`apply_rules hs with attrs n` applies the list of lemmas `hs` and all lemmas tagged with an\nattribute from the list `attrs`, as well as the `assumption` tactic on the\nfirst goal and the resulting subgoals, iteratively, at most `n` times.\n`n` is optional, equal to 50 by default.\nYou can pass an `apply_cfg` option argument as `apply_rules hs n opt`.\n(A typical usage would be with `apply_rules hs n { md := reducible }`,\nwhich asks `apply_rules` to not unfold `semireducible` definitions (i.e. most)\nwhen checking if a lemma matches the goal.)\n\nFor instance:\n\n```lean\n@[user_attribute]\nmeta def mono_rules : user_attribute :=\n{ name := `mono_rules,\n  descr := \"lemmas usable to prove monotonicity\" }\n\nattribute [mono_rules] add_le_add mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_right\n\nlemma my_test {a b c d e : real} (h1 : a \u2264 b) (h2 : c \u2264 d) (h3 : 0 \u2264 e) :\na + c * e + a + c + 0 \u2264 b + d * e + b + d + e :=\n-- any of the following lines solve the goal:\nadd_le_add (add_le_add (add_le_add (add_le_add h1 (mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_right h2 h3)) h1 ) h2) h3\nby apply_rules [add_le_add, mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_right]\nby apply_rules with mono_rules\nby apply_rules [add_le_add] with mono_rules\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.h_generalize", "description": "`h_generalize Hx : e == x` matches on `cast _ e` in the goal and replaces it with\n`x`. It also adds `Hx : e == x` as an assumption. If `cast _ e` appears multiple\ntimes (not necessarily with the same proof), they are all replaced by `x`. `cast`\n`eq.mp`, `eq.mpr`, `eq.subst`, `eq.substr`, `eq.rec` and `eq.rec_on` are all treated\nas casts.\n\n- `h_generalize Hx : e == x with h` adds hypothesis `\u03b1 = \u03b2` with `e : \u03b1, x : \u03b2`;\n- `h_generalize Hx : e == x with _` chooses automatically chooses the name of\n  assumption `\u03b1 = \u03b2`;\n- `h_generalize! Hx : e == x` reverts `Hx`;\n- when `Hx` is omitted, assumption `Hx : e == x` is not added.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.extract_goal", "description": "Format the current goal as a stand-alone example. Useful for testing tactics\nor creating [minimal working examples](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mwe.html).\n\n* `extract_goal`: formats the statement as an `example` declaration\n* `extract_goal my_decl`: formats the statement as a `lemma` or `def` declaration\n  called `my_decl`\n* `extract_goal with i j k:` only use local constants `i`, `j`, `k` in the declaration\n\nExamples:\n\n```lean\nexample (i j k : \u2115) (h\u2080 : i \u2264 j) (h\u2081 : j \u2264 k) : i \u2264 k :=\nbegin\n  extract_goal,\n     -- prints:\n     -- example (i j k : \u2115) (h\u2080 : i \u2264 j) (h\u2081 : j \u2264 k) : i \u2264 k :=\n     -- begin\n     --   admit,\n     -- end\n  extract_goal my_lemma\n     -- prints:\n     -- lemma my_lemma (i j k : \u2115) (h\u2080 : i \u2264 j) (h\u2081 : j \u2264 k) : i \u2264 k :=\n     -- begin\n     --   admit,\n     -- end\nend\n\nexample {i j k x y z w p q r m n : \u2115} (h\u2080 : i \u2264 j) (h\u2081 : j \u2264 k) (h\u2081 : k \u2264 p) (h\u2081 : p \u2264 q) : i \u2264 k :=\nbegin\n  extract_goal my_lemma,\n    -- prints:\n    -- lemma my_lemma {i j k x y z w p q r m n : \u2115}\n    --   (h\u2080 : i \u2264 j)\n    --   (h\u2081 : j \u2264 k)\n    --   (h\u2081 : k \u2264 p)\n    --   (h\u2081 : p \u2264 q) :\n    --   i \u2264 k :=\n    -- begin\n    --   admit,\n    -- end\n\n  extract_goal my_lemma with i j k\n    -- prints:\n    -- lemma my_lemma {p i j k : \u2115}\n    --   (h\u2080 : i \u2264 j)\n    --   (h\u2081 : j \u2264 k)\n    --   (h\u2081 : k \u2264 p) :\n    --   i \u2264 k :=\n    -- begin\n    --   admit,\n    -- end\nend\n\nexample : true :=\nbegin\n  let n := 0,\n  have m : \u2115, admit,\n  have k : fin n, admit,\n  have : n + m + k.1 = 0, extract_goal,\n    -- prints:\n    -- example (m : \u2115)  : let n : \u2115 := 0 in \u2200 (k : fin n), n + m + k.val = 0 :=\n    -- begin\n    --   intros n k,\n    --   admit,\n    -- end\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.source_fields", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.fconstructor", "description": "Similar to `constructor`, but does not reorder goals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.unfold_wf", "description": "Unfold `has_well_founded.r`, `sizeof` and other such definitions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.revert_after", "description": "`revert_after n` reverts all the hypotheses after `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.classical", "description": " Make every proposition in the context decidable.\n\n`classical!` does this more aggressively, such that even if a decidable instance is already\navailable for a specific proposition, the noncomputable one will be used instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.unfold_coes", "description": "Unfold coercion-related definitions", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.collect_struct", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.change'", "description": "The logic of `change x with y at l` fails when there are dependencies.\n`change'` mimics the behavior of `change`, except in the case of `change x with y at l`.\nIn this case, it will correctly replace occurences of `x` with `y` at all possible hypotheses\nin `l`. As long as `x` and `y` are defeq, it should never fail.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.clear_value", "description": " `clear_value n\u2081 n\u2082 ...` clears the bodies of the local definitions `n\u2081, n\u2082 ...`, changing them\ninto regular hypotheses. A hypothesis `n : \u03b1 := t` is changed to `n : \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.compact_decl", "description": "go from (x\u2080 : t\u2080) (x\u2081 : t\u2080) (x\u2082 : t\u2080) to (x\u2080 x\u2081 x\u2082 : t\u2080)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.u_int_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.W_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.u_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.\u03c6", "description": " An auxiliary function to construct partitions of unity on finite-dimensional real vector spaces.\nIt is the characteristic function of the closed unit ball.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.u_exists", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.W_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.u_le_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.Y_pos_of_mem_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_smooth_tsupport_subset", "description": " If a set `s` is a neighborhood of `x`, then there exists a smooth function `f` taking\nvalues in `[0, 1]`, supported in `s` and with `f x = 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.u_smooth", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.Y_le_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.u_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.Y_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.u_compact_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.W_mul_\u03c6_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.W_compact_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.has_cont_diff_bump", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.Y", "description": " An auxiliary function to construct partitions of unity on finite-dimensional real vector spaces.\nIt is the convolution between a smooth function of integral `1` supported in the ball of radius `D`,\nwith the indicator function of the closed unit ball. Therefore, it is smooth, equal to `1` on the\nball of radius `1 - D`, with support equal to the ball of radius `1 + D`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.W_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.Y_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.W", "description": " An auxiliary function to construct partitions of unity on finite-dimensional real vector spaces,\nwhich is smooth, symmetric, with support equal to the ball of radius `D` and integral `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.Y_eq_one_of_mem_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.Y_eq_zero_of_not_mem_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.Y_smooth", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.u", "description": " An auxiliary function to construct partitions of unity on finite-dimensional real vector spaces,\nwhich is smooth, symmetric, and with support equal to the unit ball.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.Y_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_open.exists_smooth_support_eq", "description": " Given an open set `s` in a finite-dimensional real normed vector space, there exists a smooth\nfunction with values in `[0, 1]` whose support is exactly `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.u_continuous", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.u_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_cont_diff_bump_base.W_integral", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.monoid", "description": " A type endowed with `1` and `*` is a monoid,\nif it admits a surjective map that preserves `1` and `*` to a monoid.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.left_cancel_monoid", "description": " A type endowed with `1` and `*` is a left cancel monoid,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `1` and `*` to a left cancel monoid.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.add_comm_monoid", "description": "A type endowed with `0` and `+` is an additive commutative monoid,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `0` and `+` to an additive commutative monoid.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.subtraction_monoid", "description": "A type endowed with `0`, `+`, unary `-`, and binary `-` is a `subtraction_monoid`\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `0`, `+`, unary `-`, and binary `-` to\na `subtraction_monoid`.\nThis version takes custom `nsmul` and `zsmul` as `[has_smul \u2115 M\u2081]` and\n`[has_smul \u2124 M\u2081]` arguments.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.add_group_with_one", "description": " A type endowed with `0`, `1` and `+` is an additive group with one,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `0`, `1` and `+` to an additive group with one.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.has_involutive_inv", "description": " A type has an involutive inversion if it admits a surjective map that preserves `\u207b\u00b9` to a type\nwhich has an involutive inversion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.add_cancel_monoid", "description": "A type endowed with `0` and `+` is an additive left cancel monoid,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `0` and `+` to an additive left cancel monoid.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.add_comm_semigroup", "description": "A type endowed with `+` is an additive commutative semigroup,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `+` to an additive commutative semigroup.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.add_monoid", "description": "A type endowed with `0` and `+` is an additive monoid,\nif it admits a surjective map that preserves `0` and `+` to an additive monoid.\nThis version takes a custom `nsmul` as a `[has_smul \u2115 M\u2082]` argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.add_monoid_with_one", "description": " A type endowed with `0`, `1` and `+` is an additive monoid with one,\nif it admits a surjective map that preserves `0`, `1` and `*` from an additive monoid with one.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.add_left_cancel_monoid", "description": "A type endowed with `0` and `+` is an additive left cancel monoid,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `0` and `+` to an additive left cancel monoid.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.add_monoid_with_one", "description": " A type endowed with `0`, `1` and `+` is an additive monoid with one,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `0`, `1` and `+` to an additive monoid with one.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.has_involutive_inv", "description": " A type has an involutive inversion if it admits a surjective map that preserves `\u207b\u00b9` to a type\nwhich has an involutive inversion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.division_monoid", "description": " A type endowed with `1`, `*`, `\u207b\u00b9`, and `/` is a `division_monoid`\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `1`, `*`, `\u207b\u00b9`, and `/` to a `division_monoid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.group", "description": " A type endowed with `1`, `*` and `\u207b\u00b9` is a group,\nif it admits a surjective map that preserves `1`, `*` and `\u207b\u00b9` to a group.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.add_monoid", "description": "A type endowed with `0` and `+` is an additive monoid,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `0` and `+` to an additive monoid.\nThis version takes a custom `nsmul` as a `[has_smul \u2115 M\u2081]` argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.add_group", "description": "A type endowed with `0` and `+` is an additive group,\nif it admits a surjective map that preserves `0` and `+` to an additive group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.add_zero_class", "description": "A type endowed with `0` and `+` is an add_zero_class,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `0` and `+` to an add_zero_class.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.add_cancel_comm_monoid", "description": "A type endowed with `0` and `+` is an additive cancel commutative monoid,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `0` and `+` to an additive cancel commutative monoid.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.comm_group", "description": " A type endowed with `1`, `*`, `\u207b\u00b9`, and `/` is a commutative group,\nif it admits a surjective map that preserves `1`, `*`, `\u207b\u00b9`, and `/` from a commutative group.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.add_semigroup", "description": "A type endowed with `+` is an additive semigroup,\nif it admits a surjective map that preserves `+` from an additive semigroup.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.semigroup", "description": " A type endowed with `*` is a semigroup,\nif it admits a surjective map that preserves `*` from a semigroup.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.comm_group", "description": " A type endowed with `1`, `*` and `\u207b\u00b9` is a commutative group,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `1`, `*` and `\u207b\u00b9` to a commutative group.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.has_involutive_neg", "description": "A type has an involutive negation if it admits a surjective map that\npreserves `\u207b\u00b9` to a type which has an involutive inversion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.division_comm_monoid", "description": " A type endowed with `1`, `*`, `\u207b\u00b9`, and `/` is a `division_comm_monoid`\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `1`, `*`, `\u207b\u00b9`, and `/` to a `division_comm_monoid`.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.right_cancel_monoid", "description": " A type endowed with `1` and `*` is a right cancel monoid,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `1` and `*` to a right cancel monoid.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.group", "description": " A type endowed with `1`, `*` and `\u207b\u00b9` is a group,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `1`, `*` and `\u207b\u00b9` to a group.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.left_cancel_semigroup", "description": " A type endowed with `*` is a left cancel semigroup,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `*` to a left cancel semigroup.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.semigroup", "description": " A type endowed with `*` is a semigroup,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `*` to a semigroup.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.subtraction_comm_monoid", "description": "A type endowed with `0`, `+`, unary `-`, and binary `-` is a `subtraction_comm_monoid`\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `0`, `+`, unary `-`, and binary `-` to\na `subtraction_comm_monoid`.\nThis version takes custom `nsmul` and `zsmul` as `[has_smul \u2115 M\u2081]` and\n`[has_smul \u2124 M\u2081]` arguments.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.add_group_with_one", "description": " A type endowed with `0`, `1`, `+` is an additive group with one,\nif it admits a surjective map that preserves `0`, `1`, and `+` to an additive group with one.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.add_comm_group", "description": "A type endowed with `0` and `+` is an additive commutative group,\nif it admits a surjective map that preserves `0` and `+` to an additive commutative group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.cancel_monoid", "description": " A type endowed with `1` and `*` is a cancel monoid,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `1` and `*` to a cancel monoid.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.comm_semigroup", "description": " A type endowed with `*` is a commutative semigroup,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `*` to a commutative semigroup.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.sub_neg_monoid", "description": "A type endowed with `0`, `+`, unary `-`, and binary `-` is a `sub_neg_monoid`\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `0`, `+`, unary `-`, and binary `-` to\na `sub_neg_monoid`.\nThis version takes custom `nsmul` and `zsmul` as `[has_smul \u2115 M\u2081]` and\n`[has_smul \u2124 M\u2081]` arguments.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.div_inv_monoid", "description": " A type endowed with `1`, `*`, `\u207b\u00b9`, and `/` is a `div_inv_monoid`\nif it admits a surjective map that preserves `1`, `*`, `\u207b\u00b9`, and `/` to a `div_inv_monoid`.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.mul_one_class", "description": " A type endowed with `1` and `*` is a mul_one_class,\nif it admits a surjective map that preserves `1` and `*` from a mul_one_class.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.add_comm_monoid", "description": "A type endowed with `0` and `+` is an additive commutative monoid,\nif it admits a surjective map that preserves `0` and `+` to an additive commutative monoid.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.add_right_cancel_monoid", "description": "A type endowed with `0` and `+` is an additive left cancel monoid,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `0` and `+` to an additive left cancel monoid.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.add_comm_group_with_one", "description": " A type endowed with `0`, `1`, `+` is an additive commutative group with one, if it admits a\nsurjective map that preserves `0`, `1`, and `+` to an additive commutative group with one.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.add_comm_monoid_with_one", "description": " A type endowed with `0`, `1` and `+` is an additive monoid with one,\nif it admits a surjective map that preserves `0`, `1` and `*` from an additive monoid with one.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.right_cancel_semigroup", "description": " A type endowed with `*` is a right cancel semigroup,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `*` to a right cancel semigroup.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.add_right_cancel_semigroup", "description": "A type endowed with `+` is an additive right cancel semigroup,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `+` to an additive right cancel semigroup.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.sub_neg_monoid", "description": "A type endowed with `0`, `+`, unary `-`, and binary `-` is a `sub_neg_monoid`\nif it admits a surjective map that preserves `0`, `+`, unary `-`, and binary `-` to\na `sub_neg_monoid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.add_comm_group", "description": "A type endowed with `0` and `+` is an additive commutative group,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `0` and `+` to an additive commutative group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.add_comm_group_with_one", "description": " A type endowed with `0`, `1` and `+` is an additive commutative group with one, if it admits an\ninjective map that preserves `0`, `1` and `+` to an additive commutative group with one.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.comm_monoid", "description": " A type endowed with `1` and `*` is a commutative monoid,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `1` and `*` to a commutative monoid.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.add_group", "description": "A type endowed with `0` and `+` is an additive group,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `0` and `+` to an additive group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.comm_semigroup", "description": " A type endowed with `*` is a commutative semigroup,\nif it admits a surjective map that preserves `*` from a commutative semigroup.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.comm_monoid", "description": " A type endowed with `1` and `*` is a commutative monoid,\nif it admits a surjective map that preserves `1` and `*` from a commutative monoid.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.add_comm_monoid_with_one", "description": " A type endowed with `0`, `1` and `+` is an additive commutative monoid with one, if it admits an\ninjective map that preserves `0`, `1` and `+` to an additive commutative monoid with one.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.add_comm_semigroup", "description": "A type endowed with `+` is an additive commutative semigroup,\nif it admits a surjective map that preserves `+` from an additive commutative semigroup.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.add_zero_class", "description": "A type endowed with `0` and `+` is an add_zero_class,\nif it admits a surjective map that preserves `0` and `+` to an add_zero_class.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.has_involutive_neg", "description": "A type has an involutive negation if it admits a surjective map that\npreserves `\u207b\u00b9` to a type which has an involutive inversion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.monoid", "description": " A type endowed with `1` and `*` is a monoid,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `1` and `*` to a monoid.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.cancel_comm_monoid", "description": " A type endowed with `1` and `*` is a cancel commutative monoid,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `1` and `*` to a cancel commutative monoid.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.add_left_cancel_semigroup", "description": "A type endowed with `+` is an additive left cancel semigroup,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `+` to an additive left cancel semigroup.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.add_semigroup", "description": "A type endowed with `+` is an additive semigroup,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `+` to an additive semigroup.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.div_inv_monoid", "description": " A type endowed with `1`, `*`, `\u207b\u00b9`, and `/` is a `div_inv_monoid`\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `1`, `*`, `\u207b\u00b9`, and `/` to a `div_inv_monoid`.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.mul_one_class", "description": " A type endowed with `1` and `*` is a mul_one_class,\nif it admits an injective map that preserves `1` and `*` to a mul_one_class.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.submonoid_presheaf_of_stalk_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.submonoid_presheaf.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.submonoid_presheaf.to_localization_presheaf.category_theory.epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.total_quotient_presheaf", "description": "The localization of a presheaf of `CommRing`s at locally non-zero-divisor sections.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.submonoid_presheaf.localization_presheaf", "description": "The localization of a presheaf of `CommRing`s with respect to a `submonoid_presheaf`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.submonoid_presheaf", "description": "A subpresheaf with a submonoid structure on each of the components.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.submonoid_presheaf.obj", "description": "A subpresheaf with a submonoid structure on each of the components.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.submonoid_presheaf.map", "description": "A subpresheaf with a submonoid structure on each of the components.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.submonoid_presheaf_of_stalk", "description": " Given a submonoid at each of the stalks, we may define a submonoid presheaf consisting of\nsections whose restriction onto each stalk falls in the given submonoid.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.to_total_quotient_presheaf.epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.to_total_quotient_presheaf", "description": "The map into the presheaf of total quotient rings", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.to_total_quotient_presheaf.category_theory.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.submonoid_presheaf.to_localization_presheaf", "description": "The map into the localization presheaf.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "summable_geometric_of_lt_1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_pow_at_top_nhds_within_0_of_lt_1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.tsum_geometric", "description": " The series `pow r` converges to `(1-r)\u207b\u00b9`. For `r < 1` the RHS is a finite number,\nand for `1 \u2264 r` the RHS equals `\u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.exists_pos_sum_of_countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_sum_geometric_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geom_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsum_geometric_nnreal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_one_div_add_at_top_nhds_0_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "aux_has_sum_of_le_geometric", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_add_one_pow_at_top_at_top_of_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "edist_le_of_edist_le_geometric_of_tendsto", "description": " If `edist (f n) (f (n+1))` is bounded by `C * r^n`, then the distance from\n`f n` to the limit of `f` is bounded above by `C * r^n / (1 - r)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_at_top_of_geom_le", "description": " If a sequence `v` of real numbers satisfies `k * v n \u2264 v (n+1)` with `1 < k`,\nthen it goes to +\u221e.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_sum_geometric_of_lt_1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsum_geometric_inv_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "edist_le_of_edist_le_geometric_of_tendsto\u2080", "description": " If `edist (f n) (f (n+1))` is bounded by `C * r^n`, then the distance from\n`f 0` to the limit of `f` is bounded above by `C / (1 - r)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_nat_floor_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_pow_at_top_nhds_0_of_lt_1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum_geometric_two_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsum_geometric_inv_two_ge", "description": "The sum of `2\u207b\u00b9 ^ i` for `n \u2264 i` equals `2 * 2\u207b\u00b9 ^ n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "edist_le_of_edist_le_geometric_two_of_tendsto", "description": " If `edist (f n) (f (n+1))` is bounded by `C * 2^-n`, then the distance from\n`f n` to the limit of `f` is bounded above by `2 * C * 2^-n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_factorial_div_pow_self_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_nat_floor_mul_div_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_geom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_nat_ceil_mul_div_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_coe_nat_div_add_at_top", "description": " The limit of `n / (n + x)` is 1, for any constant `x` (valid in `\u211d` or any topological division\nalgebra over `\u211d`, e.g., `\u2102`).\n\nTODO: introduce a typeclass saying that `1 / n` tends to 0 at top, making it possible to get this\nstatement simultaneously on `\u211a`, `\u211d` and `\u2102`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_nat_floor_div_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cauchy_seq_of_le_geometric", "description": " If `dist (f n) (f (n+1))` is bounded by `C * r^n`, `r < 1`, then `f` is a Cauchy sequence.\nNote that this lemma does not assume `0 \u2264 C` or `0 \u2264 r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.has_sum_geometric", "description": "**Sum of a Geometric Series**", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.countable.exists_pos_forall_sum_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.tendsto_const_div_at_top_nhds_0_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_le_of_le_geometric_two_of_tendsto", "description": " If `dist (f n) (f (n+1))` is bounded by `(C / 2) / 2^n`, then the distance from\n`f n` to the limit of `f` is bounded above by `C / 2^n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.exists_pos_sum_of_countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "summable_geometric_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniformity_basis_dist_pow_of_lt_1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_le_of_le_geometric_of_tendsto", "description": " If `dist (f n) (f (n+1))` is bounded by `C * r^n`, `r < 1`, then the distance from\n`f 0` to the limit of `f` is bounded above by `C / (1 - r)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.tendsto_pow_at_top_nhds_0_of_lt_1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.tendsto_pow_at_top_nhds_0_of_lt_1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsum_geometric_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cauchy_seq_of_edist_le_geometric_two", "description": "If `edist (f n) (f (n+1))` is bounded by `C * 2^-n`, then `f` is a Cauchy sequence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.summable_geometric", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.exists_pos_tsum_mul_lt_of_countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "edist_le_of_edist_le_geometric_two_of_tendsto\u2080", "description": " If `edist (f n) (f (n+1))` is bounded by `C * 2^-n`, then the distance from\n`f 0` to the limit of `f` is bounded above by `2 * C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_geom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.tendsto_inverse_at_top_nhds_0_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geom_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_sum_geometric_two'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cauchy_seq_of_le_geometric_two", "description": "If `dist (f n) (f (n+1))` is bounded by `(C / 2) / 2^n`, then `f` is a Cauchy sequence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "summable_one_div_pow_of_le", "description": "A series whose terms are bounded by the terms of a converging geometric series converges.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_nat_ceil_div_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_le_of_le_geometric_two_of_tendsto\u2080", "description": " If `dist (f n) (f (n+1))` is bounded by `(C / 2) / 2^n`, then the distance from\n`f 0` to the limit of `f` is bounded above by `C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.countable.exists_pos_has_sum_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsum_geometric_two'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cauchy_seq_of_edist_le_geometric", "description": " If `edist (f n) (f (n+1))` is bounded by `C * r^n`, `C \u2260 \u221e`, `r < 1`,\nthen `f` is a Cauchy sequence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_inverse_at_top_nhds_0_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_pow_at_top_at_top_of_one_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.exists_pos_sum_of_countable'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_le_of_le_geometric_of_tendsto\u2080", "description": " If `dist (f n) (f (n+1))` is bounded by `C * r^n`, `r < 1`, then the distance from\n`f n` to the limit of `f` is bounded above by `C * r^n / (1 - r)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_const_div_at_top_nhds_0_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"