"mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.of_real_average", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.laverage_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.laverage_mem_open_segment_compl_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_lintegral_le_pos", "description": " **First moment method**. A measurable function is greater than its integral on a set of\npositive measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.to_real_laverage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_average_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.average_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_average_le_pos", "description": " **First moment method**. An integrable function is greater than its mean on a set of positive\nmeasure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_smul_set_average", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.average_union_mem_segment", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_not_mem_null_integral_le", "description": " **First moment method**. The maximum of an integrable function is greater than its integral,\nwhile avoiding a null set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.average_eq_integral", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.set_average_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.laverage_add_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_average", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.average_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.average_union_mem_open_segment", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_le_integral", "description": "**First moment method**. The minimum of an integrable function is smaller than its integral.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_set_laverage_le_pos", "description": " **First moment method**. A measurable function is greater than its mean on a set of positive\nmeasure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.to_real_set_laverage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.set_laverage_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.set_lintegral_set_laverage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_le_lintegral_pos", "description": " **First moment method**. A measurable function is smaller than its integral on a set of\npositive measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_average_le", "description": "**First moment method**. The maximum of an integrable function is greater than its mean.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_integral_le", "description": "**First moment method**. The maximum of an integrable function is greater than its integral.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_sub_average", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.set_average_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_not_mem_null_le_lintegral", "description": " **First moment method**. The minimum of a measurable function is smaller than its integral,\nwhile avoiding a null set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_le_set_average", "description": "**First moment method**. The minimum of an integrable function is smaller than its mean.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_not_mem_null_le_laverage", "description": " **First moment method**. The minimum of a measurable function is smaller than its mean, while\navoiding a null set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.average_eq'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_set_laverage_le", "description": "**First moment method**. The maximum of a measurable function is greater than its mean.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_le_laverage_pos", "description": " **First moment method**. A measurable function is smaller than its mean on a set of positive\nmeasure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.set_average_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.laverage", "description": " Average value of an `\u211d\u22650\u221e`-valued function `f` w.r.t. a measure `\u03bc`, notation: `\u2a0d\u207b x, f x \u2202\u03bc`.\nIt is defined as `\u03bc univ\u207b\u00b9 * \u222b\u207b x, f x \u2202\u03bc`, so it is equal to zero if `\u03bc` is an infinite measure. If\n`\u03bc` is a probability measure, then the average of any function is equal to its integral.\n\nFor the average on a set, use `\u2a0d\u207b x in s, f x \u2202\u03bc` (defined as `\u2a0d\u207b x, f x \u2202(\u03bc.restrict s)`). For\naverage w.r.t. the volume, one can omit `\u2202volume`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_laverage_le", "description": "**First moment method**. The maximum of a measurable function is greater than its mean.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.set_integral_set_average_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.laverage_eq'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.set_laverage_lt_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.average_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_le_set_laverage_pos", "description": " **First moment method**. A measurable function is smaller than its mean on a set of positive\nmeasure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.laverage_eq_lintegral", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.set_laverage_eq'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.average_mem_open_segment_compl_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.laverage_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_le_integral_pos", "description": " **First moment method**. An integrable function is smaller than its integral on a set of\npositive measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_le_laverage", "description": "**First moment method**. The minimum of a measurable function is smaller than its mean.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.set_laverage_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.set_laverage_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.laverage_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.average_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.average", "description": " Average value of a function `f` w.r.t. a measure `\u03bc`, notation: `\u2a0d x, f x \u2202\u03bc`. It is defined as\n`(\u03bc univ).to_real\u207b\u00b9 \u2022 \u222b x, f x \u2202\u03bc`, so it is equal to zero if `f` is not integrable or if `\u03bc` is an\ninfinite measure. If `\u03bc` is a probability measure, then the average of any function is equal to its\nintegral.\n\nFor the average on a set, use `\u2a0d x in s, f x \u2202\u03bc` (defined as `\u2a0d x, f x \u2202(\u03bc.restrict s)`). For\naverage w.r.t. the volume, one can omit `\u2202volume`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.laverage_zero_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.laverage_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.average_add_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_has_fpower_series_on_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_apply_eq_div'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_has_sum_exp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_add_of_commute", "description": " In a Banach-algebra `\ud835\udd38` over `\ud835\udd42 = \u211d` or `\ud835\udd42 = \u2102`, if `x` and `y` commute, then\n`exp \ud835\udd42 (x+y) = (exp \ud835\udd42 x) * (exp \ud835\udd42 y)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "invertible_exp", "description": "`exp \ud835\udd42 x` has explicit two-sided inverse `exp \ud835\udd42 (-x)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_div_has_sum_exp_of_mem_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_radius_eq_top", "description": " In a normed algebra `\ud835\udd38` over `\ud835\udd42 = \u211d` or `\ud835\udd42 = \u2102`, the series defining the exponential map\nhas an infinite radius of convergence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_exp_series_summable'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inv_of_exp_of_mem_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_has_fpower_series_at_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_div_summable_of_mem_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_sum_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.snd_exp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_apply_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_radius_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.exp_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.fst_exp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_eq_tsum_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_apply_eq'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_has_sum_exp_of_mem_ball'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "of_real_exp_\u211d_\u211d", "description": "A version of `complex.of_real_exp` for `exp` instead of `complex.exp`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_unit_exp_of_mem_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_eq_exp", "description": " If a normed ring `\ud835\udd38` is a normed algebra over two fields, then they define the same\nexponential function on `\ud835\udd38`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_div_has_sum_exp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_continuous", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series", "description": " `exp_series \ud835\udd42 \ud835\udd38` is the `formal_multilinear_series` whose `n`-th term is the map\n`(x\u1d62) : \ud835\udd38\u207f \u21a6 (1/n! : \ud835\udd42) \u2022 \u220f x\u1d62`. Its sum is the exponential map `exp \ud835\udd42 : \ud835\udd38 \u2192 \ud835\udd38`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_add_of_mem_ball", "description": " In a commutative Banach-algebra `\ud835\udd38` over a normed field `\ud835\udd42` of characteristic zero,\n`exp \ud835\udd42 (x+y) = (exp \ud835\udd42 x) * (exp \ud835\udd42 y)` for all `x`, `y` in the disk of convergence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_div_summable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_units_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_exp_series_div_summable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_mem_unitary_of_mem_skew_adjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra_map_exp_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_summable'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_exp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_op", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_has_sum_exp_of_mem_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_exp_series_summable_of_mem_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "invertible_exp_of_mem_ball", "description": "`exp \ud835\udd42 x` has explicit two-sided inverse `exp \ud835\udd42 (-x)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_eq_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_exp_series_summable_of_mem_ball'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_unit_exp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_summable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_exp_series_summable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "map_exp", "description": "Any continuous ring homomorphism commutes with `exp`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_apply_eq_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.exp_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_unop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_at_exp_of_mem_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp", "description": " `exp \ud835\udd42 : \ud835\udd38 \u2192 \ud835\udd38` is the exponential map determined by the action of `\ud835\udd42` on `\ud835\udd38`.\nIt is defined as the sum of the `formal_multilinear_series` `exp_series \ud835\udd42 \ud835\udd38`.\n\nNote that when `\ud835\udd38 = matrix n n \ud835\udd42`, this is the **Matrix Exponential**; see\n[`analysis.normed_space.matrix_exponential`](../matrix_exponential) for lemmas specific to that\ncase.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fpower_series_at_exp_zero_of_radius_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_add_of_commute_of_mem_ball", "description": " In a Banach-algebra `\ud835\udd38` over a normed field `\ud835\udd42` of characteristic zero, if `x` and `y` are\nin the disk of convergence and commute, then `exp \ud835\udd42 (x + y) = (exp \ud835\udd42 x) * (exp \ud835\udd42 y)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_nsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_add", "description": " In a commutative Banach-algebra `\ud835\udd38` over `\ud835\udd42 = \u211d` or `\ud835\udd42 = \u2102`,\n`exp \ud835\udd42 (x+y) = (exp \ud835\udd42 x) * (exp \ud835\udd42 y)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_sum", "description": "A version of `exp_sum_of_commute` for a commutative Banach-algebra.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_zsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_\u211d_\u2102_eq_exp_\u2102_\u2102", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_conj'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_sum_eq_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_neg_of_mem_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.exp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.exp_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on_exp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_summable_of_mem_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.update_exp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_self_adjoint.exp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_summable_of_mem_ball'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_sum_of_commute", "description": " In a Banach-algebra `\ud835\udd38` over `\ud835\udd42 = \u211d` or `\ud835\udd42 = \u2102`, if a family of elements `f i` mutually\ncommute then `exp \ud835\udd42 (\u2211 i, f i) = \u220f i, exp \ud835\udd42 (f i)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_analytic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "map_exp_of_mem_ball", "description": "Any continuous ring homomorphism commutes with `exp`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_has_sum_exp'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.inverse_exp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_eq_exp_series", "description": " If a normed ring `\ud835\udd38` is a normed algebra over two fields, then they define the same\n`exp_series` on `\ud835\udd38`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.exp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fpower_series_on_ball_exp_of_radius_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inv_of_exp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_units_conj'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_series_apply_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_exp_series_div_summable_of_mem_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra_map_exp_comm_of_mem_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_count_disjoint_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_count_inter'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_count_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_count_inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_count_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_count_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_count_is_probability_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_count", "description": " Given a set `s`, `cond_count s` is the counting measure conditioned on `s`. In particular,\n`cond_count s t` is the proportion of `s` that is contained in `t`.\n\nThis is a probability measure when `s` is finite and nonempty and is given by\n`probability_theory.cond_count_is_probability_measure`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.finite_of_cond_count_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_count_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.pred_true_of_cond_count_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_count_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_count_empty_meas", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_count_of_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_count_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_count_eq_one_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_count_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_count_add_compl_eq", "description": "A version of the law of total probability for counting probabilites.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_count_inter_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.path.cast_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.hom.eq_cast_iff_heq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.path.eq_cast_iff_heq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.hom.cast_rfl_rfl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.eq_nil_of_length_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.hom_cast_eq_of_cons_eq_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.cast_eq_of_cons_eq_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.hom.cast", "description": "Change the endpoints of an arrow using equalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.hom.cast_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.hom.cast_eq_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.hom.cast_heq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.path.cast_heq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.path.cast_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.path.cast_eq_iff_heq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.path.cast_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.path.cast_eq_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.path.cast", "description": "Change the endpoints of a path using equalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.hom.cast_eq_iff_heq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.path.cast_rfl_rfl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_le_opow_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.log_opow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.log_one_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.log_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.left_le_opow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.log_le_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_mul_add_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.log_one_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.zero_opow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.log_of_not_one_lt_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_right_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.log_of_left_le_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_lt_opow_left_of_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.div_opow_log_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.right_le_opow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.succ_log_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_dvd_opow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.log_zero_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.has_pow", "description": "The ordinal exponential, defined by transfinite recursion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.div_opow_log_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.sup_opow_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_le_opow_iff_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_log_le_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.log_mod_opow_log_lt_log_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.mod_opow_log_lt_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.lt_opow_of_limit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.nat_cast_opow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_one_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_lt_opow_iff_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.lt_opow_succ_log_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.zero_opow'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.log_opow_mul_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.log", "description": "The ordinal logarithm is the solution `u` to the equation `x = b ^ u * v + w` where `v < b` and\n   `w < b ^ u`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_le_iff_le_log", "description": "`opow b` and `log b` (almost) form a Galois connection.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_mul_add_lt_opow_mul_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_limit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.lt_opow_iff_log_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.log_nonempty", "description": "The set in the definition of `log` is nonempty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_is_limit_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_is_normal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.log_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.add_log_le_log_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.log_zero_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_is_limit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_le_of_limit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_le_opow_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.log_mono_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_mul_add_lt_opow_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.positivity_opow", "description": "Extension for the `positivity` tactic: `ordinal.opow` takes positive values on positive inputs.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.log_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_dvd_opow_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.opow_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.one_opow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.is_lawful_traversable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.comp_traverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum.id_traverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.traverse_eq_map_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.id_traverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.id_traverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum.traverse_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum.traverse_eq_map_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.mem_traverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum.is_lawful_traversable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.traverse_append", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.traverse_eq_map_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.traverse_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum.comp_traverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.comp_traverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.is_lawful_traversable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.traverse_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum.naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum.map_traverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "domain_mvt", "description": "Lagrange's Mean Value Theorem, applied to convex domains.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.strict_anti_on_of_deriv_neg", "description": " Let `f` be a function continuous on a convex (or, equivalently, connected) subset `D`\nof the real line. If `f` is differentiable on the interior of `D` and `f'` is negative, then\n`f` is a strictly antitone function on `D`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_ratio_has_deriv_at_eq_ratio_slope'", "description": "Cauchy's **Mean Value Theorem**, extended `has_deriv_at` version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_convex_on_of_deriv2_pos'", "description": " If a function `f` is continuous on a convex set `D \u2286 \u211d` and `f''` is strictly positive on `D`,\nthen `f` is strictly convex on `D`.\nNote that we don't require twice differentiability explicitly as it is already implied by the second\nderivative being strictly positive, except at at most one point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_on_of_deriv2_nonneg'", "description": " If a function `f` is twice differentiable on a open convex set `D \u2286 \u211d` and\n`f''` is nonnegative on `D`, then `f` is convex on `D`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_const_of_deriv_eq_zero", "description": " If `f : \ud835\udd5c \u2192 G`, `\ud835\udd5c = R` or `\ud835\udd5c = \u2102`, is differentiable everywhere and its derivative equal zero,\nthen it is a constant function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_concave_on_univ_of_deriv2_neg", "description": " If a function `f` is continuous on `\u211d`, and `f''` is strictly negative on `\u211d`,\nthen `f` is strictly concave on `\u211d`.\nNote that we don't require twice differentiability explicitly as it is already implied by the second\nderivative being strictly negative, except at at most one point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "image_sub_le_mul_sub_of_deriv_le", "description": " Let `f : \u211d \u2192 \u211d` be a differentiable function. If `f' \u2264 C`, then `f` grows at most as fast\nas `C * x`, i.e., `f y - f x \u2264 C * (y - x)` whenever `x \u2264 y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "constant_of_has_deriv_right_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.mul_sub_lt_image_sub_of_lt_deriv", "description": " Let `f` be a function continuous on a convex (or, equivalently, connected) subset `D`\nof the real line. If `f` is differentiable on the interior of `D` and `C < f'`, then\n`f` grows faster than `C * x` on `D`, i.e., `C * (y - x) < f y - f x` whenever `x, y \u2208 D`,\n`x < y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_image_sub_le_of_norm_deriv_le_segment", "description": " A function on `[a, b]` with the norm of the derivative within `[a, b]`\nbounded by `C` satisfies `\u2016f x - f a\u2016 \u2264 C * (x - a)`, `deriv_within`\nversion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.lipschitz_on_with_of_nnnorm_deriv_within_le", "description": " The mean value theorem on a convex set in dimension 1: if the derivative of a function is\nbounded by `C` on `s`, then the function is `C`-Lipschitz on `s`.\nVersion with `deriv_within` and `lipschitz_on_with`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "image_norm_le_of_liminf_right_slope_norm_lt_deriv_boundary", "description": " General fencing theorem for continuous functions with an estimate on the derivative.\nLet `f` and `B` be continuous functions on `[a, b]` such that\n\n* `\u2016f a\u2016 \u2264 B a`;\n* `B` has right derivative at every point of `[a, b)`;\n* for each `x \u2208 [a, b)` the right-side limit inferior of `(\u2016f z\u2016 - \u2016f x\u2016) / (z - x)`\n  is bounded above by a function `f'`;\n* we have `f' x < B' x` whenever `\u2016f x\u2016 = B x`.\n\nThen `\u2016f x\u2016 \u2264 B x` everywhere on `[a, b]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "image_le_of_liminf_slope_right_le_deriv_boundary", "description": " General fencing theorem for continuous functions with an estimate on the derivative.\nLet `f` and `B` be continuous functions on `[a, b]` such that\n\n* `f a \u2264 B a`;\n* `B` has right derivative `B'` at every point of `[a, b)`;\n* for each `x \u2208 [a, b)` the right-side limit inferior of `(f z - f x) / (z - x)`\n  is bounded above by `B'`.\n\nThen `f x \u2264 B x` everywhere on `[a, b]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_of_fderiv_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.norm_image_sub_le_of_norm_deriv_le", "description": " The mean value theorem on a convex set in dimension 1: if the derivative of a function is\nbounded by `C`, then the function is `C`-Lipschitz. Version with `deriv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_deriv_at_of_has_deriv_at_of_continuous_at", "description": " Over the reals or the complexes, a continuously differentiable function is strictly\ndifferentiable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_anti.strict_concave_on_univ_of_deriv", "description": " If a function `f` is continuous and `f'` is strictly antitone on `\u211d` then `f` is strictly\nconcave. Note that we don't require differentiability, since it is guaranteed at all but at most\none point by the strict antitonicity of `f'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_const_of_fderiv_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "image_le_of_liminf_slope_right_lt_deriv_boundary", "description": " General fencing theorem for continuous functions with an estimate on the derivative.\nLet `f` and `B` be continuous functions on `[a, b]` such that\n\n* `f a \u2264 B a`;\n* `B` has derivative `B'` everywhere on `\u211d`;\n* for each `x \u2208 [a, b)` the right-side limit inferior of `(f z - f x) / (z - x)`\n  is bounded above by a function `f'`;\n* we have `f' x < B' x` whenever `f x = B x`.\n\nThen `f x \u2264 B x` everywhere on `[a, b]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lipschitz_with_of_nnnorm_deriv_le", "description": " The mean value theorem set in dimension 1: if the derivative of a function is bounded by `C`,\nthen the function is `C`-Lipschitz.  Version with `deriv` and `lipschitz_with`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.eq_on_of_fderiv_within_eq", "description": " If two functions have equal Fr\u00e9chet derivatives at every point of a convex set, and are equal at\none point in that set, then they are equal on that set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.exists_nhds_within_lipschitz_on_with_of_has_fderiv_within_at_of_nnnorm_lt", "description": " Let `s` be a convex set in a real normed vector space `E`, let `f : E \u2192 G` be a function\ndifferentiable within `s` in a neighborhood of `x : E` with derivative `f'`. Suppose that `f'` is\ncontinuous within `s` at `x`. Then for any number `K : \u211d\u22650` larger than `\u2016f' x\u2016\u208a`, `f` is\n`K`-Lipschitz on some neighborhood of `x` within `s`. See also\n`convex.exists_nhds_within_lipschitz_on_with_of_has_fderiv_within_at` for a version that claims\nexistence of `K` instead of an explicit estimate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_anti_of_deriv_neg", "description": " Let `f : \u211d \u2192 \u211d` be a differentiable function. If `f'` is negative, then\n`f` is a strictly antitone function.\nNote that we don't require differentiability explicitly as it already implied by the derivative\nbeing strictly negative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_mono_of_deriv_pos", "description": " Let `f : \u211d \u2192 \u211d` be a differentiable function. If `f'` is positive, then\n`f` is a strictly monotone function.\nNote that we don't require differentiability explicitly as it already implied by the derivative\nbeing strictly positive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_image_sub_le_of_norm_deriv_le_segment_01", "description": " A function on `[0, 1]` with the norm of the derivative within `[0, 1]`\nbounded by `C` satisfies `\u2016f 1 - f 0\u2016 \u2264 C`, `deriv_within` version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_mono_on.exists_deriv_lt_slope", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.mul_sub_le_image_sub_of_le_deriv", "description": " Let `f` be a function continuous on a convex (or, equivalently, connected) subset `D`\nof the real line. If `f` is differentiable on the interior of `D` and `C \u2264 f'`, then\n`f` grows at least as fast as `C * x` on `D`, i.e., `C * (y - x) \u2264 f y - f x` whenever `x, y \u2208 D`,\n`x \u2264 y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "constant_of_deriv_within_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.exists_nhds_within_lipschitz_on_with_of_has_fderiv_within_at", "description": " Let `s` be a convex set in a real normed vector space `E`, let `f : E \u2192 G` be a function\ndifferentiable within `s` in a neighborhood of `x : E` with derivative `f'`. Suppose that `f'` is\ncontinuous within `s` at `x`. Then for any number `K : \u211d\u22650` larger than `\u2016f' x\u2016\u208a`, `f` is Lipschitz\non some neighborhood of `x` within `s`. See also\n`convex.exists_nhds_within_lipschitz_on_with_of_has_fderiv_within_at_of_nnnorm_lt` for a version\nwith an explicit estimate on the Lipschitz constant.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.lipschitz_on_with_of_nnnorm_has_fderiv_within_le", "description": " The mean value theorem on a convex set: if the derivative of a function is bounded by `C` on\n`s`, then the function is `C`-Lipschitz on `s`. Version with `has_fderiv_within` and\n`lipschitz_on_with`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "image_norm_le_of_norm_deriv_right_le_deriv_boundary", "description": " General fencing theorem for continuous functions with an estimate on the norm of the derivative.\nLet `f` and `B` be continuous functions on `[a, b]` such that\n\n* `\u2016f a\u2016 \u2264 B a`;\n* `f` has right derivative `f'` at every point of `[a, b)`;\n* `B` has derivative `B'` everywhere on `\u211d`;\n* we have `\u2016f' x\u2016 \u2264 B x` everywhere on `[a, b)`.\n\nThen `\u2016f x\u2016 \u2264 B x` everywhere on `[a, b]`. We use one-sided derivatives in the assumptions\nto make this theorem work for piecewise differentiable functions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_on_of_deriv2_nonneg", "description": " If a function `f` is continuous on a convex set `D \u2286 \u211d`, is twice differentiable on its\ninterior, and `f''` is nonnegative on the interior, then `f` is convex on `D`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_anti_on.strict_concave_on_of_deriv", "description": " If a function `f` is continuous on a convex set `D \u2286 \u211d` and `f'` is strictly antitone on the\ninterior, then `f` is strictly concave on `D`.\nNote that we don't require differentiability, since it is guaranteed at all but at most\none point by the strict antitonicity of `f'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.monotone_on_of_deriv_nonneg", "description": " Let `f` be a function continuous on a convex (or, equivalently, connected) subset `D`\nof the real line. If `f` is differentiable on the interior of `D` and `f'` is nonnegative, then\n`f` is a monotone function on `D`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.norm_image_sub_le_of_norm_fderiv_within_le", "description": " The mean value theorem on a convex set: if the derivative of a function within this set is\nbounded by `C`, then the function is `C`-Lipschitz. Version with `fderiv_within`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.lipschitz_on_with_of_nnnorm_deriv_le", "description": " The mean value theorem on a convex set in dimension 1: if the derivative of a function is\nbounded by `C` on `s`, then the function is `C`-Lipschitz on `s`.\nVersion with `deriv` and `lipschitz_on_with`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.norm_image_sub_le_of_norm_fderiv_within_le'", "description": "Variant of the mean value inequality on a convex set. Version with `fderiv_within`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.norm_image_sub_le_of_norm_has_fderiv_within_le", "description": " The mean value theorem on a convex set: if the derivative of a function is bounded by `C`, then\nthe function is `C`-Lipschitz. Version with `has_fderiv_within`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "image_le_of_liminf_slope_right_lt_deriv_boundary'", "description": " General fencing theorem for continuous functions with an estimate on the derivative.\nLet `f` and `B` be continuous functions on `[a, b]` such that\n\n* `f a \u2264 B a`;\n* `B` has right derivative `B'` at every point of `[a, b)`;\n* for each `x \u2208 [a, b)` the right-side limit inferior of `(f z - f x) / (z - x)`\n  is bounded above by a function `f'`;\n* we have `f' x < B' x` whenever `f x = B x`.\n\nThen `f x \u2264 B x` everywhere on `[a, b]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_ratio_deriv_eq_ratio_slope'", "description": "Cauchy's Mean Value Theorem, extended `deriv` version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_of_deriv_within_eq", "description": "If two differentiable functions on `[a, b]` have the same derivative within `[a, b]` everywhere\n on `[a, b)` and are equal at `a`, then they are equal everywhere on `[a, b]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_sub_le_image_sub_of_le_deriv", "description": " Let `f : \u211d \u2192 \u211d` be a differentiable function. If `C \u2264 f'`, then `f` grows at least as fast\nas `C * x`, i.e., `C * (y - x) \u2264 f y - f x` whenever `x \u2264 y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.lipschitz_on_with_of_nnnorm_has_deriv_within_le", "description": " The mean value theorem on a convex set in dimension 1: if the derivative of a function is\nbounded by `C` on `s`, then the function is `C`-Lipschitz on `s`.\nVersion with `has_deriv_within` and `lipschitz_on_with`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_mono.strict_convex_on_univ_of_deriv", "description": " If a function `f` is continuous and `f'` is strictly monotone on `\u211d` then `f` is strictly\nconvex. Note that we don't require differentiability, since it is guaranteed at all but at most\none point by the strict monotonicity of `f'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.is_const_of_fderiv_within_eq_zero", "description": " If a function has zero Fr\u00e9chet derivative at every point of a convex set,\nthen it is a constant on this set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "image_sub_lt_mul_sub_of_deriv_lt", "description": " Let `f : \u211d \u2192 \u211d` be a differentiable function. If `f' < C`, then `f` grows slower than\n`C * x` on `D`, i.e., `f y - f x < C * (y - x)` whenever `x < y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_on_univ_of_deriv2_nonneg", "description": " If a function `f` is twice differentiable on `\u211d`, and `f''` is nonnegative on `\u211d`,\nthen `f` is convex on `\u211d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_convex_on_of_deriv2_pos", "description": " If a function `f` is continuous on a convex set `D \u2286 \u211d` and `f''` is strictly positive on the\ninterior, then `f` is strictly convex on `D`.\nNote that we don't require twice differentiability explicitly as it is already implied by the second\nderivative being strictly positive, except at at most one point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "image_norm_le_of_norm_deriv_right_le_deriv_boundary'", "description": " General fencing theorem for continuous functions with an estimate on the norm of the derivative.\nLet `f` and `B` be continuous functions on `[a, b]` such that\n\n* `\u2016f a\u2016 \u2264 B a`;\n* `f` and `B` have right derivatives `f'` and `B'` respectively at every point of `[a, b)`;\n* we have `\u2016f' x\u2016 \u2264 B x` everywhere on `[a, b)`.\n\nThen `\u2016f x\u2016 \u2264 B x` everywhere on `[a, b]`. We use one-sided derivatives in the assumptions\nto make this theorem work for piecewise differentiable functions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_mono_on.strict_convex_on_of_deriv", "description": " If a function `f` is continuous on a convex set `D \u2286 \u211d`, and `f'` is strictly monotone on the\ninterior, then `f` is strictly convex on `D`.\nNote that we don't require differentiability, since it is guaranteed at all but at most\none point by the strict monotonicity of `f'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone_of_deriv_nonpos", "description": " Let `f : \u211d \u2192 \u211d` be a differentiable function. If `f'` is nonpositive, then\n`f` is an antitone function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_mono_on.exists_deriv_lt_slope_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.norm_image_sub_le_of_norm_deriv_within_le", "description": " The mean value theorem on a convex set in dimension 1: if the derivative of a function within\nthis set is bounded by `C`, then the function is `C`-Lipschitz. Version with `deriv_within`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "image_le_of_deriv_right_lt_deriv_boundary'", "description": " General fencing theorem for continuous functions with an estimate on the derivative.\nLet `f` and `B` be continuous functions on `[a, b]` such that\n\n* `f a \u2264 B a`;\n* `B` has right derivative `B'` at every point of `[a, b)`;\n* `f` has right derivative `f'` at every point of `[a, b)`;\n* we have `f' x < B' x` whenever `f x = B x`.\n\nThen `f x \u2264 B x` everywhere on `[a, b]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_has_deriv_at_eq_slope", "description": "Lagrange's Mean Value Theorem, `has_deriv_at` version", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_sub_lt_image_sub_of_lt_deriv", "description": " Let `f : \u211d \u2192 \u211d` be a differentiable function. If `C < f'`, then `f` grows faster than\n`C * x`, i.e., `C * (y - x) < f y - f x` whenever `x < y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "image_le_of_deriv_right_le_deriv_boundary", "description": " General fencing theorem for continuous functions with an estimate on the derivative.\nLet `f` and `B` be continuous functions on `[a, b]` such that\n\n* `f a \u2264 B a`;\n* `B` has derivative `B'` everywhere on `\u211d`;\n* `f` has right derivative `f'` at every point of `[a, b)`;\n* we have `f' x \u2264 B' x` on `[a, b)`.\n\nThen `f x \u2264 B x` everywhere on `[a, b]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "image_le_of_deriv_right_lt_deriv_boundary", "description": " General fencing theorem for continuous functions with an estimate on the derivative.\nLet `f` and `B` be continuous functions on `[a, b]` such that\n\n* `f a \u2264 B a`;\n* `B` has derivative `B'` everywhere on `\u211d`;\n* `f` has right derivative `f'` at every point of `[a, b)`;\n* we have `f' x < B' x` whenever `f x = B x`.\n\nThen `f x \u2264 B x` everywhere on `[a, b]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.norm_image_sub_le_of_norm_fderiv_le", "description": " The mean value theorem on a convex set: if the derivative of a function is bounded by `C`,\nthen the function is `C`-Lipschitz. Version with `fderiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone.convex_on_univ_of_deriv", "description": "If a function `f` is differentiable and `f'` is monotone on `\u211d` then `f` is convex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_image_sub_le_of_norm_deriv_le_segment_01'", "description": " A function on `[0, 1]` with the norm of the derivative within `[0, 1]`\nbounded by `C` satisfies `\u2016f 1 - f 0\u2016 \u2264 C`, `has_deriv_within_at`\nversion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "image_norm_le_of_norm_deriv_right_lt_deriv_boundary", "description": " General fencing theorem for continuous functions with an estimate on the norm of the derivative.\nLet `f` and `B` be continuous functions on `[a, b]` such that\n\n* `\u2016f a\u2016 \u2264 B a`;\n* `f` has right derivative `f'` at every point of `[a, b)`;\n* `B` has derivative `B'` everywhere on `\u211d`;\n* the norm of `f'` is strictly less than `B'` whenever `\u2016f x\u2016 = B x`.\n\nThen `\u2016f x\u2016 \u2264 B x` everywhere on `[a, b]`. We use one-sided derivatives in the assumptions\nto make this theorem work for piecewise differentiable functions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.norm_image_sub_le_of_norm_fderiv_le'", "description": "Variant of the mean value inequality on a convex set. Version with `fderiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "image_norm_le_of_norm_deriv_right_lt_deriv_boundary'", "description": " General fencing theorem for continuous functions with an estimate on the norm of the derivative.\nLet `f` and `B` be continuous functions on `[a, b]` such that\n\n* `\u2016f a\u2016 \u2264 B a`;\n* `f` and `B` have right derivatives `f'` and `B'` respectively at every point of `[a, b)`;\n* the norm of `f'` is strictly less than `B'` whenever `\u2016f x\u2016 = B x`.\n\nThen `\u2016f x\u2016 \u2264 B x` everywhere on `[a, b]`. We use one-sided derivatives in the assumptions\nto make this theorem work for piecewise differentiable functions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "concave_on_of_deriv2_nonpos'", "description": " If a function `f` is twice differentiable on an open convex set `D \u2286 \u211d` and\n`f''` is nonpositive on `D`, then `f` is concave on `D`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.norm_image_sub_le_of_norm_has_deriv_within_le", "description": " The mean value theorem on a convex set in dimension 1: if the derivative of a function is\nbounded by `C`, then the function is `C`-Lipschitz. Version with `has_deriv_within`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.image_sub_lt_mul_sub_of_deriv_lt", "description": " Let `f` be a function continuous on a convex (or, equivalently, connected) subset `D`\nof the real line. If `f` is differentiable on the interior of `D` and `f' < C`, then\n`f` grows slower than `C * x` on `D`, i.e., `f y - f x < C * (y - x)` whenever `x, y \u2208 D`,\n`x < y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_mono_on.exists_slope_lt_deriv_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.lipschitz_on_with_of_nnnorm_fderiv_le", "description": " The mean value theorem on a convex set: if the derivative of a function is bounded by `C` on\n`s`, then the function is `C`-Lipschitz on `s`. Version with `fderiv` and `lipschitz_on_with`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.strict_mono_on_of_deriv_pos", "description": " Let `f` be a function continuous on a convex (or, equivalently, connected) subset `D`\nof the real line. If `f` is differentiable on the interior of `D` and `f'` is positive, then\n`f` is a strictly monotone function on `D`.\nNote that we don't require differentiability explicitly as it already implied by the derivative\nbeing strictly positive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone_of_deriv_nonneg", "description": " Let `f : \u211d \u2192 \u211d` be a differentiable function. If `f'` is nonnegative, then\n`f` is a monotone function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.antitone_on_of_deriv_nonpos", "description": " Let `f` be a function continuous on a convex (or, equivalently, connected) subset `D`\nof the real line. If `f` is differentiable on the interior of `D` and `f'` is nonpositive, then\n`f` is an antitone function on `D`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone_on.convex_on_of_deriv", "description": " If a function `f` is continuous on a convex set `D \u2286 \u211d`, is differentiable on its interior,\nand `f'` is monotone on the interior, then `f` is convex on `D`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_image_sub_le_of_norm_deriv_right_le_segment", "description": " A function on `[a, b]` with the norm of the right derivative bounded by `C`\nsatisfies `\u2016f x - f a\u2016 \u2264 C * (x - a)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "concave_on_of_deriv2_nonpos", "description": " If a function `f` is continuous on a convex set `D \u2286 \u211d`, is twice differentiable on its\ninterior, and `f''` is nonpositive on the interior, then `f` is concave on `D`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone.concave_on_univ_of_deriv", "description": "If a function `f` is differentiable and `f'` is antitone on `\u211d` then `f` is concave.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_concave_on_of_deriv2_neg", "description": " If a function `f` is continuous on a convex set `D \u2286 \u211d` and `f''` is strictly negative on the\ninterior, then `f` is strictly concave on `D`.\nNote that we don't require twice differentiability explicitly as it already implied by the second\nderivative being strictly negative, except at at most one point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at_of_has_fderiv_at_of_continuous_at", "description": " Over the reals or the complexes, a continuously differentiable function is strictly\ndifferentiable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_image_sub_le_of_norm_deriv_le_segment'", "description": " A function on `[a, b]` with the norm of the derivative within `[a, b]`\nbounded by `C` satisfies `\u2016f x - f a\u2016 \u2264 C * (x - a)`, `has_deriv_within_at`\nversion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.norm_image_sub_le_of_norm_has_fderiv_within_le'", "description": " Variant of the mean value inequality on a convex set, using a bound on the difference between\nthe derivative and a fixed linear map, rather than a bound on the derivative itself. Version with\n`has_fderiv_within`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_convex_on_univ_of_deriv2_pos", "description": " If a function `f` is continuous on `\u211d`, and `f''` is strictly positive on `\u211d`,\nthen `f` is strictly convex on `\u211d`.\nNote that we don't require twice differentiability explicitly as it is already implied by the second\nderivative being strictly positive, except at at most one point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone_on.concave_on_of_deriv", "description": " If a function `f` is continuous on a convex set `D \u2286 \u211d`, is differentiable on its interior,\nand `f'` is antitone on the interior, then `f` is concave on `D`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.image_sub_le_mul_sub_of_deriv_le", "description": " Let `f` be a function continuous on a convex (or, equivalently, connected) subset `D`\nof the real line. If `f` is differentiable on the interior of `D` and `f' \u2264 C`, then\n`f` grows at most as fast as `C * x` on `D`, i.e., `f y - f x \u2264 C * (y - x)` whenever `x, y \u2208 D`,\n`x \u2264 y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_of_has_deriv_right_eq", "description": "If two continuous functions on `[a, b]` have the same right derivative and are equal at `a`,\n then they are equal everywhere on `[a, b]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_mono_on.exists_slope_lt_deriv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "concave_on_univ_of_deriv2_nonpos", "description": " If a function `f` is twice differentiable on `\u211d`, and `f''` is nonpositive on `\u211d`,\nthen `f` is concave on `\u211d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_ratio_deriv_eq_ratio_slope", "description": "Cauchy's Mean Value Theorem, `deriv` version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.lipschitz_on_with_of_nnnorm_fderiv_within_le", "description": " The mean value theorem on a convex set: if the derivative of a function is bounded by `C` on\n`s`, then the function is `C`-Lipschitz on `s`. Version with `fderiv_within` and\n`lipschitz_on_with`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_concave_on_of_deriv2_neg'", "description": " If a function `f` is continuous on a convex set `D \u2286 \u211d` and `f''` is strictly negative on `D`,\nthen `f` is strictly concave on `D`.\nNote that we don't require twice differentiability explicitly as it is already implied by the second\nderivative being strictly negative, except at at most one point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_deriv_eq_slope", "description": "Lagrange's **Mean Value Theorem**, `deriv` version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_ratio_has_deriv_at_eq_ratio_slope", "description": "Cauchy's **Mean Value Theorem**, `has_deriv_at` version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.dirac_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.skew_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.prod_prod", "description": " The product measure of the product of two sets is the product of their measures. Note that we\ndo not need the sets to be measurable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.absolutely_continuous.prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.snd_map_prod_mk\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.quasi_measure_preserving.prod_of_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.prod_assoc_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.fst_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generate_from_prod", "description": "The product \u03c3-algebra is generated from boxes, i.e. `s \u00d7\u02e2 t` for sets `s : set \u03b1` and\n `t : set \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.measure_preserving_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.lintegral_lintegral_symm", "description": " The reversed version of **Tonelli's Theorem** (symmetric version). In this version `f` is in\ncurried form, which makes it easier for the elaborator to figure out `f` automatically.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.fst.measure_theory.is_finite_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generate_from_prod_eq", "description": "The product of generated \u03c3-algebras is the one generated by rectangles, if both generating sets\n are countably spanning.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.prod.measure_theory.is_finite_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.quasi_measure_preserving_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.fst_map_prod_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_countably_spanning.prod", "description": "Rectangles of countably spanning sets are countably spanning.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.lintegral_prod_left'", "description": "The Lebesgue integral is measurable. This shows that the integrand of (the right-hand-side of)\n the symmetric version of Tonelli's theorem is measurable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.map_prod_mk_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.add_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.prod_dirac", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.measure_ae_null_of_prod_null", "description": "Note: the converse is not true without assuming that `s` is measurable. For a counterexample,\n see Walter Rudin *Real and Complex Analysis*, example (c) in section 8.9.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.fst_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.fst.measure_theory.is_probability_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.dirac_prod_dirac", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.map_prod_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.prod.is_open_pos_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.lintegral_prod_of_measurable", "description": "**Tonelli's Theorem**: For `\u211d\u22650\u221e`-valued measurable functions on `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2`,\n the integral of `f` is equal to the iterated integral.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.sum_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.prod.measure_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.fst", "description": "Marginal measure on `\u03b1` obtained from a measure `\u03c1` on `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2`, defined by `\u03c1.map prod.fst`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.prod.measure_theory.is_probability_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.prod_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.lintegral_prod_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.lintegral_prod_right'", "description": "The Lebesgue integral is measurable. This shows that the integrand of (the right-hand-side of)\n Tonelli's theorem is measurable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.snd", "description": "Marginal measure on `\u03b2` obtained from a measure on `\u03c1` `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2`, defined by `\u03c1.map prod.snd`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.ae_measure_lt_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_pi_system.prod", "description": "Rectangles formed by \u03c0-systems form a \u03c0-system.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.prod_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.restrict_prod_eq_prod_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.quasi_measure_preserving.prod_of_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_measure_prod_mk_right", "description": "If `\u03bc` is a \u03c3-finite measure, and `s \u2286 \u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2` is measurable, then `y \u21a6 \u03bc { x | (x, y) \u2208 s }` is\n a measurable function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.prod_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_embedding.prod_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_measure_prod_mk_left_finite", "description": "If `\u03bd` is a finite measure, and `s \u2286 \u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2` is measurable, then `x \u21a6 \u03bd { y | (x, y) \u2208 s }` is\n a measurable function. `measurable_measure_prod_mk_left` is strictly more general.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.lintegral_prod", "description": "**Tonelli's Theorem**: For `\u211d\u22650\u221e`-valued almost everywhere measurable functions on `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2`,\n the integral of `f` is equal to the iterated integral.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.prod", "description": " If `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` sends the measure `\u03bca` to `\u03bcb` and `g : \u03b3 \u2192 \u03b4` sends the measure `\u03bcc` to `\u03bcd`,\nthen `prod.map f g` sends `\u03bca.prod \u03bcc` to `\u03bcb.prod \u03bcd`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.map_prod_mk_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.snd_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.snd.measure_theory.is_finite_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.finite_spanning_sets_in.prod", "description": "`\u03bc.prod \u03bd` has finite spanning sets in rectangles of finite spanning sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.volume_eq_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.prod_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.lintegral_prod_left", "description": "The Lebesgue integral is measurable. This shows that the integrand of (the right-hand-side of)\n the symmetric version of Tonelli's theorem is measurable.\n This version has the argument `f` in curried form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_measurable.snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.prod.sigma_finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.prod_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_measure_prod_mk_left", "description": "If `\u03bd` is a \u03c3-finite measure, and `s \u2286 \u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2` is measurable, then `x \u21a6 \u03bd { y | (x, y) \u2208 s }` is\n a measurable function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.snd_map_prod_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.lintegral_prod_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.prod_apply_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.lintegral_prod_symm'", "description": " The symmetric verion of Tonelli's Theorem: For `\u211d\u22650\u221e`-valued measurable\nfunctions on `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2`,  the integral of `f` is equal to the iterated integral, in reverse order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.quasi_measure_preserving_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.prod.measure_theory.is_finite_measure_on_compacts", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.lintegral_lintegral", "description": " The reversed version of **Tonelli's Theorem**. In this version `f` is in curried form, which\nmakes it easier for the elaborator to figure out `f` automatically.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.lintegral_lintegral_swap", "description": "Change the order of Lebesgue integration.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.measure_prod_null", "description": "Note: the assumption `hs` cannot be dropped. For a counterexample, see\n Walter Rudin *Real and Complex Analysis*, example (c) in section 8.9.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.snd.measure_theory.is_probability_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generate_from_eq_prod", "description": "If `C` and `D` generate the \u03c3-algebras on `\u03b1` resp. `\u03b2`, then rectangles formed by `C` and `D`\n generate the \u03c3-algebra on `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.ae_ae_of_ae_prod", "description": "Note: the converse is not true. For a counterexample, see\n Walter Rudin *Real and Complex Analysis*, example (c) in section 8.9.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_pi_system_prod", "description": "Rectangles form a \u03c0-system.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_measurable.fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.prod_restrict", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.fst_map_prod_mk\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_measurable.prod_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.lintegral_prod_symm", "description": " The symmetric verion of Tonelli's Theorem: For `\u211d\u22650\u221e`-valued almost everywhere measurable\nfunctions on `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2`,  the integral of `f` is equal to the iterated integral, in reverse order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.snd_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.prod_eq_generate_from", "description": "A measure on a product space equals the product measure if they are equal on rectangles\n with as sides sets that generate the corresponding \u03c3-algebras.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.zero_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.prod_eq", "description": "A measure on a product space equals the product measure if they are equal on rectangles.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.lintegral_prod_right", "description": "The Lebesgue integral is measurable. This shows that the integrand of (the right-hand-side of)\n Tonelli's theorem is measurable.\n This version has the argument `f` in curried form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.prod", "description": "The binary product of measures. They are defined for arbitrary measures, but we basically\n prove all properties under the assumption that at least one of them is \u03c3-finite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "max_add_add_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "min_add_max", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "min_le_add_of_nonneg_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fn_min_mul_fn_max", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_lt_mul_iff_of_le_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "min_mul_mul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_or_lt_of_mul_le_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "min_le_add_of_nonneg_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "min_le_mul_of_one_le_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "min_add_add_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "min_le_mul_of_one_le_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "min_add_add_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_or_le_of_mul_le_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "min_mul_mul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "max_mul_mul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_or_le_of_add_le_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "min_mul_max", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_or_lt_of_add_lt_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_or_lt_of_mul_lt_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "max_le_mul_of_one_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_or_lt_of_add_le_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "max_mul_mul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "max_add_add_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_or_le_of_mul_le_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "max_le_add_of_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fn_min_add_fn_max", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_lt_add_iff_of_le_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_or_le_of_add_le_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.Union_lift_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.Union_lift_inclusion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.lift_cover_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.Union_lift_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.Union_lift_binary", "description": "`Union_lift_binary` is useful for proving that `Union_lift` is a homomorphism\n of algebraic structures when defined on the Union of algebraic subobjects.\n For example, it could be used to prove that the lift of a collection\n of group homomorphisms on a union of subgroups preserves `*`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.lift_cover", "description": "Glue together functions defined on each of a collection `S` of sets that cover a type. See\n also `set.Union_lift`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.Union_lift_unary", "description": "`Union_lift_unary` is useful for proving that `Union_lift` is a homomorphism\n of algebraic structures when defined on the Union of algebraic subobjects.\n For example, it could be used to prove that the lift of a collection\n of linear_maps on a union of submodules preserves scalar multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.lift_cover_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.Union_lift", "description": " Given a Union of sets `Union S`, define a function on the Union by defining\nit on each component, and proving that it agrees on the intersections.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.Union_lift_const", "description": "`Union_lift_const` is useful for proving that `Union_lift` is a homomorphism\n of algebraic structures when defined on the Union of algebraic subobjects.\n For example, it could be used to prove that the lift of a collection\n of group homomorphisms on a union of subgroups preserves `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.nonarchimedean", "description": " The `p`-adic norm is nonarchimedean: the norm of `p + q` is at most the max of the norm of `p`\nand the norm of `q`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.sum_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.sub", "description": " The `p`-adic norm of a difference is at most the max of each component. Restates the archimedean\nproperty of the `p`-adic norm.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.sum_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.zero_of_padic_norm_eq_zero", "description": "If the `p`-adic norm of `q` is 0, then `q` is `0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm", "description": " If `q \u2260 0`, the `p`-adic norm of a rational `q` is `p ^ -padic_val_rat p q`.\nIf `q = 0`, the `p`-adic norm of `q` is `0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.eq_zpow_of_nonzero", "description": "Unfolds the definition of the `p`-adic norm of `q` when `q \u2260 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.values_discrete", "description": "`padic_norm p q` takes discrete values `p ^ -z` for `z : \u2124`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.mul", "description": "The `p`-adic norm is multiplicative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.padic_norm_p", "description": " The `p`-adic norm of `p` is `p\u207b\u00b9` if `p > 1`.\n\nSee also `padic_norm.padic_norm_p_of_prime` for a version assuming `p` is prime.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.div", "description": "The `p`-adic norm respects division.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.nat_lt_one_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.nonneg", "description": "The `p`-adic norm is nonnegative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.of_int", "description": "The `p`-adic norm of an integer is at most `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.nonzero", "description": "If `q \u2260 0`, then `padic_norm p q \u2260 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.neg", "description": "`padic_norm p` is symmetric.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.padic_norm_p_lt_one_of_prime", "description": " The `p`-adic norm of `p` is less than `1` if `p` is prime.\n\nSee also `padic_norm.padic_norm_p_lt_one` for a version assuming `1 < p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.dvd_iff_norm_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.is_absolute_value", "description": " The `p`-adic norm is an absolute value: positive-definite and multiplicative, satisfying the\ntriangle inequality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.padic_norm_of_prime_of_ne", "description": "The `p`-adic norm of `q` is `1` if `q` is prime and not equal to `p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.sum_lt'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.padic_norm_p_of_prime", "description": " The `p`-adic norm of `p` is `p\u207b\u00b9` if `p` is prime.\n\nSee also `padic_norm.padic_norm_p` for a version assuming `1 < p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.sum_le'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.one", "description": "The `p`-adic norm of `1` is `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.triangle_ineq", "description": " The `p`-adic norm respects the triangle inequality: the norm of `p + q` is at most the norm of\n`p` plus the norm of `q`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.zero", "description": "The `p`-adic norm of `0` is `0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.of_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.padic_norm_p_lt_one", "description": " The `p`-adic norm of `p` is less than `1` if `1 < p`.\n\nSee also `padic_norm.padic_norm_p_lt_one_of_prime` for a version assuming `p` is prime.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.nat_eq_one_iff", "description": "The `p`-adic norm of a natural `m` is one iff `p` doesn't divide `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.add_eq_max_of_ne", "description": " If the `p`-adic norms of `q` and `r` are different, then the norm of `q + r` is equal to the max\nof the norms of `q` and `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.int_eq_one_iff", "description": "The `p`-adic norm of an integer `m` is one iff `p` doesn't divide `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_norm.int_lt_one_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.sub_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.restrict_sub_eq_restrict_sub_restrict", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.sub_apply_eq_zero_of_restrict_le_restrict", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.zero_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.is_finite_measure_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.sub_le_of_le_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.sub_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.sub_apply", "description": " This application lemma only works in special circumstances. Given knowledge of\nwhen `\u03bc \u2264 \u03bd` and `\u03bd \u2264 \u03bc`, a more general application lemma can be written.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.has_sub", "description": " The measure `\u03bc - \u03bd` is defined to be the least measure `\u03c4` such that `\u03bc \u2264 \u03c4 + \u03bd`.\nIt is the equivalent of `(\u03bc - \u03bd) \u2294 0` if `\u03bc` and `\u03bd` were signed measures.\nCompare with `ennreal.has_sub`.\nSpecifically, note that if you have `\u03b1 = {1,2}`, and  `\u03bc {1} = 2`, `\u03bc {2} = 0`, and\n`\u03bd {2} = 2`, `\u03bd {1} = 0`, then `(\u03bc - \u03bd) {1, 2} = 2`. However, if `\u03bc \u2264 \u03bd`, and\n`\u03bd univ \u2260 \u221e`, then `(\u03bc - \u03bd) + \u03bd = \u03bc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.sub_eq_zero_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.sub_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.sub_add_cancel_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.sub_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup.forget\u2082.creates_limit", "description": "We show that the forgetful functor `CommGroup \u2964 Group` creates limits.\n\nAll we need to do is notice that the limit point has a `comm_group` instance available,\nand then reuse the existing limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.limit_cone_is_limit", "description": "The chosen cone is a limit cone.\n(Generally, you'll just want to use `limit.cone F`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddGroup.forget_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddGroup.forget\u2082_Mon_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.forget\u2082_Mon_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": " The forgetful functor from groups to monoids preserves all limits.\n\nThis means the underlying monoid of a limit can be computed as a limit in the category of monoids.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddGroup.has_limits_of_size", "description": "The category of additive groups has all limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddGroup.forget\u2082_AddMon_preserves_limits", "description": "The forgetful functor from additive groups\nto additive monoids preserves all limits.\n\nThis means the underlying additive monoid of a limit can be computed as a limit in the category of\nadditive monoids.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.limit_cone_is_limit", "description": "The chosen cone is a limit cone.\n(Generally, you'll just wantto use `limit.cone F`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.forget_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.kernel_iso_ker_over", "description": "The categorical kernel inclusion for `f : G \u27f6 H`, as an object over `G`,\nagrees with the `subtype` map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.limit_add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.kernel_iso_ker_over_inv_left_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddGroup.limit_cone", "description": "A choice of limit cone for a functor into `Group`.\n(Generally, you'll just want to use `limit F`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup.has_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.has_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.kernel_iso_ker_inv_comp_\u03b9", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup.forget\u2082_CommMon_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": "The forgetful functor from commutative groups to commutative monoids preserves all limits.\n(That is, the underlying commutative monoids could have been computed instead as limits\nin the category of commutative monoids.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.forget\u2082_AddCommMon_preserves_limits", "description": "The forgetful functor from additive commutative groups to additive commutative monoids preserves\nall limits. (That is, the underlying additive commutative monoids could have been computed instead\nas limits in the category of additive commutative monoids.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddGroup.forget\u2082.creates_limit", "description": "We show that the forgetful functor `AddGroup \u2964 AddMon` creates limits.\n\nAll we need to do is notice that the limit point has an `add_group` instance available, and then\nreuse the existing limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.add_comm_group_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.kernel_iso_ker", "description": "The categorical kernel of a morphism in `AddCommGroup`\nagrees with the usual group-theoretical kernel.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.has_limits_of_size", "description": "The category of additive commutative groups has all limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup.has_limits_of_size", "description": "The category of commutative groups has all limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.kernel_iso_ker_over_hom_right_down_down", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup.limit_cone", "description": "A choice of limit cone for a functor into `CommGroup`.\n(Generally, you'll just want to use `limit F`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.forget\u2082_AddGroup_preserves_limits", "description": "The forgetful functor from additive commutative groups to groups preserves all limits.\n(That is, the underlying group could have been computed instead as limits in the category\nof additive groups.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup.forget\u2082_Group_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": "The forgetful functor from commutative groups to groups preserves all limits.\n(That is, the underlying group could have been computed instead as limits in the category\nof groups.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.forget\u2082_Group_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddGroup.has_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup.limit_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.forget\u2082_AddCommMon_preserves_limits_aux", "description": "An auxiliary declaration to speed up typechecking.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddGroup.limit_cone_is_limit", "description": "The chosen cone is a limit cone.\n(Generally, you'll just want to use `limit.cone F`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.has_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.limit_cone", "description": "A choice of limit cone for a functor into `Group`.\n(Generally, you'll just want to use `limit F`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddGroup.forget_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": "The forgetful functor from additive groups to types preserves all limits.\n\nThis means the underlying type of a limit can be computed as a limit in the category of types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.forget\u2082.creates_limit", "description": "We show that the forgetful functor `AddCommGroup \u2964 AddGroup` creates limits.\n\nAll we need to do is notice that the limit point has an `add_comm_group` instance available, and\nthen reuse the existing limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup.forget\u2082_Group_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddGroup.add_group_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.forget_preserves_limits", "description": "The forgetful functor from additive commutative groups to types preserves all limits. (That is,\nthe underlying types could have been computed instead as limits in the category of types.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddGroup.limit_add_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.limit_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.kernel_iso_ker_over_inv_right_down_down", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.forget\u2082_Mon_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.sections_subgroup", "description": "The flat sections of a functor into `Group` form a subgroup of all sections.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup.limit_cone_is_limit", "description": "The chosen cone is a limit cone.\n(Generally, you'll just want to use `limit.cone F`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.forget\u2082.creates_limit", "description": " We show that the forgetful functor `Group \u2964 Mon` creates limits.\n\nAll we need to do is notice that the limit point has a `group` instance available, and then reuse\nthe existing limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.limit_cone", "description": "A choice of limit cone for a functor into `CommGroup`.\n(Generally, you'll just want to use `limit F`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddGroup.sections_add_subgroup", "description": "The flat sections of a functor into `AddGroup` form an additive subgroup of all sections.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup.comm_group_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup.forget\u2082_CommMon_preserves_limits_aux", "description": "An auxiliary declaration to speed up typechecking.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.kernel_iso_ker_over_hom_left_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.kernel_iso_ker_hom_comp_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.has_limits_of_size", "description": "The category of groups has all limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.forget_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": " The forgetful functor from groups to types preserves all limits.\n\nThis means the underlying type of a limit can be computed as a limit in the category of types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup.forget_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": "The forgetful functor from commutative groups to types preserves all limits. (That is, the\nunderlying types could have been computed instead as limits in the category of types.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.group_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.left_neg_equiv_symm_from_left_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.left_neg", "description": "`S.left_neg` is the additive submonoid containing all the left additive inverses\nof `S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.left_inv_equiv", "description": "The submonoid of pointwise inverse of `S` is `mul_equiv` to `S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.is_unit.submonoid.group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.left_neg_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.add_from_left_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.from_left_inv_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.is_unit.submonoid.coe_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.left_inv_left_inv_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.is_unit.submonoid.coe_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.add_left_neg_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.left_inv_equiv_symm_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.add_left_neg_equiv_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.is_unit.submonoid.comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.from_left_neg_eq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.from_left_inv_eq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.mul_left_inv_equiv_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.from_left_neg", "description": "The function from `S.left_add` to `S` sending an element to its right additive\ninverse in `S`. This is an `add_monoid_hom` when `M` is commutative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.left_inv_eq_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.from_comm_left_neg_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.left_neg_equiv", "description": "The additive submonoid of pointwise additive inverse of `S` is\n`add_equiv` to `S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.from_left_neg_eq_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.left_inv_equiv_symm_eq_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.left_inv_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.is_unit.submonoid.add_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.mul_from_left_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.left_neg_equiv_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.left_neg_left_neg_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.from_left_inv", "description": " The function from `S.left_inv` to `S` sending an element to its right inverse in `S`.\nThis is a `monoid_hom` when `M` is commutative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.from_left_neg_left_neg_equiv_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.from_comm_left_neg", "description": "The `add_monoid_hom` from `S.left_neg` to `S` sending an element to its\nright additive inverse in `S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.is_unit.submonoid.add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.from_left_neg_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.from_lef