"wbtw.symm", "description": "**Alias** of the forward direction of `wbtw_comm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "wbtw.left_mem_affine_span_of_right_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "wbtw_vsub_const_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.trans_wbtw_right_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "wbtw_or_wbtw_smul_vadd_of_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw_iff_right_ne_and_left_mem_image_IoI", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw_line_map_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.trans_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.right_mem_image_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "wbtw.left_ne_right_of_ne_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "wbtw.rotate_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.left_mem_affine_span", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.left_ne_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.ne_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "wbtw_self_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "wbtw_const_vsub_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw_vadd_const_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "wbtw.left_mem_image_Ici_of_right_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw_mul_sub_add_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "wbtw_swap_right_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.left_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.rcons", "description": " Given a pair `(head, tail)`, we can form a word by prepending `head` to `tail`, except if `head`\nis `1 : M i` then we have to just return `word` since we need the result to be reduced.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.range_eq_supr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.of_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.inv_last", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.to_list_ne_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.to_list_head'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.to_list_last'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.is_free_group_mul_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword", "description": " A `neword M i j` is a representation of a non-empty reduced words where the first letter comes\nfrom `M i` and the last letter comes from `M j`. It can be constructed from singletons and via\nconcatentation, and thus provides a useful induction principle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.singleton", "description": " A `neword M i j` is a representation of a non-empty reduced words where the first letter comes\nfrom `M i` and the last letter comes from `M j`. It can be constructed from singletons and via\nconcatentation, and thus provides a useful induction principle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.append", "description": " A `neword M i j` is a representation of a non-empty reduced words where the first letter comes\nfrom `M i` and the last letter comes from `M j`. It can be constructed from singletons and via\nconcatentation, and thus provides a useful induction principle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.inv_head", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_group_equiv_free_product_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.lift_word_ping_pong", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.mul_head_head", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.lift_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.mrange_eq_supr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.equiv_pair_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.lift_word_prod_nontrivial_of_head_eq_last", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.inv_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.pair.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.free_product.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.mul_head_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.prod_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.to_list", "description": "The list represented by a given `neword`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.singleton_last", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.cons_eq_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.to_word", "description": "The `word M` represented by a `neword M i j`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_group.injective_lift_of_ping_pong", "description": "The Ping-Pong-Lemma.\n\nGiven a group action of `G` on `X` so that the generators of the free groups act in specific\nways on disjoint subsets `X i` and `Y i` we can prove that `lift f` is injective, and thus the image\nof `lift f` is isomorphic to the free group.\n\nOften the Ping-Pong-Lemma is stated with regard to group elements that generate the whole group;\nwe generalize to arbitrary group homomorphisms from the free group to `G`  and do not require the\ngroup to be generated by the elements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.rel", "description": " A relation on the free monoid on alphabet `\u03a3 i, M i`, relating `\u27e8i, 1\u27e9` with `1` and\n`\u27e8i, x\u27e9 * \u27e8i, y\u27e9` with `\u27e8i, x * y\u27e9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.rel.of_one", "description": " A relation on the free monoid on alphabet `\u03a3 i, M i`, relating `\u27e8i, 1\u27e9` with `1` and\n`\u27e8i, x\u27e9 * \u27e8i, y\u27e9` with `\u27e8i, x * y\u27e9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.rel.of_mul", "description": " A relation on the free monoid on alphabet `\u03a3 i, M i`, relating `\u27e8i, 1\u27e9` with `1` and\n`\u27e8i, x\u27e9 * \u27e8i, y\u27e9` with `\u27e8i, x * y\u27e9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.prod_rcons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.head", "description": "The first letter of a `neword`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.fst_idx_ne_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.has_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.of_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.is_free_group", "description": "The free product of free groups is itself a free group", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.summand_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.rcons_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.prod_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.lift_mrange_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.lift_word_prod_nontrivial_of_head_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.lift_injective_of_ping_pong", "description": "The Ping-Pong-Lemma.\n\nGiven a group action of `G` on `X` so that the `H i` acts in a specific way on disjoint subsets\n`X i` we can prove that `lift f` is injective, and thus the image of `lift f` is isomorphic to the\nfree product of the `H i`.\n\nOften the Ping-Pong-Lemma is stated with regard to subgroups `H i` that generate the whole group;\nwe generalize to arbitrary group homomorphisms `f i : H i \u2192* G` and do not require the group to be\ngenerated by the images.\n\nUsually the Ping-Pong-Lemma requires that one group `H i` has at least three elements. This\ncondition is only needed if `# \u03b9 = 2`, and we accept `3 \u2264 # \u03b9` as an alternative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.induction_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.lift", "description": " A map out of the free product corresponds to a family of maps out of the summands. This is the\nuniversal property of the free product, charaterizing it as a categorical coproduct.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.prod_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.mul_head", "description": " One can multiply an element from the left to a non-empty reduced word if it does not cancel\nwith the first element in the word.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_group_equiv_free_product_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.lift_word_prod_nontrivial_of_not_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.last", "description": "The last letter of a `neword`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.of", "description": "The inclusion of a summand into the free product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.empty_of_word_prod_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.fst_idx", "description": " `fst_idx w` is `some i` if the first letter of `w` is `\u27e8i, m\u27e9` with `m : M i`. If `w` is empty\nthen it's `none`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.prod", "description": "A reduced word determines an element of the free product, given by multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product", "description": "The free product (categorical coproduct) of an indexed family of monoids.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.append_last", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.replace_head", "description": " One can replace the first letter in a non-empty reduced word by an element of the same\ngroup", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.lift_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.of_word", "description": "Every nonempty `word M` can be constructed as a `neword M i j`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.pair.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.lift_range_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.append_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.pair", "description": " Given an index `i : \u03b9`, `pair M i` is the type of pairs `(head, tail)` where `head : M i` and\n`tail : word M`, subject to the constraint that first letter of `tail` can't be `\u27e8i, m\u27e9`.\nBy prepending `head` to `tail`, one obtains a new word. We'll show that any word can be uniquely\nobtained in this way.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.pair.head", "description": " Given an index `i : \u03b9`, `pair M i` is the type of pairs `(head, tail)` where `head : M i` and\n`tail : word M`, subject to the constraint that first letter of `tail` can't be `\u27e8i, m\u27e9`.\nBy prepending `head` to `tail`, one obtains a new word. We'll show that any word can be uniquely\nobtained in this way.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.pair.tail", "description": " Given an index `i : \u03b9`, `pair M i` is the type of pairs `(head, tail)` where `head : M i` and\n`tail : word M`, subject to the constraint that first letter of `tail` can't be `\u27e8i, m\u27e9`.\nBy prepending `head` to `tail`, one obtains a new word. We'll show that any word can be uniquely\nobtained in this way.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.pair.fst_idx_ne", "description": " Given an index `i : \u03b9`, `pair M i` is the type of pairs `(head, tail)` where `head : M i` and\n`tail : word M`, subject to the constraint that first letter of `tail` can't be `\u27e8i, m\u27e9`.\nBy prepending `head` to `tail`, one obtains a new word. We'll show that any word can be uniquely\nobtained in this way.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.pair.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.equiv", "description": "Each element of the free product corresponds to a unique reduced word.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.smul_induction", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.ext_hom", "description": "See note [partially-applied ext lemmas].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.of_left_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_group_equiv_free_product", "description": "A free group is a free product of copies of the free_group over one generator.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.of_smul_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.inv", "description": "The inverse of a non-empty reduced word", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.prod", "description": "A non-empty reduced word determines an element of the free product, given by multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.singleton_head", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.inv_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word", "description": " The type of reduced words. A reduced word cannot contain a letter `1`, and no two adjacent\nletters can come from the same summand.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.to_list", "description": " The type of reduced words. A reduced word cannot contain a letter `1`, and no two adjacent\nletters can come from the same summand.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.ne_one", "description": " The type of reduced words. A reduced word cannot contain a letter `1`, and no two adjacent\nletters can come from the same summand.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.chain_ne", "description": " The type of reduced words. A reduced word cannot contain a letter `1`, and no two adjacent\nletters can come from the same summand.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.equiv_pair", "description": " The equivalence between words and pairs. Given a word, it decomposes it as a pair by removing\nthe first letter if it comes from `M i`. Given a pair, it prepends the head to the tail.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.mul_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.equiv_pair_eq_of_fst_idx_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.replace_head_head", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.empty", "description": "The empty reduced word.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.neword.append_head", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.word.cons_eq_rcons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.is_free_group_generators", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_product.lift_word_prod_nontrivial_of_other_i", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.connected_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.one_minus_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Icc_homeo_I", "description": "The affine homeomorphism from a nontrivial interval `[a,b]` to `[0,1]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.symm_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.continuous_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.coe_symm_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.mul_le_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.le_one'", "description": "like `unit_interval.le_one`, but with the inequality in `I`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.one_minus_le_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.has_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.fract_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.has_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.zero_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval", "description": "The unit interval `[0,1]` in \u211d.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.one_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_homeomorph_image_I", "description": "The image of `[0,1]` under the homeomorphism `\u03bb x, a * x + b` is `[b, a+b]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.add_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.coe_ne_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "proj_Icc_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.symm", "description": "Unit interval central symmetry.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.nonneg'", "description": "like `unit_interval.nonneg`, but with the inequality in `I`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.mem_iff_one_sub_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.symm_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.symm_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "proj_Icc_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.coe_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.mul_le_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.mul_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.le_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.mul_pos_mem_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.unit_interval", "description": "A tactic that solves `0 \u2264 \u2191x`, `0 \u2264 1 - \u2191x`, `\u2191x \u2264 1`, and `1 - \u2191x \u2264 1` for `x : I`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.has_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Icc_homeo_I_symm_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Icc_homeo_I_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.div_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.two_mul_sub_one_mem_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.nhds_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.continuous_at_add_bot_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.tendsto_nhds_bot_iff_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.continuous_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.continuous_coe_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.topological_space.second_countable_topology", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_coe_real_ereal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.embedding_coe_ennreal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.mem_nhds_bot_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.continuous_coe_ennreal_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.continuous_at_add_coe_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.continuous_at_add_top_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.nhds_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.tendsto_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.nhds_top'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.nhds_bot'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.tendsto_to_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.continuous_at_add_coe_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.continuous_at_add_bot_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.continuous_at_add_coe_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.embedding_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.neg_homeo", "description": "Negation on `ereal` as a homeomorphism", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.nhds_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.tendsto_coe_ennreal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.t2_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.continuous_on_to_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.nhds_coe_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.tendsto_nhds_top_iff_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.mem_nhds_top_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_coe_ennreal_ereal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.ne_bot_top_homeomorph_real", "description": "The set of finite `ereal` numbers is homeomorphic to `\u211d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.open_embedding_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.continuous_at_add_top_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.topological_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.continuous_at_add", "description": " The addition on `ereal` is continuous except where it doesn't make sense (i.e., at `(\u22a5, \u22a4)`\nand at `(\u22a4, \u22a5)`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ereal.order_topology", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.uniform_integrable_zero_meas", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.snorm_indicator_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.snorm_sub_le_of_dist_bdd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.integral_indicator_norm_ge_nonneg_le_of_meas", "description": " This lemma is superceded by `measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.integral_indicator_norm_ge_nonneg_le`\nwhich does not require measurability.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.uniform_integrable.ae_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.uniform_integrable_of", "description": " A sequene of functions `(f\u2099)` is uniformly integrable in the probability sense if for all\n`\u03b5 > 0`, there exists some `C` such that `\u222b x in {|f\u2099| \u2265 C}, f\u2099 x \u2202\u03bc \u2264 \u03b5` for all `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.unif_integrable_zero_meas", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.integral_indicator_norm_ge_le", "description": " This lemma is weaker than `measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.integral_indicator_norm_ge_nonneg_le`\nas the latter provides `0 \u2264 M` and does not require the measurability of `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.uniform_integrable.unif_integrable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure_iff_tendsto_Lp", "description": " **Vitali's convergence theorem**: A sequence of functions `f` converges to `g` in Lp if and\nonly if it is uniformly integrable and converges to `g` in measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.uniform_integrable_average", "description": "The averaging of a uniformly integrable sequence is also uniformly integrable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.uniform_integrable_finite", "description": "A finite sequence of Lp functions is uniformly integrable in the probability sense.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.snorm_indicator_le_of_meas", "description": " This lemma is superceded by `measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.snorm_indicator_le` which does not require\nmeasurability on `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.uniform_integrable", "description": " In probability theory, a family of measurable functions is uniformly integrable if it is\nuniformly integrable in the measure theory sense and is uniformly bounded.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.snorm_indicator_le_of_bound", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.unif_integrable_subsingleton", "description": "A single function is uniformly integrable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.unif_integrable_const", "description": "A constant function is uniformly integrable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_Lp_of_tendsto_ae", "description": "A sequence of uniformly integrable functions which converges \u03bc-a.e. converges in Lp.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.unif_integrable_fin", "description": " This lemma is less general than `measure_theory.unif_integrable_fintype` which applies to\nall sequences indexed by a finite type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.unif_integrable_congr_ae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.uniform_integrable_const", "description": "A constant sequence of functions is uniformly integrable in the probability sense.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.snorm_indicator_le'", "description": "Auxiliary lemma for `measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.snorm_indicator_le`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.snorm_indicator_norm_ge_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_indicator_ge", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.uniform_integrable_of'", "description": " This lemma is superceded by `uniform_integrable_of` which only requires\n`ae_strongly_measurable`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.unif_integrable", "description": " Uniform integrability in the measure theory sense.\n\nA sequence of functions `f` is said to be uniformly integrable if for all `\u03b5 > 0`, there exists\nsome `\u03b4 > 0` such that for all sets `s` with measure less than `\u03b4`, the Lp-norm of `f i`\nrestricted on `s` is less than `\u03b5`.\n\nUniform integrablility is also known as uniformly absolutely continuous integrals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.uniform_integrable.mem_\u2112p", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.unif_integrable.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.unif_integrable.neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.snorm_ess_sup_indicator_norm_ge_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.uniform_integrable.spec'", "description": "This lemma is superceded by `uniform_integrable.spec` which does not require measurability.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.snorm_indicator_norm_ge_pos_le", "description": "This lemma implies that a single function is uniformly integrable (in the probability sense).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_Lp_of_tendsto_ae_of_meas", "description": "A sequence of uniformly integrable functions which converges \u03bc-a.e. converges in Lp.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.uniform_integrable.ae_strongly_measurable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_Lp_of_tendsto_in_measure", "description": " Forward direction of Vitali's convergence theorem: if `f` is a sequence of uniformly integrable\nfunctions that converge in measure to some function `g` in a finite measure space, then `f`\nconverge in Lp to `g`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.unif_integrable.ae_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.uniform_integrable.spec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.unif_integrable_of_tendsto_Lp_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.unif_integrable_of_tendsto_Lp", "description": "Convergence in Lp implies uniform integrability.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.integral_indicator_norm_ge_nonneg_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.unif_integrable_finite", "description": "A finite sequence of Lp functions is uniformly integrable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.uniform_integrable_congr_ae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.unif_integrable_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.uniform_integrable_subsingleton", "description": "A single function is uniformly integrable in the probability sense.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.unif_integrable.sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.uniform_integrable_iff", "description": " The definition of uniform integrable in mathlib is equivalent to the definition commonly\nfound in literature.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.unif_integrable_of'", "description": "This lemma is superceded by `unif_integrable_of` which do not require `C` to be positive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.comp_has_strict_fderiv_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.of_local_left_inverse", "description": " If `f (g y) = y` for `y` in some neighborhood of `a`, `g` is continuous at `a`, and `f` has an\ninvertible derivative `f'` at `g a`, then `g` has the derivative `f'\u207b\u00b9` at `a`.\n\nThis is one of the easy parts of the inverse function theorem: it assumes that we already have\nan inverse function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.has_fderiv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_right_has_fderiv_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.has_strict_fderiv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.comp_differentiable_on_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.differentiable_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.has_fderiv_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.comp_fderiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_has_fderiv_at_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.fderiv_within", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_fderiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.has_fderiv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_differentiable_within_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.fderiv_within", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.eventually_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.differentiable_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.lim_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.differentiable_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.unique_diff_within_at", "description": " If a set has the unique differentiability property at a point x, then the image of this set\nunder a map with onto derivative has also the unique differentiability property at the image point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.unique_diff_on_preimage_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_differentiable_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.has_strict_fderiv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.comp_has_fderiv_within_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_right_fderiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.differentiable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_differentiable_on_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_has_strict_fderiv_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.has_fderiv_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_right_differentiable_within_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.unique_diff_on_image_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_has_fderiv_within_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_differentiable_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.unique_diff_within_at_of_continuous_linear_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.eventually_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_has_fderiv_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.comp_differentiable_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_right_fderiv_within", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.differentiable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.comp_differentiable_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_fderiv_within", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.unique_diff_on_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.fderiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.fderiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_right_differentiable_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_filter_real_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.of_local_left_inverse", "description": " If `f (g y) = y` for `y` in some neighborhood of `a`, `g` is continuous at `a`, and `f` has an\ninvertible derivative `f'` at `g a` in the strict sense, then `g` has the derivative `f'\u207b\u00b9` at `a`\nin the strict sense.\n\nThis is one of the easy parts of the inverse function theorem: it assumes that we already have an\ninverse function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_right_has_fderiv_at_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_has_fderiv_within_at_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_right_has_fderiv_within_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_right_has_fderiv_within_at_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_diff_on.image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.comp_differentiable_within_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_homeomorph.has_fderiv_at_symm", "description": " If `f` is a local homeomorphism defined on a neighbourhood of `f.symm a`, and `f` has an\ninvertible derivative `f'` at `f.symm a`, then `f.symm` has the derivative `f'\u207b\u00b9` at `a`.\n\nThis is one of the easy parts of the inverse function theorem: it assumes that we already have\nan inverse function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_right_differentiable_on_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.comp_has_fderiv_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.differentiable_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.comp_right_differentiable_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_homeomorph.has_strict_fderiv_at_symm", "description": " If `f` is a local homeomorphism defined on a neighbourhood of `f.symm a`, and `f` has an\ninvertible derivative `f'` in the sense of strict differentiability at `f.symm a`, then `f.symm` has\nthe derivative `f'\u207b\u00b9` at `a`.\n\nThis is one of the easy parts of the inverse function theorem: it assumes that we already have\nan inverse function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.differentiable_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.comp_has_fderiv_at_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.maps_to_tangent_cone", "description": " The image of a tangent cone under the differential of a map is included in the tangent cone to\nthe image.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.comp_fderiv_within", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.differentiable_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry_equiv.comp_has_fderiv_within_at_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.diagonal_invertible_equiv_invertible_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_smul_inv_mul_vec_eq_cramer", "description": "One form of **Cramer's rule**. See `matrix.mul_vec_cramer` for a stronger form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.nonsing_inv_nonsing_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_unit_submatrix_equiv", "description": "When lowered to a prop, `matrix.invertible_of_submatrix_equiv_invertible` forms an `iff`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_invertible_of_left_inverse", "description": "`A.det` is invertible if `A` has a left inverse.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_eq_right_inv", "description": "If matrix A is right invertible, then its inverse equals its right inverse.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_diagonal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.invertible_of_diagonal_invertible", "description": "`v` is invertible if `diagonal v` is", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.diagonal_invertible_equiv_invertible_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.nonsing_inv_cancel_or_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_ne_zero_of_right_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_nonsing_inv_mul_det", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.nonsing_inv_apply_not_is_unit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.list_prod_inv_reverse", "description": "A version of `list.prod_inv_reverse` for `matrix.has_inv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_inv_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_conj'", "description": "A variant of `matrix.det_units_conj'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transpose_nonsing_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.nonsing_inv_eq_ring_inverse", "description": "The nonsingular inverse is the same as the general `ring.inverse`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_unit_det_of_right_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_unit_det_transpose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_unit_det_of_invertible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_unit_of_left_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.nonsing_inv_unit", "description": " A version of `matrix.units_of_det_invertible` with the inverse defeq to `A\u207b\u00b9` that is therefore\nnoncomputable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.invertible_of_right_inverse", "description": "We can construct an instance of invertible A if A has a right inverse.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.invertible_equiv_det_invertible_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_invertible_of_invertible", "description": "If `A` has a constructive inverse, produce one for `A.det`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_inv_cancel_left_of_invertible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.invertible_of_left_inverse", "description": "We can construct an instance of invertible A if A has a left inverse.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.unit_of_det_invertible_eq_nonsing_inv_unit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_unit_nonsing_inv_det_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_eq_one_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.diagonal_invertible", "description": "`diagonal v` is invertible if `v` is", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_inv_of_invertible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_mul_cancel_right_of_invertible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.right_inv_eq_left_inv", "description": "The right inverse of matrix A equals the left inverse of A when they exist.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.invertible_of_det_invertible", "description": "If `A.det` has a constructive inverse, produce one for `A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_smul_inv_vec_mul_eq_cramer_transpose", "description": "One form of **Cramer's rule**. See `matrix.mul_vec_cramer` for a stronger form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.has_inv.inv.invertible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.submatrix_equiv_invertible", "description": "`A.submatrix e\u2081 e\u2082` is invertible if `A` is", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_of_eq_nonsing_inv", "description": "The nonsingular inverse is the same as `inv_of` when `A` is invertible.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.coe_units_inv", "description": " Coercing the result of `units.has_inv` is the same as coercing first and applying the\nnonsingular inverse.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.invertible_of_submatrix_equiv_invertible", "description": "`A` is invertible if `A.submatrix e\u2081 e\u2082` is", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_one_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_invertible_of_right_inverse", "description": "`A.det` is invertible if `A` has a right inverse.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_unit_nonsing_inv_det", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_inv_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_inv_eq_iff_eq_mul_of_invertible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_of_diagonal_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_mul_of_invertible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_nonsing_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.nonsing_inv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.nonsing_inv_mul_cancel_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.diagonal_invertible_equiv_invertible", "description": " Together `matrix.diagonal_invertible` and `matrix.invertible_of_diagonal_invertible` form an\nequivalence, although both sides of the equiv are subsingleton anyway.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.submatrix_equiv_invertible_equiv_invertible", "description": " Together `matrix.submatrix_equiv_invertible` and\n`matrix.invertible_of_submatrix_equiv_invertible` form an equivalence, although both sides of the\nequiv are subsingleton anyway.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_conj", "description": "A variant of `matrix.det_units_conj`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_inv_cancel_right_of_invertible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.nonsing_inv_mul_cancel_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.unit_of_det_invertible", "description": "Given a proof that `A.det` has a constructive inverse, lift `A` to `(matrix n n \u03b1)\u02e3`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.invertible__of_invertible_transpose", "description": "A matrix is invertible if the transpose is invertible.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.invertible_of_is_unit_det", "description": " A version of `matrix.invertible_of_det_invertible` with the inverse defeq to `A\u207b\u00b9` that is\ntherefore noncomputable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.invertible_equiv_det_invertible_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_inv_rev", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_eq_left_inv", "description": "If matrix A is left invertible, then its inverse equals its left inverse.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_unit_diagonal", "description": "When lowered to a prop, `matrix.diagonal_invertible_equiv_invertible` forms an `iff`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_smul'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_mul_eq_iff_eq_mul_of_invertible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.submatrix_equiv_invertible_equiv_invertible_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.conj_transpose_nonsing_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_ne_zero_of_left_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_reindex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_nonsing_inv", "description": "The `nonsing_inv` of `A` is a right inverse.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.has_inv", "description": "The inverse of a square matrix, when it is invertible (and zero otherwise).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_unit_det_of_left_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_nonsing_inv_cancel_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.left_inv_eq_left_inv", "description": "The left inverse of matrix A is unique when existing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.submatrix_equiv_invertible_equiv_invertible_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_submatrix_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.right_inv_eq_right_inv", "description": "The right inverse of matrix A is unique when existing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_unit_iff_is_unit_det", "description": "When lowered to a prop, `matrix.invertible_equiv_det_invertible` forms an `iff`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_adjugate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.invertible_transpose", "description": "The transpose of an invertible matrix is invertible.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_nonsing_inv_cancel_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.nonsing_inv_mul", "description": "The `nonsing_inv` of `A` is a left inverse.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.invertible_of_invertible_conj_transpose", "description": "A matrix is invertible if the conjugate transpose is invertible.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.invertible_equiv_det_invertible", "description": " Together `matrix.det_invertible_of_invertible` and `matrix.invertible_of_det_invertible` form an\nequivalence, although both sides of the equiv are subsingleton anyway.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_of_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_inv_of_invertible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_mul_cancel_left_of_invertible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_unit_of_right_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_of_submatrix_equiv_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_self_adjoint.has_eigenvector_of_is_min_on", "description": " For a self-adjoint operator `T`, a minimum of the Rayleigh quotient of `T` on a sphere centred\nat the origin is an eigenvector of `T`, with eigenvalue the global infimum of the Rayleigh\nquotient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.infi_rayleigh_eq_infi_rayleigh_sphere", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.has_eigenvalue_supr_of_finite_dimensional", "description": " The supremum of the Rayleigh quotient of a symmetric operator `T` on a nontrivial\nfinite-dimensional vector space is an eigenvalue for that operator.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_self_adjoint.eq_smul_self_of_is_local_extr_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_self_adjoint.has_eigenvector_of_is_local_extr_on", "description": " For a self-adjoint operator `T`, a local extremum of the Rayleigh quotient of `T` on a sphere\ncentred at the origin is an eigenvector of `T`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.image_rayleigh_eq_image_rayleigh_sphere", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.supr_rayleigh_eq_supr_rayleigh_sphere", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.has_strict_fderiv_at_re_apply_inner_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_self_adjoint.eq_smul_self_of_is_local_extr_on_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_self_adjoint.has_eigenvector_of_is_max_on", "description": " For a self-adjoint operator `T`, a maximum of the Rayleigh quotient of `T` on a sphere centred\nat the origin is an eigenvector of `T`, with eigenvalue the global supremum of the Rayleigh\nquotient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_self_adjoint.linearly_dependent_of_is_local_extr_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.has_eigenvalue_infi_of_finite_dimensional", "description": " The infimum of the Rayleigh quotient of a symmetric operator `T` on a nontrivial\nfinite-dimensional vector space is an eigenvalue for that operator.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.rayleigh_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.subsingleton_of_no_eigenvalue_finite_dimensional", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.op_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.remove_left_op_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.left_op_right_op_equiv_unit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_op_equivalence_counit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.remove_right_op", "description": "Given a natural transformation `\u03b1 : F.right_op \u27f6 G.right_op`, for `F G : C\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 D`,\ntaking `unop` of each component gives a natural transformation `G \u27f6 F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op_unop_equiv_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op_unop_equiv", "description": "The equivalence of functor categories induced by `op` and `unop`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.op_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.left_op_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.unop", "description": "Given a functor `F : C\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 D\u1d52\u1d56` we can take the \"unopposite\" functor `F : C \u2964 D`.\nIn informal mathematics no distinction is made between these.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.equivalence.op_counit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso_op_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.right_op", "description": "Another variant of the opposite of functor, turning a functor `C\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 D` into a functor `C \u2964 D\u1d52\u1d56`.\nIn informal mathematics no distinction is made.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_iso.remove_op_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_equiv", "description": " The equivalence between arrows of the form `A \u27f6 B` and `B.unop \u27f6 A.unop`. Useful for building\nadjunctions.\nNote that this (definitionally) gives variants\n```lean\ndef op_equiv' (A : C) (B : C\u1d52\u1d56) : (opposite.op A \u27f6 B) \u2243 (B.unop \u27f6 A) :=\nop_equiv _ _\n\ndef op_equiv'' (A : C\u1d52\u1d56) (B : C) : (A \u27f6 opposite.op B) \u2243 (B \u27f6 A.unop) :=\nop_equiv _ _\n\ndef op_equiv''' (A B : C) : (opposite.op A \u27f6 opposite.op B) \u2243 (B \u27f6 A) :=\nop_equiv _ _\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.hom.unop_op", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.unop_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.unop_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_iso.op_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op_unop_equiv_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op.category_theory.full", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.remove_right_op_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_op_equivalence_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.left_op_right_op_equiv", "description": "The equivalence of functor categories induced by `left_op` and `right_op`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_iso.unop_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op_unop_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.op", "description": "The opposite of a natural transformation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.unop", "description": "The \"unopposite\" of a natural transformation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.unop_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.right_op_faithful", "description": "If F is faithful then the right_op of F is also faithful.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.op_unop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op_inv_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.left_op_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_iso.unop_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.left_op_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unop_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unop_id_op", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_iso.op", "description": " The natural isomorphism between opposite functors `G.op \u2245 F.op` induced by a natural\nisomorphism between the original functors `F \u2245 G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.unop_op_iso", "description": "The isomorphism between `F.unop.op` and `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op.category_theory.faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.left_op", "description": "Another variant of the opposite of functor, turning a functor `C \u2964 D\u1d52\u1d56` into a functor `C\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 D`.\nIn informal mathematics no distinction is made.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op_hom", "description": "Taking the opposite of a functor is functorial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.op", "description": "The opposite isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_op_equivalence", "description": "The double opposite category is equivalent to the original.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.equivalence.op_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.left_op", "description": "Given a natural transformation `\u03b1 : F \u27f6 G`, for `F G : C \u2964 D\u1d52\u1d56`,\ntaking `unop` of each component gives a natural transformation `G.left_op \u27f6 F.left_op`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op_unop_equiv_counit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso_unop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op_hom_map_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.right_op_left_op_eq", "description": " Whenever possible, it is advisable to use the isomorphism `right_op_left_op_iso`\ninstead of this equality of functors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.right_op_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.equivalence.unop_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unop_unop_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.left_op_right_op_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_iso.unop", "description": " The natural isomorphism between functors `G.unop \u2245 F.unop` induced by a natural isomorphism\nbetween the original functors `F \u2245 G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.unop_op_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.equivalence.op_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.right_op_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op_unop_equiv_unit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso_op", "description": "If `f` is an isomorphism, so is `f.op`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.op_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso_op_equiv", "description": "The equivalence between isomorphisms of the form `A \u2245 B` and `B.unop \u2245 A.unop`.\n\nNote this is definitionally the same as the other three variants:\n* `(opposite.op A \u2245 B) \u2243 (B.unop \u2245 A)`\n* `(A \u2245 opposite.op B) \u2243 (B \u2245 A.unop)`\n* `(opposite.op A \u2245 opposite.op B) \u2243 (B \u2245 A)`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.equivalence.unop_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_iso.remove_op_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.right_op_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.left_op_right_op_equiv_inverse_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.left_op_right_op_iso", "description": "The isomorphism between `F.left_op.right_op` and `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.category.opposite", "description": "The opposite category.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001M>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op_inv_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.left_op_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.remove_left_op_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op_hom_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unop_unop_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.equivalence.op_unit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.op_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_iso.op_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso_op_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.left_op_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_iso.remove_op", "description": " The natural isomorphism between functors `G \u2245 F` induced by a natural isomorphism\nbetween the opposite functors `F.op \u2245 G.op`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_op_equivalence_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.unop_op", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unop_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.left_op_faithful", "description": "If F is faithful then the left_op of F is also faithful.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_op", "description": "The functor from the double-opposite of a category to the underlying category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.remove_left_op", "description": "Given a natural transformation `\u03b1 : F.left_op \u27f6 G.left_op`, for `F G : C \u2964 D\u1d52\u1d56`,\ntaking `op` of each component gives a natural transformation `G \u27f6 F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_op_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.right_op_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.unop_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.decidable_eq_of_unop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op_unop_iso", "description": "The isomorphism between `F.op.unop` and `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.remove_unop_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op_inv", "description": "Take the \"unopposite\" of a functor is functorial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op", "description": "The opposite of a functor, i.e. considering a functor `F : C \u2964 D` as a functor `C\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 D\u1d52\u1d56`.\nIn informal mathematics no distinction is made between these.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.remove_op_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.left_op_right_op_equiv_functor_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.right_op", "description": "Given a natural transformation `\u03b1 : F \u27f6 G`, for `F G : C\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 D`,\ntaking `op` of each component gives a natural transformation `G.right_op \u27f6 F.right_op`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.equivalence.unop", "description": "An equivalence between opposite categories gives an equivalence between the original categories.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.right_op_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.unop_op_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.left_op_right_op_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.unop_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.equivalence.unop_counit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.remove_unop_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.hom.unop_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.left_op_right_op_equiv_counit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.remove_op", "description": "Given a natural transformation `\u03b1 : F.op \u27f6 G.op`,\nwe can take the \"unopposite\" of each component obtaining a natural transformation `G \u27f6 F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.hom.op_unop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.left_op_right_op_equiv_inverse_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.right_op_left_op_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.unop", "description": "The isomorphism obtained from an isomorphism in the opposite category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.remove_right_op_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.remove_unop", "description": " Given a natural transformation `\u03b1 : F.unop \u27f6 G.unop`, we can take the opposite of each\ncomponent obtaining a natural transformation `G \u27f6 F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_id_unop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unop_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_op_equivalence_unit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.unop_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subsingleton_of_unop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.right_op_left_op_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso_of_op", "description": "If `f.op` is an isomorphism `f` must be too.\n(This cannot be an instance as it would immediately loop!)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op_unop_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.equivalence.op", "description": "An equivalence between categories gives an equivalence between the opposite categories.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.hom.op_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unop_unop", "description": "The functor from a category to its double-opposite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso_op_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.right_op_left_op_iso", "description": "The isomorphism between `F.right_op.left_op` and `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.left_op_right_op_equiv_functor_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.equivalence.unop_unit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_op_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso_unop_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.remove_op_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.finite_spanning_sets_in.outer_regular", "description": " If a measure `\u03bc` admits finite spanning open sets such that the restriction of `\u03bc` to each set\nis outer regular, then the original measure is outer regular as well.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.regular.exists_compact_not_null", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.exists_is_open_le_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_open.measure_eq_supr_is_closed", "description": "If `\u03bc` is a weakly regular measure, then any open set can be approximated by a closed subset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.measure_eq_infi_is_open", "description": " For an outer regular measure, the measure of a set is the infimum of the measures of open sets\ncontaining it.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.inner_regular.measure_eq_supr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.regular.sigma_finite", "description": "A regular measure in a \u03c3-compact space is \u03c3-finite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.regular.of_sigma_compact_space_of_is_locally_finite_measure", "description": "Any locally finite measure on a `\u03c3`-compact (e)metric space is regular.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.regular", "description": "A measure `\u03bc` is regular if\n - it is finite on all compact sets;\n - it is outer regular: `\u03bc(A) = inf {\u03bc(U) | A \u2286 U open}` for `A` measurable;\n - it is inner regular for open sets, using compact sets:\n   `\u03bc(U) = sup {\u03bc(K) | K \u2286 U compact}` for `U` open.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.regular.to_is_finite_measure_on_compacts", "description": "A measure `\u03bc` is regular if\n - it is finite on all compact sets;\n - it is outer regular: `\u03bc(A) = inf {\u03bc(U) | A \u2286 U open}` for `A` measurable;\n - it is inner regular for open sets, using compact sets:\n   `\u03bc(U) = sup {\u03bc(K) | K \u2286 U compact}` for `U` open.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.regular.to_outer_regular", "description": "A measure `\u03bc` is regular if\n - it is finite on all compact sets;\n - it is outer regular: `\u03bc(A) = inf {\u03bc(U) | A \u2286 U open}` for `A` measurable;\n - it is inner regular for open sets, using compact sets:\n   `\u03bc(U) = sup {\u03bc(K) | K \u2286 U compact}` for `U` open.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.regular.inner_regular", "description": "A measure `\u03bc` is regular if\n - it is finite on all compact sets;\n - it is outer regular: `\u03bc(A) = inf {\u03bc(U) | A \u2286 U open}` for `A` measurable;\n - it is inner regular for open sets, using compact sets:\n   `\u03bc(U) = sup {\u03bc(K) | K \u2286 U compact}` for `U` open.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.inner_regular.trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_open.measure_eq_supr_is_compact", "description": "The measure of an open set is the supremum of the measures of compact sets it contains.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.outer_regular.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.inner_regular.is_compact_is_closed", "description": "In a `\u03c3`-compact space, any closed set can be approximated by a compact subset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set.exists_lt_is_closed_of_ne_top", "description": " Given a weakly regular measure, any measurable set of finite mass can be approximated from\ninside by closed sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.inner_regular", "description": " We say that a measure `\u03bc` is *inner regular* with respect to predicates `p q : set \u03b1 \u2192 Prop`,\nif for every `U` such that `q U` and `r < \u03bc U`, there exists a subset `K \u2286 U` satisfying `p K`\nof measure greater than `r`.\n\nThis definition is used to prove some facts about regular and weakly regular measures without\nrepeating the proofs.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set.exists_lt_is_compact_of_ne_top", "description": " If `\u03bc` is a regular measure, then any measurable set of finite measure can be approximated by a\ncompact subset. See also `measurable_set.exists_is_compact_lt_add`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.inner_regular.weakly_regular_of_finite", "description": " In a finite measure space, assume that any open set can be approximated from inside by closed\nsets. Then the measure is weakly regular.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.inner_regular.of_pseudo_emetric_space", "description": " In a metric space (or even a pseudo emetric space), an open set can be approximated from inside\nby closed sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.weakly_regular.inner_regular_measurable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.exists_is_open_lt_of_lt", "description": " Given `r` larger than the measure of a set `A`, there exists an open superset of `A` with\nmeasure less than `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set.exists_is_closed_diff_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.weakly_regular.restrict_of_measurable_set", "description": " The restriction of a weakly regular measure to a measurable set of finite measure is\nweakly regular.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.outer_regular.map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set.exists_is_compact_diff_lt", "description": " If `\u03bc` is a regular measure, then any measurable set of finite measure can be approximated by a\ncompact subset. See also `measurable_set.exists_is_compact_lt_add` and\n`measurable_set.exists_lt_is_compact_of_ne_top`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.regular.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.regular.inner_regular_measurable", "description": " If `\u03bc` is a regular measure, then any measurable set of finite measure can be approximated by a\ncompact subset. See also `measurable_set.exists_is_compact_lt_add` and\n`measurable_set.exists_lt_is_compact_of_ne_top`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.regular.weakly_regular", "description": "A regular measure is weakly regular.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.regular.map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.inner_regular.map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.regular.zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.inner_regular.exists_subset_lt_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.weakly_regular", "description": "A measure `\u03bc` is weakly regular if\n - it is outer regular: `\u03bc(A) = inf {\u03bc(U) | A \u2286 U open}` for `A` measurable;\n - it is inner regular for open sets, using closed sets:\n   `\u03bc(U) = sup {\u03bc(F) | F \u2286 U compact}` for `U` open.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.weakly_regular.to_outer_regular", "description": "A measure `\u03bc` is weakly regular if\n - it is outer regular: `\u03bc(A) = inf {\u03bc(U) | A \u2286 U open}` for `A` measurable;\n - it is inner regular for open sets, using closed sets:\n   `\u03bc(U) = sup {\u03bc(F) | F \u2286 U compact}` for `U` open.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.weakly_regular.inner_regular", "description": "A measure `\u03bc` is weakly regular if\n - it is outer regular: `\u03bc(A) = inf {\u03bc(U) | A \u2286 U open}` for `A` measurable;\n - it is inner regular for open sets, using closed sets:\n   `\u03bc(U) = sup {\u03bc(F) | F \u2286 U compact}` for `U` open.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.inner_regular.measurable_set_of_open", "description": " If a measure is inner regular (using closed or compact sets), then every measurable set of\nfinite measure can by approximated by a (closed or compact) subset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_open.exists_lt_is_closed", "description": "If `\u03bc` is a weakly regular measure, then any open set can be approximated by a closed subset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set.exists_is_closed_lt_add", "description": " If `s` is a measurable set, a weakly regular measure `\u03bc` is finite on `s`, and `\u03b5` is a positive\nnumber, then there exist a closed set `K \u2286 s` such that `\u03bc s < \u03bc K + \u03b5`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_open.exists_lt_is_compact", "description": "If `\u03bc` is a regular measure, then any open set can be approximated by a compact subset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.exists_is_open_lt_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.outer_regular", "description": " A measure `\u03bc` is outer regular if `\u03bc(A) = inf {\u03bc(U) | A \u2286 U open}` for a measurable set `A`.\n\nThis definition implies the same equality for any (not necessarily measurable) set, see\n`set.measure_eq_infi_is_open`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.outer_regular.outer_regular", "description": " A measure `\u03bc` is outer regular if `\u03bc(A) = inf {\u03bc(U) | A \u2286 U open}` for a measurable set `A`.\n\nThis definition implies the same equality for any (not necessarily measurable) set, see\n`set.measure_eq_infi_is_open`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set.measure_eq_supr_is_compact_of_ne_top", "description": " Given a regular measure, any measurable set of finite mass can be approximated from\ninside by compact sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set.exists_is_open_diff_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.weakly_regular.of_pseudo_emetric_space_of_is_finite_measure", "description": "Any finite measure on a metric space (or even a pseudo emetric space) is weakly regular.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set.measure_eq_supr_is_closed_of_ne_top", "description": " Given a weakly regular measure, any measurable set of finite mass can be approximated from\ninside by closed sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.weakly_regular.of_pseudo_emetric_second_countable_of_locally_finite", "description": " Any locally finite measure on a second countable metric space (or even a pseudo emetric space)\nis weakly regular.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.inner_regular.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.outer_regular.zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set.exists_is_compact_lt_add", "description": " If `\u03bc` is a regular measure, then any measurable set of finite measure can be approximated by a\ncompact subset. See also `measurable_set.exists_lt_is_compact_of_ne_top`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.prod_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.prod_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.prod_eq_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.mem_prod_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.prod_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_swap_iff", "description"