"description": "A vector in `K` is orthogonal to one in `K\u15ee`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_ortho_span", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_ortho_sup_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.sub_mem_orthogonal_of_inner_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_ortho.mono_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.orthogonal_family_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.orthogonal_orthogonal_monotone", "description": " `orthogonal.orthogonal` preserves the `\u2264` ordering of two\nsubspaces.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.inf_orthogonal_eq_bot", "description": "`K` and `K\u15ee` have trivial intersection.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_ortho.inner_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_ortho_bot_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_ortho.symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.orthogonal_disjoint", "description": "`K` and `K\u15ee` have trivial intersection.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_ortho_top_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.orthogonal_le", "description": " `orthogonal` reverses the `\u2264` ordering of two\nsubspaces.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_ortho.le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.mem_orthogonal_singleton_iff_inner_right", "description": "A vector is in `(\ud835\udd5c \u2219 u)\u15ee` iff it is orthogonal to `u`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_ortho_orthogonal_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.sub_mem_orthogonal_of_inner_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.top_orthogonal_eq_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.inf_orthogonal", "description": " The inf of two orthogonal subspaces equals the subspace orthogonal\nto the sup.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.bot_orthogonal_eq_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.inner_left_of_mem_orthogonal", "description": "A vector in `K\u15ee` is orthogonal to one in `K`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_ortho.mono_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_ortho_sup_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.mem_orthogonal", "description": "When a vector is in `K\u15ee`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.infi_orthogonal", "description": " The inf of an indexed family of orthogonal subspaces equals the\nsubspace orthogonal to the sup.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_ortho.comap_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_ortho.map_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_ortho_iff_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_ortho.comap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_ortho_top_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form_of_real_inner_orthogonal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_ortho_Sup_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "denoms_clearable_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "denoms_clearable", "description": " `denoms_clearable` formalizes the property that `b ^ N * f (a / b)`\ndoes not have denominators, if the inequality `f.nat_degree \u2264 N` holds.\n\nThe definition asserts the existence of an element `D` of `R` and an\nelement `bi = 1 / i b` of `K` such that clearing the denominators of\nthe fraction equals `i D`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "denoms_clearable_nat_degree", "description": " If `i : R \u2192 K` is a ring homomorphism, `f` is a polynomial with coefficients in `R`,\n`a, b` are elements of `R`, with `i b` invertible, then there is a `D \u2208 R` such that\n`b ^ f.nat_degree * f (a / b)` equals `i D`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "denoms_clearable_C_mul_X_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "denoms_clearable_of_nat_degree_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "denoms_clearable.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_le_pow_mul_abs_eval_div", "description": "  Evaluating a polynomial with integer coefficients at a rational number and clearing\ndenominators, yields a number greater than or equal to one.  The target can be any\n`linear_ordered_field K`.\nThe assumption on `K` could be weakened to `linear_ordered_comm_ring` assuming that the\nimage of the denominator is invertible in `K`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_universal_colimit", "description": "A (colimit) cocone over a diagram `F : J \u2964 C` is universal if it is stable under pullbacks.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.binary_cofan.is_van_kampen_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_strict_initial_of_is_universal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_van_kampen_colimit_of_evaluation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.finitary_extensive", "description": "A category is (finitary) extensive if it has finite coproducts,\nand binary coproducts are van Kampen.\n\nTODO: Show that this is iff all finite coproducts are van Kampen.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.finitary_extensive.has_finite_coproducts", "description": "A category is (finitary) extensive if it has finite coproducts,\nand binary coproducts are van Kampen.\n\nTODO: Show that this is iff all finite coproducts are van Kampen.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.finitary_extensive.van_kampen'", "description": "A category is (finitary) extensive if it has finite coproducts,\nand binary coproducts are van Kampen.\n\nTODO: Show that this is iff all finite coproducts are van Kampen.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.coprod.inl.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.finitary_extensive_iff_of_is_terminal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.equifibered.comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_initial.is_van_kampen_colimit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.finitary_extensive_of_preserves_and_reflects", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.binary_cofan.mono_inr_of_is_van_kampen", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.equifibered.whisker_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.coprod.inr.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_van_kampen_colimit.is_universal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_van_kampen_colimit.is_colimit", "description": "A van Kampen colimit is a colimit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.finitary_extensive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.finitary_extensive.mono_inl_of_is_colimit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.finitary_extensive.is_pullback_initial_to_binary_cofan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_van_kampen_colimit.of_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.finitary_extensive_of_preserves_and_reflects_isomorphism", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.binary_cofan.is_van_kampen_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.binary_cofan.is_pullback_initial_to_of_is_van_kampen", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.equifibered_of_is_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_van_kampen_colimit.of_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_van_kampen_colimit", "description": " A (colimit) cocone over a diagram `F : J \u2964 C` is van Kampen if for every cocone `c'` over the\npullback of the diagram `F' : J \u2964 C'`, `c'` is colimiting iff `c'` is the pullback of `c`.\n\nTODO: Show that this is iff the functor `C \u2964 Cat\u1d52\u1d56` sending `x` to `C/x` preserves it.\nTODO: Show that this is iff the inclusion functor `C \u2964 Span(C)` preserves it.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.types.finitary_extensive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.finitary_extensive.mono_inr_of_is_colimit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.map_pair_equifibered", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.finitary_extensive_Top_aux", "description": "(Implementation) An auxiliary lemma for the proof that `Top` is finitary extensive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.finitary_extensive.van_kampen", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_strict_initial_objects_of_finitary_extensive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Top.finitary_extensive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.equifibered", "description": " A natural transformation is equifibered if every commutative square of the following form is\na pullback.\n```lean\nF(X) \u2192 F(Y)\n \u2193      \u2193\nG(X) \u2192 G(Y)\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dense_liouville", "description": "The set of Liouville numbers in dense.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_G\u03b4_set_of_liouville", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eventually_residual_liouville", "description": "The set of Liouville numbers is a residual set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_of_liouville_eq_Inter_Union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_of_liouville_eq_irrational_inter_Inter_Union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.fork", "description": "The equalizer cone for a parallel pair of morphisms of seminormed groups.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_\u03c0", "description": "The projection from `Y` to the explicit cokernel of `X \u27f6 Y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_\u03c0_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_desc", "description": "Descend to the explicit cokernel.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup\u2081.cokernel_cocone", "description": "Auxiliary definition for `has_cokernels SemiNormedGroup\u2081`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup\u2081.category_theory.limits.has_cokernels", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_hom_ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.has_limit_parallel_pair", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_\u03c0_desc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_desc_comp_eq_desc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_iso_hom_\u03c0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_\u03c0_apply_dom_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_\u03c0_desc_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_\u03c0_desc_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_desc_norm_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_\u03c0.epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_desc_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.cokernel_cocone", "description": "Auxiliary definition for `has_cokernels SemiNormedGroup`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_iso_inv_\u03c0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.is_quotient_explicit_cokernel_\u03c0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_desc_comp_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_desc_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.is_colimit_cokernel_cocone", "description": "Auxiliary definition for `has_cokernels SemiNormedGroup`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.category_theory.limits.has_cokernels", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel", "description": "An explicit choice of cokernel, which has good properties with respect to the norm.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_desc_norm_noninc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.category_theory.limits.has_equalizers", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.comp_explicit_cokernel_\u03c0_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.comp_explicit_cokernel_\u03c0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_coker.map_desc", "description": "A special case of `category_theory.limits.cokernel.map_desc` adapted to `explicit_cokernel`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel.map", "description": "A special case of `category_theory.limits.cokernel.map` adapted to `explicit_cokernel`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_iso", "description": "The explicit cokernel is isomorphic to the usual cokernel.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_iso_hom_desc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup\u2081.cokernel_lift", "description": "Auxiliary definition for `has_cokernels SemiNormedGroup\u2081`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.cokernel_lift", "description": "Auxiliary definition for `has_cokernels SemiNormedGroup`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.norm_noninc_explicit_cokernel_\u03c0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.explicit_cokernel_desc_norm_le_of_norm_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_card_fiber_le_of_card_le_nsmul", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons counted by heads: given a function `f`, a\nfinite sets `s` and `t`, and a number `b` such that `card s \u2264 card t \u2022 b`, there exists `y \u2208 t` such\nthat its preimage in `s` has no more than `b` elements.\nSee also `finset.exists_card_fiber_lt_of_card_lt_nsmul` for a stronger statement.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.exists_card_fiber_lt_of_card_lt_nsmul", "description": " The strong pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons and pigeonholes. There is a pigeonhole\nwith at most as many pigeons as the floor of the average number of pigeons across all pigeonholes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.exists_lt_modeq_of_infinite", "description": " If `s` is an infinite set of natural numbers and `k > 0`, then `s` contains two elements `m < n`\nthat are equal mod `k`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_lt_sum_fiber_of_maps_to_of_nsmul_lt_sum", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons counted by weight, strict inequality version:\nif the total weight of a finite set of pigeons is greater than `n \u2022 b`, and they are sorted into\n`n` pigeonholes, then for some pigeonhole, the total weight of the pigeons in this pigeonhole is\ngreater than `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.exists_card_fiber_le_of_card_le_mul", "description": " The strong pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons and pigeonholes.  Given a function `f`\nbetween finite types `\u03b1` and `\u03b2` and a number `n` such that `card \u03b1 \u2264 card \u03b2 * n`, there exists an\nelement `y : \u03b2` such that its preimage has at most `n` elements. See also\n`fintype.exists_card_fiber_lt_of_card_lt_mul` for a stronger statement.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.exists_sum_fiber_lt_of_sum_lt_nsmul", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons of different weights, strict inequality\nversion: there is a pigeonhole with the total weight of pigeons in it less than `b` provided that\nthe total number of pigeonholes times `b` is greater than the total weight of all pigeons.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_lt_card_fiber_of_nsmul_lt_card_of_maps_to", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons counted by heads: there is a pigeonhole with\nat least as many pigeons as the ceiling of the average number of pigeons across all pigeonholes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_sum_fiber_lt_of_maps_to_of_sum_lt_nsmul", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons counted by weight, strict inequality version:\nif the total weight of a finite set of pigeons is less than `n \u2022 b`, and they are sorted into `n`\npigeonholes, then for some pigeonhole, the total weight of the pigeons in this pigeonhole is less\nthan `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.exists_le_sum_fiber_of_nsmul_le_sum", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons of different weights, non-strict inequality\nversion: there is a pigeonhole with the total weight of pigeons in it greater than or equal to `b`\nprovided that the total number of pigeonholes times `b` is less than or equal to the total weight of\nall pigeons.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_le_card_fiber_of_nsmul_le_card_of_maps_to", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons counted by heads: given a function between\nfinite sets `s` and `t` and a number `b` such that `card t \u2022 b \u2264 card s`, there exists `y \u2208 t` such\nthat its preimage in `s` has at least `b` elements.\nSee also `finset.exists_lt_card_fiber_of_nsmul_lt_card_of_maps_to` for a stronger statement.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_card_fiber_lt_of_card_lt_nsmul", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons counted by heads: there is a pigeonhole with\nat most as many pigeons as the floor of the average number of pigeons across all pigeonholes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.exists_le_card_fiber_of_nsmul_le_card", "description": " The strong pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons and pigeonholes.  Given a function `f`\nbetween finite types `\u03b1` and `\u03b2` and a number `b` such that `card \u03b2 \u2022 b \u2264 card \u03b1`, there exists an\nelement `y : \u03b2` such that its preimage has at least `b` elements.\nSee also `fintype.exists_lt_card_fiber_of_nsmul_lt_card` for a stronger statement.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_card_fiber_le_of_card_le_mul", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons counted by heads: given a function `f`, a\nfinite sets `s` in its domain, a finite set `t` in its codomain, and a natural number `n` such that\n`card s \u2264 card t * n`, there exists `y \u2208 t` such that its preimage in `s` has no more than `n`\nelements. See also `finset.exists_card_fiber_lt_of_card_lt_mul` for a stronger statement.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.exists_lt_card_fiber_of_mul_lt_card", "description": "The strong pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons and pigeonholes.\nThere is a pigeonhole with at least as many pigeons as\nthe ceiling of the average number of pigeons across all pigeonholes.\n(\"The maximum is at least the mean\" specialized to integers.)\n\nMore formally, given a function `f` between finite types `\u03b1` and `\u03b2` and a number `n` such that\n`card \u03b2 * n < card \u03b1`, there exists an element `y : \u03b2` such that its preimage has more than `n`\nelements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.exists_lt_card_fiber_of_nsmul_lt_card", "description": "The strong pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons and pigeonholes. There is a pigeonhole\nwith at least as many pigeons as the ceiling of the average number of pigeons across all\npigeonholes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_le_sum_fiber_of_maps_to_of_nsmul_le_sum", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons counted by weight, non-strict inequality\nversion: if the total weight of a finite set of pigeons is greater than or equal to `n \u2022 b`, and\nthey are sorted into `n > 0` pigeonholes, then for some pigeonhole, the total weight of the pigeons\nin this pigeonhole is greater than or equal to `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_card_fiber_lt_of_card_lt_mul", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons counted by heads: there is a pigeonhole with\nat most as many pigeons as the floor of the average number of pigeons across all pigeonholes.  (\"The\nminimum is at most the mean\" specialized to integers.)\n\nMore formally, given a function `f`, a finite sets `s` in its domain, a finite set `t` in its\ncodomain, and a natural number `n` such that `card s < card t * n`, there exists `y \u2208 t` such that\nits preimage in `s` has less than `n` elements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_sum_fiber_lt_of_sum_fiber_nonneg_of_sum_lt_nsmul", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons counted by weight, strict inequality version:\nif the total weight of a finite set of pigeons is less than `n \u2022 b`, they are sorted into some\npigeonholes, and for all but `n` pigeonholes the total weight of the pigeons there is nonnegative,\nthen for at least one of these `n` pigeonholes, the total weight of the pigeons in this pigeonhole\nis less than `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_lt_sum_fiber_of_sum_fiber_nonpos_of_nsmul_lt_sum", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons counted by weight, strict inequality version:\nif the total weight of a finite set of pigeons is greater than `n \u2022 b`, they are sorted into some\npigeonholes, and for all but `n` pigeonholes the total weight of the pigeons there is nonpositive,\nthen for at least one of these `n` pigeonholes, the total weight of the pigeons in this pigeonhole\nis greater than `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.exists_lt_sum_fiber_of_nsmul_lt_sum", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons of different weights, strict inequality\nversion: there is a pigeonhole with the total weight of pigeons in it greater than `b` provided that\nthe total number of pigeonholes times `b` is less than the total weight of all pigeons.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.exists_card_fiber_le_of_card_le_nsmul", "description": " The strong pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons and pigeonholes.  Given a function `f`\nbetween finite types `\u03b1` and `\u03b2` and a number `b` such that `card \u03b1 \u2264 card \u03b2 \u2022 b`, there exists an\nelement `y : \u03b2` such that its preimage has at most `b` elements.\nSee also `fintype.exists_card_fiber_lt_of_card_lt_nsmul` for a stronger statement.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_le_sum_fiber_of_sum_fiber_nonpos_of_nsmul_le_sum", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons counted by weight, non-strict inequality\nversion: if the total weight of a finite set of pigeons is greater than or equal to `n \u2022 b`, they\nare sorted into some pigeonholes, and for all but `n > 0` pigeonholes the total weight of the\npigeons there is nonpositive, then for at least one of these `n` pigeonholes, the total weight of\nthe pigeons in this pigeonhole is greater than or equal to `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.exists_card_fiber_lt_of_card_lt_mul", "description": "The strong pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons and pigeonholes.\nThere is a pigeonhole with at most as many pigeons as\nthe floor of the average number of pigeons across all pigeonholes.\n(\"The minimum is at most the mean\" specialized to integers.)\n\nMore formally, given a function `f` between finite types `\u03b1` and `\u03b2` and a number `n` such that\n`card \u03b1 < card \u03b2 * n`, there exists an element `y : \u03b2` such that its preimage has less than `n`\nelements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.exists_le_card_fiber_of_mul_le_card", "description": " The strong pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons and pigeonholes.  Given a function `f`\nbetween finite types `\u03b1` and `\u03b2` and a number `n` such that `card \u03b2 * n \u2264 card \u03b1`, there exists an\nelement `y : \u03b2` such that its preimage has at least `n` elements. See also\n`fintype.exists_lt_card_fiber_of_mul_lt_card` for a stronger statement.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.exists_sum_fiber_le_of_sum_le_nsmul", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons of different weights, non-strict inequality\nversion: there is a pigeonhole with the total weight of pigeons in it less than or equal to `b`\nprovided that the total number of pigeonholes times `b` is greater than or equal to the total weight\nof all pigeons.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_lt_card_fiber_of_mul_lt_card_of_maps_to", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons counted by heads: there is a pigeonhole with\nat least as many pigeons as the ceiling of the average number of pigeons across all pigeonholes.\n(\"The maximum is at least the mean\" specialized to integers.)\n\nMore formally, given a function between finite sets `s` and `t` and a natural number `n` such that\n`card t * n < card s`, there exists `y \u2208 t` such that its preimage in `s` has more than `n`\nelements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_sum_fiber_le_of_maps_to_of_sum_le_nsmul", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons counted by weight, non-strict inequality\nversion: if the total weight of a finite set of pigeons is less than or equal to `n \u2022 b`, and they\nare sorted into `n > 0` pigeonholes, then for some pigeonhole, the total weight of the pigeons in\nthis pigeonhole is less than or equal to `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_sum_fiber_le_of_sum_fiber_nonneg_of_sum_le_nsmul", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons counted by weight, non-strict inequality\nversion: if the total weight of a finite set of pigeons is less than or equal to `n \u2022 b`, they are\nsorted into some pigeonholes, and for all but `n > 0` pigeonholes the total weight of the pigeons\nthere is nonnegative, then for at least one of these `n` pigeonholes, the total weight of the\npigeons in this pigeonhole is less than or equal to `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_le_card_fiber_of_mul_le_card_of_maps_to", "description": " The pigeonhole principle for finitely many pigeons counted by heads: given a function between\nfinite sets `s` and `t` and a natural number `b` such that `card t * n \u2264 card s`, there exists `y \u2208\nt` such that its preimage in `s` has at least `n` elements. See also\n`finset.exists_lt_card_fiber_of_mul_lt_card_of_maps_to` for a stronger statement.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_seq_set", "description": " If we have the additional hypothesis `\u2200\u1d50 x \u2202\u03bc, p x (\u03bb n, f n x)`, this is a measurable set\nwhose complement has measure 0 such that for all `x \u2208 ae_seq_set`, `f i x` is equal to\n`(hf i).mk (f i) x` for all `i` and we have the pointwise property `p x (\u03bb n, f n x)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_seq.ae_seq_set_measurable_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_seq.ae_seq_n_eq_fun_n_ae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_seq.measure_compl_ae_seq_set_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_seq.ae_seq_eq_mk_ae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_seq.ae_seq_eq_fun_of_mem_ae_seq_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_seq.measurable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_seq", "description": " A sequence of measurable functions that are equal to `f` and verify property `p` on the\nmeasurable set `ae_seq_set hf p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_seq.mk_eq_fun_of_mem_ae_seq_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_seq.ae_seq_eq_mk_of_mem_ae_seq_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_seq.supr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_seq.prop_of_mem_ae_seq_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_seq.fun_prop_of_mem_ae_seq_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_seq.ae_seq_eq_fun_ae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.coe_weight_space'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.zero_weight_space_eq_top_of_nilpotent", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.zero_root_space_eq_top_of_nilpotent", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.root_space_comap_eq_weight_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.is_weight_zero_of_nilpotent", "description": " For a non-trivial nilpotent Lie module over a nilpotent Lie algebra, the zero character is a\nweight with respect to the `\u22a4` Lie subalgebra.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.weight_space.is_nilpotent", "description": " By Engel's theorem, when the Lie algebra is Noetherian, the zero weight space of a Noetherian\nLie module is nilpotent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.lie_mem_weight_space_of_mem_weight_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.weight_vector_multiplication", "description": "See also `bourbaki1975b` Chapter VII \u00a71.1, Proposition 2 (ii).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.le_zero_root_subalgebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.coe_root_space_weight_space_product_tmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.is_cartan_of_zero_root_subalgebra_eq", "description": " If the zero root subalgebra of a nilpotent Lie subalgebra `H` is just `H` then `H` is a Cartan\nsubalgebra.\n\nWhen `L` is Noetherian, it follows from Engel's theorem that the converse holds. See\n`lie_algebra.zero_root_subalgebra_eq_iff_is_cartan`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.root_space_product", "description": " Given a nilpotent Lie subalgebra `H \u2286 L` together with `\u03c7\u2081 \u03c7\u2082 : H \u2192 R`, there is a natural\n`R`-bilinear product of root vectors, compatible with the actions of `H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.exists_pre_weight_space_zero_le_ker_of_is_noetherian", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.zero_weight_space_eq_top_of_nilpotent'", "description": "See also the more useful form `lie_module.zero_weight_space_eq_top_of_nilpotent`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.zero_root_subalgebra_normalizer_eq_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.coe_weight_space_of_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.zero_root_subalgebra_eq_iff_is_cartan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.pre_weight_space", "description": " Given a Lie module `M` over a Lie algebra `L`, the pre-weight space of `M` with respect to a\nmap `\u03c7 : L \u2192 R` is the simultaneous generalized eigenspace of the action of all `x : L` on `M`,\nwith eigenvalues `\u03c7 x`.\n\nSee also `lie_module.weight_space`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.weight_space'", "description": " A priori, weight spaces are Lie submodules over the Lie subalgebra `H` used to define them.\nHowever they are naturally Lie submodules over the (in general larger) Lie subalgebra\n`zero_root_subalgebra R L H`. Even though it is often the case that\n`zero_root_subalgebra R L H = H`, it is likely to be useful to have the flexibility not to have\nto invoke this equality (as well as to work more generally).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.root_space_product_tmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.root_space", "description": " Given a nilpotent Lie subalgebra `H \u2286 L`, the root space of a map `\u03c7 : H \u2192 R` is the weight\nspace of `L` regarded as a module of `H` via the adjoint action.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.to_lie_submodule_le_root_space_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.is_nilpotent_to_endomorphism_weight_space_zero", "description": " A (nilpotent) Lie algebra acts nilpotently on the zero weight space of a Noetherian Lie\nmodule.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.mem_weight_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.is_root", "description": " A root of a Lie algebra `L` with respect to a nilpotent subalgebra `H \u2286 L` is a weight of `L`,\nregarded as a module of `H` via the adjoint action.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.lie_mem_pre_weight_space_of_mem_pre_weight_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.weight_space", "description": "If a Lie algebra is nilpotent, then pre-weight spaces are Lie submodules.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.mem_zero_root_subalgebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.root_space_product_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.coe_zero_root_subalgebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.root_space_weight_space_product", "description": " Given a nilpotent Lie subalgebra `H \u2286 L` together with `\u03c7\u2081 \u03c7\u2082 : H \u2192 R`, there is a natural\n`R`-bilinear product of root vectors and weight vectors, compatible with the actions of `H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.is_weight", "description": " Given a Lie module `M` of a Lie algebra `L`, a weight of `M` with respect to a nilpotent\nsubalgebra `H \u2286 L` is a Lie character whose corresponding weight space is non-empty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.root_space_weight_space_product_aux", "description": "Auxiliary definition for `root_space_weight_space_product`,\nwhich is close to the deterministic timeout limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.mem_pre_weight_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.zero_root_subalgebra", "description": " Given a nilpotent Lie subalgebra `H \u2286 L`, the root space of the zero map `0 : H \u2192 R` is a Lie\nsubalgebra of `L`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.zero_root_subalgebra_eq_of_is_cartan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whiskering_left_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whiskering_right_preserves_limits_of_shape", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor_category.prod_preserves_colimits", "description": "If `X \u00d7 -` preserves colimits in `D` for any `X : D`, then the product functor `F \u2a2f -` for\n`F : C \u2964 D` also preserves colimits.\n\nNote this is (mathematically) a special case of the statement that\n\"if limits commute with colimits in `D`, then they do as well in `C \u2964 D`\"\nbut the story in Lean is a bit more complex, and this statement isn't directly a special case.\nThat is, even with a formalised proof of the general statement, there would still need to be some\nwork to convert to this version: namely, the natural isomorphism\n`(evaluation C D).obj k \u22d9 prod.functor.obj (F.obj k) \u2245 prod.functor.obj F \u22d9 (evaluation C D).obj k`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whiskering_right_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preserves_limit_of_Lan_preserves_limit", "description": "If `Lan F.op : (C\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 Type*) \u2964 (D\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 Type*)` preserves limits of shape `J`, so will `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "counterexample.from_Bhavik.K", "description": "  Bhavik Mehta's example.  There are only the initial definitions, but no proofs.  The Type\n`K` is a canonically ordered commutative semiring with the property that `2 * (1/2) \u2264 2 * 1`, even\nthough it is not true that `1/2 \u2264 1`, since `1/2` and `1` are not comparable.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.ex_L.can", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.ex_L.bot_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.Nxzmod_2.mul_lt_mul_of_pos_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.ex_L.le_self_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.from_Bhavik.inhabited_K", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.Nxzmod_2.add_le_add_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.Nxzmod_2.socsN2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.add_self_zmod_2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.Nxzmod_2.le_of_add_le_add_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.ex_L.order_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.from_Bhavik.K.comm_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.Nxzmod_2.prod.zero_le_one_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.ex_L.eq_zero_or_eq_zero_of_mul_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.from_Bhavik.K.preorder", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.Nxzmod_2.csrN2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.ex_L.exists_add_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.ex_L.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.mem_zmod_2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.Nxzmod_2.add_left_cancel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.Nxzmod_2.preN2", "description": " The preorder relation on `\u2115 \u00d7 \u2124/2\u2124` where we only compare the first coordinate,\nexcept that we leave incomparable each pair of elements with the same first component.\nFor instance, `\u2200 \u03b1, \u03b2 \u2208 \u2124/2\u2124`, the inequality `(1,\u03b1) \u2264 (2,\u03b2)` holds,\nwhereas, `\u2200 n \u2208 \u2124`, the elements `(n,0)` and `(n,1)` are incomparable.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.from_Bhavik.rat.has_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.Nxzmod_2.lt_def", "description": "A strict inequality forces the first components to be different.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.ex_L.L_subsemiring", "description": "The subsemiring corresponding to the elements of `L`, used to transfer instances.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.ex_L.add_L", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.ex_L.L", "description": "Initially, `L` was defined as the subsemiring closure of `(1,0)`.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.ex_L.L.ordered_comm_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.Nxzmod_2.mul_lt_mul_of_pos_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.ex_L.mul_L", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.Nxzmod_2.csrN2_1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.ker_closed_complemented_of_finite_dimensional_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.coe_equiv_prod_of_surjective_of_is_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subspace.closed_complemented_of_closed_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subspace.prod_equiv_of_closed_compl", "description": " If `q` is a closed complement of a closed subspace `p`, then `p \u00d7 q` is continuously\nisomorphic to `E`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subspace.coe_prod_equiv_of_closed_compl_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.equiv_prod_of_surjective_of_is_compl_to_linear_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.equiv_prod_of_surjective_of_is_compl_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subspace.linear_proj_of_closed_compl", "description": "Projection to a closed submodule along a closed complement.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subspace.closed_complemented_of_quotient_finite_dimensional", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.equiv_prod_of_surjective_of_is_compl", "description": " If `f : E \u2192L[R] F` and `g : E \u2192L[R] G` are two surjective linear maps and\ntheir kernels are complement of each other, then `x \u21a6 (f x, g x)` defines\na linear equivalence `E \u2243L[R] F \u00d7 G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subspace.coe_continuous_linear_proj_of_closed_compl'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subspace.closed_complemented_iff_has_closed_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subspace.coe_continuous_linear_proj_of_closed_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subspace.coe_prod_equiv_of_closed_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent_iff_pairwise_disjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep_univ_bool", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep.independent_of_univ", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `complete_lattice.independent_iff_sup_indep_univ`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep.product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep_iff_disjoint_erase", "description": "The RHS looks like the definition of `complete_lattice.independent`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent.set_independent_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.set_independent.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent.injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep.pairwise_disjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent.sup_indep", "description": "**Alias** of the forward direction of `complete_lattice.independent_iff_sup_indep`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep.independent", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `complete_lattice.independent_iff_sup_indep`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent_pempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.set_independent_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent.comp'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent.sup_indep_univ", "description": "**Alias** of the forward direction of `complete_lattice.independent_iff_sup_indep_univ`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent_iff_sup_indep", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent_def''", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.set_independent", "description": "An independent set of elements in a complete lattice is one in which every element is disjoint\n from the `Sup` of the rest.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep", "description": " Supremum independence of finite sets. We avoid the \"obvious\" definition using `s.erase i`\nbecause `erase` would require decidable equality on `\u03b9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent", "description": "An independent indexed family of elements in a complete lattice is one in which every element\n is disjoint from the `supr` of the rest.\n\n Example: an indexed family of non-zero elements in a\n vector space is linearly independent iff the indexed family of subspaces they generate is\n independent in this sense.\n\n Example: an indexed family of submodules of a module is independent in this sense if\n and only the natural map from the direct sum of the submodules to the module is injective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.set_independent_iff_pairwise_disjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent_pair", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent.comp", "description": " Composing an independent indexed family with an injective function on the index results in\nanother indepedendent indexed family.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep_attach", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep.attach", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.set_independent.pairwise_disjoint", "description": "If the elements of a set are independent, then any pair within that set is disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep.sup", "description": "Bind operation for `sup_indep`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent.pairwise_disjoint", "description": "If the elements of a set are independent, then any pair within that set is disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent_iff_sup_indep_univ", "description": "A variant of `complete_lattice.independent_iff_sup_indep` for `fintype`s.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep.image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.set_independent_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent.map_order_iso", "description": " Composing an indepedent indexed family with an order isomorphism on the elements results in\nanother indepedendent indexed family.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep_product_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep.decidable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.pairwise_disjoint.sup_indep", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `finset.sup_indep_iff_pairwise_disjoint`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent_def'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep_univ_fin_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep_iff_pairwise_disjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep.sigma", "description": "Bind operation for `sup_indep`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep.bUnion", "description": "Bind operation for `sup_indep`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent.disjoint_bsupr", "description": " If the elements of a set are independent, then any element is disjoint from the `supr` of some\nsubset of the rest.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.set_independent.disjoint_Sup", "description": " If the elements of a set are independent, then any element is disjoint from the `Sup` of some\nsubset of the rest.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep_pair", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep.le_sup_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.set_independent_pair", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.pairwise_disjoint.set_independent", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `complete_lattice.set_independent_iff_pairwise_disjoint`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent_map_order_iso_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_lattice.independent_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_indep.subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.fpower_series_radius", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.uncurry_bilinear", "description": " Reinterpret a bilinear map `f : E \u2192L[\ud835\udd5c] F \u2192L[\ud835\udd5c] G` as a multilinear map\n`(E \u00d7 F) [\u00d72]\u2192L[\ud835\udd5c] G`. This multilinear map is the second term in the formal\nmultilinear series expansion of `uncurry f`. It is given by\n`f.uncurry_bilinear ![(x, y), (x', y')] = f x y'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.uncurry_bilinear_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.has_fpower_series_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.fpower_series_bilinear_radius", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.fpower_series", "description": " Formal power series of a continuous linear map `f : E \u2192L[\ud835\udd5c] F` at `x : E`:\n`f y = f x + f (y - x)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.has_fpower_series_at_bilinear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.has_fpower_series_on_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.fpower_series_bilinear", "description": "Formal multilinear series expansion of a bilinear function `f : E \u2192L[\ud835\udd5c] F \u2192L[\ud835\udd5c] G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.has_fpower_series_on_ball_bilinear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.analytic_at_bilinear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.analytic_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.fpower_series_apply_add_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Gromov_Hausdorff.premetric_optimal_GH_dist", "description": " With the optimal candidate, construct a premetric space structure on `X \u2295 Y`, on which the\npredistance is given by the candidate. Then, we will identify points at `0` predistance\nto obtain a genuine metric space", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Gromov_Hausdorff.candidates_b_dist_mem_candidates_b", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Gromov_Hausdorff.candidates_b_dist", "description": "The distance on `X \u2295 Y` as a candidate", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Gromov_Hausdorff.candidates_b_of_candidates_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Gromov_Hausdorff.Hausdorff_dist_optimal_le_HD", "description": " For any candidate `f`, `HD(f)` is larger than or equal to the Hausdorff distance in the\noptimal coupling. This follows from the fact that HD of the optimal candidate is exactly\nthe Hausdorff distance in the optimal coupling, although we only prove here the inequality\nwe need.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Gromov_Hausdorff.candidates", "description": " The set of functions on `X \u2295 Y` that are candidates distances to realize the\nminimum of the Hausdorff distances between `X` and `Y` in a coupling", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Gromov_Hausdorff.isometry_optimal_GH_injr", "description": "The injection of `Y` in the optimal coupling between `X` and `Y` is an isometry.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Gromov_Hausdorff.compact_space_optimal_GH_coupling", "description": "The optimal coupling between two compact spaces `X` and `Y` is still a compact space", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Gromov_Hausdorff.HD_candidates_b_dist_le", "description": " Explicit bound on `HD (dist)`. This means that when looking for minimizers it will\nbe sufficient to look for functions with `HD(f)` bounded by this bound.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Gromov_Hausdorff.candidates_b_of_candidates", "description": "candidates give rise to elements of bounded_continuous_functions", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Gromov_Hausdorff.optimal_GH_coupling", "description": "A metric space which realizes the optimal coupling between `X` and `Y`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Gromov_Hausdorff.optimal_GH_injl", "description": "Injection of `X` in the optimal coupling between `X` and `Y`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Gromov_Hausdorff.HD", "description": " We will then choose the candidate minimizing the Hausdorff distance. Except that we are not\nin a metric space setting, so we need to define our custom version of Hausdorff distance,\ncalled HD, and prove its basic properties.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Gromov_Hausdorff.isometry_optimal_GH_injl", "description": "The injection of `X` in the optimal coupling between `X` and `Y` is an isometry.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Gromov_Hausdorff.optimal_GH_coupling.metric_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Gromov_Hausdorff.optimal_GH_injr", "description": "Injection of `Y` in the optimal coupling between `X` and `Y`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Gromov_Hausdorff.HD_below_aux2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Gromov_Hausdorff.HD_below_aux1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon.units", "description": "The functor taking a monoid to its subgroup of units.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon.units_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.category_theory.forget.category_theory.is_right_adjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon.units", "description": "The functor taking a monoid to its subgroup of units.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon.units.category_theory.is_right_adjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon.units.category_theory.is_right_adjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.free_map_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.forget\u2082_Mon_adj", "description": "The forgetful-units adjunction between `Group` and `Mon`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.adj", "description": "The free-forgetful adjunction for abelian groups.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon.units_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup.forget\u2082_CommMon_adj", "description": "The forgetful-units adjunction between `CommGroup` and `CommMon`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.adj", "description": "The free-forgetful adjunction for groups.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abelianize_adj", "description": "The abelianization-forgetful adjuction from `Group` to `CommGroup`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abelianize", "description": "The abelianization functor `Group \u2964 CommGroup` sending a group `G` to its abelianization `G\u1d43\u1d47`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon.units_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.free", "description": "The free functor `Type u \u2964 Group` sending a type `X` to the free group with generators `x : X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon.units_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.category_theory.forget.category_theory.is_right_adjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.free", "description": "The free functor `Type u \u2964 AddCommGroup` sending a type `X` to the\nfree abelian group with generators `x : X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.free_obj_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.small_category_small_model", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs", "description": "We define a type alias `shrink_homs C` for `C`. When we have `locally_small.{w} C`,\nwe'll put a `category.{w}` instance on `shrink_homs C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.equivalence_functor_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.essentially_small_of_small", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.equivalence", "description": "The categorical equivalence between `C` and `shrink_homs C`, when `C` is locally small.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.from_shrink_homs", "description": "Help the typechecker by explicitly translating from `shrink_homs C` to `C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.locally_small_of_essentially_small", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.functor_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.equivalence_unit_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.to_shrink_homs", "description": "Help the typechecker by explicitly translating from `C` to `shrink_homs C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.category_theory.category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.essentially_small.mk'", "description": "Constructor for `essentially_small C` from an explicit small category witness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.inverse_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.locally_small_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.locally_small_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.equivalence_inverse_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.category_theory.category_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.inverse_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.locally_small", "description": "A category is `w`-locally small if every hom set is `w`-small.\n\nSee `shrink_homs C` for a category instance where every hom set has been replaced by a small model.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.locally_small.hom_small", "description": "A category is `w`-locally small if every hom set is `w`-small.\n\nSee `shrink_homs C` for a category instance where every hom set has been replaced by a small model.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.inverse", "description": "Implementation of `shrink_homs.equivalence`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.locally_small_of_thin", "description": "Any thin category is locally small.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.small_model", "description": "An arbitrarily chosen small model for an essentially small category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.quiver.hom.small", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.essentially_small", "description": " A category is `essentially_small.{w}` if there exists\nan equivalence to some `S : Type w` with `[small_category S]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.essentially_small.equiv_small_category", "description": " A category is `essentially_small.{w}` if there exists\nan equivalence to some `S : Type w` with `[small_category S]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.equivalence_inverse_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.to_from", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.functor", "description": "Implementation of `shrink_homs.equivalence`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.functor_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.essentially_small_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.from_to", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.category_theory.category_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.equivalence_counit_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.essentially_small_iff", "description": "A category is essentially small if and only if\nthe underlying type of its skeleton (i.e. the \"set\" of isomorphism classes) is small,\nand it is locally small.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.equivalence_functor_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.equivalence_counit_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.equiv_small_model", "description": "The (noncomputable) categorical equivalence between\nan essentially small category and its small model.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.essentially_small_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.essentially_small_iff_of_thin", "description": "A thin category is essentially small if and only if the underlying type of its skeleton is small.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.shrink_homs.equivalence_unit_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_polynomial_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "X_in_terms_of_W_vars_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_polynomial_zmod_self", "description": "Over the ring `zmod (p^(n+1))`, we produce the `n+1`st Witt polynomial\nby expanding the `n`th Witt polynomial by `p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_polynomial_vars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "X_in_terms_of_W_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_polynomial_vars_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bind\u2081_X_in_terms_of_W_witt_polynomial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "constant_coeff_X_in_terms_of_W", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "map_witt_polynomial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "X_in_terms_of_W", "description": " The `X_in_terms_of_W p R n` is the polynomial on the basis of Witt polynomials\nthat corresponds to the ordinary `X n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_polynomial", "description": " `witt_polynomial p R n` is the `n`-th Witt polynomial\nwith respect to a prime `p` with coefficients in a commutative ring `R`.\nIt is defined as:\n\n`\u2211_{i \u2264 n} p^i X_i^{p^{n-i}} \u2208 R[X_0, X_1, X_2, \u2026]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "X_in_terms_of_W_vars_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "X_in_terms_of_W_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bind\u2081_witt_polynomial_X_in_terms_of_W", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_polynomial_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "constant_coeff_witt_polynomial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "aeval_witt_polynomial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_polynomial_eq_sum_C_mul_X_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "X_in_terms_of_W_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.cesaro_smul", "description": "The Cesaro average of a converging sequence converges to the same limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.cesaro", "description": "The Cesaro average of a converging sequence converges to the same limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.is_bounded_under.is_o_sub_self_inv", "description": " If `f : \ud835\udd5c \u2192 E` is bounded in a punctured neighborhood of `a`, then `f(x) = o((x - a)\u207b\u00b9)` as\n`x \u2192 a`, `x \u2260 a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "asymptotics.is_o_pow_pow_at_top_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "asymptotics.is_o_sum_range_of_tendsto_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_zpow_at_top_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pow_div_pow_eventually_eq_at_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_pow_div_pow_at_top_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "asymptotics.is_O.trans_tendsto_norm_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_pow_div_pow_at_top_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "asymptotics.is_o.sum_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pow_div_pow_eventually_eq_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.sub_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.head_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.empty_add_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.head_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_add_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.vec_cons_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.head_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.range_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_vec_alt1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_vec_bit0_eq_alt0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.empty_vec_alt0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_head_tail", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.vec_append_apply_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.tail_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.tail_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.vec_append_eq_ite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_fin_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.neg_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_sub_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.smul_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.smul_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_val_zero'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_val_succ'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.empty_val'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.empty_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.vec_alt0", "description": " `vec_alt0 v` gives a vector with half the length of `v`, with\nonly alternate elements (even-numbered).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.add_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.vec_alt1", "description": " `vec_alt1 v` gives a vector with half the length of `v`, with\nonly alternate elements (odd-numbered).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.head_fin_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_nonzero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.vec_empty", "description": "`![]` is the vector with no entries.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_zero_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_fin.reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.tail_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.tail_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.tail_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.vec_single_eq_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_fin.has_repr", "description": " Use `![...]` notation for displaying a vector `fin n \u2192 \u03b1`, for example:\n\n```lean\n#eval ![1, 2] + ![3, 4] -- ![4, 6]\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_val_fin_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.vec_head", "description": "`vec_head v` gives the first entry of the vector `v`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_fin.to_pexpr", "description": "Convert a vector of pexprs to the pexpr constructing that vector.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_val_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.zero_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.vec_cons", "description": " `vec_cons h t` prepends an entry `h` to a vector `t`.\n\nThe inverse functions are `vec_head` and `vec_tail`.\nThe notation `![a, b, ...]` expands to `vec_cons a (vec_cons b ...)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.range_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.vec_tail", "description": "`vec_tail v` gives a vector consisting of all entries of `v` except the first", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.head_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.vec_append", "description": " `vec_append ho u v` appends two vectors of lengths `m` and `n` to produce\none of length `o = m + n`. This is a variant of `fin.append` with an additional `ho` argument,\nwhich provides control of definitional equality for the vector length.\n\nThis turns out to be helpful when providing simp lemmas to reduce `![a, b, c] n`, and also means\nthat `vec_append ho u v 0` is valid. `fin.append u v 0` is not valid in this case because there is\nno `has_zero (fin (m + n))` instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.empty_vec_append", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.range_cons_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.vec_alt1_vec_append", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.empty_vec_alt1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.empty_sub_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_val_one", "description": "`![a, b, ...] 1` is equal to `b`.\n\n The simplifier needs a special lemma for length `\u2265 2`, in addition to\n `cons_val_succ`, because `1 : fin 1 = 0 : fin 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_vec_append", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.head_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_vec_alt0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.vec_head_vec_alt0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.vec_alt0_vec_append", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_val_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.range_cons_cons_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.vec_head_vec_alt1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_vec_bit1_eq_alt1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.neg_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cons_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_bounded_of_bounded_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_bounded.of_compl", "description": "**Alias** of the forward direction of `bornology.is_bounded_compl_iff`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "punit.bornology", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_bounded_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_cobounded_compl_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_bounded.all", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_bounded_bUnion_finset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.comap_cobounded_le_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.cobounded_eq_bot_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_cobounded_bInter_finset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_cobounded_sInter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_cobounded.superset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_bounded_Union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_bounded.union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.sUnion_bounded_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_bounded_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_cobounded.all", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.cofinite", "description": "The cofinite filter as a bornology", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology", "description": " A **bornology** on a type `\u03b1` is a filter of cobounded sets which contains the cofinite filter.\nSuch spaces are equivalently specified by their bounded sets, see `bornology.of_bounded`\nand `bornology.ext_iff_is_bounded`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.cobounded", "description": " A **bornology** on a type `\u03b1` is a filter of cobounded sets which contains the cofinite filter.\nSuch spaces are equivalently specified by their bounded sets, see `bornology.of_bounded`\nand `bornology.ext_iff_is_bounded`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.le_cofinite", "description": " A **bornology** on a type `\u03b1` is a filter of cobounded sets which contains the cofinite filter.\nSuch spaces are equivalently specified by their bounded sets, see `bornology.of_bounded`\nand `bornology.ext_iff_is_bounded`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.ext_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_bounded.subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.of_bounded_cobounded_sets", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_cobounded_of_bounded_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.ext_iff_is_bounded", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_cobounded_Inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_bounded_compl_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_bounded", "description": "`is_bounded` is the predicate that `s` is bounded relative to the ambient bornology on `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_bounded_bUnion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.of_bounded", "description": " A constructor for bornologies by specifying the bounded sets,\nand showing that they satisfy the appropriate conditions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_bounded_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_cobounded_bInter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_cobounded.inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_bounded_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_cobounded_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_cobounded.of_compl", "description": "**Alias** of the forward direction of `bornology.is_cobounded_compl_iff`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.of_bounded'", "description": " A constructor for bornologies by specifying the bounded sets,\nand showing that they satisfy the appropriate conditions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_cobounded_inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bounded_space", "description": "A space with a `bornology` is a **bounded space** if `set.univ : set \u03b1` is bounded.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bounded_space.bounded_univ", "description": "A space with a `bornology` is a **bounded space** if `set.univ : set \u03b1` is bounded.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_bounded_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_bounded.compl", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `bornology.is_cobounded_compl_iff`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.of_bounded'_cobounded_sets", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_cobounded", "description": " `is_cobounded` is the predicate that `s` is in the filter of cobounded sets in the ambient\nbornology on `\u03b1`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_cobounded_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_bounded_sUnion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.is_cobounded.compl", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `bornology.is_bounded_compl_iff`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite.is_bounded", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bornology.cobounded_eq_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_n_pis_whnf", "description": "`open_n_pis_whnf e n md unfold_ginductive` instantiates the first `n` \u03a0 binders\nof `e` with fresh local constants. The leading \u03a0 binders of `e` are matched up\nto normalisation with transparency `md`. `unfold_ginductive` determines whether\nconstructors of generalised inductive types are unfolded during normalisation.\nThis is `open_pis_whnf` but restricted to `n` binders.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_lambdas_whnf", "description": "`open_lambdas_whnf e md unfold_ginductive` instantiates all leading \u03bb binders of\n`e` with fresh local constants. The leading \u03bb binders of `e` are matched up to\nnormalisation with transparency `md`. `unfold_ginductive` determines whether\nconstructors of generalised inductive types are unfolded during normalisation.\nThis is `open_lambdas` up to normalisation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_binder_replacement", "description": "`mk_binder_replacement local_or_meta b` creates an expression that can be used\nto replace the binder `b`. If `local_or_meta` is true, we create a fresh local\nconstant with `b`'s display name, `binder_info` and type; otherwise a fresh\nmetavariable with `b`'s type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.get_pi_binders", "description": "`get_pi_binders e` instantiates all leading \u03a0 binders of `e` with fresh local\nconstants (like `open_pis`). Returns the remainder of `e` and information about\nthe binders that were instantiated (but not the new local constants). See also\n`expr.pi_binders` (which produces open terms).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_n_pis_metas", "description": "`open_n_pis_metas e n` instantiates the first `n` \u03a0 binders of `e` with fresh\nmetavariables. Returns the metavariables and the remainder of `e`. This is\n`open_n_pis` but with metavariables instead of local constants.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_n_pis", "description": "`open_n_pis e n` instantiates the first `n` \u03a0 binders of `e` with fresh local\nconstants. Returns the local constants and the remainder of `e`. Fails if\n`e` does not start with at least `n` \u03a0 binders. This is `open_pis` but limited\nto `n` binders.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_n_pis_metas_whnf", "description": "`open_n_pis_metas_whnf e n md unfold_ginductive` instantiates the first `n` \u03a0\nbinders of `e` with fresh metavariables. The leading \u03a0 binders of `e` are\nmatched up to normalisation with transparency `md`. `unfold_ginductive`\ndetermines whether constructors of generalised inductive types are unfolded\nduring normalisation. This is `open_pis_metas_whnf` but restricted to `n`\nbinders.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.get_pi_binders_nondep", "description": "`get_pi_binders_nondep e` instantiates all leading \u03a0 binders of `e` with fresh\nlocal constants (like `open_pis`). Returns the remainder of `e` and information\nabout the *nondependent* binders that were instantiated (but not the new local\nconstants). A nondependent binder is one that does not appear later in the\nexpression. Also returns the index of each returned binder (starting at 0).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_n_pis_metas'", "description": "`open_n_pis_metas' e n` instantiates the first `n` leading \u03a0 binders of `e` with\nfresh metavariables. It returns the remainder of the expression and, for each\nbinder, the corresponding metavariable, the name of the bound variable and the\nbinder's `binder_info`. Fails if `e` does not have at least `n` leading \u03a0\nbinders.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_pis_whnf_dep", "description": "`open_pis_whnf_dep e` instantiates all leading \u03a0 binders of `e` with fresh local\nconstants (like `tactic.open_pis`). It returns the remainder of the expression\nand, for each binder, the corresponding local constant and whether the binder\nwas dependent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_pis_whnf", "description": "`open_pis_whnf e md unfold_ginductive` instantiates all leading \u03a0 binders of `e`\nwith fresh local constants. The leading \u03a0 binders of `e` are matched up to\nnormalisation with transparency `md`. `unfold_ginductive` determines whether\nconstructors of generalised inductive types are unfolded during normalisation.\nThis is `open_pis` up to normalisation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_n_lambdas_whnf", "description": "`open_n_lambdas_whnf e md unfold_ginductive` instantiates the first `n` \u03bb\nbinders of `e` with fresh local constants. The \u03bb binders are matched up to\nnormalisation with transparency `md`. `unfold_ginductive` determines whether\nconstructors of generalised inductive types are unfolded during normalisation.\nFails if `e` does not start with `n` \u03bb binders (after normalisation). This is\n`open_n_lambdas` up to normalisation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_pis_metas_whnf", "description": "`open_pis_metas_whnf e md unfold_ginductive` instantiates all leading \u03a0 binders\nof `e` with fresh metavariables. The leading \u03a0 binders of `e` are matched up to\nnormalisation with transparency `md`. `unfold_ginductive` determines whether\nconstructors of generalised inductive types are unfolded during normalisation.\nThis is `open_pis_metas` up to normalisation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_lambdas_metas_whnf", "description": "`open_lambdas_metas_whnf e md unfold_ginductive` instantiates all leading \u03bb\nbinders of `e` with fresh metavariables. The leading \u03bb binders of `e` are\nmatched up to normalisation with transparency `md`. `unfold_ginductive`\ndetermines whether constructors of generalised inductive types are unfolded\nduring normalisation. This is `open_lambdas_metas` up to normalisation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_pis_metas", "description": "`open_pis_metas e` instantiates all leading \u03a0 binders of `e` with fresh\nmetavariables. Returns the metavariables and the remainder of `e`. This is\n`open_pis` but with metavariables instead of local constants.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_n_binders", "description": "`open_n_binders do_whnf pis_or_lambdas local_or_metas e n` is like\n`open_binders do_whnf pis_or_lambdas local_or_metas e`, but it matches exactly `n`\nleading \u03a0/\u03bb binders of `e`. If `e` does not start with at least `n` \u03a0/\u03bb binders,\n(after normalisation, if `do_whnf` is given), the tactic fails.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_n_lambdas_metas_whnf", "description": "`open_n_lambdas_metas_whnf e md unfold_ginductive` instantiates the first `n` \u03bb\nbinders of `e` with fresh metavariables. The \u03bb binders are matched up to\nnormalisation with transparency `md`. `unfold_ginductive` determines whether\nconstructors of generalised inductive types are unfolded during normalisation.\nFails if `e` does not start with `n` \u03bb binders (after normalisation). This is\n`open_n_lambdas_metas` up to normalisation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_lambdas_metas", "description": "`open_lambdas_metas e` instantiates all leading \u03bb binders of `e` with fresh\nmetavariables. Returns the new metavariables and the remainder of `e`. This is\n`open_lambdas` but with metavariables instead of local constants.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_n_lambdas", "description": "`open_n_lambdas e n` instantiates the first `n` \u03bb binders of `e` with fresh\nlocal constants. Returns the new local constants and the remainder of `e`. Fails\nif `e` does not start with at least `n` \u03bb binders. This is `open_lambdas` but\nrestricted to the first `n` binders.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.get_binder", "description": "`get_binder do_whnf pi_or_lambda e` matches `e` of the form `\u03bb x, e'` or\n`\u03a0 x, e`. Returns information about the leading binder (its name, `binder_info`,\ntype and body), or `none` if `e` does not start with a binder.\n\nIf `do_whnf = some (md, unfold_ginductive)`, then `e` is weak head normalised\nwith transparency `md` before matching on it. `unfold_ginductive` controls\nwhether constructors of generalised inductive data types are unfolded during\nnormalisation.\n\nIf `pi_or_lambda` is `tt`, we match a leading \u03a0 binder; otherwise a leading \u03bb\nbinder.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_pis", "description": "`open_pis e` instantiates all leading \u03a0 binders of `e` with fresh local\nconstants. Returns the local constants and the remainder of `e`. This is an\nalias for `tactic.mk_local_pis`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_binders", "description": "`open_binders` is a generalisation of functions like `open_pis`,\n`mk_meta_lambdas` etc. `open_binders do_whnf pis_or_lamdas local_or_metas e`\nproceeds as follows:\n\n- Match a leading \u03bb or \u03a0 binder using `get_binder do_whnf pis_or_lambdas`.\n  See `get_binder` for details. Return `e` unchanged (and an empty list) if\n  `e` does not start with a \u03bb/\u03a0.\n- Construct a replacement for the bound variable using\n  `mk_binder_replacement locals_or_metas`. See `mk_binder_replacement` for\n  details. Replace the bound variable with this replacement in the binder body.\n- Recurse into the binder body.\n\nReturns the constructed replacement expressions and `e` without its leading\nbinders.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_n_lambdas_metas", "description": "`open_n_lambdas_metas e n` instantiates the first `n` \u03bb binders of `e` with\nfresh metavariables. Returns the new metavariables and the remainder of `e`.\nFails if `e` does not start with at least `n` \u03bb binders. This is\n`open_lambdas_metas` but restricted to the first `n` binders.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.open_lambdas", "description": "`open_lambdas e` instantiates all leading \u03bb binders of `e` with fresh local\nconstants. Returns the new local constants and the remainder of `e`. This is\n`open_pis` but for \u03bb binders rather than \u03a0 binders.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.add_equiv_prod_direct_sum_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.support_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.mk", "description": " `mk \u03b2 s x` is the element of `\u2a01 i, \u03b2 i` that is zero outside `s`\nand has coefficient `x i` for `i` in `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum", "description": " `direct_sum \u03b2` is the direct sum of a family of additive commutative monoids `\u03b2 i`.\n\nNote: `open_locale direct_sum` will enable the notation `\u2a01 i, \u03b2 i` for `direct_sum \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.coe_add_monoid_hom_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.has_coe_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.add_hom_ext'", "description": " If two additive homomorphisms from `\u2a01 i, \u03b2 i` are equal on each `of \u03b2 i y`,\nthen they are equal.\n\nSee note [partially-applied ext lemmas].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.to_add_monoid_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.sigma_curry_equiv", "description": "The natural map between `\u2a01 (i : \u03a3 i, \u03b1 i), \u03b4 i.1 i.2` and `\u2a01 i (j : \u03b1 i), \u03b4 i j`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.coe_add_monoid_hom", "description": " The canonical embedding from `\u2a01 i, A i` to `M` where `A` is a collection of `add_submonoid M`\nindexed by `\u03b9`.\n\nWhen `S = submodule _ M`, this is available as a `linear_map`, `direct_sum.coe_linear_map`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.of", "description": "`of i` is the natural inclusion map from `\u03b2 i` to `\u2a01 i, \u03b2 i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.add_hom_ext", "description": " If two additive homomorphisms from `\u2a01 i, \u03b2 i` are equal on each `of \u03b2 i y`,\nthen they are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.sum_support_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.from_add_monoid_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.sub_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.support_of_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.support_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.unique_of_is_empty", "description": "A direct sum over an empty type is trivial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.is_internal", "description": " The `direct_sum` formed by a collection of additive submonoids (or subgroups, or submodules) of\n`M` is said to be internal if the canonical map `(\u2a01 i, A i) \u2192+ M` is bijective.\n\nFor the alternate statement in terms of independence and spanning, see\n`direct_sum.subgroup_is_internal_iff_independent_and_supr_eq_top` and\n`direct_sum.is_internal_submodule_iff_independent_and_supr_eq_top`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.from_add_monoid_of_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.mk_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.add_equiv_prod_direct_sum_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.of_eq_same", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.sigma_curry_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.sigma_uncurry", "description": "The natural map between `\u2a01 i (j : \u03b1 i), \u03b4 i j` and `\u03a0\u2080 (i : \u03a3 i, \u03b1 i), \u03b4 i.1 i.2`, inverse of\n`curry`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.equiv_congr_left_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.is_internal.add_submonoid_supr_eq_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.to_add_monoid", "description": " `to_add_monoid \u03c6` is the natural homomorphism from `\u2a01 i, \u03b2 i` to `\u03b3`\ninduced by a family `\u03c6` of homomorphisms `\u03b2 i \u2192 \u03b3`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.add_equiv_prod_direct_sum", "description": "Isomorphism obtained by separating the term of index `none` of a direct sum over `option \u03b9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.id", "description": "The natural equivalence between `\u2a01 _ : \u03b9, M` and `M` when `unique \u03b9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.add_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.add_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.of_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.set_to_set", "description": " `set_to_set \u03b2 S T h` is the natural homomorphism `\u2a01 (i : S), \u03b2 i \u2192 \u2a01 (i : T), \u03b2 i`,\nwhere `h : S \u2286 T`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.coe_of_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.sigma_uncurry_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.from_add_monoid", "description": " `from_add_monoid \u03c6` is the natural homomorphism from `\u03b3` to `\u2a01 i, \u03b2 i`\ninduced by a family `\u03c6` of homomorphisms `\u03b3 \u2192 \u03b2 i`.\n\nNote that this is not an isomorphism. Not every homomorphism `\u03b3 \u2192+ \u2a01 i, \u03b2 i` arises in this way.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.equiv_congr_left", "description": "Reindexing terms of a direct sum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.zero_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.induction_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.of_eq_of_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.sigma_curry", "description": "The natural map between `\u2a01 (i : \u03a3 i, \u03b1 i), \u03b4 i.1 i.2` and `\u2a01 i (j : \u03b1 i), \u03b4 i j`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.to_add_monoid.unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_def'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_orthonormal_basis_apply_of_orthogonal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_orthogonal", "description": " **Gram-Schmidt Orthogonalisation**:\n`gram_schmidt` produces an orthogonal system of vectors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "span_gram_schmidt_Iio", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_normed", "description": " the normalized `gram_schmidt`\n(i.e each vector in `gram_schmidt_normed` has unit length.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_normed_unit_length'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_def", "description": "This lemma uses `\u2211 i in` instead of `\u2211 i :`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_span_gram_schmidt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_mem_span", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_ne_zero_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_normed_unit_length_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_orthonormal_basis_inv_triangular'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_orthonormal_basis", "description": " Given an indexed family `f : \u03b9 \u2192 E` of vectors in an inner product space `E`, for which the\nsize of the index set is the dimension of `E`, produce an orthonormal basis for `E` which agrees\nwith the orthonormal set produced by the Gram-Schmidt orthonormalization process on the elements of\n`\u03b9` for which this process gives a nonzero number.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "span_gram_schmidt_normed_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_basis", "description": "When given a basis, `gram_schmidt` produces a basis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_orthonormal_basis_inv_block_triangular", "description": " Given an indexed family `f : \u03b9 \u2192 E` of vectors in an inner product space `E`, for which the\nsize of the index set is the dimension of `E`, the matrix of coefficients of `f` with respect to the\northonormal basis `gram_schmidt_orthonormal_basis` constructed from `f` is upper-triangular.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_orthonormal", "description": " **Gram-Schmidt Orthonormalization**:\n`gram_schmidt_normed` applied to a linearly independent set of vectors produces an orthornormal\nsystem of vectors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_gram_schmidt_orthonormal_basis_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_def''", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_pairwise_orthogonal", "description": "This is another version of `gram_schmidt_orthogonal` using `pairwise` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_normed_unit_length", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_orthonormal_basis_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_orthonormal_basis_inv_triangular", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_orthonormal'", "description": " **Gram-Schmidt Orthonormalization**:\n`gram_schmidt_normed` produces an orthornormal system of vectors after removing the vectors which\nbecome zero in the process.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt", "description": " The Gram-Schmidt process takes a set of vectors as input\nand outputs a set of orthogonal vectors which have the same span.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_of_orthogonal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_inv_triangular", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_triangular", "description": "`gram_schmidt` produces a triangular matrix of vectors when given a basis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_gram_schmidt_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_orthonormal_basis_det", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "span_gram_schmidt_Iic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "span_gram_schmidt", "description": "`gram_schmidt` preserves span of vectors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_linear_independent", "description": "`gram_schmidt` produces linearly independent vectors when given linearly independent vectors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "span_gram_schmidt_normed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gram_schmidt_ne_zero", "description": " If the input vectors of `gram_schmidt` are linearly independent,\nthen the output vectors are non-zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_monoidal_subcategory.map_to_lax_monoidal_functor_to_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_monoidal_subcategory.map", "description": " An implication of predicates `P \u2192 P'` induces a monoidal functor between full monoidal\nsubcategories.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_braided_subcategory", "description": "The braided structure on a full subcategory inherited by the braided structure on `C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_monoidal_closed_subcategory_ihom_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_monoidal_subcategory.map_full", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.category_theory.full_subcategory.category_theory.monoidal_preadditive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_braided_subcategory.map_full", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_monoidal_subcategory_inclusion", "description": "The forgetful monoidal functor from a full monoidal subcategory into the original category\n(\"forgetting\" the condition).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.category_theory.full_subcategory.category_theory.monoidal_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_monoidal_subcategory.map_to_lax_monoidal_functor_\u03bc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_monoidal_subcategory.full", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_monoidal_subcategory_inclusion_to_lax_monoidal_functor_to_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_monoidal_subcategory.map_to_lax_monoidal_functor_\u03b5", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_monoidal_closed_subcategory", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_braided_subcategory.map", "description": " An implication of predicates `P \u2192 P'` induces a braided functor between full braided\nsubcategories.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_monoidal_subcategory_inclusion_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.monoidal_predicate", "description": "A property `C \u2192 Prop` is a monoidal predicate if it is closed under `\ud835\udfd9_` and `\u2297`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.monoidal_predicate.prop_id'", "description": "A property `C \u2192 Prop` is a monoidal predicate if it is closed under `\ud835\udfd9_` and `\u2297`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.monoidal_predicate.prop_tensor'", "description": "A property `C \u2192 Prop` is a monoidal predicate if it is closed under `\ud835\udfd9_` and `\u2297`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_braided_subcategory.map_to_monoidal_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_symmetric_subcategory", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_monoidal_subcategory", "description": "When `P` is a monoidal predicate, the full subcategory for `P` inherits the monoidal structure of\n  `C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_braided_subcategory.full", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_monoidal_subcategory.faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_monoidal_subcategory_inclusion_to_lax_monoidal_functor_\u03bc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_monoidal_closed_subcategory_ihom_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.closed_predicate.prop_ihom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_monoidal_subcategory_inclusion_additive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_monoidal_subcategory.map_faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_braided_subcategory.map_faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.monoidal_predicate.prop_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_braided_subcategory_inclusion_to_monoidal_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_braided_subcategory.faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_monoidal_subcategory_inclusion_to_lax_monoidal_functor_\u03b5", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.closed_predicate", "description": "A property `C \u2192 Prop` is a closed predicate if it is closed under taking internal homs", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.closed_predicate.prop_ihom'", "description": "A property `C \u2192 Prop` is a closed predicate if it is closed under taking internal homs", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.monoidal_predicate.prop_tensor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.full_braided_subcategory_inclusion", "description": "The forgetful braided functor from a full braided subcategory into the original category\n(\"forgetting\" the condition).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.zero_ne_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mul_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.of_int_eq_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.add_left_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk", "description": "Form the quotient `n / d` where `n d : \u2124`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.num_denom_cases_on", "description": " Define a (dependent) function or prove `\u2200 r : \u211a, p r` by dealing with rational\nnumbers of the form `n /. d` with `0 < d` and coprime `n`, `d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_neg_one_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_pnat_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.coe_nat_eq_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.has_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.add_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.has_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.add_left_cancel_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mul_num_denom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.eq_iff_mul_eq_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.num_neg_eq_neg_num", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_div_mk_cancel_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.num_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.zero_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.add_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_nat", "description": "Form the quotient `n / d` where `n:\u2124` and `d:\u2115`. In the case `d = 0`, we\n define `n / 0 = 0` by convention.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.add_comm_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mul", "description": "Multiplication of rational numbers. Use `(*)` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.nontrivial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.is_domain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.neg_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.coe_int_denom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_one_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.neg", "description": "Negation of rational numbers. Use `-r` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.add_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.coe_int_div_eq_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.can_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.inv_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.add_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.denom_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mul_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.add_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.comm_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_num_ne_zero_of_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.comm_group_with_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.add_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.inv", "description": "Inverse rational number. Use `r\u207b\u00b9` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_div_mk_cancel_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mul_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.coe_int_eq_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.add_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.coe_int_num", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_zero_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.of_int", "description": "Embed an integer as a rational number. You should use the coercion `coe : \u2124 \u2192 \u211a` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.denom_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.num_div_denom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.num_ne_zero_of_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.add", "description": "Addition of rational numbers. Use `(+)` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.has_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.denom_eq_one_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_ne_zero_of_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.has_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.div_mk_div_cancel_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.lift_binop_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.div_num_denom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.has_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_nat_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.add_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.zero_mk_pnat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_denom_ne_zero_of_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.add_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.comm_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.num_denom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.coe_int_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.zero_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.add_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.sub_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.zero_iff_num_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.one_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.has_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.has_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_pnat", "description": "Form the quotient `n / d` where `n:\u2124` and `d:\u2115+` (not necessarily coprime)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.comm_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_mul_mk_cancel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.coe_nat_denom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.denom_neg_eq_denom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.num_denom_cases_on'", "description": " Define a (dependent) function or prove `\u2200 r : \u211a, p r` by dealing with rational\nnumbers of the form `n /. d` with `d \u2260 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.zero_of_num_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_neg_denom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.coe_nat_num", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.has_int_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.inv_mul_cancel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.num_denom'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_eq_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.zero_mk_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.coe_int_num_of_denom_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.denom_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mul_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mul_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mul_inv_cancel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.add_right_cancel_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.num_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "elemental_star_algebra.normed_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_subalgebra.coe_is_unit", "description": " For a unital C\u22c6-subalgebra `S` of `A` and `x : S`, if `\u2191x : A` is invertible in `A`, then\n`x` is invertible in `S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "elemental_star_algebra.continuous_character_space_to_spectrum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "elemental_star_algebra.complex.normed_algebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "elemental_star_algebra.character_space_to_spectrum", "description": " The natural map from `character_space \u2102 (elemental_star_algebra \u2102 x)` to `spectrum \u2102 x` given\nby evaluating `\u03c6` at `x`. This is essentially just evaluation of the `gelfand_transform` of `x`,\nbut because we want something in `spectrum \u2102 x`, as opposed to\n`spectrum \u2102 \u27e8x, elemental_star_algebra.self_mem \u2102 x\u27e9` there is slightly more work to do.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "elemental_star_algebra.character_space_homeo", "description": " The homeomorphism between the character space of the unital C\u22c6-subalgebra generated by a\nsingle normal element `a : A` and `spectrum \u2102 a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum_star_mul_self_of_is_star_normal", "description": " This lemma is used in the proof of `star_subalgebra.is_unit_of_is_unit_of_is_star_normal`,\nwhich in turn is the key to spectral permanence `star_subalgebra.spectrum_eq`, which is itself\nnecessary for the continuous functional calculus. Using the continuous functional calculus, this\nlemma can be superseded by one that omits the `is_star_normal` hypothesis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_functional_calculus", "description": " **Continuous functional calculus.** Given a normal element `a : A` of a unital C\u22c6-algebra,\nthe continuous functional calculus is a `star_alg_equiv` from the complex-valued continuous\nfunctions on the spectrum of `a` to the unital C\u22c6-subalgebra generated by `a`. Moreover, this\nequivalence identifies `(continuous_map.id \u2102).restrict (spectrum \u2102 a))` with `a`; see\n`continuous_functional_calculus_map_id`. As such it extends the polynomial functional calculus.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "elemental_star_algebra.normed_algebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "elemental_star_algebra.is_unit_of_is_unit_of_is_star_normal", "description": " This is the key lemma on the way to establishing spectral permanence for C\u22c6-algebras, which is\nestablished in `star_subalgebra.spectrum_eq`. This lemma is superseded by\n`star_subalgebra.coe_is_unit`, which does not require an `is_star_normal` hypothesis and holds for\nany closed star subalgebra.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "elemental_star_algebra.bijective_character_space_to_spectrum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "elemental_star_algebra.character_space_to_spectrum_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_functional_calculus_map_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "elemental_star_algebra.normed_comm_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_subalgebra.mem_spectrum_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_subalgebra.is_unit_coe_inv_mem", "description": " For `x : A` which is invertible in `A`, the inverse lies in any unital C\u22c6-subalgebra `S`\ncontaining `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_subalgebra.spectrum_eq", "description": " **Spectral permanence.** The spectrum of an element is invariant of the (closed)\n`star_subalgebra` in which it is contained.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.is_colimit_sheafify_cocone_desc_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.category_theory.Sheaf_to_presheaf.category_theory.creates_limit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.is_limit_multifork_of_is_limit", "description": " If `E` is a cone of shape `K` of sheaves, which is a limit on the level of presheves,\nthis definition shows that the limit presheaf satisfies the multifork variant of the sheaf\ncondition, at a given covering `W`.\n\nThis is used below in `is_sheaf_of_is_limit` to show that the limit presheaf is indeed a sheaf.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.category_theory.limits.has_colimits_of_shape", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.sheafify_cocone", "description": " Construct a cocone by sheafifying a cocone point of a cocone `E` of presheaves\nover a functor which factors through sheaves.\nIn `is_colimit_sheafify_cocone`, we show that this is a colimit cocone when `E` is a colimit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.multifork_evaluation_cone", "description": " An auxiliary definition to be used below.\n\nWhenever `E` is a cone of shape `K` of sheaves, and `S` is the multifork associated to a\ncovering `W` of an object `X`, with respect to the cone point `E.X`, this provides a cone of\nshape `K` of objects in `D`, with cone point `S.X`.\n\nSee `is_limit_multifork_of_is_limit` for more on how this definition is used.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.category_theory.limits.has_colimits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.sheafify_cocone_X_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.is_colimit_sheafify_cocone", "description": " If `E` is a colimit cocone of presheaves, over a diagram factoring through sheaves,\nthen `sheafify_cocone E` is a colimit cocone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.sheafify_cocone_\u03b9_app_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.category_theory.limits.has_limits_of_shape", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.category_theory.Sheaf_to_presheaf.category_theory.creates_limits_of_shape", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.is_sheaf_of_is_limit", "description": " If `E` is a cone which is a limit on the level of presheaves,\nthen the limit presheaf is again a sheaf.\n\nThis is used to show that the forgetful functor from sheaves to presheaves creates limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.category_theory.Sheaf_to_presheaf.category_theory.creates_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.category_theory.limits.has_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.le_sqrt_log", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.exists_large_sphere_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.sphere_subset_preimage_metric_sphere", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.bound_aux'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.map_monotone", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.box_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.roth_lower_bound_explicit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.map_le_of_mem_box", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.sphere_zero_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.card_sphere_le_roth_number_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.ceil_lt_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.sphere_zero_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.le_N", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.three_le_n_value", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.d_value", "description": "The (almost) optimal value of `d` in `behrend.bound_aux`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.norm_of_mem_sphere", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.exists_large_sphere", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.map_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.exp_neg_two_mul_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.div_lt_floor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.map_eq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.roth_lower_bound", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.add_salem_spencer_image_sphere", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.map_inj_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.card_box", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.sum_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.lower_bound_le_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.bound_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.n_value_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.sphere_subset_box", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.log_two_mul_two_le_sqrt_log_eight", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.two_le_n_value", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.map_succ'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.map_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.box", "description": "The box `{0, ..., d - 1}^n` as a finset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.map", "description": "The map that appears in Behrend's bound on Roth numbers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.lower_bound_le_one'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.sum_sq_le_of_mem_box", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.d_value_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.four_zero_nine_six_lt_exp_sixteen", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.sphere", "description": " The intersection of the sphere of radius `sqrt k` with the integer points in the positive\nquadrant.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.bound", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.two_div_one_sub_two_div_e_le_eight", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.exp_four_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.sum_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.map_mod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.n_value", "description": "The (almost) optimal value of `n` in `behrend.bound_aux`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.add_salem_spencer_sphere", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "behrend.mem_box", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv2_sqrt_mul_log", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_convex_on_zpow", "description": "`x^m`, `m : \u2124` is convex on `(0, +\u221e)` for all `m` except `0` and `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_concave_on_sqrt_mul_log_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int_prod_range_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_nonneg_of_card_nonpos_even", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv_sqrt_mul_log", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_convex_on_pow", "description": "`x^n`, `n : \u2115` is strictly convex on `[0, +\u221e)` for all `n` greater than `2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv_sqrt_mul_log'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int_prod_range_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_concave_on_cos_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "even.strict_convex_on_pow", "description": "`x^n`, `n : \u2115` is strictly convex on the whole real line whenever `n \u2260 0` is even.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_concave_on_sin_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at_sqrt_mul_log", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf_compose", "description": " Composing a sheaf with a functor preserving the appropriate limits yields a functor\nbetween sheaf categories.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.cover.multicospan_comp_hom_inv_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.cover.multicospan_comp_app_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf_compose_obj_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.cover.map_multifork", "description": " Mapping the multifork associated to a cover `S : J.cover X` and a presheaf `P` with\nrespect to a functor `F` is isomorphic (upto a natural isomorphism of the underlying functors)\nto the multifork associated to `S` and `P \u22d9 F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_sheaf.comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.cover.multicospan_comp_app_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.cover.multicospan_comp", "description": " The multicospan associated to a cover `S : J.cover X` and a presheaf of the form `P \u22d9 F`\nis isomorphic to the composition of the multicospan associated to `S` and `P`,\ncomposed with `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.cover.multicospan_comp_hom_app_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.cover.multicospan_comp_hom_inv_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf_compose_map_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.cover.multicospan_comp_hom_app_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.mk_eq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.mk_complex.apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.mk_real_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.embeddings.card", "description": "The number of embeddings of a number field is equal to its finrank.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.embeddings.pow_eq_one_of_norm_eq_one", "description": "An algebraic integer whose conjugates are all of norm one is a root of unity.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.coe_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.card_real_embeddings", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.mk_complex_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.complex_embedding.is_real", "description": "A embedding into `\u2102` is real if it is fixed by complex conjugation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.eq_iff_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place", "description": "An infinite place of a number field `K` is a place associated to a complex embedding.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.complex_embedding.is_real.coe_embedding_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.embedding", "description": "For an infinite place `w`, return an embedding `\u03c6` such that `w = infinite_place \u03c6` .", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.mk_conjugate_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.has_coe_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.real.nonneg_hom_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.is_real.abs_embedding_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.complex_embedding.is_real_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.complex_embedding.conjugate_coe_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.mk_complex", "description": "The map from nonreal embeddings to complex infinite places", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.embeddings.range_eval_eq_root_set_minpoly", "description": " Let `A` be an algebraically closed field and let `x \u2208 K`, with `K` a number field.\nThe images of `x` by the embeddings of `K` in `A` are exactly the roots in `A` of\nthe minimal polynomial of `x` over `\u211a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.mk_complex.filter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.is_complex_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.pos_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.is_real_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.complex_embedding.place_conjugate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.is_real.place_embedding_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.abs_embedding", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.complex_embedding.conjugate", "description": "The conjugate of a complex embedding as a complex embedding.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.card_complex_embeddings", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.mk_embedding", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.not_is_complex_iff_is_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.mk", "description": "Return the infinite place defined by a complex embedding `\u03c6`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.mk_complex_embedding", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.complex_embedding.is_real_conjugate_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.mk_real.apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.not_is_real_iff_is_complex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.number_field.infinite_place.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.complex_embedding.is_real.place_embedding", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.prod_eq_abs_norm", "description": " The infinite part of the product formula : for `x \u2208 K`, we have `\u03a0_w \u2016x\u2016_w = |norm(x)|` where\n`\u2016\u00b7\u2016_w` is the normalized absolute value for `w`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.embeddings.coeff_bdd_of_norm_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.embeddings.ring_hom.nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.is_real.embedding", "description": "For `w` a real infinite place, return the corresponding embedding as a morphism `K \u2192+* \u211d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.is_complex", "description": "An infinite place is complex if it is defined by a complex (ie. not real) embedding.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.embeddings.ring_hom.fintype", "description": "There are finitely many embeddings of a number field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.is_real_or_is_complex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.real.monoid_with_zero_hom_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.le_iff_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.mk_real", "description": "The map from real embeddings to real infinite places as an equiv", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.place_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.place", "description": "An embedding into a normed division ring defines a place of `K`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.complex_embeddings.is_real.embedding_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.complex_embedding.is_real.embedding", "description": "A real embedding as a ring homomorphism from `K` to `\u211d` .", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.is_real", "description": "An infinite place is real if it is defined by a real embedding.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.embeddings.finite_of_norm_le", "description": " Let `B` be a real number. The set of algebraic integers in `K` whose conjugates are all\nsmaller in norm than `B` is finite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.infinite_place.mk_complex.filter_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.const_comp", "description": "These are actually equal, of course, but not definitionally equal\n (the equality requires F.map (\ud835\udfd9 _) = \ud835\udfd9 _). A natural isomorphism is\n more convenient than an equality between functors (compare id_to_iso).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.const", "description": "The functor sending `X : C` to the constant functor `J \u2964 C` sending everything to `X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.const.op_obj_unop_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.const.category_theory.faithful", "description": "If `J` is nonempty, then the constant functor over `J` is faithful.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.const.unop_functor_op_obj_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.const_obj_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.const_comp_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.const.op_obj_op_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.const.op_obj_unop_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.const.op_obj_unop", "description": "The contant functor `J\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 C` sending everything to `unop X`\nis (naturally isomorphic to) the opposite of\nthe constant functor `J \u2964 C\u1d52\u1d56` sending everything to `X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.const.op_obj_op_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.const.op_obj_op", "description": "The contant functor `J\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 C\u1d52\u1d56` sending everything to `op X`\nis (naturally isomorphic to) the opposite of the constant functor `J \u2964 C` sending everything to `X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.const_obj_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.const_comp_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.const_map_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Lan_obj_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Lan_map_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Lan.cocone", "description": "A cocone over `Lan.diagram \u03b9 F x` used to define `Lan`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Lan.equiv_symm_apply_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Lan.equiv", "description": "An auxiliary definition used to define `Lan` and `Lan.adjunction`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Ran.adjunction", "description": "The adjunction associated to `Ran`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Lan.loc_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Lan.loc_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Lan.adjunction", "description": "The adjunction associated to `Lan`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Ran.loc", "description": "An auxiliary definition used to define `Ran`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Ran.cone", "description": "A cone over `Ran.diagram \u03b9 F x` used to define `Ran`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Lan", "description": "The left Kan extension of a functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Ran.loc_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Lan.loc", "description": "An auxiliary definition used to define `Lan`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Lan.coreflective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Ran.equiv_symm_apply_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Lan.diagram", "description": "The diagram indexed by `Ran.index \u03b9 x` used to define `Ran`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Ran_obj_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Lan.equiv_apply_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Ran", "description": "The right Kan extension of a functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Ran_obj_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Ran_map_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Lan_obj_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Ran.equiv", "description": "An auxiliary definition used to define `Ran` and `Ran.adjunction`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Ran.equiv_apply_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Ran.loc_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Ran.diagram", "description": "The diagram indexed by `Ran.index \u03b9 x` used to define `Ran`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Ran.reflective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.category.comp_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.eq_of_comp_right_eq'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.ite_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.comp_ite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.id_of_comp_left_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.category_struct.id.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cancel_mono_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.mono", "description": "A morphism `f` is a monomorphism if it can be \"cancelled\" when postcomposed:\n`g \u226b f = h \u226b f` implies `g = h`.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/003B>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.mono.right_cancellation", "description": "A morphism `f` is a monomorphism if it can be \"cancelled\" when postcomposed:\n`g \u226b f = h \u226b f` implies `g = h`.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/003B>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "library_note.category_theory universes", "description": "The typeclass `category C` describes morphisms associated to objects of type `C : Type u`.\n\nThe universe levels of the objects and morphisms are independent, and will often need to be\nspecified explicitly, as `category.{v} C`.\n\nTypically any concrete example will either be a `small_category`, where `v = u`,\nwhich can be introduced as\n```lean\nuniverses u\nvariables {C : Type u} [small_category C]\n```\nor a `large_category`, where `u = v+1`, which can be introduced as\n```lean\nuniverses u\nvariables {C : Type (u+1)} [large_category C]\n```\n\nIn order for the library to handle these cases uniformly,\nwe generally work with the unconstrained `category.{v u}`,\nfor which objects live in `Type u` and morphisms live in `Type v`.\n\nBecause the universe parameter `u` for the objects can be inferred from `C`\nwhen we write `category C`, while the universe parameter `v` for the morphisms\ncan not be automatically inferred, through the category theory library\nwe introduce universe parameters with morphism levels listed first,\nas in\n```lean\nuniverses v u\n```\nor\n```lean\nuniverses v\u2081 v\u2082 u\u2081 u\u2082\n```\nwhen multiple independent universes are needed.\n\nThis has the effect that we can simply write `category.{v} C`\n(that is, only specifying a single parameter) while `u` will be inferred.\n\nOften, however, it's not even necessary to include the `.{v}`.\n(Although it was in earlier versions of Lean.)\nIf it is omitted a \"free\" universe will be used.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.category_struct.id.epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.mono_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.id_of_comp_right_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.eq_of_comp_right_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.epi_of_epi_fac", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.category.id_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.category_struct", "description": " A preliminary structure on the way to defining a category,\ncontaining the data, but none of the axioms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.category_struct.to_quiver", "description": " A preliminary structure on the way to defining a category,\ncontaining the data, but none of the axioms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.category_struct.id", "description": " A preliminary structure on the way to defining a category,\ncontaining the data, but none of the axioms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.category_struct.comp", "description": " A preliminary structure on the way to defining a category,\ncontaining the data, but none of the axioms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.eq_of_comp_left_eq'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.category", "description": "The typeclass `category C` describes morphisms associated to objects of type `C`.\nThe universe levels of the objects and morphisms are unconstrained, and will often need to be\nspecified explicitly, as `category.{v} C`. (See also `large_category` and `small_category`.)\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0014>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.category.to_category_struct", "description": "The typeclass `category C` describes morphisms associated to objects of type `C`.\nThe universe levels of the objects and morphisms are unconstrained, and will often need to be\nspecified explicitly, as `category.{v} C`. (See also `large_category` and `small_category`.)\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0014>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.category.id_comp'", "description": "The typeclass `category C` describes morphisms associated to objects of type `C`.\nThe universe levels of the objects and morphisms are unconstrained, and will often need to be\nspecified explicitly, as `category.{v} C`. (See also `large_category` and `small_category`.)\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0014>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.category.comp_id'", "description": "The typeclass `category C` describes morphisms associated to objects of type `C`.\nThe universe levels of the objects and morphisms are unconstrained, and will often need to be\nspecified explicitly, as `category.{v} C`. (See also `large_category` and `small_category`.)\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0014>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.category.assoc'", "description": "The typeclass `category C` describes morphisms associated to objects of type `C`.\nThe universe levels of the objects and morphisms are unconstrained, and will often need to be\nspecified explicitly, as `category.{v} C`. (See also `large_category` and `small_category`.)\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0014>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.mono_of_mono_fac", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cancel_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cancel_epi_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.large_category", "description": "A `large_category` has objects in one universe level higher than the universe level of\nthe morphisms. It is useful for examples such as the category of types, or the category\nof groups, etc.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cancel_epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.ulift_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.category.assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.epi_of_epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.epi", "description": "A morphism `f` is an epimorphism if it can be \"cancelled\" when precomposed:\n`f \u226b g = f \u226b h` implies `g = h`.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/003B>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.epi.left_cancellation", "description": "A morphism `f` is an epimorphism if it can be \"cancelled\" when precomposed:\n`f \u226b g = f \u226b h` implies `g = h`.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/003B>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.mono_of_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.dite_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.eq_whisker", "description": "postcompose an equation between morphisms by another morphism", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.epi_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_eq", "description": "precompose an equation between morphisms by another morphism", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.eq_of_comp_left_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.small_category", "description": "A `small_category` has objects and morphisms in the same universe level.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "library_note.dsimp, simp", "description": "Many proofs in the category theory library use the `dsimp, simp` pattern,\nwhich typically isn't necessary elsewhere.\n\nOne would usually hope that the same effect could be achieved simply with `simp`.\n\nThe essential issue is that composition of morphisms involves dependent types.\nWhen you have a chain of morphisms being composed, say `f : X \u27f6 Y` and `g : Y \u27f6 Z`,\nthen `simp` can operate succesfully on the morphisms\n(e.g. if `f` is the identity it can strip that off).\n\nHowever if we have an equality of objects, say `Y = Y'`,\nthen `simp` can't operate because it would break the typing of the composition operations.\nWe rarely have interesting equalities of objects\n(because that would be \"evil\" --- anything interesting should be expressed as an isomorphism\nand tracked explicitly),\nexcept of course that we have plenty of definitional equalities of objects.\n\n`dsimp` can apply these safely, even inside a composition.\n\nAfter `dsimp` has cleared up the object level, `simp` can resume work on the morphism level ---\nbut without the `dsimp` step, because `simp` looks at expressions syntactically,\nthe relevant lemmas might not fire.\n\nThere's no bound on how many times you potentially could have to switch back and forth,\nif the `simp` introduced new objects we again need to `dsimp`.\nIn practice this does occur, but only rarely, because `simp` tends to shorten chains of compositions\n(i.e. not introduce new objects at all).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.comp_dite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "miu.goodm_of_derivable", "description": "Any derivable string must begin with `M` and have no `M` in its tail.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.goodm_of_rule2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.goodm_of_rule1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.goodmi", "description": "Demonstration that `\"MI\"` starts with `M` and has no `M` in its tail.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.decstr_of_der", "description": "Suppose `en : miustr`. If `en` is `derivable`, then the condition `decstr en` holds.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.not_derivable_mu", "description": "Using the above theorem, we solve the MU puzzle, showing that `\"MU\"` is not derivable.\nOnce we have proved that `derivable` is an instance of `decidable_pred`, this will follow\nimmediately from `dec_trivial`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.decstr", "description": "`decstr en` is the condition that `count I en` is 1 or 2 modulo 3, that `en` starts with `M`, and\nthat `en` has no `M` in its tail. We automatically derive that this is a decidable predicate.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.count_equiv_or_equiv_two_mul_mod3", "description": "Given `st en : miustr`, the relation `count_equiv_or_equiv_two_mul_mod3 st en` holds if `st` and\n`en` either have equal `count I`, modulo 3, or `count I en` is twice `count I st`, modulo 3.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.goodm.decidable_pred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.mod3_eq_1_or_mod3_eq_2", "description": "If `a` is 1 or 2 mod 3 and if `b` is `a` or twice `a` mod 3, then `b` is 1 or 2 mod 3.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.decstr.decidable_pred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.goodm_of_rule4", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.goodm", "description": "`goodm xs` holds if `xs : miustr` begins with `M` and has no `M` in its tail.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.goodm_of_rule3", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.count_equiv_one_or_two_mod3_of_derivable", "description": "`count_equiv_one_or_two_mod3_of_derivable` shows any derivable string must have a `count I` that\nis 1 or 2 modulo 3.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "padic_int.algebra_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.is_fraction_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.has_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.norm_p_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.coe_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.norm_le_pow_iff_norm_lt_pow_add_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.coe_nat_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.valuation_p_pow_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.norm_eq_pow_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.norm_one_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.of_int_seq", "description": " A sequence of integers that is Cauchy with respect to the `p`-adic norm converges to a `p`-adic\ninteger.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.char_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.valuation", "description": "`padic_int.valuation` lifts the `p`-adic valuation on `\u211a` to `\u2124_[p]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.val_eq_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.valuation_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.irreducible_p", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.coe_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.unit_coeff_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.mem_subring_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.norm_add_eq_max_of_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.coe_int_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.mk_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.valuation_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_int.ideal_eq_span_pow_p", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}