, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.add_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.coe_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.incl'", "description": "The embedding of a Lie subalgebra into the ambient space as a morphism of Lie modules.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.mem_lie_span", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.mem_mk_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.coe_to_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.well_founded_of_noetherian", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.top_coe_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.lie_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.of_le_eq_comap_incl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.equiv_of_le_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.of_mul_of_fin", "description": " This lemma assumes that `a_coeffs` and `b_coeffs` refer to expressions of the form\n`![![x\u2080\u2080, x\u2080\u2081], ![x\u2081\u2080, x\u2081\u2081]]`. It then uses an `auto_param` to populate `ab_coeffs` with an\nexpression of the same form, containing the appropriate expressions in terms of `+`, `*`, `a\u1d62\u2c7c`,\nand `b\u2c7c\u2096`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.of_mul_of_fin.prove", "description": " Prove a statement of the form\n```lean\n\u2200 \u03b1 [has_mul \u03b1] [add_comm_monoid \u03b1] (a\u2081\u2081 ... a\u2097\u2098 b\u2081\u2081 ... b\u2098\u2099 : \u03b1),\n   !![a\u2081\u2081 \u22f1 a\u2097\u2098] \u2b1d !![b\u2081\u2081 \u22f1 b\u2098\u2099] = !![\u22f1]\n```\nReturns the type of this statement and its proof.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.fin_eta", "description": "This lemma expands `A` into `!![A 0 0, ...]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.mmap", "description": "Like `list.mmap` but for a vector.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.of_mul_of_fin_aux", "description": "This statement is defeq to `of_mul_of_fin`, but syntactically worse", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.of_mul_of_fin.derive", "description": "Helper tactic used as an `auto_param` for `matrix.of_mul_of_fin`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.fin_to_pexpr", "description": "`pi_fin.to_pexpr` but for matrices", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.fin_eta.prove", "description": " Prove a statement of the form `\u2200 A : matrix m n \u03b1, A = !![A 0 0, ...]`.\nReturns the type of this statement and its proof.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.fin_eta.derive", "description": "Helper tactic used as an `auto_param` for `matrix.fin_eta`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "perms_of_finset", "description": "Given a finset, produce the finset of all permutations of its elements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_perms_of_list_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nodup_perms_of_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "length_perms_of_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.card_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_perms_of_list_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype_perm", "description": "The collection of permutations of a fintype is a fintype.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.card_perm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_of_mem_perms_of_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "card_perms_of_finset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_perms_of_finset_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "perms_of_list", "description": "Given a list, produce a list of all permutations of its elements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.fine_subfamily_on", "description": " Given a Vitali family `v` for a measure `\u03bc`, a family `f` is a fine subfamily on a set `s` if\nevery point `x` in `s` belongs to arbitrarily small sets in `v.sets_at x \u2229 f x`. This is precisely\nthe subfamilies for which the Vitali family definition ensures that one can extract a disjoint\ncovering of almost all `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.fine_subfamily_on.covering_disjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.filter_at_ne_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family", "description": " On a metric space `X` with a measure `\u03bc`, consider for each `x : X` a family of measurable sets\nwith nonempty interiors, called `sets_at x`. This family is a Vitali family if it satisfies the\nfollowing property: consider a (possibly non-measurable) set `s`, and for any `x` in `s` a\nsubfamily `f x` of `sets_at x` containing sets of arbitrarily small diameter. Then one can extract\na disjoint subfamily covering almost all `s`.\n\nVitali families are provided by covering theorems such as the Besicovitch covering theorem or the\nVitali covering theorem. They make it possible to formulate general versions of theorems on\ndifferentiations of measure that apply in both contexts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.sets_at", "description": " On a metric space `X` with a measure `\u03bc`, consider for each `x : X` a family of measurable sets\nwith nonempty interiors, called `sets_at x`. This family is a Vitali family if it satisfies the\nfollowing property: consider a (possibly non-measurable) set `s`, and for any `x` in `s` a\nsubfamily `f x` of `sets_at x` containing sets of arbitrarily small diameter. Then one can extract\na disjoint subfamily covering almost all `s`.\n\nVitali families are provided by covering theorems such as the Besicovitch covering theorem or the\nVitali covering theorem. They make it possible to formulate general versions of theorems on\ndifferentiations of measure that apply in both contexts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.measurable_set'", "description": " On a metric space `X` with a measure `\u03bc`, consider for each `x : X` a family of measurable sets\nwith nonempty interiors, called `sets_at x`. This family is a Vitali family if it satisfies the\nfollowing property: consider a (possibly non-measurable) set `s`, and for any `x` in `s` a\nsubfamily `f x` of `sets_at x` containing sets of arbitrarily small diameter. Then one can extract\na disjoint subfamily covering almost all `s`.\n\nVitali families are provided by covering theorems such as the Besicovitch covering theorem or the\nVitali covering theorem. They make it possible to formulate general versions of theorems on\ndifferentiations of measure that apply in both contexts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.nonempty_interior", "description": " On a metric space `X` with a measure `\u03bc`, consider for each `x : X` a family of measurable sets\nwith nonempty interiors, called `sets_at x`. This family is a Vitali family if it satisfies the\nfollowing property: consider a (possibly non-measurable) set `s`, and for any `x` in `s` a\nsubfamily `f x` of `sets_at x` containing sets of arbitrarily small diameter. Then one can extract\na disjoint subfamily covering almost all `s`.\n\nVitali families are provided by covering theorems such as the Besicovitch covering theorem or the\nVitali covering theorem. They make it possible to formulate general versions of theorems on\ndifferentiations of measure that apply in both contexts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.nontrivial", "description": " On a metric space `X` with a measure `\u03bc`, consider for each `x : X` a family of measurable sets\nwith nonempty interiors, called `sets_at x`. This family is a Vitali family if it satisfies the\nfollowing property: consider a (possibly non-measurable) set `s`, and for any `x` in `s` a\nsubfamily `f x` of `sets_at x` containing sets of arbitrarily small diameter. Then one can extract\na disjoint subfamily covering almost all `s`.\n\nVitali families are provided by covering theorems such as the Besicovitch covering theorem or the\nVitali covering theorem. They make it possible to formulate general versions of theorems on\ndifferentiations of measure that apply in both contexts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.covering", "description": " On a metric space `X` with a measure `\u03bc`, consider for each `x : X` a family of measurable sets\nwith nonempty interiors, called `sets_at x`. This family is a Vitali family if it satisfies the\nfollowing property: consider a (possibly non-measurable) set `s`, and for any `x` in `s` a\nsubfamily `f x` of `sets_at x` containing sets of arbitrarily small diameter. Then one can extract\na disjoint subfamily covering almost all `s`.\n\nVitali families are provided by covering theorems such as the Besicovitch covering theorem or the\nVitali covering theorem. They make it possible to formulate general versions of theorems on\ndifferentiations of measure that apply in both contexts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.fine_subfamily_on.covering", "description": " Given `h : v.fine_subfamily_on f s`, then `h.covering p` is a set in the family,\nfor `p \u2208 h.index`, such that these sets form a disjoint covering of almost every `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.fine_subfamily_on.measure_le_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.fine_subfamily_on.covering_disjoint_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.fine_subfamily_on.index_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.eventually_filter_at_measurable_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.fine_subfamily_on.measurable_set_u", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.eventually_filter_at_mem_sets", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.fine_subfamily_on_of_frequently", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.eventually_filter_at_subset_of_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.tendsto_filter_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.frequently_filter_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.eventually_filter_at_subset_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.mono", "description": " A Vitali family for a measure `\u03bc` is also a Vitali family for any measure absolutely continuous\nwith respect to `\u03bc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.eventually_filter_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.fine_subfamily_on.index_countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.fine_subfamily_on.exists_disjoint_covering_ae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.fine_subfamily_on.covering_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.enlarge", "description": " One can enlarge a Vitali family by adding to the sets `f x` at `x` all sets which are not\ncontained in a `\u03b4`-neighborhood on `x`. This does not change the local filter at a point, but it\ncan be convenient to get a nicer global behavior.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.fine_subfamily_on.measure_le_tsum_of_absolutely_continuous", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.fine_subfamily_on.index", "description": " Given `h : v.fine_subfamily_on f s`, then `h.index` is a set parametrizing a disjoint\ncovering of almost every `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.mem_filter_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.fine_subfamily_on.measure_diff_bUnion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.filter_at", "description": " Given a vitali family `v`, then `v.filter_at x` is the filter on `set \u03b1` made of those families\nthat contain all sets of `v.sets_at x` of a sufficiently small diameter. This filter makes it\npossible to express limiting behavior when sets in `v.sets_at x` shrink to `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali_family.fine_subfamily_on.covering_mem_family", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.exists_comap_eq_of_mem_minimal_primes_of_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.minimal_primes_eq_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.exists_comap_eq_of_mem_minimal_primes", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.mimimal_primes_comap_of_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minimal_primes", "description": " `minimal_primes R` is the set of minimal primes of `R`.\nThis is defined as `ideal.minimal_primes \u22a5`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.comap_minimal_primes_eq_of_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.minimal_primes", "description": "`I.minimal_primes` is the set of ideals that are minimal primes over `I`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.Inf_minimal_primes", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.exists_minimal_primes_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.radical_minimal_primes", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.minimal_primes_eq_subsingleton_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.minimal_primes_eq_comap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.exists_minimal_primes_comap_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.map_homotopy_category_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_category.homotopy_out_map", "description": "An arbitrarily chosen representation of the image of a chain map in the homotopy category\nis homotopic to the original chain map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.map_homotopy_category_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_category.category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopic", "description": "The congruence on `homological_complex V c` given by the existence of a homotopy.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_category.eq_of_homotopy", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_category.quotient_map_out", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.map_homotopy_category_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.map_homotopy_category", "description": "A natural transformation induces a natural transformation between\n the induced functors on the homotopy category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_category.iso_of_homotopy_equiv_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.map_homotopy_category_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_category.homotopy_of_eq", "description": "If two chain maps become equal in the homotopy category, then they are homotopic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_category", "description": " `homotopy_category V c` is the category of chain complexes of shape `c` in `V`,\nwith chain maps identified when they are homotopic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_category.iso_of_homotopy_equiv_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.map_homotopy_category", "description": "An additive functor induces a functor between homotopy categories.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_category.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_congruence", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_category.quotient_obj_as", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_category.homology_factors_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.map_homotopy_category_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_category.quotient", "description": "The quotient functor from complexes to the homotopy category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_category.iso_of_homotopy_equiv", "description": "Homotopy equivalent complexes become isomorphic in the homotopy category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_category.homology_factors_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_category.quotient_map_out_comp_out", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_category.homology_functor", "description": "The `i`-th homology, as a functor from the homotopy category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_category.homotopy_equiv_of_iso", "description": "If two complexes become isomorphic in the homotopy category,\n then they were homotopy equivalent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_category.homology_factors", "description": "The homology functor on the homotopy category is just the usual homology functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_category.homology_functor_map_factors", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminals_equiv_mono_over_terminal_inverse_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminal_inclusion.faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_subterminal.mono_is_terminal_from", "description": "If `A` is subterminal, the unique morphism from it to a terminal object is a monomorphism.\nThe converse of `is_subterminal_of_mono_is_terminal_from`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminals_thin", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_subterminal.iso_diag", "description": "If `A` is subterminal, it is isomorphic to `A \u2a2f A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminals_equiv_mono_over_terminal_functor_obj_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminals", "description": "The (full sub)category of subterminal objects.\nTODO: If `C` is the category of sheaves on a topological space `X`, this category is equivalent\nto the lattice of open subsets of `X`. More generally, if `C` is a topos, this is the lattice of\n\"external truth values\".", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_subterminal_of_is_terminal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_subterminal", "description": "An object `A` is subterminal iff for any `Z`, there is at most one morphism `Z \u27f6 A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminals_equiv_mono_over_terminal_inverse_obj_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminals_to_mono_over_terminal_comp_forget", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_subterminal.mono_terminal_from", "description": "If `A` is subterminal, the unique morphism from it to the terminal object is a monomorphism.\nThe converse of `is_subterminal_of_mono_terminal_from`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_subterminal.iso_diag_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.mono_over_terminal_to_subterminals_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_subterminal.def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminals_equiv_mono_over_terminal_functor_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminals_equiv_mono_over_terminal_unit_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminal_inclusion_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminals_equiv_mono_over_terminal_unit_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminal_inclusion_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminal_inclusion", "description": "The inclusion of the subterminal objects into the original category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_subterminal_of_mono_is_terminal_from", "description": "If the unique morphism from `A` to a terminal object is a monomorphism, `A` is subterminal.\nThe converse of `is_subterminal.mono_is_terminal_from`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminals_equiv_mono_over_terminal_counit_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminals.category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminals_equiv_mono_over_terminal", "description": "The category of subterminal objects is equivalent to the category of monomorphisms to the terminal\nobject (which is in turn equivalent to the subobjects of the terminal object).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_subterminal.iso_diag_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminals.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_subterminal_of_mono_terminal_from", "description": "If the unique morphism from `A` to the terminal object is a monomorphism, `A` is subterminal.\nThe converse of `is_subterminal.mono_terminal_from`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_subterminal_of_is_iso_diag", "description": "If the diagonal morphism of `A` is an isomorphism, then it is subterminal.\nThe converse of `is_subterminal.is_iso_diag`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_subterminal.is_iso_diag", "description": "If `A` is subterminal, its diagonal morphism is an isomorphism.\nThe converse of `is_subterminal_of_is_iso_diag`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_subterminal_of_terminal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminal_inclusion.full", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminals_equiv_mono_over_terminal_counit_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.relabelling_move_right_aux", "description": "The relabelling showing `move_right` applied to a game constructed using `of_state_aux`\nhas itself been constructed using `of_state_aux`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.turn_bound_ne_zero_of_right_move", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.turn_bound_of_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.fintype_right_moves_of_state_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.left_moves_of_state_aux", "description": " The equivalence between `left_moves` for a `pgame` constructed using `of_state_aux _ s _`, and\n`L s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.state", "description": "`pgame_state S` describes how to interpret `s : S` as a state of a combinatorial game.\nUse `pgame.of_state s` or `game.of_state s` to construct the game.\n\n`pgame_state.L : S \u2192 finset S` and `pgame_state.R : S \u2192 finset S` describe the states reachable\nby a move by Left or Right. `pgame_state.turn_bound : S \u2192 \u2115` gives an upper bound on the number of\npossible turns remaining from this state.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.state.turn_bound", "description": "`pgame_state S` describes how to interpret `s : S` as a state of a combinatorial game.\nUse `pgame.of_state s` or `game.of_state s` to construct the game.\n\n`pgame_state.L : S \u2192 finset S` and `pgame_state.R : S \u2192 finset S` describe the states reachable\nby a move by Left or Right. `pgame_state.turn_bound : S \u2192 \u2115` gives an upper bound on the number of\npossible turns remaining from this state.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.state.L", "description": "`pgame_state S` describes how to interpret `s : S` as a state of a combinatorial game.\nUse `pgame.of_state s` or `game.of_state s` to construct the game.\n\n`pgame_state.L : S \u2192 finset S` and `pgame_state.R : S \u2192 finset S` describe the states reachable\nby a move by Left or Right. `pgame_state.turn_bound : S \u2192 \u2115` gives an upper bound on the number of\npossible turns remaining from this state.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.state.R", "description": "`pgame_state S` describes how to interpret `s : S` as a state of a combinatorial game.\nUse `pgame.of_state s` or `game.of_state s` to construct the game.\n\n`pgame_state.L : S \u2192 finset S` and `pgame_state.R : S \u2192 finset S` describe the states reachable\nby a move by Left or Right. `pgame_state.turn_bound : S \u2192 \u2115` gives an upper bound on the number of\npossible turns remaining from this state.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.state.left_bound", "description": "`pgame_state S` describes how to interpret `s : S` as a state of a combinatorial game.\nUse `pgame.of_state s` or `game.of_state s` to construct the game.\n\n`pgame_state.L : S \u2192 finset S` and `pgame_state.R : S \u2192 finset S` describe the states reachable\nby a move by Left or Right. `pgame_state.turn_bound : S \u2192 \u2115` gives an upper bound on the number of\npossible turns remaining from this state.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.state.right_bound", "description": "`pgame_state S` describes how to interpret `s : S` as a state of a combinatorial game.\nUse `pgame.of_state s` or `game.of_state s` to construct the game.\n\n`pgame_state.L : S \u2192 finset S` and `pgame_state.R : S \u2192 finset S` describe the states reachable\nby a move by Left or Right. `pgame_state.turn_bound : S \u2192 \u2115` gives an upper bound on the number of\npossible turns remaining from this state.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.relabelling_move_left_aux", "description": "The relabelling showing `move_left` applied to a game constructed using `of_state_aux`\nhas itself been constructed using `of_state_aux`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.short_of_state", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "game.of_state", "description": "Construct a combinatorial `game` from a state.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.of_state_aux_relabelling", "description": "Two different (valid) turn bounds give equivalent games.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.short_of_state_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.relabelling_move_left", "description": "The relabelling showing `move_left` applied to a game constructed using `of`\nhas itself been constructed using `of`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.turn_bound_of_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.relabelling_move_right", "description": "The relabelling showing `move_right` applied to a game constructed using `of`\nhas itself been constructed using `of`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.of_state_aux", "description": "Construct a `pgame` from a state and a (not necessarily optimal) bound on the number of\nturns remaining.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.left_moves_of_state", "description": "The equivalence between `left_moves` for a `pgame` constructed using `of_state s`, and `L s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.turn_bound_ne_zero_of_left_move", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.right_moves_of_state", "description": " The equivalence between `right_moves` for a `pgame` constructed using `of_state s`, and\n`R s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.right_moves_of_state_aux", "description": " The equivalence between `right_moves` for a `pgame` constructed using `of_state_aux _ s _`, and\n`R s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.fintype_left_moves_of_state_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.of_state", "description": "Construct a combinatorial `pgame` from a state.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.homeo_of_iso_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.comp_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.bundled_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.trivial", "description": "The trivial topology on any type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.obj.discrete_topology", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.of", "description": "Construct a bundled `Top` from the underlying type and the typeclass.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.homeo_of_iso", "description": "Any isomorphism in `Top` induces a homeomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.topological_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.concrete_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.iso_of_homeo", "description": "Any homeomorphisms induces an isomorphism in `Top`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.large_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.id_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.open_embedding_iff_comp_is_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.of_iso_of_homeo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.iso_of_homeo_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top", "description": "The category of topological spaces and continuous maps.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.of_homeo_of_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.topological_space_unbundled", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.coe_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.homeo_of_iso_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.iso_of_homeo_inv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.open_embedding_iff_is_iso_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.has_coe_to_sort", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.discrete", "description": "The discrete topology on any type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.normal_mono_category.pullback_of_mono", "description": "The pullback of two monomorphisms exists.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.normal_epi_category.has_coequalizers", "description": "A `normal_epi_category` category with finite coproducts and cokernels has all coequalizers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.normal_mono_category.epi_of_zero_cokernel", "description": "If a zero morphism is a cokernel of `f`, then `f` is an epimorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.normal_mono_category.epi_of_zero_cancel", "description": "If `f \u226b g = 0` implies `g = 0` for all `g`, then `g` is a monomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.normal_epi_category.mono_of_cancel_zero", "description": "If `g \u226b f = 0` implies `g = 0` for all `g`, then `f` is a monomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.normal_epi_category.mono_of_zero_kernel", "description": "If a zero morphism is a kernel of `f`, then `f` is a monomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.normal_mono_category.has_limit_parallel_pair", "description": "The equalizer of `f` and `g` exists.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.normal_mono_category.has_equalizers", "description": "A `normal_mono_category` category with finite products and kernels has all equalizers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.normal_epi_category.pushout_of_epi", "description": "The pushout of two epimorphisms exists.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.normal_epi_category.has_colimit_parallel_pair", "description": "The coequalizer of `f` and `g` exists.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set.const_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set.const_smul\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set.const_smul_of_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set.const_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.taylor_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.taylor_taylor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.taylor", "description": "The Taylor expansion of a polynomial `f` at `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.taylor_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.taylor_coeff_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.taylor_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.taylor_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.taylor_zero'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.taylor_coeff_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.taylor_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.taylor_eval", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.taylor_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.taylor_monomial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.sum_taylor_eq", "description": "Taylor's formula.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.taylor_alg_hom", "description": "`polynomial.taylor` as a `alg_hom` for commutative semirings", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.taylor_eval_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.taylor_alg_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.taylor_coeff", "description": "The `k`th coefficient of `polynomial.taylor r f` is `(polynomial.hasse_deriv k f).eval r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_taylor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.eq_zero_of_hasse_deriv_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.taylor_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_convex_space.convex_basis_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_convex_space_infi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_convex_space_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_convex_space_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_convex_space.convex_open_basis_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_convex_space_induced", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_convex_space.to_locally_connected_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_convex_space.of_basis_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_convex_space_iff_exists_convex_subset_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_convex_space_Inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_convex_space.of_bases", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.locally_convex_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "disjoint.exists_open_convexes", "description": " In a locally convex space, if `s`, `t` are disjoint convex sets, `s` is compact and `t` is\nclosed, then we can find open disjoint convex sets containing them.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_convex_space_iff_exists_convex_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_convex_space", "description": " A `locally_convex_space` is a topological semimodule over an ordered semiring in which convex\nneighborhoods of a point form a neighborhood basis at that point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_convex_space.convex_basis", "description": " A `locally_convex_space` is a topological semimodule over an ordered semiring in which convex\nneighborhoods of a point form a neighborhood basis at that point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_convex_space_iff_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.locally_convex_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Tor'_obj_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Tor_succ_of_projective", "description": "The higher `Tor` groups for `X` and `Y` are zero if `Y` is projective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Tor_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Tor'_succ_of_projective", "description": "The higher `Tor'` groups for `X` and `Y` are zero if `X` is projective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Tor'_map_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Tor'", "description": "An alternative definition of `Tor`, where we left-derive in the first factor instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Tor'_obj_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Tor", "description": "We define `Tor C n : C \u2964 C \u2964 C` by left-deriving in the second factor of `(X, Y) \u21a6 X \u2297 Y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Tor_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.skeleton.category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.from_skeleton_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.map_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.to_thin_skeleton", "description": "The functor from a category to its thin skeleton.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.map\u2082_obj_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.equivalence.skeleton_equiv", "description": "Two categories which are categorically equivalent have skeletons with equivalent objects.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.to_thin_skeleton_faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.from_thin_skeleton_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.map_iso_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.thin_skeleton_is_skeleton", "description": "`from_thin_skeleton C` exhibits the thin skeleton as a skeleton.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.from_skeleton.full", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.to_thin_skeleton_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.to_thin_skeleton_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.skeleton", "description": "Construct the skeleton category as the induced category on the isomorphism classes, and derive\nits category structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.map_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.inhabited_thin_skeleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.skeleton_skeletal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.skeletal", "description": "A category is skeletal if isomorphic objects are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.map\u2082_obj_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_skeleton_of", "description": "`is_skeleton_of C D F` says that `F : D \u2964 C` exhibits `D` as a skeletal full subcategory of `C`,\nin particular `F` is a (strong) equivalence and `D` is skeletal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_skeleton_of.skel", "description": "`is_skeleton_of C D F` says that `F : D \u2964 C` exhibits `D` as a skeletal full subcategory of `C`,\nin particular `F` is a (strong) equivalence and `D` is skeletal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_skeleton_of.eqv", "description": "`is_skeleton_of C D F` says that `F : D \u2964 C` exhibits `D` as a skeletal full subcategory of `C`,\nin particular `F` is a (strong) equivalence and `D` is skeletal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.from_skeleton.is_equivalence", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.from_skeleton", "description": "The functor from the skeleton of `C` to `C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.from_thin_skeleton", "description": "Use `quotient.out` to create a functor out of the thin skeleton.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.map_comp_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.equivalence.thin_skeleton_order_iso", "description": "When `e : C \u224c \u03b1` is a categorical equivalence from a thin category `C` to some partial order `\u03b1`,\nthe `thin_skeleton C` is order isomorphic to `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.thin", "description": "The thin skeleton is thin.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.map_nat_trans", "description": "Given a natural transformation `F\u2081 \u27f6 F\u2082`, induce a natural transformation `map F\u2081 \u27f6 map F\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.eq_of_iso", "description": "If `C` is thin and skeletal, then any naturally isomorphic functors to `C` are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.from_skeleton.faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.is_skeleton_of_inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.equiv_of_both_ways", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.skeleton_is_skeleton", "description": "The `skeleton` of `C` given by choice is a skeleton of `C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.map", "description": "A functor `C \u2964 D` computably lowers to a functor `thin_skeleton C \u2964 thin_skeleton D`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.lower_adjunction", "description": "An adjunction between thin categories gives an adjunction between their thin skeletons.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.preorder", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.equivalence", "description": "The equivalence between the thin skeleton and the category itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.from_thin_skeleton_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.skeleton.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.from_skeleton_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.map_id_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.skeletal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton", "description": "Construct the skeleton category by taking the quotient of objects. This construction gives a\npreorder with nice definitional properties, but is only really appropriate for thin categories.\nIf your original category is not thin, you probably want to be using `skeleton` instead of this.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.skeleton_equivalence", "description": "The equivalence between the skeleton and the category itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.map\u2082_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor_skeletal", "description": "If `C` is thin and skeletal, `D \u2964 C` is skeletal.\n`category_theory.functor_thin` shows it is thin also.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.thin_skeleton_partial_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.from_skeleton.ess_surj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.map\u2082", "description": " A functor `C \u2964 D \u2964 E` computably lowers to a functor\n`thin_skeleton C \u2964 thin_skeleton D \u2964 thin_skeleton E`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.from_thin_skeleton_equivalence", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_skeleton.comp_to_thin_skeleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.mem_destutter'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter_sublist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter'_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter'_of_chain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter_eq_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter'_cons_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter'_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter'_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter_is_chain'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter'_ne_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter'_is_chain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter_idem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter_pair", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter_cons_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter'_cons_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter_cons'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter_eq_self_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter_of_chain'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter'_eq_self_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter'_sublist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.destutter'_is_chain'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.coe_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.order_ring_iso_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.copy_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.to_order_add_monoid_hom", "description": "Reinterpret an ordered ring homomorphism as an ordered additive monoid homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso_class.to_order_iso_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.to_fun_eq_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.to_order_ring_hom_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.coe_to_ring_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso_class", "description": " `order_ring_iso_class F \u03b1 \u03b2` states that `F` is a type of ordered semiring isomorphisms.\nYou should extend this class when you extend `order_ring_iso`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso_class.to_ring_equiv_class", "description": " `order_ring_iso_class F \u03b1 \u03b2` states that `F` is a type of ordered semiring isomorphisms.\nYou should extend this class when you extend `order_ring_iso`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso_class.map_le_map_iff", "description": " `order_ring_iso_class F \u03b1 \u03b2` states that `F` is a type of ordered semiring isomorphisms.\nYou should extend this class when you extend `order_ring_iso`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.coe_copy", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.to_fun_eq_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.trans_to_ring_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.to_order_ring_hom", "description": "Reinterpret an ordered ring isomorphism as an ordered ring homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.comp", "description": "Composition of two `order_ring_hom`s as an `order_ring_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.id_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.trans", "description": "Composition of `order_ring_iso`s as an `order_ring_iso`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.id", "description": "The identity as an ordered ring homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.comp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.symm_bijective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.coe_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.coe_to_order_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.comp_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom_class.to_order_add_monoid_hom_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.has_coe_t", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.to_order_ring_hom_eq_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.symm", "description": "The inverse of an ordered ring isomorphism as an ordered ring isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.trans_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.coe_coe_order_add_monoid_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.coe_ring_hom_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.coe_coe_ring_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.mk_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.coe_ring_equiv_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.subsingleton_right", "description": " There is at most one ordered ring isomorphism between a linear ordered field and an archimedean\nlinear ordered field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom_class", "description": " `order_ring_hom_class F \u03b1 \u03b2` states that `F` is a type of ordered semiring homomorphisms.\nYou should extend this typeclass when you extend `order_ring_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom_class.to_ring_hom_class", "description": " `order_ring_hom_class F \u03b1 \u03b2` states that `F` is a type of ordered semiring homomorphisms.\nYou should extend this typeclass when you extend `order_ring_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom_class.monotone", "description": " `order_ring_hom_class F \u03b1 \u03b2` states that `F` is a type of ordered semiring homomorphisms.\nYou should extend this typeclass when you extend `order_ring_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.to_order_iso", "description": "Reinterpret an ordered ring isomorphism as an order isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.to_order_monoid_with_zero_hom", "description": "Reinterpret an ordered ring homomorphism as an order homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.subsingleton_left", "description": " There is at most one ordered ring isomorphism between an archimedean linear ordered field and a\nlinear ordered field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.simps.symm_apply", "description": "See Note [custom simps projection]", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom", "description": " `order_ring_hom \u03b1 \u03b2` is the type of monotone semiring homomorphisms from `\u03b1` to `\u03b2`.\n\nWhen possible, instead of parametrizing results over `(f : order_ring_hom \u03b1 \u03b2)`,\nyou should parametrize over `(F : Type*) [order_ring_hom_class F \u03b1 \u03b2] (f : F)`.\n\nWhen you extend this structure, make sure to extend `order_ring_hom_class`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.to_ring_hom", "description": " `order_ring_hom \u03b1 \u03b2` is the type of monotone semiring homomorphisms from `\u03b1` to `\u03b2`.\n\nWhen possible, instead of parametrizing results over `(f : order_ring_hom \u03b1 \u03b2)`,\nyou should parametrize over `(F : Type*) [order_ring_hom_class F \u03b1 \u03b2] (f : F)`.\n\nWhen you extend this structure, make sure to extend `order_ring_hom_class`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.monotone'", "description": " `order_ring_hom \u03b1 \u03b2` is the type of monotone semiring homomorphisms from `\u03b1` to `\u03b2`.\n\nWhen possible, instead of parametrizing results over `(f : order_ring_hom \u03b1 \u03b2)`,\nyou should parametrize over `(F : Type*) [order_ring_hom_class F \u03b1 \u03b2] (f : F)`.\n\nWhen you extend this structure, make sure to extend `order_ring_hom_class`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.cancel_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.copy", "description": " Copy of a `order_ring_hom` with a new `to_fun` equal to the old one. Useful to fix definitional\nequalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.cancel_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.symm_trans_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso", "description": " `order_ring_hom \u03b1 \u03b2` is the type of order-preserving semiring isomorphisms between `\u03b1` and `\u03b2`.\n\nWhen possible, instead of parametrizing results over `(f : order_ring_iso \u03b1 \u03b2)`,\nyou should parametrize over `(F : Type*) [order_ring_iso_class F \u03b1 \u03b2] (f : F)`.\n\nWhen you extend this structure, make sure to extend `order_ring_iso_class`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.to_ring_equiv", "description": " `order_ring_hom \u03b1 \u03b2` is the type of order-preserving semiring isomorphisms between `\u03b1` and `\u03b2`.\n\nWhen possible, instead of parametrizing results over `(f : order_ring_iso \u03b1 \u03b2)`,\nyou should parametrize over `(F : Type*) [order_ring_iso_class F \u03b1 \u03b2] (f : F)`.\n\nWhen you extend this structure, make sure to extend `order_ring_iso_class`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.map_le_map_iff'", "description": " `order_ring_hom \u03b1 \u03b2` is the type of order-preserving semiring isomorphisms between `\u03b1` and `\u03b2`.\n\nWhen possible, instead of parametrizing results over `(f : order_ring_iso \u03b1 \u03b2)`,\nyou should parametrize over `(F : Type*) [order_ring_iso_class F \u03b1 \u03b2] (f : F)`.\n\nWhen you extend this structure, make sure to extend `order_ring_iso_class`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.coe_order_monoid_with_zero_hom_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.to_order_monoid_with_zero_hom_eq_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.id_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.coe_order_monoid_with_zero_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.coe_order_add_monoid_hom_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.to_order_add_monoid_hom_eq_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.to_order_iso_eq_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.symm_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso_class.to_order_ring_hom_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.coe_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.refl", "description": "The identity map as an ordered ring isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.refl_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.coe_coe_order_monoid_with_zero_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.subsingleton", "description": " There is at most one ordered ring homomorphism from a linear ordered field to an archimedean\nlinear ordered field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.partial_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.has_coe_to_fun", "description": " Helper instance for when there's too many metavariables to apply `fun_like.has_coe_to_fun`\ndirectly.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom_class.to_order_monoid_with_zero_hom_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.coe_order_iso_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.order_ring_hom_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.coe_order_add_monoid_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.comp_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.coe_ring_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.has_coe_to_fun", "description": " Helper instance for when there's too many metavariables to apply `fun_like.has_coe_to_fun`\ndirectly.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.coe_to_order_ring_hom_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.has_coe_t", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.to_ring_hom_eq_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_hom.preorder", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.to_ring_equiv_eq_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.self_trans_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_ring_iso.coe_to_order_ring_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.map\u2082_span_span", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.map\u2082_span_singleton_eq_map_flip", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.map\u2082_sup_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.map\u2082_span_singleton_eq_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.map\u2082_eq_span_image2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.image2_subset_map\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.map\u2082_supr_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.map\u2082_le_map\u2082_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.map\u2082_flip", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.map\u2082_supr_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.apply_mem_map\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.map\u2082", "description": " Map a pair of submodules under a bilinear map.\n\nThis is the submodule version of `set.image2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.map\u2082_bot_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.map\u2082_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.map\u2082_le_map\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.map\u2082_sup_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.map\u2082_bot_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.map\u2082_le_map\u2082_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opposite.op", "description": "The canonical map `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1\u1d52\u1d56`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opposite.equiv_to_opposite_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opposite", "description": "The type of objects of the opposite of `\u03b1`; used to define the opposite category.\n\n In order to avoid confusion between `\u03b1` and its opposite type, we\n set up the type of objects `opposite \u03b1` using the following pattern,\n which will be repeated later for the morphisms.\n\n 1. Define `opposite \u03b1 := \u03b1`.\n 2. Define the isomorphisms `op : \u03b1 \u2192 opposite \u03b1`, `unop : opposite \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1`.\n 3. Make the definition `opposite` irreducible.\n\n This has the following consequences.\n\n * `opposite \u03b1` and `\u03b1` are distinct types in the elaborator, so you\n   must use `op` and `unop` explicitly to convert between them.\n * Both `unop (op X) = X` and `op (unop X) = X` are definitional\n   equalities. Notably, every object of the opposite category is\n   definitionally of the form `op X`, which greatly simplifies the\n   definition of the structure of the opposite category, for example.\n\n (If Lean supported definitional eta equality for records, we could\n achieve the same goals using a structure with one field.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opposite.rec", "description": "A recursor for `opposite`. Use as `induction x using opposite.rec`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opposite.op_unop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opposite.equiv_to_opposite", "description": "The type-level equivalence between a type and its opposite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opposite.unop_inj_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.op_induction'", "description": " A version of `induction x using opposite.rec` which finds the appropriate hypothesis\nautomatically, for use with `local attribute [tidy] op_induction'`. This is necessary because\n`induction x` is not able to deduce that `opposite.rec` should be used.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opposite.unop_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opposite.equiv_to_opposite_symm_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.op_induction.is_opposite", "description": "Test if `e : expr` is of type `opposite \u03b1` for some `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.op_induction.find_opposite_hyp", "description": " Find the first hypothesis of type `opposite _`. Fail if no such hypothesis exist in the local\ncontext.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opposite.op_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opposite.unop_op", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opposite.op_inj_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opposite.unop", "description": "The canonical map `\u03b1\u1d52\u1d56 \u2192 \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opposite.op_eq_iff_eq_unop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opposite.unop_eq_iff_eq_op", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opposite.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.clauses.unsat_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.clause", "description": " (([t\u2081,...t\u2098],[s\u2081,...,s\u2099]) : clause) encodes the constraints\n0 = \u27e6t\u2081\u27e7 \u2227 ... \u2227 0 = \u27e6t\u2098\u27e7 \u2227 0 \u2264 \u27e6s\u2081\u27e7 \u2227 ... \u2227 0 \u2264 \u27e6s\u2099\u27e7, where\n\u27e6t\u27e7 is the value of (t : term).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.clauses.unsat", "description": "There is no satisfiable clause c in argument", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.clause.holds_append", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.clause.sat", "description": "sat c := there exists a valuation v under which c holds", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.clause.unsat", "description": "unsat c := there is no valuation v under which c holds", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.clause.holds", "description": "holds v c := clause c holds under valuation v", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.clauses.sat", "description": "There exists a satisfiable clause c in argument", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.clause.append", "description": "append two clauses by elementwise appending", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.clauses.unsat_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prop_encodable.prop_form", "description": "Propositional formulas with labels from `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "prop_encodable.prop_form.var", "description": "Propositional formulas with labels from `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "prop_encodable.prop_form.not", "description": "Propositional formulas with labels from `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "prop_encodable.prop_form.and", "description": "Propositional formulas with labels from `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "prop_encodable.prop_form.or", "description": "Propositional formulas with labels from `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "prop_encodable.mk_fn1", "description": "defines a function out of `fin 1`", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "prop_encodable.prop_form.encodable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "prop_encodable.mk_fn0", "description": "the trivial function out of `fin 0`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "prop_encodable.mk_fn2", "description": "defines a function out of `fin 2`", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "strict_convex_space.of_pairwise_sphere_norm_ne_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_add_lt_of_not_same_ray", "description": " In a strictly convex space, if `x` and `y` are not in the same ray, then `\u2016x + y\u2016 < \u2016x\u2016 +\n\u2016y\u2016`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_combo_lt_of_ne", "description": " If `x` and `y` are two distinct vectors of norm at most `r`, then a convex combination of `x`\nand `y` with positive coefficients has norm strictly less than `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_same_ray_iff_norm_add_lt", "description": " In a strictly convex space, two vectors `x`, `y` are not in the same ray if and only if the\ntriangle inequality for `x` and `y` is strict.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_convex_closed_ball", "description": "A closed ball in a strictly convex space is strictly convex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abs_lt_norm_sub_of_not_same_ray", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "isometry.coe_affine_isometry_of_strict_convex_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "isometry.affine_isometry_of_strict_convex_space_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_convex_space.of_norm_add", "description": " If `\u2016x + y\u2016 = \u2016x\u2016 + \u2016y\u2016` implies that `x y : E` are in the same ray, then `E` is a strictly\nconvex space. See also a more", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_of_norm_eq_of_norm_add_eq", "description": " If `x` and `y` are two vectors in a strictly convex space have the same norm and the norm of\ntheir sum is equal to the sum of their norms, then they are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lt_norm_sub_of_not_same_ray", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_convex_space.of_norm_combo_ne_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_line_map_of_dist_eq_mul_of_dist_eq_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_convex_space", "description": " A *strictly convex space* is a normed space where the closed balls are strictly convex. We only\nrequire balls of positive radius with center at the origin to be strictly convex in the definition,\nthen prove that any closed ball is strictly convex in `strict_convex_closed_ball` below.\n\nSee also `strict_convex_space.of_strict_convex_closed_unit_ball`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_convex_space.strict_convex_closed_ball", "description": " A *strictly convex space* is a normed space where the closed balls are strictly convex. We only\nrequire balls of positive radius with center at the origin to be strictly convex in the definition,\nthen prove that any closed ball is strictly convex in `strict_convex_closed_ball` below.\n\nSee also `strict_convex_space.of_strict_convex_closed_unit_ball`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "open_segment_subset_ball_of_ne", "description": " If `x \u2260 y` belong to the same closed ball, then the open segment with endpoints `x` and `y` is\nincluded in the corresponding open ball.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_convex_space.of_norm_combo_lt_one", "description": " Strict convexity is equivalent to `\u2016a \u2022 x + b \u2022 y\u2016 < 1` for all `x` and `y` of norm at most `1`\nand all strictly positive `a` and `b` such that `a + b = 1`. This lemma shows that it suffices to\ncheck this for points of norm one and some `a`, `b` such that `a + b = 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "combo_mem_ball_of_ne", "description": " If `x \u2260 y` belong to the same closed ball, then a convex combination of `x` and `y` with\npositive coefficients belongs to the corresponding open ball.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_same_ray_iff_abs_lt_norm_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_add_dist_eq_iff", "description": " In a strictly convex space, the triangle inequality turns into an equality if and only if the\nmiddle point belongs to the segment joining two other points.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_convex_space.of_norm_add_ne_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "isometry.affine_isometry_of_strict_convex_space", "description": " An isometry of `normed_add_torsor`s for real normed spaces, strictly convex in the case of\nthe codomain, is an affine isometry.  Unlike Mazur-Ulam, this does not require the isometry to\nbe surjective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "same_ray_iff_norm_add", "description": " In a strictly convex space, two vectors `x`, `y` are in the same ray if and only if the triangle\ninequality for `x` and `y` becomes an equality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_convex_space.of_strict_convex_closed_unit_ball", "description": "A real normed vector space is strictly convex provided that the unit ball is strictly convex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_midpoint_lt_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_midpoint_of_dist_eq_half", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "same_ray_iff_norm_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.exprterm", "description": " The shadow syntax for arithmetic terms. All constants are reified to `cst`\n(e.g., `-5` is reified to `cst -5`) and all other atomic terms are reified to\n`exp` (e.g., `-5 * (gcd 14 -7)` is reified to `exp -5 \\`(gcd 14 -7)`).\n`exp` accepts a coefficient of type `int` as its first argument because\nmultiplication by constant is allowed by the omega test.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.exprterm.cst", "description": " The shadow syntax for arithmetic terms. All constants are reified to `cst`\n(e.g., `-5` is reified to `cst -5`) and all other atomic terms are reified to\n`exp` (e.g., `-5 * (gcd 14 -7)` is reified to `exp -5 \\`(gcd 14 -7)`).\n`exp` accepts a coefficient of type `int` as its first argument because\nmultiplication by constant is allowed by the omega test.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.exprterm.exp", "description": " The shadow syntax for arithmetic terms. All constants are reified to `cst`\n(e.g., `-5` is reified to `cst -5`) and all other atomic terms are reified to\n`exp` (e.g., `-5 * (gcd 14 -7)` is reified to `exp -5 \\`(gcd 14 -7)`).\n`exp` accepts a coefficient of type `int` as its first argument because\nmultiplication by constant is allowed by the omega test.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.exprterm.add", "description": " The shadow syntax for arithmetic terms. All constants are reified to `cst`\n(e.g., `-5` is reified to `cst -5`) and all other atomic terms are reified to\n`exp` (e.g., `-5 * (gcd 14 -7)` is reified to `exp -5 \\`(gcd 14 -7)`).\n`exp` accepts a coefficient of type `int` as its first argument because\nmultiplication by constant is allowed by the omega test.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.preterm.val", "description": "Preterm evaluation", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.val_canonize", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.preterm.has_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.canonize", "description": "Return a term (which is in canonical form by definition)\n   that is equivalent to the input preterm", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.preterm.add_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.preterm.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.preterm", "description": " Similar to `exprterm`, except that all exprs are now replaced with\nde Brujin indices of type `nat`. This is akin to generalizing over\nthe terms represented by the said exprs.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.preterm.cst", "description": " Similar to `exprterm`, except that all exprs are now replaced with\nde Brujin indices of type `nat`. This is akin to generalizing over\nthe terms represented by the said exprs.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.preterm.var", "description": " Similar to `exprterm`, except that all exprs are now replaced with\nde Brujin indices of type `nat`. This is akin to generalizing over\nthe terms represented by the said exprs.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.preterm.add", "description": " Similar to `exprterm`, except that all exprs are now replaced with\nde Brujin indices of type `nat`. This is akin to generalizing over\nthe terms represented by the said exprs.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.preterm.repr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.preterm.fresh_index", "description": "Fresh de Brujin index not used by any variable in argument", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.to_list_nodup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.not_mem_erase", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.filter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.erase", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup_iff_count_le_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup_map_iff_inj_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup_iff_sublist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.sublist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.pairwise_of_set_pairwise", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nth_le_index_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.of_append_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.insert", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.sigma", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup_middle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.not_nodup_cons_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.append", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup_attach", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.mem_erase_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.erase_eq_filter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup_bind", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup_append", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.pairwise_of_forall_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.map_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.rel_nodup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.count_eq_one_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.update_nth", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.nth_le_inj_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.mem_diff_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.of_append_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup_iff_nth_ne_nth", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.ne_singleton_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup_append_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.diff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.disjoint_of_nodup_append", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.pmap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.of_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nth_le_eq_of_ne_imp_not_nodup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.inj_on_of_nodup_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.pairwise.nodup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.of_attach", "description": "**Alias** of the forward direction of `list.nodup_attach`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.not_nodup_pair", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.attach", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `list.nodup_attach`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.filter_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup_iff_nth_le_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.diff_eq_filter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.map_update", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup_replicate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup_map_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.of_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup_join", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.pairwise_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.count_eq_of_nodup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall_mem_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.concat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "theorems_100.two_series", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.odd_gf_prop", "description": "If m is big enough, the partial product's coefficient counts the number of odd partitions", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.coeff_indicator_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.partial_distinct_gf_prop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.same_gf", "description": "The key proof idea for the partition theorem, showing that the generating functions for both\nsequences are ultimately the same (since the factor converges to 0 as m tends to infinity).\nIt's enough to not take the limit though, and just consider large enough `m`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.cut", "description": "Functions defined only on `s`, which sum to `n`. In other words, a partition of `n` indexed by `s`.\nEvery function in here is finitely supported, and the support is a subset of `s`.\nThis should be thought of as a generalisation of `finset.nat.antidiagonal_tuple` where\n`antidiagonal_tuple k n` is the same thing as `cut (s : finset.univ (fin k)) n`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.cut_insert", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.cut_zero", "description": "There is only one `cut` of 0.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.num_series'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.mem_cut", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.partial_gf_prop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.coeff_indicator_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.cut_univ_fin_eq_antidiagonal_tuple", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.same_coeffs", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.mk_odd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.constant_coeff_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.cut_equiv_antidiag", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.partial_distinct_gf", "description": "The partial product for the generating function for distinct partitions.\nTODO: As `m` tends to infinity, this converges (in the `X`-adic topology).\n\nIf `m` is sufficiently large, the `i`th coefficient gives the number of distinct partitions of the\nnatural number `i`: proved in `distinct_gf_prop`.\nIt is stated for an arbitrary commutative semiring `\u03b1`, though it usually suffices to use `\u2115`, `\u211a`\nor `\u211d`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.partial_odd_gf_prop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.coeff_prod_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.partition_theorem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.cut_empty_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.indicator_series", "description": "A convenience constructor for the power series whose coefficients indicate a subset.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.distinct_gf_prop", "description": "If m is big enough, the partial product's coefficient counts the number of distinct partitions", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.partial_odd_gf", "description": "The partial product for the generating function for odd partitions.\nTODO: As `m` tends to infinity, this converges (in the `X`-adic topology).\n\nIf `m` is sufficiently large, the `i`th coefficient gives the number of odd partitions of the\nnatural number `i`: proved in `odd_gf_prop`.\nIt is stated for an arbitrary field `\u03b1`, though it usually suffices to use `\u211a` or `\u211d`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.coeff_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "star_convex.ord_connected", "description": "**Alias** of the forward direction of `star_convex_iff_ord_connected`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.add_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.linear_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex_iff_open_segment_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.affinity", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex_sUnion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.affine_image", "description": "The image of a star-convex set under an affine map is star-convex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.preimage_add_right", "description": "The translation of a star-convex set is also star-convex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.open_segment_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex_iff_forall_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.preimage_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.linear_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.sub'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.is_linear_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex_sInter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex_iff_ord_connected", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.add_smul_sub_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex_Inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.is_linear_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex", "description": " Star-convexity of sets. `s` is star-convex at `x` if every segment from `x` to a point in `s` is\ncontained in `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.add_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.affine_preimage", "description": "The preimage of a star-convex set under an affine map is star-convex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex_iff_forall_ne_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.mem_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.ord_connected.star_convex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex_Union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.preimage_add_left", "description": "The translation of a star-convex set is also star-convex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex_iff_pointwise_add_subset", "description": "Alternative definition of star-convexity, in terms of pointwise set operations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.add_smul_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.smul_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex_iff_div", "description": "Alternative definition of star-convexity, using division.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex.segment_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_convex_iff_segment_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.restrict_stalk_iso_inv_eq_of_restrict", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.stalk_map.id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.stalk_map", "description": "A morphism of presheafed spaces induces a morphism of stalks.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.restrict_stalk_iso_inv_eq_germ_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.stalk", "description": "The stalk at `x` of a `PresheafedSpace`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.of_restrict_stalk_map_is_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.restrict_stalk_iso_hom_eq_germ_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.stalk_map.stalk_specializes_stalk_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.restrict_stalk_iso_inv_eq_germ_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.stalk_map.comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.stalk_map_germ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.stalk_map_germ_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.restrict_stalk_iso", "description": "For an open embedding `f : U \u27f6 X` and a point `x : U`, we get an isomorphism between the stalk\nof `X` at `f x` and the stalk of the restriction of `X` along `f` at t `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.stalk_map.stalk_specializes_stalk_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.stalk_map_germ_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.restrict_stalk_iso_inv_eq_germ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.restrict_stalk_iso_hom_eq_germ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.restrict_stalk_iso_hom_eq_germ_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.stalk_map.congr", "description": "If `\u03b1 = \u03b2` and `x = x'`, we would like to say that `stalk_map \u03b1 x = stalk_map \u03b2 x'`.\nUnfortunately, this equality is not well-formed, as their types are not _definitionally_ the same.\nTo get a proper congruence lemma, we therefore have to introduce these `eq_to_hom` arrows on\neither side of the equality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.stalk_map.stalk_iso", "description": "An isomorphism between presheafed spaces induces an isomorphism of stalks.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.stalk_map.stalk_specializes_stalk_map_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.stalk_map.congr_point", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.stalk_map.is_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.PresheafedSpace.stalk_map.congr_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.InjectiveResolution.self", "description": "An injective object admits a trivial injective resolution: itself in degree 0.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.InjectiveResolution.complex_d_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.InjectiveResolution.\u03b9_f_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_injective_resolutions", "description": " You will rarely use this typeclass directly: it is implied by the combination\n`[enough_injectives C]` and `[abelian C]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_injective_resolutions.out", "description": " You will rarely use this typeclass directly: it is implied by the combination\n`[enough_injectives C]` and `[abelian C]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.InjectiveResolution", "description": "An `InjectiveResolution Z` consists of a bundled `\u2115`-indexed cochain complex of injective objects,\nalong with a quasi-isomorphism to the complex consisting of just `Z` supported in degree `0`.\n\nExcept in situations where you want to provide a particular injective resolution\n(for example to compute a derived functor),\nyou will not typically need to use this bundled object, and will instead use\n* `injective_resolution Z`: the `\u2115`-indexed cochain complex\n  (equipped with `injective` and `exact` instances)\n* `injective_resolution.\u03b9 Z`: the cochain map from  `(single C _ 0).obj Z` to\n  `injective_resolution Z` (all the components are equipped with `mono` instances,\n  and when the category is `abelian` we will show `\u03b9` is a quasi-iso).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.InjectiveResolution.cocomplex", "description": "An `InjectiveResolution Z` consists of a bundled `\u2115`-indexed cochain complex of injective objects,\nalong with a quasi-isomorphism to the complex consisting of just `Z` supported in degree `0`.\n\nExcept in situations where you want to provide a particular injective resolution\n(for example to compute a derived functor),\nyou will not typically need to use this bundled object, and will instead use\n* `injective_resolution Z`: the `\u2115`-indexed cochain complex\n  (equipped with `injective` and `exact` instances)\n* `injective_resolution.\u03b9 Z`: the cochain map from  `(single C _ 0).obj Z` to\n  `injective_resolution Z` (all the components are equipped with `mono` instances,\n  and when the category is `abelian` we will show `\u03b9` is a quasi-iso).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.InjectiveResolution.\u03b9", "description": "An `InjectiveResolution Z` consists of a bundled `\u2115`-indexed cochain complex of injective objects,\nalong with a quasi-isomorphism to the complex consisting of just `Z` supported in degree `0`.\n\nExcept in situations where you want to provide a particular injective resolution\n(for example to compute a derived functor),\nyou will not typically need to use this bundled object, and will instead use\n* `injective_resolution Z`: the `\u2115`-indexed cochain complex\n  (equipped with `injective` and `exact` instances)\n* `injective_resolution.\u03b9 Z`: the cochain map from  `(single C _ 0).obj Z` to\n  `injective_resolution Z` (all the components are equipped with `mono` instances,\n  and when the category is `abelian` we will show `\u03b9` is a quasi-iso).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.InjectiveResolution.injective", "description": "An `InjectiveResolution Z` consists of a bundled `\u2115`-indexed cochain complex of injective objects,\nalong with a quasi-isomorphism to the complex consisting of just `Z` supported in degree `0`.\n\nExcept in situations where you want to provide a particular injective resolution\n(for example to compute a derived functor),\nyou will not typically need to use this bundled object, and will instead use\n* `injective_resolution Z`: the `\u2115`-indexed cochain complex\n  (equipped with `injective` and `exact` instances)\n* `injective_resolution.\u03b9 Z`: the cochain map from  `(single C _ 0).obj Z` to\n  `injective_resolution Z` (all the components are equipped with `mono` instances,\n  and when the category is `abelian` we will show `\u03b9` is a quasi-iso).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.InjectiveResolution.exact\u2080", "description": "An `InjectiveResolution Z` consists of a bundled `\u2115`-indexed cochain complex of injective objects,\nalong with a quasi-isomorphism to the complex consisting of just `Z` supported in degree `0`.\n\nExcept in situations where you want to provide a particular injective resolution\n(for example to compute a derived functor),\nyou will not typically need to use this bundled object, and will instead use\n* `injective_resolution Z`: the `\u2115`-indexed cochain complex\n  (equipped with `injective` and `exact` instances)\n* `injective_resolution.\u03b9 Z`: the cochain map from  `(single C _ 0).obj Z` to\n  `injective_resolution Z` (all the components are equipped with `mono` instances,\n  and when the category is `abelian` we will show `\u03b9` is a quasi-iso).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.InjectiveResolution.exact", "description": "An `InjectiveResolution Z` consists of a bundled `\u2115`-indexed cochain complex of injective objects,\nalong with a quasi-isomorphism to the complex consisting of just `Z` supported in degree `0`.\n\nExcept in situations where you want to provide a particular injective resolution\n(for example to compute a derived functor),\nyou will not typically need to use this bundled object, and will instead use\n* `injective_resolution Z`: the `\u2115`-indexed cochain complex\n  (equipped with `injective` and `exact` instances)\n* `injective_resolution.\u03b9 Z`: the cochain map from  `(single C _ 0).obj Z` to\n  `injective_resolution Z` (all the components are equipped with `mono` instances,\n  and when the category is `abelian` we will show `\u03b9` is a quasi-iso).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.InjectiveResolution.mono", "description": "An `InjectiveResolution Z` consists of a bundled `\u2115`-indexed cochain complex of injective objects,\nalong with a quasi-isomorphism to the complex consisting of just `Z` supported in degree `0`.\n\nExcept in situations where you want to provide a particular injective resolution\n(for example to compute a derived functor),\nyou will not typically need to use this bundled object, and will instead use\n* `injective_resolution Z`: the `\u2115`-indexed cochain complex\n  (equipped with `injective` and `exact` instances)\n* `injective_resolution.\u03b9 Z`: the cochain map from  `(single C _ 0).obj Z` to\n  `injective_resolution Z` (all the components are equipped with `mono` instances,\n  and when the category is `abelian` we will show `\u03b9` is a quasi-iso).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_injective_resolution", "description": "An object admits a injective resolution.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_injective_resolution.out", "description": "An object admits a injective resolution.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.InjectiveResolution.\u03b9_f_zero_comp_complex_d", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.InjectiveResolution.f.category_theory.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.restrict_map_of_ae_measurable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_measurable_indicator_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae