sumption is convenient to get a\nstatement that works on `\u2115`, `\u2124` and `\u211d`, although not necessary (a version in ordered fields is\ngiven in `filter.tendsto.const_mul_at_top`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.at_bot_mul_neg_const'", "description": " See also `filter.tendsto.at_bot_mul_neg_const` for a version of this lemma for\n`linear_ordered_field`s which does not require the `archimedean` assumption.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_coe_int_at_bot_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "at_top_countably_generated_of_archimedean", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.at_top_zsmul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.comap_coe_at_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_coe_int_at_top_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "at_bot_countable_basis_of_archimedean", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.at_top_mul_neg_const'", "description": " See also `filter.tendsto.at_top_mul_neg_const` for a version of this lemma for\n`linear_ordered_field`s which does not require the `archimedean` assumption.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.at_top_nsmul_neg_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.at_bot_mul_const'", "description": " See also `filter.tendsto.at_bot_mul_const` for a version of this lemma for\n`linear_ordered_field`s which does not require the `archimedean` assumption.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.at_top_mul_const'", "description": " If a function tends to infinity along a filter, then this function multiplied by a positive\nconstant (on the right) also tends to infinity. The archimedean assumption is convenient to get a\nstatement that works on `\u2115`, `\u2124` and `\u211d`, although not necessary (a version in ordered fields is\ngiven in `filter.tendsto.at_top_mul_const`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.at_bot_zsmul_neg_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.at_bot_zsmul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_coe_rat_at_top_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.comap_coe_at_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.comap_coe_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.other_spec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.other_invol", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.map.injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.map_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.is_diag.decidable_pred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.to_rel_from_rel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.mem_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.eq_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.from_rel_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.map_id'", "description": "Note: `sym2.map_id` will not simplify `sym2.map id z` due to `sym2.map_congr`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.from_rel_proj_prop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.ind", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.lift\u2082_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.rel", "description": "This is the relation capturing the notion of pairs equivalent up to permutations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.rel.refl", "description": "This is the relation capturing the notion of pairs equivalent up to permutations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.rel.swap", "description": "This is the relation capturing the notion of pairs equivalent up to permutations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.congr_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.eq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.mem.other'", "description": "Get the other element of the unordered pair using the decidable equality.\nThis is the computable version of `mem.other`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.mem.decidable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.mem_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.induction_on\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.mem_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.rel.is_equivalence", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.filter_image_quotient_mk_is_diag", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.lift\u2082", "description": "A two-argument version of `sym2.lift`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.rel_bool_spec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.nontrivial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.filter_image_quotient_mk_not_is_diag", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.map_pair_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.congr_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.rel_bool", "description": "An algorithm for computing `sym2.rel`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.rel.decidable_rel", "description": "Given `[decidable_eq \u03b1]` and `[fintype \u03b1]`, the following instance gives `fintype (sym2 \u03b1)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.out_snd_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.mem_and_mem_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.to_rel_symmetric", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.other_spec'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.map_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.from_rel", "description": "Symmetric relations define a set on `sym2 \u03b1` by taking all those pairs\nof elements that are related.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.other_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.mem_mk_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.other_mem'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.mem_mk_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.mem.other", "description": "Given an element of the unordered pair, give the other element using `classical.some`.\nSee also `mem.other'` for the computable version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.rel.setoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.equiv_multiset", "description": "The symmetric square is equivalent to multisets of cardinality\ntwo. (This is currently a synonym for `equiv_sym`, but it's provided\nin case the definition for `sym` changes.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.is_diag_iff_proj_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2", "description": "`sym2 \u03b1` is the symmetric square of `\u03b1`, which, in other words, is the\ntype of unordered pairs.\n\nIt is equivalent in a natural way to multisets of cardinality 2 (see\n`sym2.equiv_multiset`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.is_diag", "description": "A predicate for testing whether an element of `sym2 \u03b1` is on the diagonal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.other_eq_other'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.is_diag_iff_mem_range_diag", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.equiv_sym", "description": "The symmetric square is equivalent to the second symmetric power.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.forall", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.is_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.coe_lift_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.lift", "description": " The universal property of `sym2`; symmetric functions of two arguments are equivalent to\nfunctions from `sym2`. Note that when `\u03b2` is `Prop`, it can sometimes be more convenient to use\n`sym2.from_rel` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.other_invol'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.eq_of_ne_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.is_diag.mem_range_diag", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.map", "description": "The functor `sym2` is functorial, and this function constructs the induced maps.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.diag_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.mem_from_rel_irrefl_other_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.coe_lift\u2082_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.diag_is_diag", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.map_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.diag", "description": "A type `\u03b1` is naturally included in the diagonal of `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b1`, and this function gives the image\nof this diagonal in `sym2 \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.mk_eq_mk_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.from_rel_irreflexive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.set_like", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.rel_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.from_rel_prop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.sym2_equiv_sym'", "description": "The symmetric square is equivalent to length-2 vectors up to permutations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.mk_is_diag_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.to_rel", "description": " The inverse to `sym2.from_rel`. Given a set on `sym2 \u03b1`, give a symmetric relation on `\u03b1`\n(see `sym2.to_rel_symmetric`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.mk_prod_swap_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.mem_iff_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.from_rel.decidable_pred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.mem", "description": "This is a predicate that determines whether a given term is a member of a term of the\nsymmetric square.  From this point of view, the symmetric square is the subtype of\ncardinality-two multisets on `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.map_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.out_fst_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.from_rel_to_rel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.from_rel_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.exists", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.mem_iff_exists", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.rel.symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.other_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.induction_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.rel.trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.to_rel_prop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.lift_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.ideal_prod_prime", "description": "Classification of prime ideals in product rings: the prime ideals of `R \u00d7 S` are precisely the\n   ideals of the form `p \u00d7 S` or `R \u00d7 p`, where `p` is a prime ideal of `R` or `S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.mem_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.ideal_prod_equiv", "description": "Ideals of `R \u00d7 S` are in one-to-one correspondence with pairs of ideals of `R` and ideals of\n   `S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.map_prod_comm_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_prime_ideal_prod_top'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_prime_of_is_prime_prod_top'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.prod_top_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.map_fst_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_prime_ideal_prod_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.prod", "description": "`I \u00d7 J` as an ideal of `R \u00d7 S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.ideal_prod_eq", "description": "Every ideal of the product ring is of the form `I \u00d7 J`, where `I` and `J` can be explicitly\n   given as the image under the projection maps.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.ideal_prod_prime_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.ideal_prod_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.map_snd_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.prod.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_prime_of_is_prime_prod_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.naturality_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.hcomp_id_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.map_hom_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.app_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.hcomp", "description": "`hcomp \u03b1 \u03b2` is the horizontal composition of natural transformations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.vcomp_app'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.vcomp_eq_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.comp_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.epi_of_epi_app", "description": "A natural transformation is an epimorphism if each component is.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.comp_app_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.hcomp_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.flip_obj_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.flip_map_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.flip", "description": "Flip the arguments of a bifunctor. See also `currying.lean`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.id_hcomp_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.exchange", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.category", "description": "`functor.category C D` gives the category structure on functors and natural transformations\nbetween categories `C` and `D`.\n\nNotice that if `C` and `D` are both small categories at the same universe level,\nthis is another small category at that level.\nHowever if `C` and `D` are both large categories at the same universe level,\nthis is a small category at the next higher level.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.flip_obj_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.map_hom_inv_app_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.congr_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.id_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.map_inv_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.map_inv_hom_app_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.mono_of_mono_app", "description": "A natural transformation is a monomorphism if each component is.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.map_polynomial_aeval", "description": "The **spectral mapping theorem** for polynomials in a Banach algebra over `\u2102`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "alg_hom.norm_apply_le_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.pow_norm_pow_one_div_tendsto_nhds_spectral_radius", "description": " **Gelfand's formula**: Given an element `a : A` of a complex Banach algebra, the\n`spectral_radius` of `a` is the limit of the sequence `\u2016a ^ n\u2016\u208a ^ (1 / n)`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_ring.alg_equiv_complex_of_complete", "description": " **Gelfand-Mazur theorem**: For a complex Banach division algebra, the natural `algebra_map \u2102 A`\nis an algebra isomorphism whose inverse is given by selecting the (unique) element of\n`spectrum \u2102 a`. In addition, `algebra_map_isometry` guarantees this map is an isometry.\n\nNote: because `normed_division_ring` requires the field `norm_mul' : \u2200 a b, \u2016a * b\u2016 = \u2016a\u2016 * \u2016b\u2016`, we\ndon't use this type class and instead opt for a `normed_ring` in which the nonzero elements are\nprecisely the units. This allows for the application of this isomorphism in broader contexts, e.g.,\nto the quotient of a complex Banach algebra by a maximal ideal. In the case when `A` is actually a\n`normed_division_ring`, one may fill in the argument `hA` with the lemma `is_unit_iff_ne_zero`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.spectral_radius.of_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.is_unit_one_sub_smul_of_lt_inv_radius", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.equiv_alg_hom", "description": "The equivalence between characters and algebra homomorphisms into the base field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.exists_nnnorm_eq_spectral_radius", "description": " In a complex Banach algebra, the spectral radius is always attained by some element of the\nspectrum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.exp_mem_exp", "description": "For `\ud835\udd5c = \u211d` or `\ud835\udd5c = \u2102`, `exp \ud835\udd5c` maps the spectrum of `a` into the spectrum of `exp \ud835\udd5c a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.norm_le_norm_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.is_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.mem_resolvent_set_of_spectral_radius_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.spectral_radius_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.equiv_alg_hom_symm_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.has_fpower_series_on_ball_inverse_one_sub_smul", "description": " In a Banach algebra `A` over a nontrivially normed field `\ud835\udd5c`, for any `a : A` the\npower series with coefficients `a ^ n` represents the function `(1 - z \u2022 a)\u207b\u00b9` in a disk of\nradius `\u2016a\u2016\u208a\u207b\u00b9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.map_pow", "description": " A specialization of the spectral mapping theorem for polynomials in a Banach algebra over `\u2102`\nto monic monomials.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.pow_nnnorm_pow_one_div_tendsto_nhds_spectral_radius", "description": " **Gelfand's formula**: Given an element `a : A` of a complex Banach algebra, the\n`spectral_radius` of `a` is the limit of the sequence `\u2016a ^ n\u2016\u208a ^ (1 / n)`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.nonempty", "description": "In a (nontrivial) complex Banach algebra, every element has nonempty spectrum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.algebra_map_eq_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectral_radius", "description": "The *spectral radius* is the supremum of the `nnnorm` (`\u2016\u2b1d\u2016\u208a`) of elements in the spectrum,\n   coerced into an element of `\u211d\u22650\u221e`. Note that it is possible for `spectrum \ud835\udd5c a = \u2205`. In this\n   case, `spectral_radius a = 0`.  It is also possible that `spectrum \ud835\udd5c a` be unbounded (though\n   not for Banach algebras, see `spectrum.is_bounded`, below).  In this case,\n   `spectral_radius a = \u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.differentiable_on_inverse_one_sub_smul", "description": " In a Banach algebra `A` over `\ud835\udd5c`, for `a : A` the function `\u03bb z, (1 - z \u2022 a)\u207b\u00b9` is\ndifferentiable on any closed ball centered at zero of radius `r < (spectral_radius \ud835\udd5c a)\u207b\u00b9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.spectral_radius_lt_of_forall_lt", "description": " In a complex Banach algebra, if every element of the spectrum has norm strictly less than\n`r : \u211d\u22650`, then the spectral radius is also strictly less than `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.subset_closed_ball_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.spectral_radius_le_liminf_pow_nnnorm_pow_one_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.limsup_pow_nnnorm_pow_one_div_le_spectral_radius", "description": "The `limsup` relationship for the spectral radius used to prove `spectrum.gelfand_formula`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_ring.alg_equiv_complex_of_complete_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "alg_hom.to_continuous_linear_map_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.equiv_alg_hom_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.spectral_radius_le_nnnorm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.mem_resolvent_set_of_norm_lt_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "alg_hom.to_continuous_linear_map", "description": " An algebra homomorphism into the base field, as a continuous linear map (since it is\nautomatically bounded).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.is_bounded", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.is_compact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.subset_closed_ball_norm_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.norm_resolvent_le_forall", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.exists_nnnorm_eq_spectral_radius_of_nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.spectral_radius_le_pow_nnnorm_pow_one_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "alg_hom.norm_apply_le_self_mul_norm_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "alg_hom.coe_to_continuous_linear_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.is_open_resolvent_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.norm_le_norm_mul_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.has_deriv_at_resolvent", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "alg_hom.continuous_linear_map_class", "description": " An algebra homomorphism into the base field, as a continuous linear map (since it is\nautomatically bounded). See note [lower instance priority]", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.mem_resolvent_set_of_norm_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_ring.alg_equiv_complex_of_complete_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.spectral_radius_lt_of_forall_lt_of_nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bdd_below.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_lub_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "csupr_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_glb_neg'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "csupr_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_glb.inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_glb_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_glb_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bdd_below.inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_mem_lower_bounds_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_csupr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bdd_below.neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bdd_below_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subset_lower_bounds_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bdd_above_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subset_lower_bounds_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_lub_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bdd_above_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_lub_neg'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_mem_upper_bounds_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_mem_upper_bounds_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_mem_lower_bounds_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_lub_inv'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bdd_above.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_lub.inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "csupr_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bdd_above.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subset_upper_bounds_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subset_upper_bounds_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bdd_above.neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bdd_below_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bdd_below.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bdd_above.inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "csupr_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_glb_inv'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_csupr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_glb.neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_lub.neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.option_congr_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.option_congr", "description": "A universe-polymorphic version of `equiv_functor.map_equiv option e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.option_subtype_symm_apply_apply_some", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.option_symm_apply_none_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.some_remove_none_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.remove_none_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.remove_none_option_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.remove_none_some", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.option_subtype_symm_apply_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.option_subtype_symm_apply_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.option_congr_eq_equiv_function_map_equiv", "description": " When `\u03b1` and `\u03b2` are in the same universe, this is the same as the result of\n`equiv_functor.map_equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.option_congr_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.option_subtype_symm_apply_apply_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.option_subtype_apply_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.coe_option_subtype_apply_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.option_congr_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.option_subtype_apply_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.remove_none", "description": " Given an equivalence between two `option` types, eliminate `none` from that equivalence by\nmapping `e.symm none` to `e none`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.option_congr_trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.option_subtype", "description": " Equivalences between `option \u03b1` and `\u03b2` that send `none` to `x` are equivalent to\nequivalences between `\u03b1` and `{y : \u03b2 // y \u2260 x}`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.option_congr_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.remove_none_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.quot_ker_equiv_of_surjective", "description": "The first isomorphism theorem for surjective linear maps.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.card_quotient_mul_card_quotient", "description": "Corollary of the third isomorphism theorem: `[S : T] [M : S] = [M : T]`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.quot_ker_equiv_range_apply_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.quotient_inf_equiv_sup_quotient_apply_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.quotient_inf_equiv_sup_quotient_symm_apply_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.coe_quotient_inf_to_sup_quotient", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.quotient_inf_equiv_sup_quotient", "description": "Second Isomorphism Law : the canonical map from `p/(p \u2229 p')` to `(p+p')/p'` as a linear isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.quotient_inf_to_sup_quotient", "description": "Canonical linear map from the quotient `p/(p \u2229 p')` to `(p+p')/p'`, mapping `x + (p \u2229 p')`\nto `x + p'`, where `p` and `p'` are submodules of an ambient module.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.quotient_inf_equiv_sup_quotient_symm_apply_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.quotient_quotient_equiv_quotient_aux", "description": "The map from the third isomorphism theorem for modules: `(M / S) / (T / S) \u2192 M / T`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.quot_ker_equiv_range_symm_apply_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.quotient_quotient_equiv_quotient_aux_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.quotient_inf_equiv_sup_quotient_symm_apply_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.quotient_quotient_equiv_quotient", "description": "**Noether's third isomorphism theorem** for modules: `(M / S) / (T / S) \u2243 M / T`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.quot_ker_equiv_range", "description": " The first isomorphism law for modules. The quotient of `M` by the kernel of `f` is linearly\nequivalent to the range of `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.quotient_quotient_equiv_quotient_aux_mk_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_divergence_prod_Icc_of_has_fderiv_within_at_off_countable_of_le", "description": " **Divergence theorem** for functions on the plane along rectangles. It is formulated in terms of\ntwo functions `f g : \u211d \u00d7 \u211d \u2192 E` and an integral over `Icc a b = [a.1, b.1] \u00d7 [a.2, b.2]`, where\n`a b : \u211d \u00d7 \u211d`, `a \u2264 b`. When thinking of `f` and `g` as the two coordinates of a single function\n`F : \u211d \u00d7 \u211d \u2192 E \u00d7 E` and when `E = \u211d`, this is the usual statement that the integral of the\ndivergence of `F` inside the rectangle equals the integral of the normal derivative of `F` along the\nboundary.\n\nSee also `measure_theory.integral2_divergence_prod_of_has_fderiv_within_at_off_countable` for a\nversion that does not assume `a \u2264 b` and uses iterated interval integral instead of the integral\nover `Icc a b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_divergence_of_has_fderiv_within_at_off_countable_aux\u2082", "description": " An auxiliary lemma for\n`measure_theory.integral_divergence_of_has_fderiv_within_at_off_countable`. Compared to the previous\nlemma, here we drop the assumption of differentiability on the boundary of the box.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral2_divergence_prod_of_has_fderiv_within_at_off_countable", "description": " **Divergence theorem** for functions on the plane. It is formulated in terms of two functions\n`f g : \u211d \u00d7 \u211d \u2192 E` and iterated integral `\u222b x in a\u2081..b\u2081, \u222b y in a\u2082..b\u2082, _`, where\n`a\u2081 a\u2082 b\u2081 b\u2082 : \u211d`. When thinking of `f` and `g` as the two coordinates of a single function\n`F : \u211d \u00d7 \u211d \u2192 E \u00d7 E` and when `E = \u211d`, this is the usual statement that the integral of the\ndivergence of `F` inside the rectangle with vertices `(a\u1d62, b\u2c7c)`, `i, j =1,2`, equals the integral of\nthe normal derivative of `F` along the boundary.\n\nSee also `measure_theory.integral_divergence_prod_Icc_of_has_fderiv_within_at_off_countable_of_le`\nfor a version that uses an integral over `Icc a b`, where `a b : \u211d \u00d7 \u211d`, `a \u2264 b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_eq_of_has_deriv_within_at_off_countable", "description": " **Fundamental theorem of calculus, part 2**. This version assumes that `f` is continuous on the\ninterval and is differentiable off a countable set `s`.\n\nSee also `measure_theory.interval_integral.integral_eq_sub_of_has_deriv_right` for a version that\nonly assumes right differentiability of `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_divergence_of_has_fderiv_within_at_off_countable_aux\u2081", "description": " An auxiliary lemma for\n`measure_theory.integral_divergence_of_has_fderiv_within_at_off_countable`. This is exactly\n`box_integral.has_integral_GP_divergence_of_forall_has_deriv_within_at` reformulated for the\nBochner integral.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_divergence_of_has_fderiv_within_at_off_countable_of_equiv", "description": " An auxiliary lemma that is used to specialize the general divergence theorem to spaces that do\nnot have the form `fin n \u2192 \u211d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_divergence_of_has_fderiv_within_at_off_countable", "description": " **Divergence theorem** for Bochner integral. If `f : \u211d\u207f\u207a\u00b9 \u2192 E\u207f\u207a\u00b9` is continuous on a rectangular\nbox `[a, b] : set \u211d\u207f\u207a\u00b9`, `a \u2264 b`, is differentiable on its interior with derivative\n`f' : \u211d\u207f\u207a\u00b9 \u2192 \u211d\u207f\u207a\u00b9 \u2192L[\u211d] E\u207f\u207a\u00b9` and the divergence `\u03bb x, \u2211 i, f' x e\u1d62 i` is integrable on `[a, b]`,\nwhere `e\u1d62 = pi.single i 1` is the `i`-th basis vector, then its integral is equal to the sum of\nintegrals of `f` over the faces of `[a, b]`, taken with appropriat signs.\n\nMoreover, the same is true if the function is not differentiable at countably many\npoints of the interior of `[a, b]`.\n\nWe represent both faces `x i = a i` and `x i = b i` as the box\n`face i = [a \u2218 fin.succ_above i, b \u2218 fin.succ_above i]` in `\u211d\u207f`, where\n`fin.succ_above : fin n \u21aao fin (n + 1)` is the order embedding with range `{i}\u1d9c`. The restrictions\nof `f : \u211d\u207f\u207a\u00b9 \u2192 E\u207f\u207a\u00b9` to these faces are given by `f \u2218 back_face i` and `f \u2218 front_face i`, where\n`back_face i = fin.insert_nth i (a i)` and `front_face i = fin.insert_nth i (b i)` are embeddings\n`\u211d\u207f \u2192 \u211d\u207f\u207a\u00b9` that take `y : \u211d\u207f` and insert `a i` (resp., `b i`) as `i`-th coordinate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_divergence_of_has_fderiv_within_at_off_countable'", "description": " **Divergence theorem** for a family of functions `f : fin (n + 1) \u2192 \u211d\u207f\u207a\u00b9 \u2192 E`. See also\n`measure_theory.integral_divergence_of_has_fderiv_within_at_off_countable'` for a version formulated\nin terms of a vector-valued function `f : \u211d\u207f\u207a\u00b9 \u2192 E\u207f\u207a\u00b9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_eq_of_has_deriv_within_at_off_countable_of_le", "description": " **Fundamental theorem of calculus, part 2**. This version assumes that `f` is continuous on the\ninterval and is differentiable off a countable set `s`.\n\nSee also\n\n* `interval_integral.integral_eq_sub_of_has_deriv_right_of_le` for a version that only assumes right\ndifferentiability of `f`;\n\n* `measure_theory.integral_eq_of_has_deriv_within_at_off_countable` for a version that works both\n  for `a \u2264 b` and `b \u2264 a` at the expense of using unordered intervals instead of `set.Icc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_solid_norm", "description": " Let `\u03b1` be an `add_comm_group` with a `lattice` structure. A norm on `\u03b1` is *solid* if, for `a`\nand `b` in `\u03b1`, with absolute values `|a|` and `|b|` respectively, `|a| \u2264 |b|` implies `\u2016a\u2016 \u2264 \u2016b\u2016`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_solid_norm.solid", "description": " Let `\u03b1` be an `add_comm_group` with a `lattice` structure. A norm on `\u03b1` is *solid* if, for `a`\nand `b` in `\u03b1`, with absolute values `|a|` and `|b|` respectively, `|a| \u2264 |b|` implies `\u2016a\u2016 \u2264 \u2016b\u2016`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_closed_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_closed_le_of_is_closed_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_lattice_add_comm_group_has_continuous_inf", "description": "Let `\u03b1` be a normed lattice ordered group. Then the infimum is jointly continuous.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_sup_sub_sup_le_add_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_lattice_add_comm_group.order_closed_topology", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_neg'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_sup_le_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lattice_ordered_add_comm_group.is_solid_ball", "description": "If `\u03b1` has a solid norm, then the balls centered at the origin of `\u03b1` are solid sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.has_solid_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.has_solid_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_sup_sub_sup_le_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lipschitz_with_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lipschitz_with_sup_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_lattice_add_comm_group_has_continuous_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.normed_lattice_add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dual_solid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_dual.normed_lattice_add_comm_group", "description": "Let `\u03b1` be a normed lattice ordered group, then the order dual is also a\nnormed lattice ordered group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_lattice_add_comm_group_to_ordered_add_comm_group", "description": "A normed lattice ordered group is an ordered additive commutative group", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_lattice_add_comm_group", "description": "Let `\u03b1` be a normed commutative group equipped with a partial order covariant with addition, with\nrespect which `\u03b1` forms a lattice. Suppose that `\u03b1` is *solid*, that is to say, for `a` and `b` in\n`\u03b1`, with absolute values `|a|` and `|b|` respectively, `|a| \u2264 |b|` implies `\u2016a\u2016 \u2264 \u2016b\u2016`. Then `\u03b1` is\nsaid to be a normed lattice ordered group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_lattice_add_comm_group.to_normed_add_comm_group", "description": "Let `\u03b1` be a normed commutative group equipped with a partial order covariant with addition, with\nrespect which `\u03b1` forms a lattice. Suppose that `\u03b1` is *solid*, that is to say, for `a` and `b` in\n`\u03b1`, with absolute values `|a|` and `|b|` respectively, `|a| \u2264 |b|` implies `\u2016a\u2016 \u2264 \u2016b\u2016`. Then `\u03b1` is\nsaid to be a normed lattice ordered group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_lattice_add_comm_group.to_lattice", "description": "Let `\u03b1` be a normed commutative group equipped with a partial order covariant with addition, with\nrespect which `\u03b1` forms a lattice. Suppose that `\u03b1` is *solid*, that is to say, for `a` and `b` in\n`\u03b1`, with absolute values `|a|` and `|b|` respectively, `|a| \u2264 |b|` implies `\u2016a\u2016 \u2264 \u2016b\u2016`. Then `\u03b1` is\nsaid to be a normed lattice ordered group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_lattice_add_comm_group.to_has_solid_norm", "description": "Let `\u03b1` be a normed commutative group equipped with a partial order covariant with addition, with\nrespect which `\u03b1` forms a lattice. Suppose that `\u03b1` is *solid*, that is to say, for `a` and `b` in\n`\u03b1`, with absolute values `|a|` and `|b|` respectively, `|a| \u2264 |b|` implies `\u2016a\u2016 \u2264 \u2016b\u2016`. Then `\u03b1` is\nsaid to be a normed lattice ordered group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_lattice_add_comm_group.add_le_add_left", "description": "Let `\u03b1` be a normed commutative group equipped with a partial order covariant with addition, with\nrespect which `\u03b1` forms a lattice. Suppose that `\u03b1` is *solid*, that is to say, for `a` and `b` in\n`\u03b1`, with absolute values `|a|` and `|b|` respectively, `|a| \u2264 |b|` implies `\u2016a\u2016 \u2264 \u2016b\u2016`. Then `\u03b1` is\nsaid to be a normed lattice ordered group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_abs_sub_abs", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_inf_sub_inf_le_add_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_inf_sub_inf_le_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_le_norm_of_abs_le_abs", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_inf_le_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_abs_eq_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_lattice_add_comm_group_topological_lattice", "description": "Let `\u03b1` be a normed lattice ordered group. Then `\u03b1` is a topological lattice in the norm topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.degree_to_subring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_prime_map_C_of_is_prime", "description": "If `P` is a prime ideal of `R`, then `P.R[x]` is a prime ideal of `R[x]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.prime_rename_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.restriction", "description": " Given a polynomial, return the polynomial whose coefficients are in\nthe ring closure of the original coefficients.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.exists_irreducible_of_degree_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.no_zero_divisors", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.exists_irreducible_of_nat_degree_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.degree_lt", "description": "The `R`-submodule of `R[X]` consisting of polynomials of degree < `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_restriction", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.frange_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.degree_lt_equiv", "description": "The first `n` coefficients on `degree_lt n` form a linear equivalence with `fin n \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.eval_eq_sum_degree_lt_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.frange", "description": "The finset of nonzero coefficients of a polynomial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_fg_degree_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.monic_to_subring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.monic.geom_sum'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.sup_ker_aeval_eq_ker_aeval_mul_of_coprime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.prime_C_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.leading_coeff", "description": " Given an ideal `I` in `R[X]`, make the ideal in `R` of the\nleading coefficients in `I`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.unique_factorization_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.polynomial_not_is_field", "description": "`R[X]` is never a field for any ring `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.of_subring", "description": " Given a polynomial whose coefficients are in some subring, return\nthe corresponding polynomial whose coefficients are in the ambient ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.mem_frange_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.mem_degree_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.sup_ker_aeval_le_ker_aeval_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.mem_of_polynomial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.frange_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_restriction'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.mem_leading_coeff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.monic_geom_sum_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.monic.geom_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.degree_lt_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.map_to_subring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_to_subring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_prime_map_C_iff_is_prime", "description": "If `P` is a prime ideal of `R`, then `P.R[x]` is a prime ideal of `R[x]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.mem_ker_mod_by_monic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.leading_coeff_nth", "description": " Given an ideal `I` of `R[X]`, make the ideal in `R` of\nleading coefficients of polynomials in `I` with degree \u2264 `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.polynomial_mem_ideal_of_coeff_mem_ideal", "description": "If every coefficient of a polynomial is in an ideal `I`, then so is the polynomial itself", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.prime_C_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.degree_le_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.ker_map_ring_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.restriction_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.ker_mod_by_monic_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.degree_le_eq_span_X_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.degree_lt_equiv_eq_zero_iff_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.to_subring_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.map_mv_polynomial_eq_eval\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.support_to_subring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_to_subring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.is_domain", "description": "The multivariate polynomial ring over an integral domain is an integral domain.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.no_zero_divisors_fin", "description": " Auxiliary lemma:\nMultivariate polynomials over an integral domain\nwith variables indexed by `fin n` form an integral domain.\nThis fact is proven inductively,\nand then used to prove the general case without any finiteness hypotheses.\nSee `mv_polynomial.no_zero_divisors` for the general case.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.map_restriction", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.to_subring", "description": " Given a polynomial `p` and a subring `T` that contains the coefficients of `p`,\nreturn the corresponding polynomial whose coefficients are in `T`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_prod_mem_ideal_pow_tsub", "description": "If `I` is an ideal, and `p\u1d62` is a finite family of polynomials each satisfying\n`\u2200 k, (p\u1d62)\u2096 \u2208 I\u207f\u2071\u207b\u1d4f` for some `n\u1d62`, then `p = \u220f p\u1d62` also satisfies `\u2200 k, p\u2096 \u2208 I\u207f\u207b\u1d4f` with `n = \u2211 n\u1d62`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.of_polynomial", "description": "Transport an ideal of `R[X]` to an `R`-submodule of `R[X]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.no_zero_divisors_of_finite", "description": " Auxiliary definition:\nMultivariate polynomials in finitely many variables over an integral domain form an integral domain.\nThis fact is proven by transport of structure from the `mv_polynomial.no_zero_divisors_fin`,\nand then used to prove the general case without finiteness hypotheses.\nSee `mv_polynomial.no_zero_divisors` for the general case.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.char_p", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_of_subring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.disjoint_ker_aeval_of_coprime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.mem_leading_coeff_nth_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.degree_le", "description": "The `R`-submodule of `R[X]` consisting of polynomials of degree \u2264 `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.mem_ideal_of_coeff_mem_ideal", "description": " If every coefficient of a polynomial is in an ideal `I`, then so is the polynomial itself,\nmultivariate version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.mem_degree_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.linear_independent_powers_iff_aeval", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.to_subring_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.leading_coeff_nth_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.restriction_one", "description":