: "with_top.preimage_coe_Iic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.preimage_coe_Ioc_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.preimage_coe_Ici", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.image_coe_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_bot.range_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glued_cover_to_glue_data_U", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.\u03b9_iso_carrier_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glued_cover_t'_fst_fst_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.to_LocallyRingedSpace_glue_data", "description": "The glue data of locally ringed spaces spaces associated to a family of glue data of schemes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.from_glued_open_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.open_cover", "description": "The open cover of the glued space given by the glue data.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.\u03b9_glue_morphisms_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glued_cover_t'_snd_snd_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.algebraic_geometry.Scheme.forget_to_Top.category_theory.limits.preserves_colimit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glued_cover_to_glue_data_J", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glued_cover_to_glue_data_t", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glued_cover_t'_snd_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.from_glued", "description": " The canonical morphism from the gluing of an open cover of `X` into `X`.\nThis is an isomorphism, as witnessed by an `is_iso` instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data", "description": "A family of gluing data consists of\n1. An index type `J`\n2. An scheme `U i` for each `i : J`.\n3. An scheme `V i j` for each `i j : J`.\n  (Note that this is `J \u00d7 J \u2192 Scheme` rather than `J \u2192 J \u2192 Scheme` to connect to the\n  limits library easier.)\n4. An open immersion `f i j : V i j \u27f6 U i` for each `i j : \u03b9`.\n5. A transition map `t i j : V i j \u27f6 V j i` for each `i j : \u03b9`.\nsuch that\n6. `f i i` is an isomorphism.\n7. `t i i` is the identity.\n8. `V i j \u00d7[U i] V i k \u27f6 V i j \u27f6 V j i` factors through `V j k \u00d7[U j] V j i \u27f6 V j i` via some\n    `t' : V i j \u00d7[U i] V i k \u27f6 V j k \u00d7[U j] V j i`.\n9. `t' i j k \u226b t' j k i \u226b t' k i j = \ud835\udfd9 _`.\n\nWe can then glue the schemes `U i` together by identifying `V i j` with `V j i`, such\nthat the `U i`'s are open subschemes of the glued space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.to_glue_data", "description": "A family of gluing data consists of\n1. An index type `J`\n2. An scheme `U i` for each `i : J`.\n3. An scheme `V i j` for each `i j : J`.\n  (Note that this is `J \u00d7 J \u2192 Scheme` rather than `J \u2192 J \u2192 Scheme` to connect to the\n  limits library easier.)\n4. An open immersion `f i j : V i j \u27f6 U i` for each `i j : \u03b9`.\n5. A transition map `t i j : V i j \u27f6 V j i` for each `i j : \u03b9`.\nsuch that\n6. `f i i` is an isomorphism.\n7. `t i i` is the identity.\n8. `V i j \u00d7[U i] V i k \u27f6 V i j \u27f6 V j i` factors through `V j k \u00d7[U j] V j i \u27f6 V j i` via some\n    `t' : V i j \u00d7[U i] V i k \u27f6 V j k \u00d7[U j] V j i`.\n9. `t' i j k \u226b t' j k i \u226b t' k i j = \ud835\udfd9 _`.\n\nWe can then glue the schemes `U i` together by identifying `V i j` with `V j i`, such\nthat the `U i`'s are open subschemes of the glued space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.f_open", "description": "A family of gluing data consists of\n1. An index type `J`\n2. An scheme `U i` for each `i : J`.\n3. An scheme `V i j` for each `i j : J`.\n  (Note that this is `J \u00d7 J \u2192 Scheme` rather than `J \u2192 J \u2192 Scheme` to connect to the\n  limits library easier.)\n4. An open immersion `f i j : V i j \u27f6 U i` for each `i j : \u03b9`.\n5. A transition map `t i j : V i j \u27f6 V j i` for each `i j : \u03b9`.\nsuch that\n6. `f i i` is an isomorphism.\n7. `t i i` is the identity.\n8. `V i j \u00d7[U i] V i k \u27f6 V i j \u27f6 V j i` factors through `V j k \u00d7[U j] V j i \u27f6 V j i` via some\n    `t' : V i j \u00d7[U i] V i k \u27f6 V j k \u00d7[U j] V j i`.\n9. `t' i j k \u226b t' j k i \u226b t' k i j = \ud835\udfd9 _`.\n\nWe can then glue the schemes `U i` together by identifying `V i j` with `V j i`, such\nthat the `U i`'s are open subschemes of the glued space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glued_cover_t'_fst_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.iso_LocallyRingedSpace", "description": "The gluing as sheafed spaces is isomorphic to the gluing as presheafed spaces.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glued_cover_cocycle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glued_cover_t'_snd_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glued_cover_to_glue_data_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glued_cover_t'_snd_fst_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.rel", "description": " An equivalence relation on `\u03a3 i, D.U i` that holds iff `\ud835\udda3 .\u03b9 i x = \ud835\udda3 .\u03b9 j y`.\nSee `Scheme.gluing_data.\u03b9_eq_iff`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.hom_ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glued_cover_cocycle_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.\u03b9_eq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glued_cover_to_glue_data_V", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.\u03b9_from_glued", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.iso_carrier", "description": " The underlying topological space of the glued scheme is isomorphic to the gluing of the\nunderlying spacess", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.from_glued_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.from_glued_open_embedding", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.\u03b9_is_open_immersion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.is_open_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.glue_condition_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.\u03b9_glue_morphisms", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.from_glued_open_immersion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.algebraic_geometry.Scheme.forget_to_LocallyRingedSpace.category_theory.creates_colimit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.from_glued.category_theory.is_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glue_morphisms", "description": " Given an open cover of `X`, and a morphism `\ud835\udcb0.obj x \u27f6 Y` for each open subscheme in the cover,\nsuch that these morphisms are compatible in the intersection (pullback), we may glue the morphisms\ntogether into a morphism `X \u27f6 Y`.\n\nNote:\nIf `X` is exactly (defeq to) the gluing of `U i`, then using `multicoequalizer.desc` suffices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.from_glued_stalk_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.\u03b9_jointly_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.glue_condition", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glued_cover_cocycle_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glued_cover_t'", "description": "(Implementation) the transition maps in the glue data associated with an open cover.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.\u03b9_iso_LocallyRingedSpace_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.diagram.category_theory.limits.has_multicoequalizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.glued", "description": "The glued scheme of a glued space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glued_cover_t'_fst_snd_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.V_pullback_cone", "description": " The pullback cone spanned by `V i j \u27f6 U i` and `V i j \u27f6 U j`.\nThis is a pullback diagram (`V_pullback_cone_is_limit`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.V_pullback_cone_is_limit", "description": " The following diagram is a pullback, i.e. `V\u1d62\u2c7c` is the intersection of `U\u1d62` and `U\u2c7c` in `X`.\n\nV\u1d62\u2c7c \u27f6 U\u1d62\n |      |\n \u2193      \u2193\n U\u2c7c \u27f6 X", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.glued_Scheme", "description": " (Implementation). The glued scheme of a glue data.\nThis should not be used outside this file. Use `Scheme.glue_data.glued` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glued_cover_to_glue_data_t'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glued_cover", "description": " The glue data associated with an open cover.\nThe canonical isomorphism `\ud835\udcb0.glued_cover.glued \u27f6 X` is provided by `\ud835\udcb0.from_glued`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.base.category_theory.epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.\u03b9_from_glued_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.open_cover.glued_cover_t'_fst_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.glue_data.\u03b9", "description": "The immersion from `D.U i` into the glued space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_add_antidiagonal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.add_antidiagonal.finite_of_is_wf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_antidiagonal_mono_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.add_antidiagonal.eq_of_snd_eq_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_antidiagonal", "description": " `set.mul_antidiagonal s t a` is the set of all pairs of an element in `s` and an element in `t`\nthat multiply to `a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_antidiagonal.fst_eq_fst_iff_snd_eq_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.add_antidiagonal.eq_of_fst_eq_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_antidiagonal_mono_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_antidiagonal.eq_of_snd_eq_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_antidiagonal.eq_of_fst_le_fst_of_snd_le_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.add_antidiagonal", "description": "`set.add_antidiagonal s t a` is the set of all pairs of an element in `s` and an\nelement in `t` that add to `a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.add_antidiagonal.fst_eq_fst_iff_snd_eq_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.add_antidiagonal_mono_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_antidiagonal.finite_of_is_wf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.swap_mem_mul_antidiagonal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.add_antidiagonal.eq_of_fst_le_fst_of_snd_le_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_antidiagonal.finite_of_is_pwo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.add_antidiagonal_mono_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.add_antidiagonal.finite_of_is_pwo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_mul_antidiagonal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mul_antidiagonal.eq_of_fst_eq_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.swap_mem_add_antidiagonal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nth_le_of_fn", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.of_fn_mul'", "description": "This breaks a list of `m*n` items into `n` groups each containing `m` elements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.last_of_fn_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall_mem_of_fn_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.of_fn_injective", "description": "Note we can only state this when the two functions are indexed by defeq `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.of_fn_rec_of_fn", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.equiv_sigma_tuple", "description": "Lists are equivalent to the sigma type of tuples of a given length.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.map_of_fn", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.last_of_fn", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.length_of_fn", "description": "The length of a list converted from a function is the size of the domain.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nth_le_of_fn'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.equiv_sigma_tuple_apply_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.of_fn_fin_repeat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall_iff_forall_tuple", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.array_eq_of_fn", "description": "Arrays converted to lists are the same as `of_fn` on the indexing function of the array.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.of_fn_zero", "description": "`of_fn` on an empty domain is the empty list.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.of_fn_succ'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.of_fn_add", "description": "Note this matches the convention of `list.of_fn_succ'`, putting the `fin m` elements first.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.length_of_fn_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.of_fn_inj'", "description": "`fin.sigma_eq_iff_eq_comp_cast` may be useful to work with the RHS of this expression.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.of_fn_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.of_fn_fin_append", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.of_fn_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nth_of_fn_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.pairwise_of_fn", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.of_fn_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nth_of_fn", "description": "The `n`th element of a list", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.of_fn_rec", "description": " A recursor for lists that expands a list into a function mapping to its elements.\n\nThis can be used with `induction l using list.of_fn_rec`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.of_fn_eq_nil_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.exists_iff_exists_tuple", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.mem_of_fn", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.equiv_sigma_tuple_apply_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.of_fn_inj", "description": "A special case of `list.of_fn_inj'` for when the two functions are indexed by defeq `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.of_fn_mul", "description": "This breaks a list of `m*n` items into `m` groups each containing `n` elements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.of_fn_nth_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.equiv_sigma_tuple_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_with_one.to_has_nat_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.bin_cast_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_with_one.unary", "description": "`add_monoid_with_one` implementation using unary recursion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ne_zero.pos_of_ne_zero_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast_add_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_with_one", "description": "An `add_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid` with a `1`.\nIt also contains data for the unique homomorphism `\u2115 \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_with_one.nat_cast", "description": "An `add_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid` with a `1`.\nIt also contains data for the unique homomorphism `\u2115 \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_with_one.add", "description": "An `add_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid` with a `1`.\nIt also contains data for the unique homomorphism `\u2115 \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_with_one.add_assoc", "description": "An `add_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid` with a `1`.\nIt also contains data for the unique homomorphism `\u2115 \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_with_one.zero", "description": "An `add_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid` with a `1`.\nIt also contains data for the unique homomorphism `\u2115 \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_with_one.zero_add", "description": "An `add_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid` with a `1`.\nIt also contains data for the unique homomorphism `\u2115 \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_with_one.add_zero", "description": "An `add_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid` with a `1`.\nIt also contains data for the unique homomorphism `\u2115 \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_with_one.nsmul", "description": "An `add_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid` with a `1`.\nIt also contains data for the unique homomorphism `\u2115 \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_with_one.nsmul_zero'", "description": "An `add_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid` with a `1`.\nIt also contains data for the unique homomorphism `\u2115 \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_with_one.nsmul_succ'", "description": "An `add_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid` with a `1`.\nIt also contains data for the unique homomorphism `\u2115 \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_with_one.one", "description": "An `add_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid` with a `1`.\nIt also contains data for the unique homomorphism `\u2115 \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_with_one.nat_cast_zero", "description": "An `add_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid` with a `1`.\nIt also contains data for the unique homomorphism `\u2115 \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_with_one.nat_cast_succ", "description": "An `add_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid` with a `1`.\nIt also contains data for the unique homomorphism `\u2115 \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast_bit0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_with_one.to_has_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast_bit1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_with_one.binary", "description": "`add_monoid_with_one` implementation using binary recursion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ne_zero.of_ne_zero_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ne_zero.nat_cast_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast", "description": "Canonical homomorphism from `\u2115` to a additive monoid `R` with a `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_monoid_with_one.to_add_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_with_one.to_add_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "library_note.coercion into rings", "description": "Coercions such as `nat.cast_coe` that go from a concrete structure such as\n`\u2115` to an arbitrary ring `R` should be set up as follows:\n```lean\n@[priority 900] instance : has_coe_t \u2115 R := \u27e8...\u27e9\n```\n\nIt needs to be `has_coe_t` instead of `has_coe` because otherwise type-class\ninference would loop when constructing the transitive coercion `\u2115 \u2192 \u2115 \u2192 \u2115 \u2192 ...`.\nThe reduced priority is necessary so that it doesn't conflict with instances\nsuch as `has_coe_t R (option R)`.\n\nFor this to work, we reduce the priority of the `coe_base` and `coe_trans`\ninstances because we want the instances for `has_coe_t` to be tried in the\nfollowing order:\n\n 1. `has_coe_t` instances declared in mathlib (such as `has_coe_t R (with_top R)`, etc.)\n 2. `coe_base`, which contains instances such as `has_coe (fin n) n`\n 3. `nat.cast_coe : has_coe_t \u2115 R` etc.\n 4. `coe_trans`\n\nIf `coe_trans` is tried first, then `nat.cast_coe` doesn't get a chance to apply.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast_ite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.unary_cast", "description": "The numeral `((0+1)+\u22ef)+1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_monoid_with_one", "description": "An `add_comm_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid_with_one` satisfying `a + b = b + a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_monoid_with_one.nat_cast", "description": "An `add_comm_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid_with_one` satisfying `a + b = b + a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_monoid_with_one.add", "description": "An `add_comm_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid_with_one` satisfying `a + b = b + a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_monoid_with_one.add_assoc", "description": "An `add_comm_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid_with_one` satisfying `a + b = b + a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_monoid_with_one.zero", "description": "An `add_comm_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid_with_one` satisfying `a + b = b + a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_monoid_with_one.zero_add", "description": "An `add_comm_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid_with_one` satisfying `a + b = b + a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_monoid_with_one.add_zero", "description": "An `add_comm_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid_with_one` satisfying `a + b = b + a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_monoid_with_one.nsmul", "description": "An `add_comm_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid_with_one` satisfying `a + b = b + a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_monoid_with_one.nsmul_zero'", "description": "An `add_comm_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid_with_one` satisfying `a + b = b + a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_monoid_with_one.nsmul_succ'", "description": "An `add_comm_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid_with_one` satisfying `a + b = b + a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_monoid_with_one.one", "description": "An `add_comm_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid_with_one` satisfying `a + b = b + a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_monoid_with_one.nat_cast_zero", "description": "An `add_comm_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid_with_one` satisfying `a + b = b + a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_monoid_with_one.nat_cast_succ", "description": "An `add_comm_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid_with_one` satisfying `a + b = b + a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_monoid_with_one.add_comm", "description": "An `add_comm_monoid_with_one` is an `add_monoid_with_one` satisfying `a + b = b + a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.bin_cast", "description": "Computationally friendlier cast than `nat.unary_cast`, using binary representation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_nat_cast", "description": "Type class for the canonical homomorphism `\u2115 \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_nat_cast.nat_cast", "description": "Type class for the canonical homomorphism `\u2115 \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_monoid_with_one.to_add_monoid_with_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.bertrand", "description": "**Alias** of `nat.exists_prime_lt_and_le_two_mul`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "central_binom_le_of_no_bertrand_prime", "description": "An upper bound on the central binomial coefficient used in the proof of Bertrand's postulate.\nThe bound splits the prime factors of `central_binom n` into those\n1. At most `sqrt (2 * n)`, which contribute at most `2 * n` for each such prime.\n2. Between `sqrt (2 * n)` and `2 * n / 3`, which contribute at most `4^(2 * n / 3)` in total.\n3. Between `2 * n / 3` and `n`, which do not exist.\n4. Between `n` and `2 * n`, which would not exist in the case where Bertrand's postulate is false.\n5. Above `2 * n`, which do not exist.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "central_binom_factorization_small", "description": "A lemma that tells us that, in the case where Bertrand's postulate does not hold, the prime\nfactorization of the central binomial coefficent only has factors at most `2 * n / 3 + 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bertrand_main_inequality", "description": "The inequality which contradicts Bertrand's postulate, for large enough `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bertrand.real_main_inequality", "description": "A reified version of the `bertrand.main_inequality` below.\nThis is not best possible: it actually holds for 464 \u2264 x.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.exists_prime_lt_and_le_two_mul_eventually", "description": "Proves that Bertrand's postulate holds for all sufficiently large `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.exists_prime_lt_and_le_two_mul", "description": "**Bertrand's Postulate**: For any positive natural number, there is a prime which is greater than\nit, but no more than twice as large.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.exists_prime_lt_and_le_two_mul_succ", "description": "Proves that Bertrand's postulate holds over all positive naturals less than n by identifying a\ndescending list of primes, each no more than twice the next, such that the list contains a witness\nfor each number \u2264 n.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "path.continuous_delay_refl_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "path.continuous_delay_refl_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "H_space", "description": "A topological space `X` is an H-space if it behaves like a (potentially non-associative)\ntopological group, but where the axioms for a group only hold up to homotopy.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "H_space.Hmul", "description": "A topological space `X` is an H-space if it behaves like a (potentially non-associative)\ntopological group, but where the axioms for a group only hold up to homotopy.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "H_space.e", "description": "A topological space `X` is an H-space if it behaves like a (potentially non-associative)\ntopological group, but where the axioms for a group only hold up to homotopy.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "H_space.Hmul_e_e", "description": "A topological space `X` is an H-space if it behaves like a (potentially non-associative)\ntopological group, but where the axioms for a group only hold up to homotopy.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "H_space.e_Hmul", "description": "A topological space `X` is an H-space if it behaves like a (potentially non-associative)\ntopological group, but where the axioms for a group only hold up to homotopy.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "H_space.Hmul_e", "description": "A topological space `X` is an H-space if it behaves like a (potentially non-associative)\ntopological group, but where the axioms for a group only hold up to homotopy.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.Q_right", "description": " `Q_right` is analogous to the function `Q` defined on p. 475 of [serre1951] that helps proving\ncontinuity of `delay_refl_right`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.continuous_Q_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "path.delay_refl_right_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_group.to_H_space", "description": " The definition `to_H_space` is not an instance because its `@additive` version would\nlead to a diamond since a topological field would inherit two `H_space` structures, one from the\n`mul_one_class` and one from the `add_zero_class`. In the case of a group, we make\n`topological_group.H_space` an instance.\"", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "path.delay_refl_left_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_add_group.H_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "path.delay_refl_right_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.Q_right_one_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_group.one_eq_H_space_e", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "path.delay_refl_left", "description": " This is the function on p. 475 of [serre1951], defining a homotopy from a path `\u03b3` to the\nproduct path `e \u2227 \u03b3`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_add_group.to_H_space", "description": "The definition `to_H_space` is not an instance because it comes together with a\nmultiplicative version which would lead to a diamond since a topological field would inherit two\n`H_space` structures, one from the `mul_one_class` and one from the `add_zero_class`. In the case\nof an additive group, we make `topological_group.H_space` an instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.Q_right_one_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "path.H_space", "description": "The loop space at x carries a structure of a `H-space`. Note that the field `e_Hmul`\n(resp. `Hmul_e`) neither implies nor is implied by `path.homotopy.refl_trans`\n(resp. `path.homotopy.trans_refl`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.Q_right_zero_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.Q_right_zero_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_group.H_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "H_space.prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "path.delay_refl_left_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "path.delay_refl_right", "description": " This is the function analogous to the one on p. 475 of [serre1951], defining a homotopy from\nthe product path `\u03b3 \u2227 e` to `\u03b3`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.euclidean_dist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean.ball", "description": "Open ball w.r.t. the euclidean distance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean.closed_ball_mem_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean.is_open_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean.mem_ball_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean.dist", "description": " If `x` and `y` are two points in a finite dimensional space over `\u211d`, then `euclidean.dist x y`\nis the distance between these points in the metric defined by some inner product space structure on\n`E`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean.nhds_basis_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean.ball_mem_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean.nhds_basis_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean.is_compact_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "to_euclidean", "description": " If `E` is a finite dimensional space over `\u211d`, then `to_euclidean` is a continuous `\u211d`-linear\nequivalence between `E` and the Euclidean space of the same dimension.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean.ball_subset_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean.exists_pos_lt_subset_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean.closed_ball", "description": "Closed ball w.r.t. the euclidean distance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean.closure_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean.closed_ball_eq_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean.is_closed_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean.ball_eq_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean.closed_ball_eq_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional.rank_lt_aleph_0", "description": "The rank of a finite module is finite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional.finrank_prod", "description": "The finrank of `M \u00d7 N` is `(finrank R M) + (finrank R N)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.finrank_quotient_le", "description": "The dimension of a quotient is bounded by the dimension of the ambient space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional.finrank_pi_fintype", "description": "The finrank of a finite product is the sum of the finranks.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.finrank_le_finrank_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional.finrank_finsupp", "description": "The finrank of `(\u03b9 \u2192\u2080 R)` is `fintype.card \u03b9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional.finrank_pi", "description": "The finrank of `(\u03b9 \u2192 R)` is `fintype.card \u03b9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_equiv.of_finrank_eq", "description": "Two finite and free modules are isomorphic if they have the same (finite) rank.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional.nonempty_linear_equiv_iff_finrank_eq", "description": "Two finite and free modules are isomorphic if and only if they have the same (finite) rank.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.finrank_le_finrank_of_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional.nonempty_linear_equiv_of_finrank_eq", "description": "Two finite and free modules are isomorphic if they have the same (finite) rank.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional.finrank_direct_sum", "description": "The finrank of the direct sum is the sum of the finranks.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional.finrank_matrix", "description": "If `m` and `n` are `fintype`, the finrank of `m \u00d7 n` matrices is\n `(fintype.card m) * (fintype.card n)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.finrank_map_le", "description": "Pushforwards of finite submodules have a smaller finrank.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional.submodule.finrank_map_subtype_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.finrank_le", "description": "The dimension of a submodule is bounded by the dimension of the ambient space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional.finrank_eq_rank", "description": "If `M` is finite, `finrank M = rank M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional.finrank_tensor_product", "description": "The finrank of `M \u2297[R] N` is `(finrank R M) * (finrank R N)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional.finrank_eq_card_choose_basis_index", "description": "The finrank of a free module `M` over `R` is the cardinality of `choose_basis_index R M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.finrank_range_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integral_gaussian_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_at_gaussian_integral", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integral_gaussian", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integral_cexp_neg_mul_sq_add_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gaussian_fourier.integrable_cexp_neg_mul_sq_add_real_mul_I", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gaussian_fourier.tendsto_vertical_integral", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integrable_on_rpow_mul_exp_neg_mul_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.Gamma_one_half_eq", "description": "The special-value formula `\u0393(1/2) = \u221a\u03c0`, which is equivalent to the Gaussian integral.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integrable_exp_neg_mul_sq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integrable_on_Ioi_exp_neg_mul_sq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fourier_transform_gaussian_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rpow_mul_exp_neg_mul_sq_is_o_exp_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gaussian_fourier.norm_cexp_neg_mul_sq_add_mul_I'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gaussian_fourier.vertical_integral_norm_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.Gamma_one_half_eq", "description": "The special-value formula `\u0393(1/2) = \u221a\u03c0`, which is equivalent to the Gaussian integral.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integrable_cexp_neg_mul_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integrable_mul_cexp_neg_mul_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fourier_transform_gaussian", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_rpow_abs_mul_exp_neg_mul_sq_cocompact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integral_mul_cexp_neg_mul_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.tsum_exp_neg_mul_int_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integral_gaussian_sq_complex", "description": "The *square* of the Gaussian integral `\u222b x:\u211d, exp (-b * x^2)` is equal to `\u03c0 / b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integrable_rpow_mul_exp_neg_mul_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gaussian_fourier.integral_cexp_neg_mul_sq_add_real_mul_I", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integral_gaussian_complex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_o_exp_neg_mul_sq_cocompact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.tsum_exp_neg_mul_int_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_cexp_neg_mul_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gaussian_fourier.norm_cexp_neg_mul_sq_add_mul_I", "description": " Explicit formula for the norm of the Gaussian function along the vertical\nedges.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integral_gaussian_complex_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integrable_exp_neg_mul_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_neg_mul_sq_is_o_exp_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integrable_mul_exp_neg_mul_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gaussian_fourier.vertical_integral", "description": " The integral of the Gaussian function over the vertical edges of a rectangle\nwith vertices at `(\u00b1T, 0)` and `(\u00b1T, c)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.decl_reducibility.inhabited", "description": "Satisfy the inhabited linter", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.decl_reducibility.to_attribute", "description": "Return the attribute (as a `name`) corresponding to a reducibility level.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.decl_reducibility", "description": " Possible reducibility attributes for a declaration:\nreducible, semireducible (the default), irreducible.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.decl_reducibility.reducible", "description": " Possible reducibility attributes for a declaration:\nreducible, semireducible (the default), irreducible.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.decl_reducibility.semireducible", "description": " Possible reducibility attributes for a declaration:\nreducible, semireducible (the default), irreducible.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.decl_reducibility.irreducible", "description": " Possible reducibility attributes for a declaration:\nreducible, semireducible (the default), irreducible.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.with_local_reducibility", "description": "Execute a tactic with a temporarily modified reducibility attribute for a declaration.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.decl_reducibility.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.get_decl_reducibility", "description": " Get the reducibility attribute of a declaration.\nFails if the name does not refer to an existing declaration.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.set_decl_reducibility", "description": " Set the reducibility attribute of a declaration.\nIf `persistent := ff`, this is scoped to the enclosing `section`, like `local attribute`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.join_filter_empty_eq_ff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.drop_sum_join", "description": " In a join, dropping all the elements up to an index which is the sum of the lengths of the\nfirst `i` sublists, is the same as taking the join after dropping the first `i` sublists.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.join_drop_length_sub_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.join_filter_ne_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.drop_take_succ_join_eq_nth_le", "description": " In a join of sublists, taking the slice between the indices `A` and `B - 1` gives back the\noriginal sublist of index `i` if `A` is the sum of the lenghts of sublists of index `< i`, and\n`B` is the sum of the lengths of sublists of index `\u2264 i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.drop_take_succ_eq_cons_nth_le", "description": " Taking only the first `i+1` elements in a list, and then dropping the first `i` ones, one is\nleft with a list of length `1` made of the `i`-th element of the original list.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.join_append", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.append_join_map_append", "description": " We can rebracket `x ++ (l\u2081 ++ x) ++ (l\u2082 ++ x) ++ ... ++ (l\u2099 ++ x)` to\n`(x ++ l\u2081) ++ (x ++ l\u2082) ++ ... ++ (x ++ l\u2099) ++ x` where `L = [l\u2081, l\u2082, ..., l\u2099]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.length_join", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.take_sum_join", "description": " In a join, taking the first elements up to an index which is the sum of the lengths of the\nfirst `i` sublists, is the same as taking the join of the first `i` sublists.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.join_join", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.join_reverse", "description": "Joining a reverse is the same as reversing all parts and reversing the joined result.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.join_eq_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.length_bind", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.eq_iff_join_eq", "description": " Two lists of sublists are equal iff their joins coincide, as well as the lengths of the\nsublists.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.join_concat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.reverse_join", "description": "Reversing a join is the same as reversing the order of parts and reversing all parts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nth_le_join", "description": " The `n`-th element in a join of sublists is the `j`-th element of the `i`th sublist,\nwhere `n` can be obtained in terms of `i` and `j` by adding the lengths of all the sublists\nof index `< i`, and adding `j`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.bind_eq_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.join_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.sum_take_map_length_lt1", "description": "Auxiliary lemma to control elements in a join.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.sum_take_map_length_lt2", "description": "Auxiliary lemma to control elements in a join.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_comm_class_sphere_ball_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ne_neg_of_mem_sphere", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_comm_class_sphere_closed_ball_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_scalar_tower_closed_ball_closed_ball_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_scalar_tower_closed_ball_ball_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_scalar_tower_sphere_closed_ball_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_scalar_tower_sphere_sphere_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action_closed_ball_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ne_neg_of_mem_unit_sphere", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_scalar_tower_sphere_sphere_sphere", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_continuous_smul_closed_ball_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action_sphere_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_continuous_smul_sphere_sphere", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_comm_class_sphere_closed_ball_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_scalar_tower_sphere_ball_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_comm_class_sphere_sphere_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_continuous_smul_closed_ball_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action_closed_ball_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_comm_class_sphere_sphere_sphere", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_scalar_tower_sphere_closed_ball_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_continuous_smul_sphere_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action_sphere_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_comm_class_sphere_sphere_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_comm_class_closed_ball_closed_ball_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_scalar_tower_sphere_sphere_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_continuous_smul_sphere_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_comm_class_closed_ball_closed_ball_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action_sphere_sphere", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_scalar_tower_closed_ball_closed_ball_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "theorems_100.fin.measurable_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.pi.measure_theory.measure_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.measure_theory.measure_space.volume.measure_theory.is_probability_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.fin.measurable_singleton_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.birthday_measure", "description": "**Birthday Problem**: first probabilistic interpretation.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.birthday", "description": "**Birthday Problem**: set cardinality interpretation.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.fin_fin.measure_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "order_dual.linear_ordered_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_dual.ordered_add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_dual.linear_ordered_add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_dual.ordered_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pochhammer_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pochhammer_succ_comp_X_add_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pochhammer_eval_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pochhammer_nat_eq_desc_factorial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pochhammer_eval_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pochhammer_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.mul_X_add_nat_cast_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "factorial_mul_pochhammer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pochhammer_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pochhammer_nat_eq_asc_factorial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pochhammer", "description": "`pochhammer S n` is the polynomial `X * (X+1) * ... * (X + n - 1)`,\nwith coefficients in the semiring `S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pochhammer_nat_eval_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pochhammer_eval_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pochhammer_succ_eval", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pochhammer_succ_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pochhammer_ne_zero_eval_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pochhammer_eval_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pochhammer_succ_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pochhammer_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pochhammer_zero_eval_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pochhammer_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_detector.is_separator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_codetecting.is_coseparating", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_codetecting_op_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_detector_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparator_op_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid_of_is_detecting_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_terminal_of_is_separating", "description": "An ingredient of the proof of the Special Adjoint Functor Theorem: a cocomplete well-copowered\n   category with a small separating set has a terminal object.\n\n   In fact, it follows from the Special Adjoint Functor Theorem that `C` is already complete, see\n   `has_limits_of_has_colimits_of_is_separating`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparator_prod_of_is_coseparator_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_detecting_op_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separating_op_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.structured_arrow.is_coseparating_proj_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_codetector.is_coseparator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separator_op_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_detecting.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separator.def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separator_iff_faithful_coyoneda_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparator_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_detector.def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparating.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_detecting", "description": "We say that `\ud835\udca2` is a detecting set if the functors `C(G, -)` collectively reflect isomorphisms,\n   i.e., if any `h` with domain in `\ud835\udca2` uniquely factors through `f`, then `f` is an isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.costructured_arrow.is_separating_proj_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.well_powered_of_is_detector", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.eq_of_le_of_is_detecting", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparator_prod_of_is_coseparator_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparator_iff_faithful_yoneda_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_codetector", "description": "We say that `G` is a codetector if the functor `C(-, G)` reflects isomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separator_iff_epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separating.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separator_coprod_of_is_separator_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparator_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separator_coprod_of_is_separator_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separator", "description": "We say that `G` is a separator if the functor `C(G, -)` is faithful.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_detector_op_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separating_iff_epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparating_empty_of_thin", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_codetecting", "description": "We say that `\ud835\udca2` is a codetecting set if the functors `C(-, G)` collectively reflect\n   isomorphisms, i.e., if any `h` with codomain in `G` uniquely factors through `f`, then `f` is\n   an isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_codetector.def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparating", "description": "We say that `\ud835\udca2` is a coseparating set if the functors `C(-, G)` for `G \u2208 \ud835\udca2` are collectively\n   faithful, i.e., if `f \u226b h = g \u226b h` for all `h` with codomain in `\ud835\udca2` implies `f = g`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_detecting.is_separating", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_codetector_op_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparator_iff_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separating_empty_of_thin", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.well_powered_of_is_detecting", "description": "A category with pullbacks and a small detecting set is well-powered.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparating_op_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparator", "description": "We say that `G` is a coseparator if the functor `C(-, G)` is faithful.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separating", "description": "We say that `\ud835\udca2` is a separating set if the functors `C(G, -)` for `G \u2208 \ud835\udca2` are collectively\n   faithful, i.e., if `h \u226b f = h \u226b g` for all `h` with domain in `\ud835\udca2` implies `f = g`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_codetecting_iff_is_coseparating", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separating_unop_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separator_unop_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparator_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_codetecting_empty_of_groupoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separator_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_codetector_iff_reflects_isomorphisms_yoneda_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_detector", "description": "We say that `G` is a detector if the functor `C(G, -)` reflects isomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparator.def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_cospearator.is_codetector", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparator_pi_of_is_coseparator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_initial_of_is_coseparating", "description": "An ingredient of the proof of the Special Adjoint Functor Theorem: a complete well-powered\n   category with a small coseparating set has an initial object.\n\n   In fact, it follows from the Special Adjoint Functor Theorem that `C` is already cocomplete,\n   see `has_colimits_of_has_limits_of_is_coseparating`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparating_iff_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_detecting_unop_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_detecting_empty_of_groupoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.eq_of_is_detecting", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separator_sigma_of_is_separator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_detector_unop_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separator_sigma", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_of_is_coseparating_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparating_unop_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separating.is_detecting", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_codetecting_unop_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparating.is_codetecting", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparator_unop_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separator.is_detector", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_of_is_separating_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_detecting_iff_is_separating", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid_of_is_codetecting_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.inf_eq_of_is_detecting", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_codetector_unop_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separator_coprod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_codetector_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_codetecting.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_detector_iff_reflects_isomorphisms_coyoneda_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.run_linarith_on_pfs", "description": "Given a list `hyps` of proofs of comparisons, `run_linarith_on_pfs cfg hyps pref_type`\npreprocesses `hyps` according to the list of preprocessors in `cfg`.\nThis results in a list of branches (typically only one),\neach of which must succeed in order to close the goal.\n\nIn each branch, we partition the  list of hypotheses by type, and run `linarith` on each class\nin the partition; one of these must succeed in order for `linarith` to succeed on this branch.\nIf `pref_type` is given, it will first use the class of proofs of comparisons over that type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.apply_contr_lemma", "description": "`apply_contr_lemma` inspects the target to see if it can be moved to a hypothesis by negation.\nFor example, a goal `\u22a2 a \u2264 b` can become `a > b \u22a2 false`.\nIf this is the case, it applies the appropriate lemma and introduces the new hypothesis.\nIt returns the type of the terms in the comparison (e.g. the type of `a` and `b` above) and the\nnewly introduced local constant.\nOtherwise returns `none`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.nlinarith", "description": "An extension of `linarith` with some preprocessing to allow it to solve some nonlinear arithmetic\nproblems. (Based on Coq's `nra` tactic.) See `linarith` for the available syntax of options,\nwhich are inherited by `nlinarith`; that is, `nlinarith!` and `nlinarith only [h1, h2]` all work as\nin `linarith`. The preprocessing is as follows:\n\n* For every subterm `a ^ 2` or `a * a` in a hypothesis or the goal,\n  the assumption `0 \u2264 a ^ 2` or `0 \u2264 a * a` is added to the context.\n* For every pair of hypotheses `a1 R1 b1`, `a2 R2 b2` in the context, `R1, R2 \u2208 {<, \u2264, =}`,\n  the assumption `0 R' (b1 - a1) * (b2 - a2)` is added to the context (non-recursively),\n  where `R \u2208 {<, \u2264, =}` is the appropriate comparison derived from `R1, R2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.linarith", "description": "`linarith reduce_semi only_on hyps cfg` tries to close the goal using linear arithmetic. It fails\nif it does not succeed at doing this.\n\n* If `reduce_semi` is true, it will unfold semireducible definitions when trying to match atomic\nexpressions.\n* `hyps` is a list of proofs of comparisons to include in the search.\n* If `only_on` is true, the search will be restricted to `hyps`. Otherwise it will use all\n  comparisons in the local context.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.linarith", "description": "Tries to prove a goal of `false` by linear arithmetic on hypotheses.\nIf the goal is a linear (in)equality, tries to prove it by contradiction.\nIf the goal is not `false` or an inequality, applies `exfalso` and tries linarith on the\nhypotheses.\n\n* `linarith` will use all relevant hypotheses in the local context.\n* `linarith [t1, t2, t3]` will add proof terms t1, t2, t3 to the local context.\n* `linarith only [h1, h2, h3, t1, t2, t3]` will use only the goal (if relevant), local hypotheses\n  `h1`, `h2`, `h3`, and proofs `t1`, `t2`, `t3`. It will ignore the rest of the local context.\n* `linarith!` will use a stronger reducibility setting to identify atoms.\n\nConfig options:\n* `linarith {exfalso := ff}` will fail on a goal that is neither an inequality nor `false`\n* `linarith {restrict_type := T}` will run only on hypotheses that are inequalities over `T`\n* `linarith {discharger := tac}` will use `tac` instead of `ring` for normalization.\n  Options: `ring2`, `ring SOP`, `simp`\n* `linarith {split_hypotheses := ff}` will not destruct conjunctions in the context.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.nlinarith", "description": "An extension of `linarith` with some preprocessing to allow it to solve some nonlinear arithmetic\nproblems. (Based on Coq's `nra` tactic.) See `linarith` for the available syntax of options,\nwhich are inherited by `nlinarith`; that is, `nlinarith!` and `nlinarith only [h1, h2]` all work as\nin `linarith`. The preprocessing is as follows:\n\n* For every subterm `a ^ 2` or `a * a` in a hypothesis or the goal,\n  the assumption `0 \u2264 a ^ 2` or `0 \u2264 a * a` is added to the context.\n* For every pair of hypotheses `a1 R1 b1`, `a2 R2 b2` in the context, `R1, R2 \u2208 {<, \u2264, =}`,\n  the assumption `0 R' (b1 - a1) * (b2 - a2)` is added to the context (non-recursively),\n  where `R \u2208 {<, \u2264, =}` is the appropriate comparison derived from `R1, R2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.try_linarith_on_lists", "description": "Given a list `ls` of lists of proofs of comparisons, `try_linarith_on_lists cfg ls` will try to\nprove `false` by calling `linarith` on each list in succession. It will stop at the first proof of\n`false`, and fail if no contradiction is found with any list.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.linarith", "description": "`linarith` attempts to find a contradiction between hypotheses that are linear (in)equalities.\nEquivalently, it can prove a linear inequality by assuming its negation and proving `false`.\n\nIn theory, `linarith` should prove any goal that is true in the theory of linear arithmetic over\nthe rationals. While there is some special handling for non-dense orders like `nat` and `int`,\nthis tactic is not complete for these theories and will not prove every true goal. It will solve\ngoals over arbitrary types that instantiate `linear_ordered_comm_ring`.\n\nAn example:\n```lean\nexample (x y z : \u211a) (h1 : 2*x  < 3*y) (h2 : -4*x + 2*z < 0)\n        (h3 : 12*y - 4* z < 0)  : false :=\nby linarith\n```\n\n`linarith` will use all appropriate hypotheses and the negation of the goal, if applicable.\n\n`linarith [t1, t2, t3]` will additionally use proof terms `t1, t2, t3`.\n\n`linarith only [h1, h2, h3, t1, t2, t3]` will use only the goal (if relevant), local hypotheses\n`h1`, `h2`, `h3`, and proofs `t1`, `t2`, `t3`. It will ignore the rest of the local context.\n\n`linarith!` will use a stronger reducibility setting to try to identify atoms. For example,\n```lean\nexample (x : \u211a) : id x \u2265 x :=\nby linarith\n```\nwill fail, because `linarith` will not identify `x` and `id x`. `linarith!` will.\nThis can sometimes be expensive.\n\n`linarith {discharger := tac, restrict_type := tp, exfalso := ff}` takes a config object with five\noptional arguments:\n* `discharger` specifies a tactic to be used for reducing an algebraic equation in the\n  proof stage. The default is `ring`. Other options currently include `ring SOP` or `simp` for basic\n  problems.\n* `restrict_type` will only use hypotheses that are inequalities over `tp`. This is useful\n  if you have e.g. both integer and rational valued inequalities in the local context, which can\n  sometimes confuse the tactic.\n* `transparency` controls how hard `linarith` will try to match atoms to each other. By default\n  it will only unfold `reducible` definitions.\n* If `split_hypotheses` is true, `linarith` will split conjunctions in the context into separate\n  hypotheses.\n* If `exfalso` is false, `linarith` will fail when the goal is neither an inequality nor `false`.\n  (True by default.)\n\nA variant, `nlinarith`, does some basic preprocessing to handle some nonlinear goals.\n\nThe option `set_option trace.linarith true` will trace certain intermediate stages of the `linarith`\nroutine.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.get_contr_lemma_name_and_type", "description": "If `e` is a comparison `a R b` or the negation of a comparison `\u00ac a R b`, found in the target,\n`get_contr_lemma_name_and_type e` returns the name of a lemma that will change the goal to an\nimplication, along with the type of `a` and `b`.\n\nFor example, if `e` is `(a : \u2115) < b`, returns ``(`lt_of_not_ge, \u2115)``.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.partition_by_type", "description": "`partition_by_type l` takes a list `l` of proofs of comparisons. It sorts these proofs by\nthe type of the variables in the comparison, e.g. `(a : \u211a) < 1` and `(b : \u2124) > c` will be separated.\nReturns a map from a type to a list of comparisons over that type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.get_restrict_type", "description": "A hack to allow users to write `{restr_type := \u211a}` in configuration structures.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.filter_hyps_to_type", "description": "`filter_hyps_to_type restr_type hyps` takes a list of proofs of comparisons `hyps`, and filters it\nto only those that are comparisons over the type `restr_type`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Kleisli.category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Kleisli.mk", "description": "Construct an object of the Kleisli category from a type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Kleisli.mk.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Kleisli", "description": " The Kleisli category on the (type-)monad `m`. Note that the monad is not assumed to be lawful\nyet.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Kleisli.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Kleisli.comp_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Kleisli.category_struct", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Kleisli.id_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "caratheodory.min_card_finset_of_mem_convex_hull_card_le_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "caratheodory.min_card_finset_of_mem_convex_hull", "description": " Given a point `x` in the convex hull of a set `s`, this is a finite subset of `s` of minimum\ncardinality, whose convex hull contains `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "caratheodory.affine_independent_min_card_finset_of_mem_convex_hull", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_eq_union", "description": "**Carath\u00e9odory's convexity theorem**", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_pos_convex_span_of_mem_convex_hull", "description": "A more explicit version of `convex_hull_eq_union`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "caratheodory.min_card_finset_of_mem_convex_hull_subseteq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "caratheodory.min_card_finset_of_mem_convex_hull_nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "caratheodory.mem_min_card_finset_of_mem_convex_hull", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "caratheodory.mem_convex_hull_erase", "description": " If `x` is in the convex hull of some finset `t` whose elements are not affine-independent,\nthen it is in the convex hull of a strict subset of `t`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "functor.map_const_rev", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "functor", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "functor.map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "functor.map_const", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "mem_circle_iff_abs", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_map_circle_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circle.compact_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circle.of_conj_div_self_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_circle_iff_norm_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_map_circle_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circle.to_units", "description": "The elements of the circle embed into the units.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abs_coe_circle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circle.topological_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circle.to_units_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_map_circle_hom", "description": " The map `\u03bb t, exp (t * I)` from `\u211d` to the unit circle in `\u2102`, considered as a homomorphism of\ngroups.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ne_zero_of_mem_circle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_map_circle_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_map_circle_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_sq_eq_of_mem_circle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_map_circle_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_div_circle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_map_circle", "description": "The map `\u03bb t, exp (t * I)` from `\u211d` to the unit circle in `\u2102`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circle_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_inv_circle_eq_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circle", "description": "The unit circle in `\u2102`, here given the structure of a submonoid of `\u2102`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circle.of_conj_div_self", "description": "If `z` is a nonzero complex number, then `conj z / z` belongs to the unit circle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_map_circle_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_inv_circle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circle.comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.has_mul", "description": "The product of `f g : \u03b1 \u2192\u2080 \u03b2` is the finitely supported function\n whose value at `a` is `f a * g a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.non_unital_comm_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.non_unital_non_assoc_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.pointwise_scalar", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.coe_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.support_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.pointwise_module", "description": "The pointwise multiplicative action of functions on finitely supported functions", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.non_unital_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.non_unital_non_assoc_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.semigroup_with_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.mul_zero_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.non_unital_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.non_unital_comm_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.mul_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.coe_pointwise_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.has_le.le.contravariant_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.sub_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.nat_pred_monotone", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.bit0_le_bit0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.nat_pred_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.nat_pred_le_nat_pred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.nat_pred_add_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.mod_add_div'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.order_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.coe_add_hom", "description": "`pnat.coe` promoted to an `add_hom`, that is, a morphism which preserves addition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.coe_bit0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.succ_pnat_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.rec_on_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.mul_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.add_comm_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.add_one_le_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.add_sub_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.pnat_equiv_nat_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.nat_pred_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.has_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.pos_of_div_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.distrib", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.has_lt.lt.is_well_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.lt_add_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.pnat_iso_nat", "description": "The order isomorphism between \u2115 and \u2115+ given by `succ`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.pnat_equiv_nat", "description": "An equivalence between `\u2115+` and `\u2115` given by `pnat.nat_pred` and `nat.succ_pnat`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.pow_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.one_add_nat_pred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.has_le.le.covariant_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.bit1_le_bit1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.rec_on", "description": " An induction principle for `\u2115+`: it takes values in `Sort*`, so it applies also to Types,\nnot only to `Prop`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.case_strong_induction_on", "description": "Strong induction on `\u2115+`, with `n = 1` treated separately.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.nat_pred_strict_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.div_add_mod'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.has_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.le_one_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.succ_pnat_strict_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.pnat_iso_nat_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.coe_monoid_hom", "description": "`pnat.coe` promoted to a `monoid_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.mod_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.add_right_cancel_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.mod_div_aux_spec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.bot_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.lt_add_one_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.mod_add_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.nat_pred_lt_nat_pred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.succ_pnat_le_succ_pnat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.succ_pnat_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.pnat_equiv_nat_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.exists_eq_succ_of_ne_one", "description": "If `n : \u2115+` is different from `1`, then it is the successor of some `k : \u2115+`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.le_of_dvd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.mul_div_exact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.add_left_cancel_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.mk_bit0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.add_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.div_add_mod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.has_lt.lt.contravariant_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.pnat_iso_nat_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.bit1_le_bit0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.dvd_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.dvd_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.has_sub", "description": "Subtraction a - b is defined in the obvious way when\n a > b, and by a - b = 1 if a \u2264 b.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.succ_pnat_lt_succ_pnat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.coe_bit1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.has_lt.lt.covariant_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.linear_ordered_cancel_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.lt_add_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.rec_on_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.coe_coe_monoid_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.dvd_one_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.bit0_le_bit1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.succ_pnat_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.dvd_antisymm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.coe_inj", "description": "We now define a long list of structures on \u2115+ induced by\nsimilar structures on \u2115. Most of these behave in a completely\nobvious way, but there are a few things to be said about\nsubtraction, division and powers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pnat.mk_bit1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unsigned.has_mod", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unsigned.has_le", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unsigned.has_zero", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unsigned.of_nat", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unsigned.has_lt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unsigned.has_mul", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unsigned.has_one", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unsigned.has_div", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unsigned.has_add", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unsigned.has_sub", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tensor_product.lie_module.map_incl", "description": "Given Lie submodules `M' \u2286 M` and `N' \u2286 N`, this is the natural map: `M' \u2297 N' \u2192 M \u2297 N`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.lie_module.lie_module", "description": "The tensor product of two Lie modules is a Lie module.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.lie_module.has_bracket_aux", "description": " It is useful to define the bracket via this auxiliary function so that we have a type-theoretic\nexpression of the fact that `L` acts by linear endomorphisms. It simplifies the proofs in\n`lie_ring_module` below.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.lie_module.lift", "description": " The universal property for tensor product of modules of a Lie algebra: the `R`-linear\ntensor-hom adjunction is equivariant with respect to the `L` action.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.to_module_hom", "description": "The action of the Lie algebra on one of its modules, regarded as a morphism of Lie modules.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.lie_module.coe_lift_lie_eq_lift_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.to_module_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.lie_module.map_tmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.lie_ideal_oper_eq_tensor_map_range", "description": " A useful alternative characterisation of Lie ideal operations on Lie submodules.\n\nGiven a Lie ideal `I \u2286 L` and a Lie submodule `N \u2286 M`, by tensoring the inclusion maps and then\napplying the action of `L` on `M`, we obtain morphism of Lie modules `f : I \u2297 N \u2192 L \u2297 M \u2192 M`.\n\nThis lemma states that `\u2045I, N\u2046 = range f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.lie_module.lie_tmul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.lie_module.coe_linear_map_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.lie_module.map", "description": " A pair of Lie module morphisms `f : M \u2192 P` and `g : N \u2192 Q`, induce a Lie module morphism:\n`M \u2297 N \u2192 P \u2297 Q`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.lie_module.lift_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.lie_module.map_incl_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.lie_module.lift_lie_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.lie_module.lie_ring_module", "description": "The tensor product of two Lie modules is a Lie ring module.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.lie_module.lift_lie", "description": " A weaker form of the universal property for tensor product of modules of a Lie algebra.\n\nNote that maps `f` of type `M \u2192\u2097\u2045R,L\u2046 N \u2192\u2097[R] P` are exactly those `R`-bilinear maps satisfying\n`\u2045x, f m n\u2046 = f \u2045x, m\u2046 n + f m \u2045x, n\u2046` for all `x, m, n` (see e.g, `lie_module_hom.map_lie\u2082`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvfunctor.liftr_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvfunctor.supp", "description": " given `x : F \u03b1` and a projection `i` of type vector `\u03b1`, `supp x i` is the set\nof `\u03b1.i` contained in `x`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvfunctor.id_map'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvfunctor.map_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvfunctor", "description": " multivariate functors, i.e. functor between the category of type vectors\nand the category of Type", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvfunctor.map", "description": " multivariate functors, i.e. functor between the category of type vectors\nand the category of Type", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvfunctor.liftr", "description": "relational lifting over multivariate functors", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvfunctor.liftp'", "description": "adapt `mvfunctor.liftp` to accept predicates as arrows", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvfunctor.liftp_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_lawful_mvfunctor", "description": "laws for `mvfunctor`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_lawful_mvfunctor.id_map", "description": "laws for `mvfunctor`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_lawful_mvfunctor.comp_map", "description": "laws for `mvfunctor`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvfunctor.of_mem_supp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvfunctor.liftr'", "description": "adapt `mvfunctor.liftp` to accept relations as arrows", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvfunctor.liftr_last_rel_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvfunctor.liftp", "description": "predicate lifting over multivariate functors", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvfunctor.id_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvfunctor.liftp_last_pred_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvfunctor.exists_iff_exists_of_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "explicit_vars_of_iff", "description": "This function works as follows:\n1. Call `unravel_explicits_of_pi` to obtain the names, complements of de-Bruijn indexes and the\nremaining non-Pi expression;\n2. Check if the remaining non-Pi expression is an iff, already obtaining the respective left and\nright expressions if this is the case. Returns `none` otherwise;\n3. Filter the explicit variables that appear on the left *and* right side of the iff;\n4. If no variable satisfies the condition above, return `none`;\n5. Return a message mentioning the variables that do, otherwise.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "find_unused_have_suffices_macros", "description": "Return a list of unused have and suffices terms in an expression", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.explicit_vars_of_iff", "description": "A linter for checking if variables appearing on both sides of an iff are explicit. Ideally, such\nvariables should be implicit instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_unused_haves_suffices", "description": "Checks whether a declaration contains term mode have statements that have no effect on the resulting\nterm.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.unused_arguments", "description": "A linter object for checking for unused arguments. This is in the default linter set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "check_type", "description": "Checks whether the statement of a declaration is well-typed.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bad_params", "description": "The good parameters are the parameters that occur somewhere in the `rb_set` as a singleton or\n(recursively) with only other good parameters.\nAll other parameters in the `rb_set` are bad.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.dup_namespace", "description": "A linter for checking whether a declaration has a namespace twice consecutively in its name.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.unused_haves_suffices", "description": " A linter for checking that declarations don't have unused term mode have statements. We do not\ntag this as `@[linter]` so that it is not in the default linter set as it is slow and an uncommon\nproblem.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.syn_taut", "description": "A linter for checking that declarations aren't syntactic tautologies.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "expr.has_zero_var", "description": "Check if an expression contains `var 0` by folding over the expression and matching the binder depth", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.check_univs", "description": "A linter for checking that there are no bad `max u v` universe levels.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.ge_or_gt", "description": "A linter for checking whether illegal constants (\u2265, >) appear in a declaration's type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unprintable_interactive", "description": "Ensures that every interactive tactic has arguments for which `interactive.param_desc` succeeds.\nThis is used to generate the parser documentation that appears in hovers on interactive tactics.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "expr.univ_params_grouped", "description": "`univ_params_grouped e` computes for each `level` `u` of `e` the parameters that occur in `u`,\nand returns the corresponding set of lists of parameters.\nIn pseudo-mathematical form, this returns `{ { p : parameter | p \u2208 u } | (u : level) \u2208 e }`\nWe use `list name` instead of `name_set`, since `name_set` does not have an order.\nIt will ignore `nm\u2080._proof_i` declarations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "check_unused_arguments", "description": " Check which arguments of a declaration are not used.\nPrints a list of natural numbers corresponding to which arguments are not used (e.g.\n  this outputs [1, 4] if the first and fourth arguments are unused).\nChecks both the type and the value of `d` for whether the argument is used\n(in rare cases an argument is used in the type but not in the value).\nWe return [] if the declaration was automatically generated.\nWe print arguments that are larger than the arity of the type of the declaration\n(without unfolding definitions).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.doc_blame_thm", "description": "A linter for checking theorem doc strings. This is not in the default linter set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.unprintable_interactive", "description": "A linter for checking that interactive tactics have parser documentation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.check_type", "description": "A linter for missing checking whether statements of declarations are well-typed.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unravel_explicits_of_pi", "description": "Recursively consumes a Pi expression while accumulating names and the complement of de-Bruijn\nindexes of explicit variables, ultimately obtaining the remaining non-Pi expression as well.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.doc_blame", "description": "A linter for checking definition doc strings", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unused_have_of_decl", "description": "Return a list of unused have and suffices terms in a declaration", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "syn_taut", "description": "Checks whether a lemma is a declaration of the form `\u2200 a b ... z, e\u2081 = e\u2082`\nwhere `e\u2081` and `e\u2082` are identical exprs.\nWe call declarations of this form syntactic tautologies.\nSuch lemmas are (mostly) useless and sometimes introduced unintentionally when proving basic facts\nwith rfl when elaboration results in a different term than the user intended.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "library_note.nolint_ge", "description": "Currently, the linter forbids the use of `>` and `\u2265` in definitions and\nstatements, as they cause problems in rewrites.\nThey are still allowed in statements such as `bounded (\u2265)` or `\u2200 \u03b5 > 0` or `\u2a06 n \u2265 m`,\nand the linter allows that.\nIf you write a pattern where you bind two or more variables, like `\u2203 n m > 0`, the linter will\nflag this as illegal, but it is also allowed. In this case, add the line\n```lean\n@[nolint ge_or_gt] -- see Note [nolint_ge]\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "check_univs", "description": "Checks whether all universe levels `u` in the type of `d` are \"good\".\nThis means that `u` either occurs in a `level` of `d` by itself, or (recursively)\nwith only other good levels.\nWhen this fails, usually this means that there is a level `max u v`, where neither `u` nor `v`\noccur by themselves in a level. It is ok if *one* of `u` or `v` never occurs alone. For example,\n`(\u03b1 : Type u) (\u03b2 : Type (max u v))` is a occasionally useful method of saying that `\u03b2` lives in\na higher universe level than `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.def_lemma", "description": " A linter for checking whether the correct declaration constructor (definition or theorem)\nhas been used.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast_with_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast_with_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.alternative", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.fmap_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.pure_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.monad", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.map_comp_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.applicative", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.seq_right_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.seq_left_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.is_lawful_applicative", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.traverse", "description": "Traverse function for `finset`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.is_comm_applicative", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.image\u2082_def", "description": " `finset.image\u2082` in terms of monadic operations. Note that this can't be taken as the definition\nbecause of the lack of universe polymorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.has_pure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.bind_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.seq_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.is_lawful_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.is_lawful_monad", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.id_traverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.map_traverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.functor", "description": " Because `finset.image` requires a `decidable_eq` instance for the target type, we can only\nconstruct `functor finset` when working classically.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.graded_algebra.\u03b9_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.graded_algebra", "description": "The clifford algebra is graded by the even and odd parts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.range_\u03b9_le_even_odd_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.even_induction", "description": " To show a property is true on the even parts, it suffices to show it is true on the\nscalars, closed under addition, and under left-multiplication by a pair of vectors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.supr_\u03b9_range_eq_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.odd_induction", "description": " To show a property is true on the odd parts, it suffices to show it is true on the\nvectors, closed under addition, and under left-multiplication by a pair of vectors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.\u03b9_mul_\u03b9_mem_even_odd_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.graded_algebra.lift_\u03b9_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.graded_algebra.\u03b9", "description": " A version of `clifford_algebra.\u03b9` that maps directly into the graded structure. This is\nprimarily an auxiliary construction used to provide `clifford_algebra.graded_algebra`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.even_odd_mul_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.\u03b9_mem_even_odd_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.one_le_even_odd_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.graded_algebra.\u03b9_sq_scalar", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.even_odd.graded_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.even_odd", "description": " The even or odd submodule, defined as the supremum of the even or odd powers of\n`(\u03b9 Q).range`. `even_odd 0` is the even submodule, and `even_odd 1` is the odd submodule.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.even_odd_induction", "description": " To show a property is true on the even or odd part, it suffices to show it is true on the\nscalars or vectors (respectively), closed under addition, and under left-multiplication by a pair\nof vectors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.even_odd_is_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian.exists_pow_succ_smul_dvd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian.induction", "description": "If `\u2200 I > J, P I` implies `P J`, then `P` holds for all submodules.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_of_tower", "description": " If `M / S / R` is a scalar tower, and `M / R` is Artinian, then `M / S` is\nalso Artinian.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_has_minimal_iff_artinian", "description": "A module is Artinian iff every nonempty set of submodules has a minimal submodule among them.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_of_fg_of_artinian", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_of_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.is_artinian_of_zero_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_span_of_finite", "description": " In a module over a artinian ring, the submodule generated by finitely many vectors is\nartinian.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian.disjoint_partial_infs_eventually_top", "description": "A sequence `f` of submodules of a artinian module,\nwith the supremum `f (n+1)` and the infinum of `f 0`, ..., `f n` being \u22a4,\nis eventually \u22a4.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_of_linear_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_of_range_eq_ker", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian.set_has_minimal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian.monotone_stabilizes", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_of_submodule_of_artinian", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_ring.localization_artinian", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_ring_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_of_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian.finite_of_linear_independent", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_ring.localization_surjective", "description": "Localizing an artinian ring can only reduce the amount of elements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_of_fg_of_artinian'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian.exists_endomorphism_iterate_ker_sup_range_eq_top", "description": "For any endomorphism of a Artinian module, there is some nontrivial iterate\nwith disjoint kernel and range.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_pi'", "description": " A version of `is_artinian_pi` for non-dependent functions. We need this instance because\nsometimes Lean fails to apply the dependent version in non-dependent settings (e.g., it fails to\nprove that `\u03b9 \u2192 \u211d` is finite dimensional over `\u211d`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone_stabilizes_iff_artinian", "description": "A module is Artinian iff every decreasing chain of submodules stabilizes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_iff_well_founded", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_ring.is_nilpotent_jacobson_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_of_quotient_of_artinian", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian.bijective_of_injective_endomorphism", "description": "Any injective endomorphism of an Artinian module is bijective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_of_finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_ring", "description": " A ring is Artinian if it is Artinian as a module over itself.\n\nStrictly speaking, this should be called `is_left_artinian_ring` but we omit the `left_` for\nconvenience in the commutative case. For a right Artinian ring, use `is_artinian R\u1d50\u1d52\u1d56 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_ring.localization.is_artinian_ring", "description": " `is_artinian_ring.localization_artinian` can't be made an instance, as it would make `S` + `R`\ninto metavariables. However, this is safe.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian", "description": "`is_artinian R M` is the proposition that `M` is an Artinian `R`-module,\nimplemented as the well-foundedness of submodule inclusion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian.well_founded_submodule_lt", "description": "`is_artinian R M` is the proposition that `M` is an Artinian `R`-module,\nimplemented as the well-foundedness of submodule inclusion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.is_artinian_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian.range_smul_pow_stabilizes", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian.surjective_of_injective_endomorphism", "description": "Any injective endomorphism of an Artinian module is surjective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_ring_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_artinian_submodule'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.rsimp", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "rsimp.rsimplify_goal", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "no_rsimp", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "mk_hinst_lemma_attr_from_simp_attr", "description": "Create a rsimp attribute named `attr_name`, the attribute declaration is named `attr_decl_name`.\n   The cached hinst_lemmas structure is built using the lemmas marked with simp attribute `simp_attr_name`,\n   but *not* marked with `ex_attr_name`.\n\n   We say `ex_attr_name` is the \"exception set\". It is useful for excluding lemmas in `simp_attr_name`\n   which are not good or redundant for ematching.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "rsimp_attr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "rsimp.explicit_size", "description": "Return the size of term by considering only explicit arguments.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "rsimp.rsimplify_at", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "rsimp.to_repr_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "rsimp.collect_implied_eqs", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "rsimp.repr_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "rsimp.choose", "description": "Choose smallest element (with respect to explicit_size) in `e`s equivalence class.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "rsimp.rsimplify", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.rsimp", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.rsimp_at", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "rsimp.is_value_like", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.id_tag.rsimp", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "rsimp.config", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "rsimp.config.attr_name", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "rsimp.config.max_rounds", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "rsimp.mk_repr_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.homotopy_equiv_normalized_Moore_complex_alternating_face_map_complex_homotopy_inv_hom_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.homotopy_P_infty_to_id", "description": " Construction of the homotopy from `P_infty` to the identity using eventually\n(termwise) constant homotopies from `P q` to the identity for all `q`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.homotopy_Q_to_zero", "description": "The complement projection `Q q` to `P q` is homotopic to zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.homotopy_equiv_normalized_Moore_complex_alternating_face_map_complex_homotopy_hom_inv_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.homotopy_equiv_normalized_Moore_complex_alternating_face_map_complex", "description": " The inclusion of the Moore complex in the alternating face map complex\nis an homotopy equivalence", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.homotopy_equiv_normalized_Moore_complex_alternating_face_map_complex_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.homotopy_equiv_normalized_Moore_complex_alternating_face_map_complex_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.homotopy_P_to_id_eventually_constant", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.homotopy_P_infty_to_id_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.homotopy_P_to_id", "description": "Inductive construction of homotopies from `P q` to `\ud835\udfd9 _`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_of_degree_le_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_of_splits_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_iff_exists_multiset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_map_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.root_of_splits'_eq_root_of_splits", "description": "`root_of_splits'` is definitionally equal to `root_of_splits`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.adjoin_root_set_eq_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.prod_roots_eq_coeff_zero_of_monic_of_split", "description": "If `P` is a monic polynomial that splits, then `coeff P 0` equals the product of the roots.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.roots_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_of_splits_of_dvd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_of_degree_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_X_sub_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.mem_lift_of_splits_of_roots_mem_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.roots_ne_zero_of_splits'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.eq_prod_roots_of_splits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits", "description": "A polynomial `splits` iff it is zero or all of its irreducible factors have `degree` 1.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_eq_card_roots'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.exists_root_of_splits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.roots_ne_zero_of_splits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_of_splits_mul'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_of_splits_gcd_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.eq_prod_roots_of_monic_of_splits_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_iff", "description": "This lemma is for polynomials over a field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_of_map_degree_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_id_iff_splits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits.def", "description": "This lemma is for polynomials over a field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.eq_prod_roots_of_splits_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.exists_root_of_splits'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.map_root_of_splits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_X_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_comp_of_splits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.degree_eq_card_roots", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_iff_card_roots", "description": "A polynomial splits if and only if it has as many roots as its degree.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_eq_card_roots", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.sum_roots_eq_next_coeff_of_monic_of_split", "description": "If `P` is a monic polynomial that splits, then `P.next_coeff` equals the sum of the roots.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_of_nat_degree_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.root_of_splits'", "description": " Pick a root of a polynomial that splits. See `root_of_splits` for polynomials over a field\nwhich has simpler assumptions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_mul_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.root_of_splits", "description": " Pick a root of a polynomial that splits. This version is for polynomials over a field and has\nsimpler assumptions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_of_is_unit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_of_exists_multiset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.map_root_of_splits'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_of_splits_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.aeval_root_derivative_of_splits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_of_splits_gcd_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.eq_X_sub_C_of_splits_of_single_root", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.degree_eq_card_roots'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_of_nat_degree_le_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_of_map_eq_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.image_root_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.splits_prod_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.degree_eq_one_of_irreducible_of_splits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_subset_image2_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_union_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_image2_antidistrib_left", "description": "Symmetric statement to `set.image2_image_left_anticomm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_image2_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_image_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_image2_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_subset_iff_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_subset_iff_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_union_inter_subset_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.forall_image2_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_image2_image2_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_singleton_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_inter_union_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_union_inter_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_mk_eq_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_right_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_eq_empty_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_left_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_inter_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_inter_union_subset_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_right_identity", "description": " If `b` is a right identity for `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b1`, then `{b}` is a right identity for\n`set.image2 f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_image2_distrib_right", "description": "Symmetric statement to `set.image_image2_right_comm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_image2_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.nonempty.of_image2_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_image2_right_anticomm", "description": "Symmetric statement to `set.image_image2_antidistrib_right`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image3", "description": "The image of a ternary function `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b3 \u2192 \u03b4` as a function\n `set \u03b1 \u2192 set \u03b2 \u2192 set \u03b3 \u2192 set \u03b4`. Mathematically this should be thought of as the image of the\n corresponding function `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2 \u00d7 \u03b3 \u2192 \u03b4`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_subset_image2_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image3_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_image2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_singleton_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_distrib_subset_right", "description": "The other direction does not hold because of the `u`-`u` cross terms on the RHS.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.nonempty.image2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_empty_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_image2_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_nonempty_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image3_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_inter_subset_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_subset", "description": "image2 is monotone with respect to `\u2286`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image3_congr'", "description": "A common special case of `image3_congr`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_subset_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_inter_subset_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_uncurry_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_distrib_subset_left", "description": "The other direction does not hold because of the `s`-`s` cross terms on the RHS.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_image2_antidistrib", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_image2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_image_left_anticomm", "description": "Symmetric statement to `set.image_image2_antidistrib_left`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_union_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_congr'", "description": "A common special case of `image2_congr`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_image2_distrib_left", "description": "Symmetric statement to `set.image2_image_left_comm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_image2_antidistrib_right", "description": "Symmetric statement to `set.image_image2_right_anticomm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.nonempty.of_image2_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_curry", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2", "description": " The image of a binary function `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b3` as a function `set \u03b1 \u2192 set \u03b2 \u2192 set \u03b3`.\nMathematically this should be thought of as the image of the corresponding function `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b3`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_image2_right_comm", "description": "Symmetric statement to `set.image_image2_distrib_right`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_image_left_comm", "description": "Symmetric statement to `set.image_image2_distrib_left`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_subset_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_image2_distrib", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_insert_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_image_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_insert_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_inter_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_image3", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_empty_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_left_identity", "description": " If `a` is a left identity for `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b2`, then `{a}` is a left identity for\n`set.image2 f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_subset_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pequiv.single_mul_single_of_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pequiv.mul_to_pequiv_to_matrix", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pequiv.single_mul_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pequiv.to_pequiv_mul_matrix", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pequiv.to_matrix_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pequiv.to_matrix_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pequiv.equiv_to_pequiv_to_matrix", "description": "We can also define permutation matrices by permuting the rows of the identity matrix.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pequiv.matrix_mul_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pequiv.mul_matrix_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pequiv.to_matrix", "description": "`to_matrix` returns a matrix containing ones and zeros. `f.to_matrix i j` is `1` if\n `f i = some j` and `0` otherwise", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pequiv.single_mul_single_right", "description": "Restatement of `single_mul_single`, which will simplify expressions in `simp` normal form,\n when associativity may otherwise need to be carefully applied.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pequiv.to_matrix_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pequiv.to_matrix_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pequiv.to_matrix_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pequiv.to_matrix_trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pequiv.to_matrix_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.of_\u03c1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.preadditive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.of", "description": "Lift an unbundled representation to `fdRep`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.forget\u2082_hom_linear_equiv", "description": "The forgetful functor to `Rep k G` preserves hom-sets and their vector space structure", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.category_theory.linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.finrank_hom_simple_simple", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.concrete_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.category_theory.right_rigid_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.dual_tensor_iso_lin_hom_aux", "description": "Auxiliary definition for `fdRep.dual_tensor_iso_lin_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.has_coe_to_sort", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.dual_tensor_iso_lin_hom", "description": " When `V` and `W` are finite dimensional representations of a group `G`, the isomorphism\n`dual_tensor_hom_equiv k V W` of vector spaces induces an isomorphism of representations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.category_theory.limits.has_kernels", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep", "description": "The category of finite dimensional `k`-linear representations of a monoid `G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.dual_tensor_iso_lin_hom_hom_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.iso_to_linear_equiv", "description": "The underlying `linear_equiv` of an isomorphism of representations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.forget\u2082_\u03c1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.finite_dimensional", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.has_finite_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.quiver.hom.finite_dimensional", "description": "All hom spaces are finite dimensional.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.iso.conj_\u03c1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.module", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.\u03c1", "description": "The monoid homomorphism corresponding to the action of `G` onto `V : fdRep k G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.large_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.Rep.category_theory.has_forget\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "extensional_attribute", "description": "Tag lemmas of the form:\n\n```lean\n@[ext]\nlemma my_collection.ext (a b : my_collection)\n  (h : \u2200 x, a.lookup x = b.lookup y) :\n  a = b := ...\n```\n\nThe attribute indexes extensionality lemma using the type of the\nobjects (i.e. `my_collection`) which it gets from the statement of\nthe lemma.  In some cases, the same lemma can be used to state the\nextensionality of multiple types that are definitionally equivalent.\n\n```lean\nattribute [ext thunk, ext stream] funext\n```\n\nAlso, the following:\n\n```lean\n@[ext]\nlemma my_collection.ext (a b : my_collection)\n  (h : \u2200 x, a.lookup x = b.lookup y) :\n  a = b := ...\n```\n\nis equivalent to\n\n```lean\n@[ext my_collection]\nlemma my_collection.ext (a b : my_collection)\n  (h : \u2200 x, a.lookup x = b.lookup y) :\n  a = b := ...\n```\n\nThis allows us specify type synonyms along with the type\nthat is referred to in the lemma statement.\n\n```lean\n@[ext, ext my_type_synonym]\nlemma my_collection.ext (a b : my_collection)\n  (h : \u2200 x, a.lookup x = b.lookup y) :\n  a = b := ...\n```\n\nThe `ext` attribute can be applied to a structure to generate its extensionality lemmas:\n\n```lean\n@[ext]\nstructure foo (\u03b1 : Type*) :=\n(x y : \u2115)\n(z : {z // z < x})\n(k : \u03b1)\n(h : x < y)\n```\n\nwill generate:\n\n```lean\n@[ext] lemma foo.ext : \u2200 {\u03b1 : Type u_1} (x y : foo \u03b1),\nx.x = y.x \u2192 x.y = y.y \u2192 x.z == y.z \u2192 x.k = y.k \u2192 x = y\nlemma foo.ext_iff : \u2200 {\u03b1 : Type u_1} (x y : foo \u03b1),\nx = y \u2194 x.x = y.x \u2227 x.y = y.y \u2227 x.z == y.z \u2227 x.k = y.k\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.ext1 / ext", "description": "* `ext1 id` selects and apply one extensionality lemma (with\n  attribute `ext`), using `id`, if provided, to name a\n  local constant introduced by the lemma. If `id` is omitted, the\n  local constant is named automatically, as per `intro`.\n\n* `ext` applies as many extensionality lemmas as possible;\n* `ext ids`, with `ids` a list of identifiers, finds extensionality lemmas\n  and applies them until it runs out of identifiers in `ids` to name\n  the local constants.\n* `ext` can also be given an `rcases` pattern in place of an identifier.\n  This will destruct the introduced local constant.\n- Placing a `?` after `ext`/`ext1` (e.g. `ext? i \u27e8a,b\u27e9 : 3`) will display\n  a sequence of tactic applications that can replace the call to `ext`/`ext1`.\n- `set_option trace.ext true` will trace every attempted lemma application,\n  along with the time it takes for the application to succeed or fail.\n  This is useful for debugging slow `ext` calls.\n\nWhen trying to prove:\n\n```lean\n\u03b1 \u03b2 : Type,\nf g : \u03b1 \u2192 set \u03b2\n\u22a2 f = g\n```\n\napplying `ext x y` yields:\n\n```lean\n\u03b1 \u03b2 : Type,\nf g : \u03b1 \u2192 set \u03b2,\nx : \u03b1,\ny : \u03b2\n\u22a2 y \u2208 f x \u2194 y \u2208 g x\n```\nby applying functional extensionality and set extensionality.\n\nWhen trying to prove:\n\n```lean\n\u03b1 \u03b2 \u03b3 : Type\nf g : \u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b3\n\u22a2 f = g\n```\n\napplying `ext \u27e8a, b\u27e9` yields:\n\n```lean\n\u03b1 \u03b2 \u03b3 : Type,\nf g : \u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b3,\na : \u03b1,\nb : \u03b2\n\u22a2 f (a, b) = g (a, b)\n```\n\nby applying functional extensionality and destructing the introduced pair.\n\nIn the previous example, applying `ext? \u27e8a,b\u27e9` will produce the trace message:\n\n```lean\nTry this: apply funext, rintro \u27e8a, b\u27e9\n```\n\nA maximum depth can be provided with `ext x y z : 3`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.try_intros", "description": "Try to introduce as many arguments as possible, using the given patterns to destruct the\n introduced variables. Returns the unused patterns.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ext_core", "description": "Apply multiple extensionality lemmas, destructing the arguments using the given patterns.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_zero.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "library_note.user attribute parameters", "description": "For performance reasons, it is inadvisable to use `user_attribute.get_param`.\nThe parameter is stored as a reflected expression.  When calling `get_param`,\nthe stored parameter is evaluated using `eval_expr`, which first compiles the\nexpression into VM bytecode. The unevaluated expression is available using\n`user_attribute.get_param_untyped`.\n\nIn particular, `user_attribute.get_param` MUST NEVER BE USED in the\nimplementation of an attribute cache. This is because calling `eval_expr`\ndisables the attribute cache.\n\nThere are several possible workarounds:\n 1. Set a different attribute depending on the parameter.\n 2. Use your own evaluation function instead of `eval_expr`, such as e.g. `expr.to_nat`.\n 3. Write your own `has_reflect Param` instance (using a more efficient serialization format).\n   The `user_attribute` code unfortunately checks whether the expression has the correct type,\n   but you can use `` `(id %%e : Param) `` to pretend that your expression `e` has type `Param`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "punit.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ext", "description": "Apply multiple extensionality lemmas, destructing the arguments using the given patterns.\n `ext ps (some n)` applies at most `n` extensionality lemmas. Returns the unused patterns.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "plift.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.ext1", "description": "`ext1 id` selects and apply one extensionality lemma (with attribute\n`ext`), using `id`, if provided, to name a local constant\nintroduced by the lemma. If `id` is omitted, the local constant is\nnamed automatically, as per `intro`. Placing a `?` after `ext1`\n (e.g. `ext1? i \u27e8a,b\u27e9 : 3`) will display a sequence of tactic\napplications that can replace the call to `ext1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_ext_lemma", "description": " Marks `lem` as an extensionality lemma corresponding to type constructor `constr`;\nif `persistent` is true then this is a global attribute, else local.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.attribute.ext", "description": "Tag lemmas of the form:\n\n```lean\n@[ext]\nlemma my_collection.ext (a b : my_collection)\n  (h : \u2200 x, a.lookup x = b.lookup y) :\n  a = b := ...\n```\n\nThe attribute indexes extensionality lemma using the type of the\nobjects (i.e. `my_collection`) which it gets from the statement of\nthe lemma.  In some cases, the same lemma can be used to state the\nextensionality of multiple types that are definitionally equivalent.\n\n```lean\nattribute [ext thunk, ext stream] funext\n```\n\nAlso, the following:\n\n```lean\n@[ext]\nlemma my_collection.ext (a b : my_collection)\n  (h : \u2200 x, a.lookup x = b.lookup y) :\n  a = b := ...\n```\n\nis equivalent to\n\n```lean\n@[ext my_collection]\nlemma my_collection.ext (a b : my_collection)\n  (h : \u2200 x, a.lookup x = b.lookup y) :\n  a = b := ...\n```\n\nThis allows us specify type synonyms along with the type\nthat is referred to in the lemma statement.\n\n```lean\n@[ext, ext my_type_synonym]\nlemma my_collection.ext (a b : my_collection)\n  (h : \u2200 x, a.lookup x = b.lookup y) :\n  a = b := ...\n```\n\nThe `ext` attribute can be applied to a structure to generate its extensionality lemmas:\n\n```lean\n@[ext]\nstructure foo (\u03b1 : Type*) :=\n(x y : \u2115)\n(z : {z // z < x})\n(k : \u03b1)\n(h : x < y)\n```\n\nwill generate:\n\n```lean\n@[ext] lemma foo.ext : \u2200 {\u03b1 : Type u_1} (x y : foo \u03b1),\nx.x = y.x \u2192 x.y = y.y \u2192 x.z == y.z \u2192 x.k = y.k \u2192 x = y\nlemma foo.ext_iff : \u2200 {\u03b1 : Type u_1} (x y : foo \u03b1),\nx = y \u2194 x.x = y.x \u2227 x.y = y.y \u2227 x.z == y.z \u2227 x.k = y.k\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "get_ext_subject", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.ext", "description": "- `ext` applies as many extensionality lemmas as possible;\n- `ext ids`, with `ids` a list of identifiers, finds extentionality and applies them\n  until it runs out of identifiers in `ids` to name the local constants.\n- `ext` can also be given an `rcases` pattern in place of an identifier.\n  This will destruct the introduced local constant.\n- Placing a `?` after `ext` (e.g. `ext? i \u27e8a,b\u27e9 : 3`) will display\n  a sequence of tactic applications that can replace the call to `ext`.\n- `set_option trace.ext true` will trace every attempted lemma application,\n  along with the time it takes for the application to succeed or fail.\n  This is useful for debugging slow `ext` calls.\n\nWhen trying to prove:\n\n```lean\n\u03b1 \u03b2 : Type,\nf g : \u03b1 \u2192 set \u03b2\n\u22a2 f = g\n```\n\napplying `ext x y` yields:\n\n```lean\n\u03b1 \u03b2 : Type,\nf g : \u03b1 \u2192 set \u03b2,\nx : \u03b1,\ny : \u03b2\n\u22a2 y \u2208 f x \u2194 y \u2208 f x\n```\n\nby applying functional extensionality and set extensionality.\n\nWhen trying to prove:\n\n```lean\n\u03b1 \u03b2 \u03b3 : Type\nf g : \u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b3\n\u22a2 f = g\n```\n\napplying `ext \u27e8a, b\u27e9` yields:\n\n```lean\n\u03b1 \u03b2 \u03b3 : Type,\nf g : \u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b3,\na : \u03b1,\nb : \u03b2\n\u22a2 f (a, b) = g (a, b)\n```\n\nby applying functional extensionality and destructing the introduced pair.\n\nIn the previous example, applying `ext? \u27e8a,b\u27e9` will produce the trace message:\n\n```lean\nTry this: apply funext, rintro \u27e8a, b\u27e9\n```\n\nA maximum depth can be provided with `ext x y z : 3`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "get_ext_lemma_names", "description": "Returns the extensionality lemmas in the environment, as a list of lemma names.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ext1", "description": "Apply one extensionality lemma, and destruct the arguments using the given patterns.\n Returns the unused patterns.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "get_ext_lemmas", "description": "Returns the extensionality lemmas in the environment, as a map from structure\nname to lemma name.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "library_note.partially-applied ext lemmas", "description": "When possible, `ext` lemmas are stated without a full set of arguments. As an example, for bundled\nhoms `f`, `g`, and `of`, `f.comp of = g.comp of \u2192 f = g` is a better `ext` lemma than\n`(\u2200 x, f (of x) = g (of x)) \u2192 f = g`, as the former allows a second type-specific extensionality\nlemmas to be applied to `f.comp of = g.comp of`.\nIf the domain of `of` is `\u2115` or `\u2124` and `of` is a `ring_hom`, such a lemma could then make the goal\n`f (of 1) = g (of 1)`.\n\nFor bundled morphisms, there is a `ext` lemma that always applies of the form\n`(\u2200 x, \u21d1f x = \u21d1g x) \u2192 f = g`. When adding type-specific `ext` lemmas like the one above, we want\nthese to be tried first. This happens automatically since the type-specific lemmas are inevitably\ndefined later.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "saturate_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_type_constr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "derive_struct_ext_lemma", "description": "`derive_struct_ext_lemma n` generates two extensionality lemmas based on\nthe equality of all non-propositional projections.\n\nOn the following:\n\n```lean\n@[ext]\nstructure foo (\u03b1 : Type*) :=\n(x y : \u2115)\n(z : {z // z < x})\n(k : \u03b1)\n(h : x < y)\n```\n\n`derive_struct_lemma` generates:\n\n```lean\nlemma foo.ext : \u2200 {\u03b1 : Type u_1} (x y : foo \u03b1),\n  x.x = y.x \u2192 x.y = y.y \u2192 x.z == y.z \u2192 x.k = y.k \u2192 x = y\nlemma foo.ext_iff : \u2200 {\u03b1 : Type u_1} (x y : foo \u03b1),\n  x = y \u2194 x.x = y.x \u2227 x.y = y.y \u2227 x.z == y.z \u2227 x.k = y.k\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ext1_core", "description": "Apply one extensionality lemma, and destruct the arguments using the patterns\n in the ext_state.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ext_state", "description": "Helper structure for `ext` and `ext1`. `lemmas` keeps track of extensionality lemmas\n applied so far.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ext_state.patts", "description": "Helper structure for `ext` and `ext1`. `lemmas` keeps track of extensionality lemmas\n applied so far.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ext_state.trace_msg", "description": "Helper structure for `ext` and `ext1`. `lemmas` keeps track of extensionality lemmas\n applied so far.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.ext_state.fuel", "description": "Helper structure for `ext` and `ext1`. `lemmas` keeps track of extensionality lemmas\n applied so far.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_zero.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ord_connected_subset_conditionally_complete_linear_order", "description": " A nonempty `ord_connected` set in a conditionally complete linear order is naturally a\nconditionally complete linear order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subset_has_Sup", "description": " `has_Sup` structure on a nonempty subset `s` of an object with `has_Sup`. This definition is\nnon-canonical (it uses `default s`); it should be used only as here, as an auxiliary instance in the\nconstruction of the `conditionally_complete_linear_order` structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subset_Sup_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subset_Sup_of_within", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subset_conditionally_complete_linear_order", "description": " For a nonempty subset of a conditionally complete linear order to be a conditionally complete\nlinear order, it suffices that it contain the `Sup` of all its nonempty bounded-above subsets, and\nthe `Inf` of all its nonempty bounded-below subsets.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Inf_within_of_ord_connected", "description": " The `Inf` function on a nonempty `ord_connected` set `s` in a conditionally complete linear\norder takes values within `s`, for all nonempty bounded-below subsets of `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subset_Inf_of_within", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subset_Inf_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Sup_within_of_ord_connected", "description": " The `Sup` function on a nonempty `ord_connected` set `s` in a conditionally complete linear\norder takes values within `s`, for all nonempty bounded-above subsets of `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subset_has_Inf", "description": " `has_Inf` structure on a nonempty subset `s` of an object with `has_Inf`. This definition is\nnon-canonical (it uses `default s`); it should be used only as here, as an auxiliary instance in the\nconstruction of the `conditionally_complete_linear_order` structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_mdiff_at.mdifferentiable_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_mdiff_at.mfderiv_apply", "description": " The function that sends `x` to the `y`-derivative of `f(x,y)` at `g(x)` applied to `g\u2082(x)` is\n`C^n` at `x\u2080`, where the derivative is taken as a continuous linear map.\nWe have to assume that `f` is `C^(n+1)` at `(x\u2080, g(x\u2080))` and `g` is `C^n` at `x\u2080`.\nWe have to insert a coordinate change from `x\u2080` to `g\u2081(x)` to make the derivative sensible.\n\nThis is  similar to `cont_mdiff_at.mfderiv`, but where the continuous linear map is applied to a\n(variable) vector.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_mdiff.continuous_tangent_map", "description": "If a function is `C^n`, with `1 \u2264 n`, then its bundled derivative is continuous.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_mdiff_on.mdifferentiable_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_mdiff_map.mdifferentiable_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_mdiff_on.continuous_on_tangent_map_within", "description": " If a function is `C^n` on a domain with unique derivatives, with `1 \u2264 n`, then its bundled\nderivative is continuous there.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tangent_bundle.tangent_map_tangent_bundle_pure", "description": " The derivative of the zero section of the tangent bundle maps `\u27e8x, v\u27e9` to `\u27e8\u27e8x, 0\u27e9, \u27e8v, 0\u27e9\u27e9`.\n\nNote that, as currently framed, this is a statement in coordinates, thus reliant on the choice\nof the coordinate system we use on the tangent bundle.\n\nHowever, the result itself is coordinate-dependent only to the extent that the coordinates\ndetermine a splitting of the tangent bundle.  Moreover, there is a canonical splitting at each\npoint of the zero section (since there is a canonical horizontal space there, the tangent space\nto the zero section, in addition to the canonical vertical space which is the kernel of the\nderivative of the projection), and this canonical splitting is also the one that comes from the\ncoordinates on the tangent bundle in our definitions. So this statement is not as crazy as it\nmay seem.\n\nTODO define splittings of vector bundles; state this result invariantly.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_mdiff_on.cont_mdiff_on_tangent_map_within_aux", "description": " If a function is `C^n` on a domain with unique derivatives, then its bundled derivative is\n`C^m` when `m+1 \u2264 n`. In this auxiliary lemma, we prove this fact when the source and target space\nare model spaces in models with corners. The general fact is proved in\n`cont_mdiff_on.cont_mdiff_on_tangent_map_within`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_mdiff_at.mfderiv", "description": " The function that sends `x` to the `y`-derivative of `f(x,y)` at `g(x)` is `C^m` at `x\u2080`,\nwhere the derivative is taken as a continuous linear map.\nWe have to assume that `f` is `C^n` at `(x\u2080, g(x\u2080))` for `n \u2265 m + 1` and `g` is `C^m` at `x\u2080`.\nWe have to insert a coordinate change from `x\u2080` to `x` to make the derivative sensible.\nThis result is used to show that maps into the 1-jet bundle and cotangent bundle are smooth.\n`cont_mdiff_at.mfderiv_id` and `cont_mdiff_at.mfderiv_const` are special cases of this.\n\nThis result should be generalized to a `cont_mdiff_within_at` for `mfderiv_within`.\nIf we do that, we can deduce `cont_mdiff_on.cont_mdiff_on_tangent_map_within` from this.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth.mdifferentiable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_mdiff_on.cont_mdiff_on_tangent_map_within", "description": " If a function is `C^n` on a domain with unique derivatives, then its bundled derivative\nis `C^m` when `m+1 \u2264 n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_within_at.mdifferentiable_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth.mdifferentiable_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth.mdifferentiable_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_mdiff_on.continuous_on_tangent_map_within_aux", "description": " If a function is `C^n` with `1 \u2264 n` on a domain with unique derivatives, then its bundled\nderivative is continuous. In this auxiliary lemma, we prove this fact when the source and target\nspace are model spaces in models with corners. The general fact is proved in\n`cont_mdiff_on.continuous_on_tangent_map_within`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_mdiff.mdifferentiable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_on.mdifferentiable_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_at.mdifferentiable_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_mdiff.cont_mdiff_tangent_map", "description": "If a function is `C^n`, then its bundled derivative is `C^m` when `m+1 \u2264 n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_mdiff_within_at.mdifferentiable_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_mdiff_map.mdifferentiable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_mdiff_map.mdifferentiable'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_mdiff_at.mfderiv_const", "description": " The derivative `D_yf(y)` is `C^m` at `x\u2080`, where the derivative is taken as a continuous\nlinear map. We have to assume that `f` is `C^n` at `x\u2080` for some `n \u2265 m + 1`.\nWe have to insert a coordinate change from `x\u2080` to `x` to make the derivative sensible.\nThis is a special case of `cont_mdiff_at.mfderiv` where `f` does not contain any parameters and\n`g = id`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_degree.of_le_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.computable.trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_degree.lift_on\u2082_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_degree.add_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "to_nat", "description": "Computable and injective mapping of predicates to sets of natural numbers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_equiv.congr_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_degree.lift_on\u2082", "description": "Lifts a binary function on sets of natural numbers to many-one degrees.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulower.down_computable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_degree.le_add_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_reducible_to_nat_to_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_one_reducible_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "computable.eqv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_reducible.trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_degree.of_eq_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_degree.has_add", "description": "The join of two degrees, induced by the disjoint union of two underlying sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_degree.le_add_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_one_equiv.to_many_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_one_equiv.congr_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_reducible_to_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_degree.semilattice_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_degree.ind_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_equiv.le_congr_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_equiv_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "transitive_many_one_reducible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "computable_pred.computable_of_one_one_reducible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_equiv.le_congr_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflexive_one_one_reducible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_reducible.mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "computable_pred.computable_of_many_one_reducible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_equiv.congr_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_one_equiv.trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.computable", "description": "a computable bijection", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_one_equiv.le_congr_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_degree", "description": "A many-one degree is an equivalence class of sets up to many-one equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_one_reducible.disjoin_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_degree.has_le", "description": "For many-one degrees `d\u2081` and `d\u2082`, `d\u2081 \u2264 d\u2082` if the sets in `d\u2081` are many-one reducible to the\nsets in `d\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "disjoin_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "computable.equiv\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_degree.of", "description": "The many-one degree of a set on a primcodable type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_equiv_to_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_one_reducible", "description": "`p` is one-one reducible to `q` if there is an injective computable function translating questions\nabout `p` to questions about `q`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_one_equiv.symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_degree.add_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_one_reducible.of_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_one_reducible.disjoin_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equivalence_of_many_one_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_degree.lift_on", "description": "Lifts a function on sets of natural numbers to many-one degrees.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_equiv.trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_one_equiv.of_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.computable.symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_equiv.symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_one_reducible.of_equiv_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_equiv", "description": "`p` and `q` are many-one equivalent if each one is many-one reducible to the other.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_degree.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_equiv_up", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_one_reducible.trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "disjoin_many_one_reducible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_one_equiv", "description": "`p` and `q` are one-one equivalent if each one is one-one reducible to the other.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_degree.lift_on_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_equiv.of_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_reducible_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equivalence_of_one_one_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_reducible", "description": "`p` is many-one reducible to `q` if there is a computable function translating questions about `p`\nto questions about `q`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "to_nat_many_one_reducible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_one_equiv.le_congr_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_one_reducible.mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflexive_many_one_reducible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_one_reducible.to_many_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_one_equiv_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "many_one_degree.partial_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "to_nat_many_one_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "transitive_one_one_reducible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_one_equiv.congr_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.to_equiv_range_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.to_equiv_range", "description": "Computably turn an embedding `f : \u03b1 \u21aa \u03b2` into an equiv `\u03b1 \u2243 set.range f`,\nif `\u03b1` is a `fintype`. Has poor computational performance, due to exhaustive searching in\nconstructed inverse. When a better inverse is known, use `equiv.of_left_inverse'` or\n`equiv.of_left_inverse` instead. This is the computable version of `equiv.of_injective`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.extend_subtype", "description": " If `e` is an equivalence between two subtypes of a fintype `\u03b1`, `e.extend_subtype`\nis a permutation of `\u03b1` acting like `e` on the subtypes and doing something arbitrary outside.\n\nNote that when `p = q`, `equiv.perm.subtype_congr e (equiv.refl _)` can be used instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.via_fintype_embedding_apply_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.extend_subtype_not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.via_fintype_embedding_apply_not_mem_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.via_fintype_embedding_apply_mem_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.extend_subtype_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.to_equiv_range_symm_apply_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.to_compl", "description": " If `e` is an equivalence between two subtypes of a fintype `\u03b1`, `e.to_compl`\nis an equivalence between the complement of those subtypes.\n\nSee also `equiv.compl`, for a computable version when a term of type\n`{e' : \u03b1 \u2243 \u03b1 // \u2200 x : {x // p x}, e' x = e x}` is known.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.via_fintype_embedding", "description": "Extend the domain of `e : equiv.perm \u03b1`, mapping it through `f : \u03b1 \u21aa \u03b2`.\nEverything outside of `set.range f` is kept fixed. Has poor computational performance,\ndue to exhaustive searching in constructed inverse due to using `function.embedding.to_equiv_range`.\nWhen a better `\u03b1 \u2243 set.range f` is known, use `equiv.perm.via_set_range`.\nWhen `[fintype \u03b1]` is not available, a noncomputable version is available as\n`equiv.perm.via_embedding`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.via_fintype_embedding_sign", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.to_equiv_range_eq_of_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.extend_subtype_apply_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.extend_subtype_apply_of_not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.has_strict_deriv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.has_deriv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.deriv_within", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.has_strict_deriv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.has_deriv_at_filter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.has_deriv_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.deriv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.has_deriv_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.deriv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.has_deriv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.deriv_within", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.has_deriv_at_filter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_complex_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_complex_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.trace_complex_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.left_mul_matrix_complex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_on.eq_on_of_preconnected_of_eventually_eq", "description": " The *identity principle* for analytic functions: If two analytic functions coincide in a whole\nneighborhood of a point `z\u2080`, then they coincide globally along a connected set.\nFor a one-dimensional version assuming only that the functions coincide at some points\narbitrarily close to `z\u2080`, see `eq_on_of_preconnected_of_frequently_eq`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_on.eq_of_eventually_eq", "description": " The *identity principle* for analytic functions: If two analytic functions on a normed space\ncoincide in a neighborhood of a point `z\u2080`, then they coincide everywhere.\nFor a one-dimensional version assuming only that the functions coincide at some points\narbitrarily close to `z\u2080`, see `eq_of_frequently_eq`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_on.eq_on_zero_of_preconnected_of_eventually_eq_zero", "description": " The *identity principle* for analytic functions: If an analytic function vanishes in a whole\nneighborhood of a point `z\u2080`, then it is uniformly zero along a connected set. For a one-dimensional\nversion assuming only that the function vanishes at some points arbitrarily close to `z\u2080`, see\n`eq_on_zero_of_preconnected_of_frequently_eq_zero`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_on.eq_on_zero_of_preconnected_of_eventually_eq_zero_aux", "description": " If an analytic function vanishes around a point, then it is uniformly zero along\na connected set. Superseded by `eq_on_zero_of_preconnected_of_locally_zero` which does not assume\ncompleteness of the target space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.card_shadow_compression_le", "description": " UV-compression reduces the size of the shadow of `\ud835\udc9c` if, for all `x \u2208 u` there is `y \u2208 v`\nsuch that `\ud835\udc9c` is `(u.erase x, v.erase y)`-compressed. This is the key UV-compression fact needed for\nKruskal-Katona.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.is_compressed.eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.compress_disjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.sup_sdiff_mem_of_mem_compression", "description": " If `a` is in the family compression and can be compressed, then its compression is in the\noriginal family.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.card_compression", "description": "Compressing a family doesn't change its size.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.compress_idem", "description": "Compressing an element is idempotent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.compression_idem", "description": "Compressing a family is idempotent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.compress_of_disjoint_of_le'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.compression_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.is_compressed_self", "description": "Any family is compressed along two identical elements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.compress_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.shadow_compression_subset_compression_shadow", "description": " UV-compression reduces the size of the shadow of `\ud835\udc9c` if, for all `x \u2208 u` there is `y \u2208 v` such\nthat `\ud835\udc9c` is `(u.erase x, v.erase y)`-compressed. This is the key fact about compression for\nKruskal-Katona.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.is_compressed", "description": "`is_compressed u v s` expresses that `s` is UV-compressed.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.disjoint_of_mem_compression_of_not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.compress_sdiff_sdiff", "description": "An element can be compressed to any other element by removing/adding the differences.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.compress", "description": " UV-compressing `a` means removing `v` from it and adding `u` if `a` and `u` are disjoint and\n`v \u2264 a` (it replaces the `v` part of `a` by the `u` part). Else, UV-compressing `a` doesn't do\nanything. This is most useful when `u` and `v` are disjoint finsets of the same size.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.compress_of_disjoint_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.compress_mem_compression_of_mem_compression", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_sdiff_inj_on", "description": "UV-compression is injective on the elements it moves. See `uv.compress`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.compress_mem_compression", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.mem_of_mem_compression", "description": " If `a` is in the `u, v`-compression but `v \u2264 a`, then `a` must have been in the original\nfamily.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.card_compress", "description": "Compressing a finset doesn't change its size.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.sup_sdiff_mem_of_mem_compression_of_not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.mem_compression", "description": " `a` is in the UV-compressed family iff it's in the original and its compression is in the\noriginal, or it's not in the original but it's the compression of something in the original.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.le_of_mem_compression_of_not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uv.compression", "description": " To UV-compress a set family, we compress each of its elements, except that we don't want to\nreduce the cardinality, so we keep all elements whose compression is already present.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.mem_local_triv_target", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle.is_open_map_proj", "description": "The projection from a fiber bundle to its base is an open map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle.continuous_at_total_space", "description": "Characterization of continuous functions (at a point) into a fiber bundle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.local_triv_as_local_equiv_trans", "description": "The composition of two local trivializations is the trivialization change Z.triv_change i j.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.local_triv_as_local_equiv", "description": " Associate to a trivialization index `i : \u03b9` the corresponding trivialization, i.e., a bijection\nbetween `proj \u207b\u00b9 (base_set i)` and `base_set i \u00d7 F`. As the fiber above `x` is `F` but read in the\nchart with index `index_at x`, the trivialization in the fiber above x is by definition the\ncoordinate change from i to `index_at x`, so it depends on `x`.\nThe local trivialization will ultimately be a local homeomorphism. For now, we only introduce the\nlocal equiv version, denoted with a prime. In further developments, avoid this auxiliary version,\nand use `Z.local_triv` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_prebundle.is_open_target_of_mem_pretrivialization_atlas_inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.local_triv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_prebundle.mem_trivialization_at_source", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.fiber", "description": " The fiber of a fiber bundle core, as a convenience function for dot notation and\ntypeclass inference", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.triv_change", "description": "Local homeomorphism version of the trivialization change.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle.continuous_within_at_total_space", "description": "Characterization of continuous functions (at a point, within a set) into a fiber bundle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.to_topological_space", "description": " Topological structure on the total space of a fiber bundle created from core, designed so\nthat all the local trivialization are continuous.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_prebundle.continuous_symm_of_mem_pretrivialization_atlas", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.local_triv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle.map_proj_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle.mem_trivialization_at_proj_source", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.local_triv_as_local_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.is_open_map_proj", "description": "The projection on the base of a fiber bundle created from core is an open map", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.topological_space_fiber", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.local_triv_at", "description": " Preferred local trivialization of a fiber bundle constructed from core, at a given point, as\na bundle trivialization", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.proj", "description": "The projection from the total space of a fiber bundle core, on its base.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.mem_local_triv_at_base_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core", "description": " Core data defining a locally trivial bundle with fiber `F` over a topological\nspace `B`. Note that \"bundle\" is used in its mathematical sense. This is the (computer science)\nbundled version, i.e., all the relevant data is contained in the following structure. A family of\nlocal trivializations is indexed by a type `\u03b9`, on open subsets `base_set i` for each `i : \u03b9`.\nTrivialization changes from `i` to `j` are given by continuous maps `coord_change i j` from\n`base_set i \u2229 base_set j` to the set of homeomorphisms of `F`, but we express them as maps\n`B \u2192 F \u2192 F` and require continuity on `(base_set i \u2229 base_set j) \u00d7 F` to avoid the topology on the\nspace of continuous maps on `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.base_set", "description": " Core data defining a locally trivial bundle with fiber `F` over a topological\nspace `B`. Note that \"bundle\" is used in its mathematical sense. This is the (computer science)\nbundled version, i.e., all the relevant data is contained in the following structure. A family of\nlocal trivializations is indexed by a type `\u03b9`, on open subsets `base_set i` for each `i : \u03b9`.\nTrivialization changes from `i` to `j` are given by continuous maps `coord_change i j` from\n`base_set i \u2229 base_set j` to the set of homeomorphisms of `F`, but we express them as maps\n`B \u2192 F \u2192 F` and require continuity on `(base_set i \u2229 base_set j) \u00d7 F` to avoid the topology on the\nspace of continuous maps on `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.is_open_base_set", "description": " Core data defining a locally trivial bundle with fiber `F` over a topological\nspace `B`. Note that \"bundle\" is used in its mathematical sense. This is the (computer science)\nbundled version, i.e., all the relevant data is contained in the following structure. A family of\nlocal trivializations is indexed by a type `\u03b9`, on open subsets `base_set i` for each `i : \u03b9`.\nTrivialization changes from `i` to `j` are given by continuous maps `coord_change i j` from\n`base_set i \u2229 base_set j` to the set of homeomorphisms of `F`, but we express them as maps\n`B \u2192 F \u2192 F` and require continuity on `(base_set i \u2229 base_set j) \u00d7 F` to avoid the topology on the\nspace of continuous maps on `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.index_at", "description": " Core data defining a locally trivial bundle with fiber `F` over a topological\nspace `B`. Note that \"bundle\" is used in its mathematical sense. This is the (computer science)\nbundled version, i.e., all the relevant data is contained in the following structure. A family of\nlocal trivializations is indexed by a type `\u03b9`, on open subsets `base_set i` for each `i : \u03b9`.\nTrivialization changes from `i` to `j` are given by continuous maps `coord_change i j` from\n`base_set i \u2229 base_set j` to the set of homeomorphisms of `F`, but we express them as maps\n`B \u2192 F \u2192 F` and require continuity on `(base_set i \u2229 base_set j) \u00d7 F` to avoid the topology on the\nspace of continuous maps on `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.mem_base_set_at", "description": " Core data defining a locally trivial bundle with fiber `F` over a topological\nspace `B`. Note that \"bundle\" is used in its mathematical sense. This is the (computer science)\nbundled version, i.e., all the relevant data is contained in the following structure. A family of\nlocal trivializations is indexed by a type `\u03b9`, on open subsets `base_set i` for each `i : \u03b9`.\nTrivialization changes from `i` to `j` are given by continuous maps `coord_change i j` from\n`base_set i \u2229 base_set j` to the set of homeomorphisms of `F`, but we express them as maps\n`B \u2192 F \u2192 F` and require continuity on `(base_set i \u2229 base_set j) \u00d7 F` to avoid the topology on the\nspace of continuous maps on `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.coord_change", "description": " Core data defining a locally trivial bundle with fiber `F` over a topological\nspace `B`. Note that \"bundle\" is used in its mathematical sense. This is the (computer science)\nbundled version, i.e., all the relevant data is contained in the following structure. A family of\nlocal trivializations is indexed by a type `\u03b9`, on open subsets `base_set i` for each `i : \u03b9`.\nTrivialization changes from `i` to `j` are given by continuous maps `coord_change i j` from\n`base_set i \u2229 base_set j` to the set of homeomorphisms of `F`, but we express them as maps\n`B \u2192 F \u2192 F` and require continuity on `(base_set i \u2229 base_set j) \u00d7 F` to avoid the topology on the\nspace of continuous maps on `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.coord_change_self", "description": " Core data defining a locally trivial bundle with fiber `F` over a topological\nspace `B`. Note that \"bundle\" is used in its mathematical sense. This is the (computer science)\nbundled version, i.e., all the relevant data is contained in the following structure. A family of\nlocal trivializations is indexed by a type `\u03b9`, on open subsets `base_set i` for each `i : \u03b9`.\nTrivialization changes from `i` to `j` are given by continuous maps `coord_change i j` from\n`base_set i \u2229 base_set j` to the set of homeomorphisms of `F`, but we express them as maps\n`B \u2192 F \u2192 F` and require continuity on `(base_set i \u2229 base_set j) \u00d7 F` to avoid the topology on the\nspace of continuous maps on `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.continuous_on_coord_change", "description": " Core data defining a locally trivial bundle with fiber `F` over a topological\nspace `B`. Note that \"bundle\" is used in its mathematical sense. This is the (computer science)\nbundled version, i.e., all the relevant data is contained in the following structure. A family of\nlocal trivializations is indexed by a type `\u03b9`, on open subsets `base_set i` for each `i : \u03b9`.\nTrivialization changes from `i` to `j` are given by continuous maps `coord_change i j` from\n`base_set i \u2229 base_set j` to the set of homeomorphisms of `F`, but we express them as maps\n`B \u2192 F \u2192 F` and require continuity on `(base_set i \u2229 base_set j) \u00d7 F` to avoid the topology on the\nspace of continuous maps on `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.coord_change_comp", "description": " Core data defining a locally trivial bundle with fiber `F` over a topological\nspace `B`. Note that \"bundle\" is used in its mathematical sense. This is the (computer science)\nbundled version, i.e., all the relevant data is contained in the following structure. A family of\nlocal trivializations is indexed by a type `\u03b9`, on open subsets `base_set i` for each `i : \u03b9`.\nTrivialization changes from `i` to `j` are given by continuous maps `coord_change i j` from\n`base_set i \u2229 base_set j` to the set of homeomorphisms of `F`, but we express them as maps\n`B \u2192 F \u2192 F` and require continuity on `(base_set i \u2229 base_set j) \u00d7 F` to avoid the topology on the\nspace of continuous maps on `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle.quotient_map_proj", "description": " The projection from a fiber bundle with a nonempty fiber to its base is a quotient\nmap.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.open_source'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.base", "description": "The base space of a fiber bundle core, as a convenience function for dot notation", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.continuous_proj", "description": "The projection on the base of a fiber bundle created from core is continuous", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle.trivialization_at_proj_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.mem_triv_change_source", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_prebundle.is_open_source", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.local_triv_as_local_equiv_source", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.mem_local_triv_at_source", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_prebundle.continuous_on_of_comp_right", "description": " For a fiber bundle `E` over `B` constructed using the `fiber_prebundle` mechanism,\ncontinuity of a function `total_space F E \u2192 X` on an open set `s` can be checked by precomposing at\neach point with the pretrivialization used for the construction at that point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.mem_local_triv_as_local_equiv_target", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.local_triv_as_local_equiv_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.local_triv_at_apply_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_prebundle.inducing_total_space_mk_of_inducing_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_prebundle.continuous_total_space_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.mem_local_triv_at_target", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.continuous_total_space_mk", "description": "The inclusion of a fiber into the total space is a continuous map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.fiber_bundle", "description": "A fiber bundle constructed from core is indeed a fiber bundle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle.continuous_proj", "description": "The projection from a fiber bundle to its base is continuous.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_trivialization_atlas", "description": " Given a type `E` equipped with a fiber bundle structure, this is a `Prop` typeclass\nfor trivializations of `E`, expressing that a trivialization is in the designated atlas for the\nbundle.  This is needed because lemmas about the linearity of trivializations or the continuity (as\nfunctions to `F \u2192L[R] F`, where `F` is the model fiber) of the transition functions are only\nexpected to hold for trivializations in the designated atlas.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_trivialization_atlas.out", "description": " Given a type `E` equipped with a fiber bundle structure, this is a `Prop` typeclass\nfor trivializations of `E`, expressing that a trivialization is in the designated atlas for the\nbundle.  This is needed because lemmas about the linearity of trivializations or the continuity (as\nfunctions to `F \u2192L[R] F`, where `F` is the model fiber) of the transition functions are only\nexpected to hold for trivializations in the designated atlas.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_prebundle.continuous_proj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_prebundle.to_fiber_bundle", "description": " Make a `fiber_bundle` from a `fiber_prebundle`.  Concretely this means\nthat, given a `fiber_prebundle` structure for a sigma-type `E` -- which consists of a\nnumber of \"pretrivializations\" identifying parts of `E` with product spaces `U \u00d7 F` -- one\nestablishes that for the topology constructed on the sigma-type using\n`fiber_prebundle.total_space_topology`, these \"pretrivializations\" are actually\n\"trivializations\" (i.e., homeomorphisms with respect to the constructed topology).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.total_space", "description": " The total space of the fiber bundle, as a convenience function for dot notation.\nIt is by definition equal to `bundle.total_space Z.fiber`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle.exists_trivialization_Icc_subset", "description": " If `E` is a fiber bundle over a conditionally complete linear order,\nthen it is trivial over any closed interval.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.local_triv_as_local_equiv_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_prebundle.total_space_topology", "description": "Topology on the total space that will make the prebundle into a bundle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle.trivialization_at.mem_trivialization_atlas", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.local_triv_at_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.continuous_const_section", "description": " If an element of `F` is invariant under all coordinate changes, then one can define a\ncorresponding section of the fiber bundle, which is continuous. This applies in particular to the\nzero section of a vector bundle. Another example (not yet defined) would be the identity\nsection of the endomorphism bundle of a vector bundle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.index", "description": "The index set of a fiber bundle core, as a convenience function for dot notation", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle.surjective_proj", "description": " The projection from a fiber bundle with a nonempty fiber to its base is a surjective\nmap.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.local_triv", "description": " Extended version of the local trivialization of a fiber bundle constructed from core,\nregistering additionally in its type that it is a local bundle trivialization.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle", "description": " A (topological) fiber bundle with fiber `F` over a base `B` is a space projecting on `B`\nfor which the fibers are all homeomorphic to `F`, such that the local situation around each point\nis a direct product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle.total_space_mk_inducing", "description": " A (topological) fiber bundle with fiber `F` over a base `B` is a space projecting on `B`\nfor which the fibers are all homeomorphic to `F`, such that the local situation around each point\nis a direct product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle.trivialization_atlas", "description": " A (topological) fiber bundle with fiber `F` over a base `B` is a space projecting on `B`\nfor which the fibers are all homeomorphic to `F`, such that the local situation around each point\nis a direct product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle.trivialization_at", "description": " A (topological) fiber bundle with fiber `F` over a base `B` is a space projecting on `B`\nfor which the fibers are all homeomorphic to `F`, such that the local situation around each point\nis a direct product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle.mem_base_set_trivialization_at", "description": " A (topological) fiber bundle with fiber `F` over a base `B` is a space projecting on `B`\nfor which the fibers are all homeomorphic to `F`, such that the local situation around each point\nis a direct product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle.trivialization_mem_atlas", "description": " A (topological) fiber bundle with fiber `F` over a base `B` is a space projecting on `B`\nfor which the fibers are all homeomorphic to `F`, such that the local situation around each point\nis a direct product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.local_triv_at_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.base_set_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.local_triv_as_local_equiv_target", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.mem_source_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle.continuous_total_space_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_prebundle", "description": " This structure permits to define a fiber bundle when trivializations are given as local\nequivalences but there is not yet a topology on the total space. The total space is hence given a\ntopology in such a way that there is a fiber bundle structure for which the local equivalences\nare also local homeomorphism and hence local trivializations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_prebundle.pretrivialization_atlas", "description": " This structure permits to define a fiber bundle when trivializations are given as local\nequivalences but there is not yet a topology on the total space. The total space is hence given a\ntopology in such a way that there is a fiber bundle structure for which the local equivalences\nare also local homeomorphism and hence local trivializations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_prebundle.pretrivialization_at", "description": " This structure permits to define a fiber bundle when trivializations are given as local\nequivalences but there is not yet a topology on the total space. The total space is hence given a\ntopology in such a way that there is a fiber bundle structure for which the local equivalences\nare also local homeomorphism and hence local trivializations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_prebundle.mem_base_pretrivialization_at", "description": " This structure permits to define a fiber bundle when trivializations are given as local\nequivalences but there is not yet a topology on the total space. The total space is hence given a\ntopology in such a way that there is a fiber bundle structure for which the local equivalences\nare also local homeomorphism and hence local trivializations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_prebundle.pretrivialization_mem_atlas", "description": " This structure permits to define a fiber bundle when trivializations are given as local\nequivalences but there is not yet a topology on the total space. The total space is hence given a\ntopology in such a way that there is a fiber bundle structure for which the local equivalences\nare also local homeomorphism and hence local trivializations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_prebundle.continuous_triv_change", "description": " This structure permits to define a fiber bundle when trivializations are given as local\nequivalences but there is not yet a topology on the total space. The total space is hence given a\ntopology in such a way that there is a fiber bundle structure for which the local equivalences\nare also local homeomorphism and hence local trivializations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_prebundle.total_space_mk_inducing", "description": " This structure permits to define a fiber bundle when trivializations are given as local\nequivalences but there is not yet a topology on the total space. The total space is hence given a\ntopology in such a way that there is a fiber bundle structure for which the local equivalences\nare also local homeomorphism and hence local trivializations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_trivialization_atlas_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.mem_local_triv_source", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_bundle_core.mem_local_triv_as_local_equiv_source", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_prebundle.trivialization_of_mem_pretrivialization_atlas", "description": "Promotion from a `pretrivialization` to a `trivialization`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiber_prebundle.total_space_mk_preimage_source", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.xgcd_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd.induction", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.mod_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.xgcd_aux_rec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.lcm", "description": "`lcm a b` is a (non-unique) element such that `a \u2223 lcm a b` `b \u2223 lcm a b`, and for\n any element `c` such that `a \u2223 c` and `b \u2223 c`, then `lcm a b \u2223 c`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.xgcd_zero_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.div_add_mod'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.mod_add_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd_a_zero_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd", "description": "`gcd a b` is a (non-unique) element such that `gcd a b \u2223 a` `gcd a b \u2223 b`, and for\n any element `c` such that `c \u2223 a` and `c \u2223 b`, then `c \u2223 gcd a b`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd_b_zero_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.lt_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd_zero_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.mod_add_div'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain", "description": "A `euclidean_domain` is an non-trivial commutative ring with a division and a remainder,\n satisfying `b * (a / b) + a % b = a`.\n The definition of a euclidean domain usually includes a valuation function `R \u2192 \u2115`.\n This definition is slightly generalised to include a well founded relation\n `r` with the property that `r (a % b) b`, instead of a valuation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.to_comm_ring", "description": "A `euclidean_domain` is an non-trivial commutative ring with a division and a remainder,\n satisfying `b * (a / b) + a % b = a`.\n The definition of a euclidean domain usually includes a valuation function `R \u2192 \u2115`.\n This definition is slightly generalised to include a well founded relation\n `r` with the property that `r (a % b) b`, instead of a valuation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.to_nontrivial", "description": "A `euclidean_domain` is an non-trivial commutative ring with a division and a remainder,\n satisfying `b * (a / b) + a % b = a`.\n The definition of a euclidean domain usually includes a valuation function `R \u2192 \u2115`.\n This definition is slightly generalised to include a well founded relation\n `r` with the property that `r (a % b) b`, instead of a valuation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.quotient", "description": "A `euclidean_domain` is an non-trivial commutative ring with a division and a remainder,\n satisfying `b * (a / b) + a % b = a`.\n The definition of a euclidean domain usually includes a valuation function `R \u2192 \u2115`.\n This definition is slightly generalised to include a well founded relation\n `r` with the property that `r (a % b) b`, instead of a valuation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.quotient_zero", "description": "A `euclidean_domain` is an non-trivial commutative ring with a division and a remainder,\n satisfying `b * (a / b) + a % b = a`.\n The definition of a euclidean domain usually includes a valuation function `R \u2192 \u2115`.\n This definition is slightly generalised to include a well founded relation\n `r` with the property that `r (a % b) b`, instead of a valuation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.remainder", "description": "A `euclidean_domain` is an non-trivial commutative ring with a division and a remainder,\n satisfying `b * (a / b) + a % b = a`.\n The definition of a euclidean domain usually includes a valuation function `R \u2192 \u2115`.\n This definition is slightly generalised to include a well founded relation\n `r` with the property that `r (a % b) b`, instead of a valuation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.quotient_mul_add_remainder_eq", "description": "A `euclidean_domain` is an non-trivial commutative ring with a division and a remainder,\n satisfying `b * (a / b) + a % b = a`.\n The definition of a euclidean domain usually includes a valuation function `R \u2192 \u2115`.\n This definition is slightly generalised to include a well founded relation\n `r` with the property that `r (a % b) b`, instead of a valuation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.r", "description": "A `euclidean_domain` is an non-trivial commutative ring with a division and a remainder,\n satisfying `b * (a / b) + a % b = a`.\n The definition of a euclidean domain usually includes a valuation function `R \u2192 \u2115`.\n This definition is slightly generalised to include a well founded relation\n `r` with the property that `r (a % b) b`, instead of a valuation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.r_well_founded", "description": "A `euclidean_domain` is an non-trivial commutative ring with a division and a remainder,\n satisfying `b * (a / b) + a % b = a`.\n The definition of a euclidean domain usually includes a valuation function `R \u2192 \u2115`.\n This definition is slightly generalised to include a well founded relation\n `r` with the property that `r (a % b) b`, instead of a valuation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.remainder_lt", "description": "A `euclidean_domain` is an non-trivial commutative ring with a division and a remainder,\n satisfying `b * (a / b) + a % b = a`.\n The definition of a euclidean domain usually includes a valuation function `R \u2192 \u2115`.\n This definition is slightly generalised to include a well founded relation\n `r` with the property that `r (a % b) b`, instead of a valuation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.mul_left_not_lt", "description": "A `euclidean_domain` is an non-trivial commutative ring with a division and a remainder,\n satisfying `b * (a / b) + a % b = a`.\n The definition of a euclidean domain usually includes a valuation function `R \u2192 \u2115`.\n This definition is slightly generalised to include a well founded relation\n `r` with the property that `r (a % b) b`, instead of a valuation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.xgcd_aux", "description": "An implementation of the extended GCD algorithm.\nAt each step we are computing a triple `(r, s, t)`, where `r` is the next value of the GCD\nalgorithm, to compute the greatest common divisor of the input (say `x` and `y`), and `s` and `t`\nare the coefficients in front of `x` and `y` to obtain `r` (i.e. `r = s * x + t * y`).\nThe function `xgcd_aux` takes in two triples, and from these recursively computes the next triple:\n```lean\nxgcd_aux (r, s, t) (r', s', t') = xgcd_aux (r' % r, s' - (r' / r) * s, t' - (r' / r) * t) (r, s, t)\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.has_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.div_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd_b", "description": "The extended GCD `b` value in the equation `gcd x y = x * a + y * b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.xgcd", "description": "Use the extended GCD algorithm to generate the `a` and `b` values\n satisfying `gcd x y = x * a + y * b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.mul_right_not_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd_a", "description": "The extended GCD `a` value in the equation `gcd x y = x * a + y * b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.val_dvd_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.has_mod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.div_add_mod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.mod_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.mod_eq_sub_mul_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "interval_average_eq_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "interval_average_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "interval_average_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator.coe_fn_eq_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator_le_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator_aux_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator_aux_le_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "indicator_le_thickened_indicator_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator_aux_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator", "description": " The `\u03b4`-thickened indicator of a set `E` is the function that equals `1` on `E`\nand `0` outside a `\u03b4`-thickening of `E` and interpolates (continuously) between\nthese values using `inf_edist _ E`.\n\n`thickened_indicator` is the (bundled) bounded continuous function with `\u211d\u22650`-values.\nSee `thickened_indicator_aux` for the unbundled `\u211d\u22650\u221e`-valued function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator_aux_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_thickened_indicator_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator_aux_tendsto_indicator_closure", "description": " As the thickening radius \u03b4 tends to 0, the \u03b4-thickened indicator of a set E (in \u03b1) tends\npointwise (i.e., w.r.t. the product topology on `\u03b1 \u2192 \u211d\u22650\u221e`) to the indicator function of the\nclosure of E.\n\nThis statement is for the unbundled `\u211d\u22650\u221e`-valued functions `thickened_indicator_aux \u03b4 E`, see\n`thickened_indicator_tendsto_indicator_closure` for the version for bundled `\u211d\u22650`-valued\nbounded continuous functions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator_aux_closure_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator_aux", "description": " The `\u03b4`-thickened indicator of a set `E` is the function that equals `1` on `E`\nand `0` outside a `\u03b4`-thickening of `E` and interpolates (continuously) between\nthese values using `inf_edist _ E`.\n\n`thickened_indicator_aux` is the unbundled `\u211d\u22650\u221e`-valued function. See `thickened_indicator`\nfor the (bundled) bounded continuous function with `\u211d\u22650`-values.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "indicator_le_thickened_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator_aux_lt_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator_one_of_mem_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator_aux_one_of_mem_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator_to_continuous_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator_aux_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thickened_indicator_tendsto_indicator_closure", "description": " As the thickening radius \u03b4 tends to 0, the \u03b4-thickened indicator of a set E (in \u03b1) tends\npointwise to the indicator function of the closure of E.\n\nNote: This version is for the bundled bounded continuous functions, but the topology is not\nthe topology on `\u03b1 \u2192\u1d47 \u211d\u22650`. Coercions to functions `\u03b1 \u2192 \u211d\u22650` are done first, so the topology\ninstance is the product topology (the topology of pointwise convergence).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bitvec.to_fin_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bitvec.of_fin", "description": "convert `fin` to `bitvec`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bitvec.to_fin", "description": "convert `bitvec` to `fin`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bitvec.of_fin_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bitvec.to_nat_eq_foldr_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bitvec.to_nat_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bitvec.of_nat_to_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bitvec.to_fin_le_to_fin_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bitvec.preorder", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bitvec.of_fin_le_of_fin_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bitvec.add_lsb_div_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bitvec.to_fin_of_fin", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bitvec.of_fin_to_fin", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bitvec.to_bool_add_lsb_mod_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bitvec.add_lsb_eq_twice_add_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "partial_order.to_circular_partial_order", "description": " The circular partial order obtained from \"looping around\" a partial order.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw_cyclic_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_btw_of_sbtw", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_cIoo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.left_mem_cIcc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_sbtw.sbtw.not_btw", "description": "**Alias** of `not_btw_of_sbtw`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "btw_refl_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_btw", "description": "Syntax typeclass for a betweenness relation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_btw.btw", "description": "Syntax typeclass for a betweenness relation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw_trans_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_lt.to_has_sbtw", "description": " The strict betweenness relation obtained from \"looping around\" `<`.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.cIcc", "description": "Closed-closed circular interval", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_dual.has_sbtw", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_btw.btw.not_sbtw", "description": "**Alias** of `not_sbtw_of_btw`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_sbtw.sbtw.trans_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_sbtw.sbtw.not_sbtw", "description": "**Alias** of `sbtw_asymm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_btw.btw.antisymm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "preorder.to_circular_preorder", "description": " The circular preorder obtained from \"looping around\" a preorder.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_sbtw.sbtw.trans_right", "description": "**Alias** of `sbtw_trans_right`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "btw_refl_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_cIcc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "btw_rfl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_btw.btw.cyclic_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_btw.btw.cyclic_right", "description": "**Alias** of `btw_cyclic_right`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "btw_rfl_left_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.compl_cIcc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw_irrefl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw_iff_btw_not_btw", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw_irrefl_left_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_btw.btw.sbtw_of_not_btw", "description": "**Alias** of `sbtw_of_btw_not_btw`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "btw_rfl_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw_cyclic", "description": " The order of the `\u2194` has been chosen so that `rw sbtw_cyclic` cycles to the right while\n`rw \u2190sbtw_cyclic` cycles to the left (thus following the prepended arrow).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "btw_iff_not_sbtw", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_dual.circular_partial_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.right_mem_cIcc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw_irrefl_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_le.to_has_btw", "description": " The betweenness relation obtained from \"looping around\" `\u2264`.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_sbtw.sbtw.btw", "description": "**Alias** of `btw_of_sbtw`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw_irrefl_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_sbtw.sbtw.cyclic_left", "description": "**Alias** of `sbtw_cyclic_left`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circular_preorder", "description": " A circular preorder is the analogue of a preorder where you can loop around. `\u2264` and `<` are\nreplaced by ternary relations `btw` and `sbtw`. `btw` is reflexive and cyclic. `sbtw` is transitive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circular_preorder.to_has_btw", "description": " A circular preorder is the analogue of a preorder where you can loop around. `\u2264` and `<` are\nreplaced by ternary relations `btw` and `sbtw`. `btw` is reflexive and cyclic. `sbtw` is transitive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circular_preorder.to_has_sbtw", "description": " A circular preorder is the analogue of a preorder where you can loop around. `\u2264` and `<` are\nreplaced by ternary relations `btw` and `sbtw`. `btw` is reflexive and cyclic. `sbtw` is transitive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circular_preorder.btw_refl", "description": " A circular preorder is the analogue of a preorder where you can loop around. `\u2264` and `<` are\nreplaced by ternary relations `btw` and `sbtw`. `btw` is reflexive and cyclic. `sbtw` is transitive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circular_preorder.btw_cyclic_left", "description": " A circular preorder is the analogue of a preorder where you can loop around. `\u2264` and `<` are\nreplaced by ternary relations `btw` and `sbtw`. `btw` is reflexive and cyclic. `sbtw` is transitive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circular_preorder.sbtw_iff_btw_not_btw", "description": " A circular preorder is the analogue of a preorder where you can loop around. `\u2264` and `<` are\nreplaced by ternary relations `btw` and `sbtw`. `btw` is reflexive and cyclic. `sbtw` is transitive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circular_preorder.sbtw_trans_left", "description": " A circular preorder is the analogue of a preorder where you can loop around. `\u2264` and `<` are\nreplaced by ternary relations `btw` and `sbtw`. `btw` is reflexive and cyclic. `sbtw` is transitive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.cIoo", "description": "Open-open circular interval", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "btw_cyclic_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_sbtw", "description": "Syntax typeclass for a strict betweenness relation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_sbtw.sbtw", "description": "Syntax typeclass for a strict betweenness relation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw_asymm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_dual.circular_preorder", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "btw_of_sbtw", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw_iff_not_btw", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_sbtw.sbtw.cyclic_right", "description": "**Alias** of `sbtw_cyclic_right`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_dual.circular_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circular_order", "description": " A circular order is the analogue of a linear order where you can loop around. `\u2264` and `<` are\nreplaced by ternary relations `btw` and `sbtw`. `btw` is reflexive, cyclic, antisymmetric and total.\n`sbtw` is transitive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circular_order.to_circular_partial_order", "description": " A circular order is the analogue of a linear order where you can loop around. `\u2264` and `<` are\nreplaced by ternary relations `btw` and `sbtw`. `btw` is reflexive, cyclic, antisymmetric and total.\n`sbtw` is transitive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circular_order.btw_total", "description": " A circular order is the analogue of a linear order where you can loop around. `\u2264` and `<` are\nreplaced by ternary relations `btw` and `sbtw`. `btw` is reflexive, cyclic, antisymmetric and total.\n`sbtw` is transitive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw_cyclic_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circular_partial_order", "description": " A circular partial order is the analogue of a partial order where you can loop around. `\u2264` and\n`<` are replaced by ternary relations `btw` and `sbtw`. `btw` is reflexive, cyclic and\nantisymmetric. `sbtw` is transitive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circular_partial_order.to_circular_preorder", "description": " A circular partial order is the analogue of a partial order where you can loop around. `\u2264` and\n`<` are replaced by ternary relations `btw` and `sbtw`. `btw` is reflexive, cyclic and\nantisymmetric. `sbtw` is transitive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "circular_partial_order.btw_antisymm", "description": " A circular partial order is the analogue of a partial order where you can loop around. `\u2264` and\n`<` are replaced by ternary relations `btw` and `sbtw`. `btw` is reflexive, cyclic and\nantisymmetric. `sbtw` is transitive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw_of_btw_not_btw", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "btw_rfl_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "btw_cyclic", "description": " The order of the `\u2194` has been chosen so that `rw btw_cyclic` cycles to the right while\n`rw \u2190btw_cyclic` cycles to the left (thus following the prepended arrow).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_order.to_circular_order", "description": " The circular order obtained from \"looping around\" a linear order.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.compl_cIoo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_dual.has_btw", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "btw_refl_left_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_sbtw_of_btw", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_factor_order_iso_map_eq_one_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "divisor_chain.card_subset_divisors_le_length_of_chain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiplicity_prime_le_multiplicity_image_by_factor_order_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "divisor_chain.exists_chain_of_prime_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "divisor_chain.element_of_chain_eq_pow_second_of_chain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mk_factor_order_iso_of_factor_dvd_equiv", "description": "The order isomorphism between the factors of `mk m` and the factors of `mk n` induced by a\n bijection between the factors of `m` and the factors of `n` that preserves `\u2223`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "divisor_chain.first_of_chain_is_unit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mk_factor_order_iso_of_factor_dvd_equiv_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "divisor_chain.eq_pow_second_of_chain_of_has_chain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "divisor_chain.is_prime_pow_of_has_chain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_normalized_factors_factor_order_iso_of_mem_normalized_factors", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "divisor_chain.element_of_chain_not_is_unit_of_index_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiplicity_factor_dvd_iso_eq_multiplicity_of_mem_normalized_factor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pow_image_of_prime_by_factor_order_iso_dvd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "divisor_chain.second_of_chain_is_irreducible", "description": "The second element of a chain is irreducible.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiplicity_prime_eq_multiplicity_image_by_factor_order_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mk_factor_order_iso_of_factor_dvd_equiv_symm_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "divisor_chain.eq_second_of_chain_of_prime_dvd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_normalized_factors_factor_dvd_iso_of_mem_normalized_factors", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "map_prime_of_factor_order_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "associates.is_atom_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "factor_order_iso_map_one_eq_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.egorov.exists_not_convergent_seq_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_uniformly_on_of_ae_tendsto'", "description": "Egorov's theorem for finite measure spaces.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.egorov.not_convergent_seq_lt_index", "description": " Given some `\u03b5 > 0`, `not_convergent_seq_lt_index` provides the index such that\n`not_convergent_seq` (intersected with a set of finite measure) has measure less than\n`\u03b5 * 2\u207b\u00b9 ^ n`.\n\nThis definition is useful for Egorov's theorem.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.egorov.mem_not_convergent_seq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.egorov.not_convergent_seq", "description": " Given a sequence of functions `f` and a function `g`, `not_convergent_seq f g n j` is the\nset of elements such that `f k x` and `g x` are separated by at least `1 / (n + 1)` for some\n`k \u2265 j`.\n\nThis definition is useful for Egorov's theorem.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.egorov.not_convergent_seq_antitone", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.egorov.not_convergent_seq_measurable_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.egorov.measure_Union_not_convergent_seq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.egorov.tendsto_uniformly_on_diff_Union_not_convergent_seq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.egorov.Union_not_convergent_seq", "description": " Given some `\u03b5 > 0`, `Union_not_convergent_seq` is the union of `not_convergent_seq` with\nspecific indicies such that `Union_not_convergent_seq` has measure less equal than `\u03b5`.\n\nThis definition is useful for Egorov's theorem.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_uniformly_on_of_ae_tendsto", "description": " **Egorov's theorem**: If `f : \u03b9 \u2192 \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` is a sequence of strongly measurable functions that\nconverges to `g : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` almost everywhere on a measurable set `s` of finite measure,\nthen for all `\u03b5 > 0`, there exists a subset `t \u2286 s` such that `\u03bc t \u2264 \u03b5` and `f` converges to `g`\nuniformly on `s \\ t`. We require the index type `\u03b9` to be countable, and usually `\u03b9 = \u2115`.\n\nIn other words, a sequence of almost everywhere convergent functions converges uniformly except on\nan arbitrarily small set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.egorov.measure_inter_not_convergent_seq_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.egorov.measure_not_convergent_seq_tendsto_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.egorov.not_convergent_seq_lt_index_spec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.egorov.Union_not_convergent_seq_measurable_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.egorov.Union_not_convergent_seq_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.continuous_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.has_continuous_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.inr_clm", "description": "`triv_sq_zero_ext.inr` as a continuous linear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.snd_clm_coe_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.has_continuous_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.has_continuous_const_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.fst_clm_coe_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.has_continuous_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.has_sum_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.snd_clm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.nhds_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.fst_clm", "description": "`triv_sq_zero_ext.fst` as a continuous linear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.continuous_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.has_continuous_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.topological_semiring", "description": " This is not an instance due to complaints by the `fails_quickly` linter. At any rate, we only\nreally care about the `topological_ring` instance below.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.continuous_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.inl_clm_coe_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.inr_clm_coe_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.nhds_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.continuous_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.nhds_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.inl_clm", "description": "`triv_sq_zero_ext.inl` as a continuous linear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.snd_clm", "description": "`triv_sq_zero_ext.snd` as a continuous linear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.has_sum_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.inr_clm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.inl_clm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.fst_clm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.t2_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.has_sum_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.topological_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.topological_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.embedding_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.has_sum_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.embedding_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.carrier.t0_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_integral_of_open_immersion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.reduce_to_affine_global", "description": " To show that a statement `P` holds for all open subsets of all schemes, it suffices to show that\n1. In any scheme `X`, if `P` holds for an open cover of `U`, then `P` holds for `U`.\n2. For an open immerison `f : X \u27f6 Y`, if `P` holds for the entire space of `X`, then `P` holds for\n  the image of `f`.\n3. `P` holds for the entire space of an affine scheme.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.basic_open_eq_bot_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_reduced_of_open_immersion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.carrier.irreducible_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.stalk_is_reduced_of_reduced", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.carrier.quasi_sober", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.obj.is_domain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_reduced_of_is_affine_is_reduced", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.obj.is_reduced", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.eq_zero_of_basic_open_eq_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_reduced", "description": "A scheme `X` is reduced if all `\ud835\udcaa\u2093(U)` are reduced.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_reduced.component_reduced", "description": "A scheme `X` is reduced if all `\ud835\udcaa\u2093(U)` are reduced.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_reduced_of_stalk_is_reduced", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_integral_of_is_irreducible_is_reduced", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_reduced_of_is_integral", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.affine_is_reduced_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.affine_is_integral_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_integral", "description": " A scheme `X` is integral if its carrier is nonempty,\nand `\ud835\udcaa\u2093(U)` is an integral domain for each `U \u2260 \u2205`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_integral.nonempty", "description": " A scheme `X` is integral if its carrier is nonempty,\nand `\ud835\udcaa\u2093(U)` is an integral domain for each `U \u2260 \u2205`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_integral.component_integral", "description": " A scheme `X` is integral if its carrier is nonempty,\nand `\ud835\udcaa\u2093(U)` is an integral domain for each `U \u2260 \u2205`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_integral_of_is_affine_is_domain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_integral_iff_is_irreducible_and_is_reduced", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.obj.is_integral", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.reduce_to_affine_nbhd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.map_injective_of_is_integral", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_irreducible_of_is_integral", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.has_continuous_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.comap_coe_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.topological_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_nnreal_real", "description": "Embedding of `\u211d\u22650` to `\u211d` as a bundled continuous map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.tendsto_at_top_zero_of_summable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.summable_nat_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_real_to_nnreal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.summable_comp_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.tendsto_tsum_compl_at_top_zero", "description": " The sum over the complement of a finset tends to `0` when the finset grows to cover the whole\nspace. This does not need a summability assumption, as otherwise all sums are zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.infi_real_pos_eq_infi_nnreal_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.tendsto_coe'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.continuous_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.tsum_mul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.summable_coe_of_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.has_sum_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_real_to_nnreal_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.coe_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.has_continuous_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.has_sum_nat_add_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.tendsto_cofinite_zero_of_summable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.tendsto_coe_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.topological_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.tsum_mul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.coe_nnreal_real_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.coe_tsum_of_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.has_sum_real_to_nnreal_of_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.tendsto_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.nhds_zero_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.map_coe_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.has_continuous_inv\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.nhds_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_real_to_nnreal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.summable_nat_add_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.summable_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.sum_add_tsum_nat_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.order_topology", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.topological_space.second_countable_topology", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.continuous_map.can_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.pow_order_iso", "description": "`x \u21a6 x ^ n` as an order isomorphism of `\u211d\u22650`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.satellite_config.hlast'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.exists_closed_ball_covering_tsum_measure_le", "description": " In a space with the Besicovitch property, any set `s` can be covered with balls whose measures\nadd up to at most `\u03bc s + \u03b5`, for any positive `\u03b5`. This works even if one restricts the set of\nallowed radii around a point `x` to a set `f x` which accumulates at `0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.exist_finset_disjoint_balls_large_measure", "description": " Consider, for each `x` in a set `s`, a radius `r x \u2208 (0, 1]`. Then one can find finitely\nmany disjoint balls of the form `closed_ball x (r x)` covering a proportion `1/(N+1)` of `s`, if\nthere are no satellite configurations with `N+1` points.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.exists_disjoint_closed_ball_covering_ae", "description": " The measurable Besicovitch covering theorem. Assume that, for any `x` in a set `s`,\none is given a set of admissible closed balls centered at `x`, with arbitrarily small radii.\nThen there exists a disjoint covering of almost all `s` by admissible closed balls centered at some\npoints of `s`. We can even require that the radius at `x` is bounded by a given function `R x`.\n(Take `R = 1` if you don't need this additional feature).\nThis version requires that the underlying measure is sigma-finite, and that the space has the\nBesicovitch covering property (which is satisfied for instance by normed real vector spaces).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.ae_tendsto_measure_inter_div_of_measurable_set", "description": " Given a measurable set `s`, then `\u03bc (s \u2229 closed_ball x r) / \u03bc (closed_ball x r)` converges when\n`r` tends to `0`, for almost every `x`. The limit is `1` for `x \u2208 s` and `0` for `x \u2209 s`.\nThis shows that almost every point of `s` is a Lebesgue density point for `s`.\nA version for non-measurable sets holds, but it only gives the first conclusion,\nsee `ae_tendsto_measure_inter_div`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.satellite_config", "description": " A satellite configuration is a configuration of `N+1` points that shows up in the inductive\nconstruction for the Besicovitch covering theorem. It depends on some parameter `\u03c4 \u2265 1`.\n\nThis is a family of balls (indexed by `i : fin N.succ`, with center `c i` and radius `r i`) such\nthat the last ball intersects all the other balls (condition `inter`),\nand given any two balls there is an order between them, ensuring that the first ball does not\ncontain the center of the other one, and the radius of the second ball can not be larger than\nthe radius of the first ball (up to a factor `\u03c4`). This order corresponds to the order of choice\nin the inductive construction: otherwise, the second ball would have been chosen before.\nThis is the condition `h`.\n\nFinally, the last ball is chosen after all the other ones, meaning that `h` can be strengthened\nby keeping only one side of the alternative in `hlast`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.satellite_config.c", "description": " A satellite configuration is a configuration of `N+1` points that shows up in the inductive\nconstruction for the Besicovitch covering theorem. It depends on some parameter `\u03c4 \u2265 1`.\n\nThis is a family of balls (indexed by `i : fin N.succ`, with center `c i` and radius `r i`) such\nthat the last ball intersects all the other balls (condition `inter`),\nand given any two balls there is an order between them, ensuring that the first ball does not\ncontain the center of the other one, and the radius of the second ball can not be larger than\nthe radius of the first ball (up to a factor `\u03c4`). This order corresponds to the order of choice\nin the inductive construction: otherwise, the second ball would have been chosen before.\nThis is the condition `h`.\n\nFinally, the last ball is chosen after all the other ones, meaning that `h` can be strengthened\nby keeping only one side of the alternative in `hlast`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.satellite_config.r", "description": " A satellite configuration is a configuration of `N+1` points that shows up in the inductive\nconstruction for the Besicovitch covering theorem. It depends on some parameter `\u03c4 \u2265 1`.\n\nThis is a family of balls (indexed by `i : fin N.succ`, with center `c i` and radius `r i`) such\nthat the last ball intersects all the other balls (condition `inter`),\nand given any two balls there is an order between them, ensuring that the first ball does not\ncontain the center of the other one, and the radius of the second ball can not be larger than\nthe radius of the first ball (up to a factor `\u03c4`). This order corresponds to the order of choice\nin the inductive construction: otherwise, the second ball would have been chosen before.\nThis is the condition `h`.\n\nFinally, the last ball is chosen after all the other ones, meaning that `h` can be strengthened\nby keeping only one side of the alternative in `hlast`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.satellite_config.rpos", "description": " A satellite configuration is a configuration of `N+1` points that shows up in the inductive\nconstruction for the Besicovitch covering theorem. It depends on some parameter `\u03c4 \u2265 1`.\n\nThis is a family of balls (indexed by `i : fin N.succ`, with center `c i` and radius `r i`) such\nthat the last ball intersects all the other balls (condition `inter`),\nand given any two balls there is an order between them, ensuring that the first ball does not\ncontain the center of the other one, and the radius of the second ball can not be larger than\nthe radius of the first ball (up to a factor `\u03c4`). This order corresponds to the order of choice\nin the inductive construction: otherwise, the second ball would have been chosen before.\nThis is the condition `h`.\n\nFinally, the last ball is chosen after all the other ones, meaning that `h` can be strengthened\nby keeping only one side of the alternative in `hlast`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.satellite_config.h", "description": " A satellite configuration is a configuration of `N+1` points that shows up in the inductive\nconstruction for the Besicovitch covering theorem. It depends on some parameter `\u03c4 \u2265 1`.\n\nThis is a family of balls (indexed by `i : fin N.succ`, with center `c i` and radius `r i`) such\nthat the last ball intersects all the other balls (condition `inter`),\nand given any two balls there is an order between them, ensuring that the first ball does not\ncontain the center of the other one, and the radius of the second ball can not be larger than\nthe radius of the first ball (up to a factor `\u03c4`). This order corresponds to the order of choice\nin the inductive construction: otherwise, the second ball would have been chosen before.\nThis is the condition `h`.\n\nFinally, the last ball is chosen after all the other ones, meaning that `h` can be strengthened\nby keeping only one side of the alternative in `hlast`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.satellite_config.hlast", "description": " A satellite configuration is a configuration of `N+1` points that shows up in the inductive\nconstruction for the Besicovitch covering theorem. It depends on some parameter `\u03c4 \u2265 1`.\n\nThis is a family of balls (indexed by `i : fin N.succ`, with center `c i` and radius `r i`) such\nthat the last ball intersects all the other balls (condition `inter`),\nand given any two balls there is an order between them, ensuring that the first ball does not\ncontain the center of the other one, and the radius of the second ball can not be larger than\nthe radius of the first ball (up to a factor `\u03c4`). This order corresponds to the order of choice\nin the inductive construction: otherwise, the second ball would have been chosen before.\nThis is the condition `h`.\n\nFinally, the last ball is chosen after all the other ones, meaning that `h` can be strengthened\nby keeping only one side of the alternative in `hlast`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.satellite_config.inter", "description": " A satellite configuration is a configuration of `N+1` points that shows up in the inductive\nconstruction for the Besicovitch covering theorem. It depends on some parameter `\u03c4 \u2265 1`.\n\nThis is a family of balls (indexed by `i : fin N.succ`, with center `c i` and radius `r i`) such\nthat the last ball intersects all the other balls (condition `inter`),\nand given any two balls there is an order between them, ensuring that the first ball does not\ncontain the center of the other one, and the radius of the second ball can not be larger than\nthe radius of the first ball (up to a factor `\u03c4`). This order corresponds to the order of choice\nin the inductive construction: otherwise, the second ball would have been chosen before.\nThis is the condition `h`.\n\nFinally, the last ball is chosen after all the other ones, meaning that `h` can be strengthened\nby keeping only one side of the alternative in `hlast`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.exist_disjoint_covering_families", "description": " The topological Besicovitch covering theorem: there exist finitely many families of disjoint\nballs covering all the centers in a package. More specifically, one can use `N` families if there\nare no satellite configurations with `N+1` points.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.tau_package.color", "description": " Group the balls into disjoint families, by assigning to a ball the smallest color for which\nit does not intersect any already chosen ball of this color.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.tau_package", "description": " A Besicovitch tau-package is a family of balls in a metric space with positive bounded radii,\ntogether with enough data to proceed with the Besicovitch greedy algorithm. We register this in\na single structure to make sure that all our constructions in this algorithm only depend on\none variable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.tau_package.to_ball_package", "description": " A Besicovitch tau-package is a family of balls in a metric space with positive bounded radii,\ntogether with enough data to proceed with the Besicovitch greedy algorithm. We register this in\na single structure to make sure that all our constructions in this algorithm only depend on\none variable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.tau_package.\u03c4", "description": " A Besicovitch tau-package is a family of balls in a metric space with positive bounded radii,\ntogether with enough data to proceed with the Besicovitch greedy algorithm. We register this in\na single structure to make sure that all our constructions in this algorithm only depend on\none variable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.tau_package.one_lt_tau", "description": " A Besicovitch tau-package is a family of balls in a metric space with positive bounded radii,\ntogether with enough data to proceed with the Besicovitch greedy algorithm. We register this in\na single structure to make sure that all our constructions in this algorithm only depend on\none variable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_besicovitch_covering", "description": " A metric space has the Besicovitch covering property if there exist `N` and `\u03c4 > 1` such that\nthere are no satellite configuration of parameter `\u03c4` with `N+1` points. This is the condition that\nguarantees that the measurable Besicovitch covering theorem holds. It is satified by\nfinite-dimensional real vector spaces.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_besicovitch_covering.no_satellite_config", "description": " A metric space has the Besicovitch covering property if there exist `N` and `\u03c4 > 1` such that\nthere are no satellite configuration of parameter `\u03c4` with `N+1` points. This is the condition that\nguarantees that the measurable Besicovitch covering theorem holds. It is satified by\nfinite-dimensional real vector spaces.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.tau_package.last_step", "description": " `p.last_step` is the first ordinal where the construction stops making sense, i.e., `f` returns\ngarbage since there is no point left to be chosen. We will only use ordinals before this step.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.exists_disjoint_closed_ball_covering_ae_aux", "description": " The measurable Besicovitch covering theorem. Assume that, for any `x` in a set `s`,\none is given a set of admissible closed balls centered at `x`, with arbitrarily small radii.\nThen there exists a disjoint covering of almost all `s` by admissible closed balls centered at some\npoints of `s`.\nThis version requires that the underlying measure is sigma-finite, and that the space has the\nBesicovitch covering property (which is satisfied for instance by normed real vector spaces).\nIt expresses the conclusion in a slightly awkward form (with a subset of `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u211d`) coming from the\nproof technique.\nFor a version giving the conclusion in a nicer form, see `exists_disjoint_closed_ball_covering_ae`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.vitali_family", "description": " In a space with the Besicovitch covering property, the set of closed balls with positive radius\nforms a Vitali family. This is essentially a restatement of the measurable Besicovitch theorem.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.tau_package.mem_Union_up_to_last_step", "description": "Every point is covered by chosen balls, before `p.last_step`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.satellite_config.inhabited", "description": "There is always a satellite configuration with a single point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.satellite_config.inter'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.tendsto_filter_at", "description": " The main feature of the Besicovitch Vitali family is that its filter at a point `x` corresponds\nto convergence along closed balls. We record one of the two implications here, which will enable us\nto deduce specific statements on differentiation of measures in this context from the general\nversions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.ball_package.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.unit_ball_package", "description": "The ball package made of unit balls.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.tau_package.index", "description": " Choose inductively large balls with centers that are not contained in the union of already\nchosen balls. This is a transfinite induction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.tau_package.monotone_Union_up_to", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.tau_package.Union_up_to", "description": "The set of points that are covered by the union of balls selected at steps `< i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.ball_package", "description": "A ball package is a family of balls in a metric space with positive bounded radii.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.ball_package.c", "description": "A ball package is a family of balls in a metric space with positive bounded radii.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.ball_package.r", "description": "A ball package is a family of balls in a metric space with positive bounded radii.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.ball_package.rpos", "description": "A ball package is a family of balls in a metric space with positive bounded radii.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.ball_package.r_bound", "description": "A ball package is a family of balls in a metric space with positive bounded radii.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.ball_package.r_le", "description": "A ball package is a family of balls in a metric space with positive bounded radii.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.tau_package.last_step_nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.tau_package.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.ae_tendsto_rn_deriv", "description": " In a space with the Besicovitch covering property, the ratio of the measure of balls converges\nalmost surely to to the Radon-Nikodym derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.tau_package.color_lt", "description": " If there are no configurations of satellites with `N+1` points, one never uses more than `N`\ndistinct families in the Besicovitch inductive construction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.exists_disjoint_closed_ball_covering_ae_of_finite_measure_aux", "description": " The measurable Besicovitch covering theorem. Assume that, for any `x` in a set `s`,\none is given a set of admissible closed balls centered at `x`, with arbitrarily small radii.\nThen there exists a disjoint covering of almost all `s` by admissible closed balls centered at some\npoints of `s`.\nThis version requires that the underlying measure is finite, and that the space has the Besicovitch\ncovering property (which is satisfied for instance by normed real vector spaces). It expresses the\nconclusion in a slightly awkward form (with a subset of `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u211d`) coming from the proof technique.\nFor a version assuming that the measure is sigma-finite,\nsee `exists_disjoint_closed_ball_covering_ae_aux`.\nFor a version giving the conclusion in a nicer form, see `exists_disjoint_closed_ball_covering_ae`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.tau_package.R", "description": "Supremum of the radii of balls whose centers are not yet covered at step `i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "besicovitch.ae_tendsto_measure_inter_div", "description": " Given an arbitrary set `s`, then `\u03bc (s \u2229 closed_ball x r) / \u03bc (closed_ball x r)` converges\nto `1` when `r` tends to `0`, for almost every `x` in `s`.\nThis shows that almost every point of `s` is a Lebesgue density point for `s`.\nA stronger version holds for measurable sets, see `ae_tendsto_measure_inter_div_of_measurable_set`.\n\nSee also `is_unif_loc_doubling_measure.ae_tendsto_measure_inter_div`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.algebra_equiv_under", "description": "The equivalence from algebras for the coproduct monad to the under category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coprod_monad_\u03bc_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coalgebra_to_over_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.over_to_coalgebra_map_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coprod_monad", "description": "`X \u2a3f -` has a monad structure. This is sometimes called the either monad.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.over_to_coalgebra_obj_a", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.prod_comonad_\u03b4_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.over_to_coalgebra_obj_A", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.over_to_coalgebra", "description": "The backward direction of the equivalence from coalgebras for the product comonad to the over\ncategory.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.under_to_algebra", "description": "The backward direction of the equivalence from algebras for the coproduct monad to the under\ncategory.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coalgebra_equiv_over_unit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.prod_comonad_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coalgebra_to_over", "description": "The forward direction of the equivalence from coalgebras for the product comonad to the over\ncategory.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.under_to_algebra_map_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.algebra_equiv_under_unit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coprod_monad_\u03b7_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.algebra_to_under", "description": "The forward direction of the equivalence from algebras for the coproduct monad to the under\ncategory.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.algebra_to_under_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.under_to_algebra_obj_a", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coalgebra_equiv_over_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coalgebra_equiv_over", "description": "The equivalence from coalgebras for the product comonad to the over category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.under_to_algebra_obj_A", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coalgebra_to_over_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coprod_monad_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.prod_comonad", "description": "`X \u2a2f -` has a comonad structure. This is sometimes called the writer comonad.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.prod_comonad_\u03b5_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coalgebra_equiv_over_counit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.algebra_equiv_under_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.algebra_equiv_under_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coalgebra_equiv_over_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.algebra_to_under_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.algebra_equiv_under_counit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.flip'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.iterate_derivative_at_0_eq_zero_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.flip", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.derivative_succ_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.derivative_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.iterate_derivative_at_1_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.derivative_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.eq_zero_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.iterate_derivative_at_0_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.iterate_derivative_at_0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.eval_at_0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.linear_independent_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.iterate_derivative_at_1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.linear_independent", "description": "The Bernstein polynomials are linearly independent.\n\nWe prove by induction that the collection of `bernstein_polynomial n \u03bd` for `\u03bd = 0, ..., k`\nare linearly independent.\nThe inductive step relies on the observation that the `(n-k)`-th derivative, evaluated at 1,\nannihilates `bernstein_polynomial n \u03bd` for `\u03bd < k`, but has a nonzero value at `\u03bd = k`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.eval_at_1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.variance", "description": "A certain linear combination of the previous three identities,\nwhich we'll want later.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial", "description": "`bernstein_polynomial R n \u03bd` is `(choose n \u03bd) * X^\u03bd * (1 - X)^(n - \u03bd)`.\n\nAlthough the coefficients are integers, it is convenient to work over an arbitrary commutative ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.iterate_derivative_at_1_eq_zero_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.sum_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.iterate_derivative_succ_at_0_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_polynomial.sum_mul_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_core", "description": "Rewrite the expression `e` using `h`.\n   The unification is performed using the transparency mode in `cfg`.\n   If `cfg.approx` is `tt`, then fallback to first-order unification, \n   and approximate context during unification.\n   `cfg.new_goals` specifies which unassigned metavariables become new goals, \n   and their order.\n   If `cfg.instances` is `tt`, then use type class resolution to instantiate \n   unassigned meta-variables.\n   The fields `cfg.auto_param` and `cfg.opt_param` are ignored by this tactic \n   (See `tactic.rewrite`).\n   It a triple `(new_e, prf, metas)` where `prf : e = new_e`, and `metas` \n   is a list of all introduced meta variables,\n   even the assigned ones.\n\n   TODO(Leo): improve documentation and explain symm/occs", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_hyp", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_cfg", "description": "Configuration options for the `rewrite` tactic.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_cfg.to_apply_cfg", "description": "Configuration options for the `rewrite` tactic.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_cfg.symm", "description": "Configuration options for the `rewrite` tactic.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_cfg.occs", "description": "Configuration options for the `rewrite` tactic.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_target", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.id_tag.rw", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.universally_closed_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.universally_closed_respects_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.universally_closed_stable_under_base_change", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.universally_closed_is_local_at_target", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.universally_closed_stable_under_composition", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.universally_closed_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.universally_closed_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.morphism_restrict_base", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.universally_closed", "description": "A morphism of schemes `f : X \u27f6 Y` is universally closed if the base change `X \u00d7[Y] Y' \u27f6 Y'`\nalong any morphism `Y' \u27f6 Y` is (topologically) a closed map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.universally_closed.out", "description": "A morphism of schemes `f : X \u27f6 Y` is universally closed if the base change `X \u00d7[Y] Y' \u27f6 Y'`\nalong any morphism `Y' \u27f6 Y` is (topologically) a closed map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.universally_closed.open_cover_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.universally_closed_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.universally_closed_type_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.map_units_int_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.map_units_int", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.nat_abs_hom", "description": "`int.nat_abs` as a bundled monoid with zero hom.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.map_units_int_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.nat_abs_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.C_mem_lifts", "description": "If `(r : R)`, then `C (f r)` lifts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.monomial_mem_lifts", "description": "If `(s : S)` is in the image of `f`, then `monomial n s` lifts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.X_mem_lifts", "description": "The polynomial `X` lifts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.lifts_iff_ring_hom_srange", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.map_alg", "description": "The map `R[X] \u2192 S[X]` as an algebra homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.mem_lifts", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.lifts_iff_lifts_ring", "description": " If `R` and `S` are rings, `p` is in the subring of polynomials that lift if and only if it is in\nthe subsemiring of polynomials that lift.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.lifts_and_degree_eq_and_monic", "description": " A monic polynomial lifts if and only if it can be lifted to a monic polynomial\nof the same degree.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.lifts", "description": "We define the subsemiring of polynomials that lifts as the image of `ring_hom.of (map f)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.base_mul_mem_lifts", "description": "If `p` lifts and `(r : R)` then `r * p` lifts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.lifts_iff_set_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.monomial_mem_lifts_and_degree_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.mem_lifts_and_degree_eq", "description": "A polynomial lifts if and only if it can be lifted to a polynomial of the same degree.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.erase_mem_lifts", "description": "If `p` lifts then `p.erase n` lifts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.map_alg_eq_map", "description": "`map_alg` is the morphism induced by `R \u2192 S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.smul_mem_lifts", "description": "If `p` lifts and `(r : R)` then `r \u2022 p` lifts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.lifts_iff_coeff_lifts", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.lifts_ring", "description": "The subring of polynomials that lift.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.lifts_and_nat_degree_eq_and_monic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.C'_mem_lifts", "description": "If `(s : S)` is in the image of `f`, then `C s` lifts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.X_pow_mem_lifts", "description": "The polynomial `X ^ n` lifts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.mem_lifts_iff_mem_alg", "description": "A polynomial `p` lifts if and only if it is in the image of `map_alg`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.completion.mul_action_with_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.completion.sub_neg_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_hom.continuous_completion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_hom.extension_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.completion.module", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.completion.add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.compl