: "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "true.unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique.default_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique.eq_default", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.default_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "punit.unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique.subtype_eq'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_iff_subsingleton_and_nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.subsingleton", "description": " If the codomain of an injective function is a subsingleton, then the domain\nis a subsingleton as well.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.subsingleton_iff_is_empty", "description": "`option \u03b1` is a `subsingleton` if and only if `\u03b1` is empty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_of_subsingleton", "description": " Given an explicit `a : \u03b1` with `[subsingleton \u03b1]`, we can construct\na `[unique \u03b1]` instance. This is a def because the typeclass search cannot\narbitrarily invent the `a : \u03b1` term. Nevertheless, these instances are all\nequivalent by `unique.subsingleton.unique`.\n\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.subsingleton", "description": " If the domain of a surjective function is a subsingleton, then the codomain is a subsingleton as\nwell.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique.mk'", "description": " Construct `unique` from `inhabited` and `subsingleton`. Making this an instance would create\na loop in the class inheritance graph.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "punit.default_eq_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_iff_exists_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_subtype_iff_exists_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.unique", "description": " If the domain of a surjective function is a singleton,\nthen the codomain is a singleton as well.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique.bijective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_prop", "description": "Every provable proposition is unique, as all proofs are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique.subsingleton_unique'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique.exists_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique.subsingleton_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.unique_of_surjective_const", "description": "If a constant function is surjective, then the codomain is a singleton.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "heq_const_of_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.unique", "description": "If `\u03b1` is inhabited and admits an injective map to a subsingleton type, then `\u03b1` is `unique`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique.subtype_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.shiftl_eq_mul_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.shiftl'_tt_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.size_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.shiftr_eq_div_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.size_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.size_eq_bits_len", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.zero_shiftr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.size_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.size_le_size", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.one_shiftl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.lt_size_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.lt_size", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.size_shiftl'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.size_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.size_bit0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.shiftl'_ne_zero_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.size_bit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.zero_shiftl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.size_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.size_shiftl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.size_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.shiftl'_tt_eq_mul_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.size_bit1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.nat.antidiagonal_tuple_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.nat.antidiagonal_tuple_zero_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.nat.antidiagonal_tuple_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.nat.antidiagonal_tuple", "description": "`finset.antidiagonal_tuple k n` is a finset of `k`-tuples summing to `n`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.nat.sigma_antidiagonal_tuple_equiv_tuple_symm_apply_snd_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.nat.antidiagonal_tuple", "description": "`multiset.antidiagonal_tuple k n` is a multiset of `k`-tuples summing to `n`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.nat.sigma_antidiagonal_tuple_equiv_tuple_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.nat.nodup_antidiagonal_tuple", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.nat.antidiagonal_tuple_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.nat.sigma_antidiagonal_tuple_equiv_tuple_symm_apply_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nat.antidiagonal_tuple", "description": " `list.antidiagonal_tuple k n` is a list of all `k`-tuples which sum to `n`.\n\nThis list contains no duplicates (`list.nat.nodup_antidiagonal_tuple`), and is sorted\nlexicographically (`list.nat.antidiagonal_tuple_pairwise_pi_lex`), starting with `![0, ..., n]`\nand ending with `![n, ..., 0]`.\n\n```lean\n#eval antidiagonal_tuple 3 2\n-- [![0, 0, 2], ![0, 1, 1], ![0, 2, 0], ![1, 0, 1], ![1, 1, 0], ![2, 0, 0]]\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nat.antidiagonal_tuple_zero_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.nat.mem_antidiagonal_tuple", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.nat.sigma_antidiagonal_tuple_equiv_tuple", "description": " The disjoint union of antidiagonal tuples `\u03a3 n, antidiagonal_tuple k n` is equivalent to the\n`k`-tuple `fin k \u2192 \u2115`. This is such an equivalence, obtained by mapping `(n, x)` to `x`.\n\nThis is the tuple version of `finset.nat.sigma_antidiagonal_equiv_prod`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.nat.antidiagonal_tuple_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nat.antidiagonal_tuple_zero_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.nat.antidiagonal_tuple_zero_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.nat.antidiagonal_tuple_zero_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.nat.antidiagonal_tuple_zero_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nat.antidiagonal_tuple_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.nat.antidiagonal_tuple_zero_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nat.antidiagonal_tuple_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.nat.mem_antidiagonal_tuple", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nat.antidiagonal_tuple_pairwise_pi_lex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nat.antidiagonal_tuple_zero_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nat.mem_antidiagonal_tuple", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nat.nodup_antidiagonal_tuple", "description": "The antidiagonal of `n` does not contain duplicate entries.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.nat.antidiagonal_tuple_zero_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.bijective_of_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "jordan_holder_module", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_coatom_ker_of_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.surjective_or_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_semisimple_module.Sup_simples_eq_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.division_ring", "description": "Schur's Lemma makes the endomorphism ring of a simple module a division ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_simple_module.nontrivial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "covby_iff_quot_is_simple", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.bijective_or_eq_zero", "description": "**Schur's Lemma** for linear maps between (possibly distinct) simple modules", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_simple_module.is_atom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.surjective_of_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_semisimple_module", "description": "A module is semisimple when every submodule has a complement, or equivalently, the module\n is a direct sum of simple modules.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_semisimple_module.is_semisimple_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_simple_module", "description": "A module is simple when it has only two submodules, `\u22a5` and `\u22a4`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_semisimple_iff_top_eq_Sup_simples", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_simple_module_iff_is_coatom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.injective_or_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.injective_of_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_simple_module_iff_is_atom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_simple_module.congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_semisimple_of_Sup_simples_eq_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "theorems_100.nat.eq_two_pow_mul_prime_mersenne_of_even_perfect", "description": "**Perfect Number Theorem**: Euler's theorem that even perfect numbers can be factored as a\n power of two times a Mersenne prime.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.nat.ne_zero_of_prime_mersenne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.nat.perfect_two_pow_mul_mersenne_of_prime", "description": "Euclid's theorem that Mersenne primes induce perfect numbers", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.odd_mersenne_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.nat.even_two_pow_mul_mersenne_of_prime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.nat.even_and_perfect_iff", "description": "The Euclid-Euler theorem characterizing even perfect numbers", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.nat.sigma_two_pow_eq_mersenne_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.nat.eq_two_pow_mul_odd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.of_is_iso", "description": "A category where every morphism `is_iso` is a groupoid.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.inv_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor_map_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.inv_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.induced_category.groupoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.of_hom_unique", "description": "A category with a unique morphism between any two objects is a groupoid", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.inv_functor_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.inv_functor_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.inv_equiv", "description": "`groupoid.inv` is involutive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.inv_eq_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid_has_involutive_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid", "description": "A `groupoid` is a category such that all morphisms are isomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.to_category", "description": "A `groupoid` is a category such that all morphisms are isomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.inv", "description": "A `groupoid` is a category such that all morphisms are isomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.inv_comp'", "description": "A `groupoid` is a category such that all morphisms are isomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.comp_inv'", "description": "A `groupoid` is a category such that all morphisms are isomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.comp_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.inv_functor", "description": "The functor from a groupoid `C` to its opposite sending every morphism to its inverse.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.reverse_eq_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.iso_equiv_hom", "description": "In a groupoid, isomorphisms are equivalent to morphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso.of_groupoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.large_groupoid", "description": "A `large_groupoid` is a groupoid\nwhere the objects live in `Type (u+1)` while the morphisms live in `Type u`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.inv_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.small_groupoid", "description": "A `small_groupoid` is a groupoid\nwhere the objects and morphisms live in the same universe.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO.category_theory.limits.has_equalizers", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO.omega_complete_partial_order_equalizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO.has_equalizers.equalizer_\u03b9", "description": "The equalizer inclusion function as a `continuous_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO.coe_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO.has_equalizers.is_equalizer", "description": "The equalizer fork is a limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO.has_products.product", "description": "The pi-type gives a cone for a product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO.of", "description": "Construct a bundled \u03c9CPO from the underlying type and typeclass.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO", "description": "The category of types with a omega complete partial order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO.has_products.is_product", "description": "The pi-type is a limit cone for the product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO.has_products.category_theory.limits.has_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO.concrete_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO.omega_complete_partial_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO.has_coe_to_sort", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO.category_theory.limits.has_products", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO.category_theory.limits.parallel_pair.category_theory.limits.has_limit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO.omega_complete_partial_order.continuous_hom.category_theory.bundled_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO.category_theory.limits.has_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO.has_equalizers.equalizer", "description": "A construction of the equalizer fork.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "\u03c9CPO.large_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.card_Ici", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.card_uIcc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.card_Iio", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.locally_finite_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.card_Iic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.locally_finite_order_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.uIcc_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.card_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.card_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.Icc_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.card_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.locally_finite_order_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.card_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.card_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.foldr_with_index_aux_spec", "description": "Specification of `foldr_with_index_aux`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.map_with_index_eq_enum_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.mmap_with_index'_eq_mmap_with_index", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.mmap_with_index_aux_spec", "description": "Specification of `mmap_with_index_aux`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.mfoldl_with_index_eq_mfoldl_enum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.foldl_with_index_aux_spec_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.mmap_with_index'_aux_eq_mmap_with_index_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nth_le_map_with_index", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.map_with_index_core_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.mmap_with_index_eq_mmap_enum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.foldl_with_index_aux_spec", "description": "Specification of `foldl_with_index_aux`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.foldl_with_index_aux_eq_foldl_with_index_aux_spec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.map_with_index_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.map_with_index_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.length_map_with_index", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.mmap_with_index_aux_spec_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.foldl_with_index_eq_foldl_enum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.foldr_with_index_aux_spec_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.indexes_values_eq_filter_enum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.foldr_with_index_eq_foldr_enum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.mmap_with_index_aux_eq_mmap_with_index_aux_spec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.map_with_index_append", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.mfoldr_with_index_eq_mfoldr_enum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.map_with_index_eq_of_fn", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.foldr_with_index_aux_eq_foldr_with_index_aux_spec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.find_indexes_eq_map_indexes_values", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.add_group_with_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.snd_int_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.fst_int_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.exists_mem_subalgebra_near_continuous_map_of_separates_points", "description": "An alternative statement of the Stone-Weierstrass theorem,\nfor those who like their epsilons.\n\nIf `A` is a subalgebra of `C(X, \u211d)` which separates points (and `X` is compact),\nevery real-valued continuous function on `X` is within any `\u03b5 > 0` of some element of `A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.mem_conj_invariant_subalgebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.sup_mem_subalgebra_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.subalgebra_topological_closure_eq_top_of_separates_points", "description": "The **Stone-Weierstrass Approximation Theorem**,\nthat a subalgebra `A` of `C(X, \u211d)`, where `X` is a compact topological space,\nis dense if it separates points.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subalgebra.separates_points.is_R_or_C_to_real", "description": " If a conjugation-invariant subalgebra of `C(X, \ud835\udd5c)` separates points, then the real subalgebra\nof its purely real-valued elements also separates points.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.attach_bound", "description": "Turn a function `f : C(X, \u211d)` into a continuous map into `set.Icc (-\u2016f\u2016) (\u2016f\u2016)`,\nthereby explicitly attaching bounds.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.inf_mem_closed_subalgebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.sublattice_closure_eq_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.polynomial_comp_attach_bound_mem", "description": "Given a continuous function `f` in a subalgebra of `C(X, \u211d)`, postcomposing by a polynomial\ngives another function in `A`.\n\nThis lemma proves something slightly more subtle than this:\nwe take `f`, and think of it as a function into the restricted target `set.Icc (-\u2016f\u2016) \u2016f\u2016)`,\nand then postcompose with a polynomial function on that interval.\nThis is in fact the same situation as above, and so also gives a function in `A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.comp_attach_bound_mem_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.exists_mem_subalgebra_near_continuous_of_separates_points", "description": "An alternative statement of the Stone-Weierstrass theorem,\nfor those who like their epsilons and don't like bundled continuous functions.\n\nIf `A` is a subalgebra of `C(X, \u211d)` which separates points (and `X` is compact),\nevery real-valued continuous function on `X` is within any `\u03b5 > 0` of some element of `A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.attach_bound_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.polynomial_comp_attach_bound", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.abs_mem_subalgebra_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.sup_mem_closed_subalgebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.continuous_map_mem_subalgebra_closure_of_separates_points", "description": "An alternative statement of the Stone-Weierstrass theorem.\n\nIf `A` is a subalgebra of `C(X, \u211d)` which separates points (and `X` is compact),\nevery real-valued continuous function on `X` is a uniform limit of elements of `A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.subalgebra_conj_invariant", "description": "If a set `S` is conjugation-invariant, then its `\ud835\udd5c`-span is conjugation-invariant.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.conj_invariant_subalgebra", "description": "A real subalgebra of `C(X, \ud835\udd5c)` is `conj_invariant`, if it contains all its conjugates.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.inf_mem_subalgebra_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.subalgebra_is_R_or_C_topological_closure_eq_top_of_separates_points", "description": "The Stone-Weierstrass approximation theorem, `is_R_or_C` version,\nthat a subalgebra `A` of `C(X, \ud835\udd5c)`, where `X` is a compact topological space and `is_R_or_C \ud835\udd5c`,\nis dense if it is conjugation-invariant and separates points.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function.apply_id_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.total_function.pi_pred.sampleable_ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.total_function.zero_default", "description": "Map a total_function to one whose default value is zero so that it represents a finsupp.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.total_function.repr_aux", "description": "Implementation of `has_repr (total_function \u03b1 \u03b2)`.\n\nCreates a string for a given `finmap` and output, `x\u2080 \u21a6 y\u2080, .. x\u2099 \u21a6 y\u2099`\nfor each of the entries. The brackets are provided by the calling function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.total_function.apply_finsupp", "description": " Create a finitely supported function from a total function by taking the default value to\nzero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function.shrink_perm", "description": "Shrink a permutation of a list, slicing a segment in the middle.\n\nThe sizes of the slice being removed start at `n` (with `n` the length\nof the list) and then `n / 2`, then `n / 4`, etc down to 1. The slices\nwill be taken at index `0`, `n / k`, `2n / k`, `3n / k`, etc.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.total_function.shrink", "description": "Shrink a total function by shrinking the lists that represent it.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function.shrink", "description": "Shrink an injective function slicing a segment in the middle of the domain and removing\nthe corresponding elements in the codomain, hence maintaining the property that\none is a permutation of the other.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function.list.apply_id_eq_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function.repr", "description": "Produce a string for a given `total_function`.\nThe output is of the form `[x\u2080 \u21a6 f x\u2080, .. x\u2099 \u21a6 f x\u2099, x \u21a6 x]`.\nUnlike for `total_function`, the default value is not a constant\nbut the identity function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.total_function.zero_default_supp", "description": "The support of a zero default `total_function`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function.slice_sizes", "description": "A lazy list, in decreasing order, of sizes that should be\nsliced off a list of length `n`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.antitone.testable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.total_function.pi.sampleable_ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.monotone.testable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function.has_repr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function.injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.total_function", "description": " Data structure specifying a total function using a list of pairs\nand a default value returned when the input is not in the domain of\nthe partial function.\n\n`with_default f y` encodes `x \u21a6 f x` when `x \u2208 f` and `x \u21a6 y`\notherwise.\n\nWe use `\u03a3` to encode mappings instead of `\u00d7` because we\nrely on the association list API defined in `data.list.sigma`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.total_function.with_default", "description": " Data structure specifying a total function using a list of pairs\nand a default value returned when the input is not in the domain of\nthe partial function.\n\n`with_default f y` encodes `x \u21a6 f x` when `x \u2208 f` and `x \u21a6 y`\notherwise.\n\nWe use `\u03a3` to encode mappings instead of `\u00d7` because we\nrely on the association list API defined in `data.list.sigma`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function.pi_injective.sampleable_ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function.apply", "description": "Apply a total function to an argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function.list.apply_id_zip_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.total_function.has_sizeof", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.total_function.pi_uncurry.sampleable_ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function", "description": "Data structure specifying a total function using a list of pairs\nand a default value returned when the input is not in the domain of\nthe partial function.\n\n`map_to_self f` encodes `x \u21a6 f x` when `x \u2208 f` and `x \u21a6 x`,\ni.e. `x` to itself, otherwise.\n\nWe use `\u03a3` to encode mappings instead of `\u00d7` because we\nrely on the association list API defined in `data.list.sigma`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function.map_to_self", "description": "Data structure specifying a total function using a list of pairs\nand a default value returned when the input is not in the domain of\nthe partial function.\n\n`map_to_self f` encodes `x \u21a6 f x` when `x \u2208 f` and `x \u21a6 x`,\ni.e. `x` to itself, otherwise.\n\nWe use `\u03a3` to encode mappings instead of `\u00d7` because we\nrely on the association list API defined in `data.list.sigma`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.total_function.dfinsupp.sampleable_ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.total_function.list.to_finmap'", "description": "Create a `finmap` from a list of pairs.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.total_function.repr", "description": "Produce a string for a given `total_function`.\nThe output is of the form `[x\u2080 \u21a6 f x\u2080, .. x\u2099 \u21a6 f x\u2099, _ \u21a6 y]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function.list.apply_id_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function.apply_id_mem_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function.perm.slice", "description": "Remove a slice of length `m` at index `n` in a list and a permutation, maintaining the property\nthat it is a permutation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.total_function.has_repr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function.list.apply_id", "description": " Interpret a list of pairs as a total function, defaulting to\nthe identity function when no entries are found for a given function", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.total_function.total.sizeof", "description": "Redefine `sizeof` to follow the structure of `sampleable` instances.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function.has_sizeof", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective.testable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.total_function.apply", "description": "Apply a total function to an argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.total_function.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.injective_function.mk", "description": "Create an injective function from one list and a permutation of that list.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slim_check.total_function.finsupp.sampleable_ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.ideal.exists_pow_le_of_le_radical_of_fg", "description": "PORTING NOTE: This lemma should probably be moved to `ring_theory/finiteness.lean`\nto be near `ideal.exists_radical_pow_le_of_fg`, which it generalizes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.iso_of_same_radical", "description": "Local cohomology agrees on ideals with the same radical.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.of_diagram", "description": " `local_cohomology.of_diagram I i` is the the functor sending a module `M` over a commutative\nring `R` to the direct limit of `Ext^i(R/J, M)`, where `J` ranges over a collection of ideals\nof `R`, represented as a functor `I`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.iso_of_final", "description": "Local cohomology agrees along precomposition with a cofinal diagram.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.self_le_radical.cast_is_equivalence", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.self_le_radical.cast", "description": " Casting from the full subcategory of ideals with radical containing `J` to the full\nsubcategory of ideals with radical containing `K`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.ideal_powers_to_self_le_radical", "description": " Lifting `ideal_powers_diagram J` from a diagram valued in `ideals R` to a diagram\nvalued in `self_le_radical J`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.ideal_powers_diagram", "description": "The functor sending a natural number `i` to the `i`-th power of the ideal `J`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.diagram_comp", "description": "Local cohomology along a composition of diagrams.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.Module_enough_projectives'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.self_le_radical.category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.of_self_le_radical", "description": " Local cohomology as the direct limit of `Ext^i(R/J', M)` over *all* ideals `J'` with radical\ncontaining `J`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.ring_mod_ideals", "description": "  The directed system of `R`-modules of the form `R/J`, where `J` is an ideal of `R`,\ndetermined by the functor `I`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology", "description": " `local_cohomology J i` is `i`-th the local cohomology module of a module `M` over\na commutative ring `R` with support in the ideal `J` of `R`, defined as the direct limit\nof `Ext^i(R/J^t, M)` over all powers `t : \u2115`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.self_le_radical.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.iso_self_le_radical", "description": " Local cohomology (defined in terms of powers of `J`) agrees with local\ncohomology computed over all ideals with radical containing `J`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.ideal_powers_initial", "description": " The diagram of powers of `J` is initial in the diagram of all ideals with\nradical containing `J`. This uses noetherianness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.self_le_radical", "description": "The full subcategory of all ideals with radical containing `J`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.self_le_radical_diagram", "description": " The diagram of all ideals with radical containing `J`, represented as a functor.\nThis is the \"largest\" diagram that computes local cohomology with support in `J`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.diagram", "description": " The diagram we will take the colimit of to define local cohomology, corresponding to the\ndirected system determined by the functor `I`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_cohomology.self_le_radical.iso_of_same_radical", "description": " The natural isomorphism between local cohomology defined using the `of_self_le_radical`\ndiagram, assuming `J.radical = K.radical`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.commute.id_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj.id_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj\u2082.comp_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj.id_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj.eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj.option_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.commute.refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj", "description": " We say that `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` semiconjugates `ga : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1` to `gb : \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b2` if `f \u2218 ga = gb \u2218 f`.\nWe use `\u2200 x, f (ga x) = gb (f x)` as the definition, so given `h : function.semiconj f ga gb` and\n`a : \u03b1`, we have `h a : f (ga a) = gb (f a)` and `h.comp_eq : f \u2218 ga = gb \u2218 f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj.comp_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.commute.id_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.commute.option_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj\u2082.is_associative_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj.comp_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj\u2082.is_idempotent_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.commute.comp_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj\u2082.eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj\u2082.id_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj\u2082.comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.commute", "description": " Two maps `f g : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1` commute if `f (g x) = g (f x)` for all `x : \u03b1`.\nGiven `h : function.commute f g` and `a : \u03b1`, we have `h a : f (g a) = g (f a)` and\n`h.comp_eq : f \u2218 g = g \u2218 f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.commute.comp_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj.inverses_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj.commute", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj\u2082.is_idempotent_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj\u2082", "description": " A map `f` semiconjugates a binary operation `ga` to a binary operation `gb` if\nfor all `x`, `y` we have `f (ga x y) = gb (f x) (f y)`. E.g., a `monoid_hom`\nsemiconjugates `(*)` to `(*)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.commute.symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj.comp_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj\u2082.is_associative_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommMon.forget_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon.forget_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon.has_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommMon.forget_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": "The forgetful functor from additive commutative monoids to types preserves all\nlimits.\n\nThis means the underlying type of a limit can be computed as a limit in the category of types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon.forget_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": " The forgetful functor from commutative monoids to types preserves all limits.\n\nThis means the underlying type of a limit can be computed as a limit in the category of types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddMon.limit_add_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommMon.limit_cone_is_limit", "description": "The chosen cone is a limit cone. (Generally, you'll just want to use\n`limit.cone F`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon.has_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon.limit_cone", "description": "A choice of limit cone for a functor into `CommMon`.\n(Generally, you'll just want to use `limit F`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommMon.limit_add_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon.forget\u2082_Mon_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": " The forgetful functor from commutative monoids to monoids preserves all limits.\n\nThis means the underlying type of a limit can be computed as a limit in the category of monoids.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddMon.forget_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon.has_limits.limit_cone", "description": "Construction of a limit cone in `Mon`.\n(Internal use only; use the limits API.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon.has_limits_of_size", "description": "The category of monoids has all limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddMon.has_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon.limit_cone_is_limit", "description": "The chosen cone is a limit cone.\n(Generally, you'll just want to use `limit.cone F`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon.has_limits.limit_cone_is_limit", "description": "Witness that the limit cone in `Mon` is a limit cone.\n(Internal use only; use the limits API.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddMon.limit_\u03c0_add_monoid_hom", "description": "`limit.\u03c0 (F \u22d9 forget AddMon) j` as an `add_monoid_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommMon.has_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddMon.sections_add_submonoid", "description": "The flat sections of a functor into `AddMon` form an additive submonoid of all sections.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon.forget_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": " The forgetful functor from monoids to types preserves all limits.\n\nThis means the underlying type of a limit can be computed as a limit in the category of types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddMon.add_monoid_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommMon.has_limits_of_size", "description": "The category of commutative monoids has all limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon.category_theory.forget\u2082.category_theory.creates_limit", "description": " We show that the forgetful functor `CommMon \u2964 Mon` creates limits.\n\nAll we need to do is notice that the limit point has a `comm_monoid` instance available,\nand then reuse the existing limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddMon.forget_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": "The forgetful functor from additive monoids to types preserves all limits.\n\nThis means the underlying type of a limit can be computed as a limit in the category of types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon.limit_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon.sections_submonoid", "description": "The flat sections of a functor into `Mon` form a submonoid of all sections.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommMon.limit_cone", "description": "A choice of limit cone for a functor into `CommMon`. (Generally, you'll just want\nto use `limit F`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon.forget_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon.limit_\u03c0_monoid_hom", "description": "`limit.\u03c0 (F \u22d9 forget Mon) j` as a `monoid_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddMon.has_limits.limit_cone_is_limit", "description": "(Internal use only; use the limits API.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon.monoid_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon.comm_monoid_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddMon.has_limits_of_size", "description": "The category of additive monoids has all limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon.has_limits_of_size", "description": "The category of commutative monoids has all limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddMon.has_limits.limit_cone", "description": "(Internal use only; use the limits API.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon.forget\u2082_Mon_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon.limit_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommMon.add_comm_monoid_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommMon.category_theory.forget\u2082.category_theory.creates_limit", "description": "We show that the forgetful functor `AddCommMon \u2964 AddMon` creates limits.\n\nAll we need to do is notice that the limit point has an `add_comm_monoid` instance available,\nand then reuse the existing limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommMon.forget\u2082_Mon_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommMon.forget\u2082_AddMon_preserves_limits", "description": "The forgetful functor from additive\ncommutative monoids to additive monoids preserves all limits.\n\nThis means the underlying type of a limit can be computed as a limit in the category of additive\nmonoids.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zorn_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zorn_nonempty_partial_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zorn_nonempty_preorder", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zorn_preorder\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_maximal_of_chains_bounded", "description": " **Zorn's lemma**\n\nIf every chain has an upper bound, then there exists a maximal element.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zorn_preorder", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zorn_partial_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zorn_subset_nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zorn_nonempty_Ici\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zorn_nonempty_preorder\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zorn_superset_nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zorn_superset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_maximal_of_nonempty_chains_bounded", "description": " A variant of Zorn's lemma. If every nonempty chain of a nonempty type has an upper bound, then\nthere is a maximal element.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zorn_nonempty_partial_order\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zorn_partial_order\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_chain.exists_max_chain", "description": "Every chain is contained in a maximal chain. This generalizes Hausdorff's maximality principle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bounded_continuous_function.exists_forall_mem_restrict_eq_of_closed", "description": " **Tietze extension theorem** for real-valued bounded continuous maps, a version for a closed\nset. Let `s` be a closed set in a normal topological space `Y`. Let `f` be a bounded continuous\nreal-valued function on `s`. Let `t` be a nonempty convex set of real numbers (we use\n`ord_connected` instead of `convex` to automatically deduce this argument by typeclass search) such\nthat `f x \u2208 t` for all `x : s`. Then there exists a bounded continuous real-valued function\n`g : Y \u2192\u1d47 \u211d` such that `g y \u2208 t` for all `y` and `g.restrict s = f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bounded_continuous_function.exists_extension_forall_mem_of_closed_embedding", "description": " **Tietze extension theorem** for real-valued bounded continuous maps, a version for a closed\nembedding. Let `e` be a closed embedding of a nonempty topological space `X` into a normal\ntopological space `Y`. Let `f` be a bounded continuous real-valued function on `X`. Let `t` be\na nonempty convex set of real numbers (we use `ord_connected` instead of `convex` to automatically\ndeduce this argument by typeclass search) such that `f x \u2208 t` for all `x`. Then there exists\na bounded continuous real-valued function `g : Y \u2192\u1d47 \u211d` such that `g y \u2208 t` for all `y` and\n`g \u2218 e = f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bounded_continuous_function.exists_extension_forall_mem_Icc_of_closed_embedding", "description": " **Tietze extension theorem** for real-valued bounded continuous maps, a version for a closed\nembedding and a bounded continuous function that takes values in a non-trivial closed interval.\nSee also `exists_extension_forall_mem_of_closed_embedding` for a more general statement that works\nfor any interval (finite or infinite, open or closed).\n\nIf `e : X \u2192 Y` is a closed embedding and `f : X \u2192\u1d47 \u211d` is a bounded continuous function such that\n`f x \u2208 [a, b]` for all `x`, where `a \u2264 b`, then there exists a bounded continuous function\n`g : Y \u2192\u1d47 \u211d` such that `g y \u2208 [a, b]` for all `y` and `g \u2218 e = f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bounded_continuous_function.tietze_extension_step", "description": " One step in the proof of the Tietze extension theorem. If `e : C(X, Y)` is a closed embedding\nof a topological space into a normal topological space and `f : X \u2192\u1d47 \u211d` is a bounded continuous\nfunction, then there exists a bounded continuous function `g : Y \u2192\u1d47 \u211d` of the norm `\u2016g\u2016 \u2264 \u2016f\u2016 / 3`\nsuch that the distance between `g \u2218 e` and `f` is at most `(2 / 3) * \u2016f\u2016`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bounded_continuous_function.exists_norm_eq_restrict_eq_of_closed", "description": " **Tietze extension theorem** for real-valued bounded continuous maps, a version for a closed\nset. If `f` is a bounded continuous real-valued function defined on a closed set in a normal\ntopological space, then it can be extended to a bounded continuous function of the same norm defined\non the whole space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bounded_continuous_function.exists_extension_norm_eq_of_closed_embedding", "description": " **Tietze extension theorem** for real-valued bounded continuous maps, a version with a closed\nembedding and unbundled composition. If `e : C(X, Y)` is a closed embedding of a topological space\ninto a normal topological space and `f : X \u2192\u1d47 \u211d` is a bounded continuous function, then there exists\na bounded continuous function `g : Y \u2192\u1d47 \u211d` of the same norm such that `g \u2218 e = f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bounded_continuous_function.exists_extension_forall_exists_le_ge_of_closed_embedding", "description": " **Tietze extension theorem** for real-valued bounded continuous maps, a version for a closed\nembedding. Let `e` be a closed embedding of a nonempty topological space `X` into a normal\ntopological space `Y`. Let `f` be a bounded continuous real-valued function on `X`. Then there\nexists a bounded continuous function `g : Y \u2192\u1d47 \u211d` such that `g \u2218 e = f` and each value `g y` belongs\nto a closed interval `[f x\u2081, f x\u2082]` for some `x\u2081` and `x\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bounded_continuous_function.exists_extension_norm_eq_of_closed_embedding'", "description": " **Tietze extension theorem** for real-valued bounded continuous maps, a version with a closed\nembedding and bundled composition. If `e : C(X, Y)` is a closed embedding of a topological space\ninto a normal topological space and `f : X \u2192\u1d47 \u211d` is a bounded continuous function, then there exists\na bounded continuous function `g : Y \u2192\u1d47 \u211d` of the same norm such that `g \u2218 e = f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.exists_restrict_eq_of_closed", "description": " **Tietze extension theorem** for real-valued continuous maps, a version for a closed set. Let\n`s` be a closed set in a normal topological space `Y`. Let `f` be a continuous real-valued function\non `s`. Then there exists a continuous real-valued function `g : C(Y, \u211d)` such that\n`g.restrict s = f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.exists_extension_of_closed_embedding", "description": " **Tietze extension theorem** for real-valued continuous maps, a version for a closed\nembedding. Let `e` be a closed embedding of a nonempty topological space `X` into a normal\ntopological space `Y`. Let `f` be a continuous real-valued function on `X`. Then there exists a\ncontinuous real-valued function `g : C(Y, \u211d)` such that `g \u2218 e = f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.exists_restrict_eq_forall_mem_of_closed", "description": " **Tietze extension theorem** for real-valued continuous maps, a version for a closed set. Let\n`s` be a closed set in a normal topological space `Y`. Let `f` be a continuous real-valued function\non `s`. Let `t` be a nonempty convex set of real numbers (we use `ord_connected` instead of `convex`\nto automatically deduce this argument by typeclass search) such that `f x \u2208 t` for all `x : s`. Then\nthere exists a continuous real-valued function `g : C(Y, \u211d)` such that `g y \u2208 t` for all `y` and\n`g.restrict s = f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.exists_extension_forall_mem_of_closed_embedding", "description": " **Tietze extension theorem** for real-valued continuous maps, a version for a closed\nembedding. Let `e` be a closed embedding of a nonempty topological space `X` into a normal\ntopological space `Y`. Let `f` be a continuous real-valued function on `X`. Let `t` be a nonempty\nconvex set of real numbers (we use `ord_connected` instead of `convex` to automatically deduce this\nargument by typeclass search) such that `f x \u2208 t` for all `x`. Then there exists a continuous\nreal-valued function `g : C(Y, \u211d)` such that `g y \u2208 t` for all `y` and `g \u2218 e = f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.smul_comm_class_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.smul_comm_class_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_subgroup.add_action", "description": "The additive action by an add_subgroup is the action by the underlying\nadd_group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.smul_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.mul_distrib_mul_action", "description": "The action by a subgroup is the action by the underlying group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_subgroup.vadd_comm_class_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.distrib_mul_action", "description": "The action by a subgroup is the action by the underlying group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.center.smul_comm_class_left", "description": "The center of a group acts commutatively on that group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.center.smul_comm_class_right", "description": "The center of a group acts commutatively on that group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.has_faithful_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_subgroup.vadd_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.mul_action", "description": "The action by a subgroup is the action by the underlying group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.is_scalar_tower", "description": "Note that this provides `is_scalar_tower S G G` which is needed by `smul_mul_assoc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_subgroup.vadd_comm_class_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stirling.stirling_seq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stirling.log_stirling_seq_bounded_by_constant", "description": "The sequence `log_stirling_seq` is bounded below for `n \u2265 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stirling.log_stirling_seq_sub_log_stirling_seq_succ", "description": "We have the bound  `log (stirling_seq n) - log (stirling_seq (n+1))` \u2264 1/(4 n^2)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stirling.stirling_seq_has_pos_limit_a", "description": "The limit `a` of the sequence `stirling_seq` satisfies `0 < a`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stirling.log_stirling_seq_diff_has_sum", "description": "The sequence `log (stirling_seq (m + 1)) - log (stirling_seq (m + 2))` has the series expansion\n   `\u2211 1 / (2 * (k + 1) + 1) * (1 / 2 * (m + 1) + 1)^(2 * (k + 1))`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stirling.log_stirling_seq'_antitone", "description": "The sequence `log \u2218 stirling_seq \u2218 succ` is monotone decreasing", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stirling.second_wallis_limit", "description": "Suppose the sequence `stirling_seq` (defined above) has the limit `a \u2260 0`.\nThen the Wallis sequence `W n` has limit `a^2 / 2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stirling.stirling_seq_pow_four_div_stirling_seq_pow_two_eq", "description": " For any `n \u2260 0`, we have the identity\n`(stirling_seq n)^4 / (stirling_seq (2*n))^2 * (n / (2 * n + 1)) = W n`, where `W n` is the\n`n`-th partial product of Wallis' formula for `\u03c0 / 2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stirling.stirling_seq'_pos", "description": "The sequence `stirling_seq` is positive for `n > 0`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stirling.log_stirling_seq_bounded_aux", "description": "For any `n`, we have `log_stirling_seq 1 - log_stirling_seq n \u2264 1/4 * \u2211' 1/k^2`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stirling.log_stirling_seq_diff_le_geo_sum", "description": "We have a bound for successive elements in the sequence `log (stirling_seq k)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stirling.tendsto_self_div_two_mul_self_add_one", "description": "The sequence `n / (2 * n + 1)` tends to `1/2`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stirling.log_stirling_seq_formula", "description": "We have the expression\n`log (stirling_seq (n + 1)) = log(n + 1)! - 1 / 2 * log(2 * n) - n * log ((n + 1) / e)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stirling.stirling_seq'_antitone", "description": "The sequence `stirling_seq \u2218 succ` is monotone decreasing", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stirling.stirling_seq", "description": "Define `stirling_seq n` as $\\frac{n!}{\\sqrt{2n}(\\frac{n}{e})^n}$.\nStirling's formula states that this sequence has limit $\\sqrt(\u03c0)$.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stirling.stirling_seq'_bounded_by_pos_constant", "description": "The sequence `stirling_seq` has a positive lower bound.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stirling.stirling_seq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stirling.tendsto_stirling_seq_sqrt_pi", "description": "**Stirling's Formula**", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_coe_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_sub_nat_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_bit0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.neg_of_nat_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_bit1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_four", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.coe_nat_bit0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_three", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.coe_nat_bit1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_neg_of_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast_pred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_neg_succ_of_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_approximation.\u03b4_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein.z", "description": "Send `k : fin (n+1)` to the equally spaced points `k/n` in the unit interval.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_approximation.le_of_mem_S_compl", "description": "If `k \u2209 S`, then as `\u03b4 \u2264 |x - k/n|`, we have the inequality `1 \u2264 \u03b4^-2 * (x - k/n)^2`.\nThis particular formulation will be helpful later.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein.variance", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein", "description": "The Bernstein polynomials, as continuous functions on `[0,1]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_approximation", "description": "The `n`-th approximation of a continuous function on `[0,1]` by Bernstein polynomials,\ngiven by `\u2211 k, f (k/n) * bernstein n k x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_approximation.lt_of_mem_S", "description": "If `k \u2208 S`, then `f(k/n)` is close to `f x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_approximation_uniform", "description": "The Bernstein approximations\n```lean\n\u2211 k : fin (n+1), f (k/n : \u211d) * n.choose k * x^k * (1-x)^(n-k)\n```\nfor a continuous function `f : C([0,1], \u211d)` converge uniformly to `f` as `n` tends to infinity.\n\nThis is the proof given in [Richard Beals' *Analysis, an introduction*][beals-analysis], \u00a77D,\nand reproduced on wikipedia.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein.probability", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_approximation.S", "description": "The set of points `k` so `k/n` is within `\u03b4` of `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_approximation.\u03b4", "description": "The modulus of (uniform) continuity for `f`, chosen so `|f x - f y| < \u03b5/2` when `|x - y| < \u03b4`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bernstein_approximation.apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_measurable_of_re_im", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.re", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.im", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_of_re_im", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_measurable.re", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_R_or_C.measurable_im", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_R_or_C.measurable_of_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_measurable.im", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_R_or_C.measurable_re", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.tmul", "description": "The tensor product of two bilinear forms, a shorthand for dot notation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.tensor_distrib", "description": "The tensor product of two bilinear forms injects into bilinear forms on tensor products.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.tensor_distrib_equiv", "description": "`tensor_distrib` as an equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.tensor_distrib_tmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.tensor_distrib_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.id_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.id_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.to_prefunctor_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.map_dite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.to_prefunctor", "description": "The prefunctor between the underlying quivers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor", "description": "`functor C D` represents a functor between categories `C` and `D`.\n\nTo apply a functor `F` to an object use `F.obj X`, and to a morphism use `F.map f`.\n\nThe axiom `map_id` expresses preservation of identities, and\n`map_comp` expresses functoriality.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001B>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.obj", "description": "`functor C D` represents a functor between categories `C` and `D`.\n\nTo apply a functor `F` to an object use `F.obj X`, and to a morphism use `F.map f`.\n\nThe axiom `map_id` expresses preservation of identities, and\n`map_comp` expresses functoriality.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001B>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.map", "description": "`functor C D` represents a functor between categories `C` and `D`.\n\nTo apply a functor `F` to an object use `F.obj X`, and to a morphism use `F.map f`.\n\nThe axiom `map_id` expresses preservation of identities, and\n`map_comp` expresses functoriality.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001B>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.map_id'", "description": "`functor C D` represents a functor between categories `C` and `D`.\n\nTo apply a functor `F` to an object use `F.obj X`, and to a morphism use `F.map f`.\n\nThe axiom `map_id` expresses preservation of identities, and\n`map_comp` expresses functoriality.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001B>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.map_comp'", "description": "`functor C D` represents a functor between categories `C` and `D`.\n\nTo apply a functor `F` to an object use `F.obj X`, and to a morphism use `F.map f`.\n\nThe axiom `map_id` expresses preservation of identities, and\n`map_comp` expresses functoriality.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001B>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.comp_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.comp_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.id", "description": "`\ud835\udfed C` is the identity functor on a category `C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.id_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.map_comp_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.comp", "description": "`F \u22d9 G` is the composition of a functor `F` and a functor `G` (`F` first, then `G`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.map_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.comp_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.to_prefunctor_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.to_prefunctor_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.map_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_to_module.from_matrix", "description": " The composition of a matrix (as an endomporphism of `\u03b9 \u2192 R`) with the projection\n`(\u03b9 \u2192 R) \u2192\u2097[R] M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.represents.one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_representation.eq_to_End_of_represents", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_representation.to_End_represents", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_to_module.from_matrix_apply_single_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.represents", "description": " We say that a matrix represents an endomorphism of `M` if the matrix acting on `\u03b9 \u2192 R` is\nequal to `f` via the projection `(\u03b9 \u2192 R) \u2192\u2097[R] M` given by a fixed (spanning) set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_to_module.from_End", "description": " The endomorphisms of `M` acts on `(\u03b9 \u2192 R) \u2192\u2097[R] M`, and takes the projection\nto a `(\u03b9 \u2192 R) \u2192\u2097[R] M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.represents.zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.represents.eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.represents.congr_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_representation.to_End", "description": "The map sending a matrix to the endomorphism it represents. This is an `R`-algebra morphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.represents_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.represents.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.exists_monic_and_aeval_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_representation", "description": " The subalgebra of `matrix \u03b9 \u03b9 R` that consists of matrices that actually represent\nendomorphisms on `M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.represents_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.represents.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.represents.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_to_module.from_End_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_representation.to_End_exists_mem_ideal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_to_module.from_End_apply_single_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_to_module.from_matrix_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_to_module.from_End_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_representation.to_End_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.exists_monic_and_coeff_mem_pow_and_aeval_eq_zero_of_range_le_smul", "description": "The **Cayley-Hamilton Theorem** for f.g. modules over arbitrary rings states that for each\n`R`-endomorphism `\u03c6` of an `R`-module `M` such that `\u03c6(M) \u2264 I \u2022 M` for some ideal `I`, there\nexists some `n` and some `a\u1d62 \u2208 I\u2071` such that `\u03c6\u207f + a\u2081 \u03c6\u207f\u207b\u00b9 + \u22ef + a\u2099 = 0`.\n\nThis is the version found in Eisenbud 4.3, which is slightly weaker than Matsumura 2.1\n(this lacks the constraint on `n`), and is slightly stronger than Atiyah-Macdonald 2.4.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.compl_section_ord_separating_set_mem_nhds_within_Ici", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.ord_connected_component_mem_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.compl_section_ord_separating_set_mem_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.compl_section_ord_separating_set_mem_nhds_within_Iic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.ord_t5_nhd_mem_nhds_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_topology.t5_space", "description": "A linear order with order topology is a completely normal Hausdorff topological space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.finsubgraph.order_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.finsubgraph_of_adj", "description": "The finite subgraph of G generated by a single edge.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.finsubgraph.has_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.singleton_finsubgraph_le_adj_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.finsubgraph.has_Sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.finsubgraph_hom", "description": "A graph homomorphism from a finite subgraph of G to F.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.finsubgraph.has_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.finsubgraph.has_Inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.finsubgraph_hom.restrict", "description": "Given a homomorphism from a subgraph to `F`, construct its restriction to a sub-subgraph.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.singleton_finsubgraph", "description": "The finite subgraph of G generated by a single vertex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.nonempty_hom_of_forall_finite_subgraph_hom", "description": " If every finite subgraph of a graph `G` has a homomorphism to a finite graph `F`, then there is\na homomorphism from the whole of `G` to `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.finsubgraph.complete_distrib_lattice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.finsubgraph.distrib_lattice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.finsubgraph.has_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.finsubgraph_hom_functor", "description": "The inverse system of finite homomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.singleton_finsubgraph_le_adj_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.finsubgraph", "description": "The subtype of `G.subgraph` comprising those subgraphs with finite vertex sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.with_density_rn_deriv_to_real_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.with_density_rn_deriv_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.signed_measure.absolutely_continuous_iff_with_density\u1d65_rn_deriv_eq", "description": "The Radon-Nikodym theorem for signed measures.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.signed_measure.with_density\u1d65_rn_deriv_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.absolutely_continuous_iff_with_density_rn_deriv_eq", "description": " **The Radon-Nikodym theorem**: Given two measures `\u03bc` and `\u03bd`, if\n`have_lebesgue_decomposition \u03bc \u03bd`, then `\u03bc` is absolutely continuous to `\u03bd` if and only if\n`\u03bd.with_density (rn_deriv \u03bc \u03bd) = \u03bc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nhds_eq_map_mul_left_nhds_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_zpow_at_top_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.at_top_mul_neg", "description": " In a linearly ordered field with the order topology, if `f` tends to `at_top` and `g` tends to\na negative constant `C` then `f * g` tends to `at_bot`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.has_continuous_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_inv_at_top_zero'", "description": "The function `r \u21a6 r\u207b\u00b9` tends to `0` on the right as `r \u2192 +\u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.div_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.at_bot_mul", "description": " In a linearly ordered field with the order topology, if `f` tends to `at_bot` and `g` tends to\na positive constant `C` then `f * g` tends to `at_bot`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_const_mul_pow_nhds_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_tendsto_nhds_one_nhds_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eventually_nhds_within_pos_mul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_inv_zero_at_top", "description": "The function `x \u21a6 x\u207b\u00b9` tends to `+\u221e` on the right of `0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_const_mul_zpow_at_top_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_const_mul_pow_nhds_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.mul_at_top", "description": " In a linearly ordered field with the order topology, if `f` tends to a positive constant `C` and\n`g` tends to `at_top` then `f * g` tends to `at_top`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_pow_neg_at_top", "description": " The function `x^(-n)` tends to `0` at `+\u221e` for any positive natural `n`.\nA version for positive real powers exists as `tendsto_rpow_neg_at_top`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_inv_at_top_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.to_topological_division_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nhds_within_pos_comap_mul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.inv_tendsto_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.neg_mul_at_top", "description": " In a linearly ordered field with the order topology, if `f` tends to a negative constant `C` and\n`g` tends to `at_top` then `f * g` tends to `at_bot`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_const_mul_zpow_at_top_nhds_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.at_bot_mul_neg", "description": " In a linearly ordered field with the order topology, if `f` tends to `at_bot` and `g` tends to\na negative constant `C` then `f * g` tends to `at_top`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_tendsto_nhds_zero_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.mul_at_bot", "description": " In a linearly ordered field with the order topology, if `f` tends to a positive constant `C` and\n`g` tends to `at_bot` then `f * g` tends to `at_bot`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.neg_mul_at_bot", "description": " In a linearly ordered field with the order topology, if `f` tends to a negative constant `C` and\n`g` tends to `at_bot` then `f * g` tends to `at_top`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_tendsto_nhds_zero_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.at_top_mul", "description": " In a linearly ordered field with the order topology, if `f` tends to `at_top` and `g` tends to\na positive constant `C` then `f * g` tends to `at_top`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.inv_tendsto_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nhds_eq_map_mul_right_nhds_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_kernel_pair.lift'", "description": "Given a pair of morphisms `p`, `q` to `X` which factor through `f`, they factor through any kernel\npair of `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_kernel_pair.pullback", "description": " If `a\u2081 a\u2082 : A \u27f6 Y` is a kernel pair for `g : Y \u27f6 Z`, then `a\u2081 \u00d7[Z] X` and `a\u2082 \u00d7[Z] X`\n(`A \u00d7[Z] X \u27f6 Y \u00d7[Z] X`) is a kernel pair for `Y \u00d7[Z] X \u27f6 X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_kernel_pair.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_kernel_pair.mono_of_is_iso_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_kernel_pair.is_iso_of_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_kernel_pair.cancel_right", "description": "If `(a,b)` is a kernel pair for `f\u2081 \u226b f\u2082` and `a \u226b f\u2081 = b \u226b f\u2081`, then `(a,b)` is a kernel pair for\njust `f\u2081`.\nThat is, to show that `(a,b)` is a kernel pair for `f\u2081` it suffices to only show the square\ncommutes, rather than to additionally show it's a pullback.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_kernel_pair.to_coequalizer", "description": "If `(a,b)` is the kernel pair of `f`, and `f` is a coequalizer morphism for some parallel pair, then\n`f` is a coequalizer morphism of `a` and `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_kernel_pair.id_of_mono", "description": "If `f` is a monomorphism, then `(\ud835\udfd9 _, \ud835\udfd9 _)`  is a kernel pair for `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_kernel_pair.subsingleton", "description": "The data expressing that `(a, b)` is a kernel pair is subsingleton.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_kernel_pair.comp_of_mono", "description": "If `(a,b)` is a kernel pair for `f\u2081` and `f\u2082` is mono, then `(a,b)` is a kernel pair for `f\u2081 \u226b f\u2082`.\nThe converse of `cancel_right_of_mono`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_kernel_pair.of_is_iso_of_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_kernel_pair", "description": "`is_kernel_pair f a b` expresses that `(a, b)` is a kernel pair for `f`, i.e. `a \u226b f = b \u226b f`\nand the square\n  R \u2192 X\n  \u2193   \u2193\n  X \u2192 Y\nis a pullback square.\nThis is just an abbreviation for `is_pullback a b f f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_kernel_pair.cancel_right_of_mono", "description": "If `(a,b)` is a kernel pair for `f\u2081 \u226b f\u2082` and `f\u2082` is mono, then `(a,b)` is a kernel pair for\njust `f\u2081`.\nThe converse of `comp_of_mono`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.is_central_scalar", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.vadd_assoc_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.smul_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.has_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.vadd_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.has_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.is_central_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.vadd_some", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.vadd_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.has_faithful_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.vadd_comm_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.is_scalar_tower", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.mul_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.smul_comm_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.smul_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.has_faithful_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.smul_some", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.generalizes_intro", "description": "Like `generalizes'`, but also introduces the generalized constants and their\nassociated equations into the context.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.generalizes.step1", "description": "Input:\n\n- Target expression `e`.\n- List of expressions `j\u1d62` to be generalised, along with a name for the local\n  const that will replace them. The `j\u1d62` must be in dependency order:\n  `[n, fin n]` is okay but `[fin n, n]` is not.\n\nOutput:\n\n- List of new local constants `k\u1d62`, one for each `j\u1d62`.\n- `e` with the `j\u1d62` replaced by the `k\u1d62`, i.e. `e[j\u1d62 := k\u1d62]...[j\u2080 := k\u2080]`.\n\nNote that the substitution also affects the types of the `k\u1d62`: If `j\u1d62 : J\u1d62` then\n`k\u1d62 : J\u1d62[j\u1d62\u208b\u2081 := k\u1d62\u208b\u2081]...[j\u2080 := k\u2080]`.\n\nThe transparency `md` and the boolean `unify` are passed to `kabstract` when we\nabstract over occurrences of the `j\u1d62` in `e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.generalizes", "description": "Generalizes the target over multiple expressions. For example, given the goal\n\n    P : \u2200 n, fin n \u2192 Prop\n    n : \u2115\n    f : fin n\n    \u22a2 P (nat.succ n) (fin.succ f)\n\nyou can use `generalizes [n'_eq : nat.succ n = n', f'_eq : fin.succ f == f']` to\nget\n\n    P : \u2200 n, fin n \u2192 Prop\n    n : \u2115\n    f : fin n\n    n' : \u2115\n    n'_eq : n' = nat.succ n\n    f' : fin n'\n    f'_eq : f' == fin.succ f\n    \u22a2 P n' f'\n\nThe expressions must be given in dependency order, so\n`[f'_eq : fin.succ f == f', n'_eq : nat.succ n = n']` would fail since the type\nof `fin.succ f` is `nat.succ n`.\n\nYou can choose to omit some or all of the generated equations. For the above\nexample, `generalizes [nat.succ n = n', fin.succ f == f']` gets you\n\n    P : \u2200 n, fin n \u2192 Prop\n    n : \u2115\n    f : fin n\n    n' : \u2115\n    f' : fin n'\n    \u22a2 P n' f'\n\nAfter generalization, the target type may no longer type check. `generalizes`\nwill then raise an error.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.generalizes'", "description": "Generalizes the target over each of the expressions in `args`. Given\n`args = [(a\u2081, h\u2081, arg\u2081), ...]`, this changes the target to\n\n    \u2200 (a\u2081 : T\u2081) ... (h\u2081 : a\u2081 = arg\u2081) ..., U\n\nwhere `U` is the current target with every occurrence of `arg\u1d62` replaced by\n`a\u1d62`. A similar effect can be achieved by using `generalize` once for each of\nthe `args`, but if there are dependencies between the `args`, this may fail to\nperform some generalizations.\n\nThe replacement is performed using keyed matching/unification with transparency\n`md`. `unify` determines whether matching or unification is used. See\n`kabstract`.\n\nThe `args` must be given in dependency order, so `[n, fin n]` is okay but\n`[fin n, n]` will result in an error.\n\nAfter generalizing the `args`, the target type may no longer type check.\n`generalizes'` will then raise an error.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.generalizes.step2", "description": "Input: for each equation that should be generated: the equation name, the\nargument `j\u1d62` and the corresponding local constant `k\u1d62` from step 1.\n\nOutput: for each element of the input list a new local constant of type\n`j\u1d62 = k\u1d62` or `j\u1d62 == k\u1d62` and a proof of `j\u1d62 = j\u1d62` or `j\u1d62 == j\u1d62`.\n\nThe transparency `md` is used when determining whether the type of `j\u1d62` is defeq\nto the type of `k\u1d62` (and thus whether to generate a homogeneous or heterogeneous\nequation).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.generalizes.step3", "description": "Input: The `j\u1d62`; the local constants `k\u1d62` from step 1; the equations and their\nproofs from step 2.\n\nThis step is the first one that changes the goal (and also the last one\noverall). It asserts the generalized goal, then derives the current goal from\nit. This leaves us with the generalized goal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.generalizes", "description": "Generalizes the target over multiple expressions. For example, given the goal\n\n    P : \u2200 n, fin n \u2192 Prop\n    n : \u2115\n    f : fin n\n    \u22a2 P (nat.succ n) (fin.succ f)\n\nyou can use `generalizes [n'_eq : nat.succ n = n', f'_eq : fin.succ f == f']` to\nget\n\n    P : \u2200 n, fin n \u2192 Prop\n    n : \u2115\n    f : fin n\n    n' : \u2115\n    n'_eq : n' = nat.succ n\n    f' : fin n'\n    f'_eq : f' == fin.succ f\n    \u22a2 P n' f'\n\nThe expressions must be given in dependency order, so\n`[f'_eq : fin.succ f == f', n'_eq : nat.succ n = n']` would fail since the type\nof `fin.succ f` is `nat.succ n`.\n\nYou can choose to omit some or all of the generated equations. For the above\nexample, `generalizes [nat.succ n = n', fin.succ f == f']` gets you\n\n    P : \u2200 n, fin n \u2192 Prop\n    n : \u2115\n    f : fin n\n    n' : \u2115\n    f' : fin n'\n    \u22a2 P n' f'\n\nAfter generalization, the target type may no longer type check. `generalizes`\nwill then raise an error.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_stone_cech_extend", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dense_embedding_pure", "description": "`pure : \u03b1 \u2192 ultrafilter \u03b1` defines a dense embedding of `\u03b1` in `ultrafilter \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ultrafilter_is_closed_basic", "description": "The basic open sets for the topology on ultrafilters are also closed.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stone_cech_unit", "description": "The natural map from \u03b1 to its Stone-\u010cech compactification.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ultrafilter.totally_disconnected_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ultrafilter_basis", "description": "Basis for the topology on `ultrafilter \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ultrafilter.t2_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ultrafilter_compact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ultrafilter_basis_is_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stone_cech.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stone_cech_extend", "description": "The extension of a continuous function from \u03b1 to a compact\n Hausdorff space \u03b3 to the Stone-\u010cech compactification of \u03b1.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stone_cech.t2_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ultrafilter_comap_pure_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ultrafilter_converges_iff", "description": "Every ultrafilter `u` on `ultrafilter \u03b1` converges to a unique\n point of `ultrafilter \u03b1`, namely `mjoin u`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ultrafilter.extend", "description": "The extension of a function `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b3` to a function `ultrafilter \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b3`.\n When `\u03b3` is a compact Hausdorff space it will be continuous.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_ultrafilter_extend", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ultrafilter.topological_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dense_range_pure", "description": "The range of `pure : \u03b1 \u2192 ultrafilter \u03b1` is dense in `ultrafilter \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_stone_cech_unit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ultrafilter_extend_extends", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stone_cech.topological_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ultrafilter_pure_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ultrafilter_extend_eq_iff", "description": "The value of `ultrafilter.extend f` on an ultrafilter `b` is the\n unique limit of the ultrafilter `b.map f` in `\u03b3`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stone_cech", "description": "The Stone-\u010cech compactification of a topological space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ultrafilter_is_open_basic", "description": "The basic open sets for the topology on ultrafilters are open.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stone_cech_setoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convergent_eqv_pure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dense_inducing_pure", "description": "`pure : \u03b1 \u2192 ultrafilter \u03b1` defines a dense inducing of `\u03b1` in `ultrafilter \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dense_range_stone_cech_unit", "description": "The image of stone_cech_unit is dense. (But stone_cech_unit need\n not be an embedding, for example if \u03b1 is not Hausdorff.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stone_cech.compact_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "induced_topology_pure", "description": "The map `pure : \u03b1 \u2192 ultra_filter \u03b1` induces on `\u03b1` the discrete topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stone_cech_extend_extends", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stone_cech_hom_ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hinst_lemma.id", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "ematch_state.internalize", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "hinst_lemma.mk", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "hinst_lemma.has_to_tactic_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "mk_hinst_singleton", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "ematch_config", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "ematch_config.max_instances", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "ematch_config.max_generation", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "hinst_lemma.pp", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.ematch_all_core", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "hinst_lemma", "description": "Heuristic instantiation lemma", "p": "core"}, {"name": "merge_hinst_lemma_attrs", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "mk_hinst_lemma_attrs_core", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "hinst_lemmas.fold", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "hinst_lemma.mk_from_decl", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.ematch_core", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "ematch_state.mk", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.ematch_all", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "hinst_lemmas", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.ematch", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "hinst_lemmas.pp", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "get_hinst_lemmas_for_attr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "to_hinst_lemmas_core", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "hinst_lemmas.has_to_tactic_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "ematch_state", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "mk_hinst_lemma_attr_set", "description": "Create a new \"cached\" attribute (attr_name : user_attribute hinst_lemmas).\nIt also creates \"cached\" attributes for each attr_names and simp_attr_names if they have not been defined\nyet. Moreover, the hinst_lemmas for attr_name will be the union of the lemmas tagged with\n    attr_name, attrs_name, and simp_attr_names.\nFor the ones in simp_attr_names, we use the left-hand-side of the conclusion as the pattern.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "hinst_lemma.mk_from_decl_core", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "mk_hinst_lemma_attr_core", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "hinst_lemmas.add", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "hinst_lemmas.merge", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "hinst_lemma.mk_core", "description": "`mk_core m e as_simp`, m is used to decide which definitions will be unfolded in patterns.\n  If as_simp is tt, then this tactic will try to use the left-hand-side of the conclusion\n  as a pattern.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "hinst_lemmas.mk", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "blimsup_cthickening_ae_eq_blimsup_thickening", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "blimsup_cthickening_ae_le_of_eventually_mul_le", "description": " This is really an auxiliary result en route to `blimsup_cthickening_mul_ae_eq`.\n\nNB: The `set : \u03b1` type ascription is present because of issue #16932 on GitHub.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "blimsup_cthickening_mul_ae_eq", "description": " Given a sequence of subsets `s\u1d62` of a metric space, together with a sequence of radii `r\u1d62`\nsuch that `r\u1d62 \u2192 0`, the set of points which belong to infinitely many of the closed\n`r\u1d62`-thickenings of `s\u1d62` is unchanged almost everywhere for a uniformly locally doubling measure if\nthe `r\u1d62` are all scaled by a positive constant.\n\nThis lemma is a generalisation of Lemma 9 appearing on page 217 of\n[J.W.S. Cassels, *Some metrical theorems in Diophantine approximation. I*](cassels1950).\n\nSee also `blimsup_thickening_mul_ae_eq`.\n\nNB: The `set : \u03b1` type ascription is present because of issue #16932 on GitHub.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "blimsup_thickening_mul_ae_eq_aux", "description": "An auxiliary result en route to `blimsup_thickening_mul_ae_eq`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "blimsup_thickening_mul_ae_eq", "description": " Given a sequence of subsets `s\u1d62` of a metric space, together with a sequence of radii `r\u1d62`\nsuch that `r\u1d62 \u2192 0`, the set of points which belong to infinitely many of the\n`r\u1d62`-thickenings of `s\u1d62` is unchanged almost everywhere for a uniformly locally doubling measure if\nthe `r\u1d62` are all scaled by a positive constant.\n\nThis lemma is a generalisation of Lemma 9 appearing on page 217 of\n[J.W.S. Cassels, *Some metrical theorems in Diophantine approximation. I*](cassels1950).\n\nSee also `blimsup_cthickening_mul_ae_eq`.\n\nNB: The `set : \u03b1` type ascription is present because of issue #16932 on GitHub.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "blimsup_cthickening_ae_le_of_eventually_mul_le_aux", "description": " This is really an auxiliary result en route to `blimsup_cthickening_ae_le_of_eventually_mul_le`\n(which is itself an auxiliary result en route to `blimsup_cthickening_mul_ae_eq`).\n\nNB: The `set : \u03b1` type ascription is present because of issue #16932 on GitHub.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_map.restrict.bijective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_map.restrict.surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_map.restrict", "description": "Restrict domain and codomain of an affine map to the given subspaces.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_map.restrict.injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_subspace.nonempty_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_map.restrict.linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_map.restrict.coe_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_map.restrict.linear_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "card_units_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.simple_func.box_integral_eq_integral", "description": " For a simple function, its McShane (or Henstock, or `\u22a5`) box integral is equal to its\nintegral in the sense of `measure_theory.simple_func.integral`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.has_integral_zero_of_ae_eq_zero", "description": " If `f` is a.e. equal to zero on a rectangular box, then it has McShane integral zero on this\nbox.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.has_integral_indicator_const", "description": " The indicator function of a measurable set is McShane integrable with respect to any\nlocally-finite measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.simple_func.has_box_integral", "description": "A simple function is McShane integrable w.r.t. any locally finite measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable_on.has_box_integral", "description": " If `f : \u211d\u207f \u2192 E` is Bochner integrable w.r.t. a locally finite measure `\u03bc` on a rectangular box\n`I`, then it is McShane integrable on `I` with the same integral.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.has_integral.congr_ae", "description": " If `f` has integral `y` on a box `I` with respect to a locally finite measure `\u03bc` and `g` is\na.e. equal to `f` on `I`, then `g` has the same integral on `I`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_extreme_sInter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_extreme.is_partial_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "extreme_points_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_extreme.extreme_points_subset_extreme_points", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "extreme_points_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_extreme.inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "extreme_points_convex_hull_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_extreme.extreme_points_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.extreme_points", "description": " A point `x` is an extreme point of a set `A` if `x` belongs to no open segment with ends in\n`A`, except for the obvious `open_segment x x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_extreme_points_iff_extreme_singleton", "description": "x is an extreme point to A iff {x} is an extreme set of A.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_extreme.trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inter_extreme_points_subset_extreme_points_of_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_extreme_points", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_extreme.refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_extreme_points_iff_forall_segment", "description": " A useful restatement using `segment`: `x` is an extreme point iff the only (closed) segments\nthat contain it are those with `x` as one of their endpoints.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_extreme.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "extreme_points_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "extreme_points_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_extreme.rfl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_extreme_bInter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_extreme.convex_diff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.mem_extreme_points_iff_convex_diff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_extreme", "description": " A set `B` is an extreme subset of `A` if `B \u2286 A` and all points of `B` only belong to open\nsegments whose ends are in `B`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_extreme_Inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "extreme_points_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.mem_extreme_points_iff_mem_diff_convex_hull_diff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_extreme.antisymm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quasi_iso_of_comp_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quasi_iso_of_comp_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homological_complex.hom.from_single\u2080_mono_at_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homological_complex.hom.to_single\u2080_epi_at_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homological_complex.hom.from_single\u2080_kernel_at_zero_iso", "description": " If a cochain map `f : Y[0] \u27f6 X` is a quasi-isomorphism, then the kernel of the differential\n`d : X\u2080 \u2192 X\u2081` is isomorphic to `Y.`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homological_complex.hom.to_single\u2080_cokernel_at_zero_iso_hom_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quasi_iso_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_equiv.to_quasi_iso_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homological_complex.hom.from_single\u2080_exact_f_d_at_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homotopy_equiv.to_quasi_iso", "description": "An homotopy equivalence is a quasi-isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.quasi_iso_of_map_quasi_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homological_complex.hom.to_single\u2080_cokernel_at_zero_iso", "description": " If a chain map `f : X \u27f6 Y[0]` is a quasi-isomorphism, then the cokernel of the differential\n`d : X\u2081 \u2192 X\u2080` is isomorphic to `Y.`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homological_complex.hom.to_single\u2080_exact_d_f_at_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homological_complex.hom.to_single\u2080_exact_at_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homological_complex.hom.from_single\u2080_exact_at_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quasi_iso_of_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homological_complex.hom.from_single\u2080_kernel_at_zero_iso_inv_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quasi_iso", "description": "A chain map is a quasi-isomorphism if it induces isomorphisms on homology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quasi_iso.is_iso", "description": "A chain map is a quasi-isomorphism if it induces isomorphisms on homology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_inverse_obj_str_zpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_functor_obj_str_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_functor_obj_str_nsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_inverse_obj_\u03b1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence", "description": "The equivalence of categories between `CommGroup` and `AddCommGroup`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddGroup.to_Group_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.to_AddGroup_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_inverse_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddGroup.to_Group_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_functor_obj_str_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_inverse_obj_str_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_inverse_obj_str_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_counit_iso_inv_app_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_counit_iso_hom_app_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.to_CommGroup", "description": "The functor `AddCommGroup \u2964 CommGroup` by sending `X \u21a6 multiplicative Y` and `f \u21a6 f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.to_CommGroup_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_counit_iso_hom_app_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_functor_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_counit_iso_inv_app_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_functor_obj_\u03b1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_functor_obj_str_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup.to_AddCommGroup_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_functor_obj_\u03b1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_inverse_obj_str_npow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_unit_iso_inv_app_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_functor_obj_str_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_inverse_obj_str_npow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_functor_obj_str_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_inverse_obj_str_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_inverse_obj_\u03b1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_functor_obj_str_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_functor_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_functor_obj_str_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.to_AddGroup", "description": "The functor `Group \u2964 AddGroup` by sending `X \u21a6 additive X` and `f \u21a6 f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_unit_iso_hom_app_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_functor_obj_str_zsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_inverse_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.to_AddGroup_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_inverse_obj_str_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddGroup.to_Group", "description": "The functor `AddGroup \u2964 Group` by sending `X \u21a6 multiplicative Y` and `f \u21a6 f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence", "description": "The equivalence of categories between `Group` and `AddGroup`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup.to_AddCommGroup", "description": "The functor `CommGroup \u2964 AddCommGroup` by sending `X \u21a6 additive X` and `f \u21a6 f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.to_CommGroup_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_functor_obj_str_zsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup.to_AddCommGroup_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_unit_iso_inv_app_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_functor_obj_str_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_inverse_obj_str_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_inverse_obj_str_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_inverse_obj_str_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_inverse_obj_str_zpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_functor_obj_str_nsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group_AddGroup_equivalence_inverse_obj_str_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup_AddCommGroup_equivalence_unit_iso_hom_app_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.ne_locus_neg_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.ne_locus_zero_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.ne_locus_sub_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.map_range_ne_locus_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.zip_with_ne_locus_eq_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.ne_locus_sub_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.mem_ne_locus", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.subset_map_range_ne_locus", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.not_mem_ne_locus", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.ne_locus_self_add_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.ne_locus_eq_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.ne_locus_zero_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.ne_locus_add_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.ne_locus_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.ne_locus_add_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.ne_locus_eq_support_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.zip_with_ne_locus_eq_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.ne_locus_self_add_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.ne_locus_self_sub_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.ne_locus", "description": "  Given two finitely supported functions `f g : \u03b1 \u2192\u2080 N`, `finsupp.ne_locus f g` is the `finset`\nwhere `f` and `g` differ. This generalizes `(f - g).support` to situations without subtraction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.ne_locus_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.coe_ne_locus", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.ne_locus_self_sub_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.nonempty_ne_locus_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_unitor_inv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_unitor_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.associator_inv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_functor.map_pi", "description": " If `F` is a monoidal functor out of `Type`, it takes the (n+1)st cartesian power\nof a type to the image of that type, tensored with the image of the nth cartesian power.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.associator_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_unitor_inv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.types_monoidal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_unitor_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monad_reader_trans", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_reader", "description": "An implementation of [MonadReader](https://hackage.haskell.org/package/mtl-2.2.2/docs/Control-Monad-Reader-Class.html#t:MonadReader).\n   It does not contain `local` because this function cannot be lifted using `monad_lift`.\n   Instead, the `monad_reader_adapter` class provides the more general `adapt_reader` function.\n\n   Note: This class can be seen as a simplification of the more \"principled\" definition\n   ```lean\n   class monad_reader (\u03c1 : out_param (Type u)) (n : Type u \u2192 Type u) :=\n   (lift {\u03b1 : Type u} : (\u2200 {m : Type u \u2192 Type u} [monad m], reader_t \u03c1 m \u03b1) \u2192 n \u03b1)\n   ```", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_reader.read", "description": "An implementation of [MonadReader](https://hackage.haskell.org/package/mtl-2.2.2/docs/Control-Monad-Reader-Class.html#t:MonadReader).\n   It does not contain `local` because this function cannot be lifted using `monad_lift`.\n   Instead, the `monad_reader_adapter` class provides the more general `adapt_reader` function.\n\n   Note: This class can be seen as a simplification of the more \"principled\" definition\n   ```lean\n   class monad_reader (\u03c1 : out_param (Type u)) (n : Type u \u2192 Type u) :=\n   (lift {\u03b1 : Type u} : (\u2200 {m : Type u \u2192 Type u} [monad m], reader_t \u03c1 m \u03b1) \u2192 n \u03b1)\n   ```", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_reader_adapter", "description": "Adapt a monad stack, changing the type of its top-most environment.\n\n   This class is comparable to [Control.Lens.Magnify](https://hackage.haskell.org/package/lens-4.15.4/docs/Control-Lens-Zoom.html#t:Magnify), but does not use lenses (why would it), and is derived automatically for any transformer implementing `monad_functor`.\n\n   Note: This class can be seen as a simplification of the more \"principled\" definition\n   ```lean\n   class monad_reader_functor (\u03c1 \u03c1' : out_param (Type u)) (n n' : Type u \u2192 Type u) :=\n   (map {\u03b1 : Type u} : (\u2200 {m : Type u \u2192 Type u} [monad m], reader_t \u03c1 m \u03b1 \u2192 reader_t \u03c1' m \u03b1) \u2192 n \u03b1 \u2192 n' \u03b1)\n   ```", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_reader_adapter.adapt_reader", "description": "Adapt a monad stack, changing the type of its top-most environment.\n\n   This class is comparable to [Control.Lens.Magnify](https://hackage.haskell.org/package/lens-4.15.4/docs/Control-Lens-Zoom.html#t:Magnify), but does not use lenses (why would it), and is derived automatically for any transformer implementing `monad_functor`.\n\n   Note: This class can be seen as a simplification of the more \"principled\" definition\n   ```lean\n   class monad_reader_functor (\u03c1 \u03c1' : out_param (Type u)) (n n' : Type u \u2192 Type u) :=\n   (map {\u03b1 : Type u} : (\u2200 {m : Type u \u2192 Type u} [monad m], reader_t \u03c1 m \u03b1 \u2192 reader_t \u03c1' m \u03b1) \u2192 n \u03b1 \u2192 n' \u03b1)\n   ```", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_reader_adapter_trans", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.monad_except", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.adapt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.monad_reader_adapter", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.read", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.monad_reader", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.bind", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.has_monad_lift", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.lift", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.monad_functor", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.monad_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.orelse", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.pure", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.alternative", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.failure", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t", "description": "An implementation of [ReaderT](https://hackage.haskell.org/package/transformers-0.5.5.0/docs/Control-Monad-Trans-Reader.html#t:ReaderT)", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.run", "description": "An implementation of [ReaderT](https://hackage.haskell.org/package/transformers-0.5.5.0/docs/Control-Monad-Trans-Reader.html#t:ReaderT)", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.monad", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.monad_run", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "algebra.char_p_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_p_of_injective_algebra_map", "description": "If the algebra map `R \u2192+* A` is injective then `A` has the same characteristic as `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_algebra.char_p", "description": "If `R` has characteristic `p`, then so does `free_algebra R X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra_rat.char_zero", "description": " A nontrivial `\u211a`-algebra has characteristic zero.\n\nThis cannot be a (local) instance because it would immediately form a loop with the\ninstance `algebra_rat`. It's probably easier to go the other way: prove `char_zero R` and\nautomatically receive an `algebra \u211a R` instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.char_p_of_is_fraction_ring", "description": "If `R` has characteristic `p`, then so does Frac(R).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_algebra.char_zero", "description": "If `R` has characteristic `0`, then so does `free_algebra R X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.char_p", "description": "If `R` has characteristic `p`, then so does `fraction_ring R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.char_zero", "description": "If `R` has characteristic `0`, then so does `fraction_ring R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_zero_of_injective_algebra_map", "description": "If the algebra map `R \u2192+* A` is injective and `R` has characteristic zero then so does `A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.ring_char_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra_rat.char_p_zero", "description": " A nontrivial `\u211a`-algebra has `char_p` equal to zero.\n\nThis cannot be a (local) instance because it would immediately form a loop with the\ninstance `algebra_rat`. It's probably easier to go the other way: prove `char_zero R` and\nautomatically receive an `algebra \u211a R` instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.char_zero_of_is_fraction_ring", "description": "If `R` has characteristic `0`, then so does Frac(R).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_p_of_injective_algebra_map'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_seq_tendsto_ae.seq_tendsto_ae_seq_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure_of_tendsto_ae", "description": "Convergence a.e. implies convergence in measure in a finite measure space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_seq_tendsto_ae.seq_tendsto_ae_seq_strict_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure_of_tendsto_snorm_top", "description": " See also `measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure_of_tendsto_snorm` which work for general\nLp-convergence for all `p \u2260 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure.ae_measurable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure_of_tendsto_snorm_of_strongly_measurable", "description": " This lemma is superceded by `measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure_of_tendsto_snorm` where we\nallow `p = \u221e` and only require `ae_strongly_measurable`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure.exists_seq_tendsto_ae", "description": " If `f` is a sequence of functions which converges in measure to `g`, then there exists a\nsubsequence of `f` which converges a.e. to `g`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure_of_tendsto_snorm", "description": "Convergence in Lp implies convergence in measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure.exists_seq_tendsto_ae'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure.exists_seq_tendsto_in_measure_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure_of_tendsto_snorm_of_ne_top", "description": " This lemma is superceded by `measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure_of_tendsto_snorm` where we\nallow `p = \u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure", "description": " A sequence of functions `f` is said to converge in measure to some function `g` if for all\n`\u03b5 > 0`, the measure of the set `{x | \u03b5 \u2264 dist (f i x) (g x)}` tends to 0 as `i` converges along\nsome given filter `l`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure_iff_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure.congr'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure.congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure_of_tendsto_Lp", "description": "Convergence in Lp implies convergence in measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_seq_tendsto_ae.seq_tendsto_ae_seq_aux", "description": " Given a sequence of functions `f` which converges in measure to `g`,\n`seq_tendsto_ae_seq_aux` is a sequence such that\n`\u2200 m \u2265 seq_tendsto_ae_seq_aux n, \u03bc {x | 2\u207b\u00b9 ^ n \u2264 dist (f m x) (g x)} \u2264 2\u207b\u00b9 ^ n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_seq_tendsto_ae.exists_nat_measure_lt_two_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure_of_tendsto_ae_of_strongly_measurable", "description": "Auxiliary lemma for `tendsto_in_measure_of_tendsto_ae`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_seq_tendsto_ae.seq_tendsto_ae_seq", "description": "Transformation of `seq_tendsto_ae_seq_aux` to makes sure it is strictly monotone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_seq_tendsto_ae.seq_tendsto_ae_seq_spec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure.congr_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_in_measure.congr_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "push_neg.not_and_distrib_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "push_neg.not_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "push_neg.not_le_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "push_neg.normalize_negations", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.push_neg_cmd", "description": "The syntax is `#push_neg e`, where `e` is an expression,\nwhich will print the `push_neg` form of `e`.\n\n`#push_neg` understands local variables, so you can use them to\nintroduce parameters.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.push_neg", "description": "Push negations in the goal of some assumption.\n\nFor instance, a hypothesis `h : \u00ac \u2200 x, \u2203 y, x \u2264 y` will be transformed by `push_neg at h` into\n`h : \u2203 x, \u2200 y, y < x`. Variables names are conserved.\n\nThis tactic pushes negations inside expressions. For instance, given an assumption\n```lean\nh : \u00ac \u2200 \u03b5 > 0, \u2203 \u03b4 > 0, \u2200 x, |x - x\u2080| \u2264 \u03b4 \u2192 |f x - y\u2080| \u2264 \u03b5)\n```\nwriting `push_neg at h` will turn `h` into\n```lean\nh : \u2203 \u03b5, \u03b5 > 0 \u2227 \u2200 \u03b4, \u03b4 > 0 \u2192 (\u2203 x, |x - x\u2080| \u2264 \u03b4 \u2227 \u03b5 < |f x - y\u2080|),\n```\n\n(the pretty printer does *not* use the abreviations `\u2200 \u03b4 > 0` and `\u2203 \u03b5 > 0` but this issue\nhas nothing to do with `push_neg`).\nNote that names are conserved by this tactic, contrary to what would happen with `simp`\nusing the relevant lemmas. One can also use this tactic at the goal using `push_neg`,\nat every assumption and the goal using `push_neg at *` or at selected assumptions and the goal\nusing say `push_neg at h h' \u22a2` as usual.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "push_neg.not_lt_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "push_neg.whnf_reducible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "push_neg.not_and_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.command.#push_neg", "description": "The syntax is `#push_neg e`, where `e` is an expression,\nwhich will print the `push_neg` form of `e`.\n\n`#push_neg` understands local variables, so you can use them to\nintroduce parameters.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "push_neg.not_forall_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "name_with_opt", "description": "Matches either an identifier \"h\" or a pair of identifiers \"h with k\"", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "push_neg.classical.implies_iff_not_or", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "push_neg.not_implies_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.contrapose", "description": "Transforms the goal into its contrapositive.\n\n* `contrapose`     turns a goal `P \u2192 Q` into `\u00ac Q \u2192 \u00ac P`\n* `contrapose!`    turns a goal `P \u2192 Q` into `\u00ac Q \u2192 \u00ac P` and pushes negations inside `P` and `Q`\n  using `push_neg`\n* `contrapose h`   first reverts the local assumption `h`, and then uses `contrapose` and `intro h`\n* `contrapose! h`  first reverts the local assumption `h`, and then uses `contrapose!` and `intro h`\n* `contrapose h with new_h` uses the name `new_h` for the introduced hypothesis", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "push_neg.push_neg_at_goal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "push_neg.push_neg_at_hyp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "push_neg.not_exists_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "push_neg.not_or_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "push_neg.not_not_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.contrapose", "description": "Transforms the goal into its contrapositive.\n\n* `contrapose`     turns a goal `P \u2192 Q` into `\u00ac Q \u2192 \u00ac P`\n* `contrapose!`    turns a goal `P \u2192 Q` into `\u00ac Q \u2192 \u00ac P` and pushes negations inside `P` and `Q`\n  using `push_neg`\n* `contrapose h`   first reverts the local assumption `h`, and then uses `contrapose` and `intro h`\n* `contrapose! h`  first reverts the local assumption `h`, and then uses `contrapose!` and `intro h`\n* `contrapose h with new_h` uses the name `new_h` for the introduced hypothesis", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.push_neg", "description": "Push negations in the goal of some assumption.\n\nFor instance, a hypothesis `h : \u00ac \u2200 x, \u2203 y, x \u2264 y` will be transformed by `push_neg at h` into\n`h : \u2203 x, \u2200 y, y < x`. Variables names are conserved.\n\nThis tactic pushes negations inside expressions. For instance, given an assumption\n```lean\nh : \u00ac \u2200 \u03b5 > 0, \u2203 \u03b4 > 0, \u2200 x, |x - x\u2080| \u2264 \u03b4 \u2192 |f x - y\u2080| \u2264 \u03b5)\n```\nwriting `push_neg at h` will turn `h` into\n```lean\nh : \u2203 \u03b5, \u03b5 > 0 \u2227 \u2200 \u03b4, \u03b4 > 0 \u2192 (\u2203 x, |x - x\u2080| \u2264 \u03b4 \u2227 \u03b5 < |f x - y\u2080|),\n```\n\n(the pretty printer does *not* use the abreviations `\u2200 \u03b4 > 0` and `\u2203 \u03b5 > 0` but this issue\nhas nothing to do with `push_neg`).\nNote that names are conserved by this tactic, contrary to what would happen with `simp`\nusing the relevant lemmas. One can also use this tactic at the goal using `push_neg`,\nat every assumption and the goal using `push_neg at *` or at selected assumptions and the goal\nusing say `push_neg at h h' \u22a2` as usual.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imp_of_not_imp_not", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Profinite.projective_ultrafilter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Profinite.category_theory.enough_projectives", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Profinite.projective_presentation", "description": "For any profinite `X`, the natural map `ultrafilter X \u2192 X` is a projective presentation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.iterate_derivative_X_sub_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.derivative_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.derivative_X_add_C_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.derivative_of_nat_degree_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.derivative_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.derivative_bit1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.derivative_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.mem_support_derivative", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.iterate_derivative_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.derivative_bit0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.derivative_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.iterate_derivative_X_pow_eq_C_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_derivative_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.iterate_derivative_X_add_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.derivative_X_sub_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.derivative", "description": "`derivative p` is the formal derivative of the polynomial `p`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.iterate_derivative_X_pow_eq_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.derivative_int_cast_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.derivative_X_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.