ib"}, {"name": "tensor_power.mul_algebra_map\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_power.mul_equiv", "description": "A variant of `pi_tensor_prod.tmul_equiv` with the result indexed by `fin (n + m)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_power.cast_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_power.ghas_one_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_power.algebra_map\u2080_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_power.ghas_mul", "description": "As a graded monoid, `\u2a02[R]^i M` has a `(*) : \u2a02[R]^i M \u2192 \u2a02[R]^j M \u2192 \u2a02[R]^(i + j) M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_power.cast_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_power", "description": " Homogenous tensor powers $M^{\\otimes n}$. `\u2a02[R]^n M` is a shorthand for\n`\u2a02[R] (i : fin n), M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_power.ghas_mul_eq_coe_linear_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_power.cast_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_power.gsemiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_tensor_product.graded_monoid_eq_of_reindex_cast", "description": " Two dependent pairs of tensor products are equal if their index is equal and the contents\nare equal after a canonical reindexing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_power.mul_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_power.tprod_mul_tprod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_power.gmonoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_power.cast_eq_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_power.galgebra_to_fun_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_power.cast_trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_power.algebra_map\u2080", "description": " The canonical map from `R` to `\u2a02[R]^0 M` corresponding to the algebra_map of the tensor\nalgebra.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_power.ghas_mul_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.eval_from_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.eval_from_of_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.eval_from", "description": "`M.eval_from s x` evaluates `M` with input `x` starting from the state `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.eval_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.mem_accepts", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.eval_from_append_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.eval_from_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.eval_append_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.accepts", "description": "`M.accepts` is the language of `x` such that `M.eval x` is an accept state.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.eval_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.eval_from_of_append", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.pumping_lemma", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA", "description": "A DFA is a set of states (`\u03c3`), a transition function from state to state labelled by the\n alphabet (`step`), a starting state (`start`) and a set of acceptance states (`accept`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.step", "description": "A DFA is a set of states (`\u03c3`), a transition function from state to state labelled by the\n alphabet (`step`), a starting state (`start`) and a set of acceptance states (`accept`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.start", "description": "A DFA is a set of states (`\u03c3`), a transition function from state to state labelled by the\n alphabet (`step`), a starting state (`start`) and a set of acceptance states (`accept`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.accept", "description": "A DFA is a set of states (`\u03c3`), a transition function from state to state labelled by the\n alphabet (`step`), a starting state (`start`) and a set of acceptance states (`accept`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.eval", "description": "`M.eval x` evaluates `M` with input `x` starting from the state `M.start`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.eval_from_split", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "part.to_unit_cont", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "part.fix_eq_\u03c9Sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "part.fix.approx_le_fix", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "part.fix_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "part.fix.approx_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.monotone_curry_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.monotone_uncurry_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lawful_fix.fix_eq'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "part.fix.le_f_of_mem_approx", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "part.fix.mem_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.pi.lawful_fix'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "part.to_unit_mono", "description": "`to_unit` as a monotone function", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.monotone_uncurry", "description": "`sigma.uncurry` as a monotone function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "part.to_unit_mono_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "part.fix.approx_mono'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "part.fix.approx_chain", "description": "The series of approximations of `fix f` (see `approx`) as a `chain`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "part.fix_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.has_fix", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "part.fix.approx_mem_approx_chain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.continuous_curry", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.monotone_curry", "description": "`sigma.curry` as a monotone function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.lawful_fix", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "part.lawful_fix", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.uncurry_curry_continuous", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.continuous_uncurry", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lawful_fix", "description": " Intuitively, a fixed point operator `fix` is lawful if it satisfies `fix f = f (fix f)` for all\n`f`, but this is inconsistent / uninteresting in most cases due to the existence of \"exotic\"\nfunctions `f`, such as the function that is defined iff its argument is not, familiar from the\nhalting problem. Instead, this requirement is limited to only functions that are `continuous` in the\nsense of `\u03c9`-complete partial orders, which excludes the example because it is not monotone\n(making the input argument less defined can make `f` more defined).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lawful_fix.to_has_fix", "description": " Intuitively, a fixed point operator `fix` is lawful if it satisfies `fix f = f (fix f)` for all\n`f`, but this is inconsistent / uninteresting in most cases due to the existence of \"exotic\"\nfunctions `f`, such as the function that is defined iff its argument is not, familiar from the\nhalting problem. Instead, this requirement is limited to only functions that are `continuous` in the\nsense of `\u03c9`-complete partial orders, which excludes the example because it is not monotone\n(making the input argument less defined can make `f` more defined).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lawful_fix.fix_eq", "description": " Intuitively, a fixed point operator `fix` is lawful if it satisfies `fix f = f (fix f)` for all\n`f`, but this is inconsistent / uninteresting in most cases due to the existence of \"exotic\"\nfunctions `f`, such as the function that is defined iff its argument is not, familiar from the\nhalting problem. Instead, this requirement is limited to only functions that are `continuous` in the\nsense of `\u03c9`-complete partial orders, which excludes the example because it is not monotone\n(making the input argument less defined can make `f` more defined).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "part.fix.exists_fix_le_approx", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.jacobson_eq_iff_jacobson_quotient_eq_bot", "description": " An ideal `I` of `R` is equal to its Jacobson radical if and only if\nthe Jacobson radical of the quotient ring `R/I` is the zero ideal", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_primary_of_is_maximal_radical", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.exists_mul_sub_mem_of_sub_one_mem_jacobson", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.le_jacobson", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.eq_jacobson_iff_not_mem", "description": " An ideal `I` equals its Jacobson radical if and only if every element outside `I`\nalso lies outside of a maximal ideal containing `I`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.jacobson_eq_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.jacobson_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_unit_of_sub_one_mem_jacobson_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.map_jacobson_of_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.jacobson_radical_eq_jacobson", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_radical_of_eq_jacobson", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.radical_eq_jacobson_iff_radical_quotient_eq_jacobson_bot", "description": " The standard radical and Jacobson radical of an ideal `I` of `R` are equal if and only if\nthe nilradical and Jacobson radical of the quotient ring `R/I` coincide", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_local", "description": "An ideal `I` is local iff its Jacobson radical is maximal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_local.out", "description": "An ideal `I` is local iff its Jacobson radical is maximal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.jacobson_bot_polynomial_of_jacobson_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_local_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.mem_jacobson_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.eq_jacobson_iff_Inf_maximal", "description": " An ideal equals its Jacobson radical iff it is the intersection of a set of maximal ideals.\nAllowing the set to include \u22a4 is equivalent, and is included only to simplify some proofs.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_local_of_is_maximal_radical", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.jacobson_eq_self_of_is_maximal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.jacobson_bot_polynomial_le_Inf_map_maximal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.jacobson_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.mem_jacobson_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.comap_jacobson_of_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_local.mem_jacobson_or_exists_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.jacobson.is_maximal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.comap_jacobson", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.jacobson_eq_top_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.jacobson_idem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.radical_le_jacobson", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.eq_jacobson_iff_Inf_maximal'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.jacobson", "description": "The Jacobson radical of `I` is the infimum of all maximal (left) ideals containing `I`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.map_jacobson_of_bijective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_local.le_jacobson", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "liouville_number.partial_sum", "description": "`liouville_number.partial_sum` is the sum of the first `k + 1` terms of Liouville's constant,\ni.e.\n$$\n\\sum_{i=0}^k\\frac{1}{m^{i!}}.\n$$", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "liouville_number.remainder_lt", "description": "  An upper estimate on the remainder. This estimate works with `m \u2208 \u211d` satisfying `2 \u2264 m` and is\nweaker than the estimate `liouville_number.remainder_lt'` above. However, this estimate is\nmore useful for the proof.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "transcendental_liouville_number", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "liouville_number.aux_calc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "liouville_number.partial_sum_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "liouville_number.remainder_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "liouville_liouville_number", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "liouville_number.remainder_lt'", "description": "  An upper estimate on the remainder. This estimate works with `m \u2208 \u211d` satisfying `1 < m` and is\nstronger than the estimate `liouville_number.remainder_lt` below. However, the latter estimate is\nmore useful for the proof.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "liouville_number.remainder", "description": "`liouville_number.remainder` is the sum of the series of the terms in `liouville_number m`\nstarting from `k+1`, i.e\n$$\n\\sum_{i=k+1}^\\infty\\frac{1}{m^{i!}}.\n$$", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "liouville_number.partial_sum_eq_rat", "description": "  The sum of the `k` initial terms of the Liouville number to base `m` is a ratio of natural\nnumbers where the denominator is `m ^ k!`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "liouville_number.remainder_summable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "liouville_number.summable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "liouville_number.partial_sum_add_remainder", "description": "Split the sum definining a Liouville number into the first `k` term and the rest.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "liouville_number", "description": "For a real number `m`, Liouville's constant is\n$$\n\\sum_{i=0}^\\infty\\frac{1}{m^{i!}}.\n$$\nThe series converges only for `1 < m`.  However, there is no restriction on `m`, since,\nif the series does not converge, then the sum of the series is defined to be zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.le_tsum_condensed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.summable_rpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.summable_one_div_rpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.tsum_condensed_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum_Ioo_inv_sq_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.le_sum_condensed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.not_summable_one_div_nat_cast", "description": "Harmonic series is not unconditionally summable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.summable_nat_pow_inv", "description": " Test for convergence of the `p`-series: the real-valued series `\u2211' n : \u2115, (n ^ p)\u207b\u00b9` converges\nif and only if `1 < p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.summable_one_div_int_pow", "description": "Summability of the `p`-series over `\u2124`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.summable_one_div_nat_rpow", "description": " Test for convergence of the `p`-series: the real-valued series `\u2211' n : \u2115, 1 / n ^ p` converges\nif and only if `1 < p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.le_sum_condensed'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_condensed_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.summable_abs_int_rpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.summable_nat_rpow_inv", "description": " Test for convergence of the `p`-series: the real-valued series `\u2211' n : \u2115, (n ^ p)\u207b\u00b9` converges\nif and only if `1 < p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.not_summable_nat_cast_inv", "description": "Harmonic series is not unconditionally summable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.summable_condensed_iff", "description": "Cauchy condensation test for a series of `nnreal` version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum_Ioc_inv_sq_le_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.summable_rpow_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "summable_condensed_iff_of_nonneg", "description": "Cauchy condensation test for series of nonnegative real numbers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.summable_nat_rpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.summable_one_div_nat_pow", "description": " Test for convergence of the `p`-series: the real-valued series `\u2211' n : \u2115, 1 / n ^ p` converges\nif and only if `1 < p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_condensed_le'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.tendsto_sum_range_one_div_nat_succ_at_top", "description": "**Divergence of the Harmonic Series**", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.has_fderiv_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.differentiable_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_bounded_linear_map.has_fderiv_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_bounded_linear_map.differentiable_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_bounded_linear_map.differentiable_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_bounded_linear_map.differentiable_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.differentiable_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.differentiable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_bounded_linear_map.fderiv_within", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.differentiable_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.has_strict_fderiv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.has_fderiv_at_filter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.has_fderiv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.fderiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.fderiv_within", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_bounded_linear_map.has_fderiv_at_filter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_bounded_linear_map.fderiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_bounded_linear_map.has_fderiv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_bounded_linear_map.differentiable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.card_finset_len", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.powerset_eq_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.card_finset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.univ_filter_card_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.card_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_powerset_len_univ_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.powerset_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.pi", "description": "`pi m t` constructs the Cartesian product over `t` indexed by `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.pi.cons_same", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.pi.cons_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.pi_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.pi.cons", "description": " Given `\u03b4 : \u03b1 \u2192 Type*`, a multiset `m` and a term `a`, as well as a term `b : \u03b4 a` and a\nfunction `f` such that `f a' : \u03b4 a'` for all `a'` in `m`, `pi.cons m a b f` is a function `g` such\nthat `g a'' : \u03b4 a''` for all `a''` in `a ::\u2098 m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.pi_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.pi.cons_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.pi.empty", "description": " Given `\u03b4 : \u03b1 \u2192 Type*`, `pi.empty \u03b4` is the trivial dependent function out of the empty\nmultiset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.pi.cons_eta", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.nodup.pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.mem_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.pi.cons_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.card_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "LDL.diag_eq_lower_inv_conj", "description": " Inverse statement of **LDL decomposition**: we can conjugate a positive definite matrix\nby some lower triangular matrix and get a diagonal matrix.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "LDL.lower_inv_eq_gram_schmidt_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "LDL.invertible_lower_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "LDL.lower_inv_orthogonal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "LDL.lower", "description": "The lower triangular matrix `L` of the LDL decomposition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "LDL.lower_inv_triangular", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "LDL.diag_entries", "description": "The entries of the diagonal matrix `D` of the LDL decomposition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "LDL.lower_inv", "description": " The inverse of the lower triangular matrix `L` of the LDL-decomposition. It is obtained by\napplying Gram-Schmidt-Orthogonalization w.r.t. the inner product induced by `S\u1d40` on the standard\nbasis vectors `pi.basis_fun`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "LDL.diag", "description": "The diagonal matrix `D` of the LDL decomposition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "LDL.lower_conj_diag", "description": " **LDL decomposition**: any positive definite matrix `S` can be\ndecomposed as `S = LDL\u1d34` where `L` is a lower-triangular matrix and `D` is a diagonal matrix.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.supp_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.fst_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.W.mk_dest", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.liftr_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.obj.iget", "description": " `x.iget i` takes the component of `x` designated by `i` if any is or returns\na default value", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.id_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.W.dest", "description": "destructor for W-types", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor", "description": "A polynomial functor `P` is given by a type `A` and a family `B` of types over `A`. `P` maps\nany type `\u03b1` to a new type `P.obj \u03b1`, which is defined as the sigma type `\u03a3 x, P.B x \u2192 \u03b1`.\n\nAn element of `P.obj \u03b1` is a pair `\u27e8a, f\u27e9`, where `a` is an element of a type `A` and\n`f : B a \u2192 \u03b1`. Think of `a` as the shape of the object and `f` as an index to the relevant\nelements of `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.A", "description": "A polynomial functor `P` is given by a type `A` and a family `B` of types over `A`. `P` maps\nany type `\u03b1` to a new type `P.obj \u03b1`, which is defined as the sigma type `\u03a3 x, P.B x \u2192 \u03b1`.\n\nAn element of `P.obj \u03b1` is a pair `\u27e8a, f\u27e9`, where `a` is an element of a type `A` and\n`f : B a \u2192 \u03b1`. Think of `a` as the shape of the object and `f` as an index to the relevant\nelements of `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.B", "description": "A polynomial functor `P` is given by a type `A` and a family `B` of types over `A`. `P` maps\nany type `\u03b1` to a new type `P.obj \u03b1`, which is defined as the sigma type `\u03a3 x, P.B x \u2192 \u03b1`.\n\nAn element of `P.obj \u03b1` is a pair `\u27e8a, f\u27e9`, where `a` is an element of a type `A` and\n`f : B a \u2192 \u03b1`. Think of `a` as the shape of the object and `f` as an index to the relevant\nelements of `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.W.head", "description": "root element  of a W tree", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.W.mk", "description": "constructor for W-types", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.map_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.obj.functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.comp.mk", "description": "constructor for composition", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.iget_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.comp.get", "description": "destructor for composition", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.Idx.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.W.children", "description": "children of the root of a W tree", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.comp", "description": "functor composition for polynomial functors", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.map", "description": "Applying `P` to a morphism of `Type`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.W.dest_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.comp_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.Idx", "description": " `Idx` identifies a location inside the application of a pfunctor.\nFor `F : pfunctor`, `x : F.obj \u03b1` and `i : F.Idx`, `i` can designate\none part of `x` or is invalid, if `i.1 \u2260 x.1`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.obj.is_lawful_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.obj.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.liftp_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.obj", "description": "Applying `P` to an object of `Type`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.liftp_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pfunctor.W", "description": " re-export existing definition of W-types and\nadapt it to a packaged definition of polynomial functor", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.functor_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete_equiv", "description": "`discrete \u03b1` is equivalent to the original type `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.eq_to_iso'", "description": "A variant of `eq_to_iso` that lifts terms to the discrete category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.equiv_of_equivalence_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.mk_as", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.opposite", "description": "A discrete category is equivalent to its opposite category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete_equiv_symm_apply_as", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.equivalence_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.nat_iso", "description": "For functors out of a discrete category,\na natural isomorphism is just a collection of isomorphisms,\nas the naturality squares are trivial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.comp_nat_iso_discrete", "description": "Composing `discrete.functor F` with another functor `G` amounts to composing `F` with `G.obj`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.opposite_functor_obj_as", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.equiv_of_equivalence_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.functor_map_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.eq_to_hom'", "description": "A variant of `eq_to_hom` that lifts terms to the discrete category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete_category", "description": "The \"discrete\" category on a type, whose morphisms are equalities.\n\nBecause we do not allow morphisms in `Prop` (only in `Type`),\nsomewhat annoyingly we have to define `X \u27f6 Y` as `ulift (plift (X = Y))`.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001A>", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.functor", "description": "Any function `I \u2192 C` gives a functor `discrete I \u2964 C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.eq_to_hom", "description": " Promote an equation between the wrapped terms in `X Y : discrete \u03b1` to a morphism `X \u27f6 Y`\nin the discrete category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.functor_comp_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.eq_of_hom", "description": "Extract the equation from a morphism in a discrete category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.eq_to_iso", "description": " Promote an equation between the wrapped terms in `X Y : discrete \u03b1` to an isomorphism `X \u2245 Y`\nin the discrete category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.functor_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.nat_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.functor_comp_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete", "description": "A wrapper for promoting any type to a category,\nwith the only morphisms being equalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.as", "description": "A wrapper for promoting any type to a category,\nwith the only morphisms being equalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.equivalence_unit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.nat_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.equivalence", "description": "We can promote a type-level `equiv` to\nan equivalence between the corresponding `discrete` categories.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.nat_iso_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.category_theory.is_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.nat_trans", "description": "For functors out of a discrete category,\na natural transformation is just a collection of maps,\nas the naturality squares are trivial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.nat_iso_functor", "description": "Every functor `F` from a discrete category is naturally isomorphic (actually, equal) to\n `discrete.functor (F.obj)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.opposite_inverse_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.equivalence_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.discrete_cases", "description": "A simple tactic to run `cases` on any `discrete \u03b1` hypotheses.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.functor_comp", "description": "The discrete functor induced by a composition of maps can be written as a\ncomposition of two discrete functors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.nat_trans_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.id_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.equiv_of_equivalence", "description": "We can convert an equivalence of `discrete` categories to a type-level `equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.discrete_cases", "description": "A simple tactic to run `cases` on any `discrete \u03b1` hypotheses.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.equivalence_counit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.Completion.lift_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.Completion_complete_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.Completion", "description": "The completion of a seminormed group, as an endofunctor on `SemiNormedGroup`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.Completion.map_hom", "description": " Given a normed group hom `V \u27f6 W`, this defines the associated morphism\nfrom the completion of `V` to the completion of `W`.\nThe difference from the definition obtained from the functoriality of completion is in that the\nmap sending a morphism `f` to the associated morphism of completions is itself additive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.Completion.map_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.Completion.incl_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.Completion.category_theory.functor.additive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.Completion.incl", "description": "The canonical morphism from a seminormed group `V` to its completion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.Completion.map_norm_noninc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.Completion.lift_comp_incl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.category_theory.preadditive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.Completion_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.Completion_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.Completion.lift", "description": " Given a normed group hom `f : V \u2192 W` with `W` complete, this provides a lift of `f` to\nthe completion of `V`. The lemmas `lift_unique` and `lift_comp_incl` provide the api for the\nuniversal property of the completion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "SemiNormedGroup.Completion.norm_incl_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.normalizer_eq_self_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.mem_normalizer_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.normalizer", "description": " Regarding a Lie subalgebra `H \u2286 L` as a module over itself, its normalizer is in fact a Lie\nsubalgebra.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.exists_nested_lie_ideal_of_le_normalizer", "description": " A Lie subalgebra `H` is an ideal of any Lie subalgebra `K` containing `H` and contained in the\nnormalizer of `H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.top_lie_le_iff_le_normalizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.ideal_in_normalizer", "description": "A Lie subalgebra is an ideal of its normalizer.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.normalizer_bot_eq_max_triv_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.normalizer", "description": "The normalizer of a Lie submodule.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.monotone_normalizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.le_normalizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.coe_normalizer_eq_normalizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.lie_mem_sup_of_mem_normalizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.mem_normalizer_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.mem_normalizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.le_normalizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.normalizer_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.comap_normalizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.gc_top_lie_normalizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bnot", "description": "Boolean NOT", "p": "core"}, {"name": "bxor", "description": "Boolean XOR", "p": "core"}, {"name": "bor", "description": "Boolean OR", "p": "core"}, {"name": "band", "description": "Boolean AND", "p": "core"}, {"name": "cond", "description": "`cond b x y` is `x` if `b = tt` and `y` otherwise.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "isometry.hausdorff_measure_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lipschitz_on_with.hausdorff_measure_image_le", "description": "If `f : X \u2192 Y` is `K`-Lipschitz on `s`, then `\u03bcH[d] (f '' s) \u2264 K ^ d * \u03bcH[d] s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.isometry_equiv_comap_mk_metric", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hausdorff_measure_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hausdorff_measure_measure_preserving_pi_fin_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hausdorff_measure_smul_right_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.mk_metric_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hausdorff_measure_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.mk_metric'_to_outer_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.mk_metric_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hausdorff_measure_segment", "description": "The measure of a segment is the distance between its endpoints.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.is_metric.le_caratheodory", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hausdorff_measure_homothety_image", "description": "Scaling by `c` around `x` scales the measure by `\u2016c\u2016\u208a ^ d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.mk_metric", "description": " Given a function `m : \u211d\u22650\u221e \u2192 \u211d\u22650\u221e` and `r > 0`, let `\u03bc r` be the maximal outer measure such that\n`\u03bc s \u2264 m (emetric.diam s)` whenever `emetric.diam s < r`. Then\n`mk_metric m = \u2a06 r > 0, \u03bc r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.trim_mk_metric", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.mk_metric'.eq_supr_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.mk_metric_to_outer_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.hausdorff_measure.measure.is_mul_right_invariant", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.isometry_map_mk_metric", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lipschitz_with.hausdorff_measure_image_le", "description": " If `f` is a `K`-Lipschitz map, then it increases the Hausdorff `d`-measures of sets at most\nby the factor of `K ^ d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.hausdorff_measure_le_liminf_sum", "description": " To bound the Hausdorff measure of a set, one may use coverings with maximum diameter tending\nto `0`, indexed by any sequence of finite types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antilipschitz_with.hausdorff_measure_preimage_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hausdorff_measure_pi_real", "description": "In the space `\u03b9 \u2192 \u211d`, the Hausdorff measure coincides exactly with the Lebesgue measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.mk_metric'.trim_pre", "description": " `measure_theory.outer_measure.mk_metric'.pre m r` is a trimmed measure provided that\n`m (closure s) = m s` for any set `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antilipschitz_with.le_hausdorff_measure_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.hausdorff_measure.measure.is_mul_left_invariant", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.le_mk_metric", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "isometry_equiv.measure_preserving_hausdorff_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.hausdorff_measure.measure.is_add_left_invariant", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hausdorff_measure_affine_segment", "description": "The measure of a segment is the distance between its endpoints.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.mk_metric_mono", "description": " If `m\u2081 d \u2264 m\u2082 d` for `d < \u03b5` for some `\u03b5 > 0` (we use `\u2264\u1da0[\ud835\udcdd[\u2265] 0]` to state this), then\n`mk_metric m\u2081 hm\u2081 \u2264 mk_metric m\u2082 hm\u2082`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.hausdorff_measure_zero_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.mk_metric_mono", "description": " If `m\u2081 d \u2264 m\u2082 d` for `d < \u03b5` for some `\u03b5 > 0` (we use `\u2264\u1da0[\ud835\udcdd[\u2265] 0]` to state this), then\n`mk_metric m\u2081 hm\u2081 \u2264 mk_metric m\u2082 hm\u2082`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.mk_metric", "description": " Given a function `m : \u211d\u22650\u221e \u2192 \u211d\u22650\u221e`, `mk_metric m` is the supremum of `\u03bc r` over `r > 0`, where\n`\u03bc r` is the maximal outer measure `\u03bc` such that `\u03bc s \u2264 m s` for all sets `s` that contain at least\ntwo points. While each `mk_metric'.pre` is an *outer* measure, the supremum is a measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "isometry_equiv.map_hausdorff_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.hausdorff_measure_apply", "description": "A formula for `\u03bcH[d] s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.mk_metric_nnreal_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.is_metric.borel_le_caratheodory", "description": " Caratheodory theorem. If `m` is a metric outer measure, then every Borel measurable set `t` is\nCaratheodory measurable: for any (not necessarily measurable) set `s` we have\n`\u03bc (s \u2229 t) + \u03bc (s \\ t) = \u03bc s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with.hausdorff_measure_image_le", "description": " If `f : X \u2192 Y` is H\u00f6lder continuous on `s` with a positive exponent `r`, then\n`\u03bcH[d] (f '' s) \u2264 C ^ d * \u03bcH[r * d] s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.isometry_comap_mk_metric", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.mk_metric'.tendsto_pre", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.mk_metric'", "description": " Given a function `m : set X \u2192 \u211d\u22650\u221e`, `mk_metric' m` is the supremum of `mk_metric'.pre m r`\nover `r > 0`. Equivalently, it is the limit of `mk_metric'.pre m r` as `r` tends to zero from\nthe right.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.mk_metric_mono_smul", "description": " If `c \u2209 {0, \u221e}` and `m\u2081 d \u2264 c * m\u2082 d` for `d < \u03b5` for some `\u03b5 > 0`\n(we use `\u2264\u1da0[\ud835\udcdd[\u2265] 0]` to state this), then `mk_metric m\u2081 hm\u2081 \u2264 c \u2022 mk_metric m\u2082 hm\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.is_metric", "description": " We say that an outer measure `\u03bc` in an (e)metric space is *metric* if `\u03bc (s \u222a t) = \u03bc s + \u03bc t`\nfor any two metric separated sets `s`, `t`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.mk_metric_le_liminf_tsum", "description": " To bound the Hausdorff measure (or, more generally, for a measure defined using\n`measure_theory.measure.mk_metric`) of a set, one may use coverings with maximum diameter tending to\n`0`, indexed by any sequence of countable types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.mk_metric'.mono_pre_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.mk_metric'.le_pre", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hausdorff_measure_homothety_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.hausdorff_measure_zero_or_top", "description": "If `d\u2081 < d\u2082`, then for any set `s` we have either `\u03bcH[d\u2082] s = 0`, or `\u03bcH[d\u2081] s = \u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.mk_metric_apply", "description": "A formula for `measure_theory.measure.mk_metric`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "isometry_equiv.hausdorff_measure_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.hausdorff_measure_mono", "description": "Hausdorff measure `\u03bcH[d] s` is monotone in `d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hausdorff_measure_line_map_image", "description": " Mapping a set of reals along a line segment scales the measure by the length of a segment.\n\nThis is an auxiliary result used to prove `hausdorff_measure_affine_segment`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.hausdorff_measure_smul\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.hausdorff_measure_le_liminf_tsum", "description": " To bound the Hausdorff measure of a set, one may use coverings with maximum diameter tending\nto `0`, indexed by any sequence of countable types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.le_mk_metric", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.mk_metric'.pre", "description": " Auxiliary definition for `outer_measure.mk_metric'`: given a function on sets\n`m : set X \u2192 \u211d\u22650\u221e`, returns the maximal outer measure `\u03bc` such that `\u03bc s \u2264 m s`\nfor any set `s` of diameter at most `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.mk_metric'.pre_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.hausdorff_measure_le_one_of_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hausdorff_measure_measure_preserving_fun_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.mk_metric'_is_metric", "description": "An outer measure constructed using `outer_measure.mk_metric'` is a metric outer measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.mk_metric_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.le_hausdorff_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.hausdorff_measure", "description": "Hausdorff measure on an (e)metric space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hausdorff_measure_prod_real", "description": "In the space `\u211d \u00d7 \u211d`, the Hausdorff measure coincides exactly with the Lebesgue measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.mk_metric_mono_smul", "description": " If `c \u2209 {0, \u221e}` and `m\u2081 d \u2264 c * m\u2082 d` for `d < \u03b5` for some `\u03b5 > 0`\n(we use `\u2264\u1da0[\ud835\udcdd[\u2265] 0]` to state this), then `mk_metric m\u2081 hm\u2081 \u2264 c \u2022 mk_metric m\u2082 hm\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.is_metric.finset_Union_of_pairwise_separated", "description": "A metric outer measure is additive on a finite set of pairwise metric separated sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.mk_metric_le_liminf_sum", "description": " To bound the Hausdorff measure (or, more generally, for a measure defined using\n`measure_theory.measure.mk_metric`) of a set, one may use coverings with maximum diameter tending to\n`0`, indexed by any sequence of finite types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.no_atoms_hausdorff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.one_le_hausdorff_measure_zero_of_nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "isometry.map_hausdorff_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.coe_mk_metric", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hausdorff_measure_real", "description": "In the space `\u211d`, the Hausdorff measure coincides exactly with the Lebesgue measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.mk_metric'.mono_pre", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "isometry_equiv.hausdorff_measure_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.hausdorff_measure.measure.is_add_right_invariant", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.isometry_equiv_map_mk_metric", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.mk_metric'", "description": " Given a function `m : set X \u2192 \u211d\u22650\u221e`, `mk_metric' m` is the supremum of `\u03bc r`\nover `r > 0`, where `\u03bc r` is the maximal outer measure `\u03bc` such that `\u03bc s \u2264 m s`\nfor all `s`. While each `\u03bc r` is an *outer* measure, the supremum is a measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "isometry.hausdorff_measure_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.outer_measure.mk_metric'.tendsto_pre_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_null_frontier_thickening", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_lintegral_thickened_indicator_of_is_closed", "description": " The integrals of thickened indicators of a closed set against a finite measure tend to the\nmeasure of the closed set if the thickening radii tend to zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.probability_measure.tendsto_measure_of_null_frontier_of_tendsto", "description": " One implication of the portmanteau theorem:\nWeak convergence of probability measures implies that if the boundary of a Borel set\ncarries no probability mass under the limit measure, then the limit of the measures of the set\nequals the measure of the set under the limit probability measure.\n\nA version with coercions to ordinary `\u211d\u22650\u221e`-valued measures is\n`measure_theory.probability_measure.tendsto_measure_of_null_frontier_of_tendsto'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_of_cont_bdd_of_tendsto_indicator", "description": " If a sequence of bounded continuous functions tends to the indicator of a measurable set and\nthe functions are uniformly bounded, then their integrals against a finite measure tend to the\nmeasure of the set.\n\nA similar result with more general assumptions is\n`measure_theory.measure_of_cont_bdd_of_tendsto_filter_indicator`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.probability_measure.limsup_measure_closed_le_of_tendsto", "description": " One implication of the portmanteau theorem:\nWeak convergence of probability measures implies that the limsup of the measures of any closed\nset is at most the measure of the closed set under the limit probability measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.le_measure_compl_liminf_of_limsup_measure_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.limsup_measure_closed_le_iff_liminf_measure_open_ge", "description": " One pair of implications of the portmanteau theorem:\nFor a sequence of Borel probability measures, the following two are equivalent:\n\n(C) The limsup of the measures of any closed set is at most the measure of the closed set\nunder a candidate limit measure.\n\n(O) The liminf of the measures of any open set is at least the measure of the open set\nunder a candidate limit measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.limsup_measure_le_of_le_liminf_measure_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_of_cont_bdd_of_tendsto_filter_indicator", "description": " If bounded continuous functions tend to the indicator of a measurable set and are\nuniformly bounded, then their integrals against a finite measure tend to the measure of the set.\nThis formulation assumes:\n * the functions tend to a limit along a countably generated filter;\n * the limit is in the almost everywhere sense;\n * boundedness holds almost everywhere.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_measure_of_null_frontier", "description": " One implication of the portmanteau theorem:\nFor a sequence of Borel probability measures, if the liminf of the measures of any open set is at\nleast the measure of the open set under a candidate limit measure, then for any set whose\nboundary carries no probability mass under the candidate limit measure, then its measures under the\nsequence converge to its measure under the candidate limit measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.probability_measure.tendsto_measure_of_null_frontier_of_tendsto'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.finite_measure.limsup_measure_closed_le_of_tendsto", "description": " One implication of the portmanteau theorem:\nWeak convergence of finite measures implies that the limsup of the measures of any closed set is\nat most the measure of the closed set under the limit measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.le_measure_liminf_of_limsup_measure_compl_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_null_frontiers_thickening", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.probability_measure.le_liminf_measure_open_of_tendsto", "description": " One implication of the portmanteau theorem:\nWeak convergence of probability measures implies that the liminf of the measures of any open set\nis at least the measure of the open set under the limit probability measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.tendsto_measure_of_le_liminf_measure_of_limsup_measure_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.limsup_measure_compl_le_of_le_liminf_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.ker_eq_bot_of_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.epi_iff_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.forget_preserves_epimorphisms", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.unique_of_epi_zero", "description": "If the zero morphism is an epi then the codomain is trivial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.mono_iff_ker_eq_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.forget_preserves_monomorphisms", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.epi_iff_range_eq_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.mono_as_hom'_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.epi_as_hom''_mkq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.range_eq_top_of_epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.mono_iff_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2081\u0393\u2080", "description": "The natural isomorphism `\u0393\u2080 \u22d9 N\u2081 \u2245 to_karoubi (chain_complex C \u2115)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2082\u0393\u2082_inv_app_f_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2082\u0393\u2082", "description": "The counit isomorphism of the Dold-Kan equivalence for additive categories.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2082\u0393\u2082_compatible_with_N\u2081\u0393\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2081\u0393\u2080_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2081\u0393\u2080_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.\u0393\u2080_nondeg_complex_iso_inv_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2081\u0393\u2080_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2082\u0393\u2082_to_karoubi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2082\u0393\u2082_to_karoubi_iso", "description": " Compatibility isomorphism between `to_karoubi _ \u22d9 \u0393\u2082 \u22d9 N\u2082` and `\u0393\u2080 \u22d9 N\u2081` which\nare functors `chain_complex C \u2115 \u2964 karoubi (chain_complex C \u2115)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2081\u0393\u2080_inv_app_f_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2082\u0393\u2082_to_karoubi_iso_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2081\u0393\u2080_hom_app_f_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.\u0393\u2080_nondeg_complex_iso_hom_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.\u0393\u2080'_comp_nondeg_complex_functor", "description": "The natural isomorphism `(\u0393\u2080.splitting K).nondeg_complex \u2245 K` for `K : chain_complex C \u2115`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2082\u0393\u2082_to_karoubi_iso_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.\u0393\u2080_nondeg_complex_iso", "description": "The isomorphism  `(\u0393\u2080.splitting K).nondeg_complex \u2245 K` for all `K : chain_complex C \u2115`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_vec_cramer", "description": " A stronger form of **Cramer's rule** that allows us to solve some instances of `A \u2b1d x = b` even\nif the determinant is not a unit. A sufficient (but still not necessary) condition is that `A.det`\ndivides `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_adjugate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_submatrix_equiv_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cramer_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cramer_map_is_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cramer_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cramer_reindex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_mul_distrib", "description": "Proof follows from \"The trace Cayley-Hamilton theorem\" by Darij Grinberg, Section 5.3", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_conj_transpose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_fin_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.sum_cramer_apply", "description": "Use linearity of `cramer` and vector evaluation to take `cramer A _ i` out of a summation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_fin_succ_eq_det_submatrix", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_adjugate_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_reindex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate", "description": "The adjugate matrix is the transpose of the cofactor matrix.\n\n Typically, the cofactor matrix is defined by taking minors,\n i.e. the determinant of the matrix with a row and column removed.\n However, the proof of `mul_adjugate` becomes a lot easier if we use the\n matrix replacing a column with a basis vector, since it allows us to use\n facts about the `cramer` map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_adjugate'", "description": "A weaker version of `matrix.adjugate_adjugate` that uses `nontrivial`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_adjugate", "description": "Note that this is not true for `fintype.card n = 1` since `1 - 2 = 0` and not `-1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_mul_distrib_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_fin_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_eq_sum_mul_adjugate_col", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cramer_eq_adjugate_mul_vec", "description": " Since the map `b \u21a6 cramer A b` is linear in `b`, it must be multiplication by some matrix. This\nmatrix is `A.adjugate`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "alg_hom.map_adjugate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cramer_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_fin_two_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.map_adjugate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cramer_is_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_eq_one_of_card_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_regular_of_is_left_regular_det", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_fin_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cramer_transpose_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cramer", "description": "`cramer A b i` is the determinant of the matrix `A` with column `i` replaced with `b`,\nand thus `cramer A b` is the vector output by Cramer's rule on `A` and `b`.\n\nIf `A \u2b1d x = b` has a unique solution in `x`, `cramer A` sends the vector `b` to `A.det \u2022 x`.\nOtherwise, the outcome of `cramer` is well-defined but not necessarily useful.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cramer_submatrix_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cramer_transpose_row_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cramer_map", "description": "`cramer_map A b i` is the determinant of the matrix `A` with column `i` replaced with `b`,\nand thus `cramer_map A b` is the vector output by Cramer's rule on `A` and `b`.\n\nIf `A \u2b1d x = b` has a unique solution in `x`, `cramer_map A` sends the vector `b` to `A.det \u2022 x`.\nOtherwise, the outcome of `cramer_map` is well-defined but not necessarily useful.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_smul_adjugate_adjugate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_diagonal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cramer_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_eq_sum_mul_adjugate_row", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_transpose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.sum_cramer", "description": "Use linearity of `cramer` to take it out of a summation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cramer_row_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.cramer_subsingleton_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.adjugate_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_adjugate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_measurable.apply_continuous_linear_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.apply_continuous_linear_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.borel_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.measurable_apply'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_measurable_smul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.measurable_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.measurable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.measurable_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_smul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.measurable_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.measurable_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.using_new_ref", "description": "Create a new reference `r` with initial value `a`, execute `t r`, and then delete `r`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.read_ref", "description": "Read the value stored in the given reference.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.ref", "description": "A `ref` performs the role of a mutable variable within a tactic.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.write_ref", "description": "Update the value stored in the given reference.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_splitting_field", "description": "Typeclass characterising splitting fields.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_splitting_field.splits", "description": "Typeclass characterising splitting fields.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_splitting_field.adjoin_root_set", "description": "Typeclass characterising splitting fields.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_splitting_field.finite_dimensional", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_splitting_field.of_alg_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_splitting_field.splits_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "intermediate_field.splits_of_splits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_splitting_field.map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_splitting_field.lift", "description": "Splitting field of `f` embeds into any field that splits `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_splitting_field.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_frobenius_number", "description": " A natural number `n` is the **Frobenius number** of a set of natural numbers `s` if it is an\nupper bound on the complement of the additive submonoid generated by `s`.\nIn other words, it is the largest number that can not be expressed as a sum of numbers in `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_frobenius_number_pair", "description": "The **Chicken Mcnugget theorem** stating that the Frobenius number\n of positive numbers `m` and `n` is `m * n - m - n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "old_conv.congr_rule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "forall_eq_elim", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "old_conv.head_beta", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "old_conv.applyc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "old_conv.propext'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "binder_eq_elim.push", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "old_conv.congr_arg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "old_conv.apply'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "forall_elim_eq_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "old_conv.has_monad_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "binder_eq_elim.check_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "old_conv.monad_fail", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "forall_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "binder_eq_elim.pull", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "binder_eq_elim.old_conv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "old_conv.apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_elim_eq_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "binder_eq_elim.check", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "old_conv.current_relation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "supr_eq_elim", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "infi_eq_elim", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_eq_elim", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "old_conv.has_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "binder_eq_elim", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "binder_eq_elim.match_binder", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "binder_eq_elim.adapt_rel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "binder_eq_elim.apply_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "binder_eq_elim.apply_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "binder_eq_elim.apply_elim_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "old_conv.funext'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_elim_eq_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "old_conv.congr_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "forall_elim_eq_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "old_conv.congr_binder", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compact_t2.projective", "description": "  The assertion `compact_t2.projective` states that given continuous maps\n`f : X \u2192 Z` and `g : Y \u2192 Z` with `g` surjective between `t_2`, compact topological spaces,\nthere exists a continuous lift `h : X \u2192 Y`, such that `f = g \u2218 h`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stone_cech.projective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compact_t2.projective.extremally_disconnected", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "extremally_disconnected", "description": " An extremally disconnected topological space is a space\nin which the closure of every open set is open.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "extremally_disconnected.open_closure", "description": " An extremally disconnected topological space is a space\nin which the closure of every open set is open.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.path_star_bijective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.symmetrify_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.is_covering.symmetrify", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.star_equiv_costar_symm_apply_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.costar", "description": "A prefunctor induces a map of `quiver.costar` at every vertex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.is_covering.of_comp_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.star.mk", "description": "Constructor for `quiver.star`. Defined to be `sigma.mk`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.is_covering", "description": "A prefunctor is a covering of quivers if it defines bijections on all stars and costars.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.is_covering.star_bijective", "description": "A prefunctor is a covering of quivers if it defines bijections on all stars and costars.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.is_covering.costar_bijective", "description": "A prefunctor is a covering of quivers if it defines bijections on all stars and costars.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.is_covering.of_comp_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.is_covering.map_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.path_star.mk", "description": "Constructor for `quiver.path_star`. Defined to be `sigma.mk`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.star_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.path_star_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.costar_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.star_equiv_costar_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.is_covering_of_bijective_costar", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.path_star_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.star_equiv_costar_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.star_equiv_costar_symm_apply_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.star", "description": "A prefunctor induces a map of `quiver.star` at every vertex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.costar", "description": " The `quiver.costar` at a vertex is the collection of arrows whose target is the vertex.\nThe type `quiver.costar v` is defined to be `\u03a3 (u : U), (u \u27f6 v)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.costar_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.symmetrify_costar", "description": " The costar of the symmetrification of a quiver at a vertex `u` is equivalent to the sum of the\ncostar and the star at `u` in the original quiver.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.star_equiv_costar", "description": " In a quiver with involutive inverses, the star and costar at every vertex are equivalent.\nThis map is induced by `quiver.reverse`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.costar_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.path_star_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.star_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.path_star", "description": "A prefunctor induces a map of path stars.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.costar.mk", "description": "Constructor for `quiver.costar`. Defined to be `sigma.mk`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.is_covering_of_bijective_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.star_equiv_costar_apply_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.star_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.is_covering.comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.symmetrify_star", "description": " The star of the symmetrification of a quiver at a vertex `u` is equivalent to the sum of the\nstar and the costar at `u` in the original quiver.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.costar_conj_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.symmetrify_costar", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.costar_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.bijective_costar_iff_bijective_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.star_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.star_equiv_costar_apply_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.path_star", "description": " The path star at a vertex `u` is the type of all paths starting at `u`.\nThe type `quiver.path_star u` is defined to be `\u03a3 v : U, path u v`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.is_covering.path_star_bijective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.star", "description": " The `quiver.star` at a vertex is the collection of arrows whose source is the vertex.\nThe type `quiver.star u` is defined to be `\u03a3 (v : U), (u \u27f6 v)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_eq_zero_iff", "description": "The value of the quadratic character on `a` is zero iff `a = 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_eq_pow_of_char_ne_two", "description": " If `ring_char F` is odd, then `quadratic_char F a` can be computed in\nterms of `a ^ (fintype.card F / 2)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_sq_one'", "description": "The quadratic character takes the value `1` on nonzero squares.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_eq_pow_of_char_ne_two'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_sum_zero", "description": "The sum over the values of the quadratic character is zero when the characteristic is odd.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_is_nontrivial", "description": " The quadratic character is nontrivial as a multiplicative character\nwhen the domain has odd characteristic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_sq_one", "description": "The square of the quadratic character on nonzero arguments is `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_fun_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char", "description": "The quadratic character as a multiplicative character.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_is_quadratic", "description": "The quadratic character is quadratic as a multiplicative character.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_exists_neg_one", "description": "If `F` has odd characteristic, then `quadratic_char F` takes the value `-1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_dichotomy", "description": "The quadratic character is `1` or `-1` on nonzero arguments.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_fun_mul", "description": "The quadratic character is multiplicative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_eq_one_of_char_two", "description": "If `ring_char F = 2`, then `quadratic_char F` takes the value `1` on nonzero elements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_fun", "description": " Define the quadratic character with values in \u2124 on a monoid with zero `\u03b1`.\nIt takes the value zero at zero; for non-zero argument `a : \u03b1`, it is `1`\nif `a` is a square, otherwise it is `-1`.\n\nThis only deserves the name \"character\" when it is multiplicative,\ne.g., when `\u03b1` is a finite field. See `quadratic_char_fun_mul`.\n\nWe will later define `quadratic_char` to be a multiplicative character\nof type `mul_char F \u2124`, when the domain is a finite field `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_fun_eq_one_of_char_two", "description": "If `ring_char F = 2`, then `quadratic_char_fun F` takes the value `1` on nonzero elements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_field.is_square_neg_one_iff", "description": "`-1` is a square in `F` iff `#F` is not congruent to `3` mod `4`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_fun_eq_zero_iff", "description": "Some basic API lemmas", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_neg_one_iff_not_is_square", "description": "For `a : F`, `quadratic_char F a = -1 \u2194 \u00ac is_square a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_fun_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_fun_eq_pow_of_char_ne_two", "description": " If `ring_char F` is odd, then `quadratic_char_fun F a` can be computed in\nterms of `a ^ (fintype.card F / 2)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_neg_one", "description": "The value of the quadratic character at `-1`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_one_iff_is_square", "description": "For nonzero `a : F`, `quadratic_char F a = 1 \u2194 is_square a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_eq_neg_one_iff_not_one", "description": "The quadratic character is `1` or `-1` on nonzero arguments.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_char_card_sqrts", "description": "The number of solutions to `x^2 = a` is determined by the quadratic character.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.of_localization_finite_span_target", "description": " A property `P` of ring homs satisfies `ring_hom.of_localization_finite_span_target`\nif `P` holds for `R \u2192+* S` whenever there exists a finite set `{ r }` that spans `S` such that\n`P` holds for `R \u2192+* S\u1d63`.\n\nNote that this is equivalent to `ring_hom.of_localization_span_target` via\n`ring_hom.of_localization_span_target_iff_finite`, but this is easier to prove.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "localization_preserves_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_zero_of_localization", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_type_of_localization_span", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "localization_finite", "description": "If `S` is a finite `R`-algebra, then `S' = M\u207b\u00b9S` is a finite `R' = M\u207b\u00b9R`-algebra.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_reduced_of_localization_maximal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.property_is_local.of_localization_span", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "surjective_of_localization_span", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.of_localization_span_target_iff_finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.exists_smul_mem_of_mem_adjoin", "description": "Let `S` be an `R`-algebra, `M` a submonoid of `S`, `S' = M\u207b\u00b9S`.\nSuppose the image of some `x : S` falls in the adjoin of some finite `s \u2286 S'` over `R`,\nand `A` is an `R`-subalgebra of `S` containing both `M` and the numerators of `s`.\nThen, there exists some `m : M` such that `m \u2022 x` falls in `A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiple_mem_adjoin_of_mem_localization_adjoin", "description": " If `S` is an `R' = M\u207b\u00b9R` algebra, and `x \u2208 adjoin R' s`,\nthen `t \u2022 x \u2208 adjoin R s` for some `t : M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.localization_preserves.away", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.of_localization_span", "description": " A property `P` of ring homs satisfies `ring_hom.of_localization_finite_span`\nif `P` holds for `R \u2192+* S` whenever there exists a set `{ r }` that spans `R` such that\n`P` holds for `R\u1d63 \u2192+* S\u1d63`.\n\nNote that this is equivalent to `ring_hom.of_localization_finite_span` via\n`ring_hom.of_localization_span_iff_finite`, but this has less restrictions when applying.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.smul_mem_finset_integer_multiple_span", "description": "Let `S` be an `R`-algebra, `M` an submonoid of `R`, and `S' = M\u207b\u00b9S`.\nIf the image of some `x : S` falls in the span of some finite `s \u2286 S'` over `R`,\nthen there exists some `m : M` such that `m \u2022 x` falls in the\nspan of `finset_integer_multiple _ s` over `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal_eq_bot_of_localization", "description": "An ideal is trivial if its localization at every maximal ideal is trivial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.le_of_localization_maximal", "description": " Let `I J : ideal R`. If the localization of `I` at each maximal ideal `P` is included in\nthe localization of `J` at `P`, then `I \u2264 J`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.property_is_local", "description": " A property of ring homs is local if it is preserved by localizations and compositions, and for\neach `{ r }` that spans `S`, we have `P (R \u2192+* S) \u2194 \u2200 r, P (R \u2192+* S\u1d63)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.property_is_local.localization_preserves", "description": " A property of ring homs is local if it is preserved by localizations and compositions, and for\neach `{ r }` that spans `S`, we have `P (R \u2192+* S) \u2194 \u2200 r, P (R \u2192+* S\u1d63)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.property_is_local.of_localization_span_target", "description": " A property of ring homs is local if it is preserved by localizations and compositions, and for\neach `{ r }` that spans `S`, we have `P (R \u2192+* S) \u2194 \u2200 r, P (R \u2192+* S\u1d63)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.property_is_local.stable_under_composition", "description": " A property of ring homs is local if it is preserved by localizations and compositions, and for\neach `{ r }` that spans `S`, we have `P (R \u2192+* S) \u2194 \u2200 r, P (R \u2192+* S\u1d63)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.property_is_local.holds_for_localization_away", "description": " A property of ring homs is local if it is preserved by localizations and compositions, and for\neach `{ r }` that spans `S`, we have `P (R \u2192+* S) \u2194 \u2200 r, P (R \u2192+* S\u1d63)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "of_localization_maximal", "description": "A property `P` of comm rings satisfies `of_localization_maximal` if\n if `P` holds for `R` whenever `P` holds for `R\u2098` for all maximal ideal `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "localization_away_map_finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.of_localization_span_target", "description": " A property `P` of ring homs satisfies `ring_hom.of_localization_span_target`\nif `P` holds for `R \u2192+* S` whenever there exists a set `{ r }` that spans `S` such that\n`P` holds for `R \u2192+* S\u1d63`.\n\nNote that this is equivalent to `ring_hom.of_localization_finite_span_target` via\n`ring_hom.of_localization_span_target_iff_finite`, but this has less restrictions when applying.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.localization_preserves", "description": "A property `P` of ring homs is said to be preserved by localization\nif `P` holds for `M\u207b\u00b9R \u2192+* M\u207b\u00b9S` whenever `P` holds for `R \u2192+* S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "localization_away_map_finite_type", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.property_is_local.respects_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal_eq_bot_of_localization'", "description": "An ideal is trivial if its localization at every maximal ideal is trivial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.eq_of_localization_maximal", "description": " Let `I J : ideal R`. If the localization of `I` at each maximal ideal `P` is equal to\nthe localization of `J` at `P`, then `I = J`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "localization_finite_type", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.lift_mem_adjoin_finset_integer_multiple", "description": "Let `S` be an `R`-algebra, `M` an submonoid of `R`, and `S' = M\u207b\u00b9S`.\nIf the image of some `x : S` falls in the adjoin of some finite `s \u2286 S'` over `R`,\nthen there exists some `m : M` such that `m \u2022 x` falls in the\nadjoin of `finset_integer_multiple _ s` over `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.of_localization_prime", "description": "A property `P` of ring homs satisfies `of_localization_prime` if\n if `P` holds for `R` whenever `P` holds for `R\u2098` for all prime ideals `p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_of_localization_span", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.of_localization_span_iff_finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.holds_for_localization_away", "description": "A property `P` of ring homs satisfies `ring_hom.holds_for_localization_away`\nif `P` holds for each localization map `R \u2192+* R\u1d63`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "localization.is_reduced", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "localization_is_reduced", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.of_localization_finite_span", "description": " A property `P` of ring homs satisfies `ring_hom.of_localization_finite_span`\nif `P` holds for `R \u2192+* S` whenever there exists a finite set `{ r }` that spans `R` such that\n`P` holds for `R\u1d63 \u2192+* S\u1d63`.\n\nNote that this is equivalent to `ring_hom.of_localization_span` via\n`ring_hom.of_localization_span_iff_finite`, but this is easier to prove.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "localization_preserves", "description": "A property `P` of comm rings is said to be preserved by localization\n if `P` holds for `M\u207b\u00b9R` whenever `P` holds for `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiple_mem_span_of_mem_localization_span", "description": " If `S` is an `R' = M\u207b\u00b9R` algebra, and `x \u2208 span R' s`,\nthen `t \u2022 x \u2208 span R s` for some `t : M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.convex_independent_iff_set", "description": "The range of an injective indexed family of points is convex independent iff that family is.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.convex_independent_extreme_points", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsingleton.convex_independent", "description": "A family with at most one point is convex independent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_independent_iff_not_mem_convex_hull_diff", "description": " If a family is convex independent, a point in the family is not in the convex hull of the other\npoints. See `convex_independent_set_iff_not_mem_convex_hull_diff` for the `set` version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_independent_set_iff_inter_convex_hull_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_independent.subtype", "description": "If a family is convex independent, so is any subfamily indexed by a subtype of the index type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_independent_iff_finset", "description": "To check convex independence, one only has to check finsets thanks to Carath\u00e9odory's theorem.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_independent.comp_embedding", "description": " If a family is convex independent, so is any subfamily given by composition of an embedding into\nindex type with the original family.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_independent", "description": " An indexed family is said to be convex independent if every point only belongs to convex hulls\nof sets containing it.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_independent.mem_convex_hull_iff", "description": " If a family is convex independent, a point in the family is in the convex hull of some of the\npoints given by a subset of the index type if and only if the point's index is in this subset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_independent_set_iff_not_mem_convex_hull_diff", "description": " If a set is convex independent, a point in the set is not in the convex hull of the other\npoints. See `convex_independent_iff_not_mem_convex_hull_diff` for the indexed family version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_independent.injective", "description": "A convex independent family is injective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_independent.mono", "description": "A subset of a convex independent set of points is convex independent as well.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_independent.range", "description": "If an indexed family of points is convex independent, so is the corresponding set of points.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with.edist_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_with.ediam_image_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with.ediam_image_le_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_with.uniform_continuous", "description": "A H\u00f6lder continuous function is uniformly continuous", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_with.comp_holder_on_with", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with.ediam_image_le_of_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with.comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_with.dist_le_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.subsingleton.holder_on_with", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with.ediam_image_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with.ediam_image_inter_le_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_with.holder_on_with", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with.continuous_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with.ediam_image_inter_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_with.continuous", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_with", "description": " A function `f : X \u2192 Y` between two `pseudo_emetric_space`s is H\u00f6lder continuous with constant\n`C : \u211d\u22650` and exponent `r : \u211d\u22650`, if `edist (f x) (f y) \u2264 C * edist x y ^ r` for all `x y : X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_with_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lipschitz_with.holder_with", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `holder_with_one`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_with.dist_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with.edist_le_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_with.edist_le_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with", "description": " A function `f : X \u2192 Y` between two `pseudo_emeteric_space`s is H\u00f6lder continuous with constant\n`C : \u211d\u22650` and exponent `r : \u211d\u22650` on a set `s : set X`, if `edist (f x) (f y) \u2264 C * edist x y ^ r`\nfor all `x y \u2208 s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with.comp_holder_with", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_with.comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with.ediam_image_le_of_subset_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with.uniform_continuous_on", "description": "A H\u00f6lder continuous function is uniformly continuous", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_with.edist_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_with_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_with.nndist_le_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_with.nndist_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lipschitz_on_with.holder_on_with", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `holder_on_with_one`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eqv_gen.is_equivalence", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quot.exact", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient.rec_on_subsingleton\u2082", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient.ind\u2082", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient.hrec_on", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient.induction_on\u2083", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient.mk", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quot.hrec_on", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient.lift_on\u2082", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient.exact", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quot.lift_beta", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient.sound", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quot.lift_on", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quot.ind_beta", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quot.exists_rep", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quot.rec_on_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient.induction_on", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quot.rec", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "iff_subst", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient.ind", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient.lift_on", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quot.indep", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient.lift", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quot.rec_on", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "eqv_gen.setoid", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient.rec_on", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient.exists_rep", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quot.eqv_gen_sound", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quot.sound", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quot.lift_indep_pr1", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient.induction_on\u2082", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "eqv_gen", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "eqv_gen.rel", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "eqv_gen.refl", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "eqv_gen.symm", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "eqv_gen.trans", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient.rec", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient.rec_on_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quotient.lift\u2082", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quot.indep_coherent", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "quot.induction_on", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_compact.has_extreme_point", "description": "**Krein-Milman lemma**: In a LCTVS, any nonempty compact set has an extreme point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "closure_convex_hull_extreme_points", "description": "**Krein-Milman theorem**: In a LCTVS, any compact convex set is the closure of the convex hull\n   of its extreme points.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_on.Iic_extend", "description": "A convex antitone function extended constantly towards infinity is convex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_on.Iic_extend_of_antitone", "description": "A convex antitone function extended constantly towards infinity is convex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_on.Ici_extend_of_monotone", "description": "A convex monotone function extended constantly towards minus infinity is convex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_on.Ici_extend", "description": "A convex monotone function extended constantly towards minus infinity is convex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "concave_on.Iic_extend", "description": "A concave monotone function extended constantly towards infinity is concave.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.Ici_extend", "description": "A convex set extended towards minus infinity is convex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "concave_on.Iic_extend_of_monotone", "description": "A concave monotone function extended constantly towards infinity is concave.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "concave_on.Ici_extend", "description": "A concave antitone function extended constantly minus towards infinity is concave.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "concave_on.Ici_extend_of_antitone", "description": "A concave antitone function extended constantly minus towards infinity is concave.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.Iic_extend", "description": "A convex set extended towards infinity is convex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.exists_bounded_continuous_snorm_sub_le", "description": " Any function in `\u2112p` can be approximated by bounded continuous functions when `p < \u221e`,\nversion in terms of `snorm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.exists_has_compact_support_snorm_sub_le", "description": " In a locally compact space, any function in `\u2112p` can be approximated by compactly supported\ncontinuous functions when `p < \u221e`, version in terms of `snorm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.exists_has_compact_support_integral_rpow_sub_le", "description": " In a locally compact space, any function in `\u2112p` can be approximated by compactly supported\ncontinuous functions when `0 < p < \u221e`, version in terms of `\u222b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp.bounded_continuous_function_dense", "description": "A function in `Lp` can be approximated in `Lp` by continuous functions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable.exists_bounded_continuous_lintegral_sub_le", "description": " Any integrable function can be approximated by bounded continuous functions,\nversion in terms of `\u222b\u207b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable.exists_has_compact_support_integral_sub_le", "description": " In a locally compact space, any integrable function can be approximated by compactly supported\ncontinuous functions, version in terms of `\u222b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.to_Lp_dense_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_continuous_snorm_sub_le_of_closed", "description": " A variant of Urysohn's lemma, `\u2112^p` version, for an outer regular measure `\u03bc`:\nconsider two sets `s \u2286 u` which are respectively closed and open with `\u03bc s < \u221e`, and a vector `c`.\nThen one may find a continuous function `f` equal to `c` on `s` and to `0` outside of `u`,\nbounded by `\u2016c\u2016` everywhere, and such that the `\u2112^p` norm of `f - s.indicator (\u03bb y, c)` is\narbitrarily small. Additionally, this function `f` belongs to `\u2112^p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable.exists_has_compact_support_lintegral_sub_le", "description": " In a locally compact space, any integrable function can be approximated by compactly supported\ncontinuous functions, version in terms of `\u222b\u207b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bounded_continuous_function.to_Lp_dense_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.exists_bounded_continuous_integral_rpow_sub_le", "description": " Any function in `\u2112p` can be approximated by bounded continuous functions when `0 < p < \u221e`,\nversion in terms of `\u222b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable.exists_bounded_continuous_integral_sub_le", "description": " Any integrable function can be approximated by bounded continuous functions,\nversion in terms of `\u222b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unbundled_hom.bundled_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unbundled_hom.mk_has_forget\u2082", "description": " A custom constructor for forgetful functor\nbetween concrete categories defined using `unbundled_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unbundled_hom", "description": " A class for unbundled homs used to define a category. `hom` must\ntake two types `\u03b1`, `\u03b2` and instances of the corresponding structures,\nand return a predicate on `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unbundled_hom.hom_id", "description": " A class for unbundled homs used to define a category. `hom` must\ntake two types `\u03b1`, `\u03b2` and instances of the corresponding structures,\nand return a predicate on `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unbundled_hom.hom_comp", "description": " A class for unbundled homs used to define a category. `hom` must\ntake two types `\u03b1`, `\u03b2` and instances of the corresponding structures,\nand return a predicate on `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.limsup_const_mul_of_ne_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.limsup_const_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.limsup_add_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.limsup_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.eventually_le_limsup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.limsup_liminf_le_liminf_limsup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.limsup_mul_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.fourier_integral_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fourier.fourier_integral_smul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector_fourier.norm_fourier_integral_le_integral_norm", "description": "The uniform norm of the Fourier integral of `f` is bounded by the `L\u00b9` norm of `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector_fourier.fourier_integral_convergent_iff", "description": "For any `w`, the Fourier integral is convergent iff `f` is integrable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector_fourier.fourier_integral", "description": " The Fourier transform integral for `f : V \u2192 E`, with respect to a bilinear form `L : V \u00d7 W \u2192 \ud835\udd5c`\nand an additive character `e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.continuous_fourier_char", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector_fourier.fourier_integral_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.vector_fourier_integral_eq_integral_exp_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fourier.norm_fourier_integral_le_integral_norm", "description": "The uniform norm of the Fourier transform of `f` is bounded by the `L\u00b9` norm of `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector_fourier.fourier_integral_comp_add_right", "description": "The Fourier integral converts right-translation into scalar multiplication by a phase factor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.fourier_integral", "description": " The Fourier integral for `f : \u211d \u2192 E`, with respect to the standard additive character and\nmeasure on `\u211d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.fourier_integral_eq_integral_exp_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.fourier_char_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector_fourier.fourier_integral_smul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector_fourier.fourier_integral_continuous", "description": "The Fourier integral of an `L^1` function is a continuous function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fourier.fourier_integral", "description": " The Fourier transform integral for `f : \ud835\udd5c \u2192 E`, with respect to the measure `\u03bc` and additive\ncharacter `e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.fourier_char", "description": "The standard additive character of `\u211d`, given by `\u03bb x, exp (2 * \u03c0 * x * I)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fourier.fourier_integral_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fourier.fourier_integral_comp_add_right", "description": "The Fourier transform converts right-translation into scalar multiplication by a phase factor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_lt_one_of_mem_of_open", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_seminorm_lt_one_of_open", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absorbent.gauge_set_nonempty", "description": " If the given subset is `absorbent` then the set we take an infimum over in `gauge` is nonempty,\nwhich is useful for proving many properties about the gauge.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "seminorm.gauge_ball", "description": "Any seminorm arises as the gauge of its unit ball.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_gauge_of_not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_neg_set_eq_gauge_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_seminorm_ball_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_lt_one_eq_self_of_open", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_gauge_le_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_lt_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_smul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_of_subset_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_add_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "interior_subset_gauge_lt_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_lt_eq'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_def'", "description": " An alternative definition of the gauge using scalar multiplication on the element rather than on\nthe set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_lt_one_subset_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.lipschitz_with_gauge", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_zero'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "self_subset_gauge_le_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_lt_of_mem_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "balanced.star_convex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_le_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_smul", "description": "If `s` is balanced, then the Minkowski functional is \u2102-homogeneous.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_le_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_neg_set_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_unit_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "seminorm.gauge_seminorm_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_seminorm", "description": "`gauge s` as a seminorm when `s` is  balanced, convex and absorbent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge", "description": "The Minkowski functional. Given a set `s` in a real vector space, `gauge s` is the functional\nwhich sends `x : E` to the smallest `r : \u211d` such that `x` is in `s` scaled by `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_nonneg", "description": "The gauge is always nonnegative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.uniform_continuous_gauge", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_le_one_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_smul_left_of_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_norm_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_seminorm_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_zero", "description": " The gauge evaluated at `0` is always zero (mathematically this requires `0` to be in the set `s`\nbut, the real infimum of the empty set in Lean being defined as `0`, it holds unconditionally).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_lt_of_gauge_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.gauge_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_le_gauge_of_not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_smul_of_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.injections_with", "description": "Simplifies equations in the context using injectivity of constructors. For\neach equation `h : C x\u2081 ... x\u2099 = D y\u2081 ... y\u2098` in the context, where `C` and `D`\nare constructors of the same data type, `injections_with` does the following:\n\n- If `C = D`, it adds equations `x\u2081 = y\u2081`, ..., `x\u2099 = y\u2099`.\n- If `C \u2260 D`, it solves the goal by contradiction.\n\nSee `injection_with` for details, including information about `base` and\n`offset`.\n\n`injections_with` also recurses into the new equations `x\u1d62 = y\u1d62`. If it has to\nrecurse more than five times, it fails.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.injection_with", "description": " Given a local constant `H : C x\u2081 ... x\u2099 = D y\u2081 ... y\u2098`, where `C` and `D` are\nfully applied constructors, `injection_with H ns base offset` does the\nfollowing:\n\n- If `C \u2260 D`, it solves the goal (using the no-confusion rule).\n- If `C = D` (and thus `n = m`), it adds hypotheses\n  `h\u2081 : x\u2081 = y\u2081, ..., h\u2099 : x\u2099 = y\u2099` to the local context. Names for the `h\u1d62`\n  are taken from `ns`. If `ns` does not contain enough names, then the names\n  are derived from `base` and `offset` (by default `h_1`, `h_2` etc.; see\n  `intro_fresh`).\n- Special case: if `C = D` and `n = 0` (i.e. the constructors have no\n  arguments), the hypothesis `h : true` is added to the context.\n\n`injection_with` returns the new hypotheses and the leftover names from `ns`\n(i.e. those names that were not used to name the new hypotheses). If (and only\nif) the goal was solved, the list of new hypotheses is empty.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.injection", "description": "Simplify the equation `h` using injectivity of constructors. See\n`injection_with`. Returns the hypotheses that were added to the context, or an\nempty list if the goal was solved by contradiction.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_limits_of_size.preserves_finite_limits", "description": "If we preserve limits of some arbitrary size, then we preserve all finite limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.id_preserves_finite_colimits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.comp_preserves_finite_colimits", "description": "The composition of two right exact functors is right exact.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_finite_colimits", "description": "A functor is said to preserve finite colimits, if it preserves all colimits of\nshape `J`, where `J : Type` is a finite category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_finite_colimits.preserves_finite_colimits", "description": "A functor is said to preserve finite colimits, if it preserves all colimits of\nshape `J`, where `J : Type` is a finite category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.comp_preserves_finite_limits", "description": "The composition of two left exact functors is left exact.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_limits.preserves_finite_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_limits_of_size.zero.preserves_finite_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.id_preserves_finite_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_colimits_of_shape_of_preserves_finite_colimits", "description": " Preserving finite limits also implies preserving limits over finite shapes in higher universes,\nthough through a noncomputable instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_colimits.preserves_finite_colimits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_colimits_of_size.zero.preserves_finite_colimits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_finite_colimits_of_preserves_finite_colimits_of_size", "description": " We can always derive `preserves_finite_limits C` by showing that we are preserving limits at an\narbitrary universe.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_colimits_of_size.preserves_finite_colimits", "description": "If we preserve colimits of some arbitrary size, then we preserve all finite colimits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_limits_of_shape_of_preserves_finite_limits", "description": " Preserving finite limits also implies preserving limits over finite shapes in higher universes,\nthough through a noncomputable instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_finite_limits_of_preserves_finite_limits_of_size", "description": " We can always derive `preserves_finite_limits C` by showing that we are preserving limits at an\narbitrary universe.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_finite_limits", "description": "A functor is said to preserve finite limits, if it preserves all limits of shape `J`,\nwhere `J : Type` is a finite category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_finite_limits.preserves_finite_limits", "description": "A functor is said to preserve finite limits, if it preserves all limits of shape `J`,\nwhere `J : Type` is a finite category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.coe_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.coe_copy", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homeomorph.to_cocompact_map", "description": "A homemomorphism is a cocompact map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.id", "description": "The identity as a cocompact continuous map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homeomorph.to_cocompact_map_to_continuous_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map", "description": " A *cocompact continuous map* is a continuous function between topological spaces which\ntends to the cocompact filter along the cocompact filter. Functions for which preimages of compact\nsets are compact always satisfy this property, and the converse holds for cocompact continuous maps\nwhen the codomain is Hausdorff (see `cocompact_map.tendsto_of_forall_preimage` and\n`cocompact_map.is_compact_preimage`).\n\nCocompact maps thus generalise proper maps, with which they correspond when the codomain is\nHausdorff.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.to_continuous_map", "description": " A *cocompact continuous map* is a continuous function between topological spaces which\ntends to the cocompact filter along the cocompact filter. Functions for which preimages of compact\nsets are compact always satisfy this property, and the converse holds for cocompact continuous maps\nwhen the codomain is Hausdorff (see `cocompact_map.tendsto_of_forall_preimage` and\n`cocompact_map.is_compact_preimage`).\n\nCocompact maps thus generalise proper maps, with which they correspond when the codomain is\nHausdorff.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.cocompact_tendsto'", "description": " A *cocompact continuous map* is a continuous function between topological spaces which\ntends to the cocompact filter along the cocompact filter. Functions for which preimages of compact\nsets are compact always satisfy this property, and the converse holds for cocompact continuous maps\nwhen the codomain is Hausdorff (see `cocompact_map.tendsto_of_forall_preimage` and\n`cocompact_map.is_compact_preimage`).\n\nCocompact maps thus generalise proper maps, with which they correspond when the codomain is\nHausdorff.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.id_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.tendsto_of_forall_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.comp_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map_class.cocompact_map.has_coe_t", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map_class", "description": " `cocompact_map_class F \u03b1 \u03b2` states that `F` is a type of cocompact continuous maps.\n\nYou should also extend this typeclass when you extend `cocompact_map`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map_class.coe", "description": " `cocompact_map_class F \u03b1 \u03b2` states that `F` is a type of cocompact continuous maps.\n\nYou should also extend this typeclass when you extend `cocompact_map`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map_class.coe_injective'", "description": " `cocompact_map_class F \u03b1 \u03b2` states that `F` is a type of cocompact continuous maps.\n\nYou should also extend this typeclass when you extend `cocompact_map`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map_class.map_continuous", "description": " `cocompact_map_class F \u03b1 \u03b2` states that `F` is a type of cocompact continuous maps.\n\nYou should also extend this typeclass when you extend `cocompact_map`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map_class.cocompact_tendsto", "description": " `cocompact_map_class F \u03b1 \u03b2` states that `F` is a type of cocompact continuous maps.\n\nYou should also extend this typeclass when you extend `cocompact_map`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.comp", "description": "The composition of cocompact continuous maps, as a cocompact continuous map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.has_coe_to_fun", "description": " Helper instance for when there's too many metavariables to apply `fun_like.has_coe_to_fun`\ndirectly.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.coe_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map_class.to_continuous_map_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.cocompact_map_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.coe_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.comp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.copy_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.is_compact_preimage", "description": " If the codomain is Hausdorff, preimages of compact sets are compact under a cocompact\ncontinuous map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.coe_to_continuous_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.comp_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cocompact_map.copy", "description": " Copy of a `cocompact_map` with a new `to_fun` equal to the old one. Useful\nto fix definitional equalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "options.get_nat", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "options.mk", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "options.set_bool", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "options.get_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "options.join", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "options.set_nat", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "options", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "options.fold", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "options.size", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "options.get_bool", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "options.has_add", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "options.set_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "options.contains", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "options.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "options.has_decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.nat_abs_bit1_nonneg_step", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.neg_ne_of_pos", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.zero_ne_neg_of_ne", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.ne_of_nat_ne_nonneg_case", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.zero_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.neg_ne_zero_of_ne", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.one_pos", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.zero_le_of_nat", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.nat_abs_add_neg", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.nat_abs_add_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.nat_abs_bit0_step", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.of_nat_nat_abs_eq_of_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.ne_neg_of_ne", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.bit1_pos", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.bit1_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.one_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.nat_abs_bit0", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.nonneg_of_pos", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.nat_abs_of_nat_core", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.bit0_pos", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.ne_of_nat_abs_ne_nat_abs_of_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.nat_abs_bit1_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.nat_abs_of_neg_succ_of_nat", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.ne_neg_of_pos", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.bit0_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.neg_succ_of_nat_lt_zero", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "real.cont_diff_at_tan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_deriv_at.arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.tendsto_abs_tan_of_cos_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.differentiable_arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.has_strict_deriv_at_tan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.deriv_arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.differentiable_at_tan_of_mem_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.has_deriv_at_arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.tendsto_abs_tan_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_within_at.arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv_arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.has_deriv_at_tan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.differentiable_at_arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.has_deriv_at_tan_of_mem_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv_within_arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_within_at.arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.has_strict_deriv_at_arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.differentiable_at_tan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.cont_diff_arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.continuous_at_tan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.deriv_tan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.elide.unelide", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.elide.replace", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.elide", "description": " The `elide n (at ...)` tactic hides all subterms of the target goal or hypotheses\nbeyond depth `n` by replacing them with `hidden`, which is a variant\non the identity function. (Tactics should still mostly be able to see\nthrough the abbreviation, but if you want to unhide the term you can use\n`unelide`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.elide / unelide", "description": "The `elide n (at ...)` tactic hides all subterms of the target goal or hypotheses\nbeyond depth `n` by replacing them with `hidden`, which is a variant\non the identity function. (Tactics should still mostly be able to see\nthrough the abbreviation, but if you want to unhide the term you can use\n`unelide`.)\n\nThe `unelide (at ...)` tactic removes all `hidden` subterms in the target\ntypes (usually added by `elide`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.unelide", "description": " The `unelide (at ...)` tactic removes all `hidden` subterms in the target\ntypes (usually added by `elide`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.homotopy.hcast_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.homotopy.prod_to_prod_Top_I", "description": "An abbreviation for `prod_to_prod_Top`, with some types already in place to help the\ntypechecker. In particular, the first path should be on the ulifted unit interval.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.upath01", "description": "The path 0 \u27f6 1 in ulift I", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.homotopy.diagonal_path", "description": "The diagonal path `d` of a homotopy `H` on a path `p`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.homotopy.apply_one_path", "description": "Proof that `g(p) = H(1 \u27f6 1, p)`, with the appropriate casts", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fundamental_groupoid_functor.homotopic_maps_nat_iso.category_theory.is_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.homotopy.apply_zero_path", "description": "Proof that `f(p) = H(0 \u27f6 0, p)`, with the appropriate casts", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.path01", "description": "The path 0 \u27f6 1 in I", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unit_interval.uhpath01", "description": "The homotopy path class of 0 \u2192 1 in `ulift I`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.homotopy.ulift_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.homotopy.eq_diag_path", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.homotopy.ulift_map", "description": "Interpret a homotopy `H : C(I \u00d7 X, Y) as a map C(ulift I \u00d7 X, Y)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fundamental_groupoid_functor.equiv_of_homotopy_equiv", "description": "Homotopy equivalent topological spaces have equivalent fundamental groupoids.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.homotopy.hcast", "description": "Abbreviation for `eq_to_hom` that accepts points in a topological space", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.homotopy.eq_path_of_eq_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.homotopy.heq_path_of_eq_image", "description": " If `f(p(t) = g(q(t))` for two paths `p` and `q`, then the induced path homotopy classes\n`f(p)` and `g(p)` are the same as well, despite having a priori different types", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.homotopy.eval_at_eq", "description": "Proof that `H.eval_at x = H(0 \u27f6 1, x \u27f6 x)`, with the appropriate casts", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fundamental_groupoid_functor.homotopic_maps_nat_iso", "description": " Given a homotopy H : f \u223c g, we have an associated natural isomorphism between the induced\nfunctors `f` and `g`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map.homotopy.diagonal_path'", "description": "The diagonal path, but starting from `f x\u2080` and going to `g x\u2081`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.ring_hom_ext'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.ring_hom_ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.monomial_one_eq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.card_support_le_one_iff_monomial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.infinite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo2005_q3.key_insight", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2005_q3", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.map_restrict_quotient", "description": "Given a normal subgroup `\u0393` of an additive topological group `G` with Haar measure\n  `\u03bc`, which is also right-invariant, and a finite volume fundamental domain `\ud835\udcd5`, the pushforward\n  to the quotient group `G \u29f8 \u0393` of the restriction of `\u03bc` to `\ud835\udcd5` is a multiple of Haar measure on\n  `G \u29f8 \u0393`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quotient_add_group.has_measurable_vadd", "description": "Measurability of the action of the additive topological group `G` on the left-coset\n  space `G/\u0393`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.vadd_invariant_measure_map", "description": "The pushforward to the coset space `G \u29f8 \u0393` of the restriction of a both left- and\n  right-invariant measure on an additive topological group `G` to a fundamental domain `\ud835\udcd5` is a\n  `G`-invariant measure on `G \u29f8 \u0393`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quotient_add_group.integral_eq_integral_automorphize", "description": "This is a simple version of the **Unfolding Trick**: Given a subgroup `\u0393` of an\n  additive  group `G`, the integral of a function `f` on `G` with respect to a right-invariant\n  measure `\u03bc` is equal to the integral over the quotient `G \u29f8 \u0393` of the automorphization of `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.is_add_left_invariant_map", "description": "Assuming `\u0393` is a normal subgroup of an additive topological group `G`, the\n  pushforward to the quotient group `G \u29f8 \u0393` of the restriction of a both left- and right-invariant\n  measure on `G` to a fundamental domain `\ud835\udcd5` is a left-invariant measure on `G \u29f8 \u0393`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quotient_add_group.integral_mul_eq_integral_automorphize_mul", "description": "This is the **Unfolding Trick**: Given an additive subgroup `\u0393'` of an additive group `G'`, the\n integral of a function `f` on `G'` times the lift to `G'` of a function `g` on the quotient\n `G' \u29f8 \u0393'` with respect to a right-invariant measure `\u03bc` on `G'`, is equal to the integral over\n the quotient of the automorphization of `f` times `g`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.absolutely_continuous_map", "description": "Given a quotient space `G \u29f8 \u0393` where `\u0393` is `countable`, and the restriction,\n `\u03bc_\ud835\udcd5`, of a right-invariant measure `\u03bc` on `G` to a fundamental domain `\ud835\udcd5`, a set\n in the quotient which has `\u03bc_\ud835\udcd5`-measure zero, also has measure zero under the\n folding of `\u03bc` under the quotient. Note that, if `\u0393` is infinite, then the folded map\n will take the value `\u221e` on any open set in the quotient!", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_sup_comp_quotient_add_group_mk", "description": "The `ess_sup` of a function `g` on the additive quotient space `G \u29f8 \u0393` with respect\n  to the pushforward of the restriction, `\u03bc_\ud835\udcd5`, of a right-invariant measure `\u03bc` to a fundamental\n  domain `\ud835\udcd5`, is the same as the `ess_sup` of `g`'s lift to the universal cover `G` with respect\n  to `\u03bc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_preserving_quotient_add_group.mk'", "description": "Given a normal subgroup `\u0393` of an additive\n  topological group `G` with Haar measure `\u03bc`, which is also right-invariant, and a finite volume\n  fundamental domain `\ud835\udcd5`, the quotient map to `G \u29f8 \u0393` is measure-preserving between appropriate\n  multiples of Haar measure on `G` and `G \u29f8 \u0393`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_preserving_quotient_group.mk'", "description": "Given a normal subgroup `\u0393` of a topological group `G` with Haar measure `\u03bc`, which is also\n right-invariant, and a finite volume fundamental domain `\ud835\udcd5`, the quotient map to `G \u29f8 \u0393` is\n measure-preserving between appropriate multiples of Haar measure on `G` and `G \u29f8 \u0393`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.is_mul_left_invariant_map", "description": "Assuming `\u0393` is a normal subgroup of a topological group `G`, the pushforward to the quotient\n group `G \u29f8 \u0393` of the restriction of a both left- and right-invariant measure on `G` to a\n fundamental domain `\ud835\udcd5` is a left-invariant measure on `G \u29f8 \u0393`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.smul_invariant_measure_map", "description": "The pushforward to the coset space `G \u29f8 \u0393` of the restriction of a both left- and right-\n invariant measure on `G` to a fundamental domain `\ud835\udcd5` is a `G`-invariant measure on `G \u29f8 \u0393`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quotient_group.has_measurable_smul", "description": "Measurability of the action of the topological group `G` on the left-coset space `G/\u0393`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quotient_group.integral_mul_eq_integral_automorphize_mul", "description": "This is the **Unfolding Trick**: Given a subgroup `\u0393` of a group `G`, the integral of a\n function `f` on `G` times the lift to `G` of a function `g` on the quotient `G \u29f8 \u0393` with respect\n to a right-invariant measure `\u03bc` on `G`, is equal to the integral over the quotient of the\n automorphization of `f` times `g`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_fundamental_domain.map_restrict_quotient", "description": "Given a normal subgroup `\u0393` of a topological group `G` with Haar measure `\u03bc`, which is also\n right-invariant, and a finite volume fundamental domain `\ud835\udcd5`, the pushforward to the quotient\n group `G \u29f8 \u0393` of the restriction of `\u03bc` to `\ud835\udcd5` is a multiple of Haar measure on `G \u29f8 \u0393`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_sup_comp_quotient_group_mk", "description": "The `ess_sup` of a function `g` on the quotient space `G \u29f8 \u0393` with respect to the pushforward\n of the restriction, `\u03bc_\ud835\udcd5`, of a right-invariant measure `\u03bc` to a fundamental domain `\ud835\udcd5`, is the\n same as the `ess_sup` of `g`'s lift to the universal cover `G` with respect to `\u03bc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quotient_group.integral_eq_integral_automorphize", "description": "This is a simple version of the **Unfolding Trick**: Given a subgroup `\u0393` of a group `G`, the\n integral of a function `f` on `G` with respect to a right-invariant measure `\u03bc` is equal to the\n integral over the quotient `G \u29f8 \u0393` of the automorphization of `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_add_fundamental_domain.absolutely_continuous_map", "description": "Given an additive quotient space `G \u29f8 \u0393` where `\u0393` is `countable`, and the\n  restriction, `\u03bc_\ud835\udcd5`, of a right-invariant measure `\u03bc` on `G` to a fundamental domain `\ud835\udcd5`, a set\n  in the quotient which has `\u03bc_\ud835\udcd5`-measure zero, also has measure zero under the\n  folding of `\u03bc` under the quotient. Note that, if `\u0393` is infinite, then the folded map\n  will take the value `\u221e` on any open set in the quotient!", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_idempotent_elem_iff_of_fg", "description": "A finitely generated idempotent ideal is generated by an idempotent element", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_idempotent_elem_iff_eq_bot_or_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_integral_exp_smul_cocompact_of_inner_product", "description": " Riemann-Lebesgue lemma for functions on a finite-dimensional inner-product space, formulated\nvia dual space. **Do not use** -- it is only a stepping stone to\n`tendsto_integral_exp_smul_cocompact` where the inner-product-space structure isn't required.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.zero_at_infty_vector_fourier_integral", "description": " The Riemann-Lebesgue lemma, formulated in terms of `vector_fourier.fourier_integral` (with the\npairing in the definition of `fourier_integral` taken to be the canonical pairing between `V` and\nits dual space).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fourier_integrand_integrable", "description": "The integrand in the Riemann-Lebesgue lemma for `f` is integrable iff `f` is.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_integral_exp_inner_smul_cocompact_of_continuous_compact_support", "description": " Riemann-Lebesgue Lemma for continuous and compactly-supported functions: the integral\n`\u222b v, exp (-2 * \u03c0 * \u27eaw, v\u27eb * I) \u2022 f v` tends to 0 wrt `cocompact V`. Note that this is primarily\nof interest as a preparatory step for the more general result\n`tendsto_integral_exp_inner_smul_cocompact` in which `f` can be arbitrary.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_integral_exp_smul_cocompact", "description": " Riemann-Lebesgue lemma for functions on a finite-dimensional real vector space, formulated via\ndual space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.tendsto_integral_exp_smul_cocompact", "description": "The Riemann-Lebesgue lemma for functions on `\u211d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.zero_at_infty_fourier_integral", "description": "The Riemann-Lebesgue lemma for functions on `\u211d`, formulated via `real.fourier_integral`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_integral_exp_inner_smul_cocompact", "description": " Riemann-Lebesgue lemma for functions on a real inner-product space: the integral\n`\u222b v, exp (-2 * \u03c0 * \u27eaw, v\u27eb * I) \u2022 f v` tends to 0 as `w \u2192 \u221e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fourier_integral_half_period_translate", "description": "Shifting `f` by `(1 / (2 * \u2016w\u2016 ^ 2)) \u2022 w` negates the integral in the Riemann-Lebesgue lemma.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fourier_integral_eq_half_sub_half_period_translate", "description": "Rewrite the Fourier integral in a form that allows us to use uniform continuity.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.le_of_eq_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.mul_nonpos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.mul_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.lt_of_lt_of_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.zero_mul_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.eq_of_not_lt_of_not_gt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.mul_zero_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.zero_lt_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.mul_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.le_of_le_of_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.lt_of_eq_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.eq_of_eq_of_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.quotient_diff", "description": "The quotient of the transversals of an abelian normal `N` by the `diff` relation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.smul_diff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.eq_one_of_smul_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.quotient_diff.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.is_complement'_stabilizer_of_coprime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.exists_left_complement'_of_coprime", "description": "**Schur-Zassenhaus** for normal subgroups:\n If `H : subgroup G` is normal, and has order coprime to its index, then there exists a\n subgroup `K` which is a (left) complement of `H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.exists_smul_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.schur_zassenhaus_induction.step7", "description": "Do not use this lemma: It is made obsolete by `exists_right_complement'_of_coprime`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.smul_diff_smul'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.exists_right_complement'_of_coprime", "description": "**Schur-Zassenhaus** for normal subgroups:\n If `H : subgroup G` is normal, and has order coprime to its index, then there exists a\n subgroup `K` which is a (right) complement of `H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.exists_left_complement'_of_coprime_of_fintype", "description": "**Schur-Zassenhaus** for normal subgroups:\n If `H : subgroup G` is normal, and has order coprime to its index, then there exists a\n subgroup `K` which is a (left) complement of `H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.quotient_diff.mul_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.exists_right_complement'_of_coprime_of_fintype", "description": "**Schur-Zassenhaus** for normal subgroups:\n If `H : subgroup G` is normal, and has order coprime to its index, then there exists a\n subgroup `K` which is a (right) complement of `H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_up_shadow_iff_exists_mem_card_add", "description": "`t \u2208 \u2202^k \ud835\udc9c` iff `t` is exactly `k` elements more than something in `\ud835\udc9c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.shadow_monotone", "description": "The shadow is monotone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.sized.up_shadow", "description": "The upper shadow of a family of `r`-sets is a family of `r + 1`-sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.up_shadow_image_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.shadow_image_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.shadow_empty", "description": "The shadow of the empty set is empty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.up_shadow_monotone", "description": "The upper shadow is monotone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_shadow_iff_exists_mem_card_add", "description": "`t \u2208 \u2202^k \ud835\udc9c` iff `t` is exactly `k` elements less than something in `\ud835\udc9c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.up_shadow", "description": " The upper shadow of a set family `\ud835\udc9c` is all sets we can get by adding one element to any set in\n`\ud835\udc9c`, and the (`k` times) iterated upper shadow (`up_shadow^[k]`) is all sets we can get by adding\n`k` elements from any set in `\ud835\udc9c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.shadow_singleton_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_up_shadow_iff_exists_mem_card_add_one", "description": "`s \u2208 \u2202\u207a \ud835\udc9c` iff `s` is exactly one element less than something from `\ud835\udc9c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.shadow", "description": " The shadow of a set family `\ud835\udc9c` is all sets we can get by removing one element from any set in\n`\ud835\udc9c`, and the (`k` times) iterated shadow (`shadow^[k]`) is all sets we can get by removing `k`\nelements from any set in `\ud835\udc9c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_subset_of_mem_shadow", "description": "Being in the shadow of `\ud835\udc9c` means we have a superset in `\ud835\udc9c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_subset_of_mem_up_shadow", "description": "Being in the upper shadow of `\ud835\udc9c` means we have a superset in `\ud835\udc9c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.insert_mem_up_shadow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.up_shadow_empty", "description": "The upper shadow of the empty set is empty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_up_shadow_iff_erase_mem", "description": " `t` is in the upper shadow of `\ud835\udc9c` iff we can remove an element from it so that the resulting\nfinset is in `\ud835\udc9c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_up_shadow_iff", "description": " `s` is in the upper shadow of `\ud835\udc9c` iff there is an `t \u2208 \ud835\udc9c` from which we can remove one element\nto get `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_shadow_iff_exists_mem_card_add_one", "description": "`s \u2208 \u2202 \ud835\udc9c` iff `s` is exactly one element less than something from `\ud835\udc9c`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_shadow_iff", "description": " `s` is in the shadow of `\ud835\udc9c` iff there is an `t \u2208 \ud835\udc9c` from which we can remove one element to\nget `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_shadow_iff_insert_mem", "description": " `t` is in the shadow of `\ud835\udc9c` iff we can add an element to it so that the resulting finset is in\n`\ud835\udc9c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.sized.shadow", "description": "The shadow of a family of `r`-sets is a family of `r - 1`-sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.erase_mem_shadow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sized_shadow_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.normalized_Moore_complex_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.normalized_Moore_complex_obj_d", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.normalized_Moore_complex_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.normalized_Moore_complex.obj", "description": "The normalized Moore complex functor, on objects.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.normalized_Moore_complex", "description": "The (normalized) Moore complex of a simplicial object `X` in an abelian category `C`.\n\nThe `n`-th object is intersection of\nthe kernels of `X.\u03b4 i : X.obj n \u27f6 X.obj (n-1)`, for `i = 1, ..., n`.\n\nThe differentials are induced from `X.\u03b4 0`,\nwhich maps each of these intersections of kernels to the next.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.normalized_Moore_complex.map", "description": "The normalized Moore complex functor, on morphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.normalized_Moore_complex.obj_X", "description": "The normalized Moore complex in degree `n`, as a subobject of `X n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.normalized_Moore_complex.obj_X_2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.normalized_Moore_complex.map_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.normalized_Moore_complex.d_squared", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.normalized_Moore_complex.obj_d", "description": "The differentials in the normalized Moore complex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.normalized_Moore_complex.obj_d_2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_ne_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.injective_int", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_div_char_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.scale_roots_aeval_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.scale_roots_eval\u2082_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.scale_roots_aeval_eq_zero_of_aeval_div_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_scale_roots", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.scale_roots", "description": "`scale_roots p s` is a polynomial with root `r * s` for each root `r` of `p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_scale_roots", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.support_scale_roots_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.support_scale_roots_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.scale_roots_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_scale_roots_nat_degree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.monic_scale_roots_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.map_scale_roots", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.scale_roots_eval\u2082_eq_zero_of_eval\u2082_div_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.degree_scale_roots", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.zero_scale_roots", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.scale_roots_eval\u2082_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.centralizer_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.centralizer_eq_top_iff_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.decidable_mem_centralizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.centralizer_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.centralizer_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.mem_centralizer_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.centralizer_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.coe_centralizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.centralizer_eq_top_iff_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.coe_centralizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.center_le_centralizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.mem_centralizer_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.centralizer_to_add_subsemigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.decidable_mem_centralizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.centralizer", "description": "The centralizer of a subset of a monoid `M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.center_le_centralizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_submonoid.centralizer", "description": "The centralizer of a subset of an additive monoid.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.centralizer_to_subsemigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_exp_cmp_filter.is_o_exp_cpow", "description": " If `l : filter \u2102` is an \"exponential comparison filter\", then for any complex `a` and any\nnegative real `b`, we have `(\u03bb z, exp (b * z)) =o[l] (\u03bb z, z ^ a)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_exp_cmp_filter.of_bounded_under_abs_im", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_exp_cmp_filter", "description": " We say that `l : filter \u2102` is an *exponential comparison filter* if the real part tends to\ninfinity along `l` and the imaginary part grows subexponentially compared to the real part. These\nproperties guarantee that `(\u03bb z, z ^ a\u2081 * exp (b\u2081 * z)) =o[l] (\u03bb z, z ^ a\u2082 * exp (b\u2082 * z))` for any\ncomplex `a\u2081`, `a\u2082` and real `b\u2081 < b\u2082`.\n\nIn particular, the second property is automatically satisfied if the imaginary part is bounded along\n`l`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_exp_cmp_filter.tendsto_re", "description": " We say that `l : filter \u2102` is an *exponential comparison filter* if the real part tends to\ninfinity along `l` and the imaginary part grows subexponentially compared to the real part. These\nproperties guarantee that `(\u03bb z, z ^ a\u2081 * exp (b\u2081 * z)) =o[l] (\u03bb z, z ^ a\u2082 * exp (b\u2082 * z))` for any\ncomplex `a\u2081`, `a\u2082` and real `b\u2081 < b\u2082`.\n\nIn particular, the second property is automatically satisfied if the imaginary part is bounded along\n`l`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_exp_cmp_filter.is_O_im_pow_re", "description": " We say that `l : filter \u2102` is an *exponential comparison filter* if the real part tends to\ninfinity along `l` and the imaginary part grows subexponentially compared to the real part. These\nproperties guarantee that `(\u03bb z, z ^ a\u2081 * exp (b\u2081 * z)) =o[l] (\u03bb z, z ^ a\u2082 * exp (b\u2082 * z))` for any\ncomplex `a\u2081`, `a\u2082` and real `b\u2081 < b\u2082`.\n\nIn particular, the second property is automatically satisfied if the imaginary part is bounded along\n`l`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_exp_cmp_filter.is_o_zpow_mul_exp", "description": " If `l : filter \u2102` is an \"exponential comparison filter\", then for any complex `a\u2081`, `a\u2082` and any\ninteger `b\u2081 < b\u2082`, we have `(\u03bb z, z ^ a\u2081 * exp (b\u2081 * z)) =o[l] (\u03bb z, z ^ a\u2082 * exp (b\u2082 * z))`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_exp_cmp_filter.is_o_pow_mul_exp", "description": " If `l : filter \u2102` is an \"exponential comparison filter\", then for any complex `a\u2081`, `a\u2082` and any\nnatural `b\u2081 < b\u2082`, we have `(\u03bb z, z ^ a\u2081 * exp (b\u2081 * z)) =o[l] (\u03bb z, z ^ a\u2082 * exp (b\u2082 * z))`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_exp_cmp_filter.tendsto_abs_re", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_exp_cmp_filter.eventually_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_exp_cmp_filter.of_is_O_im_re_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_exp_cmp_filter.is_o_im_pow_exp_re", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_exp_cmp_filter.tendsto_abs", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_exp_cmp_filter.is_o_log_abs_re", "description": " If `l : filter \u2102` is an \"exponential comparison filter\", then $\\log |z| =o(\u211c z)$ along `l`.\nThis is the main lemma in the proof of `complex.is_exp_cmp_filter.is_o_cpow_exp` below.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_exp_cmp_filter.abs_im_pow_eventually_le_exp_re", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_exp_cmp_filter.of_bounded_under_im", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_exp_cmp_filter.is_o_cpow_exp", "description": " If `l : filter \u2102` is an \"exponential comparison filter\", then for any complex `a` and any\npositive real `b`, we have `(\u03bb z, z ^ a) =o[l] (\u03bb z, exp (b * z))`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_exp_cmp_filter.is_o_log_re_re", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_exp_cmp_filter.of_is_O_im_re_rpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_exp_cmp_filter.is_o_cpow_mul_exp", "description": " If `l : filter \u2102` is an \"exponential comparison filter\", then for any complex `a\u2081`, `a\u2082` and any\nreal `b\u2081 < b\u2082`, we have `(\u03bb z, z ^ a\u2081 * exp (b\u2081 * z)) =o[l] (\u03bb z, z ^ a\u2082 * exp (b\u2082 * z))`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.sub", "description": "If `\u03b1` has subtraction and `0`, we can extend the subtraction to `with_top \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.has_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.map_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.sub_eq_top_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.coe_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.has_ordered_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.sub_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.top_sub_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "miu.miu_atom_inhabited", "description": "We show that the type `miu_atom` is inhabited, giving `M` (for no particular reason) as the default\nelement.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.miurepr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.miu_atom", "description": "The atoms of MIU can be represented as an enumerated type in Lean.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.miu_atom.M", "description": "The atoms of MIU can be represented as an enumerated type in Lean.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.miu_atom.I", "description": "The atoms of MIU can be represented as an enumerated type in Lean.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.miu_atom.U", "description": "The atoms of MIU can be represented as an enumerated type in Lean.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.string_coe_miustr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.miustr.has_append", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.miu_atom.has_repr", "description": "Using `miu_atom.repr`, we prove that ``miu_atom` is an instance of `has_repr`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.miustr.has_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.miustr", "description": "For simplicity, an `miustr` is just a list of elements of type `miu_atom`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.miu_atom.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.lchar_to_miustr", "description": "In the other direction, we set up a coercion from `string` to `miustr`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.miu_atom.repr", "description": "`miu_atom.repr` is the 'natural' function from `miu_atom` to `string`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.derivable", "description": "The inductive type `derivable` has five constructors. The nonrecursive constructor `mk` corresponds\nto Hofstadter's axiom that `\"MI\"` is derivable. Each of the constructors `r1`, `r2`, `r3`, `r4`\ncorresponds to the one of Hofstadter's rules of inference.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.derivable.mk", "description": "The inductive type `derivable` has five constructors. The nonrecursive constructor `mk` corresponds\nto Hofstadter's axiom that `\"MI\"` is derivable. Each of the constructors `r1`, `r2`, `r3`, `r4`\ncorresponds to the one of Hofstadter's rules of inference.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.derivable.r1", "description": "The inductive type `derivable` has five constructors. The nonrecursive constructor `mk` corresponds\nto Hofstadter's axiom that `\"MI\"` is derivable. Each of the constructors `r1`, `r2`, `r3`, `r4`\ncorresponds to the one of Hofstadter's rules of inference.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.derivable.r2", "description": "The inductive type `derivable` has five constructors. The nonrecursive constructor `mk` corresponds\nto Hofstadter's axiom that `\"MI\"` is derivable. Each of the constructors `r1`, `r2`, `r3`, `r4`\ncorresponds to the one of Hofstadter's rules of inference.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.derivable.r3", "description": "The inductive type `derivable` has five constructors. The nonrecursive constructor `mk` corresponds\nto Hofstadter's axiom that `\"MI\"` is derivable. Each of the constructors `r1`, `r2`, `r3`, `r4`\ncorresponds to the one of Hofstadter's rules of inference.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.derivable.r4", "description": "The inductive type `derivable` has five constructors. The nonrecursive constructor `mk` corresponds\nto Hofstadter's axiom that `\"MI\"` is derivable. Each of the constructors `r1`, `r2`, `r3`, `r4`\ncorresponds to the one of Hofstadter's rules of inference.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "miu.miustr.mrepr", "description": "For display purposes, an `miustr` can be represented as a `string`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.supported_eq_adjoin_X", "description": "", "p": "ma