cription": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mul_e_transform_right", "description": " An **e-transform**. Turns `(s, t)` into `(s \u222a s \u2022 e, t \u2229 e\u207b\u00b9 \u2022 t)`. This reduces the product of\nthe two sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mul_e_transform_left_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mul_e_transform_left_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.add_e_transform_right_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mul_dyson_e_transform.card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.add_e_transform_left_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.add_e_transform_right", "description": "An **e-transform**. Turns `(s, t)` into `(s \u222a s +\u1d65 e, t \u2229 -e +\u1d65 t)`. This reduces the\nsum of the two sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.eisenstein_lemma", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.sum_mul_div_add_sum_mul_div_eq_mul", "description": "Each of the sums in this lemma is the cardinality of the set integer points in each of the\n two triangles formed by the diagonal of the rectangle `(0, p/2) \u00d7 (0, q/2)`. Adding them\n gives the number of points in the rectangle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.gauss_lemma", "description": "Gauss' lemma. The legendre symbol can be computed by considering the number of naturals less\n than `p/2` such that `(a * x) % p > p / 2`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.div_eq_filter_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.gauss_lemma_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.eisenstein_lemma_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.Ico_map_val_min_abs_nat_abs_eq_Ico_map_id", "description": "The image of the map sending a non zero natural number `x \u2264 p / 2` to the absolute value\n of the element of interger in the interval `(-p/2, p/2]` congruent to `a * x` mod p is the set\n of non zero natural numbers `x` such that `x \u2264 p / 2`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.bipartite_below", "description": "Elements of `s` which are \"below\" `b` according to relation `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.coe_bipartite_below", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.bipartite_above", "description": "Elements of `t` which are \"above\" `a` according to relation `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.card_mul_eq_card_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.card_le_card_of_right_total_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.bipartite_below_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.card_le_card_of_forall_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.coe_bipartite_above", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.bipartite_above_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.card_le_card_of_left_total_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_card_bipartite_above_eq_sum_card_bipartite_below", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_bipartite_below", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.card_mul_le_card_mul'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.card_mul_le_card_mul", "description": " Double counting argument. Considering `r` as a bipartite graph, the LHS is a lower bound on the\nnumber of edges while the RHS is an upper bound.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.card_le_card_of_forall_subsingleton'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_bipartite_above", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.slice_subset", "description": "The `r`-th slice of `\ud835\udc9c` is a subset of `\ud835\udc9c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.sized.subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.ne_of_mem_slice", "description": "Elements in distinct slices must be distinct.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.subset_powerset_len_univ_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.sized.card_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.sized.empty_mem_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.sized.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.pairwise_disjoint_slice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.sized_Union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.sized_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.slice", "description": "The `r`-th slice of a set family is the subset of its elements which have cardinality `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.bUnion_slice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.eq_of_mem_slice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_slice", "description": "`A` is in the `r`-th slice of `\ud835\udc9c` iff it's in `\ud835\udc9c` and has cardinality `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.sized", "description": "`sized r A` means that every finset in `A` has size `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.sized.subsingleton'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.sized.is_antichain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_card_slice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.sized.univ_mem_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.sized.union", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `set.sized_union`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sized_slice", "description": "Everything in the `r`-th slice of `\ud835\udc9c` has size `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.sized_Union\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.sized_powerset_len", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.sized.subset_powerset_len_univ", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `finset.subset_powerset_len_univ_iff`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fpower_series_on_ball.differentiable_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_on.differentiable_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_on.iterated_deriv", "description": "If a function is analytic on a set `s`, so are its successive derivatives.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fpower_series_at.deriv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_on.deriv", "description": "If a function is analytic on a set `s`, so is its derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fpower_series_at.differentiable_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_at.differentiable_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fpower_series_on_ball.has_fderiv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fpower_series_at.has_strict_deriv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_on.fderiv", "description": "If a function is analytic on a set `s`, so is its Fr\u00e9chet derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fpower_series_at.fderiv_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fpower_series_at.has_fderiv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fpower_series_at.has_deriv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_on.iterated_fderiv", "description": "If a function is analytic on a set `s`, so are its successive Fr\u00e9chet derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fpower_series_on_ball.fderiv_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_on.cont_diff_on", "description": "An analytic function is infinitely differentiable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fpower_series_on_ball.fderiv", "description": "If a function has a power series on a ball, then so does its derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fpower_series_at.has_strict_fderiv_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_at.differentiable_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option_t.ext", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "state_t.run_bind", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.run_pure", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "except_t.ext", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "except_t.run_pure", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "bind_pure", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.run_read", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "state_t.run_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_functor", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_functor.map_const_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_functor.id_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_functor.comp_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.run_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "id.bind_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.run_pure", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "state_t.run_monad_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.run_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "state_t.run_adapt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.run_monad_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.is_lawful_monad", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "id.map_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "except_t.run_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "state_t.run_monad_lift", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "state_t.run_get", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "except_t.run_monad_lift", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "bind_ext_congr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "control_laws_tac", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.run_bind", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "id.is_lawful_monad", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.run_monad_lift", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "state_t.ext", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "except_t.run_monad_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "map_ext_congr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "state_t.run_put", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "pure_id_seq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.run_bind", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "except_t.is_lawful_monad", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "except_t.run_bind", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.run_monad_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "id.pure_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_monad", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_monad.to_is_lawful_applicative", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_monad.bind_pure_comp_eq_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_monad.bind_map_eq_seq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_monad.pure_bind", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_monad.bind_assoc", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reader_t.ext", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.is_lawful_monad", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.run_monad_lift", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "state_t.is_lawful_monad", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "state_t.run_pure", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_applicative", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_applicative.to_is_lawful_functor", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_applicative.seq_left_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_applicative.seq_right_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_applicative.pure_seq_eq_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_applicative.map_pure", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_applicative.seq_pure", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_lawful_applicative.seq_assoc", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "conj_classes.carrier.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conj.decidable_rel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conj_classes.finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conjugates_of.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_limit_of_has_products_of_has_equalizers.build_limit_\u03c0_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_limit_of_equalizer_and_product", "description": "Given the existence of the appropriate (possibly finite) products and equalizers, we know a limit of\n`F` exists.\n(This assumes the existence of all equalizers, which is technically stronger than needed.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_colimits_of_has_coequalizers_and_coproducts", "description": "Any category with coproducts and coequalizers has all colimits.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/002P>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_finite_limits_of_preserves_terminal_and_pullbacks", "description": "If G preserves terminal objects and pullbacks, it preserves all finite limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_colimit_of_has_coproducts_of_has_coequalizers.build_is_colimit", "description": "(Implementation) Show the cocone constructed in `build_colimit` is colimiting,\nprovided the cocones used in its construction are.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_cone_of_equalizer_and_product", "description": "Given the existence of the appropriate (possibly finite) products and equalizers,\nwe can construct a limit cone for `F`.\n(This assumes the existence of all equalizers, which is technically stronger than needed.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_finite_colimits_of_preserves_coequalizers_and_finite_coproducts", "description": "If G preserves coequalizers and finite coproducts, it preserves finite colimits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_finite_colimits_of_preserves_initial_and_pushouts", "description": "If G preserves initial objects and pushouts, it preserves all finite colimits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_limit_of_has_products_of_has_equalizers.build_is_limit", "description": "(Implementation) Show the cone constructed in `build_limit` is limiting, provided the cones used in\nits construction are.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_subobject_product_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_limit_of_has_products_of_has_equalizers.build_limit_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_finite_colimits_of_has_initial_and_pushouts", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_finite_colimits_of_has_coequalizers_and_finite_coproducts", "description": "Any category with finite coproducts and coequalizers has all finite colimits.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/002Q>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_limit_of_preserves_equalizers_and_product", "description": "If a functor preserves equalizers and the appropriate products, it preserves limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_subobject_product", "description": "A limit can be realised as a subobject of a product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_finite_limits_of_has_equalizers_and_finite_products", "description": "Any category with finite products and equalizers has all finite limits.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/002O>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_limits_of_preserves_equalizers_and_products", "description": "If G preserves equalizers and products, it preserves all limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_limits_of_has_equalizers_and_products", "description": "Any category with products and equalizers has all limits.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/002N>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.colimit_quotient_coproduct_epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_limit_of_has_products_of_has_equalizers.build_limit", "description": "(Implementation) Given the appropriate product and equalizer cones, build the cone for `F` which is\nlimiting if the given cones are also.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_colimits_of_preserves_coequalizers_and_coproducts", "description": "If G preserves coequalizers and coproducts, it preserves all colimits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_colimit_of_has_coproducts_of_has_coequalizers.build_colimit", "description": "(Implementation) Given the appropriate coproduct and coequalizer cocones,\nbuild the cocone for `F` which is colimiting if the given cocones are also.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_colimit_of_coequalizer_and_coproduct", "description": "Given the existence of the appropriate (possibly finite) coproducts and coequalizers,\nwe know a colimit of `F` exists.\n(This assumes the existence of all coequalizers, which is technically stronger than needed.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_finite_limits_of_has_terminal_and_pullbacks", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_colimit_of_has_coproducts_of_has_coequalizers.build_colimit_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_colimit_of_preserves_coequalizers_and_coproduct", "description": "If a functor preserves coequalizers and the appropriate coproducts, it preserves colimits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_colimit_of_has_coproducts_of_has_coequalizers.build_colimit_\u03b9_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_finite_limits_of_preserves_equalizers_and_finite_products", "description": "If G preserves equalizers and finite products, it preserves finite limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.colimit_quotient_coproduct", "description": "A colimit can be realised as a quotient of a coproduct.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.colimit_cocone_of_coequalizer_and_coproduct", "description": "Given the existence of the appropriate (possibly finite) coproducts and coequalizers,\nwe can construct a colimit cocone for `F`.\n(This assumes the existence of all coequalizers, which is technically stronger than needed.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "counterexample.y", "description": "`y` is given by `t \u21a6 (t, t)`.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.snd_x_pseudo_eq_snd_y", "description": "`biprod.snd \u226b x` is pseudoequal to `biprod.snd y`.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.mk_x_ne_mk_y", "description": "`\u27e6x\u27e7 \u2260 \u27e6y\u27e7`.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.exist_ne_and_fst_eq_fst_and_snd_eq_snd", "description": "There are two pseudoelements `x y : \u211a \u229e \u211a` such that `x \u2260 y`, `biprod.fst x = biprod.fst y` and\n`biprod.snd x = biprod.snd y`.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.x_not_pseudo_eq", "description": "`x` is not pseudoequal to `y`.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.x", "description": "`x` is given by `t \u21a6 (t, 2 * t)`.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.snd_mk_x_eq_snd_mk_y", "description": "`biprod.snd \u27e6x\u27e7 = biprod.snd \u27e6y\u27e7`.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.fst_mk_x_eq_fst_mk_y", "description": "`biprod.fst \u27e6x\u27e7 = biprod.fst \u27e6y\u27e7`.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.fst_x_pseudo_eq_fst_y", "description": "`biprod.fst \u226b x` is pseudoequal to `biprod.fst y`.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "nat.has_dist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.noncompact_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.uniform_embedding_coe_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.closed_ball_eq_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.dist_coe_int", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.dist_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.metric_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.preimage_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.pairwise_one_le_dist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.closed_embedding_coe_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.preimage_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.proper_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.dist_cast_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp_tensor_finsupp_symm_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp_tensor_finsupp'", "description": "A variant of `finsupp_tensor_finsupp` where both modules are the ground ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp_tensor_finsupp'_apply_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp_tensor_finsupp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp_tensor_finsupp'_single_tmul_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp_tensor_finsupp_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp_tensor_finsupp", "description": "The tensor product of \u03b9 \u2192\u2080 M and \u03ba \u2192\u2080 N is linearly equivalent to (\u03b9 \u00d7 \u03ba) \u2192\u2080 (M \u2297 N).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.linear_combination", "description": "`linear_combination` attempts to simplify the target by creating a linear combination\n  of a list of equalities and subtracting it from the target.\n  The tactic will create a linear\n  combination by adding the equalities together from left to right, so the order\n  of the input hypotheses does matter.  If the `normalize` field of the\n  configuration is set to false, then the tactic will simply set the user up to\n  prove their target using the linear combination instead of normalizing the subtraction.\n\nUsers may provide an optional `with { exponent := n }`. This will raise the goal to the power `n`\n  before subtracting the linear combination.\n\nNote: The left and right sides of all the equalities should have the same\n  type, and the coefficients should also have this type.  There must be\n  instances of `has_mul` and `add_group` for this type.\n\n* Input:\n  * `input` : the linear combination of proofs of equalities, given as a sum/difference\n      of coefficients multiplied by expressions. The coefficients may be arbitrary\n      pre-expressions; if a coefficient is an application of `+` or `-` it should be\n      surrounded by parentheses. The expressions can be arbitrary proof terms proving\n      equalities. Most commonly they are hypothesis names `h1, h2, ...`.\n\n      If a coefficient is omitted, it is taken to be `1`.\n  * `config` : a `linear_combination_config`, which determines the tactic used\n      for normalization; by default, this value is the standard configuration\n      for a linear_combination_config.  In the standard configuration,\n      `normalize` is set to tt (meaning this tactic is set to use\n      normalization), and `normalization_tactic` is set to  `ring_nf SOP`.\n\nExample Usage:\n```lean\nexample (x y : \u2124) (h1 : x*y + 2*x = 1) (h2 : x = y) :\n  x*y = -2*y + 1 :=\nby linear_combination 1*h1 - 2*h2\n\nexample (x y : \u2124) (h1 : x*y + 2*x = 1) (h2 : x = y) :\n  x*y = -2*y + 1 :=\nby linear_combination h1 - 2*h2\n\nexample (x y : \u2124) (h1 : x*y + 2*x = 1) (h2 : x = y) :\n  x*y = -2*y + 1 :=\nbegin\n linear_combination -2*h2,\n /- Goal: x * y + x * 2 - 1 = 0 -/\nend\n\nexample (x y z : \u211d) (ha : x + 2*y - z = 4) (hb : 2*x + y + z = -2)\n    (hc : x + 2*y + z = 2) :\n  -3*x - 3*y - 4*z = 2 :=\nby linear_combination ha - hb - 2*hc\n\nexample (x y : \u211a) (h1 : x + y = 3) (h2 : 3*x = 7) :\n  x*x*y + y*x*y + 6*x = 3*x*y + 14 :=\nby linear_combination x*y*h1 + 2*h2\n\nexample (x y : \u2124) (h1 : x = -3) (h2 : y = 10) :\n  2*x = -6 :=\nbegin\n  linear_combination 2*h1 with {normalize := ff},\n  simp,\n  norm_cast\nend\n\nexample (x y z : \u211a) (h : x = y) (h2 : x * y = 0) : x + y*z = 0 :=\nby linear_combination (-y * z ^ 2 + x) * h + (z ^ 2 + 2 * z + 1) * h2 with { exponent := 2 }\n\nconstants (qc : \u211a) (hqc : qc = 2*qc)\n\nexample (a b : \u211a) (h : \u2200 p q : \u211a, p = q) : 3*a + qc = 3*b + 2*qc :=\nby linear_combination 3 * h a b + hqc\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.sum_two_hyps_one_mul_helper", "description": "Given that `a = b` and `c = d`, along with a coefficient, this tactic returns an\n  `expr` proving that `a + coeff * c = b + coeff * d`.\n\n* Input:\n  * `h_equality1` : an `expr`, whose type should be an equality between terms of\n      type `\u03b1`, where there are instances of `has_add \u03b1` and `has_mul \u03b1`\n  * `h_equality2` : an `expr`, whose type should be an equality between terms of\n      type `\u03b1`\n  * `coeff_for_eq2` : a `pexpr`, which should be a value of type `\u03b1`\n\n* Output: an `expr`, which proves that the result of adding the first\n  equality to the result of multiplying `coeff_for_eq2` by the second equality\n  holds", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.normalize_if_desired", "description": "This tactic attempts to prove the goal by normalizing the target if the\n`normalize` field of the given configuration is true.\n\n* Input:\n  * `config` : a `linear_combination_config`, which determines the tactic used\n      for normalization if normalization is done\n\n* Output: N/A", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.raise_goal_to_power", "description": "If an exponent `n` is provided, changes the goal from `t = 0` to `t^n = 0`.\n* Input:\n  * `exponent : \u2115`, the power to raise the goal by. If `1`, this tactic is a no-op.\n\n* Output: N/A", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.move_target_to_left_side", "description": "This tactic replaces the target with the result of moving all the terms in the\n  target to the left side of the equals sign by subtracting the right side of\n  the equation from the left side.  In other words, when the target is\n  lhs = rhs, this tactic proves that `lhs - rhs = 0` and replaces the target\n  with this new equality.\nNote: The target must be an equality when this tactic is called, and the\n  equality must have some type `\u03b1` on each side, where there is an instance of\n  `add_group \u03b1`.\n\n* Input: N/A\n\n* Output: N/A", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.replace_eq_expr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.mk_mul", "description": "`mk_mul [p\u2080, p\u2081, ..., p\u2099]` produces the pexpr `p\u2080 * p\u2081 * ... * p\u2099`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.left_minus_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.left_mul_both_sides", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.make_sum_of_hyps_helper", "description": "Given that `l_sum1 = r_sum1`, `l_h1 = r_h1`, ..., `l_hn = r_hn`, and given\n  coefficients `c_1`, ..., `c_n`, this tactic returns an `expr` proving that\n    `l_sum1 + (c_1 * l_h1) + ... + (c_n * l_hn)`\n  `= r_sum1 + (c_1 * r_h1) + ... + (c_n * r_hn)`\n\n* Input:\n  * `expected_tp`: the type of the terms being compared in the target equality\n  * an `option (tactic expr)` : `none`, if there is no sum to add to yet, or\n      `some` containing the base summation equation\n  * a `list name` : a list of names, referring to equalities in the local context\n  * a `list pexpr` : a list of coefficients to be multiplied with the\n      corresponding equalities in the list of names\n\n* Output: an `expr`, which proves that the weighted sum of the given\n    equalities added to the base equation holds", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.all_on_left_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.sum_two_equations", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.move_to_left_side", "description": "This tactic proves that the result of moving all the terms in an equality to\n  the left side of the equals sign by subtracting the right side of the\n  equation from the left side holds.  In other words, given `lhs = rhs`,\n  this tactic proves that `lhs - rhs = 0`.\n\n* Input:\n  * `h_equality` : an `expr`, whose type should be an equality between terms of\n      type `\u03b1`, where there is an instance of `add_group \u03b1`\n\n* Output: an `expr`, which proves that `lhs - rhs = 0`, where `lhs` and `rhs` are\n   the left and right sides of `h_equality` respectively", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.linear_combination", "description": "`linear_combination` attempts to simplify the target by creating a linear combination\n  of a list of equalities and subtracting it from the target.\n  The tactic will create a linear\n  combination by adding the equalities together from left to right, so the order\n  of the input hypotheses does matter.  If the `normalize` field of the\n  configuration is set to false, then the tactic will simply set the user up to\n  prove their target using the linear combination instead of normalizing the subtraction.\n\nUsers may provide an optional `with { exponent := n }`. This will raise the goal to the power `n`\n  before subtracting the linear combination.\n\nNote: The left and right sides of all the equalities should have the same\n  type, and the coefficients should also have this type.  There must be\n  instances of `has_mul` and `add_group` for this type.\n\n* Input:\n  * `input` : the linear combination of proofs of equalities, given as a sum/difference\n      of coefficients multiplied by expressions. The coefficients may be arbitrary\n      pre-expressions; if a coefficient is an application of `+` or `-` it should be\n      surrounded by parentheses. The expressions can be arbitrary proof terms proving\n      equalities. Most commonly they are hypothesis names `h1, h2, ...`.\n\n      If a coefficient is omitted, it is taken to be `1`.\n  * `config` : a `linear_combination_config`, which determines the tactic used\n      for normalization; by default, this value is the standard configuration\n      for a linear_combination_config.  In the standard configuration,\n      `normalize` is set to tt (meaning this tactic is set to use\n      normalization), and `normalization_tactic` is set to  `ring_nf SOP`.\n\nExample Usage:\n```lean\nexample (x y : \u2124) (h1 : x*y + 2*x = 1) (h2 : x = y) :\n  x*y = -2*y + 1 :=\nby linear_combination 1*h1 - 2*h2\n\nexample (x y : \u2124) (h1 : x*y + 2*x = 1) (h2 : x = y) :\n  x*y = -2*y + 1 :=\nby linear_combination h1 - 2*h2\n\nexample (x y : \u2124) (h1 : x*y + 2*x = 1) (h2 : x = y) :\n  x*y = -2*y + 1 :=\nbegin\n linear_combination -2*h2,\n /- Goal: x * y + x * 2 - 1 = 0 -/\nend\n\nexample (x y z : \u211d) (ha : x + 2*y - z = 4) (hb : 2*x + y + z = -2)\n    (hc : x + 2*y + z = 2) :\n  -3*x - 3*y - 4*z = 2 :=\nby linear_combination ha - hb - 2*hc\n\nexample (x y : \u211a) (h1 : x + y = 3) (h2 : 3*x = 7) :\n  x*x*y + y*x*y + 6*x = 3*x*y + 14 :=\nby linear_combination x*y*h1 + 2*h2\n\nexample (x y : \u2124) (h1 : x = -3) (h2 : y = 10) :\n  2*x = -6 :=\nbegin\n  linear_combination 2*h1 with {normalize := ff},\n  simp,\n  norm_cast\nend\n\nexample (x y z : \u211a) (h : x = y) (h2 : x * y = 0) : x + y*z = 0 :=\nby linear_combination (-y * z ^ 2 + x) * h + (z ^ 2 + 2 * z + 1) * h2 with { exponent := 2 }\n\nconstants (qc : \u211a) (hqc : qc = 2*qc)\n\nexample (a b : \u211a) (h : \u2200 p q : \u211a, p = q) : 3*a + qc = 3*b + 2*qc :=\nby linear_combination 3 * h a b + hqc\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.linear_combination", "description": "This is a tactic that attempts to simplify the target by creating a linear combination\n  of a list of equalities and subtracting it from the target.\n  (If the `normalize` field of the\n  configuration is set to ff, then the tactic will simply set the user up to\n  prove their target using the linear combination instead of normalizing the subtraction.)\n\nNote: The left and right sides of all the equalities should have the same\n  ring type, and the coefficients should also have this type.  There must be\n  instances of `has_mul` and `add_group` for this type.  Also note that the\n  target must involve at least one variable.\n\n* Input:\n  * `h_eqs_names` : a list of names, referring to equations in the local\n      context\n  * `coeffs` : a list of coefficients to be multiplied with the corresponding\n    equations in the list of names\n  * `config` : a `linear_combination_config`, which determines the tactic used\n      for normalization; by default, this value is the standard configuration\n      for a `linear_combination_config`\n\n* Output: N/A", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.sum_equalities", "description": "Given two hypotheses that `a = b` and `c = d`, this tactic returns an `expr` proving\n  that `a + c = b + d`.\n\n* Input:\n  * `h_equality1` : an `expr`, whose type should be an equality between terms of\n      type `\u03b1`, where there is an instance of `has_add \u03b1`\n  * `h_equality2` : an `expr`, whose type should be an equality between terms of\n      type `\u03b1`\n\n* Output: an `expr`, which proves that the result of adding the two\n    equalities holds", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.make_sum_of_hyps", "description": "Given a list of names referencing equalities and a list of pexprs representing\n  coefficients, this tactic proves that a weighted sum of the equalities\n  (where each equation is multiplied by the corresponding coefficient) holds.\n\n* Input:\n  * `expected_tp`: the type of the terms being compared in the target equality\n  * `h_eqs_names` : a list of names, referring to equalities in the local\n      context\n  * `coeffs` : a list of coefficients to be multiplied with the corresponding\n      equalities in the list of names\n\n* Output: an `expr`, which proves that the weighted sum of the equalities\n    holds", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.set_goal_to_hleft_sub_tleft", "description": "This tactic changes the goal to be that the lefthand side of the target minus the\n  lefthand side of the given expression is equal to 0.  For example,\n  if `hsum_on_left` is `5*x - 5*y = 0`, and the target is `-5*y + 5*x = 0`, this\n  tactic will change the target to be `-5*y + 5*x - (5*x - 5*y) = 0`.\n\nThis tactic only should be used when the target's type is an equality whose\n  right side is 0.\n\n* Input:\n  * `hsum_on_left` : expr, whose type should be an equality with 0 on the right\n      side of the equals sign\n\n* Output: N/A", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.eq_zero_of_sub_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.as_linear_combo", "description": "`as_linear_combo neg ms e` is used to parse the argument to `linear_combination`.\nThis argument is a sequence of literals `x`, `-x`, or `c*x` combined with `+` or `-`,\ngiven by the pexpr `e`.\nThe `neg` and `ms` arguments are used recursively; called at the top level, its usage should be\n`as_linear_combo ff [] e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.mul_equality_expr", "description": "Given that `lhs = rhs`, this tactic returns an `expr` proving that\n  `coeff * lhs = coeff * rhs`.\n\n* Input:\n  * `h_equality` : an `expr`, whose type should be an equality between terms of\n      type `\u03b1`, where there is an instance of `has_mul \u03b1`\n  * `coeff` : a `pexpr`, which should be a value of type `\u03b1`\n\n* Output: an `expr`, which proves that the result of multiplying both sides\n    of `h_equality` by the `coeff` holds", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.linear_combination_config", "description": "A configuration object for `linear_combination`.\n\n`normalize` describes whether or not the normalization step should be used.\n\n`normalization_tactic` describes the tactic used for normalization when\nchecking if the weighted sum is equivalent to the goal (when `normalize` is `tt`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.linear_combination_config.normalize", "description": "A configuration object for `linear_combination`.\n\n`normalize` describes whether or not the normalization step should be used.\n\n`normalization_tactic` describes the tactic used for normalization when\nchecking if the weighted sum is equivalent to the goal (when `normalize` is `tt`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.linear_combination_config.normalization_tactic", "description": "A configuration object for `linear_combination`.\n\n`normalize` describes whether or not the normalization step should be used.\n\n`normalization_tactic` describes the tactic used for normalization when\nchecking if the weighted sum is equivalent to the goal (when `normalize` is `tt`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_combo.linear_combination_config.exponent", "description": "A configuration object for `linear_combination`.\n\n`normalize` describes whether or not the normalization step should be used.\n\n`normalization_tactic` describes the tactic used for normalization when\nchecking if the weighted sum is equivalent to the goal (when `normalize` is `tt`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.erase_none_insert_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.mem_to_finset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.card_to_finset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.map_some_erase_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.to_finset_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_insert_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.image_some_erase_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.card_insert_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.to_finset_some", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.insert_none", "description": " Given a finset on `\u03b1`, lift it to being a finset on `option \u03b1`\nusing `option.some` and then insert `option.none`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.to_finset", "description": "Construct an empty or singleton finset from an `option`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.erase_none_inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.erase_none_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.erase_none_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_erase_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.erase_none_image_some", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.erase_none_eq_bUnion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.insert_none_erase_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.some_mem_insert_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.coe_erase_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.erase_none_map_some", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.erase_none", "description": " Given `s : finset (option \u03b1)`, `s.erase_none : finset \u03b1` is the set of `x : \u03b1` such that\n`some x \u2208 s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.erase_none_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.extend_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.compare", "description": "The comparison map between two completions of the same uniform space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.extend_comp_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion", "description": " A completion of `\u03b1` is the data of a complete separated uniform space (from the same universe)\nand a map from `\u03b1` with dense range and inducing the original uniform structure on `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.space", "description": " A completion of `\u03b1` is the data of a complete separated uniform space (from the same universe)\nand a map from `\u03b1` with dense range and inducing the original uniform structure on `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.coe", "description": " A completion of `\u03b1` is the data of a complete separated uniform space (from the same universe)\nand a map from `\u03b1` with dense range and inducing the original uniform structure on `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.uniform_struct", "description": " A completion of `\u03b1` is the data of a complete separated uniform space (from the same universe)\nand a map from `\u03b1` with dense range and inducing the original uniform structure on `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.complete", "description": " A completion of `\u03b1` is the data of a complete separated uniform space (from the same universe)\nand a map from `\u03b1` with dense range and inducing the original uniform structure on `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.separation", "description": " A completion of `\u03b1` is the data of a complete separated uniform space (from the same universe)\nand a map from `\u03b1` with dense range and inducing the original uniform structure on `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.uniform_inducing", "description": " A completion of `\u03b1` is the data of a complete separated uniform space (from the same universe)\nand a map from `\u03b1` with dense range and inducing the original uniform structure on `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.dense", "description": " A completion of `\u03b1` is the data of a complete separated uniform space (from the same universe)\nand a map from `\u03b1` with dense range and inducing the original uniform structure on `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.map\u2082", "description": "Lift two variable maps to completions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.compare_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.uniform_continuous_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.uniform_continuous_extension\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.uniform_continuous_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.uniform_continuous_extend", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.map\u2082_coe_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.extend\u2082", "description": "Extend two variable map to completions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.continuous_extend", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.extension\u2082_coe_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.uniform_continuous_compare", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.uniform_continuous_map\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.prod", "description": "Products of completions", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.extend_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.closure_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.induction_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.uniform_continuous_compare_equiv_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.inverse_compare", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.continuous_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.funext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.extend", "description": "Extension of maps to completions", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.map_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.map_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.map_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.continuous_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.compare_equiv", "description": "The uniform bijection between two completions of the same uniform space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.extend_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.continuous_map\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.dense_inducing", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.map", "description": "Lifting maps to completions", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.uniform_continuous_compare_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.map_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.extend_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abstract_completion.of_complete", "description": "If `\u03b1` is complete, then it is an abstract completion of itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo2008_q2.imo2008_q2a", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2008_q2.rational_solutions", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2008_q2.subst_abc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2008_q2.imo2008_q2b", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "quiver.weakly_connected_component", "description": "The type of weakly connected components of a directed graph. Two vertices are\n   in the same weakly connected component if there is a zigzag of arrows from one\n   to the other.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.weakly_connected_component.eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.wide_subquiver_symmetrify", "description": "A wide subquiver `H` of `G.symmetrify` determines a wide subquiver of `G`, containing an\n   an arrow `e` if either `e` or its reversal is in `H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.weakly_connected_component.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.weakly_connected_component.mk", "description": "The weakly connected component corresponding to a vertex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.weakly_connected_component.has_coe_t", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.zigzag_setoid", "description": "Two vertices are related in the zigzag setoid if there is a\n   zigzag of arrows from one to the other.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.left_nonuniform_witnesses_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.right_nonuniform_witnesses_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.nonuniform_witness_spec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.nonuniform_witness_mem_nonuniform_witnesses", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_uniform.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.mk_mem_non_uniforms_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_uniform_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_uniform_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.le_card_nonuniform_witness", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.nonuniform_witness", "description": " Arbitrary witness of non-uniformity. `G.nonuniform_witness \u03b5 s t` and\n`G.nonuniform_witness \u03b5 t s` form a pair of subsets witnessing the non-uniformity of `(s, t)`. If\n`(s, t)` is uniform, returns `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.left_nonuniform_witnesses_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.not_is_uniform_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.not_is_uniform_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.nonempty_of_not_uniform", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.is_uniform.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.nonuniform_witnesses", "description": " An arbitrary pair of subsets witnessing the non-uniformity of `(s, t)`. If `(s, t)` is uniform,\nreturns `(s, t)`. Witnesses for `(s, t)` and `(t, s)` don't necessarily match. See\n`simple_graph.nonuniform_witness`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.bot_is_uniform", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.non_uniforms", "description": " The pairs of parts of a partition `P` which are not `\u03b5`-uniform in a graph `G`. Note that we\ndismiss the diagonal. We do not care whether `s` is `\u03b5`-uniform with itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.nonuniform_witness_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_uniform", "description": " A pair of finsets of vertices is `\u03b5`-uniform (aka `\u03b5`-regular) iff their edge density is close\nto the density of any big enough pair of subsets. Intuitively, the edges between them are\nrandom-like.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.is_uniform", "description": " A finpartition of a graph's vertex set is `\u03b5`-uniform (aka `\u03b5`-regular) iff the proportion of\nits pairs of parts that are not `\u03b5`-uniform is at most `\u03b5`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.nonuniform_witnesses_spec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.right_nonuniform_witnesses_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.nonuniform_witnesses", "description": "A choice of witnesses of non-uniformity among the parts of a finpartition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.non_uniforms_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_uniform_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.non_uniforms_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.is_uniform_of_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_uniform.symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.is_uniform_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.solve_by_elim", "description": "`solve_by_elim` calls `apply` on the main goal to find an assumption whose head matches\nand then repeatedly calls `apply` on the generated subgoals until no subgoals remain,\nperforming at most `max_depth` recursive steps.\n\n`solve_by_elim` discharges the current goal or fails.\n\n`solve_by_elim` performs back-tracking if subgoals can not be solved.\n\nBy default, the assumptions passed to `apply` are the local context, `rfl`, `trivial`,\n`congr_fun` and `congr_arg`.\n\nThe assumptions can be modified with similar syntax as for `simp`:\n* `solve_by_elim [h\u2081, h\u2082, ..., h\u1d63]` also applies the named lemmas.\n* `solve_by_elim with attr\u2081 ... attr\u1d63` also applies all lemmas tagged with the specified attributes.\n* `solve_by_elim only [h\u2081, h\u2082, ..., h\u1d63]` does not include the local context,\n  `rfl`, `trivial`, `congr_fun`, or `congr_arg` unless they are explicitly included.\n* `solve_by_elim [-id_1, ... -id_n]` uses the default assumptions, removing the specified ones.\n\n`solve_by_elim*` tries to solve all goals together, using backtracking if a solution for one goal\nmakes other goals impossible.\n\noptional arguments passed via a configuration argument as `solve_by_elim { ... }`\n- max_depth: number of attempts at discharging generated sub-goals\n- discharger: a subsidiary tactic to try at each step when no lemmas apply\n  (e.g. `cc` may be helpful).\n- pre_apply: a subsidiary tactic to run at each step before applying lemmas (e.g. `intros`).\n- accept: a subsidiary tactic `list expr \u2192 tactic unit` that at each step,\n    before any lemmas are applied, is passed the original proof terms\n    as reported by `get_goals` when `solve_by_elim` started\n    (but which may by now have been partially solved by previous `apply` steps).\n    If the `accept` tactic fails,\n    `solve_by_elim` will abort searching the current branch and backtrack.\n    This may be used to filter results, either at every step of the search,\n    or filtering complete results\n    (by testing for the absence of metavariables, and then the filtering condition).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim.basic_opt", "description": "Configuration options for `solve_by_elim`.\n\n* `accept : list expr \u2192 tactic unit` determines whether the current branch should be explored.\n   At each step, before the lemmas are applied,\n   `accept` is passed the proof terms for the original goals,\n   as reported by `get_goals` when `solve_by_elim` started.\n   These proof terms may be metavariables (if no progress has been made on that goal)\n   or may contain metavariables at some leaf nodes\n   (if the goal has been partially solved by previous `apply` steps).\n   If the `accept` tactic fails `solve_by_elim` aborts searching this branch and backtracks.\n   By default `accept := \u03bb _, skip` always succeeds.\n   (There is an example usage in `tests/solve_by_elim.lean`.)\n* `pre_apply : tactic unit` specifies an additional tactic to run before each round of `apply`.\n* `discharger : tactic unit` specifies an additional tactic to apply on subgoals\n  for which no lemma applies.\n  If that tactic succeeds, `solve_by_elim` will continue applying lemmas on resulting goals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim.basic_opt.to_apply_any_opt", "description": "Configuration options for `solve_by_elim`.\n\n* `accept : list expr \u2192 tactic unit` determines whether the current branch should be explored.\n   At each step, before the lemmas are applied,\n   `accept` is passed the proof terms for the original goals,\n   as reported by `get_goals` when `solve_by_elim` started.\n   These proof terms may be metavariables (if no progress has been made on that goal)\n   or may contain metavariables at some leaf nodes\n   (if the goal has been partially solved by previous `apply` steps).\n   If the `accept` tactic fails `solve_by_elim` aborts searching this branch and backtracks.\n   By default `accept := \u03bb _, skip` always succeeds.\n   (There is an example usage in `tests/solve_by_elim.lean`.)\n* `pre_apply : tactic unit` specifies an additional tactic to run before each round of `apply`.\n* `discharger : tactic unit` specifies an additional tactic to apply on subgoals\n  for which no lemma applies.\n  If that tactic succeeds, `solve_by_elim` will continue applying lemmas on resulting goals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim.basic_opt.accept", "description": "Configuration options for `solve_by_elim`.\n\n* `accept : list expr \u2192 tactic unit` determines whether the current branch should be explored.\n   At each step, before the lemmas are applied,\n   `accept` is passed the proof terms for the original goals,\n   as reported by `get_goals` when `solve_by_elim` started.\n   These proof terms may be metavariables (if no progress has been made on that goal)\n   or may contain metavariables at some leaf nodes\n   (if the goal has been partially solved by previous `apply` steps).\n   If the `accept` tactic fails `solve_by_elim` aborts searching this branch and backtracks.\n   By default `accept := \u03bb _, skip` always succeeds.\n   (There is an example usage in `tests/solve_by_elim.lean`.)\n* `pre_apply : tactic unit` specifies an additional tactic to run before each round of `apply`.\n* `discharger : tactic unit` specifies an additional tactic to apply on subgoals\n  for which no lemma applies.\n  If that tactic succeeds, `solve_by_elim` will continue applying lemmas on resulting goals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim.basic_opt.pre_apply", "description": "Configuration options for `solve_by_elim`.\n\n* `accept : list expr \u2192 tactic unit` determines whether the current branch should be explored.\n   At each step, before the lemmas are applied,\n   `accept` is passed the proof terms for the original goals,\n   as reported by `get_goals` when `solve_by_elim` started.\n   These proof terms may be metavariables (if no progress has been made on that goal)\n   or may contain metavariables at some leaf nodes\n   (if the goal has been partially solved by previous `apply` steps).\n   If the `accept` tactic fails `solve_by_elim` aborts searching this branch and backtracks.\n   By default `accept := \u03bb _, skip` always succeeds.\n   (There is an example usage in `tests/solve_by_elim.lean`.)\n* `pre_apply : tactic unit` specifies an additional tactic to run before each round of `apply`.\n* `discharger : tactic unit` specifies an additional tactic to apply on subgoals\n  for which no lemma applies.\n  If that tactic succeeds, `solve_by_elim` will continue applying lemmas on resulting goals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim.basic_opt.discharger", "description": "Configuration options for `solve_by_elim`.\n\n* `accept : list expr \u2192 tactic unit` determines whether the current branch should be explored.\n   At each step, before the lemmas are applied,\n   `accept` is passed the proof terms for the original goals,\n   as reported by `get_goals` when `solve_by_elim` started.\n   These proof terms may be metavariables (if no progress has been made on that goal)\n   or may contain metavariables at some leaf nodes\n   (if the goal has been partially solved by previous `apply` steps).\n   If the `accept` tactic fails `solve_by_elim` aborts searching this branch and backtracks.\n   By default `accept := \u03bb _, skip` always succeeds.\n   (There is an example usage in `tests/solve_by_elim.lean`.)\n* `pre_apply : tactic unit` specifies an additional tactic to run before each round of `apply`.\n* `discharger : tactic unit` specifies an additional tactic to apply on subgoals\n  for which no lemma applies.\n  If that tactic succeeds, `solve_by_elim` will continue applying lemmas on resulting goals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim.basic_opt.max_depth", "description": "Configuration options for `solve_by_elim`.\n\n* `accept : list expr \u2192 tactic unit` determines whether the current branch should be explored.\n   At each step, before the lemmas are applied,\n   `accept` is passed the proof terms for the original goals,\n   as reported by `get_goals` when `solve_by_elim` started.\n   These proof terms may be metavariables (if no progress has been made on that goal)\n   or may contain metavariables at some leaf nodes\n   (if the goal has been partially solved by previous `apply` steps).\n   If the `accept` tactic fails `solve_by_elim` aborts searching this branch and backtracks.\n   By default `accept := \u03bb _, skip` always succeeds.\n   (There is an example usage in `tests/solve_by_elim.lean`.)\n* `pre_apply : tactic unit` specifies an additional tactic to run before each round of `apply`.\n* `discharger : tactic unit` specifies an additional tactic to apply on subgoals\n  for which no lemma applies.\n  If that tactic succeeds, `solve_by_elim` will continue applying lemmas on resulting goals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.solve_by_elim", "description": "`solve_by_elim` calls `apply` on the main goal to find an assumption whose head matches\nand then repeatedly calls `apply` on the generated subgoals until no subgoals remain,\nperforming at most `max_depth` recursive steps.\n\n`solve_by_elim` discharges the current goal or fails.\n\n`solve_by_elim` performs back-tracking if subgoals can not be solved.\n\nBy default, the assumptions passed to `apply` are the local context, `rfl`, `trivial`,\n`congr_fun` and `congr_arg`.\n\nThe assumptions can be modified with similar syntax as for `simp`:\n* `solve_by_elim [h\u2081, h\u2082, ..., h\u1d63]` also applies the named lemmas.\n* `solve_by_elim with attr\u2081 ... attr\u1d63` also applies all lemmas tagged with the specified attributes.\n* `solve_by_elim only [h\u2081, h\u2082, ..., h\u1d63]` does not include the local context,\n  `rfl`, `trivial`, `congr_fun`, or `congr_arg` unless they are explicitly included.\n* `solve_by_elim [-id_1, ... -id_n]` uses the default assumptions, removing the specified ones.\n\n`solve_by_elim*` tries to solve all goals together, using backtracking if a solution for one goal\nmakes other goals impossible.\n\noptional arguments passed via a configuration argument as `solve_by_elim { ... }`\n- max_depth: number of attempts at discharging generated sub-goals\n- discharger: a subsidiary tactic to try at each step when no lemmas apply\n  (e.g. `cc` may be helpful).\n- pre_apply: a subsidiary tactic to run at each step before applying lemmas (e.g. `intros`).\n- accept: a subsidiary tactic `list expr \u2192 tactic unit` that at each step,\n    before any lemmas are applied, is passed the original proof terms\n    as reported by `get_goals` when `solve_by_elim` started\n    (but which may by now have been partially solved by previous `apply` steps).\n    If the `accept` tactic fails,\n    `solve_by_elim` will abort searching the current branch and backtrack.\n    This may be used to filter results, either at every step of the search,\n    or filtering complete results\n    (by testing for the absence of metavariables, and then the filtering condition).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.apply_assumption", "description": "`apply_assumption` looks for an assumption of the form `... \u2192 \u2200 _, ... \u2192 head`\nwhere `head` matches the current goal.\n\nIf this fails, `apply_assumption` will call `symmetry` and try again.\n\nIf this also fails, `apply_assumption` will call `exfalso` and try again,\nso that if there is an assumption of the form `P \u2192 \u00ac Q`, the new tactic state\nwill have two goals, `P` and `Q`.\n\nOptional arguments:\n- `lemmas`: a list of expressions to apply, instead of the local constants\n- `tac`: a tactic to run on each subgoal after applying an assumption; if\n  this tactic fails, the corresponding assumption will be rejected and\n  the next one will be attempted.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim.solve_by_elim_trace", "description": "A helper function for trace messages, prepending '....' depending on the current search depth.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim.mk_assumption_set", "description": "`mk_assumption_set` builds a collection of lemmas for use in\nthe backtracking search in `solve_by_elim`.\n\n* By default, it includes all local hypotheses, along with `rfl`, `trivial`, `congr_fun` and\n  `congr_arg`.\n* The flag `no_dflt` removes these.\n* The argument `hs` is a list of `simp_arg_type`s,\n  and can be used to add, or remove, lemmas or expressions from the set.\n* The argument `attr : list name` adds all lemmas tagged with one of a specified list of attributes.\n\n`mk_assumption_set` returns not a `list expr`, but a `list (tactic expr) \u00d7 tactic (list expr)`.\nThere are two separate problems that need to be solved.\n\n### Relevant local hypotheses\n\n`solve_by_elim*` works with multiple goals,\nand we need to use separate sets of local hypotheses for each goal.\nThe second component of the returned value provides these local hypotheses.\n(Essentially using `local_context`, along with some filtering to remove hypotheses\nthat have been explicitly removed via `only` or `[-h]`.)\n\n### Stuck metavariables\n\nLemmas with implicit arguments would be filled in with metavariables if we created the\n`expr` objects immediately, so instead we return thunks that generate the expressions\non demand. This is the first component, with type `list (tactic expr)`.\n\nAs an example, we have `def rfl : \u2200 {\u03b1 : Sort u} {a : \u03b1}, a = a`, which on elaboration will become\n`@rfl ?m_1 ?m_2`.\n\nBecause `solve_by_elim` works by repeated application of lemmas against subgoals,\nthe first time such a lemma is successfully applied,\nthose metavariables will be unified, and thereafter have fixed values.\nThis would make it impossible to apply the lemma\na second time with different values of the metavariables.\n\nSee https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib/issues/2269\n\nAs an optimisation, after we build the list of `tactic expr`s, we actually run them, and replace any\nthat do not in fact produce metavariables with a simple `return` tactic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim.trace_hooks", "description": "A helper function to generate the tactic that print trace messages.\nThis function exists to ensure the target is pretty printed only as necessary.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim.solve_by_elim_aux", "description": "The internal implementation of `solve_by_elim`, with a limiting counter.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim", "description": "`solve_by_elim` repeatedly tries `apply`ing a lemma\nfrom the list of assumptions (passed via the `opt` argument),\nrecursively operating on any generated subgoals, backtracking as necessary.\n\n`solve_by_elim` succeeds only if it discharges the goal.\n(By default, `solve_by_elim` focuses on the first goal, and only attempts to solve that.\nWith the option `backtrack_all_goals := tt`,\nit attempts to solve all goals, and only succeeds if it does so.\nWith `backtrack_all_goals := tt`, `solve_by_elim` will backtrack a solution it has found for\none goal if it then can't discharge other goals.)\n\nIf passed an empty list of assumptions, `solve_by_elim` builds a default set\nas per the interactive tactic, using the `local_context` along with\n`rfl`, `trivial`, `congr_arg`, and `congr_fun`.\n\nTo pass a particular list of assumptions, use the `lemmas` field\nin the configuration argument. This expects an\n`option (list expr)`. In certain situations it may be necessary to instead use the\n`lemma_thunks` field, which expects a `option (list (tactic expr))`.\nThis allows for regenerating metavariables\nfor each application, which might otherwise get stuck.\n\nSee also the simpler tactic `apply_rules`, which does not perform backtracking.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim.opt.get_lemma_thunks", "description": "If no lemmas have been specified, generate the default set\n(local hypotheses, along with `rfl`, `trivial`, `congr_arg`, and `congr_fun`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim.opt", "description": "Arguments for `solve_by_elim`:\n* By default `solve_by_elim` operates only on the first goal,\n  but with `backtrack_all_goals := true`, it operates on all goals at once,\n  backtracking across goals as needed,\n  and only succeeds if it discharges all goals.\n* `lemmas` specifies the list of lemmas to use in the backtracking search.\n  If `none`, `solve_by_elim` uses the local hypotheses,\n  along with `rfl`, `trivial`, `congr_arg`, and `congr_fun`.\n* `lemma_thunks` provides the lemmas as a list of `tactic expr`,\n  which are used to regenerate the `expr` objects to avoid binding metavariables.\n  It should not usually be specified by the user.\n  (If both `lemmas` and `lemma_thunks` are specified, only `lemma_thunks` is used.)\n* `ctx_thunk` is for internal use only: it returns the local hypotheses which will be used.\n* `max_depth` bounds the depth of the search.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim.opt.to_basic_opt", "description": "Arguments for `solve_by_elim`:\n* By default `solve_by_elim` operates only on the first goal,\n  but with `backtrack_all_goals := true`, it operates on all goals at once,\n  backtracking across goals as needed,\n  and only succeeds if it discharges all goals.\n* `lemmas` specifies the list of lemmas to use in the backtracking search.\n  If `none`, `solve_by_elim` uses the local hypotheses,\n  along with `rfl`, `trivial`, `congr_arg`, and `congr_fun`.\n* `lemma_thunks` provides the lemmas as a list of `tactic expr`,\n  which are used to regenerate the `expr` objects to avoid binding metavariables.\n  It should not usually be specified by the user.\n  (If both `lemmas` and `lemma_thunks` are specified, only `lemma_thunks` is used.)\n* `ctx_thunk` is for internal use only: it returns the local hypotheses which will be used.\n* `max_depth` bounds the depth of the search.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim.opt.backtrack_all_goals", "description": "Arguments for `solve_by_elim`:\n* By default `solve_by_elim` operates only on the first goal,\n  but with `backtrack_all_goals := true`, it operates on all goals at once,\n  backtracking across goals as needed,\n  and only succeeds if it discharges all goals.\n* `lemmas` specifies the list of lemmas to use in the backtracking search.\n  If `none`, `solve_by_elim` uses the local hypotheses,\n  along with `rfl`, `trivial`, `congr_arg`, and `congr_fun`.\n* `lemma_thunks` provides the lemmas as a list of `tactic expr`,\n  which are used to regenerate the `expr` objects to avoid binding metavariables.\n  It should not usually be specified by the user.\n  (If both `lemmas` and `lemma_thunks` are specified, only `lemma_thunks` is used.)\n* `ctx_thunk` is for internal use only: it returns the local hypotheses which will be used.\n* `max_depth` bounds the depth of the search.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim.opt.lemmas", "description": "Arguments for `solve_by_elim`:\n* By default `solve_by_elim` operates only on the first goal,\n  but with `backtrack_all_goals := true`, it operates on all goals at once,\n  backtracking across goals as needed,\n  and only succeeds if it discharges all goals.\n* `lemmas` specifies the list of lemmas to use in the backtracking search.\n  If `none`, `solve_by_elim` uses the local hypotheses,\n  along with `rfl`, `trivial`, `congr_arg`, and `congr_fun`.\n* `lemma_thunks` provides the lemmas as a list of `tactic expr`,\n  which are used to regenerate the `expr` objects to avoid binding metavariables.\n  It should not usually be specified by the user.\n  (If both `lemmas` and `lemma_thunks` are specified, only `lemma_thunks` is used.)\n* `ctx_thunk` is for internal use only: it returns the local hypotheses which will be used.\n* `max_depth` bounds the depth of the search.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim.opt.lemma_thunks", "description": "Arguments for `solve_by_elim`:\n* By default `solve_by_elim` operates only on the first goal,\n  but with `backtrack_all_goals := true`, it operates on all goals at once,\n  backtracking across goals as needed,\n  and only succeeds if it discharges all goals.\n* `lemmas` specifies the list of lemmas to use in the backtracking search.\n  If `none`, `solve_by_elim` uses the local hypotheses,\n  along with `rfl`, `trivial`, `congr_arg`, and `congr_fun`.\n* `lemma_thunks` provides the lemmas as a list of `tactic expr`,\n  which are used to regenerate the `expr` objects to avoid binding metavariables.\n  It should not usually be specified by the user.\n  (If both `lemmas` and `lemma_thunks` are specified, only `lemma_thunks` is used.)\n* `ctx_thunk` is for internal use only: it returns the local hypotheses which will be used.\n* `max_depth` bounds the depth of the search.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim.opt.ctx_thunk", "description": "Arguments for `solve_by_elim`:\n* By default `solve_by_elim` operates only on the first goal,\n  but with `backtrack_all_goals := true`, it operates on all goals at once,\n  backtracking across goals as needed,\n  and only succeeds if it discharges all goals.\n* `lemmas` specifies the list of lemmas to use in the backtracking search.\n  If `none`, `solve_by_elim` uses the local hypotheses,\n  along with `rfl`, `trivial`, `congr_arg`, and `congr_fun`.\n* `lemma_thunks` provides the lemmas as a list of `tactic expr`,\n  which are used to regenerate the `expr` objects to avoid binding metavariables.\n  It should not usually be specified by the user.\n  (If both `lemmas` and `lemma_thunks` are specified, only `lemma_thunks` is used.)\n* `ctx_thunk` is for internal use only: it returns the local hypotheses which will be used.\n* `max_depth` bounds the depth of the search.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim.on_success", "description": "A helper function to generate trace messages on successful applications.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.apply_assumption", "description": "`apply_assumption` looks for an assumption of the form `... \u2192 \u2200 _, ... \u2192 head`\nwhere `head` matches the current goal.\n\nIf this fails, `apply_assumption` will call `symmetry` and try again.\n\nIf this also fails, `apply_assumption` will call `exfalso` and try again,\nso that if there is an assumption of the form `P \u2192 \u00ac Q`, the new tactic state\nwill have two goals, `P` and `Q`.\n\nOptional arguments:\n- `lemmas`: a list of expressions to apply, instead of the local constants\n- `tac`: a tactic to run on each subgoal after applying an assumption; if\n  this tactic fails, the corresponding assumption will be rejected and\n  the next one will be attempted.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.solve_by_elim.on_failure", "description": "A helper function to generate trace messages on unsuccessful applications.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Union_disjointed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "preimage_find_eq_disjointed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "supr_disjointed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "disjointed_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "disjointed_le_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "disjointed_rec", "description": " An induction principle for `disjointed`. To define/prove something on `disjointed f n`, it's\nenough to define/prove it for `f n` and being able to extend through diffs.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone.disjointed_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "disjointed", "description": " If `f : \u2115 \u2192 \u03b1` is a sequence of elements, then `disjointed f` is the sequence formed by\nsubtracting each element from the nexts. This is the unique disjoint sequence whose partial sups\nare the same as the original sequence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "disjointed_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "disjointed_unique", "description": " `disjointed f` is the unique sequence that is pairwise disjoint and has the same partial sups\nas `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "disjointed_eq_inter_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "disjointed_eq_inf_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "disjointed_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "partial_sups_disjointed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "disjoint_disjointed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "disjointed_rec_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "disjointed_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.cast_multiset_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.cast_list_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.cast_list_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.cast_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.cast_multiset_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.cast_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.equalizer_\u03b9_is_local_ring_hom'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.prod_fan", "description": "The product in `CommRing` is the cartesian product. This is the binary fan.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.equalizer_\u03b9_is_local_ring_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.equalizer_fork_is_limit", "description": "The equalizer in `CommRing` is the equalizer as sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.Z_is_initial", "description": "`\u2124` is the initial object of `CommRing`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.pushout_cocone", "description": "The explicit cocone with tensor products as the fibered product in `CommRing`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.pullback_cone", "description": "In the category of `CommRing`, the pullback of `f : A \u27f6 C` and `g : B \u27f6 C` is the `eq_locus` of\nthe two maps `A \u00d7 B \u27f6 C`. This is the constructed pullback cone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.pushout_cocone_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.pushout_cocone_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.prod_fan_is_limit", "description": "The product in `CommRing` is the cartesian product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.\u03b9.is_local_ring_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.pullback_cone_is_limit", "description": "The constructed pullback cone is indeed the limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.pushout_cocone_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.equalizer_fork", "description": "The equalizer in `CommRing` is the equalizer as sets. This is the equalizer fork.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.punit_is_terminal", "description": "The trivial ring is the (strict) terminal object of `CommRing`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.CommRing_has_strict_terminal_objects", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.pushout_cocone_is_colimit", "description": "Verify that the `pushout_cocone` is indeed the colimit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.subsingleton_of_is_terminal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.prod_fan_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.pushforward", "description": "The pushforward functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.sheaf_condition_pairwise_intersections.map_diagram", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.pushforward_sheaf_of_sheaf", "description": "The pushforward of a sheaf (by a continuous map) is a sheaf.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.sheaf_condition_pairwise_intersections.map_cocone", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.sheaf_condition_pairwise_intersections.pushforward_sheaf_of_sheaf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on", "description": " We say that `f` approximates a continuous linear map `f'` on `s` with constant `c`,\nif `\u2016f x - f y - f' (x - y)\u2016 \u2264 c * \u2016x - y\u2016` whenever `x, y \u2208 s`.\n\nThis predicate is defined to facilitate the splitting of the inverse function theorem into small\nlemmas. Some of these lemmas can be useful, e.g., to prove that the inverse function is defined\non a specific set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_deriv_at.local_inverse", "description": "A function that is inverse to `f` near `a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.mem_to_local_homeomorph_source", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.local_inverse_tendsto", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.map_nhds_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.to_local_homeomorph_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.open_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.to_local_inverse", "description": " If `f` has an invertible derivative `f'` at `a` in the sense of strict differentiability `(hf)`,\nthen the inverse function `hf.local_inverse f` has derivative `f'.symm` at `f a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.eventually_right_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "open_map_of_strict_deriv", "description": "If a function has a non-zero strict derivative at all points, then it is an open map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.map_nhds_eq_of_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lipschitz_on_with.approximates_linear_on", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `approximates_linear_on.approximates_linear_on_iff_lipschitz_on_with`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.to_local_homeomorph_source", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "open_map_of_strict_fderiv_equiv", "description": "If a function has an invertible strict derivative at all points, then it is an open map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.continuous", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.to_local_homeomorph_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.mem_to_local_homeomorph_source", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.surj_on_closed_ball_of_nonlinear_right_inverse", "description": " If a function is linearly approximated by a continuous linear map with a (possibly nonlinear)\nright inverse, then it is locally onto: a ball of an explicit radius is included in the image\nof the map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.image_mem_to_local_homeomorph_target", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.inverse_continuous_on", "description": "The inverse function is continuous on `f '' s`. Use properties of `local_homeomorph` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.approximates_deriv_on_nhds", "description": " If `f` has derivative `f'` at `a` in the strict sense and `c > 0`, then `f` approximates `f'`\nwith constant `c` on some neighborhood of `a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.image_mem_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.local_inverse_apply_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.lipschitz", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_deriv_at.to_local_left_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.to_local_equiv", "description": " A map approximating a linear equivalence on a set defines a local equivalence on this set.\nShould not be used outside of this file, because it is superseded by `to_local_homeomorph` below.\n\nThis is a first step towards the inverse function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.local_inverse", "description": " Given a `cont_diff` function over `\ud835\udd42` (which is `\u211d` or `\u2102`) with an invertible derivative\nat `a`, returns a function that is locally inverse to `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.to_local_homeomorph_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.local_inverse", "description": " Given a function `f` with an invertible derivative, returns a function that is locally inverse\nto `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.approximates_linear_on_iff_lipschitz_on_with", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.to_local_homeomorph", "description": " Given a `cont_diff` function over `\ud835\udd42` (which is `\u211d` or `\u2102`) with an invertible\nderivative at `a`, returns a `local_homeomorph` with `to_fun = f` and `a \u2208 source`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.mono_num", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.closed_ball_subset_target", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.to_local_inverse", "description": " Given a `cont_diff` function over `\ud835\udd42` (which is `\u211d` or `\u2102`) with an invertible derivative\nat `a`, the inverse function (produced by `cont_diff.to_local_homeomorph`) is\nalso `cont_diff`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.local_inverse_continuous_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.image_mem_to_local_homeomorph_target", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.local_inverse_apply_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_deriv_at.to_local_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.inj_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.to_homeomorph", "description": "A function `f` that approximates a linear equivalence on the whole space is a homeomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.to_inv", "description": "The inverse function is approximated linearly on `f '' s` by `f'.symm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.map_nhds_eq_of_surj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.to_local_left_inverse", "description": " If `f : E \u2192 F` has an invertible derivative `f'` at `a` in the sense of strict differentiability\nand `g (f x) = x` in a neighborhood of `a`, then `g` has derivative `f'.symm` at `f a`.\n\nFor a version assuming `f (g y) = y` and continuity of `g` at `f a` but not `[complete_space E]`\nsee `of_local_left_inverse`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.to_local_homeomorph", "description": " Given a function with an invertible strict derivative at `a`, returns a `local_homeomorph`\nwith `to_fun = f` and `a \u2208 source`. This is a part of the inverse function theorem.\nThe other part `has_strict_fderiv_at.to_local_inverse` states that the inverse function\nof this `local_homeomorph` has derivative `f'.symm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.eventually_left_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.to_local_homeomorph", "description": " Given a function `f` that approximates a linear equivalence on an open set `s`,\nreturns a local homeomorph with `to_fun = f` and `source = s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.lipschitz_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.to_local_homeomorph_target", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.approximates_deriv_on_open_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.antilipschitz", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.exists_homeomorph_extension", "description": " In a real vector space, a function `f` that approximates a linear equivalence on a subset `s`\ncan be extended to a homeomorphism of the whole space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.lipschitz_on_with", "description": "**Alias** of the forward direction of `approximates_linear_on.approximates_linear_on_iff_lipschitz_on_with`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.continuous_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_deriv_at.map_nhds_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.local_inverse_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.local_inverse_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "approximates_linear_on.mono_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.coe_mk_unit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.inverse_coeff", "description": "Recursively defines the sequence of coefficients for the inverse to a Witt vector whose first entry\nis a unit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.exists_eq_pow_p_mul'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.discrete_valuation_ring", "description": "The ring of Witt Vectors of a perfect field of positive characteristic is a DVR.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.is_unit_of_coeff_zero_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.mk_unit", "description": "Upgrade a Witt vector `A` whose first entry `A.coeff 0` is a unit to be, itself, a unit in `\ud835\udd4e k`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.exists_eq_pow_p_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.irreducible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.succ_nth_val_units", "description": "This is the `n+1`st coefficient of our inverse.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.modification.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans", "description": "If `\u03b7` is an oplax natural transformation between `F` and `G`, we have a 1-morphism\n`\u03b7.app a : F.obj a \u27f6 G.obj a` for each object `a : B`. We also have a 2-morphism\n`\u03b7.naturality f : F.map f \u226b app b \u27f6 app a \u226b G.map f` for each 1-morphism `f : a \u27f6 b`.\nThese 2-morphisms satisfies the naturality condition, and preserve the identities and\nthe compositions modulo some adjustments of domains and codomains of 2-morphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.app", "description": "If `\u03b7` is an oplax natural transformation between `F` and `G`, we have a 1-morphism\n`\u03b7.app a : F.obj a \u27f6 G.obj a` for each object `a : B`. We also have a 2-morphism\n`\u03b7.naturality f : F.map f \u226b app b \u27f6 app a \u226b G.map f` for each 1-morphism `f : a \u27f6 b`.\nThese 2-morphisms satisfies the naturality condition, and preserve the identities and\nthe compositions modulo some adjustments of domains and codomains of 2-morphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.naturality", "description": "If `\u03b7` is an oplax natural transformation between `F` and `G`, we have a 1-morphism\n`\u03b7.app a : F.obj a \u27f6 G.obj a` for each object `a : B`. We also have a 2-morphism\n`\u03b7.naturality f : F.map f \u226b app b \u27f6 app a \u226b G.map f` for each 1-morphism `f : a \u27f6 b`.\nThese 2-morphisms satisfies the naturality condition, and preserve the identities and\nthe compositions modulo some adjustments of domains and codomains of 2-morphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.naturality_naturality'", "description": "If `\u03b7` is an oplax natural transformation between `F` and `G`, we have a 1-morphism\n`\u03b7.app a : F.obj a \u27f6 G.obj a` for each object `a : B`. We also have a 2-morphism\n`\u03b7.naturality f : F.map f \u226b app b \u27f6 app a \u226b G.map f` for each 1-morphism `f : a \u27f6 b`.\nThese 2-morphisms satisfies the naturality condition, and preserve the identities and\nthe compositions modulo some adjustments of domains and codomains of 2-morphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.naturality_id'", "description": "If `\u03b7` is an oplax natural transformation between `F` and `G`, we have a 1-morphism\n`\u03b7.app a : F.obj a \u27f6 G.obj a` for each object `a : B`. We also have a 2-morphism\n`\u03b7.naturality f : F.map f \u226b app b \u27f6 app a \u226b G.map f` for each 1-morphism `f : a \u27f6 b`.\nThese 2-morphisms satisfies the naturality condition, and preserve the identities and\nthe compositions modulo some adjustments of domains and codomains of 2-morphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.naturality_comp'", "description": "If `\u03b7` is an oplax natural transformation between `F` and `G`, we have a 1-morphism\n`\u03b7.app a : F.obj a \u27f6 G.obj a` for each object `a : B`. We also have a 2-morphism\n`\u03b7.naturality f : F.map f \u226b app b \u27f6 app a \u226b G.map f` for each 1-morphism `f : a \u27f6 b`.\nThese 2-morphisms satisfies the naturality condition, and preserve the identities and\nthe compositions modulo some adjustments of domains and codomains of 2-morphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.naturality_id_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.vcomp_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.modification.whisker_left_naturality_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.modification_iso.of_components_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.category_theory.oplax_functor.category_theory.category_struct_to_quiver_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.whisker_left_naturality_naturality_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.modification.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.modification.vcomp", "description": "Vertical composition of modifications.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.modification.naturality_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.modification", "description": "A modification `\u0393` between oplax natural transformations `\u03b7` and `\u03b8` consists of a family of\n2-morphisms `\u0393.app a : \u03b7.app a \u27f6 \u03b8.app a`, which satisfies the equation\n`(F.map f \u25c1 app b) \u226b \u03b8.naturality f = \u03b7.naturality f \u226b (app a \u25b7 G.map f)`\nfor each 1-morphism `f : a \u27f6 b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.modification.app", "description": "A modification `\u0393` between oplax natural transformations `\u03b7` and `\u03b8` consists of a family of\n2-morphisms `\u0393.app a : \u03b7.app a \u27f6 \u03b8.app a`, which satisfies the equation\n`(F.map f \u25c1 app b) \u226b \u03b8.naturality f = \u03b7.naturality f \u226b (app a \u25b7 G.map f)`\nfor each 1-morphism `f : a \u27f6 b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.modification.naturality'", "description": "A modification `\u0393` between oplax natural transformations `\u03b7` and `\u03b8` consists of a family of\n2-morphisms `\u0393.app a : \u03b7.app a \u27f6 \u03b8.app a`, which satisfies the equation\n`(F.map f \u25c1 app b) \u226b \u03b8.naturality f = \u03b7.naturality f \u226b (app a \u25b7 G.map f)`\nfor each 1-morphism `f : a \u27f6 b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.whisker_left_naturality_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.whisker_right_naturality_id_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.modification.naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.naturality_naturality_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.modification.whisker_left_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.category_theory.oplax_functor.category_theory.category_struct_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.whisker_left_naturality_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.vcomp_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.whisker_left_naturality_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.id_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.id", "description": "The identity oplax natural transformation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.whisker_right_naturality_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.whisker_right_naturality_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.category_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.category", "description": "Category structure on the oplax natural transformations between oplax_functors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.modification_iso.of_components_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.whisker_right_naturality_naturality_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.naturality_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.whisker_right_naturality_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.naturality_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.whisker_right_naturality_comp_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.vcomp", "description": "Vertical composition of oplax natural transformations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.modification.id", "description": "The identity modification.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.naturality_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.naturality_comp_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.modification.whisker_right_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.modification.vcomp_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.modification.whisker_right_naturality_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.category_theory.oplax_functor.category_theory.category_struct_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.modification_iso.of_components", "description": "Construct a modification isomorphism between oplax natural transformations\nby giving object level isomorphisms, and checking naturality only in the forward direction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.whisker_left_naturality_comp_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.whisker_left_naturality_id_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.id_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.category_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.modification.id_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.modification.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.oplax_nat_trans.category_theory.oplax_functor.category_theory.category_struct", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subalgebra.pointwise_smul_to_subring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subalgebra.mul_self", "description": "As submodules, subalgebras are idempotent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subalgebra.pointwise_mul_action", "description": " The action on a subalgebra corresponding to applying the action to every element.\n\nThis is available as an instance in the `pointwise` locale.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subalgebra.mul_to_submodule_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subalgebra.smul_mem_pointwise_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subalgebra.pointwise_smul_to_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subalgebra.coe_pointwise_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subalgebra.pointwise_smul_to_subsemiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subalgebra.mul_to_submodule", "description": "When `A` is commutative, `subalgebra.mul_to_submodule_le` is strict.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_smul_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.sum_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.cast_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.sum_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.sum_smul_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp.induction_strongly_measurable", "description": " To prove something for an `Lp` function a.e. strongly measurable with respect to a\nsub-\u03c3-algebra `m` in a normed space, it suffices to show that\n* the property holds for (multiples of) characteristic functions which are measurable w.r.t. `m`;\n* is closed under addition;\n* the set of functions in `Lp` strongly measurable w.r.t. `m` for which the property holds is\n  closed.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_Lp_meas_indicator_const_Lp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable'.sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_trim_to_Lp_meas_ae_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp.induction_strongly_measurable_aux", "description": "Auxiliary lemma for `Lp.induction_strongly_measurable`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas.complete_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_to_Lp_trim_ae_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_complete_ae_strongly_measurable'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_subgroup_to_Lp_trim_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable'.ae_strongly_measurable'_of_measurable_space_le_on", "description": " If the restriction to a set `s` of a \u03c3-algebra `m` is included in the restriction to `s` of\nanother \u03c3-algebra `m\u2082` (hypothesis `hs`), the set `s` is `m` measurable and a function `f` almost\neverywhere supported on `s` is `m`-ae-strongly-measurable, then `f` is also\n`m\u2082`-ae-strongly-measurable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.strongly_measurable.ae_strongly_measurable'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.is_closed_ae_strongly_measurable'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_to_Lp_trim", "description": "Map from `Lp_meas` to `Lp F p (\u03bc.trim hm)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable'.continuous_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_subgroup_to_Lp_meas_iso", "description": "`Lp_meas_subgroup` and `Lp_meas` are isometric.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_eq_trim_iff_of_ae_strongly_measurable'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_trim_to_Lp_meas_subgroup", "description": " Map from `Lp F p (\u03bc.trim hm)` to `Lp_meas_subgroup`, inverse of\n`Lp_meas_subgroup_to_Lp_trim`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable'.ae_eq_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable'.neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_trim_to_Lp_meas_subgroup_ae_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas.ae_strongly_measurable'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_subgroup_to_Lp_trim_norm_map", "description": "`Lp_meas_subgroup_to_Lp_trim` preserves the norm.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable'", "description": " A function `f` verifies `ae_strongly_measurable' m f \u03bc` if it is `\u03bc`-a.e. equal to\nan `m`-strongly measurable function. This is similar to `ae_strongly_measurable`, but the\n`measurable_space` structures used for the measurability statement and for the measure are\ndifferent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable'.strongly_measurable_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_subgroup_to_Lp_trim_right_inv", "description": "`Lp_trim_to_Lp_meas_subgroup` is a right inverse of `Lp_meas_subgroup_to_Lp_trim`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_subgroup_to_Lp_trim_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.isometry_Lp_meas_subgroup_to_Lp_trim", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_to_Lp_trim_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_subgroup_to_Lp_trim_left_inv", "description": "`Lp_trim_to_Lp_meas_subgroup` is a left inverse of `Lp_meas_subgroup_to_Lp_trim`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_subgroup_to_Lp_trim_iso", "description": "`Lp_meas_subgroup` and `Lp F p (\u03bc.trim hm)` are isometric.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_subgroup_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_subgroup_to_Lp_trim", "description": "Map from `Lp_meas_subgroup` to `Lp F p (\u03bc.trim hm)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_Lp_meas_subgroup_to_Lp_of_trim", "description": " If `f` belongs to `Lp` for the measure `\u03bc.trim hm`, then it belongs to the subgroup\n`Lp_meas_subgroup F m p \u03bc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_to_Lp_trim_lie_symm_to_Lp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_subgroup", "description": " `Lp_meas_subgroup F m p \u03bc` is the subspace of `Lp F p \u03bc` containing functions `f` verifying\n`ae_strongly_measurable' m f \u03bc`, i.e. functions which are `\u03bc`-a.e. equal to\nan `m`-strongly measurable function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable'.const_inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable'_of_ae_strongly_measurable'_trim", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_subgroup.complete_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable'.const_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas", "description": " `Lp_meas F \ud835\udd5c m p \u03bc` is the subspace of `Lp F p \u03bc` containing functions `f` verifying\n`ae_strongly_measurable' m f \u03bc`, i.e. functions which are `\u03bc`-a.e. equal to\nan `m`-strongly measurable function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable'.mk", "description": "An `m`-strongly measurable function almost everywhere equal to `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_Lp_meas_subgroup_iff_ae_strongly_measurable'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_to_Lp_trim_lie", "description": "`Lp_meas` and `Lp F p (\u03bc.trim hm)` are isometric, with a linear equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_\u2112p_trim_of_mem_Lp_meas_subgroup", "description": " If `f` belongs to `Lp_meas_subgroup F m p \u03bc`, then the measurable function it is almost\neverywhere equal to (given by `ae_measurable.mk`) belongs to `\u2112p` for the measure `\u03bc.trim hm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_trim_to_Lp_meas", "description": "Map from `Lp F p (\u03bc.trim hm)` to `Lp_meas`, inverse of `Lp_meas_to_Lp_trim`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_Lp_meas_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable'.congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_subgroup_to_Lp_trim_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_subgroup_to_Lp_trim_ae_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_Lp_meas_iff_ae_strongly_measurable'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas.ae_fin_strongly_measurable'", "description": " We do not get `ae_fin_strongly_measurable f (\u03bc.trim hm)`, since we don't have\n`f =\u1d50[\u03bc.trim hm] Lp_meas_to_Lp_trim F \ud835\udd5c p \u03bc hm f` but only the weaker\n`f =\u1d50[\u03bc] Lp_meas_to_Lp_trim F \ud835\udd5c p \u03bc hm f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable.comp_ae_measurable'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.induction_strongly_measurable", "description": " To prove something for an arbitrary `mem_\u2112p` function a.e. strongly measurable with respect\nto a sub-\u03c3-algebra `m` in a normed space, it suffices to show that\n* the property holds for (multiples of) characteristic functions which are measurable w.r.t. `m`;\n* is closed under addition;\n* the set of functions in the `L\u1d56` space strongly measurable w.r.t. `m` for which the property\n  holds is closed.\n* the property is closed under the almost-everywhere equal relation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp_meas_to_Lp_trim_lie_symm_indicator", "description": " When applying the inverse of `Lp_meas_to_Lp_trim_lie` (which takes a function in the Lp space of\nthe sub-sigma algebra and returns its version in the larger Lp space) to an indicator of the\nsub-sigma-algebra, we obtain an indicator in the Lp space of the larger sigma-algebra.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable'.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.sr_mul_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.r_mul_sr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.r_mul_r", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.sr_mul_r", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.fintype", "description": "If `0 < n`, then `dihedral_group n` is a finite group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.exponent", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.nontrivial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group", "description": "For `n \u2260 0`, `dihedral_group n` represents the symmetry group of the regular `n`-gon.\n`r i` represents the rotations of the `n`-gon by `2\u03c0i/n`, and `sr i` represents the reflections of\nthe `n`-gon. `dihedral_group 0` corresponds to the infinite dihedral group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.r", "description": "For `n \u2260 0`, `dihedral_group n` represents the symmetry group of the regular `n`-gon.\n`r i` represents the rotations of the `n`-gon by `2\u03c0i/n`, and `sr i` represents the reflections of\nthe `n`-gon. `dihedral_group 0` corresponds to the infinite dihedral group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.sr", "description": "For `n \u2260 0`, `dihedral_group n` represents the symmetry group of the regular `n`-gon.\n`r i` represents the rotations of the `n`-gon by `2\u03c0i/n`, and `sr i` represents the reflections of\nthe `n`-gon. `dihedral_group 0` corresponds to the infinite dihedral group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.group", "description": "The group structure on `dihedral_group n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.sr_mul_sr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.r_one_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.r_one_pow_n", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.card", "description": "If `0 < n`, then `dihedral_group n` has `2n` elements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.order_of_sr", "description": "If `0 < n`, then `sr i` has order 2.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.order_of_r_one", "description": "If `0 < n`, then `r 1` has order `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.order_of_r", "description": "If `0 < n`, then `i : zmod n` has order `n / gcd n i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dihedral_group.one_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.basis_single_one", "description": "The basis on `\u03b9 \u2192\u2080 M` with basis vectors `\u03bb i, single i 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.basis_repr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.linear_independent_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.basis_single_one_repr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_single_ite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.basis", "description": "The basis on `\u03b9 \u2192\u2080 M` with basis vectors `\u03bb \u27e8i, x\u27e9, single i (b i x)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.coe_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.equiv_fun_symm_std_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.coe_basis_single_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.adjoin_restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.fg_trans'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fg_of_fg_of_fg", "description": " **Artin--Tate lemma**: if A \u2286 B \u2286 C is a chain of subrings of commutative rings, and\nA is noetherian, and C is algebra-finite over A, and C is module-finite over B,\nthen B is algebra-finite over A.\n\nReferences: Atiyah--Macdonald Proposition 7.8; Stacks 00IS; Altman--Kleiman 16.17.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_subalgebra_of_fg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.adjoin_algebra_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.adjoin_res_eq_adjoin_res", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.eval_squarefree", "description": "Evaluates the `squarefree` predicate on naturals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.min_sq_fac_aux", "description": "Assuming that `n` has no factors less than `k`, returns the smallest prime `p` such that\n `p^2 \u2223 n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.divisors_filter_squarefree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.squarefree_and_prime_pow_iff_prime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.squarefree_helper_0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.prove_squarefree_aux", "description": "Given `en`,`en1 := bit1 en`, `n1` the value of `en1`, `ek`,\n returns `\u22a2 squarefree_helper en ek`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.squarefree_bit10", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.squarefree_iff_factorization_le_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.squarefree_iff_nodup_factors", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.squarefree_helper_3", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.squarefree.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.min_sq_fac_has_prop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.min_sq_fac_prop", "description": "The correctness property of the return value of `min_sq_fac`.\n * If `none`, then `n` is squarefree;\n * If `some d`, then `d` is a minimal square factor of `n`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.min_sq_fac_le_of_dvd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.squarefree_pow_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.squarefree.factorization_le_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.squarefree_helper_4", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.squarefree_bit1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.squarefree_helper_2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.squarefree_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.min_sq_fac", "description": "Returns the smallest prime factor `p` of `n` such that `p^2 \u2223 n`, or `none` if there is no\n such `p` (that is, `n` is squarefree). See also `squarefree_iff_min_sq_fac`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.min_sq_fac_aux_has_prop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.not_squarefree_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.min_sq_fac_dvd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.squarefree_of_factorization_le_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.min_sq_fac_prop_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.squarefree_helper_1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.sum_divisors_filter_squarefree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.prove_squarefree", "description": "Given `n > 0` a squarefree natural numeral, returns `\u22a2 squarefree n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.squarefree_helper", "description": "A predicate representing partial progress in a proof of `squarefree`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.sq_mul_squarefree_of_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.squarefree_iff_prime_squarefree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.sq_mul_squarefree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.prove_non_squarefree", "description": "Given `e` a natural numeral and `a : nat` with `a^2 \u2223 n`, return `\u22a2 \u00ac squarefree e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.squarefree_iff_min_sq_fac", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.sq_mul_squarefree_of_pos'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.squarefree_mul", "description": " `squarefree` is multiplicative. Note that the \u2192 direction does not require `hmn`\nand generalizes to arbitrary commutative monoids. See `squarefree.of_mul_left` and\n`squarefree.of_mul_right` above for auxiliary lemmas.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.min_sq_fac_prime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.squarefree.decidable_pred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.lax_monoidal.\u03bc_natural", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.lax_monoidal_functor.of_\u03bc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.lax_monoidal_functor.obj.lax_monoidal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.lax_monoidal_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.lax_monoidal_functor.of", "description": "Construct a bundled `lax_monoidal_functor` from the object level function\nand `functorial` and `lax_monoidal` typeclasses.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.lax_monoidal_functor.of_to_functor_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.lax_monoidal.left_unitality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.lax_monoidal_functor.of_to_functor_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.lax_monoidal", "description": "An unbundled description of lax monoidal functors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.lax_monoidal.\u03b5", "description": "An unbundled description of lax monoidal functors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.lax_monoidal.\u03bc", "description": "An unbundled description of lax monoidal functors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.lax_monoidal.\u03bc_natural'", "description": "An unbundled description of lax monoidal functors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.lax_monoidal.associativity'", "description": "An unbundled description of lax monoidal functors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.lax_monoidal.left_unitality'", "description": "An unbundled description of lax monoidal functors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.lax_monoidal.right_unitality'", "description": "An unbundled description of lax monoidal functors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.lax_monoidal.associativity", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.lax_monoidal.right_unitality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.lax_monoidal_functor.of_\u03b5", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.monoidal_category.hexagon_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.monoidal_category.braiding_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.braiding", "description": "(implementation) the braiding for R-modules", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.monoidal_category.hexagon_forward", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.monoidal_category.braiding_inv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.monoidal_category.braiding_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.monoidal_category.symmetric_category", "description": "The symmetric monoidal structure on `Module R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.comp_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_unit", "description": "The presheaf valued in `unit` over any topological space is a sheaf.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.forget.faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf_inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.forget", "description": "The forgetful functor from sheaves to presheaves.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_iso_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_sheaf", "description": "The sheaf condition has several different equivalent formulations.\nThe official definition chosen here is in terms of grothendieck topologies so that the results on\nsites could be applied here easily, and this condition does not require additional constraints on\nthe value category.\nThe equivalent formulations of the sheaf condition on `presheaf C X` are as follows :\n\n1. `Top.presheaf.is_sheaf`: (the official definition)\n  It is a sheaf with respect to the grothendieck topology on `opens X`, which is to say:\n  For each open cover `{ U\u1d62 }` of `U`, and a family of compatible functions `A \u27f6 F(U\u1d62)` for an\n  `A : X`, there exists an unique gluing `A \u27f6 F(U)` compatible with the restriction.\n\n2. `Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_equalizer_products`: (requires `C` to have all products)\n  For each open cover `{ U\u1d62 }` of `U`, `F(U) \u27f6 \u220f F(U\u1d62)` is the equalizer of the two morphisms\n  `\u220f F(U\u1d62) \u27f6 \u220f F(U\u1d62 \u2229 U\u2c7c)`.\n  See `Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_iff_is_sheaf_equalizer_products`.\n\n3. `Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_opens_le_cover`:\n  For each open cover `{ U\u1d62 }` of `U`, `F(U)` is the limit of the diagram consisting of arrows\n  `F(V\u2081) \u27f6 F(V\u2082)` for every pair of open sets `V\u2081 \u2287 V\u2082` that are contained in some `U\u1d62`.\n  See `Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_iff_is_sheaf_opens_le_cover`.\n\n4. `Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_pairwise_intersections`:\n  For each open cover `{ U\u1d62 }` of `U`, `F(U)` is the limit of the diagram consisting of arrows\n  from `F(U\u1d62)` and `F(U\u2c7c)` to `F(U\u1d62 \u2229 U\u2c7c)` for each pair `(i, j)`.\n  See `Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_iff_is_sheaf_pairwise_intersections`.\n\nThe following requires `C` to be concrete and complete, and `forget C` to reflect isomorphisms and\npreserve limits. This applies to most \"algebraic\" categories, e.g. groups, abelian groups and rings.\n\n5. `Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_unique_gluing`:\n  (requires `C` to be concrete and complete; `forget C` to reflect isomorphisms and preserve limits)\n  For each open cover `{ U\u1d62 }` of `U`, and a compatible family of elements `x : F(U\u1d62)`, there exists\n  a unique gluing `x : F(U)` that restricts to the given elements.\n  See `Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_iff_is_sheaf_unique_gluing`.\n\n6. The underlying sheaf of types is a sheaf.\n  See `Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_iff_is_sheaf_comp` and\n  `category_theory.presheaf.is_sheaf_iff_is_sheaf_forget`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.forget.full", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.id_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf", "description": "A `sheaf C X` is a presheaf of objects from `C` over a (bundled) topological space `X`,\nsatisfying the sheaf condition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_of_iso", "description": "Transfer the sheaf condition across an isomorphism of presheaves.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.presheaf", "description": "The underlying presheaf of a sheaf", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_epi.of_arrow_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_mono_of_is_iso", "description": "An isomorphism is in particular a strong monomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_mono_category", "description": "A strong mono category is a category in which every monomorphism is strong.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_mono_category.strong_mono_of_mono", "description": "A strong mono category is a category in which every monomorphism is strong.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_epi_comp", "description": "The composition of two strong epimorphisms is a strong epimorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_mono.iff_of_arrow_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_epi_category", "description": "A strong epi category is a category in which every epimorphism is strong.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_epi_category.strong_epi_of_epi", "description": "A strong epi category is a category in which every epimorphism is strong.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_epi", "description": " A strong epimorphism `f` is an epimorphism which has the left lifting property\nwith respect to monomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_epi.epi", "description": " A strong epimorphism `f` is an epimorphism which has the left lifting property\nwith respect to monomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_epi.llp", "description": " A strong epimorphism `f` is an epimorphism which has the left lifting property\nwith respect to monomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.epi_of_strong_epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_mono.mk'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_mono", "description": " A strong monomorphism `f` is a monomorphism which has the right lifting property\nwith respect to epimorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_mono.mono", "description": " A strong monomorphism `f` is a monomorphism which has the right lifting property\nwith respect to epimorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_mono.rlp", "description": " A strong monomorphism `f` is a monomorphism which has the right lifting property\nwith respect to epimorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_epi_of_epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso_of_epi_of_strong_mono", "description": "A strong monomorphism that is an epimorphism is an isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_epi_of_is_iso", "description": "An isomorphism is in particular a strong epimorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_epi.iff_of_arrow_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_mono_of_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.balanced_of_strong_epi_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.mono_of_strong_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_epi.mk'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_mono_of_strong_mono", "description": "If `f \u226b g` is a strong monomorphism, then so is `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_mono_comp", "description": "The composition of two strong monomorphisms is a strong monomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso_of_mono_of_strong_epi", "description": "A strong epimorphism that is a monomorphism is an isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_epi_of_strong_epi", "description": "If `f \u226b g` is a strong epimorphism, then so is `g`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.balanced_of_strong_mono_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strong_mono.of_arrow_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bundle.continuous_linear_map.module", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "trivialization.continuous_linear_map", "description": " Given trivializations `e\u2081`, `e\u2082` in the atlas for vector bundles `E\u2081`, `E\u2082` over a base `B`,\nthe induced trivialization for the continuous `\u03c3`-semilinear maps from `E\u2081` to `E\u2082`,\nwhose base set is `e\u2081.base_set \u2229 e\u2082.base_set`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pretrivialization.continuous_linear_map", "description": " Given trivializations `e\u2081`, `e\u2082` for vector bundles `E\u2081`, `E\u2082` over a base `B`,\n`pretrivialization.continuous_linear_map \u03c3 e\u2081 e\u2082` is the induced pretrivialization for the\ncontinuous `\u03c3`-semilinear maps from `E\u2081` to `E\u2082`. That is, the map which will later become a\ntrivialization, after the bundle of continuous semilinear maps is equipped with the right\ntopological vector bundle structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pretrivialization.continuous_linear_map_symm_apply'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pretrivialization.continuous_linear_map_coord_change_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bundle.continuous_linear_map.mem_trivialization_atlas", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "trivialization.base_set_continuous_linear_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pretrivialization.continuous_linear_map_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hom_trivialization_at_source", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pretrivialization.continuous_linear_map.is_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hom_trivialization_at_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "trivialization.continuous_linear_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bundle.continuous_linear_map.vector_prebundle", "description": " The continuous `\u03c3`-semilinear maps between two topological vector bundles form a\n`vector_prebundle` (this is an auxiliary construction for the\n`vector_bundle` instance, in which the pretrivializations are collated but no topology\non the total space is yet provided).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pretrivialization.continuous_on_continuous_linear_map_coord_change", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bundle.continuous_linear_map.vector_bundle", "description": "The continuous `\u03c3`-semilinear_maps between two vector bundles form a vector bundle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hom_trivialization_at_target", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pretrivialization.continuous_linear_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bundle.continuous_linear_map", "description": " A reducible type synonym for the bundle of continuous (semi)linear maps. For some reason, it\nhelps with instance search.\n\nPorting note: after the port is done, we may want to remove this definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pretrivialization.continuous_linear_map_coord_change", "description": " Assume `e\u1d62` and `e\u1d62'` are trivializations of the bundles `E\u1d62` over base `B` with fiber `F\u1d62`\n(`i \u2208 {1,2}`), then `continuous_linear_map_coord_change \u03c3 e\u2081 e\u2081' e\u2082 e\u2082'` is the coordinate change\nfunction between the two induced (pre)trivializations\n`pretrivialization.continuous_linear_map \u03c3 e\u2081 e\u2082` and\n`pretrivialization.continuous_linear_map \u03c3 e\u2081' e\u2082'` of `bundle.continuous_linear_map`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bundle.continuous_linear_map.fiber_bundle", "description": "The continuous `\u03c3`-semilinear_maps between two vector bundles form a fiber bundle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bundle.continuous_linear_map.topological_space_total_space", "description": " Topology on the total space of the continuous `\u03c3`-semilinear_maps between two \"normable\" vector\nbundles over the same base.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.metrizable_space", "description": " A T\u2080 uniform space with countably generated `\ud835\udce4 X` is metrizable. This is not an instance to\navoid loops.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.dist_of_prenndist_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.metrizable_uniformity", "description": " If `X` is a uniform space with countably generated uniformity filter, there exists a\n`pseudo_metric_space` structure compatible with the `uniform_space` structure. Use\n`uniform_space.pseudo_metric_space` or `uniform_space.metric_space` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.dist_of_prenndist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.pseudo_metric_space", "description": "A `pseudo_metric_space` instance compatible with a given `uniform_space` structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.metric_space", "description": "A `metric_space` instance compatible with a given `uniform_space` structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.pseudo_metrizable_space", "description": "A uniform space with countably generated `\ud835\udce4 X` is pseudo metrizable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.of_prenndist", "description": " The maximal pseudo metric space structure on `X` such that `dist x y \u2264 d x y` for all `x y`,\nwhere `d : X \u2192 X \u2192 \u211d\u22650` is a function such that `d x x = 0` and `d x y = d y x` for all `x`, `y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.le_two_mul_dist_of_prenndist", "description": " Consider a function `d : X \u2192 X \u2192 \u211d\u22650` such that `d x x = 0` and `d x y = d y x` for all `x`,\n`y`. Let `dist` be the largest pseudometric distance such that `dist x y \u2264 d x y`, see\n`pseudo_metric_space.of_prenndist`. Suppose that `d` satisfies the following triangle-like\ninequality: `d x\u2081 x\u2084 \u2264 2 * max (d x\u2081 x\u2082, d x\u2082 x\u2083, d x\u2083 x\u2084)`. Then `d x y \u2264 2 * dist x y` for all\n`x`, `y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.Mon_.X.ring_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.inverse_obj", "description": "Converting a bundled algebra to a monoid object in `Module R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra_forget", "description": "The equivalence `Mon_ (Module R) \u224c Algebra R`\nis naturally compatible with the forgetful functors to `Module R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.Mon_.X.ring_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra", "description": "The category of internal monoid objects in `Module R`\nis equivalent to the category of \"native\" bundled `R`-algebras.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.Mon_.X.ring_npow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.functor_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.algebra_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.Mon_.X.algebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.inverse_map_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.Mon_.X.ring_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.functor", "description": "Converting a monoid object in `Module R` to a bundled algebra.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.functor_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.inverse_obj_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.Mon_.X.ring_nsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.Mon_.X.ring_zsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.Mon_.X.ring_int_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.inverse", "description": "Converting a bundled algebra to a monoid object in `Module R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.Mon_.X.ring_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.Mon_.X.ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.Mon_.X.ring_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.Mon_.X.ring_nat_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.inverse_obj_2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.Mon_.X.ring_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.inverse_obj_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Mon_Module_equivalence_Algebra.inverse_obj_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.metric_space", "description": "Convex bodies in a fixed normed space `V` form a metric space under the Hausdorff metric.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.add_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.convex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.coe_smul'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.has_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.coe_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.module", "description": "The convex bodies in a fixed space $V$ form a module over the nonnegative reals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body", "description": "Let `V` be a real topological vector space. A subset of `V` is a convex body if and only if\nit is convex, compact, and nonempty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.carrier", "description": "Let `V` be a real topological vector space. A subset of `V` is a convex body if and only if\nit is convex, compact, and nonempty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.convex'", "description": "Let `V` be a real topological vector space. A subset of `V` is a convex body if and only if\nit is convex, compact, and nonempty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.is_compact'", "description": "Let `V` be a real topological vector space. A subset of `V` is a convex body if and only if\nit is convex, compact, and nonempty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.nonempty'", "description": "Let `V` be a real topological vector space. A subset of `V` is a convex body if and only if\nit is convex, compact, and nonempty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.Hausdorff_edist_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.Hausdorff_edist_ne_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.pseudo_metric_space", "description": " Convex bodies in a fixed seminormed space $V$ form a pseudo-metric space under the Hausdorff\nmetric.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.coe_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.bounded", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.coe_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.coe_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.add_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.Hausdorff_dist_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.set_like", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.distrib_mul_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.is_compact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_body.nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_functor_perm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_functor_fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_functor_finset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_functor_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.to_list", "description": "create an exhaustive list of the values of a given type", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.psigma.fin_enum_prop_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum", "description": "`fin_enum \u03b1` means that `\u03b1` is finite and can be enumerated in some order,\n i.e. `\u03b1` has an explicit bijection with `fin n` for some n.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.card", "description": "`fin_enum \u03b1` means that `\u03b1` is finite and can be enumerated in some order,\n i.e. `\u03b1` has an explicit bijection with `fin n` for some n.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.equiv", "description": "`fin_enum \u03b1` means that `\u03b1` is finite and can be enumerated in some order,\n i.e. `\u03b1` has an explicit bijection with `fin n` for some n.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.dec_eq", "description": "`fin_enum \u03b1` means that `\u03b1` is finite and can be enumerated in some order,\n i.e. `\u03b1` has an explicit bijection with `fin n` for some n.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.of_injective", "description": "create a `fin_enum` instance using an injection", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.nodup_to_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.finset.enum", "description": "enumerate all finite sets of a given type", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.fin", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.of_nodup_list", "description": "create a `fin_enum` instance from an exhaustive list without duplicates", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.mem_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.of_surjective", "description": "create a `fin_enum` instance using a surjection", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.finset.mem_enum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.finset.fin_enum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.punit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.of_equiv", "description": "transport a `fin_enum` instance across an equivalence", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.pi", "description": "`pi xs f` creates the list of functions `g` such that, for `x \u2208 xs`, `g x \u2208 f x`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.pfun_fin_enum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.quotient.enum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.pi.fin_enum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.psigma.fin_enum_prop_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.subtype.fin_enum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.psigma.fin_enum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.mem_to_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.sigma.fin_enum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.of_list", "description": "create a `fin_enum` instance from an exhaustive list; duplicates are removed", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.psigma.fin_enum_prop_prop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.pi.mem_enum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.pempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.pi.enum", "description": "enumerate all functions whose domain and range are finitely enumerable", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.pi.tail", "description": " Given `f` a function whose domain is `x :: xs`, produce a function whose domain\nis restricted to `xs`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin_enum.pi.cons", "description": " For `pi.cons x xs y f` create a function where every `i \u2208 xs` is mapped to `f i` and\n`x` is mapped to `y`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_of_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_Pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_sigma", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_type", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_max", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small.mk'", "description": "Constructor for `small \u03b1` from an explicit witness type and equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_ulift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "shrink", "description": "An arbitrarily chosen model in `Type w` for a `w`-small type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_shrink", "description": "The noncomputable equivalence between a `w`-small type and a model.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_small_type", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small", "description": "A type is `small.{w}` if there exists an equivalence to some `S : Type w`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small.equiv_small", "description": "A type is `small.{w}` if there exists an equivalence to some `S : Type w`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_of_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone.ae_has_deriv_at", "description": " A monotone function is almost everywhere differentiable, with derivative equal to the\nRadon-Nikodym derivative of the associated Stieltjes measure with respect to Lebesgue.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone_on.ae_differentiable_within_at_of_mem", "description": " A real function which is monotone on a set is differentiable Lebesgue-almost everywhere on\nthis set. This version does not assume that `s` is measurable. For a formulation with\n`volume.restrict s` assuming that `s` is measurable, see `monotone_on.ae_differentiable_within_at`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stieltjes_function.ae_has_deriv_at", "description": " A Stieltjes function is almost everywhere differentiable, with derivative equal to the\nRadon-Nikodym derivative of the associated Stieltjes measure with respect to Lebesgue.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone.ae_differentiable_at", "description": "A monotone real function is differentiable Lebesgue-almost everywhere.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone_on.ae_differentiable_within_at", "description": " A real function which is monotone on a set is differentiable Lebesgue-almost everywhere on\nthis set. This version assumes that `s` is measurable and uses `volume.restrict s`.\nFor a formulation without measurability assumption,\nsee `monotone_on.ae_differentiable_within_at_of_mem`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_apply_add_mul_sq_div_sub", "description": " If `(f y - f x) / (y - x)` converges to a limit as `y` tends to `x`, then the same goes if\n`y` is shifted a little bit, i.e., `f (y + (y-x)^2) - f x) / (y - x)` converges to the same limit.\nThis lemma contains a slightly more general version of this statement (where one considers\nconvergence along some subfilter, typically `\ud835\udcdd[<] x` or `\ud835\udcdd[>] x`) tailored to the application\nto almost everywhere differentiability of monotone functions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.chebyshev.T_complex_cos", "description": " The `n`-th Chebyshev polynomial of the first kind evaluates on `cos \u03b8` to the\nvalue `cos (n * \u03b8)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.chebyshev.complex_of_real_eval_U", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.chebyshev.complex_of_real_eval_T", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.chebyshev.aeval_T", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.chebyshev.U_real_cos", "description": " The `n`-th Chebyshev polynomial of the second kind evaluates on `cos \u03b8` to the\nvalue `sin ((n + 1) * \u03b8) / sin \u03b8`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.chebyshev.U_complex_cos", "description": " The `n`-th Chebyshev polynomial of the second kind evaluates on `cos \u03b8` to the\nvalue `sin ((n + 1) * \u03b8) / sin \u03b8`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.chebyshev.aeval_U", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.chebyshev.algebra_map_eval_T", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.chebyshev.algebra_map_eval_U", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.chebyshev.T_real_cos", "description": " The `n`-th Chebyshev polynomial of the first kind evaluates on `cos \u03b8` to the\nvalue `cos (n * \u03b8)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "derangements.equiv.remove_none.fiber_some", "description": "For any `a : \u03b1`, the fiber over `some a` is the set of permutations\n   where `a` is the only possible fixed point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_derangements_iff_fixed_points_eq_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "derangements", "description": "A permutation is a derangement if it has no fixed points.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "derangements.derangements_recursion_equiv", "description": "The set of derangements on `option \u03b1` is equivalent to the union over all `a : \u03b1` of\n   \"derangements on `\u03b1` \u2295 derangements on `{a}\u1d9c`\".", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "derangements.derangements_option_equiv_sigma_at_most_one_fixed_point", "description": "The set of derangements on `option \u03b1` is equivalent to the union over `a : \u03b1`\n   of \"permutations with `a` the only possible fixed point\".", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "derangements.equiv.remove_none.mem_fiber", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "derangements.at_most_one_fixed_point_equiv_sum_derangements", "description": "The set of permutations that fix either `a` or nothing is equivalent to the sum of:\n   - derangements on `\u03b1`\n   - derangements on `\u03b1` minus `a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "derangements.equiv.remove_none.fiber", "description": "The set of permutations `f` such that the preimage of `(a, f)` under\n   `equiv.perm.decompose_option` is a derangement.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "derangements.subtype_equiv", "description": " Derangements on a subtype are equivalent to permutations on the original type where points are\nfixed iff they are not in the subtype.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.derangements_congr", "description": "If `\u03b1` is equivalent to `\u03b2`, then `derangements \u03b1` is equivalent to `derangements \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "derangements.equiv.remove_none.fiber_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.unique_units", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.unique_add_units", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.add_units_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.units_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.is_unit_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_Union_ball_eq_radius_pos_lt", "description": " Shrinking lemma for coverings by open balls in a proper metric space. A point-finite open cover\nof a proper metric space by nonempty open balls can be shrunk to a new cover by nonempty open balls\nso that each of the new balls has strictly smaller radius than the old one.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_locally_finite_subset_Union_ball_radius_lt", "description": " Let `R : \u03b1 \u2192 \u211d` be a (possibly discontinuous) function on a proper metric space.\nLet `s` be a closed set in `\u03b1` such that `R` is positive on `s`. Then there exists a collection of\npairs of balls `metric.ball (c i) (r i)`, `metric.ball (c i) (r' i)` such that\n\n* all centers belong to `s`;\n* for all `i` we have `0 < r i < r' i < R (c i)`;\n* the family of balls `metric.ball (c i) (r' i)` is locally finite;\n* the balls `metric.ball (c i) (r i)` cover `s`.\n\nThis is a simple corollary of `refinement_of_locally_compact_sigma_compact_of_nhds_basis_set`\nand `exists_subset_Union_ball_radius_pos_lt`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_locally_finite_Union_eq_ball_radius_lt", "description": " Let `R : \u03b1 \u2192 \u211d` be a (possibly discontinuous) positive function on a proper metric space. Then\nthere exists a collection of pairs of balls `metric.ball (c i) (r i)`, `metric.ball (c i) (r' i)`\nsuch that\n\n* for all `i` we have `0 < r i < r' i < R (c i)`;\n* the family of balls `metric.ball (c i) (r' i)` is locally finite;\n* the balls `metric.ball (c i) (r i)` cover the whole space.\n\nThis is a simple corollary of `refinement_of_locally_compact_sigma_compact_of_nhds_basis`\nand `exists_Union_ball_eq_radius_pos_lt` or `exists_locally_finite_subset_Union_ball_radius_lt`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_subset_Union_ball_radius_pos_lt", "description": " Shrinking lemma for coverings by open balls in a proper metric space. A point-finite open cover\nof a closed subset of a proper metric space by nonempty open balls can be shrunk to a new cover by\nnonempty open balls so that each of the new balls has strictly smaller radius than the old one.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_subset_Union_ball_radius_lt", "description": " Shrinking lemma for coverings by open balls in a proper metric space. A point-finite open cover\nof a closed subset of a proper metric space by open balls can be shrunk to a new cover by open balls\nso that each of the new balls has strictly smaller radius than the old one. This version assumes\nthat `\u03bb x, ball (c i) (r i)` is a locally finite covering and provides a covering indexed by the\nsame type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_Union_ball_eq_radius_lt", "description": " Shrinking lemma for coverings by open balls in a proper metric space. A point-finite open cover\nof a proper metric space by open balls can be shrunk to a new cover by open balls so that each of\nthe new balls has strictly smaller radius than the old one.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "emetric.exists_forall_closed_ball_subset_aux\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.exists_continuous_real_forall_closed_ball_subset", "description": " Let `X` be a metric space. Let `K : \u03b9 \u2192 set X` be a locally finite family of closed sets, let\n`U : \u03b9 \u2192 set X` be a family of open sets such that `K i \u2286 U i` for all `i`. Then there exists a\npositive continuous function `\u03b4 : C(X, \u211d)` such that for any `i` and `x \u2208 K i`, we have\n`metric.closed_ball x (\u03b4 x) \u2286 U i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "emetric.eventually_nhds_zero_forall_closed_ball_subset", "description": " Let `K : \u03b9 \u2192 set X` be a locally finitie family of closed sets in an emetric space. Let\n`U : \u03b9 \u2192 set X` be a family of open sets such that `K i \u2286 U i` for all `i`. Then for any point\n`x : X`, for sufficiently small `r : \u211d\u22650\u221e` and for `y` sufficiently close to `x`, for all `i`, if\n`y \u2208 K i`, then `emetric.closed_ball y r \u2286 U i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.exists_continuous_nnreal_forall_closed_ball_subset", "description": " Let `X` be a metric space. Let `K : \u03b9 \u2192 set X` be a locally finite family of closed sets, let\n`U : \u03b9 \u2192 set X` be a family of open sets such that `K i \u2286 U i` for all `i`. Then there exists a\npositive continuous function `\u03b4 : C(X, \u211d\u22650)` such that for any `i` and `x \u2208 K i`, we have\n`metric.closed_ball x (\u03b4 x) \u2286 U i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "emetric.exists_forall_closed_ball_subset_aux\u2081", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "emetric.exists_continuous_ennreal_forall_closed_ball_subset", "description": " Let `X` be an extended metric space. Let `K : \u03b9 \u2192 set X` be a locally finite family of closed\nsets, let `U : \u03b9 \u2192 set X` be a family of open sets such that `K i \u2286 U i` for all `i`. Then there\nexists a positive continuous function `\u03b4 : C(X, \u211d\u22650\u221e)` such that for any `i` and `x \u2208 K i`,\nwe have `emetric.closed_ball x (\u03b4 x) \u2286 U i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "emetric.exists_continuous_real_forall_closed_ball_subset", "description": " Let `X` be an extended metric space. Let `K : \u03b9 \u2192 set X` be a locally finite family of closed\nsets, let `U : \u03b9 \u2192 set X` be a family of open sets such that `K i \u2286 U i` for all `i`. Then there\nexists a positive continuous function `\u03b4 : C(X, \u211d)` such that for any `i` and `x \u2208 K i`,\nwe have `emetric.closed_ball x (ennreal.of_real (\u03b4 x)) \u2286 U i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "emetric.exists_continuous_nnreal_forall_closed_ball_subset", "description": " Let `X` be an extended metric space. Let `K : \u03b9 \u2192 set X` be a locally finite family of closed\nsets, let `U : \u03b9 \u2192 set X` be a family of open sets such that `K i \u2286 U i` for all `i`. Then there\nexists a positive continuous function `\u03b4 : C(X, \u211d\u22650)` such that for any `i` and `x \u2208 K i`,\nwe have `emetric.closed_ball x (\u03b4 x) \u2286 U i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.well_founded_lt_of_finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.lex.acc_of_single_erase", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.lex.well_founded", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.lex.well_founded_of_finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.lex.acc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.lex.well_founded_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.lex.acc_of_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.lex.well_founded_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.lex_fibration", "description": "This key lemma says that if a finitely supported dependent function `x\u2080` is obtained by merging\n two such functions `x\u2081` and `x\u2082`, and if we evolve `x\u2080` down the `dfinsupp.lex` relation one\n step and get `x`, we can always evolve one of `x\u2081` and `x\u2082` down the `dfinsupp.lex` relation\n one step while keeping the other unchanged, and merge them back (possibly in a different way)\n to get back `x`. In other words, the two parts evolve essentially independently under\n `dfinsupp.lex`. This is used to show that a function `x` is accessible if\n `dfinsupp.single i (x i)` is accessible for each `i` in the (finite) support of `x`\n (`dfinsupp.lex.acc_of_single`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.well_founded_lt'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.well_founded_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.lex.acc_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.lex.well_founded", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.lex.well_founded_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.lex.well_founded_lt_of_finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.well_founded_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.well_founded_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.lex.well_founded'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.lex.acc_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty.forall_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty.exists_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.is_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty.subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.is_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty_elim", "description": "Eliminate out of a type that `is_empty` (without using projection notation).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "empty.is_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_is_empty_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.is_empty_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty_or_nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum.is_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.extend_of_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty_pprod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pempty.is_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_nonempty_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pprod.is_empty_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_is_empty_of_nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded_of_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.is_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.is_empty_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype.is_empty_of_false", "description": "subtypes by an all-false predicate are false.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype.is_empty", "description": "subtypes of an empty type are empty", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "psum.is_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype.is_empty_false", "description": "subtypes by false are false.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty", "description": "`is_empty \u03b1` expresses that `\u03b1` is empty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty.false", "description": "`is_empty \u03b1` expresses that `\u03b1` is empty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty.elim", "description": "Eliminate out of a type that `is_empty` (using projection notation).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty.prop_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty_ulift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty.elim'", "description": "Non-dependent version of `is_empty.elim`. Helpful if the elaborator cannot elaborate `h.elim a`\n correctly.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pprod.is_empty_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty_sigma", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty_plift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty_Prop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty_psigma", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.is_empty_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "false.is_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_empty_psum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "map_lt_line_map_iff_slope_lt_slope", "description": " Given `c = line_map a b r`, `a < c < b`, the point `(c, f c)` is strictly below the\nsegment `[(a, f a), (b, f b)]` if and only if `slope f a c < slope f c b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_le_map_iff_slope_le_slope_left", "description": " Given `c = line_map a b r`, `a < c`, the point `(c, f c)` is non-strictly above the\nsegment `[(a, f a), (b, f b)]` if and only if `slope f a b \u2264 slope f a c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "right_lt_line_map_iff_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_le_right_iff_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_le_map_iff_slope_le_slope", "description": " Given `c = line_map a b r`, `a < c < b`, the point `(c, f c)` is non-strictly above the\nsegment `[(a, f a), (b, f b)]` if and only if `slope f c b \u2264 slope f a c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "map_le_line_map_iff_slope_le_slope_left", "description": " Given `c = line_map a b r`, `a < c`, the point `(c, f c)` is non-strictly below the\nsegment `[(a, f a), (b, f b)]` if and only if `slope f a c \u2264 slope f a b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_lt_left_iff_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "map_lt_line_map_iff_slope_lt_slope_left", "description": " Given `c = line_map a b r`, `a < c`, the point `(c, f c)` is strictly below the\nsegment `[(a, f a), (b, f b)]` if and only if `slope f a c < slope f a b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_strict_mono_endpoints", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_lt_map_iff_slope_lt_slope", "description": " Given `c = line_map a b r`, `a < c < b`, the point `(c, f c)` is strictly above the\nsegment `[(a, f a), (b, f b)]` if and only if `slope f c b < slope f a c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_le_left_iff_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_lt_map_iff_slope_lt_slope_left", "description": " Given `c = line_map a b r`, `a < c`, the point `(c, f c)` is strictly above the\nsegment `[(a, f a), (b, f b)]` if and only if `slope f a b < slope f a c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "map_lt_line_map_iff_slope_lt_slope_right", "description": " Given `c = line_map a b r`, `c < b`, the point `(c, f c)` is strictly below the\nsegment `[(a, f a), (b, f b)]` if and only if `slope f a b < slope f c b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "left_lt_line_map_iff_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "map_le_line_map_iff_slope_le_slope", "description": " Given `c = line_map a b r`, `a < c < b`, the point `(c, f c)` is non-strictly below the\nsegment `[(a, f a), (b, f b)]` if and only if `slope f a c \u2264 slope f c b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "midpoint_le_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_lt_right_iff_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_lt_line_map_iff_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_le_map_iff_slope_le_slope_right", "description": " Given `c = line_map a b r`, `c < b`, the point `(c, f c)` is non-strictly above the\nsegment `[(a, f a), (b, f b)]` if and only if `slope f c b \u2264 slope f a b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "map_le_line_map_iff_slope_le_slope_right", "description": " Given `c = line_map a b r`, `c < b`, the point `(c, f c)` is non-strictly below the\nsegment `[(a, f a), (b, f b)]` if and only if `slope f a b \u2264 slope f c b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_mono_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "midpoint_le_midpoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "midpoint_le_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_mono_endpoints", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_strict_mono_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "left_le_midpoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_le_line_map_iff_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "right_le_midpoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "right_le_line_map_iff_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "left_le_line_map_iff_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_strict_mono_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_lt_map_iff_slope_lt_slope_right", "description": " Given `c = line_map a b r`, `c < b`, the point `(c, f c)` is strictly above the\nsegment `[(a, f a), (b, f b)]` if and only if `slope f c b < slope f a b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_mono_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.of_convergents_eq_convergents'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.of_convergence", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.convergents_succ", "description": "The recurrence relation for the `convergents` of the continued fraction expansion\nof an element `v` of `K` in terms of the convergents of the inverse of its fractional part.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.of_is_simple_continued_fraction", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.of_convergence_epsilon", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continued_fraction.of", "description": "Creates the continued fraction of a value.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_continued_fraction.of_is_continued_fraction", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_continued_fraction.of", "description": "Creates the simple continued fraction of a value.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial", "description": "  The semiring of Laurent polynomials with coefficients in the semiring `R`.\nWe denote it by `R[T;T\u207b\u00b9]`.\nThe ring homomorphism `C : R \u2192+* R[T;T\u207b\u00b9]` includes `R` as the constant polynomials.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.degree_eq_bot_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.induction_on'", "description": "  To prove something about Laurent polynomials, it suffices to show that\n* the condition is closed under taking sums, and\n* it holds for monomials.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.reduce_to_polynomial_of_mul_T", "description": "  Suppose that `Q` is a statement about Laurent polynomials such that\n* `Q` is true on *ordinary* polynomials;\n* `Q (f * T)` implies `Q f`;\nit follow that `Q` is true on all Laurent polynomials.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.exists_T_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.degree", "description": "  The degree of a Laurent polynomial takes values in `with_bot \u2124`.\nIf `f : R[T;T\u207b\u00b9]` is a Laurent polynomial, then `f.degree` is the maximum of its support of `f`,\nor `\u22a5`, if `f = 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.induction_on_mul_T", "description": "This is a version of `exists_T_pow` stated as an induction principle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.trunc_C_mul_T", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.T_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.invertible_T", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.to_laurent", "description": "  The ring homomorphism, taking a polynomial with coefficients in `R` to a Laurent polynomial\nwith coefficients in `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.T_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.degree_T", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.degree_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.to_laurent_C_mul_T", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.degree_C_mul_T_ite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.to_laurent_alg_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.support_C_mul_T_of_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.support_C_mul_T", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.to_laurent_X_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.left_inverse_trunc_to_laurent", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.single_eq_C_mul_T", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.to_laurent_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.algebra_map_X_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.C", "description": "  The ring homomorphism `C`, including `R` into the ring of Laurent polynomials over `R` as\nthe constant Laurent polynomials.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.induction_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.degree_C_ite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.degree_C_mul_T", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.algebra_polynomial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.single_eq_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.to_laurent_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.module", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.T_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.algebra_map_eq_to_laurent", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.to_laurent_alg", "description": "  The `R`-algebra map, taking a polynomial with coefficients in `R` to a Laurent polynomial\nwith coefficients in `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.degree_C_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.degree_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.trunc", "description": "  `trunc : R[T;T\u207b\u00b9] \u2192+ R[X]` maps a Laurent polynomial `f` to the polynomial whose terms of\nnonnegative degree coincide with the ones of `f`.  The terms of negative degree of `f` \"vanish\".\n`trunc` is a left-inverse to `polynomial.to_laurent`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.single_zero_one_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.commute_T", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.T_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.to_laurent_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.inv_of_T", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.to_laurent_C_mul_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.to_laurent_apply", "description": " This is not a simp lemma, as it is usually preferable to use the lemmas about `C` and `X`\ninstead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.is_scalar_tower", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.is_unit_T", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.C_eq_algebra_map", "description": "When we have `[comm_semiring R]`, the function `C` is the same as `algebra_map R R[T;T\u207b\u00b9]`.\n(But note that `C` is defined when `R` is not necessarily commutative, in which case\n`algebra_map` is not available.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.degree_C_mul_T_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.to_laurent_support", "description": "  The support of a polynomial `f` is a finset in `\u2115`.  The lemma `to_laurent_support f`\nshows that the support of `f.to_laurent` is the same finset, but viewed in `\u2124` under the natural\ninclusion `\u2115 \u21aa \u2124`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.T", "description": "  The function `n \u21a6 T ^ n`, implemented as a sequence `\u2124 \u2192 R[T;T\u207b\u00b9]`.\n\nUsing directly `T ^ n` does not work, since we want the exponents to be of Type `\u2124` and there\nis no `\u2124`-power defined on `R[T;T\u207b\u00b9]`.  Using that `T` is a unit introduces extra coercions.\nFor these reasons, the definition of `T` is as a sequence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.algebra_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.to_laurent_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.trunc_to_laurent", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.is_localization", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.mul_T_assoc", "description": "The `simp` version of `mul_assoc`, in the presence of `T`'s.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.to_laurent_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.degree_T_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.to_laurent_C_mul_X_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.to_laurent_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "laurent_polynomial.T_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.signed_measure.exists_compl_positive_negative", "description": " Alternative formulation of `measure_theory.signed_measure.exists_is_compl_positive_negative`\n(the Hahn decomposition theorem) using set complements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.signed_measure.zero_mem_measure_of_negatives", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.signed_measure.exists_subset_restrict_nonpos", "description": "A measurable set of negative measure has a negative subset of negative measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.signed_measure.exists_is_compl_positive_negative", "description": " **The Hahn decomposition thoerem**: Given a signed measure `s`, there exist\ncomplement measurable sets `i` and `j` such that `i` is positive, `j` is negative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.signed_measure.of_symm_diff_compl_positive_negative", "description": "The symmetric difference of two Hahn decompositions has measure zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.signed_measure.bdd_below_measure_of_negatives", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.signed_measure.measure_of_negatives", "description": "The set of measures of the set of measurable negative sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_equiv.pi_fin_two_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_equiv.pi_fin_two_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_equiv.pi_fin_two", "description": "The product over `fin 2` of some rings is just the cartesian product of these rings.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.mul_infi_of_nonpos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.infi_mul_of_nonpos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.smul_infi_of_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.supr_mul_of_nonpos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.infi_mul_of_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.smul_supr_of_nonpos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.mul_supr_of_nonpos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.Inf_smul_of_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.Sup_smul_of_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.smul_infi_of_nonpos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.Inf_smul_of_nonpos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.mul_infi_of_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.Sup_smul_of_nonpos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.smul_supr_of_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.supr_mul_of_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.mul_supr_of_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.dual_prod_prod_isometry_to_linear_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.dual_prod", "description": " The symmetric bilinear form on `module.dual R M \u00d7 M` defined as\n`B (f, x) (g, y) = f y + g x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.dual_prod_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.to_dual_prod_isometry", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.dual_prod", "description": "The quadratic form on `module.dual R M \u00d7 M` defined as `Q (f, x) = f x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.is_symm_dual_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.dual_prod_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.to_dual_prod_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.nondenerate_dual_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bilin_form.dual_prod.to_quadratic_form", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.dual_prod_prod_isometry", "description": "`quadratic_form.dual_prod` commutes (isometrically) with `quadratic_form.prod`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.to_dual_prod", "description": " The isometry sending `(Q.prod $ -Q)` to `(quadratic_form.dual_prod R M)`.\n\nThis is `\u03c3` from Proposition 4.8, page 84 of\n[*Hermitian K-Theory and Geometric Applications*][hyman1973]; though we swap the order of the pairs.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.dual_prod_isometry", "description": "Any module isomorphism induces a quadratic isomorphism between the corresponding `dual_prod.`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.dual_prod_isometry_to_linear_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_noetherian.iff_fg", "description": "A module over a division ring is noetherian if and only if it is finitely generated.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_noetherian.finset_basis", "description": "In a noetherian module over a division ring, there exists a finite basis.\nThis is indexed by the `finset` `finite_dimensional.finset_basis_index`.\nThis is in contrast to the result `finite_basis_index (basis.of_vector_space K V)`,\nwhich provides a set and a `set.finite`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_noetherian.finset_basis_index", "description": " In a noetherian module over a division ring,\nthere exists a finite basis. This is the indexing `finset`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_noetherian.coe_sort_finset_basis_index", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_noetherian.coe_finset_basis_index", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_noetherian.finite_basis_index", "description": " In a noetherian module over a division ring,\nif a basis is indexed by a set, that set is finite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_noetherian.fintype_basis_index", "description": "In a noetherian module over a division ring, all bases are indexed by a finite type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_noetherian.range_finset_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_noetherian.iff_rank_lt_aleph_0", "description": "A module over a division ring is noetherian if and only if\nits dimension (as a cardinal) is strictly less than the first infinite cardinal `\u2135\u2080`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_noetherian.rank_lt_aleph_0", "description": " The dimension of a noetherian module over a division ring, as a cardinal,\nis strictly less than the first infinite cardinal `\u2135\u2080`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_noetherian.basis.of_vector_space_index.fintype", "description": " In a noetherian module over a division ring,\n`basis.of_vector_space` is indexed by a finite type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "transpose_dual_tensor_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hom_tensor_hom_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dual_tensor_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dual_tensor_hom_equiv_of_basis", "description": " If `M` is free, the natural linear map $M^* \u2297 N \u2192 Hom(M, N)$ is an equivalence. This function\nprovides this equivalence in return for a basis of `M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rtensor_hom_equiv_hom_rtensor_to_linear_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dual_tensor_hom_equiv_of_basis_to_linear_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_prod_map_dual_tensor_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "contract_right", "description": "The natural right-handed pairing between a module and its dual.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ltensor_hom_equiv_hom_ltensor_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hom_tensor_hom_equiv", "description": "When `M` and `N` are free `R` modules, the map `hom_tensor_hom_map` is an equivalence. Note that\n`hom_tensor_hom_equiv` is not defined directly in terms of `hom_tensor_hom_map`, but the equivalence\nbetween the two is given by `hom_tensor_hom_equiv_to_linear_map` and `hom_tensor_hom_equiv_apply`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "contract_left", "description": "The natural left-handed pairing between a module and its dual.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dual_tensor_hom_prod_map_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dual_tensor_hom_equiv_of_basis_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dual_tensor_hom_equiv_of_basis_symm_cancel_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ltensor_hom_equiv_hom_ltensor", "description": " When `M` is a finite free module, the map `ltensor_hom_to_hom_ltensor` is an equivalence. Note\nthat `ltensor_hom_equiv_hom_ltensor` is not defined directly in terms of\n`ltensor_hom_to_hom_ltensor`, but the equivalence between the two is given by\n`ltensor_hom_equiv_hom_ltensor_to_linear_map` and `ltensor_hom_equiv_hom_ltensor_apply`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dual_tensor_hom_equiv_of_basis_symm_cancel_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hom_tensor_hom_equiv_to_linear_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "contract_left_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rtensor_hom_equiv_hom_rtensor", "description": " When `M` is a finite free module, the map `rtensor_hom_to_hom_rtensor` is an equivalence. Note\nthat `rtensor_hom_equiv_hom_rtensor` is not defined directly in terms of\n`rtensor_hom_to_hom_rtensor`, but the equivalence between the two is given by\n`rtensor_hom_equiv_hom_rtensor_to_linear_map` and `rtensor_hom_equiv_hom_rtensor_apply`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "comp_dual_tensor_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "contract_right_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rtensor_hom_equiv_hom_rtensor_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ltensor_hom_equiv_hom_ltensor_to_linear_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "map_dual_tensor_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dual_tensor_hom", "description": "The natural map associating a linear map to the tensor product of two modules.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dual_tensor_hom_equiv", "description": " If `M` is finite free, the natural map $M^* \u2297 N \u2192 Hom(M, N)$ is an\nequivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "to_matrix_dual_tensor_hom", "description": " As a matrix, `dual_tensor_hom` evaluated on a basis element of `M* \u2297 N` is a matrix with a\nsingle one and zeros elsewhere", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "trans_gen_of_succ_of_lt", "description": "For `n < m`, `(n, m)` is in the transitive closure of a relation `~` if `i ~ succ i`\n for all `i` between `n` and `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "refl_trans_gen_of_succ", "description": "`(n, m)` is in the reflexive-transitive closure of `~` if `i ~ succ i` and `succ i ~ i`\n for all `i` between `n` and `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "trans_gen_of_succ_of_reflexive", "description": "`(n, m)` is in the transitive closure of a reflexive relation `~` if `i ~ succ i` and\n `succ i ~ i` for all `i` between `n` and `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "refl_trans_gen_of_succ_of_le", "description": "For `n \u2264 m`, `(n, m)` is in the reflexive-transitive closure of `~` if `i ~ succ i`\n for all `i` between `n` and `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "trans_gen_of_pred_of_ne", "description": "For `n \u2260 m`, `(n, m)` is in the transitive closure of a relation `~` if `i ~ pred i` and\n `pred i ~ i` for all `i` between `n` and `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "trans_gen_of_pred_of_lt", "description": "For `n < m`, `(n, m)` is in the transitive closure of a relation `~` for `n \u2260 m` if `pred i ~ i`\n for all `i` between `n` and `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "refl_trans_gen_of_pred_of_le", "description": "For `n \u2264 m`, `(n, m)` is in the reflexive-transitive closure of `~` if `pred i ~ i`\n for all `i` between `n` and `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "trans_gen_of_succ_of_ne", "description": "For `n \u2260 m`,`(n, m)` is in the transitive closure of a relation `~` if `i ~ succ i` and\n `succ i ~ i` for all `i` between `n` and `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "refl_trans_gen_of_pred", "description": "`(n, m)` is in the reflexive-transitive closure of `~` if `i ~ pred i` and `pred i ~ i`\n for all `i` between `n` and `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "refl_trans_gen_of_pred_of_ge", "description": "For `m \u2264 n`, `(n, m)` is in the reflexive-transitive closure of `~` if `i ~ pred i`\n for all `i` between `n` and `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "trans_gen_of_pred_of_gt", "description": "For `m < n`, `(n, m)` is in the transitive closure of a relation `~` for `n \u2260 m` if `i ~ pred i`\n for all `i` between `n` and `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "trans_gen_of_succ_of_gt", "description": "For `m < n`, `(n, m)` is in the transitive closure of a relation `~` if `succ i ~ i`\n for all `i` between `n` and `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "refl_trans_gen_of_succ_of_ge", "description": "For `m \u2264 n`, `(n, m)` is in the reflexive-transitive closure of `~` if `succ i ~ i`\n for all `i` between `n` and `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "trans_gen_of_pred_of_reflexive", "description": "`(n, m)` is in the transitive closure of a reflexive relation `~` if `i ~ pred i` and\n `pred i ~ i` for all `i` between `n` and `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_integer_of_is_root_of_monic", "description": " Integral root theorem:\nif `r : f.codomain` is a root of a monic polynomial over the ufd `A`,\nthen `r` is an integer", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "num_is_root_scale_roots_of_aeval_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "denom_dvd_of_is_root", "description": " Rational root theorem part 2:\nif `r : f.codomain` is a root of a polynomial over the ufd `A`,\nthen the denominator of `r` divides the leading coefficient", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "scale_roots_aeval_eq_zero_of_aeval_mk'_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_factorization_monoid.integer_of_integral", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "num_dvd_of_is_root", "description": " Rational root theorem part 1:\nif `r : f.codomain` is a root of a polynomial over the ufd `A`,\nthen the numerator of `r` divides the constant coefficient", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unique_factorization_monoid.is_integrally_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_field.two_pow_card", "description": "For every finite field `F` of odd characteristic, we have `2^(#F/2) = \u03c7\u2088(#F)` in `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char.card_pow_char_pow", "description": " If the square of the Gauss sum of a quadratic character is `\u03c7(-1) * #R`,\nthen we get, for all `n : \u2115`, the relation `(\u03c7(-1) * #R) ^ (p^n/2) = \u03c7(p^n)`,\nwhere `p` is the (odd) characteristic of the target ring `R'`.\nThis version can be used when `R` is not a field, e.g., `\u2124/8\u2124`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char.card_pow_card", "description": " When `F` and `F'` are finite fields and `\u03c7 : F \u2192 F'` is a nontrivial quadratic character,\nthen `(\u03c7(-1) * #F)^(#F'/2) = \u03c7(#F')`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauss_sum_frob", "description": " When `R'` has prime characteristic `p`, then the `p`th power of the Gauss sum\nof `\u03c7` and `\u03c8` is the Gauss sum of `\u03c7^p` and `\u03c8^p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_char.is_quadratic.gauss_sum_frob", "description": " For a quadratic character `\u03c7` and when the characteristic `p` of the target ring\nis a unit in the source ring, the `p`th power of the Gauss sum of`\u03c7` and `\u03c8` is\n`\u03c7 p` times the original Gauss sum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_char.is_quadratic.gauss_sum_frob_iter", "description": " For a quadratic character `\u03c7` and when the characteristic `p` of the target ring\nis a unit in the source ring and `n` is a natural number, the `p^n`th power of the Gauss\nsum of`\u03c7` and `\u03c8` is `\u03c7 (p^n)` times the original Gauss sum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauss_sum_mul_gauss_sum_eq_card", "description": " We have `gauss_sum \u03c7 \u03c8 * gauss_sum \u03c7\u207b\u00b9 \u03c8\u207b\u00b9 = fintype.card R`\nwhen `\u03c7` is nontrivial and `\u03c8` is primitive (and `R` is a field).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauss_sum_sq", "description": " When `\u03c7` is a nontrivial quadratic character, then the square of `gauss_sum \u03c7 \u03c8`\nis `\u03c7(-1)` times the cardinality of `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauss_sum", "description": "Definition of the Gauss sum associated to a multiplicative and an additive character.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauss_sum_mul_shift", "description": "Replacing `\u03c8` by `mul_shift \u03c8 a` and multiplying the Gauss sum by `\u03c7 a` does not change it.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.is_lawful_monad", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option.eq_of_eq_some", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option.eq_some_of_is_some", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option.eq_none_of_is_none", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "mul_semiring_action_hom.polynomial", "description": "An equivariant map induces an equivariant map on polynomials.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_semiring_action_hom.coe_polynomial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod_X_sub_smul.eval", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.eval_smul'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.smul_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.mul_semiring_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.smul_eval_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod_X_sub_smul.coeff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.smul_eval", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod_X_sub_smul.monic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod_X_sub_smul", "description": "the product of `(X - g \u2022 x)` over distinct `g \u2022 x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.smul_eq_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod_X_sub_smul.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "types.mono_over_equivalence_set_counit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "types.mono_over_equivalence_set_inverse_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sort.category_theory.well_powered", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "types.mono_over_equivalence_set", "description": "The category of `mono_over \u03b1`, for `\u03b1 : Type u`, is equivalent to the partial order `set \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "types.subobject_equiv_set", "description": "For `\u03b1 : Type u`, `subobject \u03b1` is order isomorphic to `set \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "types.mono_over_equivalence_set_functor_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype_val_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "types.mono_over_equivalence_set_functor_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "types.mono_over_equivalence_set_unit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "types.mono_over_equivalence_set_inverse_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "char.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "subtype.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "string.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unsigned.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "list.to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "nat.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "sum.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_to_string", "description": " Convert the object into a string for tracing/display purposes.\nSimilar to Haskell's `show`.\nSee also `has_repr`, which is used to output a string which is a valid lean code.\nSee also `has_to_format` and `has_to_tactic_format`, `format` has additional support for colours and pretty printing multilines.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_to_string.to_string", "description": " Convert the object into a string for tracing/display purposes.\nSimilar to Haskell's `show`.\nSee also `has_repr`, which is used to output a string which is a valid lean code.\nSee also `has_to_format` and `has_to_tactic_format`, `format` has additional support for colours and pretty printing multilines.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "bool.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "decidable.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "prod.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "list.to_string_aux", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unit.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "fin.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "list.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "omega.int.exprform.to_preform", "description": "Reification to an intermediate shadow syntax which eliminates exprs,\n   but still includes non-canonical terms", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.desugar", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.preprocess", "description": " If the goal has universal quantifiers over integers, introduce all of them.\nOtherwise, revert all hypotheses that are formulas of linear integer arithmetic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.to_exprterm", "description": "Reification to imtermediate shadow syntax that retains exprs", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.eq_int", "description": "Succeed iff argument is the expr of \u2124", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.prove_univ_close", "description": "Given a (p : preform), return the expr of a (t : univ_close m p)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.univ_close_of_unsat_clausify", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.intro_ints_core", "description": "Intro all universal quantifiers over \u2124", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.prove", "description": "Return expr of proof of current LIA goal", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.to_exprform", "description": "Reification to imtermediate shadow syntax that retains exprs", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega_int", "description": "The core omega tactic for integers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simp_attr.sugar", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.exprterm.exprs", "description": "List of all unreified exprs", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.wfx", "description": "Succeed iff argument is expr of term whose type is wff", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.intro_ints", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.exprterm.to_preterm", "description": "Reification to an intermediate shadow syntax which eliminates exprs,\n   but still includes non-canonical terms", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.wff", "description": "Check whether argument is expr of a well-formed formula of LIA", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.exprform.exprs", "description": "List of all unreified exprs", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.to_preform", "description": "Reification to an intermediate shadow syntax which eliminates exprs,\n   but still includes non-canonical terms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "interaction_monad", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interaction_monad.orelse'", "description": "Alternative orelse operator that allows to select which exception should be used.\n  The default is to use the first exception since the standard `orelse` uses the second.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interaction_monad.fail", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interaction_monad.result_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interaction_monad.result", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interaction_monad.result.success", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interaction_monad.result.exception", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interaction_monad_return", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interaction_monad_seq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interaction_monad.mk_exception", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interaction_monad.monad", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interaction_monad.failed", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interaction_monad_fmap", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interaction_monad_orelse", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interaction_monad.result.clamp_pos", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interaction_monad.bracket", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interaction_monad.result_has_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interaction_monad_bind", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interaction_monad.monad_fail", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "interaction_monad.silent_fail", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_eq_prod_automorphisms", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.power_basis.norm_gen_eq_prod_roots", "description": " Given `pb : power_basis R S`, then the norm of `pb.gen` is\n`((minpoly R pb.gen).map (algebra_map R F)).roots.prod`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_eq_prod_roots", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.is_integral_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_eq_zero_iff'", "description": "This is `algebra.norm_eq_zero_iff` composed with `algebra.norm_apply`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "intermediate_field.adjoin_simple.norm_gen_eq_prod_roots", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_eq_norm_adjoin", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_eq_matrix_det", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.prod_embeddings_eq_finrank_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.power_basis.norm_gen_eq_coeff_zero_minpoly", "description": " Given `pb : power_basis K S`, then the norm of `pb.gen` is\n`(-1) ^ pb.dim * coeff (minpoly K pb.gen) 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_eq_prod_embeddings_gen", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_algebra_map", "description": " If `x` is in the base field `K`, then the norm is `x ^ [L : K]`.\n\n(If `L` is not finite-dimensional over `K`, then `norm = 1 = x ^ 0 = x ^ (finrank L K)`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_eq_zero_iff_of_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_ne_zero_iff_of_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "intermediate_field.adjoin_simple.norm_gen_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm", "description": "The norm of an element `s` of an `R`-algebra is the determinant of `(*) s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_ne_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_eq_one_of_not_exists_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_eq_prod_embeddings", "description": " For `L/K` a finite separable extension of fields and `E` an algebraically closed extension\nof `K`, the norm (down to `K`) of an element `x` of `L` is equal to the product of the images\nof `x` over all the `K`-embeddings `\u03c3`  of `L` into `E`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_eq_one_of_not_module_finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_algebra_map_of_basis", "description": "If `x` is in the base ring `K`, then the norm is `x ^ [L : K]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.mono_of_nonzero_from_simple", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso_iff_nonzero", "description": "As a corollary of Schur's lemma for preadditive categories,\nany morphism between simple objects is (exclusively) either an isomorphism or zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.finrank_endomorphism_simple_eq_one", "description": "**Schur's lemma** for endomorphisms in `\ud835\udd5c`-linear categories.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.endomorphism_simple_eq_smul_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.finrank_hom_simple_simple_eq_zero_of_not_iso", "description": "Part of **Schur's lemma** for `\ud835\udd5c`-linear categories:\nthe hom space between two non-isomorphic simple objects is 0-dimensional.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.finrank_hom_simple_simple_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.End.division_ring", "description": "In any preadditive category with kernels,\nthe endomorphisms of a simple object form a division ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.finrank_endomorphism_eq_one", "description": "An auxiliary lemma for Schur's lemma.\n\nIf `X \u27f6 X` is finite dimensional, and every nonzero endomorphism is invertible,\nthen `X \u27f6 X` is 1-dimensional.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.finrank_hom_simple_simple_le_one", "description": "**Schur's lemma** for `\ud835\udd5c`-linear categories:\nif hom spaces are finite dimensional, then the hom space between simples is at most 1-dimensional.\n\nSee `finrank_hom_simple_simple_eq_one_iff` and `finrank_hom_simple_simple_eq_zero_iff` below\nfor the refinements when we know whether or not the simples are isomorphic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.finrank_hom_simple_simple_eq_one_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso_of_hom_simple", "description": "The part of **Schur's lemma** that holds in any preadditive category with kernels:\nthat a nonzero morphism between simple objects is an isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.field_End_of_finite_dimensional", "description": "Endomorphisms of a simple object form a field if they are finite dimensional.\nThis can't be an instance as `\ud835\udd5c` would be undetermined.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.finrank_hom_simple_simple", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "paracompact_of_compact", "description": "A compact space is paracompact.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "precise_refinement_set", "description": " In a paracompact space, every open covering of a closed set admits a locally finite refinement\nindexed by the same type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normal_of_paracompact_t2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "paracompact_of_locally_compact_sigma_compact", "description": " A locally compact sigma compact Hausdorff space is paracompact. See also\n`refinement_of_locally_compact_sigma_compact_of_nhds_basis` for a more precise statement.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "refinement_of_locally_compact_sigma_compact_of_nhds_basis", "description": " Let `X` be a locally compact sigma compact Hausdorff topological space. Suppose that for each\n`x` the sets `B x : \u03b9 x \u2192 set X` with the predicate `p x : \u03b9 x \u2192 Prop` form a basis of the filter\n`\ud835\udcdd x`. Then there exists a locally finite covering `\u03bb i, B (c i) (r i)` of `X` such that each `r i`\nsatisfies `p (c i)`\n\nThe notation is inspired by the case `B x r = metric.ball x r` but the theorem applies to\n`nhds_basis_opens` as well. If the covering must be subordinate to some open covering of `s`, then\nthe user should use a basis obtained by `filter.has_basis.restrict_subset` or a similar lemma, see\nthe proof of `paracompact_of_locally_compact_sigma_compact` for an example.\n\nThe formalization is based on two [ncatlab](https://ncatlab.org/) proofs:\n* [locally compact and sigma compact spaces are paracompact](https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/locally+compact+and+sigma-compact+spaces+are+paracompact);\n* [open cover of smooth manifold admits locally finite refinement by closed balls](https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/partition+of+unity#ExistenceOnSmoothManifolds).\n\nSee also `refinement_of_locally_compact_sigma_compact_of_nhds_basis_set` for a version of this lemma\ndealing with a covering of a closed set.\n\nIn most cases (namely, if `B c r \u222a B c r'` is again a set of the form `B c r''`) it is possible\nto choose `\u03b1 = X`. This fact is not yet formalized in `mathlib`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "precise_refinement", "description": " Any open cover of a paracompact space has a locally finite *precise* refinement, that is,\none indexed on the same type with each open set contained in the corresponding original one.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "paracompact_space", "description": " A topological space is called paracompact, if every open covering of this space admits a locally\nfinite refinement. We use the same universe for all types in the definition to avoid creating a\nclass like `paracompact_space.{u v}`. Due to lemma `precise_refinement` below, every open covering\n`s : \u03b1 \u2192 set X` indexed on `\u03b1 : Type v` has a *precise* locally finite refinement, i.e., a locally\nfinite refinement `t : \u03b1 \u2192 set X` indexed on the same type such that each `\u2200 i, t i \u2286 s i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "paracompact_space.locally_finite_refinement", "description": " A topological space is called paracompact, if every open covering of this space admits a locally\nfinite refinement. We use the same universe for all types in the definition to avoid creating a\nclass like `paracompact_space.{u v}`. Due to lemma `precise_refinement` below, every open covering\n`s : \u03b1 \u2192 set X` indexed on `\u03b1 : Type v` has a *precise* locally finite refinement, i.e., a locally\nfinite refinement `t : \u03b1 \u2192 set X` indexed on the same type such that each `\u2200 i, t i \u2286 s i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "refinement_of_locally_compact_sigma_compact_of_nhds_basis_set", "description": " Let `X` be a locally compact sigma compact Hausdorff topological space, let `s` be a closed set\nin `X`. Suppose that for each `x \u2208 s` the sets `B x : \u03b9 x \u2192 set X` with the predicate\n`p x : \u03b9 x \u2192 Prop` form a basis of the filter `\ud835\udcdd x`. Then there exists a locally finite covering\n`\u03bb i, B (c i) (r i)` of `s` such that all \u201ccenters\u201d `c i` belong to `s` and each `r i` satisfies\n`p (c i)`.\n\nThe notation is inspired by the case `B x r = metric.ball x r` but the theorem applies to\n`nhds_basis_opens` as well. If the covering must be subordinate to some open covering of `s`, then\nthe user should use a basis obtained by `filter.has_basis.restrict_subset` or a similar lemma, see\nthe proof of `paracompact_of_locally_compact_sigma_compact` for an example.\n\nThe formalization is based on two [ncatlab](https://ncatlab.org/) proofs:\n* [locally compact and sigma compact spaces are paracompact](https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/locally+compact+and+sigma-compact+spaces+are+paracompact);\n* [open cover of smooth manifold admits locally finite refinement by closed balls](https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/partition+of+unity#ExistenceOnSmoothManifolds).\n\nSee also `refinement_of_locally_compact_sigma_compact_of_nhds_basis` for a version of this lemma\ndealing with a covering of the whole space.\n\nIn most cases (namely, if `B c r \u222a B c r'` is again a set of the form `B c r''`) it is possible\nto choose `\u03b1 = X`. This fact is not yet formalized in `mathlib`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.append_stream", "description": "Append a stream to a list.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.tail", "description": "Tail of a stream: `stream.tail (h :: t) = t`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.cons", "description": "Prepend an element to a stream.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream", "description": "A stream `stream \u03b1` is an infinite sequence of elements of `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.head", "description": "Head of a stream: `stream.head s = stream.nth 0 s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.corec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.interleave", "description": "Interleave two streams.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.tails", "description": "Tails of a stream, starting with `stream.tail s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.drop", "description": "Drop first `n` elements of a stream.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.any", "description": "Proposition saying that at least one element of a stream satisfies a predicate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.cycle", "description": "Interpret a nonempty list as a cyclic stream.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.cycle_f", "description": "An auxiliary definition for `stream.cycle` corecursive def", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.corec_state", "description": "Use a state monad to generate a stream through corecursion", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.corec_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.odd", "description": "Elements of a stream with odd indices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.const", "description": "The constant stream: `stream.nth n (stream.const a) = a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.pure", "description": "A constant stream, same as `stream.const`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.cycle_g", "description": "An auxiliary definition for `stream.cycle` corecursive def", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.nats", "description": "The stream of natural numbers: `stream.nth n stream.nats = n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.zip", "description": " Zip two streams using a binary operation:\n`stream.nth n (stream.zip f s\u2081 s\u2082) = f (stream.nth s\u2081) (stream.nth s\u2082)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.map", "description": "Apply a function `f` to all elements of a stream `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.even", "description": "Elements of a stream with even indices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.inits_core", "description": "An auxiliary definition for `stream.inits`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.apply", "description": "Given a stream of functions and a stream of values, apply `n`-th function to `n`-th value.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.has_mem", "description": "`a \u2208 s` means that `a = stream.nth n s` for some `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.corec'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.take", "description": "`take n s` returns a list of the `n` first elements of stream `s`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.unfolds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.iterate", "description": "Iterates of a function as a stream.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.all", "description": "Proposition saying that all elements of a stream satisfy a predicate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.nth", "description": "`n`-th element of a stream.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.enum", "description": "Enumerate a stream by tagging each element with its index.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "stream.inits", "description": "Nonempty initial segments of a stream.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "presented_group.closure_rels_subset_ker", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "presented_group", "description": " Given a set of relations, rels, over a type `\u03b1`, presented_group constructs the group with\ngenerators `x : \u03b1` and relations `rels` as a quotient of free_group `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "presented_group.group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "presented_group.to_group.unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "presented_group.to_group.of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "presented_group.to_group_eq_one_of_mem_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "presented_group.of", "description": " `of` is the canonical map from `\u03b1` to a presented group with generators `x : \u03b1`. The term `x` is\nmapped to the equivalence class of the image of `x` in `free_group \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "presented_group.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "presented_group.to_group", "description": " The extension of a map `f : \u03b1 \u2192 G` that satisfies the given relations to a group homomorphism\nfrom `presented_group rels \u2192 G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.invertible_of_invertible_mul", "description": "A copy of `invertible_of_invertible_mul` using `\u2b1d` not `*`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_of_mul_self", "description": "A copy of `inv_of_mul_self` using `\u2b1d` not `*`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_of_mul_self_assoc", "description": "A copy of `inv_of_mul_self_assoc` using `\u2b1d` not `*`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "invertible.matrix_mul_left", "description": "A copy of `invertible.mul_left` using `\u2b1d` not `*`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.inv_of_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_inv_of_mul_self_cancel", "description": "A copy of `mul_inv_of_mul_self_cancel` using `\u2b1d` not `*`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_mul_inv_of_self_cancel", "description": "A copy of `mul_mul_inv_of_self_cancel` using `\u2b1d` not `*`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.invertible_mul", "description": "A copy of `invertible_mul` using `\u2b1d` not `*`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "invertible.matrix_mul", "description": "A copy of `invertible.mul` using `\u2b1d` not `*`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_inv_of_self_assoc", "description": "A copy of `mul_inv_of_self_assoc` using `\u2b1d` not `*`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.invertible_of_mul_invertible", "description": "A copy of `invertible_of_mul_invertible` using `\u2b1d` not `*`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "invertible.matrix_mul_right", "description": "A copy of `invertible.mul_right` using `\u2b1d` not `*`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_inv_of_self", "description": "A copy of `mul_inv_of_self` using `\u2b1d` not `*`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.coe_complex_im_j", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.norm_one_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.cstar_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.coe_of_complex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.coe_complex_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.tsum_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.continuous_im_i", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.linear_isometry_equiv_tuple_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.normed_division_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.linear_isometry_equiv_tuple_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.coe_complex_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.complete_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.continuous_im_k", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.coe_complex_im_k", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.inner_product_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.nnnorm_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.continuous_re", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.nnnorm_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.continuous_im", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.coe_complex_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.norm_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.inner_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.continuous_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.continuous_im_j", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.coe_complex_im_i", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.has_inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.summable_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.has_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.coe_complex_re", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.inner_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.has_sum_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.norm_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.normed_algebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.linear_isometry_equiv_tuple", "description": "`quaternion_algebra.linear_equiv_tuple` as a `linear_isometry_equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.norm_pi_Lp_equiv_symm_equiv_tuple", "description": "The norm of the components as a euclidean vector equals the norm of the quaternion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.continuous_norm_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.coe_complex_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.coe_real_complex_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.of_complex", "description": "Coercion `\u2102 \u2192\u2090[\u211d] \u210d` as an algebra homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.norm_sq_eq_norm_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.normed_add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion.coe_complex_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cantor_scheme.disjoint", "description": "A scheme is disjoint if the children of each set of pairwise disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cantor_scheme.induced_map", "description": " From a `\u03b2`-scheme on `\u03b1` `A`, we define a partial function from `(\u2115 \u2192 \u03b2)` to `\u03b1`\nwhich sends each infinite sequence `x` to an element of the intersection along the\nbranch corresponding to `x`, if it exists.\nWe call this the map induced by the scheme.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cantor_scheme.closure_antitone.map_of_vanishing_diam", "description": " A scheme on a complete space with vanishing diameter\nsuch that each set contains the closure of its children\ninduces a total map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cantor_scheme.map_mem", "description": " If `x` is in the domain of the induced map of a scheme `A`,\nits image under this map is in each set along the corresponding branch.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cantor_scheme.vanishing_diam.map_continuous", "description": "A scheme with vanishing diameter along each branch induces a continuous map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cantor_scheme.antitone", "description": "A scheme is antitone if each set contains its children.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cantor_scheme.disjoint.map_injective", "description": "A scheme where the children of each set are pairwise disjoint induces an injective map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cantor_scheme.closure_antitone", "description": " A useful strengthening of being antitone is to require that each set contains\nthe closure of each of its children.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cantor_scheme.antitone.closure_antitone", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cantor_scheme.closure_antitone.antitone", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cantor_scheme.vanishing_diam.dist_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cantor_scheme.vanishing_diam", "description": "A scheme on a metric space has vanishing diameter if diameter approaches 0 along each branch.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.cod_restrict_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.option_embedding_equiv_apply_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype_or_equiv", "description": " A subtype `{x // p x \u2228 q x}` over a disjunction of `p q : \u03b1 \u2192 Prop` is equivalent to a sum of\nsubtypes `{x // p x} \u2295 {x // q x}` such that `\u00ac p x` is sent to the right, when\n`disjoint p q`.\n\nSee also `equiv.sum_compl`, for when `is_compl p q`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.cod_restrict", "description": "Restrict the codomain of an embedding.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.as_embedding_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.option_embedding_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype_or_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.embedding_of_subset", "description": "The injection map is an embedding between subsets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.option_embedding_equiv_apply_snd_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.option_elim", "description": " Given an embedding `f : \u03b1 \u21aa \u03b2` and a point outside of `set.range f`, construct an embedding\n`option \u03b1 \u21aa \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.coe_with_top", "description": "Embedding into `with_top \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.option_elim_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.image", "description": "`set.image` as an embedding `set \u03b1 \u21aa set \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.coe_with_top_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype_or_equiv_symm_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.embedding_of_subset_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.image_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype_or_equiv_symm_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.option_embedding_equiv", "description": "Equivalence between embeddings of `option \u03b1` and a sigma type over the embeddings of `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "relation.cut_expand_le_inv_image_lex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.cut_expand", "description": "`cut_expand r` is well-founded when `r` is.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "relation.cut_expand_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "relation.cut_expand_singleton_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "relation.cut_expand_add_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "relation.cut_expand_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "relation.not_cut_expand_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "relation.cut_expand_fibration", "description": "For any relation `r` on `\u03b1`, multiset addition `multiset \u03b1 \u00d7 multiset \u03b1 \u2192 multiset \u03b1` is a\n fibration between the game sum of `cut_expand r` with itself and `cut_expand r` itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "relation.acc_of_singleton", "description": "A multiset is accessible under `cut_expand` if all its singleton subsets are,\n assuming `r` is irreflexive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "acc.cut_expand", "description": "A singleton `{a}` is accessible under `cut_expand r` if `a` is accessible under `r`,\n assuming `r` is irreflexive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "relation.cut_expand", "description": "The relation that specifies valid moves in our hydra game. `cut_expand r s' s`\n means that `s'` is obtained by removing one head `a \u2208 s` and adding back an arbitrary\n multiset `t` of heads such that all `a' \u2208 t` satisfy `r a' a`.\n\n This is most directly translated into `s' = s.erase a + t`, but `multiset.erase` requires\n `decidable_eq \u03b1`, so we use the equivalent condition `s' + {a} = s + t` instead, which\n is also easier to verify for explicit multisets `s'`, `s` and `t`.\n\n We also don't include the condition `a \u2208 s` because `s' + {a} = s + t` already\n guarantees `a \u2208 s + t`, and if `r` is irreflexive then `a \u2209 t`, which is the\n case when `r` is well-founded, the case we are primarily interested in.\n\n The lemma `relation.cut_expand_iff` below converts between this convenient definition\n and the direct translation when `r` is irreflexive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.is_positive.orthogonal_projection_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.is_positive.conj_orthogonal_projection", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.is_positive_iff_complex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.is_positive.is_self_adjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.is_positive.inner_nonneg_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.is_positive_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.is_positive.adjoint_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.is_positive", "description": "A continuous linear endomorphism `T` of a Hilbert space is **positive** if it is self adjoint\n and `\u2200 x, 0 \u2264 re \u27eaT x, x\u27eb`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.is_positive.conj_adjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.is_positive.inner_nonneg_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.is_positive.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.is_positive_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.symmetric_of_chosen_finite_products", "description": "The monoidal structure coming from finite products is symmetric.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_of_chosen_finite_products.hexagon_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_of_chosen_finite_products.hexagon_forward", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_of_chosen_finite_products.braiding_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_of_chosen_finite_products.symmetry", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.degree_le_of_ne_zero", "description": " If an element `x` is a root of a nonzero polynomial `p`, then the degree of `p` is at least the\ndegree of the minimal polynomial of `x`. See also `gcd_domain_degree_le_of_ne_zero` which relaxes\nthe assumptions on `A` in exchange for stronger assumptions on `B`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.fintype.subtype_prod", "description": "A technical finiteness result.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.eq_X_sub_C'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.unique", "description": " The minimal polynomial of an element `x` is uniquely characterized by its defining property:\nif there is another monic polynomial of minimal degree that has `x` as a root, then this polynomial\nis equal to the minimal polynomial of `x`. See also `minpoly.gcd_unique` which relaxes the\nassumptions on `A` in exchange for stronger assumptions on `B`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.aeval_of_is_scalar_tower", "description": "If `y` is a conjugate of `x` over a field `K`, then it is a conjugate over a subring `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.coeff_zero_ne_zero", "description": "The minimal polynomial of a nonzero element has nonzero constant coefficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.one", "description": "The minimal polynomial of `1` is `X - 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.roots_of_min_poly_pi_type", "description": "Function from Hom_K(E,L) to pi type \u03a0 (x : basis), roots of min poly of x", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.zero", "description": "The minimal polynomial of `0` is `X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.coeff_zero_eq_zero", "description": "The constant coefficient of the minimal polynomial of `x` is `0` if and only if `x = 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.eq_of_algebra_map_eq", "description": " If `y` is the image of `x` in an extension, their minimal polynomials coincide.\n\nWe take `h : y = algebra_map L T x` as an argument because `rw h` typically fails\nsince `is_integral R y` depends on y.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.dvd_map_of_is_scalar_tower", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.dvd_map_of_is_scalar_tower'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.root", "description": " If `L/K` is a field extension and an element `y` of `K` is a root of the minimal polynomial\nof an element `x \u2208 L`, then `y` maps to `x` under the field embedding.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.add_algebra_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.aux_inj_roots_of_min_poly", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.sub_algebra_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.dvd", "description": " If an element `x` is a root of a polynomial `p`, then the minimal polynomial of `x` divides `p`.\nSee also `minpoly.gcd_domain_dvd` which relaxes the assumptions on `A` in exchange for stronger\nassumptions on `B`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.alg_hom.fintype", "description": "Given field extensions `E/F` and `K/F`, with `E/F` finite, there are finitely many `F`-algebra\n homomorphisms `E \u2192\u2090[K] K`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.prime", "description": "A minimal polynomial is prime.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.ne_zero_of_finite_field_extension", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.eq_X_sub_C", "description": " If `B/K` is a nontrivial algebra over a field, and `x` is an element of `K`,\nthen the minimal polynomial of `algebra_map K B x` is `X - C x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.eq_of_irreducible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minpoly.eq_of_irreducible_of_monic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_clopen_upper_of_not_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "priestley_space", "description": " A Priestley space is an ordered topological space such that any two distinct points can be\nseparated by a clopen upper set. Compactness is often assumed, but we do not include it here.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "priestley_space.priestley", "description": " A Priestley space is an ordered topological space such that any two distinct points can be\nseparated by a clopen upper set. Compactness is often assumed, but we do not include it here.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_clopen_lower_of_not_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "priestley_space.to_t2_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_clopen_upper_or_lower_of_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coheyting.boundary_sup_sup_boundary_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coheyting.boundary", "description": " The boundary of an element of a co-Heyting algebra is the intersection of its Heyting negation\nwith itself. Note that this is always `\u22a5` for a boolean algebra.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coheyting.boundary_sup_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coheyting.boundary_inf", "description": "**Leibniz rule** for the co-Heyting boundary.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coheyting.boundary_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coheyting.boundary_inf_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coheyting.hnot_boundary", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coheyting.boundary_hnot_hnot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coheyting.boundary_hnot_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coheyting.boundary_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coheyting.hnot_hnot_sup_boundary", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coheyting.inf_hnot_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coheyting.boundary_eq_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coheyting.hnot_eq_top_iff_exists_boundary", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coheyting.boundary_le_hnot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coheyting.boundary_le_boundary_sup_sup_boundary_inf_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coheyting.boundary_le_boundary_sup_sup_boundary_inf_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coheyting.boundary_idem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coheyting.boundary_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inter_minimals_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minimals_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximals_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_antichain.maximals_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximals_antichain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximals_inter_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minimals_antichain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximals_eq_minimals", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximals_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minimals_idem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minimals_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_antichain.max_maximals", "description": " If `maximals r s` is included in but *shadows* the antichain `t`, then it is actually\nequal to `t`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_of_mem_minimals", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_antichain.maximals_lower_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximals_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.subsingleton.minimals_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inter_maximals_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minimals_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minimals", "description": "Turns a set into an antichain by keeping only the \"minimal\" elements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minimals_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_antichain.minimals_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_least.mem_minimals", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_antichain.minimals_upper_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximals", "description": "Turns a set into an antichain by keeping only the \"maximal\" elements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minimals_inter_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_greatest.maximals_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximals_idem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_greatest.mem_maximals", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_of_mem_maximals", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximals_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_least.minimals_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximals_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.subsingleton.maximals_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minimals_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_antichain.max_minimals", "description": " If `minimals r s` is included in but *shadows* the antichain `t`, then it is actually\nequal to `t`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximals_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "minimals_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.morph_components.post_comp_b", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.morph_components.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.morph_components.pre_comp_b", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.morph_components", "description": " The structure `morph_components` is an ad hoc structure that is used in\nthe proof that `N\u2081 : simplicial_object C \u2964 karoubi (chain_complex C \u2115))`\nreflects isomorphisms. The fields are the data that are needed in order to\nconstruct a morphism `X _[n+1] \u27f6 Z` (see `\u03c6`) using the decomposition of the\nidentity given by `decomposition_Q n (n+1)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.morph_components.a", "description": " The structure `morph_components` is an ad hoc structure that is used in\nthe proof that `N\u2081 : simplicial_object C \u2964 karoubi (chain_complex C \u2115))`\nreflects isomorphisms. The fields are the data that are needed in order to\nconstruct a morphism `X _[n+1] \u27f6 Z` (see `\u03c6`) using the decomposition of the\nidentity given by `decomposition_Q n (n+1)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.morph_components.b", "description": " The structure `morph_components` is an ad hoc structure that is used in\nthe proof that `N\u2081 : simplicial_object C \u2964 karoubi (chain_complex C \u2115))`\nreflects isomorphisms. The fields are the data that are needed in order to\nconstruct a morphism `X _[n+1] \u27f6 Z` (see `\u03c6`) using the decomposition of the\nidentity given by `decomposition_Q n (n+1)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.morph_components.post_comp_\u03c6", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.morph_components.pre_comp_\u03c6", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.morph_components.pre_comp_a", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.morph_components.post_comp_a", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.morph_components.post_comp", "description": "A `morph_components` can be postcomposed with a morphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.decomposition_Q", "description": " In each positive degree, this lemma decomposes the idempotent endomorphism\n`Q q` as a sum of morphisms which are postcompositions with suitable degeneracies.\nAs `Q q` is the complement projection to `P q`, this implies that in the case of\nsimplicial abelian groups, any $(n+1)$-simplex $x$ can be decomposed as\n$x = x' + \\sum (i=0}^{q-1} \u03c3_{n-i}(y_i)$ where $x'$ is in the image of `P q` and\nthe $y_i$ are in degree $n$.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.morph_components.pre_comp", "description": "A `morph_components` can be precomposed with a morphism of simplicial objects.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.morph_components.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.morph_components.id_b", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.morph_components.\u03c6", "description": "The morphism `X _[n+1] \u27f6 Z ` associated to `f : morph_components X n Z`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.morph_components.id", "description": " the canonical `morph_components` whose associated morphism is the identity\n(see `F_id`) thanks to `decomposition_Q n (n+1)`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.morph_components.id_a", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.morph_components.id_\u03c6", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "should_be_linted", "description": " `should_be_linted linter decl` returns true if `decl` should be checked\nusing `linter`, i.e., if there is no `nolint` attribute.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nolint_attr", "description": "Defines the user attribute `nolint` for skipping `#lint`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.attribute.nolint", "description": "Defines the user attribute `nolint` for skipping `#lint`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.attribute.linter", "description": " Defines the user attribute `linter` for adding a linter to the default set.\nLinters should be defined in the `linter` namespace.\nA linter `linter.my_new_linter` is referred to as `my_new_linter` (without the `linter` namespace)\nwhen used in `#lint`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter_attr", "description": " Defines the user attribute `linter` for adding a linter to the default set.\nLinters should be defined in the `linter` namespace.\nA linter `linter.my_new_linter` is referred to as `my_new_linter` (without the `linter` namespace)\nwhen used in `#lint`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter", "description": "A linting test for the `#lint` command.\n\n`test` defines a test to perform on every declaration. It should never fail. Returning `none`\nsignifies a passing test. Returning `some msg` reports a failing test with error `msg`.\n\n`no_errors_found` is the message printed when all tests are negative, and `errors_found` is printed\nwhen at least one test is positive.\n\nIf `is_fast` is false, this test will be omitted from `#lint-`.\n\nIf `auto_decls` is true, this test will also be executed on automatically generated declarations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.test", "description": "A linting test for the `#lint` command.\n\n`test` defines a test to perform on every declaration. It should never fail. Returning `none`\nsignifies a passing test. Returning `some msg` reports a failing test with error `msg`.\n\n`no_errors_found` is the message printed when all tests are negative, and `errors_found` is printed\nwhen at least one test is positive.\n\nIf `is_fast` is false, this test will be omitted from `#lint-`.\n\nIf `auto_decls` is true, this test will also be executed on automatically generated declarations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.no_errors_found", "description": "A linting test for the `#lint` command.\n\n`test` defines a test to perform on every declaration. It should never fail. Returning `none`\nsignifies a passing test. Returning `some msg` reports a failing test with error `msg`.\n\n`no_errors_found` is the message printed when all tests are negative, and `errors_found` is printed\nwhen at least one test is positive.\n\nIf `is_fast` is false, this test will be omitted from `#lint-`.\n\nIf `auto_decls` is true, this test will also be executed on automatically generated declarations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.errors_found", "description": "A linting test for the `#lint` command.\n\n`test` defines a test to perform on every declaration. It should never fail. Returning `none`\nsignifies a passing test. Returning `some msg` reports a failing test with error `msg`.\n\n`no_errors_found` is the message printed when all tests are negative, and `errors_found` is printed\nwhen at least one test is positive.\n\nIf `is_fast` is false, this test will be omitted from `#lint-`.\n\nIf `auto_decls` is true, this test will also be executed on automatically generated declarations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.is_fast", "description": "A linting test for the `#lint` command.\n\n`test` defines a test to perform on every declaration. It should never fail. Returning `none`\nsignifies a passing test. Returning `some msg` reports a failing test with error `msg`.\n\n`no_errors_found` is the message printed when all tests are negative, and `errors_found` is printed\nwhen at least one test is positive.\n\nIf `is_fast` is false, this test will be omitted from `#lint-`.\n\nIf `auto_decls` is true, this test will also be executed on automatically generated declarations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.auto_decls", "description": "A linting test for the `#lint` command.\n\n`test` defines a test to perform on every declaration. It should never fail. Returning `none`\nsignifies a passing test. Returning `some msg` reports a failing test with error `msg`.\n\n`no_errors_found` is the message printed when all tests are negative, and `errors_found` is printed\nwhen at least one test is positive.\n\nIf `is_fast` is false, this test will be omitted from `#lint-`.\n\nIf `auto_decls` is true, this test will also be executed on automatically generated declarations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "get_linters", "description": " Takes a list of names that resolve to declarations of type `linter`,\nand produces a list of linters.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.linear_ordered_field", "description": "Pullback a `linear_ordered_field` under an injective map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.linear_ordered_semifield", "description": "Pullback a `linear_ordered_semifield` under an injective map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.map_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.id_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.lex.is_trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.map_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.swap_injective", "d