, {"name": "monotone_on.monovary_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary.comp_monotone_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary", "description": "`f` antivaries with `g` if `g i < g j` implies `f j \u2264 f i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary.dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_on.dual_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_on_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_on_to_dual_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_on.dual_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_on.symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_to_dual_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_on.dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_on.dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_on.comp_monotone_on_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_id_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_on.subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_on_const_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary.dual_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_on_id_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_on", "description": "`f` monovaries with `g` on `s` if `g i < g j` implies `f i \u2264 f j` for all `i, j \u2208 s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone.antivary", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_on.comp_antitone_on_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_const_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_const_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_on_to_dual_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary", "description": "`f` monovaries with `g` if `g i < g j` implies `f i \u2264 f j`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_to_dual_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_on_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsingleton.antivary_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_on.empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_on.comp_monotone_on_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsingleton.monovary", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsingleton.antivary", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_on.comp_monotone_on_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_on_const_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary.dual_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_on_to_dual_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary.symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_on.empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone_on.antivary_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_const_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_id_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone.monovary", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone_on.monovary_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_on.dual_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary.dual_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_on_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_on_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_on_const_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_on.comp_antitone_on_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_on.subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_on.symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone.monovary", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_const_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_on_const_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary.dual_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary.comp_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsingleton.monovary_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_on.comp_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_on.comp_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_on.comp_monotone_on_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary.symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary.comp_antitone_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary.comp_monotone_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_to_dual_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary.dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary.comp_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.eta", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.coe_strict_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.coe_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like", "description": " A class to indicate that there is a canonical injection between `A` and `set B`.\n\nThis has the effect of giving terms of `A` elements of type `B` (through a `has_mem`\ninstance) and a compatible coercion to `Type*` as a subtype.\n\nNote: if `set_like.coe` is a projection, implementers should create a simp lemma such as\n```lean\n@[simp] lemma mem_carrier {p : my_subobject X} : x \u2208 p.carrier \u2194 x \u2208 (p : set X) := iff.rfl\n```\nto normalize terms.\n\nIf you declare an unbundled subclass of `set_like`, for example:\n```lean\nclass mul_mem_class (S : Type*) (M : Type*) [has_mul M] [set_like S M] where\n  ...\n```\nThen you should *not* repeat the `out_param` declaration, `set_like` will supply the value instead.\nThis ensures in Lean 4 your subclass will not have issues with synthesis of the `[has_mul M]`\nparameter starting before the value of `M` is known.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.coe", "description": " A class to indicate that there is a canonical injection between `A` and `set B`.\n\nThis has the effect of giving terms of `A` elements of type `B` (through a `has_mem`\ninstance) and a compatible coercion to `Type*` as a subtype.\n\nNote: if `set_like.coe` is a projection, implementers should create a simp lemma such as\n```lean\n@[simp] lemma mem_carrier {p : my_subobject X} : x \u2208 p.carrier \u2194 x \u2208 (p : set X) := iff.rfl\n```\nto normalize terms.\n\nIf you declare an unbundled subclass of `set_like`, for example:\n```lean\nclass mul_mem_class (S : Type*) (M : Type*) [has_mul M] [set_like S M] where\n  ...\n```\nThen you should *not* repeat the `out_param` declaration, `set_like` will supply the value instead.\nThis ensures in Lean 4 your subclass will not have issues with synthesis of the `[has_mul M]`\nparameter starting before the value of `M` is known.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.coe_injective'", "description": " A class to indicate that there is a canonical injection between `A` and `set B`.\n\nThis has the effect of giving terms of `A` elements of type `B` (through a `has_mem`\ninstance) and a compatible coercion to `Type*` as a subtype.\n\nNote: if `set_like.coe` is a projection, implementers should create a simp lemma such as\n```lean\n@[simp] lemma mem_carrier {p : my_subobject X} : x \u2208 p.carrier \u2194 x \u2208 (p : set X) := iff.rfl\n```\nto normalize terms.\n\nIf you declare an unbundled subclass of `set_like`, for example:\n```lean\nclass mul_mem_class (S : Type*) (M : Type*) [has_mul M] [set_like S M] where\n  ...\n```\nThen you should *not* repeat the `out_param` declaration, `set_like` will supply the value instead.\nThis ensures in Lean 4 your subclass will not have issues with synthesis of the `[has_mul M]`\nparameter starting before the value of `M` is known.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.ext", "description": "Note: implementers of `set_like` must copy this lemma in order to tag it with `@[ext]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.coe_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.coe_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.set.has_coe_t", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.has_coe_to_sort", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.coe_subset_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.forall", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.lt_iff_le_and_exists", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.coe_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.has_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.ext'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.partial_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.mem_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.coe_ssubset_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.ext'_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.exists", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.coe_eq_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.not_le_iff_exists", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.exists_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.coe_sort_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.le_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.coe_set_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "counterexample.bilin_form.not_inj_on_to_quadratic_form_is_symm", "description": " `bilin_form.to_quadratic_form` is not injective on symmetric bilinear forms.\n\nThis disproves a weaker version of `quadratic_form.associated_left_inverse`.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.is_symm_B", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.is_alt_B", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.B_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.B", "description": "The bilinear form we will use as a counterexample, over some field `F` of characteristic two.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.B_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "has_strict_deriv_at.complex_to_real_fderiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_at.real_of_complex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_deriv_at.complex_to_real_fderiv'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.real_of_complex", "description": " If a complex function `e` is differentiable at a real point, then the function `\u211d \u2192 \u211d` given by\nthe real part of `e` is also differentiable at this point, with a derivative equal to the real part\nof the complex derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal_at_iff_differentiable_at_or_differentiable_at_comp_conj", "description": "A complex function is conformal if and only if the function is holomorphic or antiholomorphic\n   with a nonvanishing differential.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.comp_of_real", "description": " If a complex function `e` is differentiable at a real point, then its restriction to `\u211d` is\ndifferentiable there as a function `\u211d \u2192 \u2102`, with the same derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.of_real_comp", "description": " If a function `f : \u211d \u2192 \u211d` is differentiable at a (real) point `x`, then it is also\ndifferentiable as a function `\u211d \u2192 \u2102`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_within_at.complex_to_real_fderiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.complex_to_real_fderiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.complex_to_real_fderiv'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.conformal_at", "description": "A real differentiable function of the complex plane into some complex normed space `E` is\n   conformal at a point `z` if it is holomorphic at that point with a nonvanishing differential.\n   This is a version of the Cauchy-Riemann equations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.real_of_complex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_within_at.complex_to_real_fderiv'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_deriv_at.real_of_complex", "description": " If a complex function is differentiable at a real point, then the induced real function is also\ndifferentiable at this point, with a derivative equal to the real part of the complex derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_\u2113p.all", "description": "When `\u03b1` is `finite`, every `f : pre_lp E p` satisfies `mem_\u2113p f p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_equiv_lp_pi_Lp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_equiv.lp_pi_Lp", "description": " The canonical `add_equiv` between `lp E p` and `pi_Lp p E` when `E : \u03b1 \u2192 Type u` with\n`[fintype \u03b1]` and `[fact (1 \u2264 p)]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lp_pi_Lp\u2097\u1d62", "description": " The canonical `linear_isometry_equiv` between `lp E p` and `pi_Lp p E` when `E : \u03b1 \u2192 Type u`\nwith `[fintype \u03b1]` and `[fact (1 \u2264 p)]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "alg_equiv.lp_bcf", "description": "The canonical map between `lp (\u03bb (_ : \u03b1), A) \u221e` and `\u03b1 \u2192\u1d47 A` as an `alg_equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_ring_equiv_lp_bcf_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_add_equiv_lp_bcf_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lp_bcf\u2097\u1d62", "description": "The canonical map between `lp (\u03bb (_ : \u03b1), E) \u221e` and `\u03b1 \u2192\u1d47 E` as a `linear_isometry_equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_equiv.lp_bcf", "description": "The canonical map between `lp (\u03bb (_ : \u03b1), E) \u221e` and `\u03b1 \u2192\u1d47 E` as an `add_equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_add_equiv_lp_pi_Lp_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_lp_pi_Lp\u2097\u1d62", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_ring_equiv_lp_bcf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_lp_bcf\u2097\u1d62_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_add_equiv_lp_pi_Lp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_lp_bcf\u2097\u1d62", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_lp_pi_Lp\u2097\u1d62_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_alg_equiv_lp_bcf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_equiv_lp_pi_Lp_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.lp_pi_Lp", "description": "The canonical `equiv` between `lp E p \u2243 pi_Lp p E` when `E : \u03b1 \u2192 Type u` with `[fintype \u03b1]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_alg_equiv_lp_bcf_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_lp_pi_Lp_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_equiv.lp_bcf", "description": "The canonical map between `lp (\u03bb (_ : \u03b1), R) \u221e` and `\u03b1 \u2192\u1d47 R` as a `ring_equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_add_equiv_lp_bcf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.ordered_add_comm_group", "description": "Pullback an `ordered_add_comm_group` under an injective map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.linear_ordered_comm_group", "description": " Pullback a `linear_ordered_comm_group` under an injective map.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.linear_ordered_add_comm_group", "description": "Pullback a `linear_ordered_add_comm_group` under an injective map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.ordered_comm_group", "description": " Pullback an `ordered_comm_group` under an injective map.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_subtype.can_lift'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.can_lift", "description": "Enable automatic handling of pi types in `can_lift`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.to_texpr", "description": "Parses a token \"to\" followed by a trailing `pexpr`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.lift", "description": "Lift an expression to another type.\n* Usage: `'lift' expr 'to' expr ('using' expr)? ('with' id (id id?)?)?`.\n* If `n : \u2124` and `hn : n \u2265 0` then the tactic `lift n to \u2115 using hn` creates a new\n  constant of type `\u2115`, also named `n` and replaces all occurrences of the old variable `(n : \u2124)`\n  with `\u2191n` (where `n` in the new variable). It will remove `n` and `hn` from the context.\n  + So for example the tactic `lift n to \u2115 using hn` transforms the goal\n    `n : \u2124, hn : n \u2265 0, h : P n \u22a2 n = 3` to `n : \u2115, h : P \u2191n \u22a2 \u2191n = 3`\n    (here `P` is some term of type `\u2124 \u2192 Prop`).\n* The argument `using hn` is optional, the tactic `lift n to \u2115` does the same, but also creates a\n  new subgoal that `n \u2265 0` (where `n` is the old variable).\n  This subgoal will be placed at the top of the goal list.\n  + So for example the tactic `lift n to \u2115` transforms the goal\n    `n : \u2124, h : P n \u22a2 n = 3` to two goals\n    `n : \u2124, h : P n \u22a2 n \u2265 0` and `n : \u2115, h : P \u2191n \u22a2 \u2191n = 3`.\n* You can also use `lift n to \u2115 using e` where `e` is any expression of type `n \u2265 0`.\n* Use `lift n to \u2115 with k` to specify the name of the new variable.\n* Use `lift n to \u2115 with k hk` to also specify the name of the equality `\u2191k = n`. In this case, `n`\n  will remain in the context. You can use `rfl` for the name of `hk` to substitute `n` away\n  (i.e. the default behavior).\n* You can also use `lift e to \u2115 with k hk` where `e` is any expression of type `\u2124`.\n  In this case, the `hk` will always stay in the context, but it will be used to rewrite `e` in\n  all hypotheses and the target.\n  + So for example the tactic `lift n + 3 to \u2115 using hn with k hk` transforms the goal\n    `n : \u2124, hn : n + 3 \u2265 0, h : P (n + 3) \u22a2 n + 3 = 2 * n` to the goal\n    `n : \u2124, k : \u2115, hk : \u2191k = n + 3, h : P \u2191k \u22a2 \u2191k = 2 * n`.\n* The tactic `lift n to \u2115 using h` will remove `h` from the context. If you want to keep it,\n  specify it again as the third argument to `with`, like this: `lift n to \u2115 using h with n rfl h`.\n* More generally, this can lift an expression from `\u03b1` to `\u03b2` assuming that there is an instance\n  of `can_lift \u03b1 \u03b2`. In this case the proof obligation is specified by `can_lift.cond`.\n* Given an instance `can_lift \u03b2 \u03b3`, it can also lift `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` to `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b3`; more generally, given\n  `\u03b2 : \u03a0 a : \u03b1, Type*`, `\u03b3 : \u03a0 a : \u03b1, Type*`, and `[\u03a0 a : \u03b1, can_lift (\u03b2 a) (\u03b3 a)]`, it\n  automatically generates an instance `can_lift (\u03a0 a, \u03b2 a) (\u03a0 a, \u03b3 a)`.\n\n`lift` is in some sense dual to the `zify` tactic. `lift (z : \u2124) to \u2115` will change the type of an\ninteger `z` (in the supertype) to `\u2115` (the subtype), given a proof that `z \u2265 0`;\npropositions concerning `z` will still be over `\u2124`. `zify` changes propositions about `\u2115` (the\nsubtype) to propositions about `\u2124` (the supertype), without changing the type of any variable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype.exists_pi_extension", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "can_lift", "description": "A class specifying that you can lift elements from `\u03b1` to `\u03b2` assuming `cond` is true.\n Used by the tactic `lift`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "can_lift.prf", "description": "A class specifying that you can lift elements from `\u03b1` to `\u03b2` assuming `cond` is true.\n Used by the tactic `lift`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.lift", "description": "Lift the expression `p` to the type `t`, with proof obligation given by `h`.\n The list `n` is used for the two newly generated names, and to specify whether `h` should\n remain in the local context. See the doc string of `tactic.interactive.lift` for more information.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype.can_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_subtype.can_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.lift", "description": "Lift an expression to another type.\n* Usage: `'lift' expr 'to' expr ('using' expr)? ('with' id (id id?)?)?`.\n* If `n : \u2124` and `hn : n \u2265 0` then the tactic `lift n to \u2115 using hn` creates a new\n  constant of type `\u2115`, also named `n` and replaces all occurrences of the old variable `(n : \u2124)`\n  with `\u2191n` (where `n` in the new variable). It will remove `n` and `hn` from the context.\n  + So for example the tactic `lift n to \u2115 using hn` transforms the goal\n    `n : \u2124, hn : n \u2265 0, h : P n \u22a2 n = 3` to `n : \u2115, h : P \u2191n \u22a2 \u2191n = 3`\n    (here `P` is some term of type `\u2124 \u2192 Prop`).\n* The argument `using hn` is optional, the tactic `lift n to \u2115` does the same, but also creates a\n  new subgoal that `n \u2265 0` (where `n` is the old variable).\n  This subgoal will be placed at the top of the goal list.\n  + So for example the tactic `lift n to \u2115` transforms the goal\n    `n : \u2124, h : P n \u22a2 n = 3` to two goals\n    `n : \u2124, h : P n \u22a2 n \u2265 0` and `n : \u2115, h : P \u2191n \u22a2 \u2191n = 3`.\n* You can also use `lift n to \u2115 using e` where `e` is any expression of type `n \u2265 0`.\n* Use `lift n to \u2115 with k` to specify the name of the new variable.\n* Use `lift n to \u2115 with k hk` to also specify the name of the equality `\u2191k = n`. In this case, `n`\n  will remain in the context. You can use `rfl` for the name of `hk` to substitute `n` away\n  (i.e. the default behavior).\n* You can also use `lift e to \u2115 with k hk` where `e` is any expression of type `\u2124`.\n  In this case, the `hk` will always stay in the context, but it will be used to rewrite `e` in\n  all hypotheses and the target.\n  + So for example the tactic `lift n + 3 to \u2115 using hn with k hk` transforms the goal\n    `n : \u2124, hn : n + 3 \u2265 0, h : P (n + 3) \u22a2 n + 3 = 2 * n` to the goal\n    `n : \u2124, k : \u2115, hk : \u2191k = n + 3, h : P \u2191k \u22a2 \u2191k = 2 * n`.\n* The tactic `lift n to \u2115 using h` will remove `h` from the context. If you want to keep it,\n  specify it again as the third argument to `with`, like this: `lift n to \u2115 using h with n rfl h`.\n* More generally, this can lift an expression from `\u03b1` to `\u03b2` assuming that there is an instance\n  of `can_lift \u03b1 \u03b2`. In this case the proof obligation is specified by `can_lift.cond`.\n* Given an instance `can_lift \u03b2 \u03b3`, it can also lift `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` to `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b3`; more generally, given\n  `\u03b2 : \u03a0 a : \u03b1, Type*`, `\u03b3 : \u03a0 a : \u03b1, Type*`, and `[\u03a0 a : \u03b1, can_lift (\u03b2 a) (\u03b3 a)]`, it\n  automatically generates an instance `can_lift (\u03a0 a, \u03b2 a) (\u03a0 a, \u03b3 a)`.\n\n`lift` is in some sense dual to the `zify` tactic. `lift (z : \u2124) to \u2115` will change the type of an\ninteger `z` (in the supertype) to `\u2115` (the subtype), given a proof that `z \u2265 0`;\npropositions concerning `z` will still be over `\u2124`. `zify` changes propositions about `\u2115` (the\nsubtype) to propositions about `\u2124` (the supertype), without changing the type of any variable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.using_texpr", "description": "Parses an optional token \"using\" followed by a trailing `pexpr`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_le.le.can_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.get_lift_prf", "description": "Construct the proof of `cond x` in the lift tactic.\n*  `e` is the expression being lifted and `h` is the specified proof of `can_lift.cond e`.\n*  `old_tp` and `new_tp` are the arguments to `can_lift` and `inst` is the `can_lift`-instance.\n*  `s` and `to_unfold` contain the information of the simp set used to simplify.\n\nIf the proof was specified, we check whether it has the correct type.\nIf it doesn't have the correct type, we display an error message.\n\nIf the proof was not specified, we create assert it as a local constant.\n(The name of this local constant doesn't matter, since `lift` will remove it from the context.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_le_is_associated_prime_of_is_noetherian_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "associated_primes.nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_equiv.associated_primes.eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_is_associated_prime_of_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "associated_primes.eq_empty_of_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "associated_primes.subset_of_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_associated_prime.annihilator_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_associated_prime.is_prime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_equiv.is_associated_prime_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "associated_primes.eq_singleton_of_is_primary", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "associate_primes.mem_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_associated_prime", "description": "`is_associated_prime I M` if the prime ideal `I` is the annihilator of some `x : M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "associated_primes", "description": "The set of associated primes of a module.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_associated_prime.eq_radical", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_associated_prime.map_of_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximal_spectrum.zariski_topology", "description": " The Zariski topology on the maximal spectrum of a commutative ring is defined as the subspace\ntopology induced by the natural inclusion into the prime spectrum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximal_spectrum.infi_localization_eq_bot", "description": " An integral domain is equal to the intersection of its localizations at all its maximal ideals\nviewed as subalgebras of its field of fractions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximal_spectrum.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximal_spectrum.to_prime_spectrum_continuous", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximal_spectrum.to_prime_spectrum", "description": "The natural inclusion from the maximal spectrum to the prime spectrum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximal_spectrum.to_prime_spectrum_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximal_spectrum.to_prime_spectrum_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prime_spectrum.infi_localization_eq_bot", "description": " An integral domain is equal to the intersection of its localizations at all its prime ideals\nviewed as subalgebras of its field of fractions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximal_spectrum.nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximal_spectrum.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximal_spectrum", "description": "The maximal spectrum of a commutative ring `R` is the type of all maximal ideals of `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximal_spectrum.as_ideal", "description": "The maximal spectrum of a commutative ring `R` is the type of all maximal ideals of `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximal_spectrum.is_maximal", "description": "The maximal spectrum of a commutative ring `R` is the type of all maximal ideals of `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximal_spectrum.t1_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.indiscrete_is_equipartition", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.is_equipartition.card_parts_eq_average", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.is_equipartition.average_le_card_part", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.top_is_equipartition", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.is_equipartition_iff_card_parts_eq_average", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.subsingleton.is_equipartition", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.is_equipartition", "description": "An equipartition is a partition whose parts are all the same size, up to a difference of `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.is_equipartition.card_part_le_average_add_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.bot_is_equipartition", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.bounded_at_filter.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.zero_at_filter.neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.zero_at_filter", "description": "If `l` is a filter on `\u03b1`, then a function `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` is `zero_at_filter l`\n if it tends to zero along `l`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.zero_zero_at_filter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.zero_at_filter_add_submonoid", "description": " `zero_at_filter_add_submonoid l` is the additive submonoid of `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2`\nwhich tend to zero along `l`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.bounded_at_filter", "description": " If `l` is a filter on `\u03b1`, then a function `f: \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` is `bounded_at_filter l`\nif `f =O[l] 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.bounded_at_filter.neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.zero_at_filter.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.bounded_filter_subalgebra", "description": "The subalgebra of functions that are bounded along a filter `l`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.bounded_at_filter.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.const_bounded_at_filter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.zero_at_filter_submodule", "description": " `zero_at_filter_submodule l` is the submodule of `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` which\ntend to zero along `l`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.zero_at_filter.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.bounded_at_filter.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.bounded_filter_submodule", "description": "The submodule of functions that are bounded along a filter `l`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.zero_at_filter.bounded_at_filter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bicategory.strict.right_unitor_eq_to_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bicategory.strict", "description": "A bicategory is called `strict` if the left unitors, the right unitors, and the associators are\nisomorphisms given by equalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bicategory.strict.id_comp'", "description": "A bicategory is called `strict` if the left unitors, the right unitors, and the associators are\nisomorphisms given by equalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bicategory.strict.comp_id'", "description": "A bicategory is called `strict` if the left unitors, the right unitors, and the associators are\nisomorphisms given by equalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bicategory.strict.assoc'", "description": "A bicategory is called `strict` if the left unitors, the right unitors, and the associators are\nisomorphisms given by equalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bicategory.strict.left_unitor_eq_to_iso'", "description": "A bicategory is called `strict` if the left unitors, the right unitors, and the associators are\nisomorphisms given by equalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bicategory.strict.right_unitor_eq_to_iso'", "description": "A bicategory is called `strict` if the left unitors, the right unitors, and the associators are\nisomorphisms given by equalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bicategory.strict.associator_eq_to_iso'", "description": "A bicategory is called `strict` if the left unitors, the right unitors, and the associators are\nisomorphisms given by equalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bicategory.strict.left_unitor_eq_to_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bicategory.strict.assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bicategory.strict.comp_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bicategory.eq_to_hom_whisker_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.strict_bicategory.category", "description": "Category structure on a strict bicategory", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bicategory.whisker_left_eq_to_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bicategory.strict.id_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bicategory.strict.associator_eq_to_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting", "description": "A set family is intersecting if every pair of elements is non-disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting.exists_mem_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting.exists_card_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting.exists_mem_finset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting.is_max_iff_card_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting.not_compl_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting.not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting_insert", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting.not_bot_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting_iff_eq_empty_of_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.subsingleton.intersecting", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting_iff_pairwise_not_disjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting.is_upper_set", "description": "Maximal intersecting families are upper sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting.card_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting.is_upper_set'", "description": "Maximal intersecting families are upper sets. Finset version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting.disjoint_map_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting.ne_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.intersecting.insert", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.explode_widget.assemble", "description": "Render a table for a given entry.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.explode_widget.assemble_table", "description": "Combine the goal row, id row, rule row and proof row to make them a table.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.explode_widget.rule_row", "description": "Render a row that shows the rule or theorem being applied.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.explode_widget.goal_row", "description": "Render a row that shows a goal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.explode_widget.insert_explode", "description": "Explode button for subsequent exploding.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.explode_widget.explode_component", "description": "Render a widget from given entries.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.explode_widget.show_constant_component", "description": "Component that shows a constant.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.explode_widget.show_type_component", "description": "Component that shows a type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.explode_widget.explode_entries", "description": "Explode a theorem and return entries.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.explode_widget.type_tooltip", "description": "Component for the type tooltip.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.explode_widget.id_row", "description": "Render a row that shows the ID of a goal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.explode_widget.proof_row", "description": "Render a row that contains the sub-proofs, i.e., the proofs of the\narguments.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.explode_widget.implicit_arg_list", "description": "Render the implicit arguments for an expression in fancy, little pills.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.explode_widget_cmd", "description": "User command of the explode widget.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.explode_widget.view", "description": "Render a subexpression as a list of html elements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.explode_widget.lookup_lines", "description": "Search for an entry that has the specified line number.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.explode_widget.get_block_attrs", "description": "Redefine some of the style attributes for better formatting.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.explode_widget.mk", "description": "Make an interactive expression.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "widget.string_to_html", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.exists_unique_gluing'", "description": "In this version of the lemma, the inclusion homs `iUV` can be specified directly by the user,\nwhich can be more convenient in practice.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.exists_unique_gluing", "description": "A more convenient way of obtaining a unique gluing of sections for a sheaf.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_of_is_sheaf_unique_gluing_types", "description": "The \"equalizer\" sheaf condition can be obtained from the sheaf condition\nin terms of unique gluings.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.eq_of_locally_eq'", "description": "In this version of the lemma, the inclusion homs `iUV` can be specified directly by the user,\nwhich can be more convenient in practice.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.compatible_iff_left_res_eq_right_res", "description": "Under the isomorphism `pi_opens_iso_sections_family`, compatibility of sections is the same\nas being equalized by the arrows `left_res` and `right_res` of the equalizer diagram.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_iff_is_sheaf_unique_gluing", "description": "For presheaves valued in a concrete category, whose forgetful functor reflects isomorphisms and\npreserves limits, the sheaf condition in terms of unique gluings is equivalent to the usual one\nin terms of equalizer diagrams.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.eq_of_locally_eq\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_unique_gluing", "description": "The sheaf condition in terms of unique gluings. A presheaf `F : presheaf C X` satisfies this sheaf\ncondition if and only if, for every compatible family of sections `sf : \u03a0 i : \u03b9, F.obj (op (U i))`,\nthere exists a unique gluing `s : F.obj (op (supr U))`.\n\nWe prove this to be equivalent to the usual one below in\n`is_sheaf_iff_is_sheaf_unique_gluing`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_compatible", "description": "A family of sections `sf` is compatible, if the restrictions of `sf i` and `sf j` to `U i \u2293 U j`\nagree, for all `i` and `j`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_gluing", "description": "A section `s` is a gluing for a family of sections `sf` if it restricts to `sf i` on `U i`,\nfor all `i`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.pi_opens_iso_sections_family", "description": "For presheaves of types, terms of `pi_opens F U` are just families of sections.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_unique_gluing_of_is_sheaf_types", "description": "The sheaf condition in terms of unique gluings can be obtained from the usual\n\"equalizer\" sheaf condition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.eq_of_locally_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_gluing_iff_eq_res", "description": "Under the isomorphism `pi_opens_iso_sections_family`, being a gluing of a family of\nsections `sf` is the same as lying in the preimage of `res` (the leftmost arrow of the\nequalizer diagram).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_iff_is_sheaf_unique_gluing_types", "description": "For type-valued presheaves, the sheaf condition in terms of unique gluings is equivalent to the\nusual sheaf condition in terms of equalizer diagrams.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_set_integral_peak_smul_of_integrable_on_of_continuous_within_at_aux", "description": " If a sequence of peak functions `\u03c6\u1d62` converges uniformly to zero away from a point `x\u2080`, and\n`g` is integrable and continuous at `x\u2080`, then `\u222b \u03c6\u1d62 \u2022 g` converges to `x\u2080`. Auxiliary lemma\nwhere one assumes additionally `g x\u2080 = 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_set_integral_pow_smul_of_unique_maximum_of_is_compact_of_integrable_on", "description": " If a continuous function `c` realizes its maximum at a unique point `x\u2080` in a compact set `s`,\nthen the sequence of functions `(c x) ^ n / \u222b (c x) ^ n` is a sequence of peak functions\nconcentrating around `x\u2080`. Therefore, `\u222b (c x) ^ n * g / \u222b (c x) ^ n` converges to `g x\u2080` if `g` is\nintegrable on `s` and continuous at `x\u2080`.\n\nVersion assuming that `\u03bc` gives positive mass to all open sets.\nFor a less precise but more usable version, see\n`tendsto_set_integral_pow_smul_of_unique_maximum_of_is_compact_of_continuous_on`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.disjoint_sdiff_inter", "description": " This lemma exists for finsets, but not for sets currently. porting note: move to\ndata.set.basic after the port.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_set_integral_peak_smul_of_integrable_on_of_continuous_within_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_set_integral_pow_smul_of_unique_maximum_of_is_compact_of_measure_nhds_within_pos", "description": " If a continuous function `c` realizes its maximum at a unique point `x\u2080` in a compact set `s`,\nthen the sequence of functions `(c x) ^ n / \u222b (c x) ^ n` is a sequence of peak functions\nconcentrating around `x\u2080`. Therefore, `\u222b (c x) ^ n * g / \u222b (c x) ^ n` converges to `g x\u2080` if `g` is\nintegrable on `s` and continuous at `x\u2080`.\n\nVersion assuming that `\u03bc` gives positive mass to all neighborhoods of `x\u2080` within `s`.\nFor a less precise but more usable version, see\n`tendsto_set_integral_pow_smul_of_unique_maximum_of_is_compact_of_continuous_on`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_set_integral_pow_smul_of_unique_maximum_of_is_compact_of_continuous_on", "description": " If a continuous function `c` realizes its maximum at a unique point `x\u2080` in a compact set `s`,\nthen the sequence of functions `(c x) ^ n / \u222b (c x) ^ n` is a sequence of peak functions\nconcentrating around `x\u2080`. Therefore, `\u222b (c x) ^ n * g / \u222b (c x) ^ n` converges to `g x\u2080` if `g` is\ncontinuous on `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integrable_on_peak_smul_of_integrable_on_of_continuous_within_at", "description": " If a sequence of peak functions `\u03c6\u1d62` converges uniformly to zero away from a point `x\u2080`, and\n`g` is integrable and continuous at `x\u2080`, then `\u03c6\u1d62 \u2022 g` is eventually integrable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.adjoint_is_formal_adjoint", "description": "The fundamental property of the adjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.adjoint_domain_mk_clm", "description": " The operator `\u03bb x, \u27eay, T x\u27eb` considered as a continuous linear operator from `T.adjoint_domain`\nto `\ud835\udd5c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.adjoint", "description": "The adjoint operator as a partially defined linear operator.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.adjoint_aux_inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.is_formal_adjoint.symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.is_formal_adjoint.le_adjoint", "description": "The adjoint is maximal in the sense that it contains every formal adjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.adjoint_domain_mk_clm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.adjoint_apply_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.adjoint_apply_of_not_dense", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.adjoint_domain_mk_clm_extend_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.mem_adjoint_domain_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.adjoint_domain", "description": " The domain of the adjoint operator.\n\nThis definition is needed to construct the adjoint operator and the preferred version to use is\n`T.adjoint.domain` instead of `T.adjoint_domain`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.mem_adjoint_domain_of_exists", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.to_pmap_adjoint_eq_adjoint_to_pmap_of_dense", "description": " Restricting `A` to a dense submodule and taking the `linear_pmap.adjoint` is the same\nas taking the `continuous_linear_map.adjoint` interpreted as a `linear_pmap`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.adjoint_apply_of_dense", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.is_formal_adjoint", "description": " An operator `T` is a formal adjoint of `S` if for all `x` in the domain of `T` and `y` in the\ndomain of `S`, we have that `\u27eaT x, y\u27eb = \u27eax, S y\u27eb`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.adjoint_aux", "description": " The adjoint as a linear map from its domain to `E`.\n\nThis is an auxiliary definition needed to define the adjoint operator as a `linear_pmap` without\nthe assumption that `T.domain` is dense.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.adjoint_domain_mk_clm_extend", "description": "The unique continuous extension of the operator `adjoint_domain_mk_clm` to `E`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.adjoint_aux_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.prime_counting", "description": "The prime counting function: Returns the number of primes less than or equal to the input.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.prime_nth_prime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.monotone_prime_counting", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.monotone_prime_counting'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.prime_counting'_nth_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.prime_counting'", "description": "A variant of the traditional prime counting function which gives the number of primes\n*strictly* less than the input. More convenient for avoiding off-by-one errors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.prime_counting'_add_le", "description": "A linear upper bound on the size of the `prime_counting'` function", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.sigma_finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.exists_mem_image_mem_of_volume_lt_mul_volume", "description": " If `\u03bc univ < n * \u03bc s` and `f` is a map preserving measure `\u03bc`,\nthen for some `x \u2208 s` and `0 < m < n`, `f^[m] x \u2208 s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measurable_equiv.measure_preserving_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.measure_preimage_emb", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.restrict_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.comp_left_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.restrict_image_emb", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.exists_mem_image_mem", "description": " A self-map preserving a finite measure is conservative: if `\u03bc s \u2260 0`, then at least one point\n`x \u2208 s` comes back to `s` under iterations of `f`. Actually, a.e. point of `s` comes back to `s`\ninfinitely many times, see `measure_theory.measure_preserving.conservative` and theorems about\n`measure_theory.conservative`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.quasi_measure_preserving", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.ae_measurable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving", "description": " `f` is a measure preserving map w.r.t. measures `\u03bca` and `\u03bcb` if `f` is measurable\nand `map f \u03bca = \u03bcb`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.measurable", "description": " `f` is a measure preserving map w.r.t. measures `\u03bca` and `\u03bcb` if `f` is measurable\nand `map f \u03bca = \u03bcb`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.map_eq", "description": " `f` is a measure preserving map w.r.t. measures `\u03bca` and `\u03bcb` if `f` is measurable\nand `map f \u03bca = \u03bcb`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.restrict_preimage_emb", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.iterate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.measure_preserving", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.comp_right_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.measure_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure_preserving.ae_measurable_comp_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.completion.field", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.completion.mul_hat_inv_cancel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "completable_top_field_of_complete", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.completable_top_field", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "completable_top_field", "description": "A topological field is completable if it is separated and the image under\nthe mapping x \u21a6 x\u207b\u00b9 of every Cauchy filter (with respect to the additive uniform structure)\nwhich does not have a cluster point at 0 is a Cauchy filter\n(with respect to the additive uniform structure). This ensures the completion is\na field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "completable_top_field.to_separated_space", "description": "A topological field is completable if it is separated and the image under\nthe mapping x \u21a6 x\u207b\u00b9 of every Cauchy filter (with respect to the additive uniform structure)\nwhich does not have a cluster point at 0 is a Cauchy filter\n(with respect to the additive uniform structure). This ensures the completion is\na field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "completable_top_field.nice", "description": "A topological field is completable if it is separated and the image under\nthe mapping x \u21a6 x\u207b\u00b9 of every Cauchy filter (with respect to the additive uniform structure)\nwhich does not have a cluster point at 0 is a Cauchy filter\n(with respect to the additive uniform structure). This ensures the completion is\na field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.completion.topological_division_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.completion.coe_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.completion.nontrivial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.completion.continuous_hat_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.completion.hat_inv", "description": "extension of inversion to the completion of a field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.completion.has_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.completion.hat_inv_extends", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unop_hom", "description": "`unop` induces morphisms of monoids on hom groups of a preadditive category", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unop_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_hom", "description": "`op` induces morphisms of monoids on hom groups of a preadditive category", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.module_End_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.right_op_additive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unop_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_zsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.op_additive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.left_op_additive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.opposite.preadditive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unop_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unop_zsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.unop_additive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unop_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unop_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.op_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "expr.has_add", "description": " Produce a `has_add` instance for the type cached by `t`, such that `(+) : expr \u2192 expr \u2192 expr` is\nthe addition of that type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "expr.has_mul", "description": " Produce a `has_mul` instance for the type cached by `t`, such that `(*) : expr \u2192 expr \u2192 expr` is\nthe multiplication of that type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "expr.has_zero", "description": " Produce a `has_zero` instance for the type cached by `t`, such that `0 : expr` is the zero of\nthat type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "expr.has_one", "description": " Produce a `has_one` instance for the type cached by `t`, such that `1 : expr` is the one of that\ntype.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "banach_steinhaus_supr_nnnorm", "description": " This version of Banach-Steinhaus is stated in terms of suprema of `\u2191\u2016\u2b1d\u2016\u208a : \u211d\u22650\u221e`\nfor convenience.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "banach_steinhaus", "description": " This is the standard Banach-Steinhaus theorem, or Uniform Boundedness Principle.\nIf a family of continuous linear maps from a Banach space into a normed space is pointwise\nbounded, then the norms of these linear maps are uniformly bounded.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map_of_tendsto", "description": " Given a *sequence* of continuous linear maps which converges pointwise and for which the\ndomain is complete, the Banach-Steinhaus theorem is used to guarantee that the limit map\nis a *continuous* linear map as well.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.mk'_mk_eq_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fraction_ring.unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fraction_ring.no_zero_smul_divisors", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.lift", "description": " Given an integral domain `A` with field of fractions `K`,\nand an injective ring hom `g : A \u2192+* L` where `L` is a field, we get a\nfield hom sending `z : K` to `g x * (g y)\u207b\u00b9`, where `(x, y) : A \u00d7 (non_zero_divisors A)` are\nsuch that `z = f x * (f y)\u207b\u00b9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.mk'_eq_one_iff_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.to_map_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring", "description": "`is_fraction_ring R K` states `K` is the field of fractions of an integral domain `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.lift_algebra_map", "description": " Given an integral domain `A` with field of fractions `K`,\nand an injective ring hom `g : A \u2192+* L` where `L` is a field,\nthe field hom induced from `K` to `L` maps `x` to `g x` for all\n`x : A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.is_fraction_ring", "description": "The cast from `int` to `rat` as a `fraction_ring`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.lift_mk'", "description": " Given an integral domain `A` with field of fractions `K`,\nand an injective ring hom `g : A \u2192+* L` where `L` is a field,\nfield hom induced from `K` to `L` maps `f x / f y` to `g x / g y` for all\n`x : A, y \u2208 non_zero_divisors A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fraction_ring.algebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fraction_ring", "description": " The fraction ring of a commutative ring `R` as a quotient type.\n\nWe instantiate this definition as generally as possible, and assume that the\ncommutative ring `R` is an integral domain only when this is needed for proving.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fraction_ring.nontrivial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.no_zero_smul_divisors", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.coe_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.map", "description": " Given integral domains `A, B` with fields of fractions `K`, `L`\nand an injective ring hom `j : A \u2192+* B`, we get a field hom\nsending `z : K` to `g (j x) * (g (j y))\u207b\u00b9`, where `(x, y) : A \u00d7 (non_zero_divisors A)` are\nsuch that `z = f x * (f y)\u207b\u00b9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.to_field", "description": " A `comm_ring` `K` which is the localization of an integral domain `R` at `R - {0}` is a field.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.is_unit_map_of_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.is_domain", "description": " A `comm_ring` `K` which is the localization of an integral domain `R` at `R - {0}` is an\nintegral domain.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fraction_ring.field", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.div_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.mk'_eq_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.mk'_eq_zero_iff_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.to_map_ne_zero_of_mem_non_zero_divisors", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.nontrivial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fraction_ring.alg_equiv", "description": " Given an integral domain `A` and a localization map to a field of fractions\n`f : A \u2192+* K`, we get an `A`-isomorphism between the field of fractions of `A` as a quotient\ntype and `K`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.field_equiv_of_ring_equiv", "description": " Given integral domains `A, B` and localization maps to their fields of fractions\n`f : A \u2192+* K, g : B \u2192+* L`, an isomorphism `j : A \u2243+* B` induces an isomorphism of\nfields of fractions `K \u2243+* L`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fraction_ring.mk_eq_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fraction_ring.is_scalar_tower", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.is_fraction_ring_iff_of_base_ring_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.inv", "description": "The inverse of an element in the field of fractions of an integral domain.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.mul_inv_cancel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.noncomm_ring", "description": " A tactic for simplifying identities in not-necessarily-commutative rings.\n\nAn example:\n```lean\nexample {R : Type*} [ring R] (a b c : R) : a * (b + c + c - b) = 2*a*c :=\nby noncomm_ring\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.noncomm_ring", "description": " A tactic for simplifying identities in not-necessarily-commutative rings.\n\nAn example:\n```lean\nexample {R : Type*} [ring R] (a b c : R) : a * (b + c + c - b) = 2*a*c :=\nby noncomm_ring\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rel_iso.inv_apply_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rel_iso.group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rel_iso.mul_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rel_iso.coe_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rel_iso.apply_inv_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rel_iso.coe_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent.one_lt_nnreal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent.one_div_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent.inv_add_inv_conj_ennreal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent.ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent.div_conj_eq_sub_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent.sub_one_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent.sub_one_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent.inv_add_inv_conj_nnreal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent_conjugate_exponent", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent", "description": " Two real exponents `p, q` are conjugate if they are `> 1` and satisfy the equality\n`1/p + 1/q = 1`. This condition shows up in many theorems in analysis, notably related to `L^p`\nnorms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent.one_lt", "description": " Two real exponents `p, q` are conjugate if they are `> 1` and satisfy the equality\n`1/p + 1/q = 1`. This condition shows up in many theorems in analysis, notably related to `L^p`\nnorms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent.inv_add_inv_conj", "description": " Two real exponents `p, q` are conjugate if they are `> 1` and satisfy the equality\n`1/p + 1/q = 1`. This condition shows up in many theorems in analysis, notably related to `L^p`\nnorms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.conjugate_exponent", "description": "The conjugate exponent of `p` is `q = p/(p-1)`, so that `1/p + 1/q = 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent.one_div_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent.conj_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent.pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent.mul_eq_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent.symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent.nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent.one_div_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent.sub_one_mul_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent.conjugate_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.is_conjugate_exponent_one_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.gsmul_hom_apply_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.gmodule", "description": "`[set_like.graded_monoid \ud835\udcd0] [set_like.has_graded_smul \ud835\udcd0 \ud835\udcdc]` is the internal version of graded\n module, the internal version can be translated into the external version `gmodule`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.gdistrib_mul_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.gmodule.smul_add_monoid_hom", "description": " For graded monoid `A` and a graded module `M` over `A`. `gmodule.smul_add_monoid_hom` is the\n`\u2a01\u1d62 A\u1d62`-scalar multiplication on `\u2a01\u1d62 M\u1d62` induced by `gsmul_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.gsemiring.to_gmodule", "description": "A graded version of `semiring.to_module`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.gmodule.smul_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.gsmul_hom", "description": "The piecewise multiplication from the `has_mul` instance, as a bundled homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.gmodule.of_smul_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.gdistrib_mul_action", "description": "A graded version of `distrib_mul_action`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.gdistrib_mul_action.to_gmul_action", "description": "A graded version of `distrib_mul_action`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.gdistrib_mul_action.smul_add", "description": "A graded version of `distrib_mul_action`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.gdistrib_mul_action.smul_zero", "description": "A graded version of `distrib_mul_action`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.gmodule.module", "description": "The `module` derived from `gmodule A M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.gmodule.direct_sum.has_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.gmodule.smul_add_monoid_hom_apply_of_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "graded_module.linear_equiv", "description": "`\u2a01 i, \ud835\udcdc i` and `M` are isomorphic as `A`-modules.\n\"The internal version\" and \"the external version\" are isomorphism as `A`-modules.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.gmodule", "description": "A graded version of `module`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.gmodule.to_gdistrib_mul_action", "description": "A graded version of `module`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.gmodule.add_smul", "description": "A graded version of `module`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "direct_sum.gmodule.zero_smul", "description": "A graded version of `module`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.gmul_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "graded_module.is_module", "description": "The smul multiplication of `A` on `\u2a01 i, \ud835\udcdc i` from `(\u2a01 i, \ud835\udcd0 i) \u2192+ (\u2a01 i, \ud835\udcdc i) \u2192+ \u2a01 i, \ud835\udcdc i`\nturns `\u2a01 i, \ud835\udcdc i` into an `A`-module", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_monad_lift_t_trans", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_lift_refl", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_map_refl", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_functor_t_trans", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_monad_lift", "description": "A function for lifting a computation from an inner monad to an outer monad.\n   Like [MonadTrans](https://hackage.haskell.org/package/transformers-0.5.5.0/docs/Control-Monad-Trans-Class.html),\n   but `n` does not have to be a monad transformer.\n   Alternatively, an implementation of [MonadLayer](https://hackage.haskell.org/package/layers-0.1/docs/Control-Monad-Layer.html#t:MonadLayer) without `layerInvmap` (so far).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_monad_lift.monad_lift", "description": "A function for lifting a computation from an inner monad to an outer monad.\n   Like [MonadTrans](https://hackage.haskell.org/package/transformers-0.5.5.0/docs/Control-Monad-Trans-Class.html),\n   but `n` does not have to be a monad transformer.\n   Alternatively, an implementation of [MonadLayer](https://hackage.haskell.org/package/layers-0.1/docs/Control-Monad-Layer.html#t:MonadLayer) without `layerInvmap` (so far).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_monad_lift_to_has_coe", "description": "A coercion that may reduce the need for explicit lifting.\n   Because of [limitations of the current coercion resolution](https://github.com/leanprover/lean/issues/1402), this definition is not marked as a global instance and should be marked locally instead.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_functor", "description": "A functor in the category of monads. Can be used to lift monad-transforming functions.\n   Based on pipes' [MFunctor](https://hackage.haskell.org/package/pipes-2.4.0/docs/Control-MFunctor.html),\n   but not restricted to monad transformers.\n   Alternatively, an implementation of [MonadTransFunctor](http://duairc.netsoc.ie/layers-docs/Control-Monad-Layer.html#t:MonadTransFunctor).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_functor.monad_map", "description": "A functor in the category of monads. Can be used to lift monad-transforming functions.\n   Based on pipes' [MFunctor](https://hackage.haskell.org/package/pipes-2.4.0/docs/Control-MFunctor.html),\n   but not restricted to monad transformers.\n   Alternatively, an implementation of [MonadTransFunctor](http://duairc.netsoc.ie/layers-docs/Control-Monad-Layer.html#t:MonadTransFunctor).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_functor_t", "description": "The reflexive-transitive closure of `monad_functor`.\n   `monad_map` is used to transitively lift monad morphisms such as `state_t.zoom`.\n   A generalization of [MonadLiftFunctor](http://duairc.netsoc.ie/layers-docs/Control-Monad-Layer.html#t:MonadLiftFunctor), which can only lift endomorphisms (i.e. m = m', n = n').", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_functor_t.monad_map", "description": "The reflexive-transitive closure of `monad_functor`.\n   `monad_map` is used to transitively lift monad morphisms such as `state_t.zoom`.\n   A generalization of [MonadLiftFunctor](http://duairc.netsoc.ie/layers-docs/Control-Monad-Layer.html#t:MonadLiftFunctor), which can only lift endomorphisms (i.e. m = m', n = n').", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_monad_lift_t_refl", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_functor_t_refl", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_monad_lift_t", "description": "The reflexive-transitive closure of `has_monad_lift`.\n   `monad_lift` is used to transitively lift monadic computations such as `state_t.get` or `state_t.put s`.\n   Corresponds to [MonadLift](https://hackage.haskell.org/package/layers-0.1/docs/Control-Monad-Layer.html#t:MonadLift).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_monad_lift_t.monad_lift", "description": "The reflexive-transitive closure of `has_monad_lift`.\n   `monad_lift` is used to transitively lift monadic computations such as `state_t.get` or `state_t.put s`.\n   Corresponds to [MonadLift](https://hackage.haskell.org/package/layers-0.1/docs/Control-Monad-Layer.html#t:MonadLift).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_run", "description": "Run a monad stack to completion.\n   `run` should be the composition of the transformers' individual `run` functions.\n   This class mostly saves some typing when using highly nested monad stacks:\n   ```lean\n   @[reducible] def my_monad := reader_t my_cfg $ state_t my_state $ except_t my_err id\n   -- def my_monad.run {\u03b1 : Type} (x : my_monad \u03b1) (cfg : my_cfg) (st : my_state) := ((x.run cfg).run st).run\n   def my_monad.run {\u03b1 : Type} (x : my_monad \u03b1) := monad_run.run x\n   ```", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_run.run", "description": "Run a monad stack to completion.\n   `run` should be the composition of the transformers' individual `run` functions.\n   This class mostly saves some typing when using highly nested monad stacks:\n   ```lean\n   @[reducible] def my_monad := reader_t my_cfg $ state_t my_state $ except_t my_err id\n   -- def my_monad.run {\u03b1 : Type} (x : my_monad \u03b1) (cfg : my_cfg) (st : my_state) := ((x.run cfg).run st).run\n   def my_monad.run {\u03b1 : Type} (x : my_monad \u03b1) := monad_run.run x\n   ```", "p": "core"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.decompose_option_symm_of_none_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.option_congr_sign", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.option_congr_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.decompose_option", "description": " Permutations of `option \u03b1` are equivalent to fixing an\n`option \u03b1` and permuting the remaining with a `perm \u03b1`.\nThe fixed `option \u03b1` is swapped with `none`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.decompose_option_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.univ_perm_option", "description": " The set of all permutations of `option \u03b1` can be constructed by augmenting the set of\npermutations of `\u03b1` by each element of `option \u03b1` in turn.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.option_congr_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.decompose_option_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.decompose_option_symm_sign", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "map_equiv_remove_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_single_obj.bicategory", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_single_obj.End_monoidal_star_functor_is_equivalence", "description": "The equivalence between the endomorphisms of the single object\nwhen we promote a monoidal category to a single object bicategory,\nand the original monoidal category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_single_obj.End_monoidal_star_functor_to_lax_monoidal_functor_\u03b5", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_single_obj", "description": "Promote a monoidal category to a bicategory with a single object.\n(The objects of the monoidal category become the 1-morphisms,\nwith composition given by tensor product,\nand the morphisms of the monoidal category become the 2-morphisms.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_single_obj.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_single_obj.End_monoidal_star_functor_to_lax_monoidal_functor_to_functor_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_single_obj.End_monoidal_star_functor_to_lax_monoidal_functor_to_functor_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_single_obj.End_monoidal_star_functor", "description": "The monoidal functor from the endomorphisms of the single object\nwhen we promote a monoidal category to a single object bicategory,\nto the original monoidal category.\n\nWe subsequently show this is an equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_single_obj.End_monoidal_star_functor_to_lax_monoidal_functor_\u03bc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_single_obj.star", "description": "The unique object in the bicategory obtained by \"promoting\" a monoidal category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrim_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_quadratic_eq_zero", "description": "A quadratic has roots if its discriminant has square roots", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrim_le_zero_of_nonpos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrim", "description": "Discriminant of a quadratic", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_ne_zero_of_discrim_ne_sq", "description": "A quadratic has no root if its discriminant has no square root.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrim_lt_zero", "description": "If a polynomial of degree 2 is always positive, then its discriminant is negative,\nat least when the coefficient of the quadratic term is nonzero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_eq_zero_iff", "description": "Roots of a quadratic equation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_eq_zero_iff_discrim_eq_sq", "description": "A quadratic has roots if and only if its discriminant equals some square.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrim_eq_sq_of_quadratic_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrim_le_zero", "description": "If a polynomial of degree 2 is always nonnegative, then its discriminant is nonpositive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_eq_zero_iff_of_discrim_eq_zero", "description": "Root of a quadratic when its discriminant equals zero", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrim_lt_zero_of_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.mem_of_min_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.max_is_maximal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.mem_of_max_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.eq_leaf_of_max_eq_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.eq_leaf_of_min_eq_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.min_is_minimal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.impartial_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.le_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.impartial_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.exists_right_move_equiv_iff_fuzzy_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.equiv_iff_add_equiv_zero'", "description": "This lemma doesn't require `H` to be impartial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial", "description": "A typeclass on impartial games.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.out", "description": "A typeclass on impartial games.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.equiv_zero_iff_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.forall_right_moves_fuzzy_iff_equiv_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.fuzzy_zero_iff_gf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.not_fuzzy_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.mk_neg_equiv_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.mk_add_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.equiv_iff_add_equiv_zero", "description": "This lemma doesn't require `H` to be impartial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.fuzzy_zero_iff_lf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.impartial_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.move_left_impartial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.impartial_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.exists_left_move_equiv_iff_fuzzy_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.forall_left_moves_fuzzy_iff_equiv_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.equiv_zero_iff_ge", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.add_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.equiv_or_fuzzy_zero", "description": "In an impartial game, either the first player always wins, or the second player always wins.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.not_equiv_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial_aux_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.impartial_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial_aux", "description": "The definition for a impartial game, defined using Conway induction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.neg_equiv_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.move_right_impartial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.lf_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial_iff_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.impartial.nonpos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.homogeneous_submodule", "description": "The submodule of homogeneous `mv_polynomial`s of degree `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.homogeneous_component_eq_zero'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.is_homogeneous_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.homogeneous_component_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.homogeneous_component_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.homogeneous_submodule_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.is_homogeneous.sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.is_homogeneous.inj_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.homogeneous_component_C_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.is_homogeneous_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.is_homogeneous_monomial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.mem_homogeneous_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.is_homogeneous.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.sum_homogeneous_component", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.is_homogeneous", "description": " A multivariate polynomial `\u03c6` is homogeneous of degree `n`\nif all monomials occuring in `\u03c6` have degree `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.is_homogeneous.total_degree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.is_homogeneous_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.is_homogeneous.homogeneous_submodule.gcomm_semiring", "description": "The homogeneous submodules form a graded ring. This instance is used by `direct_sum.comm_semiring`\nand `direct_sum.algebra`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.homogeneous_submodule_eq_finsupp_supported", "description": "While equal, the former has a convenient definitional reduction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.is_homogeneous_of_total_degree_zero", "description": "", 