"", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.expand_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.map_expand", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.expand", "description": "Expand the polynomial by a factor of p, so `\u2211 a\u2099 x\u207f` becomes `\u2211 a\u2099 x\u207f\u1d56`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.expand_eval", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.expand_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.expand_eq_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.map_expand_pow_char", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.monic.expand", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_expand_mul'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.expand_injective", "description": "Expansion is injective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_contract", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.expand_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.expand_expand", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.expand_aeval", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.expand_char", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_expand", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_expand", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.expand_eq_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.of_irreducible_expand_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_local_ring_hom_expand", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.is_asymm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.induction_bot", "description": " Let `r` be a well-founded relation on `\u03b1`, let `C : \u03b1 \u2192 Prop`, and let `bot : \u03b1`.\nThis induction principle shows that `C bot` holds, given that\n* some `a` satisfies `C a`, and\n* for each `b` that satisfies `C b`, there is `c` satisfying `r c b` and `C c`.\n\nThe naming is inspired by the fact that when `r` is transitive, it follows that `bot` is\nthe smallest element w.r.t. `r` that satisfies `C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.self_le_of_strict_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.has_min", "description": " If `r` is a well-founded relation, then any nonempty set has a minimal element\nwith respect to `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.not_lt_argmin", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.on_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.eq_strict_mono_iff_eq_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.is_irrefl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.has_well_founded.r.is_irrefl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.not_lt_argmin_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.sup", "description": "The supremum of a bounded, well-founded order", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.argmin_on", "description": " Given a function `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` where `\u03b2` carries a well-founded `<`, and a non-empty subset `s`\nof `\u03b1`, this is an element of `s` whose image under `f` is minimal in the sense of\n`function.not_lt_argmin_on`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.lt_succ_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.not_lt_min", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.has_well_founded.r.is_asymm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "acc.induction_bot'", "description": " Let `r` be a relation on `\u03b1`, let `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` be a function, let `C : \u03b2 \u2192 Prop`, and\nlet `bot : \u03b1`. This induction principle shows that `C (f bot)` holds, given that\n* some `a` that is accessible by `r` satisfies `C (f a)`, and\n* for each `b` such that `f b \u2260 f bot` and `C (f b)` holds, there is `c`\n  satisfying `r c b` and `C (f c)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.argmin_on_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.argmin", "description": " Given a function `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` where `\u03b2` carries a well-founded `<`, this is an element of `\u03b1`\nwhose image under `f` is minimal in the sense of `function.not_lt_argmin`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.succ", "description": " A successor of an element `x` in a well-founded order is a minimal element `y` such that\n`x < y` if one exists. Otherwise it is `x` itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.lt_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.argmin_on_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.argmin_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.induction_bot'", "description": " Let `r` be a well-founded relation on `\u03b1`, let `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` be a function,\nlet `C : \u03b2 \u2192 Prop`, and  let `bot : \u03b1`.\nThis induction principle shows that `C (f bot)` holds, given that\n* some `a` satisfies `C (f a)`, and\n* for each `b` such that `f b \u2260 f bot` and `C (f b)` holds, there is `c`\n  satisfying `r c b` and `C (f c)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "acc.induction_bot", "description": " Let `r` be a relation on `\u03b1`, let `C : \u03b1 \u2192 Prop` and let `bot : \u03b1`.\nThis induction principle shows that `C bot` holds, given that\n* some `a` that is accessible by `r` satisfies `C a`, and\n* for each `b \u2260 bot` such that `C b` holds, there is `c` satisfying `r c b` and `C c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.well_founded_iff_has_min", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.min", "description": " A minimal element of a nonempty set in a well-founded order.\n\nIf you're working with a nonempty linear order, consider defining a\n`conditionally_complete_linear_order_bot` instance via\n`well_founded.conditionally_complete_linear_order_with_bot` and using `Inf` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.min_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.min_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.lt_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.field_simp", "description": "The goal of `field_simp` is to reduce an expression in a field to an expression of the form `n / d`\nwhere neither `n` nor `d` contains any division symbol, just using the simplifier (with a carefully\ncrafted simpset named `field_simps`) to reduce the number of division symbols whenever possible by\niterating the following steps:\n\n- write an inverse as a division\n- in any product, move the division to the right\n- if there are several divisions in a product, group them together at the end and write them as a\n  single division\n- reduce a sum to a common denominator\n\nIf the goal is an equality, this simpset will also clear the denominators, so that the proof\ncan normally be concluded by an application of `ring` or `ring_exp`.\n\n`field_simp [hx, hy]` is a short form for\n`simp [-one_div, -mul_eq_zero, hx, hy] with field_simps {discharger := tactic.field_simp.ne_zero}`\n\nNote that this naive algorithm will not try to detect common factors in denominators to reduce the\ncomplexity of the resulting expression. Instead, it relies on the ability of `ring` to handle\ncomplicated expressions in the next step.\n\nAs always with the simplifier, reduction steps will only be applied if the preconditions of the\nlemmas can be checked. This means that proofs that denominators are nonzero should be included. The\nfact that a product is nonzero when all factors are, and that a power of a nonzero number is\nnonzero, are included in the simpset, but more complicated assertions (especially dealing with sums)\nshould be given explicitly. If your expression is not completely reduced by the simplifier\ninvocation, check the denominators of the resulting expression and provide proofs that they are\nnonzero to enable further progress.\n\nTo check that denominators are nonzero, `field_simp` will look for facts in the context, and\nwill try to apply `norm_num` to close numerical goals.\n\nThe invocation of `field_simp` removes the lemma `one_div` from the simpset, as this lemma\nworks against the algorithm explained above. It also removes\n`mul_eq_zero : x * y = 0 \u2194 x = 0 \u2228 y = 0`, as `norm_num` can not work on disjunctions to\nclose goals of the form `24 \u2260 0`, and replaces it with `mul_ne_zero : x \u2260 0 \u2192 y \u2260 0 \u2192 x * y \u2260 0`\ncreating two goals instead of a disjunction.\n\nFor example,\n```lean\nexample (a b c d x y : \u2102) (hx : x \u2260 0) (hy : y \u2260 0) :\n  a + b / x + c / x^2 + d / x^3 = a + x\u207b\u00b9 * (y * b / y + (d / x + c) / x) :=\nbegin\n  field_simp,\n  ring\nend\n```\n\nMoreover, the `field_simp` tactic can also take care of inverses of units in\na general (commutative) monoid/ring and partial division `/\u209a`, see `algebra.group.units`\nfor the definition. Analogue to the case above, the lemma `one_divp` is removed from the simpset\nas this works against the algorithm. If you have objects with a `is_unit x` instance like\n`(x : R) (hx : is_unit x)`, you should lift them with\n`lift x to R\u02e3 using id hx, rw is_unit.unit_of_coe_units, clear hx`\nbefore using `field_simp`.\n\nSee also the `cancel_denoms` tactic, which tries to do a similar simplification for expressions\nthat have numerals in denominators.\nThe tactics are not related: `cancel_denoms` will only handle numeric denominators, and will try to\nentirely remove (numeric) division from the expression by multiplying by a factor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.field_simp", "description": "The goal of `field_simp` is to reduce an expression in a field to an expression of the form `n / d`\nwhere neither `n` nor `d` contains any division symbol, just using the simplifier (with a carefully\ncrafted simpset named `field_simps`) to reduce the number of division symbols whenever possible by\niterating the following steps:\n\n- write an inverse as a division\n- in any product, move the division to the right\n- if there are several divisions in a product, group them together at the end and write them as a\n  single division\n- reduce a sum to a common denominator\n\nIf the goal is an equality, this simpset will also clear the denominators, so that the proof\ncan normally be concluded by an application of `ring` or `ring_exp`.\n\n`field_simp [hx, hy]` is a short form for\n`simp [-one_div, -mul_eq_zero, hx, hy] with field_simps {discharger := tactic.field_simp.ne_zero}`\n\nNote that this naive algorithm will not try to detect common factors in denominators to reduce the\ncomplexity of the resulting expression. Instead, it relies on the ability of `ring` to handle\ncomplicated expressions in the next step.\n\nAs always with the simplifier, reduction steps will only be applied if the preconditions of the\nlemmas can be checked. This means that proofs that denominators are nonzero should be included. The\nfact that a product is nonzero when all factors are, and that a power of a nonzero number is\nnonzero, are included in the simpset, but more complicated assertions (especially dealing with sums)\nshould be given explicitly. If your expression is not completely reduced by the simplifier\ninvocation, check the denominators of the resulting expression and provide proofs that they are\nnonzero to enable further progress.\n\nTo check that denominators are nonzero, `field_simp` will look for facts in the context, and\nwill try to apply `norm_num` to close numerical goals.\n\nThe invocation of `field_simp` removes the lemma `one_div` from the simpset, as this lemma\nworks against the algorithm explained above. It also removes\n`mul_eq_zero : x * y = 0 \u2194 x = 0 \u2228 y = 0`, as `norm_num` can not work on disjunctions to\nclose goals of the form `24 \u2260 0`, and replaces it with `mul_ne_zero : x \u2260 0 \u2192 y \u2260 0 \u2192 x * y \u2260 0`\ncreating two goals instead of a disjunction.\n\nFor example,\n```lean\nexample (a b c d x y : \u2102) (hx : x \u2260 0) (hy : y \u2260 0) :\n  a + b / x + c / x^2 + d / x^3 = a + x\u207b\u00b9 * (y * b / y + (d / x + c) / x) :=\nbegin\n  field_simp,\n  ring\nend\n```\n\nMoreover, the `field_simp` tactic can also take care of inverses of units in\na general (commutative) monoid/ring and partial division `/\u209a`, see `algebra.group.units`\nfor the definition. Analogue to the case above, the lemma `one_divp` is removed from the simpset\nas this works against the algorithm. If you have objects with a `is_unit x` instance like\n`(x : R) (hx : is_unit x)`, you should lift them with\n`lift x to R\u02e3 using id hx, rw is_unit.unit_of_coe_units, clear hx`\nbefore using `field_simp`.\n\nSee also the `cancel_denoms` tactic, which tries to do a similar simplification for expressions\nthat have numerals in denominators.\nThe tactics are not related: `cancel_denoms` will only handle numeric denominators, and will try to\nentirely remove (numeric) division from the expression by multiplying by a factor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.field_simp.ne_zero", "description": " Try to prove a goal of the form `x \u2260 0` by calling `assumption`, or `norm_num1` if `x` is\na numeral.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_limit_pair.iso_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_limit_pair.of_iso_prod_comparison", "description": "If the product comparison map for `G` at `(X,Y)` is an isomorphism, then `G` preserves the\npair of `(X,Y)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_limit_pair.iso", "description": "If `G` preserves the product of `(X,Y)`, then the product comparison map for `G` at `(X,Y)` is\nan isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.map_is_limit_of_preserves_of_is_limit", "description": "The property of preserving products expressed in terms of binary fans.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_colimit_pair.iso_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.coprod_comparison.category_theory.is_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_colimit_pair.of_iso_coprod_comparison", "description": "If the coproduct comparison map for `G` at `(X,Y)` is an isomorphism, then `G` preserves the\npair of `(X,Y)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.prod_comparison.category_theory.is_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_limit_of_has_binary_product_of_preserves_limit", "description": "If `G` preserves binary products and `C` has them, then the binary fan constructed of the mapped\nmorphisms of the binary product cone is a limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_colimit_of_reflects_of_map_is_colimit", "description": "The property of reflecting coproducts expressed in terms of binary cofans.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_limit_of_reflects_of_map_is_limit", "description": "The property of reflecting products expressed in terms of binary fans.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_colimit_map_cocone_binary_cofan_equiv", "description": "The map of a binary cofan is a colimit iff\nthe cofork consisting of the mapped morphisms is a colimit.\nThis essentially lets us commute `binary_cofan.mk` with `functor.map_cocone`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_colimit_pair.iso", "description": "If `G` preserves the coproduct of `(X,Y)`, then the coproduct comparison map for `G` at `(X,Y)` is\nan isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_limit_map_cone_binary_fan_equiv", "description": "The map of a binary fan is a limit iff the fork consisting of the mapped morphisms is a limit. This\nessentially lets us commute `binary_fan.mk` with `functor.map_cone`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_colimit_of_has_binary_coproduct_of_preserves_colimit", "description": "If `G` preserves binary coproducts and `C` has them, then the binary cofan constructed of the mapped\nmorphisms of the binary product cocone is a colimit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.map_is_colimit_of_preserves_of_is_colimit", "description": "The property of preserving coproducts expressed in terms of binary cofans.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_indicator_eq_indicator_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_indicator_le_of_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnnorm_indicator_eq_indicator_nnnorm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_indicator_le_norm_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "indicator_norm_le_norm_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.subset_dedup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.head_dedup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.dedup_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.sum_map_count_dedup_eq_length", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.dedup_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.replicate_dedup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.dedup_append", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.tail_dedup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.dedup_cons_of_not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.dedup_cons_of_mem'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.mem_dedup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.dedup_cons_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.count_dedup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.dedup_idempotent", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.dedup_eq_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.dedup_sublist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup_dedup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.sum_map_count_dedup_filter_eq_countp", "description": "Summing the count of `x` over a list filtered by some `p` is just `countp` applied to `p`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.dedup_eq_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup.dedup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.dedup_eq_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.dedup_cons_of_not_mem'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.card_disj_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.mem_disj_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.disj_sum", "description": "Disjoint sum of multisets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.disj_sum_mono_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.disj_sum_strict_mono_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.disj_sum_lt_disj_sum_of_lt_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.disj_sum_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.disj_sum_strict_mono_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.nodup.disj_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.disj_sum_lt_disj_sum_of_le_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.zero_disj_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.disj_sum_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.disj_sum_mono_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.inl_mem_disj_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.inr_mem_disj_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.coe_max", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.Ioc_eq_finset_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_fintype_Iic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_Iio", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.Iic_eq_finset_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.Ico_eq_finset_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_fintype_Ici", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.Ioi_eq_finset_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.map_subtype_embedding_uIcc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.coe_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.Ioo_eq_finset_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.coe_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.map_subtype_embedding_Iio", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.locally_finite_order_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.map_subtype_embedding_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.map_subtype_embedding_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.map_subtype_embedding_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_fintype_uIcc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_fintype_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.Iio_eq_finset_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.Icc_eq_finset_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_fintype_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_fintype_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.Ici_eq_finset_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_Iic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_fintype_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_fintype_Iio", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.map_subtype_embedding_Iic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.map_subtype_embedding_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.locally_finite_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_Ici", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.coe_min", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.uIcc_eq_finset_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_uIcc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.map_subtype_embedding_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.map_subtype_embedding_Ici", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_fintype_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.locally_finite_order_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.pred_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "covby.coe_fin", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `fin.coe_covby_iff`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.is_pred_archimedean", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.coe_covby_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.pred_iterate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.succ_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.succ_eq_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.succ_iterate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.is_succ_archimedean", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.covby_iff_succ_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.pred_eq_pred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.is_searchable_none_high_of_is_searchable_some_high", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.is_red_black", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.is_red_black.leaf_rb", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.is_red_black.red_rb", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.is_red_black.black_rb", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.lt_of_mem_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.depth_max'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.is_searchable_of_incomp_of_is_searchable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.lt_of_mem_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.lo_lt_hi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.is_searchable_of_is_searchable_of_incomp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.depth_max", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.is_searchable_some_high_of_is_searchable_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.is_searchable_some_low_of_is_searchable_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.depth_min", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.is_searchable_tactic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.is_searchable_none_low_of_is_searchable_some_low", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.lt_of_mem_left_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.is_node_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.is_node_of.of_red", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.is_node_of.of_black", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.blast_disjs", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.is_searchable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.is_searchable.leaf_s", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.is_searchable.red_s", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.is_searchable.black_s", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbnode.balanced", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on_Ico_extend_from_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on_Ioc_extend_from_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_lim_at_left_extend_from_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_lim_at_right_extend_from_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on_Icc_extend_from_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_ring.to_nonarchimedean_add_group", "description": "Every nonarchimedean ring is naturally a nonarchimedean additive group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_ring.mul_subset", "description": "An open subgroup of a nonarchimedean ring contains the square of another one.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_ring.prod.nonarchimedean_ring", "description": "The cartesian product of two nonarchimedean rings is nonarchimedean.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_add_group.nonarchimedean_of_emb", "description": "If a topological group embeds into a\nnonarchimedean group, then it is nonarchimedean.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_add_group.prod_self_subset", "description": "An open neighborhood of the identity in the\ncartesian square of a nonarchimedean group contains the cartesian square of an open neighborhood in\nthe group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_add_group.prod_subset", "description": "An open neighborhood of the identity in the\ncartesian product of two nonarchimedean groups contains the cartesian product of an open\nneighborhood in each group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_ring.left_mul_subset", "description": "Given an open subgroup `U` and an element `r` of a nonarchimedean ring, there is an open\n subgroup `V` such that `r \u2022 V` is contained in `U`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_group.prod_subset", "description": " An open neighborhood of the identity in the cartesian product of two nonarchimedean groups\ncontains the cartesian product of an open neighborhood in each group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_add_group.prod.nonarchimedean_add_group", "description": "The cartesian product of two nonarchimedean groups is nonarchimedean.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_group.prod.nonarchimedean_group", "description": "The cartesian product of two nonarchimedean groups is nonarchimedean.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_ring", "description": "An topological ring is nonarchimedean if its underlying topological additive\n group is nonarchimedean.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_ring.to_topological_ring", "description": "An topological ring is nonarchimedean if its underlying topological additive\n group is nonarchimedean.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_ring.is_nonarchimedean", "description": "An topological ring is nonarchimedean if its underlying topological additive\n group is nonarchimedean.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_add_group", "description": "An topological additive group is nonarchimedean if every neighborhood of 0\n contains an open subgroup.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_add_group.to_topological_add_group", "description": "An topological additive group is nonarchimedean if every neighborhood of 0\n contains an open subgroup.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_add_group.is_nonarchimedean", "description": "An topological additive group is nonarchimedean if every neighborhood of 0\n contains an open subgroup.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_group", "description": "A topological group is nonarchimedean if every neighborhood of 1 contains an open subgroup.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_group.to_topological_group", "description": "A topological group is nonarchimedean if every neighborhood of 1 contains an open subgroup.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_group.is_nonarchimedean", "description": "A topological group is nonarchimedean if every neighborhood of 1 contains an open subgroup.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_group.prod_self_subset", "description": " An open neighborhood of the identity in the cartesian square of a nonarchimedean group\ncontains the cartesian square of an open neighborhood in the group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonarchimedean_group.nonarchimedean_of_emb", "description": "If a topological group embeds into a nonarchimedean group, then it is nonarchimedean.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.term.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.term.val_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.term.val_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.term.val_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.term.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.term.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.term.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.term.fresh_index", "description": "Fresh de Brujin index not used by any variable ocurring in the term", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.term.div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.term.neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.term.val", "description": "Evaluate a term using the valuation v.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.term.sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.term.val_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.term.to_string", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.term.has_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.term.val_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.terms.fresh_index", "description": "Fresh de Brujin index not used by any variable ocurring in the list of terms", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.term", "description": "Shadow syntax of normalized terms. The first element\n   represents the constant term and the list represents\n   the coefficients.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.ne_of_odd_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.even_coe_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.not_even_bit1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.even_mul_succ_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.mod_two_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.odd_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.even_xor_odd'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.even_add'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.odd_coe_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.mod_two_ne_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.not_even_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.odd_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "even.nat_abs", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `int.nat_abs_even`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.odd_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.div_two_mul_two_add_one_of_odd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.two_dvd_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.four_dvd_add_or_sub_of_odd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.two_mul_div_two_of_odd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.even_pow'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.even_sub'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.odd_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.odd.decidable_pred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.nat_abs_odd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.odd.of_mul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.two_not_dvd_two_mul_add_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.even_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.even_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.even_add_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.even_or_odd'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.not_even_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.nat_abs_even", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.not_odd_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.odd_iff_not_even", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.even_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.div_two_mul_two_of_even", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.is_compl_even_odd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "odd.nat_abs", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `int.nat_abs_odd`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.even_xor_odd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.odd.of_mul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.odd_sub'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.two_mul_div_two_add_one_of_odd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.two_mul_div_two_of_even", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.even.decidable_pred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.even_or_odd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.even_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.add_one_div_two_mul_two_of_odd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.even_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.odd_add'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.even_iff_not_odd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.push_neg_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.le_and_le_iff_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.neg_free_neg_elim", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.nnf", "description": "NNF transformation", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.implies_neg_elim_core", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.is_nnf_push_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.neg_elim", "description": "Eliminate all negations in a preform", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.neg_elim_core", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.is_nnf_nnf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.neg_free_neg_elim_core", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.push_neg", "description": "push_neg p returns the result of normalizing \u00ac p by\n   pushing the outermost negation all the way down,\n   until it reaches either a negation or an atom", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.implies_neg_elim", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.nnf_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.is_nnf", "description": "Asserts that the given preform is in NNF", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.eq_one_or_neg_one_of_mul_eq_neg_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.is_unit.nat_abs_eq", "description": "**Alias** of the forward direction of `int.is_unit_iff_nat_abs_eq`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.eq_one_or_neg_one_of_mul_eq_neg_one'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.is_unit_eq_or_eq_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.eq_one_or_neg_one_of_mul_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.units_nat_abs", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.is_unit_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.is_unit_mul_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.mul_eq_neg_one_iff_eq_one_or_neg_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.eq_of_mul_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.mul_eq_one_iff_eq_one_or_neg_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.eq_one_or_neg_one_of_mul_eq_one'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.units_eq_one_or", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.is_unit_iff_nat_abs_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.is_unit_add_is_unit_eq_is_unit_add_is_unit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.of_nat_is_unit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.is_unit_eq_one_or", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.subgraph.is_perfect_matching.even_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.subgraph.is_matching.to_edge_eq_to_edge_of_adj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.subgraph.is_matching", "description": "The subgraph `M` of `G` is a matching if every vertex of `M` is incident to exactly one edge in `M`.\nWe say that the vertices in `M.support` are *matched* or *saturated*.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.subgraph.is_perfect_matching_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.subgraph.is_matching.to_edge", "description": "Given a vertex, returns the unique edge of the matching it is incident to.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.subgraph.is_matching.even_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.subgraph.is_matching.to_edge_eq_of_adj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.subgraph.is_matching.to_edge.surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.subgraph.is_perfect_matching", "description": "The subgraph `M` of `G` is a perfect matching on `G` if it's a matching and every vertex `G` is\nmatched.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.subgraph.is_matching.support_eq_verts", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.subgraph.is_perfect_matching_iff_forall_degree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.subgraph.is_matching_iff_forall_degree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.dist_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.has_dist", "description": " The distance function on a product space `\u03a0 n, E n`, given by `dist x y = (1/2)^n` where `n` is\nthe first index at which `x` and `y` differ.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.cylinder", "description": " In a product space `\u03a0 n, E n`, the cylinder set of length `n` around `x`, denoted\n`cylinder x n`, is the set of sequences `y` that coincide with `x` on the first `n` symbols, i.e.,\nsuch that `y i = x i` for all `i < n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.Union_cylinder_update", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.is_open_cylinder", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.mem_cylinder_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.exists_lipschitz_retraction_of_is_closed", "description": " Given a closed nonempty subset `s` of `\u03a0 (n : \u2115), E n`, there exists a Lipschitz retraction\nonto this set, i.e., a Lipschitz map with range equal to `s`, equal to the identity on `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.apply_first_diff_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.dist_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_countable.min_dist_le_dist_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.cylinder_anti", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.cylinder_eq_res", "description": "`cylinder x n` is equal to the set of sequences `y` with the same restriction to `n` as `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.is_open_iff_dist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_countable.dist_le_dist_pi_of_dist_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.mem_cylinder_iff_dist_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.first_diff", "description": " In a product space `\u03a0 n, E n`, then `first_diff x y` is the first index at which `x` and `y`\ndiffer. If `x = y`, then by convention we set `first_diff x x = 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.res_length", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_nat_nat_continuous_surjective_of_complete_space", "description": " Any nonempty complete second countable metric space is the continuous image of the\nfundamental space `\u2115 \u2192 \u2115`. For a version of this theorem in the context of Polish spaces, see\n`exists_nat_nat_continuous_surjective_of_polish_space`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.metric_space", "description": " Metric space structure on `\u03a0 (n : \u2115), E n` when the spaces `E n` have the discrete topology,\nwhere the distance is given by `dist x y = (1/2)^n`, where `n` is the smallest index where `x` and\n`y` differ. Not registered as a global instance by default.\nWarning: this definition makes sure that the topology is defeq to the original product topology,\nbut it does not take care of a possible uniformity. If the `E n` have a uniform structure, then\nthere will be two non-defeq uniform structures on `\u03a0 n, E n`, the product one and the one coming\nfrom the metric structure. In this case, use `metric_space_of_discrete_uniformity` instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.lipschitz_with_one_iff_forall_dist_image_le_of_mem_cylinder", "description": " A function to a pseudo-metric-space is `1`-Lipschitz if and only if points in the same cylinder\nof length `n` are sent to points within distance `(1/2)^n`.\nNot expressed using `lipschitz_with` as we don't have a metric space structure", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.first_diff_le_longest_prefix", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_countable.dist_eq_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.mem_cylinder_first_diff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.exists_disjoint_cylinder", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.dist_triangle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.metric_space_of_discrete_uniformity", "description": " Metric space structure on `\u03a0 (n : \u2115), E n` when the spaces `E n` have the discrete uniformity,\nwhere the distance is given by `dist x y = (1/2)^n`, where `n` is the smallest index where `x` and\n`y` differ. Not registered as a global instance by default.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.mem_cylinder_iff_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.exists_retraction_of_is_closed", "description": " Given a closed nonempty subset `s` of `\u03a0 (n : \u2115), E n`, there exists a retraction onto this\nset, i.e., a continuous map with range equal to `s`, equal to the identity on `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.res", "description": " In the case where `E` has constant value `\u03b1`,\nthe cylinder `cylinder x n` can be identified with the element of `list \u03b1`\nconsisting of the first `n` entries of `x`. See `cylinder_eq_res`.\nWe call this list `res x n`, the restriction of `x` to `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.exists_retraction_subtype_of_is_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.dist_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.is_topological_basis_cylinders", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.disjoint_cylinder_of_longest_prefix_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.cylinder_eq_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.apply_eq_of_dist_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.mem_cylinder_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.res_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.res_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.eq_of_dist_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.mem_cylinder_iff_le_first_diff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_countable.metric_space", "description": " Given a countable family of metric spaces, one may put a distance on their product `\u03a0 i, E i`,\ndefining the right topology and uniform structure. It is highly non-canonical, though, and therefore\nnot registered as a global instance.\nThe distance we use here is `dist x y = \u2211' n, min (1/2)^(encode i) (dist (x n) (y n))`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.longest_prefix", "description": " Given a point `x` in a product space `\u03a0 (n : \u2115), E n`, and `s` a subset of this space, then\n`longest_prefix x s` if the largest `n` for which there is an element of `s` having the same\nprefix of length `n` as `x`. If there is no such `n`, use `0` by convention.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.shortest_prefix_diff_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.first_diff_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.cylinder_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.update_mem_cylinder", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.dist_eq_of_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.inter_cylinder_longest_prefix_nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.self_mem_cylinder", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_countable.dist_summable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.first_diff_lt_shortest_prefix_diff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.dist_triangle_nonarch", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.res_eq_res", "description": "The restrictions of `x` and `y` to `n` are equal if and only if `x m = y m` for all `m < n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.cylinder_longest_prefix_eq_of_longest_prefix_lt_first_diff", "description": " If two points `x, y` coincide up to length `n`, and the longest common prefix of `x` with `s`\nis strictly shorter than `n`, then the longest common prefix of `y` with `s` is the same, and both\ncylinders of this length based at `x` and `y` coincide.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_countable.has_dist", "description": " Given a countable family of metric spaces, one may put a distance on their product `\u03a0 i, E i`.\nIt is highly non-canonical, though, and therefore not registered as a global instance.\nThe distance we use here is `dist x y = \u2211' i, min (1/2)^(encode i) (dist (x i) (y i))`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.shortest_prefix_diff", "description": " Given a point `x` in a product space `\u03a0 (n : \u2115), E n`, and `s` a subset of this space, then\n`shortest_prefix_diff x s` if the smallest `n` for which there is no element of `s` having the same\nprefix of length `n` as `x`. If there is no such `n`, then use `0` by convention.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.complete_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.metric_space_nat_nat", "description": " Metric space structure on `\u2115 \u2192 \u2115` where the distance is given by `dist x y = (1/2)^n`,\nwhere `n` is the smallest index where `x` and `y` differ.\nNot registered as a global instance by default.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.apply_eq_of_lt_first_diff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.cylinder_eq_cylinder_of_le_first_diff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.res_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_nat.min_first_diff_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "volume_set_of_liouville", "description": "The set of Liouville numbers has Lebesgue measure zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_not_liouville_with", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.disjoint_residual_ae", "description": " The filters `residual \u211d` and `volume.ae` are disjoint. This means that there exists a residual\nset of Lebesgue measure zero (e.g., the set of Liouville numbers).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_of_liouville_with_subset_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "volume_Union_set_of_liouville_with", "description": " The set of numbers satisfying the Liouville condition with some exponent `p > 2` has Lebesgue\nmeasure zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_not_liouville", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_of_countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.pivot.exists_is_two_block_diagonal_of_ne_zero", "description": " There exist two lists of `transvection_struct` such that multiplying by them on the left and\non the right makes a matrix block-diagonal, when the last coefficient is nonzero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.pivot.list_transvec_col_mul_last_row", "description": "Multiplying by all the matrices in `list_transvec_col M` does not change the last row.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.prod_mul_reverse_inv_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.pivot.list_transvec_col_mul_last_col", "description": " Multiplying by all the matrices in `list_transvec_col M` kills all the coefficients in the\nlast column but the last one.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.pivot.list_transvec_col_mul_mul_list_transvec_row_last_col", "description": " Multiplying by all the matrices either in `list_transvec_col M` and `list_transvec_row M` kills\nall the coefficients in the last row but the last one.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.pivot.list_transvec_row", "description": " A list of transvections such that multiplying on the right with these transvections will replace\nthe last row with zeroes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.mul_sum_inl_to_matrix_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.pivot.list_transvec_col_mul_last_row_drop", "description": "Multiplying by some of the matrices in `list_transvec_col M` does not change the last row.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.diagonal_transvection_induction_of_det_ne_zero", "description": " Induction principle for invertible matrices based on transvections: if a property is true for\nall invertible diagonal matrices, all transvections, and is stable under product of invertible\nmatrices, then it is true for all invertible matrices. This is the useful way to say that\ninvertible matrices are generated by invertible diagonal matrices and transvections.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.det_to_matrix_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.det", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.pivot.exists_list_transvec_mul_mul_list_transvec_eq_diagonal", "description": "Any matrix can be reduced to diagonal form by elementary operations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.inv_j", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_transvection_apply_same", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.reindex_equiv", "description": " Given a `transvection_struct` on `n` and an equivalence between `n` and `p`, define the\ncorresponding `transvection_struct` on `p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.reverse_inv_prod_mul_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.mul_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.to_matrix_sum_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_mul_apply_of_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.pivot.mul_list_transvec_row_last_col_take", "description": "Multiplying by some of the matrices in `list_transvec_row M` does not change the last column.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct", "description": " A structure containing all the information from which one can build a nontrivial transvection.\nThis structure is easier to manipulate than transvections as one has a direct access to all the\nrelevant fields.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.i", "description": " A structure containing all the information from which one can build a nontrivial transvection.\nThis structure is easier to manipulate than transvections as one has a direct access to all the\nrelevant fields.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.j", "description": " A structure containing all the information from which one can build a nontrivial transvection.\nThis structure is easier to manipulate than transvections as one has a direct access to all the\nrelevant fields.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.hij", "description": " A structure containing all the information from which one can build a nontrivial transvection.\nThis structure is easier to manipulate than transvections as one has a direct access to all the\nrelevant fields.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.c", "description": " A structure containing all the information from which one can build a nontrivial transvection.\nThis structure is easier to manipulate than transvections as one has a direct access to all the\nrelevant fields.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_mul_transvection_same", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.pivot.list_transvec_col_mul_mul_list_transvec_row_last_row", "description": " Multiplying by all the matrices either in `list_transvec_col M` and `list_transvec_row M` kills\nall the coefficients in the last column but the last one.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.to_matrix_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.inv", "description": " The inverse of a `transvection_struct`, designed so that `t.inv.to_matrix` is the inverse of\n`t.to_matrix`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.pivot.list_transvec_col", "description": " A list of transvections such that multiplying on the left with these transvections will replace\nthe last column with zeroes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.pivot.exists_list_transvec_mul_diagonal_mul_list_transvec", "description": " Any matrix can be written as the product of transvections, a diagonal matrix, and\ntransvections.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.pivot.reindex_exists_list_transvec_mul_mul_list_transvec_eq_diagonal", "description": "Reduction to diagonal form by elementary operations is invariant under reindexing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_mul_apply_same", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.diagonal_transvection_induction", "description": " Induction principle for matrices based on transvections: if a property is true for all diagonal\nmatrices, all transvections, and is stable under product, then it is true for all matrices. This is\nthe useful way to say that matrices are generated by diagonal matrices and transvections.\n\nWe state a slightly more general version: to prove a property for a matrix `M`, it suffices to\nassume that the diagonal matrices we consider have the same determinant as `M`. This is useful to\nobtain similar principles for `SL\u2099` or `GL\u2099`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_transvection_of_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.pivot.is_two_block_diagonal_list_transvec_col_mul_mul_list_transvec_row", "description": " Multiplying by all the matrices either in `list_transvec_col M` and `list_transvec_row M` turns\nthe matrix in block-diagonal form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.pivot.exists_list_transvec_mul_mul_list_transvec_eq_diagonal_induction", "description": " Inductive step for the reduction: if one knows that any size `r` matrix can be reduced to\ndiagonal form by elementary operations, then one deduces it for matrices over `fin r \u2295 unit`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.to_matrix_reindex_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.inv_i", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.pivot.mul_list_transvec_row_last_col", "description": "Multiplying by all the matrices in `list_transvec_row M` does not change the last column.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.inv_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.sum_inl", "description": " Given a `transvection_struct` on `n`, define the corresponding `transvection_struct` on `n \u2295 p`\nusing the identity on `p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.to_matrix", "description": "Associating to a `transvection_struct` the corresponding transvection matrix.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mul_transvection_apply_of_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.pivot.exists_is_two_block_diagonal_list_transvec_mul_mul_list_transvec", "description": " There exist two lists of `transvection_struct` such that multiplying by them on the left and\non the right makes a matrix block-diagonal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.pivot.mul_list_transvec_row_last_row", "description": " Multiplying by all the matrices in `list_transvec_row M` kills all the coefficients in the\nlast row but the last one.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection", "description": " The transvection matrix `transvection i j c` is equal to the identity plus `c` at position\n`(i, j)`. Multiplying by it on the left (as in `transvection i j c \u2b1d M`) corresponds to adding\n`c` times the `j`-th line of `M` to its `i`-th line. Multiplying by it on the right corresponds\nto adding `c` times the `i`-th column to the `j`-th column.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.pivot.exists_list_transvec_mul_mul_list_transvec_eq_diagonal_aux", "description": " Any matrix can be reduced to diagonal form by elementary operations. Formulated here on `Type 0`\nbecause we will make an induction using `fin r`.\nSee `exists_list_transvec_mul_mul_list_transvec_eq_diagonal` for the general version (which follows\nfrom this one and reindexing).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.update_row_eq_transvection", "description": " A transvection matrix is obtained from the identity by adding `c` times the `j`-th row to\nthe `i`-th row.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.to_matrix_reindex_equiv_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.inv_c", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.transvection_struct.sum_inl_to_matrix_prod_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "guard", "description": "If the condition `p` is decided to be false, then fail, otherwise, return unit.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "optional", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "alternative", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "alternative.to_applicative", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "alternative.to_has_orelse", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "alternative.failure", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "failure", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "assert", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "guardb", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_orelse", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_orelse.orelse", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_lifting_property.iff_op", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_lifting_property.iff_of_arrow_iso_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_lifting_property.iff_unop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_lifting_property", "description": " `has_lifting_property i p` means that `i` has the left lifting\nproperty with respect to `p`, or equivalently that `p` has\nthe right lifting property with respect to `i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_lifting_property.sq_has_lift", "description": " `has_lifting_property i p` means that `i` has the left lifting\nproperty with respect to `p`, or equivalently that `p` has\nthe right lifting property with respect to `i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_lifting_property.of_right_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_lifting_property.of_arrow_iso_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_lifting_property.of_comp_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_lifting_property.of_comp_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_lifting_property.of_left_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_lifting_property.unop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_lifting_property.of_arrow_iso_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_lifting_property.iff_of_arrow_iso_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sq_has_lift_of_has_lifting_property", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_lifting_property.op", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_non_unital_non_assoc_algebra.lift_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_non_unital_non_assoc_algebra.lift", "description": " The functor `X \u21a6 free_non_unital_non_assoc_algebra R X` from the category of types to the\ncategory of non-unital, non-associative algebras over `R` is adjoint to the forgetful functor in the\nother direction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_non_unital_non_assoc_algebra.of", "description": "The embedding of `X` into the free algebra with coefficients in `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_non_unital_non_assoc_algebra.lift_of_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_non_unital_non_assoc_algebra.of_comp_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_non_unital_non_assoc_algebra.lift_comp_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_non_unital_non_assoc_algebra.hom_ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_non_unital_non_assoc_algebra", "description": "The free non-unital, non-associative algebra on the type `X` with coefficients in `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_non_unital_non_assoc_algebra.lift_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_right_bijective\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.mul_right\u2080_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.mul_left\u2080_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.mul_left\u2080_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.mul_right\u2080_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.mul_left\u2080", "description": " Left multiplication by a nonzero element in a `group_with_zero` is a permutation of the\nunderlying type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_left_bijective\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.mul_right\u2080", "description": " Right multiplication by a nonzero element in a `group_with_zero` is a permutation of the\nunderlying type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.inf_mem_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.partial_order", "description": "The partial ordering by subset inclusion, inherited from `set P`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.principal_le_principal_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.principal_le_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.is_pfilter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.mem_principal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.directed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.top_mem", "description": "A specific witness of `pfilter.nonempty` when `P` has a top element.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.is_pfilter.to_pfilter", "description": " Create an element of type `order.pfilter` from a set satisfying the predicate\n`order.is_pfilter`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.mem_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.Inf_gc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.ext", "description": "Two filters are equal when their underlying sets are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.order_bot", "description": "There is a bottom filter when `P` has a top element.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.mem_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.is_pfilter", "description": "A predicate for when a subset of `P` is a filter.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.principal", "description": "The smallest filter containing a given element.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.has_mem", "description": "For the notation `x \u2208 F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.is_pfilter.of_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter", "description": "A filter on a preorder `P` is a subset of `P` that is\n - nonempty\n - downward directed\n - upward closed.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.dual", "description": "A filter on a preorder `P` is a subset of `P` that is\n - nonempty\n - downward directed\n - upward closed.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.antitone_principal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.Inf_gi", "description": "If a poset `P` admits arbitrary `Inf`s, then `principal` and `Inf` form a Galois coinsertion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.set.has_coe", "description": "A filter on `P` is a subset of `P`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.inf_mem", "description": "A specific witness of `pfilter.directed` when `P` has meets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.order_top", "description": "There is a top filter when `P` has a bottom element.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.mem_of_mem_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.pfilter.mem_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.nlinarith_extras", "description": "`nlinarith_extras` is the preprocessor corresponding to the `nlinarith` tactic.\n\n* For every term `t` such that `t^2` or `t*t` appears in the input, adds a proof of `t^2 \u2265 0`\n  or `t*t \u2265 0`.\n* For every pair of comparisons `t1 R1 0` and `t2 R2 0`, adds a proof of `t1*t2 R 0`.\n\nThis preprocessor is typically run last, after all inputs have been canonized.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.get_nat_comps", "description": "`get_nat_comps e` returns a list of all subexpressions of `e` of the form `((t : \u2115) : \u2124)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.strengthen_strict_int", "description": " `strengthen_strict_int h` turns a proof `h` of a strict integer inequality `t1 < t2`\ninto a proof of `t1 \u2264 t2 + 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.remove_negations", "description": "Replaces proofs of negations of comparisons with proofs of the reversed comparisons.\nFor example, a proof of `\u00ac a < b` will become a proof of `a \u2265 b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.nat_to_int", "description": "If `h` is an equality or inequality between natural numbers,\n`nat_to_int` lifts this inequality to the integers.\nIt also adds the facts that the integers involved are nonnegative.\nTo avoid adding the same nonnegativity facts many times, it is a global preprocessor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.normalize_denominators_in_lhs", "description": "`normalize_denominators_in_lhs h lhs` assumes that `h` is a proof of `lhs R 0`.\nIt creates a proof of `lhs' R 0`, where all numeric division in `lhs` has been cancelled.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.rem_neg", "description": "If `prf` is a proof of `\u00ac e`, where `e` is a comparison,\n`rem_neg prf e` flips the comparison in `e` and returns a proof.\nFor example, if `prf : \u00ac a < b`, ``rem_neg prf `(a < b)`` returns a proof of `a \u2265 b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.is_strict_int_prop", "description": "`is_strict_int_prop tp` is true iff `tp` is a strict inequality between integers\nor the negation of a weak inequality between integers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.is_nat_prop", "description": "`is_nat_prop tp` is true iff `tp` is an inequality or equality between natural numbers\nor the negation thereof.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.is_nat_int_coe", "description": "If `e` is of the form `((n : \u2115) : \u2124)`, `is_nat_int_coe e` returns `n : \u2115`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.mk_non_strict_int_pf_of_strict_int_pf", "description": "If `pf` is a proof of a strict inequality `(a : \u2124) < b`,\n`mk_non_strict_int_pf_of_strict_int_pf pf` returns a proof of `a + 1 \u2264 b`,\nand similarly if `pf` proves a negated weak inequality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.remove_ne", "description": "`remove_ne` case splits on any proof `h : a \u2260 b` in the input, turning it into `a < b \u2228 a > b`,\nby calling `linarith.remove_ne_aux`.\nThis produces `2^n` branches when there are `n` such hypotheses in the input.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.remove_ne_aux", "description": "`remove_ne_aux` case splits on any proof `h : a \u2260 b` in the input, turning it into `a < b \u2228 a > b`.\nThis produces `2^n` branches when there are `n` such hypotheses in the input.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.cancel_denoms", "description": "`cancel_denoms pf` assumes `pf` is a proof of `t R 0`. If `t` contains the division symbol `/`,\nit tries to scale `t` to cancel out division by numerals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.filter_comparisons", "description": "Removes any expressions that are not proofs of inequalities, equalities, or negations thereof.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.default_preprocessors", "description": "The default list of preprocessors, in the order they should typically run.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.mk_coe_nat_nonneg_prf", "description": "If `e : \u2115`, returns a proof of `0 \u2264 (e : \u2124)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.preprocess", "description": "`preprocess pps l` takes a list `l` of proofs of propositions.\nIt maps each preprocessor `pp \u2208 pps` over this list.\nThe preprocessors are run sequentially: each recieves the output of the previous one.\nNote that a preprocessor may produce multiple or no expressions from each input expression,\nso the size of the list may change.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.rearr_comp", "description": "`rearr_comp e` takes a proof `e` of an equality, inequality, or negation thereof,\nand turns it into a proof of a comparison `_ R 0`, where `R \u2208 {=, \u2264, <}`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.find_squares", "description": "`find_squares m e` collects all terms of the form `a ^ 2` and `a * a` that appear in `e`\nand adds them to the set `m`.\nA pair `(a, tt)` is added to `m` when `a^2` appears in `e`, and `(a, ff)` is added to `m`\nwhen `a*a` appears in `e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linarith.make_comp_with_zero", "description": "`mk_comp_with_zero h` takes a proof `h` of an equality, inequality, or negation thereof,\nand turns it into a proof of a comparison `_ R 0`, where `R \u2208 {=, \u2264, <}`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.to_finsupp_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.to_dfinsupp_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.to_finsupp_to_dfinsupp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp_equiv_dfinsupp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.to_finsupp_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp_equiv_dfinsupp_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.to_dfinsupp_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma_finsupp_equiv_dfinsupp_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.to_dfinsupp_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp_lequiv_dfinsupp", "description": " The additive version of `finsupp.to_finsupp`. Note that this is `noncomputable` because\n`finsupp.has_add` is noncomputable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma_finsupp_equiv_dfinsupp_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma_finsupp_lequiv_dfinsupp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp_lequiv_dfinsupp_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp_add_equiv_dfinsupp", "description": " The additive version of `finsupp.to_finsupp`. Note that this is `noncomputable` because\n`finsupp.has_add` is noncomputable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.to_dfinsupp_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.to_dfinsupp", "description": "Interpret a `finsupp` as a homogenous `dfinsupp`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp_equiv_dfinsupp", "description": "`finsupp.to_dfinsupp` and `dfinsupp.to_finsupp` together form an equiv.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma_finsupp_add_equiv_dfinsupp", "description": "`finsupp.split` is an additive equivalence between `(\u03a3 i, \u03b7 i) \u2192\u2080 N` and `\u03a0\u2080 i, (\u03b7 i \u2192\u2080 N)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.to_dfinsupp_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp_add_equiv_dfinsupp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp_lequiv_dfinsupp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma_finsupp_add_equiv_dfinsupp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma_finsupp_equiv_dfinsupp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.to_finsupp_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma_finsupp_lequiv_dfinsupp_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp_add_equiv_dfinsupp_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.to_finsupp_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma_finsupp_equiv_dfinsupp_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "to_dfinsupp_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.to_finsupp_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma_finsupp_equiv_dfinsupp_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.to_finsupp_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.to_finsupp_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.to_finsupp", "description": " Interpret a homogenous `dfinsupp` as a `finsupp`.\n\nNote that the elaborator has a lot of trouble with this definition - it is often necessary to\nwrite `(dfinsupp.to_finsupp f : \u03b9 \u2192\u2080 M)` instead of `f.to_finsupp`, as for some unknown reason\nusing dot notation or omitting the type ascription prevents the type being resolved correctly.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.to_dfinsupp_to_finsupp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.to_finsupp_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.to_dfinsupp_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma_finsupp_equiv_dfinsupp_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma_finsupp_equiv_dfinsupp", "description": "`finsupp.split` is an equivalence between `(\u03a3 i, \u03b7 i) \u2192\u2080 N` and `\u03a0\u2080 i, (\u03b7 i \u2192\u2080 N)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma_finsupp_lequiv_dfinsupp", "description": "`finsupp.split` is a linear equivalence between `(\u03a3 i, \u03b7 i) \u2192\u2080 N` and `\u03a0\u2080 i, (\u03b7 i \u2192\u2080 N)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma_finsupp_add_equiv_dfinsupp_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.to_dfinsupp_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_dual.topological_lattice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_continuous_inf", "description": "Let `L` be a topological space and let `L\u00d7L` be equipped with the product topology and let\n`\u2293:L\u00d7L \u2192 L` be an infimum. Then `L` is said to have *(jointly) continuous infimum* if the map\n`\u2293:L\u00d7L \u2192 L` is continuous.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_continuous_inf.continuous_inf", "description": "Let `L` be a topological space and let `L\u00d7L` be equipped with the product topology and let\n`\u2293:L\u00d7L \u2192 L` be an infimum. Then `L` is said to have *(jointly) continuous infimum* if the map\n`\u2293:L\u00d7L \u2192 L` is continuous.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_continuous_sup", "description": "Let `L` be a topological space and let `L\u00d7L` be equipped with the product topology and let\n`\u2293:L\u00d7L \u2192 L` be a supremum. Then `L` is said to have *(jointly) continuous supremum* if the map\n`\u2293:L\u00d7L \u2192 L` is continuous.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_continuous_sup.continuous_sup", "description": "Let `L` be a topological space and let `L\u00d7L` be equipped with the product topology and let\n`\u2293:L\u00d7L \u2192 L` be a supremum. Then `L` is said to have *(jointly) continuous supremum* if the map\n`\u2293:L\u00d7L \u2192 L` is continuous.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_dual.has_continuous_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.sup_right_nhds'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.inf_right_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.sup_right_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_order.topological_lattice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_lattice", "description": "Let `L` be a lattice equipped with a topology such that `L` has continuous infimum and supremum.\nThen `L` is said to be a *topological lattice*.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_lattice.to_has_continuous_inf", "description": "Let `L` be a lattice equipped with a topology such that `L` has continuous infimum and supremum.\nThen `L` is said to be a *topological lattice*.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_lattice.to_has_continuous_sup", "description": "Let `L` be a lattice equipped with a topology such that `L` has continuous infimum and supremum.\nThen `L` is said to be a *topological lattice*.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.inf_right_nhds'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_dual.has_continuous_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slash_action.neg_slash", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "modular_form.is_invariant_one", "description": "The constant function 1 is invariant under any element of `SL(2, \u2124)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "modular_form.SL_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "modular_form.complex.slash_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "modular_form.subgroup_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "modular_form.SL_slash", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "modular_form.mul_slash_SL2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "modular_form.slash", "description": "The weight `k` action of `GL(2, \u211d)\u207a` on functions `f : \u210d \u2192 \u2102`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "modular_form.mul_slash", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "modular_form.mul_slash_subgroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slash_action.smul_slash_of_tower", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "modular_form.slash_action_eq'_iff", "description": "A function `f : \u210d \u2192 \u2102` is `slash_invariant`, of weight `k \u2208 \u2124` and level `\u0393`,\n if for every matrix `\u03b3 \u2208 \u0393` we have `f(\u03b3 \u2022 z)= (c*z+d)^k f(z)` where `\u03b3= ![![a, b], ![c, d]]`,\n and it acts on `\u210d` via M\u00f6bius transformations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slash_action", "description": "A general version of the slash action of the space of modular forms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slash_action.map", "description": "A general version of the slash action of the space of modular forms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slash_action.zero_slash", "description": "A general version of the slash action of the space of modular forms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slash_action.slash_one", "description": "A general version of the slash action of the space of modular forms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slash_action.slash_mul", "description": "A general version of the slash action of the space of modular forms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slash_action.smul_slash", "description": "A general version of the slash action of the space of modular forms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "slash_action.add_slash", "description": "A general version of the slash action of the space of modular forms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "modular_form.slash_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monoid_hom_slash_action", "description": "Slash_action induced by a monoid homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "modular_form.subgroup_slash", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.existsi", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.get_constructors_for", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.left", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.econstructor", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.constructor_idx", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.right", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.constructor", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.fconstructor", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.split", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "linear_map.polar_gc", "description": " The map `B.polar : set E \u2192 set F` forms an order-reversing Galois connection with\n`B.flip.polar : set F \u2192 set E`. We use `order_dual.to_dual` and `order_dual.of_dual` to express\nthat `polar` is order-reversing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.polar_antitone", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.zero_mem_polar", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.polar_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.polar_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.polar_weak_closed", "description": "The polar set is closed in the weak topology induced by `B.flip`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.polar_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.polar_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.polar_mem_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.tripolar_eq_polar", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.polar_eq_Inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.polar", "description": " The (absolute) polar of `s : set E` is given by the set of all `y : F` such that `\u2016B x y\u2016 \u2264 1`\nfor all `x \u2208 s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.subset_bipolar", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.polar_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.polar_Union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.idempotents.karoubi.complement_p", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.idempotents.karoubi.complement_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.idempotents.karoubi.complement", "description": " `P.complement` is the formal direct factor of `P.X` given by the idempotent\nendomorphism `\ud835\udfd9 P.X - P.p`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.idempotents.karoubi.decomposition", "description": " A formal direct factor `P : karoubi C` of an object `P.X : C` in a\npreadditive category is actually a direct factor of the image `(to_karoubi C).obj P.X`\nof `P.X` in the category `karoubi C`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.idempotents.karoubi.karoubi_has_finite_biproducts", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.idempotents.karoubi.biproducts.bicone_\u03c0_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.idempotents.karoubi.biproducts.bicone_X_p", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.idempotents.karoubi.complement.category_theory.limits.has_binary_biproduct", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.idempotents.karoubi.biproducts.bicone_X_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.idempotents.karoubi.biproducts.bicone_\u03b9_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.idempotents.karoubi.biproducts.bicone", "description": " The `bicone` used in order to obtain the existence of\nthe biproduct of a functor `J \u2964 karoubi C` when the category `C` is additive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.to_hom", "description": "The type of arrows from `star \u03b1` to itself is equivalent to the original type `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.path_to_list_to_path", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.path_equiv_list_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.to_prefunctor_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.quiver", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.path_to_list", "description": "Auxiliary definition for `quiver.single_obj.path_equiv_list`.\nConverts a path in the quiver `single_obj \u03b1` into a list of elements of type `a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.to_hom_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.star", "description": "The single object in `single_obj \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.to_prefunctor_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.path_equiv_list_symm_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.list_to_path_to_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.path_equiv_list_symm_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj", "description": "Type tag on `unit` used to define single-object quivers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.to_prefunctor_apply_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.path_equiv_list_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.to_prefunctor", "description": "Prefunctors between two `single_obj` quivers correspond to functions between the corresponding\narrows types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.has_involutive_reverse", "description": "Equip `single_obj \u03b1` with an involutive reverse operation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.to_prefunctor_symm_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.has_reverse", "description": "Equip `single_obj \u03b1` with a reverse operation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.to_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.to_prefunctor_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.to_prefunctor_symm_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.path_equiv_list", "description": "Paths in `single_obj \u03b1` quiver correspond to lists of elements of type `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.to_prefunctor_apply_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.single_obj.list_to_path", "description": "Auxiliary definition for `quiver.single_obj.path_equiv_list`.\nConverts a list of elements of type `\u03b1` into a path in the quiver `single_obj \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone.tendsto_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_indicator_bUnion_finset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "indicator_union_eventually_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "indicator_eventually_le_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_eq.indicator_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "indicator_eventually_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_eq.support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_eq.indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone.tendsto_indicator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.punit_cone_is_limit", "description": "Any cone over a functor into `punit` is a limit cone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.punit_cone", "description": "A trivial cone for a functor into `punit`. `punit_cone_is_limit` shows it is a limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.category_theory.discrete.has_colimits_of_size", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.punit_cocone_is_colimit", "description": "Any cocone over a functor into `punit` is a colimit cocone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.category_theory.discrete.has_limits_of_size", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.punit_cocone", "description": "A trivial cocone for a functor into `punit`. `punit_cocone_is_limit` shows it is a colimit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.smul_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut.inv_apply_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.to_perm", "description": "Monoid hom from the group of multiplicative automorphisms to the group of permutations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.conj_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut.apply_inv_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.one_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut.one_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.coe_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut.conj_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut.apply_mul_distrib_mul_action", "description": " The tautological action by `mul_aut M` on `M`.\n\nThis generalizes `function.End.apply_mul_action`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut.inv_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.conj_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut.mul_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.apply_distrib_mul_action", "description": " The tautological action by `add_aut A` on `A`.\n\nThis generalizes `function.End.apply_mul_action`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut.conj_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.conj_inv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut.coe_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.inv_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.group", "description": "The group operation on additive automorphisms is defined by\n`\u03bb g h, add_equiv.trans h g`.\nThis means that multiplication agrees with composition, `(g*h)(x) = g (h x)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.one_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut.conj", "description": " Group conjugation, `mul_aut.conj g h = g * h * g\u207b\u00b9`, as a monoid homomorphism\nmapping multiplication in `G` into multiplication in the automorphism group `mul_aut G`.\nSee also the type `conj_act G` for any group `G`, which has a `mul_action (conj_act G) G` instance\nwhere `conj G` acts on `G` by conjugation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut.coe_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut.one_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut.to_perm", "description": "Monoid hom from the group of multiplicative automorphisms to the group of permutations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut.conj_inv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.apply_has_faithful_smul", "description": "`add_aut.apply_distrib_mul_action` is faithful.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut.smul_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut.mul_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut.group", "description": "The group operation on multiplicative automorphisms is defined by\n`\u03bb g h, mul_equiv.trans h g`.\nThis means that multiplication agrees with composition, `(g*h)(x) = g (h x)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.inv_apply_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.mul_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.apply_inv_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.coe_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.mul_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut", "description": "The group of multiplicative automorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_aut.apply_has_faithful_smul", "description": "`mul_aut.apply_mul_action` is faithful.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut", "description": "The group of additive automorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.conj", "description": " Additive group conjugation, `add_aut.conj g h = g + h - g`, as an additive monoid\nhomomorphism mapping addition in `G` into multiplication in the automorphism group `add_aut G`\n(written additively in order to define the map).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.card_filter_equitabilise_small", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.equitabilise_is_equipartition", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.card_parts_equitabilise_subset_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.card_parts_equitabilise", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.card_eq_of_mem_parts_equitabilise", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.card_filter_equitabilise_big", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.equitabilise_aux", "description": " Given a partition `P` of `s`, as well as a proof that `a * m + b * (m + 1) = s.card`, we can\nfind a new partition `Q` of `s` where each part has size `m` or `m + 1`, every part of `P` is the\nunion of parts of `Q` plus at most `m` extra elements, there are `b` parts of size `m + 1` and\n(provided `m > 0`, because a partition does not have parts of size `0`) there are `a` parts of size\n`m` and hence `a + b` parts in total.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.exists_equipartition_card_eq", "description": "We can find equipartitions of arbitrary size.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finpartition.equitabilise", "description": " Given a partition `P` of `s`, as well as a proof that `a * m + b * (m + 1) = s.card`, build a\nnew partition `Q` of `s` where each part has size `m` or `m + 1`, every part of `P` is the union of\nparts of `Q` plus at most `m` extra elements, there are `b` parts of size `m + 1` and (provided\n`m > 0`, because a partition does not have parts of size `0`) there are `a` parts of size `m` and\nhence `a + b` parts in total.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rational_cau_seq_pkg", "description": "Cauchy reals packaged as a completion of \u211a using the absolute value route.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.uniform_space_eq", "description": " The metric space uniform structure on \u211a (which presupposes the existence\nof real numbers) agrees with the one coming directly from (abs : \u211a \u2192 \u211a).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compare_reals.compare_uc_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compare_reals.compare_uc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compare_reals.Q.comm_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compare_reals.Q.uniform_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compare_reals.Q", "description": " Type wrapper around \u211a to make sure the absolute value uniform space instance is picked up\ninstead of the metric space one. We proved in rat.uniform_space_eq that they are equal,\nbut they are not definitionaly equal, so it would confuse the type class system (and probably\nalso human readers).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compare_reals.Bourbaki\u211d", "description": "Real numbers constructed as in Bourbaki.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compare_reals.Bourbaki\u211d.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compare_reals.compare_equiv", "description": "The uniform bijection between Bourbaki and Cauchy reals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compare_reals.bourbaki.uniform_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compare_reals.Bourbaki_pkg", "description": "Bourbaki reals packaged as a completion of Q using the general theory.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compare_reals.Q.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv.lhopital_zero_at_top", "description": "**L'H\u00f4pital's rule** for approaching +\u221e, `deriv` version", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv.lhopital_zero_nhds'", "description": "**L'H\u00f4pital's rule** for approaching a real, `deriv` version. This\n does not require anything about the situation at `a`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv.lhopital_zero_at_bot_on_Iio", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.lhopital_zero_at_top_on_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv.lhopital_zero_at_bot", "description": "**L'H\u00f4pital's rule** for approaching -\u221e, `deriv` version", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.lhopital_zero_nhds'", "description": "L'H\u00f4pital's rule for approaching a real, `has_deriv_at` version. This\n does not require anything about the situation at `a`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.lhopital_zero_at_top", "description": "L'H\u00f4pital's rule for approaching +\u221e, `has_deriv_at` version", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv.lhopital_zero_nhds_left", "description": "**L'H\u00f4pital's rule** for approaching a real from the left, `deriv` version", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv.lhopital_zero_right_on_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.lhopital_zero_left_on_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.lhopital_zero_nhds", "description": "**L'H\u00f4pital's rule** for approaching a real, `has_deriv_at` version", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.lhopital_zero_nhds_left", "description": "L'H\u00f4pital's rule for approaching a real from the left, `has_deriv_at` version", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.lhopital_zero_right_on_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv.lhopital_zero_right_on_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.lhopital_zero_left_on_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.lhopital_zero_at_bot_on_Iio", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv.lhopital_zero_nhds", "description": "**L'H\u00f4pital's rule** for approaching a real, `deriv` version", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv.lhopital_zero_at_top_on_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.lhopital_zero_nhds_right", "description": "L'H\u00f4pital's rule for approaching a real from the right, `has_deriv_at` version", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv.lhopital_zero_left_on_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv.lhopital_zero_nhds_right", "description": "**L'H\u00f4pital's rule** for approaching a real from the right, `deriv` version", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.lhopital_zero_at_bot", "description": "L'H\u00f4pital's rule for approaching -\u221e, `has_deriv_at` version", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.lhopital_zero_right_on_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.mk_factors_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factors_right_of_factors_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factor_thru_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factors_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.mk_factors_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factor_thru_arrow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factors_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factors_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factor_thru_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factors_left_of_factors_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factors_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factor_thru", "description": " `P.factor_thru f h` provides a factorisation of `f : X \u27f6 Y` through some `P : subobject Y`,\ngiven the evidence `h : P.factors f` that such a factorisation exists.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factor_thru_comp_arrow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factor_thru_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.mono_over.factors_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factors_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factor_thru_mk_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factors_of_factors_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factor_thru_add_sub_factor_thru_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factor_thru_add_sub_factor_thru_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.mono_over.factors", "description": " When `f : X \u27f6 Y` and `P : mono_over Y`,\n`P.factors f` expresses that there exists a factorisation of `f` through `P`.\nGiven `h : P.factors f`, you can recover the morphism as `P.factor_thru f h`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factors_comp_arrow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factors", "description": " When `f : X \u27f6 Y` and `P : subobject Y`,\n`P.factors f` expresses that there exists a factorisation of `f` through `P`.\nGiven `h : P.factors f`, you can recover the morphism as `P.factor_thru f h`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factor_thru_arrow_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.mono_over.factor_thru", "description": " `P.factor_thru f h` provides a factorisation of `f : X \u27f6 Y` through some `P : mono_over Y`,\ngiven the evidence `h : P.factors f` that such a factorisation exists.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factor_thru_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factor_thru_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subobject.factor_thru_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_cfg", "description": "Configuration options for the `@[simps]` attribute.\n* `attrs` specifies the list of attributes given to the generated lemmas. Default: ``[`simp]``.\n  The attributes can be either basic attributes, or user attributes without parameters.\n  There are two attributes which `simps` might add itself:\n  * If ``[`simp]`` is in the list, then ``[`_refl_lemma]`` is added automatically if appropriate.\n  * If the definition is marked with `@[to_additive ...]` then all generated lemmas are marked\n    with `@[to_additive]`. This is governed by the `add_additive` configuration option.\n* if `simp_rhs` is `tt` then the right-hand-side of the generated lemmas will be put in\n  simp-normal form. More precisely: `dsimp, simp` will be called on all these expressions.\n  See note [dsimp, simp].\n* `type_md` specifies how aggressively definitions are unfolded in the type of expressions\n  for the purposes of finding out whether the type is a function type.\n  Default: `instances`. This will unfold coercion instances (so that a coercion to a function type\n  is recognized as a function type), but not declarations like `set`.\n* `rhs_md` specifies how aggressively definition in the declaration are unfolded for the purposes\n  of finding out whether it is a constructor.\n  Default: `none`\n  Exception: `@[simps]` will automatically add the options\n  `{rhs_md := semireducible, simp_rhs := tt}` if the given definition is not a constructor with\n  the given reducibility setting for `rhs_md`.\n* If `fully_applied` is `ff` then the generated `simp` lemmas will be between non-fully applied\n  terms, i.e. equalities between functions. This does not restrict the recursive behavior of\n  `@[simps]`, so only the \"final\" projection will be non-fully applied.\n  However, it can be used in combination with explicit field names, to get a partially applied\n  intermediate projection.\n* The option `not_recursive` contains the list of names of types for which `@[simps]` doesn't\n  recursively apply projections. For example, given an equivalence `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2 \u2243 \u03b2 \u00d7 \u03b1` one usually\n  wants to only apply the projections for `equiv`, and not also those for `\u00d7`. This option is\n  only relevant if no explicit projection names are given as argument to `@[simps]`.\n* The option `trace` is set to `tt` when you write `@[simps?]`. In this case, the attribute will\n  print all generated lemmas. It is almost the same as setting the option `trace.simps.verbose`,\n  except that it doesn't print information about the found projections.\n* if `add_additive` is `some nm` then `@[to_additive]` is added to the generated lemma. This\n  option is automatically set to `tt` when the original declaration was tagged with\n  `@[to_additive, simps]` (in that order), where `nm` is the additive name of the original\n  declaration.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_cfg.attrs", "description": "Configuration options for the `@[simps]` attribute.\n* `attrs` specifies the list of attributes given to the generated lemmas. Default: ``[`simp]``.\n  The attributes can be either basic attributes, or user attributes without parameters.\n  There are two attributes which `simps` might add itself:\n  * If ``[`simp]`` is in the list, then ``[`_refl_lemma]`` is added automatically if appropriate.\n  * If the definition is marked with `@[to_additive ...]` then all generated lemmas are marked\n    with `@[to_additive]`. This is governed by the `add_additive` configuration option.\n* if `simp_rhs` is `tt` then the right-hand-side of the generated lemmas will be put in\n  simp-normal form. More precisely: `dsimp, simp` will be called on all these expressions.\n  See note [dsimp, simp].\n* `type_md` specifies how aggressively definitions are unfolded in the type of expressions\n  for the purposes of finding out whether the type is a function type.\n  Default: `instances`. This will unfold coercion instances (so that a coercion to a function type\n  is recognized as a function type), but not declarations like `set`.\n* `rhs_md` specifies how aggressively definition in the declaration are unfolded for the purposes\n  of finding out whether it is a constructor.\n  Default: `none`\n  Exception: `@[simps]` will automatically add the options\n  `{rhs_md := semireducible, simp_rhs := tt}` if the given definition is not a constructor with\n  the given reducibility setting for `rhs_md`.\n* If `fully_applied` is `ff` then the generated `simp` lemmas will be between non-fully applied\n  terms, i.e. equalities between functions. This does not restrict the recursive behavior of\n  `@[simps]`, so only the \"final\" projection will be non-fully applied.\n  However, it can be used in combination with explicit field names, to get a partially applied\n  intermediate projection.\n* The option `not_recursive` contains the list of names of types for which `@[simps]` doesn't\n  recursively apply projections. For example, given an equivalence `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2 \u2243 \u03b2 \u00d7 \u03b1` one usually\n  wants to only apply the projections for `equiv`, and not also those for `\u00d7`. This option is\n  only relevant if no explicit projection names are given as argument to `@[simps]`.\n* The option `trace` is set to `tt` when you write `@[simps?]`. In this case, the attribute will\n  print all generated lemmas. It is almost the same as setting the option `trace.simps.verbose`,\n  except that it doesn't print information about the found projections.\n* if `add_additive` is `some nm` then `@[to_additive]` is added to the generated lemma. This\n  option is automatically set to `tt` when the original declaration was tagged with\n  `@[to_additive, simps]` (in that order), where `nm` is the additive name of the original\n  declaration.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_cfg.simp_rhs", "description": "Configuration options for the `@[simps]` attribute.\n* `attrs` specifies the list of attributes given to the generated lemmas. Default: ``[`simp]``.\n  The attributes can be either basic attributes, or user attributes without parameters.\n  There are two attributes which `simps` might add itself:\n  * If ``[`simp]`` is in the list, then ``[`_refl_lemma]`` is added automatically if appropriate.\n  * If the definition is marked with `@[to_additive ...]` then all generated lemmas are marked\n    with `@[to_additive]`. This is governed by the `add_additive` configuration option.\n* if `simp_rhs` is `tt` then the right-hand-side of the generated lemmas will be put in\n  simp-normal form. More precisely: `dsimp, simp` will be called on all these expressions.\n  See note [dsimp, simp].\n* `type_md` specifies how aggressively definitions are unfolded in the type of expressions\n  for the purposes of finding out whether the type is a function type.\n  Default: `instances`. This will unfold coercion instances (so that a coercion to a function type\n  is recognized as a function type), but not declarations like `set`.\n* `rhs_md` specifies how aggressively definition in the declaration are unfolded for the purposes\n  of finding out whether it is a constructor.\n  Default: `none`\n  Exception: `@[simps]` will automatically add the options\n  `{rhs_md := semireducible, simp_rhs := tt}` if the given definition is not a constructor with\n  the given reducibility setting for `rhs_md`.\n* If `fully_applied` is `ff` then the generated `simp` lemmas will be between non-fully applied\n  terms, i.e. equalities between functions. This does not restrict the recursive behavior of\n  `@[simps]`, so only the \"final\" projection will be non-fully applied.\n  However, it can be used in combination with explicit field names, to get a partially applied\n  intermediate projection.\n* The option `not_recursive` contains the list of names of types for which `@[simps]` doesn't\n  recursively apply projections. For example, given an equivalence `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2 \u2243 \u03b2 \u00d7 \u03b1` one usually\n  wants to only apply the projections for `equiv`, and not also those for `\u00d7`. This option is\n  only relevant if no explicit projection names are given as argument to `@[simps]`.\n* The option `trace` is set to `tt` when you write `@[simps?]`. In this case, the attribute will\n  print all generated lemmas. It is almost the same as setting the option `trace.simps.verbose`,\n  except that it doesn't print information about the found projections.\n* if `add_additive` is `some nm` then `@[to_additive]` is added to the generated lemma. This\n  option is automatically set to `tt` when the original declaration was tagged with\n  `@[to_additive, simps]` (in that order), where `nm` is the additive name of the original\n  declaration.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_cfg.type_md", "description": "Configuration options for the `@[simps]` attribute.\n* `attrs` specifies the list of attributes given to the generated lemmas. Default: ``[`simp]``.\n  The attributes can be either basic attributes, or user attributes without parameters.\n  There are two attributes which `simps` might add itself:\n  * If ``[`simp]`` is in the list, then ``[`_refl_lemma]`` is added automatically if appropriate.\n  * If the definition is marked with `@[to_additive ...]` then all generated lemmas are marked\n    with `@[to_additive]`. This is governed by the `add_additive` configuration option.\n* if `simp_rhs` is `tt` then the right-hand-side of the generated lemmas will be put in\n  simp-normal form. More precisely: `dsimp, simp` will be called on all these expressions.\n  See note [dsimp, simp].\n* `type_md` specifies how aggressively definitions are unfolded in the type of expressions\n  for the purposes of finding out whether the type is a function type.\n  Default: `instances`. This will unfold coercion instances (so that a coercion to a function type\n  is recognized as a function type), but not declarations like `set`.\n* `rhs_md` specifies how aggressively definition in the declaration are unfolded for the purposes\n  of finding out whether it is a constructor.\n  Default: `none`\n  Exception: `@[simps]` will automatically add the options\n  `{rhs_md := semireducible, simp_rhs := tt}` if the given definition is not a constructor with\n  the given reducibility setting for `rhs_md`.\n* If `fully_applied` is `ff` then the generated `simp` lemmas will be between non-fully applied\n  terms, i.e. equalities between functions. This does not restrict the recursive behavior of\n  `@[simps]`, so only the \"final\" projection will be non-fully applied.\n  However, it can be used in combination with explicit field names, to get a partially applied\n  intermediate projection.\n* The option `not_recursive` contains the list of names of types for which `@[simps]` doesn't\n  recursively apply projections. For example, given an equivalence `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2 \u2243 \u03b2 \u00d7 \u03b1` one usually\n  wants to only apply the projections for `equiv`, and not also those for `\u00d7`. This option is\n  only relevant if no explicit projection names are given as argument to `@[simps]`.\n* The option `trace` is set to `tt` when you write `@[simps?]`. In this case, the attribute will\n  print all generated lemmas. It is almost the same as setting the option `trace.simps.verbose`,\n  except that it doesn't print information about the found projections.\n* if `add_additive` is `some nm` then `@[to_additive]` is added to the generated lemma. This\n  option is automatically set to `tt` when the original declaration was tagged with\n  `@[to_additive, simps]` (in that order), where `nm` is the additive name of the original\n  declaration.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_cfg.rhs_md", "description": "Configuration options for the `@[simps]` attribute.\n* `attrs` specifies the list of attributes given to the generated lemmas. Default: ``[`simp]``.\n  The attributes can be either basic attributes, or user attributes without parameters.\n  There are two attributes which `simps` might add itself:\n  * If ``[`simp]`` is in the list, then ``[`_refl_lemma]`` is added automatically if appropriate.\n  * If the definition is marked with `@[to_additive ...]` then all generated lemmas are marked\n    with `@[to_additive]`. This is governed by the `add_additive` configuration option.\n* if `simp_rhs` is `tt` then the right-hand-side of the generated lemmas will be put in\n  simp-normal form. More precisely: `dsimp, simp` will be called on all these expressions.\n  See note [dsimp, simp].\n* `type_md` specifies how aggressively definitions are unfolded in the type of expressions\n  for the purposes of finding out whether the type is a function type.\n  Default: `instances`. This will unfold coercion instances (so that a coercion to a function type\n  is recognized as a function type), but not declarations like `set`.\n* `rhs_md` specifies how aggressively definition in the declaration are unfolded for the purposes\n  of finding out whether it is a constructor.\n  Default: `none`\n  Exception: `@[simps]` will automatically add the options\n  `{rhs_md := semireducible, simp_rhs := tt}` if the given definition is not a constructor with\n  the given reducibility setting for `rhs_md`.\n* If `fully_applied` is `ff` then the generated `simp` lemmas will be between non-fully applied\n  terms, i.e. equalities between functions. This does not restrict the recursive behavior of\n  `@[simps]`, so only the \"final\" projection will be non-fully applied.\n  However, it can be used in combination with explicit field names, to get a partially applied\n  intermediate projection.\n* The option `not_recursive` contains the list of names of types for which `@[simps]` doesn't\n  recursively apply projections. For example, given an equivalence `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2 \u2243 \u03b2 \u00d7 \u03b1` one usually\n  wants to only apply the projections for `equiv`, and not also those for `\u00d7`. This option is\n  only relevant if no explicit projection names are given as argument to `@[simps]`.\n* The option `trace` is set to `tt` when you write `@[simps?]`. In this case, the attribute will\n  print all generated lemmas. It is almost the same as setting the option `trace.simps.verbose`,\n  except that it doesn't print information about the found projections.\n* if `add_additive` is `some nm` then `@[to_additive]` is added to the generated lemma. This\n  option is automatically set to `tt` when the original declaration was tagged with\n  `@[to_additive, simps]` (in that order), where `nm` is the additive name of the original\n  declaration.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_cfg.fully_applied", "description": "Configuration options for the `@[simps]` attribute.\n* `attrs` specifies the list of attributes given to the generated lemmas. Default: ``[`simp]``.\n  The attributes can be either basic attributes, or user attributes without parameters.\n  There are two attributes which `simps` might add itself:\n  * If ``[`simp]`` is in the list, then ``[`_refl_lemma]`` is added automatically if appropriate.\n  * If the definition is marked with `@[to_additive ...]` then all generated lemmas are marked\n    with `@[to_additive]`. This is governed by the `add_additive` configuration option.\n* if `simp_rhs` is `tt` then the right-hand-side of the generated lemmas will be put in\n  simp-normal form. More precisely: `dsimp, simp` will be called on all these expressions.\n  See note [dsimp, simp].\n* `type_md` specifies how aggressively definitions are unfolded in the type of expressions\n  for the purposes of finding out whether the type is a function type.\n  Default: `instances`. This will unfold coercion instances (so that a coercion to a function type\n  is recognized as a function type), but not declarations like `set`.\n* `rhs_md` specifies how aggressively definition in the declaration are unfolded for the purposes\n  of finding out whether it is a constructor.\n  Default: `none`\n  Exception: `@[simps]` will automatically add the options\n  `{rhs_md := semireducible, simp_rhs := tt}` if the given definition is not a constructor with\n  the given reducibility setting for `rhs_md`.\n* If `fully_applied` is `ff` then the generated `simp` lemmas will be between non-fully applied\n  terms, i.e. equalities between functions. This does not restrict the recursive behavior of\n  `@[simps]`, so only the \"final\" projection will be non-fully applied.\n  However, it can be used in combination with explicit field names, to get a partially applied\n  intermediate projection.\n* The option `not_recursive` contains the list of names of types for which `@[simps]` doesn't\n  recursively apply projections. For example, given an equivalence `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2 \u2243 \u03b2 \u00d7 \u03b1` one usually\n  wants to only apply the projections for `equiv`, and not also those for `\u00d7`. This option is\n  only relevant if no explicit projection names are given as argument to `@[simps]`.\n* The option `trace` is set to `tt` when you write `@[simps?]`. In this case, the attribute will\n  print all generated lemmas. It is almost the same as setting the option `trace.simps.verbose`,\n  except that it doesn't print information about the found projections.\n* if `add_additive` is `some nm` then `@[to_additive]` is added to the generated lemma. This\n  option is automatically set to `tt` when the original declaration was tagged with\n  `@[to_additive, simps]` (in that order), where `nm` is the additive name of the original\n  declaration.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_cfg.not_recursive", "description": "Configuration options for the `@[simps]` attribute.\n* `attrs` specifies the list of attributes given to the generated lemmas. Default: ``[`simp]``.\n  The attributes can be either basic attributes, or user attributes without parameters.\n  There are two attributes which `simps` might add itself:\n  * If ``[`simp]`` is in the list, then ``[`_refl_lemma]`` is added automatically if appropriate.\n  * If the definition is marked with `@[to_additive ...]` then all generated lemmas are marked\n    with `@[to_additive]`. This is governed by the `add_additive` configuration option.\n* if `simp_rhs` is `tt` then the right-hand-side of the generated lemmas will be put in\n  simp-normal form. More precisely: `dsimp, simp` will be called on all these expressions.\n  See note [dsimp, simp].\n* `type_md` specifies how aggressively definitions are unfolded in the type of expressions\n  for the purposes of finding out whether the type is a function type.\n  Default: `instances`. This will unfold coercion instances (so that a coercion to a function type\n  is recognized as a function type), but not declarations like `set`.\n* `rhs_md` specifies how aggressively definition in the declaration are unfolded for the purposes\n  of finding out whether it is a constructor.\n  Default: `none`\n  Exception: `@[simps]` will automatically add the options\n  `{rhs_md := semireducible, simp_rhs := tt}` if the given definition is not a constructor with\n  the given reducibility setting for `rhs_md`.\n* If `fully_applied` is `ff` then the generated `simp` lemmas will be between non-fully applied\n  terms, i.e. equalities between functions. This does not restrict the recursive behavior of\n  `@[simps]`, so only the \"final\" projection will be non-fully applied.\n  However, it can be used in combination with explicit field names, to get a partially applied\n  intermediate projection.\n* The option `not_recursive` contains the list of names of types for which `@[simps]` doesn't\n  recursively apply projections. For example, given an equivalence `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2 \u2243 \u03b2 \u00d7 \u03b1` one usually\n  wants to only apply the projections for `equiv`, and not also those for `\u00d7`. This option is\n  only relevant if no explicit projection names are given as argument to `@[simps]`.\n* The option `trace` is set to `tt` when you write `@[simps?]`. In this case, the attribute will\n  print all generated lemmas. It is almost the same as setting the option `trace.simps.verbose`,\n  except that it doesn't print information about the found projections.\n* if `add_additive` is `some nm` then `@[to_additive]` is added to the generated lemma. This\n  option is automatically set to `tt` when the original declaration was tagged with\n  `@[to_additive, simps]` (in that order), where `nm` is the additive name of the original\n  declaration.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_cfg.trace", "description": "Configuration options for the `@[simps]` attribute.\n* `attrs` specifies the list of attributes given to the generated lemmas. Default: ``[`simp]``.\n  The attributes can be either basic attributes, or user attributes without parameters.\n  There are two attributes which `simps` might add itself:\n  * If ``[`simp]`` is in the list, then ``[`_refl_lemma]`` is added automatically if appropriate.\n  * If the definition is marked with `@[to_additive ...]` then all generated lemmas are marked\n    with `@[to_additive]`. This is governed by the `add_additive` configuration option.\n* if `simp_rhs` is `tt` then the right-hand-side of the generated lemmas will be put in\n  simp-normal form. More precisely: `dsimp, simp` will be called on all these expressions.\n  See note [dsimp, simp].\n* `type_md` specifies how aggressively definitions are unfolded in the type of expressions\n  for the purposes of finding out whether the type is a function type.\n  Default: `instances`. This will unfold coercion instances (so that a coercion to a function type\n  is recognized as a function type), but not declarations like `set`.\n* `rhs_md` specifies how aggressively definition in the declaration are unfolded for the purposes\n  of finding out whether it is a constructor.\n  Default: `none`\n  Exception: `@[simps]` will automatically add the options\n  `{rhs_md := semireducible, simp_rhs := tt}` if the given definition is not a constructor with\n  the given reducibility setting for `rhs_md`.\n* If `fully_applied` is `ff` then the generated `simp` lemmas will be between non-fully applied\n  terms, i.e. equalities between functions. This does not restrict the recursive behavior of\n  `@[simps]`, so only the \"final\" projection will be non-fully applied.\n  However, it can be used in combination with explicit field names, to get a partially applied\n  intermediate projection.\n* The option `not_recursive` contains the list of names of types for which `@[simps]` doesn't\n  recursively apply projections. For example, given an equivalence `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2 \u2243 \u03b2 \u00d7 \u03b1` one usually\n  wants to only apply the projections for `equiv`, and not also those for `\u00d7`. This option is\n  only relevant if no explicit projection names are given as argument to `@[simps]`.\n* The option `trace` is set to `tt` when you write `@[simps?]`. In this case, the attribute will\n  print all generated lemmas. It is almost the same as setting the option `trace.simps.verbose`,\n  except that it doesn't print information about the found projections.\n* if `add_additive` is `some nm` then `@[to_additive]` is added to the generated lemma. This\n  option is automatically set to `tt` when the original declaration was tagged with\n  `@[to_additive, simps]` (in that order), where `nm` is the additive name of the original\n  declaration.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_cfg.add_additive", "description": "Configuration options for the `@[simps]` attribute.\n* `attrs` specifies the list of attributes given to the generated lemmas. Default: ``[`simp]``.\n  The attributes can be either basic attributes, or user attributes without parameters.\n  There are two attributes which `simps` might add itself:\n  * If ``[`simp]`` is in the list, then ``[`_refl_lemma]`` is added automatically if appropriate.\n  * If the definition is marked with `@[to_additive ...]` then all generated lemmas are marked\n    with `@[to_additive]`. This is governed by the `add_additive` configuration option.\n* if `simp_rhs` is `tt` then the right-hand-side of the generated lemmas will be put in\n  simp-normal form. More precisely: `dsimp, simp` will be called on all these expressions.\n  See note [dsimp, simp].\n* `type_md` specifies how aggressively definitions are unfolded in the type of expressions\n  for the purposes of finding out whether the type is a function type.\n  Default: `instances`. This will unfold coercion instances (so that a coercion to a function type\n  is recognized as a function type), but not declarations like `set`.\n* `rhs_md` specifies how aggressively definition in the declaration are unfolded for the purposes\n  of finding out whether it is a constructor.\n  Default: `none`\n  Exception: `@[simps]` will automatically add the options\n  `{rhs_md := semireducible, simp_rhs := tt}` if the given definition is not a constructor with\n  the given reducibility setting for `rhs_md`.\n* If `fully_applied` is `ff` then the generated `simp` lemmas will be between non-fully applied\n  terms, i.e. equalities between functions. This does not restrict the recursive behavior of\n  `@[simps]`, so only the \"final\" projection will be non-fully applied.\n  However, it can be used in combination with explicit field names, to get a partially applied\n  intermediate projection.\n* The option `not_recursive` contains the list of names of types for which `@[simps]` doesn't\n  recursively apply projections. For example, given an equivalence `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2 \u2243 \u03b2 \u00d7 \u03b1` one usually\n  wants to only apply the projections for `equiv`, and not also those for `\u00d7`. This option is\n  only relevant if no explicit projection names are given as argument to `@[simps]`.\n* The option `trace` is set to `tt` when you write `@[simps?]`. In this case, the attribute will\n  print all generated lemmas. It is almost the same as setting the option `trace.simps.verbose`,\n  except that it doesn't print information about the found projections.\n* if `add_additive` is `some nm` then `@[to_additive]` is added to the generated lemma. This\n  option is automatically set to `tt` when the original declaration was tagged with\n  `@[to_additive, simps]` (in that order), where `nm` is the additive name of the original\n  declaration.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_get_projection_exprs", "description": "Get the projections of a structure used by `@[simps]` applied to the appropriate arguments.\nReturns a list of tuples\n```lean\n(corresponding right-hand-side, given projection name, projection expression, projection numbers,\n  used by default, is prefix)\n```\n(where all fields except the first are packed in a `projection_data` structure)\none for each projection. The given projection name is the name for the projection used by the user\nused to generate (and parse) projection names. For example, in the structure\n\nExample 1: ``simps_get_projection_exprs env `(\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2) `(\u27e8x, y\u27e9)`` will give the output\n```lean\n  [(`(x), `fst, `(@prod.fst.{u v} \u03b1 \u03b2), [0], tt, ff),\n   (`(y), `snd, `(@prod.snd.{u v} \u03b1 \u03b2), [1], tt, ff)]\n```\n\nExample 2: ``simps_get_projection_exprs env `(\u03b1 \u2243 \u03b1) `(\u27e8id, id, \u03bb _, rfl, \u03bb _, rfl\u27e9)``\nwill give the output\n```lean\n  [(`(id), `apply, `(coe), [0], tt, ff),\n   (`(id), `symm_apply, `(\u03bb f, \u21d1f.symm), [1], tt, ff),\n   ...,\n   ...]\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_aux", "description": "The `@[_simps_aux]` attribute specifies which lemmas are added by `simps`.\nThis should not be used manually and it only exists for mathport", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "projection_data.has_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "initialize_simps_projections_cmd", "description": "This command specifies custom names and custom projections for the simp attribute `simps_attr`.\n* You can specify custom names by writing e.g.\n  `initialize_simps_projections equiv (to_fun \u2192 apply, inv_fun \u2192 symm_apply)`.\n* See Note [custom simps projection] and the examples below for information how to declare custom\n  projections.\n* If no custom projection is specified, the projection will be `coe_fn`/`\u21d1` if a `has_coe_to_fun`\n  instance has been declared, or the notation of a notation class (like `has_mul`) if such an\n  instance is available. If none of these cases apply, the projection itself will be used.\n* You can disable a projection by default by running\n  `initialize_simps_projections equiv (-inv_fun)`\n  This will ensure that no simp lemmas are generated for this projection,\n  unless this projection is explicitly specified by the user.\n* If you want the projection name added as a prefix in the generated lemma name, you can add the\n  `as_prefix` modifier:\n  `initialize_simps_projections equiv (to_fun \u2192 coe as_prefix)`\n  Note that this does not influence the parsing of projection names: if you have a declaration\n  `foo` and you want to apply the projections `snd`, `coe` (which is a prefix) and `fst`, in that\n  order you can run `@[simps snd_coe_fst] def foo ...` and this will generate a lemma with the\n  name `coe_foo_snd_fst`.\n  * Run `initialize_simps_projections?` (or `set_option trace.simps.verbose true`)\n  to see the generated projections.\n* You can declare a new name for a projection that is the composite of multiple projections, e.g.\n  ```lean\n    structure A := (proj : \u2115)\n    structure B extends A\n    initialize_simps_projections? B (to_A_proj \u2192 proj, -to_A)\n  ```\n  You can also make your custom projection that is definitionally equal to a composite of\n  projections. In this case, coercions and notation classes are not automatically recognized, and\n  should be manually given by giving a custom projection.\n  This is especially useful when extending a structure (without `old_structure_cmd`).\n  In the above example, it is desirable to add `-to_A`, so that `@[simps]` doesn't automatically\n  apply the `B.to_A` projection and then recursively the `A.proj` projection in the lemmas it\n  generates. If you want to get both the `foo_proj` and `foo_to_A` simp lemmas, you can use\n  `@[simps, simps to_A]`.\n* Running `initialize_simps_projections my_struc` without arguments is not necessary, it has the\n  same effect if you just add `@[simps]` to a declaration.\n* If you do anything to change the default projections, make sure to call either `@[simps]` or\n  `initialize_simps_projections` in the same file as the structure declaration. Otherwise, you might\n  have a file that imports the structure, but not your custom projections.\n\nSome common uses:\n* If you define a new homomorphism-like structure (like `mul_hom`) you can just run\n  `initialize_simps_projections` after defining the `has_coe_to_fun` instance\n  ```lean\n    instance {mM : has_mul M} {mN : has_mul N} : has_coe_to_fun (M \u2192\u2099* N) := ...\n    initialize_simps_projections mul_hom (to_fun \u2192 apply)\n  ```\n  This will generate `foo_apply` lemmas for each declaration `foo`.\n* If you prefer `coe_foo` lemmas that state equalities between functions, use\n  `initialize_simps_projections mul_hom (to_fun \u2192 coe as_prefix)`\n  In this case you have to use `@[simps {fully_applied := ff}]` or equivalently `@[simps as_fn]`\n  whenever you call `@[simps]`.\n* You can also initialize to use both, in which case you have to choose which one to use by default,\n  by using either of the following\n  ```lean\n    initialize_simps_projections mul_hom (to_fun \u2192 apply, to_fun \u2192 coe, -coe as_prefix)\n    initialize_simps_projections mul_hom (to_fun \u2192 apply, to_fun \u2192 coe as_prefix, -apply)\n  ```\n  In the first case, you can get both lemmas using `@[simps, simps coe as_fn]` and in the second\n  case you can get both lemmas using `@[simps as_fn, simps apply]`.\n* If your new homomorphism-like structure extends another structure (without `old_structure_cmd`)\n  (like `rel_embedding`), then you have to specify explicitly that you want to use a coercion\n  as a custom projection. For example\n  ```lean\n    def rel_embedding.simps.apply (h : r \u21aar s) : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2 := h\n    initialize_simps_projections rel_embedding (to_embedding_to_fun \u2192 apply, -to_embedding)\n  ```\n* If you have an isomorphism-like structure (like `equiv`) you often want to define a custom\n  projection for the inverse:\n  ```lean\n    def equiv.simps.symm_apply (e : \u03b1 \u2243 \u03b2) : \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b1 := e.symm\n    initialize_simps_projections equiv (to_fun \u2192 apply, inv_fun \u2192 symm_apply)\n  ```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "parsed_projection_data", "description": "Temporary projection data parsed from `initialize_simps_projections` before the expression\n matching this projection has been found. Only used internally in `simps_get_raw_projections`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "parsed_projection_data.orig_name", "description": "Temporary projection data parsed from `initialize_simps_projections` before the expression\n matching this projection has been found. Only used internally in `simps_get_raw_projections`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "parsed_projection_data.new_name", "description": "Temporary projection data parsed from `initialize_simps_projections` before the expression\n matching this projection has been found. Only used internally in `simps_get_raw_projections`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "parsed_projection_data.is_default", "description": "Temporary projection data parsed from `initialize_simps_projections` before the expression\n matching this projection has been found. Only used internally in `simps_get_raw_projections`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "parsed_projection_data.is_prefix", "description": "Temporary projection data parsed from `initialize_simps_projections` before the expression\n matching this projection has been found. Only used internally in `simps_get_raw_projections`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_add_projection", "description": "Add a lemma with `nm` stating that `lhs = rhs`. `type` is the type of both `lhs` and `rhs`,\n `args` is the list of local constants occurring, and `univs` is the list of universe variables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_tac", "description": "`simps_tac` derives `simp` lemmas for all (nested) non-Prop projections of the declaration.\n If `todo` is non-empty, it will generate exactly the names in `todo`.\n If `short_nm` is true, the generated names will only use the last projection name.\n If `trc` is true, trace as if `trace.simps.verbose` is true.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_cfg.has_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.attribute.simps", "description": "The `@[simps]` attribute automatically derives lemmas specifying the projections of this\ndeclaration.\n\nExample:\n```lean\n@[simps] def foo : \u2115 \u00d7 \u2124 := (1, 2)\n```\nderives two `simp` lemmas:\n```lean\n@[simp] lemma foo_fst : foo.fst = 1\n@[simp] lemma foo_snd : foo.snd = 2\n```\n\n* It does not derive `simp` lemmas for the prop-valued projections.\n* It will automatically reduce newly created beta-redexes, but will not unfold any definitions.\n* If the structure has a coercion to either sorts or functions, and this is defined to be one\n  of the projections, then this coercion will be used instead of the projection.\n* If the structure is a class that has an instance to a notation class, like `has_mul`, then this\n  notation is used instead of the corresponding projection.\n* You can specify custom projections, by giving a declaration with name\n  `{structure_name}.simps.{projection_name}`. See Note [custom simps projection].\n\n  Example:\n  ```lean\n  def equiv.simps.inv_fun (e : \u03b1 \u2243 \u03b2) : \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b1 := e.symm\n  @[simps] def equiv.trans (e\u2081 : \u03b1 \u2243 \u03b2) (e\u2082 : \u03b2 \u2243 \u03b3) : \u03b1 \u2243 \u03b3 :=\n  \u27e8e\u2082 \u2218 e\u2081, e\u2081.symm \u2218 e\u2082.symm\u27e9\n  ```\n  generates\n  ```lean\n  @[simp] lemma equiv.trans_to_fun : \u2200 {\u03b1 \u03b2 \u03b3} (e\u2081 e\u2082) (a : \u03b1), \u21d1(e\u2081.trans e\u2082) a = (\u21d1e\u2082 \u2218 \u21d1e\u2081) a\n  @[simp] lemma equiv.trans_inv_fun : \u2200 {\u03b1 \u03b2 \u03b3} (e\u2081 e\u2082) (a : \u03b3),\n    \u21d1((e\u2081.trans e\u2082).symm) a = (\u21d1(e\u2081.symm) \u2218 \u21d1(e\u2082.symm)) a\n  ```\n\n* You can specify custom projection names, by specifying the new projection names using\n  `initialize_simps_projections`.\n  Example: `initialize_simps_projections equiv (to_fun \u2192 apply, inv_fun \u2192 symm_apply)`.\n  See `initialize_simps_projections_cmd` for more information.\n\n* If one of the fields itself is a structure, this command will recursively create\n  `simp` lemmas for all fields in that structure.\n  * Exception: by default it will not recursively create `simp` lemmas for fields in the structures\n    `prod` and `pprod`. You can give explicit projection names or change the value of\n    `simps_cfg.not_recursive` to override this behavior.\n\n  Example:\n  ```lean\n  structure my_prod (\u03b1 \u03b2 : Type*) := (fst : \u03b1) (snd : \u03b2)\n  @[simps] def foo : prod \u2115 \u2115 \u00d7 my_prod \u2115 \u2115 := \u27e8\u27e81, 2\u27e9, 3, 4\u27e9\n  ```\n  generates\n  ```lean\n  @[simp] lemma foo_fst : foo.fst = (1, 2)\n  @[simp] lemma foo_snd_fst : foo.snd.fst = 3\n  @[simp] lemma foo_snd_snd : foo.snd.snd = 4\n  ```\n\n* You can use `@[simps proj1 proj2 ...]` to only generate the projection lemmas for the specified\n  projections.\n* Recursive projection names can be specified using `proj1_proj2_proj3`.\n  This will create a lemma of the form `foo.proj1.proj2.proj3 = ...`.\n\n  Example:\n  ```lean\n  structure my_prod (\u03b1 \u03b2 : Type*) := (fst : \u03b1) (snd : \u03b2)\n  @[simps fst fst_fst snd] def foo : prod \u2115 \u2115 \u00d7 my_prod \u2115 \u2115 := \u27e8\u27e81, 2\u27e9, 3, 4\u27e9\n  ```\n  generates\n  ```lean\n  @[simp] lemma foo_fst : foo.fst = (1, 2)\n  @[simp] lemma foo_fst_fst : foo.fst.fst = 1\n  @[simp] lemma foo_snd : foo.snd = {fst := 3, snd := 4}\n  ```\n* If one of the values is an eta-expanded structure, we will eta-reduce this structure.\n\n  Example:\n  ```lean\n  structure equiv_plus_data (\u03b1 \u03b2) extends \u03b1 \u2243 \u03b2 := (data : bool)\n  @[simps] def bar {\u03b1} : equiv_plus_data \u03b1 \u03b1 := { data := tt, ..equiv.refl \u03b1 }\n  ```\n  generates the following:\n  ```lean\n  @[simp] lemma bar_to_equiv : \u2200 {\u03b1 : Sort*}, bar.to_equiv = equiv.refl \u03b1\n  @[simp] lemma bar_data : \u2200 {\u03b1 : Sort*}, bar.data = tt\n  ```\n  This is true, even though Lean inserts an eta-expanded version of `equiv.refl \u03b1` in the\n  definition of `bar`.\n* For configuration options, see the doc string of `simps_cfg`.\n* The precise syntax is `('simps' ident* e)`, where `e` is an expression of type `simps_cfg`.\n* `@[simps]` reduces let-expressions where necessary.\n* When option `trace.simps.verbose` is true, `simps` will print the projections it finds and the\n  lemmas it generates. The same can be achieved by using `@[simps?]`, except that in this case it\n  will not print projection information.\n* Use `@[to_additive, simps]` to apply both `to_additive` and `simps` to a definition, making sure\n  that `simps` comes after `to_additive`. This will also generate the additive versions of all\n  `simp` lemmas.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_add_projections", "description": "Derive lemmas specifying the projections of the declaration.\n If `todo` is non-empty, it will generate exactly the names in `todo`.\n `to_apply` is non-empty after a custom projection that is a composition of multiple projections\n was just used. In that case we need to apply these projections before we continue changing lhs.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_cfg.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "projection_rule", "description": "The type of rules that specify how metadata for projections in changes.\n See `initialize_simps_projection`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "projection_data.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_attr", "description": "The `@[simps]` attribute automatically derives lemmas specifying the projections of this\ndeclaration.\n\nExample:\n```lean\n@[simps] def foo : \u2115 \u00d7 \u2124 := (1, 2)\n```\nderives two `simp` lemmas:\n```lean\n@[simp] lemma foo_fst : foo.fst = 1\n@[simp] lemma foo_snd : foo.snd = 2\n```\n\n* It does not derive `simp` lemmas for the prop-valued projections.\n* It will automatically reduce newly created beta-redexes, but will not unfold any definitions.\n* If the structure has a coercion to either sorts or functions, and this is defined to be one\n  of the projections, then this coercion will be used instead of the projection.\n* If the structure is a class that has an instance to a notation class, like `has_mul`, then this\n  notation is used instead of the corresponding projection.\n* You can specify custom projections, by giving a declaration with name\n  `{structure_name}.simps.{projection_name}`. See Note [custom simps projection].\n\n  Example:\n  ```lean\n  def equiv.simps.inv_fun (e : \u03b1 \u2243 \u03b2) : \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b1 := e.symm\n  @[simps] def equiv.trans (e\u2081 : \u03b1 \u2243 \u03b2) (e\u2082 : \u03b2 \u2243 \u03b3) : \u03b1 \u2243 \u03b3 :=\n  \u27e8e\u2082 \u2218 e\u2081, e\u2081.symm \u2218 e\u2082.symm\u27e9\n  ```\n  generates\n  ```lean\n  @[simp] lemma equiv.trans_to_fun : \u2200 {\u03b1 \u03b2 \u03b3} (e\u2081 e\u2082) (a : \u03b1), \u21d1(e\u2081.trans e\u2082) a = (\u21d1e\u2082 \u2218 \u21d1e\u2081) a\n  @[simp] lemma equiv.trans_inv_fun : \u2200 {\u03b1 \u03b2 \u03b3} (e\u2081 e\u2082) (a : \u03b3),\n    \u21d1((e\u2081.trans e\u2082).symm) a = (\u21d1(e\u2081.symm) \u2218 \u21d1(e\u2082.symm)) a\n  ```\n\n* You can specify custom projection names, by specifying the new projection names using\n  `initialize_simps_projections`.\n  Example: `initialize_simps_projections equiv (to_fun \u2192 apply, inv_fun \u2192 symm_apply)`.\n  See `initialize_simps_projections_cmd` for more information.\n\n* If one of the fields itself is a structure, this command will recursively create\n  `simp` lemmas for all fields in that structure.\n  * Exception: by default it will not recursively create `simp` lemmas for fields in the structures\n    `prod` and `pprod`. You can give explicit projection names or change the value of\n    `simps_cfg.not_recursive` to override this behavior.\n\n  Example:\n  ```lean\n  structure my_prod (\u03b1 \u03b2 : Type*) := (fst : \u03b1) (snd : \u03b2)\n  @[simps] def foo : prod \u2115 \u2115 \u00d7 my_prod \u2115 \u2115 := \u27e8\u27e81, 2\u27e9, 3, 4\u27e9\n  ```\n  generates\n  ```lean\n  @[simp] lemma foo_fst : foo.fst = (1, 2)\n  @[simp] lemma foo_snd_fst : foo.snd.fst = 3\n  @[simp] lemma foo_snd_snd : foo.snd.snd = 4\n  ```\n\n* You can use `@[simps proj1 proj2 ...]` to only generate the projection lemmas for the specified\n  projections.\n* Recursive projection names can be specified using `proj1_proj2_proj3`.\n  This will create a lemma of the form `foo.proj1.proj2.proj3 = ...`.\n\n  Example:\n  ```lean\n  structure my_prod (\u03b1 \u03b2 : Type*) := (fst : \u03b1) (snd : \u03b2)\n  @[simps fst fst_fst snd] def foo : prod \u2115 \u2115 \u00d7 my_prod \u2115 \u2115 := \u27e8\u27e81, 2\u27e9, 3, 4\u27e9\n  ```\n  generates\n  ```lean\n  @[simp] lemma foo_fst : foo.fst = (1, 2)\n  @[simp] lemma foo_fst_fst : foo.fst.fst = 1\n  @[simp] lemma foo_snd : foo.snd = {fst := 3, snd := 4}\n  ```\n* If one of the values is an eta-expanded structure, we will eta-reduce this structure.\n\n  Example:\n  ```lean\n  structure equiv_plus_data (\u03b1 \u03b2) extends \u03b1 \u2243 \u03b2 := (data : bool)\n  @[simps] def bar {\u03b1} : equiv_plus_data \u03b1 \u03b1 := { data := tt, ..equiv.refl \u03b1 }\n  ```\n  generates the following:\n  ```lean\n  @[simp] lemma bar_to_equiv : \u2200 {\u03b1 : Sort*}, bar.to_equiv = equiv.refl \u03b1\n  @[simp] lemma bar_data : \u2200 {\u03b1 : Sort*}, bar.data = tt\n  ```\n  This is true, even though Lean inserts an eta-expanded version of `equiv.refl \u03b1` in the\n  definition of `bar`.\n* For configuration options, see the doc string of `simps_cfg`.\n* The precise syntax is `('simps' ident* e)`, where `e` is an expression of type `simps_cfg`.\n* `@[simps]` reduces let-expressions where necessary.\n* When option `trace.simps.verbose` is true, `simps` will print the projections it finds and the\n  lemmas it generates. The same can be achieved by using `@[simps?]`, except that in this case it\n  will not print projection information.\n* Use `@[to_additive, simps]` to apply both `to_additive` and `simps` to a definition, making sure\n  that `simps` comes after `to_additive`. This will also generate the additive versions of all\n  `simp` lemmas.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "parsed_projection_data.has_to_format", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "library_note.custom simps projection", "description": "You can specify custom projections for the `@[simps]` attribute.\nTo do this for the projection `my_structure.original_projection` by adding a declaration\n`my_structure.simps.my_projection` that is definitionally equal to\n`my_structure.original_projection` but has the projection in the desired (simp-normal) form.\nThen you can call\n```lean\ninitialize_simps_projections (original_projection \u2192 my_projection, ...)\n```\nto register this projection. See `initialize_simps_projections_cmd` for more information.\n\nYou can also specify custom projections that are definitionally equal to a composite of multiple\nprojections. This is often desirable when extending structures (without `old_structure_cmd`).\n\n`has_coe_to_fun` and notation class (like `has_mul`) instances will be automatically used, if they\nare definitionally equal to a projection of the structure (but not when they are equal to the\ncomposite of multiple projections).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_str_attr", "description": "The `@[_simps_str]` attribute specifies the preferred projections of the given structure,\nused by the `@[simps]` attribute.\n- This will usually be tagged by the `@[simps]` tactic.\n- You can also generate this with the command `initialize_simps_projections`.\n- To change the default value, see Note [custom simps projection].\n- You are strongly discouraged to add this attribute manually.\n- The first argument is the list of names of the universe variables used in the structure\n- The second argument is a list that consists of the projection data for each projection.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "projections_info", "description": "Returns the projection information of a structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_parser", "description": "The parser for the `@[simps]` attribute.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "get_composite_of_projections", "description": "Given a structure `str` and a projection `proj`, that could be multiple nested projections\n (separated by `_`), returns an expression that is the composition of these projections and a\n list of natural numbers, that are the projection numbers of the applied projections.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lemmas_only", "description": "A common configuration for `@[simps]`: don't tag the generated lemmas with `@[simp]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "as_fn", "description": "A common configuration for `@[simps]`: generate equalities between functions instead equalities\n between fully applied expressions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_parse_rule", "description": "Parse a rule for `initialize_simps_projections`. It is either `<name>\u2192<name>` or `-<name>`,\n possibly following by `as_prefix`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "get_composite_of_projections_aux", "description": "Auxiliary function of `get_composite_of_projections`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "projection_data.has_to_tactic_format", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "notation_class_attr", "description": "The `@[notation_class]` attribute specifies that this is a notation class,\nand this notation should be used instead of projections by @[simps].\n* The first argument `tt` for notation classes and `ff` for classes applied to the structure,\n  like `has_coe_to_sort` and `has_coe_to_fun`\n* The second argument is the name of the projection (by default it is the first projection\n  of the structure)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "projection_data", "description": "Projection data for a single projection of a structure, consisting of the following fields:\n- the name used in the generated `simp` lemmas\n- an expression used by simps for the projection. It must be definitionally equal to an original\n  projection (or a composition of multiple projections).\n  These expressions can contain the universe parameters specified in the first argument of\n  `simps_str_attr`.\n- a list of natural numbers, which is the projection number(s) that have to be applied to the\n  expression. For example the list `[0, 1]` corresponds to applying the first projection of the\n  structure, and then the second projection of the resulting structure (this assumes that the\n  target of the first projection is a structure with at least two projections).\n  The composition of these projections is required to be definitionally equal to the provided\n  expression.\n- A boolean specifying whether `simp` lemmas are generated for this projection by default.\n- A boolean specifying whether this projection is written as prefix.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "projection_data.name", "description": "Projection data for a single projection of a structure, consisting of the following fields:\n- the name used in the generated `simp` lemmas\n- an expression used by simps for the projection. It must be definitionally equal to an original\n  projection (or a composition of multiple projections).\n  These expressions can contain the universe parameters specified in the first argument of\n  `simps_str_attr`.\n- a list of natural numbers, which is the projection number(s) that have to be applied to the\n  expression. For example the list `[0, 1]` corresponds to applying the first projection of the\n  structure, and then the second projection of the resulting structure (this assumes that the\n  target of the first projection is a structure with at least two projections).\n  The composition of these projections is required to be definitionally equal to the provided\n  expression.\n- A boolean specifying whether `simp` lemmas are generated for this projection by default.\n- A boolean specifying whether this projection is written as prefix.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "projection_data.expr", "description": "Projection data for a single projection of a structure, consisting of the following fields:\n- the name used in the generated `simp` lemmas\n- an expression used by simps for the projection. It must be definitionally equal to an original\n  projection (or a composition of multiple projections).\n  These expressions can contain the universe parameters specified in the first argument of\n  `simps_str_attr`.\n- a list of natural numbers, which is the projection number(s) that have to be applied to the\n  expression. For example the list `[0, 1]` corresponds to applying the first projection of the\n  structure, and then the second projection of the resulting structure (this assumes that the\n  target of the first projection is a structure with at least two projections).\n  The composition of these projections is required to be definitionally equal to the provided\n  expression.\n- A boolean specifying whether `simp` lemmas are generated for this projection by default.\n- A boolean specifying whether this projection is written as prefix.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "projection_data.proj_nrs", "description": "Projection data for a single projection of a structure, consisting of the following fields:\n- the name used in the generated `simp` lemmas\n- an expression used by simps for the projection. It must be definitionally equal to an original\n  projection (or a composition of multiple projections).\n  These expressions can contain the universe parameters specified in the first argument of\n  `simps_str_attr`.\n- a list of natural numbers, which is the projection number(s) that have to be applied to the\n  expression. For example the list `[0, 1]` corresponds to applying the first projection of the\n  structure, and then the second projection of the resulting structure (this assumes that the\n  target of the first projection is a structure with at least two projections).\n  The composition of these projections is required to be definitionally equal to the provided\n  expression.\n- A boolean specifying whether `simp` lemmas are generated for this projection by default.\n- A boolean specifying whether this projection is written as prefix.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "projection_data.is_default", "description": "Projection data for a single projection of a structure, consisting of the following fields:\n- the name used in the generated `simp` lemmas\n- an expression used by simps for the projection. It must be definitionally equal to an original\n  projection (or a composition of multiple projections).\n  These expressions can contain the universe parameters specified in the first argument of\n  `simps_str_attr`.\n- a list of natural numbers, which is the projection number(s) that have to be applied to the\n  expression. For example the list `[0, 1]` corresponds to applying the first projection of the\n  structure, and then the second projection of the resulting structure (this assumes that the\n  target of the first projection is a structure with at least two projections).\n  The composition of these projections is required to be definitionally equal to the provided\n  expression.\n- A boolean specifying whether `simp` lemmas are generated for this projection by default.\n- A boolean specifying whether this projection is written as prefix.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "projection_data.is_prefix", "description": "Projection data for a single projection of a structure, consisting of the following fields:\n- the name used in the generated `simp` lemmas\n- an expression used by simps for the projection. It must be definitionally equal to an original\n  projection (or a composition of multiple projections).\n  These expressions can contain the universe parameters specified in the first argument of\n  `simps_str_attr`.\n- a list of natural numbers, which is the projection number(s) that have to be applied to the\n  expression. For example the list `[0, 1]` corresponds to applying the first projection of the\n  structure, and then the second projection of the resulting structure (this assumes that the\n  target of the first projection is a structure with at least two projections).\n  The composition of these projections is required to be definitionally equal to the provided\n  expression.\n- A boolean specifying whether `simp` lemmas are generated for this projection by default.\n- A boolean specifying whether this projection is written as prefix.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.command.initialize_simps_projections", "description": "This command specifies custom names and custom projections for the simp attribute `simps_attr`.\n* You can specify custom names by writing e.g.\n  `initialize_simps_projections equiv (to_fun \u2192 apply, inv_fun \u2192 symm_apply)`.\n* See Note [custom simps projection] and the examples below for information how to declare custom\n  projections.\n* If no custom projection is specified, the projection will be `coe_fn`/`\u21d1` if a `has_coe_to_fun`\n  instance has been declared, or the notation of a notation class (like `has_mul`) if such an\n  instance is available. If none of these cases apply, the projection itself will be used.\n* You can disable a projection by default by running\n  `initialize_simps_projections equiv (-inv_fun)`\n  This will ensure that no simp lemmas are generated for this projection,\n  unless this projection is explicitly specified by the user.\n* If you want the projection name added as a prefix in the generated lemma name, you can add the\n  `as_prefix` modifier:\n  `initialize_simps_projections equiv (to_fun \u2192 coe as_prefix)`\n  Note that this does not influence the parsing of projection names: if you have a declaration\n  `foo` and you want to apply the projections `snd`, `coe` (which is a prefix) and `fst`, in that\n  order you can run `@[simps snd_coe_fst] def foo ...` and this will generate a lemma with the\n  name `coe_foo_snd_fst`.\n  * Run `initialize_simps_projections?` (or `set_option trace.simps.verbose true`)\n  to see the generated projections.\n* You can declare a new name for a projection that is the composite of multiple projections, e.g.\n  ```lean\n    structure A := (proj : \u2115)\n    structure B extends A\n    initialize_simps_projections? B (to_A_proj \u2192 proj, -to_A)\n  ```\n  You can also make your custom projection that is definitionally equal to a composite of\n  projections. In this case, coercions and notation classes are not automatically recognized, and\n  should be manually given by giving a custom projection.\n  This is especially useful when extending a structure (without `old_structure_cmd`).\n  In the above example, it is desirable to add `-to_A`, so that `@[simps]` doesn't automatically\n  apply the `B.to_A` projection and then recursively the `A.proj` projection in the lemmas it\n  generates. If you want to get both the `foo_proj` and `foo_to_A` simp lemmas, you can use\n  `@[simps, simps to_A]`.\n* Running `initialize_simps_projections my_struc` without arguments is not necessary, it has the\n  same effect if you just add `@[simps]` to a declaration.\n* If you do anything to change the default projections, make sure to call either `@[simps]` or\n  `initialize_simps_projections` in the same file as the structure declaration. Otherwise, you might\n  have a file that imports the structure, but not your custom projections.\n\nSome common uses:\n* If you define a new homomorphism-like structure (like `mul_hom`) you can just run\n  `initialize_simps_projections` after defining the `has_coe_to_fun` instance\n  ```lean\n    instance {mM : has_mul M} {mN : has_mul N} : has_coe_to_fun (M \u2192\u2099* N) := ...\n    initialize_simps_projections mul_hom (to_fun \u2192 apply)\n  ```\n  This will generate `foo_apply` lemmas for each declaration `foo`.\n* If you prefer `coe_foo` lemmas that state equalities between functions, use\n  `initialize_simps_projections mul_hom (to_fun \u2192 coe as_prefix)`\n  In this case you have to use `@[simps {fully_applied := ff}]` or equivalently `@[simps as_fn]`\n  whenever you call `@[simps]`.\n* You can also initialize to use both, in which case you have to choose which one to use by default,\n  by using either of the following\n  ```lean\n    initialize_simps_projections mul_hom (to_fun \u2192 apply, to_fun \u2192 coe, -coe as_prefix)\n    initialize_simps_projections mul_hom (to_fun \u2192 apply, to_fun \u2192 coe as_prefix, -apply)\n  ```\n  In the first case, you can get both lemmas using `@[simps, simps coe as_fn]` and in the second\n  case you can get both lemmas using `@[simps as_fn, simps apply]`.\n* If your new homomorphism-like structure extends another structure (without `old_structure_cmd`)\n  (like `rel_embedding`), then you have to specify explicitly that you want to use a coercion\n  as a custom projection. For example\n  ```lean\n    def rel_embedding.simps.apply (h : r \u21aar s) : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2 := h\n    initialize_simps_projections rel_embedding (to_embedding_to_fun \u2192 apply, -to_embedding)\n  ```\n* If you have an isomorphism-like structure (like `equiv`) you often want to define a custom\n  projection for the inverse:\n  ```lean\n    def equiv.simps.symm_apply (e : \u03b1 \u2243 \u03b2) : \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b1 := e.symm\n    initialize_simps_projections equiv (to_fun \u2192 apply, inv_fun \u2192 symm_apply)\n  ```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simps_get_raw_projections", "description": "  Get the projections used by `simps` associated to a given structure `str`.\n\n  The returned information is also stored in a parameter of the attribute `@[_simps_str]`, which\n  is given to `str`. If `str` already has this attribute, the information is read from this\n  attribute instead. See the documentation for this attribute for the data this tactic returns.\n\n  The returned universe levels are the universe levels of the structure. For the projections there\n  are three cases\n  * If the declaration `{structure_name}.simps.{projection_name}` has been declared, then the value\n    of this declaration is used (after checking that it is definitionally equal to the actual\n    projection. If you rename the projection name, the declaration should have the *new* projection\n    name.\n  * You can also declare a custom projection that is a composite of multiple projections.\n  * Otherwise, for every class with the `notation_class` attribute, and the structure has an\n    instance of that notation class, then the projection of that notation class is used for the\n    projection that is definitionally equal to it (if there is such a projection).\n    This means in practice that coercions to function types and sorts will be used instead of\n    a projection, if this coercion is definitionally equal to a projection. Furthermore, for\n    notation classes like `has_mul` and `has_zero` those projections are used instead of the\n    corresponding projection.\n    Projections for coercions and notation classes are not automatically generated if they are\n    composites of multiple projections (for example when you use `extend` without the\n    `old_structure_cmd`).\n  * Otherwise, the projection of the structure is chosen.\n    For example: ``simps_get_raw_projections env `prod`` gives the default projections\n```lean\n  ([u, v], [prod.fst.{u v}, prod.snd.{u v}])\n```\n    while ``simps_get_raw_projections env `equiv`` gives\n```lean\n  ([u_1, u_2], [\u03bb \u03b1 \u03b2, coe_fn, \u03bb {\u03b1 \u03b2} (e : \u03b1 \u2243 \u03b2), \u21d1(e.symm), left_inv, right_inv])\n```\n    after declaring the coercion from `equiv` to function and adding the declaration\n```lean\n  def equiv.simps.inv_fun {\u03b1 \u03b2} (e : \u03b1 \u2243 \u03b2) : \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b1 := e.symm\n```\n\n  Optionally, this command accepts three optional arguments:\n  * If `trace_if_exists` the command will always generate a trace message when the structure already\n    has the attribute `@[_simps_str]`.\n  * The `rules` argument accepts a list of pairs `sum.inl (old_name, new_name)`. This is used to\n    change the projection name `old_name` to the custom projection name `new_name`. Example:\n    for the structure `equiv` the projection `to_fun` could be renamed `apply`. This name will be\n    used for parsing and generating projection names. This argument is ignored if the structure\n    already has an existing attribute. If an element of `rules` is of the form `sum.inr name`, this\n    means that the projection `name` will not be applied by default.\n  * if `trc` is true, this tactic will trace information.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.compute_degree_le", "description": "  `compute_degree_le` tries to solve a goal of the form `f.nat_degree \u2264 d` or `f.degree \u2264 d`,\nwhere `f : R[X]` and `d : \u2115` or `d : with_bot \u2115`.\n\nIf the given degree `d` is smaller than the one that the tactic computes,\nthen the tactic suggests the degree that it computed.\n\nExamples:\n\n```lean\nopen polynomial\nopen_locale polynomial\n\nvariables {R : Type*} [semiring R] {a b c d e : R}\n\nexample {F} [ring F] {a : F} {n : \u2115} (h : n \u2264 10) :\n  nat_degree (X ^ n + C a * X ^ 10 : F[X]) \u2264 10 :=\nby compute_degree_le\n\nexample : nat_degree (7 * X : R[X]) \u2264 1 :=\nby compute_degree_le\n\nexample {p : R[X]} {n : \u2115} {p0 : p.nat_degree = 0} :\n (p ^ n).nat_degree \u2264 0 :=\nby compute_degree_le\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.compute_degree.guess_degree", "description": "  `guess_degree e` assumes that `e` is an expression in a polynomial ring, and makes an attempt\nat guessing the `nat_degree` of `e`.  Heuristics for `guess_degree`:\n* `0, 1, C a`,      guess `0`,\n* `polynomial.X`,   guess `1`,\n*  `bit0/1 f, -f`,  guess `guess_degree f`,\n* `f + g, f - g`,   guess `max (guess_degree f) (guess_degree g)`,\n* `f * g`,          guess `guess_degree f + guess_degree g`,\n* `f ^ n`,          guess `guess_degree f * n`,\n* `monomial n r`,   guess `n`,\n* `f` not as above, guess `f.nat_degree`.\n\nThe guessed degree should coincide with the behaviour of `resolve_sum_step`:\n`resolve_sum_step` cannot solve a goal `f.nat_degree \u2264 d` if `guess_degree f < d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.compute_degree_le", "description": "  `compute_degree_le` tries to solve a goal of the form `f.nat_degree \u2264 d` or `f.degree \u2264 d`,\nwhere `f : R[X]` and `d : \u2115` or `d : with_bot \u2115`.\n\nIf the given degree `d` is smaller than the one that the tactic computes,\nthen the tactic suggests the degree that it computed.\n\nExamples:\n\n```lean\nopen polynomial\nopen_locale polynomial\n\nvariables {R : Type*} [semiring R] {a b c d e : R}\n\nexample {F} [ring F] {a : F} {n : \u2115} (h : n \u2264 10) :\n  nat_degree (X ^ n + C a * X ^ 10 : F[X]) \u2264 10 :=\nby compute_degree_le\n\nexample : nat_degree (7 * X : R[X]) \u2264 1 :=\nby compute_degree_le\n\nexample {p : R[X]} {n : \u2115} {p0 : p.nat_degree = 0} :\n (p ^ n).nat_degree \u2264 0 :=\nby compute_degree_le\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.compute_degree.eval_guessing", "description": "  `eval_guessing n e` takes a natural number `n` and an expression `e` and gives an\nestimate for the evaluation of `eval_expr' \u2115 e`.  It is tailor made for estimating degrees of\npolynomials.\n\nIt decomposes `e` recursively as a sequence of additions, multiplications and `max`.\nOn the atoms of the process, `eval_guessing` tries to use `eval_expr' \u2115`, resorting to using\n`n` if `eval_expr' \u2115` fails.\n\nFor use with degree of polynomials, we mostly use `n = 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.compute_degree.norm_assum", "description": "  `norm_assum` simply tries `norm_num` and `assumption`.\nIt is used to try to discharge as many as possible of the side-goals of `compute_degree_le`.\nSeveral side-goals are of the form `m \u2264 n`, for natural numbers `m, n` or of the form `c \u2260 0`,\nwith `c` a coefficient of the polynomial `f` in question.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.compute_degree.compute_degree_le_aux", "description": "  A general description of `compute_degree_le_aux` is in the doc-string of `compute_degree`.\nThe difference between the two is that `compute_degree_le_aux` makes no effort to close side-goals,\nnor fails if the goal does not change.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.compute_degree.resolve_sum_step", "description": " `resolve_sum_step` assumes that the current goal is of the form `f.nat_degree \u2264 d`, failing\notherwise.  It tries to make progress on the goal by progressing into `f` if `f` is\n* a sum, difference, opposite, product, or a power;\n* a monomial;\n* `C a`;\n* `0, 1` or `bit0 a, bit1 a` (to deal with numerals).\n\nThe side-goals produced by `resolve_sum_step` are either again of the same shape `f'.nat_degree \u2264 d`\nor of the form `m \u2264 n`, where `m n : \u2115`.\n\nIf `d` is less than `guess_degree f`, this tactic will create unsolvable goals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.comparison_left_adjoint_hom_equiv_apply_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.counit_coequalizer_of_reflects_coequalizer", "description": "The counit cofork is a colimit provided `G` reflects it.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.comparison_left_adjoint_hom_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.comparison_adjunction_counit_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadic_of_has_preserves_reflects_G_split_coequalizers", "description": "To show `G` is a monadic right adjoint, we can show it preserves and reflects `G`-split\ncoequalizers, and `C` has them.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.comparison_adjunction_unit_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.unit_cofork_\u03c0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.comparison_adjunction_unit_f_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.left_adjoint_comparison", "description": "Construct the adjunction to the comparison functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.main_pair_reflexive", "description": "The \"main pair\" for an algebra `(A, \u03b1)` is the pair of morphisms `(F \u03b1, \u03b5_FA)`. It is always a\nreflexive pair, and will be used to construct the left adjoint to the comparison functor and show it\nis an equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadic_of_has_preserves_reflexive_coequalizers_of_reflects_isomorphisms", "description": "Reflexive (crude) monadicity theorem. If `G` has a right adjoint, `D` has and `G` preserves\nreflexive coequalizers and `G` reflects isomorphisms, then `G` is monadic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.comparison_left_adjoint_hom_equiv", "description": "We have a bijection of homsets which will be used to construct the left adjoint to the comparison\nfunctor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadic_of_creates_G_split_coequalizers", "description": "Beck's monadicity theorem. If `G` has a right adjoint and creates coequalizers of `G`-split pairs,\nthen it is monadic.\nThis is the converse of `creates_G_split_of_monadic`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.counit_cofork_\u03b9_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.unit_colimit_of_preserves_coequalizer", "description": "The unit cofork is a colimit provided `G` preserves it.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.comparison_adjunction", "description": "Provided we have the appropriate coequalizers, we have an adjunction to the comparison functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.counit_cofork", "description": "The cofork which describes the counit of the adjunction: the morphism from the coequalizer of\nthis pair to this morphism is the counit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.counit_cofork_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.comparison_adjunction_counit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.comparison_left_adjoint_obj", "description": "The object function for the left adjoint to the comparison functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.unit_cofork_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.main_pair_G_split", "description": "The \"main pair\" for an algebra `(A, \u03b1)` is the pair of morphisms `(F \u03b1, \u03b5_FA)`. It is always a\n`G`-split pair, and will be used to construct the left adjoint to the comparison functor and show it\nis an equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadic_of_has_preserves_G_split_coequalizers_of_reflects_isomorphisms", "description": "An alternate version of Beck's monadicity theorem. If `G` reflects isomorphisms, preserves\ncoequalizers of `G`-split pairs and `C` has coequalizers of `G`-split pairs, then it is monadic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.creates_G_split_coequalizers_of_monadic", "description": "If `G` is monadic, it creates colimits of `G`-split pairs. This is the \"boring\" direction of Beck's\nmonadicity theorem, the converse is given in `monadic_of_creates_G_split_coequalizers`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monad.monadicity_internal.unit_cofork", "description": "This is a cofork which is helpful for establishing monadicity: the morphism from the Beck\ncoequalizer to this cofork is the unit for the adjunction on the comparison functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rank_matrix", "description": "If `m` and `n` are `fintype`, the rank of `m \u00d7 n` matrices is `(# m).lift * (# n).lift`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rank_finsupp'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rank_tensor_product", "description": "The rank of `M \u2297[R] N` is `(module.rank R M).lift * (module.rank R N).lift`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rank_matrix''", "description": "If `m` and `n` are `fintype` that lie in the same universe as `R`, the rank of `m \u00d7 n` matrices\n is `# m * # n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rank_direct_sum", "description": "The rank of the direct sum is the sum of the ranks.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rank_tensor_product'", "description": "If `M` and `N` lie in the same universe, the rank of `M \u2297[R] N` is\n `(module.rank R M) * (module.rank R N)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rank_matrix'", "description": "If `m` and `n` are `fintype` that lie in the same universe, the rank of `m \u00d7 n` matrices is\n `(# n * # m).lift`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rank_finsupp_self", "description": "The rank of `(\u03b9 \u2192\u2080 R)` is `(# \u03b9).lift`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rank_finsupp_self'", "description": "If `R` and `\u03b9` lie in the same universe, the rank of `(\u03b9 \u2192\u2080 R)` is `# \u03b9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rank_finsupp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lean.githash", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "lean.special_version_desc", "description": "Additional version description like \"nightly-2018-03-11\"", "p": "core"}, {"name": "lean.is_release", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "lean.version", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "pairwise", "description": "A relation `r` holds pairwise if `r i j` for all `i \u2260 j`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.pairwise.on_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reflexive.set_pairwise_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pairwise.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.pairwise.eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.pairwise_of_forall", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective_iff_pairwise_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.pairwise.imp_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.pairwise.imp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.pairwise", "description": "The relation `r` holds pairwise on the set `s` if `r x y` for all *distinct* `x y \u2208 s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pairwise.eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pairwise.set_pairwise", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.pairwise_ne", "description": "**Alias** of the forward direction of `function.injective_iff_pairwise_ne`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.coe_sections", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.card_sections", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.prod_map_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.sections_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.sections_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.mem_sections", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.sections_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.sections", "description": "The sections of a multiset of multisets `s` consists of all those multisets\nwhich can be put in bijection with `s`, so each element is an member of the corresponding multiset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "congr_arg_kind.has_to_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "congr_arg_kind.to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_congr_lemma_simp", "description": "`mk_congr_lemma_simp f nargs md`\ncreates a congruence lemma for the simplifier for the given function argument `f`.\nIf `nargs` is not none, then it tries to create a lemma for an application of arity `nargs`.\nIf `nargs` is none then the number of arguments will be guessed from the type signature of `f`.\n\nThat is, given `f : \u03a0 {\u03b1 \u03b2 \u03b3 \u03b4 : Type}, \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b3 \u2192 \u03b4` and `nargs = some 6`, we get a congruence lemma:\n``` lean\n{ type := \u2200 (\u03b1 \u03b2 \u03b3 \u03b4 : Type), \u2200 (a\u2081 a\u2082 : \u03b1), a\u2081 = a\u2082 \u2192 \u2200 (b\u2081 b\u2082 : \u03b2), b\u2081 = b\u2082 \u2192 f a\u2081 b\u2081 = f a\u2082 b\u2082\n, proof := ...\n, arg_kinds := [fixed, fixed, fixed, fixed, eq,eq]\n}\n```\nSee the docstrings for the cases of `congr_arg_kind` for more detail on how `arg_kinds` are chosen.\nThe system chooses the `arg_kinds` depending on what the other arguments depend on and whether the arguments have subsingleton types.\n\nNote that the number of arguments that `proof` takes can be inferred from `arg_kinds`: `arg_kinds.sum (fixed,cast \u21a6 1 | eq,heq \u21a6 3 | fixed_no_param \u21a6 0)`.\n\nFrom `congr_lemma.cpp`:\n> Create a congruence lemma that is useful for the simplifier.\n> In this kind of lemma, if the i-th argument is a Cast argument, then the lemma\n> contains an input a_i representing the i-th argument in the left-hand-side, and\n> it appears with a cast (e.g., eq.drec ... a_i ...) in the right-hand-side.\n> The idea is that the right-hand-side of this lemma \"tells\" the simplifier\n> how the resulting term looks like.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_specialized_congr_lemma", "description": " Create a specialized theorem using (a prefix of) the arguments of the given application.\n\nFor more information on specialization see the comment in the method body for `get_specialization_prefix_size` in `src/library/fun_info.cpp`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_specialized_congr_lemma_simp", "description": " Create a specialized theorem using (a prefix of) the arguments of the given application.\n\nAn example of usage can be found in `tests/lean/simp_subsingleton.lean`.\nFor more information on specialization see the comment in the method body for `get_specialization_prefix_size` in `src/library/fun_info.cpp`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "congr_arg_kind", "description": " This is a kind attached to an argument of a congruence lemma that tells the simplifier how to fill it in.\n- `fixed`: It is a parameter for the congruence lemma, the parameter occurs in the left and right hand sides.\n  For example the \u03b1 in the congruence generated from `f: \u03a0 {\u03b1 : Type} \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1`.\n- `fixed_no_param`: It is not a parameter for the congruence lemma, the lemma was specialized for this parameter.\n  This only happens if the parameter is a subsingleton/proposition, and other parameters depend on it.\n- `eq`: The lemma contains three parameters for this kind of argument `a_i`, `b_i` and `(eq_i : a_i = b_i)`.\n  `a_i` and `b_i` represent the left and right hand sides, and `eq_i` is a proof for their equality.\n  For example the second argument in `f: \u03a0 {\u03b1 : Type}, \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1`.\n- `cast`: corresponds to arguments that are subsingletons/propositions.\n  For example the `p` in the congruence generated from `f : \u03a0 (x y : \u2115) (p: x < y), \u2115`.\n- `heq` The lemma contains three parameters for this kind of argument `a_i`, `b_i` and `(eq_i : a_i == b_i)`.\n   `a_i` and `b_i` represent the left and right hand sides, and eq_i is a proof for their heterogeneous equality.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "congr_arg_kind.fixed", "description": " This is a kind attached to an argument of a congruence lemma that tells the simplifier how to fill it in.\n- `fixed`: It is a parameter for the congruence lemma, the parameter occurs in the left and right hand sides.\n  For example the \u03b1 in the congruence generated from `f: \u03a0 {\u03b1 : Type} \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1`.\n- `fixed_no_param`: It is not a parameter for the congruence lemma, the lemma was specialized for this parameter.\n  This only happens if the parameter is a subsingleton/proposition, and other parameters depend on it.\n- `eq`: The lemma contains three parameters for this kind of argument `a_i`, `b_i` and `(eq_i : a_i = b_i)`.\n  `a_i` and `b_i` represent the left and right hand sides, and `eq_i` is a proof for their equality.\n  For example the second argument in `f: \u03a0 {\u03b1 : Type}, \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1`.\n- `cast`: corresponds to arguments that are subsingletons/propositions.\n  For example the `p` in the congruence generated from `f : \u03a0 (x y : \u2115) (p: x < y), \u2115`.\n- `heq` The lemma contains three parameters for this kind of argument `a_i`, `b_i` and `(eq_i : a_i == b_i)`.\n   `a_i` and `b_i` represent the left and right hand sides, and eq_i is a proof for their heterogeneous equality.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "congr_arg_kind.fixed_no_param", "description": " This is a kind attached to an argument of a congruence lemma that tells the simplifier how to fill it in.\n- `fixed`: It is a parameter for the congruence lemma, the parameter occurs in the left and right hand sides.\n  For example the \u03b1 in the congruence generated from `f: \u03a0 {\u03b1 : Type} \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1`.\n- `fixed_no_param`: It is not a parameter for the congruence lemma, the lemma was specialized for this parameter.\n  This only happens if the parameter is a subsingleton/proposition, and other parameters depend on it.\n- `eq`: The lemma contains three parameters for this kind of argument `a_i`, `b_i` and `(eq_i : a_i = b_i)`.\n  `a_i` and `b_i` represent the left and right hand sides, and `eq_i` is a proof for their equality.\n  For example the second argument in `f: \u03a0 {\u03b1 : Type}, \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1`.\n- `cast`: corresponds to arguments that are subsingletons/propositions.\n  For example the `p` in the congruence generated from `f : \u03a0 (x y : \u2115) (p: x < y), \u2115`.\n- `heq` The lemma contains three parameters for this kind of argument `a_i`, `b_i` and `(eq_i : a_i == b_i)`.\n   `a_i` and `b_i` represent the left and right hand sides, and eq_i is a proof for their heterogeneous equality.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "congr_arg_kind.eq", "description": " This is a kind attached to an argument of a congruence lemma that tells the simplifier how to fill it in.\n- `fixed`: It is a parameter for the congruence lemma, the parameter occurs in the left and right hand sides.\n  For example the \u03b1 in the congruence generated from `f: \u03a0 {\u03b1 : Type} \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1`.\n- `fixed_no_param`: It is not a parameter for the congruence lemma, the lemma was specialized for this parameter.\n  This only happens if the parameter is a subsingleton/proposition, and other parameters depend on it.\n- `eq`: The lemma contains three parameters for this kind of argument `a_i`, `b_i` and `(eq_i : a_i = b_i)`.\n  `a_i` and `b_i` represent the left and right hand sides, and `eq_i` is a proof for their equality.\n  For example the second argument in `f: \u03a0 {\u03b1 : Type}, \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1`.\n- `cast`: corresponds to arguments that are subsingletons/propositions.\n  For example the `p` in the congruence generated from `f : \u03a0 (x y : \u2115) (p: x < y), \u2115`.\n- `heq` The lemma contains three parameters for this kind of argument `a_i`, `b_i` and `(eq_i : a_i == b_i)`.\n   `a_i` and `b_i` represent the left and right hand sides, and eq_i is a proof for their heterogeneous equality.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "congr_arg_kind.cast", "description": " This is a kind attached to an argument of a congruence lemma that tells the simplifier how to fill it in.\n- `fixed`: It is a parameter for the congruence lemma, the parameter occurs in the left and right hand sides.\n  For example the \u03b1 in the congruence generated from `f: \u03a0 {\u03b1 : Type} \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1`.\n- `fixed_no_param`: It is not a parameter for the congruence lemma, the lemma was specialized for this parameter.\n  This only happens if the parameter is a subsingleton/proposition, and other parameters depend on it.\n- `eq`: The lemma contains three parameters for this kind of argument `a_i`, `b_i` and `(eq_i : a_i = b_i)`.\n  `a_i` and `b_i` represent the left and right hand sides, and `eq_i` is a proof for their equality.\n  For example the second argument in `f: \u03a0 {\u03b1 : Type}, \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1`.\n- `cast`: corresponds to arguments that are subsingletons/propositions.\n  For example the `p` in the congruence generated from `f : \u03a0 (x y : \u2115) (p: x < y), \u2115`.\n- `heq` The lemma contains three parameters for this kind of argument `a_i`, `b_i` and `(eq_i : a_i == b_i)`.\n   `a_i` and `b_i` represent the left and right hand sides, and eq_i is a proof for their heterogeneous equality.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "congr_arg_kind.heq", "description": " This is a kind attached to an argument of a congruence lemma that tells the simplifier how to fill it in.\n- `fixed`: It is a parameter for the congruence lemma, the parameter occurs in the left and right hand sides.\n  For example the \u03b1 in the congruence generated from `f: \u03a0 {\u03b1 : Type} \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1`.\n- `fixed_no_param`: It is not a parameter for the congruence lemma, the lemma was specialized for this parameter.\n  This only happens if the parameter is a subsingleton/proposition, and other parameters depend on it.\n- `eq`: The lemma contains three parameters for this kind of argument `a_i`, `b_i` and `(eq_i : a_i = b_i)`.\n  `a_i` and `b_i` represent the left and right hand sides, and `eq_i` is a proof for their equality.\n  For example the second argument in `f: \u03a0 {\u03b1 : Type}, \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1`.\n- `cast`: corresponds to arguments that are subsingletons/propositions.\n  For example the `p` in the congruence generated from `f : \u03a0 (x y : \u2115) (p: x < y), \u2115`.\n- `heq` The lemma contains three parameters for this kind of argument `a_i`, `b_i` and `(eq_i : a_i == b_i)`.\n   `a_i` and `b_i` represent the left and right hand sides, and eq_i is a proof for their heterogeneous equality.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "congr_arg_kind.subsingleton_inst", "description": " This is a kind attached to an argument of a congruence lemma that tells the simplifier how to fill it in.\n- `fixed`: It is a parameter for the congruence lemma, the parameter occurs in the left and right hand sides.\n  For example the \u03b1 in the congruence generated from `f: \u03a0 {\u03b1 : Type} \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1`.\n- `fixed_no_param`: It is not a parameter for the congruence lemma, the lemma was specialized for this parameter.\n  This only happens if the parameter is a subsingleton/proposition, and other parameters depend on it.\n- `eq`: The lemma contains three parameters for this kind of argument `a_i`, `b_i` and `(eq_i : a_i = b_i)`.\n  `a_i` and `b_i` represent the left and right hand sides, and `eq_i` is a proof for their equality.\n  For example the second argument in `f: \u03a0 {\u03b1 : Type}, \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1`.\n- `cast`: corresponds to arguments that are subsingletons/propositions.\n  For example the `p` in the congruence generated from `f : \u03a0 (x y : \u2115) (p: x < y), \u2115`.\n- `heq` The lemma contains three parameters for this kind of argument `a_i`, `b_i` and `(eq_i : a_i == b_i)`.\n   `a_i` and `b_i` represent the left and right hand sides, and eq_i is a proof for their heterogeneous equality.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_hcongr_lemma", "description": " Make a congruence lemma using hetrogeneous equality `heq` instead of `eq`.\nFor example `mk_hcongr_lemma (f : \u03a0 (\u03b1 : \u2115 \u2192 Type) (n:\u2115) (b:\u03b1 n), \u2115` )` will make\n\n``` lean\n{ type := \u2200 \u03b1 \u03b1', \u03b1 = \u03b1' \u2192 \u2200 n n', n = n' \u2192 \u2200 (b : \u03b1 n) (b' : \u03b1' n'), b == b' \u2192 f \u03b1 n b == f \u03b1' n' b'\n, proof := ...\n, arg_kinds := [eq,eq,heq]\n}\n```\n\n(Using merely `mk_congr_lemma` instead will produce `[fixed,fixed,eq]` instaed.)", "p": "core"}, {"name": "congr_arg_kind.has_repr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "congr_lemma", "description": "A congruence lemma is a proof that two terms are equal using a congruence proof generated by `mk_congr_lemma_simp` and friends.\nSee the docstring for `mk_congr_lemma_simp` and `congr_arg_kind` for more information.\nThe conclusion is prepended by a set of arguments. `arg_kinds` gives a suggestion of how that argument should be filled in using a simplifier.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "congr_lemma.type", "description": "A congruence lemma is a proof that two terms are equal using a congruence proof generated by `mk_congr_lemma_simp` and friends.\nSee the docstring for `mk_congr_lemma_simp` and `congr_arg_kind` for more information.\nThe conclusion is prepended by a set of arguments. `arg_kinds` gives a suggestion of how that argument should be filled in using a simplifier.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "congr_lemma.proof", "description": "A congruence lemma is a proof that two terms are equal using a congruence proof generated by `mk_congr_lemma_simp` and friends.\nSee the docstring for `mk_congr_lemma_simp` and `congr_arg_kind` for more information.\nThe conclusion is prepended by a set of arguments. `arg_kinds` gives a suggestion of how that argument should be filled in using a simplifier.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "congr_lemma.arg_kinds", "description": "A congruence lemma is a proof that two terms are equal using a congruence proof generated by `mk_congr_lemma_simp` and friends.\nSee the docstring for `mk_congr_lemma_simp` and `congr_arg_kind` for more information.\nThe conclusion is prepended by a set of arguments. `arg_kinds` gives a suggestion of how that argument should be filled in using a simplifier.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_congr_lemma", "description": " Similar to `mk_congr_lemma_simp`, this will make a `congr_lemma` object.\nThe difference is that for each `congr_arg_kind.cast` argument, two proof arguments are generated.\n\nConsider some function `f : \u03a0 (x : \u2115) (p : x < 4), \u2115`.\n- `mk_congr_simp` will produce a congruence lemma with type `\u2200 (x x_1 : \u2115) (e_1 : x = x_1) (p : x < 4), f x p = f x_1 _`.\n- `mk_congr` will produce a congruence lemma with type `\u2200 (x x_1 : \u2115) (e_1 : x = x_1) (p : x < 4) (p_1 : x_1 < 4), f x p = f x_1 p_1`.\n\nFrom `congr_lemma.cpp`:\n> Create a congruence lemma for the congruence closure module.\n> In this kind of lemma, if the i-th argument is a Cast argument, then the lemma\n> contains two inputs a_i and b_i representing the i-th argument in the left-hand-side and\n> right-hand-side.\n> This lemma is based on the congruence lemma for the simplifier.\n> It uses subsinglenton elimination to show that the congr-simp lemma right-hand-side\n> is equal to the right-hand-side of this lemma.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "zero_lt_one'", "description": "See `zero_lt_one` for a version with the type implicit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_le_one", "description": "`zero_le_one` with the type argument implicit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_pos", "description": "**Alias** of `zero_lt_one`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_le_one_class", "description": "Typeclass for expressing that the `0` of a type is less or equal to its `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_le_one_class.zero_le_one", "description": "Typeclass for expressing that the `0` of a type is less or equal to its `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_lt_one", "description": "See `zero_lt_one'` for a version with the type explicit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zero_le_one'", "description": "`zero_le_one` with the type argument explicit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.field", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.up_rat_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.down_rat_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.division_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.semifield", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.division_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.has_rat_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monad_io_environment", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_environment.get_env", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_environment.get_cwd", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_environment.set_cwd", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.error", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.error.other", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.error.sys", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_random", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_random.set_rand_gen", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_random.rand", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.process.spawn_args", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.process.spawn_args.cmd", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.process.spawn_args.args", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.process.spawn_args.stdin", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.process.spawn_args.stdout", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.process.spawn_args.stderr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.process.spawn_args.cwd", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.process.spawn_args.env", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_is_alternative", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_process", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_process.child", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_process.stdin", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_process.stdout", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_process.stderr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_process.spawn", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_process.wait", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_process.sleep", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.process.stdio", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.process.stdio.piped", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.process.stdio.inherit", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.process.stdio.null", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_serial", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_serial.serialize", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_serial.deserialize", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_terminal", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_terminal.put_str", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_terminal.get_line", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_terminal.cmdline_args", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_file_system", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_file_system.mk_file_handle", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_file_system.is_eof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_file_system.flush", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_file_system.close", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_file_system.read", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_file_system.write", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_file_system.get_line", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_file_system.stdin", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_file_system.stdout", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_file_system.stderr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_file_system.file_exists", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_file_system.dir_exists", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_file_system.remove", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_file_system.rename", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_file_system.mkdir", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_file_system.rmdir", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.mode", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.mode.read", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.mode.write", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.mode.read_write", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "io.mode.append", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_is_monad_fail", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_net_system", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_net_system.socket", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_net_system.listen", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_net_system.accept", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_net_system.connect", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_net_system.recv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_net_system.send", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_net_system.close", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io_is_monad", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io.monad", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io.catch", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io.fail", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io.iterate", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_io.handle", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_open_map.cover_preserving", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.covering_of_presieve", "description": "Given a presieve `R` on `U`, we obtain a covering family of open sets in `X`, by taking as index\ntype the type of dependent pairs `(V, f)`, where `f : V \u27f6 U` is in `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_of_open_embedding", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.is_terminal_of_empty", "description": "The empty component of a sheaf is terminal", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.opens.cover_dense_induced_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "open_embedding.compatible_preserving", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.presieve_of_covering.index_of_hom_spec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.presieve_of_covering", "description": "Take `Y` to be `supr U` and obtain a presieve over `supr U`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.presieve_of_covering.index_of_hom", "description": "By using the axiom of choice, a dependent pair `(V, f)` where `f : V \u27f6 supr U` is a member of\n`presieve_of_covering U f` can be turned into an index `i : \u03b9`, such that `V = U i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.presieve_of_covering.mem_grothendieck_topology", "description": "The sieve generated by `presieve_of_covering U` is a member of the grothendieck topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.restrict_hom_equiv_hom", "description": "If a family `B` of open sets forms a basis of the topology on `X`, and if `F'`\n   is a sheaf on `X`, then a homomorphism between a presheaf `F` on `X` and `F'`\n   is equivalent to a homomorphism between their restrictions to the indexing type\n   `\u03b9` of `B`, with the induced category structure on `\u03b9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.covering_presieve_eq_self", "description": "Given a presieve `R` on `Y`, if we take its associated family of opens via\n   `covering_of_presieve` (which may not cover `Y` if `R` is not covering), and take\n   the presieve on `Y` associated to the family of opens via `presieve_of_covering_aux`,\n   then we get back the original presieve `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.opens.cover_dense_iff_is_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.covering_of_presieve.supr_eq_of_mem_grothendieck", "description": "If `R` is a presieve in the grothendieck topology on `opens X`, the covering family associated to\n`R` really is _covering_, i.e. the union of all open sets equals `U`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.covering_of_presieve_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.is_terminal_of_eq_empty", "description": "A variant of `is_terminal_of_empty` that is easier to `apply`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.presieve_of_covering_aux", "description": "Given a family of opens `U : \u03b9 \u2192 opens X` and any open `Y : opens X`, we obtain a presieve\non `Y` by declaring that a morphism `f : V \u27f6 Y` is a member of the presieve if and only if\nthere exists an index `i : \u03b9` such that `V = U i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.extend_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.hom_ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.presieve_of_covering.hom_of_index", "description": "An index `i : \u03b9` can be turned into a dependent pair `(V, f)`, where `V` is an open set and\n`f : V \u27f6 supr U` is a member of `presieve_of_covering U f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_integral_succ", "description": "Recurrent formula for `torus_integral`, see also `torus_integral_succ_above`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_integral", "description": "The definition of the integral over a generalized torus with center `c \u2208 \u2102\u207f` and radius `R \u2208 \u211d\u207f`\nas the `\u2022`-product of the derivative of `torus_map` and `f (torus_map c R \u03b8)`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_integral_succ_above", "description": "Recurrent formula for `torus_integral`, see also `torus_integral_succ`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_map_sub_center", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_integral_dim1", "description": " In dimension one, `torus_integral` is the same as `circle_integral`\n(up to the natural equivalence between `\u2102` and `fin 1 \u2192 \u2102`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_integrable.neg", "description": "If `f` is torus integrable then `-f` is torus integrable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_integrable.function_integrable", "description": "The function given in the definition of `torus_integral` is integrable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_map", "description": " The n dimensional exponential map $\u03b8_i \u21a6 c + R e^{\u03b8_i*I}, \u03b8 \u2208 \u211d\u207f$ representing\na torus in `\u2102\u207f` with center `c \u2208 \u2102\u207f` and generalized radius `R \u2208 \u211d\u207f`, so we can adjust\nit to every n axis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_integral_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_integrable.add", "description": "If `f` and `g` are two torus integrable functions, then so is `f + g`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_integral_radius_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_integral_const_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_torus_integral_le_of_norm_le_const", "description": "If for all `\u03b8 : \u211d\u207f`, `\u2016f (torus_map c R \u03b8)\u2016` is less than or equal to a constant `C : \u211d`, then\n`\u2016\u222f x in T(c, R), f x\u2016` is less than or equal to `(2 * \u03c0)^n * (\u220f i, |R i|) * C`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_map_zero_radius", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_integrable.sub", "description": "If `f` and `g` are two torus integrable functions, then so is `f - g`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_integrable", "description": " A function `f : \u2102\u207f \u2192 E` is integrable on the generalized torus if the function\n`f \u2218 torus_map c R \u03b8` is integrable on `Icc (0 : \u211d\u207f) (\u03bb _, 2 * \u03c0)`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_integral_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_integral_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_map_eq_center_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_integral_dim0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_integrable.torus_integrable_zero_radius", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_integral_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "torus_integrable.torus_integrable_const", "description": "Constant functions are torus integrable", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at_star_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at_star_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on_star_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_star_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_filter.star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lex.well_founded_lt_of_finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lex.well_founded_of_finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lex.acc", "description": "Transferred from `dfinsupp.lex.acc`. See the top of that file for an explanation for the\n appearance of the relation `r\u1d9c \u2293 (\u2260)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.well_founded_lt_of_finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.well_founded_lt'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lex.well_founded", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lex.well_founded'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.well_founded_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lex.well_founded_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.nondeg_complex_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.\u03b9_\u03c0_summand_eq_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.split.nondeg_complex_functor_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.\u03c3_comp_\u03c0_summand_id_eq_zero_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.to_karoubi_nondeg_complex_iso_N\u2081", "description": " The chain complex `s.nondeg_complex` attached to a splitting of a simplicial object `X`\nbecomes isomorphic to the normalized Moore complex `N\u2081.obj X` defined as a formal direct\nfactor in the category `karoubi (chain_complex C \u2115)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.d", "description": " The differentials `s.d i j : s.N i \u27f6 s.N j` on nondegenerate simplices of a split\nsimplicial object are induced by the differentials on the alternating face map complex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.to_karoubi_nondeg_complex_iso_N\u2081_inv_f_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.decomposition_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.\u03c0_summand_comp_\u03b9_summand_comp_P_infty_eq_P_infty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.split.nondeg_complex_functor", "description": " The functor which sends a split simplicial object in a preadditive category to\nthe chain complex which consists of nondegenerate simplices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.\u03b9_summand_comp_P_infty_eq_zero", "description": " If a simplicial object `X` in an additive category is split,\nthen `P_infty` vanishes on all the summands of `X _[n]` which do\nnot correspond to the identity of `[n]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.split.to_karoubi_nondeg_complex_functor_iso_N\u2081_hom_app_f_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.nondeg_complex", "description": " If `s` is a splitting of a simplicial object `X` in a preadditive category,\n`s.nondeg_complex` is a chain complex which is given in degree `n` by\nthe nondegenerate `n`-simplices of `X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.comp_P_infty_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.P_infty_comp_\u03c0_summand_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.split.to_karoubi_nondeg_complex_functor_iso_N\u2081_inv_app_f_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.\u03c0_summand_comp_\u03b9_summand_comp_P_infty_eq_P_infty_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.\u03b9_summand_comp_d_comp_\u03c0_summand_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.\u03c3_comp_\u03c0_summand_id_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.\u03c0_summand", "description": " The projection on a summand of the coproduct decomposition given\nby a splitting of a simplicial object.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.split.nondeg_complex_functor_map_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.nondeg_complex_d", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.\u03b9_\u03c0_summand_eq_id_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.to_karoubi_nondeg_complex_iso_N\u2081_hom_f_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.P_infty_comp_\u03c0_summand_id_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.\u03b9_\u03c0_summand_eq_zero_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.split.to_karoubi_nondeg_complex_functor_iso_N\u2081", "description": " The natural isomorphism (in `karoubi (chain_complex C \u2115)`) between the chain complex\nof nondegenerate simplices of a split simplicial object and the normalized Moore complex\ndefined as a formal direct factor of the alternating face map complex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplicial_object.splitting.\u03b9_\u03c0_summand_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.preterm.sub_subst", "description": "Find (t - s) inside a preterm and replace it with variable k", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.is_diff", "description": "Preform which asserts that the value of variable k is\n   the truncated difference between preterms t and s", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.preform.sub_terms", "description": "Find subtraction inside preform and return its operands", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.preterm.val_sub_subst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.unsat_of_unsat_sub_elim", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.preterm.sub_terms", "description": "Find subtraction inside preterm and return its operands", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.preform.sub_subst", "description": "Find (t - s) inside a preform and replace it with variable k", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.sat_sub_elim", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.sub_fresh_index", "description": "Return de Brujin index of fresh variable that does not occur\n   in any of the arguments", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.holds_is_diff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.sub_subst_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.sub_elim_core", "description": "Helper function for sub_elim", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.nat.sub_elim", "description": "Return a new preform with all subtractions eliminated", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.map_zero_object", "description": "A functor that preserves zero morphisms also preserves the zero object.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.map_zero_object_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_terminal_object_of_preserves_zero_morphisms", "description": "Preserving zero morphisms implies preserving terminal objects.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_zero_morphisms_of_is_left_adjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.map_zero_object_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_zero_morphisms_of_is_right_adjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_zero_morphisms_of_preserves_terminal_object", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.zero_of_map_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.map_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_zero_morphisms", "description": "A functor preserves zero morphisms if it sends zero morphisms to zero morphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_zero_morphisms.map_zero'", "description": "A functor preserves zero morphisms if it sends zero morphisms to zero morphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_zero_morphisms_of_full", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_initial_object_of_preserves_zero_morphisms", "description": "Preserving zero morphisms implies preserving terminal objects.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.map_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_zero_morphisms_of_preserves_initial_object", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_zero_morphisms_of_map_zero_object", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.map_comp_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.mk_eq_inl_mul_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.right_hom_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.inl_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.right_hom_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.right_hom_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.inv_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.inl_aut_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.inr_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.right_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.right_hom_comp_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.map_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.map_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.one_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.mul_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.inl_left_mul_inr_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.lift_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.map_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.lift_comp_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.right_hom", "description": "The canonical projection map `N \u22ca[\u03c6] G \u2192* G`, as a group hom.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.right_hom_eq_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.lift_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.map", "description": "Define a map from `N \u22ca[\u03c6] G` to `N\u2081 \u22ca[\u03c6\u2081] G\u2081` given maps `N \u2192* N\u2081` and `G \u2192* G\u2081` that\n satisfy a commutativity condition `\u2200 n g, f\u2081 (\u03c6 g n) = \u03c6\u2081 (f\u2082 g) (f\u2081 n)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product", "description": "The semidirect product of groups `N` and `G`, given a map `\u03c6` from `G` to the automorphism\n group of `N`. It the product of sets with the group operation\n `\u27e8n\u2081, g\u2081\u27e9 * \u27e8n\u2082, g\u2082\u27e9 = \u27e8n\u2081 * \u03c6 g\u2081 n\u2082, g\u2081 * g\u2082\u27e9`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.left", "description": "The semidirect product of groups `N` and `G`, given a map `\u03c6` from `G` to the automorphism\n group of `N`. It the product of sets with the group operation\n `\u27e8n\u2081, g\u2081\u27e9 * \u27e8n\u2082, g\u2082\u27e9 = \u27e8n\u2081 * \u03c6 g\u2081 n\u2082, g\u2081 * g\u2082\u27e9`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.right", "description": "The semidirect product of groups `N` and `G`, given a map `\u03c6` from `G` to the automorphism\n group of `N`. It the product of sets with the group operation\n `\u27e8n\u2081, g\u2081\u27e9 * \u27e8n\u2082, g\u2082\u27e9 = \u27e8n\u2081 * \u03c6 g\u2081 n\u2082, g\u2081 * g\u2082\u27e9`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.inr_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.mul_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.range_inl_eq_ker_right_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.map_comp_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.map_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.right_hom_comp_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.lift_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.inr", "description": "The canonical map `G \u2192* N \u22ca[\u03c6] G` sending `g` to `\u27e81, g\u27e9`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.inv_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.right_hom_comp_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.inl_aut", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.lift", "description": "Define a group hom `N \u22ca[\u03c6] G \u2192* H`, by defining maps `N \u2192* H` and `G \u2192* H`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.inl", "description": "The canonical map `N \u2192* N \u22ca[\u03c6] G` sending `n` to `\u27e8n, 1\u27e9`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.hom_ext", "description": "Two maps out of the semidirect product are equal if they're equal after composition\n with both `inl` and `inr`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.left_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.one_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.inl_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.right_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.lift_comp_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semidirect_product.left_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.poly_of_interest_vars_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.witt_poly_prod", "description": "```lean\n(\u2211 i in range n, (y.coeff i)^(p^(n-i)) * p^i.val) *\n  (\u2211 i in range n, (y.coeff i)^(p^(n-i)) * p^i.val)\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.witt_poly_prod_vars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.remainder_vars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.nth_remainder", "description": "Produces the \"remainder function\" of the `n+1`st coefficient, which does not depend on the `n+1`st\ncoefficients of the inputs.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.peval_poly_of_interest'", "description": "The characteristic `p` version of `peval_poly_of_interest`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.peval_poly_of_interest", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.remainder", "description": "`remainder p n` represents the remainder term from `mul_poly_of_interest_aux3`.\n`witt_poly_prod p (n+1)` will have variables up to `n+1`,\nbut `remainder` will only have variables up to `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.poly_of_interest_vars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.witt_poly_prod_remainder_vars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.nth_mul_coeff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.poly_of_interest", "description": "This is the polynomial whose degree we want to get a handle on.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.nth_remainder_spec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.mul_poly_of_interest_aux5", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.mul_poly_of_interest_aux1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.mul_poly_of_interest_aux2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.mul_poly_of_interest_vars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.witt_poly_prod_remainder", "description": "The \"remainder term\" of `witt_vector.witt_poly_prod`. See `mul_poly_of_interest_aux2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.mul_poly_of_interest_aux4", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.nth_mul_coeff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.mul_poly_of_interest_aux3", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.concrete_category.epi_of_surjective", "description": "In any concrete category, surjective morphisms are epimorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.induced_category.has_forget\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.concrete_category.has_coe_to_sort", "description": "Provide a coercion to `Type u` for a concrete category. This is not marked as an instance\nas it could potentially apply to every type, and so is too expensive in typeclass search.\n\nYou can use it on particular examples as:\n```lean\ninstance : has_coe_to_sort X := concrete_category.has_coe_to_sort X\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_forget\u2082", "description": "`has_forget\u2082 C D`, where `C` and `D` are both concrete categories, provides a functor\n`forget\u2082 C D : C \u2964 D` and a proof that `forget\u2082 \u22d9 (forget D) = forget C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_forget\u2082.forget\u2082", "description": "`has_forget\u2082 C D`, where `C` and `D` are both concrete categories, provides a functor\n`forget\u2082 C D : C \u2964 D` and a proof that `forget\u2082 \u22d9 (forget D) = forget C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_forget\u2082.forget_comp", "description": "`has_forget\u2082 C D`, where `C` and `D` are both concrete categories, provides a functor\n`forget\u2082 C D : C \u2964 D` and a proof that `forget\u2082 \u22d9 (forget D) = forget C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.forget\u2082_preserves_monomorphisms", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.comp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.forget\u2082", "description": " The forgetful functor `C \u2964 D` between concrete categories for which we have an instance\n`has_forget\u2082 C `.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.concrete_category.surjective_of_epi_of_preserves_pushout", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_forget_to_Type", "description": "Every forgetful functor factors through the identity functor. This is not a global instance as\n   it is prone to creating type class resolution loops.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_forget\u2082.mk'", "description": "In order to construct a \u201cpartially forgetting\u201d functor, we do not need to verify functor laws;\nit suffices to ensure that compositions agree with `forget\u2082 C D \u22d9 forget D = forget C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coe_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.concrete_category.congr_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.id_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.congr_hom", "description": "Analogue of `congr_fun h x`,\nwhen `h : f = g` is an equality between morphisms in a concrete category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.concrete_category.epi_iff_surjective_of_preserves_pushout", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.concrete_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.forget_obj_eq_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.full_subcategory.has_forget\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.forget\u2082_faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.concrete_category.has_coe_to_fun_Type", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.forget", "description": "The forgetful functor from a concrete category to `Type u`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.induced_category.concrete_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.forget\u2082_preserves_epimorphisms", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.concrete_category.hom_ext", "description": "In any concrete category, we can test equality of morphisms by pointwise evaluations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.concrete_category.types", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.concrete_category.bijective_of_is_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.concrete_category.mono_iff_injective_of_preserves_pullback", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.concrete_category.injective_of_mono_of_preserves_pullback", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.concrete_category.has_coe_to_fun", "description": " Usually a bundled hom structure already has a coercion to function\nthat works with different universes. So we don't use this as a global instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.concrete_category.congr_arg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.forget_map_eq_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coe_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.concrete_category", "description": "A concrete category is a category `C` with a fixed faithful functor `forget : C \u2964 Type`.\n\nNote that `concrete_category` potentially depends on three independent universe levels,\n* the universe level `w` appearing in `forget : C \u2964 Type w`\n* the universe level `v` of the morphisms (i.e. we have a `category.{v} C`)\n* the universe level `u` of the objects (i.e `C : Type u`)\nThey are specified that order, to avoid unnecessary universe annotations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.concrete_category.forget", "description": "A concrete category is a category `C` with a fixed faithful functor `forget : C \u2964 Type`.\n\nNote that `concrete_category` potentially depends on three independent universe levels,\n* the universe level `w` appearing in `forget : C \u2964 Type w`\n* the universe level `v` of the morphisms (i.e. we have a `category.{v} C`)\n* the universe level `u` of the objects (i.e `C : Type u`)\nThey are specified that order, to avoid unnecessary universe annotations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.concrete_category.forget_faithful", "description": "A concrete category is a category `C` with a fixed faithful functor `forget : C \u2964 Type`.\n\nNote that `concrete_category` potentially depends on three independent universe levels,\n* the universe level `w` appearing in `forget : C \u2964 Type w`\n* the universe level `v` of the morphisms (i.e. we have a `category.{v} C`)\n* the universe level `u` of the objects (i.e `C : Type u`)\nThey are specified that order, to avoid unnecessary universe annotations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.concrete_category.mono_of_injective", "description": "In any concrete category, injective morphisms are monomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.eval\u2082_reflect_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.rev_at_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reflect", "description": " `reflect N f` is the polynomial such that `(reflect N f).coeff i = f.coeff (rev_at N i)`.\nIn other words, the terms with exponent `[0, ..., N]` now have exponent `[N, ..., 0]`.\n\nIn practice, `reflect` is only used when `N` is at least as large as the degree of `f`.\n\nEventually, it will be used with `N` exactly equal to the degree of `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reflect_C_mul_X_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_eq_reverse_nat_degree_add_nat_trailing_degree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reflect_mul_induction", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reflect_monomial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reverse_trailing_coeff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reflect_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reflect_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reverse_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.rev_at_invol", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.rev_at", "description": " If `i \u2264 N`, then `rev_at N i` returns `N - i`, otherwise it returns `i`.\nEssentially, this embedding is only used for `i \u2264 N`.\nThe advantage of `rev_at N i` over `N - i` is that `rev_at` is an involution.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.rev_at_fun_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.trailing_coeff_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.rev_at_fun_eq", "description": "We prefer to use the bundled `rev_at` over unbundled `rev_at_fun`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reflect_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reverse_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.eval\u2082_reflect_mul_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reverse_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.rev_at_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_zero_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reflect_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.rev_at_fun", "description": " If `i \u2264 N`, then `rev_at_fun N i` returns `N - i`, otherwise it returns `i`.\nThis is the map used by the embedding `rev_at`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reverse_nat_degree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reflect_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.eval\u2082_reverse_mul_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reverse_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reverse_mul_of_domain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reflect_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reflect_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reverse", "description": " The reverse of a polynomial f is the polynomial obtained by \"reading f backwards\".\nEven though this is not the actual definition, reverse f = f (1/X) * X ^ f.nat_degree.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reflect_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reverse_nat_trailing_degree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.eval\u2082_reverse_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.rev_at_fun_invol", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.rev_at_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reverse_nat_degree_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reverse_leading_coeff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.reflect_C_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_one_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.tendsto_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.continuous_remove_nth", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.continuous_insert_nth", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nhds_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.continuous_insert_nth'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.tendsto_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nhds_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.continuous_at_length", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.tendsto_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.tendsto_insert_nth", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.tendsto_insert_nth'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.topological_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.tendsto_prod", "description": "", "p": "mat