vd_gcd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_bezout.tfae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_bezout.gcd_eq_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_bezout.gcd", "description": "The gcd of two elements in a bezout domain.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_bezout.gcd_dvd_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_Ioc_succ_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_Ico_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_Ico_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_range_add_one_eq_factorial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_range_succ_div_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_Ico_eq_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_Ico_div_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_Ico_eq_sum_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_Ico_Ico_comm", "description": "The two ways of summing over `(i,j)` in the range `a<=i<=j<b` are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_Ioc_succ_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_range_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_Ico_eq_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_Ioc_consecutive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_Ico_id_eq_factorial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_range_id_mul_two", "description": "Gauss' summation formula", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_Ico_consecutive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_Ico_sub_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_range_mul_prod_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_Ico_succ_div_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_Ico_consecutive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_eq_prod_Ico_succ_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_range_by_parts", "description": "**Summation by parts** for ranges", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_Ico_add'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_Ico_by_parts", "description": "**Summation by parts**, also known as **Abel's lemma** or an **Abel transformation**", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_range_sub_sum_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_Ico_eq_mul_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_range_id", "description": "Gauss' summation formula", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_Ico_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_range_succ_div_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_Ico_eq_prod_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_eq_sum_Ico_succ_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_Ioc_consecutive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_Ico_succ_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_Ico_eq_add_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_Ico_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_range_succ_sub_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_Ico_add'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_range_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_Ico_succ_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_range_sub_prod_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_range_add_sum_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_Ico_succ_sub_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_range_succ_sub_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.by_contra'", "description": "If the target of the main goal is a proposition `p`,\n`by_contra'` reduces the goal to proving `false` using the additional hypothesis `h : \u00ac p`.\n`by_contra' h` can be used to name the hypothesis `h : \u00ac p`.\nThe hypothesis `\u00ac p` will be negation normalized using `push_neg`.\nFor instance, `\u00ac a < b` will be changed to `b \u2264 a`.\n`by_contra' h : q` will normalize negations in `\u00ac p`, normalize negations in `q`,\nand then check that the two normalized forms are equal.\nThe resulting hypothesis is the pre-normalized form, `q`.\n\nIf the name `h` is not explicitly provided, then `this` will be used as name.\n\nThis tactic uses classical reasoning.\nIt is a variant on the tactic `by_contra` (`tactic.interactive.by_contra`).\n\nExamples:\n\n```lean\nexample : 1 < 2 :=\nbegin\n  by_contra' h,\n  -- h : 2 \u2264 1 \u22a2 false\nend\n\nexample : 1 < 2 :=\nbegin\n  by_contra' h : \u00ac 1 < 2,\n  -- h : \u00ac 1 < 2 \u22a2 false\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.by_contra'", "description": "If the target of the main goal is a proposition `p`,\n`by_contra'` reduces the goal to proving `false` using the additional hypothesis `h : \u00ac p`.\n`by_contra' h` can be used to name the hypothesis `h : \u00ac p`.\nThe hypothesis `\u00ac p` will be negation normalized using `push_neg`.\nFor instance, `\u00ac a < b` will be changed to `b \u2264 a`.\n`by_contra' h : q` will normalize negations in `\u00ac p`, normalize negations in `q`,\nand then check that the two normalized forms are equal.\nThe resulting hypothesis is the pre-normalized form, `q`.\n\nIf the name `h` is not explicitly provided, then `this` will be used as name.\n\nThis tactic uses classical reasoning.\nIt is a variant on the tactic `by_contra` (`tactic.interactive.by_contra`).\n\nExamples:\n\n```lean\nexample : 1 < 2 :=\nbegin\n  by_contra' h,\n  -- h : 2 \u2264 1 \u22a2 false\nend\n\nexample : 1 < 2 :=\nbegin\n  by_contra' h : \u00ac 1 < 2,\n  -- h : \u00ac 1 < 2 \u22a2 false\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.nat_predicate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.solution_greatest", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.nat_predicate.eq_imp_1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.problem_predicate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.problem_predicate.m_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.problem_predicate.n_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.problem_predicate.eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.problem_predicate.eq_imp_1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.specified_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.problem_predicate_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.m_n_bounds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.k_bound", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.nat_predicate.n_le_N", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.solution_bound", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.nat_predicate.reduction", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.nat_predicate.m_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.nat_predicate.n_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.problem_predicate.reduction", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.problem_predicate.m_le_n", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.nat_predicate.imp_fib", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1981_q3.nat_predicate.m_le_n", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "ring_of_integers.norm_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_of_integers.is_unit_norm_of_is_galois", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_of_integers.norm_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_of_integers.norm_algebra_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_of_integers.coe_algebra_map_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_of_integers.is_unit_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_of_integers.dvd_norm", "description": " If `L/K` is a finite Galois extension of fields, then, for all `(x : \ud835\udcde L)` we have that\n`x \u2223 algebra_map (\ud835\udcde K) (\ud835\udcde L) (norm K x)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_of_integers.norm", "description": "`algebra.norm` as a morphism betwen the rings of integers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_of_integers.coe_norm_algebra_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preserves_limits_preadditive_yoneda.obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preserves_limits_preadditive_coyoneda_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preserves_limits_preadditive_coyoneda.obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preserves_limits_preadditive_yoneda_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.supports_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_action.supports_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.supports.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.supports.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_action.supports", "description": "A set `s` supports `b` if `g +\u1d65 b = b` whenever `g +\u1d65 a = a` for all `a \u2208 s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.supports", "description": "A set `s` supports `b` if `g \u2022 b = b` whenever `g \u2022 a = a` for all `a \u2208 s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_action.supports.vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_action.supports.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cokleisli.adjunction.to_cokleisli", "description": "The right adjoint of the adjunction which induces the comonad `(U, \u03b5_ U, \u03b4_ U)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.kleisli.adjunction.to_kleisli_comp_from_kleisli_iso_self", "description": "The composition of the adjunction gives the original functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cokleisli.adjunction.to_cokleisli_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cokleisli.adjunction.from_cokleisli_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.kleisli.adjunction.to_kleisli_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cokleisli.adjunction.from_cokleisli", "description": "The left adjoint of the adjunction which induces the comonad `(U, \u03b5_ U, \u03b4_ U)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cokleisli.adjunction.from_cokleisli_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cokleisli.adjunction.adj", "description": "The co-Kleisli adjunction which gives rise to the monad `(U, \u03b5_ U, \u03b4_ U)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cokleisli.adjunction.to_cokleisli_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.kleisli.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.kleisli.adjunction.from_kleisli", "description": "The right adjoint of the adjunction which induces the monad `(T, \u03b7_ T, \u03bc_ T)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.kleisli.adjunction.from_kleisli_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.kleisli.adjunction.adj", "description": "The Kleisli adjunction which gives rise to the monad `(T, \u03b7_ T, \u03bc_ T)`.\n   cf Lemma 5.2.11 of [Riehl][riehl2017].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.kleisli.adjunction.to_kleisli_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cokleisli.adjunction.to_cokleisli_comp_from_cokleisli_iso_self", "description": "The composition of the adjunction gives the original functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.kleisli.adjunction.from_kleisli_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cokleisli.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.kleisli.kleisli.category", "description": "The Kleisli category on a monad `T`.\n   cf Definition 5.2.9 in [Riehl][riehl2017].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cokleisli.cokleisli.category", "description": "The co-Kleisli category on a comonad `U`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.kleisli", "description": "The objects for the Kleisli category of the monad `T : monad C`, which are the same\nthing as objects of the base category `C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.kleisli.adjunction.to_kleisli", "description": "The left adjoint of the adjunction which induces the monad `(T, \u03b7_ T, \u03bc_ T)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cokleisli", "description": "The objects for the co-Kleisli category of the comonad `U : monad C`, which are the same\nthing as objects of the base category `C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurability", "description": "User attribute used to mark tactics used by `measurability`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.apply_measurable.comp_ae_measurable", "description": "Tactic to apply `measurable.comp_ae_measurable` when appropriate.\n\nApplying `measurable.comp_ae_measurable` is not always a good idea, so we have some\nextra logic here to try to avoid bad cases.\n\n* If the function we're trying to prove measurable is actually\n  constant, and that constant is a function application `f z`, then\n  `measurable.comp_ae_measurable` would produce new goals `measurable f`, `ae_measurable\n  (\u03bb _, z) \u03bc`, which is silly. We avoid this by failing if we could\n  apply `ae_measurable_const`.\n\n* `measurable.comp_ae_measurable` will always succeed on `ae_measurable (\u03bb x, f x) \u03bc` and\n  can produce new goals (`measurable (\u03bb x, x)`, `ae_measurable f \u03bc`) or\n  (`measurable f`, `ae_measurable (\u03bb x, x) \u03bc`). We detect those by failing if a new goal can be\n  closed by applying `measurable_id` or `ae_measurable_id`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.goal_is_not_measurable", "description": "We don't want the intro1 tactic to apply to a goal of the form `measurable f`, `ae_measurable f \u03bc`\nor `measurable_set s`. This tactic tests the target to see if it matches that form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.measurability", "description": "Solve goals of the form `measurable f`, `ae_measurable f \u03bc`, `ae_strongly_measurable f \u03bc` or\n`measurable_set s`. `measurability?` reports back the proof term it found.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.measurability / measurability'", "description": "`measurability` solves goals of the form `measurable f`, `ae_measurable f \u03bc`,\n`ae_strongly_measurable f \u03bc` or `measurable_set s` by applying lemmas tagged with the\n`measurability` user attribute.\n\nYou can also use `measurability!`, which applies lemmas with `{ md := semireducible }`.\nThe default behaviour is more conservative, and only unfolds `reducible` definitions\nwhen attempting to match lemmas with the goal.\n\n`measurability?` reports back the proof term it found.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.apply_measurable.comp", "description": "Tactic to apply `measurable.comp` when appropriate.\n\nApplying `measurable.comp` is not always a good idea, so we have some\nextra logic here to try to avoid bad cases.\n\n* If the function we're trying to prove measurable is actually\n  constant, and that constant is a function application `f z`, then\n  measurable.comp would produce new goals `measurable f`, `measurable\n  (\u03bb _, z)`, which is silly. We avoid this by failing if we could\n  apply `measurable_const`.\n\n* measurable.comp will always succeed on `measurable (\u03bb x, f x)` and\n  produce new goals `measurable (\u03bb x, x)`, `measurable f`. We detect\n  this by failing if a new goal can be closed by applying\n  measurable_id.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.measurability'", "description": "Version of `measurability` for use with auto_param.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.measurability_tactics", "description": " List of tactics used by `measurability` internally. The option `use_exfalso := ff` is passed to\nthe tactic `apply_assumption` in order to avoid loops in the presence of negated hypotheses in\nthe context.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear_coyoneda_faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear_yoneda", "description": " The Yoneda embedding for `R`-linear categories `C`,\nsending an object `X : C` to the `Module R`-valued presheaf on `C`,\nwith value on `Y : C\u1d52\u1d56` given by `Module.of R (unop Y \u27f6 X)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear_coyoneda_full", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whiskering_linear_yoneda", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear_yoneda_map_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whiskering_linear_coyoneda", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear_coyoneda_map_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear_yoneda_obj_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear_coyoneda_obj_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear_yoneda_obj_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear_yoneda_faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear_yoneda_full", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whiskering_linear_coyoneda\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear_coyoneda_obj_additive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear_coyoneda", "description": " The Yoneda embedding for `R`-linear categories `C`,\nsending an object `Y : C\u1d52\u1d56` to the `Module R`-valued copresheaf on `C`,\nwith value on `X : C` given by `Module.of R (unop Y \u27f6 X)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whiskering_linear_yoneda\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear_coyoneda_obj_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear_yoneda_obj_additive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "d_array.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "array.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_multiset_prod_of_monic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_multiset_sum_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.degree_multiset_prod_le", "description": "The degree of a product of polynomials is at most the sum of the degrees,\nwhere the degree of the zero polynomial is \u22a5.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.degree_list_sum_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.degree_prod", "description": "The degree of a product of polynomials is equal to\nthe sum of the degrees, where the degree of the zero polynomial is \u22a5.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_prod_of_monic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_multiset_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_prod'", "description": "The degree of a product of polynomials is equal to\nthe sum of the degrees, provided that the product of leading coefficients is nonzero.\n\nSee `polynomial.nat_degree_prod` (without the `'`) for a version for integral domains,\nwhere this condition is automatically satisfied.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_list_prod_of_nat_degree_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.prod_X_sub_C_coeff_card_pred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.degree_prod_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_zero_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.leading_coeff_prod", "description": "The leading coefficient of a product of polynomials is equal to\nthe product of the leading coefficients.\n\nSee `polynomial.leading_coeff_prod'` (with a `'`) for a version for commutative semirings,\nwhere additionally, the product of the leading coefficients must be nonzero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_multiset_prod'", "description": "The degree of a product of polynomials is equal to\nthe sum of the degrees, provided that the product of leading coefficients is nonzero.\n\nSee `polynomial.nat_degree_multiset_prod` (without the `'`) for a version for integral domains,\nwhere this condition is automatically satisfied.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.degree_list_prod_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_multiset_prod_of_nat_degree_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_multiset_prod_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.prod_X_sub_C_next_coeff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_prod_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_prod_of_nat_degree_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.multiset_prod_X_sub_C_coeff_card_pred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_sum_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.leading_coeff_prod'", "description": "The leading coefficient of a product of polynomials is equal to\nthe product of the leading coefficients, provided that this product is nonzero.\n\nSee `polynomial.leading_coeff_prod` (without the `'`) for a version for integral domains,\nwhere this condition is automatically satisfied.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.leading_coeff_multiset_prod'", "description": "The leading coefficient of a product of polynomials is equal to\nthe product of the leading coefficients, provided that this product is nonzero.\n\nSee `polynomial.leading_coeff_multiset_prod` (without the `'`) for a version for integral domains,\nwhere this condition is automatically satisfied.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.multiset_prod_X_sub_C_next_coeff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.leading_coeff_multiset_prod", "description": "The leading coefficient of a product of polynomials is equal to\nthe product of the leading coefficients.\n\nSee `polynomial.leading_coeff_multiset_prod'` (with a `'`) for a version for commutative semirings,\nwhere additionally, the product of the leading coefficients must be nonzero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.degree_list_prod", "description": "The degree of a product of polynomials is equal to\nthe sum of the degrees, where the degree of the zero polynomial is \u22a5.\n`[nontrivial R]` is needed, otherwise for `l = []` we have `\u22a5` in the LHS and `0` in the RHS.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_list_sum_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_prod", "description": "The degree of a product of polynomials is equal to\nthe sum of the degrees.\n\nSee `polynomial.nat_degree_prod'` (with a `'`) for a version for commutative semirings,\nwhere additionally, the product of the leading coefficients must be nonzero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.degree_multiset_prod", "description": "The degree of a product of polynomials is equal to\nthe sum of the degrees, where the degree of the zero polynomial is \u22a5.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_zero_multiset_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_list_prod_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.enumerate_eq_none_of_sel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.enumerate_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.enumerate_eq_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.enumerate_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.enumerate", "description": " Given a choice function `sel`, enumerates the elements of a set in the order\n`a 0 = sel s`, `a 1 = sel (s \\ {a 0})`, `a 2 = sel (s \\ {a 0, a 1})`, ... and stops when\n`sel (s \\ {a 0, ..., a n}) = none`. Note that we don't require `sel` to be a choice function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.back_lemmas_find", "description": "Return the lemmas that have the same head symbol of the given expression", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.back_chaining_using_hs", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.back_lemmas_add_extra", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.back_lemmas_insert_core", "description": "(back_lemmas_insert_core m lemmas lemma) adds the given lemma to the set back_lemmas.\n  It infers the type of the lemma, and uses its head-symbol as an index.\n  The head-symbol is computed with respect to the given transparency setting.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.back_chaining", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_back_lemmas_core", "description": "Create a datastructure containing all lemmas tagged as [intro].\n  Lemmas are indexed using their head-symbol.\n  The head-symbol is computed with respect to the given transparency setting.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.back_lemmas", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.back_chaining_using", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.back_lemmas_insert", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.back_chaining_core", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_back_lemmas", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.backward_chaining_core", "description": "(backward_chaining_core t insts max_depth pre_tactic leaf_tactic lemmas): perform backward chaining using\n  the lemmas marked as [intro] and extra_lemmas.\n\n  The search maximum depth is \\c max_depth.\n\n  Before processing each goal, the tactic pre_tactic is invoked. The possible outcomes are:\n     1) it closes the goal\n     2) it does nothing, and backward_chaining_core tries applicable lemmas.\n     3) it fails, and backward_chaining_core backtracks.\n\n  Whenever no lemma is applicable, the leaf_tactic is invoked, to try to close the goal.\n  If insts is tt, then type class resolution is used to discharge goals.\n\n  Remark pre_tactic may also be used to trace the execution of backward_chaining_core", "p": "core"}, {"name": "set.singleton_unop_op", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.unop_mem_unop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_op", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.unop_op", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.op_equiv_self_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_unop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.op_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.singleton_unop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.op_equiv", "description": "Taking opposites as an equivalence of powersets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.singleton_op_unop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.op_unop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.singleton_op", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.op_equiv_self_symm_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.op_equiv_self", "description": "The members of the opposite of a set are in bijection with the members of the set itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.unop", "description": "The unop of a set `s` is the set obtained by taking the unop of each member of `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.op", "description": "The opposite of a set `s` is the set obtained by taking the opposite of each member of `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.op_mem_op", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.op_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "counterexample.foo", "description": "The three element monoid.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.foo.zero", "description": "The three element monoid.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.foo.eps", "description": "The three element monoid.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.foo.one", "description": "The three element monoid.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.foo.aux1_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.foo.aux1", "description": "The order on `foo` is the one induced by the natural order on the image of `aux1`.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.foo.has_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.foo.linear_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.foo.linear_ordered_comm_monoid_with_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.foo.boom", "description": "A tactic to prove facts by cases.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.foo.has_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.foo.comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.foo.not_mul_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.foo.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.foo.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.foo.mul", "description": "Multiplication on `foo`: the only external input is that `\u03b5 ^ 2 = 0`.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "dimH", "description": "Hausdorff dimension of a set in an (e)metric space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hausdorff_measure_of_dimH_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dimH_Union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.dimH_of_nonempty_interior", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lipschitz_with.dimH_range_le", "description": " If `f` is a Lipschitz continuous map, then the Hausdorff dimension of its range is at most the\nHausdorff dimension of its domain.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_mem_nhds_within_lt_dimH_of_lt_dimH", "description": " If `r` is less than the Hausdorff dimension of a set `s` in an (extended) metric space with\nsecond countable topology, then there exists a point `x \u2208 s` such that every neighborhood\n`t` of `x` within `s` has Hausdorff dimension greater than `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "isometry_equiv.dimH_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dimH_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dimH_image_le_of_locally_holder_on", "description": " If `s` is a set in a space `X` with second countable topology and `f : X \u2192 Y` is H\u00f6lder\ncontinuous in a neighborhood within `s` of every point `x \u2208 s` with the same positive exponent `r`\nbut possibly different coefficients, then the Hausdorff dimension of the image `f '' s` is at most\nthe Hausdorff dimension of `s` divided by `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.dimH_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bsupr_limsup_dimH", "description": " In an (extended) metric space with second countable topology, the Hausdorff dimension\nof a set `s` is the supremum over `x \u2208 s` of the limit superiors of `dimH t` along\n`(\ud835\udcdd[s] x).small_sets`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.dimH_univ_eq_finrank", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.countable.dimH_zero", "description": "**Alias** of `dimH_countable`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dimH_def", "description": " Unfold the definition of `dimH` using `[measurable_space X] [borel_space X]` from the\nenvironment.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.dimH_ball_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_with.dimH_image_le", "description": " If `f : X \u2192 Y` is H\u00f6lder continuous with a positive exponent `r`, then the Hausdorff dimension\nof the image of a set `s` is at most `dimH s / r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dimH_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "isometry_equiv.dimH_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dimH_of_hausdorff_measure_ne_zero_ne_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dimH_range_le_of_locally_holder_on", "description": " If `f : X \u2192 Y` is H\u00f6lder continuous in a neighborhood of every point `x : X` with the same\npositive exponent `r` but possibly different coefficients, then the Hausdorff dimension of the range\nof `f` is at most the Hausdorff dimension of `X` divided by `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.dimH_univ_pi_fin", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dimH_sUnion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dimH_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.dimH_ball_pi_fin", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dense_compl_of_dimH_lt_finrank", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite.dimH_zero", "description": "**Alias** of `dimH_finite`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.dimH_univ_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dimH_image_le_of_locally_lipschitz_on", "description": " If `s` is a set in an extended metric space `X` with second countable topology and `f : X \u2192 Y`\nis Lipschitz in a neighborhood within `s` of every point `x \u2208 s`, then the Hausdorff dimension of\nthe image `f '' s` is at most the Hausdorff dimension of `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dimH_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.dimH_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "isometry_equiv.dimH_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lipschitz_with.dimH_image_le", "description": "If `f` is a Lipschitz continuous map, then `dimH (f '' s) \u2264 dimH s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_dimH_of_hausdorff_measure_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dimH_finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_dimH_of_hausdorff_measure_eq_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_with.dimH_range_le", "description": " If `f` is a H\u00f6lder continuous map with exponent `r > 0`, then the Hausdorff dimension of its\nrange is at most the Hausdorff dimension of its domain divided by `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antilipschitz_with.dimH_preimage_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.dense_compl_range_of_finrank_lt_finrank", "description": " A particular case of Sard's Theorem. If `f` is a `C\u00b9` smooth map from a real vector space to a\nreal vector space `F` of strictly larger dimension, then the complement of the range of `f` is dense\nin `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "holder_on_with.dimH_image_le", "description": "If `f` is a H\u00f6lder continuous map with exponent `r > 0`, then `dimH (f '' s) \u2264 dimH s / r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.dimH_image_le", "description": " Let `f` be a function defined on a finite dimensional real normed space. If `f` is `C\u00b9`-smooth\non a convex set `s`, then the Hausdorff dimension of `f '' s` is less than or equal to the Hausdorff\ndimension of `s`.\n\nTODO: do we actually need `convex \u211d s`?", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "isometry.dimH_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.dimH_zero", "description": "**Alias** of `dimH_coe_finset`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antilipschitz_with.le_dimH_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dimH_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_on.dense_compl_image_of_dimH_lt_finrank", "description": " A particular case of Sard's Theorem. Let `f : E \u2192 F` be a map between finite dimensional real\nvector spaces. Suppose that `f` is `C\u00b9` smooth on a convex set `s` of Hausdorff dimension strictly\nless than the dimension of `F`. Then the complement of the image `f '' s` is dense in `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dimH_countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dimH_coe_finset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dimH_bUnion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.dimH_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dimH_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hausdorff_measure_of_lt_dimH", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dimH_range_le_of_locally_lipschitz_on", "description": " If `f : X \u2192 Y` is Lipschitz in a neighborhood of each point `x : X`, then the Hausdorff\ndimension of `range f` is at most the Hausdorff dimension of `X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_zero_of_dimH_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.dimH_of_mem_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "supr_limsup_dimH", "description": " In an (extended) metric space with second countable topology, the Hausdorff dimension\nof a set `s` is the supremum over all `x` of the limit superiors of `dimH t` along\n`(\ud835\udcdd[s] x).small_sets`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dimH_le_of_hausdorff_measure_ne_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.dimH_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff.dimH_range_le", "description": " The Hausdorff dimension of the range of a `C\u00b9`-smooth function defined on a finite dimensional\nreal normed space is at most the dimension of its domain as a vector space over `\u211d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lipschitz_on_with.dimH_image_le", "description": "If `f : X \u2192 Y` is Lipschitz continuous on `s`, then `dimH (f '' s) \u2264 dimH s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.subsingleton.dimH_zero", "description": "**Alias** of `dimH_subsingleton`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.coprime_prod_left", "description": "See `is_coprime.prod_left` for the corresponding lemma about `is_coprime`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.coprime_prod_right", "description": "See `is_coprime.prod_right` for the corresponding lemma about `is_coprime`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conv.interactive.slice", "description": "`slice` is a conv tactic; if the current focus is a composition of several morphisms,\n`slice a b` reassociates as needed, and zooms in on the `a`-th through `b`-th morphisms.\n\nThus if the current focus is `(a \u226b b) \u226b ((c \u226b d) \u226b e)`, then `slice 2 3` zooms to `b \u226b c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conv.repeat_with_results", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conv.slice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.slice_lhs", "description": "`slice_lhs a b { tac }` zooms to the left hand side, uses associativity for categorical\ncomposition as needed, zooms in on the `a`-th through `b`-th morphisms, and invokes `tac`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.slice", "description": "`slice_lhs a b { tac }` zooms to the left hand side, uses associativity for categorical\ncomposition as needed, zooms in on the `a`-th through `b`-th morphisms, and invokes `tac`.\n\n`slice_rhs a b { tac }` zooms to the right hand side, uses associativity for categorical\ncomposition as needed, zooms in on the `a`-th through `b`-th morphisms, and invokes `tac`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conv.slice_lhs", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conv.slice_rhs", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.repeat_count", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.slice_rhs", "description": "`slice_rhs a b { tac }` zooms to the right hand side, uses associativity for categorical\ncomposition as needed, zooms in on the `a`-th through `b`-th morphisms, and invokes `tac`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.repeat_with_results", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conv.repeat_count", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Quiv.adj", "description": "The adjunction between forming the free category on a quiver, and forgetting a category to a quiver.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.free_map_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Quiv.forget_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Quiv.forget_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Quiv", "description": "Category of quivers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Quiv.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.free_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Quiv.lift", "description": "Any prefunctor into a category lifts to a functor from the path category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Quiv.lift_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.free_map_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.free", "description": "The functor sending each quiver to its path category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Quiv.of", "description": "Construct a bundled `Quiv` from the underlying type and the typeclass.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Quiv.lift_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Quiv.has_coe_to_sort", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Quiv.forget", "description": "The forgetful functor from categories to quivers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Quiv.str", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Quiv.category", "description": "Category structure on `Quiv`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "diff_to_ideal_of_quotient_comp_eq_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "derivation_to_square_zero_of_lift_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "derivation_to_square_zero_of_lift", "description": " Given a tower of algebras `R \u2192 A \u2192 B`, and a square-zero `I : ideal B`, each lift `A \u2192\u2090[R] B`\nof the canonical map `A \u2192\u2090[R] B \u29f8 I` corresponds to a `R`-derivation from `A` to `I`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "derivation_to_square_zero_equiv_lift", "description": " Given a tower of algebras `R \u2192 A \u2192 B`, and a square-zero `I : ideal B`,\nthere is a 1-1 correspondance between `R`-derivations from `A` to `I` and\nlifts `A \u2192\u2090[R] B` of the canonical map `A \u2192\u2090[R] B \u29f8 I`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "derivation_to_square_zero_equiv_lift_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lift_of_derivation_to_square_zero_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lift_of_derivation_to_square_zero", "description": " Given a tower of algebras `R \u2192 A \u2192 B`, and a square-zero `I : ideal B`, each `R`-derivation\nfrom `A` to `I` corresponds to a lift `A \u2192\u2090[R] B` of the canonical map `A \u2192\u2090[R] B \u29f8 I`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "diff_to_ideal_of_quotient_comp_eq", "description": "If `f\u2081 f\u2082 : A \u2192\u2090[R] B` are two lifts of the same `A \u2192\u2090[R] B \u29f8 I`,\n we may define a map `f\u2081 - f\u2082 : A \u2192\u2097[R] I`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "derivation_to_square_zero_equiv_lift_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lift_of_derivation_to_square_zero_mk_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mk_definition", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration.type", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration.to_name", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration.value_task", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration.update_value_task", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration.is_definition", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration.to_definition", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration.update_name", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration.instantiate_value_univ_params", "description": "Instantiate a universe polymorphic declaration value with the given universes.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration.map_value", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration.update_value", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration.value", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration.is_trusted", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration", "description": "Reflect a C++ declaration object. The VM replaces it with the C++ implementation.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration.defn", "description": "Reflect a C++ declaration object. The VM replaces it with the C++ implementation.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration.thm", "description": "Reflect a C++ declaration object. The VM replaces it with the C++ implementation.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration.cnst", "description": "Reflect a C++ declaration object. The VM replaces it with the C++ implementation.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration.ax", "description": "Reflect a C++ declaration object. The VM replaces it with the C++ implementation.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration.update_type", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reducibility_hints", "description": "Reducibility hints are used in the convertibility checker.\nWhen trying to solve a constraint such a\n\n    (f ...) =?= (g ...)\n\nwhere `f` and `g` are definitions, the checker has to decide which one will be unfolded.\n* If      `f` (`g`) is opaque,     then `g` (`f`) is unfolded if it is also not marked as opaque,\n* Else if `f` (`g`) is abbrev,     then `f` (`g`) is unfolded if `g` (`f`) is also not marked as abbrev,\n* Else if `f` and `g` are regular, then we unfold the one with the biggest definitional height.\n* Otherwise both are unfolded.\n\nThe arguments of the `regular` constructor are: the definitional height and the flag `self_opt`.\n\nThe definitional height is by default computed by the kernel. It only takes into account\nother regular definitions used in a definition. When creating declarations using meta-programming,\nwe can specify the definitional depth manually.\n\nFor definitions marked as regular, we also have a hint for constraints such as\n\n    (f a) =?= (f b)\n\nif `self_opt = tt`, then checker will first try to solve `a =?= b`, only if it fails,\nit unfolds `f`.\n\nRemark: the hint only affects performance. None of the hints prevent the kernel from unfolding a\ndeclaration during type checking.\n\nRemark: the reducibility_hints are not related to the attributes: reducible/irrelevance/semireducible.\nThese attributes are used by the elaborator. The reducibility_hints are used by the kernel (and elaborator).\nMoreover, the reducibility_hints cannot be changed after a declaration is added to the kernel.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reducibility_hints.opaque", "description": "Reducibility hints are used in the convertibility checker.\nWhen trying to solve a constraint such a\n\n    (f ...) =?= (g ...)\n\nwhere `f` and `g` are definitions, the checker has to decide which one will be unfolded.\n* If      `f` (`g`) is opaque,     then `g` (`f`) is unfolded if it is also not marked as opaque,\n* Else if `f` (`g`) is abbrev,     then `f` (`g`) is unfolded if `g` (`f`) is also not marked as abbrev,\n* Else if `f` and `g` are regular, then we unfold the one with the biggest definitional height.\n* Otherwise both are unfolded.\n\nThe arguments of the `regular` constructor are: the definitional height and the flag `self_opt`.\n\nThe definitional height is by default computed by the kernel. It only takes into account\nother regular definitions used in a definition. When creating declarations using meta-programming,\nwe can specify the definitional depth manually.\n\nFor definitions marked as regular, we also have a hint for constraints such as\n\n    (f a) =?= (f b)\n\nif `self_opt = tt`, then checker will first try to solve `a =?= b`, only if it fails,\nit unfolds `f`.\n\nRemark: the hint only affects performance. None of the hints prevent the kernel from unfolding a\ndeclaration during type checking.\n\nRemark: the reducibility_hints are not related to the attributes: reducible/irrelevance/semireducible.\nThese attributes are used by the elaborator. The reducibility_hints are used by the kernel (and elaborator).\nMoreover, the reducibility_hints cannot be changed after a declaration is added to the kernel.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reducibility_hints.abbrev", "description": "Reducibility hints are used in the convertibility checker.\nWhen trying to solve a constraint such a\n\n    (f ...) =?= (g ...)\n\nwhere `f` and `g` are definitions, the checker has to decide which one will be unfolded.\n* If      `f` (`g`) is opaque,     then `g` (`f`) is unfolded if it is also not marked as opaque,\n* Else if `f` (`g`) is abbrev,     then `f` (`g`) is unfolded if `g` (`f`) is also not marked as abbrev,\n* Else if `f` and `g` are regular, then we unfold the one with the biggest definitional height.\n* Otherwise both are unfolded.\n\nThe arguments of the `regular` constructor are: the definitional height and the flag `self_opt`.\n\nThe definitional height is by default computed by the kernel. It only takes into account\nother regular definitions used in a definition. When creating declarations using meta-programming,\nwe can specify the definitional depth manually.\n\nFor definitions marked as regular, we also have a hint for constraints such as\n\n    (f a) =?= (f b)\n\nif `self_opt = tt`, then checker will first try to solve `a =?= b`, only if it fails,\nit unfolds `f`.\n\nRemark: the hint only affects performance. None of the hints prevent the kernel from unfolding a\ndeclaration during type checking.\n\nRemark: the reducibility_hints are not related to the attributes: reducible/irrelevance/semireducible.\nThese attributes are used by the elaborator. The reducibility_hints are used by the kernel (and elaborator).\nMoreover, the reducibility_hints cannot be changed after a declaration is added to the kernel.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reducibility_hints.regular", "description": "Reducibility hints are used in the convertibility checker.\nWhen trying to solve a constraint such a\n\n    (f ...) =?= (g ...)\n\nwhere `f` and `g` are definitions, the checker has to decide which one will be unfolded.\n* If      `f` (`g`) is opaque,     then `g` (`f`) is unfolded if it is also not marked as opaque,\n* Else if `f` (`g`) is abbrev,     then `f` (`g`) is unfolded if `g` (`f`) is also not marked as abbrev,\n* Else if `f` and `g` are regular, then we unfold the one with the biggest definitional height.\n* Otherwise both are unfolded.\n\nThe arguments of the `regular` constructor are: the definitional height and the flag `self_opt`.\n\nThe definitional height is by default computed by the kernel. It only takes into account\nother regular definitions used in a definition. When creating declarations using meta-programming,\nwe can specify the definitional depth manually.\n\nFor definitions marked as regular, we also have a hint for constraints such as\n\n    (f a) =?= (f b)\n\nif `self_opt = tt`, then checker will first try to solve `a =?= b`, only if it fails,\nit unfolds `f`.\n\nRemark: the hint only affects performance. None of the hints prevent the kernel from unfolding a\ndeclaration during type checking.\n\nRemark: the reducibility_hints are not related to the attributes: reducible/irrelevance/semireducible.\nThese attributes are used by the elaborator. The reducibility_hints are used by the kernel (and elaborator).\nMoreover, the reducibility_hints cannot be changed after a declaration is added to the kernel.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration.instantiate_type_univ_params", "description": "Instantiate a universe polymorphic declaration type with the given universes.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "declaration.univ_params", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "category_theory.braiding_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.braiding_inv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.types_symmetric", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_aut.smul_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_aut.group", "description": "The group operation on automorphisms of a ring is defined by\n`\u03bb g h, ring_equiv.trans h g`.\nThis means that multiplication agrees with composition, `(g*h)(x) = g (h x)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_aut.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_aut.apply_has_faithful_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_semiring_action.to_ring_aut_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_aut.apply_mul_semiring_action", "description": "The tautological action by the group of automorphism of a ring `R` on `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_aut.to_add_aut", "description": "Monoid homomorphism from ring automorphisms to additive automorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_aut.to_mul_aut", "description": "Monoid homomorphism from ring automorphisms to multiplicative automorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_aut.to_perm", "description": "Monoid homomorphism from ring automorphisms to permutations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_aut", "description": "The group of ring automorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_semiring_action.to_ring_aut", "description": " Each element of the group defines a ring automorphism.\n\nThis is a stronger version of `distrib_mul_action.to_add_aut` and\n`mul_distrib_mul_action.to_mul_aut`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_has_sizeof_instance_core", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_has_sizeof_instance", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.has_core_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.closure_has_core", "description": " For every unbounded operator `f` the submodule `f.domain` is a core of its closure.\n\nNote that we don't require that `f` is closable, due to the definition of the closure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.closure", "description": " If `f` is closable, then `f.closure` is the closure. Otherwise it is defined\nas `f.closure = f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.is_closable.closure_is_closable", "description": "If `f` is closable, then the closure is closable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.le_closure", "description": "A `linear_pmap` is contained in its closure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.is_closable.closure_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.is_closable.exists_unique", "description": "The closure is unique.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.closure_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.is_closable_iff_exists_closed_extension", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.closure_def'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.is_closable", "description": "An unbounded operator is closable iff the closure of its graph is a graph.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.has_core", "description": "A submodule `S` is a core of `f` if the closure of the restriction of `f` to `S` is again `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.has_core.le_domain", "description": "A submodule `S` is a core of `f` if the closure of the restriction of `f` to `S` is again `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.has_core.closure_eq", "description": "A submodule `S` is a core of `f` if the closure of the restriction of `f` to `S` is again `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.is_closable.le_is_closable", "description": "If `g` has a closable extension `f`, then `g` itself is closable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.is_closable.graph_closure_eq_closure_graph", "description": "The closure (as a submodule) of the graph is equal to the graph of the closure\n (as a `linear_pmap`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.is_closed.is_closable", "description": "A closed operator is trivially closable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.is_closable.closure_is_closed", "description": "If `f` is closable, then the closure is closed.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_pmap.is_closed", "description": "An unbounded operator is closed iff its graph is closed.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hyperoperation", "description": "Implementation of the hyperoperation sequence\nwhere `hyperoperation n m k` is the `n`th hyperoperation between `m` and `k`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hyperoperation_three", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hyperoperation_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hyperoperation_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hyperoperation_ge_two_eq_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hyperoperation_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hyperoperation_two_two_eq_four", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hyperoperation_ge_four_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hyperoperation_ge_three_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hyperoperation_recursion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hyperoperation_ge_three_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.second_derivative_within_at_symmetric_of_mem_interior", "description": " Assume that `f` is differentiable inside a convex set `s`, and that its derivative `f'` is\ndifferentiable at a point `x`. Then, given two vectors `v` and `w` pointing inside `s`, one\nhas `f'' v w = f'' w v`. Superseded by `convex.second_derivative_within_at_symmetric`, which\nremoves the assumption that `v` and `w` point inside `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "second_derivative_symmetric_of_eventually", "description": " If a function is differentiable around `x`, and has two derivatives at `x`, then the second\nderivative is symmetric.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.taylor_approx_two_segment", "description": " Assume that `f` is differentiable inside a convex set `s`, and that its derivative `f'` is\ndifferentiable at a point `x`. Then, given two vectors `v` and `w` pointing inside `s`, one can\nTaylor-expand to order two the function `f` on the segment `[x + h v, x + h (v + w)]`, giving a\nbilinear estimate for `f (x + hv + hw) - f (x + hv)` in terms of `f' w` and of `f'' \u2b1d w`, up to\n`o(h^2)`.\n\nThis is a technical statement used to show that the second derivative is symmetric.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "second_derivative_symmetric", "description": " If a function is differentiable, and has two derivatives at `x`, then the second\nderivative is symmetric.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.is_o_alternate_sum_square", "description": " One can get `f'' v w` as the limit of `h ^ (-2)` times the alternate sum of the values of `f`\nalong the vertices of a quadrilateral with sides `h v` and `h w` based at `x`.\nIn a setting where `f` is not guaranteed to be continuous at `f`, we can still\nget this if we use a quadrilateral based at `h v + h w`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.second_derivative_within_at_symmetric", "description": " If a function is differentiable inside a convex set with nonempty interior, and has a second\nderivative at a point of this convex set, then this second derivative is symmetric.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.vertices_subset_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.face_subset_face_iff", "description": "A face is a subset of another one iff its vertices are.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.of_erase", "description": "Construct a simplicial complex by removing the empty face for you.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.facets_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.facets", "description": "A facet of a simplicial complex is a maximal face.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.has_mem", "description": "A `finset` belongs to a `simplicial_complex` if it's a face of it.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.facets_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.convex_hull_subset_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.vertices", "description": "The vertices of a simplicial complex are its zero dimensional faces.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.vertex_mem_convex_hull_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.subset_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.mem_facets", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex", "description": " A simplicial complex in a `\ud835\udd5c`-module is a collection of simplices which glue nicely together.\nNote that the textbook meaning of \"glue nicely\" is given in\n`geometry.simplicial_complex.disjoint_or_exists_inter_eq_convex_hull`. It is mostly useless, as\n`geometry.simplicial_complex.convex_hull_inter_convex_hull` is enough for all purposes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.faces", "description": " A simplicial complex in a `\ud835\udd5c`-module is a collection of simplices which glue nicely together.\nNote that the textbook meaning of \"glue nicely\" is given in\n`geometry.simplicial_complex.disjoint_or_exists_inter_eq_convex_hull`. It is mostly useless, as\n`geometry.simplicial_complex.convex_hull_inter_convex_hull` is enough for all purposes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.not_empty_mem", "description": " A simplicial complex in a `\ud835\udd5c`-module is a collection of simplices which glue nicely together.\nNote that the textbook meaning of \"glue nicely\" is given in\n`geometry.simplicial_complex.disjoint_or_exists_inter_eq_convex_hull`. It is mostly useless, as\n`geometry.simplicial_complex.convex_hull_inter_convex_hull` is enough for all purposes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.indep", "description": " A simplicial complex in a `\ud835\udd5c`-module is a collection of simplices which glue nicely together.\nNote that the textbook meaning of \"glue nicely\" is given in\n`geometry.simplicial_complex.disjoint_or_exists_inter_eq_convex_hull`. It is mostly useless, as\n`geometry.simplicial_complex.convex_hull_inter_convex_hull` is enough for all purposes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.down_closed", "description": " A simplicial complex in a `\ud835\udd5c`-module is a collection of simplices which glue nicely together.\nNote that the textbook meaning of \"glue nicely\" is given in\n`geometry.simplicial_complex.disjoint_or_exists_inter_eq_convex_hull`. It is mostly useless, as\n`geometry.simplicial_complex.convex_hull_inter_convex_hull` is enough for all purposes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.inter_subset_convex_hull", "description": " A simplicial complex in a `\ud835\udd5c`-module is a collection of simplices which glue nicely together.\nNote that the textbook meaning of \"glue nicely\" is given in\n`geometry.simplicial_complex.disjoint_or_exists_inter_eq_convex_hull`. It is mostly useless, as\n`geometry.simplicial_complex.convex_hull_inter_convex_hull` is enough for all purposes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.has_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.faces_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.mem_space_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.of_subcomplex_faces", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.convex_hull_inter_convex_hull", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.space", "description": "The underlying space of a simplicial complex is the union of its faces.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.of_erase_faces", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.disjoint_or_exists_inter_eq_convex_hull", "description": " The conclusion is the usual meaning of \"glue nicely\" in textbooks. It turns out to be quite\nunusable, as it's about faces as sets in space rather than simplices. Further,  additional structure\non `\ud835\udd5c` means the only choice of `u` is `s \u2229 t` (but it's hard to prove).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.not_facet_iff_subface", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.semilattice_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.order_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.of_subcomplex", "description": "Construct a simplicial complex as a subset of a given simplicial complex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.vertices_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.space_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.has_inf", "description": "The complex consisting of only the faces present in both of its arguments.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geometry.simplicial_complex.mem_vertices", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_primitive_root.is_integral", "description": "`\u03bc` is integral over `\u2124`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_primitive_root.minpoly_dvd_mod_p", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_primitive_root.minpoly_eq_pow", "description": " If `p` is a prime that does not divide `n`,\nthen the minimal polynomials of a primitive `n`-th root of unity `\u03bc`\nand of `\u03bc ^ p` are the same.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_primitive_root.pow_is_root_minpoly", "description": " If `m : \u2115` is coprime with `n`,\nthen the minimal polynomial of a primitive `n`-th root of unity `\u03bc`\nhas `\u03bc ^ m` as root.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_primitive_root.separable_minpoly_mod", "description": "The reduction modulo `p` of the minimal polynomial of a root of unity `\u03bc` is separable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_primitive_root.minpoly_eq_pow_coprime", "description": " If `m : \u2115` is coprime with `n`,\nthen the minimal polynomials of a primitive `n`-th root of unity `\u03bc`\nand of `\u03bc ^ m` are the same.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_primitive_root.is_roots_of_minpoly", "description": " `primitive_roots n K` is a subset of the roots of the minimal polynomial of a primitive\n`n`-th root of unity `\u03bc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_primitive_root.minpoly_dvd_expand", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_primitive_root.squarefree_minpoly_mod", "description": "The reduction modulo `p` of the minimal polynomial of a root of unity `\u03bc` is squarefree.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_primitive_root.minpoly_dvd_pow_mod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_primitive_root.minpoly_dvd_X_pow_sub_one", "description": "The minimal polynomial of a root of unity `\u03bc` divides `X ^ n - 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_primitive_root.totient_le_degree_minpoly", "description": "The degree of the minimal polynomial of `\u03bc` is at least `totient n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.birthday_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.birthday_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.relabelling.birthday_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.birthday_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.neg_birthday_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.birthday_add_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.birthday_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.neg_birthday", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.birthday_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.birthday_nat_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.birthday_move_left_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.lt_birthday_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.le_birthday", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.birthday_zero_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.birthday_add_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.birthday_add_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.birthday_nat_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.birthday_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.birthday", "description": " The birthday of a pre-game is inductively defined as the least strict upper bound of the\nbirthdays of its left and right games. It may be thought as the \"step\" in which a certain game is\nconstructed.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.to_pgame_birthday", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.birthday_one_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pgame.birthday_move_right_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.lintegral_mul_le_one_of_lintegral_rpow_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.lintegral_Lp_add_le_of_le_one", "description": " Variant of Minkowski's inequality for functions `\u03b1 \u2192 \u211d\u22650\u221e` in `\u2112p` with `p \u2264 1`: the `\u2112p`\nseminorm of the sum of two functions is bounded by a constant multiple of the sum\nof their `\u2112p` seminorms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.lintegral_Lp_add_le", "description": " Minkowski's inequality for functions `\u03b1 \u2192 \u211d\u22650\u221e`: the `\u2112p` seminorm of the sum of two\nfunctions is bounded by the sum of their `\u2112p` seminorms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.ae_eq_zero_of_lintegral_rpow_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.lintegral_mul_eq_zero_of_lintegral_rpow_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.fun_mul_inv_snorm", "description": "Function multiplied by the inverse of its p-seminorm `(\u222b\u207b f^p \u2202\u03bc) ^ 1/p`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.fun_mul_inv_snorm_rpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.lintegral_mul_le_Lp_mul_Lq", "description": " H\u00f6lder's inequality for functions `\u03b1 \u2192 \u211d\u22650\u221e`. The integral of the product of two functions\nis bounded by the product of their `\u2112p` and `\u2112q` seminorms when `p` and `q` are conjugate\nexponents.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.lintegral_mul_le_Lp_mul_Lq_of_ne_zero_of_ne_top", "description": "H\u00f6lder's inequality in case of finite non-zero integrals", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.lintegral_rpow_fun_mul_inv_snorm_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.lintegral_rpow_add_lt_top_of_lintegral_rpow_lt_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.lintegral_Lp_mul_le_Lq_mul_Lr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.lintegral_mul_le_Lp_mul_Lq_of_ne_zero_of_eq_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nnreal.lintegral_mul_le_Lp_mul_Lq", "description": " H\u00f6lder's inequality for functions `\u03b1 \u2192 \u211d\u22650`. The integral of the product of two functions\nis bounded by the product of their `\u2112p` and `\u2112q` seminorms when `p` and `q` are conjugate\nexponents.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.lintegral_rpow_add_le_add_snorm_mul_lintegral_rpow_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.fun_eq_fun_mul_inv_snorm_mul_snorm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.lintegral_mul_rpow_le_lintegral_rpow_mul_lintegral_rpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_left_dual_right_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_left_hom_equiv_tensor", "description": "`tensor_left_hom_equiv` commutes with tensoring on the right", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_left_adjunction", "description": "If `Y Y'` have an exact pairing,\nthen the functor `tensor_left Y'` is left adjoint to `tensor_left Y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_adjoint_mate", "description": "The left adjoint mate `\u1601f : \u1601Y \u27f6 \u1601X` of a morphism `f : X \u27f6 Y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_dual_iso_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact_pairing.coevaluation_evaluation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.comp_left_adjoint_mate", "description": "The composition of left adjoint mates is the adjoint mate of the composition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact_pairing_congr_left", "description": "Transport an exact pairing across an isomorphism in the first argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_left_hom_equiv_symm_coevaluation_comp_id_tensor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_right_hom_equiv_symm_coevaluation_comp_tensor_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_dual_right_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.rigid_category", "description": "A rigid monoidal category is a monoidal category which is left rigid and right rigid.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.rigid_category.to_right_rigid_category", "description": "A rigid monoidal category is a monoidal category which is left rigid and right rigid.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.rigid_category.to_left_rigid_category", "description": "A rigid monoidal category is a monoidal category which is left rigid and right rigid.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_adjoint_mate_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coevaluation_comp_right_adjoint_mate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_right_hom_equiv_id_tensor_comp_evaluation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_right_dual", "description": "A class of objects which have a right dual.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_right_dual.right_dual", "description": "A class of objects which have a right dual.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_right_dual.exact", "description": "A class of objects which have a right dual.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_rigid_category", "description": "A left rigid monoidal category is one in which every object has a right dual.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_rigid_category.left_dual", "description": "A left rigid monoidal category is one in which every object has a right dual.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_right_hom_equiv_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_left_dual", "description": "A class of objects with have a left dual.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_left_dual.left_dual", "description": "A class of objects with have a left dual.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_left_dual.exact", "description": "A class of objects with have a left dual.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_adjoint_mate_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_left_hom_equiv_tensor_id_comp_evaluation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_dual_iso", "description": "Right duals are isomorphic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_dual_iso_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact_pairing.evaluation_coevaluation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_left_hom_equiv_symm_coevaluation_comp_tensor_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_adjoint_mate_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact_pairing", "description": "An exact pairing is a pair of objects `X Y : C` which admit\n a coevaluation and evaluation morphism which fulfill two triangle equalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact_pairing.coevaluation", "description": "An exact pairing is a pair of objects `X Y : C` which admit\n a coevaluation and evaluation morphism which fulfill two triangle equalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact_pairing.evaluation", "description": "An exact pairing is a pair of objects `X Y : C` which admit\n a coevaluation and evaluation morphism which fulfill two triangle equalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact_pairing.coevaluation_evaluation'", "description": "An exact pairing is a pair of objects `X Y : C` which admit\n a coevaluation and evaluation morphism which fulfill two triangle equalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact_pairing.evaluation_coevaluation'", "description": "An exact pairing is a pair of objects `X Y : C` which admit\n a coevaluation and evaluation morphism which fulfill two triangle equalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_left_dual_unit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_right_hom_equiv_tensor", "description": "`tensor_right_hom_equiv` commutes with tensoring on the left", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_right_hom_equiv_tensor_id_comp_evaluation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_rigid_category", "description": "A right rigid monoidal category is one in which every object has a right dual.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_rigid_category.right_dual", "description": "A right rigid monoidal category is one in which every object has a right dual.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_adjoint_mate_comp_evaluation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_left_hom_equiv_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_adjoint_mate_comp_evaluation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.comp_left_adjoint_mate_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_right_dual_unit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_adjoint_mate_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact_pairing_congr", "description": "Transport an exact pairing across isomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_adjoint_mate", "description": "The right adjoint mate `f\u1601 : X\u1601 \u27f6 Y\u1601` of a morphism `f : X \u27f6 Y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_closed_of_left_rigid_category", "description": " Any left rigid category is monoidal closed, with the internal hom `X \u27f6[C] Y = \u1601X \u2297 Y`.\nThis has to be a definition rather than an instance to avoid diamonds, for example between\n`category_theory.monoidal_closed.functor_category` and\n`category_theory.monoidal.left_rigid_functor_category`. Moreover, in concrete applications there is\noften a more useful definition of the internal hom object than `\u1601Y \u2297 X`, in which case the monoidal\nclosed structure shouldn't come the rigid structure (e.g. in the category `FinVect k`, it is more\nconvenient to define the internal hom as `Y \u2192\u2097[k] X` rather than `\u1601Y \u2297 X` even though these are\nnaturally isomorphic).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_right_adjunction", "description": "If `Y Y'` have an exact pairing,\nthen the functor `tensor_right Y` is left adjoint to `tensor_right Y'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact_pairing.coevaluation_evaluation_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_right_hom_equiv_symm_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_left_hom_equiv_symm_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_dual_left_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_left_hom_equiv", "description": "Given an exact pairing on `Y Y'`,\nwe get a bijection on hom-sets `(Y' \u2297 X \u27f6 Z) \u2243 (X \u27f6 Y \u2297 Z)`\nby \"pulling the string on the left\" up or down.\n\nThis gives the adjunction `tensor_left_adjunction Y Y' : tensor_left Y' \u22a3 tensor_left Y`.\n\nThis adjunction is often referred to as \"Frobenius reciprocity\" in the\nfusion categories / planar algebras / subfactors literature.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_right_dual_left_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.closed_of_has_left_dual", "description": "If `Y` has a left dual `\u1601Y`, then it is a closed object, with the internal hom functor `Y \u27f6[C] -`\ngiven by left tensoring by `\u1601Y`.\nThis has to be a definition rather than an instance to avoid diamonds, for example between\n`category_theory.monoidal_closed.functor_closed` and\n`category_theory.monoidal.functor_has_left_dual`. Moreover, in concrete applications there is often\na more useful definition of the internal hom object than `\u1601Y \u2297 X`, in which case the closed\nstructure shouldn't come from `has_left_dual` (e.g. in the category `FinVect k`, it is more\nconvenient to define the internal hom as `Y \u2192\u2097[k] X` rather than `\u1601Y \u2297 X` even though these are\nnaturally isomorphic).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_right_hom_equiv_symm_coevaluation_comp_id_tensor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_right_hom_equiv", "description": "Given an exact pairing on `Y Y'`,\nwe get a bijection on hom-sets `(X \u2297 Y \u27f6 Z) \u2243 (X \u27f6 Z \u2297 Y')`\nby \"pulling the string on the right\" up or down.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact_pairing_congr_right", "description": "Transport an exact pairing across an isomorphism in the second argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact_pairing.evaluation_coevaluation_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coevaluation_comp_left_adjoint_mate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact_pairing_unit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_dual_iso", "description": "Left duals are isomorphic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_left_hom_equiv_id_tensor_comp_evaluation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.comp_right_adjoint_mate", "description": "The composition of right adjoint mates is the adjoint mate of the composition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.comp_right_adjoint_mate_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.positive_compacts.locally_compact_space_of_add_group", "description": "Every separated topological group in which there exists a compact set with nonempty\ninterior is locally compact.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.positive_compacts.locally_compact_space_of_group", "description": " Every separated topological group in which there exists a compact set with nonempty interior\nis locally compact.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quotient_add_group.has_continuous_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quotient_group.has_continuous_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.cut_map_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.induced_order_ring_iso_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.induced_map_rat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.induced_map", "description": " The induced order preserving function from a linear ordered field to a conditionally complete\nlinear ordered field, defined by taking the Sup in the codomain of all the rationals less than the\ninput.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.ring_hom.unique", "description": "There exists no nontrivial ring homomorphism `\u211d \u2192+* \u211d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.induced_map_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.induced_map_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_ordered_field.to_linear_ordered_field", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.coe_induced_order_ring_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.induced_order_ring_iso_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_ordered_field.to_conditionally_complete_linear_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.le_induced_map_mul_self_of_mem_cut_map", "description": "Preparatory lemma for `induced_ring_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.order_ring_iso.unique", "description": " There is a unique ordered ring isomorphism between two conditionally complete linear ordered\nfields.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.cut_map_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.cut_map_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.exists_mem_cut_map_mul_self_of_lt_induced_map_mul_self", "description": "Preparatory lemma for `induced_ring_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.induced_map_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.conditionally_complete_linear_ordered_field", "description": "The reals are a conditionally complete linearly ordered field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.induced_order_ring_iso", "description": "The isomorphism of ordered rings between two conditionally complete linearly ordered fields.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.induced_map_induced_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.induced_map_inv_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.induced_order_ring_hom_to_ring_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.coe_lt_induced_map_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.cut_map_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.mem_cut_map_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.induced_order_ring_hom", "description": "`induced_map` as an `order_ring_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.cut_map_nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.induced_map_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.lt_induced_map_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom_monotone", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.induced_map_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.coe_mem_cut_map_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.order_ring_hom.unique", "description": " There is a unique ordered ring homomorphism from an archimedean linear ordered field to a\nconditionally complete linear ordered field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_field.cut_map_bdd_above", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_ordered_field", "description": " A field which is both linearly ordered and conditionally complete with respect to the order.\nThis axiomatizes the reals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_ordered_field.add", "description": " A field which is both linearly ordered and conditionally complete with respect to the order.\nThis axiomatizes the reals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_ordered_field.add_assoc", "description": " A field which is both linearly ordered and conditionally complete with respect to the order.\nThis axiomatizes the reals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_ordered_field.zero", "description": " A field which is both linearly ordered and conditionally complete with respect to the order.\nThis axiomatizes the reals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_ordered_field.zero_add", "description": " A field which is both linearly ordered and conditionally complete with respect to the order.\nThis axiomatizes the reals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_ordered_field.add_zero", "description": " A field which is both linearly ordered and conditionally complete with respect to the order.\nThis axiomatizes the reals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_ordered_field.nsmul", "description": " A field which is both linearly ordered and conditionally complete with respect to the order.\nThis axiomatizes the reals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_ordered_field.nsmul_zero'", "description": " A field which is both linearly ordered and conditionally complete with respect to the order.\nThis axiomatizes the reals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_ordered_field.nsmul_succ'", "description": " A field which is both linearly ordered and conditionally complete with respect to the order.\nThis axiomatizes the reals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_ordered_field.neg", "description": " A field which is both linearly ordered and conditionally complete with respect to the order.\nThis axiomatizes the reals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_ordered_field.sub", "description": " A field which is both linearly ordered and conditionally complete with respect to the order.\nThis axiomatizes the reals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_ordered_field.sub_eq_add_neg", "description": " A field which is both linearly ordered and conditionally complete with respect to the order.\nThis axiomatizes the reals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conditionally_complete_linear_ordered_field.zsmul", "description": " A field 