"mathlib"}, {"name": "to_Z", "description": " `to_Z` numbers elements of `\u03b9` according to their order, starting from `i0`. We prove in\n`order_iso_range_to_Z_of_linear_succ_pred_arch` that this defines an `order_iso` between `\u03b9` and\nthe range of `to_Z`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "injective_to_Z", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_locally_finite_order.succ_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso_range_of_linear_succ_pred_arch", "description": " If the order has both a bot and a top, `to_Z` gives an `order_iso` between `\u03b9` and\n`finset.range n` for some `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "to_Z_of_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_locally_finite_order.succ_fn_spec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso_nat_of_linear_succ_pred_arch", "description": "If the order has a bot but no top, `to_Z` defines an `order_iso` between `\u03b9` and `\u2115`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_of_to_Z_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_locally_finite_order.is_max_of_succ_fn_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_locally_finite_order.succ_fn_le_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_locally_finite_order.le_of_lt_succ_fn", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "to_Z_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_order.pred_archimedean_of_succ_archimedean", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "to_Z_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "to_Z_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "to_Z_le_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_locally_finite_order.pred_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso_int_of_linear_succ_pred_arch", "description": "If the order has neither bot nor top, `to_Z` defines an `order_iso` between `\u03b9` and `\u2124`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "to_Z_iterate_pred_ge", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "to_Z_iterate_pred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preserves_finite_colimits_preadditive_yoneda_obj_of_injective", "description": "The preadditive Yoneda functor on `J` preserves colimits if `J` is injective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.injective_of_preserves_finite_colimits_preadditive_yoneda_obj", "description": "An object is injective if its preadditive Yoneda functor preserves finite colimits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at_of_restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_filter.restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at_iff_restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.fderiv_restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at_iff_restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_of_restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_continuous_forall_mem_convex_of_local_const", "description": " Let `X` be a normal paracompact topological space (e.g., any extended metric space). Let `E` be\na topological real vector space. Let `t : X \u2192 set E` be a family of convex sets. Suppose that for\neach point `x : X`, there exists a vector `c : E` that belongs to `t y` for all `y` in a\nneighborhood of `x`. Then there exists a continuous map `g : C(X, E)` such that `g x \u2208 t x` for all\n`x`. See also `exists_continuous_forall_mem_convex_of_local`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_continuous_forall_mem_convex_of_local", "description": " Let `X` be a normal paracompact topological space (e.g., any extended metric space). Let `E` be\na topological real vector space. Let `t : X \u2192 set E` be a family of convex sets. Suppose that for\neach point `x : X`, there exists a neighborhood `U \u2208 \ud835\udcdd X` and a function `g : X \u2192 E` that is\ncontinuous on `U` and sends each `y \u2208 U` to a point of `t y`. Then there exists a continuous map\n`g : C(X, E)` such that `g x \u2208 t x` for all `x`. See also\n`exists_continuous_forall_mem_convex_of_local_const`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "partition_of_unity.finsum_smul_mem_convex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.has_limits.hom_diagram", "description": " Auxiliary definition:\nthe diagram whose limit gives the morphism space between two objects of the limit category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.has_limits.category_theory.limits.limit.category_theory.category_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.has_limits.category_theory.limits.limit.category_theory.category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.has_limits.limit_cone_lift_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.has_limits.limit_cone_X_2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.objects.category_theory.limits.preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.has_limits.limit_\u03c0_hom_diagram_eq_to_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.has_limits.limit_cone_X_\u03b1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.category_theory.limits.has_limits", "description": "The category of small categories has all small limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.has_limits.limit_cone_\u03c0_app_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.has_limits.category_objects", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.has_limits.limit_cone_lift", "description": "Auxiliary definition: the universal morphism to the proposed limit cone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.has_limits.limit_cone_lift_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.has_limits.limit_cone_is_limit", "description": "Auxiliary definition: the proposed cone is a limit cone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.has_limits.hom_diagram_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.has_limits.category_theory.limits.limit.category_theory.category_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.has_limits.limit_cone_X", "description": "Auxiliary definition: the limit category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.has_limits.limit_cone_\u03c0_app_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.has_limits.hom_diagram_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.has_limits.limit_cone", "description": "Auxiliary definition: the cone over the limit category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Cat.has_limits.limit_cone_X_str", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "formal_multilinear_series.left_inv_eq_right_inv", "description": " The left inverse and the right inverse of a formal multilinear series coincide. This is not at\nall obvious from their definition, but it follows from uniqueness of inverses (which comes from the\nfact that composition is associative on formal multilinear series).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "formal_multilinear_series.right_inv_coeff_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "formal_multilinear_series.left_inv", "description": " The left inverse of a formal multilinear series, where the `n`-th term is defined inductively\nin terms of the previous ones to make sure that `(left_inv p i) \u2218 p = id`. For this, the linear term\n`p\u2081` in `p` should be invertible. In the definition, `i` is a linear isomorphism that should\ncoincide with `p\u2081`, so that one can use its inverse in the construction. The definition does not\nuse that `i = p\u2081`, but proofs that the definition is well-behaved do.\n\nThe `n`-th term in `q \u2218 p` is `\u2211 q\u2096 (p_{j\u2081}, ..., p_{j\u2096})` over `j\u2081 + ... + j\u2096 = n`. In this\nexpression, `q\u2099` appears only once, in `q\u2099 (p\u2081, ..., p\u2081)`. We adjust the definition so that this\nterm compensates the rest of the sum, using `i\u207b\u00b9` as an inverse to `p\u2081`.\n\nThese formulas only make sense when the constant term `p\u2080` vanishes. The definition we give is\ngeneral, but it ignores the value of `p\u2080`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "formal_multilinear_series.radius_right_inv_pos_of_radius_pos_aux1", "description": " First technical lemma to control the growth of coefficients of the inverse. Bound the explicit\nexpression for `\u2211_{k<n+1} a\u1d4f Q\u2096` in terms of a sum of powers of the same sum one step before,\nin a general abstract setup.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "formal_multilinear_series.comp_right_inv_aux1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "formal_multilinear_series.right_inv_remove_zero", "description": " The right inverse does not depend on the zeroth coefficient of a formal multilinear\nseries.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "formal_multilinear_series.right_inv_coeff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "formal_multilinear_series.right_inv", "description": " The right inverse of a formal multilinear series, where the `n`-th term is defined inductively\nin terms of the previous ones to make sure that `p \u2218 (right_inv p i) = id`. For this, the linear\nterm `p\u2081` in `p` should be invertible. In the definition, `i` is a linear isomorphism that should\ncoincide with `p\u2081`, so that one can use its inverse in the construction. The definition does not\nuse that `i = p\u2081`, but proofs that the definition is well-behaved do.\n\nThe `n`-th term in `p \u2218 q` is `\u2211 p\u2096 (q_{j\u2081}, ..., q_{j\u2096})` over `j\u2081 + ... + j\u2096 = n`. In this\nexpression, `q\u2099` appears only once, in `p\u2081 (q\u2099)`. We adjust the definition of `q\u2099` so that this\nterm compensates the rest of the sum, using `i\u207b\u00b9` as an inverse to `p\u2081`.\n\nThese formulas only make sense when the constant term `p\u2080` vanishes. The definition we give is\ngeneral, but it ignores the value of `p\u2080`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "formal_multilinear_series.comp_right_inv_aux2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "formal_multilinear_series.left_inv_comp", "description": " The left inverse to a formal multilinear series is indeed a left inverse, provided its linear\nterm is invertible.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "formal_multilinear_series.comp_right_inv", "description": " The right inverse to a formal multilinear series is indeed a right inverse, provided its linear\nterm is invertible and its constant term vanishes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "formal_multilinear_series.radius_right_inv_pos_of_radius_pos_aux2", "description": " Second technical lemma to control the growth of coefficients of the inverse. Bound the explicit\nexpression for `\u2211_{k<n+1} a\u1d4f Q\u2096` in terms of a sum of powers of the same sum one step before,\nin the specific setup we are interesting in, by reducing to the general bound in\n`radius_right_inv_pos_of_radius_pos_aux1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "formal_multilinear_series.right_inv_coeff_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "formal_multilinear_series.left_inv_coeff_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "formal_multilinear_series.radius_right_inv_pos_of_radius_pos", "description": " If a a formal multilinear series has a positive radius of convergence, then its right inverse\nalso has a positive radius of convergence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "formal_multilinear_series.left_inv_coeff_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "formal_multilinear_series.left_inv_remove_zero", "description": " The left inverse does not depend on the zeroth coefficient of a formal multilinear\nseries.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.not_mem_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.mem_kstar", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language", "description": "A language is a set of strings over an alphabet.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.complete_boolean_algebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.map_kstar", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.kstar_eq_supr_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.mul_supr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.mem_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.mem_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.has_one", "description": "`1 : language \u03b1` contains only one element `[]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.add_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.has_zero", "description": "Zero language has no elements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.mul_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.append_mem_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.zero_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.mem_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.nil_mem_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.nil_mem_kstar", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.has_insert", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.le_mul_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.one_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.add_supr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.one_add_self_mul_kstar_eq_kstar", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.has_kstar", "description": " The Kleene star of a language `L` is the set of all strings which can be written by\nconcatenating strings from `L`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.map_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.one_add_kstar_mul_self_eq_kstar", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.kstar_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.map", "description": "Maps the alphabet of a language.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.join_mem_kstar", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.le_add_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.has_mul", "description": " The product of two languages `l` and `m` is the language made of the strings `x ++ y` where\n`x \u2208 l` and `y \u2208 m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.add_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.supr_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.has_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.has_add", "description": "The sum of two languages is their union.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.mul_self_kstar_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.kstar_def_nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.mem_supr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.supr_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.map_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.has_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.mem_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.le_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "language.kleene_algebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.schroeder_bernstein", "description": " **The Schr\u00f6der-Bernstein Theorem**:\nGiven injections `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` and `\u03b2 \u2192 \u03b1`, we can get a bijection `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.min_injective", "description": " The cardinals are well-ordered. We express it here by the fact that in any set of cardinals\nthere is an element that injects into the others. See `cardinal.linear_order` for (one of) the\nlattice instances.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.total", "description": " The cardinals are totally ordered. See `cardinal.linear_order` for (one of) the lattice\ninstance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.antisymm", "description": " **The Schr\u00f6der-Bernstein Theorem**: Given embeddings `\u03b1 \u21aa \u03b2` and `\u03b2 \u21aa \u03b1`, there exists an\nequivalence `\u03b1 \u2243 \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_eq_mul_prime_prod", "description": "If `x * y = a * \u220f i in s, p i` where `p i` is always prime, then\n `x` and `y` can both be written as a divisor of `a` multiplied by\n a product over a subset of `s`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prime.abs", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prime.neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_eq_mul_prime_pow", "description": "If ` x * y = a * p ^ n` where `p` is prime, then `x` and `y` can both be written\n as the product of a power of `p` and a divisor of `a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_sup_le_of_ae_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_inf_const'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_sup", "description": " Essential supremum of `f` with respect to measure `\u03bc`: the smallest `c : \u03b2` such that\n`f x \u2264 c` a.e.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.ess_sup_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.ess_sup_liminf_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_sup_smul_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.ess_sup_mul_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_sup_mono_measure'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.ess_sup_add_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_le_ess_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_inf_congr_ae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_sup_indicator_eq_ess_sup_restrict", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_sup_measure_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_lt_of_ess_sup_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_sup_mono_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_embedding.ess_sup_map_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.ess_sup_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_inf_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_sup_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_inf_antitone_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_sup_congr_ae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_inf_const_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_inf_mono_ae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_sup_map_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.coe_ess_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_sup_const'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.ess_sup_const_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_ess_inf_of_ae_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_inf_eq_Sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_sup_const_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.ess_inf_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_ess_inf_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_inf_measure_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_inf", "description": " Essential infimum of `f` with respect to measure `\u03bc`: the greatest `c : \u03b2` such that\n`c \u2264 f x` a.e.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_lt_of_lt_ess_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "meas_ess_sup_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_sup_eq_Inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_sup_mono_ae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_sup_map_measure_of_measurable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ess_sup_comp_le_ess_sup_map_measure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "meas_lt_ess_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ennreal.ae_le_ess_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.topological_closure", "description": " The (topological-space) closure of a subfield of a topological field is\nitself a subfield.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.is_closed_topological_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_preconnected.eq_one_or_eq_neg_one_of_sq_eq", "description": " If `f` is a function `\u03b1 \u2192 \ud835\udd5c` which is continuous on a preconnected set `S`, and\n`f ^ 2 = 1` on `S`, then either `f = 1` on `S`, or `f = -1` on `S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_division_ring", "description": "A topological division ring is a division ring with a topology where all operations are\n   continuous, including inversion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_division_ring.to_topological_ring", "description": "A topological division ring is a division ring with a topology where all operations are\n   continuous, including inversion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_division_ring.to_has_continuous_inv\u2080", "description": "A topological division ring is a division ring with a topology where all operations are\n   continuous, including inversion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_homeomorph", "description": "The map `\u03bb x, a * x + b`, as a homeomorphism from `\ud835\udd5c` (a topological field) to itself, when `a \u2260 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto_cocompact_mul_left\u2080", "description": " Left-multiplication by a nonzero element of a topological division ring is proper, i.e.,\ninverse images of compact sets are compact.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_preconnected.eq_of_sq_eq", "description": " If `f, g` are functions `\u03b1 \u2192 \ud835\udd5c`, both continuous on a preconnected set `S`, with\n`f ^ 2 = g ^ 2` on `S`, and `g z \u2260 0` all `z \u2208 S`, then as soon as `f = g` holds at\none point of `S` it holds for all points.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_homeomorph_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_preconnected.eq_or_eq_neg_of_sq_eq", "description": " If `f, g` are functions `\u03b1 \u2192 \ud835\udd5c`, both continuous on a preconnected set `S`, with\n`f ^ 2 = g ^ 2` on `S`, and `g z \u2260 0` all `z \u2208 S`, then either `f = g` or `f = -g` on\n`S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.topological_closure_minimal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_homeomorph_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_local_min.inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subfield.le_topological_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto_cocompact_mul_right\u2080", "description": " Right-multiplication by a nonzero element of a topological division ring is proper, i.e.,\ninverse images of compact sets are compact.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.bit", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.nat_bitwise", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.bitwise", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.div2", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.land", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.ldiff", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.lnot", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.shiftr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.bodd", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.shiftl", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.test_bit", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.lor", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "int.lxor", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "commute.zero_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.mul_inverse_rev'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.inv_left_iff\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.inv_right\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.inv_right_iff\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.inv_left\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.div_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.zero_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.mul_inverse_rev", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.ring_inverse_ring_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.div_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_internal_prime_power_torsion_of_pid", "description": "A finitely generated torsion module over a PID is an internal direct sum of its\n`p i ^ e i`-torsion submodules for some primes `p i` and numbers `e i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.torsion_of_eq_span_pow_p_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.torsion_by_prime_power_decomposition", "description": "A finitely generated `p ^ \u221e`-torsion module over a PID is isomorphic to a direct sum of some\n  `R \u29f8 R \u2219 (p ^ e i)` for some `e i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.equiv_free_prod_direct_sum", "description": "**Structure theorem of finitely generated modules over a PID** : A finitely generated\n  module over a PID is isomorphic to the product of a free module and a direct sum of some\n  `R \u29f8 R \u2219 (p i ^ e i)` where the `p i ^ e i` are prime powers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.p_pow_smul_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.exists_is_internal_prime_power_torsion_of_pid", "description": "A finitely generated torsion module over a PID is an internal direct sum of its\n`p i ^ e i`-torsion submodules for some primes `p i` and numbers `e i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.exists_smul_eq_zero_and_mk_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.equiv_direct_sum_of_is_torsion", "description": "A finitely generated torsion module over a PID is isomorphic to a direct sum of some\n  `R \u29f8 R \u2219 (p i ^ e i)` where the `p i ^ e i` are prime powers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "riesz_content_aux_sup_le", "description": " The Riesz content \u03bb associated to a given positive linear functional \u039b is\nfinitely subadditive: `\u03bb(K\u2081 \u222a K\u2082) \u2264 \u03bb(K\u2081) + \u03bb(K\u2082)` for any compact subsets `K\u2081, K\u2082 \u2286 X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "riesz_content_aux", "description": " Given a positive linear functional \u039b on X, for `K \u2286 X` compact define\n`\u03bb(K) = inf {\u039bf | 1\u2264f on K}`. When X is a compact Hausdorff space, this will be shown to be a\ncontent, and will be shown to agree with the Riesz measure on the compact subsets `K \u2286 X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "riesz_content_aux_image_nonempty", "description": " For any compact subset `K \u2286 X`, there exist some bounded continuous nonnegative\nfunctions f on X such that `f \u2265 1` on K.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_lt_riesz_content_aux_add_pos", "description": " The Riesz content can be approximated arbitrarily well by evaluating the positive linear\nfunctional on test functions: for any `\u03b5 > 0`, there exists a bounded continuous nonnegative\nfunction f on X such that `f \u2265 1` on K and such that `\u03bb(K) \u2264 \u039b f < \u03bb(K) + \u03b5`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "riesz_content_aux_le", "description": " Any bounded continuous nonnegative f such that `f \u2265 1` on K gives an upper bound on the\ncontent of K; namely `\u03bb(K) \u2264 \u039b f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "riesz_content_aux_mono", "description": " Riesz content \u03bb (associated with a positive linear functional \u039b) is\nmonotone: if `K\u2081 \u2286 K\u2082` are compact subsets in X, then `\u03bb(K\u2081) \u2264 \u03bb(K\u2082)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_complete.is_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dense_inducing.continuous_extend_of_cauchy", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "counterexample.counterexample_not_prime_but_homogeneous_prime.I_not_prime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.counterexample_not_prime_but_homogeneous_prime.I", "description": "The counterexample is the ideal `I = span {(2, 2)}`.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.counterexample_not_prime_but_homogeneous_prime.grading.right_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.counterexample_not_prime_but_homogeneous_prime.grading.graded_algebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.counterexample_not_prime_but_homogeneous_prime.grading.mul_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.counterexample_not_prime_but_homogeneous_prime.two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.counterexample_not_prime_but_homogeneous_prime.submodule_o", "description": "The grade 1 part of `R\u00b2` is `{(0, b) | b \u2208 R}`.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.counterexample_not_prime_but_homogeneous_prime.grading", "description": "Given the above grading (see `submodule_z` and `submodule_o`),\n we turn `R\u00b2` into a graded ring.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.counterexample_not_prime_but_homogeneous_prime.grading.left_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.counterexample_not_prime_but_homogeneous_prime.submodule_z", "description": "The grade 0 part of `R\u00b2` is `{(a, a) | a \u2208 R}`.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.counterexample_not_prime_but_homogeneous_prime.grading.one_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.counterexample_not_prime_but_homogeneous_prime.two.linear_ordered_add_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.counterexample_not_prime_but_homogeneous_prime.grading.decompose", "description": "`R\u00b2 \u2245 {(a, a) | a \u2208 R} \u2a01 {(0, b) | b \u2208 R}` by `(x, y) \u21a6 (x, x) + (0, y - x)`.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.counterexample_not_prime_but_homogeneous_prime.homogeneous_mem_or_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.counterexample_not_prime_but_homogeneous_prime.I_is_homogeneous", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "enat.complete_linear_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.is_scalar_tower_circle_circle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.coe_smul_circle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.smul_comm_class_closed_ball'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.conj_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.is_scalar_tower_circle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.coe_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.topological_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.semigroup_with_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.one_add_coe_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.smul_comm_class_circle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.coe_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.coe_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.im", "description": "Imaginary part of a point of the unit disc.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.is_scalar_tower_closed_ball_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.coe_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.im_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.has_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.coe_ne_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.coe_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.conj", "description": "Conjugate point of the unit disc.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.closed_ball_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc", "description": "Complex unit disc.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.is_scalar_tower_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.im_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.conj_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.norm_sq_lt_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.mk_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.smul_comm_class_circle'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.has_distrib_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.smul_comm_class_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.comm_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.re_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.mk", "description": " A constructor that assumes `abs z < 1` instead of `dist z 0 < 1` and returns an element \nof `\ud835\udd3b` instead of `\u21a5metric.ball (0 : \u2102) 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.im_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.coe_smul_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.abs_lt_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.conj_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.re_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.smul_comm_class_closed_ball_circle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.conj_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.abs_ne_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.re_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.coe_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.coe_ne_neg_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.mk_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.smul_comm_class_circle_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.re", "description": "Real part of a point of the unit disc.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.circle_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.unit_disc.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "std_range", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "std_gen.random_gen", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "random_gen", "description": "Interface for random number generators.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "random_gen.range", "description": "Interface for random number generators.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "random_gen.next", "description": "Interface for random number generators.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "random_gen.split", "description": "Interface for random number generators.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "std_gen", "description": "\"Standard\" random number generator.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "std_gen.s1", "description": "\"Standard\" random number generator.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "std_gen.s2", "description": "\"Standard\" random number generator.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "std_next", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "rand_nat", "description": "Generate a random natural number in the interval [lo, hi].", "p": "core"}, {"name": "std_split", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "std_gen.has_repr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "rand_bool", "description": "Generate a random Boolean.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "mk_std_gen", "description": "Return a standard number generator.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.typename", "description": "Describe the type of a json value", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bool.non_null_json_serializable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "non_null_json_serializable_handler", "description": " A derive handler to serialize structures by their fields.\n\nFor the following structure:\n```lean\nstructure has_default : Type :=\n(x : \u2115 := 2)\n(y : fin x.succ := 3 * fin.of_nat x)\n(z : \u2115 := 3)\n```\nthis generates an `of_json` parser along the lines of\n\n```lean\nmeta def has_default.of_json (j : json) : exceptional (has_default) :=\ndo\n  p \u2190 json_serializable.field_starter j,\n  (f_y, p) \u2190 json_serializable.field_get p \"y\",\n  (f_z, p) \u2190 json_serializable.field_get p \"z\",\n  f_y.mmap (of_json _) >>= option.elim\n    (f_z.mmap (of_json _) >>= option.elim\n      (pure {has_default.})\n      (\u03bb z, pure {has_default. z := z})\n    )\n    (\u03bb y, f_z.mmap (of_json _) >>= option.elim\n        (pure {has_default.})\n        (\u03bb z, pure {has_default. y := y, z := z})\n    )\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbmap.non_null_json_serializable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.non_null_json_serializable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "json_serializable.field_terminator", "description": "Check no fields remain", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "of_json_helper", "description": " Generate an expression that builds a term of type `t` (which is itself a parametrization of\nthe structure `struct_name`) using the expressions resolving to parsed fields in `vars` and the\nexpressions resolving to unparsed `option json` objects in `js`. This can handled\ndependently-typed and defaulted (via `:=` which for structures is not the same as `opt_param`)\nfields.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.non_null_json_serializable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "json_serializable.field_get", "description": "Check a field exists and is unique", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "non_null_json_serializable", "description": "A class for types which never serialize to null", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "non_null_json_serializable.to_json_serializable", "description": "A class for types which never serialize to null", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "get_constructor_and_projections", "description": " ``((c_name, c_fun), [(p_name, p_fun), ...]) \u2190 get_constructor_and_projections `(struct n)``\ngets the names and partial invocations of the constructor and projections of a structure", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype.non_null_json_serializable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "json_serializable.field_starter", "description": "Begin parsing fields", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exceptional.has_orelse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "punit.json_serializable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "native.float.non_null_json_serializable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.to_expr", "description": "Flatten a list of (p)exprs into a (p)expr forming a list of type `list t`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.non_null_json_serializable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.json_serializable", "description": "Note this only makes sense on types which do not themselves serialize to `null`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "json_serializable", "description": "A class to indicate that a type is json serializable", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "json_serializable.to_json", "description": "A class to indicate that a type is json serializable", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "json_serializable.of_json", "description": "A class to indicate that a type is json serializable", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.non_null_json_serializable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.non_null_json_serializable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype.json_serializable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pow_four_add_four_mul_pow_four", "description": "Sophie Germain's identity, see <https://www.cut-the-knot.org/blue/SophieGermainIdentity.shtml>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum_four_sq_mul_sum_four_sq", "description": "Euler's four-square identity, see <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler%27s_four-square_identity>.\n\nThis sign choice here corresponds to the signs obtained by multiplying two quaternions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sq_add_sq_mul_sq_add_sq", "description": "Brahmagupta-Fibonacci identity or Diophantus identity, see\n<https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brahmagupta%E2%80%93Fibonacci_identity>.\n\nThis sign choice here corresponds to the signs obtained by multiplying two complex numbers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sq_add_mul_sq_mul_sq_add_mul_sq", "description": "Brahmagupta's identity, see <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brahmagupta%27s_identity>", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pow_four_add_four_mul_pow_four'", "description": "Sophie Germain's identity, see <https://www.cut-the-knot.org/blue/SophieGermainIdentity.shtml>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum_eight_sq_mul_sum_eight_sq", "description": "Degen's eight squares identity, see <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Degen%27s_eight-square_identity>.\n\nThis sign choice here corresponds to the signs obtained by multiplying two octonions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conv.monad", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "conv.lhs", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "conv.alternative", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "conv.monad_fail", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "conv.update_lhs", "description": "`\u22a2 lhs = rhs` ~~> `\u22a2 lhs' = rhs` using `h : lhs = lhs'`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "conv.funext", "description": "Create a conversion from the function extensionality tactic.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "conv.dsimp", "description": "dsimp the LHS.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "conv.whnf", "description": "Put LHS in WHNF.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "conv.skip", "description": "Use reflexivity to prove.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "conv", "description": " `conv \u03b1` is a tactic for discharging goals of the form `lhs ~ rhs` for some relation `~` (usually equality) and fixed lhs, rhs.\nKnown in the literature as a __conversion__ tactic.\nSo for example, if one had the lemma `p : x = y`, then the conversion for `p` would be one that solves `p`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.id_tag.conv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "conv.change", "description": "Change `lhs` to something definitionally equal to it.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "conv.congr", "description": "Take the target equality `f x y = X` and try to apply the congruence lemma for `f` to it (namely `x = x' \u2192 y = y' \u2192 f x y = f x' y'`).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "conv.rhs", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "conv.convert", "description": "Applies the conversion `c`. Returns `(rhs,p)` where `p : r lhs rhs`. Throws away the return value of `c`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "deriv_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_deriv_at_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_within_at_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv_inv''", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_within_at.inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_within_at.div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv_inv'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_within_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv_within_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv_within_inv'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_deriv_at.div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv_within_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bifunctor.map_comp_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bifunctor.map_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bifunctor.diagonal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bifunctor.map_id_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bifunctor.diagonal'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.to_cycles", "description": "Bundle an element `C.X i` such that `C.d_from i x = 0` as a term of `C.cycles i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.cycles_ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.cycles_map_to_cycles", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.homology_ext", "description": "To prove that two maps out of a homology group are equal,\nit suffices to check they are equal on the images of cycles.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.to_homology", "description": "Build a term of `C.homology i` from an element `C.X i` such that `C.d_from i x = 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.homology_ext'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_within_at_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_deriv_at_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at_filter.prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_within_at.prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_deriv_at.prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv_within_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at_filter_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "countable_iff_nonempty_embedding", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "psigma.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "psum.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_coe.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonempty_embedding_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pprod.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.embedding.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "no_zero_divisors.to_cancel_monoid_with_zero", "description": " A ring with no zero divisors is a `cancel_monoid_with_zero`.\n\nNote this is not an instance as it forms a typeclass loop.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_right_regular_of_non_zero_divisor", "description": " Right `mul` by a `k : \u03b1` over `[ring \u03b1]` is injective, if `k` is not a zero divisor.\nThe typeclass that restricts all terms of `\u03b1` to have this property is `no_zero_divisors`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_left_regular_of_non_zero_divisor", "description": " Left `mul` by a `k : \u03b1` over `[ring \u03b1]` is injective, if `k` is not a zero divisor.\nThe typeclass that restricts all terms of `\u03b1` to have this property is `no_zero_divisors`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_domain.to_cancel_monoid_with_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_regular_of_ne_zero'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "no_zero_divisors.to_cancel_comm_monoid_with_zero", "description": " A commutative ring with no zero divisors is a `cancel_comm_monoid_with_zero`.\n\nNote this is not an instance as it forms a typeclass loop.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_regular_iff_ne_zero'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_domain.to_cancel_comm_monoid_with_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sheaf.subcanonical.is_sheaf_of_representable", "description": "If `J` is subcanonical, then any representable is a `J`-sheaf.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sheaf.sheaf_for_finest_topology", "description": "Check that if `P \u2208 Ps`, then `P` is indeed a sheaf for the finest topology on `Ps`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sheaf.canonical_topology", "description": "The `canonical_topology` on a category is the finest (largest) topology for which every\nrepresentable presheaf is a sheaf.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00ZA>", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sheaf.is_sheaf_for_bind", "description": "To show `P` is a sheaf for the binding of `U` with `B`, it suffices to show that `P` is a sheaf for\n`U`, that `P` is a sheaf for each sieve in `B`, and that it is separated for any pullback of any\nsieve in `B`.\n\nThis is mostly an auxiliary lemma to show `is_sheaf_for_trans`.\nAdapted from [Elephant], Lemma C2.1.7(i) with suggestions as mentioned in\nhttps://math.stackexchange.com/a/358709/", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sheaf.le_finest_topology", "description": "Check that if each `P \u2208 Ps` is a sheaf for `J`, then `J` is a subtopology of `finest_topology Ps`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sheaf.finest_topology", "description": "Construct the finest (largest) Grothendieck topology for which all the given presheaves are sheaves.\n\nThis is equal to the construction of <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00Z9>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sheaf.is_sheaf_yoneda_obj", "description": "`yoneda.obj X` is a sheaf for the canonical topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sheaf.subcanonical", "description": "A subcanonical topology is a topology which is smaller than the canonical topology.\nEquivalently, a topology is subcanonical iff every representable is a sheaf.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sheaf.is_sheaf_of_representable", "description": "A representable functor is a sheaf for the canonical topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sheaf.subcanonical.of_yoneda_is_sheaf", "description": "If every functor `yoneda.obj X` is a `J`-sheaf, then `J` is subcanonical.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sheaf.finest_topology_single", "description": "Construct the finest (largest) Grothendieck topology for which the given presheaf is a sheaf.\n\nThis is a special case of https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00Z9, but following a different\nproof (see the comments there).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sheaf.is_sheaf_for_trans", "description": "Given two sieves `R` and `S`, to show that `P` is a sheaf for `S`, we can show:\n* `P` is a sheaf for `R`\n* `P` is a sheaf for the pullback of `S` along any arrow in `R`\n* `P` is separated for the pullback of `R` along any arrow in `S`.\n\nThis is mostly an auxiliary lemma to construct `finest_topology`.\nAdapted from [Elephant], Lemma C2.1.7(ii) with suggestions as mentioned in\nhttps://math.stackexchange.com/a/358709", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.thickening", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_on_univ_norm", "description": " The norm on a real normed space is convex on the whole space. See also `seminorm.convex_on`\nand `convex_on_norm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bounded_convex_hull", "description": "Convex hull of `s` is bounded if and only if `s` is bounded.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_add_dist_of_mem_segment", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_connected_set_of_same_ray_and_ne_zero", "description": "The set of nonzero vectors in the same ray as the nonzero vector `x` is connected.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_space.path_connected", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_diam", "description": "Diameter of the convex hull of a set `s` equals the emetric diameter of `s.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_space.loc_path_connected", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_ediam", "description": "Emetric diameter of the convex hull of a set `s` equals the emetric diameter of `s.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_exists_dist_ge2", "description": " Given a point `x` in the convex hull of `s` and a point `y` in the convex hull of `t`,\nthere exist points `x' \u2208 s` and `y' \u2208 t` at distance at least `dist x y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_on_dist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_connected_set_of_same_ray", "description": "The set of vectors in the same ray as `x` is connected.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_on_norm", "description": " The norm on a real normed space is convex on any convex set. See also `seminorm.convex_on`\nand `convex_on_univ_norm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.cthickening", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_on_univ_dist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_hull_exists_dist_ge", "description": " Given a point `x` in the convex hull of `s` and a point `y`, there exists a point\nof `s` at distance at least `dist x y` from `y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_inter_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_cond_eq_cond_inter'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_eq_inv_mul_cond_mul", "description": "**Bayes' Theorem**", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_apply", "description": "The axiomatic definition of conditional probability derived from a measure-theoretic one.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_cond_eq_cond_inter", "description": " Conditioning first on `s` and then on `t` results in the same measure as conditioning\non `s \u2229 t`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_add_cond_compl_eq", "description": "A version of the law of total probability.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_mul_eq_inter'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond", "description": " The conditional probability measure of measure `\u03bc` on set `s` is `\u03bc` restricted to `s`\nand scaled by the inverse of `\u03bc s` (to make it a probability measure):\n`(\u03bc s)\u207b\u00b9 \u2022 \u03bc.restrict s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.inter_pos_of_cond_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_pos_of_inter_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_mul_eq_inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.cond_is_probability_measure", "description": " The conditional probability measure of any finite measure on any set of positive measure\nis a probability measure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prime.is_prime_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_prime_pow.ne_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.prime.is_prime_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_prime_pow.decidable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_prime_pow.two_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_prime_pow", "description": " `n` is a prime power if there is a prime `p` and a positive natural `k` such that `n` can be\nwritten as `p^k`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_prime_pow.pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_prime_pow_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_unit.not_is_prime_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_prime_pow.dvd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.disjoint_divisors_filter_prime_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_prime_pow.one_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_is_prime_pow_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_prime_pow_nat_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_prime_pow.ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_prime_pow_nat_iff_bounded", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_prime_pow_iff_pow_succ", "description": " An equivalent definition for prime powers: `n` is a prime power iff there is a prime `p` and a\nnatural `k` such that `n` can be written as `p^(k+1)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_prime_pow.pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_prime_pow.not_unit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_is_prime_pow_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.topology_of_closure_operator_sieves", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.is_closed_iff_close_eq_self", "description": "The sieve `S` is closed iff its closure is equal to itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.le_topology_of_closed_sieves_is_sheaf", "description": "If presheaf of `J\u2081`-closed sieves is a `J\u2082`-sheaf then `J\u2081 \u2264 J\u2082`. Note the converse is true by\n`classifier_is_sheaf` and `is_sheaf_of_le`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.closed_sieves_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.closed_sieves_map_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.close_eq_top_iff_mem", "description": "The sieve `S` is in the topology iff its closure is the maximal sieve. This shows that the closure\noperator determines the topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.topology_eq_iff_same_sheaves", "description": "If being a sheaf for `J\u2081` is equivalent to being a sheaf for `J\u2082`, then `J\u2081 = J\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.close_close", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.close_is_closed", "description": "The closure of a sieve is closed.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.le_close_of_is_closed", "description": "The closure of a sieve `S` is the largest closed sieve which contains `S` (justifying the name\n\"closure\").", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.topology_of_closure_operator_close", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.is_closed_pullback", "description": "Being `J`-closed is stable under pullback.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.closure_operator", "description": "A Grothendieck topology induces a natural family of closure operators on sieves.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.close", "description": "The `J`-closure of a sieve is the collection of arrows which it covers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.closure_operator_apply_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.topology_of_closure_operator_self", "description": "The topology given by the closure operator `J.close` on a Grothendieck topology is the same as `J`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.covers_iff_mem_of_closed", "description": "If `S` is `J\u2081`-closed, then `S` covers exactly the arrows it contains.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.classifier_is_sheaf", "description": "The presheaf of `J`-closed sieves is a `J`-sheaf.\nThe proof of this is adapted from [MM92], Chatper III, Section 7, Lemma 1.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.le_close", "description": "Any sieve is smaller than its closure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.pullback_close", "description": "Closing under `J` is stable under pullback.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.closed_iff_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.close_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.close_eq_self_of_is_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.is_closed", "description": "A sieve is closed for the Grothendieck topology if it contains every arrow it covers.\nIn the case of the usual topology on a topological space, this means that the open cover contains\nevery open set which it covers.\n\nNote this has no relation to a closed subset of a topological space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.topology_of_closure_operator", "description": "A closure (increasing, inflationary and idempotent) operation on sieves that commutes with pullback\ninduces a Grothendieck topology.\nIn fact, such operations are in bijection with Grothendieck topologies.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.monotone_close", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.closed_sieves", "description": "The presheaf sending each object to the set of `J`-closed sieves on it. This presheaf is a `J`-sheaf\n(and will turn out to be a subobject classifier for the category of `J`-sheaves).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "theorems_100.erdos_szekeres", "description": "**Erd\u0151s\u2013Szekeres Theorem**: Given a sequence of more than `r * s` distinct values, there is an\nincreasing sequence of length longer than `r` or a decreasing sequence of length longer than `s`.\n\nProof idea:\nWe label each value in the sequence with two numbers specifying the longest increasing\nsubsequence ending there, and the longest decreasing subsequence ending there.\nWe then show the pair of labels must be unique. Now if there is no increasing sequence longer than\n`r` and no decreasing sequence longer than `s`, then there are at most `r * s` possible labels,\nwhich is a contradiction if there are more than `r * s` elements.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "fdRep.char_orthonormal", "description": " Orthogonality of characters for irreducible representations of finite group over an\nalgebraically closed field whose characteristic doesn't divide the order of the group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.char_mul_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.char_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.character", "description": " The character of a representation `V : fdRep k G` is the function associating to `g : G` the\ntrace of the linear map `V.\u03c1 g`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.char_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.char_tensor", "description": "The character is multiplicative under the tensor product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.char_iso", "description": "The character of isomorphic representations is the same.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.char_lin_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.char_conj", "description": "The character of a representation is constant on conjugacy classes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fdRep.average_char_eq_finrank_invariants", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.class_number_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.class_number_eq_one_iff", "description": "The class number of a number field is `1` iff the ring of integers is a PID.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.class_number", "description": "The class number of a number field is the (finite) cardinality of the class group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "number_field.ring_of_integers.class_group.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "counterexample.proper_divisors_35", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.proper_divisors_105", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.cyclotomic_15", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.proper_divisors_21", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.cyclotomic_105", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.not_forall_coeff_cyclotomic_neg_one_zero_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.nat.fact_prime_five", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.coeff_cyclotomic_105", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.cyclotomic_7", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.cyclotomic_21", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.cyclotomic_5", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.nat.fact_prime_seven", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.cyclotomic_35", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.cyclotomic_3", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.proper_divisors_15", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "functor.map_equiv", "description": "Apply a functor to an `equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "functor.map_equiv_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bifunctor.map_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bifunctor.map_equiv", "description": "Apply a bifunctor to a pair of `equiv`s.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bifunctor.map_equiv_refl_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "functor.map_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "functor.map_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bifunctor.map_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric_space.to_metrizable_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inducing.pseudo_metrizable_space", "description": " Given an inducing map of a topological space into a pseudo metrizable space, the source space\nis also pseudo metrizable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.metrizable_space_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.pseudo_metrizable_space", "description": " A topological space is *pseudo metrizable* if there exists a pseudo metric space structure\ncompatible with the topology. To endow such a space with a compatible distance, use\n`letI : pseudo_metric_space X := topological_space.pseudo_metrizable_space_pseudo_metric X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.pseudo_metrizable_space.exists_pseudo_metric", "description": " A topological space is *pseudo metrizable* if there exists a pseudo metric space structure\ncompatible with the topology. To endow such a space with a compatible distance, use\n`letI : pseudo_metric_space X := topological_space.pseudo_metrizable_space_pseudo_metric X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.pseudo_metrizable_space.subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.metrizable_space_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.metrizable_space.to_pseudo_metrizable_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.t2_space_of_metrizable_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.metrizable_space_metric", "description": "Construct on a metrizable space a metric compatible with the topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.pseudo_metrizable_space_pseudo_metric", "description": "Construct on a metrizable space a metric compatible with the topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.exists_embedding_l_infty", "description": "A T\u2083 topological space with second countable topology can be embedded into `l^\u221e = \u2115 \u2192\u1d47 \u211d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.metrizable_space.subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.ennreal.metrizable_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.pseudo_metrizable_space_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.pseudo_metrizable_space.first_countable_topology", "description": "Every pseudo-metrizable space is first countable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.metrizable_space_of_t3_second_countable", "description": " *Urysohn's metrization theorem* (Tychonoff's version): a T\u2083 topological space with second\ncountable topology `X` is metrizable, i.e., there exists a metric space structure that generates the\nsame topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "embedding.metrizable_space", "description": " Given an embedding of a topological space into a metrizable space, the source space is also\nmetrizable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.pseudo_metrizable_space_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.metrizable_space", "description": " A topological space is metrizable if there exists a metric space structure compatible with the\ntopology. To endow such a space with a compatible distance, use\n`letI : metric_space X := topological_space.metrizable_space_metric X`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.metrizable_space.exists_metric", "description": " A topological space is metrizable if there exists a metric space structure compatible with the\ntopology. To endow such a space with a compatible distance, use\n`letI : metric_space X := topological_space.metrizable_space_metric X`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pseudo_metric_space.to_pseudo_metrizable_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.finite_type_of_monoid_fg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.span_inv_submonoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.smul_to_inv_submonoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.equiv_inv_submonoid", "description": "There is an equivalence of monoids between the image of `M` and `inv_submonoid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.mul_to_inv_submonoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.mem_inv_submonoid_iff_exists_mk'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.surj'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.submonoid_map_le_is_unit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.to_inv_submonoid_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.to_inv_submonoid_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.to_inv_submonoid", "description": "There is a canonical map from `M` to `inv_submonoid` sending `x` to `1 / x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.to_inv_submonoid_eq_mk'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.inv_submonoid", "description": "The submonoid of `S = M\u207b\u00b9R` consisting of `{ 1 / x | x \u2208 M }`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "extend_from_extends", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_extend_from", "description": " If `f` converges to some `y` as `x` tends to `x\u2080` within `A`,\nthen `f` tends to `extend_from A f x` as `x` tends to `x\u2080`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "extend_from_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "extend_from", "description": " Extend a function from a set `A`. The resulting function `g` is such that\nat any `x\u2080`, if `f` converges to some `y` as `x` tends to `x\u2080` within `A`,\nthen `g x\u2080` is defined to be one of these `y`. Else, `g x\u2080` could be anything.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on_extend_from", "description": " If `f` is a function to a T\u2083 space `Y` which has a limit within `A` at any\npoint of a set `B \u2286 closure A`, then `extend_from A f` is continuous on `B`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_extend_from", "description": " If a function `f` to a T\u2083 space `Y` has a limit within a\ndense set `A` for any `x`, then `extend_from A f` is continuous.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.eq_zero_iff_gcd_ne_one", "description": " If `p` is a prime and `a` is an integer, then `a : zmod p` is zero if and only if\n`gcd a p \u2260 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.ne_zero_of_gcd_eq_one", "description": "If an integer `a` and a prime `p` satisfy `gcd a p = 1`, then `a : zmod p` is nonzero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.eq_zero_of_gcd_ne_one", "description": "If an integer `a` and a prime `p` satisfy `gcd a p \u2260 1`, then `a : zmod p` is zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.trunc_cases", "description": "`trunc_cases e` performs case analysis on a `trunc` expression `e`,\nattempting the following strategies:\n1. when the goal is a subsingleton, calling `induction e using trunc.rec_on_subsingleton`,\n2. when the goal does not depend on `e`, calling `fapply trunc.lift_on e`,\n   and using `intro` and `clear` afterwards to make the goals look like we used `induction`,\n3. otherwise, falling through to `trunc.rec_on`, and in the new invariance goal\n   calling `cases h_p` on the useless `h_p : true` hypothesis,\n   and then attempting to simplify the `eq.rec`.\n\n`trunc_cases e with h` names the new hypothesis `h`.\nIf `e` is a local hypothesis already,\n`trunc_cases` defaults to reusing the same name.\n\n`trunc_cases e with h h_a h_b` will use the names `h_a` and `h_b` for the new hypothesis\nin the invariance goal if `trunc_cases` uses `trunc.lift_on` or `trunc.rec_on`.\n\nFinally, if the new hypothesis from inside the `trunc` is a type class,\n`trunc_cases` resets the instance cache so that it is immediately available.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.trunc_cases", "description": "`trunc_cases e` performs case analysis on a `trunc` expression `e`,\nattempting the following strategies:\n1. when the goal is a subsingleton, calling `induction e using trunc.rec_on_subsingleton`,\n2. when the goal does not depend on `e`, calling `fapply trunc.lift_on e`,\n   and using `intro` and `clear` afterwards to make the goals look like we used `induction`,\n3. otherwise, falling through to `trunc.rec_on`, and in the new invariance goal\n   calling `cases h_p` on the useless `h_p : true` hypothesis,\n   and then attempting to simplify the `eq.rec`.\n\n`trunc_cases e with h` names the new hypothesis `h`.\nIf `e` is a local hypothesis already,\n`trunc_cases` defaults to reusing the same name.\n\n`trunc_cases e with h h_a h_b` will use the names `h_a` and `h_b` for the new hypothesis\nin the invariance goal if `trunc_cases` uses `trunc.lift_on` or `trunc.rec_on`.\n\nFinally, if the new hypothesis from inside the `trunc` is a type class,\n`trunc_cases` resets the instance cache so that it is immediately available.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon_Type_equivalence_CommMon.functor", "description": "Converting a commutative monoid object in `Type` to a bundled commutative monoid.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon_Type_inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon_Type_equivalence_CommMon", "description": "The category of internal commutative monoid objects in `Type`\nis equivalent to the category of \"native\" bundled commutative monoids.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon_Type_equivalence_Mon", "description": "The category of internal monoid objects in `Type`\nis equivalent to the category of \"native\" bundled monoids.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon_Type_equivalence_Mon.functor", "description": "Converting a monoid object in `Type` to a bundled monoid.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon_Type_equivalence_Mon_forget", "description": "The equivalence `Mon_ (Type u) \u224c Mon.{u}`\nis naturally compatible with the forgetful functors to `Type u`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon_Type_inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon_Type_equivalence_Mon.Mon_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon_Type_equivalence_CommMon.inverse", "description": "Converting a bundled commutative monoid to a commutative monoid object in `Type`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon_Type_equivalence_CommMon.CommMon_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Mon_Type_equivalence_Mon.inverse", "description": "Converting a bundled monoid to a monoid object in `Type`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommMon_Type_equivalence_CommMon_forget", "description": "The equivalences `Mon_ (Type u) \u224c Mon.{u}` and `CommMon_ (Type u) \u224c CommMon.{u}`\nare naturally compatible with the forgetful functors to `Mon` and `Mon_ (Type u)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fermat_42.minimal", "description": " We say a solution to `a ^ 4 + b ^ 4 = c ^ 2` is minimal if there is no other solution with\na smaller `c` (in absolute value).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fermat_42.exists_minimal", "description": "if we have a solution to `a ^ 4 + b ^ 4 = c ^ 2` then there must be a minimal one.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fermat_42.ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fermat_42.not_minimal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fermat_42", "description": " Shorthand for three non-zero integers `a`, `b`, and `c` satisfying `a ^ 4 + b ^ 4 = c ^ 2`.\nWe will show that no integers satisfy this equation. Clearly Fermat's Last theorem for n = 4\nfollows.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fermat_42.comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fermat_42.exists_pos_odd_minimal", "description": " We can assume that a minimal solution to `a ^ 4 + b ^ 4 = c ^ 2` has\n`a` odd and `c` positive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_fermat_42", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fermat_42.coprime_of_minimal", "description": "a minimal solution to `a ^ 4 + b ^ 4 = c ^ 2` must have `a` and `b` coprime.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fermat_42.exists_odd_minimal", "description": "We can assume that a minimal solution to `a ^ 4 + b ^ 4 = c ^ 2` has `a` odd.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fermat_42.neg_of_minimal", "description": "We can assume that a minimal solution to `a ^ 4 + b ^ 4 = c ^ 2` has positive `c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.coprime_of_sq_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.coprime_of_sq_sum'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fermat_42.minimal_comm", "description": "We can swap `a` and `b` in a minimal solution to `a ^ 4 + b ^ 4 = c ^ 2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fermat_42.mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_fermat_4", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cSup_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cInf_eq_least_of_bdd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cSup_eq_greatest_of_bdd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cInf_of_not_bdd_below", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cInf_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cSup_of_not_bdd_above", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cInf_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.conditionally_complete_linear_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cSup_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_ring", "description": "The free ring over a type `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_ring.lift_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_ring.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_ring.map_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_ring.lift_comp_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_ring.lift", "description": "The ring homomorphism `free_ring \u03b1 \u2192+* R` induced from a map `\u03b1 \u2192 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_ring.map", "description": "The canonical ring homomorphism `free_ring \u03b1 \u2192+* free_ring \u03b2` generated by a map `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_ring.of_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_ring.hom_ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_ring.induction_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_ring.ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_ring.of", "description": "The canonical map from \u03b1 to `free_ring \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.factorization_central_binom_eq_zero_of_two_mul_lt", "description": "If a prime `p` has positive multiplicity in the `n`th central binomial coefficient,\n`p` is no more than `2 * n`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.factorization_choose_le_one", "description": "Primes greater than about `sqrt n` appear only to multiplicity 0 or 1 in the binomial coefficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.factorization_choose_of_lt_three_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.factorization_choose_eq_zero_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.le_two_mul_of_factorization_central_binom_pos", "description": "Contrapositive form of `nat.factorization_central_binom_eq_zero_of_two_mul_lt`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.pow_factorization_choose_le", "description": "A `pow` form of `nat.factorization_choose_le`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.factorization_choose_le_log", "description": "A logarithmic upper bound on the multiplicity of a prime in a binomial coefficient.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.factorization_factorial_eq_zero_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.prod_pow_factorization_choose", "description": "A binomial coefficient is the product of its prime factors, which are at most `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.prod_pow_factorization_central_binom", "description": " The `n`th central binomial coefficient is the product of its prime factors, which are\nat most `2n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.factorization_central_binom_of_two_mul_self_lt_three_mul", "description": "Primes greater than about `2 * n / 3` and less than `n` do not appear in the factorization of\n`central_binom n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.monoidal_functor", "description": "A multiplicative morphism between monoids gives a monoidal functor between the corresponding\ndiscrete monoidal categories.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrete.add_monoidal_functor_to_lax_monoidal_functor_\u03b5", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrete.add_monoidal_functor_comp", "description": "The monoidal natural isomorphism corresponding to\ncomposing two additive morphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrete.add_monoidal_tensor_add_unit_as", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.monoidal_functor_comp", "description": "The monoidal natural isomorphism corresponding to composing two multiplicative morphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.monoidal_functor_to_lax_monoidal_functor_\u03bc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrete.add_monoidal_functor_to_lax_monoidal_functor_to_functor_obj_as", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.monoidal_tensor_unit_as", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.monoidal_tensor_obj_as", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrete.add_monoidal_functor_to_lax_monoidal_functor_\u03bc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.monoidal_functor_to_lax_monoidal_functor_\u03b5", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.monoidal_functor_to_lax_monoidal_functor_to_functor_obj_as", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrete.add_monoidal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.monoidal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrete.add_monoidal_functor", "description": "An additive morphism between add_monoids gives a\n  monoidal functor between the corresponding discrete monoidal categories.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrete.add_monoidal_functor_to_lax_monoidal_functor_to_functor_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.discrete.monoidal_functor_to_lax_monoidal_functor_to_functor_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrete.add_monoidal_tensor_obj_as", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian_mul_mul_conj_transpose", "description": "Note this is more general than `is_self_adjoint.conjugate` as `B` can be rectangular.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian.coe_re_apply_self", "description": "The diagonal elements of a complex hermitian matrix are real.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian.apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian.adjugate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian.transpose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian_transpose_mul_self", "description": "Note this is more general than `is_self_adjoint.star_mul_self` as `B` can be rectangular.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian_transpose_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian_transpose_add_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian_from_blocks_iff", "description": "This is the `iff` version of `matrix.is_hermitian.from_blocks`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian_conj_transpose_mul_mul", "description": "Note this is more general than `is_self_adjoint.conjugate'` as `B` can be rectangular.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian.is_self_adjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian.map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian.conj_transpose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian_submatrix_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian.sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian_one", "description": " Note this is more general for matrices than `is_self_adjoint_one` as it does not\nrequire `fintype n`, which is necessary for `monoid (matrix n n R)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian_iff_is_symmetric", "description": "A matrix is hermitian iff the corresponding linear map is self adjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian_diagonal_of_self_adjoint", "description": "A diagonal matrix is hermitian if the entries are self-adjoint", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian.neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian", "description": " A matrix is hermitian if it is equal to its conjugate transpose. On the reals, this definition\ncaptures symmetric matrices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian.submatrix", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian.coe_re_diag", "description": "The diagonal elements of a complex hermitian matrix are real.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian_mul_conj_transpose_self", "description": "Note this is more general than `is_self_adjoint.mul_star_self` as `B` can be rectangular.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian.from_blocks", "description": "A block matrix `A.from_blocks B C D` is hermitian,\n   if `A` and `D` are hermitian and `B\u1d34 = C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian_conj_transpose_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian.inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian_add_transpose_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian_diagonal", "description": " A diagonal matrix is hermitian if the entries have the trivial `star` operation\n(such as on the reals).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.is_hermitian.eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pprod.forall'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pprod.exists'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pprod.mk.eta", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pprod.exists", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pprod.forall", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.pprod_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.form_perm_apply_mem_eq_next", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.to_list_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cycle.form_perm_eq_self_of_not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.is_cycle.exists_unique_cycle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cycle.form_perm_apply_mem_eq_next", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.iso_cycle'", "description": "Any cyclic `f : perm \u03b1` is isomorphic to the nontrivial `cycle \u03b1`\nthat corresponds to repeated application of `f`.\nThe forward direction is implemented by finding this `cycle \u03b1` using `fintype.choose`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.nodup_to_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.to_list_form_perm_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.form_perm_disjoint_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.is_cycle.exists_unique_cycle_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.length_to_list_pos_of_mem_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.length_to_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.to_list_form_perm_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.nodup_to_cycle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.nontrivial_to_cycle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.repr_perm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.to_list_form_perm_is_rotated_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.cycle_type_form_perm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cycle.form_perm", "description": "A cycle `s : cycle \u03b1` , given `nodup s` can be interpreted as a `equiv.perm \u03b1`\nwhere each element in the list is permuted to the next one, defined as `form_perm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.is_cycle.exists_unique_cycle_nontrivial_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.to_cycle_eq_to_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.pow_apply_mem_to_list_iff_mem_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.form_perm_to_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.to_list_form_perm_nontrivial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.same_cycle.to_list_is_rotated", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.to_list_ne_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.cycle_of_form_perm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cycle.form_perm_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.is_cycle_form_perm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.two_le_length_to_list_iff_mem_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cycle.support_form_perm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.pairwise_same_cycle_form_perm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cycle.is_cycle_form_perm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.nth_le_to_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.to_list_pow_apply_eq_rotate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.to_list_eq_nil_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.mem_to_list_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.to_list", "description": "`equiv.perm.to_list (f : perm \u03b1) (x : \u03b1)` generates the list `[x, f x, f (f x), ...]`\nuntil looping. That means when `f x = x`, `to_list f x = []`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.next_to_list_eq_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.iso_cycle", "description": "Any cyclic `f : perm \u03b1` is isomorphic to the nontrivial `cycle \u03b1`\nthat corresponds to repeated application of `f`.\nThe forward direction is implemented by `equiv.perm.to_cycle`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.to_list_nth_le_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cycle.form_perm_eq_form_perm_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.to_cycle", "description": "Given a cyclic `f : perm \u03b1`, generate the `cycle \u03b1` in the order\nof application of `f`. Implemented by finding an element `x : \u03b1`\nin the support of `f` in `finset.univ`, and iterating on using\n`equiv.perm.to_list f x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cycle.form_perm_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cycle.form_perm_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.is_domain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.verschiebung_nonzero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.shift_coeff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.shift", "description": "`witt_vector.verschiebung` translates the entries of a Witt vector upward, inserting 0s in the gaps.\n`witt_vector.shift` does the opposite, removing the first entries.\nThis is mainly useful as an auxiliary construction for `witt_vector.verschiebung_nonzero`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.verschiebung_shift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.eq_iterate_verschiebung", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.no_zero_divisors", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hensels_lemma", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "limit_zero_of_norm_tendsto_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "padic_polynomial_dist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_vector_bundle.pullback", "description": " For a smooth vector bundle `E` over a manifold `B` and a smooth map `f : B' \u2192 B`, the pullback\nvector bundle `f *\u1d56 E` is a smooth vector bundle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.Indep_set.condexp_indicator_filtration_of_set_ae_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.measure_limsup_eq_one", "description": " **The second Borel-Cantelli lemma**: Given a sequence of independent sets `(s\u2099)` such that\n`\u2211 n, \u03bc s\u2099 = \u221e`, `limsup s\u2099` has measure 1.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.Indep_fun.indep_comap_natural_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.Indep_fun.condexp_natural_ae_eq_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mk_iff.list_option_merge", "description": "Iterate over two lists, if the first element of the first list is `none`, insert `none` into the\nresult and continue with the tail of first list. Otherwise, wrap the first element of the second\nlist with `some` and continue with the tails of both lists. Return when either list is empty.\n\nExample:\n```lean\nlist_option_merge [none, some (), none, some ()] [0, 1, 2, 3, 4] = [none, (some 0), none, (some 1)]\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.command.mk_iff_of_inductive_prop", "description": "`mk_iff_of_inductive_prop i r` makes an `iff` rule for the inductively-defined proposition `i`.\nThe new rule `r` has the shape `\u2200ps is, i as \u2194 \u22c1_j, \u2203cs, is = cs`, where `ps` are the type\nparameters, `is` are the indices, `j` ranges over all possible constructors, the `cs` are the\nparameters for each of the constructors, and the equalities `is = cs` are the instantiations for\neach constructor for each of the indices to the inductive type `i`.\n\nIn each case, we remove constructor parameters (i.e. `cs`) when the corresponding equality would\nbe just `c = i` for some index `i`.\n\nFor example, `mk_iff_of_inductive_prop` on `list.chain` produces:\n\n```lean\n\u2200 {\u03b1 : Type*} (R : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1 \u2192 Prop) (a : \u03b1) (l : list \u03b1),\n  chain R a l \u2194 l = [] \u2228 \u2203{b : \u03b1} {l' : list \u03b1}, R a b \u2227 chain R b l \u2227 l = b :: l'\n```\n\nSee also the `mk_iff` user attribute.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mk_iff.to_cases", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mk_iff.compact_relation", "description": " `compact_relation bs as_ps`: Produce a relation of the form:\n```lean\nR as := \u2203 bs, \u039b_i a_i = p_i[bs]\n```\nThis relation is user-visible, so we compact it by removing each `b_j` where a `p_i = b_j`, and\nhence `a_i = b_j`. We need to take care when there are `p_i` and `p_j` with `p_i = p_j = b_k`.\n\nTODO: this is a variant of `compact_relation` in `coinductive_predicates.lean`, export it there.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_iff_of_inductive_prop", "description": "`mk_iff_of_inductive_prop i r` makes an `iff` rule for the inductively-defined proposition `i`.\nThe new rule `r` has the shape `\u2200ps is, i as \u2194 \u22c1_j, \u2203cs, is = cs`, where `ps` are the type\nparameters, `is` are the indices, `j` ranges over all possible constructors, the `cs` are the\nparameters for each of the constructors, and the equalities `is = cs` are the instantiations for\neach constructor for each of the indices to the inductive type `i`.\n\nIn each case, we remove constructor parameters (i.e. `cs`) when the corresponding equality would\nbe just `c = i` for some index `i`.\n\nFor example, `mk_iff_of_inductive_prop` on `list.chain` produces:\n\n```lean\n\u2200 {\u03b1 : Type*} (R : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1 \u2192 Prop) (a : \u03b1) (l : list \u03b1),\n  chain R a l \u2194 l = [] \u2228 \u2203{b : \u03b1} {l' : list \u03b1}, R a b \u2227 chain R b l \u2227 l = b :: l'\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mk_iff.to_inductive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mk_iff_attr", "description": "Applying the `mk_iff` attribute to an inductively-defined proposition `mk_iff` makes an `iff` rule\n`r` with the shape `\u2200ps is, i as \u2194 \u22c1_j, \u2203cs, is = cs`, where `ps` are the type parameters, `is` are\nthe indices, `j` ranges over all possible constructors, the `cs` are the parameters for each of the\nconstructors, and the equalities `is = cs` are the instantiations for each constructor for each of\nthe indices to the inductive type `i`.\n\nIn each case, we remove constructor parameters (i.e. `cs`) when the corresponding equality would\nbe just `c = i` for some index `i`.\n\nFor example, if we try the following:\n```lean\n@[mk_iff] structure foo (m n : \u2115) : Prop :=\n(equal : m = n)\n(sum_eq_two : m + n = 2)\n```\n\nThen `#check foo_iff` returns:\n```lean\nfoo_iff : \u2200 (m n : \u2115), foo m n \u2194 m = n \u2227 m + n = 2\n```\n\nYou can add an optional string after `mk_iff` to change the name of the generated lemma.\nFor example, if we try the following:\n```lean\n@[mk_iff bar] structure foo (m n : \u2115) : Prop :=\n(equal : m = n)\n(sum_eq_two : m + n = 2)\n```\n\nThen `#check bar` returns:\n```lean\nbar : \u2200 (m n : \u2115), foo m n \u2194 m = n \u2227 m + n = 2\n```\n\nSee also the user command `mk_iff_of_inductive_prop`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mk_iff_of_inductive_prop_cmd", "description": "`mk_iff_of_inductive_prop i r` makes an `iff` rule for the inductively-defined proposition `i`.\nThe new rule `r` has the shape `\u2200ps is, i as \u2194 \u22c1_j, \u2203cs, is = cs`, where `ps` are the type\nparameters, `is` are the indices, `j` ranges over all possible constructors, the `cs` are the\nparameters for each of the constructors, and the equalities `is = cs` are the instantiations for\neach constructor for each of the indices to the inductive type `i`.\n\nIn each case, we remove constructor parameters (i.e. `cs`) when the corresponding equality would\nbe just `c = i` for some index `i`.\n\nFor example, `mk_iff_of_inductive_prop` on `list.chain` produces:\n\n```lean\n\u2200 {\u03b1 : Type*} (R : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1 \u2192 Prop) (a : \u03b1) (l : list \u03b1),\n  chain R a l \u2194 l = [] \u2228 \u2203{b : \u03b1} {l' : list \u03b1}, R a b \u2227 chain R b l \u2227 l = b :: l'\n```\n\nSee also the `mk_iff` user attribute.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mk_iff.select", "description": " `select m n` runs `tactic.right` `m` times, and then `tactic.left` `(n-m)` times.\nFails if `n < m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mk_iff.constr_to_prop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.attribute.mk_iff", "description": "Applying the `mk_iff` attribute to an inductively-defined proposition `mk_iff` makes an `iff` rule\n`r` with the shape `\u2200ps is, i as \u2194 \u22c1_j, \u2203cs, is = cs`, where `ps` are the type parameters, `is` are\nthe indices, `j` ranges over all possible constructors, the `cs` are the parameters for each of the\nconstructors, and the equalities `is = cs` are the instantiations for each constructor for each of\nthe indices to the inductive type `i`.\n\nIn each case, we remove constructor parameters (i.e. `cs`) when the corresponding equality would\nbe just `c = i` for some index `i`.\n\nFor example, if we try the following:\n```lean\n@[mk_iff] structure foo (m n : \u2115) : Prop :=\n(equal : m = n)\n(sum_eq_two : m + n = 2)\n```\n\nThen `#check foo_iff` returns:\n```lean\nfoo_iff : \u2200 (m n : \u2115), foo m n \u2194 m = n \u2227 m + n = 2\n```\n\nYou can add an optional string after `mk_iff` to change the name of the generated lemma.\nFor example, if we try the following:\n```lean\n@[mk_iff bar] structure foo (m n : \u2115) : Prop :=\n(equal : m = n)\n(sum_eq_two : m + n = 2)\n```\n\nThen `#check bar` returns:\n```lean\nbar : \u2200 (m n : \u2115), foo m n \u2194 m = n \u2227 m + n = 2\n```\n\nSee also the user command `mk_iff_of_inductive_prop`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "psigma.lex_wf", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma.rev_lex_wf", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma.has_well_founded", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma.rev_lex_accessible", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma.mk_skip_left", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma.rev_lex", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma.rev_lex.left", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma.rev_lex.right", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma.lex", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma.lex.left", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma.lex.right", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma.skip_left_wf", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma.skip_left", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma.lex_accessible", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma.lex_ndep_wf", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma.lex_ndep", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_reverse_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.sublist_forall\u2082.is_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.rel_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_nil_left_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082.mp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.left_unique_forall\u2082'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_cons_right_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.rel_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_and_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_iff_nth_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.rel_bind", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.rel_append", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082.flip", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.sublist_forall\u2082.is_trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_map_right_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.rel_foldr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.rel_filter_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "relator.left_unique.forall\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_of_length_eq_of_nth_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "relator.right_unique.forall\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.rel_filter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082.imp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_drop_append", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.sublist.sublist_forall\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_same", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "relator.bi_unique.forall\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.rel_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_cons_left_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_take_append", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.sublist_forall\u2082", "description": "Given a relation `R`, `sublist_forall\u2082 r l\u2081 l\u2082` indicates that there is a sublist of `l\u2082` such\n that `forall\u2082 r l\u2081 l\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.sublist_forall\u2082.nil", "description": "Given a relation `R`, `sublist_forall\u2082 r l\u2081 l\u2082` indicates that there is a sublist of `l\u2082` such\n that `forall\u2082 r l\u2081 l\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.sublist_forall\u2082.cons", "description": "Given a relation `R`, `sublist_forall\u2082 r l\u2081 l\u2082` indicates that there is a sublist of `l\u2082` such\n that `forall\u2082 r l\u2081 l\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.sublist_forall\u2082.cons_right", "description": "Given a relation `R`, `sublist_forall\u2082 r l\u2081 l\u2082` indicates that there is a sublist of `l\u2082` such\n that `forall\u2082 r l\u2081 l\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082.nth_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_iff_zip", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_zip", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_drop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.rel_join", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.right_unique_forall\u2082'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.rel_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.sublist_forall\u2082_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_eq_eq_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_take", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082.length_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.rel_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_map_left_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.rel_foldl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_nil_right_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.forall\u2082_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.tail_sublist_forall\u2082_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.traverse_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.is_lawful_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.comp_traverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.monad", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.fmap_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.map_traverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.traverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.map_comp_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.id_traverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.lift_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.is_lawful_monad", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.pure_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.bind_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.ae_fin_strongly_measurable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable.ae_fin_strongly_measurable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.mem_\u2112p.fin_strongly_measurable_of_strongly_measurable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.Lp.fin_strongly_measurable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.coe_least_of_bdd_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.least_of_bdd", "description": " A computable version of `exists_least_of_bdd`: given a decidable predicate on the\nintegers, with an explicit lower bound and a proof that it is somewhere true, return\nthe least value for which the predicate is true.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.greatest_of_bdd", "description": " A computable version of `exists_greatest_of_bdd`: given a decidable predicate on the\nintegers, with an explicit upper bound and a proof that it is somewhere true, return\nthe greatest value for which the predicate is true.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.exists_greatest_of_bdd", "description": " If `P : \u2124 \u2192 Prop` is a predicate such that the set `{m : P m}` is bounded above and nonempty,\nthen this set has the greatest element. This lemma uses classical logic to avoid assumption\n`[decidable_pred P]`. See `int.greatest_of_bdd` for a constructive counterpart.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.exists_least_of_bdd", "description": " If `P : \u2124 \u2192 Prop` is a predicate such that the set `{m : P m}` is bounded below and nonempty,\nthen this set has the least element. This lemma uses classical logic to avoid assumption\n`[decidable_pred P]`. See `int.least_of_bdd` for a constructive counterpart.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.coe_greatest_of_bdd_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "wbtw.dist_le_max_dist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear.sbtw_of_dist_eq_of_dist_lt", "description": " Given three collinear points, two (not equal) with distance `r` from `p` and one with\ndistance less than `r` from `p`, the third point is strictly between the other two points.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sbtw.dist_lt_max_dist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "collinear.wbtw_of_dist_eq_of_dist_le", "description": " Given three collinear points, two (not equal) with distance `r` from `p` and one with\ndistance at most `r` from `p`, the third point is weakly between the other two points.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.equitable_on_iff_exists_image_subset_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.equitable_on.le_add_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.equitable_on_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.equitable_on", "description": "A set is equitable if no element value is more than one bigger than another.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.equitable_on_iff_le_le_add_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.equitable_on.le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.equitable_on_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.equitable_on_iff_exists_eq_eq_add_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.subsingleton.equitable_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.equitable_on_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.equitable_on_iff_exists_le_le_add_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_pushout.is_van_kampen.is_pullback_of_mono_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adhesive.is_pullback_of_is_pushout_of_mono_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.type.adhesive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_pushout.is_van_kampen.is_pullback_of_mono_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adhesive.mono_of_is_pushout_of_mono_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coprod_iff_is_pushout", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_pushout.is_van_kampen", "description": " A convenience formulation for a pushout being a van Kampen colimit.\nSee `is_pushout.is_van_kampen_iff` below.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adhesive.is_pullback_of_is_pushout_of_mono_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adhesive.to_regular_mono_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adhesive.van_kampen'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_pushout.is_van_kampen_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adhesive", "description": " A category is adhesive if it has pushouts and pullbacks along monomorphisms,\nand such pushouts are van Kampen.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adhesive.has_pullback_of_mono_left", "description": " A category is adhesive if it has pushouts and pullbacks along monomorphisms,\nand such pushouts are van Kampen.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adhesive.has_pushout_of_mono_left", "description": " A category is adhesive if it has pushouts and pullbacks along monomorphisms,\nand such pushouts are van Kampen.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adhesive.van_kampen", "description": " A category is adhesive if it has pushouts and pullbacks along monomorphisms,\nand such pushouts are van Kampen.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_pushout.is_van_kampen_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adhesive.mono_of_is_pushout_of_mono_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_pushout.is_van_kampen.mono_of_mono_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_pushout.is_van_kampen.mono_of_mono_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_pushout.is_van_kampen.flip", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.get_rewrites", "description": "Get all rewrites that start at the given expression and use one of the given rewrite rules.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.rewrite_search_attr", "description": "Use this attribute to make `rewrite_search` use this definition during search.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.collect_rules", "description": "Collect rewrite rules to use from the environment.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.coe_submodule_fg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.coe_submodule_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.coe_submodule_is_principal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.coe_submodule_le_coe_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.mem_span_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.coe_submodule_le_coe_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.coe_submodule_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.coe_submodule_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.coe_submodule_is_principal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.coe_submodule_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fraction_ring.coe_submodule_strict_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.coe_submodule_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.coe_submodule_span_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.coe_submodule", "description": "Map from ideals of `R` to submodules of `S` induced by `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.mem_span_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.coe_submodule_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.coe_submodule_span", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.coe_submodule_strict_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.mem_coe_submodule", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.coe_submodule_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.is_noetherian_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_p.quotient", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.quotient.index_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_p.quotient'", "description": " If an ideal does not contain any coercions of natural numbers other than zero, then its quotient\ninherits the characteristic of the underlying ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.plus_functor_whisker_left_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.plus_comp_iso", "description": "The isomorphism between `P\u207a \u22d9 F` and `(P \u22d9 F)\u207a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.diagram_comp_iso", "description": " The diagram used to define `P\u207a`, composed with `F`, is isomorphic\nto the diagram used to define `P \u22d9 F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.to_plus_comp_plus_comp_iso_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.plus_functor_whisker_left_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.whisker_right_to_plus_comp_plus_comp_iso_hom_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.plus_comp_iso_whisker_left_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.plus_functor_whisker_right_iso", "description": "The isomorphism between `P\u207a \u22d9 F` and `(P \u22d9 F)\u207a`, functorially in `P`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.plus_comp_iso_whisker_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.plus_functor_whisker_right_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.plus_comp_iso_whisker_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.plus_functor_whisker_left_iso", "description": "The isomorphism between `P\u207a \u22d9 F` and `(P \u22d9 F)\u207a`, functorially in `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.plus_comp_iso_inv_eq_plus_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.diagram_comp_iso_hom_\u03b9_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.plus_functor_whisker_right_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.plus_comp_iso_whisker_right_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.whisker_right_to_plus_comp_plus_comp_iso_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.diagram_comp_iso_hom_\u03b9", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.\u03b9_plus_comp_iso_hom_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.\u03b9_plus_comp_iso_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "expr.coord.repr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.address", "description": " An address is a list of coordinates used to reference subterms of an expression.\nThe first coordinate in the list corresponds to the root of the expression.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.address.is_below", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.coord.follow", "description": " Use this to pick the subexpression of a given expression that cooresponds\nto the given coordinate.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.coord.has_lt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.coord", "description": " An enum representing a recursive argument in an `expr` constructor.\nTypes of local and meta variables are not included because they are not consistently set and\ndepend on context.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.coord.app_fn", "description": " An enum representing a recursive argument in an `expr` constructor.\nTypes of local and meta variables are not included because they are not consistently set and\ndepend on context.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.coord.app_arg", "description": " An enum representing a recursive argument in an `expr` constructor.\nTypes of local and meta variables are not included because they are not consistently set and\ndepend on context.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.coord.lam_var_type", "description": " An enum representing a recursive argument in an `expr` constructor.\nTypes of local and meta variables are not included because they are not consistently set and\ndepend on context.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.coord.lam_body", "description": " An enum representing a recursive argument in an `expr` constructor.\nTypes of local and meta variables are not included because they are not consistently set and\ndepend on context.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.coord.pi_var_type", "description": " An enum representing a recursive argument in an `expr` constructor.\nTypes of local and meta variables are not included because they are not consistently set and\ndepend on context.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.coord.pi_body", "description": " An enum representing a recursive argument in an `expr` constructor.\nTypes of local and meta variables are not included because they are not consistently set and\ndepend on context.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.coord.elet_var_type", "description": " An enum representing a recursive argument in an `expr` constructor.\nTypes of local and meta variables are not included because they are not consistently set and\ndepend on context.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.coord.elet_assignment", "description": " An enum representing a recursive argument in an `expr` constructor.\nTypes of local and meta variables are not included because they are not consistently set and\ndepend on context.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.coord.elet_body", "description": " An enum representing a recursive argument in an `expr` constructor.\nTypes of local and meta variables are not included because they are not consistently set and\ndepend on context.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.address.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.coord.has_repr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.coord.has_dec_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.coord.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.address.has_append", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.address.has_dec_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.address.has_repr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.coord.has_to_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.address.has_to_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.coord.code", "description": "Convert the coord enum to its index number.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.address.follow", "description": "`follow a e` finds the subexpression of `e` at the given address `a`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.address.to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.address.as_below", "description": "`as_below x y` is some z when it finds `\u2203 z, x = y ++ z`", "p": "core"}, {"name": "order.exists_between_finsets", "description": "Suppose `\u03b1` is a nonempty dense linear order without endpoints, and\n   suppose `lo`, `hi`, are finite subssets with all of `lo` strictly\n   before `hi`. Then there is an element of `\u03b1` strictly between `lo`\n   and `hi`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.iso_of_countable_dense", "description": "Any two countable dense, nonempty linear orders without endpoints are order isomorphic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.partial_iso.defined_at_right", "description": "The set of partial isomorphisms defined at `b : \u03b2`, together with a proof that any\n   partial isomorphism can be extended to include `b`. We prove this by symmetry.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.embedding_from_countable_to_dense", "description": "Any countable linear order embeds in any nontrivial dense linear order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.partial_iso.exists_across", "description": " For each `a`, we can find a `b` in the codomain, such that `a`'s relation to\nthe domain of `f` is `b`'s relation to the image of `f`.\n\nThus, if `a` is not already in `f`, then we can extend `f` by sending `a` to `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.partial_iso.comm", "description": "A partial isomorphism between `\u03b1` and `\u03b2` is also a partial isomorphism between `\u03b2` and `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.partial_iso.inv_of_ideal", "description": "Given an ideal which intersects `defined_at_right \u03b1 b`, pick `a : \u03b1` such that\n   some partial function in the ideal maps `a` to `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.partial_iso.fun_of_ideal", "description": "Given an ideal which intersects `defined_at_left \u03b2 a`, pick `b : \u03b2` such that\n   some partial function in the ideal maps `a` to `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.partial_iso.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.partial_iso.defined_at_left", "description": "The set of partial isomorphisms defined at `a : \u03b1`, together with a proof that any\n   partial isomorphism can be extended to one defined at `a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.partial_iso.preorder", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order.partial_iso", "description": "The type of partial order isomorphisms between `\u03b1` and `\u03b2` defined on finite subsets.\n   A partial order isomorphism is encoded as a finite subset of `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2`, consisting\n   of pairs which should be identified.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.mono_iff_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.epi_iff_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "json.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.has_to_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.to_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.unparse", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.int_coe", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.array_coe", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.float_coe", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.of_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.of_int", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.of_float", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.of_bool", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.null", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.object", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.array", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.has_repr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.string_coe", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.parse", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "json.bool_coe", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "measure_theory.condexp_indep_eq", "description": " If `m\u2081, m\u2082` are independent \u03c3-algebras and `f` is `m\u2081`-measurable, then `\ud835\udd3c[f | m\u2082] = \ud835\udd3c[f]`\nalmost everywhere.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.chain_height_insert_of_forall_gt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.cons_mem_subchain_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.nil_mem_subchain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.chain_height_eq_supr_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.chain_height_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.subchain.nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.chain_height_le_chain_height_tfae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.chain_height_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.chain_height_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.chain_height_union_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.well_founded_gt_of_chain_height_ne_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.well_founded_lt_of_chain_height_ne_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.exists_chain_of_le_chain_height", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.chain_height_le_chain_height_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.chain_height_eq_supr_Iic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.le_chain_height_add_nat_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.chain_height", "description": "The maximal length of a strictly ascending sequence in a partial order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.one_le_chain_height_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.length_le_chain_height_of_mem_subchain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.chain_height_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.chain_height_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.chain_height_union_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.chain_height_add_le_chain_height_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.chain_height_le_chain_height_iff_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.chain_height_eq_top_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.chain_height_eq_supr_Ici", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.chain_height_of_is_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.le_chain_height_tfae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.subchain", "description": "The set of strictly ascending lists of `\u03b1` contained in a `set \u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.chain_height_insert_of_forall_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.le_chain_height_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.top_is_cartan_subalgebra_of_nilpotent", "description": "A nilpotent Lie algebra is its own Cartan subalgebra.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.normalizer_eq_self_of_is_cartan_subalgebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.is_cartan_subalgebra", "description": "A Cartan subalgebra is a nilpotent, self-normalizing subalgebra.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.is_cartan_subalgebra.nilpotent", "description": "A Cartan subalgebra is a nilpotent, self-normalizing subalgebra.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.is_cartan_subalgebra.self_normalizing", "description": "A Cartan subalgebra is a nilpotent, self-normalizing subalgebra.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.ucs_eq_self_of_is_cartan_subalgebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_submodule.is_ucs_limit", "description": " Given a Lie module `M` of a Lie algebra `L`, `lie_submodule.is_ucs_limit` is the proposition\nthat a Lie submodule `N \u2286 M` is the limiting value for the upper central series.\n\nThis is a characteristic property of Cartan subalgebras with the roles of `L`, `M`, `N` played by\n`H`, `L`, `H`, respectively. See `lie_subalgebra.is_cartan_subalgebra_iff_is_ucs_limit`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.lie_algebra.is_nilpotent", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_subalgebra.is_cartan_subalgebra_iff_is_ucs_limit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_ideal.normalizer_eq_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bind\u2081_rename_expand_witt_polynomial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "constant_coeff_witt_structure_int_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_structure_int_exists_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "constant_coeff_witt_structure_rat_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_structure_rat", "description": " `witt_structure_rat \u03a6` is a family of polynomials `\u2115 \u2192 mv_polynomial (idx \u00d7 \u2115) \u211a`\nthat are uniquely characterised by the property that\n```lean\nbind\u2081 (witt_structure_rat p \u03a6) (witt_polynomial p \u211a n) =\nbind\u2081 (\u03bb i, (rename (prod.mk i) (witt_polynomial p \u211a n))) \u03a6\n```\nIn other words: evaluating the `n`-th Witt polynomial on the family `witt_structure_rat \u03a6`\nis the same as evaluating `\u03a6` on the (appropriately renamed) `n`-th Witt polynomials.\n\nSee `witt_structure_rat_prop` for this property,\nand `witt_structure_rat_exists_unique` for the fact that `witt_structure_rat`\ngives the unique family of polynomials with this property.\n\nThese polynomials turn out to have integral coefficients,\nbut it requires some effort to show this.\nSee `witt_structure_int` for the version with integral coefficients,\nand `map_witt_structure_int` for the fact that it is equal to `witt_structure_rat`\nwhen mapped to polynomials over the rationals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "constant_coeff_witt_structure_int", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_structure_rat_rec_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_structure_int_prop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_structure_int_vars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "constant_coeff_witt_structure_rat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_structure_int_rename", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_structure_rat_rec", "description": "Write `witt_structure_rat p \u03c6 n` in terms of `witt_structure_rat p \u03c6 i` for `i < n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_structure_prop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_structure_rat_vars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_structure_int", "description": " `witt_structure_int \u03a6` is a family of polynomials `\u2115 \u2192 mv_polynomial (idx \u00d7 \u2115) \u2124`\nthat are uniquely characterised by the property that\n```lean\nbind\u2081 (witt_structure_int p \u03a6) (witt_polynomial p \u2124 n) =\nbind\u2081 (\u03bb i, (rename (prod.mk i) (witt_polynomial p \u2124 n))) \u03a6\n```\nIn other words: evaluating the `n`-th Witt polynomial on the family `witt_structure_int \u03a6`\nis the same as evaluating `\u03a6` on the (appropriately renamed) `n`-th Witt polynomials.\n\nSee `witt_structure_int_prop` for this property,\nand `witt_structure_int_exists_unique` for the fact that `witt_structure_int`\ngives the unique family of polynomials with this property.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_witt_structure_int", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_structure_rat_exists_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_structure_rat_prop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "C_p_pow_dvd_bind\u2081_rename_witt_polynomial_sub_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "map_witt_structure_int", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.pderiv_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.pderiv_X_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.pderiv_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.pderiv_X_of_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.pderiv_monomial_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.pderiv_eq_zero_of_not_mem_vars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.pderiv_monomial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.pderiv_C_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.pderiv_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.pderiv", "description": "`pderiv i p` is the partial derivative of `p` with respect to `i`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.pderiv_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.pderiv_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.integral_normalization", "description": " If `f : R[X]` is a nonzero polynomial with root `z`, `integral_normalization f` is\na monic polynomial with root `leading_coeff f * z`.\n\nMoreover, `integral_normalization 0 = 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.integral_normalization_coeff_ne_degree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.integral_normalization_coeff_nat_degree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.integral_normalization_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.monic_integral_normalization", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.integral_normalization_coeff_ne_nat_degree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.integral_normalization_eval\u2082_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.support_integral_normalization", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.integral_normalization_coeff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.integral_normalization_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.integral_normalization_coeff_degree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.integral_normalization_aeval_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.functor.exact_of_map_projective_resolution", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.functor.left_derived_zero_to_self_app_comp_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.functor.left_derived_zero_to_self_app_inv_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.functor.left_derived_zero_to_self_app_inv", "description": " Given `P : ProjectiveResolution X`, a morphism `F.obj X \u27f6 (F.left_derived 0).obj X` given\n`preserves_finite_colimits F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.functor.left_derived_zero_iso_self", "description": "Given `preserves_finite_colimits F`, the natural isomorphism `(F.left_derived 0) \u2245 F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.functor.preserves_exact_of_preserves_finite_colimits_of_epi", "description": " If `preserves_finite_colimits F` and `epi g`, then `exact (F.map f) (F.map g)` if\n`exact f g`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.functor.left_derived_zero_to_self_app_iso", "description": " Given `P : ProjectiveResolution X`, the isomorphism `(F.left_derived 0).obj X \u2245 F.obj X` if\n`preserves_finite_colimits F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.functor.left_derived_zero_to_self_natural", "description": " Given `P : ProjectiveResolution X` and `Q : ProjectiveResolution Y` and a morphism `f : X \u27f6 Y`,\nnaturality of the square given by `left_derived_zero_to_self_obj_hom.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.functor.left_derived_zero_to_self_app", "description": "Given `P : ProjectiveResolution X`, a morphism `(F.left_derived 0).obj X \u27f6 F.obj X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.nth_rewrite.tracked_rewrite.eval", "description": " Postprocess a tracked rewrite into a pair\nof a rewritten expression and a proof witness of the rewrite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.nth_rewrite.tracked_rewrite", "description": " A data structure to track rewrites of subexpressions.\nThe field `exp` contains the new expression,\nwhile `proof` contains a proof that `exp` is equivalent to the expression that was rewritten.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.nth_rewrite.tracked_rewrite.exp", "description": " A data structure to track rewrites of subexpressions.\nThe field `exp` contains the new expression,\nwhile `proof` contains a proof that `exp` is equivalent to the expression that was rewritten.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.nth_rewrite.tracked_rewrite.proof", "description": " A data structure to track rewrites of subexpressions.\nThe field `exp` contains the new expression,\nwhile `proof` contains a proof that `exp` is equivalent to the expression that was rewritten.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.nth_rewrite.tracked_rewrite.addr", "description": " A data structure to track rewrites of subexpressions.\nThe field `exp` contains the new expression,\nwhile `proof` contains a proof that `exp` is equivalent to the expression that was rewritten.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.nth_rewrite.cfg", "description": "Configuration options for nth_rewrite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.nth_rewrite.cfg.to_rewrite_cfg", "description": "Configuration options for nth_rewrite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.nth_rewrite.cfg.try_simp", "description": "Configuration options for nth_rewrite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.nth_rewrite.cfg.discharger", "description": "Configuration options for nth_rewrite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.nth_rewrite.cfg.simplifier", "description": "Configuration options for nth_rewrite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing", "description": "A bundled topological commutative ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing.\u03b1", "description": "A bundled topological commutative ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing.is_comm_ring", "description": "A bundled topological commutative ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing.is_topological_space", "description": "A bundled topological commutative ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing.is_topological_ring", "description": "A bundled topological commutative ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing.forget_comm_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing.forget_to_Top_comm_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing.forget_to_Top_topological_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing.of", "description": "Construct a bundled `TopCommRing` from the underlying type and the appropriate typeclasses.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing.coe_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing.forget_to_CommRing_topological_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing.forget_topological_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing.has_coe_to_sort", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing.forget_topological_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing.category_theory.category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing.category_theory.forget\u2082.category_theory.reflects_isomorphisms", "description": "The forgetful functors to `Type` do not reflect isomorphisms,\nbut the forgetful functor from `TopCommRing` to `Top` does.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing.has_forget_to_Top", "description": "The forgetful functor to Top.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing.category_theory.concrete_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "TopCommRing.has_forget_to_CommRing", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.show_term", "description": "`show_term { tac }` runs the tactic `tac`,\nand then prints the term that was constructed.\n\nThis is useful for\n* constructing term mode proofs from tactic mode proofs, and\n* understanding what tactics are doing, and how metavariables are handled.\n\nAs an example, in\n```lean\nexample {P Q R : Prop} (h\u2081 : Q \u2192 P) (h\u2082 : R) (h\u2083 : R \u2192 Q) : P \u2227 R :=\nby show_term { tauto }\n```\nthe term mode proof `\u27e8h\u2081 (h\u2083 h\u2082), eq.mpr rfl h\u2082\u27e9` produced by `tauto` will be printed.\n\nAs another example, if the goal is `\u2115 \u00d7 \u2115`, `show_term { split, exact 0 }` will\nprint `refine (0, _)`, and afterwards there will be one remaining goal (of type `\u2115`).\nThis indicates that `split, exact 0` partially filled in the original metavariable,\nbut created a new metavariable for the resulting sub-goal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.show_term", "description": "`show_term { tac }` runs the tactic `tac`,\nand then prints the term that was constructed.\n\nThis is useful for\n* constructing term mode proofs from tactic mode proofs, and\n* understanding what tactics are doing, and how metavariables are handled.\n\nAs an example, in\n```lean\nexample {P Q R : Prop} (h\u2081 : Q \u2192 P) (h\u2082 : R) (h\u2083 : R \u2192 Q) : P \u2227 R :=\nby show_term { tauto }\n```\nthe term mode proof `\u27e8h\u2081 (h\u2083 h\u2082), eq.mpr rfl h\u2082\u27e9` produced by `tauto` will be printed.\n\nAs another example, if the goal is `\u2115 \u00d7 \u2115`, `show_term { split, exact 0 }` will\nprint `refine (0, _)`, and afterwards there will be one remaining goal (of type `\u2115`).\nThis indicates that `split, exact 0` partially filled in the original metavariable,\nbut created a new metavariable for the resulting sub-goal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "infinite.nonempty_field", "description": "Any infinite type can be endowed a field structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.nonempty_field_iff", "description": "A `fintype` can be given a field structure iff its cardinality is a prime power.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "field.nonempty_iff", "description": "There is a field structure on type if and only if its cardinality is a prime power.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.is_prime_pow_card_of_field", "description": "A finite field has prime power cardinality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.not_is_field_of_card_not_prime_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "upper_half_plane.t3_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "upper_half_plane.loc_path_connected_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "upper_half_plane.continuous_re", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "upper_half_plane.continuous_im", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "upper_half_plane.topological_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "upper_half_plane.locally_compact_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "upper_half_plane.contractible_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "upper_half_plane.open_embedding_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "upper_half_plane.normal_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "upper_half_plane.topological_space.second_countable_topology", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "upper_half_plane.embedding_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "upper_half_plane.continuous_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "upper_half_plane.noncompact_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.hint", "description": "Report a list of tactics that can make progress against the current goal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.hint", "description": "`hint` lists possible tactics which will make progress (that is, not fail) against the current goal.\n\n```lean\nexample {P Q : Prop} (p : P) (h : P \u2192 Q) : Q :=\nbegin\n  hint,\n  /- the following tactics make progress:\n     ----\n     Try this: solve_by_elim\n     Try this: finish\n     Try this: tauto\n  -/\n  solve_by_elim,\nend\n```\n\nYou can add a tactic to the list that `hint` tries by either using\n1. `attribute [hint_tactic] my_tactic`, if `my_tactic` is already of type `tactic string`\n(`tactic unit` is allowed too, in which case the printed string will be the name of the\ntactic), or\n2. `add_hint_tactic \"my_tactic\"`, specifying a string which works as an interactive tactic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.hint", "description": "Report a list of tactics that can make progress against the current goal,\nand for each such tactic, the number of remaining goals afterwards.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.hint.hint_tactic_attribute", "description": " An attribute marking a `tactic unit` or `tactic string` which should be used by the `hint`\ntactic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.attribute.hint_tactic", "description": " An attribute marking a `tactic unit` or `tactic string` which should be used by the `hint`\ntactic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.hint.add_hint_tactic", "description": "`add_hint_tactic t` runs the tactic `t` whenever `hint` is invoked.\nThe typical use case is `add_hint_tactic \"foo\"` for some interactive tactic `foo`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.command.add_hint_tactic", "description": "`add_hint_tactic t` runs the tactic `t` whenever `hint` is invoked.\nThe typical use case is `add_hint_tactic \"foo\"` for some interactive tactic `foo`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.generalized_eigenspace_eq_generalized_eigenspace_finrank_of_le", "description": "Generalized eigenspaces for exponents at least `finrank K V` are equal to each other.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.eigenspace", "description": "The submodule `eigenspace f \u03bc` for a linear map `f` and a scalar `\u03bc` consists of all vectors `x`\n   such that `f x = \u03bc \u2022 x`. (Def 5.36 of [axler2015])", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.generalized_eigenspace_le_generalized_eigenspace_finrank", "description": "Every generalized eigenvector is a generalized eigenvector for exponent `finrank K V`.\n   (Lemma 8.11 of [axler2015])", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.generalized_eigenspace_le_maximal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.has_generalized_eigenvalue_of_has_eigenvalue", "description": "All eigenvalues are generalized eigenvalues.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.generalized_eigenspace_restrict", "description": "If `f` maps a subspace `p` into itself, then the generalized eigenspace of the restriction\n   of `f` to `p` is the part of the generalized eigenspace of `f` that lies in `p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.has_generalized_eigenvalue_iff_has_eigenvalue", "description": "Generalized eigenvalues are actually just eigenvalues.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.has_generalized_eigenvalue", "description": "A scalar `\u03bc` is a generalized eigenvalue for a linear map `f` and an exponent `k \u2208 \u2115` if there\n   are generalized eigenvectors for `f`, `k`, and `\u03bc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.generalized_eigenspace_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.pos_finrank_generalized_eigenspace_of_has_eigenvalue", "description": "The generalized eigenspace of an eigenvalue has positive dimension for positive exponents.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.generalized_eigenspace", "description": " The generalized eigenspace for a linear map `f`, a scalar `\u03bc`, and an exponent `k \u2208 \u2115` is the\nkernel of `(f - \u03bc \u2022 id) ^ k`. (Def 8.10 of [axler2015]). Furthermore, a generalized eigenspace for\nsome exponent `k` is contained in the generalized eigenspace for exponents larger than `k`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.has_eigenvector.apply_eq_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.eigenspace_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.maximal_generalized_eigenspace_index", "description": " If there exists a natural number `k` such that the kernel of `(f - \u03bc \u2022 id) ^ k` is the\nmaximal generalized eigenspace, then this value is the least such `k`. If not, this value is not\nmeaningful.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.exp_ne_zero_of_has_generalized_eigenvalue", "description": "The exponent of a generalized eigenvalue is never 0.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.has_eigenvector", "description": "A nonzero element of an eigenspace is an eigenvector. (Def 5.7 of [axler2015])", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.has_generalized_eigenvector", "description": "A nonzero element of a generalized eigenspace is a generalized eigenvector.\n   (Def 8.9 of [axler2015])", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.eigenvalues", "description": "The eigenvalues of the endomorphism `f`, as a subtype of `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.maximal_generalized_eigenspace_eq", "description": " For an endomorphism of a Noetherian module, the maximal eigenspace is always of the form kernel\n`(f - \u03bc \u2022 id) ^ k` for some `k`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.has_generalized_eigenvalue_of_has_generalized_eigenvalue_of_le", "description": "A generalized eigenvalue for some exponent `k` is also\n   a generalized eigenvalue for exponents larger than `k`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.mem_spectrum_of_has_eigenvalue", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.has_eigenvalue_of_has_generalized_eigenvalue", "description": "All generalized eigenvalues are eigenvalues.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.has_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.generalized_eigenrange", "description": "The generalized eigenrange for a linear map `f`, a scalar `\u03bc`, and an exponent `k \u2208 \u2115` is the\n   range of `(f - \u03bc \u2022 id) ^ k`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.eigenspace_le_generalized_eigenspace", "description": "The eigenspace is a subspace of the generalized eigenspace.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.generalized_eigenvec_disjoint_range_ker", "description": "Generalized eigenrange and generalized eigenspace for exponent `finrank K V` are disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.has_eigenvalue", "description": "A scalar `\u03bc` is an eigenvalue for a linear map `f` if there are nonzero vectors `x`\n   such that `f x = \u03bc \u2022 x`. (Def 5.5 of [axler2015])", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.mem_generalized_eigenspace", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.maximal_generalized_eigenspace", "description": "The union of the kernels of `(f - \u03bc \u2022 id) ^ k` over all `k`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.eigenspace_restrict_le_eigenspace", "description": " If `p` is an invariant submodule of an endomorphism `f`, then the `\u03bc`-eigenspace of the\nrestriction of `f` to `p` is a submodule of the `\u03bc`-eigenspace of `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.has_eigenvalue.exists_has_eigenvector", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.mem_eigenspace_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.eigenspace_restrict_eq_bot", "description": " If an invariant subspace `p` of an endomorphism `f` is disjoint from the `\u03bc`-eigenspace of `f`,\nthen the restriction of `f` to `p` has trivial `\u03bc`-eigenspace.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.mem_maximal_generalized_eigenspace", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.map_generalized_eigenrange_le", "description": "A linear map maps a generalized eigenrange into itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.has_eigenvalue_of_has_eigenvector", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.eigenspaces_independent", "description": " The eigenspaces of a linear operator form an independent family of subspaces of `V`.  That is,\nany eigenspace has trivial intersection with the span of all the other eigenspaces.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.eigenvectors_linear_independent", "description": "Eigenvectors corresponding to distinct eigenvalues of a linear operator are linearly\n   independent. (Lemma 5.10 of [axler2015])\n\n   We use the eigenvalues as indexing set to ensure that there is only one eigenvector for each\n   eigenvalue in the image of `xs`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.has_eigenvalue_iff_mem_spectrum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.eigenspace_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.free.lift_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.free.quiver.hom.to_pos_path", "description": "Shorthand for the \"forward\" arrow corresponding to `f` in `paths $ symmetrify V`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.free.quiver.hom.to_neg_path", "description": "Shorthand for the \"forward\" arrow corresponding to `f` in `paths $ symmetrify V`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.free.lift_spec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.free.congr_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.free_groupoid", "description": "The underlying vertices of the free groupoid", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.free.category_theory.free_groupoid.category_theory.category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.free.congr_reverse_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.free.congr_comp_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.free.category_theory.free_groupoid.nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.free.free_groupoid_functor_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.free.of_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.free.free_groupoid_functor_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.free.of", "description": "The inclusion of the quiver on `V` to the underlying quiver on `free_groupoid V`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.free.red_step", "description": "The \"reduction\" relation", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.free.red_step.step", "description": "The \"reduction\" relation", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.free.lift", "description": "The lift of a prefunctor to a groupoid, to a functor from `free_groupoid V`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.free.quot_inv", "description": "The inverse of an arrow in the free groupoid", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.groupoid.free.category_theory.free_groupoid.category_theory.groupoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.free_groupoid_functor", "description": "The functor of free groupoid induced by a prefunctor of quivers", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eckmann_hilton.mul", "description": " If a type carries two unital binary operations that distribute over each other,\nthen these operations are equal.\n\nIn fact, they give a commutative monoid structure, see `eckmann_hilton.comm_monoid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eckmann_hilton.add_zero_class.is_unital", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eckmann_hilton.mul_assoc", "description": " If a type carries two unital binary operations that distribute over each other,\nthen these operations are associative.\n\nIn fact, they give a commutative monoid structure, see `eckmann_hilton.comm_monoid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eckmann_hilton.comm_group", "description": " If a type carries a group structure that distributes over a unital binary operation,\nthen the group is commutative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eckmann_hilton.is_unital", "description": " `is_unital m e` expresses that `e : X` is a left and right unit\nfor the binary operation `m : X \u2192 X \u2192 X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eckmann_hilton.is_unital.to_is_left_id", "description": " `is_unital m e` expresses that `e : X` is a left and right unit\nfor the binary operation `m : X \u2192 X \u2192 X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eckmann_hilton.is_unital.to_is_right_id", "description": " `is_unital m e` expresses that `e : X` is a left and right unit\nfor the binary operation `m : X \u2192 X \u2192 X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eckmann_hilton.add_comm_group", "description": "If a type carries an additive group structure that\ndistributes over a unital binary operation, then the additive group is commutative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eckmann_hilton.mul_comm", "description": " If a type carries two unital binary operations that distribute over each other,\nthen these operations are commutative.\n\nIn fact, they give a commutative monoid structure, see `eckmann_hilton.comm_monoid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eckmann_hilton.one", "description": " If a type carries two unital binary operations that distribute over each other,\nthen they have the same unit elements.\n\nIn fact, the two operations are the same, and give a commutative monoid structure,\nsee `eckmann_hilton.comm_monoid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eckmann_hilton.mul_one_class.is_unital", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eckmann_hilton.add_comm_monoid", "description": "If a type carries a unital additive magma structure that distributes over\na unital binary operations, then the additive magma structure is a commutative additive monoid.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eckmann_hilton.comm_monoid", "description": " If a type carries a unital magma structure that distributes over a unital binary\noperations, then the magma structure is a commutative monoid.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opens.pretopology_of_grothendieck", "description": "The pretopology associated to a space is the largest pretopology that\n   generates the Grothendieck topology associated to the space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opens.pretopology", "description": "The Grothendieck pretopology associated to a topological space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opens.grothendieck_topology", "description": "The Grothendieck topology associated to a topological space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "opens.pretopology_to_grothendieck", "description": "The pretopology associated to a space induces the Grothendieck topology associated to the space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_left_dual_of_equivalence", "description": "Pull back a left dual along an equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact_pairing_of_fully_faithful", "description": "Given a pair of objects which are sent by a fully faithful functor to a pair of objects\nwith an exact pairing, we get an exact pairing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_right_dual_of_equivalence", "description": "Pull back a right dual along an equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_rigid_category_of_equivalence", "description": "Pull back a right rigid structure along an equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_rigid_category_of_equivalence", "description": "Pull back a left rigid structure along an equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.rigid_category_of_equivalence", "description": "Pull back a rigid structure along an equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exact_pairing_of_faithful", "description": " Given candidate data for an exact pairing,\nwhich is sent by a faithful monoidal functor to an exact pairing,\nthe equations holds automatically.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_left_adjoint_of_preserves_colimits_of_is_separatig", "description": "The special adjoint functor theorem: if `F : C \u2964 D` preserves colimits and `C` is cocomplete,\nwell-copowered and has a small separating set, then `F` has a right adjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_right_adjoint_of_preserves_limits_of_is_coseparating", "description": "The special adjoint functor theorem: if `G : D \u2964 C` preserves limits and `D` is complete,\nwell-powered and has a small coseparating set, then `G` has a left adjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_limits_of_has_colimits_of_is_separating", "description": "A consequence of the special adjoint functor theorem: if `C` is cocomplete, well-copowered and\n   has a small separating set, then it is complete.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.solution_set_condition_of_is_right_adjoint", "description": "If `G : D \u2964 C` is a right adjoint it satisfies the solution set condition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_colimits_of_has_limits_of_is_coseparating", "description": "A consequence of the special adjoint functor theorem: if `C` is complete, well-powered and\n   has a small coseparating set, then it is cocomplete.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_right_adjoint_of_preserves_limits_of_solution_set_condition", "description": "The general adjoint functor theorem says that if `G : D \u2964 C` preserves limits and `D` has them,\nif `G` satisfies the solution set condition then `G` is a right adjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.solution_set_condition", "description": "The functor `G : D \u2964 C` satisfies the *solution set condition* if for every `A : C`, there is a\nfamily of morphisms `{f_i : A \u27f6 G (B_i) // i \u2208 \u03b9}` such that given any morphism `h : A \u27f6 G X`,\nthere is some `i \u2208 \u03b9` such that `h` factors through `f_i`.\n\nThe key part of this definition is that the indexing set `\u03b9` lives in `Type v`, where `v` is the\nuniverse of morphisms of the category: this is the \"smallness\" condition which allows the general\nadjoint functor theorem to go through.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rank_condition_of_strong_rank_condition", "description": "By the universal property for free modules, any surjective map `(fin n \u2192 R) \u2192\u2097[R] (fin m \u2192 R)`\nhas an injective splitting `(fin m \u2192 R) \u2192\u2097[R] (fin n \u2192 R)`\nfrom which the strong rank condition gives the necessary inequality for the rank condition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_of_fin_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_of_fin_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nontrivial_of_invariant_basis_number", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rank_condition", "description": "We say that `R` satisfies the rank condition if `(fin n \u2192 R) \u2192\u2097[R] (fin m \u2192 R)` surjective\n   implies `m \u2264 n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rank_condition.le_of_fin_surjective", "description": "We say that `R` satisfies the rank condition if `(fin n \u2192 R) \u2192\u2097[R] (fin m \u2192 R)` surjective\n   implies `m \u2264 n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "invariant_basis_number_of_rank_condition", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "card_le_of_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_of_fin_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strong_rank_condition", "description": "We say that `R` satisfies the strong rank condition if `(fin n \u2192 R) \u2192\u2097[R] (fin m \u2192 R)` injective\n   implies `n \u2264 m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strong_rank_condition.le_of_fin_injective", "description": "We say that `R` satisfies the strong rank condition if `(fin n \u2192 R) \u2192\u2097[R] (fin m \u2192 R)` injective\n   implies `n \u2264 m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strong_rank_condition_iff_succ", "description": " A ring satisfies the strong rank condition if and only if, for all `n : \u2115`, any linear map\n`(fin (n + 1) \u2192 R) \u2192\u2097[R] (fin n \u2192 R)` is not injective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "invariant_basis_number", "description": "We say that `R` has the invariant basis number property if `(fin n \u2192 R) \u2243\u2097[R] (fin m \u2192 R)`\n   implies `n = m`. This gives rise to a well-defined notion of rank of a finitely generated free\n   module.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "invariant_basis_number.eq_of_fin_equiv", "description": "We say that `R` has the invariant basis number property if `(fin n \u2192 R) \u2243\u2097[R] (fin m \u2192 R)`\n   implies `n = m`. This gives rise to a well-defined notion of rank of a finitely generated free\n   module.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "noetherian_ring_strong_rank_condition", "description": "Any nontrivial noetherian ring satisfies the strong rank condition.\n\nAn injective map `((fin n \u2295 fin (1 + m)) \u2192 R) \u2192\u2097[R] (fin n \u2192 R)` for some left-noetherian `R`\nwould force `fin (1 + m) \u2192 R \u2243\u2097 punit` (via `is_noetherian.equiv_punit_of_prod_injective`),\nwhich is not the case!", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "card_le_of_surjective'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "card_le_of_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "card_eq_of_lequiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strong_rank_condition_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "invariant_basis_number_of_nontrivial_of_comm_ring", "description": " Nontrivial commutative rings have the invariant basis number property.\n\nIn fact, any nontrivial commutative ring satisfies the strong rank condition, see\n`comm_ring_strong_rank_condition`. We prove this instance separately to avoid dependency on\n`linear_algebra.charpoly.basic`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "card_le_of_injective'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.measure_diff_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.Union_right_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_null_pairwise_disjoint_diff", "description": " If `s : \u03b9 \u2192 set \u03b1` is a countable family of pairwise a.e. disjoint sets, then there exists a\nfamily of measurable null sets `t i` such that `s i \\ t i` are pairwise disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.diff_ae_eq_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint_compl_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.pairwise_disjoint.ae_disjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "disjoint.ae_disjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.union_right_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.mono_ae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pairwise.ae_disjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.of_null_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.diff_ae_eq_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint", "description": "Two sets are said to be `\u03bc`-a.e. disjoint if their intersection has measure zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.of_null_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.union_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.union_left_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.measure_diff_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint_compl_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.union_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.symmetric", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.Union_left_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_disjoint.exists_disjoint_diff", "description": " If `s` and `t` are `\u03bc`-a.e. disjoint, then `s \\ u` and `t` are disjoint for some measurable null\nset `u`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.rcomap_rcomap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.pmap_res", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.ptendsto_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.rcomap", "description": " One way of taking the inverse map of a filter under a relation. One generalization of\n`filter.comap` to relations. Note that `rel.core` generalizes `set.preimage`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.rmap_compose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto_iff_ptendsto_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.ptendsto_of_ptendsto'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.rtendsto_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.ptendsto", "description": " Generic \"limit of a partial function\" predicate. `ptendsto r l\u2081 l\u2082` asserts that for every\n`l\u2082`-neighborhood `a`, the `p`-core of `a` is an `l\u2081`-neighborhood. One generalization of\n`filter.tendsto` to partial function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.rmap_sets", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto_iff_ptendsto", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.rcomap'", "description": " One way of taking the inverse map of a filter under a relation. Generalization of `filter.comap`\nto relations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.ptendsto'", "description": " Generic \"limit of a partial function\" predicate. `ptendsto' r l\u2081 l\u2082` asserts that for every\n`l\u2082`-neighborhood `a`, the `p`-preimage of `a` is an `l\u2081`-neighborhood. One generalization of\n`filter.tendsto` to partial functions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.rcomap_compose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.rtendsto'_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.ptendsto'_of_ptendsto", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.mem_pmap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.rtendsto_iff_le_rcomap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto_iff_rtendsto", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.pmap", "description": " The forward map of a filter under a partial function. Generalization of `filter.map` to partial\nfunctions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.rtendsto", "description": " Generic \"limit of a relation\" predicate. `rtendsto r l\u2081 l\u2082` asserts that for every\n`l\u2082`-neighborhood `a`, the `r`-core of `a` is an `l\u2081`-neighborhood. One generalization of\n`filter.tendsto` to relations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.mem_rcomap'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.rtendsto'", "description": " Generic \"limit of a relation\" predicate. `rtendsto' r l\u2081 l\u2082` asserts that for every\n`l\u2082`-neighborhood `a`, the `r`-preimage of `a` is an `l\u2081`-neighborhood. One generalization of\n`filter.tendsto` to relations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.rmap", "description": " The forward map of a filter under a relation. Generalization of `filter.map` to relations. Note\nthat `rel.core` generalizes `set.preimage`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.rcomap_sets", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto_iff_rtendsto'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.ptendsto_iff_rtendsto", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.rcomap'_compose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.ptendsto'_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.rcomap'_sets", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.pcomap'", "description": " Inverse map of a filter under a partial function. One generalization of `filter.comap` to\npartial functions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.rcomap'_rcomap'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.rmap_rmap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.mem_rmap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "min_eq_left_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "max_self", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.min_tac", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "max_comm", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "max_lt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "max_assoc", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "le_min", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "max_eq_right", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "le_max_left", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "eq_min", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "lt_min", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "min_eq_left", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "min_comm", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "max_le", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "min_def", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "min_le_right", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "max_eq_left", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "min_assoc", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "min_left_comm", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "max_def", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "min_self", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "min_eq_right", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "min_eq_right_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "max_left_comm", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "eq_max", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "max_eq_right_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "min_le_left", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "le_max_right", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "max_eq_left_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "Module.monoidal_closed_hom_equiv", "description": "Auxiliary definition for the `monoidal_closed` instance on `Module R`.\n(This is only a separate definition in order to speed up typechecking. )", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.monoidal_closed_hom_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.category_theory.monoidal_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.ihom_ev_app", "description": " Describes the counit of the adjunction `M \u2297 - \u22a3 Hom(M, -)`. Given an `R`-module `N` this\nshould give a map `M \u2297 Hom(M, N) \u27f6 N`, so we flip the order of the arguments in the identity map\n`Hom(M, N) \u27f6 (M \u27f6 N)` and uncurry the resulting map `M \u27f6 Hom(M, N) \u27f6 N.`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.ihom_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.monoidal_closed_pre_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.monoidal_closed_hom_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.monoidal_closed_uncurry", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.monoidal_closed_curry", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.ihom_coev_app", "description": " Describes the unit of the adjunction `M \u2297 - \u22a3 Hom(M, -)`. Given an `R`-module `N` this should\ndefine a map `N \u27f6 Hom(M, M \u2297 N)`, which is given by flipping the arguments in the natural\n`R`-bilinear map `M \u27f6 N \u27f6 M \u2297 N`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_inner_dual_cone", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_dual_cone_sUnion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pointed_inner_dual_cone", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_dual_cone_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_dual_cone_insert", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_dual_cone_Union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_dual_cone_zero", "description": "Dual cone of the convex cone {0} is the total space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.inner_dual_cone", "description": " The dual cone is the cone consisting of all points `y` such that for\nall points `x` in a given set `0 \u2264 \u27ea x, y \u27eb`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_cone.hyperplane_separation_of_nonempty_of_is_closed_of_nmem", "description": " This is a stronger version of the Hahn-Banach separation theorem for closed convex cones. This\nis also the geometric interpretation of Farkas' lemma.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_cone.inner_dual_cone_of_inner_dual_cone_eq_self", "description": "The inner dual of inner dual of a non-empty, closed convex cone is itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_dual_cone_union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_closed_inner_dual_cone", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_cone.pointed_of_nonempty_of_is_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_dual_cone_eq_Inter_inner_dual_cone_singleton", "description": "The dual cone of `s` equals the intersection of dual cones of the points in `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_dual_cone_singleton", "description": " The inner dual cone of a singleton is given by the preimage of the positive cone under the\nlinear map `\u03bb y, \u27eax, y\u27eb`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_dual_cone_le_inner_dual_cone", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_dual_cone_univ", "description": "Dual cone of the total space is the convex cone {0}.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.all_card_le_filter_rel_iff_exists_injective", "description": "This is a version of **Hall's Marriage Theorem** in terms of a relation to a finite type.\nThere is a transversal of the relation (an injective function `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` whose graph is a subrelation\nof the relation) iff every subset of `k` terms of `\u03b1` is related to at least `k` terms of `\u03b2`.\n\nIt is like `fintype.all_card_le_rel_image_card_iff_exists_injective` but uses `finset.filter`\nrather than `rel.image`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hall_matchings_on.finite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hall_matchings_on.nonempty", "description": " When the Hall condition is satisfied, the set of matchings on a finite set is nonempty.\nThis is where `finset.all_card_le_bUnion_card_iff_exists_injective'` comes into the argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hall_matchings_on", "description": "The set of matchings for `t` when restricted to a `finset` of `\u03b9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hall_matchings_functor", "description": "This is the `hall_matchings_on` sets assembled into a directed system.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.all_card_le_rel_image_card_iff_exists_injective", "description": "This is a version of **Hall's Marriage Theorem** in terms of a relation\nbetween types `\u03b1` and `\u03b2` such that `\u03b1` is finite and the image of\neach `x : \u03b1` is finite (it suffices for `\u03b2` to be finite; see\n`fintype.all_card_le_filter_rel_iff_exists_injective`).  There is\na transversal of the relation (an injective function `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` whose graph is\na subrelation of the relation) iff every subset of\n`k` terms of `\u03b1` is related to at least `k` terms of `\u03b2`.\n\nNote: if `[fintype \u03b2]`, then there exist instances for `[\u2200 (a : \u03b1), fintype (rel.image r {a})]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hall_matchings_on.restrict", "description": "Given a matching on a finset, construct the restriction of that matching to a subset.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.all_card_le_bUnion_card_iff_exists_injective", "description": "This is the version of **Hall's Marriage Theorem** in terms of indexed\nfamilies of finite sets `t : \u03b9 \u2192 finset \u03b1`.  It states that there is a\nset of distinct representatives if and only if every union of `k` of the\nsets has at least `k` elements.\n\nRecall that `s.bUnion t` is the union of all the sets `t i` for `i \u2208 s`.\n\nThis theorem is bootstrapped from `finset.all_card_le_bUnion_card_iff_exists_injective'`,\nwhich has the additional constraint that `\u03b9` is a `fintype`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rel.image.fintype", "description": " Given a relation such that the image of every singleton set is finite, then the image of every\nfinite set is finite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.tfae_have", "description": " In a goal of the form `tfae [a\u2080, a\u2081, a\u2082]`,\n`tfae_have : i \u2192 j` creates the assertion `a\u1d62 \u2192 a\u2c7c`. The other possible\nnotations are `tfae_have : i \u2190 j` and `tfae_have : i \u2194 j`. The user can\nalso provide a label for the assertion, as with `have`: `tfae_have h : i \u2194 j`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.tfae.arrow.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.tfae.mk_implication", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.parse_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.tfae.mk_name", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.tfae_finish", "description": " Finds all implications and equivalences in the context\nto prove a goal of the form `tfae [...]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.tfae.arrow.has_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.tfae.arrow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.tfae.arrow.right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.tfae.arrow.left_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.tfae.arrow.left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.tfae", "description": "The `tfae` tactic suite is a set of tactics that help with proving that certain\npropositions are equivalent.\nIn `data/list/basic.lean` there is a section devoted to propositions of the\nform\n```lean\ntfae [p1, p2, ..., pn]\n```\nwhere `p1`, `p2`, through, `pn` are terms of type `Prop`.\nThis proposition asserts that all the `pi` are pairwise equivalent.\nThere are results that allow to extract the equivalence\nof two propositions `pi` and `pj`.\n\nTo prove a goal of the form `tfae [p1, p2, ..., pn]`, there are two\ntactics.  The first tactic is `tfae_have`.  As an argument it takes an\nexpression of the form `i arrow j`, where `i` and `j` are two positive\nnatural numbers, and `arrow` is an arrow such as `\u2192`, `->`, `\u2190`, `<-`,\n`\u2194`, or `<->`.  The tactic `tfae_have : i arrow j` sets up a subgoal in\nwhich the user has to prove the equivalence (or implication) of `pi` and `pj`.\n\nThe remaining tactic, `tfae_finish`, is a finishing tactic. It\ncollects all implications and equivalences from the local context and\ncomputes their transitive closure to close the\nmain goal.\n\n`tfae_have` and `tfae_finish` can be used together in a proof as\nfollows:\n\n```lean\nexample (a b c d : Prop) : tfae [a,b,c,d] :=\nbegin\n  tfae_have : 3 \u2192 1,\n  { /- prove c \u2192 a -/ },\n  tfae_have : 2 \u2192 3,\n  { /- prove b \u2192 c -/ },\n  tfae_have : 2 \u2190 1,\n  { /- prove a \u2192 b -/ },\n  tfae_have : 4 \u2194 2,\n  { /- prove d \u2194 b -/ },\n    -- a b c d : Prop,\n    -- tfae_3_to_1 : c \u2192 a,\n    -- tfae_2_to_3 : b \u2192 c,\n    -- tfae_1_to_2 : a \u2192 b,\n    -- tfae_4_iff_2 : d \u2194 b\n    -- \u22a2 tfae [a, b, c, d]\n  tfae_finish,\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.as_string_inv_to_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.ltb", "description": "`<` on string iterators. This coincides with `<` on strings as lists.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.as_string_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.lt_iff_to_list_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.length_to_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.to_list_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.length_as_string", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.as_string_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.to_list_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.decidable_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.nil_as_string_eq_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.has_lt'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.to_list_nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.to_list_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.head_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.le_iff_to_list_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.to_list_inv_as_string", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.has_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.popn_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.decidable_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.linear_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.mem_restrict_total_degree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.basis_monomials", "description": "The monomials form a basis on `mv_polynomial \u03c3 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.map_range_eq_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.char_p", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.basis_monomials", "description": "The monomials form a basis on `R[X]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.linear_independent_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.coe_basis_monomials", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.mem_restrict_degree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.restrict_total_degree", "description": "The submodule of polynomials of total degree less than or equal to `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.mem_restrict_degree_iff_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coe_basis_monomials", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.restrict_degree", "description": " The submodule of polynomials such that the degree with respect to each individual variable is\nless than or equal to `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.set_like", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.has_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.bounded_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.coe_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.has_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.clopen", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.has_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.upper", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.coe_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.coe_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.coe_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.to_upper_set_carrier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set", "description": "The type of clopen upper sets of a topological space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.to_clopens", "description": "The type of clopen upper sets of a topological space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.upper'", "description": "The type of clopen upper sets of a topological space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.lattice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.coe_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.to_upper_set", "description": "Reinterpret a upper clopen as an upper set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clopen_upper_set.has_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo2005_q4.a", "description": "The sequence considered in the problem, `2 ^ n + 3 ^ n + 6 ^ n - 1`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2005_q4", "description": "Main statement:  The only positive integer coprime to all terms of the sequence `a` is `1`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2005_q4.find_specified_factor", "description": " Key lemma (a modular arithmetic calculation):  Given a prime `p` other than `2` or `3`, the\n`p - 2`th term of the sequence has `p` as a factor.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.eq_empty_to", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.bot_is_affine_open", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.category_theory.limits.has_strict_initial_objects", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.category_theory.limits.has_finite_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.empty_ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.category_theory.limits.has_initial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.empty_to_val_c_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.category_theory.limits.terminal.is_affine", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Spec_punit_is_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Spec_Z_is_terminal", "description": "`Spec \u2124` is the terminal object in the category of schemes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.empty_is_initial", "description": "The empty scheme is the initial object in the category of schemes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.empty_to_val_base_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_affine_of_is_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.empty_is_initial_to", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_open_immersion_of_is_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.initial_is_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.carrier.is_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_initial_of_is_empty", "description": "A scheme is initial if its underlying space is empty .", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_iso_of_is_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.quiver.hom.unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.category_theory.limits.has_terminal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Scheme.empty_to", "description": "The map from the empty scheme.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.Spec_punit_is_initial", "description": "`Spec 0` is the initial object in the category of schemes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.measurable_floor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.floor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.fract", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.ceil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.measurable_floor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.measurable_ceil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_fract", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.measurable_ceil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.nat_floor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set.image_fract", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.nat_ceil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inv_gold", "description": "The inverse of the golden ratio is the opposite of its conjugate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "neg_one_lt_gold_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "golden_conj", "description": "The conjugate of the golden ratio `\u03c8 := (1 - \u221a5)/2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.coe_fib_eq", "description": "Binet's formula as a dependent equality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gold_conj_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inv_gold_conj", "description": "The opposite of the golden ratio is the inverse of its conjugate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fib_rec_char_poly_eq", "description": "The characteristic polynomial of `fib_rec` is `X\u00b2 - (X + 1)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gold_irrational", "description": "The golden ratio is irrational.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gold_conj_irrational", "description": "The conjugate of the golden ratio is irrational.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gold_sub_gold_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gold_conj_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gold_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gold_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fib_rec", "description": "The recurrence relation satisfied by the Fibonacci sequence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gold_add_gold_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_sub_gold", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_lt_gold", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_sub_gold_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fib_is_sol_fib_rec", "description": "As expected, the Fibonacci sequence is a solution of `fib_rec`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geom_gold_is_sol_fib_rec", "description": "The geometric sequence `\u03bb n, \u03c6^n` is a solution of `fib_rec`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gold_mul_gold_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "golden_ratio", "description": "The golden ratio `\u03c6 := (1 + \u221a5)/2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gold_conj_mul_gold", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gold_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.coe_fib_eq'", "description": "Binet's formula as a function equality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "geom_gold_conj_is_sol_fib_rec", "description": "The geometric sequence `\u03bb n, \u03c8^n` is a solution of `fib_rec`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gold_conj_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.strongly_measurable.integral_prod_right", "description": "The Bochner integral is measurable. This shows that the integrand of (the right-hand-side of)\n Fubini's theorem is measurable.\n This version has `f` in curried form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_prod_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.strongly_measurable.integral_prod_left", "description": "The Bochner integral is measurable. This shows that the integrand of (the right-hand-side of)\n the symmetric version of Fubini's theorem is measurable.\n This version has `f` in curried form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.set_integral_prod_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_prod", "description": "**Fubini's Theorem**: For integrable functions on `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2`,\n the Bochner integral of `f` is equal to the iterated Bochner integral.\n `integrable_prod_iff` can be useful to show that the function in question in integrable.\n `measure_theory.integrable.integral_prod_right` is useful to show that the inner integral\n of the right-hand side is integrable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_set_integrable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_integral_sub'", "description": "Double integrals commute with subtraction. This is the version with `(f - g) (x, y)`\n (instead of `f (x, y) - g (x, y)`) in the LHS.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable_prod_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_integral", "description": "Reversed version of **Fubini's Theorem**.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable.prod_left_ae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_prod_symm", "description": "Symmetric version of **Fubini's Theorem**: For integrable functions on `\u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2`,\n the Bochner integral of `f` is equal to the iterated Bochner integral.\n This version has the integrals on the right-hand side in the other order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_fn_integral_add", "description": "Integrals commute with addition inside another integral. `F` can be any function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable.integral_norm_prod_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable_swap_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.measure.integrable_measure_prod_mk_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.lintegral_fn_integral_sub", "description": "Integrals commute with subtraction inside a lower Lebesgue integral.\n `F` can be any function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.has_finite_integral_prod_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_integral_sub", "description": "Double integrals commute with subtraction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable.prod_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_prod_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.strongly_measurable.integral_prod_right'", "description": "The Bochner integral is measurable. This shows that the integrand of (the right-hand-side of)\n Fubini's theorem is measurable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable.integral_prod_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable_prod_iff", "description": "A binary function is integrable if the function `y \u21a6 f (x, y)` is integrable for almost every\n `x` and the function `x \u21a6 \u222b \u2016f (x, y)\u2016 dy` is integrable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable.prod_mk_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable.swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.continuous_integral_integral", "description": "The map that sends an L\u00b9-function `f : \u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2 \u2192 E` to `\u222b\u222bf` is continuous.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable.snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_integral_symm", "description": "Reversed version of **Fubini's Theorem** (symmetric version).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.strongly_measurable.integral_prod_left'", "description": "The Bochner integral is measurable. This shows that the integrand of (the right-hand-side of)\n the symmetric version of Fubini's theorem is measurable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable.fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable.integral_prod_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.set_integral_prod", "description": "**Fubini's Theorem** for set integrals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable_prod_iff'", "description": "A binary function is integrable if the function `x \u21a6 f (x, y)` is integrable for almost every\n `y` and the function `y \u21a6 \u222b \u2016f (x, y)\u2016 dx` is integrable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_integral_add'", "description": "Double integrals commute with addition. This is the version with `(f + g) (x, y)`\n (instead of `f (x, y) + g (x, y)`) in the LHS.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_fn_integral_sub", "description": "Integrals commute with subtraction inside another integral.\n `F` can be any measurable function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable.integral_prod_right'", "description": "The Bochner integral is a.e.-measurable.\n This shows that the integrand of (the right-hand-side of) Fubini's theorem is a.e.-measurable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable.integral_norm_prod_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable.prod_right_ae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_integral_swap", "description": "Change the order of Bochner integration.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.has_finite_integral_prod_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integral_integral_add", "description": "Double integrals commute with addition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.charpoly_to_matrix", "description": "`charpoly f` is the characteristic polynomial of the matrix of `f` in any basis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.exists_finset_card_le_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.fg_of_index_ne_zero", "description": "**Schreier's Lemma**: A finite index subgroup of a finitely generated\n group is finitely generated.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.closure_mul_image_eq_top'", "description": "**Schreier's Lemma**: If `R : finset G` is a right_transversal of `H : subgroup G`\n with `1 \u2208 R`, and if `G` is generated by `S : finset G`, then `H` is generated by the `finset`\n `(R * S).image (\u03bb g, g * (to_fun hR g)\u207b\u00b9)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.card_commutator_le_of_finite_commutator_set", "description": "A theorem of Schur: The size of the commutator subgroup is bounded in terms of the number of\n commutators.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.card_commutator_dvd_index_center_pow", "description": " If `G` has `n` commutators `[g\u2081, g\u2082]`, then `|G'| \u2223 [G : Z(G)] ^ ([G : Z(G)] * n + 1)`,\nwhere `G'` denotes the commutator of `G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.commutator.finite", "description": "A theorem of Schur: A group with finitely many commutators has finite commutator subgroup.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.card_commutator_bound", "description": "A bound for the size of the commutator subgroup in terms of the number of commutators.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.rank_le_index_mul_rank", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.closure_mul_image_eq", "description": "**Schreier's Lemma**: If `R : set G` is a right_transversal of `H : subgroup G`\n with `1 \u2208 R`, and if `G` is generated by `S : set G`, then `H` is generated by the `set`\n `(R * S).image (\u03bb g, g * (to_fun hR g)\u207b\u00b9)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.closure_mul_image_mul_eq_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.closure_mul_image_eq_top", "description": "**Schreier's Lemma**: If `R : set G` is a right_transversal of `H : subgroup G`\n with `1 \u2208 R`, and if `G` is generated by `S : set G`, then `H` is generated by the `set`\n `(R * S).image (\u03bb g, g * (to_fun hR g)\u207b\u00b9)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_left_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensoring_right_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_linear.tensor_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_linear.smul_tensor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_linear", "description": "A category is `monoidal_linear R` if tensoring is `R`-linear in both factors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_linear.tensor_smul'", "description": "A category is `monoidal_linear R` if tensoring is `R`-linear in both factors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_linear.smul_tensor'", "description": "A category is `monoidal_linear R` if tensoring is `R`-linear in both factors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_right_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_linear_of_faithful", "description": " A faithful linear monoidal functor to a linear monoidal category\nensures that the domain is linear monoidal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensoring_left_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.continuous_linear_map_of_bilin_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.to_dual_map", "description": "An element `x` of an inner product space `E` induces an element of the dual space `dual \ud835\udd5c E`,\nthe map `\u03bb y, \u27eax, y\u27eb`; moreover this operation is a conjugate-linear isometric embedding of `E`\ninto `dual \ud835\udd5c E`.\nIf `E` is complete, this operation is surjective, hence a conjugate-linear isometric equivalence;\nsee `to_dual`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.to_dual", "description": "Fr\u00e9chet-Riesz representation: any `\u2113` in the dual of a Hilbert space `E` is of the form\n`\u03bb u, \u27eay, u\u27eb` for some `y : E`, i.e. `to_dual_map` is surjective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.to_dual_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.to_dual_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.ext_inner_right_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.ext_inner_left_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.continuous_linear_map_of_bilin", "description": "Maps a bounded sesquilinear form to its continuous linear map,\ngiven by interpreting the form as a map `B : E \u2192L\u22c6[\ud835\udd5c] normed_space.dual \ud835\udd5c E`\nand dualizing the result using `to_dual`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.innerSL_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.to_dual_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.unique_continuous_linear_map_of_bilin", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.to_continuous_map", "description": "Reinterpret a `continuous_order_hom` as a `continuous_map`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.has_coe_to_fun", "description": " Helper instance for when there's too many metavariables to apply `fun_like.has_coe_to_fun`\ndirectly.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.comp", "description": "Composition of `continuous_order_hom`s as a `continuous_order_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.cancel_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.comp_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.continuous_order_hom_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.coe_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom_class.to_continuous_map_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.cancel_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom", "description": "The type of continuous monotone maps from `\u03b1` to `\u03b2`, aka Priestley homomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.to_order_hom", "description": "The type of continuous monotone maps from `\u03b1` to `\u03b2`, aka Priestley homomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.continuous_to_fun", "description": "The type of continuous monotone maps from `\u03b1` to `\u03b2`, aka Priestley homomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.id_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.comp_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.id", "description": "`id` as a `continuous_order_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.partial_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.comp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.has_coe_t", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom_class.to_rel_hom_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.copy_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.copy", "description": " Copy of a `continuous_order_hom` with a new `continuous_map` equal to the old one. Useful to fix\ndefinitional equalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom_class", "description": " `continuous_order_hom_class F \u03b1 \u03b2` states that `F` is a type of continuous monotone maps.\n\nYou should extend this class when you extend `continuous_order_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom_class.coe", "description": " `continuous_order_hom_class F \u03b1 \u03b2` states that `F` is a type of continuous monotone maps.\n\nYou should extend this class when you extend `continuous_order_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom_class.coe_injective'", "description": " `continuous_order_hom_class F \u03b1 \u03b2` states that `F` is a type of continuous monotone maps.\n\nYou should extend this class when you extend `continuous_order_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom_class.map_rel", "description": " `continuous_order_hom_class F \u03b1 \u03b2` states that `F` is a type of continuous monotone maps.\n\nYou should extend this class when you extend `continuous_order_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom_class.map_continuous", "description": " `continuous_order_hom_class F \u03b1 \u03b2` states that `F` is a type of continuous monotone maps.\n\nYou should extend this class when you extend `continuous_order_hom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.id_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.to_fun_eq_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.preorder", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.coe_copy", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_order_hom.coe_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrete_topology.second_countable_topology_of_encodable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrete_topology.order_topology_of_pred_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bot_topological_space_eq_generate_from_of_pred_succ_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrete_topology_iff_order_topology_of_pred_succ'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrete_topology.order_topology_of_pred_succ'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrete_topology.metrizable_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrete_topology.first_countable_topology", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrete_topology_iff_order_topology_of_pred_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_order.bot_topological_space_eq_generate_from", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.compl_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.symm_image_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.image_symm_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.preimage_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_mono.order_iso_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_mono.order_iso", "description": " A strictly monotone function from a linear order is an order isomorphism between its domain and\nits range.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.range_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.preimage_symm_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_mono.order_iso_of_surjective_self_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compl_antitone", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.set_congr", "description": "Order isomorphism between two equal sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.compl", "description": "Taking complements as an order isomorphism to the order dual.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_mono.coe_order_iso_of_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_mono_on.order_iso", "description": " If a function `f` is strictly monotone on a set `s`, then it defines an order isomorphism\nbetween `s` and its image.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.image_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.symm_preimage_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.image_eq_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_mono.order_iso_of_surjective", "description": "A strictly monotone surjective function from a linear order is an order isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.set.univ", "description": "Order isomorphism between `univ : set \u03b1` and `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "compl_strict_anti", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.compl_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_mono.order_iso_of_surjective_symm_apply_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_iso_limit_curry_comp_lim_hom_\u03c0_\u03c0_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.cone_of_cone_uncurry", "description": "Given a diagram `D` of limit cones over the `F.obj j`, and a cone over `uncurry.obj F`,\nwe can construct a cone over the diagram consisting of the cone points from `D`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.cone_of_cone_uncurry_\u03c0_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.diagram_of_cones.mk_of_has_limits", "description": "Given a functor `F : J \u2964 K \u2964 C`, with all needed limits,\nwe can construct a diagram consisting of the limit cone over each functor `F.obj j`,\nand the universal cone morphisms between these.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_curry_swap_comp_lim_iso_limit_curry_comp_lim_inv_\u03c0_\u03c0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.diagram_of_cones.cone_points_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_curry_swap_comp_lim_iso_limit_curry_comp_lim_hom_\u03c0_\u03c0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.diagram_of_cones.cone_points", "description": "Extract the functor `J \u2964 C` consisting of the cone points and the maps between them,\nfrom a `diagram_of_cones`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.diagram_of_cones.mk_of_has_limits_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_flip_comp_lim_iso_limit_comp_lim_inv_\u03c0_\u03c0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_uncurry_iso_limit_comp_lim_inv_\u03c0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.cone_of_cone_uncurry_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.diagram_of_cones", "description": "A structure carrying a diagram of cones over the functors `F.obj j`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.diagram_of_cones.obj", "description": "A structure carrying a diagram of cones over the functors `F.obj j`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.diagram_of_cones.map", "description": "A structure carrying a diagram of cones over the functors `F.obj j`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.diagram_of_cones.id", "description": "A structure carrying a diagram of cones over the functors `F.obj j`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.diagram_of_cones.comp", "description": "A structure carrying a diagram of cones over the functors `F.obj j`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_uncurry_iso_limit_comp_lim_hom_\u03c0_\u03c0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.diagram_of_cones_inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_flip_comp_lim_iso_limit_comp_lim_inv_\u03c0_\u03c0_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_flip_comp_lim_iso_limit_comp_lim_hom_\u03c0_\u03c0_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_curry_swap_comp_lim_iso_limit_curry_comp_lim", "description": "A variant of the Fubini theorem for a functor `G : J \u00d7 K \u2964 C`,\nshowing that $\\lim_k \\lim_j G(j,k) \u2245 \\lim_j \\lim_k G(j,k)$.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.diagram_of_cones.cone_points_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_iso_limit_curry_comp_lim_inv_\u03c0_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_flip_comp_lim_iso_limit_comp_lim", "description": "The limit of `F.flip \u22d9 lim` is isomorphic to the limit of `F \u22d9 lim`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_flip_comp_lim_iso_limit_comp_lim_hom_\u03c0_\u03c0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_iso_limit_curry_comp_lim_inv_\u03c0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.diagram_of_cones.mk_of_has_limits_map_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_iso_limit_curry_comp_lim", "description": "The Fubini theorem for a functor `G : J \u00d7 K \u2964 C`,\nshowing that the limit of `G` can be computed as\nthe limit of the limits of the functors `G.obj (j, _)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_iso_limit_curry_comp_lim_hom_\u03c0_\u03c0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.diagram_of_cones.mk_of_has_limits_cone_points", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.cone_of_cone_uncurry_is_limit", "description": "`cone_of_cone_uncurry Q c` is a limit cone when `c` is a limit cone.`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_uncurry_iso_limit_comp_lim_hom_\u03c0_\u03c0_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_uncurry_iso_limit_comp_lim_inv_\u03c0_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_uncurry_iso_limit_comp_lim", "description": "The Fubini theorem for a functor `F : J \u2964 K \u2964 C`,\nshowing that the limit of `uncurry.obj F` can be computed as\nthe limit of the limits of the functors `F.obj j`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo1977_q6", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1977_q6.imo1977_q6_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "tensor_product.direct_sum", "description": " The linear equivalence `(\u2a01 i\u2081, M\u2081 i\u2081) \u2297 (\u2a01 i\u2082, M\u2082 i\u2082) \u2243 (\u2a01 i\u2081, \u2a01 i\u2082, M\u2081 i\u2081 \u2297 M\u2082 i\u2082)`, i.e.\n\"tensor product distributes over direct sum\".", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.direct_sum_lof_tmul_lof", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.direct_sum_right_tmul_lof", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.direct_sum_right_symm_lof_tmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.direct_sum_left", "description": "Tensor products distribute over a direct sum on the left .", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.direct_sum_left_tmul_lof", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.direct_sum_left_symm_lof_tmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_product.direct_sum_right", "description": "Tensor products distribute over a direct sum on the right.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo.has_coe_to_fun", "description": " Helper instance for when there's too many metavariables to apply\n`fun_like.has_coe_to_fun` directly.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo.map_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo.id", "description": "The identity function of a `comm_ring` is a ring involution.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo", "description": "A ring involution", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo.to_fun", "description": "A ring involution", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo.inv_fun", "description": "A ring involution", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo.left_inv", "description": "A ring involution", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo.right_inv", "description": "A ring involution", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo.map_mul'", "description": "A ring involution", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo.map_add'", "description": "A ring involution", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo.involution'", "description": "A ring involution", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo.mk'", "description": "Construct a ring involution from a ring homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo.coe_ring_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo_class.to_ring_equiv_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo.ring_invo_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo_class", "description": " `ring_invo_class F R S` states that `F` is a type of ring involutions.\nYou should extend this class when you extend `ring_invo`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo_class.coe", "description": " `ring_invo_class F R S` states that `F` is a type of ring involutions.\nYou should extend this class when you extend `ring_invo`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo_class.inv", "description": " `ring_invo_class F R S` states that `F` is a type of ring involutions.\nYou should extend this class when you extend `ring_invo`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo_class.left_inv", "description": " `ring_invo_class F R S` states that `F` is a type of ring involutions.\nYou should extend this class when you extend `ring_invo`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo_class.right_inv", "description": " `ring_invo_class F R S` states that `F` is a type of ring involutions.\nYou should extend this class when you extend `ring_invo`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo_class.coe_injective'", "description": " `ring_invo_class F R S` states that `F` is a type of ring involutions.\nYou should extend this class when you extend `ring_invo`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo_class.map_mul", "description": " `ring_invo_class F R S` states that `F` is a type of ring involutions.\nYou should extend this class when you extend `ring_invo`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo_class.map_add", "description": " `ring_invo_class F R S` states that `F` is a type of ring involutions.\nYou should extend this class when you extend `ring_invo`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo_class.involution", "description": " `ring_invo_class F R S` states that `F` is a type of ring involutions.\nYou should extend this class when you extend `ring_invo`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo.to_fun_eq_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo.to_ring_equiv", "description": "The equivalence of rings underlying a ring involution.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo.has_coe_to_ring_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_invo.involution", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_subgroup.ker_saturated", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.saturated_iff_npow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_subgroup.saturated", "description": "An additive subgroup `H` of `G` is *saturated* if\nfor all `n : \u2115` and `g : G` with `n\u2022g \u2208 H` we have `n = 0` or `g \u2208 H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_subgroup.saturated_iff_zsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.saturated", "description": " A subgroup `H` of `G` is *saturated* if for all `n : \u2115` and `g : G` with `g^n \u2208 H`\nwe have `n = 0` or `g \u2208 H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_subgroup.saturated_iff_nsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.saturated_iff_zpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exterior_algebra.graded_algebra", "description": "The exterior algebra is graded by the powers of the submodule `(exterior_algebra.\u03b9 R).range`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exterior_algebra.graded_algebra.lift_\u03b9", "description": " `exterior_algebra.graded_algebra.\u03b9` lifted to exterior algebra. This is\nprimarily an auxiliary construction used to provide `exterior_algebra.graded_algebra`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exterior_algebra.graded_algebra.\u03b9_sq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exterior_algebra.graded_algebra.\u03b9_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exterior_algebra.graded_algebra.lift_\u03b9_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exterior_algebra.graded_algebra.\u03b9", "description": " A version of `exterior_algebra.\u03b9` that maps directly into the graded structure. This is\nprimarily an auxiliary construction used to provide `exterior_algebra.graded_algebra`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uliftable.adapt_down", "description": "convenient shortcut to avoid manipulating `ulift`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uliftable.down_map", "description": "map function that moves down universes", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reader_t.uliftable'", "description": "for specific reader monads, this function helps to create a uliftable instance", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uliftable", "description": " Given a universe polymorphic type family `M.{u} : Type u\u2081 \u2192 Type\nu\u2082`, this class convert between instantiations, from\n`M.{u} : Type u\u2081 \u2192 Type u\u2082` to `M.{v} : Type v\u2081 \u2192 Type v\u2082` and back", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uliftable.congr", "description": " Given a universe polymorphic type family `M.{u} : Type u\u2081 \u2192 Type\nu\u2082`, this class convert between instantiations, from\n`M.{u} : Type u\u2081 \u2192 Type u\u2082` to `M.{v} : Type v\u2081 \u2192 Type v\u2082` and back", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uliftable.down", "description": " The most common practical use of `uliftable` (together with `up`), this function takes\n`x : M.{max u v} (ulift.{v} \u03b1)` and lowers it to `M.{u} \u03b1`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "state_t.uliftable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uliftable.up", "description": " The most common practical use `uliftable` (together with `up`), this function takes\n`x : M.{u} \u03b1` and lifts it to M.{max u v} (ulift.{v} \u03b1)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "state_t.uliftable'", "description": "for specific state types, this function helps to create a uliftable instance", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "id.uliftable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uliftable.adapt_up", "description": "convenient shortcut to avoid manipulating `ulift`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uliftable.up_down", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "reader_t.uliftable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_t.uliftable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "writer_t.uliftable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uliftable.down_up", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_t.uliftable'", "description": "for specific continuation passing monads, this function helps to create a uliftable instance", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "writer_t.uliftable'", "description": "for specific writer monads, this function helps to create a uliftable instance", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uliftable.up_map", "description": "map function that moves up universes", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_mul_tsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_hom.le_map_tsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_tsub_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_hom.le_map_tsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso.map_tsub", "description": " An order isomorphism between types with ordered subtraction preserves subtraction provided that\nit preserves addition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparator_iff_faithful_preadditive_yoneda_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preadditive.is_coseparator_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_coseparator_iff_faithful_preadditive_yoneda", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preadditive.is_separating_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separator_iff_faithful_preadditive_coyoneda_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_separator_iff_faithful_preadditive_coyoneda", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preadditive.is_separator_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preadditive.is_coseparating_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "krull_topology_t2", "description": "If `L/K` is an algebraic extension, then the Krull topology on `L \u2243\u2090[K] L` is Hausdorff.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "intermediate_field.fixing_subgroup.bot", "description": "This lemma says that `Gal(L/K) = L \u2243\u2090[K] L`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "intermediate_field.fixing_subgroup_is_open", "description": "Let `L/E/K` be a tower of fields with `E/K` finite. Then `Gal(L/E)` is an open subgroup of\n `L \u2243\u2090[K] L`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_exts", "description": " Given a field extension `L/K`, `finite_exts K L` is the set of\nintermediate field extensions `L/E/K` such that `E/K` is finite", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_dimensional_sup", "description": " If `E1` and `E2` are finite-dimensional intermediate fields, then so is their compositum.\nThis rephrases a result already in mathlib so that it is compatible with our type classes", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "im_finite_dimensional", "description": " Mapping a finite dimensional intermediate field along an algebra equivalence gives\na finite-dimensional intermediate field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "alg_equiv.topological_group", "description": "For a field extension `L/K`, the Krull topology on `L \u2243\u2090[K] L` makes it a topological group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "intermediate_field.fixing_subgroup.antimono", "description": "The map `E \u21a6 Gal(L/E)` is inclusion-reversing", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "intermediate_field.map_id", "description": "Mapping intermediate fields along the identity does not change them", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "intermediate_field.map_mono", "description": "Mapping intermediate fields along algebra equivalences preserves the partial order", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "krull_topology_totally_disconnected", "description": "If `L/K` is an algebraic field extension, then the Krull topology on `L \u2243\u2090[K] L` is\n totally disconnected.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gal_basis", "description": " Given a field extension `L/K`, `gal_basis K L` is the filter basis on `L \u2243\u2090[K] L` whose sets\nare `Gal(L/E)` for intermediate fields `E` with `E/K` finite dimensional", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fixed_by_finite", "description": " Given a field extension `L/K`, `fixed_by_finite K L` is the set of\nsubsets `Gal(L/E)` of `L \u2243\u2090[K] L`, where `E/K` is finite", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "intermediate_field.finite_dimensional_bot", "description": "For an field extension `L/K`, the intermediate field `K` is finite-dimensional over `K`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gal_group_basis", "description": " For a field extension `L/K`, `gal_group_basis K L` is the group filter basis on `L \u2243\u2090[K] L`\nwhose sets are `Gal(L/E)` for finite subextensions `E/K`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "intermediate_field.mem_fixing_subgroup_iff", "description": "An element of `L \u2243\u2090[K] L` is in `Gal(L/E)` if and only if it fixes every element of `E`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "top_fixed_by_finite", "description": "If `L/K` is a field extension, then we have `Gal(L/K) \u2208 fixed_by_finite K L`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_gal_basis_iff", "description": " A subset of `L \u2243\u2090[K] L` is a member of `gal_basis K L` if and only if it is the underlying set\nof `Gal(L/E)` for some finite subextension `E/K`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "intermediate_field.fixing_subgroup_is_closed", "description": "Given a tower of fields `L/E/K`, with `E/K` finite, the subgroup `Gal(L/E) \u2264 L \u2243\u2090[K] L` is\n closed.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "krull_topology", "description": " For a field extension `L/K`, `krull_topology K L` is the topological space structure on\n`L \u2243\u2090[K] L` induced by the group filter basis `gal_group_basis K L`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.tfae_cons_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.tfae.out", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.tfae_cons_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.tfae_of_forall", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.tfae", "description": "tfae: The Following (propositions) Are Equivalent.\n\nThe `tfae_have` and `tfae_finish` tactics can be useful in proofs with `tfae` goals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.tfae_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.tfae_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.tfae_of_cycle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.a_mul_xa", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.xa_mul_a", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.a_one_pow_n", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.fintype", "description": "If `0 < n`, then `quaternion_group n` is a finite group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.quaternion_group_zero_equiv_dihedral_group_zero", "description": " The special case that more or less by definition `quaternion_group 0` is isomorphic to the\ninfinite dihedral group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.quaternion_group_one_is_cyclic", "description": "In the special case `n = 1`, `quaternion 1` is a cyclic group (of order `4`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.one_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.order_of_a_one", "description": "If `0 < n`, then `a 1` has order `2 * n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.nontrivial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.xa_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.xa_mul_xa", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.exponent", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.card", "description": "If `0 < n`, then `quaternion_group n` has `4n` elements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group", "description": "The (generalised) quaternion group `quaternion_group n` of order `4n`. It can be defined by the\npresentation $\\langle a, x | a^{2n} = 1, x^2 = a^n, x^{-1}ax=a^{-1}\\rangle$. We write `a i` for\n$a^i$ and `xa i` for $x * a^i$.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.a", "description": "The (generalised) quaternion group `quaternion_group n` of order `4n`. It can be defined by the\npresentation $\\langle a, x | a^{2n} = 1, x^2 = a^n, x^{-1}ax=a^{-1}\\rangle$. We write `a i` for\n$a^i$ and `xa i` for $x * a^i$.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.xa", "description": "The (generalised) quaternion group `quaternion_group n` of order `4n`. It can be defined by the\npresentation $\\langle a, x | a^{2n} = 1, x^2 = a^n, x^{-1}ax=a^{-1}\\rangle$. We write `a i` for\n$a^i$ and `xa i` for $x * a^i$.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.group", "description": "The group structure on `quaternion_group n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.order_of_a", "description": "If `0 < n`, then `a i` has order `(2 * n) / gcd (2 * n) i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.order_of_xa", "description": "If `0 < n`, then `xa i` has order 4.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.xa_pow_four", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.a_one_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quaternion_group.a_mul_a", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.fix", "description": "Well-founded fixpoint", "p": "core"}, {"name": "prod.rprod_wf", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "nat.lt_wf", "description": "less-than is well-founded", "p": "core"}, {"name": "prod.rprod", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "prod.rprod.intro", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "subrelation.accessible", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "inv_image.accessible", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "sizeof_measure", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "prod.lex", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "prod.lex.left", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "prod.lex.right", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "measure", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "acc", "description": " A value `x : \u03b1` is accessible from `r` when every value that's lesser under `r` is also\naccessible. Note that any value that's minimal under `r` is vacuously accessible.\n\nEquivalently, `acc r x` when there is no infinite chain of elements starting at `x` that are related\nunder `r`.\n\nThis is used to state the definition of well-foundedness (see `well_founded`).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "acc.intro", "description": " A value `x : \u03b1` is accessible from `r` when every value that's lesser under `r` is also\naccessible. Note that any value that's minimal under `r` is vacuously accessible.\n\nEquivalently, `acc r x` when there is no infinite chain of elements starting at `x` that are related\nunder `r`.\n\nThis is used to state the definition of well-foundedness (see `well_founded`).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "well_founded", "description": " A relation `r : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1 \u2192 Prop` is well-founded when `\u2200 x, (\u2200 y, r y x \u2192 P y \u2192 P x) \u2192 P x` for all\npredicates `P`. Equivalently, `acc r x` for all `x`.\n\nOnce you know that a relation is well_founded, you can use it to define fixpoint functions on `\u03b1`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "well_founded.apply", "description": " A relation `r : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1 \u2192 Prop` is well-founded when `\u2200 x, (\u2200 y, r y x \u2192 P y \u2192 P x) \u2192 P x` for all\npredicates `P`. Equivalently, `acc r x` for all `x`.\n\nOnce you know that a relation is well_founded, you can use it to define fixpoint functions on `\u03b1`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "subrelation.wf", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "well_founded.fix_F", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "prod.lex_wf", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "inv_image.wf", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "well_founded.recursion", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "well_founded.induction", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "prod.lex_accessible", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_well_founded_of_has_sizeof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "prod.has_well_founded", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "sizeof_measure_wf", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "well_founded.fix_F_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "empty_wf", "description": "Empty relation is well-founded", "p": "core"}, {"name": "measure_wf", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "well_founded.fix_eq", "description": "Well-founded fixpoint satisfies fixpoint equation", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_well_founded", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_well_founded.r", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_well_founded.wf", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "acc.inv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "prod.rprod_sub_lex", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "wstar_algebra", "description": "Sakai's definition of a von Neumann algebra as a C^* algebra with a Banach space predual.\n\nSo that we can unambiguously talk about these \"abstract\" von Neumann algebras\nin parallel with the \"concrete\" ones (weakly closed *-subalgebras of B(H)),\nwe name this definition `wstar_algebra`.\n\nNote that for now we only assert the mere existence of predual, rather than picking one.\nThis may later prove problematic, and need to be revisited.\nPicking one may cause problems with definitional unification of different instances.\nOne the other hand, not picking one means that the weak-* topology\n(which depends on a choice of predual) must be defined using the choice,\nand we may be unhappy with the resulting opaqueness of the definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "wstar_algebra.exists_predual", "description": "Sakai's definition of a von Neumann algebra as a C^* algebra with a Banach space predual.\n\nSo that we can unambiguously talk about these \"abstract\" von Neumann algebras\nin parallel with the \"concrete\" ones (weakly closed *-subalgebras of B(H)),\nwe name this definition `wstar_algebra`.\n\nNote that for now we only assert the mere existence of predual, rather than picking one.\nThis may later prove problematic, and need to be revisited.\nPicking one may cause problems with definitional unification of different instances.\nOne the other hand, not picking one means that the weak-* topology\n(which depends on a choice of predual) must be defined using the choice,\nand we may be unhappy with the resulting opaqueness of the definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra.continuous_linear_map.star_mem_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra.commutant_commutant", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra.centralizer_centralizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra.commutant", "description": "The centralizer of a `von_neumann_algebra`, as a `von_neumann_algebra`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra.continuous_linear_map.set_like", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra.continuous_linear_map.subring_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra", "description": "The double commutant definition of a von Neumann algebra,\nas a *-closed subalgebra of bounded operators on a Hilbert space,\nwhich is equal to its double commutant.\n\nNote that this definition is parameterised by the Hilbert space\non which the algebra faithfully acts, as is standard in the literature.\nSee `wstar_algebra` for the abstract notion (a C^*-algebra with Banach space predual).\n\nNote this is a bundled structure, parameterised by the Hilbert space `H`,\nrather than a typeclass on the type of elements.\nThus we can't say that the bounded operators `H \u2192L[\u2102] H` form a `von_neumann_algebra`\n(although we will later construct the instance `wstar_algebra (H \u2192L[\u2102] H)`),\nand instead will use `\u22a4 : von_neumann_algebra H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra.carrier", "description": "The double commutant definition of a von Neumann algebra,\nas a *-closed subalgebra of bounded operators on a Hilbert space,\nwhich is equal to its double commutant.\n\nNote that this definition is parameterised by the Hilbert space\non which the algebra faithfully acts, as is standard in the literature.\nSee `wstar_algebra` for the abstract notion (a C^*-algebra with Banach space predual).\n\nNote this is a bundled structure, parameterised by the Hilbert space `H`,\nrather than a typeclass on the type of elements.\nThus we can't say that the bounded operators `H \u2192L[\u2102] H` form a `von_neumann_algebra`\n(although we will later construct the instance `wstar_algebra (H \u2192L[\u2102] H)`),\nand instead will use `\u22a4 : von_neumann_algebra H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra.mul_mem'", "description": "The double commutant definition of a von Neumann algebra,\nas a *-closed subalgebra of bounded operators on a Hilbert space,\nwhich is equal to its double commutant.\n\nNote that this definition is parameterised by the Hilbert space\non which the algebra faithfully acts, as is standard in the literature.\nSee `wstar_algebra` for the abstract notion (a C^*-algebra with Banach space predual).\n\nNote this is a bundled structure, parameterised by the Hilbert space `H`,\nrather than a typeclass on the type of elements.\nThus we can't say that the bounded operators `H \u2192L[\u2102] H` form a `von_neumann_algebra`\n(although we will later construct the instance `wstar_algebra (H \u2192L[\u2102] H)`),\nand instead will use `\u22a4 : von_neumann_algebra H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra.one_mem'", "description": "The double commutant definition of a von Neumann algebra,\nas a *-closed subalgebra of bounded operators on a Hilbert space,\nwhich is equal to its double commutant.\n\nNote that this definition is parameterised by the Hilbert space\non which the algebra faithfully acts, as is standard in the literature.\nSee `wstar_algebra` for the abstract notion (a C^*-algebra with Banach space predual).\n\nNote this is a bundled structure, parameterised by the Hilbert space `H`,\nrather than a typeclass on the type of elements.\nThus we can't say that the bounded operators `H \u2192L[\u2102] H` form a `von_neumann_algebra`\n(although we will later construct the instance `wstar_algebra (H \u2192L[\u2102] H)`),\nand instead will use `\u22a4 : von_neumann_algebra H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra.add_mem'", "description": "The double commutant definition of a von Neumann algebra,\nas a *-closed subalgebra of bounded operators on a Hilbert space,\nwhich is equal to its double commutant.\n\nNote that this definition is parameterised by the Hilbert space\non which the algebra faithfully acts, as is standard in the literature.\nSee `wstar_algebra` for the abstract notion (a C^*-algebra with Banach space predual).\n\nNote this is a bundled structure, parameterised by the Hilbert space `H`,\nrather than a typeclass on the type of elements.\nThus we can't say that the bounded operators `H \u2192L[\u2102] H` form a `von_neumann_algebra`\n(although we will later construct the instance `wstar_algebra (H \u2192L[\u2102] H)`),\nand instead will use `\u22a4 : von_neumann_algebra H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra.zero_mem'", "description": "The double commutant definition of a von Neumann algebra,\nas a *-closed subalgebra of bounded operators on a Hilbert space,\nwhich is equal to its double commutant.\n\nNote that this definition is parameterised by the Hilbert space\non which the algebra faithfully acts, as is standard in the literature.\nSee `wstar_algebra` for the abstract notion (a C^*-algebra with Banach space predual).\n\nNote this is a bundled structure, parameterised by the Hilbert space `H`,\nrather than a typeclass on the type of elements.\nThus we can't say that the bounded operators `H \u2192L[\u2102] H` form a `von_neumann_algebra`\n(although we will later construct the instance `wstar_algebra (H \u2192L[\u2102] H)`),\nand instead will use `\u22a4 : von_neumann_algebra H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra.algebra_map_mem'", "description": "The double commutant definition of a von Neumann algebra,\nas a *-closed subalgebra of bounded operators on a Hilbert space,\nwhich is equal to its double commutant.\n\nNote that this definition is parameterised by the Hilbert space\non which the algebra faithfully acts, as is standard in the literature.\nSee `wstar_algebra` for the abstract notion (a C^*-algebra with Banach space predual).\n\nNote this is a bundled structure, parameterised by the Hilbert space `H`,\nrather than a typeclass on the type of elements.\nThus we can't say that the bounded operators `H \u2192L[\u2102] H` form a `von_neumann_algebra`\n(although we will later construct the instance `wstar_algebra (H \u2192L[\u2102] H)`),\nand instead will use `\u22a4 : von_neumann_algebra H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra.star_mem'", "description": "The double commutant definition of a von Neumann algebra,\nas a *-closed subalgebra of bounded operators on a Hilbert space,\nwhich is equal to its double commutant.\n\nNote that this definition is parameterised by the Hilbert space\non which the algebra faithfully acts, as is standard in the literature.\nSee `wstar_algebra` for the abstract notion (a C^*-algebra with Banach space predual).\n\nNote this is a bundled structure, parameterised by the Hilbert space `H`,\nrather than a typeclass on the type of elements.\nThus we can't say that the bounded operators `H \u2192L[\u2102] H` form a `von_neumann_algebra`\n(although we will later construct the instance `wstar_algebra (H \u2192L[\u2102] H)`),\nand instead will use `\u22a4 : von_neumann_algebra H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra.centralizer_centralizer'", "description": "The double commutant definition of a von Neumann algebra,\nas a *-closed subalgebra of bounded operators on a Hilbert space,\nwhich is equal to its double commutant.\n\nNote that this definition is parameterised by the Hilbert space\non which the algebra faithfully acts, as is standard in the literature.\nSee `wstar_algebra` for the abstract notion (a C^*-algebra with Banach space predual).\n\nNote this is a bundled structure, parameterised by the Hilbert space `H`,\nrather than a typeclass on the type of elements.\nThus we can't say that the bounded operators `H \u2192L[\u2102] H` form a `von_neumann_algebra`\n(although we will later construct the instance `wstar_algebra (H \u2192L[\u2102] H)`),\nand instead will use `\u22a4 : von_neumann_algebra H`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra.coe_commutant", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra.mem_carrier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra.to_star_subalgebra", "description": "Consider a von Neumann algebra acting on a Hilbert space `H` as a *-subalgebra of `H \u2192L[\u2102] H`.\n(That is, we forget that it is equal to its double commutant\nor equivalently that it is closed in the weak and strong operator topologies.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "von_neumann_algebra.mem_commutant_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integral_closure.mem_lifts_of_monic_of_dvd_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_primitive.irreducible_of_irreducible_map_of_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_primitive.irreducible_iff_irreducible_map_fraction_map", "description": "**Gauss's Lemma** for GCD domains states that a primitive polynomial is irreducible iff it is\n irreducible in the fraction field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_integrally_closed.eq_map_mul_C_of_dvd", "description": "If `K = Frac(R)` and `g : K[X]` divides a monic polynomial with coefficients in `R`, then\n   `g * (C g.leading_coeff\u207b\u00b9)` has coefficients in `R`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_primitive.dvd_of_fraction_map_dvd_fraction_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.monic.irreducible_iff_irreducible_map_fraction_map", "description": "**Gauss's Lemma** for integrally closed domains states that a monic polynomial is irreducible\n iff it is irreducible in the fraction field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_unit_or_eq_zero_of_is_unit_integer_normalization_prim_part", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_primitive.is_unit_iff_is_unit_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_primitive.int.irreducible_iff_irreducible_map_cast", "description": "**Gauss's Lemma** for `\u2124` states that a primitive integer polynomial is irreducible iff it is\n irreducible over `\u211a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_primitive.dvd_iff_fraction_map_dvd_fraction_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_primitive.int.dvd_iff_map_cast_dvd_map_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_integrally_closed_iff'", "description": "Integrally closed domains are precisely the domains for in which Gauss's lemma holds\n   for monic polynomials", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_primitive.is_unit_iff_is_unit_map_of_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.monic.dvd_of_fraction_map_dvd_fraction_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.monic.dvd_iff_fraction_map_dvd_fraction_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "odd.neg_one_zpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zpow_bit1_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "odd.neg_zpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conformal_map", "description": "A continuous linear map `f'` is said to be conformal if it's\n   a nonzero multiple of a linear isometry.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_isometry.is_conformal_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conformal_map.smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conformal_map.comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conformal_map.ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conformal_map_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conformal_map_of_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conformal_map_const_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conformal_map.injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.to_matrix_row_sum_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.to_matrix_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.to_matrix", "description": " Given an affine basis `p`, and a family of points `q : \u03b9' \u2192 P`, this is the matrix whose\nrows are the barycentric coordinates of `q` with respect to `p`.\n\nIt is an affine equivalent of `basis.to_matrix`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.to_matrix_vec_mul_coords", "description": " A change of basis formula for barycentric coordinates.\n\nSee also `affine_basis.to_matrix_inv_mul_affine_basis_to_matrix`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.is_unit_to_matrix_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.affine_span_eq_top_of_to_matrix_left_inv", "description": " Given a family of points `p : \u03b9' \u2192 P` and an affine basis `b`, if the matrix whose rows are the\ncoordinates of `p` with respect `b` has a left inverse, then `p` spans the entire space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.to_matrix_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.is_unit_to_matrix", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.affine_independent_of_to_matrix_right_inv", "description": " Given a family of points `p : \u03b9' \u2192 P` and an affine basis `b`, if the matrix whose rows are the\ncoordinates of `p` with respect `b` has a right inverse, then `p` is affine independent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.to_matrix_mul_to_matrix", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.to_matrix_inv_vec_mul_to_matrix", "description": " A change of basis formula for barycentric coordinates.\n\nSee also `affine_basis.to_matrix_vec_mul_coords`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.det_smul_coords_eq_cramer_coords", "description": " If we fix a background affine basis `b`, then for any other basis `b\u2082`, we can characterise\nthe barycentric coordinates provided by `b\u2082` in terms of determinants relative to `b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.division_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.field", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.zpow\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_continuous_inv\u2080", "description": " A type with `0` and `has_inv` such that `\u03bb x, x\u207b\u00b9` is continuous at all nonzero points. Any\nnormed (semi)field has this property.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_continuous_inv\u2080.continuous_at_inv\u2080", "description": " A type with `0` and `has_inv` such that `\u03bb x, x\u207b\u00b9` is continuous at all nonzero points. Any\nnormed (semi)field has this property.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on.inv\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_at.div", "description": " Continuity at a point of the result of dividing two functions continuous at that point, where\nthe denominator is nonzero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_within_at.div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homeomorph.mul_left\u2080", "description": " Left multiplication by a nonzero element in a `group_with_zero` with continuous multiplication\nis a homeomorphism of the underlying type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.inv\u2080", "description": " If a function converges to a nonzero value, its inverse converges to the inverse of this value.\nWe use the name `tendsto.inv\u2080` as `tendsto.inv` is already used in multiplicative topological\ngroups.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homeomorph.coe_mul_right\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_at.div_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.inv\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homeomorph.mul_left\u2080_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.div_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_at.zpow\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto_mul_iff_of_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on.div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homeomorph.coe_mul_left\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.comp_div_cases", "description": "`h x (f x / g x)` is continuous under certain conditions, even if the denominator is sometimes\n `0`. See docstring of `continuous_at.comp_div_cases`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.div_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_within_at.zpow\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "units.embedding_coe\u2080", "description": " If `G\u2080` is a group with zero with topology such that `x \u21a6 x\u207b\u00b9` is continuous at all nonzero\npoints. Then the coercion `M\u02e3 \u2192 M` is a topological embedding.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on.div_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on_inv\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_at.comp_div_cases", "description": " The function `f x / g x` is discontinuous when `g x = 0`.\nHowever, under appropriate conditions, `h x (f x / g x)` is still continuous.\nThe condition is that if `g a = 0` then `h x y` must tend to `h a 0` when `x` tends to `a`,\nwith no information about `y`. This is represented by the `\u22a4` filter.\nNote: `filter.tendsto_prod_top_iff` characterizes this convergence in uniform spaces.\nSee also `filter.prod_top` and `filter.mem_prod_top`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_within_at.div_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on.zpow\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_inv\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_within_at.inv\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_at.inv\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous.zpow\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homeomorph.mul_right\u2080_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homeomorph.mul_right\u2080", "description": " Right multiplication by a nonzero element in a `group_with_zero` with continuous multiplication\nis a homeomorphism of the underlying type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_at_zpow\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on_zpow\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option_t.monad_run", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.fail", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.bind", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.orelse", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.monad_map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.alternative", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.pure", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.catch", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.monad_functor", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.of_option", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.has_monad_lift", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.run", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.bind_cont", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.monad_except", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.monad", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "option_t.lift", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_free_group.to_free_group", "description": "Any free group is isomorphic to \"the\" free group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_free_group.lift", "description": " The equivalence between functions on the generators and group homomorphisms from a free group\ngiven by those generators.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_group.is_free_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_free_group", "description": "`is_free_group G` means that `G` isomorphic to a free group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_free_group.generators", "description": "`is_free_group G` means that `G` isomorphic to a free group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_free_group.mul_equiv", "description": "`is_free_group G` means that `G` isomorphic to a free group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_free_group.to_free_group_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_free_group.to_free_group_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_free_group.of", "description": "The canonical injection of G's generators into G", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_free_group.lift_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_free_group.of_eq_free_group_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_free_group.of_mul_equiv", "description": "Being a free group transports across group isomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_free_group.of_unique_lift", "description": " If a group satisfies the universal property of a free group, then it is a free group, where\nthe universal property is expressed as in `is_free_group.unique_lift`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_free_group.lift'_eq_free_group_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_free_group.lift_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_free_group.unique_lift", "description": " The universal property of a free group: A functions from the generators of `G` to another\ngroup extends in a unique way to a homomorphism from `G`.\n\nNote that since `is_free_group.lift` is expressed as a bijection, it already\nexpresses the universal property.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_free_group.of_lift", "description": " If a group satisfies the universal property of a free group, then it is a free group, where\nthe universal property is expressed as in `is_free_group.lift` and its properties.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_free_group.ext_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "restate_axiom", "description": "`restate_axiom` takes a structure field, and makes a new, definitionally simplified copy of it.\nIf the existing field name ends with a `'`, the new field just has the prime removed. Otherwise,\nwe append `_lemma`.\nThe main application is to provide clean versions of structure fields that have been tagged with\nan auto_param.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "restate_axiom_cmd", "description": "`restate_axiom` makes a new copy of a structure field, first definitionally simplifying the type.\nThis is useful to remove `auto_param` or `opt_param` from the statement.\n\nAs an example, we have:\n```lean\nstructure A :=\n(x : \u2115)\n(a' : x = 1 . skip)\n\nexample (z : A) : z.x = 1 := by rw A.a' -- rewrite tactic failed, lemma is not an equality nor a iff\n\nrestate_axiom A.a'\nexample (z : A) : z.x = 1 := by rw A.a\n```\n\nBy default, `restate_axiom` names the new lemma by removing a trailing `'`, or otherwise appending\n`_lemma` if there is no trailing `'`. You can also give `restate_axiom` a second argument to\nspecify the new name, as in\n```lean\nrestate_axiom A.a f\nexample (z : A) : z.x = 1 := by rw A.f\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.command.restate_axiom", "description": "`restate_axiom` makes a new copy of a structure field, first definitionally simplifying the type.\nThis is useful to remove `auto_param` or `opt_param` from the statement.\n\nAs an example, we have:\n```lean\nstructure A :=\n(x : \u2115)\n(a' : x = 1 . skip)\n\nexample (z : A) : z.x = 1 := by rw A.a' -- rewrite tactic failed, lemma is not an equality nor a iff\n\nrestate_axiom A.a'\nexample (z : A) : z.x = 1 := by rw A.a\n```\n\nBy default, `restate_axiom` names the new lemma by removing a trailing `'`, or otherwise appending\n`_lemma` if there is no trailing `'`. You can also give `restate_axiom` a second argument to\nspecify the new name, as in\n```lean\nrestate_axiom A.a f\nexample (z : A) : z.x = 1 := by rw A.f\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal_factor_at_inner_eq_mul_inner'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal_factor_at_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal_at_iff", "description": "A real differentiable map `f` is conformal at point `x` if and only if its\n   differential `f'` at that point scales every inner product by a positive scalar.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal_factor_at", "description": "The conformal factor of a conformal map at some point `x`. Some authors refer to this function\n   as the characteristic function of the conformal map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal_at_iff'", "description": "A real differentiable map `f` is conformal at point `x` if and only if its\n   differential `fderiv \u211d f x` at that point scales every inner product by a positive scalar.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal_factor_at_inner_eq_mul_inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial_functions_closure_eq_top", "description": "The **Weierstrass Approximation Theorem**:\npolynomials functions on `[a, b] \u2286 \u211d` are dense in `C([a,b],\u211d)`\n\n(While we could deduce this as an application of the Stone-Weierstrass theorem,\nour proof of that relies on the fact that `abs` is in the closure of polynomials on `[-M, M]`,\nso we may as well get this done first.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_map_mem_polynomial_functions_closure", "description": "An alternative statement of Weierstrass' theorem.\n\nEvery real-valued continuous function on `[a,b]` is a uniform limit of polynomials.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_polynomial_near_of_continuous_on", "description": "Another alternative statement of Weierstrass's theorem,\nfor those who like epsilons, but not bundled continuous functions.\n\nEvery real-valued function `\u211d \u2192 \u211d` which is continuous on `[a,b]`\ncan be approximated to within any `\u03b5 > 0` on `[a,b]` by some polynomial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial_functions_closure_eq_top'", "description": "The special case of the Weierstrass approximation theorem for the interval `[0,1]`.\nThis is just a matter of unravelling definitions and using the Bernstein approximations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_polynomial_near_continuous_map", "description": "An alternative statement of Weierstrass' theorem,\nfor those who like their epsilons.\n\nEvery real-valued continuous function on `[a,b]` is within any `\u03b5 > 0` of some polynomial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.right_inv_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.nat_\u03b1_equiv_punit_sum_punit", "description": "`W_type.nat_\u03b1` is equivalent to `punit \u2295 punit`.\nThis is useful when considering the associated polynomial endofunctor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.equiv_list", "description": "Lists are equivalent to their associated `W_type`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.list_\u03b1_equiv_punit_sum", "description": "`W_type.list_\u03b1` is equivalent to `\u03b3` with an extra point.\nThis is useful when considering the associated polynomial endofunctor", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.nat_\u03b1.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.to_nat", "description": "The isomorphism from the `W_type` of the naturals to the naturals", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.left_inv_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.list_\u03b2", "description": "The arities of each constructor for lists, `nil` takes no arguments, `cons hd` takes one", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.to_list", "description": "The isomorphism from the `W_type` construction of lists to lists", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.list_\u03b2.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.equiv_nat", "description": "The naturals are equivalent to their associated `W_type`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.nat_\u03b2.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.list_\u03b1.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.nat_\u03b1", "description": "The constructors for the naturals", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.nat_\u03b1.zero", "description": "The constructors for the naturals", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.nat_\u03b1.succ", "description": "The constructors for the naturals", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.right_inv_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.of_nat", "description": "The isomorphism from the naturals to its corresponding `W_type`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.nat_\u03b1_equiv_punit_sum_punit_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.nat_\u03b1_equiv_punit_sum_punit_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.left_inv_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.list_\u03b1", "description": "The constructors for lists.\nThere is \"one constructor `cons x` for each `x : \u03b3`\",\nsince we view `list \u03b3` as\n```lean\n| nil : list \u03b3\n| cons x\u2080 : list \u03b3 \u2192 list \u03b3\n| cons x\u2081 : list \u03b3 \u2192 list \u03b3\n|   \u22ee      \u03b3 many times\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.list_\u03b1.nil", "description": "The constructors for lists.\nThere is \"one constructor `cons x` for each `x : \u03b3`\",\nsince we view `list \u03b3` as\n```lean\n| nil : list \u03b3\n| cons x\u2080 : list \u03b3 \u2192 list \u03b3\n| cons x\u2081 : list \u03b3 \u2192 list \u03b3\n|   \u22ee      \u03b3 many times\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.list_\u03b1.cons", "description": "The constructors for lists.\nThere is \"one constructor `cons x` for each `x : \u03b3`\",\nsince we view `list \u03b3` as\n```lean\n| nil : list \u03b3\n| cons x\u2080 : list \u03b3 \u2192 list \u03b3\n| cons x\u2081 : list \u03b3 \u2192 list \u03b3\n|   \u22ee      \u03b3 many times\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.of_list", "description": "The isomorphism from lists to the `W_type` construction of lists", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "W_type.nat_\u03b2", "description": "The arity of the constructors for the naturals, `zero` takes no arguments, `succ` takes one", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.const_ring_hom_eq_algebra_map", "description": " When `R` is commutative and permits an `algebra_map`, `pi.const_ring_hom` is equal to that\nmap.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.const_alg_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.algebra_map_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.algebra_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "alg_equiv.Pi_congr_right_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "alg_equiv.Pi_congr_right_trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "alg_equiv.Pi_congr_right_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "alg_hom.comp_left", "description": " `R`-algebra homomorphism between the function spaces `I \u2192 A` and `I \u2192 B`, induced by an\n`R`-algebra homomorphism `f` between `A` and `B`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "alg_equiv.Pi_congr_right_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "alg_hom.comp_left_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.const_alg_hom_eq_algebra_of_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.eval_alg_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.const_alg_hom", "description": " `function.const` as an `alg_hom`. The name matches `pi.const_ring_hom`, `pi.const_monoid_hom`,\netc.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.algebra", "description": " A special case of `pi.algebra` for non-dependent types. Lean struggles to elaborate\ndefinitions elsewhere in the library without this,", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.eval_alg_hom", "description": " `function.eval` as an `alg_hom`. The name matches `pi.eval_ring_hom`, `pi.eval_monoid_hom`,\netc.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.algebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "alg_equiv.Pi_congr_right", "description": " A family of algebra equivalences `\u03a0 j, (A\u2081 j \u2243\u2090 A\u2082 j)` generates a\nmultiplicative equivalence between `\u03a0 j, A\u2081 j` and `\u03a0 j, A\u2082 j`.\n\nThis is the `alg_equiv` version of `equiv.Pi_congr_right`, and the dependent version of\n`alg_equiv.arrow_congr`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_quotient", "description": " `has_quotient A B` is a notation typeclass that allows us to write `A \u29f8 b` for `b : B`.\nThis allows the usual notation for quotients of algebraic structures,\nsuch as groups, modules and rings.\n\n`A` is a parameter, despite being unused in the definition below, so it appears in the notation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_quotient.quotient'", "description": " `has_quotient A B` is a notation typeclass that allows us to write `A \u29f8 b` for `b : B`.\nThis allows the usual notation for quotients of algebraic structures,\nsuch as groups, modules and rings.\n\n`A` is a parameter, despite being unused in the definition below, so it appears in the notation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_quotient.quotient", "description": " `has_quotient.quotient A b` (with notation `A \u29f8 b`) is the quotient of the type `A` by `b`.\n\nThis differs from `has_quotient.quotient'` in that the `A` argument is explicit, which is necessary\nto make Lean show the notation in the goal state.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_add_monoid.countp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_monoid.countp", "description": "`list.countp` as a bundled multiplicative monoid homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_add_monoid.countp", "description": "`list.countp` as a bundled additive monoid homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_add_monoid.count_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_monoid.count_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_add_monoid.count_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_monoid.countp_of'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_add_monoid.countp_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_monoid.count", "description": "`list.count` as a bundled additive monoid homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_monoid.countp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_monoid.count_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_add_monoid.count", "description": "`list.count` as a bundled additive monoid homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_monoid.countp_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.zify_proof", "description": "A variant of `tactic.zify` that takes `h`, a proof of a proposition about natural numbers,\nand returns a proof of the zified version of that propositon.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.zify", "description": "The `zify` tactic is used to shift propositions from `\u2115` to `\u2124`.\nThis is often useful since `\u2124` has well-behaved subtraction.\n\n```lean\nexample (a b c x y z : \u2115) (h : \u00ac x*y*z < 0) : c < a + 3*b :=\nbegin\n  zify,\n  zify at h,\n  /-\n  h : \u00ac\u2191x * \u2191y * \u2191z < 0\n  \u22a2 \u2191c < \u2191a + 3 * \u2191b\n  -/\nend\n```\n\n`zify` can be given extra lemmas to use in simplification. This is especially useful in the\npresence of nat subtraction: passing `\u2264` arguments will allow `push_cast` to do more work.\n```lean\nexample (a b c : \u2115) (h : a - b < c) (hab : b \u2264 a) : false :=\nbegin\n  zify [hab] at h,\n  /- h : \u2191a - \u2191b < \u2191c -/\nend\n```\n\n`zify` makes use of the `@[zify]` attribute to move propositions,\nand the `push_cast` tactic to simplify the `\u2124`-valued expressions.\n\n`zify` is in some sense dual to the `lift` tactic. `lift (z : \u2124) to \u2115` will change the type of an\ninteger `z` (in the supertype) to `\u2115` (the subtype), given a proof that `z \u2265 0`;\npropositions concerning `z` will still be over `\u2124`. `zify` changes propositions about `\u2115` (the\nsubtype) to propositions about `\u2124` (the supertype), without changing the type of any variable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.attribute.zify", "description": "The `zify` attribute is used by the `zify` tactic. It applies to lemmas that shift propositions\nbetween `nat` and `int`.\n\n`zify` lemmas should have the form `\u2200 a\u2081 ... a\u2099 : \u2115, Pz (a\u2081 : \u2124) ... (a\u2099 : \u2124) \u2194 Pn a\u2081 ... a\u2099`.\nFor example, `int.coe_nat_le_coe_nat_iff : \u2200 (m n : \u2115), \u2191m \u2264 \u2191n \u2194 m \u2264 n` is a `zify` lemma.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.zify", "description": "`zify extra_lems e` is used to shift propositions in `e` from `\u2115` to `\u2124`.\nThis is often useful since `\u2124` has well-behaved subtraction.\n\nThe list of extra lemmas is used in the `push_cast` step.\n\nReturns an expression `e'` and a proof that `e = e'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zify.zify_attr", "description": "The `zify` attribute is used by the `zify` tactic. It applies to lemmas that shift propositions\nbetween `nat` and `int`.\n\n`zify` lemmas should have the form `\u2200 a\u2081 ... a\u2099 : \u2115, Pz (a\u2081 : \u2124) ... (a\u2099 : \u2124) \u2194 Pn a\u2081 ... a\u2099`.\nFor example, `int.coe_nat_le_coe_nat_iff : \u2200 (m n : \u2115), \u2191m \u2264 \u2191n \u2194 m \u2264 n` is a `zify` lemma.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.zify", "description": "The `zify` tactic is used to shift propositions from `\u2115` to `\u2124`.\nThis is often useful since `\u2124` has well-behaved subtraction.\n\n```lean\nexample (a b c x y z : \u2115) (h : \u00ac x*y*z < 0) : c < a + 3*b :=\nbegin\n  zify,\n  zify at h,\n  /-\n  h : \u00ac\u2191x * \u2191y * \u2191z < 0\n  \u22a2 \u2191c < \u2191a + 3 * \u2191b\n  -/\nend\n```\n\n`zify` can be given extra lemmas to use in simplification. This is especially useful in the\npresence of nat subtraction: passing `\u2264` arguments will allow `push_cast` to do more work.\n```lean\nexample (a b c : \u2115) (h : a - b < c) (hab : b \u2264 a) : false :=\nbegin\n  zify [hab] at h,\n  /- h : \u2191a - \u2191b < \u2191c -/\nend\n```\n\n`zify` makes use of the `@[zify]` attribute to move propositions,\nand the `push_cast` tactic to simplify the `\u2124`-valued expressions.\n\n`zify` is in some sense dual to the `lift` tactic. `lift (z : \u2124) to \u2115` will change the type of an\ninteger `z` (in the supertype) to `\u2115` (the subtype), given a proof that `z \u2265 0`;\npropositions concerning `z` will still be over `\u2124`. `zify` changes propositions about `\u2115` (the\nsubtype) to propositions about `\u2124` (the supertype), without changing the type of any variable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zify.lift_to_z", "description": "Given an expression `e`, `lift_to_z e` looks for subterms of `e` that are propositions \"about\"\nnatural numbers and change them to propositions about integers.\n\nReturns an expression `e'` and a proof that `e = e'`.\n\nIncludes `ge_iff_le` and `gt_iff_lt` in the simp set. These can't be tagged with `zify` as we\nwant to use them in the \"forward\", not \"backward\", direction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.coe_nat_ne_coe_nat_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.degeneracy_comp_P_infty_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.\u03c3_comp_P_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.\u03c3_comp_P_infty_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.degeneracy_comp_P_infty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.higher_faces_vanish.comp_\u03c3", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.\u03c3_comp_P_infty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "punit.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_surjective_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "plift.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.countable_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "countable_iff_exists_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsingleton.to_countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bool.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "countable_iff_exists_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "countable.of_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Prop.countable'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quot.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "countable", "description": "A type `\u03b1` is countable if there exists an injective map `\u03b1 \u2192 \u2115`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "countable.exists_injective_nat", "description": "A type `\u03b1` is countable if there exists an injective map `\u03b1 \u2192 \u2115`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Prop.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite.to_countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quotient.countable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.to_matrix_transpose_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis_to_matrix_mul_linear_map_to_matrix", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map_to_matrix_mul_basis_to_matrix", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.to_matrix_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.to_lin_to_matrix", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.invertible_to_matrix", "description": "A matrix whose columns form a basis `b'`, expressed w.r.t. a basis `b`, is invertible.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.to_matrix_id_eq_basis_to_matrix", "description": "A generalization of `linear_map.to_matrix_id`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.to_matrix_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.coe_pi_basis_fun.to_matrix_eq_transpose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.to_matrix_mul_to_matrix_flip", "description": "`b.to_matrix b'` and `b'.to_matrix b` are inverses.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.to_matrix_update", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.to_matrix_units_smul", "description": "The basis constructed by `units_smul` has vectors given by a diagonal matrix.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.to_matrix_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.to_matrix_equiv", "description": " From a basis `e : \u03b9 \u2192 M`, build a linear equivalence between families of vectors `v : \u03b9 \u2192 M`,\nand matrices, making the matrix whose columns are the vectors `v i` written in the basis `e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.to_matrix_map_vec_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.sum_to_matrix_smul_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.to_matrix", "description": " From a basis `e : \u03b9 \u2192 M` and a family of vectors `v : \u03b9' \u2192 M`, make the matrix whose columns\nare the vectors `v i` written in the basis `e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.to_matrix_reindex'", "description": "See also `basis.to_matrix_reindex` which gives the `simp` normal form of this result.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis_to_matrix_mul_linear_map_to_matrix_mul_basis_to_matrix", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.to_matrix_eq_to_matrix_constr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis_to_matrix_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_basis_to_matrix", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.to_matrix_reindex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.to_matrix_mul_to_matrix", "description": "A generalization of `basis.to_matrix_self`, in the opposite direction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.to_matrix_is_unit_smul", "description": "The basis constructed by `is_unit_smul` has vectors given by a diagonal matrix.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis_to_matrix_basis_fun_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.card_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.card_Iic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.dfinsupp", "description": "Finitely supported product of finsets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_dfinsupp_iff_of_support_subset", "description": "When `t` is supported on `s`, `f \u2208 s.dfinsupp t` precisely means that `f` is pointwise in `t`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.card_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.support_range_Icc_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.mem_singleton_apply_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.card_uIcc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.card_dfinsupp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.card_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.card_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.mem_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.pi", "description": " Given a finitely supported function `f : \u03a0\u2080 i, finset (\u03b1 i)`, one can define the finset\n`f.pi` of all finitely supported functions whose value at `i` is in `f i` for all `i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.range_Icc_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_dfinsupp_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.mem_range_Icc_apply_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.Icc_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.card_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.range_Icc", "description": "Pointwise `finset.Icc` bundled as a `dfinsupp`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.singleton", "description": "Pointwise `finset.singleton` bundled as a `dfinsupp`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.locally_finite_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dfinsupp.card_Iio", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "GroupWithZero.iso.mk", "description": "Constructs an isomorphism of groups with zero from a group isomorphism between them.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "GroupWithZero.iso.mk_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "GroupWithZero.has_forget_to_Mon", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "GroupWithZero.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "GroupWithZero.category_theory.concrete_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "GroupWithZero", "description": "The category of groups with zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "GroupWithZero.group_with_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "GroupWithZero.category_theory.large_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "GroupWithZero.of", "description": "Construct a bundled `GroupWithZero` from a `group_with_zero`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "GroupWithZero.has_forget_to_Bipointed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "GroupWithZero.has_coe_to_sort", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "GroupWithZero.iso.mk_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.inhabited", "description": "The unique point in a single-point space is the simplest example of an affine basis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.reindex_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.affine_combination_coord_eq_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.ext_elem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.coord_apply_combination_of_not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.linear_combination_coord_eq_self", "description": " A variant of `affine_basis.affine_combination_coord_eq_self` for the special case when the\naffine space is a module so we can talk about linear combinations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.coord_apply_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.exists_affine_subbasis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.reindex_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.coord_reindex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.basis_of_reindex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.coord_apply_centroid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.fun_like", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.coord_apply_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.sum_coord_apply_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.linear_eq_sum_coords", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.reindex", "description": "Composition of an affine basis and an equivalence of index types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.coord", "description": "The `i`th barycentric coordinate of a point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.basis_of_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.basis_of", "description": " Given an affine basis for an affine space `P`, if we single out one member of the family, we\nobtain a linear basis for the model space `V`.\n\nThe linear basis corresponding to the singled-out member `i : \u03b9` is indexed by `{j : \u03b9 // j \u2260 i}`\nand its `j`th element is `b j -\u1d65 b i`. (See `basis_of_apply`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.surjective_coord", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.coe_coord_of_subsingleton_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis", "description": "An affine basis is a family of affine-independent points whose span is the top subspace.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.to_fun", "description": "An affine basis is a family of affine-independent points whose span is the top subspace.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.ind'", "description": "An affine basis is a family of affine-independent points whose span is the top subspace.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.tot'", "description": "An affine basis is a family of affine-independent points whose span is the top subspace.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.coe_reindex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.ind", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.exists_affine_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.coords", "description": "Barycentric coordinates as an affine map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.coord_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.coord_apply_combination_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.coords_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_basis.tot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_local_extr_on.exists_linear_map_of_has_strict_fderiv_at", "description": " Lagrange multipliers theorem: if `\u03c6 : E \u2192 \u211d` has a local extremum on the set `{x | f x = f x\u2080}`\nat `x\u2080`, both `f : E \u2192 F` and `\u03c6` are strictly differentiable at `x\u2080`, and the codomain of `f` is\na complete space, then there exist `\u039b : dual \u211d F` and `\u039b\u2080 : \u211d` such that `(\u039b, \u039b\u2080) \u2260 0` and\n`\u039b (f' x) + \u039b\u2080 \u2022 \u03c6' x = 0` for all `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_local_extr_on.exists_multipliers_of_has_strict_fderiv_at", "description": " Lagrange multipliers theorem, 1d version. Let `f : \u03b9 \u2192 E \u2192 \u211d` be a finite family of functions.\nSuppose that `\u03c6 : E \u2192 \u211d` has a local extremum on the set `{x | \u2200 i, f i x = f i x\u2080}` at `x\u2080`.\nSuppose that all functions `f i` as well as `\u03c6` are strictly differentiable at `x\u2080`.\nThen the derivatives `f' i : E \u2192 L[\u211d] \u211d` and `\u03c6' : E \u2192L[\u211d] \u211d` are linearly dependent:\nthere exist `\u039b : \u03b9 \u2192 \u211d` and `\u039b\u2080 : \u211d`, `(\u039b, \u039b\u2080) \u2260 0`, such that `\u2211 i, \u039b i \u2022 f' i + \u039b\u2080 \u2022 \u03c6' = 0`.\n\nSee also `is_local_extr_on.linear_dependent_of_has_strict_fderiv_at` for a version that\nstates `\u00aclinear_independent \u211d _` instead of existence of `\u039b` and `\u039b\u2080`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_local_extr_on.linear_dependent_of_has_strict_fderiv_at", "description": " Lagrange multipliers theorem. Let `f : \u03b9 \u2192 E \u2192 \u211d` be a finite family of functions.\nSuppose that `\u03c6 : E \u2192 \u211d` has a local extremum on the set `{x | \u2200 i, f i x = f i x\u2080}` at `x\u2080`.\nSuppose that all functions `f i` as well as `\u03c6` are strictly differentiable at `x\u2080`.\nThen the derivatives `f' i : E \u2192 L[\u211d] \u211d` and `\u03c6' : E \u2192L[\u211d] \u211d` are linearly dependent.\n\nSee also `is_local_extr_on.exists_multipliers_of_has_strict_fderiv_at` for a version that\nthat states existence of Lagrange multipliers `\u039b` and `\u039b\u2080` instead of using\n`\u00aclinear_independent \u211d _`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_local_extr_on.exists_multipliers_of_has_strict_fderiv_at_1d", "description": " Lagrange multipliers theorem: if `\u03c6 : E \u2192 \u211d` has a local extremum on the set `{x | f x = f x\u2080}`\nat `x\u2080`, and both `f : E \u2192 \u211d` and `\u03c6` are strictly differentiable at `x\u2080`, then there exist\n`a b : \u211d` such that `(a, b) \u2260 0` and `a \u2022 f' + b \u2022 \u03c6' = 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_local_extr_on.range_ne_top_of_has_strict_fderiv_at", "description": " Lagrange multipliers theorem: if `\u03c6 : E \u2192 \u211d` has a local extremum on the set `{x | f x = f x\u2080}`\nat `x\u2080`, both `f : E \u2192 F` and `\u03c6` are strictly differentiable at `x\u2080`, and the codomain of `f` is\na complete space, then the linear map `x \u21a6 (f' x, \u03c6' x)` is not surjective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.hom", "description": "Morphisms in `Pointed`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.hom.to_fun", "description": "Morphisms in `Pointed`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.hom.map_point", "description": "Morphisms in `Pointed`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.of", "description": "Turns a point into a pointed type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Type_to_Pointed_obj_point", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.large_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Type_to_Pointed", "description": "`option` as a functor from types to pointed types. This is the free functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.iso.mk_inv_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Type_to_Pointed_map_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed", "description": "The category of pointed types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.X", "description": "The category of pointed types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.point", "description": "The category of pointed types.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.has_coe_to_sort", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Type_to_Pointed_forget_adjunction", "description": "`Type_to_Pointed` is the free functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.hom.comp", "description": "Composition of morphisms of `Pointed`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.Pointed", "description": "**Alias** of `Pointed.of`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.hom.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.hom.id", "description": "The identity morphism of `X : Pointed`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.coe_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.iso.mk", "description": " Constructs a isomorphism between pointed types from an equivalence that preserves the point\nbetween them.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.iso.mk_hom_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.hom.comp_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.hom.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.concrete_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Type_to_Pointed_obj_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.hom.id_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Pointed.hom.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.sigma_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.product_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.sigma_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nil_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nil_sigma", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.product_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.mem_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.length_product", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.length_sigma", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.mem_sigma", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_on.exists_ge_of_mem_convex_hull", "description": " Maximum principle for convex functions. If a function `f` is convex on the convex hull of `s`,\nthen the eventual maximum of `f` on `convex_hull \ud835\udd5c s` lies in `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "concave_on.le_map_sum", "description": "Concave **Jensen's inequality**, `finset.sum` version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_on.map_center_mass_le", "description": "Convex **Jensen's inequality**, `finset.center_mass` version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_on.map_sum_le", "description": "Convex **Jensen's inequality**, `finset.sum` version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "concave_on.exists_le_of_mem_convex_hull", "description": " Minimum principle for concave functions. If a function `f` is concave on the convex hull of `s`,\nthen the eventual minimum of `f` on `convex_hull \ud835\udd5c s` lies in `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_sup_of_mem_convex_hull", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "concave_on.exists_le_of_center_mass", "description": " If a function `f` is concave on `s`, then the value it takes at some center of mass of points of\n`s` is greater than the value it takes on one of those points.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_le_of_mem_convex_hull", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_on.exists_ge_of_center_mass", "description": " If a function `f` is convex on `s`, then the value it takes at some center of mass of points of\n`s` is less than the value it takes on one of those points.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "concave_on.le_map_center_mass", "description": "Concave **Jensen's inequality**, `finset.center_mass` version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_prod_list_of_fn", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_list_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_nsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_list_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_sum_list_of_fn", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.duplicate.mono_sublist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.duplicate_cons_iff_of_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.duplicate.of_duplicate_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.nodup_iff_forall_not_duplicate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.duplicate.not_nodup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.duplicate.ne_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.duplicate.elim_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.duplicate.mem_cons_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.duplicate.duplicate_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.duplicate_cons_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.duplicate_iff_sublist", "description": "The contrapositive of `list.nodup_iff_sublist`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.mem.duplicate_cons_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.duplicate_cons_self_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.duplicate_iff_two_le_count", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.duplicate.elim_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.not_duplicate_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.decidable_duplicate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.duplicate", "description": "Property that an element `x : \u03b1` of `l : list \u03b1` can be found in the list more than once.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.duplicate.cons_mem", "description": "Property that an element `x : \u03b1` of `l : list \u03b1` can be found in the list more than once.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.duplicate.cons_duplicate", "description": "Property that an element `x : \u03b1` of `l : list \u03b1` can be found in the list more than once.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.exists_duplicate_iff_not_nodup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.duplicate.mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.not_duplicate_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.duplicate.ne_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_bump_covering.comp_embedding_pi_tangent_mfderiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_bump_covering.embedding_pi_tangent_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_bump_covering.embedding_pi_tangent_ker_mfderiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_bump_covering.embedding_pi_tangent_inj_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_bump_covering.embedding_pi_tangent", "description": "Smooth embedding of `M` into `(E \u00d7 \u211d) ^ \u03b9`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_embedding_euclidean_of_compact", "description": " Baby version of the Whitney weak embedding theorem: if `M` admits a finite covering by\nsupports of bump functions, then for some `n` it can be embedded into the `n`-dimensional\nEuclidean space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_bump_covering.exists_immersion_euclidean", "description": " Baby version of the **Whitney weak embedding theorem**: if `M` admits a finite covering by\nsupports of bump functions, then for some `n` it can be immersed into the `n`-dimensional\nEuclidean space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_bump_covering.embedding_pi_tangent_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_bump_covering.embedding_pi_tangent_injective_mfderiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.congrm", "description": "Assume that the goal is of the form `lhs = rhs` or `lhs \u2194 rhs`.\n`congrm e` takes an expression `e` containing placeholders `_` and scans `e, lhs, rhs` in parallel.\n\nIt matches both `lhs` and `rhs` to the pattern `e`, and produces one goal for each placeholder,\nstating that the corresponding subexpressions in `lhs` and `rhs` are equal.\n\nExamples:\n```lean\nexample {a b c d : \u2115} :\n  nat.pred a.succ * (d + (c + a.pred)) = nat.pred b.succ * (b + (c + d.pred)) :=\nbegin\n  congrm nat.pred (nat.succ _) * (_ + _),\n/-  Goals left:\n\u22a2 a = b\n\u22a2 d = b\n\u22a2 c + a.pred = c + d.pred\n-/\n  sorry,\n  sorry,\n  sorry,\nend\n\nexample {a b : \u2115} (h : a = b) : (\u03bb y : \u2115, \u2200 z, a + a = z) = (\u03bb x, \u2200 z, b + a = z) :=\nbegin\n  congrm \u03bb x, \u2200 w, _ + a = w,\n  -- produces one goal for the underscore: \u22a2 a = b\n  exact h,\nend\n```\n\nThe tactic also allows for \"function underscores\", denoted by `_\u2081, ..., _\u2084`.  The index denotes\nthe number of explicit arguments of the function to be matched.\nIf `e` has a \"function underscore\" in a location, then the tactic reads off the function `f` that\nappears in `lhs` at the current location, replacing the *explicit* arguments of `f` by the user\ninputs to the \"function underscore\".  After that, `congrm` continues with its matching.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.equate_with_pattern", "description": "`equate_with_pattern pat` solves a single goal of the form `lhs = rhs`\n(assuming that `lhs` and `rhs` are unifiable with `pat`)\nby applying congruence lemmas until `pat` is a metavariable.\nThe subgoals for the leafs are prepended to the goals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.equate_with_pattern_core", "description": "`equate_with_pattern_core pat` solves a single goal of the form `lhs = rhs`\n(assuming that `lhs` and `rhs` are unifiable with `pat`)\nby applying congruence lemmas until `pat` is a metavariable.\nReturns the list of metavariables for the new subgoals at the leafs.\nCalls `set_goals []` at the end.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.congrm_fun_4", "description": "A generic function with four arguments.  It is the \"function underscore\" input to `congrm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.convert_to_explicit", "description": "  Replaces a \"function underscore\" input to `congrm` into the correct expression,\nread off from the left-hand-side of the target expression.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.congrm_fun_1", "description": "A generic function with one argument.  It is the \"function underscore\" input to `congrm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.congrm", "description": "Assume that the goal is of the form `lhs = rhs` or `lhs \u2194 rhs`.\n`congrm e` takes an expression `e` containing placeholders `_` and scans `e, lhs, rhs` in parallel.\n\nIt matches both `lhs` and `rhs` to the pattern `e`, and produces one goal for each placeholder,\nstating that the corresponding subexpressions in `lhs` and `rhs` are equal.\n\nExamples:\n```lean\nexample {a b c d : \u2115} :\n  nat.pred a.succ * (d + (c + a.pred)) = nat.pred b.succ * (b + (c + d.pred)) :=\nbegin\n  congrm nat.pred (nat.succ _) * (_ + _),\n/-  Goals left:\n\u22a2 a = b\n\u22a2 d = b\n\u22a2 c + a.pred = c + d.pred\n-/\n  sorry,\n  sorry,\n  sorry,\nend\n\nexample {a b : \u2115} (h : a = b) : (\u03bb y : \u2115, \u2200 z, a + a = z) = (\u03bb x, \u2200 z, b + a = z) :=\nbegin\n  congrm \u03bb x, \u2200 w, _ + a = w,\n  -- produces one goal for the underscore: \u22a2 a = b\n  exact h,\nend\n```\n\nThe tactic also allows for \"function underscores\", denoted by `_\u2081, ..., _\u2084`.  The index denotes\nthe number of explicit arguments of the function to be matched.\nIf `e` has a \"function underscore\" in a location, then the tactic reads off the function `f` that\nappears in `lhs` at the current location, replacing the *explicit* arguments of `f` by the user\ninputs to the \"function underscore\".  After that, `congrm` continues with its matching.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.congrm_fun_2", "description": "A generic function with two arguments.  It is the \"function underscore\" input to `congrm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.congrm_fun_3", "description": "A generic function with three arguments.  It is the \"function underscore\" input to `congrm`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.decomp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.to_const_prod_continuous_linear_map_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.coe_mk_const_linear_eq_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.neg_cont_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.norm_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.comp_cont_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.to_const_prod_continuous_linear_map_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.coe_linear_eq_coe_cont_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.to_const_prod_continuous_linear_map", "description": " The space of affine maps between two normed spaces is linearly isometric to the product of the\ncodomain with the space of linear maps, by taking the value of the affine map at `(0 : V)` and the\nlinear part.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.coe_cont_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.has_norm", "description": " Note that unlike the operator norm for linear maps, this norm is _not_ submultiplicative:\nwe do _not_ necessarily have `\u2016f.comp g\u2016 \u2264 \u2016f\u2016 * \u2016g\u2016`. See `norm_comp_le` for what we can say.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.coe_cont_linear_eq_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.const_cont_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.normed_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.norm_image_zero_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.norm_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.zero_cont_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.normed_add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.to_affine_map_cont_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.cont_linear", "description": "The linear map underlying a continuous affine map is continuous.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.sub_cont_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.smul_cont_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.cont_linear_eq_zero_iff_exists_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.map_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.norm_cont_linear_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.cont_linear_map_vsub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.norm_comp_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.add_cont_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.omega", "description": " Attempts to discharge goals in the quantifier-free fragment of\nlinear integer and natural number arithmetic using the Omega test.\nGuesses the correct domain by looking at the goal and hypotheses,\nand then reverts all relevant hypotheses and variables.\nUse `omega manual` to disable automatic reverts, and `omega int` or\n`omega nat` to specify the domain.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.determine_domain", "description": "Return tt if the domain is \u2124, and return ff if it is \u2115", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.goal_domain", "description": "Use the current goal to determine.\n   Return tt if the domain is \u2124, and return ff if it is \u2115", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.type_domain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.form_domain", "description": " Detects domain of a formula from its expr.\n* Returns none, if domain can be either \u2124 or \u2115\n* Returns some tt, if domain is exclusively \u2124\n* Returns some ff, if domain is exclusively \u2115\n* Fails, if domain is neither \u2124 nor \u2115", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.omega", "description": "`omega` attempts to discharge goals in the quantifier-free fragment of linear integer and natural\nnumber arithmetic using the Omega test. In other words, the core procedure of `omega` works with\ngoals of the form\n```lean\n\u2200 x\u2081, ... \u2200 x\u2096, P\n```\nwhere `x\u2081, ... x\u2096` are integer (resp. natural number) variables, and `P` is a quantifier-free\nformula of linear integer (resp. natural number) arithmetic. For instance:\n```lean\nexample : \u2200 (x y : int), (x \u2264 5 \u2227 y \u2264 3) \u2192 x + y \u2264 8 := by omega\n```\nBy default, `omega` tries to guess the correct domain by looking at the goal and hypotheses, and\nthen reverts all relevant hypotheses and variables (e.g., all variables of type `nat` and `Prop`s\nin linear natural number arithmetic, if the domain was determined to be `nat`) to universally close\nthe goal before calling the main procedure. Therefore, `omega` will often work even if the goal\nis not in the above form:\n```lean\nexample (x y : nat) (h : 2 * x + 1 = 2 * y) : false := by omega\n```\nBut this behaviour is not always optimal, since it may revert irrelevant hypotheses or incorrectly\nguess the domain. Use `omega manual` to disable automatic reverts, and `omega int` or `omega nat`\nto specify the domain.\n```lean\nexample (x y z w : int) (h1 : 3 * y \u2265 x) (h2 : z > 19 * w) : 3 * x \u2264 9 * y :=\nby {revert h1 x y, omega manual}\n\nexample (i : int) (n : nat) (h1 : i = 0) (h2 : n < n) : false := by omega nat\n\nexample (n : nat) (h1 : n < 34) (i : int) (h2 : i * 9 = -72) : i = -8 :=\nby {revert h2 i, omega manual int}\n```\n`omega` handles `nat` subtraction by repeatedly rewriting goals of the form `P[t-s]` into\n`P[x] \u2227 (t = s + x \u2228 (t \u2264 s \u2227 x = 0))`, where `x` is fresh. This means that each (distinct)\noccurrence of subtraction will cause the goal size to double during DNF transformation.\n\n`omega` implements the real shadow step of the Omega test, but not the dark and gray shadows.\nTherefore, it should (in principle) succeed whenever the negation of the goal has no real solution,\nbut it may fail if a real solution exists, even if there is no integer/natural number solution.\n\nYou can enable `set_option trace.omega true` to see how `omega` interprets your goal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.goal_domain_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.select_domain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat.has_forget_to_Lat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat.iso.mk_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat.coe_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat_dual_comp_forget_to_Lat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat.dual_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat.dual", "description": "`order_dual` as a functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat.iso.mk_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat.iso.mk", "description": "Constructs an equivalence between distributive lattices from an order isomorphism between them.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat.large_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat.distrib_lattice.to_lattice.category_theory.bundled_hom.parent_projection", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat.dual_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat.has_coe_to_sort", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat.distrib_lattice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat", "description": "The category of distributive lattices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat.concrete_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat.dual_equiv_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat.of", "description": "Construct a bundled `DistLat` from a `distrib_lattice` underlying type and typeclass.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat.dual_equiv", "description": "The equivalence between `DistLat` and itself induced by `order_dual` both ways.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DistLat.dual_equiv_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.dvd_antisymm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.coe_nat_dvd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.coe_nat_dvd_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.eq_one_of_mul_eq_one_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.coe_nat_dvd_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.eq_one_of_mul_eq_one_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.eq_one_of_dvd_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.le_of_dvd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo1969_q1.not_prime_of_int_mul'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1969_q1", "description": " We conclude by using the fact that `a_choice` is an injective function from the natural numbers\nto the set `good_nats`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1969_q1.factorization", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1969_q1.a_choice_good", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1969_q1.int_large", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1969_q1.polynomial_not_prime", "description": "Every natural number of the form `n^4 + 4*m^4` is not prime.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1969_q1.a_choice_strict_mono", "description": " `a_choice` is a strictly monotone function; this is easily proven by chaining together lemmas\nin the `strict_mono` namespace.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1969_q1.left_factor_large", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1969_q1.right_factor_large", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1969_q1.a_choice", "description": "We define $a_{choice}(b) := 4*(2+b)^4$, so that we can take $m = 2+b$ in `polynomial_not_prime`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1969_q1.good_nats", "description": " `good_nats` is the set of natural numbers satisfying the condition in the problem\nstatement, namely the `a : \u2115` such that `n^4 + a` is not prime for any `n : \u2115`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.apply_fun", "description": "Apply a function to an equality or inequality in either a local hypothesis or the goal.\n\n* If we have `h : a = b`, then `apply_fun f at h` will replace this with `h : f a = f b`.\n* If we have `h : a \u2264 b`, then `apply_fun f at h` will replace this with `h : f a \u2264 f b`,\n  and create a subsidiary goal `monotone f`.\n  `apply_fun` will automatically attempt to discharge this subsidiary goal using `mono`,\n  or an explicit solution can be provided with `apply_fun f at h using P`, where `P : monotone f`.\n* If the goal is `a \u2260 b`, `apply_fun f` will replace this with `f a \u2260 f b`.\n* If the goal is `a = b`, `apply_fun f` will replace this with `f a = f b`,\n  and create a subsidiary goal `injective f`.\n  `apply_fun` will automatically attempt to discharge this subsidiary goal using local hypotheses,\n  or if `f` is actually an `equiv`,\n  or an explicit solution can be provided with `apply_fun f using P`, where `P : injective f`.\n* If the goal is `a \u2264 b` (or similarly for `a < b`), and `f` is actually an `order_iso`,\n  `apply_fun f` will replace the goal with `f a \u2264 f b`.\n  If `f` is anything else (e.g. just a function, or an `equiv`), `apply_fun` will fail.\n\n\nTypical usage is:\n```lean\nopen function\n\nexample (X Y Z : Type) (f : X \u2192 Y) (g : Y \u2192 Z) (H : injective $ g \u2218 f) :\n  injective f :=\nbegin\n  intros x x' h,\n  apply_fun g at h,\n  exact H h\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.apply_fun_to_goal", "description": "Attempt to \"apply\" a function `f` represented by the argument `e : pexpr` to the goal.\n\nIf the goal is of the form `a \u2260 b`, we obtain the new goal `f a \u2260 f b`.\nIf the goal is of the form `a = b`, we obtain a new goal `f a = f b`, and a subsidiary goal\n`injective f`.\n(We attempt to discharge this subsidiary goal automatically, or using the optional argument.)\nIf the goal is of the form `a \u2264 b` (or similarly for `a < b`), and `f` is an `order_iso`,\nwe obtain a new goal `f a \u2264 f b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.apply_fun", "description": "Apply a function to an equality or inequality in either a local hypothesis or the goal.\n\n* If we have `h : a = b`, then `apply_fun f at h` will replace this with `h : f a = f b`.\n* If we have `h : a \u2264 b`, then `apply_fun f at h` will replace this with `h : f a \u2264 f b`,\n  and create a subsidiary goal `monotone f`.\n  `apply_fun` will automatically attempt to discharge this subsidiary goal using `mono`,\n  or an explicit solution can be provided with `apply_fun f at h using P`, where `P : monotone f`.\n* If the goal is `a \u2260 b`, `apply_fun f` will replace this with `f a \u2260 f b`.\n* If the goal is `a = b`, `apply_fun f` will replace this with `f a = f b`,\n  and create a subsidiary goal `injective f`.\n  `apply_fun` will automatically attempt to discharge this subsidiary goal using local hypotheses,\n  or if `f` is actually an `equiv`,\n  or an explicit solution can be provided with `apply_fun f using P`, where `P : injective f`.\n* If the goal is `a \u2264 b` (or similarly for `a < b`), and `f` is actually an `order_iso`,\n  `apply_fun f` will replace the goal with `f a \u2264 f b`.\n  If `f` is anything else (e.g. just a function, or an `equiv`), `apply_fun` will fail.\n\n\nTypical usage is:\n```lean\nopen function\n\nexample (X Y Z : Type) (f : X \u2192 Y) (g : Y \u2192 Z) (H : injective $ g \u2218 f) :\n  injective f :=\nbegin\n  intros x x' h,\n  apply_fun g at h,\n  exact H h\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.apply_fun_to_hyp", "description": " Apply the function `f` given by `e : pexpr` to the local hypothesis `hyp`, which must either be\nof the form `a = b` or `a \u2264 b`, replacing the type of `hyp` with `f a = f b` or `f a \u2264 f b`. If\n`hyp` names an inequality then a new goal `monotone f` is created, unless the name of a proof of\nthis fact is passed as the optional argument `mono_lem`, or the `mono` tactic can prove it.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.wilsons_lemma", "description": "**Wilson's Lemma**: the product of `1`, ..., `p-1` is `-1` modulo `p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.prod_Ico_one_prime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.prime_of_fac_equiv_neg_one", "description": "For `n \u2260 1`, `(n-1)!` is congruent to `-1` modulo `n` only if n is prime.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.prime_iff_fac_equiv_neg_one", "description": "**Wilson's Theorem**: For `n \u2260 1`, `(n-1)!` is congruent to `-1` modulo `n` iff n is prime.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.second_map", "description": "The map `pr\u2081*` of <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00VM>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_sheaf.hom_ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_sheaf_of_is_terminal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.cones_equiv_sieve_compatible_family_symm_apply_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_sheaf_iff_is_limit_pretopology", "description": "A presheaf `P` is a sheaf for the Grothendieck topology generated by a pretopology `K`\n   iff for every covering presieve `R` of `K`, the natural cone associated to `P` and\n   `sieve.generate R` is a limit cone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.first_obj", "description": "The middle object of the fork diagram given in Equation (3) of [MM92], as well as the fork diagram\nof <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00VM>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sheaf_over", "description": "The sheaf of sections guaranteed by the sheaf condition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.category_theory.category_id_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.fork_map", "description": "The left morphism of the fork diagram given in Equation (3) of [MM92], as well as the fork diagram\nof <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00VM>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_sheaf_iff_is_sheaf_of_type", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.quiver.hom.has_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf_equiv_SheafOfTypes_counit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf_equiv_SheafOfTypes", "description": "The category of sheaves taking values in Type is the same as the category of set-valued sheaves.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.second_obj", "description": "The rightmost object of the fork diagram of https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00VM, which\ncontains the data used to check a family of elements for a presieve is compatible.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_sheaf_iff_multiequalizer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.hom.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf_equiv_SheafOfTypes_functor_obj_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf_to_presheaf_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.first_map", "description": "The map `pr\u2080*` of <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00VM>.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_sheaf_of_iso_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_sheaf_iff_multifork", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.hom.add_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf_hom_has_nsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_limit_iff_is_sheaf_for_presieve", "description": "Given presieve `R` and presheaf `P : C\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 A`, the natural cone associated to `P` and\n   the sieve `sieve.generate R` generated by `R` is a limit cone iff `Hom (E, P -)` is a\n   sheaf of types for the presieve `R` and all `E : A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.cones_equiv_sieve_compatible_family_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf_equiv_SheafOfTypes_inverse_obj_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_limit_iff_is_sheaf_for", "description": "Given sieve `S` and presheaf `P : C\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 A`, their natural associated cone is a limit cone\n   iff `Hom (E, P -)` is a sheaf of types for the sieve `S` and all `E : A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.category_theory.category_comp_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_sheaf.amalgamate_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_sheaf", "description": "A sheaf of A is a presheaf P : C\u1d52\u1d56 => A such that for every E : A, the\npresheaf of types given by sending U : C to Hom_{A}(E, P U) is a sheaf of types.\n\nhttps://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00VR", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.quiver.hom.has_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_separated_iff_subsingleton", "description": "A presheaf `P` is separated for the Grothendieck topology `J` iff for every covering sieve\n   `S` of `J`, the natural cone associated to `P` and `S` admits at most one morphism from every\n   cone in the same category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.category_theory.category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.is_terminal_of_bot_cover", "description": "If the empty sieve is a cover of `X`, then `F(X)` is terminal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf_to_presheaf", "description": "The inclusion functor from sheaves to presheaves.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.quiver.hom.has_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.hom", "description": "Morphisms between sheaves are just morphisms of presheaves.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.hom.val", "description": "Morphisms between sheaves are just morphisms of presheaves.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.hom.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sheaf_over_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.quiver.hom.has_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.hom.mono_of_presheaf_mono", "description": "This is stated as a lemma to prevent class search from forming a loop since a sheaf morphism is\nmonic if and only if it is monic as a presheaf morphism (under suitable assumption).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf_to_presheaf_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf_to_presheaf.full", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_sheaf_for_is_sheaf_for'", "description": "(Implementation). An auxiliary lemma to convert between sheaf conditions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_sheaf_iff_is_sheaf'", "description": "The equalizer definition of a sheaf given by `is_sheaf'` is equivalent to `is_sheaf`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_sheaf.amalgamate", "description": "This is a wrapper around `presieve.is_sheaf_for.amalgamate` to be used below.\n If `P`s a sheaf, `S` is a cover of `X`, and `x` is a collection of morphisms from `E`\n to `P` evaluated at terms in the cover which are compatible, then we can amalgamate\n the `x`s to obtain a single morphism `E \u27f6 P.obj (op X)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presieve.family_of_elements.sieve_compatible.cone", "description": "The cone corresponding to a sieve_compatible family of elements, dot notation enabled.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_sheaf.amalgamate_map_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_sheaf_iff_is_sheaf_forget", "description": "For a concrete category `(A, s)` where the forgetful functor `s : A \u2964 Type v` preserves limits and\nreflects isomorphisms, and `A` has limits, an `A`-valued presheaf `P : C\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 A` is a sheaf iff its\nunderlying `Type`-valued presheaf `P \u22d9 s : C\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 Type` is a sheaf.\n\nNote this lemma applies for \"algebraic\" categories, eg groups, abelian groups and rings, but not\nfor the category of topological spaces, topological rings, etc since reflecting isomorphisms doesn't\nhold.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.preadditive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.hom.epi_of_presheaf_epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf_to_presheaf.faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf_equiv_SheafOfTypes_functor_map_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.hom_equiv_amalgamation", "description": "Cone morphisms from the cone corresponding to a sieve_compatible family to the natural\n   cone associated to a sieve `S` and a presheaf `P` are in 1-1 correspondence with amalgamations\n   of the family.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf_equiv_SheafOfTypes_unit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.cones_equiv_sieve_compatible_family", "description": "Given a sieve `S` on `X : C`, a presheaf `P : C\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 A`, and an object `E` of `A`,\n   the cones over the natural diagram `S.arrows.diagram.op \u22d9 P` associated to `S` and `P`\n   with cone point `E` are in 1-1 correspondence with sieve_compatible family of elements\n   for the sieve `S` and the presheaf of types `Hom (E, P -)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf_equiv_SheafOfTypes_inverse_map_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_sheaf'", "description": "An alternative definition of the sheaf condition in terms of equalizers. This is shown to be\nequivalent in `category_theory.presheaf.is_sheaf_iff_is_sheaf'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.w", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.subsingleton_iff_is_separated_for", "description": "Given sieve `S` and presheaf `P : C\u1d52\u1d56 \u2964 A`, their natural associated cone admits at most one\n   morphism from every cone in the same category (i.e. over the same diagram),\n   iff `Hom (E, P -)`is separated for the sieve `S` and all `E : A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf_hom_has_zsmul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_limit_of_is_sheaf", "description": "When `P` is a sheaf and `S` is a cover, the associated multifork is a limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf", "description": "The category of sheaves taking values in `A` on a grothendieck topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.val", "description": "The category of sheaves taking values in `A` on a grothendieck topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.cond", "description": "The category of sheaves taking values in `A` on a grothendieck topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.hom.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf.is_sheaf_iff_is_limit", "description": "A presheaf `P` is a sheaf for the Grothendieck topology `J` iff for every covering sieve\n   `S` of `J`, the natural cone associated to `P` and `S` is a limit cone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.quiver.hom.add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.comp_preserves_filtered_colimits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_filtered_colimits", "description": "A functor is said to preserve filtered colimits, if it preserves all colimits of shape `J`, where\n`J` is a filtered category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_filtered_colimits.preserves_filtered_colimits", "description": "A functor is said to preserve filtered colimits, if it preserves all colimits of shape `J`, where\n`J` is a filtered category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.comp_preserves_cofiltered_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_cofiltered_limits", "description": "A functor is said to preserve cofiltered limits, if it preserves all limits of shape `J`, where\n`J` is a cofiltered category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_cofiltered_limits.preserves_cofiltered_limits", "description": "A functor is said to preserve cofiltered limits, if it preserves all limits of shape `J`, where\n`J` is a cofiltered category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_limits.preserves_cofiltered_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.preserves_colimits.preserves_filtered_colimits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone.pairwise_disjoint_on_Ioc_pred", "description": " If `\u03b1` is a linear pred order, `\u03b2` is a preorder, and `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` is an antitone function, then\nthe intervals `set.Ioc (f n) (f (order.pred n))` are pairwise disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone.pairwise_disjoint_on_Ioc_succ", "description": " If `\u03b1` is a linear succ order, `\u03b2` is a preorder, and `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` is a monotone function, then\nthe intervals `set.Ioc (f n) (f (order.succ n))` are pairwise disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone.pairwise_disjoint_on_Ioc_succ", "description": " If `\u03b1` is a linear succ order, `\u03b2` is a preorder, and `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` is an antitone function, then\nthe intervals `set.Ioc (f (order.succ n)) (f n)` are pairwise disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone.pairwise_disjoint_on_Ico_pred", "description": " If `\u03b1` is a linear pred order, `\u03b2` is a preorder, and `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` is an antitone function, then\nthe intervals `set.Ico (f n) (f (order.pred n))` are pairwise disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone.pairwise_disjoint_on_Ioo_succ", "description": " If `\u03b1` is a linear succ order, `\u03b2` is a preorder, and `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` is a monotone function, then\nthe intervals `set.Ioo (f n) (f (order.succ n))` are pairwise disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone.pairwise_disjoint_on_Ioo_pred", "description": " If `\u03b1` is a linear pred order, `\u03b2` is a preorder, and `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` is a monotone function, then\nthe intervals `set.Ioo (f order.pred n) (f n)` are pairwise disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone.pairwise_disjoint_on_Ico_pred", "description": " If `\u03b1` is a linear pred order, `\u03b2` is a preorder, and `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` is a monotone function, then\nthe intervals `set.Ico (f order.pred n) (f n)` are pairwise disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone.pairwise_disjoint_on_Ico_succ", "description": " If `\u03b1` is a linear succ order, `\u03b2` is a preorder, and `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` is an antitone function, then\nthe intervals `set.Ico (f (order.succ n)) (f n)` are pairwise disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone.pairwise_disjoint_on_Ioo_pred", "description": " If `\u03b1` is a linear pred order, `\u03b2` is a preorder, and `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` is an antitone function, then\nthe intervals `set.Ioo (f n) (f (order.pred n))` are pairwise disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone.bUnion_Ico_Ioc_map_succ", "description": " If `\u03b1` is a linear archimedean succ order and `\u03b2` is a linear order, then for any monotone\nfunction `f` and `m n : \u03b1`, the union of intervals `set.Ioc (f i) (f (order.succ i))`, `m \u2264 i < n`,\nis equal to `set.Ioc (f m) (f n)`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone.pairwise_disjoint_on_Ico_succ", "description": " If `\u03b1` is a linear succ order, `\u03b2` is a preorder, and `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` is a monotone function, then\nthe intervals `set.Ico (f n) (f (order.succ n))` are pairwise disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone.pairwise_disjoint_on_Ioo_succ", "description": " If `\u03b1` is a linear succ order, `\u03b2` is a preorder, and `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` is an antitone function, then\nthe intervals `set.Ioo (f (order.succ n)) (f n)` are pairwise disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone.pairwise_disjoint_on_Ioc_pred", "description": " If `\u03b1` is a linear pred order, `\u03b2` is a preorder, and `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` is a monotone function, then\nthe intervals `set.Ioc (f order.pred n) (f n)` are pairwise disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_split.splitting", "description": "A short exact sequence that is right split admits a splitting.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso_of_short_exact_of_is_iso_of_is_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.mk''", "description": " To construct a splitting of `A -f\u27f6 B -g\u27f6 C` it suffices to supply\na *morphism* `i : A \u229e C \u27f6 B` such that `p \u226b i = f` where `p` is the canonical map\n`biprod.inl : A \u27f6 A \u229e C`, and `i \u226b g` is the canonical map `biprod.snd : A \u229e C \u27f6 C`,\ntogether with proofs that `f` is mono and `g` is epi.\n\nThe morphism `i` is then automatically an isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.splitting.mk'", "description": " To construct a splitting of `A -f\u27f6 B -g\u27f6 C` it suffices to supply\na *morphism* `i : B \u27f6 A \u229e C` such that `f \u226b i` is the canonical map `biprod.inl : A \u27f6 A \u229e C` and\n`i \u226b q = g`, where `q` is the canonical map `biprod.snd : A \u229e C \u27f6 C`,\ntogether with proofs that `f` is mono and `g` is epi.\n\nThe morphism `i` is then automatically an isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_split.splitting", "description": "A short exact sequence that is left split admits a splitting.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "theorems_100.friendship.is_regular_of_not_exists_politician", "description": "If `G` is a friendship graph without a politician (a vertex adjacent to all others), then\n it is regular. We have shown that nonadjacent vertices of a friendship graph have the same degree,\n and if there isn't a politician, we can show this for adjacent vertices by finding a vertex\n neither is adjacent to, and then using transitivity.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.friendship.adj_matrix_sq_of_ne", "description": "One characterization of a friendship graph is that there is exactly one walk of length 2\n between distinct vertices. These walks are counted in off-diagonal entries of the square of\n the adjacency matrix, so for a friendship graph, those entries are all 1.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.friendship.exists_politician_of_degree_le_one", "description": "If `d \u2264 1`, a `d`-regular friendship graph has at most one vertex, which is\n trivially a politician.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.friendship.adj_matrix_pow_three_of_not_adj", "description": "This calculation amounts to counting the number of length 3 walks between nonadjacent vertices.\n We use it to show that nonadjacent vertices have equal degrees.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.friendship.card_of_regular", "description": "Let `A` be the adjacency matrix of a `d`-regular friendship graph, and let `v` be a vector\n all of whose components are `1`. Then `v` is an eigenvector of `A ^ 2`, and we can compute\n the eigenvalue to be `d * d`, or as `d + (fintype.card V - 1)`, so those quantities must be equal.\n\n This essentially means that the graph has `d ^ 2 - d + 1` vertices.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.friendship.neighbor_finset_eq_of_degree_eq_two", "description": "If `d = 2`, a `d`-regular friendship graph has 3 vertices, so it must be complete graph,\n and all the vertices are politicians.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.friendship.exists_politician_of_degree_le_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.friendship.adj_matrix_sq_mod_p_of_regular", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.friendship", "description": "This property of a graph is the hypothesis of the friendship theorem:\nevery pair of nonadjacent vertices has exactly one common friend,\na vertex to which both are adjacent.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.friendship_theorem", "description": "**Friendship theorem**: We wish to show that a friendship graph has a politician (a vertex\n adjacent to all others). We proceed by contradiction, and assume the graph has no politician.\n We have already proven that a friendship graph with no politician is `d`-regular for some `d`,\n and now we do casework on `d`.\n If the degree is at most 2, we observe by casework that it has a politician anyway.\n If the degree is at least 3, the graph cannot exist.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.friendship.adj_matrix_sq_mul_const_one_of_regular", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.friendship.adj_matrix_pow_mod_p_of_regular", "description": "Modulo a factor of `d-1`, the square and all higher powers of the adjacency matrix\n of a `d`-regular friendship graph reduce to the matrix whose entries are all 1.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.exists_politician", "description": "A politician is a vertex that is adjacent to all other vertices.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.friendship.card_mod_p_of_regular", "description": "The size of a `d`-regular friendship graph is `1 mod (d-1)`, and thus `1 mod p` for a\n factor `p \u2223 d-1`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.friendship.adj_matrix_mul_const_one_mod_p_of_regular", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.friendship.exists_politician_of_degree_eq_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.friendship.false_of_three_le_degree", "description": "This is the main proof. Assuming that `3 \u2264 d`, we take `p` to be a prime factor of `d-1`.\n Then the `p`th power of the adjacency matrix of a `d`-regular friendship graph must have trace 1\n mod `p`, but we can also show that the trace must be the `p`th power of the trace of the original\n adjacency matrix, which is 0, a contradiction.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.friendship.adj_matrix_sq_of_regular", "description": "Let `A` be the adjacency matrix of a graph `G`.\n If `G` is a friendship graph, then all of the off-diagonal entries of `A^2` are 1.\n If `G` is `d`-regular, then all of the diagonal entries of `A^2` are `d`.\n Putting these together determines `A^2` exactly for a `d`-regular friendship graph.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.friendship.degree_eq_of_not_adj", "description": "As `v` and `w` not being adjacent implies\n `degree G v = ((G.adj_matrix R) ^ 3) v w` and `degree G w = ((G.adj_matrix R) ^ 3) v w`,\n the degrees are equal if `((G.adj_matrix R) ^ 3) v w = ((G.adj_matrix R) ^ 3) w v`\n\n This is true as the adjacency matrix is symmetric.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "Ext_succ_of_projective", "description": "If `X : C` is projective and `n : \u2115`, then `Ext^(n + 1) X Y \u2245 0` for any `Y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ext_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ext_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Ext", "description": "`Ext R C n` is defined by deriving in the first argument of `(X, Y) \u21a6 Module.of R (unop X \u27f6 Y)`\n(which is the second argument of `linear_yoneda`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.pairwise_disjoint.subset_of_bUnion_subset_bUnion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.pairwise_disjoint_sUnion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.bUnion_eq_sigma_of_disjoint", "description": "Equivalence between a disjoint bounded union and a dependent sum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.pairwise_disjoint.bUnion", "description": " Bind operation for `set.pairwise_disjoint`. If you want to only consider finsets of indices, you\ncan use `set.pairwise_disjoint.bUnion_finset`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pairwise.bUnion_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.pairwise_sUnion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.pairwise_disjoint_Union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.pairwise_disjoint_prod_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.bUnion_diff_bUnion_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.pairwise_Union", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pairwise.subset_of_bUnion_subset_bUnion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.pairwise_disjoint.prod_left", "description": " If the suprema of columns are pairwise disjoint and suprema of rows as well, then everything is\npairwise disjoint. Not to be confused with `set.pairwise_disjoint.prod`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.symmetrify_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.symmetrify.lift_spec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.symmetrify.of_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.path.reverse_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.map_reverse_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.hom.to_pos", "description": "Shorthand for the \"forward\" arrow corresponding to `f` in `symmetrify V`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.symmetrify_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.hom.to_neg", "description": "Shorthand for the \"backward\" arrow corresponding to `f` in `symmetrify V`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.symmetrify.has_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.has_reverse", "description": "A quiver `has_reverse` if we can reverse an arrow `p` from `a` to `b` to get an arrow\n   `p.reverse` from `b` to `a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.has_reverse.reverse'", "description": "A quiver `has_reverse` if we can reverse an arrow `p` from `a` to `b` to get an arrow\n   `p.reverse` from `b` to `a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.symmetrify.has_involutive_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.reverse", "description": "Reverse the direction of an arrow.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.symmetrify_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.is_preconnected", "description": "A quiver is preconnected iff there exists a path between any pair of\nvertices.\nNote that if `V` doesn't `has_reverse`, then the definition is stronger than\nsimply having a preconnected underlying `simple_graph`, since a path in one\ndirection doesn't induce one in the other.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.symmetrify.lift_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.path.reverse", "description": "Reverse the direction of a path.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.map_reverse", "description": "A prefunctor preserving reversal of arrows", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.map_reverse.map_reverse'", "description": "A prefunctor preserving reversal of arrows", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.symmetrify.of", "description": "The inclusion of a quiver in its symmetrification", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.has_involutive_reverse", "description": "A\u00a0quiver `has_involutive_reverse` if reversing twice is the identity.`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.has_involutive_reverse.to_has_reverse", "description": "A\u00a0quiver `has_involutive_reverse` if reversing twice is the identity.`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.has_involutive_reverse.inv'", "description": "A\u00a0quiver `has_involutive_reverse` if reversing twice is the identity.`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.symmetrify.lift", "description": "Given a quiver `V'` with reversible arrows, a prefunctor to `V'` can be lifted to one from\n   `symmetrify V` to `V'`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.path.reverse_to_path", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.push.quiver.has_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.push.of_map_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.push.of_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.symmetrify.of_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.push.quiver.has_involutive_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.reverse_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.path.reverse_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.eq_reverse_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.symmetrify_quiver", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.symmetrify", "description": "A type synonym for the symmetrized quiver (with an arrow both ways for each original arrow).\n   NB: this does not work for `Prop`-valued quivers. It requires `[quiver.{v+1} V]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.map_reverse_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.symmetrify.lift_unique", "description": "`lift \u03c6` is the only prefunctor extending `\u03c6` and preserving reverses.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.reverse_inj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.map_reverse'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.symmetrify_map_reverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prefunctor.symmetrify", "description": "A prefunctor canonically defines a prefunctor of the symmetrifications.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "task.bind", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "task.flatten", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "task.pure", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "task.get", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "task.delay", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "task.map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "task", "description": "A task is a promise to produce a value later. They perform the same role as promises in JavaScript.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "sup_prime.of_dual", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `inf_prime_of_dual`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_sup_prime_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_min.not_sup_prime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_max.not_inf_prime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_prime.ne_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_inf_irred_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_irred.of_dual", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `sup_irred_of_dual`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_max.not_inf_irred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_prime", "description": " An inf-prime element is a non-top element which isn't bigger than the infimum of anything\nbigger.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_prime_of_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_sup_irred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_prime.not_is_min", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_irred.sup_prime", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `sup_prime_iff_sup_irred`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_min.not_sup_irred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_irred_of_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_irred.ne_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_prime_iff_not_is_max", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_prime.ne_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_prime_iff_not_is_min", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_irred_iff_not_is_min", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_irred.finset_sup_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_prime_iff_sup_irred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_inf_prime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_prime.le_finset_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_prime_of_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_prime", "description": " A sup-prime element is a non-bottom element which isn't less than the supremum of anything\nsmaller.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_prime.inf_irred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_sup_irred_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_irred.dual", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `sup_irred_to_dual`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_inf_prime_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_prime.inf_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_irred.finset_inf_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_irred", "description": "An inf-irreducible element is a non-top element which isn't the infimum of anything bigger.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_prime.finset_inf_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_prime.dual", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `inf_prime_to_dual`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_irred_to_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_irred_to_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_sup_irred_decomposition", "description": " In a well-founded lattice, any element is the supremum of finitely many sup-irreducible\nelements. This is the order-theoretic analogue of prime factorisation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_sup_prime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_irred.of_dual", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `inf_irred_of_dual`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_prime.of_dual", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `sup_prime_of_dual`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_irred.not_is_min", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_prime_to_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_prime_to_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_prime.sup_irred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_irred_iff_not_is_max", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_irred.inf_prime", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `inf_prime_iff_inf_irred`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_prime.dual", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `sup_prime_to_dual`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_irred", "description": "A sup-irreducible element is a non-bottom element which isn't the supremum of anything smaller.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_prime_iff_inf_irred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_irred_of_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inf_irred.ne_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_inf_irred_decomposition", "description": " In a cowell-founded lattice, any element is the infimum of finitely many inf-irreducible\nelements. This is the order-theoretic analogue of prime factorisation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_irred.dual", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `inf_irred_to_dual`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_inf_irred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sup_prime.le_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.get_rest", "description": "`get_rest s t` returns `some r` if `s = t ++ r`.\n If `t` is not a prefix of `s`, returns `none`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.popn", "description": "Removes the first `n` elements from the string `s`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.map_tokens", "description": " `string.map_tokens c f s` tokenizes `s : string` on `c : char`, maps `f` over each token, and\nthen reassembles the string by intercalating the separator token `c` over the mapped tokens.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.is_nat", "description": "`is_nat s` is true iff `s` is a nonempty sequence of digits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.ends_with", "description": "`x.ends_with y` is true if `y` is a suffix of `x`, and is false otherwise.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.split_on", "description": "`s.split_on c` tokenizes `s : string` on `c : char`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.is_suffix_of", "description": "Tests whether the first string is a suffix of the second string.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.is_prefix_of", "description": "Tests whether the first string is a prefix of the second string.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.starts_with", "description": "`x.starts_with y` is true if `y` is a prefix of `x`, and is false otherwise.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.head", "description": "Produce the head character from the string `s`, if `s` is not empty, otherwise 'A'.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso_of_mono_of_epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.balanced_opposite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.balanced", "description": "A category is called balanced if any morphism that is both monic and epic is an isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.balanced.is_iso_of_mono_of_epi", "description": "A category is called balanced if any morphism that is both monic and epic is an isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso_iff_mono_and_epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.arborescence", "description": "A quiver is an arborescence when there is a unique path from the default vertex\n   to every other vertex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.arborescence.root", "description": "A quiver is an arborescence when there is a unique path from the default vertex\n   to every other vertex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.arborescence.unique_path", "description": "A quiver is an arborescence when there is a unique path from the default vertex\n   to every other vertex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.root", "description": "The root of an arborescence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.arborescence_mk", "description": "To show that `[quiver V]` is an arborescence with root `r : V`, it suffices to\n - provide a height function `V \u2192 \u2115` such that every arrow goes from a\n   lower vertex to a higher vertex,\n - show that every vertex has at most one arrow to it, and\n - show that every vertex other than `r` has an arrow to it.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.rooted_connected", "description": "`rooted_connected r` means that there is a path from `r` to any other vertex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.rooted_connected.nonempty_path", "description": "`rooted_connected r` means that there is a path from `r` to any other vertex.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.geodesic_arborescence", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.shortest_path", "description": "A path from `r` of minimal length.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.path.unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.geodesic_subtree", "description": "A subquiver which by construction is an arborescence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quiver.shortest_path_spec", "description": "The length of a path is at least the length of the shortest path", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tropical.has_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tropical.conditionally_complete_linear_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tropical.has_Sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tropical.conditionally_complete_lattice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tropical.has_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tropical.lattice", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tropical.semilattice_inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tropical.semilattice_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tropical.has_Inf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.card_fintype_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.card_fintype_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.card_fintype_Ico_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.card_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.card_Ico_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.card_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.card_Ioo_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.card_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.card_fintype_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.card_uIcc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.card_fintype_Ioc_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.Ico_eq_finset_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.Ioc_eq_finset_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.Icc_eq_finset_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.card_Ioc_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.locally_finite_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.card_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.Ioo_eq_finset_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.card_fintype_uIcc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.uIcc_eq_finset_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.card_fintype_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.card_fintype_Icc_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.card_Icc_of_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.image_Ico_mod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.card_fintype_Ioo_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo2001_q2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2001_q2.imo2001_q2'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2001_q2.denom_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2001_q2.bound", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "vector.prod_mul_prod_eq_prod_zip_with", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.sum_add_sum_eq_sum_zip_with", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.zip_with", "description": "Apply the function `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b3` to each corresponding pair of elements from two vectors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.zip_with_to_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.zip_with_tail", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.zip_with_nth", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orientation.volume_form_robust'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orientation.volume_form_zero_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orientation.volume_form", "description": " The volume form on an oriented real inner product space, a nonvanishing top-dimensional\nalternating form uniquely defined by compatibility with the orientation and inner product structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal_basis.adjust_to_orientation", "description": " Given an orthonormal basis and an orientation, return an orthonormal basis giving that\norientation: either the original basis, or one constructed by negating a single (arbitrary) basis\nvector.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orientation.volume_form_zero_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orientation.fin_orthonormal_basis_orientation", "description": "`orientation.fin_orthonormal_basis` gives a basis with the required orientation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal_basis.same_orientation_iff_det_eq_det", "description": " Two orthonormal bases with the same orientation determine the same \"determinant\" top-dimensional\nform on `E`, and conversely.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal_basis.det_eq_neg_det_of_opposite_orientation", "description": " Two orthonormal bases with opposite orientations determine opposite \"determinant\"\ntop-dimensional forms on `E`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal_basis.abs_det_adjust_to_orientation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orientation.volume_form_comp_linear_isometry_equiv", "description": "The volume form is invariant under pullback by a positively-oriented isometric automorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal_basis.det_adjust_to_orientation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal_basis.det_to_matrix_orthonormal_basis_of_same_orientation", "description": " The change-of-basis matrix between two orthonormal bases with the same orientation has\ndeterminant 1.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal_basis.adjust_to_orientation_apply_eq_or_eq_neg", "description": " Every basis vector from `adjust_to_orientation` is either that from the original basis or its\nnegation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orientation.volume_form_robust", "description": " The volume form on an oriented real inner product space can be evaluated as the determinant with\nrespect to any orthonormal basis of the space compatible with the orientation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orientation.abs_volume_form_apply_of_pairwise_orthogonal", "description": " Let `v` be an indexed family of `n` orthogonal vectors in an oriented `n`-dimensional\nreal inner product space `E`. The output of the volume form of `E` when evaluated on `v` is, up to\nsign, the product of the norms of the vectors `v i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orientation.volume_form_apply_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal_basis.orientation_adjust_to_orientation", "description": "`adjust_to_orientation` gives an orthonormal basis with the required orientation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orientation.abs_volume_form_apply_le", "description": " Let `v` be an indexed family of `n` vectors in an oriented `n`-dimensional real inner\nproduct space `E`. The output of the volume form of `E` when evaluated on `v` is bounded in absolute\nvalue by the product of the norms of the vectors `v i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orientation.fin_orthonormal_basis", "description": "An orthonormal basis, indexed by `fin n`, with the given orientation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orientation.volume_form_robust_neg", "description": " The volume form on an oriented real inner product space can be evaluated as the determinant with\nrespect to any orthonormal basis of the space compatible with the orientation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orientation.abs_volume_form_apply_of_orthonormal", "description": " The output of the volume form of an oriented real inner product space `E` when evaluated on an\northonormal basis is \u00b11.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orientation.volume_form_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orientation.volume_form_neg_orientation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal_basis.orthonormal_adjust_to_orientation", "description": " `orthonormal_basis.adjust_to_orientation`, applied to an orthonormal basis, preserves the\nproperty of orthonormality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal_basis.det_to_matrix_orthonormal_basis_of_opposite_orientation", "description": " The change-of-basis matrix between two orthonormal bases with the opposite orientations has\ndeterminant -1.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal_basis.to_basis_adjust_to_orientation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.multiset.sum_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.multiset.cons_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.list.prove_prod", "description": " Evaluate `list.prod %%xs`,\nproducing the evaluated expression and an equality proof.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.multiset.map_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.eval_finset_interval", "description": "For now this only works on types that are contiguous subsets of the integers", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.list.not_mem_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.list.sum_cons_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.eval_multiset", "description": " Convert an expression denoting a multiset to a list of elements.\n\nWe return a list rather than a finset, so we can more easily iterate over it\n(without having to prove that our tactics are independent of the order of iteration,\nwhich is in general not true).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.list.cons_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.finset.eval_prod_of_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.list.map_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.eval_finset", "description": " Convert an expression denoting a finset to a list of elements,\na proof that this list is equal to the original finset,\nand a proof that the list contains no duplicates.\n\nWe return a list rather than a finset, so we can more easily iterate over it\n(without having to prove that our tactics are independent of the order of iteration,\nwhich is in general not true).\n\n`decide_eq` is a (partial) decision procedure for determining whether two\nelements of the finset are equal, for example to parse `{2, 1, 2}` into `[2, 1]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.list.prod_cons_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.finset.insert_eq_coe_list_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.list.prove_prod_map", "description": " Evaluate `(%%xs.map (%%ef : %%\u03b1 \u2192 %%\u03b2)).prod`,\nproducing the evaluated expression and an equality proof.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.eval_list", "description": "Convert an expression denoting a list to a list of elements.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.finset.eval_sum_of_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.multiset.prod_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.finset.insert_eq_coe_list_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.decide_eq", "description": " Use `norm_num` to decide equality between two expressions.\n\nIf the decision procedure succeeds, the `bool` value indicates whether the expressions are equal,\nand the `expr` is a proof of (dis)equality.\nThis procedure is partial: it will fail in cases where `norm_num` can't reduce either side\nto a rational numeral.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.list.decide_mem", "description": " Use a decision procedure for the equality of list elements to decide list membership.\n\nIf the decision procedure succeeds, the `bool` value indicates whether the expressions are equal,\nand the `expr` is a proof of (dis)equality.\nThis procedure is partial iff its parameter `decide_eq` is partial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.list.prove_sum_map", "description": " Evaluate `(%%xs.map (%%ef : %%\u03b1 \u2192 %%\u03b2)).sum`,\nproducing the evaluated expression and an equality proof.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.eval_big_operators", "description": "`norm_num` plugin for evaluating big operators:\n* `list.prod`\n* `list.sum`\n* `multiset.prod`\n* `multiset.sum`\n* `finset.prod`\n* `finset.sum`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.list.prove_sum", "description": " Evaluate `list.sum %%xs`,\nsumucing the evaluated expression and an equality proof.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.finset.mk_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.list.map_cons_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.list.prod_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.eval_list_map", "description": "Apply `ef : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` to all elements of the list, constructing an equality proof.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.norm_num.list.sum_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.exp_local_homeomorph", "description": " `complex.exp` as a `local_homeomorph` with `source = {z | -\u03c0 < im z < \u03c0}` and\n`target = {z | 0 < re z} \u222a {z | im z \u2260 0}`. This definition is used to prove that `complex.log`\nis complex differentiable at all points but the negative real semi-axis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_on.clog", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_at.clog", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_within_at.clog", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.has_strict_fderiv_at_log_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.cont_diff_at_log", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at.clog", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_within_at.clog", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_fderiv_at.clog", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.has_strict_deriv_at_log", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_within_at.clog", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.clog_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_within_at.clog_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.clog", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.clog", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_open_map_exp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_deriv_at.clog", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_deriv_at.clog_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.abs_is_euclidean", "description": "`abs : \u2124 \u2192 \u2124` is a Euclidean absolute value", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.is_euclidean.map_lt_map_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.is_euclidean.sub_mod_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.is_euclidean", "description": " An absolute value `abv : R \u2192 S` is Euclidean if it is compatible with the\n`euclidean_domain` structure on `R`, namely `abv` is strictly monotone with respect to the well\nfounded relation `\u227a` on `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.is_euclidean.map_lt_map_iff'", "description": " An absolute value `abv : R \u2192 S` is Euclidean if it is compatible with the\n`euclidean_domain` structure on `R`, namely `abv` is strictly monotone with respect to the well\nfounded relation `\u227a` on `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.cast_neg_nat_cast", "description": "Auxiliary lemma for norm_cast to move the cast `-\u2191n` upwards to `\u2191-\u2191n`.\n\n(The restriction to `division_ring` is necessary, otherwise this would also apply in the case where\n`R = \u2124` and cause nontermination.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodules_basis.to_module_filter_basis", "description": "The image of a submodules basis is a module filter basis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_subgroups_basis.nonarchimedean", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "library_note.nonarchimedean non instances", "description": " The non archimedean subgroup basis lemmas cannot be instances because some instances\n(such as `measure_theory.ae_eq_fun.add_monoid ` or `topological_add_group.to_has_continuous_add`)\ncause the search for `@topological_add_group \u03b2 ?m1 ?m2`, i.e. a search for a topological group where\nthe topology/group structure are unknown.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_filter_basis.submodules_basis", "description": " Given a ring filter basis on a commutative ring `R`, define a compatibility condition\non a family of submodules of a `R`-module `M`. This compatibility condition allows to get\na topological module structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_filter_basis.submodules_basis.inter", "description": " Given a ring filter basis on a commutative ring `R`, define a compatibility condition\non a family of submodules of a `R`-module `M`. This compatibility condition allows to get\na topological module structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_filter_basis.submodules_basis.smul", "description": " Given a ring filter basis on a commutative ring `R`, define a compatibility condition\non a family of submodules of a `R`-module `M`. This compatibility condition allows to get\na topological module structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_subgroups_basis", "description": " A family of additive subgroups on a ring `A` is a subgroups basis if it satisfies some\naxioms ensuring there is a topology on `A` which is compatible with the ring structure and\nadmits this family as a basis of neighborhoods of zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_subgroups_basis.inter", "description": " A family of additive subgroups on a ring `A` is a subgroups basis if it satisfies some\naxioms ensuring there is a topology on `A` which is compatible with the ring structure and\nadmits this family as a basis of neighborhoods of zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_subgroups_basis.mul", "description": " A family of additive subgroups on a ring `A` is a subgroups basis if it satisfies some\naxioms ensuring there is a topology on `A` which is compatible with the ring structure and\nadmits this family as a basis of neighborhoods of zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_subgroups_basis.left_mul", "description": " A family of additive subgroups on a ring `A` is a subgroups basis if it satisfies some\naxioms ensuring there is a topology on `A` which is compatible with the ring structure and\nadmits this family as a basis of neighborhoods of zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_subgroups_basis.right_mul", "description": " A family of additive subgroups on a ring `A` is a subgroups basis if it satisfies some\naxioms ensuring there is a topology on `A` which is compatible with the ring structure and\nadmits this family as a basis of neighborhoods of zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodules_basis.nonarchimedean", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_subgroups_basis.of_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodules_basis.open_add_subgroup", "description": " Given a submodules basis, the basis elements as open additive subgroups in the associated\ntopology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodules_ring_basis", "description": " A family of submodules in a commutative `R`-algebra `A` is a submodules basis if it satisfies\nsome axioms ensuring there is a topology on `A` which is compatible with the ring structure and\nadmits this family as a basis of neighborhoods of zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodules_ring_basis.inter", "description": " A family of submodules in a commutative `R`-algebra `A` is a submodules basis if it satisfies\nsome axioms ensuring there is a topology on `A` which is compatible with the ring structure and\nadmits this family as a basis of neighborhoods of zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodules_ring_basis.left_mul", "description": " A family of submodules in a commutative `R`-algebra `A` is a submodules basis if it satisfies\nsome axioms ensuring there is a topology on `A` which is compatible with the ring structure and\nadmits this family as a basis of neighborhoods of zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodules_ring_basis.mul", "description": " A family of submodules in a commutative `R`-algebra `A` is a submodules basis if it satisfies\nsome axioms ensuring there is a topology on `A` which is compatible with the ring structure and\nadmits this family as a basis of neighborhoods of zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_subgroups_basis.has_basis_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_subgroups_basis.has_basis_nhds_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodules_ring_basis.to_ring_subgroups_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_subgroups_basis.open_add_subgroup", "description": " Given a subgroups basis, the basis elements as open additive subgroups in the associated\ntopology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_subgroups_basis.topology", "description": " The topology defined from a subgroups basis, admitting the given subgroups as a basis\nof neighborhoods of zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_filter_basis.submodules_basis_is_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodules_basis", "description": " A family of submodules in an `R`-module `M` is a submodules basis if it satisfies\nsome axioms ensuring there is a topology on `M` which is compatible with the module structure and\nadmits this family as a basis of neighborhoods of zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodules_basis.inter", "description": " A family of submodules in an `R`-module `M` is a submodules basis if it satisfies\nsome axioms ensuring there is a topology on `M` which is compatible with the module structure and\nadmits this family as a basis of neighborhoods of zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodules_basis.smul", "description": " A family of submodules in an `R`-module `M` is a submodules basis if it satisfies\nsome axioms ensuring there is a topology on `M` which is compatible with the module structure and\nadmits this family as a basis of neighborhoods of zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_filter_basis.module_filter_basis", "description": " The module filter basis associated to a ring filter basis and a compatible submodule basis.\nThis allows to build a topological module structure compatible with the given module structure\nand the topology associated to the given ring filter basis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodules_ring_basis.topology", "description": "The topology associated to a basis of submodules in an algebra.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodules_basis.topology", "description": "The topology associated to a basis of submodules in a module.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_subgroups_basis.to_ring_filter_basis", "description": "Every subgroups basis on a ring leads to a ring filter basis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodules_ring_basis.to_submodules_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_subgroups_basis.mem_add_group_filter_basis_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_subgroups_basis.mem_add_group_filter_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.completion.has_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.completion.normed_add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uniform_space.completion.norm_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pi.cocone_of_cocone_eval_is_colimit", "description": "Given a family of colimit cocones over the `F \u22d9 pi.eval C i`,\nassembling them together as a `cocone F` produces a colimit cocone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pi.cocone_comp_eval", "description": "A cocone over `F : J \u2964 \u03a0 i, C i` has as its components cocones over each of the `F \u22d9 pi.eval C i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pi.cone_of_cone_eval_is_limit", "description": "Given a family of limit cones over the `F \u22d9 pi.eval C i`,\nassembling them together as a `cone F` produces a limit cone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pi.has_colimit_of_has_colimit_comp_eval", "description": "If we have a functor `F : J \u2964 \u03a0 i, C i` into a category of indexed families,\nand colimits exist for each of the `F \u22d9 pi.eval C i`,\nthere is a colimit for `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pi.cone_of_cone_comp_eval", "description": "Given a family of cones over the `F \u22d9 pi.eval C i`, we can assemble these together as a `cone F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pi.has_limit_of_has_limit_comp_eval", "description": "If we have a functor `F : J \u2964 \u03a0 i, C i` into a category of indexed families,\nand we have limits for each of the `F \u22d9 pi.eval C i`,\nthen `F` has a limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pi.cone_comp_eval", "description": "A cone over `F : J \u2964 \u03a0 i, C i` has as its components cones over each of the `F \u22d9 pi.eval C i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pi.cocone_of_cocone_comp_eval", "description": "Given a family of cocones over the `F \u22d9 pi.eval C i`,\nwe can assemble these together as a `cocone F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal.conformal_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal_at", "description": "A map `f` is said to be conformal if it has a conformal differential `f'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal_at_const_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal_at.differentiable_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal_at.comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal_at_iff_is_conformal_map_fderiv", "description": "A function is a conformal map if and only if its differential is a conformal linear map", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal_at_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal_at.congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal", "description": "A map `f` is conformal if it's conformal at every point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal.const_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal_const_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsingleton.conformal_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal_at.const_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal.differentiable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal.comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.total_positive_cone", "description": "A total positive cone in a nontrivial ring induces a linear order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.total_positive_cone.nonneg", "description": "A total positive cone in a nontrivial ring induces a linear order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.total_positive_cone.pos", "description": "A total positive cone in a nontrivial ring induces a linear order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.total_positive_cone.pos_iff", "description": "A total positive cone in a nontrivial ring induces a linear order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.total_positive_cone.zero_nonneg", "description": "A total positive cone in a nontrivial ring induces a linear order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.total_positive_cone.add_nonneg", "description": "A total positive cone in a nontrivial ring induces a linear order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.total_positive_cone.nonneg_antisymm", "description": "A total positive cone in a nontrivial ring induces a linear order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.total_positive_cone.one_nonneg", "description": "A total positive cone in a nontrivial ring induces a linear order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.total_positive_cone.mul_pos", "description": "A total positive cone in a nontrivial ring induces a linear order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.total_positive_cone.nonneg_decidable", "description": "A total positive cone in a nontrivial ring induces a linear order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.total_positive_cone.nonneg_total", "description": "A total positive cone in a nontrivial ring induces a linear order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.positive_cone.to_positive_cone", "description": "Forget that a positive cone in a ring respects the multiplicative structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.positive_cone", "description": " A positive cone in a ring consists of a positive cone in underlying `add_comm_group`,\nwhich contains `1` and such that the positive elements are closed under multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.positive_cone.nonneg", "description": " A positive cone in a ring consists of a positive cone in underlying `add_comm_group`,\nwhich contains `1` and such that the positive elements are closed under multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.positive_cone.pos", "description": " A positive cone in a ring consists of a positive cone in underlying `add_comm_group`,\nwhich contains `1` and such that the positive elements are closed under multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.positive_cone.pos_iff", "description": " A positive cone in a ring consists of a positive cone in underlying `add_comm_group`,\nwhich contains `1` and such that the positive elements are closed under multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.positive_cone.zero_nonneg", "description": " A positive cone in a ring consists of a positive cone in underlying `add_comm_group`,\nwhich contains `1` and such that the positive elements are closed under multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.positive_cone.add_nonneg", "description": " A positive cone in a ring consists of a positive cone in underlying `add_comm_group`,\nwhich contains `1` and such that the positive elements are closed under multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.positive_cone.nonneg_antisymm", "description": " A positive cone in a ring consists of a positive cone in underlying `add_comm_group`,\nwhich contains `1` and such that the positive elements are closed under multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.positive_cone.one_nonneg", "description": " A positive cone in a ring consists of a positive cone in underlying `add_comm_group`,\nwhich contains `1` and such that the positive elements are closed under multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.positive_cone.mul_pos", "description": " A positive cone in a ring consists of a positive cone in underlying `add_comm_group`,\nwhich contains `1` and such that the positive elements are closed under multiplication.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_ordered_ring.mk_of_positive_cone", "description": "Construct a `strict_ordered_ring` by designating a positive cone in an existing `ring`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.total_positive_cone.to_total_positive_cone", "description": "Forget that a `total_positive_cone` in a ring respects the multiplicative structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.total_positive_cone.to_positive_cone", "description": "Forget that a `total_positive_cone` in a ring is total.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_ordered_ring.mk_of_positive_cone", "description": " Construct a `linear_ordered_ring` by\ndesignating a positive cone in an existing `ring`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring.positive_cone.one_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_zero.to_mul_bot_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_zero.to_mul_bot_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_zero.to_mul_bot_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_zero.to_mul_bot_coe_of_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_zero.to_mul_bot_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_zero.to_mul_bot_symm_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_zero.to_mul_bot_strict_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_zero.to_mul_bot", "description": " Making an additive monoid multiplicative then adding a zero is the same as adding a bottom\nelement then making it multiplicative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.polynomial.gcd_div_id_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.int.gcd_div_eq_one", "description": " Given a nonempty finset `s` and a function `f` from `s` to `\u2124`, if `d = s.gcd`,\nthen the `gcd` of `(f i) / d` is equal to `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.int.gcd_div_id_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.nat.gcd_div_eq_one", "description": " Given a nonempty finset `s` and a function `f` from `s` to `\u2115`, if `d = s.gcd`,\nthen the `gcd` of `(f i) / d` is equal to `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.nat.gcd_div_id_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.polynomial.gcd_div_eq_one", "description": " Given a nonempty finset `s` and a function `f` from `s` to `K[X]`, if `d = s.gcd f`,\nthen the `gcd` of `(f i) / d` is equal to `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.locally_of_finite_type.open_cover_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.locally_of_finite_type", "description": "A morphism of schemes `f : X \u27f6 Y` is locally of finite type if for each affine `U \u2286 Y` and\n`V \u2286 f \u207b\u00b9' U`, The induced map `\u0393(Y, U) \u27f6 \u0393(X, V)` is of finite type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.locally_of_finite_type.finite_type_of_affine_subset", "description": "A morphism of schemes `f : X \u27f6 Y` is locally of finite type if for each affine `U \u2286 Y` and\n`V \u2286 f \u207b\u00b9' U`, The induced map `\u0393(Y, U) \u27f6 \u0393(X, V)` is of finite type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.locally_of_finite_type_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.locally_of_finite_type_of_is_open_immersion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.locally_of_finite_type_stable_under_composition", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.locally_of_finite_type_respects_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.locally_of_finite_type.affine_open_cover_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.locally_of_finite_type_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.locally_of_finite_type.source_open_cover_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.locally_of_finite_type_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.locally_of_finite_type_of_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.costructured_arrow.lift_project_quotient", "description": "Projecting and then lifting a quotient recovers the original quotient, because there is at most\n   one morphism making the projected quotient into a costructured arrow.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.costructured_arrow.well_copowered_costructured_arrow", "description": "If `C` is well-copowered and cocomplete and `S` preserves colimits, then\n   `costructured_arrow S T` is well-copowered.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.structured_arrow.subobject_equiv_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.structured_arrow.project_subobject_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.structured_arrow.subobject_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.costructured_arrow.quotient_equiv", "description": "If `A : S.obj B \u27f6 T` is a costructured arrow for `S : C \u2964 D` and `T : D`, then we can\n   explicitly describe the quotients of `A` as the quotients `P` of `B` in `C` for which `A.hom`\n   factors through the image of `P` under `S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.costructured_arrow.unop_left_comp_underlying_iso_hom_unop", "description": "Technical lemma for `lift_project_quotient`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.structured_arrow.lift_subobject", "description": "A subobject of the underlying object of a structured arrow can be lifted to a subobject of\n   the structured arrow, provided that there is a morphism making the subobject into a structured\n   arrow.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.costructured_arrow.project_quotient_factors", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.structured_arrow.project_subobject_factors", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.costructured_arrow.lift_quotient", "description": "A quotient of the underlying object of a costructured arrow can be lifted to a quotient of\n   the costructured arrow, provided that there is a morphism making the quotient into a\n   costructured arrow.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.structured_arrow.well_powered_structured_arrow", "description": "If `C` is well-powered and complete and `T` preserves limits, then `structured_arrow S T` is\n   well-powered.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.structured_arrow.project_subobject", "description": "Every subobject of a structured arrow can be projected to a subobject of the underlying\n   object.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.structured_arrow.lift_project_subobject", "description": "Projecting and then lifting a subobject recovers the original subobject, because there is at\n   most one morphism making the projected subobject into a structured arrow.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.costructured_arrow.unop_left_comp_of_mk_le_mk_unop", "description": "Technical lemma for `quotient_equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.costructured_arrow.project_quotient", "description": "Every quotient of a costructured arrow can be projected to a quotient of the underlying\n   object.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.costructured_arrow.project_quotient_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.structured_arrow.subobject_equiv", "description": "If `A : S \u2192 T.obj B` is a structured arrow for `S : D` and `T : C \u2964 D`, then we can explicitly\n   describe the subobjects of `A` as the subobjects `P` of `B` in `C` for which `A.hom` factors\n   through the image of `P` under `T`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.cons_ne_zero_of_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.cons_ne_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.tail_cons", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.cons_zero_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.tail_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.tail", "description": "`tail` for maps `fin (n + 1) \u2192\u2080 M`. See `fin.tail` for more details.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.cons_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.cons_ne_zero_of_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.cons", "description": "`cons` for maps `fin n \u2192\u2080 M`. See `fin.cons` for more details.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.cons_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.cons_tail", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.bind_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.fmap_eq_image", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.seq_eq_set_seq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.monad", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image2_def", "description": " `set.image2` in terms of monadic operations. Note that this can't be taken as the definition\nbecause of the lack of universe polymorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.alternative", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.is_lawful_monad", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.is_comm_applicative", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.pure_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.impl_graph.dfs_at", "description": "Strongly connected component algorithm inspired by Tarjan's and\nDijkstra's scc algorithm. Whereas they return strongly connected\ncomponents by enumerating them, this algorithm returns a disjoint set\ndata structure using path compression. This is a compact\nrepresentation that allows us, after the fact, to construct a proof of\nequivalence between any two members of an equivalence class.\n\n * Tarjan, R. E. (1972), \"Depth-first search and linear graph algorithms\",\n   SIAM Journal on Computing, 1 (2): 146\u2013160, doi:10.1137/0201010\n * Dijkstra, Edsger (1976), A Discipline of Programming, NJ: Prentice Hall, Ch. 25.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.scc", "description": "`scc` uses the available equivalences and implications to prove\na goal of the form `p \u2194 q`.\n\n```lean\nexample (p q r : Prop) (hpq : p \u2192 q) (hqr : q \u2194 r) (hrp : r \u2192 p) : p \u2194 r :=\nby scc\n```\n\nThe variant `scc'` populates the local context with all equivalences that `scc` is able to prove.\nThis is mostly useful for testing purposes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.impl_graph.mk_scc", "description": "Use the local assumptions to create a set of equivalence classes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.closure.is_eqv", "description": "`is_eqv cl e\u2080 e\u2081` checks whether `e\u2080` and `e\u2081` are equivalent without building a proof.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.closure.has_to_tactic_format", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.prove_eqv_target", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.closure.with_new_closure", "description": " `with_new_closure f` creates an empty `closure` `c`, executes `f` on `c`, and then deletes `c`,\nreturning the output of `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.closure.root", "description": " `(n,r,p) \u2190 root cl e` returns `r` the root of the tree that `e` is a part of (which might be\nitself) along with `p` a proof of `e \u2194 r` and `n`, the preorder numbering of the root.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.impl_graph.collapse'", "description": "(implementation of `collapse`)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.closure.prove_impl", "description": "`prove_impl cl e\u2080 e\u2081` constructs a proof of `e\u2080 -> e\u2081` if they are equivalent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.impl_graph.merge_path", "description": " `merge_path path e`, where `path` and `e` forms a cycle with proofs of implication between\nconsecutive vertices. The proofs are compiled into proofs of equivalences and added to the closure\nstructure. `e` and the first vertex of `path` do not have to be the same but they have to be\nin the same equivalence class.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.closure.assign_preorder", "description": "Sequentially assign numbers to the nodes of the graph as they are being visited.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.impl_graph", "description": "mutable graphs between local propositions that imply each other with the proof of implication", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.closure.to_tactic_format", "description": " `to_tactic_format cl` pretty-prints the `closure` `cl` as a list. Assuming `cl` was built by\n`dfs_at`, each element corresponds to a node `p\u1d62 : expr` and is one of the folllowing:\n- if `p\u1d62` is a root: `\"p\u1d62 \u21d0 i\"`, where `i` is the preorder number of `p\u1d62`,\n- otherwise: `\"(p\u1d62, p\u2c7c) : P\"`, where `P` is `p\u1d62 \u2194 p\u2c7c`.\nUseful for debugging.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.with_impl_graph", "description": " `with_impl_graph f` creates an empty `impl_graph` `g`, executes `f` on `g`, and then deletes\n`g`, returning the output of `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.closure.merge_intl", "description": "(Implementation of `merge`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.scc'", "description": " Collect all the available equivalences and implications and\nadd assumptions for every equivalence that can be proven using the\nstrongly connected components technique. Mostly useful for testing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.closure.prove_eqv", "description": " `prove_eqv cl e\u2080 e\u2081` constructs a proof of equivalence of `e\u2080` and `e\u2081` if\nthey are equivalent.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.closure.merge", "description": " `merge cl p`, with `p` a proof of `e\u2080 \u2194 e\u2081` for some `e\u2080` and `e\u2081`,\nmerges the trees of `e\u2080` and `e\u2081` and keeps the root with the smallest preorder\nnumber as the root. This ensures that, in the depth-first traversal of the graph,\nwhen encountering an edge going into a vertex whose equivalence class includes\na vertex that originated the current search, that vertex will be the root of\nthe corresponding tree.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.impl_graph.collapse", "description": " `collapse path v`, where `v` is a vertex that originated the current search\n(or a vertex in the same equivalence class as the one that originated the current search).\nIt or its equivalent should be found in `path`. Since the vertices following `v` in the path\nform a cycle with `v`, they can all be added to an equivalence class.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.closure", "description": "`closure` implements a disjoint set data structure using path compression\noptimization. For the sake of the scc algorithm, it also stores the preorder\nnumbering of the equivalence graph of the local assumptions.\n\nThe `expr_map` encodes a directed forest by storing for every non-root\nnode, a reference to its parent and a proof of equivalence between\nthat node's expression and its parent's expression. Given that data\nstructure, checking that two nodes belong to the same tree is easy and\nfast by repeatedly following the parent references until a root is reached.\nIf both nodes have the same root, they belong to the same tree, i.e. their\nexpressions are equivalent. The proof of equivalence can be formed by\ncomposing the proofs along the edges of the paths to the root.\n\nMore concretely, if we ignore preorder numbering, the set\n`{ {e\u2080,e\u2081,e\u2082,e\u2083}, {e\u2084,e\u2085} }` is represented as:\n\n```lean\ne\u2080 \u2192 \u22a5      -- no parent, i.e. e\u2080 is a root\ne\u2081 \u2192 e\u2080, p\u2081 -- with p\u2081 : e\u2081 \u2194 e\u2080\ne\u2082 \u2192 e\u2081, p\u2082 -- with p\u2082 : e\u2082 \u2194 e\u2081\ne\u2083 \u2192 e\u2080, p\u2083 -- with p\u2083 : e\u2083 \u2194 e\u2080\ne\u2084 \u2192 \u22a5      -- no parent, i.e. e\u2084 is a root\ne\u2085 \u2192 e\u2084, p\u2085 -- with p\u2085 : e\u2085 \u2194 e\u2084\n```\n\nWe can check that `e\u2082` and `e\u2083` are equivalent by seeking the root of\nthe tree of each. The parent of `e\u2082` is `e\u2081`, the parent of `e\u2081` is\n`e\u2080` and `e\u2080` does not have a parent, and thus, this is the root of its tree.\nThe parent of `e\u2083` is `e\u2080` and it's also the root, the same as for `e\u2082` and\nthey are therefore equivalent. We can build a proof of that equivalence by using\ntransitivity on `p\u2082`, `p\u2081` and `p\u2083.symm` in that order.\n\nSimilarly, we can discover that `e\u2082` and `e\u2085` aren't equivalent.\n\nA description of the path compression optimization can be found at:\n<https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disjoint-set_data_structure#Path_compression>", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.scc", "description": "`scc` uses the available equivalences and implications to prove\na goal of the form `p \u2194 q`.\n\n```lean\nexample (p q r : Prop) (hpq : p \u2192 q) (hqr : q \u2194 r) (hrp : r \u2192 p) : p \u2194 r :=\nby scc\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.impl_graph.add_edge", "description": " `add_edge g p`, with `p` a proof of `v\u2080 \u2192 v\u2081` or `v\u2080 \u2194 v\u2081`, adds an edge to the implication\ngraph `g`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exceptional.to_bool", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "exceptional.to_option", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "exceptional.has_to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "exceptional.return", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "exceptional.to_string", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "exceptional.fail", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "exceptional", "description": " An exceptional is similar to `Result` in Haskell.\n\nRemark: we use a function that produces a format object as the exception information.\nMotivation: the formatting object may be big, and we may create it on demand.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "exceptional.success", "description": " An exceptional is similar to `Result` in Haskell.\n\nRemark: we use a function that produces a format object as the exception information.\nMotivation: the formatting object may be big, and we may create it on demand.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "exceptional.exception", "description": " An exceptional is similar to `Result` in Haskell.\n\nRemark: we use a function that produces a format object as the exception information.\nMotivation: the formatting object may be big, and we may create it on demand.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "exceptional.monad", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "exceptional.bind", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "measurable_space.cardinal_generate_measurable_le_continuum", "description": " If a sigma-algebra is generated by a set of sets `s` with cardinality at most the continuum,\nthen the sigma algebra has the same cardinality bound.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_space.empty_mem_generate_measurable_rec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_space.self_subset_generate_measurable_rec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_space.generate_measurable_rec", "description": " Transfinite induction construction of the sigma-algebra generated by a set of sets `s`. At each\nstep, we add all elements of `s`, the empty set, the complements of already constructed sets, and\ncountable unions of already constructed sets. We index this construction by an ordinal `< \u03c9\u2081`, as\nthis will be enough to generate all sets in the sigma-algebra.\n\nThis construction is very similar to that of the Borel hierarchy.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_space.cardinal_generate_measurable_le", "description": " If a sigma-algebra is generated by a set of sets `s`, then the sigma-algebra has cardinality at\nmost `(max (#s) 2) ^ \u2135\u2080`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_space.cardinal_measurable_set_le", "description": " If a sigma-algebra is generated by a set of sets `s`, then the sigma\nalgebra has cardinality at most `(max (#s) 2) ^ \u2135\u2080`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_space.cardinal_measurable_set_le_continuum", "description": " If a sigma-algebra is generated by a set of sets `s` with cardinality at most the continuum,\nthen the sigma algebra has the same cardinality bound.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_space.generate_measurable_rec_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_space.cardinal_generate_measurable_rec_le", "description": "At each step of the inductive construction, the cardinality bound `\u2264 (max (#s) 2) ^ \u2135\u2080` holds.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_space.generate_measurable_eq_rec", "description": "`generate_measurable_rec s` generates precisely the smallest sigma-algebra containing `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_space.compl_mem_generate_measurable_rec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable_space.Union_mem_generate_measurable_rec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.mul_left", "description": "Left multiplication by a unit of a semiring as an additive automorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.mul_right_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.mul_left_apply_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.mul_left_apply_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.mul_right", "description": "Right multiplication by a unit of a semiring as an additive automorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_aut.mul_right_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.unfold_cases.unfold_cases_core", "description": "Tries to finish the current goal using the `inner` tactic. If the tactic\nfails, it tries to find an expression on which to do a distinction by\ncases and calls itself recursively.\n\nThe order of operations is significant. Because the unfolding can potentially\nbe infinite, it is important to apply the `inner` tactic at every step.\n\nNotice, that if the `inner` tactic succeeds, the recursive unfolding is stopped.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.unfold_cases", "description": "This tactic unfolds the definition of a function or `match` expression.\nThen it recursively introduces a distinction by cases. The decision what expression\nto do the distinction on is driven by the pattern matching expression.\n\nA typical use case is using `unfold_cases { refl }` to collapse cases that need to be\nconsidered in a pattern matching.\n\n```lean\nhave h : foo x = y, by unfold_cases { refl },\nrw h,\n```\n\nThe tactic expects a goal in the form of an equation, possibly universally quantified.\n\nWe can prove a theorem, even if the various case do not directly correspond to the\nfunction definition. Here is an example application of the tactic:\n\n```lean\ndef foo : \u2115 \u2192 \u2115 \u2192 \u2115\n| 0     0 := 17\n| (n+2) 17 := 17\n| 1     0 := 23\n| 0     (n+18) := 15\n| 0     17 := 17\n| 1     17 := 17\n| _     (n+18) := 27\n| _     _ := 15\n\nexample : \u2200 x, foo x 17 = 17 :=\nbegin\n  unfold_cases { refl },\nend\n```\n\nThe compiler generates 57 cases for `foo`. However, when we look at the definition, we see\nthat whenever the function is applied to `17` in the second argument, it returns `17`.\n\nProving this property consists of merely considering all the cases, eliminating invalid ones\nand applying `refl` on the ones which remain.\n\nFurther examples can be found in `test/unfold_cases.lean`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.unfold_cases.find_splitting_expr", "description": "Given an equation `f x = y`, this tactic tries to infer an expression that can be\nused to do distinction by cases on to make progress.\n\nPre-condition: assumes that the outer-most application cannot be beta-reduced\n(e.g. `whnf` or `dsimp`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.unfold_cases.unfold_tgt", "description": "Given a target of the form `\u22a2 f x\u2081 ... x\u2099 = y`, unfolds `f` using a delta reduction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.unfold_cases", "description": "This tactic unfolds the definition of a function or `match` expression.\nThen it recursively introduces a distinction by cases. The decision what expression\nto do the distinction on is driven by the pattern matching expression.\n\nA typical use case is using `unfold_cases { refl }` to collapse cases that need to be\nconsidered in a pattern matching.\n\n```lean\nhave h : foo x = y, by unfold_cases { refl },\nrw h,\n```\n\nThe tactic expects a goal in the form of an equation, possibly universally quantified.\n\nWe can prove a theorem, even if the various case do not directly correspond to the\nfunction definition. Here is an example application of the tactic:\n\n```lean\ndef foo : \u2115 \u2192 \u2115 \u2192 \u2115\n| 0     0 := 17\n| (n+2) 17 := 17\n| 1     0 := 23\n| 0     (n+18) := 15\n| 0     17 := 17\n| 1     17 := 17\n| _     (n+18) := 27\n| _     _ := 15\n\nexample : \u2200 x, foo x 17 = 17 :=\nbegin\n  unfold_cases { refl },\nend\n```\n\nThe compiler generates 57 cases for `foo`. However, when we look at the definition, we see\nthat whenever the function is applied to `17` in the second argument, it returns `17`.\n\nProving this property consists of merely considering all the cases, eliminating invalid ones\nand applying `refl` on the ones which remain.\n\nFurther examples can be found in `test/unfold_cases.lean`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo1959_q1.calculation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1959_q1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "finset.le_card_falling_div_choose", "description": "A bound on any top part of the sum in LYM in terms of the size of `falling k \ud835\udc9c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.falling", "description": "`falling k \ud835\udc9c` is all the finsets of cardinality `k` which are a subset of something in `\ud835\udc9c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sized_falling", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_antichain.disjoint_slice_shadow_falling", "description": " The shadow of `falling m \ud835\udc9c` is disjoint from the `n`-sized elements of `\ud835\udc9c`, thanks to the\nantichain property.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.slice_union_shadow_falling_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.falling_zero_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.card_div_choose_le_card_shadow_div_choose", "description": " The downward **local LYM inequality**. `\ud835\udc9c` takes up less of `\u03b1^(r)` (the finsets of card `r`)\nthan `\u2202\ud835\udc9c` takes up of `\u03b1^(r - 1)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.slice_subset_falling", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_falling", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_antichain.sperner", "description": " **Sperner's theorem**. The size of an antichain in `finset \u03b1` is bounded by the size of the\nmaximal layer in `finset \u03b1`. This precisely means that `finset \u03b1` is a Sperner order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.card_mul_le_card_shadow_mul", "description": " The downward **local LYM inequality**, with cancelled denominators. `\ud835\udc9c` takes up less of `\u03b1^(r)`\n(the finsets of card `r`) than `\u2202\ud835\udc9c` takes up of `\u03b1^(r - 1)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_card_slice_div_choose_le_one", "description": " The **Lubell-Yamamoto-Meshalkin inequality**. If `\ud835\udc9c` is an antichain, then the sum of the\nproportion of elements it takes from each layer is less than `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.colimit_limit_to_limit_colimit_surjective", "description": "This follows this proof from\n* Borceux, Handbook of categorical algebra 1, Theorem 2.13.4\nalthough with different names.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.filtered_colim_preserves_finite_limits_of_types", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.\u03b9_colimit_limit_iso_limit_\u03c0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.colimit_limit_to_limit_colimit_injective", "description": "This follows this proof from\n* Borceux, Handbook of categorical algebra 1, Theorem 2.13.4", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.colimit_limit_to_limit_colimit_cone_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.colimit_limit_iso", "description": "A curried version of the fact that filtered colimits commute with finite limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.colim.preserves_finite_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.filtered_colim_preserves_finite_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.\u03b9_colimit_limit_iso_limit_\u03c0_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.colimit_limit_to_limit_colimit_is_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.star_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.snd_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.has_involutive_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.star_add_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.fst_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.star_module", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "units.embed_product_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.has_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.star_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.has_trivial_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.star_semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complete_space_of_cau_seq_complete", "description": " A complete normed field is complete as a metric space, as Cauchy sequences converge by\nassumption and this suffices to characterize completeness.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cau_seq.tendsto_limit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cau_seq_iff_cauchy_seq", "description": "In a normed field, `cau_seq` coincides with the usual notion of Cauchy sequences.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cauchy_seq.is_cau_seq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cau_seq.cauchy_seq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "show_widget_cmd", "description": " Accepts terms with the type `component tactic_state empty` or `html empty` and\nrenders them interactively.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.app_linear_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.app_linear_map", "description": " Application of a natural transformation at a fixed object,\nas group homomorphism", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.app_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor_category_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semiconj_by.inv_right\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semiconj_by.div_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semiconj_by.inv_symm_left_iff\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semiconj_by.inv_symm_left\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semiconj_by.inv_right_iff\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semiconj_by.zero_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semiconj_by.zero_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.clear'", "description": " Clears all the hypotheses in `hyps`. The tactic fails if any of the `hyps`\nis not a local or if the target depends on any of the `hyps`. It also fails if\n`hyps` contains duplicates.\n\nIf there are local hypotheses or definitions, say `H`, which are not in `hyps`\nbut depend on one of the `hyps`, what we do depends on `clear_dependent`. If it\nis true, `H` is implicitly also cleared. If it is false, `clear'` fails.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.clear_dependent", "description": "A variant of `clear'` which clears not only the given hypotheses, but also any\nother hypotheses depending on them.\n\n```lean\nexample {\u03b1} {\u03b2 : \u03b1 \u2192 Type} (a : \u03b1) (b : \u03b2 a) : unit :=\nbegin\n  try { clear' a },  -- fails since `b` depends on `a`\n  clear_dependent a, -- succeeds, clearing `a` and `b`\n  exact ()\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.clear'", "description": "An improved version of the standard `clear` tactic. `clear` is sensitive to the\norder of its arguments: `clear x y` may fail even though both `x` and `y` could\nbe cleared (if the type of `y` depends on `x`). `clear'` lifts this limitation.\n\n```lean\nexample {\u03b1} {\u03b2 : \u03b1 \u2192 Type} (a : \u03b1) (b : \u03b2 a) : unit :=\nbegin\n  try { clear a b }, -- fails since `b` depends on `a`\n  clear' a b,        -- succeeds\n  exact ()\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.clear'", "description": "An improved version of the standard `clear` tactic. `clear` is sensitive to the\norder of its arguments: `clear x y` may fail even though both `x` and `y` could\nbe cleared (if the type of `y` depends on `x`). `clear'` lifts this limitation.\n\n```lean\nexample {\u03b1} {\u03b2 : \u03b1 \u2192 Type} (a : \u03b1) (b : \u03b2 a) : unit :=\nbegin\n  try { clear a b }, -- fails since `b` depends on `a`\n  clear' a b,        -- succeeds\n  exact ()\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.replacer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.command.def_replacer", "description": "`def_replacer foo` sets up a stub definition `foo : tactic unit`, which can\neffectively be defined and re-defined later, by tagging definitions with `@[foo]`.\n\n- `@[foo] meta def foo_1 : tactic unit := ...` replaces the current definition of `foo`.\n- `@[foo] meta def foo_2 (old : tactic unit) : tactic unit := ...` replaces the current\n  definition of `foo`, and provides access to the previous definition via `old`.\n  (The argument can also be an `option (tactic unit)`, which is provided as `none` if\n  this is the first definition tagged with `@[foo]` since `def_replacer` was invoked.)\n\n`def_replacer foo : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2 \u2192 tactic \u03b3` allows the specification of a replacer with\ncustom input and output types. In this case all subsequent redefinitions must have the\nsame type, or the type `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2 \u2192 tactic \u03b3 \u2192 tactic \u03b3` or\n`\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2 \u2192 option (tactic \u03b3) \u2192 tactic \u03b3` analogously to the previous cases.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.unprime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.def_replacer_cmd", "description": "`def_replacer foo` sets up a stub definition `foo : tactic unit`, which can\neffectively be defined and re-defined later, by tagging definitions with `@[foo]`.\n\n- `@[foo] meta def foo_1 : tactic unit := ...` replaces the current definition of `foo`.\n- `@[foo] meta def foo_2 (old : tactic unit) : tactic unit := ...` replaces the current\n  definition of `foo`, and provides access to the previous definition via `old`.\n  (The argument can also be an `option (tactic unit)`, which is provided as `none` if\n  this is the first definition tagged with `@[foo]` since `def_replacer` was invoked.)\n\n`def_replacer foo : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2 \u2192 tactic \u03b3` allows the specification of a replacer with\ncustom input and output types. In this case all subsequent redefinitions must have the\nsame type, or the type `\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2 \u2192 tactic \u03b3 \u2192 tactic \u03b3` or\n`\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2 \u2192 option (tactic \u03b3) \u2192 tactic \u03b3` analogously to the previous cases.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_replacer\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.def_replacer", "description": "Define a new replaceable tactic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_replacer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.replacer_core", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.replaceable_attr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_replacer\u2081", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.replacer_attr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.valid_types", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subbimodule.to_subbimodule_int", "description": " A `submodule` over `R \u2297[\u2115] S` is naturally also a `submodule` over the canonically-isomorphic\nring `R \u2297[\u2124] S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subbimodule.to_submodule'", "description": "Forgetting the `A` action, a `submodule` over `A \u2297[R] B` is just a `submodule` over `B`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subbimodule.mk", "description": " A constructor for a subbimodule which demands closure under the two sets of scalars\nindividually, rather than jointly via their tensor product.\n\nNote that `R` plays no role but it is convenient to make this generalisation to support the cases\n`R = \u2115` and `R = \u2124` which both show up naturally. See also `base_change`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subbimodule.to_subbimodule_nat_carrier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subbimodule.to_submodule_carrier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subbimodule.base_change_carrier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subbimodule.base_change", "description": " If `A` and `B` are also `algebra`s over yet another set of scalars `S` then we may \"base change\"\nfrom `R` to `S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subbimodule.to_submodule", "description": "Forgetting the `B` action, a `submodule` over `A \u2297[R] B` is just a `submodule` over `A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subbimodule.smul_mem'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subbimodule.mk_carrier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subbimodule.to_subbimodule_nat", "description": " A `submodule` over `R \u2297[\u2124] S` is naturally also a `submodule` over the canonically-isomorphic\nring `R \u2297[\u2115] S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subbimodule.smul_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subbimodule.to_submodule'_carrier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subbimodule.to_subbimodule_int_carrier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_distributor_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sum_tensor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_left_additive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_preadditive.tensor_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensoring_right_additive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensor_right_additive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_distributor", "description": "The isomorphism showing how tensor product on the left distributes over direct sums.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_distributor_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_distributor_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_preadditive", "description": "A category is `monoidal_preadditive` if tensoring is additive in both factors.\n\nNote we don't `extend preadditive C` here, as `abelian C` already extends it,\nand we'll need to have both typeclasses sometimes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_preadditive.tensor_zero'", "description": "A category is `monoidal_preadditive` if tensoring is additive in both factors.\n\nNote we don't `extend preadditive C` here, as `abelian C` already extends it,\nand we'll need to have both typeclasses sometimes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_preadditive.zero_tensor'", "description": "A category is `monoidal_preadditive` if tensoring is additive in both factors.\n\nNote we don't `extend preadditive C` here, as `abelian C` already extends it,\nand we'll need to have both typeclasses sometimes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_preadditive.tensor_add'", "description": "A category is `monoidal_preadditive` if tensoring is additive in both factors.\n\nNote we don't `extend preadditive C` here, as `abelian C` already extends it,\nand we'll need to have both typeclasses sometimes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_preadditive.add_tensor'", "description": "A category is `monoidal_preadditive` if tensoring is additive in both factors.\n\nNote we don't `extend preadditive C` here, as `abelian C` already extends it,\nand we'll need to have both typeclasses sometimes.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_distributor_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_preadditive_of_faithful", "description": " A faithful additive monoidal functor to a monoidal preadditive category\nensures that the domain is monoidal preadditive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_left.limits.preserves_finite_biproducts", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_distributor_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_preadditive.add_tensor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.right_distributor", "description": "The isomorphism showing how tensor product on the right distributes over direct sums.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.tensoring_left_additive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_distributor_right_distributor_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_preadditive.tensor_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.left_distributor_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_category.tensor_right.limits.preserves_finite_biproducts", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal_preadditive.zero_tensor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "interval_integral.has_deriv_at_integral_of_dominated_loc_of_lip", "description": " Derivative under integral of `x \u21a6 \u222b F x a` at a given point `x\u2080 : \ud835\udd5c`, `\ud835\udd5c = \u211d` or `\ud835\udd5c = \u2102`,\nassuming `F x\u2080` is integrable, `x \u21a6 F x a` is locally Lipschitz on a ball around `x\u2080` for ae `a`\n(with ball radius independent of `a`) with integrable Lipschitz bound, and `F x` is\nae-measurable for `x` in a possibly smaller neighborhood of `x\u2080`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "interval_integral.has_fderiv_at_integral_of_dominated_of_fderiv_le", "description": " Differentiation under integral of `x \u21a6 \u222b F x a` at a given point `x\u2080`, assuming\n`F x\u2080` is integrable, `x \u21a6 F x a` is differentiable on a ball around `x\u2080` for ae `a` with\nderivative norm uniformly bounded by an integrable function (the ball radius is independent of `a`),\nand `F x` is ae-measurable for `x` in a possibly smaller neighborhood of `x\u2080`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "interval_integral.has_fderiv_at_integral_of_dominated_loc_of_lip", "description": " Differentiation under integral of `x \u21a6 \u222b t in a..b, F x t` at a given point `x\u2080`, assuming\n`F x\u2080` is integrable, `x \u21a6 F x a` is locally Lipschitz on a ball around `x\u2080` for ae `a`\n(with a ball radius independent of `a`) with integrable Lipschitz bound, and `F x` is ae-measurable\nfor `x` in a possibly smaller neighborhood of `x\u2080`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "interval_integral.has_deriv_at_integral_of_dominated_loc_of_deriv_le", "description": " Derivative under integral of `x \u21a6 \u222b F x a` at a given point `x\u2080 : \ud835\udd5c`, `\ud835\udd5c = \u211d` or `\ud835\udd5c = \u2102`,\nassuming `F x\u2080` is integrable, `x \u21a6 F x a` is differentiable on an interval around `x\u2080` for ae `a`\n(with interval radius independent of `a`) with derivative uniformly bounded by an integrable\nfunction, and `F x` is ae-measurable for `x` in a possibly smaller neighborhood of `x\u2080`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.forget.category_theory.is_right_adjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_one", "description": "The functor of adjoining a neutral element `one` to a semigroup.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_one_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_forget_to_Semigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free", "description": "The free functor `Type u \u2964 Mon` sending a type `X` to the free monoid on `X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_zero_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_forget_to_AddSemigroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_zero_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_zero_adj", "description": "The adjoin_one-forgetful adjunction from `AddSemigroup` to `AddMon`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_zero", "description": "The functor of adjoining a neutral element `zero` to a semigroup", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adj", "description": "The free-forgetful adjunction for monoids.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_one_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_one_adj", "description": "The adjoin_one-forgetful adjunction from `Semigroup` to `Mon`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.int_fract_pair.stream_succ", "description": "A recurrence relation that expresses the `(n+1)`th term of the stream of `int_fract_pair`s\nof `v` for non-integer `v` in terms of the `n`th term of the stream associated to\nthe inverse of the fractional part of `v`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.int_fract_pair.nth_seq1_eq_succ_nth_stream", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.convergents'_succ", "description": "The recurrence relation for the `convergents'` of the continued fraction expansion\nof an element `v` of `K` in terms of the convergents of the inverse of its fractional part.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.int_fract_pair.exists_succ_nth_stream_of_gcf_of_nth_eq_some", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.of_s_tail", "description": "This expresses the tail of the coefficient sequence of the `generalized_continued_fraction.of`\nan element `v` of `K` as the coefficient sequence of that of the inverse of the\nfractional part of `v`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.of_terminated_at_iff_int_fract_pair_seq1_terminated_at", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.of_h_eq_int_fract_pair_seq1_fst_b", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.int_fract_pair.stream_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.of_terminated_at_n_iff_succ_nth_int_fract_pair_stream_eq_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.int_fract_pair.stream_eq_none_of_fr_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.of_h_eq_floor", "description": "The head term of the gcf of `v` is `\u230av\u230b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.nth_of_eq_some_of_nth_int_fract_pair_stream_fr_ne_zero", "description": "Shows how the entries of the sequence of the computed continued fraction can be obtained by the\nfractional parts of the stream of integer and fractional parts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.int_fract_pair.succ_nth_stream_eq_none_iff", "description": "Gives a recurrence to compute the `n + 1`th value of the sequence of integer and fractional\nparts of a value in case of termination.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.nth_of_eq_some_of_succ_nth_int_fract_pair_stream", "description": "Shows how the entries of the sequence of the computed continued fraction can be obtained by the\ninteger parts of the stream of integer and fractional parts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.of_s_succ", "description": "Recurrence for the `generalized_continued_fraction.of` an element `v` of `K` in terms of\nthat of the inverse of the fractional part of `v`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.int_fract_pair.stream_succ_of_some", "description": "An easier to use version of one direction of\n`generalized_continued_fraction.int_fract_pair.succ_nth_stream_eq_some_iff`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.convergents'_of_int", "description": "If `a` is an integer, then the `convergents'` of its continued fraction expansion\nare all equal to `a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.int_fract_pair.seq1_fst_eq_of", "description": "The head term of the sequence with head of `v` is just the integer part of `v`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.of_s_head", "description": "This gives the first pair of coefficients of the continued fraction of a non-integer `v`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.of_s_of_int", "description": "If `a` is an integer, then the coefficient sequence of its continued fraction is empty.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.of_s_head_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.int_fract_pair.succ_nth_stream_eq_some_iff", "description": "Gives a recurrence to compute the `n + 1`th value of the sequence of integer and fractional\nparts of a value in case of non-termination.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.int_fract_pair.exists_succ_nth_stream_of_fr_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.int_fract_pair.stream_succ_of_int", "description": "The stream of `int_fract_pair`s of an integer stops after the first term.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.kernel.bind_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.kernel.invariant.def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.kernel.bind_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.kernel.const_bind_eq_comp_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.kernel.comp_const_apply_eq_bind", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.kernel.invariant.comp_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.kernel.invariant", "description": " A measure `\u03bc` is invariant with respect to the kernel `\u03ba` if the push-forward measure of `\u03bc`\nalong `\u03ba` equals `\u03bc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.kernel.invariant.comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.comm_sq.left_adjoint_lift_struct_equiv", "description": " The liftings of a commutative are in bijection with the liftings of its (left)\nadjoint square.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.comm_sq.left_adjoint_has_lift_iff", "description": "A (left) adjoint square has a lifting if and only if the original square has a lifting.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.comm_sq.right_adjoint_lift_struct_equiv", "description": " The liftings of a commutative are in bijection with the liftings of its (right)\nadjoint square.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.comm_sq.right_adjoint.has_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adjunction.has_lifting_property_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.comm_sq.right_adjoint", "description": " When we have an adjunction `G \u22a3 F`, any commutative square where the left\nmap is of the form `G.map i` and the right map is `p` has an \"adjoint\" commutative\nsquare whose left map is `i` and whose right map is `F.map p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.comm_sq.left_adjoint", "description": " When we have an adjunction `G \u22a3 F`, any commutative square where the left\nmap is of the form `i` and the right map is `F.map p` has an \"adjoint\" commutative\nsquare whose left map is `G.map i` and whose right map is `p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.comm_sq.right_adjoint_has_lift_iff", "description": "A square has a lifting if and only if its (right) adjoint square has a lifting.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.comm_sq.left_adjoint.has_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.projective_def'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.projective_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.projective_of_lifting_property'", "description": " A module which satisfies the universal property is projective. Note that the universe variables\nin `huniv` are somewhat restricted.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.prod.projective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.projective_of_lifting_property", "description": " A variant of `of_lifting_property'` when we're working over a `[ring R]`,\nwhich only requires quantifying over modules with an `add_comm_group` instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.projective_lifting_property", "description": "A projective R-module has the property that maps from it lift along surjections.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.projective", "description": "An R-module is projective if it is a direct summand of a free module, or equivalently\n if maps from the module lift along surjections. There are several other equivalent\n definitions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.projective.out", "description": "An R-module is projective if it is a direct summand of a free module, or equivalently\n if maps from the module lift along surjections. There are several other equivalent\n definitions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.dfinsupp.projective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.projective_of_free", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.projective_of_basis", "description": "Free modules are projective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.of", "description": "Interpret `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` with `is_semiring_hom f` as a ring homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_semiring_hom.to_is_add_monoid_hom", "description": "A semiring homomorphism is an additive monoid homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_ring_hom.to_is_semiring_hom", "description": "A ring homomorphism is also a semiring homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_ring_hom.id", "description": "The identity map is a ring homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_ring_hom", "description": "Predicate for ring homomorphisms (deprecated -- use the bundled `ring_hom` version).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_ring_hom.map_one", "description": "Predicate for ring homomorphisms (deprecated -- use the bundled `ring_hom` version).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_ring_hom.map_mul", "description": "Predicate for ring homomorphisms (deprecated -- use the bundled `ring_hom` version).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_ring_hom.map_add", "description": "Predicate for ring homomorphisms (deprecated -- use the bundled `ring_hom` version).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_semiring_hom", "description": "Predicate for semiring homomorphisms (deprecated -- use the bundled `ring_hom` version).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_semiring_hom.map_zero", "description": "Predicate for semiring homomorphisms (deprecated -- use the bundled `ring_hom` version).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_semiring_hom.map_one", "description": "Predicate for semiring homomorphisms (deprecated -- use the bundled `ring_hom` version).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_semiring_hom.map_add", "description": "Predicate for semiring homomorphisms (deprecated -- use the bundled `ring_hom` version).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_semiring_hom.map_mul", "description": "Predicate for semiring homomorphisms (deprecated -- use the bundled `ring_hom` version).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.to_is_ring_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_semiring_hom.comp", "description": "The composition of two semiring homomorphisms is a semiring homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.to_is_semiring_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_ring_hom.comp", "description": "The composition of two ring homomorphisms is a ring homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_ring_hom.of_semiring", "description": "A map of rings that is a semiring homomorphism is also a ring homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_semiring_hom.to_is_monoid_hom", "description": "A semiring homomorphism is a monoid homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.coe_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_semiring_hom.id", "description": "The identity map is a semiring homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_ring_hom.to_is_add_group_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_ring_hom.map_zero", "description": "Ring homomorphisms map zero to zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_ring_hom.map_neg", "description": "Ring homomorphisms preserve additive inverses.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_ring_hom.map_sub", "description": "Ring homomorphisms preserve subtraction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_tsum_of_eventually", "description": " Consider a series of functions `\u2211' i, f i x`. Assume that each individual function `f i` is of\nclass `C^N`, and moreover there is a uniform summable upper bound on the `k`-th derivative\nfor each `k \u2264 N` (except maybe for finitely many `i`s). Then the series is also `C^N`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "iterated_fderiv_tsum_apply", "description": " Consider a series of smooth functions, with summable uniform bounds on the successive\nderivatives. Then the iterated derivative of the sum is the sum of the iterated derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_tsum_of_is_preconnected", "description": " Consider a series of functions `\u2211' n, f n x` on a preconnected open set. If the series converges\nat a point, and all functions in the series are differentiable with a summable bound on the\nderivatives, then the series is differentiable on the set and its derivative is the sum of the\nderivatives.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable_tsum", "description": " Consider a series of functions `\u2211' n, f n x`. If all functions in the series are differentiable\nwith a summable bound on the derivatives, then the series is differentiable.\nNote that our assumptions do not ensure the pointwise convergence, but if there is no pointwise\nconvergence then the series is zero everywhere so the result still holds.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_tsum_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_tsum", "description": " Consider a series of functions `\u2211' n, f n x`. If the series converges at a\npoint, and all functions in the series are differentiable with a summable bound on the derivatives,\nthen the series is differentiable and its derivative is the sum of the derivatives.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_uniformly_on_tsum_nat", "description": " An infinite sum of functions with summable sup norm is the uniform limit of its partial sums.\nVersion relative to a set, with index set `\u2115`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on_tsum", "description": " An infinite sum of functions with summable sup norm is continuous on a set if each individual\nfunction is.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_uniformly_on_tsum", "description": " An infinite sum of functions with summable sup norm is the uniform limit of its partial sums.\nVersion relative to a set, with general index set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "summable_of_summable_has_fderiv_at_of_is_preconnected", "description": " Consider a series of functions `\u2211' n, f n x` on a preconnected open set. If the series converges\nat a point, and all functions in the series are differentiable with a summable bound on the\nderivatives, then the series converges everywhere on the set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "iterated_fderiv_tsum", "description": " Consider a series of smooth functions, with summable uniform bounds on the successive\nderivatives. Then the iterated derivative of the sum is the sum of the iterated derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_uniformly_tsum_nat", "description": " An infinite sum of functions with summable sup norm is the uniform limit of its partial sums.\nVersion with index set `\u2115`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fderiv_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_tsum", "description": " An infinite sum of functions with summable sup norm is continuous if each individual\nfunction is.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_diff_tsum", "description": " Consider a series of functions `\u2211' i, f i x`. Assume that each individual function `f i` is of\nclass `C^N`, and moreover there is a uniform summable upper bound on the `k`-th derivative\nfor each `k \u2264 N`. Then the series is also `C^N`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "summable_of_summable_has_fderiv_at", "description": " Consider a series of functions `\u2211' n, f n x`. If the series converges at a\npoint, and all functions in the series are differentiable with a summable bound on the derivatives,\nthen the series converges everywhere.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_uniformly_tsum", "description": " An infinite sum of functions with summable sup norm is the uniform limit of its partial sums.\nVersion with general index set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simply_connected_space", "description": "A simply connected space is one whose fundamental groupoid is equivalent to `discrete unit`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simply_connected_space.equiv_unit", "description": "A simply connected space is one whose fundamental groupoid is equivalent to `discrete unit`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simply_connected_space.path_connected_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simply_connected_iff_unique_homotopic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simply_connected_iff_paths_homotopic'", "description": "Another version of `simply_connected_iff_paths_homotopic`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simply_connected_iff_paths_homotopic", "description": "A space is simply connected iff it is path connected, and there is at most one path\n up to homotopy between any two points.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simply_connected_space.of_contractible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simply_connected_space.paths_homotopic", "description": "In a simply connected space, any two paths are homotopic", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simply_connected_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simply_connected_space.path.homotopic.quotient.subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.bot_strongly_regular", "description": "Empty graphs are strongly regular. Note that `\u2113` can take any value\n for empty graphs, since there are no pairs of adjacent vertices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_SRG_with.card_neighbor_finset_union_of_not_adj", "description": "Assuming `G` is strongly regular, `2*(k + 1) - m` in `G` is the number of vertices that are\n adjacent to either `v` or `w` when `\u00acG.adj v w`. So it's the cardinality of\n `G.neighbor_set v \u222a G.neighbor_set w`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.compl_neighbor_finset_sdiff_inter_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_SRG_with.top", "description": "Complete graphs are strongly regular. Note that `\u03bc` can take any value\n for complete graphs, since there are no distinct pairs of non-adjacent vertices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_SRG_with.compl", "description": "The complement of a strongly regular graph is strongly regular.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_SRG_with.card_common_neighbors_eq_of_adj_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_SRG_with", "description": "A graph is strongly regular with parameters `n k \u2113 \u03bc` if\n * its vertex set has cardinality `n`\n * it is regular with degree `k`\n * every pair of adjacent vertices has `\u2113` common neighbors\n * every pair of nonadjacent vertices has `\u03bc` common neighbors", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_SRG_with.card", "description": "A graph is strongly regular with parameters `n k \u2113 \u03bc` if\n * its vertex set has cardinality `n`\n * it is regular with degree `k`\n * every pair of adjacent vertices has `\u2113` common neighbors\n * every pair of nonadjacent vertices has `\u03bc` common neighbors", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_SRG_with.regular", "description": "A graph is strongly regular with parameters `n k \u2113 \u03bc` if\n * its vertex set has cardinality `n`\n * it is regular with degree `k`\n * every pair of adjacent vertices has `\u2113` common neighbors\n * every pair of nonadjacent vertices has `\u03bc` common neighbors", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_SRG_with.of_adj", "description": "A graph is strongly regular with parameters `n k \u2113 \u03bc` if\n * its vertex set has cardinality `n`\n * it is regular with degree `k`\n * every pair of adjacent vertices has `\u2113` common neighbors\n * every pair of nonadjacent vertices has `\u03bc` common neighbors", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_SRG_with.of_not_adj", "description": "A graph is strongly regular with parameters `n k \u2113 \u03bc` if\n * its vertex set has cardinality `n`\n * it is regular with degree `k`\n * every pair of adjacent vertices has `\u2113` common neighbors\n * every pair of nonadjacent vertices has `\u03bc` common neighbors", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.sdiff_compl_neighbor_finset_inter_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_SRG_with.card_common_neighbors_eq_of_not_adj_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_SRG_with.card_neighbor_finset_union_of_adj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_SRG_with.compl_is_regular", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_SRG_with.card_neighbor_finset_union_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.measurable_id''", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable.norm_integral_condexp_kernel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.measurable_condexp_kernel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable.comp_snd_map_prod_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.condexp_kernel", "description": " Kernel associated with the conditional expectation with respect to a \u03c3-algebra. It satisfies\n`\u03bc[f | m] =\u1d50[\u03bc] \u03bb \u03c9, \u222b y, f y \u2202(condexp_kernel \u03bc m \u03c9)`.\nIt is defined as the conditional distribution of the identity given the identity, where the second\nidentity is understood as a map from `\u03a9` with the \u03c3-algebra `m\u03a9` to `\u03a9` with \u03c3-algebra `m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.ae_measurable_id''", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.integrable_to_real_condexp_kernel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable.integral_condexp_kernel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.condexp_ae_eq_integral_condexp_kernel", "description": " The conditional expectation of `f` with respect to a \u03c3-algebra `m` is almost everywhere equal to\nthe integral `\u222b y, f y \u2202(condexp_kernel \u03bc m \u03c9)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable.integral_condexp_kernel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable.integral_norm_condexp_kernel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.ae_strongly_measurable'_integral_condexp_kernel", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable.comp_snd_map_prod_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.integrable.condexp_kernel_ae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_succ_eq_count_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_set.fintype", "description": "A fintype instance for the set relevant to `nat.count`. Locally an instance in locale `count`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_lt_count_succ_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_succ_eq_succ_count", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `nat.count_succ_eq_succ_count_iff`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_succ'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_succ_eq_succ_count_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_eq_card_fintype", "description": "`count p n` can be expressed as the cardinality of `{k // k < n \u2227 p k}`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_le_cardinal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_succ_eq_count", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `nat.count_succ_eq_count_iff`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_eq_card_filter_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.lt_of_count_lt_count", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_le_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_monotone", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_strict_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_lt_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_mono_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count_add'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.count", "description": "Count the number of naturals `k < n` satisfying `p k`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.inner_map_self_eq_zero", "description": " A symmetric linear map `T` is zero if and only if `\u27eaT x, x\u27eb_\u211d = 0` for all `x`.\nSee `inner_map_self_eq_zero` for the complex version without the symmetric assumption.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.apply_clm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.conj_inner_sym", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.restrict_invariant", "description": " If a symmetric operator preserves a submodule, its restriction to that submodule is\nsymmetric.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric_iff_sesq_form", "description": " An operator `T` on an inner product space is symmetric if and only if it is\n`linear_map.is_self_adjoint` with respect to the sesquilinear form given by the inner product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric_iff_inner_map_self_real", "description": " A linear operator on a complex inner product space is symmetric precisely when\n`\u27eaT v, v\u27eb_\u2102` is real for all v.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.coe_re_apply_inner_self_apply", "description": "For a symmetric operator `T`, the function `\u03bb x, \u27eaT x, x\u27eb` is real-valued.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.continuous", "description": "The **Hellinger--Toeplitz theorem**: if a symmetric operator is defined on a complete space,\n then it is automatically continuous.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.restrict_scalars", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.inner_map_polarization", "description": " Polarization identity for symmetric linear maps.\nSee `inner_map_polarization` for the complex version without the symmetric assumption.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric", "description": " A (not necessarily bounded) operator on an inner product space is symmetric, if for all\n`x`, `y`, we have `\u27eaT x, y\u27eb = \u27eax, T y\u27eb`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_erase_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.prod_insert_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_insert_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sum_erase_none", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.sum_repr_mul_repr_mul", "description": " Write out `B x y` as a sum over `B (b i) (b j)` if `b` is a basis.\n\nVersion for bilinear maps, see `sum_repr_mul_repr_mul\u209b\u2097` for the semi-bilinear version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.ext_basis", "description": "Two bilinear maps are equal when they are equal on all basis vectors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.sum_repr_mul_repr_mul\u209b\u2097", "description": " Write out `B x y` as a sum over `B (b i) (b j)` if `b` is a basis.\n\nVersion for semi-bilinear maps, see `sum_repr_mul_repr_mul` for the bilinear version.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.subgroup_of_mul_action", "description": " **Cayley's theorem**: Every group G is isomorphic to a subgroup of the symmetric group acting on\n`G`. Note that we generalize this to an arbitrary \"faithful\" group action by `G`. Setting `H = G`\nrecovers the usual statement of Cayley's theorem via `right_cancel_monoid.to_has_faithful_smul`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.subtype_congr_hom.decidable_mem_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.sum_congr_hom.decidable_mem_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.subtype_congr_hom.card_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.sigma_congr_right_hom.decidable_mem_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.sigma_congr_right_hom.card_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv.perm.sum_congr_hom.card_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.decidable_le_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.mem_range_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.decidable_lt_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.decidable_le_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.decidable_lt_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.range", "description": "List enumerating `[m, n)`. This is the \u2124 variant of `list.Ico`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.frobenius_rotation_nonzero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.frobenius_rotation", "description": "For nonzero `a\u2081` and `a\u2082`, `frobenius_rotation a\u2081 a\u2082` is a Witt vector that satisfies the\nequation `frobenius (frobenius_rotation a\u2081 a\u2082) * a\u2081 = (frobenius_rotation a\u2081 a\u2082) * a\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.frobenius_frobenius_rotation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.recursion_main.succ_nth_defining_poly", "description": "The root of this polynomial determines the `n+1`st coefficient of our solution.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.recursion_main.succ_nth_defining_poly_degree", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.recursion_main.root_exists", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.recursion_base.solution_spec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.frobenius_rotation_coeff", "description": "Recursively defines the sequence of coefficients for `witt_vector.frobenius_rotation`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.recursion_main.succ_nth_val_spec'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.exists_frobenius_solution_fraction_ring_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.recursion_base.solution", "description": "The base case (0th coefficient) of our solution vector.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.recursion_base.solution_nonzero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.recursion_main.succ_nth_val_spec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.recursion_base.solution_spec'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.recursion_main.succ_nth_val", "description": "This is the `n+1`st coefficient of our solution, projected from `root_exists`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.recursion_base.solution_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.exists_frobenius_solution_fraction_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.partition.to_coloring'", "description": "Like `simple_graph.partition.to_coloring` but uses `set V` as the coloring type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.coloring.to_partition", "description": "Creates a partition from a coloring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.coloring.to_partition_parts", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.partition.to_colorable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.partition.inhabited_default", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.partitionable_iff_colorable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.partition.part_of_vertex_ne_of_adj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.partition.to_coloring", "description": " Create a coloring using the parts themselves as the colors.\nEach vertex is colored by the part it's contained in.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.partitionable", "description": " Whether a graph is `n`-partite, which is whether its vertex set\ncan be partitioned in at most `n` independent sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.partition.mem_part_of_vertex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.partition.part_of_vertex", "description": "The part in the partition that `v` belongs to", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.partition.part_of_vertex_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.partition", "description": "A `partition` of a simple graph `G` is a structure constituted by\n* `parts`: a set of subsets of the vertices `V` of `G`\n* `is_partition`: a proof that `parts` is a proper partition of `V`\n* `independent`: a proof that each element of `parts` doesn't have a pair of adjacent vertices", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.partition.parts", "description": "A `partition` of a simple graph `G` is a structure constituted by\n* `parts`: a set of subsets of the vertices `V` of `G`\n* `is_partition`: a proof that `parts` is a proper partition of `V`\n* `independent`: a proof that each element of `parts` doesn't have a pair of adjacent vertices", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.partition.is_partition", "description": "A `partition` of a simple graph `G` is a structure constituted by\n* `parts`: a set of subsets of the vertices `V` of `G`\n* `is_partition`: a proof that `parts` is a proper partition of `V`\n* `independent`: a proof that each element of `parts` doesn't have a pair of adjacent vertices", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.partition.independent", "description": "A `partition` of a simple graph `G` is a structure constituted by\n* `parts`: a set of subsets of the vertices `V` of `G`\n* `is_partition`: a proof that `parts` is a proper partition of `V`\n* `independent`: a proof that each element of `parts` doesn't have a pair of adjacent vertices", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.partition.inhabited", "description": "The partition where every vertex is in its own part.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.partition.parts_card_le", "description": " Whether a partition `P` has at most `n` parts. A graph with a partition\nsatisfying this predicate called `n`-partite. (See `simple_graph.partitionable`.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.is_simple_order.univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bool.is_simple_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite.to_is_coatomic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_simple_order.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite.to_is_atomic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.is_simple_order.card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bundle.pullback.module", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "trivialization.prod_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bundle.trivial.trivialization.coord_changeL", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector_bundle.prod", "description": "The product of two vector bundles is a vector bundle.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "trivialization.prod.is_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bundle.pullback.add_comm_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bundle.trivial.trivialization.is_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "trivialization.continuous_linear_equiv_at_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "trivialization.pullback_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector_bundle.pullback", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bundle.trivial.vector_bundle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "trivialization.coord_changeL_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.eventually_curry_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.curry_le_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.curry", "description": " This filter is characterized by `filter.eventually_curry_iff`:\n`(\u2200\u1da0 (x : \u03b1 \u00d7 \u03b2) in f.curry g, p x) \u2194 \u2200\u1da0 (x : \u03b1) in f, \u2200\u1da0 (y : \u03b2) in g, p (x, y)`. Useful\nin adding quantifiers to the middle of `tendsto`s. See\n`has_fderiv_at_of_tendsto_uniformly_on_filter`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.curry", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.command.reassoc_axiom", "description": "When declaring a class of categories, the axioms can be reformulated to be more amenable\nto manipulation in right associated expressions:\n\n```lean\nclass some_class (C : Type) [category C] :=\n(foo : \u03a0 X : C, X \u27f6 X)\n(bar : \u2200 {X Y : C} (f : X \u27f6 Y), foo X \u226b f = f \u226b foo Y)\n\nreassoc_axiom some_class.bar\n```\n\nThe above will produce:\n\n```lean\nlemma some_class.bar_assoc {Z : C} (g : Y \u27f6 Z) :\n  foo X \u226b f \u226b g = f \u226b foo Y \u226b g := ...\n```\n\nHere too, the `reassoc` attribute can be used instead. It works well when combined with\n`simp`:\n\n```lean\nattribute [simp, reassoc] some_class.bar\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.reassoc", "description": " `reassoc h`, for assumption `h : x \u226b y = z`, creates a new assumption\n`h : \u2200 {W} (f : Z \u27f6 W), x \u226b y \u226b f = z \u226b f`.\n`reassoc! h`, does the same but deletes the initial `h` assumption.\n(You can also add the attribute `@[reassoc]` to lemmas to generate new declarations generalized\nin this way.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.reassoc_of", "description": " With `h : x \u226b y \u226b z = x` (with universal quantifiers tolerated),\n`reassoc_of h : \u2200 {X'} (f : W \u27f6 X'), x \u226b y \u226b z \u226b f = x \u226b f`.\n\nThe type and proof of `reassoc_of h` is generated by `tactic.derive_reassoc_proof`\nwhich make `reassoc_of` meta-programming adjacent. It is not called as a tactic but as\nan expression. The goal is to avoid creating assumptions that are dismissed after one use:\n\n```lean\nexample (X Y Z W : C) (x : X \u27f6 Y) (y : Y \u27f6 Z) (z z' : Z \u27f6 W) (w : X \u27f6 Z)\n  (h : x \u226b y = w)\n  (h' : y \u226b z = y \u226b z') :\n  x \u226b y \u226b z = w \u226b z' :=\nbegin\n  rw [h',reassoc_of h],\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.get_cat_inst", "description": "From an expression `f \u226b g`, extract the expression representing the category instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.category_theory.reassoc_of", "description": "`reassoc_of h` takes local assumption `h` and add a ` \u226b f` term on the right of\nboth sides of the equality. Instead of creating a new assumption from the result, `reassoc_of h`\nstands for the proof of that reassociated statement. This keeps complicated assumptions that are\nused only once or twice from polluting the local context.\n\nIn the following, assumption `h` is needed in a reassociated form. Instead of proving it as a new\ngoal and adding it as an assumption, we use `reassoc_of h` as a rewrite rule which works just as\nwell.\n\n```lean\nexample (X Y Z W : C) (x : X \u27f6 Y) (y : Y \u27f6 Z) (z z' : Z \u27f6 W) (w : X \u27f6 Z)\n  (h : x \u226b y = w)\n  (h' : y \u226b z = y \u226b z') :\n  x \u226b y \u226b z = w \u226b z' :=\nbegin\n  -- reassoc_of h : \u2200 {X' : C} (f : W \u27f6 X'), x \u226b y \u226b f = w \u226b f\n  rw [h',reassoc_of h],\nend\n```\n\nAlthough `reassoc_of` is not a tactic or a meta program, its type is generated\nthrough meta-programming to make it usable inside normal expressions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.calculated_Prop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.reassoc_attr", "description": "The `reassoc` attribute can be applied to a lemma\n\n```lean\n@[reassoc]\nlemma some_lemma : foo \u226b bar = baz := ...\n```\n\nto produce\n\n```lean\nlemma some_lemma_assoc {Y : C} (f : X \u27f6 Y) : foo \u226b bar \u226b f = baz \u226b f := ...\n```\n\nThe name of the produced lemma can be specified with `@[reassoc other_lemma_name]`. If\n`simp` is added first, the generated lemma will also have the `simp` attribute.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.prove_reassoc", "description": " (internals for `@[reassoc]`)\nGiven a lemma of the form `\u2200 ..., f \u226b g = h`, proves a new lemma of the form\n`h : \u2200 ... {W} (k), f \u226b (g \u226b k) = h \u226b k`, and returns the type and proof of this lemma.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.derive_reassoc_proof", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.reassoc_axiom", "description": " (implementation for `@[reassoc]`)\nGiven a declaration named `n` of the form `\u2200 ..., f \u226b g = h`, proves a new lemma named `n'`\nof the form `\u2200 ... {W} (k), f \u226b (g \u226b k) = h \u226b k`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.reassoc_cmd", "description": "When declaring a class of categories, the axioms can be reformulated to be more amenable\nto manipulation in right associated expressions:\n\n```lean\nclass some_class (C : Type) [category C] :=\n(foo : \u03a0 X : C, X \u27f6 X)\n(bar : \u2200 {X Y : C} (f : X \u27f6 Y), foo X \u226b f = f \u226b foo Y)\n\nreassoc_axiom some_class.bar\n```\n\nThe above will produce:\n\n```lean\nlemma some_class.bar_assoc {Z : C} (g : Y \u27f6 Z) :\n  foo X \u226b f \u226b g = f \u226b foo Y \u226b g := ...\n```\n\nHere too, the `reassoc` attribute can be used instead. It works well when combined with\n`simp`:\n\n```lean\nattribute [simp, reassoc] some_class.bar\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.attribute.reassoc", "description": "The `reassoc` attribute can be applied to a lemma\n\n```lean\n@[reassoc]\nlemma some_lemma : foo \u226b bar = baz := ...\n```\n\nto produce\n\n```lean\nlemma some_lemma_assoc {Y : C} (f : X \u27f6 Y) : foo \u226b bar \u226b f = baz \u226b f := ...\n```\n\nThe name of the produced lemma can be specified with `@[reassoc other_lemma_name]`. If\n`simp` is added first, the generated lemma will also have the `simp` attribute.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.mem_sections_length", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.mem_sections", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.rel_sections", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.grades_by.decompose_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.single_mem_grade", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.grade.is_internal", "description": "`add_monoid_algebra.grades` describe an internally graded algebra", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.grade_by_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.decompose_aux_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.decompose_aux_eq_decompose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.grade_by.is_internal", "description": "`add_monoid_algebra.gradesby` describe an internally graded algebra", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.mem_grade_iff'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.grade_by.decomposition", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.grade.graded_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.mem_grade_by_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.grade.decomposition", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.grade_eq_lsingle_range", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.mem_grade_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.grade.graded_algebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.single_mem_grade_by", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.grade_by.graded_monoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.grade_by", "description": "The submodule corresponding to each grade given by the degree function `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.grade_by.graded_algebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.decompose_aux_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.grade.decompose_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.decompose_aux", "description": " Auxiliary definition; the canonical grade decomposition, used to provide\n`direct_sum.decompose`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.grade", "description": "The submodule corresponding to each grade.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.lex.decidable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.lex_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "psigma.lex.mono_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.lex.is_total", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "psigma.lex_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.lex_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.lex.is_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.lex.is_irrefl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.lex.is_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.lex.is_trichotomous", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "psigma.lex.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "psigma.lex.decidable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.lex.is_trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "psigma.lex.mono_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.lex.mono_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.lex", "description": " The lexicographical order on a sigma type. It takes in a relation on the index type and a\nrelation for each summand. `a` is related to `b` iff their summands are related or they are in the\nsame summand and are related through the summand's relation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.lex.left", "description": " The lexicographical order on a sigma type. It takes in a relation on the index type and a\nrelation for each summand. `a` is related to `b` iff their summands are related or they are in the\nsame summand and are related through the summand's relation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.lex.right", "description": " The lexicographical order on a sigma type. It takes in a relation on the index type and a\nrelation for each summand. `a` is related to `b` iff their summands are related or they are in the\nsame summand and are related through the summand's relation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.lex.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.lex.is_antisymm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.lex.mono_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup.category_theory.limits.has_zero_object", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.has_zero_object", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.is_zero_of_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddGroup.has_zero_object", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.category_theory.limits.has_zero_object", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommGroup.is_zero_of_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Group.is_zero_of_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddGroup.is_zero_of_subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_const_add_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.Ico_add_bij", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_add_const_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_add_const_Ici", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_add_const_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.Ioc_add_bij", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_const_add_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.Ioo_add_bij", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_add_const_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.Ioi_add_bij", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_add_const_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_const_add_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_add_const_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_const_add_Ici", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_const_add_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.Ici_add_bij", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.Icc_add_bij", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_const_add_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.comm_group_with_zero", "description": " Pullback a `comm_group_with_zero` class along an injective function.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.mul_zero_class", "description": " Pullback a `mul_zero_class` instance along an injective function.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.comm_monoid_with_zero", "description": " Pushforward a `monoid_with_zero` class along a surjective function.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.mul_zero_class", "description": " Pushforward a `mul_zero_class` instance along an surjective function.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.cancel_monoid_with_zero", "description": " Pullback a `monoid_with_zero` class along an injective function.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.group_with_zero", "description": " Pullback a `group_with_zero` class along an injective function.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.comm_monoid_with_zero", "description": " Pullback a `monoid_with_zero` class along an injective function.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.mul_zero_one_class", "description": " Pullback a `mul_zero_one_class` instance along an injective function.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.semigroup_with_zero", "description": " Pushforward a `semigroup_with_zero` class along an surjective function.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.monoid_with_zero", "description": " Pullback a `monoid_with_zero` class along an injective function.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.mul_zero_one_class", "description": " Pushforward a `mul_zero_one_class` instance along an surjective function.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.cancel_comm_monoid_with_zero", "description": " Pullback a `cancel_comm_monoid_with_zero` class along an injective function.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.comm_group_with_zero", "description": "Pushforward a `comm_group_with_zero` class along a surjective function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.no_zero_divisors", "description": "Pushforward a `no_zero_divisors` instance along an injective function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.group_with_zero", "description": " Pushforward a `group_with_zero` class along an surjective function.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.semigroup_with_zero", "description": " Pullback a `semigroup_with_zero` class along an injective function.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.monoid_with_zero", "description": " Pushforward a `monoid_with_zero` class along a surjective function.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.counit_nat_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.counit_nat_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.acounit", "description": " `mv_polynomial.acounit A B` is the natural surjective algebra homomorphism\n`mv_polynomial B A \u2192\u2090[A] B` obtained by `X a \u21a6 a`.\n\nSee `mv_polynomial.counit` for the \u201cabsolute\u201d variant with `A = \u2124`,\nand `mv_polynomial.counit_nat` for the \u201cabsolute\u201d variant with `A = \u2115`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.acounit_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.counit_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.counit_nat", "description": " `mv_polynomial.counit_nat A` is the natural surjective ring homomorphism\n`mv_polynomial A \u2115 \u2192+* A` obtained by `X a \u21a6 a`.\n\nSee `mv_polynomial.acounit` for a \u201crelative\u201d variant for algebras over a base ring\nand `mv_polynomial.counit` for the \u201cabsolute\u201d variant with `A = \u2124`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.acounit_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.counit_nat_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.acounit_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.counit", "description": " `mv_polynomial.counit R` is the natural surjective ring homomorphism\n`mv_polynomial R \u2124 \u2192+* R` obtained by `X r \u21a6 r`.\n\nSee `mv_polynomial.acounit` for a \u201crelative\u201d variant for algebras over a base ring,\nand `mv_polynomial.counit_nat` for the \u201cabsolute\u201d variant with `R = \u2115`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.counit_surjective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.counit_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.to_finsupp_apply_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.coe_to_finsupp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.to_finsupp_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.to_finsupp", "description": " Indexing into a `l : list M`, as a finitely-supported function,\nwhere the support are all the indices within the length of the list\nthat index to a non-zero value. Indices beyond the end of the list are sent to 0.\n\nThis is a computable version of the `finsupp.on_finset` construction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.to_finsupp_cons_eq_single_add_emb_domain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.to_finsupp_cons_apply_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.to_finsupp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.to_finsupp_concat_eq_to_finsupp_add_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.to_finsupp_cons_apply_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.to_finsupp_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.to_finsupp_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.to_finsupp_apply_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.to_finsupp_eq_sum_map_enum_single", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.reindex_lie_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.lie_conj", "description": "An invertible matrix induces a Lie algebra equivalence from the space of matrices to itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_equiv_matrix'", "description": " The natural equivalence between linear endomorphisms of finite free modules and square matrices\nis compatible with the Lie algebra structures.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.reindex_lie_equiv_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_equiv_matrix'_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_equiv_matrix'_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.lie_conj_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.reindex_lie_equiv", "description": " For square matrices, the natural map that reindexes a matrix's rows and columns with equivalent\ntypes, `matrix.reindex`, is an equivalence of Lie algebras.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.lie_conj_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.tendsto_lim_of_differentiable_on_punctured_nhds_of_bounded_under", "description": " **Removable singularity** theorem: if a function `f : \u2102 \u2192 E` is complex differentiable and\nbounded on a punctured neighborhood of `c`, then `f` has a limit at `c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.differentiable_on_update_lim_insert_of_is_o", "description": " **Removable singularity** theorem: if `s` is a punctured neighborhood of `c : \u2102`, a function\n`f : \u2102 \u2192 E` is complex differentiable on `s`, and $f(z) - f(c)=o((z-c)^{-1})$, then `f` redefined to\nbe equal to `lim (\ud835\udcdd[\u2260] c) f` at `c` is complex differentiable on `{c} \u222a s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.analytic_at_of_differentiable_on_punctured_nhds_of_continuous_at", "description": " **Removable singularity** theorem, weak version. If `f : \u2102 \u2192 E` is differentiable in a punctured\nneighborhood of a point and is continuous at this point, then it is analytic at this point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.differentiable_on_update_lim_of_is_o", "description": " **Removable singularity** theorem: if `s` is a neighborhood of `c : \u2102`, a function `f : \u2102 \u2192 E`\nis complex differentiable on `s \\ {c}`, and $f(z) - f(c)=o((z-c)^{-1})$, then `f` redefined to be\nequal to `lim (\ud835\udcdd[\u2260] c) f` at `c` is complex differentiable on `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.tendsto_lim_of_differentiable_on_punctured_nhds_of_is_o", "description": " **Removable singularity** theorem: if a function `f : \u2102 \u2192 E` is complex differentiable on a\npunctured neighborhood of `c` and $f(z) - f(c)=o((z-c)^{-1})$, then `f` has a limit at `c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.differentiable_on_dslope", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.differentiable_on_compl_singleton_and_continuous_at_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.differentiable_on_update_lim_of_bdd_above", "description": " **Removable singularity** theorem: if `s` is a neighborhood of `c : \u2102`, a function `f : \u2102 \u2192 E`\nis complex differentiable and is bounded on `s \\ {c}`, then `f` redefined to be equal to\n`lim (\ud835\udcdd[\u2260] c) f` at `c` is complex differentiable on `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.two_pi_I_inv_smul_circle_integral_sub_sq_inv_smul_of_differentiable", "description": "The Cauchy formula for the derivative of a holomorphic function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbtree.default_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.exp_pow_sum", "description": " Shows that\n$\\sum_{k = 0}^{n - 1} (e^{X})^k = \\sum_{p = 0}^{\\infty} \\sum_{k = 0}^{n - 1} \\frac{k^p}{p!}X^p$.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.coeff_sin_bit0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.inv_units_sub", "description": "The power series for `1 / (u - x)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.constant_coeff_exp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.map_cos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.exp", "description": "Power series for the exponential function at zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.inv_units_sub_mul_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.coeff_sin_bit1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.cos", "description": "Power series for the cosine function at zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.coeff_inv_units_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.sin", "description": "Power series for the sine function at zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.coeff_cos_bit1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.map_sin", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.exp_mul_exp_neg_eq_one", "description": "Shows that $e^{x} * e^{-x} = 1$", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.map_inv_units_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.map_exp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.exp_pow_eq_rescale_exp", "description": "Shows that $(e^{X})^k = e^{kX}$.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.inv_units_sub_mul_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.coeff_cos_bit0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.exp_mul_exp_eq_exp_add", "description": "Shows that $e^{aX} * e^{bX} = e^{(a + b)X}$", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.coeff_exp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "power_series.constant_coeff_inv_units_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.nontriviality_by_assumption", "description": "Tries to generate a `nontrivial \u03b1` instance using `nontrivial_of_ne` or `nontrivial_of_lt`\nand local hypotheses.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.nontriviality_by_elim", "description": "Tries to generate a `nontrivial \u03b1` instance by performing case analysis on\n`subsingleton_or_nontrivial \u03b1`,\nattempting to discharge the subsingleton branch using lemmas with `@[nontriviality]` attribute,\nincluding `subsingleton.le` and `eq_iff_true_of_subsingleton`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.nontriviality", "description": "Attempts to generate a `nontrivial \u03b1` hypothesis.\n\nThe tactic first looks for an instance using `apply_instance`.\n\nIf the goal is an (in)equality, the type `\u03b1` is inferred from the goal.\nOtherwise, the type needs to be specified in the tactic invocation, as `nontriviality \u03b1`.\n\nThe `nontriviality` tactic will first look for strict inequalities amongst the hypotheses,\nand use these to derive the `nontrivial` instance directly.\n\nOtherwise, it will perform a case split on `subsingleton \u03b1 \u2228 nontrivial \u03b1`, and attempt to discharge\nthe `subsingleton` goal using `simp [lemmas] with nontriviality`, where `[lemmas]` is a list of\nadditional `simp` lemmas that can be passed to `nontriviality` using the syntax\n`nontriviality \u03b1 using [lemmas]`.\n\n```lean\nexample {R : Type} [ordered_ring R] {a : R} (h : 0 < a) : 0 < a :=\nbegin\n  nontriviality, -- There is now a `nontrivial R` hypothesis available.\n  assumption,\nend\n```\n\n```lean\nexample {R : Type} [comm_ring R] {r s : R} : r * s = s * r :=\nbegin\n  nontriviality, -- There is now a `nontrivial R` hypothesis available.\n  apply mul_comm,\nend\n```\n\n```lean\nexample {R : Type} [ordered_ring R] {a : R} (h : 0 < a) : (2 : \u2115) \u2223 4 :=\nbegin\n  nontriviality R, -- there is now a `nontrivial R` hypothesis available.\n  dec_trivial\nend\n```\n\n```lean\ndef myeq {\u03b1 : Type} (a b : \u03b1) : Prop := a = b\n\nexample {\u03b1 : Type} (a b : \u03b1) (h : a = b) : myeq a b :=\nbegin\n  success_if_fail { nontriviality \u03b1 }, -- Fails\n  nontriviality \u03b1 using [myeq], -- There is now a `nontrivial \u03b1` hypothesis available\n  assumption\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.nontriviality", "description": "Attempts to generate a `nontrivial \u03b1` hypothesis.\n\nThe tactic first looks for an instance using `apply_instance`.\n\nIf the goal is an (in)equality, the type `\u03b1` is inferred from the goal.\nOtherwise, the type needs to be specified in the tactic invocation, as `nontriviality \u03b1`.\n\nThe `nontriviality` tactic will first look for strict inequalities amongst the hypotheses,\nand use these to derive the `nontrivial` instance directly.\n\nOtherwise, it will perform a case split on `subsingleton \u03b1 \u2228 nontrivial \u03b1`, and attempt to discharge\nthe `subsingleton` goal using `simp [lemmas] with nontriviality`, where `[lemmas]` is a list of\nadditional `simp` lemmas that can be passed to `nontriviality` using the syntax\n`nontriviality \u03b1 using [lemmas]`.\n\n```lean\nexample {R : Type} [ordered_ring R] {a : R} (h : 0 < a) : 0 < a :=\nbegin\n  nontriviality, -- There is now a `nontrivial R` hypothesis available.\n  assumption,\nend\n```\n\n```lean\nexample {R : Type} [comm_ring R] {r s : R} : r * s = s * r :=\nbegin\n  nontriviality, -- There is now a `nontrivial R` hypothesis available.\n  apply mul_comm,\nend\n```\n\n```lean\nexample {R : Type} [ordered_ring R] {a : R} (h : 0 < a) : (2 : \u2115) \u2223 4 :=\nbegin\n  nontriviality R, -- there is now a `nontrivial R` hypothesis available.\n  dec_trivial\nend\n```\n\n```lean\ndef myeq {\u03b1 : Type} (a b : \u03b1) : Prop := a = b\n\nexample {\u03b1 : Type} (a b : \u03b1) (h : a = b) : myeq a b :=\nbegin\n  success_if_fail { nontriviality \u03b1 }, -- Fails\n  nontriviality \u03b1 using [myeq], -- There is now a `nontrivial \u03b1` hypothesis available\n  assumption\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring", "description": " An integral domain is called a `valuation ring` provided that for any pair\nof elements `a b : A`, either `a` divides `b` or vice versa.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.cond", "description": " An integral domain is called a `valuation ring` provided that for any pair\nof elements `a b : A`, either `a` divides `b` or vice versa.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.iff_is_integer_or_is_integer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.mem_integer_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.value_group.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.ideal.linear_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.unique_irreducible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.surjective.valuation_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.value_group", "description": "The value group of the valuation ring `A`. Note: this is actually a group with zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.is_integer_or_is_integer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.value_group.has_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.range_algebra_map_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.value_group.has_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.is_bezout", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.dvd_total", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.value_group.has_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.of_integers", "description": " If `\ud835\udcaa` satisfies `v.integers \ud835\udcaa` where `v` is a valuation on a field, then `\ud835\udcaa`\nis a valuation ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.coe_equiv_integer_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.iff_ideal_total", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.le_total", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.valuation", "description": "Any valuation ring induces a valuation on its fraction field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.equiv_integer", "description": "The valuation ring `A` is isomorphic to the ring of integers of its associated valuation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.value_group.has_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.of_discrete_valuation_ring", "description": "A DVR is a valuation ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.value_group.linear_ordered_comm_group_with_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.iff_dvd_total", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.value_group.has_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.local_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.tfae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.iff_local_bezout_domain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_ring.of_field", "description": "A field is a valuation ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_circle.closed_ball_ae_eq_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_circle.volume_of_add_preimage_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_circle.is_add_fundamental_domain_of_ae_ball", "description": " Let `G` be the subgroup of `add_circle T` generated by a point `u` of finite order `n : \u2115`. Then\nany set `I` that is almost equal to a ball of radius `T / 2n` is a fundamental domain for the action\nof `G` on `add_circle T` by left addition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nerve_functor_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.obj.category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nerve_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nerve_functor", "description": "The nerve of a category, as a functor `Cat \u2964 sSet`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nerve_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nerve_functor_map_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nerve", "description": "The nerve of a category", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.split_center_box", "description": " For a box `I`, the hyperplanes passing through its center split `I` into `2 ^ card \u03b9` boxes.\n`box_integral.box.split_center_box I s` is one of these boxes. See also\n`box_integral.partition.split_center` for the corresponding `box_integral.partition`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.split_center_box_emb", "description": "`box_integral.box.split_center_box` bundled as a `function.embedding`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.exists_mem_split_center_box", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.disjoint_split_center_box", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.injective_split_center_box", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.mem_split_center_box", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.upper_sub_lower_split_center_box", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.subbox_induction_on'", "description": " Let `p` be a predicate on `box \u03b9`, let `I` be a box. Suppose that the following two properties\nhold true.\n\n* `H_ind` : Consider a smaller box `J \u2264 I`. The hyperplanes passing through the center of `J` split\n  it into `2 ^ n` boxes. If `p` holds true on each of these boxes, then it true on `J`.\n\n* `H_nhds` : For each `z` in the closed box `I.Icc` there exists a neighborhood `U` of `z` within\n  `I.Icc` such that for every box `J \u2264 I` such that `z \u2208 J.Icc \u2286 U`, if `J` is homothetic to `I`\n  with a coefficient of the form `1 / 2 ^ m`, then `p` is true on `J`.\n\nThen `p I` is true. See also `box_integral.box.subbox_induction_on` for a version using\n`box_integral.prepartition.split_center` instead of `box_integral.box.split_center_box`.\n\nThe proof still works if we assume `H_ind` only for subboxes `J \u2264 I` that are homothetic to `I` with\na coefficient of the form `2\u207b\u1d50` but we do not need this generalization yet.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.Union_coe_split_center_box", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.split_center_box_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.split_center_box_emb_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal.left_rigid_functor_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal.functor_has_left_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal.rigid_functor_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal.functor_has_right_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoidal.right_rigid_functor_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.comp_sort_eq_comp_iff_monotone", "description": "A permutation of a tuple `f` is `f` sorted if and only if it is monotone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.monotone_proj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.eq_sort_iff'", "description": " A permutation `\u03c3` equals `sort f` if and only if the map `i \u21a6 (f (\u03c3 i), \u03c3 i)` is\nstrictly monotone (w.r.t. the lexicographic ordering on the target).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.sort", "description": "`sort f` is the permutation that orders `fin n` according to the order of the outputs of `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.graph_equiv\u2081", "description": "`graph_equiv\u2081 f` is the natural equivalence between `fin n` and `graph f`,\nmapping `i` to `(f i, i)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.sort_eq_refl_iff_monotone", "description": "The permutation that sorts `f` is the identity if and only if `f` is monotone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.unique_monotone", "description": "If two permutations of a tuple `f` are both monotone, then they are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.antitone_pair_of_not_sorted", "description": " If the tuple `f` is not the same as `f \u2218 sort f`, then `f` has a pair of strictly decreasing\nentries.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.self_comp_sort", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.graph.proj", "description": "Given `p : \u03b1 \u00d7\u2097 (fin n) := (f i, i)` with `p \u2208 graph f`,\n`graph.proj p` is defined to be `f i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.antitone_pair_of_not_sorted'", "description": " If a permutation `f \u2218 \u03c3` of the tuple `f` is not the same as `f \u2218 sort f`, then `f \u2218 \u03c3`\nhas a pair of strictly decreasing entries.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.proj_equiv\u2081'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.graph.card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.graph_equiv\u2082_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.graph_equiv\u2082", "description": "`graph_equiv\u2082 f` is an equivalence between `fin n` and `graph f` that respects the order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.comp_perm_comp_sort_eq_comp_sort", "description": "The sorted versions of a tuple `f` and of any permutation of `f` agree.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.graph", "description": "`graph f` produces the finset of pairs `(f i, i)`\nequipped with the lexicographic order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.monotone_sort", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.eq_sort_iff", "description": " A permutation `\u03c3` equals `sort f` if and only if `f \u2218 \u03c3` is monotone and whenever `i < j`\nand `f (\u03c3 i) = f (\u03c3 j)`, then `\u03c3 i < \u03c3 j`. This means that `sort f` is the lexicographically\nsmallest permutation `\u03c3` such that `f \u2218 \u03c3` is monotone.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_spaceable.inner_prop_neg_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_spaceable.nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_spaceable.inner_.norm_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_spaceable.add_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_spaceable.continuous.inner_", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_spaceable.inner_prop", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_spaceable.to_uniform_convex_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.to_inner_product_spaceable_of_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.to_inner_product_spaceable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_spaceable", "description": " Predicate for the parallelogram identity to hold in a normed group. This is a scalar-less\nversion of `inner_product_space`. If you have an `inner_product_spaceable` assumption, you can\nlocally upgrade that to `inner_product_space \ud835\udd5c E` using `casesI nonempty_inner_product_space \ud835\udd5c E`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_spaceable.parallelogram_identity", "description": " Predicate for the parallelogram identity to hold in a normed group. This is a scalar-less\nversion of `inner_product_space`. If you have an `inner_product_spaceable` assumption, you can\nlocally upgrade that to `inner_product_space \ud835\udd5c E` using `casesI nonempty_inner_product_space \ud835\udd5c E`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonempty_inner_product_space", "description": " **Fr\u00e9chet\u2013von Neumann\u2013Jordan Theorem**. A normed space `E` whose norm satisfies the\nparallelogram identity can be given a compatible inner product. Do\n`casesI nonempty_inner_product_space \ud835\udd5c E` to locally upgrade `inner_product_spaceable E` to\n`inner_product_space \ud835\udd5c E`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_spaceable.inner_.conj_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_space.of_norm", "description": " **Fr\u00e9chet\u2013von Neumann\u2013Jordan Theorem**. A normed space `E` whose norm satisfies the\nparallelogram identity can be given a compatible inner product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lex.covariant_class_lt_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lex.partial_order", "description": " The partial order on `finsupp`s obtained by the lexicographic ordering.\nSee `finsupp.lex.linear_order` for a proof that this partial order is in fact linear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lt_of_forall_lt_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lex_lt_of_lt_of_preorder", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lex", "description": " `finsupp.lex r s` is the lexicographic relation on `\u03b1 \u2192\u2080 N`, where `\u03b1` is ordered by `r`,\nand `N` is ordered by `s`.\n\nThe type synonym `lex (\u03b1 \u2192\u2080 N)` has an order given by `finsupp.lex (<) (<)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lex.covariant_class_le_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lex.linear_order", "description": "The linear order on `finsupp`s obtained by the lexicographic ordering.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lex_eq_inv_image_dfinsupp_lex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lex_lt_of_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lex.is_strict_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.lex_eq_finsupp_lex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lex.has_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lex.covariant_class_lt_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.to_lex_monotone", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lex.covariant_class_le_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.lex_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.fst_exp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.exp_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.eq_smul_exp_of_ne_zero", "description": " More convenient version of `triv_sq_zero_ext.eq_smul_exp_of_invertible` for when `R` is a\nfield.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.has_sum_exp_series_of_smul_comm", "description": "If `exp R x.fst` converges to `e` then `exp R x` converges to `inl e + inr (e \u2022 x.snd)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.snd_exp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.exp_inr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.has_sum_snd_exp_series_of_smul_comm", "description": "If `exp R x.fst` converges to `e` then `(exp R x).snd` converges to `e \u2022 x.snd`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.has_sum_fst_exp_series", "description": "If `exp R x.fst` converges to `e` then `(exp R x).fst` converges to `e`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.exp_def_of_smul_comm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.exp_inl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "triv_sq_zero_ext.eq_smul_exp_of_invertible", "description": "Polar form of trivial-square-zero extension.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.simple_func.exists_upper_semicontinuous_le_lintegral_le", "description": " Given a simple function `f` with values in `\u211d\u22650`, there exists an upper semicontinuous\nfunction `g \u2264 f` with integral arbitrarily close to that of `f`. Formulation in terms of\n`lintegral`.\nAuxiliary lemma for Vitali-Carath\u00e9odory theorem `exists_lt_lower_semicontinuous_integral_lt`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_upper_semicontinuous_le_integral_le", "description": " Given an integrable function `f` with values in `\u211d\u22650`, there exists an upper semicontinuous\nfunction `g \u2264 f` with integral arbitrarily close to that of `f`. Formulation in terms of\n`integral`.\nAuxiliary lemma for Vitali-Carath\u00e9odory theorem `exists_lt_lower_semicontinuous_integral_lt`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_upper_semicontinuous_lt_integral_gt", "description": " **Vitali-Carath\u00e9odory Theorem**: given an integrable real function `f`, there exists an\nintegrable function `g < f` which is upper semicontinuous, with integral arbitrarily close to that\nof `f`. This function has to be `ereal`-valued in general.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_lt_lower_semicontinuous_lintegral_ge_of_ae_measurable", "description": " Given an almost everywhere measurable function `f` with values in `\u211d\u22650` in a sigma-finite space,\nthere exists a lower semicontinuous function `g > f` with integral arbitrarily close to that of `f`.\nFormulation in terms of `lintegral`.\nAuxiliary lemma for Vitali-Carath\u00e9odory theorem `exists_lt_lower_semicontinuous_integral_lt`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_le_lower_semicontinuous_lintegral_ge", "description": " Given a measurable function `f` with values in `\u211d\u22650`, there exists a lower semicontinuous\nfunction `g \u2265 f` with integral arbitrarily close to that of `f`. Formulation in terms of\n`lintegral`.\nAuxiliary lemma for Vitali-Carath\u00e9odory theorem `exists_lt_lower_semicontinuous_integral_lt`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_lt_lower_semicontinuous_integral_gt_nnreal", "description": " Given an integrable function `f` with values in `\u211d\u22650` in a sigma-finite space, there exists a\nlower semicontinuous function `g > f` with integral arbitrarily close to that of `f`.\nFormulation in terms of `integral`.\nAuxiliary lemma for Vitali-Carath\u00e9odory theorem `exists_lt_lower_semicontinuous_integral_lt`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.simple_func.exists_le_lower_semicontinuous_lintegral_ge", "description": " Given a simple function `f` with values in `\u211d\u22650`, there exists a lower semicontinuous\nfunction `g \u2265 f` with integral arbitrarily close to that of `f`. Formulation in terms of\n`lintegral`.\nAuxiliary lemma for Vitali-Carath\u00e9odory theorem `exists_lt_lower_semicontinuous_integral_lt`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_lt_lower_semicontinuous_integral_lt", "description": " **Vitali-Carath\u00e9odory Theorem**: given an integrable real function `f`, there exists an\nintegrable function `g > f` which is lower semicontinuous, with integral arbitrarily close\nto that of `f`. This function has to be `ereal`-valued in general.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_upper_semicontinuous_le_lintegral_le", "description": " Given an integrable function `f` with values in `\u211d\u22650`, there exists an upper semicontinuous\nfunction `g \u2264 f` with integral arbitrarily close to that of `f`. Formulation in terms of\n`lintegral`.\nAuxiliary lemma for Vitali-Carath\u00e9odory theorem `exists_lt_lower_semicontinuous_integral_lt`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_lt_lower_semicontinuous_lintegral_ge", "description": " Given a measurable function `f` with values in `\u211d\u22650` in a sigma-finite space, there exists a\nlower semicontinuous function `g > f` with integral arbitrarily close to that of `f`.\nFormulation in terms of `lintegral`.\nAuxiliary lemma for Vitali-Carath\u00e9odory theorem `exists_lt_lower_semicontinuous_integral_lt`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_on.sum_smul_sum_le_card_smul_sum", "description": " **Chebyshev's Sum Inequality**: When `f` and `g` monovary together (eg they are both\nmonotone/antitone), the scalar product of their sum is less than the size of the set times their\nscalar product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary.card_smul_sum_le_sum_smul_sum", "description": " **Chebyshev's Sum Inequality**: When `f` and `g` antivary together (eg one is monotone, the\nother is antitone), the scalar product of their sum is less than the size of the set times their\nscalar product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary.card_mul_sum_le_sum_mul_sum", "description": " **Chebyshev's Sum Inequality**: When `f` and `g` antivary together (eg one is monotone, the\nother is antitone), the product of their sum is less than the size of the set times their scalar\nproduct.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary.sum_smul_sum_le_card_smul_sum", "description": " **Chebyshev's Sum Inequality**: When `f` and `g` monovary together (eg they are both\nmonotone/antitone), the scalar product of their sum is less than the size of the set times their\nscalar product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum_div_card_sq_le_sum_sq_div_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sq_sum_le_card_mul_sum_sq", "description": " Special case of **Chebyshev's Sum Inequality** or the **Cauchy-Schwarz Inequality**: The square\nof the sum is less than the size of the set times the sum of the squares.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary_on.sum_mul_sum_le_card_mul_sum", "description": " **Chebyshev's Sum Inequality**: When `f` and `g` monovary together (eg they are both\nmonotone/antitone), the product of their sum is less than the size of the set times their scalar\nproduct.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_on.card_mul_sum_le_sum_mul_sum", "description": " **Chebyshev's Sum Inequality**: When `f` and `g` antivary together (eg one is monotone, the\nother is antitone), the product of their sum is greater than the size of the set times their scalar\nproduct.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antivary_on.card_smul_sum_le_sum_smul_sum", "description": " **Chebyshev's Sum Inequality**: When `f` and `g` antivary together (eg one is monotone, the\nother is antitone), the scalar product of their sum is less than the size of the set times their\nscalar product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monovary.sum_mul_sum_le_card_mul_sum", "description": " **Chebyshev's Sum Inequality**: When `f` and `g` monovary together (eg they are both\nmonotone/antitone), the product of their sum is less than the size of the set times their scalar\nproduct.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preserves_image.iso_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preserves_image.iso_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preserves_image.inv_comp_image_\u03b9_map_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preserves_image.iso", "description": "If a functor preserves span and cospan, then it preserves images.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preserves_image.factor_thru_image_comp_hom_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preserves_image.factor_thru_image_comp_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preserves_image.inv_comp_image_\u03b9_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preserves_image.hom_comp_map_image_\u03b9", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preserves_image.hom_comp_map_image_\u03b9_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mk_nat_val_le_proof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "mk_int_val_ne_proof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "mk_nat_val_ne_proof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "mk_fin_val_ne_proof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "mk_nat_val_lt_proof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.comp_val", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "mk_char_val_ne_proof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "mk_string_val_ne_proof", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.comp_val", "description": "Close goals of the form `n \u2260 m` when `n` and `m` have type `nat`, `char`, `string`, `int` or\n   subtypes `{i : \u2115 // p i}`, and they are literals.\n   It also closes goals of the form `n < m`, `n > m`, `n \u2264 m` and `n \u2265 m` for `nat`.\n   If the goal is of the form `n = m`, then it tries to close it using reflexivity.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "pexpr.of_expr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "pexpr.mk_placeholder", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "pexpr", "description": "Quoted expressions. They can be converted into expressions by using a tactic.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_to_pexpr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_to_pexpr.to_pexpr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "pexpr.mk_structure_instance", "description": "Create a structure instance expression.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "pexpr.is_placeholder", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reflected.has_to_pexpr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "expr.has_to_pexpr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "structure_instance_info", "description": "Information about unelaborated structure instance expressions.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "structure_instance_info.struct", "description": "Information about unelaborated structure instance expressions.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "structure_instance_info.field_names", "description": "Information about unelaborated structure instance expressions.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "structure_instance_info.field_values", "description": "Information about unelaborated structure instance expressions.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "structure_instance_info.sources", "description": "Information about unelaborated structure instance expressions.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "pexpr.has_to_pexpr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "pexpr.mk_explicit", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "pexpr.is_choice_macro", "description": "Choice macros are used to implement overloading.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "to_pexpr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "pexpr.mk_field_macro", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "pexpr.get_structure_instance_info", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "exists_extension_norm_eq", "description": "Hahn-Banach theorem for continuous linear functions over `\ud835\udd5c` satisyfing `is_R_or_C \ud835\udd5c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_dual_vector", "description": "Corollary of Hahn-Banach.  Given a nonzero element `x` of a normed space, there exists an\n   element of the dual space, of norm `1`, whose value on `x` is `\u2016x\u2016`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_dual_vector'", "description": "Variant of Hahn-Banach, eliminating the hypothesis that `x` be nonzero, and choosing\n   the dual element arbitrarily when `x = 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.exists_extension_norm_eq", "description": "Hahn-Banach theorem for continuous linear functions over `\u211d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_dual_vector''", "description": "Variant of Hahn-Banach, eliminating the hypothesis that `x` be nonzero, but only ensuring that\n   the dual element has norm at most `1` (this can not be improved for the trivial\n   vector space).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coord_norm'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo2008_q4.abs_eq_one_of_pow_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2008_q4", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "measure_theory.lintegral_rpow_eq_lintegral_meas_le_mul", "description": " An application of the layer cake formula / Cavalieri's principle / tail probability formula:\n\nFor a nonnegative function `f` on a sigma-finite measure space, the Lebesgue integral of `f` can\nbe written (roughly speaking) as: `\u222b\u207b f^p \u2202\u03bc = p * \u222b\u207b t in 0 .. \u221e, t^(p-1) * \u03bc {\u03c9 | f(\u03c9) \u2265 t}`.\n\nSee `lintegral_rpow_eq_lintegral_meas_lt_mul` for a version with sets of the form `{\u03c9 | f(\u03c9) > t}`\ninstead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure.meas_le_ne_meas_lt_subset_meas_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure.countable_meas_le_ne_meas_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.lintegral_comp_eq_lintegral_meas_le_mul_of_measurable", "description": " An auxiliary version of the layer cake formula (Cavalieri's principle, tail probability\nformula), with a measurability assumption that would also essentially follow from the\nintegrability assumptions.\n\nSee `measure_theory.lintegral_comp_eq_lintegral_meas_le_mul` and\n`measure_theory.lintegral_comp_eq_lintegral_meas_lt_mul` for the main formulations of the layer\ncake formula.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lintegral_comp_eq_lintegral_meas_lt_mul", "description": " The layer cake formula / Cavalieri's principle / tail probability formula:\n\nLet `f` be a non-negative measurable function on a sigma-finite measure space. Let `G` be an\nincreasing absolutely continuous function on the positive real line, vanishing at the origin,\nwith derivative `G' = g`. Then the integral of the composition `G \u2218 f` can be written as\nthe integral over the positive real line of the \"tail measures\" `\u03bc {\u03c9 | f(\u03c9) > t}` of `f`\nweighted by `g`.\n\nRoughly speaking, the statement is: `\u222b\u207b (G \u2218 f) \u2202\u03bc = \u222b\u207b t in 0 .. \u221e, g(t) * \u03bc {\u03c9 | f(\u03c9) > t}`.\n\nSee `lintegral_comp_eq_lintegral_meas_le_mul` for a version with sets of the form `{\u03c9 | f(\u03c9) \u2265 t}`\ninstead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.lintegral_comp_eq_lintegral_meas_le_mul", "description": " The layer cake formula / Cavalieri's principle / tail probability formula:\n\nLet `f` be a non-negative measurable function on a sigma-finite measure space. Let `G` be an\nincreasing absolutely continuous function on the positive real line, vanishing at the origin,\nwith derivative `G' = g`. Then the integral of the composition `G \u2218 f` can be written as\nthe integral over the positive real line of the \"tail measures\" `\u03bc {\u03c9 | f(\u03c9) \u2265 t}` of `f`\nweighted by `g`.\n\nRoughly speaking, the statement is: `\u222b\u207b (G \u2218 f) \u2202\u03bc = \u222b\u207b t in 0 .. \u221e, g(t) * \u03bc {\u03c9 | f(\u03c9) \u2265 t}`.\n\nSee `lintegral_comp_eq_lintegral_meas_lt_mul` for a version with sets of the form `{\u03c9 | f(\u03c9) > t}`\ninstead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lintegral_rpow_eq_lintegral_meas_lt_mul", "description": " An application of the layer cake formula / Cavalieri's principle / tail probability formula:\n\nFor a nonnegative function `f` on a sigma-finite measure space, the Lebesgue integral of `f` can\nbe written (roughly speaking) as: `\u222b\u207b f^p \u2202\u03bc = p * \u222b\u207b t in 0 .. \u221e, t^(p-1) * \u03bc {\u03c9 | f(\u03c9) > t}`.\n\nSee `lintegral_rpow_eq_lintegral_meas_le_mul` for a version with sets of the form `{\u03c9 | f(\u03c9) \u2265 t}`\ninstead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lintegral_eq_lintegral_meas_lt", "description": " The standard case of the layer cake formula / Cavalieri's principle / tail probability formula:\n\nFor a nonnegative function `f` on a sigma-finite measure space, the Lebesgue integral of `f` can\nbe written (roughly speaking) as: `\u222b\u207b f \u2202\u03bc = \u222b\u207b t in 0 .. \u221e, \u03bc {\u03c9 | f(\u03c9) > t}`.\n\nSee `lintegral_eq_lintegral_meas_le` for a version with sets of the form `{\u03c9 | f(\u03c9) \u2265 t}`\ninstead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure.meas_le_ae_eq_meas_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.lintegral_eq_lintegral_meas_le", "description": " The standard case of the layer cake formula / Cavalieri's principle / tail probability formula:\n\nFor a nonnegative function `f` on a sigma-finite measure space, the Lebesgue integral of `f` can\nbe written (roughly speaking) as: `\u222b\u207b f \u2202\u03bc = \u222b\u207b t in 0 .. \u221e, \u03bc {\u03c9 | f(\u03c9) \u2265 t}`.\n\nSee `lintegral_eq_lintegral_meas_lt` for a version with sets of the form `{\u03c9 | f(\u03c9) > t}`\ninstead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.card_bUnion_le_of_intersecting", "description": " **Kleitman's theorem**. An intersecting family on `n` elements contains at most `2\u207f\u207b\u00b9` sets, and\neach further intersecting family takes at most half of the sets that are in no previous family.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.invertible_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.invertible_coe_nat", "description": " A natural number that is invertible when coerced to a commutative semiring `R`\nis also invertible when coerced to any polynomial ring with rational coefficients.\n\nShort-cut for typeclass resolution.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.integral_circle_transform", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.circle_transform_bounding_function", "description": "A useful bound for circle integrals (with complex codomain)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.abs_circle_transform_bounding_function_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.continuous_circle_transform", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.circle_transform_deriv_periodic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.circle_transform_deriv_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.continuous_on_prod_circle_transform_function", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.continuous_circle_transform_deriv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.circle_transform_deriv_bound", "description": "The derivative of a `circle_transform` is locally bounded.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.continuous_on_abs_circle_transform_bounding_function", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.circle_transform", "description": " Given a function `f : \u2102 \u2192 E`, `circle_transform R z w f` is the functions mapping `\u03b8` to\n`(2 * \u2191\u03c0 * I)\u207b\u00b9 \u2022 deriv (circle_map z R) \u03b8 \u2022 ((circle_map z R \u03b8) - w)\u207b\u00b9 \u2022 f (circle_map z R \u03b8)`.\n\nIf `f` is differentiable and `w` is in the interior of the ball, then the integral from `0` to\n`2 * \u03c0` of this gives the value `f(w)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.circle_transform_deriv", "description": "The derivative of `circle_transform` w.r.t `w`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "if_false_left_eq_and", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "if_true_right_eq_or", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "if_true_left_eq_or", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "if_false_right_eq_and", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "real.tendsto_sum_pi_div_four", "description": "This theorem establishes **Leibniz's series for `\u03c0`**: The alternating sum of the reciprocals\n of the odd numbers is `\u03c0/4`. Note that this is a conditionally rather than absolutely convergent\n series. The main tool that this proof uses is the Mean Value Theorem (specifically avoiding the\n Fundamental Theorem of Calculus).\n\n Intuitively, the theorem holds because Leibniz's series is the Taylor series of `arctan x`\n centered about `0` and evaluated at the value `x = 1`. Therefore, much of this proof consists of\n reasoning about a function\n   `f := arctan x - \u2211 i in finset.range k, (-(1:\u211d))^i * x^(2*i+1) / (2*i+1)`,\n the difference between `arctan` and the `k`-th partial sum of its Taylor series. Some ingenuity is\n required due to the fact that the Taylor series is not absolutely convergent at `x = 1`.\n\n This proof requires a bound on `f 1`, the key idea being that `f 1` can be split as the sum of\n `f 1 - f u` and `f u`, where `u` is a sequence of values in [0,1], carefully chosen such that\n each of these two terms can be controlled (in different ways).\n\n We begin the proof by (1) introducing that sequence `u` and then proving that another sequence\n constructed from `u` tends to `0` at `+\u221e`. After (2) converting the limit in our goal to an\n inequality, we (3) introduce the auxiliary function `f` defined above. Next, we (4) compute the\n derivative of `f`, denoted by `f'`, first generally and then on each of two subintervals of [0,1].\n We then (5) prove a bound for `f'`, again both generally as well as on each of the two\n subintervals. Finally, we (6) apply the Mean Value Theorem twice, obtaining bounds on `f 1 - f u`\n and `f u - f 0` from the bounds on `f'` (note that `f 0 = 0`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_vector", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "small_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Monad_to_Mon", "description": "Passing from `Monad C` to `Mon_ (C \u2964 C)` is functorial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.to_Mon", "description": "To every `Monad C` we associated a monoid object in `C \u2964 C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Monad_Mon_equiv", "description": "Oh, monads are just monoids in the category of endofunctors (equivalence of categories).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Monad_Mon_equiv.unit_iso_hom_2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Monad_Mon_equiv_counit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.to_Mon_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.of_Mon_\u03bc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Monad_Mon_equiv_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Monad_Mon_equiv.unit_iso_inv_app_to_nat_trans_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.to_Mon_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Monad_Mon_equiv.unit_iso", "description": "Isomorphism of functors used in `Monad_Mon_equiv`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Mon_to_Monad", "description": "Passing from `Mon_ (C \u2964 C)` to `Monad C` is functorial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Monad_Mon_equiv.unit_iso_hom_app_to_nat_trans_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.of_Mon_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Monad_Mon_equiv.unit_iso_inv", "description": "Auxiliary definition for `Monad_Mon_equiv`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.of_Mon", "description": "To every monoid object in `C \u2964 C` we associate a `Monad C`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Monad_Mon_equiv_unit_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Mon_to_Monad_map_to_nat_trans_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Monad_to_Mon_map_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Monad_Mon_equiv.counit_iso_hom_app_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Monad_Mon_equiv.counit_iso_inv_app_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Monad_Mon_equiv.unit_iso_inv_2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Monad_Mon_equiv.unit_iso_hom", "description": "Auxiliary definition for `Monad_Mon_equiv`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Monad_Mon_equiv.counit_iso", "description": "Isomorphism of functors used in `Monad_Mon_equiv`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Monad_Mon_equiv_inverse", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Mon_to_Monad_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.of_Mon_\u03b7", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.Monad_to_Mon_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Monad.to_Mon_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.deriv_tan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.continuous_at_tan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.has_deriv_at_tan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.tendsto_abs_tan_of_cos_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.differentiable_at_tan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.has_strict_deriv_at_tan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.cont_diff_at_tan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.tendsto_abs_tan_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.eval_polynomial_eval_fin_succ_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.polynomial_eval_eval\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bin_tree.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "level.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rbtree.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_variable_names", "description": "Type class for associating a type `\u03b1` with typical variable names for elements\nof `\u03b1`. See `tactic.typical_variable_names`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_variable_names.names", "description": "Type class for associating a type `\u03b1` with typical variable names for elements\nof `\u03b1`. See `tactic.typical_variable_names`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_variable_names.names_nonempty", "description": "Type class for associating a type `\u03b1` with typical variable names for elements\nof `\u03b1`. See `tactic.typical_variable_names`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Prop.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "thunk.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "name.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "psigma.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unsigned.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_variable_names.make_inheriting_instance", "description": "`@make_inheriting_instance \u03b1 _ \u03b2` creates an instance `has_variable_names \u03b2`\nfrom an instance `has_variable_names \u03b1`. The generated instance contains the\nsame variable names as that of `\u03b1`. This can be used to create instances for\n'wrapper' types like `option` and `subtype`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "native.rb_set.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "binder_info.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.typical_variable_names", "description": "`typical_variable_names t` obtains typical names for variables of type `t`.\nThe returned list is guaranteed to be nonempty. Fails if there is no instance\n`has_typical_variable_names t`.\n\n```lean\ntypical_variable_names `(\u2115) = [`n, `m, `o]\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bool.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "expr.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_variable_names.make_listlike_instance", "description": "`@make_listlike_instance \u03b1 _ \u03b2` creates an instance `has_variable_names \u03b2` from\nan instance `has_variable_names \u03b1`. If `\u03b1` has associated names `a`, `b`, ...,\nthe generated instance for `\u03b2` has names `as`, `bs`, ... This can be used to\ncreate instances for 'containers' such as lists or sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pprod.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pexpr.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "d_array.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.has_variable_names", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.star_algebra_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.star_smul", "description": " Note that this not match the `star_smul` implied by `star_module`; it certainly could if we\nalso conjugated all the scalars, but there appears to be nothing in the literature that advocates\ndoing this.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.star_def'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.star_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.star_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "clifford_algebra.star_\u03b9", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.well_powered", "description": "A category (with morphisms in `Type v`) is well-powered if `subobject X` is `v`-small for every `X`.\n\nWe show in `well_powered_of_mono_over_essentially_small` and `mono_over_essentially_small`\nthat this is the case if and only if `mono_over X` is `v`-essentially small for every `X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.well_powered.subobject_small", "description": "A category (with morphisms in `Type v`) is well-powered if `subobject X` is `v`-small for every `X`.\n\nWe show in `well_powered_of_mono_over_essentially_small` and `mono_over_essentially_small`\nthat this is the case if and only if `mono_over X` is `v`-essentially small for every `X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.well_powered_of_essentially_small_mono_over", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.well_powered_congr", "description": "Being well-powered is preserved by equivalences, as long as the two categories involved have\n   their morphisms in the same universe.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.small_subobject", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.essentially_small_mono_over", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.well_powered_of_small_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.essentially_small_mono_over_iff_small_subobject", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.well_powered_of_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_independent.localization", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "span_eq_top.localization_localization", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.localization_localization", "description": " If `A` has an `R`-basis, then localizing `A` at `S` has a basis over `R` localized at `S`.\n\nA suitable instance for `[algebra A A\u209b]` is `localization_algebra`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.localization_localization_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_independent.localization_localization", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.localization_localization_repr_algebra_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_independent.iff_fraction_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_subring.sub_mul_action", "description": " The valuation subring `A` (considered as a subset of `L`)\nis stable under the action of the decomposition group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_subring.decomposition_subgroup", "description": " The decomposition subgroup defined as the stabilizer of the action\non the type of all valuation subrings of the field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_subring.inertia_subgroup", "description": " The inertia subgroup defined as the kernel of the group homomorphism from\nthe decomposition subgroup to the group of automorphisms of the residue field of `A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation_subring.decomposition_subgroup_mul_semiring_action", "description": "The multiplicative action of the decomposition subgroup on `A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "expr.to_rat", "description": " Evaluates an expression as a rational number,\nif that expression represents a numeral, the quotient of two numerals,\nthe negation of a numeral, or the negation of the quotient of two numerals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.reflect", "description": "`rat.reflect q` represents the rational number `q` as a numeral expression of type `\u211a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.mk_numeral", "description": "`rat.mk_numeral q` embeds `q` as a numeral expression inside a type with 0, 1, +, -, and /\n\n`type`: an expression representing the target type. This must live in Type 0.\n`has_zero`, `has_one`, `has_add`: expressions of the type `has_zero %%type`, etc.\n\nThis function is similar to `expr.of_rat` but takes more hypotheses and is not tactic valued.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "expr.eval_rat", "description": "Evaluates an expression into a rational number, if that expression is built up from\n numerals, +, -, *, /, \u207b\u00b9", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.instance_cache.of_rat", "description": " `c.of_rat q` embeds `q` as a numeral expression inside the type `\u03b1`.\nLean will try to infer the correct type classes on `c.\u03b1`, and the tactic will fail if it cannot.\nThis function is similar to `rat.mk_numeral` but it takes fewer hypotheses and is tactic valued.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.to_pexpr", "description": "`rat.to_pexpr q` creates a `pexpr` that will evaluate to `q`.\nThe `pexpr` does not hold any typing information:\n`to_expr ``((%%(rat.to_pexpr (3/4)) : K))` will create a native `K` numeral `(3/4 : K)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "expr.of_rat", "description": " `expr.of_rat \u03b1 q` embeds `q` as a numeral expression inside the type `\u03b1`.\nLean will try to infer the correct type classes on `\u03b1`, and the tactic will fail if it cannot.\nThis function is similar to `rat.mk_numeral` but it takes fewer hypotheses and is tactic valued.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "expr.to_nonneg_rat", "description": " Evaluates an expression as a rational number,\nif that expression represents a numeral or the quotient of two numerals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_left_R_unit_iso_of_is_iso_counit_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_left_L_counit_iso_of_is_iso_unit_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.L_full_of_unit_is_iso", "description": "If the unit is an isomorphism, then the left adjoint is full", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_right_counit_iso_of_L_fully_faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.inv_map_unit", "description": " If the unit of an adjunction is an isomorphism, then its inverse on the image of L is given\nby L whiskered with the counit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_left_counit_iso_of_L_fully_faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.R_faithful_of_counit_is_iso", "description": "If the counit is an isomorphism, then the right adjoint is faithful", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_left_L_counit_iso_of_is_iso_unit_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adjunction.restrict_fully_faithful", "description": "If `C` is a full subcategory of `C'` and `D` is a full subcategory of `D'`, then we can restrict\nan adjunction `L' \u22a3 R'` where `L' : C' \u2964 D'` and `R' : D' \u2964 C'` to `C` and `D`.\nThe construction here is slightly more general, in that `C` is required only to have a full and\nfaithful \"inclusion\" functor `iC : C \u2964 C'` (and similarly `iD : D \u2964 D'`) which commute (up to\nnatural isomorphism) with the proposed restrictions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_left_unit_iso_of_R_fully_faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_left_R_unit_iso_of_is_iso_counit", "description": "If the counit of an is an isomorphism, one has an isomorphism `(R \u22d9 L \u22d9 R) \u2245 R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.counit_is_iso_of_R_fully_faithful", "description": "If the right adjoint is fully faithful, then the counit is an isomorphism.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07RB> (we only prove the forward direction!)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.inv_counit_map", "description": " If the counit of an adjunction is an isomorphism, then its inverse on the image of R is given\nby R whiskered with the unit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unit_is_iso_of_L_fully_faithful", "description": "If the left adjoint is fully faithful, then the unit is an isomorphism.\n\nSee\n* Lemma 4.5.13 from [Riehl][riehl2017]\n* https://math.stackexchange.com/a/2727177\n* https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07RB (we only prove the forward direction!)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.L_faithful_of_unit_is_iso", "description": "If the unit is an isomorphism, then the left adjoint is faithful", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_right_unit_iso_of_R_fully_faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_left_R_unit_iso_of_is_iso_counit_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.whisker_left_L_counit_iso_of_is_iso_unit", "description": "If the unit is an isomorphism, bundle one has an isomorphism `L \u22d9 R \u22d9 L \u2245 L`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.R_full_of_counit_is_iso", "description": "If the counit is an isomorphism, then the right adjoint is full", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.unique_from_empty", "description": "Any functor out of the empty category is isomorphic to the canonical functor from the empty\ncategory.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.empty_ext'", "description": "Any two functors out of the empty category are *equal*. You probably want to use\n`empty_ext` instead of this.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.empty_ext", "description": "Any two functors out of the empty category are isomorphic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.empty_equivalence", "description": "Equivalence between two empty categories.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.empty", "description": "The canonical functor out of the empty category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonneg.nat_ceil_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonneg.archimedean", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonneg.nat_floor_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nonneg.floor_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.forget_preserves_colimits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.colimit_cocone", "description": "A choice of colimit cocone for a functor `F : J \u2964 Top`.\nGenerally you should just use `colimit.coone F`, unless you need the actual definition\n(which is in terms of `types.colimit_cocone`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.colimit_cocone_is_colimit", "description": "The chosen cocone `Top.colimit_cocone F` for a functor `F : J \u2964 Top` is a colimit cocone.\nGenerally you should just use `colimit.is_colimit F`, unless you need the actual definition\n(which is in terms of `types.colimit_cocone_is_colimit`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.Top_has_colimits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.is_terminal_punit", "description": "The terminal object of `Top` is `punit`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.is_initial_pempty", "description": "The initial object of `Top` is `pempty`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.Top_has_colimits_of_size", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.forget_preserves_limits_of_size", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.initial_iso_pempty", "description": "The initial object of `Top` is `pempty`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.terminal_iso_punit", "description": "The terminal object of `Top` is `punit`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.limit_cone_infi", "description": "A choice of limit cone for a functor `F : J \u2964 Top` whose topology is defined as an\ninfimum of topologies infimum.\nGenerally you should just use `limit.cone F`, unless you need the actual definition\n(which is in terms of `types.limit_cone`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.forget_preserves_colimits_of_size", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.limit_cone", "description": "A choice of limit cone for a functor `F : J \u2964 Top`.\nGenerally you should just use `limit.cone F`, unless you need the actual definition\n(which is in terms of `types.limit_cone`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.limit_cone_is_limit", "description": "The chosen cone `Top.limit_cone F` for a functor `F : J \u2964 Top` is a limit cone.\nGenerally you should just use `limit.is_limit F`, unless you need the actual definition\n(which is in terms of `types.limit_cone_is_limit`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.limit_cone_infi_is_limit", "description": "The chosen cone `Top.limit_cone_infi F` for a functor `F : J \u2964 Top` is a limit cone.\nGenerally you should just use `limit.is_limit F`, unless you need the actual definition\n(which is in terms of `types.limit_cone_is_limit`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.Top_has_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.forget_preserves_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.Top_has_limits_of_size", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "string.iterator.length_next_to_string_next", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "string.iterator.next_to_string_mk_iterator", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "string.iterator.zero_lt_length_next_to_string_of_has_next", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "string.split", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monotone_on.union_right", "description": " If `f` is monotone both on `s` and `t`, with `s` to the left of `t` and the center\npoint belonging to both `s` and `t`, then `f` is monotone on `s \u222a t`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_anti_on.Iic_union_Ici", "description": " If `f` is strictly antitone both on `(-\u221e, a]` and `[a, \u221e)`, then it is strictly antitone on the\nwhole line.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_mono_on.union", "description": " If `f` is strictly monotone both on `s` and `t`, with `s` to the left of `t` and the center\npoint belonging to both `s` and `t`, then `f` is strictly monotone on `s \u222a t`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone_on.Iic_union_Ici", "description": "If `f` is monotone both on `(-\u221e, a]` and `[a, \u221e)`, then it is monotone on the whole line.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone_on.union_right", "description": " If `f` is antitone both on `s` and `t`, with `s` to the left of `t` and the center\npoint belonging to both `s` and `t`, then `f` is antitone on `s \u222a t`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_anti_on.union", "description": " If `f` is strictly antitone both on `s` and `t`, with `s` to the left of `t` and the center\npoint belonging to both `s` and `t`, then `f` is strictly antitone on `s \u222a t`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_mono_on.Iic_union_Ici", "description": " If `f` is strictly monotone both on `(-\u221e, a]` and `[a, \u221e)`, then it is strictly monotone on the\nwhole line.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone_on.Iic_union_Ici", "description": "If `f` is antitone both on `(-\u221e, a]` and `[a, \u221e)`, then it is antitone on the whole line.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo2013_q1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2013_q1.arith_lemma", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2013_q1.prod_lemma", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "CommRing.free", "description": "The free functor `Type u \u2964 CommRing` sending a type `X` to the multivariable (commutative)\npolynomials with variables `x : X`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.category_theory.forget.category_theory.is_right_adjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.free_obj_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.free_map_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CommRing.adj", "description": "The free-forgetful adjunction for commutative rings.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tagged_format.map", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tagged_format", "description": "An alternative to format that keeps structural information stored as a tag.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tagged_format.tag", "description": "An alternative to format that keeps structural information stored as a tag.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tagged_format.compose", "description": "An alternative to format that keeps structural information stored as a tag.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tagged_format.group", "description": "An alternative to format that keeps structural information stored as a tag.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tagged_format.nest", "description": "An alternative to format that keeps structural information stored as a tag.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tagged_format.highlight", "description": "An alternative to format that keeps structural information stored as a tag.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tagged_format.of_format", "description": "An alternative to format that keeps structural information stored as a tag.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic_state.pp_tagged", "description": " A special version of pp which also preserves expression boundary information.\n\nOn a tag \u27e8e,a\u27e9, note that the given expr `e` is _not_ necessarily the subexpression of the root\nexpression that `tactic_state.pp_tagged` was called with. For example if the subexpression is\nunder a binder then all of the `expr.var 0`s will be replaced with a local constant not in\nthe local context with the name and type set to that of the binder.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tagged_format.m_untag", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tagged_format.has_to_fmt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tagged_format.untag", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "eformat", "description": "tagged_format with information about subexpressions.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.pp_tagged", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tagged_format.is_functor", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "dlist.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dlist.list_equiv_dlist", "description": " The natural equivalence between lists and difference lists, using\n`dlist.of_list` and `dlist.to_list`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dlist.is_lawful_traversable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dlist.traversable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subalgebra.aeval_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.aeval_algebra_map_eq_zero_iff_of_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.aeval_map_algebra_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.aeval_algebra_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.aeval_algebra_map_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "same_ray_iff_of_norm_eq", "description": "Two vectors of the same norm are on the same ray if and only if they are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "same_ray.norm_smul_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "same_ray.norm_add", "description": " If `x` and `y` are on the same ray, then the triangle inequality becomes the equality: the norm\nof `x + y` is the sum of the norms of `x` and `y`. The converse is true for a strictly convex\nspace.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "same_ray_iff_inv_norm_smul_eq_of_ne", "description": " Two nonzero vectors `x y` in a real normed space are on the same ray if and only if the unit\nvectors `\u2016x\u2016\u207b\u00b9 \u2022 x` and `\u2016y\u2016\u207b\u00b9 \u2022 y` are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "same_ray_iff_norm_smul_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "same_ray_iff_inv_norm_smul_eq", "description": " Two vectors `x y` in a real normed space are on the ray if and only if one of them is zero or\nthe unit vectors `\u2016x\u2016\u207b\u00b9 \u2022 x` and `\u2016y\u2016\u207b\u00b9 \u2022 y` are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "same_ray.eq_of_norm_eq", "description": "If two points on the same ray have the same norm, then they are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "same_ray.norm_eq_iff", "description": "The norms of two vectors on the same ray are equal if and only if they are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_inj_on_ray_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "same_ray.inv_norm_smul_eq", "description": "**Alias** of the forward direction of `same_ray_iff_inv_norm_smul_eq_of_ne`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_inj_on_ray_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_same_ray_iff_of_norm_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "same_ray.norm_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_open_immersion_iff_stalk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_open_immersion_respects_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_open_immersion.open_cover_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_open_immersion.open_cover_tfae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_open_immersion_stable_under_base_change", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_open_immersion_stable_under_composition", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_geometry.is_open_immersion_is_local_at_target", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "counterexample.seminorm_not_distrib.p_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.seminorm_not_distrib.q2_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.seminorm_not_distrib.p", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.seminorm_not_distrib.not_distrib", "description": "This is a counterexample to the distributivity of the lattice `seminorm \u211d (\u211d \u00d7 \u211d)`.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.seminorm_not_distrib.eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.seminorm_not_distrib.q2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.seminorm_not_distrib.q1_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.seminorm_not_distrib.q1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "canonically_linear_ordered_semifield", "description": " A canonically linear ordered field is a linear ordered field in which `a \u2264 b` iff there exists\n`c` with `b = a + c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "canonicall