{"name": "is_G\u03b4_bInter_of_open", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_G\u03b4_bUnion", "description": "The union of finitely many G\u03b4 sets is a G\u03b4 set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_G\u03b4.inter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.subsingleton.is_G\u03b4_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_G\u03b4", "description": "A G\u03b4 set is a countable intersection of open sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "residual", "description": "A set `s` is called *residual* if it includes a countable intersection of dense open sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_G\u03b4_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_open.is_G\u03b4", "description": "An open set is a G\u03b4 set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_G\u03b4_set_of_continuous_at", "description": "The set of points where a function is continuous is a G\u03b4 set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_G\u03b4_Inter", "description": "The intersection of an encodable family of G\u03b4 sets is a G\u03b4 set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite.is_G\u03b4_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_G\u03b4_Inter_of_open", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.countable.is_G\u03b4_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "residual_of_dense_G\u03b4", "description": "Dense G\u03b4 sets are residual.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.is_G\u03b4_compl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_closed.is_G\u03b4", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_G\u03b4_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_G\u03b4_sInter", "description": "A countable intersection of G\u03b4 sets is a G\u03b4 set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_G\u03b4_bInter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_G\u03b4_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "residual_of_dense_open", "description": "Dense open sets are residual.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite.is_G\u03b4", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo2021_q1.exists_triplet_summing_to_squares", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2021_q1.exists_numbers_in_interval", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2021_q1.exists_finset_3_le_card_with_pairs_summing_to_squares", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2021_q1.radical_inequality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2021_q1.upper_bound", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2021_q1.lower_bound", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2021_q1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "real.has_sum_sin", "description": "The power series expansion of `real.sin`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.has_sum_sin'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.has_sum_cos", "description": "The power series expansion of `real.cos`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.cos_eq_tsum'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.cos_eq_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.has_sum_cos", "description": "The power series expansion of `complex.cos`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.sin_eq_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.has_sum_cos'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.sin_eq_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.has_sum_sin", "description": "The power series expansion of `complex.sin`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.sin_eq_tsum'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.cos_eq_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equal_char_zero_to_Q_algebra", "description": "Equal characteristic implies `\u211a`-algebra.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "split_equal_mixed_char", "description": "Split a `Prop` in characteristic zero into equal and mixed characteristic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Q_algebra_to_equal_char_zero", "description": "`\u211a`-algebra implies equal characteristic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_mixed_char_to_equal_char_zero", "description": "Not mixed characteristic implies equal characteristic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mixed_char_zero.reduce_to_p_prime", "description": "Reduction to `p` prime: When proving any statement `P` about mixed characteristic rings we\ncan always assume that `p` is prime.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "split_by_characteristic_domain", "description": " In a `is_domain R`, split any `Prop` over `R` into the three cases:\n- *prime* characteristic.\n- equal characteristic zero.\n- mixed characteristic `(0, p)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mixed_char_zero.reduce_to_maximal_ideal", "description": "Reduction to `I` prime ideal: When proving statements about mixed characteristic rings,\nafter we reduced to `p` prime, we can assume that the ideal `I` in the definition is maximal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equal_char_zero_iff_not_mixed_char", "description": "A ring of characteristic zero has equal characteristic iff it does not\nhave mixed characteristic for any `p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Q_algebra_iff_equal_char_zero", "description": "A ring is a `\u211a`-algebra iff it has equal characteristic zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equal_char_zero_to_not_mixed_char", "description": "Equal characteristic implies not mixed characteristic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equal_char_zero.pnat_coe_units_coe_eq_coe", "description": "Internal: Not intended to be used outside this local construction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_Q_algebra_iff_not_equal_char_zero", "description": "A ring of characteristic zero is not a `\u211a`-algebra iff it has mixed characteristic for some `p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "split_by_characteristic_local_ring", "description": " In a `local_ring R`, split any `Prop` over `R` into the three cases:\n- *prime power* characteristic.\n- equal characteristic zero.\n- mixed characteristic `(0, p)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equal_char_zero.pnat_has_coe_units", "description": "Internal: Not intended to be used outside this local construction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "split_by_characteristic", "description": " Split any `Prop` over `R` into the three cases:\n- positive characteristic.\n- equal characteristic zero.\n- mixed characteristic `(0, p)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mixed_char_zero", "description": "A ring of characteristic zero is of \"mixed characteristic `(0, p)`\" if there exists an ideal\nsuch that the quotient `R \u29f8 I` has caracteristic `p`.\n\n**Remark:** For `p = 0`, `mixed_char R 0` is a meaningless definition as `R \u29f8 \u22a5 \u2245 R` has by\ndefinition always characteristic zero.\nOne could require `(I \u2260 \u22a5)` in the definition, but then `mixed_char R 0` would mean something\nlike `\u2124`-algebra of extension degree `\u2265 1` and would be completely independent from\nwhether something is a `\u211a`-algebra or not (e.g. `\u211a[X]` would satisfy it but `\u211a` wouldn't).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mixed_char_zero.to_char_zero", "description": "A ring of characteristic zero is of \"mixed characteristic `(0, p)`\" if there exists an ideal\nsuch that the quotient `R \u29f8 I` has caracteristic `p`.\n\n**Remark:** For `p = 0`, `mixed_char R 0` is a meaningless definition as `R \u29f8 \u22a5 \u2245 R` has by\ndefinition always characteristic zero.\nOne could require `(I \u2260 \u22a5)` in the definition, but then `mixed_char R 0` would mean something\nlike `\u2124`-algebra of extension degree `\u2265 1` and would be completely independent from\nwhether something is a `\u211a`-algebra or not (e.g. `\u211a[X]` would satisfy it but `\u211a` wouldn't).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mixed_char_zero.char_p_quotient", "description": "A ring of characteristic zero is of \"mixed characteristic `(0, p)`\" if there exists an ideal\nsuch that the quotient `R \u29f8 I` has caracteristic `p`.\n\n**Remark:** For `p = 0`, `mixed_char R 0` is a meaningless definition as `R \u29f8 \u22a5 \u2245 R` has by\ndefinition always characteristic zero.\nOne could require `(I \u2260 \u22a5)` in the definition, but then `mixed_char R 0` would mean something\nlike `\u2124`-algebra of extension degree `\u2265 1` and would be completely independent from\nwhether something is a `\u211a`-algebra or not (e.g. `\u211a[X]` would satisfy it but `\u211a` wouldn't).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equal_char_zero.pnat_coe_is_unit", "description": "Internal: Not intended to be used outside this local construction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equal_char_zero.pnat_coe_units_eq_one", "description": "Internal: Not intended to be used outside this local construction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.normal_mono", "description": "In the category of abelian groups, every monomorphism is normal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.category_theory.abelian", "description": "The category of abelian groups is abelian.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.normal_epi", "description": "In the category of abelian groups, every epimorphism is normal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abs_convex_open_sets.coe_zero_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_seminorm_family_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gauge_seminorm_family", "description": "The family of seminorms defined by the gauges of absolute convex open sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abs_convex_open_sets", "description": "The type of absolutely convex open sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abs_convex_open_sets.has_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_gauge_seminorm_family", "description": "The topology of a locally convex space is induced by the gauge seminorm family.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abs_convex_open_sets.nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abs_convex_open_sets.coe_balanced", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nhds_basis_abs_convex_open", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nhds_basis_abs_convex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abs_convex_open_sets.coe_convex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abs_convex_open_sets.coe_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "abs_convex_open_sets.coe_is_open", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_nilpotent.is_unit_quotient_mk_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_radical_iff_quotient_reduced", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.is_nilpotent.induction_on", "description": "Let `P` be a property on ideals. If `P` holds for square-zero ideals, and if\n `P I \u2192 P (J \u29f8 I) \u2192 P J`, then `P` holds for all nilpotent ideals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "perm_of_prod_eq_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prime.not_dvd_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prime.dvd_prod_iff", "description": "Prime `p` divides the product of a list `L` iff it divides some `a \u2208 L`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_list_primes_of_dvd_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.partition.odds", "description": "The finset of those partitions in which every part is odd.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.partition.of_composition_surj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.partition.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.partition.of_sums", "description": "Given a multiset which sums to `n`, construct a partition of `n` with the same multiset, but\nwithout the zeros.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.partition.odd_distincts", "description": "The finset of those partitions in which every part is odd and used at most once.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.partition.of_composition", "description": "A composition induces a partition (just convert the list to a multiset).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.partition.count_of_sums_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.partition.indiscrete_partition", "description": "The partition of exactly one part.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.partition.distincts", "description": "The finset of those partitions in which each part is used at most once.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.partition.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.partition.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.partition.of_multiset", "description": "A `multiset \u2115` induces a partition on its sum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.partition.fintype", "description": "Show there are finitely many partitions by considering the surjection from compositions to\npartitions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.partition.count_of_sums_of_ne_zero", "description": "The number of times a positive integer `i` appears in the partition `of_sums n l hl` is the same\nas the number of times it appears in the multiset `l`.\n(For `i = 0`, `partition.non_zero` combined with `multiset.count_eq_zero_of_not_mem` gives that\nthis is `0` instead.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.partition.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.partition", "description": "A partition of `n` is a multiset of positive integers summing to `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.partition.parts", "description": "A partition of `n` is a multiset of positive integers summing to `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.partition.parts_pos", "description": "A partition of `n` is a multiset of positive integers summing to `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.partition.parts_sum", "description": "A partition of `n` is a multiset of positive integers summing to `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.sheafification_whisker_left_iso", "description": " The isomorphism between the sheafification of `P` composed with `F` and\nthe sheafification of `P \u22d9 F`, functorially in `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.sheafify_comp_iso_inv_eq_sheafify_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.sheafification_whisker_left_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.sheafify_comp_iso", "description": " The isomorphism between the sheafification of `P` composed with `F` and\nthe sheafification of `P \u22d9 F`.\n\nUse the lemmas `whisker_right_to_sheafify_sheafify_comp_iso_hom`,\n`to_sheafify_comp_sheafify_comp_iso_inv` and `sheafify_comp_iso_inv_eq_sheafify_lift` to reduce\nthe components of this isomorphisms to a state that can be handled using the universal property\nof sheafification.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.whisker_right_to_sheafify_sheafify_comp_iso_hom_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.whisker_right_to_sheafify_sheafify_comp_iso_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.to_sheafify_comp_sheafify_comp_iso_inv_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.to_sheafify_comp_sheafify_comp_iso_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.sheafification_whisker_right_iso_inv_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.sheafification_whisker_right_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.sheafification_whisker_left_iso_hom_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.sheafification_whisker_right_iso", "description": " The isomorphism between the sheafification of `P` composed with `F` and\nthe sheafification of `P \u22d9 F`, functorially in `P`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.smul_le_stopped_value_hitting", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.submartingale_of_expected_stopped_value_mono", "description": " The converse direction of the optional stopping theorem, i.e. an adapted integrable process `f`\nis a submartingale if for all bounded stopping times `\u03c4` and `\u03c0` such that `\u03c4 \u2264 \u03c0`, the\nstopped value of `f` at `\u03c4` has expectation smaller than its stopped value at `\u03c0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.submartingale_iff_expected_stopped_value_mono", "description": " **The optional stopping theorem** (fair game theorem): an adapted integrable process `f`\nis a submartingale if and only if for all bounded stopping times `\u03c4` and `\u03c0` such that `\u03c4 \u2264 \u03c0`, the\nstopped value of `f` at `\u03c4` has expectation smaller than its stopped value at `\u03c0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.submartingale.expected_stopped_value_mono", "description": " Given a submartingale `f` and bounded stopping times `\u03c4` and `\u03c0` such that `\u03c4 \u2264 \u03c0`, the\nexpectation of `stopped_value f \u03c4` is less than or equal to the expectation of `stopped_value f \u03c0`.\nThis is the forward direction of the optional stopping theorem.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.submartingale.stopped_process", "description": "The stopped process of a submartingale with respect to a stopping time is a submartingale.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.maximal_ineq", "description": " **Doob's maximal inequality**: Given a non-negative submartingale `f`, for all `\u03b5 : \u211d\u22650`,\nwe have `\u03b5 \u2022 \u03bc {\u03b5 \u2264 f* n} \u2264 \u222b \u03c9 in {\u03b5 \u2264 f* n}, f n` where `f* n \u03c9 = max_{k \u2264 n}, f k \u03c9`.\n\nIn some literature, the Doob's maximal inequality refers to what we call Doob's Lp inequality\n(which is a corollary of this lemma and will be proved in an upcomming PR).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.exists_sq_eq_neg_two_iff", "description": "`-2` is a square modulo an odd prime `p` iff `p` is congruent to `1` or `3` mod `8`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.exists_sq_eq_prime_iff_of_mod_four_eq_three", "description": " If `p` and `q` are distinct primes that are both congruent to `3` mod `4`, then `q` is\na square mod `p` iff `p` is a nonsquare mod `q`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "legendre_sym.at_neg_two", "description": "`legendre_sym p (-2)` is given by `\u03c7\u2088' p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "legendre_sym.quadratic_reciprocity_three_mod_four", "description": " The Law of Quadratic Reciprocity: if `p` and `q` are primes that are both congruent\nto `3` mod `4`, then `(q / p) = -(p / q)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "legendre_sym.quadratic_reciprocity'", "description": " The Law of Quadratic Reciprocity: if `p` and `q` are odd primes, then\n`(q / p) = (-1)^((p-1)(q-1)/4) * (p / q)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.exists_sq_eq_two_iff", "description": "`2` is a square modulo an odd prime `p` iff `p` is congruent to `1` or `7` mod `8`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.exists_sq_eq_prime_iff_of_mod_four_eq_one", "description": " If `p` and `q` are odd primes and `p % 4 = 1`, then `q` is a square mod `p` iff\n`p` is a square mod `q`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "legendre_sym.quadratic_reciprocity", "description": " The Law of Quadratic Reciprocity: if `p` and `q` are distinct odd primes, then\n`(q / p) * (p / q) = (-1)^((p-1)(q-1)/4)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "legendre_sym.at_two", "description": "`legendre_sym p 2` is given by `\u03c7\u2088 p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "legendre_sym.quadratic_reciprocity_one_mod_four", "description": " The Law of Quadratic Reciprocity: if `p` and `q` are odd primes and `p % 4 = 1`,\nthen `(q / p) = (p / q)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.equivalent.symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.isometry.coe_to_linear_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.equivalent.trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.isometry", "description": " An isometry between two quadratic spaces `M\u2081, Q\u2081` and `M\u2082, Q\u2082` over a ring `R`,\nis a linear equivalence between `M\u2081` and `M\u2082` that commutes with the quadratic forms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.isometry.to_linear_equiv", "description": " An isometry between two quadratic spaces `M\u2081, Q\u2081` and `M\u2082, Q\u2082` over a ring `R`,\nis a linear equivalence between `M\u2081` and `M\u2082` that commutes with the quadratic forms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.isometry.map_app'", "description": " An isometry between two quadratic spaces `M\u2081, Q\u2081` and `M\u2082, Q\u2082` over a ring `R`,\nis a linear equivalence between `M\u2081` and `M\u2082` that commutes with the quadratic forms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.isometry_weighted_sum_squares", "description": "Given an orthogonal basis, a quadratic form is isometric with a weighted sum of squares.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.isometry.has_coe_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.isometry_of_comp_linear_equiv", "description": "A quadratic form composed with a `linear_equiv` is isometric to itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.isometry.linear_equiv.has_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.isometry.refl", "description": "The identity isometry from a quadratic form to itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.isometry.map_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.isometry_basis_repr", "description": "A quadratic form is isometric to its bases representations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.isometry.to_linear_equiv_eq_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.isometry.trans", "description": "The composition of two isometries between quadratic forms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.equivalent_weighted_sum_squares", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.isometry.symm", "description": "The inverse isometry of an isometry between two quadratic forms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.equivalent", "description": " Two quadratic forms over a ring `R` are equivalent\nif there exists an isometry between them:\na linear equivalence that transforms one quadratic form into the other.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.equivalent.refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.equivalent_weighted_sum_squares_units_of_nondegenerate'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretriangulated.comp_dist_triangle_mor_zero\u2083\u2081", "description": "Given any distinguished triangle\n```lean\n      f       g       h\n  X  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Y  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Z  \u2500\u2500\u2500> X\u27e61\u27e7\n```\nthe composition `h \u226b f\u27e61\u27e7 = 0`.\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0146>", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretriangulated.comp_dist_triangle_mor_zero\u2082\u2083", "description": "Given any distinguished triangle\n```lean\n      f       g       h\n  X  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Y  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Z  \u2500\u2500\u2500> X\u27e61\u27e7\n```\nthe composition `g \u226b h = 0`.\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0146>", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretriangulated.rot_of_dist_triangle", "description": "Given any distinguished triangle `T`, then we know `T.rotate` is also distinguished.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretriangulated.inv_rot_of_dist_triangle", "description": "Given any distinguished triangle `T`, then we know `T.inv_rotate` is also distinguished.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretriangulated", "description": "A preadditive category `C` with an additive shift, and a class of \"distinguished triangles\"\nrelative to that shift is called pretriangulated if the following hold:\n* Any triangle that is isomorphic to a distinguished triangle is also distinguished.\n* Any triangle of the form `(X,X,0,id,0,0)` is distinguished.\n* For any morphism `f : X \u27f6 Y` there exists a distinguished triangle of the form `(X,Y,Z,f,g,h)`.\n* The triangle `(X,Y,Z,f,g,h)` is distinguished if and only if `(Y,Z,X\u27e61\u27e7,g,h,-f\u27e61\u27e7)` is.\n* Given a diagram:\n  ```lean\n        f       g       h\n    X  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Y  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Z  \u2500\u2500\u2500> X\u27e61\u27e7\n    \u2502       \u2502                \u2502\n    \u2502a      \u2502b               \u2502a\u27e61\u27e7'\n    V       V                V\n    X' \u2500\u2500\u2500> Y' \u2500\u2500\u2500> Z' \u2500\u2500\u2500> X'\u27e61\u27e7\n        f'      g'      h'\n  ```\n  where the left square commutes, and whose rows are distinguished triangles,\n  there exists a morphism `c : Z \u27f6 Z'` such that `(a,b,c)` is a triangle morphism.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0145>", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretriangulated.distinguished_triangles", "description": "A preadditive category `C` with an additive shift, and a class of \"distinguished triangles\"\nrelative to that shift is called pretriangulated if the following hold:\n* Any triangle that is isomorphic to a distinguished triangle is also distinguished.\n* Any triangle of the form `(X,X,0,id,0,0)` is distinguished.\n* For any morphism `f : X \u27f6 Y` there exists a distinguished triangle of the form `(X,Y,Z,f,g,h)`.\n* The triangle `(X,Y,Z,f,g,h)` is distinguished if and only if `(Y,Z,X\u27e61\u27e7,g,h,-f\u27e61\u27e7)` is.\n* Given a diagram:\n  ```lean\n        f       g       h\n    X  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Y  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Z  \u2500\u2500\u2500> X\u27e61\u27e7\n    \u2502       \u2502                \u2502\n    \u2502a      \u2502b               \u2502a\u27e61\u27e7'\n    V       V                V\n    X' \u2500\u2500\u2500> Y' \u2500\u2500\u2500> Z' \u2500\u2500\u2500> X'\u27e61\u27e7\n        f'      g'      h'\n  ```\n  where the left square commutes, and whose rows are distinguished triangles,\n  there exists a morphism `c : Z \u27f6 Z'` such that `(a,b,c)` is a triangle morphism.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0145>", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretriangulated.isomorphic_distinguished", "description": "A preadditive category `C` with an additive shift, and a class of \"distinguished triangles\"\nrelative to that shift is called pretriangulated if the following hold:\n* Any triangle that is isomorphic to a distinguished triangle is also distinguished.\n* Any triangle of the form `(X,X,0,id,0,0)` is distinguished.\n* For any morphism `f : X \u27f6 Y` there exists a distinguished triangle of the form `(X,Y,Z,f,g,h)`.\n* The triangle `(X,Y,Z,f,g,h)` is distinguished if and only if `(Y,Z,X\u27e61\u27e7,g,h,-f\u27e61\u27e7)` is.\n* Given a diagram:\n  ```lean\n        f       g       h\n    X  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Y  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Z  \u2500\u2500\u2500> X\u27e61\u27e7\n    \u2502       \u2502                \u2502\n    \u2502a      \u2502b               \u2502a\u27e61\u27e7'\n    V       V                V\n    X' \u2500\u2500\u2500> Y' \u2500\u2500\u2500> Z' \u2500\u2500\u2500> X'\u27e61\u27e7\n        f'      g'      h'\n  ```\n  where the left square commutes, and whose rows are distinguished triangles,\n  there exists a morphism `c : Z \u27f6 Z'` such that `(a,b,c)` is a triangle morphism.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0145>", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretriangulated.contractible_distinguished", "description": "A preadditive category `C` with an additive shift, and a class of \"distinguished triangles\"\nrelative to that shift is called pretriangulated if the following hold:\n* Any triangle that is isomorphic to a distinguished triangle is also distinguished.\n* Any triangle of the form `(X,X,0,id,0,0)` is distinguished.\n* For any morphism `f : X \u27f6 Y` there exists a distinguished triangle of the form `(X,Y,Z,f,g,h)`.\n* The triangle `(X,Y,Z,f,g,h)` is distinguished if and only if `(Y,Z,X\u27e61\u27e7,g,h,-f\u27e61\u27e7)` is.\n* Given a diagram:\n  ```lean\n        f       g       h\n    X  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Y  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Z  \u2500\u2500\u2500> X\u27e61\u27e7\n    \u2502       \u2502                \u2502\n    \u2502a      \u2502b               \u2502a\u27e61\u27e7'\n    V       V                V\n    X' \u2500\u2500\u2500> Y' \u2500\u2500\u2500> Z' \u2500\u2500\u2500> X'\u27e61\u27e7\n        f'      g'      h'\n  ```\n  where the left square commutes, and whose rows are distinguished triangles,\n  there exists a morphism `c : Z \u27f6 Z'` such that `(a,b,c)` is a triangle morphism.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0145>", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretriangulated.distinguished_cocone_triangle", "description": "A preadditive category `C` with an additive shift, and a class of \"distinguished triangles\"\nrelative to that shift is called pretriangulated if the following hold:\n* Any triangle that is isomorphic to a distinguished triangle is also distinguished.\n* Any triangle of the form `(X,X,0,id,0,0)` is distinguished.\n* For any morphism `f : X \u27f6 Y` there exists a distinguished triangle of the form `(X,Y,Z,f,g,h)`.\n* The triangle `(X,Y,Z,f,g,h)` is distinguished if and only if `(Y,Z,X\u27e61\u27e7,g,h,-f\u27e61\u27e7)` is.\n* Given a diagram:\n  ```lean\n        f       g       h\n    X  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Y  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Z  \u2500\u2500\u2500> X\u27e61\u27e7\n    \u2502       \u2502                \u2502\n    \u2502a      \u2502b               \u2502a\u27e61\u27e7'\n    V       V                V\n    X' \u2500\u2500\u2500> Y' \u2500\u2500\u2500> Z' \u2500\u2500\u2500> X'\u27e61\u27e7\n        f'      g'      h'\n  ```\n  where the left square commutes, and whose rows are distinguished triangles,\n  there exists a morphism `c : Z \u27f6 Z'` such that `(a,b,c)` is a triangle morphism.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0145>", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretriangulated.rotate_distinguished_triangle", "description": "A preadditive category `C` with an additive shift, and a class of \"distinguished triangles\"\nrelative to that shift is called pretriangulated if the following hold:\n* Any triangle that is isomorphic to a distinguished triangle is also distinguished.\n* Any triangle of the form `(X,X,0,id,0,0)` is distinguished.\n* For any morphism `f : X \u27f6 Y` there exists a distinguished triangle of the form `(X,Y,Z,f,g,h)`.\n* The triangle `(X,Y,Z,f,g,h)` is distinguished if and only if `(Y,Z,X\u27e61\u27e7,g,h,-f\u27e61\u27e7)` is.\n* Given a diagram:\n  ```lean\n        f       g       h\n    X  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Y  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Z  \u2500\u2500\u2500> X\u27e61\u27e7\n    \u2502       \u2502                \u2502\n    \u2502a      \u2502b               \u2502a\u27e61\u27e7'\n    V       V                V\n    X' \u2500\u2500\u2500> Y' \u2500\u2500\u2500> Z' \u2500\u2500\u2500> X'\u27e61\u27e7\n        f'      g'      h'\n  ```\n  where the left square commutes, and whose rows are distinguished triangles,\n  there exists a morphism `c : Z \u27f6 Z'` such that `(a,b,c)` is a triangle morphism.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0145>", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretriangulated.complete_distinguished_triangle_morphism", "description": "A preadditive category `C` with an additive shift, and a class of \"distinguished triangles\"\nrelative to that shift is called pretriangulated if the following hold:\n* Any triangle that is isomorphic to a distinguished triangle is also distinguished.\n* Any triangle of the form `(X,X,0,id,0,0)` is distinguished.\n* For any morphism `f : X \u27f6 Y` there exists a distinguished triangle of the form `(X,Y,Z,f,g,h)`.\n* The triangle `(X,Y,Z,f,g,h)` is distinguished if and only if `(Y,Z,X\u27e61\u27e7,g,h,-f\u27e61\u27e7)` is.\n* Given a diagram:\n  ```lean\n        f       g       h\n    X  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Y  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Z  \u2500\u2500\u2500> X\u27e61\u27e7\n    \u2502       \u2502                \u2502\n    \u2502a      \u2502b               \u2502a\u27e61\u27e7'\n    V       V                V\n    X' \u2500\u2500\u2500> Y' \u2500\u2500\u2500> Z' \u2500\u2500\u2500> X'\u27e61\u27e7\n        f'      g'      h'\n  ```\n  where the left square commutes, and whose rows are distinguished triangles,\n  there exists a morphism `c : Z \u27f6 Z'` such that `(a,b,c)` is a triangle morphism.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0145>", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretriangulated.comp_dist_triangle_mor_zero\u2081\u2082", "description": "Given any distinguished triangle\n```lean\n      f       g       h\n  X  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Y  \u2500\u2500\u2500> Z  \u2500\u2500\u2500> X\u27e61\u27e7\n```\nthe composition `f \u226b g = 0`.\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0146>", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fundamental_groupoid.punit_equiv_discrete_punit", "description": "Equivalence of groupoids between fundamental groupoid of punit and punit", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "path.subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fundamental_groupoid.quiver.hom.subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.delta_instance", "description": "`delta_instance id\u2081 id\u2082 ...` tries to solve the goal by calling `apply_instance`,\nfirst unfolding the definitions in `id\u1d62`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.delta_instance_handler", "description": "Tries to derive instances by unfolding the newly introduced type and applying type class resolution.\n\nFor example,\n```lean\n@[derive ring] def new_int : Type := \u2124\n```\nadds an instance `ring new_int`, defined to be the instance of `ring \u2124` found by `apply_instance`.\n\nMultiple instances can be added with `@[derive [ring, module \u211d]]`.\n\nThis derive handler applies only to declarations made using `def`, and will fail on such a\ndeclaration if it is unable to derive an instance. It is run with higher priority than the built-in\nhandlers, which will fail on `def`s.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.delta_instance", "description": "`delta_instance ids` tries to solve the goal by calling `apply_instance`,\nfirst unfolding the definitions in `ids`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.delta_instance_name", "description": " Guess a name for an instance from its expression.\n\nThis is a poor-man's version of the C++ `heuristic_inst_name`, and tries much less hard to pick a\ngood name.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.has_smul.smul.category_theory.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.induced_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.comp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.left_comp", "description": "Composition by a fixed left argument as an `R`-linear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.category_theory.End.algebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.preadditive_nat_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.comp_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.right_comp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.hom_congr_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.comp_smul_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.full_subcategory", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.right_comp", "description": "Composition by a fixed right argument as an `R`-linear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.hom_congr_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.comp", "description": "Composition as a bilinear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.smul_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.hom_congr", "description": " Given isomorphic objects `X \u2245 Y, W \u2245 Z` in a `k`-linear category, we have a `k`-linear\nisomorphism between `Hom(X, W)` and `Hom(Y, Z).`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.smul_comp_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.preadditive_int_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.left_comp_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.has_smul.smul.category_theory.epi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.category_theory.End.module", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear", "description": "A category is called `R`-linear if `P \u27f6 Q` is an `R`-module such that composition is\n   `R`-linear in both variables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.hom_module", "description": "A category is called `R`-linear if `P \u27f6 Q` is an `R`-module such that composition is\n   `R`-linear in both variables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.smul_comp'", "description": "A category is called `R`-linear if `P \u27f6 Q` is an `R`-module such that composition is\n   `R`-linear in both variables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.linear.comp_smul'", "description": "A category is called `R`-linear if `P \u27f6 Q` is an `R`-module such that composition is\n   `R`-linear in both variables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.reflects_isomorphisms", "description": "Define what it means for a functor `F : C \u2964 D` to reflect isomorphisms: for any\nmorphism `f : A \u27f6 B`, if `F.map f` is an isomorphism then `f` is as well.\nNote that we do not assume or require that `F` is faithful.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.reflects_isomorphisms.reflects", "description": "Define what it means for a functor `F : C \u2964 D` to reflect isomorphisms: for any\nmorphism `f : A \u27f6 B`, if `F.map f` is an isomorphism then `f` is as well.\nNote that we do not assume or require that `F` is faithful.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.comp.reflects_isomorphisms", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.of_full_and_faithful", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.reflects_isomorphisms_of_reflects_monomorphisms_of_reflects_epimorphisms", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso_of_reflects_iso", "description": "If `F` reflects isos and `F.map f` is an iso, then `f` is an iso.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polar_coord_source_ae_eq_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integral_comp_polar_coord_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polar_coord_target", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polar_coord_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fderiv_at_polar_coord_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polar_coord", "description": " The polar coordinates local homeomorphism in `\u211d^2`, mapping `(r cos \u03b8, r sin \u03b8)` to `(r, \u03b8)`.\nIt is a homeomorphism between `\u211d^2 - (-\u221e, 0]` and `(0, +\u221e) \u00d7 (-\u03c0, \u03c0)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polar_coord_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polar_coord_source", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.sum_four_squares", "description": "**Four squares theorem**", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.exists_sq_add_sq_add_one_eq_k", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.sq_add_sq_of_two_mul_sq_add_sq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.discr_power_basis_eq_prod", "description": "The discriminant of a power basis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.discr_of_matrix_vec_mul", "description": " Relation between `algebra.discr A \u03b9 b` and\n`algebra.discr A ((P.map (algebra_map A B)).vec_mul b)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.discr_power_basis_eq_norm", "description": "Formula for the discriminant of a power basis using the norm of the field extension.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.discr_eq_discr_of_to_matrix_coeff_is_integral", "description": " If `b` and `b'` are `\u211a`-bases of a number field `K` such that\n`\u2200 i j, is_integral \u2124 (b.to_matrix b' i j)` and `\u2200 i j, is_integral \u2124 (b'.to_matrix b i j)` then\n`discr \u211a b = discr \u211a b'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.discr_zero_of_not_linear_independent", "description": "If `b` is not linear independent, then `algebra.discr A b = 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.discr_mul_is_integral_mem_adjoin", "description": " Let `K` be the fraction field of an integrally closed domain `R` and let `L` be a finite\nseparable extension of `K`. Let `B : power_basis K L` be such that `is_integral R B.gen`.\nThen for all, `z : L` that are integral over `R`, we have\n`(discr K B.basis) \u2022 z \u2208 adjoin R ({B.gen} : set L)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.discr", "description": " Given an `A`-algebra `B` and `b`, an `\u03b9`-indexed family of elements of `B`, we define\n`discr A \u03b9 b` as the determinant of `trace_matrix A \u03b9 b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.discr_of_matrix_mul_vec", "description": " Relation between `algebra.discr A \u03b9 b` and\n`algebra.discr A ((P.map (algebra_map A B)).mul_vec b)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.discr_reindex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.discr_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.discr_not_zero_of_basis", "description": "Over a field, if `b` is a basis, then `algebra.discr K b \u2260 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.discr_eq_det_embeddings_matrix_reindex_pow_two", "description": " If `L/K` is a field extension and `b : \u03b9 \u2192 L`, then `discr K b` is the square of the\ndeterminant of the matrix whose `(i, j)` coefficient is `\u03c3\u2c7c (b i)`, where `\u03c3\u2c7c : L \u2192\u2090[K] E` is the\nembedding in an algebraically closed field `E` corresponding to `j : \u03b9` via a bijection\n`e : \u03b9 \u2243 (L \u2192\u2090[K] E)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.discr_is_integral", "description": " If `K` and `L` are fields and `is_scalar_tower R K L`, and `b : \u03b9 \u2192 L` satisfies\n` \u2200 i, is_integral R (b i)`, then `is_integral R (discr K b)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.discr_power_basis_eq_prod'", "description": "A variation of `of_power_basis_eq_prod`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.discr_is_unit_of_basis", "description": "Over a field, if `b` is a basis, then `algebra.discr K b` is a unit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.discr_power_basis_eq_prod''", "description": "A variation of `of_power_basis_eq_prod`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mv_polynomial_X_map_eval\u2082", "description": " Any matrix `A` can be expressed as the evaluation of `matrix.mv_polynomial_X`.\n\nThis is of particular use when `mv_polynomial (m \u00d7 n) R` is an integral domain but `S` is\nnot, as if the `mv_polynomial.eval\u2082` can be pulled to the outside of a goal, it can be solved in\nunder cancellative assumptions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mv_polynomial_X_map_matrix_eval", "description": "A variant of `matrix.mv_polynomial_X_map_eval\u2082` with a bundled `ring_hom` on the LHS.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mv_polynomial_X_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mv_polynomial_X_map_matrix_aeval", "description": "A variant of `matrix.mv_polynomial_X_map_eval\u2082` with a bundled `alg_hom` on the LHS.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_mv_polynomial_X_ne_zero", "description": "In a nontrivial ring, `matrix.mv_polynomial_X m m R` has non-zero determinant.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.mv_polynomial_X", "description": "The matrix with variable `X (i,j)` at location `(i,j)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_equalizer_of_preserves_kernels", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories preserves the equalizer of two\nmorphisms if it preserves all kernels.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_coproduct_of_preserves_cokernels", "description": " A cokernel preserving functor between preadditive categories preserves any pair being\na colimit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_binary_product_of_preserves_kernels", "description": "A kernel preserving functor between preadditive categories preserves any pair being a limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.is_colimit_map_cocone_binary_cofan_of_preserves_cokernels", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories which preserves cokernels preserves finite coproducts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_binary_coproducts_of_preserves_cokernels", "description": "A cokernel preserving functor between preadditive categories preserves binary coproducts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_finite_limits_of_preserves_kernels", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories which preserves kernels preserves all finite limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_equalizers_of_preserves_kernels", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories preserves all equalizers if it preserves all kernels.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.is_limit_map_cone_binary_fan_of_preserves_kernels", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories which preserves kernels preserves that an\narbitrary binary fan is a limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_coequalizer_of_preserves_cokernels", "description": "A functor between preadditive categoris preserves the coequalizer of two\nmorphisms if it preserves all cokernels.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_binary_products_of_preserves_kernels", "description": "A kernel preserving functor between preadditive categories preserves binary products.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_coequalizers_of_preserves_cokernels", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories preserves all coequalizers if it preserves all kernels.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.preserves_finite_colimits_of_preserves_cokernels", "description": "A functor between preadditive categories which preserves kernels preserves all finite limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2082.category_theory.reflects_isomorphisms", "description": " We deduce that `N\u2082 : karoubi (simplicial_object C) \u2964 karoubi (chain_complex C \u2115))`\nreflects isomorphisms from the fact that\n`N\u2081 : simplicial_object (karoubi C) \u2964 karoubi (chain_complex (karoubi C) \u2115)` does.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2081.category_theory.reflects_isomorphisms", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.compatibility_N\u2082_N\u2081_karoubi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_root_of_unity_of_root_cyclotomic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_root_cyclotomic_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.roots_cyclotomic_nodup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_primitive_root.minpoly_dvd_cyclotomic", "description": "The minimal polynomial of a primitive `n`-th root of unity `\u03bc` divides `cyclotomic n \u2124`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.cyclotomic.irreducible", "description": "`cyclotomic n \u2124` is irreducible.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.cyclotomic.irreducible_rat", "description": "`cyclotomic n \u211a` is irreducible.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.cyclotomic_eq_minpoly", "description": "`cyclotomic n \u2124` is the minimal polynomial of a primitive `n`-th root of unity `\u03bc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_root_of_unity_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.cyclotomic_eq_minpoly_rat", "description": "`cyclotomic n \u211a` is the minimal polynomial of a primitive `n`-th root of unity `\u03bc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.cyclotomic_injective", "description": "Over a ring `R` of characteristic zero, `\u03bb n, cyclotomic n R` is injective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.is_root_cyclotomic_iff_char_zero", "description": " If `R` is of characteristic zero, then `\u03b6` is a root of `cyclotomic n R` if and only if it is a\nprimitive `n`-th root of unity.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_primitive_root.minpoly_eq_cyclotomic_of_irreducible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.cyclotomic.roots_eq_primitive_roots_val", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_primitive_root.is_root_cyclotomic", "description": "Any `n`-th primitive root of unity is a root of `cyclotomic n R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.cyclotomic.roots_to_finset_eq_primitive_roots", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.cyclotomic.is_coprime_rat", "description": "If `n \u2260 m`, then `(cyclotomic n \u211a)` and `(cyclotomic m \u211a)` are coprime.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.integrable_of_summable_norm_Icc", "description": " If the sequence with `n`-th term the the sup norm of `\u03bb x, f (x + n)` on the interval `Icc 0 1`,\nfor `n \u2208 \u2124`, is summable, then `f` is integrable on `\u211d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integral_comp_neg_Iic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "volume_region_between_eq_integral'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "volume_region_between_eq_integral", "description": "If two functions are integrable on a measurable set, and one function is less than\n   or equal to the other on that set, then the volume of the region\n   between the two functions can be represented as an integral.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integral_comp_neg_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.game_add_mk_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.game_add.to_sym2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.game_add_swap_swap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.game_add.snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.game_add.fix_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.game_add.induction", "description": "Induction on the well-founded `prod.game_add` relation.\n\n Note that it's strictly more general to induct on the lexicographic order instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.game_add.fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.game_add", "description": "`prod.game_add r\u03b1 r\u03b2 x y` means that `x` can be reached from `y` by decreasing either entry with\n respect to the relations `r\u03b1` and `r\u03b2`.\n\n It is so called, as it models game addition within combinatorial game theory. If `r\u03b1 a\u2081 a\u2082` means\n that `a\u2082 \u27f6 a\u2081` is a valid move in game `\u03b1`, and `r\u03b2 b\u2081 b\u2082` means that `b\u2082 \u27f6 b\u2081` is a valid move\n in game `\u03b2`, then `game_add r\u03b1 r\u03b2` specifies the valid moves in the juxtaposition of `\u03b1` and `\u03b2`:\n the player is free to choose one of the games and make a move in it, while leaving the other game\n unchanged.\n\n See `sym2.game_add` for the unordered pair analog.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.game_add.fst", "description": "`prod.game_add r\u03b1 r\u03b2 x y` means that `x` can be reached from `y` by decreasing either entry with\n respect to the relations `r\u03b1` and `r\u03b2`.\n\n It is so called, as it models game addition within combinatorial game theory. If `r\u03b1 a\u2081 a\u2082` means\n that `a\u2082 \u27f6 a\u2081` is a valid move in game `\u03b1`, and `r\u03b2 b\u2081 b\u2082` means that `b\u2082 \u27f6 b\u2081` is a valid move\n in game `\u03b2`, then `game_add r\u03b1 r\u03b2` specifies the valid moves in the juxtaposition of `\u03b1` and `\u03b2`:\n the player is free to choose one of the games and make a move in it, while leaving the other game\n unchanged.\n\n See `sym2.game_add` for the unordered pair analog.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.game_add.snd", "description": "`prod.game_add r\u03b1 r\u03b2 x y` means that `x` can be reached from `y` by decreasing either entry with\n respect to the relations `r\u03b1` and `r\u03b2`.\n\n It is so called, as it models game addition within combinatorial game theory. If `r\u03b1 a\u2081 a\u2082` means\n that `a\u2082 \u27f6 a\u2081` is a valid move in game `\u03b1`, and `r\u03b2 b\u2081 b\u2082` means that `b\u2082 \u27f6 b\u2081` is a valid move\n in game `\u03b2`, then `game_add r\u03b1 r\u03b2` specifies the valid moves in the juxtaposition of `\u03b1` and `\u03b2`:\n the player is free to choose one of the games and make a move in it, while leaving the other game\n unchanged.\n\n See `sym2.game_add` for the unordered pair analog.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.game_add.fix_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.game_add_le_lex", "description": "`prod.game_add` is a `subrelation` of `prod.lex`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.game_add", "description": "`sym2.game_add r\u03b1 x y` means that `x` can be reached from `y` by decreasing either entry with\n respect to the relation `r\u03b1`.\n\n See `prod.game_add` for the ordered pair analog.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.game_add_swap_swap_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.game_add.fix", "description": "Recursion on the well-founded `prod.game_add` relation.\n\n Note that it's strictly more general to recurse on the lexicographic order instead.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.game_add_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.game_add.snd_fst", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "acc.sym2_game_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.rprod_le_trans_gen_game_add", "description": "`prod.rprod` is a subrelation of the transitive closure of `prod.game_add`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.game_add_mk_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.game_add.fst_snd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.game_add.induction", "description": "Induction on the well-founded `sym2.game_add` relation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.game_add_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.sym2_game_add", "description": "The `sym2.game_add` relation on well-founded inputs is well-founded.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym2.game_add.fix", "description": "Recursion on the well-founded `sym2.game_add` relation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.prod_game_add", "description": "The `prod.game_add` relation on well-founded inputs is well-founded.\n\n In particular, the sum of two well-founded games is well-founded.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "acc.prod_game_add", "description": "If `a` is accessible under `r\u03b1` and `b` is accessible under `r\u03b2`, then `(a, b)` is\n accessible under `prod.game_add r\u03b1 r\u03b2`. Notice that `prod.lex_accessible` requires the\n stronger condition `\u2200 b, acc r\u03b2 b`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.algebra.subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.algebra'", "description": " The `zmod n`-algebra structure on rings whose characteristic `m` divides `n`.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zmod.algebra", "description": " The `zmod p`-algebra structure on a ring of characteristic `p`. This is not an\ninstance since it creates a diamond with `algebra.id`.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_pattern", "description": " `mk_pattern umetas emetas target uoutput eoutput` creates a new pattern. The arguments are\n- `umetas` a list of level params to be replaced with temporary universe metavariables.\n- `emetas` a list of local constants to be replaced with temporary metavariables.\n- `target` the expression to be matched.\n- `uoutput` a list of levels to return upon matching. Fails if this depends on metavariables.\n- `eoutput` a list of expressions to return upon matching. Fails if this depends on metavariables.\n\nThe procedure is as follows:\n1. Convert all metavariables in `target` to temporary metavariables.\n2. For each item in `umetas` amd `emetas`, create a temporary\n   metavariable and replace each instance of the item in `target` with a temporary metavariable\n3. Replace these instances in `uoutput` and `eoutput` too.\n4. Return these replaced versions as a `pattern`.\n\n## Example\nLet `h`,`t` be exprs with types `\u03b1` and `list \u03b1` respectively.\nThen `mk_pattern [] [\u03b1,h,t] `(@list.cons \u03b1 h t) [] [h,t]` would `match_pattern` against any expr which is a list.cons constructor and return the head and tail arguments.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.match_subexpr", "description": "Similar to match_expr, but it tries to match a subexpression of e.\n  Remark: the procedure does not go inside binders.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.match_hypothesis", "description": "Match hypothesis in the main goal target.\n  The result is pair (hypothesis, substitution).", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.match_expr", "description": "Convert pre-term into a pattern and try to match e.\n  Given p of the form `\u03bb x\u2081 ... x\u2099, t[x\u2081, ..., x\u2099]`, a successful\n  match will produce a list of length n.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.match_target_subexpr", "description": "Match a subterm in the main goal target.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.pattern.has_to_tactic_format", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.match_target", "description": "Match the main goal target.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.match_pattern", "description": "`mk_pattern p e m` matches (pattern.target p) and e using transparency m.\n  If the matching is successful, then return the instantiation of `pattern.output p`.\n  The tactic fails if not all (temporary) meta-variables are assigned.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.pattern", "description": " A pattern is an expression `target` containing temporary metavariables.\nA pattern also contains a list of `outputs` which also depend on these temporary metavariables.\nWhen we run `match p e`, the system will match `p.target` with `e` and assign\nthe temporary metavariables. It then returns the outputs with the assigned variables.\n\n## Fields\n\n- `target` Term to match. Contains temporary metavariables.\n- `uoutput` List of universes that are returned on a successful match.\n- `moutput` List of expressions that are returned on a successful match.\n- `nuvars` Number of (temporary) universe metavariables in this pattern.\n- `nmvars` Number of (temporary) metavariables in this pattern.\n\n## Example\n\nThe pattern for `list.cons h t` returning `h` and `t` would be\n```lean\n{ target  := `(@list.cons ?x_0 ?x_1 ?x_2),\n  uoutput := [],\n  moutput := [?x_1,?x_2],\n  nuvars  := 0,\n  nmvars  := 3\n}\n```", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.pattern.target", "description": " A pattern is an expression `target` containing temporary metavariables.\nA pattern also contains a list of `outputs` which also depend on these temporary metavariables.\nWhen we run `match p e`, the system will match `p.target` with `e` and assign\nthe temporary metavariables. It then returns the outputs with the assigned variables.\n\n## Fields\n\n- `target` Term to match. Contains temporary metavariables.\n- `uoutput` List of universes that are returned on a successful match.\n- `moutput` List of expressions that are returned on a successful match.\n- `nuvars` Number of (temporary) universe metavariables in this pattern.\n- `nmvars` Number of (temporary) metavariables in this pattern.\n\n## Example\n\nThe pattern for `list.cons h t` returning `h` and `t` would be\n```lean\n{ target  := `(@list.cons ?x_0 ?x_1 ?x_2),\n  uoutput := [],\n  moutput := [?x_1,?x_2],\n  nuvars  := 0,\n  nmvars  := 3\n}\n```", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.pattern.uoutput", "description": " A pattern is an expression `target` containing temporary metavariables.\nA pattern also contains a list of `outputs` which also depend on these temporary metavariables.\nWhen we run `match p e`, the system will match `p.target` with `e` and assign\nthe temporary metavariables. It then returns the outputs with the assigned variables.\n\n## Fields\n\n- `target` Term to match. Contains temporary metavariables.\n- `uoutput` List of universes that are returned on a successful match.\n- `moutput` List of expressions that are returned on a successful match.\n- `nuvars` Number of (temporary) universe metavariables in this pattern.\n- `nmvars` Number of (temporary) metavariables in this pattern.\n\n## Example\n\nThe pattern for `list.cons h t` returning `h` and `t` would be\n```lean\n{ target  := `(@list.cons ?x_0 ?x_1 ?x_2),\n  uoutput := [],\n  moutput := [?x_1,?x_2],\n  nuvars  := 0,\n  nmvars  := 3\n}\n```", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.pattern.moutput", "description": " A pattern is an expression `target` containing temporary metavariables.\nA pattern also contains a list of `outputs` which also depend on these temporary metavariables.\nWhen we run `match p e`, the system will match `p.target` with `e` and assign\nthe temporary metavariables. It then returns the outputs with the assigned variables.\n\n## Fields\n\n- `target` Term to match. Contains temporary metavariables.\n- `uoutput` List of universes that are returned on a successful match.\n- `moutput` List of expressions that are returned on a successful match.\n- `nuvars` Number of (temporary) universe metavariables in this pattern.\n- `nmvars` Number of (temporary) metavariables in this pattern.\n\n## Example\n\nThe pattern for `list.cons h t` returning `h` and `t` would be\n```lean\n{ target  := `(@list.cons ?x_0 ?x_1 ?x_2),\n  uoutput := [],\n  moutput := [?x_1,?x_2],\n  nuvars  := 0,\n  nmvars  := 3\n}\n```", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.pattern.nuvars", "description": " A pattern is an expression `target` containing temporary metavariables.\nA pattern also contains a list of `outputs` which also depend on these temporary metavariables.\nWhen we run `match p e`, the system will match `p.target` with `e` and assign\nthe temporary metavariables. It then returns the outputs with the assigned variables.\n\n## Fields\n\n- `target` Term to match. Contains temporary metavariables.\n- `uoutput` List of universes that are returned on a successful match.\n- `moutput` List of expressions that are returned on a successful match.\n- `nuvars` Number of (temporary) universe metavariables in this pattern.\n- `nmvars` Number of (temporary) metavariables in this pattern.\n\n## Example\n\nThe pattern for `list.cons h t` returning `h` and `t` would be\n```lean\n{ target  := `(@list.cons ?x_0 ?x_1 ?x_2),\n  uoutput := [],\n  moutput := [?x_1,?x_2],\n  nuvars  := 0,\n  nmvars  := 3\n}\n```", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.pattern.nmvars", "description": " A pattern is an expression `target` containing temporary metavariables.\nA pattern also contains a list of `outputs` which also depend on these temporary metavariables.\nWhen we run `match p e`, the system will match `p.target` with `e` and assign\nthe temporary metavariables. It then returns the outputs with the assigned variables.\n\n## Fields\n\n- `target` Term to match. Contains temporary metavariables.\n- `uoutput` List of universes that are returned on a successful match.\n- `moutput` List of expressions that are returned on a successful match.\n- `nuvars` Number of (temporary) universe metavariables in this pattern.\n- `nmvars` Number of (temporary) metavariables in this pattern.\n\n## Example\n\nThe pattern for `list.cons h t` returning `h` and `t` would be\n```lean\n{ target  := `(@list.cons ?x_0 ?x_1 ?x_2),\n  uoutput := [],\n  moutput := [?x_1,?x_2],\n  nuvars  := 0,\n  nmvars  := 3\n}\n```", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.pexpr_to_pattern", "description": "Given a pre-term of the form `\u03bb x\u2081 ... x\u2099, t[x\u2081, ..., x\u2099]`, converts it\n  into the pattern `t[?x\u2081, ..., ?x\u2099]` with outputs `[?x\u2081, ..., ?x\u2099]`", "p": "core"}, {"name": "weak_dual.is_closed_image_coe_of_bounded_of_closed", "description": " While the coercion `coe_fn : weak_dual \ud835\udd5c E \u2192 (E \u2192 \ud835\udd5c)` is not a closed map, it sends *bounded*\nclosed sets to closed sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.to_normed_dual_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.polar_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.to_normed_dual", "description": " For normed spaces `E`, there is a canonical map `weak_dual \ud835\udd5c E \u2192 dual \ud835\udd5c E` (the \"identity\"\nmapping). It is a linear equivalence. Here it is implemented as the inverse of the linear\nequivalence `normed_space.dual.to_weak_dual` in the other direction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.to_normed_dual_eq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.is_compact_of_bounded_of_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_space.dual.coe_to_weak_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_space.dual.is_closed_image_polar_of_mem_nhds", "description": " The image under `coe_fn : normed_space.dual \ud835\udd5c E \u2192 (E \u2192 \ud835\udd5c)` of a polar `polar \ud835\udd5c s` of a\nneighborhood `s` of the origin is a closed set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.is_compact_closed_ball", "description": " The **Banach-Alaoglu theorem**: closed balls of the dual of a normed space `E` are compact in\nthe weak-star topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.is_closed_image_polar_of_mem_nhds", "description": " The image under `coe_fn : weak_dual \ud835\udd5c E \u2192 (E \u2192 \ud835\udd5c)` of a polar `weak_dual.polar \ud835\udd5c s` of a\nneighborhood `s` of the origin is a closed set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_space.dual.to_weak_dual", "description": " For normed spaces `E`, there is a canonical map `dual \ud835\udd5c E \u2192 weak_dual \ud835\udd5c E` (the \"identity\"\nmapping). It is a linear equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.is_closed_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.is_closed_polar", "description": " The polar `polar \ud835\udd5c s` of a set `s : E` is a closed subset when the weak star topology\nis used.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.is_compact_polar", "description": " The **Banach-Alaoglu theorem**: the polar set of a neighborhood `s` of the origin in a\nnormed space `E` is a compact subset of `weak_dual \ud835\udd5c E`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_space.dual.to_weak_dual_continuous", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_space.dual.to_weak_dual_eq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_space.dual.continuous_linear_map_to_weak_dual", "description": " For a normed space `E`, according to `to_weak_dual_continuous` the \"identity mapping\"\n`dual \ud835\udd5c E \u2192 weak_dual \ud835\udd5c E` is continuous. This definition implements it as a continuous linear\nmap.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.coe_to_normed_dual", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_space.dual.dual_norm_topology_le_weak_dual_topology", "description": "The weak-star topology is coarser than the dual-norm topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.polar", "description": " The polar set `polar \ud835\udd5c s` of `s : set E` seen as a subset of the dual of `E` with the\nweak-star topology is `weak_dual.polar \ud835\udd5c s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.equiv.refl", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "function.equiv.trans", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "function.equiv.is_equivalence", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "function.fun_to_extfun", "description": "The map from functions into the qquotient by pointwise equality.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "funext", "description": "Function extensionality, proven using quotients.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "pi.subsingleton", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "function.equiv.symm", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "function.extfun_app", "description": "From an element of `extfun` we can retrieve an actual function.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "function.equiv", "description": "The relation stating that two functions are pointwise equal.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "function.extfun", "description": "The quotient of the function type by pointwise equality.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "function.fun_setoid", "description": "The setoid generated by pointwise equality.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_initial_of_weakly_initial_and_has_wide_equalizers", "description": "If `C` has (small) wide equalizers and a weakly initial object, then it has an initial object.\n\nThe initial object is constructed as the wide equalizer of all endomorphisms on the given weakly\ninitial object.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_weakly_initial_of_weakly_initial_set_and_has_products", "description": "If `C` has (small) products and a small weakly initial set of objects, then it has a weakly initial\nobject.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "szemeredi_regularity.off_diag_pairs_le_increment_energy", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "szemeredi_regularity.increment_is_equipartition", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "szemeredi_regularity.pair_contrib_lower_bound", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "szemeredi_regularity.card_increment", "description": "The increment partition has a prescribed (very big) size in terms of the original partition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "szemeredi_regularity.energy_increment", "description": "The increment partition has energy greater than the original one by a known fixed amount.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "szemeredi_regularity.uniform_add_nonuniform_eq_off_diag_pairs", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "szemeredi_regularity.increment", "description": " The **increment partition** in Szemer\u00e9di's Regularity Lemma.\n\nIf an equipartition is *not* uniform, then the increment partition is a (much bigger) equipartition\nwith a slightly higher energy. This is helpful since the energy is bounded by a constant (see\n`szemeredi_regularity.energy_le_one`), so this process eventually terminates and yields a\nnot-too-big uniform equipartition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.unused_decls_cmd", "description": " The command `#list_unused_decls` lists the declarations that that\nare not used the main features of the present file. The main features\nof a file are taken as the declaration tagged with\n`@[main_declaration]`.\n\nA list of files can be given to `#list_unused_decls` as follows:\n\n```lean\n#list_unused_decls [\"src/tactic/core.lean\",\"src/tactic/interactive.lean\"]\n```\n\nThey are given in a list that contains file names written as Lean\nstrings. With a list of files, the declarations from all those files\nin addition to the declarations above `#list_unused_decls` in the\ncurrent file will be considered and their interdependencies will be\nanalyzed to see which declarations are unused by declarations marked\nas `@[main_declaration]`. The files listed must be imported by the\ncurrent file. The path of the file names is expected to be relative to\nthe root of the project (i.e. the location of `leanpkg.toml` when it\nis present).\n\nNeither `#list_unused_decls` nor `@[main_declaration]` should appear\nin a finished mathlib development.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.all_unused", "description": " In the current file, list all the declaration that are not marked as `@[main_declaration]` and\nthat are not referenced by such declarations", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.main_declaration_attr", "description": " Attribute `main_declaration` is used to mark declarations that are featured\nin the current file.  Then, the `#list_unused_decls` command can be used to\nlist the declaration present in the file that are not used by the main\ndeclarations of the file.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.command.#list_unused_decls", "description": " The command `#list_unused_decls` lists the declarations that that\nare not used the main features of the present file. The main features\nof a file are taken as the declaration tagged with\n`@[main_declaration]`.\n\nA list of files can be given to `#list_unused_decls` as follows:\n\n```lean\n#list_unused_decls [\"src/tactic/core.lean\",\"src/tactic/interactive.lean\"]\n```\n\nThey are given in a list that contains file names written as Lean\nstrings. With a list of files, the declarations from all those files\nin addition to the declarations above `#list_unused_decls` in the\ncurrent file will be considered and their interdependencies will be\nanalyzed to see which declarations are unused by declarations marked\nas `@[main_declaration]`. The files listed must be imported by the\ncurrent file. The path of the file names is expected to be relative to\nthe root of the project (i.e. the location of `leanpkg.toml` when it\nis present).\n\nNeither `#list_unused_decls` nor `@[main_declaration]` should appear\nin a finished mathlib development.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.parse_file_name", "description": "expecting a string literal (e.g. `\"src/tactic/find_unused.lean\"`)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj.symm_adjoint", "description": " If an order automorphism `fa` is semiconjugate to an order embedding `fb` by a function `g`\nand `g'` is an order right adjoint of `g` (i.e. `g' y = Sup {x | f x \u2264 y}`), then `fb` is\nsemiconjugate to `fa` by `g'`.\n\nThis is a version of Proposition 2.1 from [\u00c9tienne Ghys, Groupes d'homeomorphismes du cercle et\ncohomologie bornee][ghys87:groupes].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_order_right_adjoint_cSup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.Sup_div_semiconj", "description": " Consider two actions `f\u2081 f\u2082 : G \u2192 \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1` of a group on a complete lattice by order\nisomorphisms. Then the map `x \u21a6 \u2a06 g : G, (f\u2081 g)\u207b\u00b9 (f\u2082 g x)` semiconjugates each `f\u2081 g'` to `f\u2082 g'`.\n\nThis is a version of Proposition 5.4 from [\u00c9tienne Ghys, Groupes d'homeomorphismes du cercle et\ncohomologie bornee][ghys87:groupes].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_order_right_adjoint.order_iso_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.cSup_div_semiconj", "description": " Consider two actions `f\u2081 f\u2082 : G \u2192 \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1` of a group on a conditionally complete lattice by order\nisomorphisms. Suppose that each set $s(x)=\\{f_1(g)^{-1} (f_2(g)(x)) | g \\in G\\}$ is bounded above.\nThen the map `x \u21a6 Sup s(x)` semiconjugates each `f\u2081 g'` to `f\u2082 g'`.\n\nThis is a version of Proposition 5.4 from [\u00c9tienne Ghys, Groupes d'homeomorphismes du cercle et\ncohomologie bornee][ghys87:groupes].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_order_right_adjoint.comp_order_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_order_right_adjoint", "description": " We say that `g : \u03b2 \u2192 \u03b1` is an order right adjoint function for `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` if it sends each `y`\nto a least upper bound for `{x | f x \u2264 y}`. If `\u03b1` is a partial order, and `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` has\na right adjoint, then this right adjoint is unique.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_order_right_adjoint_Sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.semiconj_of_is_lub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_order_right_adjoint.right_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_order_right_adjoint.unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_one_half", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi_midpoint_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homothety_inv_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homothety_inv_of_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "homothety_one_half", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "line_map_inv_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.card_embedding_eq_of_infinite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.card_embedding_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.card_embedding_eq_of_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_initial_of_pullback_of_is_coproduct", "description": "If the coproduct of `X\u2081` and `X\u2082` is disjoint, then provided `X\u2081 \u27f6 X \u2190 X\u2082` is a coproduct the\npullback is an initial object:\n\n        X\u2081\n        \u2193\nX\u2082 \u27f6  X", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_initial_of_is_pullback_of_coproduct", "description": "If the coproduct of `X\u2081` and `X\u2082` is disjoint, then given any pullback square\n\nZ  \u27f6  X\u2081\n\u2193       \u2193\nX\u2082 \u27f6  X\u2081 \u2a3f X\u2082\n\n`Z` is initial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.initial_mono_class_of_disjoint_coproducts", "description": " If `C` has disjoint coproducts, any morphism out of initial is mono. Note it isn't true in\ngeneral that `C` has strict initial objects, for instance consider the category of types and\npartial functions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_initial_of_pullback_of_coproduct", "description": "If the coproduct of `X\u2081` and `X\u2082` is disjoint, the pullback of `X\u2081 \u27f6 X\u2081 \u2a3f X\u2082` and `X\u2082 \u27f6 X\u2081 \u2a3f X\u2082`\nis initial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.coproduct_disjoint", "description": "Given any pullback diagram of the form\n\nZ  \u27f6  X\u2081\n\u2193      \u2193\nX\u2082 \u27f6  X\n\nwhere `X\u2081 \u27f6 X \u2190 X\u2082` is a coproduct diagram, then `Z` is initial, and both `X\u2081 \u27f6 X` and `X\u2082 \u27f6 X`\nare mono.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.coproduct_disjoint.is_initial_of_is_pullback_of_is_coproduct", "description": "Given any pullback diagram of the form\n\nZ  \u27f6  X\u2081\n\u2193      \u2193\nX\u2082 \u27f6  X\n\nwhere `X\u2081 \u27f6 X \u2190 X\u2082` is a coproduct diagram, then `Z` is initial, and both `X\u2081 \u27f6 X` and `X\u2082 \u27f6 X`\nare mono.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.coproduct_disjoint.mono_inl", "description": "Given any pullback diagram of the form\n\nZ  \u27f6  X\u2081\n\u2193      \u2193\nX\u2082 \u27f6  X\n\nwhere `X\u2081 \u27f6 X \u2190 X\u2082` is a coproduct diagram, then `Z` is initial, and both `X\u2081 \u27f6 X` and `X\u2082 \u27f6 X`\nare mono.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.coproduct_disjoint.mono_inr", "description": "Given any pullback diagram of the form\n\nZ  \u27f6  X\u2081\n\u2193      \u2193\nX\u2082 \u27f6  X\n\nwhere `X\u2081 \u27f6 X \u2190 X\u2082` is a coproduct diagram, then `Z` is initial, and both `X\u2081 \u27f6 X` and `X\u2082 \u27f6 X`\nare mono.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.is_initial_of_is_pullback_of_is_coproduct", "description": "If the coproduct of `X\u2081` and `X\u2082` is disjoint, then given any pullback square\n\nZ  \u27f6  X\u2081\n\u2193      \u2193\nX\u2082 \u27f6  X\n\nwhere `X\u2081 \u27f6 X \u2190 X\u2082` is a coproduct, then `Z` is initial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.coproducts_disjoint", "description": "`C` has disjoint coproducts if every coproduct is disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.coproducts_disjoint.coproduct_disjoint", "description": "`C` has disjoint coproducts if every coproduct is disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.coprod.inl.category_theory.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.coprod.inr.category_theory.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_dec_eq_instance", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_dec_eq_instance_core", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.binder_info.has_decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.decidable_eq_derive_handler", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "DFA.to_NFA", "description": "`M.to_NFA` is an `NFA` constructed from a `DFA` `M` by using the same start and accept\n states and a transition function which sends `s` with input `a` to the singleton `M.step s a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA.eval_from_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA.pumping_lemma", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA.accepts", "description": "`M.accepts` is the language of `x` such that there is an accept state in `M.eval x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA.to_DFA_correct", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA.eval_from_append_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA.eval_append_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA.eval_from_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.to_NFA_correct", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "DFA.to_NFA_eval_from_match", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA.eval_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA.step_set_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA", "description": "An NFA is a set of states (`\u03c3`), a transition function from state to state labelled by the\n alphabet (`step`), a starting state (`start`) and a set of acceptance states (`accept`).\n Note the transition function sends a state to a `set` of states. These are the states that it\n may be sent to.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA.step", "description": "An NFA is a set of states (`\u03c3`), a transition function from state to state labelled by the\n alphabet (`step`), a starting state (`start`) and a set of acceptance states (`accept`).\n Note the transition function sends a state to a `set` of states. These are the states that it\n may be sent to.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA.start", "description": "An NFA is a set of states (`\u03c3`), a transition function from state to state labelled by the\n alphabet (`step`), a starting state (`start`) and a set of acceptance states (`accept`).\n Note the transition function sends a state to a `set` of states. These are the states that it\n may be sent to.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA.accept", "description": "An NFA is a set of states (`\u03c3`), a transition function from state to state labelled by the\n alphabet (`step`), a starting state (`start`) and a set of acceptance states (`accept`).\n Note the transition function sends a state to a `set` of states. These are the states that it\n may be sent to.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA.eval", "description": "`M.eval x` computes all possible paths though `M` with input `x` starting at an element of\n `M.start`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA.eval_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA.mem_step_set", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA.step_set", "description": "`M.step_set S a` is the union of `M.step s a` for all `s \u2208 S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA.to_DFA", "description": "`M.to_DFA` is an `DFA` constructed from a `NFA` `M` using the subset construction. The\n states is the type of `set`s of `M.state` and the step function is `M.step_set`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "NFA.eval_from", "description": "`M.eval_from S x` computes all possible paths though `M` with input `x` starting at an element\n of `S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.charpoly_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.aeval_self_charpoly", "description": " The **Cayley-Hamilton Theorem**, that the characteristic polynomial of a linear map, applied\nto the linear map itself, is zero.\n\nSee `matrix.aeval_self_charpoly` for the equivalent statement about matrices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.charpoly_monic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_integral", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.pow_eq_aeval_mod_charpoly", "description": " Any endomorphism power can be computed as the sum of endomorphism powers less than the\ndimension of the module.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.charpoly", "description": "The characteristic polynomial of `f : M \u2192\u2097[R] M`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.minpoly_coeff_zero_of_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.aeval_eq_aeval_mod_charpoly", "description": " Any endomorphism polynomial `p` is equivalent under evaluation to `p %\u2098 f.charpoly`; that is,\n`p` is equivalent to a polynomial with degree less than the dimension of the module.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.minpoly_dvd_charpoly", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.prepartition.distortion_to_subordinate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.tagged_prepartition.is_partition_union_compl_to_subordinate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.prepartition.exists_tagged_le_is_Henstock_is_subordinate_Union_eq", "description": " Given a box `I` in `\u211d\u207f`, a function `r : \u211d\u207f \u2192 (0, \u221e)`, and a prepartition `\u03c0` of `I`, there\nexists a tagged prepartition `\u03c0'` of `I` such that\n\n* each box of `\u03c0'` is included in some box of `\u03c0`;\n* `\u03c0'` is a Henstock partition;\n* `\u03c0'` is subordinate to `r`;\n* `\u03c0'` covers exactly the same part of `I` as `\u03c0`;\n* the distortion of `\u03c0'` is equal to the distortion of `\u03c0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.tagged_prepartition.Union_union_compl_to_subordinate_boxes", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.prepartition.is_partition_split_center", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.tagged_prepartition.distortion_union_compl_to_subordinate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.tagged_prepartition.union_compl_to_subordinate_boxes", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.prepartition.mem_split_center", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.prepartition.split_center", "description": "Split a box in `\u211d\u207f` into `2 ^ n` boxes by hyperplanes passing through its center.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.subbox_induction_on", "description": " Let `p` be a predicate on `box \u03b9`, let `I` be a box. Suppose that the following two properties\nhold true.\n\n* Consider a smaller box `J \u2264 I`. The hyperplanes passing through the center of `J` split it into\n  `2 ^ n` boxes. If `p` holds true on each of these boxes, then it true on `J`.\n* For each `z` in the closed box `I.Icc` there exists a neighborhood `U` of `z` within `I.Icc` such\n  that for every box `J \u2264 I` such that `z \u2208 J.Icc \u2286 U`, if `J` is homothetic to `I` with a\n  coefficient of the form `1 / 2 ^ m`, then `p` is true on `J`.\n\nThen `p I` is true. See also `box_integral.box.subbox_induction_on'` for a version using\n`box_integral.box.split_center_box` instead of `box_integral.prepartition.split_center`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.box.exists_tagged_partition_is_Henstock_is_subordinate_homothetic", "description": " Given a box `I` in `\u211d\u207f` and a function `r : \u211d\u207f \u2192 (0, \u221e)`, there exists a tagged partition `\u03c0` of\n`I` such that\n\n* `\u03c0` is a Henstock partition;\n* `\u03c0` is subordinate to `r`;\n* each box in `\u03c0` is homothetic to `I` with coefficient of the form `1 / 2 ^ m`.\n\nThis lemma implies that the Henstock filter is nontrivial, hence the Henstock integral is\nwell-defined.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.prepartition.is_Henstock_to_subordinate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.prepartition.to_subordinate_to_prepartition_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.prepartition.is_subordinate_to_subordinate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.prepartition.Union_to_subordinate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.tagged_prepartition.union_compl_to_subordinate", "description": " Given a tagged prepartition `\u03c0\u2081`, a prepartition `\u03c0\u2082` that covers exactly `I \\ \u03c0\u2081.Union`, and\na function `r : \u211d\u207f \u2192 (0, \u221e)`, returns the union of `\u03c0\u2081` and `\u03c0\u2082.to_subordinate r`. This partition\n`\u03c0` has the following properties:\n\n* `\u03c0` is a partition, i.e. it covers the whole `I`;\n* `\u03c0\u2081.boxes \u2286 \u03c0.boxes`;\n* `\u03c0.tag J = \u03c0\u2081.tag J` whenever `J \u2208 \u03c0\u2081`;\n* `\u03c0` is Henstock outside of `\u03c0\u2081`: `\u03c0.tag J \u2208 J.Icc` whenever `J \u2208 \u03c0`, `J \u2209 \u03c0\u2081`;\n* `\u03c0` is subordinate to `r` outside of `\u03c0\u2081`;\n* the distortion of `\u03c0` is equal to the maximum of the distortions of `\u03c0\u2081` and `\u03c0\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.prepartition.to_subordinate", "description": " Given a prepartition `\u03c0` of a box `I` and a function `r : \u211d\u207f \u2192 (0, \u221e)`, `\u03c0.to_subordinate r`\nis a tagged partition `\u03c0'` such that\n\n* each box of `\u03c0'` is included in some box of `\u03c0`;\n* `\u03c0'` is a Henstock partition;\n* `\u03c0'` is subordinate to `r`;\n* `\u03c0'` covers exactly the same part of `I` as `\u03c0`;\n* the distortion of `\u03c0'` is equal to the distortion of `\u03c0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.prepartition.upper_sub_lower_of_mem_split_center", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pos_num.decidable_prime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "num.decidable_prime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pos_num.min_fac_aux_to_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pos_num.min_fac", "description": "Returns the smallest prime factor of `n \u2260 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pos_num.min_fac_to_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "num.min_fac", "description": "Returns the smallest prime factor of `n \u2260 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pos_num.prime", "description": "Primality predicate for a `pos_num`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "num.min_fac_to_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pos_num.min_fac_aux", "description": " Auxiliary function for computing the smallest prime factor of a `pos_num`. Unlike\n`nat.min_fac_aux`, we use a natural number `fuel` variable that is set to an upper bound on the\nnumber of iterations. It is initialized to the number `n` we are determining primality for. Even\nthough this is exponential in the input (since it is a `nat`, not a `num`), it will get lazily\nevaluated during kernel reduction, so we will only require about `sqrt n` unfoldings, for the\n`sqrt n` iterations of the loop.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "num.prime", "description": "Primality predicate for a `num`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.foldl_range_eq_of_range_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.foldr_range_eq_of_range_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.foldl_range_subset_of_range_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.inj_on_insert_nth_index_of_not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.foldr_range_subset_of_range_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.sup_univ_eq_supr", "description": "A special case of `finset.sup_eq_supr` that omits the useless `x \u2208 univ` binder.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.fold_sup_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite.exists_max", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.fold_inf_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.inf_univ_eq_infi", "description": "A special case of `finset.inf_eq_infi` that omits the useless `x \u2208 univ` binder.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite.exists_min", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.product_cone_is_limit", "description": "The concrete product cone is limiting.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.pi_iso_pi", "description": "The categorical product of a family of objects in `Module`\nagrees with the usual module-theoretical product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.pi_iso_pi_inv_kernel_\u03b9", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.pi_iso_pi_inv_kernel_\u03b9_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.pi_iso_pi_hom_ker_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.pi_iso_pi_hom_ker_subtype_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.product_cone", "description": "The product cone induced by the concrete product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.generalize_proofs", "description": "Generalize proofs in the goal, naming them with the provided list.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.generalize_proofs", "description": " Generalize proofs in the goal, naming them with the provided list.\n\nFor example:\n```lean\nexample : list.nth_le [1, 2] 1 dec_trivial = 2 :=\nbegin\n  -- \u22a2 [1, 2].nth_le 1 _ = 2\n  generalize_proofs h,\n  -- h : 1 < [1, 2].length\n  -- \u22a2 [1, 2].nth_le 1 h = 2\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.generalize_proofs", "description": " Generalize proofs in the goal, naming them with the provided list.\n\nFor example:\n```lean\nexample : list.nth_le [1, 2] 1 dec_trivial = 2 :=\nbegin\n  -- \u22a2 [1, 2].nth_le 1 _ = 2\n  generalize_proofs h,\n  -- h : 1 < [1, 2].length\n  -- \u22a2 [1, 2].nth_le 1 h = 2\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.det_conj_ae", "description": "The determinant of `conj_ae`, as a linear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.linear_equiv_det_conj_ae", "description": "The determinant of `conj_ae`, as a linear equiv.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.dot_product_self_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.dot_product_star_self_eq_zero", "description": "Note that this applies to `\u2102` via `complex.strict_ordered_comm_ring`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.dot_product_eq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.dot_product_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.dot_product_eq_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.dot_product_self_star_eq_zero", "description": "Note that this applies to `\u2102` via `complex.strict_ordered_comm_ring`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.dot_product_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.dot_product_std_basis_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.dot_product_std_basis_eq_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.lim_lax_\u03b5", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_lax_monoidal_\u03bc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.lim_lax_\u03bc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_lax_monoidal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_functorial_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.lim_lax_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_functorial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.lim_lax_obj'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.lim_lax_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.limit_lax_monoidal_\u03b5", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.lim_lax", "description": "The limit functor `F \u21a6 limit F` bundled as a lax monoidal functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.nondegenerate", "description": "A matrix `M` is nondegenerate if for all `v \u2260 0`, there is a `w \u2260 0` with `w \u2b1d M \u2b1d v \u2260 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.eq_zero_of_mul_vec_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.nondegenerate_of_det_ne_zero", "description": " If `M` has a nonzero determinant, then `M` as a bilinear form on `n \u2192 A` is nondegenerate.\n\nSee also `bilin_form.nondegenerate_of_det_ne_zero'` and `bilin_form.nondegenerate_of_det_ne_zero`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.eq_zero_of_vec_mul_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.nondegenerate.eq_zero_of_ortho", "description": "If `M` is nondegenerate and `w \u2b1d M \u2b1d v = 0` for all `w`, then `v = 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.nondegenerate.exists_not_ortho_of_ne_zero", "description": "If `M` is nondegenerate and `v \u2260 0`, then there is some `w` such that `w \u2b1d M \u2b1d v \u2260 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.dual_number_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.dual_number_equiv", "description": "Matrices over dual numbers and dual numbers over matrices are isomorphic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.dual_number_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.unsat_lin_comb_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.unsat_of_unsat_lin_comb", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.lin_comb", "description": "Linear combination of constraints. The second\n   argument is the list of constraints, and the first\n   argument is the list of conefficients by which the\n   constraints are multiplied", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.unsat_lin_comb", "description": "`unsat_lin_comb ns ts` asserts that the linear combination\n   `lin_comb ns ts` is unsatisfiable", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.lin_comb_holds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Borel", "description": "The Borel functor, the canonical embedding of topological spaces into measurable spaces.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Meas.Giry", "description": "The Giry monad, i.e. the monadic structure associated with `Measure`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Meas.of", "description": "Construct a bundled `Meas` from the underlying type and the typeclass.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Meas.large_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Meas.unbundled_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Meas.has_coe_to_sort", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Meas", "description": "The category of measurable spaces and measurable functions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Meas.measurable_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Meas.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Meas.Measure", "description": " `Measure X` is the measurable space of measures over the measurable space `X`. It is the\nweakest measurable space, s.t. \u03bb\u03bc, \u03bc s is measurable for all measurable sets `s` in `X`. An\nimportant purpose is to assign a monadic structure on it, the Giry monad. In the Giry monad,\nthe pure values are the Dirac measure, and the bind operation maps to the integral:\n`(\u03bc >>= \u03bd) s = \u222b x. (\u03bd x) s d\u03bc`.\n\nIn probability theory, the `Meas`-morphisms `X \u2192 Prob X` are (sub-)Markov kernels (here `Prob` is\nthe restriction of `Measure` to (sub-)probability space.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Meas.concrete_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Meas.coe_of", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.has_forget_to_Meas", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Meas.Integral", "description": " An example for an algebra on `Measure`: the nonnegative Lebesgue integral is a hom, behaving\nnicely under the monad operations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.char_p", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sym.sym'.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.nonempty.star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.Inter_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.star_mem_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.star_subset_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.has_involutive_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.star_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.compl_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.star_inv'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.star_preimage", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.star_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.star_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.nonempty_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.has_trivial_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.inter_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite.star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.Union_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.union_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.has_star", "description": " The set `(star s : set \u03b1)` is defined as `{x | star x \u2208 s}` in locale `pointwise`.\nIn the usual case where `star` is involutive, it is equal to `{star s | x \u2208 s}`, see\n`set.image_star`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.image_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.star_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.star_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.star_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.mem_star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.star_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor_thin", "description": "If `C` is a thin category, then `D \u2964 C` is a thin category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subsingleton_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso_of_both_ways", "description": "To show `X \u2245 Y` in a thin category, it suffices to just give any morphism in each direction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.thin_category", "description": "Construct a category instance from a category_struct, using the fact that\n   hom spaces are subsingletons to prove the axioms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char.is_whitespace", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "char.to_lower", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "char.is_lower", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "char.decidable_is_digit", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "char.is_punctuation", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "char.decidable_is_punctuation", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "char.decidable_is_upper", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "char.is_upper", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "char.is_alpha", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "char.is_digit", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "char.is_alphanum", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "char.decidable_is_lower", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "char.decidable_is_whitespace", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "char.decidable_is_alpha", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "char.decidable_is_alphanum", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "category_theory.adjunction.whisker_left_counit_app_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adjunction.whisker_right_unit_app_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adjunction.whisker_left_unit_app_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adjunction.whisker_right_counit_app_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adjunction.whisker_left", "description": "Given an adjunction `F \u22a3 G`, this provides the natural adjunction\n `(whiskering_left _ _ C).obj G \u22a3 (whiskering_left _ _ C).obj F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.adjunction.whisker_right", "description": "Given an adjunction `F \u22a3 G`, this provides the natural adjunction\n `(whiskering_right C _ _).obj F \u22a3 (whiskering_right C _ _).obj G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology.has_coe_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology.order_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology.order_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology.of_grothendieck", "description": "The largest pretopology generating the given Grothendieck topology.\n\nSee [MM92] Chapter III, Section 2, Equations (3,4).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology.to_grothendieck_bot", "description": "The trivial pretopology induces the trivial grothendieck topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology.mem_to_grothendieck", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology.gi", "description": "We have a galois insertion from pretopologies to Grothendieck topologies.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology.le_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology.partial_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology", "description": "A (Grothendieck) pretopology on `C` consists of a collection of families of morphisms with a fixed\ntarget `X` for every object `X` in `C`, called \"coverings\" of `X`, which satisfies the following\nthree axioms:\n1. Every family consisting of a single isomorphism is a covering family.\n2. The collection of covering families is stable under pullback.\n3. Given a covering family, and a covering family on each domain of the former, the composition\n   is a covering family.\n\nIn some sense, a pretopology can be seen as Grothendieck topology with weaker saturation conditions,\nin that each covering is not necessarily downward closed.\n\nSee: https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Grothendieck+pretopology, or\nhttps://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00VH, or [MM92] Chapter III, Section 2, Definition 2.\nNote that Stacks calls a category together with a pretopology a site, and [MM92] calls this\na basis for a topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology.coverings", "description": "A (Grothendieck) pretopology on `C` consists of a collection of families of morphisms with a fixed\ntarget `X` for every object `X` in `C`, called \"coverings\" of `X`, which satisfies the following\nthree axioms:\n1. Every family consisting of a single isomorphism is a covering family.\n2. The collection of covering families is stable under pullback.\n3. Given a covering family, and a covering family on each domain of the former, the composition\n   is a covering family.\n\nIn some sense, a pretopology can be seen as Grothendieck topology with weaker saturation conditions,\nin that each covering is not necessarily downward closed.\n\nSee: https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Grothendieck+pretopology, or\nhttps://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00VH, or [MM92] Chapter III, Section 2, Definition 2.\nNote that Stacks calls a category together with a pretopology a site, and [MM92] calls this\na basis for a topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology.has_isos", "description": "A (Grothendieck) pretopology on `C` consists of a collection of families of morphisms with a fixed\ntarget `X` for every object `X` in `C`, called \"coverings\" of `X`, which satisfies the following\nthree axioms:\n1. Every family consisting of a single isomorphism is a covering family.\n2. The collection of covering families is stable under pullback.\n3. Given a covering family, and a covering family on each domain of the former, the composition\n   is a covering family.\n\nIn some sense, a pretopology can be seen as Grothendieck topology with weaker saturation conditions,\nin that each covering is not necessarily downward closed.\n\nSee: https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Grothendieck+pretopology, or\nhttps://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00VH, or [MM92] Chapter III, Section 2, Definition 2.\nNote that Stacks calls a category together with a pretopology a site, and [MM92] calls this\na basis for a topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology.pullbacks", "description": "A (Grothendieck) pretopology on `C` consists of a collection of families of morphisms with a fixed\ntarget `X` for every object `X` in `C`, called \"coverings\" of `X`, which satisfies the following\nthree axioms:\n1. Every family consisting of a single isomorphism is a covering family.\n2. The collection of covering families is stable under pullback.\n3. Given a covering family, and a covering family on each domain of the former, the composition\n   is a covering family.\n\nIn some sense, a pretopology can be seen as Grothendieck topology with weaker saturation conditions,\nin that each covering is not necessarily downward closed.\n\nSee: https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Grothendieck+pretopology, or\nhttps://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00VH, or [MM92] Chapter III, Section 2, Definition 2.\nNote that Stacks calls a category together with a pretopology a site, and [MM92] calls this\na basis for a topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology.transitive", "description": "A (Grothendieck) pretopology on `C` consists of a collection of families of morphisms with a fixed\ntarget `X` for every object `X` in `C`, called \"coverings\" of `X`, which satisfies the following\nthree axioms:\n1. Every family consisting of a single isomorphism is a covering family.\n2. The collection of covering families is stable under pullback.\n3. Given a covering family, and a covering family on each domain of the former, the composition\n   is a covering family.\n\nIn some sense, a pretopology can be seen as Grothendieck topology with weaker saturation conditions,\nin that each covering is not necessarily downward closed.\n\nSee: https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Grothendieck+pretopology, or\nhttps://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00VH, or [MM92] Chapter III, Section 2, Definition 2.\nNote that Stacks calls a category together with a pretopology a site, and [MM92] calls this\na basis for a topology.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology.trivial", "description": "The trivial pretopology, in which the coverings are exactly singleton isomorphisms. This topology is\nalso known as the indiscrete, coarse, or chaotic topology.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07GE>", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology.has_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.pretopology.to_grothendieck", "description": "A pretopology `K` can be completed to a Grothendieck topology `J` by declaring a sieve to be\n`J`-covering if it contains a family in `K`.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00ZC>, or [MM92] Chapter III, Section 2, Equation (2).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.snd_nat_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.add_monoid_with_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.fst_nat_cast", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.P_infty_to_normalized_Moore_complex_naturality_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.N\u2081_iso_normalized_Moore_complex_comp_to_karoubi", "description": " When the category `A` is abelian,\nthe functor `N\u2081 : simplicial_object A \u2964 karoubi (chain_complex A \u2115)` defined\nusing `P_infty` identifies to the composition of the normalized Moore complex functor\nand the inclusion in the Karoubi envelope.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.P_infty_comp_P_infty_to_normalized_Moore_complex", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.P_infty_comp_P_infty_to_normalized_Moore_complex_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.split_mono_inclusion_of_Moore_complex_map", "description": "`inclusion_of_Moore_complex_map X` is a split mono.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.P_infty_to_normalized_Moore_complex_f", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.higher_faces_vanish.inclusion_of_Moore_complex_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.algebraic_topology.inclusion_of_Moore_complex_map.category_theory.mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.P_infty_to_normalized_Moore_complex_naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.P_infty_to_normalized_Moore_complex_comp_inclusion_of_Moore_complex_map_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.inclusion_of_Moore_complex_map_comp_P_infty_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.P_infty_to_normalized_Moore_complex_comp_inclusion_of_Moore_complex_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.P_infty_to_normalized_Moore_complex", "description": "P_infty factors through the normalized Moore complex", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.inclusion_of_Moore_complex_map_comp_P_infty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebraic_topology.dold_kan.factors_normalized_Moore_complex_P_infty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vadd_univ_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_pi\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vadd_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_univ_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_pi_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vadd_pi_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.alias.alias_attr", "description": "An auxiliary attribute which is placed on definitions created by the `alias` command.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.alias.target.has_reflect", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.command.alias", "description": "The `alias` command can be used to create copies\nof a theorem or definition with different names.\n\nSyntax:\n\n```lean\n/-- doc string -/\nalias my_theorem \u2190 alias1 alias2 ...\n```\n\nThis produces defs or theorems of the form:\n\n```lean\n/-- doc string -/\n@[alias] theorem alias1 : <type of my_theorem> := my_theorem\n\n/-- doc string -/\n@[alias] theorem alias2 : <type of my_theorem> := my_theorem\n```\n\nIff alias syntax:\n\n```lean\nalias A_iff_B \u2194 B_of_A A_of_B\nalias A_iff_B \u2194 ..\n```\n\nThis gets an existing biconditional theorem `A_iff_B` and produces\nthe one-way implications `B_of_A` and `A_of_B` (with no change in\nimplicit arguments). A blank `_` can be used to avoid generating one direction.\nThe `..` notation attempts to generate the 'of'-names automatically when the\ninput theorem has the form `A_iff_B` or `A_iff_B_left` etc.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.alias.mk_iff_mp_app", "description": " Given a proof of `\u03a0 x y z, a \u2194 b`, produces a proof of `\u03a0 x y z, a \u2192 b` or `\u03a0 x y z, b \u2192 a`\n(depending on whether `iffmp` is `iff.mp` or `iff.mpr`). The variable `f` supplies the proof,\nunder the specified number of binders.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.alias.target.to_string", "description": "The docstring for an alias. Used by `alias` _and_ by `to_additive`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.alias.target", "description": "An alias can be in one of three forms", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.alias.target.plain", "description": "An alias can be in one of three forms", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.alias.target.forward", "description": "An alias can be in one of three forms", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.alias.target.backwards", "description": "An alias can be in one of three forms", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.alias.alias_cmd", "description": "The `alias` command can be used to create copies\nof a theorem or definition with different names.\n\nSyntax:\n\n```lean\n/-- doc string -/\nalias my_theorem \u2190 alias1 alias2 ...\n```\n\nThis produces defs or theorems of the form:\n\n```lean\n/-- doc string -/\n@[alias] theorem alias1 : <type of my_theorem> := my_theorem\n\n/-- doc string -/\n@[alias] theorem alias2 : <type of my_theorem> := my_theorem\n```\n\nIff alias syntax:\n\n```lean\nalias A_iff_B \u2194 B_of_A A_of_B\nalias A_iff_B \u2194 ..\n```\n\nThis gets an existing biconditional theorem `A_iff_B` and produces\nthe one-way implications `B_of_A` and `A_of_B` (with no change in\nimplicit arguments). A blank `_` can be used to avoid generating one direction.\nThe `..` notation attempts to generate the 'of'-names automatically when the\ninput theorem has the form `A_iff_B` or `A_iff_B_left` etc.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.alias.alias_direct", "description": "The core tactic which handles `alias d \u2190 al`. Creates an alias `al` for declaration `d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.alias.alias_iff", "description": " The core tactic which handles `alias d \u2194 al _` or `alias d \u2194 _ al`. `ns` is the current\nnamespace, and `is_forward` is true if this is the forward implication (the first form).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.alias.get_alias_target", "description": " Given a definition, look up the definition that it is an alias of.\nReturns `none` if this defintion is not an alias.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.alias.make_left_right", "description": "Get the default names for left/right to be used by `alias d \u2194 ..`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.alias.target.to_name", "description": "The name underlying an alias target", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_functor.map_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_functor.map_equiv_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_functor.map_trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_functor", "description": "An `equiv_functor` is only functorial with respect to equivalences.\n\nTo construct an `equiv_functor`, it suffices to supply just the function `f \u03b1 \u2192 f \u03b2` from\nan equivalence `\u03b1 \u2243 \u03b2`, and then prove the functor laws. It's then a consequence that\nthis function is part of an equivalence, provided by `equiv_functor.map_equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_functor.map", "description": "An `equiv_functor` is only functorial with respect to equivalences.\n\nTo construct an `equiv_functor`, it suffices to supply just the function `f \u03b1 \u2192 f \u03b2` from\nan equivalence `\u03b1 \u2243 \u03b2`, and then prove the functor laws. It's then a consequence that\nthis function is part of an equivalence, provided by `equiv_functor.map_equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_functor.map_refl'", "description": "An `equiv_functor` is only functorial with respect to equivalences.\n\nTo construct an `equiv_functor`, it suffices to supply just the function `f \u03b1 \u2192 f \u03b2` from\nan equivalence `\u03b1 \u2243 \u03b2`, and then prove the functor laws. It's then a consequence that\nthis function is part of an equivalence, provided by `equiv_functor.map_equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_functor.map_trans'", "description": "An `equiv_functor` is only functorial with respect to equivalences.\n\nTo construct an `equiv_functor`, it suffices to supply just the function `f \u03b1 \u2192 f \u03b2` from\nan equivalence `\u03b1 \u2243 \u03b2`, and then prove the functor laws. It's then a consequence that\nthis function is part of an equivalence, provided by `equiv_functor.map_equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_functor.of_is_lawful_functor", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_functor.map_equiv.injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_functor.map_equiv_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_functor.map_equiv", "description": "An `equiv_functor` in fact takes every equiv to an equiv.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_functor.map_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_functor.map_equiv_trans", "description": "The composition of `map_equiv`s is carried over the `equiv_functor`.\nFor plain `functor`s, this lemma is named `map_map` when applied\nor `map_comp_map` when not applied.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "equiv_functor.map_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_acyclic_of_path_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_acyclic_iff_forall_edge_is_bridge", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_tree_iff_exists_unique_path", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_tree_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_acyclic.path_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_acyclic", "description": "A graph is *acyclic* (or a *forest*) if it has no cycles.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_acyclic_iff_path_unique", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_acyclic_iff_forall_adj_is_bridge", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_tree", "description": "A *tree* is a connected acyclic graph.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_tree.is_connected", "description": "A *tree* is a connected acyclic graph.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.is_tree.is_acyclic", "description": "A *tree* is a connected acyclic graph.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gronwall_bound", "description": "Upper bound used in several Gr\u00f6nwall-like inequalities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at_gronwall_bound_shift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_le_of_approx_trajectories_ODE", "description": " If `f` and `g` are two approximate solutions of the same ODE, then the distance between them\ncan't grow faster than exponentially. This is a simple corollary of Gr\u00f6nwall's inequality, and some\npeople call this Gr\u00f6nwall's inequality too.\n\nThis version assumes all inequalities to be true in the whole space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gronwall_bound_x0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gronwall_bound_\u03b50", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gronwall_bound_K0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_le_of_trajectories_ODE_of_mem_set", "description": " If `f` and `g` are two exact solutions of the same ODE, then the distance between them\ncan't grow faster than exponentially. This is a simple corollary of Gr\u00f6nwall's inequality, and some\npeople call this Gr\u00f6nwall's inequality too.\n\nThis version assumes all inequalities to be true in some time-dependent set `s t`,\nand assumes that the solutions never leave this set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ODE_solution_unique_of_mem_set", "description": " There exists only one solution of an ODE \\(\\dot x=v(t, x)\\) in a set `s \u2286 \u211d \u00d7 E` with\na given initial value provided that RHS is Lipschitz continuous in `x` within `s`,\nand we consider only solutions included in `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at_gronwall_bound", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ODE_solution_unique", "description": " There exists only one solution of an ODE \\(\\dot x=v(t, x)\\) with\na given initial value provided that RHS is Lipschitz continuous in `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_le_of_trajectories_ODE", "description": " If `f` and `g` are two exact solutions of the same ODE, then the distance between them\ncan't grow faster than exponentially. This is a simple corollary of Gr\u00f6nwall's inequality, and some\npeople call this Gr\u00f6nwall's inequality too.\n\nThis version assumes all inequalities to be true in the whole space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_le_gronwall_bound_of_norm_deriv_right_le", "description": " A Gr\u00f6nwall-like inequality: if `f : \u211d \u2192 E` is continuous on `[a, b]`, has right derivative\n`f' x` at every point `x \u2208 [a, b)`, and satisfies the inequalities `\u2016f a\u2016 \u2264 \u03b4`,\n`\u2200 x \u2208 [a, b), \u2016f' x\u2016 \u2264 K * \u2016f x\u2016 + \u03b5`, then `\u2016f x\u2016` is bounded by `gronwall_bound \u03b4 K \u03b5 (x - a)`\non `[a, b]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gronwall_bound_\u03b50_\u03b40", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_le_of_approx_trajectories_ODE_of_mem_set", "description": " If `f` and `g` are two approximate solutions of the same ODE, then the distance between them\ncan't grow faster than exponentially. This is a simple corollary of Gr\u00f6nwall's inequality, and some\npeople call this Gr\u00f6nwall's inequality too.\n\nThis version assumes all inequalities to be true in some time-dependent set `s t`,\nand assumes that the solutions never leave this set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gronwall_bound_continuous_\u03b5", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gronwall_bound_of_K_ne_0", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "le_gronwall_bound_of_liminf_deriv_right_le", "description": " A Gr\u00f6nwall-like inequality: if `f : \u211d \u2192 \u211d` is continuous on `[a, b]` and satisfies\nthe inequalities `f a \u2264 \u03b4` and\n`\u2200 x \u2208 [a, b), liminf_{z\u2192x+0} (f z - f x)/(z - x) \u2264 K * (f x) + \u03b5`, then `f x`\nis bounded by `gronwall_bound \u03b4 K \u03b5 (x - a)` on `[a, b]`.\n\nSee also `norm_le_gronwall_bound_of_norm_deriv_right_le` for a version bounding `\u2016f x\u2016`,\n`f : \u211d \u2192 E`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.is_admissible.exists_approx_aux", "description": " Any large enough family of vectors in `R^n` has a pair of elements\nwhose remainders are close together, pointwise.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.is_admissible.exists_approx", "description": " Any large enough family of vectors in `R^\u03b9` has a pair of elements\nwhose remainders are close together, pointwise.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.is_admissible", "description": " An absolute value `R \u2192 \u2124` is admissible if it respects the Euclidean domain\nstructure and a large enough set of elements in `R^n` will contain a pair of\nelements whose remainders are pointwise close together.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.is_admissible.to_is_euclidean", "description": " An absolute value `R \u2192 \u2124` is admissible if it respects the Euclidean domain\nstructure and a large enough set of elements in `R^n` will contain a pair of\nelements whose remainders are pointwise close together.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.is_admissible.card", "description": " An absolute value `R \u2192 \u2124` is admissible if it respects the Euclidean domain\nstructure and a large enough set of elements in `R^n` will contain a pair of\nelements whose remainders are pointwise close together.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.is_admissible.exists_partition'", "description": " An absolute value `R \u2192 \u2124` is admissible if it respects the Euclidean domain\nstructure and a large enough set of elements in `R^n` will contain a pair of\nelements whose remainders are pointwise close together.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.is_admissible.exists_partition", "description": " For all `\u03b5 > 0` and finite families `A`, we can partition the remainders of `A` mod `b`\ninto `abv.card \u03b5` sets, such that all elements in each part of remainders are close together.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_semiring_action.to_ring_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_semiring_action.to_ring_equiv_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_semiring_action.to_ring_equiv", "description": "Each element of the group defines a semiring isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_semiring_action.to_ring_equiv_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_semiring_action.to_mul_distrib_mul_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_semiring_action.to_ring_hom", "description": "Each element of the monoid defines a semiring homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_semiring_action", "description": " Typeclass for multiplicative actions by monoids on semirings.\n\nThis combines `distrib_mul_action` with `mul_distrib_mul_action`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_semiring_action.to_distrib_mul_action", "description": " Typeclass for multiplicative actions by monoids on semirings.\n\nThis combines `distrib_mul_action` with `mul_distrib_mul_action`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_semiring_action.smul_one", "description": " Typeclass for multiplicative actions by monoids on semirings.\n\nThis combines `distrib_mul_action` with `mul_distrib_mul_action`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_semiring_action.smul_mul", "description": " Typeclass for multiplicative actions by monoids on semirings.\n\nThis combines `distrib_mul_action` with `mul_distrib_mul_action`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "to_ring_hom_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_inv''", "description": " Note that `smul_inv'` refers to the group case, and `smul_inv` has an additional inverse\non `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_semiring_action.comp_hom", "description": " Compose a `mul_semiring_action` with a `monoid_hom`, with action `f r' \u2022 m`.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_ordered_semiring.to_char_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.functor_category_has_finite_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.functor_category_has_finite_colimits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uchange.down", "description": "`uchange.down` embeds `\u03b1` to `uchange \u03b1`.\n\nThe VM implementation is guaranteed to be the identity.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uchange.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "unchecked_cast'", "description": "`unchecked_cast' a : \u03b2` performs an unchecked cast of `(a : \u03b1)` to `\u03b2`.\n\nUnlike `unchecked_cast`, it can cast across universes. The VM implementation\nis guaranteed to be the identity.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uchange", "description": "`uchange (\u03b1 : Sort v) : Sort u` is an equivalent type in a different universe.\n\nIn the VM, both `\u03b1` and `uchange \u03b1` have the same representation.\n\nThis definition is `meta` because it collapses the universe hierarchy; if pure code could do\nthis then one could derive Girard's paradox.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "uchange.up", "description": "`uchange.up` extracts from `uchange \u03b1` an `\u03b1`.\n\nThe VM implementation is guaranteed to be the identity.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.norm_deriv_le_div_of_maps_to_ball", "description": " The **Schwarz Lemma**: if `f : \u2102 \u2192 E` sends an open disk with center `c` and a positive radius\n`R\u2081` to an open ball with center `f c` and radius `R\u2082`, then the absolute value of the derivative of\n`f` at `c` is at most the ratio `R\u2082 / R\u2081`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.abs_le_abs_of_maps_to_ball_self", "description": " The **Schwarz Lemma**: if `f : \u2102 \u2192 \u2102` sends an open disk with center `0` to itself, the for any\npoint `z` of this disk we have `abs (f z) \u2264 abs z`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.dist_le_div_mul_dist_of_maps_to_ball", "description": " The **Schwarz Lemma**: if `f : \u2102 \u2192 E` sends an open disk with center `c` and radius `R\u2081` to an\nopen ball with center `f c` and radius `R\u2082`, then for any `z` in the former disk we have\n`dist (f z) (f c) \u2264 (R\u2082 / R\u2081) * dist z c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.affine_of_maps_to_ball_of_exists_norm_dslope_eq_div'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.dist_le_dist_of_maps_to_ball_self", "description": " The **Schwarz Lemma**: if `f : \u2102 \u2192 \u2102` sends an open disk to itself and the center `c` of this\ndisk to itself, then for any point `z` of this disk we have `dist (f z) c \u2264 dist z c`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.abs_deriv_le_div_of_maps_to_ball", "description": " The **Schwarz Lemma**: if `f : \u2102 \u2192 \u2102` sends an open disk with center `c` and a positive radius\n`R\u2081` to an open disk with center `f c` and radius `R\u2082`, then the absolute value of the derivative of\n`f` at `c` is at most the ratio `R\u2082 / R\u2081`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.schwarz_aux", "description": "An auxiliary lemma for `complex.norm_dslope_le_div_of_maps_to_ball`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.norm_dslope_le_div_of_maps_to_ball", "description": "Two cases of the **Schwarz Lemma** (derivative and distance), merged together.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.abs_deriv_le_one_of_maps_to_ball", "description": " The **Schwarz Lemma**: if `f : \u2102 \u2192 \u2102` sends an open disk of positive radius to itself and the\ncenter of this disk to itself, then the absolute value of the derivative of `f` at the center of\nthis disk is at most `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.affine_of_maps_to_ball_of_exists_norm_dslope_eq_div", "description": " Equality case in the **Schwarz Lemma**: in the setup of `norm_dslope_le_div_of_maps_to_ball`, if\n`\u2016dslope f c z\u2080\u2016 = R\u2082 / R\u2081` holds at a point in the ball then the map `f` is affine.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.Ioi_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_filter_univ_succ'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.Iio_cast_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.Ioi_zero_eq_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.Iio_last_eq_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_filter_univ_eq_vector_nth_eq_count", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.card_filter_univ_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.map_subtype_embedding_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_atomic.of_is_chain_bounded", "description": " **Zorn's lemma**: A partial order is atomic if every nonempty chain `c`, `\u22a5 \u2209 c`, has an lower\nbound not equal to `\u22a5`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coatomic.of_is_chain_bounded", "description": " **Zorn's lemma**: A partial order is coatomic if every nonempty chain `c`, `\u22a4 \u2209 c`, has an upper\nbound not equal to `\u22a4`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.uniform_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.uniform_continuous", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.uniformity", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.separated_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.complete_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.comp_exact_value", "description": "Given two continuants `pconts` and `conts` and a value `fr`, this function returns\n- `conts.a / conts.b` if `fr = 0`\n- `exact_conts.a / exact_conts.b` where `exact_conts = next_continuants 1 fr\u207b\u00b9 pconts conts`\n  otherwise.\n\nThis function can be used to compute the exact value approxmated by a continued fraction\n`generalized_continued_fraction.of v` as described in lemma\n`comp_exact_value_correctness_of_stream_eq_some`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.comp_exact_value_correctness_of_stream_eq_some_aux_comp", "description": "Just a computational lemma we need for the next main proof.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.of_correctness_of_terminated_at", "description": "If `generalized_continued_fraction.of v` terminated at step `n`, then the `n`th convergent is\nexactly `v`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.of_correctness_of_terminates", "description": "If `generalized_continued_fraction.of v` terminates, then there is `n : \u2115` such that the `n`th\nconvergent is exactly `v`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.comp_exact_value_correctness_of_stream_eq_some", "description": "Shows the correctness of `comp_exact_value` in case the continued fraction\n`generalized_continued_fraction.of v` did not terminate at position `n`. That is, we obtain the\nvalue `v` if we pass the two successive (auxiliary) continuants at positions `n` and `n + 1` as well\nas the fractional part at `int_fract_pair.stream n` to `comp_exact_value`.\n\nThe correctness might be seen more readily if one uses `convergents'` to evaluate the continued\nfraction. Here is an example to illustrate the idea:\n\nLet `(v : \u211a) := 3.4`. We have\n- `generalized_continued_fraction.int_fract_pair.stream v 0 = some \u27e83, 0.4\u27e9`, and\n- `generalized_continued_fraction.int_fract_pair.stream v 1 = some \u27e82, 0.5\u27e9`.\nNow `(generalized_continued_fraction.of v).convergents' 1 = 3 + 1/2`, and our fractional term at\nposition `2` is `0.5`. We hence have `v = 3 + 1/(2 + 0.5) = 3 + 1/2.5 = 3.4`. This computation\ncorresponds exactly to the one using the recurrence equation in `comp_exact_value`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.of_correctness_at_top_of_terminates", "description": "If `generalized_continued_fraction.of v` terminates, then its convergents will eventually always\nbe `v`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.of_correctness_of_nth_stream_eq_none", "description": " The convergent of `generalized_continued_fraction.of v` at step `n - 1` is exactly `v` if the\n`int_fract_pair.stream` of the corresponding continued fraction terminated at step `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gaussian_int.mod_four_eq_three_of_nat_prime_of_prime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gaussian_int.prime_of_nat_prime_of_mod_four_eq_three", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gaussian_int.prime_iff_mod_four_eq_three_of_nat_prime", "description": "A prime natural number is prime in `\u2124[i]` if and only if it is `3` mod `4`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.infinite_set_of_prime", "description": "A version of `nat.exists_infinite_primes` using the `set.infinite` predicate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.pow_factors_to_finset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.factors_mul_to_finset_of_coprime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.pow_succ_factors_to_finset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.prime_pow_prime_divisor", "description": "The only prime divisor of positive prime power `p^k` is `p` itself", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.factors_mul_to_finset", "description": "If `a`, `b` are positive, the prime divisors of `a * b` are the union of those of `a` and `b`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.univ_sigma_univ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "psigma.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sigma.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.mem_cons_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.mem_cons_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.mem_of_mem_tail", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.mem_map_succ_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.not_mem_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.not_mem_map_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.nth_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.head_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.mem_iff_nth", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.mem_cons_of_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.mem_succ_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.not_mem_nil", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vector.mem_map_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.comm_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.non_unital_comm_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.ring_equiv", "description": "The ring equivalence between `ulift \u03b1` and `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.non_assoc_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.non_unital_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.mul_zero_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.non_unital_non_assoc_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.non_unital_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.non_unital_comm_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.non_unital_non_assoc_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.non_assoc_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.distrib", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ulift.comm_semiring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_neg_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.closed_ball.has_involutive_neg", "description": " We equip the closed ball, in a seminormed group, with a formal operation of negation, namely the\nantipodal map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.closed_ball.has_continuous_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.ball.has_continuous_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.ball.has_involutive_neg", "description": " We equip the ball, in a seminormed group, with a formal operation of negation, namely the\nantipodal map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.sphere.has_continuous_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_neg_closed_ball", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coe_neg_sphere", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.sphere.has_involutive_neg", "description": " We equip the sphere, in a seminormed group, with a formal operation of negation, namely the\nantipodal map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsirelson_inequality", "description": "In a noncommutative ordered `*`-algebra over \u211d,\nTsirelson's bound for a CHSH tuple (A\u2080, A\u2081, B\u2080, B\u2081) is\n`A\u2080 * B\u2080 + A\u2080 * B\u2081 + A\u2081 * B\u2080 - A\u2081 * B\u2081 \u2264 2^(3/2) \u2022 1`.\n\nWe prove this by providing an explicit sum-of-squares decomposition\nof the difference.\n\n(We could work over `\u2124[2^(1/2), 2^(-1/2)]` if we really wanted to!)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsirelson_inequality.tsirelson_inequality_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsirelson_inequality.sqrt_two_inv_mul_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_CHSH_tuple", "description": "A CHSH tuple in a *-monoid consists of 4 self-adjoint involutions `A\u2080 A\u2081 B\u2080 B\u2081` such that\nthe `A\u1d62` commute with the `B\u2c7c`.\n\nThe physical interpretation is that `A\u2080` and `A\u2081` are a pair of boolean observables which\nare spacelike separated from another pair `B\u2080` and `B\u2081` of boolean observables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_CHSH_tuple.A\u2080_inv", "description": "A CHSH tuple in a *-monoid consists of 4 self-adjoint involutions `A\u2080 A\u2081 B\u2080 B\u2081` such that\nthe `A\u1d62` commute with the `B\u2c7c`.\n\nThe physical interpretation is that `A\u2080` and `A\u2081` are a pair of boolean observables which\nare spacelike separated from another pair `B\u2080` and `B\u2081` of boolean observables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_CHSH_tuple.A\u2081_inv", "description": "A CHSH tuple in a *-monoid consists of 4 self-adjoint involutions `A\u2080 A\u2081 B\u2080 B\u2081` such that\nthe `A\u1d62` commute with the `B\u2c7c`.\n\nThe physical interpretation is that `A\u2080` and `A\u2081` are a pair of boolean observables which\nare spacelike separated from another pair `B\u2080` and `B\u2081` of boolean observables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_CHSH_tuple.B\u2080_inv", "description": "A CHSH tuple in a *-monoid consists of 4 self-adjoint involutions `A\u2080 A\u2081 B\u2080 B\u2081` such that\nthe `A\u1d62` commute with the `B\u2c7c`.\n\nThe physical interpretation is that `A\u2080` and `A\u2081` are a pair of boolean observables which\nare spacelike separated from another pair `B\u2080` and `B\u2081` of boolean observables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_CHSH_tuple.B\u2081_inv", "description": "A CHSH tuple in a *-monoid consists of 4 self-adjoint involutions `A\u2080 A\u2081 B\u2080 B\u2081` such that\nthe `A\u1d62` commute with the `B\u2c7c`.\n\nThe physical interpretation is that `A\u2080` and `A\u2081` are a pair of boolean observables which\nare spacelike separated from another pair `B\u2080` and `B\u2081` of boolean observables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_CHSH_tuple.A\u2080_sa", "description": "A CHSH tuple in a *-monoid consists of 4 self-adjoint involutions `A\u2080 A\u2081 B\u2080 B\u2081` such that\nthe `A\u1d62` commute with the `B\u2c7c`.\n\nThe physical interpretation is that `A\u2080` and `A\u2081` are a pair of boolean observables which\nare spacelike separated from another pair `B\u2080` and `B\u2081` of boolean observables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_CHSH_tuple.A\u2081_sa", "description": "A CHSH tuple in a *-monoid consists of 4 self-adjoint involutions `A\u2080 A\u2081 B\u2080 B\u2081` such that\nthe `A\u1d62` commute with the `B\u2c7c`.\n\nThe physical interpretation is that `A\u2080` and `A\u2081` are a pair of boolean observables which\nare spacelike separated from another pair `B\u2080` and `B\u2081` of boolean observables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_CHSH_tuple.B\u2080_sa", "description": "A CHSH tuple in a *-monoid consists of 4 self-adjoint involutions `A\u2080 A\u2081 B\u2080 B\u2081` such that\nthe `A\u1d62` commute with the `B\u2c7c`.\n\nThe physical interpretation is that `A\u2080` and `A\u2081` are a pair of boolean observables which\nare spacelike separated from another pair `B\u2080` and `B\u2081` of boolean observables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_CHSH_tuple.B\u2081_sa", "description": "A CHSH tuple in a *-monoid consists of 4 self-adjoint involutions `A\u2080 A\u2081 B\u2080 B\u2081` such that\nthe `A\u1d62` commute with the `B\u2c7c`.\n\nThe physical interpretation is that `A\u2080` and `A\u2081` are a pair of boolean observables which\nare spacelike separated from another pair `B\u2080` and `B\u2081` of boolean observables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_CHSH_tuple.A\u2080B\u2080_commutes", "description": "A CHSH tuple in a *-monoid consists of 4 self-adjoint involutions `A\u2080 A\u2081 B\u2080 B\u2081` such that\nthe `A\u1d62` commute with the `B\u2c7c`.\n\nThe physical interpretation is that `A\u2080` and `A\u2081` are a pair of boolean observables which\nare spacelike separated from another pair `B\u2080` and `B\u2081` of boolean observables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_CHSH_tuple.A\u2080B\u2081_commutes", "description": "A CHSH tuple in a *-monoid consists of 4 self-adjoint involutions `A\u2080 A\u2081 B\u2080 B\u2081` such that\nthe `A\u1d62` commute with the `B\u2c7c`.\n\nThe physical interpretation is that `A\u2080` and `A\u2081` are a pair of boolean observables which\nare spacelike separated from another pair `B\u2080` and `B\u2081` of boolean observables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_CHSH_tuple.A\u2081B\u2080_commutes", "description": "A CHSH tuple in a *-monoid consists of 4 self-adjoint involutions `A\u2080 A\u2081 B\u2080 B\u2081` such that\nthe `A\u1d62` commute with the `B\u2c7c`.\n\nThe physical interpretation is that `A\u2080` and `A\u2081` are a pair of boolean observables which\nare spacelike separated from another pair `B\u2080` and `B\u2081` of boolean observables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_CHSH_tuple.A\u2081B\u2081_commutes", "description": "A CHSH tuple in a *-monoid consists of 4 self-adjoint involutions `A\u2080 A\u2081 B\u2080 B\u2081` such that\nthe `A\u1d62` commute with the `B\u2c7c`.\n\nThe physical interpretation is that `A\u2080` and `A\u2081` are a pair of boolean observables which\nare spacelike separated from another pair `B\u2080` and `B\u2081` of boolean observables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CHSH_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CHSH_inequality_of_comm", "description": "Given a CHSH tuple (A\u2080, A\u2081, B\u2080, B\u2081) in a *commutative* ordered `*`-algebra over \u211d,\n`A\u2080 * B\u2080 + A\u2080 * B\u2081 + A\u2081 * B\u2080 - A\u2081 * B\u2081 \u2264 2`.\n\n(We could work over \u2124[\u215f2] if we wanted to!)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_units.ordered_add_comm_group", "description": "The units of an ordered commutative additive monoid form an ordered commutative\nadditive group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "units.ordered_comm_group", "description": "The units of an ordered commutative monoid form an ordered commutative group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum.inhabited_left", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "sum.inhabited_right", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "obviously'", "description": "The default implementation of `obviously`\ndischarges any goals which contain `sorry` in their type using `sorry`,\nand then calls `tidy`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "obviously", "description": "The `obviously` tactic is a \"replaceable\" tactic, which means that its meaning is defined by tactics that are defined later with the `@[obviously]` attribute. It is intended for use with `auto_param`s for structure fields.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "obviously.attr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo1975_q1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "not_is_unit_prime_of_dvd_card", "description": " A prime that does not divide the cardinality of a finite commutative ring `R`\nis a unit in `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_unit_iff_not_dvd_char_of_ring_char_ne_zero", "description": " A prime `p` is a unit in a commutative ring `R` of nonzero characterstic iff it does not divide\nthe characteristic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_unit_iff_not_dvd_char", "description": " A prime `p` is a unit in a finite commutative ring `R`\niff it does not divide the characteristic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prime_dvd_char_iff_dvd_card", "description": " The prime divisors of the characteristic of a finite commutative ring are exactly\nthe prime divisors of its cardinality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.observe", "description": " `observe hp : p` asserts the proposition `p`, and tries to prove it using `library_search`.\nIf no proof is found, the tactic fails.\nIn other words, this tactic is equivalent to `have hp : p, { library_search }`.\n\nIf `hp` is omitted, then the placeholder `this` is used.\n\nThe variant `observe? hp : p` will emit a trace message of the form `have hp : p := proof_term`.\nThis may be particularly useful to speed up proofs.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.observe", "description": " `observe hp : p` asserts the proposition `p`, and tries to prove it using `library_search`.\nIf no proof is found, the tactic fails.\nIn other words, this tactic is equivalent to `have hp : p, { library_search }`.\n\nIf `hp` is omitted, then the placeholder `this` is used.\n\nThe variant `observe? hp : p` will emit a trace message of the form `have hp : p := proof_term`.\nThis may be particularly useful to speed up proofs.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.arithmetic_function.l_series_eq_zero_of_not_l_series_summable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.arithmetic_function.zeta_l_series_summable_iff_one_lt_re", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.arithmetic_function.l_series_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.arithmetic_function.l_series_summable_iff_of_re_eq_re", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.arithmetic_function.l_series_summable", "description": "`f.l_series_summable z` indicates that the L-series of `f` converges at `z`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.arithmetic_function.l_series_summable_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.arithmetic_function.l_series_summable_of_bounded_of_one_lt_re", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.arithmetic_function.l_series", "description": "The L-series of an `arithmetic_function`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.arithmetic_function.l_series_summable_of_bounded_of_one_lt_real", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.vertex", "description": "A vertex represents an expression that is equivalent to either the left or right side\nof our initial equation.\n* `id` is a numerical id used to refer to this vertex in the context of a single graph.\n* `exp` is the expression this vertex represents.\n* `pp` is the string format of the expression; we store this in the vertex to avoid\nrecalculating it.\n* `side` is whether this vertex descends from the left or right side of the equation.\n* `parent` is the edge that originally added this vertex to the graph.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.vertex.id", "description": "A vertex represents an expression that is equivalent to either the left or right side\nof our initial equation.\n* `id` is a numerical id used to refer to this vertex in the context of a single graph.\n* `exp` is the expression this vertex represents.\n* `pp` is the string format of the expression; we store this in the vertex to avoid\nrecalculating it.\n* `side` is whether this vertex descends from the left or right side of the equation.\n* `parent` is the edge that originally added this vertex to the graph.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.vertex.exp", "description": "A vertex represents an expression that is equivalent to either the left or right side\nof our initial equation.\n* `id` is a numerical id used to refer to this vertex in the context of a single graph.\n* `exp` is the expression this vertex represents.\n* `pp` is the string format of the expression; we store this in the vertex to avoid\nrecalculating it.\n* `side` is whether this vertex descends from the left or right side of the equation.\n* `parent` is the edge that originally added this vertex to the graph.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.vertex.pp", "description": "A vertex represents an expression that is equivalent to either the left or right side\nof our initial equation.\n* `id` is a numerical id used to refer to this vertex in the context of a single graph.\n* `exp` is the expression this vertex represents.\n* `pp` is the string format of the expression; we store this in the vertex to avoid\nrecalculating it.\n* `side` is whether this vertex descends from the left or right side of the equation.\n* `parent` is the edge that originally added this vertex to the graph.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.vertex.side", "description": "A vertex represents an expression that is equivalent to either the left or right side\nof our initial equation.\n* `id` is a numerical id used to refer to this vertex in the context of a single graph.\n* `exp` is the expression this vertex represents.\n* `pp` is the string format of the expression; we store this in the vertex to avoid\nrecalculating it.\n* `side` is whether this vertex descends from the left or right side of the equation.\n* `parent` is the edge that originally added this vertex to the graph.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.vertex.parent", "description": "A vertex represents an expression that is equivalent to either the left or right side\nof our initial equation.\n* `id` is a numerical id used to refer to this vertex in the context of a single graph.\n* `exp` is the expression this vertex represents.\n* `pp` is the string format of the expression; we store this in the vertex to avoid\nrecalculating it.\n* `side` is whether this vertex descends from the left or right side of the equation.\n* `parent` is the edge that originally added this vertex to the graph.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.edge.to_string", "description": "Converting an edge to a human-readable string.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.edge", "description": "An edge represents a proof that can get from one expression to another.\nIt represents the fact that, starting from the vertex `fr`, the expression in `proof`\ncan prove the vertex `to`.\n`how` contains information that the explainer will use to generate Lean code for the\nproof.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.edge.from_id", "description": "An edge represents a proof that can get from one expression to another.\nIt represents the fact that, starting from the vertex `fr`, the expression in `proof`\ncan prove the vertex `to`.\n`how` contains information that the explainer will use to generate Lean code for the\nproof.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.edge.to_id", "description": "An edge represents a proof that can get from one expression to another.\nIt represents the fact that, starting from the vertex `fr`, the expression in `proof`\ncan prove the vertex `to`.\n`how` contains information that the explainer will use to generate Lean code for the\nproof.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.edge.proof", "description": "An edge represents a proof that can get from one expression to another.\nIt represents the fact that, starting from the vertex `fr`, the expression in `proof`\ncan prove the vertex `to`.\n`how` contains information that the explainer will use to generate Lean code for the\nproof.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.edge.how", "description": "An edge represents a proof that can get from one expression to another.\nIt represents the fact that, starting from the vertex `fr`, the expression in `proof`\ncan prove the vertex `to`.\n`how` contains information that the explainer will use to generate Lean code for the\nproof.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.graph.find_proof", "description": "Run the search to find a proof for the provided graph.\nNormally, this is the only external method needed to run the graph search.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.edge.has_to_format", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.graph", "description": "The graph represents two trees, one descending from each of the left and right sides\nof our initial equation.\n* `conf` and `rules` determine what rewrites are used to generate new graph vertices.\n  Here, the format of a rewrite rule is an expression for rewriting, plus a flag for the\n  direction to apply it in.\n* `vertices` maps vertex.id to vertex.\n* `vmap` maps vertex.pp to a list of vertex.id with that pp, so we can quickly find collisions.\n* `solving_edge` represents a solution that will prove our target equation.\n  It is an edge that would connect the two trees, so `solving_edge.fr` and `solving_edge.to`\n  are vertices in different trees.\n* `lhs` and `rhs` are the left and right expressions we are trying to prove are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.graph.conf", "description": "The graph represents two trees, one descending from each of the left and right sides\nof our initial equation.\n* `conf` and `rules` determine what rewrites are used to generate new graph vertices.\n  Here, the format of a rewrite rule is an expression for rewriting, plus a flag for the\n  direction to apply it in.\n* `vertices` maps vertex.id to vertex.\n* `vmap` maps vertex.pp to a list of vertex.id with that pp, so we can quickly find collisions.\n* `solving_edge` represents a solution that will prove our target equation.\n  It is an edge that would connect the two trees, so `solving_edge.fr` and `solving_edge.to`\n  are vertices in different trees.\n* `lhs` and `rhs` are the left and right expressions we are trying to prove are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.graph.rules", "description": "The graph represents two trees, one descending from each of the left and right sides\nof our initial equation.\n* `conf` and `rules` determine what rewrites are used to generate new graph vertices.\n  Here, the format of a rewrite rule is an expression for rewriting, plus a flag for the\n  direction to apply it in.\n* `vertices` maps vertex.id to vertex.\n* `vmap` maps vertex.pp to a list of vertex.id with that pp, so we can quickly find collisions.\n* `solving_edge` represents a solution that will prove our target equation.\n  It is an edge that would connect the two trees, so `solving_edge.fr` and `solving_edge.to`\n  are vertices in different trees.\n* `lhs` and `rhs` are the left and right expressions we are trying to prove are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.graph.vertices", "description": "The graph represents two trees, one descending from each of the left and right sides\nof our initial equation.\n* `conf` and `rules` determine what rewrites are used to generate new graph vertices.\n  Here, the format of a rewrite rule is an expression for rewriting, plus a flag for the\n  direction to apply it in.\n* `vertices` maps vertex.id to vertex.\n* `vmap` maps vertex.pp to a list of vertex.id with that pp, so we can quickly find collisions.\n* `solving_edge` represents a solution that will prove our target equation.\n  It is an edge that would connect the two trees, so `solving_edge.fr` and `solving_edge.to`\n  are vertices in different trees.\n* `lhs` and `rhs` are the left and right expressions we are trying to prove are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.graph.vmap", "description": "The graph represents two trees, one descending from each of the left and right sides\nof our initial equation.\n* `conf` and `rules` determine what rewrites are used to generate new graph vertices.\n  Here, the format of a rewrite rule is an expression for rewriting, plus a flag for the\n  direction to apply it in.\n* `vertices` maps vertex.id to vertex.\n* `vmap` maps vertex.pp to a list of vertex.id with that pp, so we can quickly find collisions.\n* `solving_edge` represents a solution that will prove our target equation.\n  It is an edge that would connect the two trees, so `solving_edge.fr` and `solving_edge.to`\n  are vertices in different trees.\n* `lhs` and `rhs` are the left and right expressions we are trying to prove are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.graph.solving_edge", "description": "The graph represents two trees, one descending from each of the left and right sides\nof our initial equation.\n* `conf` and `rules` determine what rewrites are used to generate new graph vertices.\n  Here, the format of a rewrite rule is an expression for rewriting, plus a flag for the\n  direction to apply it in.\n* `vertices` maps vertex.id to vertex.\n* `vmap` maps vertex.pp to a list of vertex.id with that pp, so we can quickly find collisions.\n* `solving_edge` represents a solution that will prove our target equation.\n  It is an edge that would connect the two trees, so `solving_edge.fr` and `solving_edge.to`\n  are vertices in different trees.\n* `lhs` and `rhs` are the left and right expressions we are trying to prove are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.graph.lhs", "description": "The graph represents two trees, one descending from each of the left and right sides\nof our initial equation.\n* `conf` and `rules` determine what rewrites are used to generate new graph vertices.\n  Here, the format of a rewrite rule is an expression for rewriting, plus a flag for the\n  direction to apply it in.\n* `vertices` maps vertex.id to vertex.\n* `vmap` maps vertex.pp to a list of vertex.id with that pp, so we can quickly find collisions.\n* `solving_edge` represents a solution that will prove our target equation.\n  It is an edge that would connect the two trees, so `solving_edge.fr` and `solving_edge.to`\n  are vertices in different trees.\n* `lhs` and `rhs` are the left and right expressions we are trying to prove are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.graph.rhs", "description": "The graph represents two trees, one descending from each of the left and right sides\nof our initial equation.\n* `conf` and `rules` determine what rewrites are used to generate new graph vertices.\n  Here, the format of a rewrite rule is an expression for rewriting, plus a flag for the\n  direction to apply it in.\n* `vertices` maps vertex.id to vertex.\n* `vmap` maps vertex.pp to a list of vertex.id with that pp, so we can quickly find collisions.\n* `solving_edge` represents a solution that will prove our target equation.\n  It is an edge that would connect the two trees, so `solving_edge.fr` and `solving_edge.to`\n  are vertices in different trees.\n* `lhs` and `rhs` are the left and right expressions we are trying to prove are equal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rewrite_search.mk_graph", "description": "Construct a graph to search for a proof of a given equation.\n\nThis graph initially contains only two disconnected vertices corresponding to the two\nsides of the equation. When `find_proof` is called, we will run a search and add\nnew vertices and edges.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subgroup.mul_semiring_action", "description": "A stronger version of `subgroup.distrib_mul_action`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submonoid.mul_semiring_action", "description": "A stronger version of `submonoid.distrib_mul_action`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_smul_inv_norm'", "description": "Lemma to normalize a vector in a normed space `E` over either `\u2102` or `\u211d` to length `r`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.bound_of_sphere_bound", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_R_or_C.norm_coe_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.op_norm_bound_of_ball_bound", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "norm_smul_inv_norm", "description": "Lemma to normalize a vector in a normed space `E` over either `\u2102` or `\u211d` to unit length.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.bound_of_ball_bound'", "description": "`linear_map.bound_of_ball_bound` is a version of this over arbitrary nontrivially normed fields.\nIt produces a less precise bound so we keep both versions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "normed_space.sphere_nonempty_is_R_or_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_taylor_mean_remainder_bound", "description": " **Taylor's theorem** with a polynomial bound on the remainder\n\nWe assume that `f` is `n+1`-times continuously differentiable on the closed set `Icc a b`.\nThere exists a constant `C` such that for all `x \u2208 Icc a b` the difference of `f` and its `n`-th\nTaylor polynomial can be estimated by `C * (x - a)^(n+1)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_on_taylor_within_eval", "description": "If `f` is `n` times continuous differentiable on a set `s`, then the Taylor polynomial\n `taylor_within_eval f n s x\u2080 x` is continuous in `x\u2080`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "taylor_within_zero_eval", "description": "The Taylor polynomial of order zero evaluates to `f x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_within_at_taylor_coeff_within", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "taylor_within_eval_self", "description": "Evaluating the Taylor polynomial at `x = x\u2080` yields `f x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "taylor_mean_remainder_lagrange", "description": " **Taylor's theorem** with the Lagrange form of the remainder.\n\nWe assume that `f` is `n+1`-times continuously differentiable in the closed set `Icc x\u2080 x` and\n`n+1`-times differentiable on the open set `Ioo x\u2080 x`. Then there exists a `x' \u2208 Ioo x\u2080 x` such that\n$$f(x) - (P_n f)(x\u2080, x) = \\frac{f^{(n+1)}(x') (x - x\u2080)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!},$$\nwhere $P_n f$ denotes the Taylor polynomial of degree $n$ and $f^{(n+1)}$ is the $n+1$-th iterated\nderivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "taylor_within_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "taylor_mean_remainder", "description": " **Taylor's theorem** with the general mean value form of the remainder.\n\nWe assume that `f` is `n+1`-times continuously differentiable in the closed set `Icc x\u2080 x` and\n`n+1`-times differentiable on the open set `Ioo x\u2080 x`, and `g` is a differentiable function on\n`Ioo x\u2080 x` and continuous on `Icc x\u2080 x`. Then there exists a `x' \u2208 Ioo x\u2080 x` such that\n$$f(x) - (P_n f)(x\u2080, x) = \\frac{(x - x')^n}{n!} \\frac{g(x) - g(x\u2080)}{g' x'},$$\nwhere $P_n f$ denotes the Taylor polynomial of degree $n$.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_within_at_taylor_within_eval", "description": " Calculate the derivative of the Taylor polynomial with respect to `x\u2080`.\n\nVersion for arbitrary sets", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monomial_has_deriv_aux", "description": "Helper lemma for calculating the derivative of the monomial that appears in Taylor expansions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "taylor_within_eval", "description": "The Taylor polynomial with derivatives inside of a set `s` considered as a function `\u211d \u2192 E`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "taylor_mean_remainder_cauchy", "description": " **Taylor's theorem** with the Cauchy form of the remainder.\n\nWe assume that `f` is `n+1`-times continuously differentiable on the closed set `Icc x\u2080 x` and\n`n+1`-times differentiable on the open set `Ioo x\u2080 x`. Then there exists a `x' \u2208 Ioo x\u2080 x` such that\n$$f(x) - (P_n f)(x\u2080, x) = \\frac{f^{(n+1)}(x') (x - x')^n (x-x\u2080)}{n!},$$\nwhere $P_n f$ denotes the Taylor polynomial of degree $n$ and $f^{(n+1)}$ is the $n+1$-th iterated\nderivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "taylor_within_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "taylor_within_eval_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "taylor_mean_remainder_bound", "description": " **Taylor's theorem** with a polynomial bound on the remainder\n\nWe assume that `f` is `n+1`-times continuously differentiable on the closed set `Icc a b`.\nThe difference of `f` and its `n`-th Taylor polynomial can be estimated by\n`C * (x - a)^(n+1) / n!` where `C` is a bound for the `n+1`-th iterated derivative of `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_within_taylor_within_eval_at_Icc", "description": " Calculate the derivative of the Taylor polynomial with respect to `x\u2080`.\n\nVersion for closed intervals", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "taylor_within_eval_has_deriv_at_Ioo", "description": " Calculate the derivative of the Taylor polynomial with respect to `x\u2080`.\n\nVersion for open intervals", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "taylor_within", "description": " The Taylor polynomial with derivatives inside of a set `s`.\n\nThe Taylor polynomial is given by\n$$\u2211_{k=0}^n \\frac{(x - x\u2080)^k}{k!} f^{(k)}(x\u2080),$$\nwhere $f^{(k)}(x\u2080)$ denotes the iterated derivative in the set `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "taylor_coeff_within", "description": "The `k`th coefficient of the Taylor polynomial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.pow_sub_pow_factor", "description": "`x^n - y^n` can be expressed as `z * (x - y)` for some `z` in the ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.pow_add_expansion", "description": "`(x + y)^n` can be expressed as `x^n + n*x^(n-1)*y + k * y^2` for some `k` in the ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.binom_expansion", "description": "A polynomial `f` evaluated at `x + y` can be expressed as\nthe evaluation of `f` at `x`, plus `y` times the (polynomial) derivative of `f` at `x`,\nplus some element `k : R` times `y^2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.eval_sub_factor", "description": "For any polynomial `f`, `f.eval x - f.eval y` can be expressed as `z * (x - y)`\nfor some `z` in the ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_map.line_map_continuous", "description": "The line map is continuous.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_map.continuous_iff", "description": " An affine map is continuous iff its underlying linear map is continuous. See also\n`affine_map.continuous_linear_iff`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_map.homothety_is_open_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "affine_map.homothety_continuous", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "entry_norm_bound_of_unitary", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "entrywise_sup_norm_bound_of_unitary", "description": "The entrywise sup norm of a unitary matrix is at most 1.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dlist_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dlist.join", "description": " Concatenates a list of difference lists to form a single difference list. Similar to\n`list.join`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dlist_lazy", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.reflect", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reflected_value.subst", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reflected_value.expr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_reflect", "description": "`has_reflect \u03b1` lets you produce an `expr` from an instance of \u03b1. That is, it is a function from \u03b1 to expr such that the expr has type \u03b1.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "punit.reflect", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "unsigned.reflect", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "name.reflect", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reflected_value", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reflected_value.val", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "reflected_value.reflect", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "nat.reflect", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "omega.int.nnf", "description": "NNF transformation", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.push_neg", "description": "push_neg p returns the result of normalizing \u00ac p by\n   pushing the outermost negation all the way down,\n   until it reaches either a negation or an atom", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.is_nnf_nnf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.neg_free_neg_elim", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.neg_free", "description": "Argument is free of negations", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.is_nnf_push_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.exists_clause_holds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.push_neg_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.dnf_core", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.is_nnf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.nnf_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.unsat_of_clauses_unsat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.neg_elim", "description": "Eliminate all negations from preform", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.le_and_le_iff_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.clauses_sat_dnf_core", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.dnf", "description": "DNF transformation", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.int.implies_neg_elim", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "self_adjoint.exp_unitary_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "self_adjoint.exp_unitary", "description": " The map from the selfadjoint real subspace to the unitary group. This map only makes sense\nover \u2102.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.exp_unitary", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.exp_unitary_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.mem_ideal_span_X_image", "description": " `x` is in a monomial ideal generated by variables `X` iff every element of of its support\nhas a component in `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.mem_ideal_span_monomial_image_iff_dvd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.mem_ideal_span_monomial_image", "description": " `x` is in a monomial ideal generated by `s` iff every element of of its support dominates one of\nthe generators. Note that `si \u2264 xi` is analogous to saying that the monomial corresponding to `si`\ndivides the monomial corresponding to `xi`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "theorems_100.card_le_mul_sum", "description": "The number of `e < x` for which `e + 1` has a prime factor `p > k` is bounded by `x` times the sum\nof reciprocals of primes in `(k, x]`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.P", "description": "The primes in `(k, x]`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.U", "description": "The union over those primes `p \u2208 (k, x]` of the sets of `e < x` for which `e + 1` is a multiple\nof `p`, i.e., those `e < x` for which there is a prime `p \u2208 (k, x]` that divides `e + 1`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.M", "description": "Those `e < x` for which `e + 1` is a product of powers of primes smaller than or equal to `k`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.card_le_two_pow", "description": "The number of `e < x` for which `e + 1` is a squarefree product of primes smaller than or equal to\n`k` is bounded by `2 ^ k`, the number of subsets of `[1, k]`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.range_sdiff_eq_bUnion", "description": "Removing from {0, ..., x - 1} those elements `e` for which `e + 1` is a product of powers of primes\nsmaller than or equal to `k` leaves those `e` for which there is a prime `p > k` that divides\n`e + 1`, or the union over those primes `p > k` of the sets of `e`s for which `e + 1` is a multiple\nof `p`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.real.tendsto_sum_one_div_prime_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.card_le_two_pow_mul_sqrt", "description": "The number of `e < x` for which `e + 1` is a product of powers of primes smaller than or equal to\n`k` is bounded by `2 ^ k * nat.sqrt x`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.sum_lt_half_of_not_tendsto", "description": "If the sum of the reciprocals of the primes converges, there exists a `k : \u2115` such that the sum of\nthe reciprocals of the primes greater than `k` is less than 1/2.\n\nMore precisely, for any `x : \u2115`, the sum of the reciprocals of the primes between `k` and `x + 1`\nis less than 1/2.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1964_q1b", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1964_q1.aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1964_q1.two_pow_three_mul_add_two_mod_seven", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1964_q1.two_pow_three_mul_add_one_mod_seven", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1964_q1.problem_predicate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1964_q1.two_pow_three_mul_mod_seven", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1964_q1.imo1964_q1a", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "fin.succ_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.pred_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.succ_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.pred_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.pred_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fin.succ_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.nonempty_limit_cone_of_compact_t2_cofiltered_system", "description": "Cofiltered limits of nonempty compact Hausdorff spaces are nonempty topological spaces.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.partial_sections.closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.partial_sections.directed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.partial_sections.nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.partial_sections", "description": "Partial sections of a cofiltered limit are sections when restricted to\na finite subset of objects and morphisms of `J`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.choose1", "description": " Changes `(h : \u2200xs, \u2203a:\u03b1, p a) \u22a2 g` to `(d : \u2200xs, a) (s : \u2200xs, p (d xs)) \u22a2 g` and\n`(h : \u2200xs, p xs \u2227 q xs) \u22a2 g` to `(d : \u2200xs, p xs) (s : \u2200xs, q xs) \u22a2 g`.\n`choose1` returns a pair of the second local constant it introduces,\nand the error result (see below).\n\nIf `nondep` is true and `\u03b1` is inhabited, then it will remove the dependency of `d` on\nall propositional assumptions in `xs`. For example if `ys` are propositions then\n`(h : \u2200xs ys, \u2203a:\u03b1, p a) \u22a2 g` becomes `(d : \u2200xs, a) (s : \u2200xs ys, p (d xs)) \u22a2 g`.\n\nThe second value returned by `choose1` is the result of nondep elimination:\n* `none`: nondep elimination was not attempted or was not applicable\n* `some none`: nondep elimination was successful\n* ``some (some `(nonempty \u03b1))``: nondep elimination was unsuccessful\n  because we could not find a `nonempty \u03b1` instance", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.choose", "description": " Changes `(h : \u2200xs, \u2203as, p as \u2227 q as) \u22a2 g` to a list of functions `as`,\nand a final hypothesis on `p as` and `q as`. If `nondep` is true then the functions will\nbe made to not depend on propositional arguments, when possible.\n\nThe last argument is an internal recursion variable, indicating whether nondep elimination\nhas been useful so far. The tactic fails if `nondep` is true, and nondep elimination is\nattempted at least once, and it fails every time it is attempted, in which case it returns\nan error complaining about the first attempt.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.choose", "description": " `choose a b h h' using hyp` takes an hypothesis `hyp` of the form\n`\u2200 (x : X) (y : Y), \u2203 (a : A) (b : B), P x y a b \u2227 Q x y a b`\nfor some `P Q : X \u2192 Y \u2192 A \u2192 B \u2192 Prop` and outputs\ninto context two functions `a : X \u2192 Y \u2192 A`, `b : X \u2192 Y \u2192 B` and two assumptions:\n`h : \u2200 (x : X) (y : Y), P x y (a x y) (b x y)` and\n`h' : \u2200 (x : X) (y : Y), Q x y (a x y) (b x y)`. It also works with dependent versions.\n\n`choose! a b h h' using hyp` does the same, except that it will remove dependency of\nthe functions on propositional arguments if possible. For example if `Y` is a proposition\nand `A` and `B` are nonempty in the above example then we will instead get\n`a : X \u2192 A`, `b : X \u2192 B`, and the assumptions\n`h : \u2200 (x : X) (y : Y), P x y (a x) (b x)` and\n`h' : \u2200 (x : X) (y : Y), Q x y (a x) (b x)`.\n\nExamples:\n\n```lean\nexample (h : \u2200n m : \u2115, \u2203i j, m = n + i \u2228 m + j = n) : true :=\nbegin\n  choose i j h using h,\n  guard_hyp i : \u2115 \u2192 \u2115 \u2192 \u2115,\n  guard_hyp j : \u2115 \u2192 \u2115 \u2192 \u2115,\n  guard_hyp h : \u2200 (n m : \u2115), m = n + i n m \u2228 m + j n m = n,\n  trivial\nend\n```\n\n```lean\nexample (h : \u2200 i : \u2115, i < 7 \u2192 \u2203 j, i < j \u2227 j < i+i) : true :=\nbegin\n  choose! f h h' using h,\n  guard_hyp f : \u2115 \u2192 \u2115,\n  guard_hyp h : \u2200 (i : \u2115), i < 7 \u2192 i < f i,\n  guard_hyp h' : \u2200 (i : \u2115), i < 7 \u2192 f i < i + i,\n  trivial,\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_sometimes", "description": " Given `\u03b1 : Sort u`, `nonemp : nonempty \u03b1`, `p : \u03b1 \u2192 Prop`, a context of local variables\n`ctxt`, and a pair of an element `val : \u03b1` and `spec : p val`,\n`mk_sometimes u \u03b1 nonemp p ctx (val, spec)` produces another pair `val', spec'`\nsuch that `val'` does not have any free variables from elements of `ctxt` whose types are\npropositions. This is done by applying `function.sometimes` to abstract over all the propositional\narguments.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.choose", "description": " `choose a b h h' using hyp` takes an hypothesis `hyp` of the form\n`\u2200 (x : X) (y : Y), \u2203 (a : A) (b : B), P x y a b \u2227 Q x y a b`\nfor some `P Q : X \u2192 Y \u2192 A \u2192 B \u2192 Prop` and outputs\ninto context two functions `a : X \u2192 Y \u2192 A`, `b : X \u2192 Y \u2192 B` and two assumptions:\n`h : \u2200 (x : X) (y : Y), P x y (a x y) (b x y)` and\n`h' : \u2200 (x : X) (y : Y), Q x y (a x y) (b x y)`. It also works with dependent versions.\n\n`choose! a b h h' using hyp` does the same, except that it will remove dependency of\nthe functions on propositional arguments if possible. For example if `Y` is a proposition\nand `A` and `B` are nonempty in the above example then we will instead get\n`a : X \u2192 A`, `b : X \u2192 B`, and the assumptions\n`h : \u2200 (x : X) (y : Y), P x y (a x) (b x)` and\n`h' : \u2200 (x : X) (y : Y), Q x y (a x) (b x)`.\n\nExamples:\n\n```lean\nexample (h : \u2200n m : \u2115, \u2203i j, m = n + i \u2228 m + j = n) : true :=\nbegin\n  choose i j h using h,\n  guard_hyp i : \u2115 \u2192 \u2115 \u2192 \u2115,\n  guard_hyp j : \u2115 \u2192 \u2115 \u2192 \u2115,\n  guard_hyp h : \u2200 (n m : \u2115), m = n + i n m \u2228 m + j n m = n,\n  trivial\nend\n```\n\n```lean\nexample (h : \u2200 i : \u2115, i < 7 \u2192 \u2203 j, i < j \u2227 j < i+i) : true :=\nbegin\n  choose! f h h' using h,\n  guard_hyp f : \u2115 \u2192 \u2115,\n  guard_hyp h : \u2200 (i : \u2115), i < 7 \u2192 i < f i,\n  guard_hyp h' : \u2200 (i : \u2115), i < 7 \u2192 f i < i + i,\n  trivial,\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bijection_natural", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.subterminal_inclusion.exponential_ideal", "description": "The subcategory of subterminal objects is an exponential ideal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exponential_ideal.mk_of_iso", "description": "Given a natural isomorphism `i \u22d9 exp A \u22d9 left_adjoint i \u22d9 i \u2245 i \u22d9 exp A`, we can show `i`\nis an exponential ideal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exponential_ideal", "description": "The subcategory `D` of `C` expressed as an inclusion functor is an *exponential ideal* if\n`B \u2208 D` implies `A \u27f9 B \u2208 D` for all `A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exponential_ideal.exp_closed", "description": "The subcategory `D` of `C` expressed as an inclusion functor is an *exponential ideal* if\n`B \u2208 D` implies `A \u27f9 B \u2208 D` for all `A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exponential_ideal.mk'", "description": "To show `i` is an exponential ideal it suffices to show that `A \u27f9 iB` is \"in\" `D` for any `A` in\n`C` and `B` in `D`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.prod_comparison_iso", "description": "The bijection allows us to show that `prod_comparison L A B` is an isomorphism, where the inverse\nis the forward map of the identity morphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exponential_ideal_reflective", "description": "If `D` is a reflective subcategory, the property of being an exponential ideal is equivalent to\nthe presence of a natural isomorphism `i \u22d9 exp A \u22d9 left_adjoint i \u22d9 i \u2245 i \u22d9 exp A`, that is:\n`(A \u27f9 iB) \u2245 i L (A \u27f9 iB)`, naturally in `B`.\nThe converse is given in `exponential_ideal.mk_of_iso`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.id.exponential_ideal", "description": "The entire category viewed as a subcategory is an exponential ideal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bijection", "description": "We construct a bijection between morphisms `L(A \u2a2f B) \u27f6 X` and morphisms `LA \u2a2f LB \u27f6 X`.\nThis bijection has two key properties:\n* It is natural in `X`: See `bijection_natural`.\n* When `X = LA \u2a2f LB`, then the backwards direction sends the identity morphism to the product\n  comparison morphism: See `bijection_symm_apply_id`.\n\nTogether these help show that `L` preserves binary products. This should be considered\n*internal implementation* towards `preserves_binary_products_of_exponential_ideal`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exponential_ideal_of_preserves_binary_products", "description": "If the reflector preserves binary products, the subcategory is an exponential ideal.\nThis is the converse of `preserves_binary_products_of_exponential_ideal`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cartesian_closed_of_reflective", "description": "If `i` witnesses that `D` is a reflective subcategory and an exponential ideal, then `D` is\nitself cartesian closed.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.reflective_products", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.bijection_symm_apply_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preserves_finite_products_of_exponential_ideal", "description": "If a reflective subcategory is an exponential ideal, then the reflector preserves finite products.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.preserves_binary_products_of_exponential_ideal", "description": "If a reflective subcategory is an exponential ideal, then the reflector preserves binary products.\nThis is the converse of `exponential_ideal_of_preserves_binary_products`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "qify.qify_attr", "description": "The `qify` attribute is used by the `qify` tactic. It applies to lemmas that shift propositions\nfrom `nat` or `int` to `rat`.\n\n`qify` lemmas should have the form `\u2200 a\u2081 ... a\u2099 : \u2115, Pq (a\u2081 : \u211a) ... (a\u2099 : \u211a) \u2194 Pn a\u2081 ... a\u2099` or\n `\u2200 a\u2081 ... a\u2099 : \u2124, Pq (a\u2081 : \u211a) ... (a\u2099 : \u211a) \u2194 Pz a\u2081 ... a\u2099`.\nFor example, `rat.coe_nat_le_coe_nat_iff : \u2200 (m n : \u2115), \u2191m \u2264 \u2191n \u2194 m \u2264 n` is a `qify` lemma.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.qify", "description": "The `qify` tactic is used to shift propositions from `\u2115` or `\u2124` to `\u211a`.\nThis is often useful since `\u211a` has well-behaved division and subtraction.\n\n```lean\nexample (a b c : \u2115) (x y z : \u2124) (h : \u00ac x*y*z < 0) : c < a + 3*b :=\nbegin\n  qify,\n  qify at h,\n  /-\n  h : \u00ac\u2191x * \u2191y * \u2191z < 0\n  \u22a2 \u2191c < \u2191a + 3 * \u2191b\n  -/\nend\n```\n\n`qify` can be given extra lemmas to use in simplification. This is especially useful in the\npresence of subtraction and division: passing `\u2264` or `\u2223` arguments will allow `push_cast`\nto do more work.\n```lean\nexample (a b c : \u2115) (h : a - b < c) (hab : b \u2264 a) : false :=\nbegin\n  qify [hab] at h,\n  /- h : \u2191a - \u2191b < \u2191c -/\nend\n```\n\n```lean\nexample (a b c : \u2115) (h : a / b = c) (hab : b \u2223 a) : false :=\nbegin\n  qify [hab] at h,\n  /- h : \u2191a / \u2191b = \u2191c -/\nend\n```\n\n`qify` makes use of the `@[qify]` attribute to move propositions,\nand the `push_cast` tactic to simplify the `\u211a`-valued expressions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.attribute.qify", "description": "The `qify` attribute is used by the `qify` tactic. It applies to lemmas that shift propositions\nfrom `nat` or `int` to `rat`.\n\n`qify` lemmas should have the form `\u2200 a\u2081 ... a\u2099 : \u2115, Pq (a\u2081 : \u211a) ... (a\u2099 : \u211a) \u2194 Pn a\u2081 ... a\u2099` or\n `\u2200 a\u2081 ... a\u2099 : \u2124, Pq (a\u2081 : \u211a) ... (a\u2099 : \u211a) \u2194 Pz a\u2081 ... a\u2099`.\nFor example, `rat.coe_nat_le_coe_nat_iff : \u2200 (m n : \u2115), \u2191m \u2264 \u2191n \u2194 m \u2264 n` is a `qify` lemma.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "qify.rat.coe_nat_ne_coe_nat_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "qify.rat.coe_int_ne_coe_int_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.qify_proof", "description": "A variant of `tactic.qify` that takes `h`, a proof of a proposition about natural numbers\nor integers, and returns a proof of the qified version of that propositon.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "qify.rat.coe_nat_le_coe_nat_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.qify", "description": "`qify extra_lems e` is used to shift propositions in `e` from `\u2115` or `\u2124` to `\u211a`.\nThis is often useful since `\u211a` has well-behaved division and subtraction.\n\nThe list of extra lemmas is used in the `push_cast` step.\n\nReturns an expression `e'` and a proof that `e = e'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "qify.lift_to_q", "description": "Given an expression `e`, `lift_to_q e` looks for subterms of `e` that are propositions \"about\"\nnatural numbers or integers and change them to propositions about rational numbers.\n\nReturns an expression `e'` and a proof that `e = e'`.\n\nIncludes `ge_iff_le` and `gt_iff_lt` in the simp set. These can't be tagged with `qify` as we\nwant to use them in the \"forward\", not \"backward\", direction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "qify.rat.coe_nat_lt_coe_nat_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "qify.rat.coe_int_le_coe_int_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "qify.rat.coe_int_eq_coe_int_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "qify.rat.coe_nat_eq_coe_nat_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "qify.rat.coe_int_lt_coe_int_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.qify", "description": "The `qify` tactic is used to shift propositions from `\u2115` or `\u2124` to `\u211a`.\nThis is often useful since `\u211a` has well-behaved division and subtraction.\n\n```lean\nexample (a b c : \u2115) (x y z : \u2124) (h : \u00ac x*y*z < 0) : c < a + 3*b :=\nbegin\n  qify,\n  qify at h,\n  /-\n  h : \u00ac\u2191x * \u2191y * \u2191z < 0\n  \u22a2 \u2191c < \u2191a + 3 * \u2191b\n  -/\nend\n```\n\n`qify` can be given extra lemmas to use in simplification. This is especially useful in the\npresence of subtraction and division: passing `\u2264` or `\u2223` arguments will allow `push_cast`\nto do more work.\n```lean\nexample (a b c : \u2115) (h : a - b < c) (hab : b \u2264 a) : false :=\nbegin\n  qify [hab] at h,\n  /- h : \u2191a - \u2191b < \u2191c -/\nend\n```\n\n```lean\nexample (a b c : \u2115) (h : a / b = c) (hab : b \u2223 a) : false :=\nbegin\n  qify [hab] at h,\n  /- h : \u2191a / \u2191b = \u2191c -/\nend\n```\n\n`qify` makes use of the `@[qify]` attribute to move propositions,\nand the `push_cast` tactic to simplify the `\u211a`-valued expressions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.eapply'", "description": "Same as `apply'` but only goals that don't depend on other goals are added to goal list.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.symmetry'", "description": "`symmetry'` behaves like `symmetry` but also offers the option `symmetry' at h` to apply symmetry\nto assumption `h`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.reorder_goals", "description": " With `gs` a list of proof goals, `reorder_goals gs new_g` will use the `new_goals` policy\n`new_g` to rearrange the dependent goals to either drop them, push them to the end of the list\nor leave them in place. The `bool` values in `gs` indicates whether the goal is dependent or not.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.reflexivity'", "description": "Similar to `reflexivity` with the difference that `apply'` is used instead of `apply`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.apply'", "description": " `apply'` mimics the behavior of `apply_core`. When\n`apply_core` fails, it is retried by providing the term with meta\nvariables as additional arguments. The meta variables can then\nbecome new goals depending on the `cfg.new_goals` policy.\n\n`apply'` also finds instances and applies opt_params and auto_params.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.apply_with'", "description": "Similar to the `apply'` tactic, but allows the user to provide a `apply_cfg` configuration object.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.mapply'", "description": "Similar to the `apply'` tactic, but uses matching instead of unification.\n`mapply' t` is equivalent to `apply_with' t {unify := ff}`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.fapply'", "description": "Similar to the `apply'` tactic, but does not reorder goals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.fapply'", "description": "Same as `apply'` but __all__ arguments that weren't inferred are added to goal list.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.symmetry'", "description": "Similar to `symmetry` with the difference that `apply'` is used instead of `apply`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.eapply'", "description": "Similar to the `apply'` tactic, but only creates subgoals for non-dependent premises that have not\nbeen fixed by type inference or type class resolution.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.transitivity'", "description": "Similar to `transitivity` with the difference that `apply'` is used instead of `apply`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.apply'", "description": "Similarly to `apply`, the `apply'` tactic tries to match the current goal against the conclusion\nof the type of term.\n\nIt differs from `apply` in that it does not unfold definition in order to find out what the\nassumptions of the provided term is. It is especially useful when defining relations on function\nspaces (e.g. `\u2264`) so that rules like transitivity on `le : (\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2) \u2192 (\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2) \u2192 (\u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2)` will be\nconsidered to have three parameters and two assumptions (i.e. `f g h : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2`, `H\u2080 : f \u2264 g`,\n`H\u2081 : g \u2264 h`) instead of three parameters, two assumptions and then one more parameter\n(i.e. `f g h : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2`, `H\u2080 : f \u2264 g`, `H\u2081 : g \u2264 h`, `x : \u03b1`). Whereas `apply` would expect the goal\n`f x \u2264 h x`, `apply'` will work with the goal `f \u2264 h`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.transitivity'", "description": "Similar to `transitivity` with the difference that `apply'` is used instead of `apply`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.reflexivity'", "description": "Similar to `reflexivity` with the difference that `apply'` is used instead of `apply`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.refl'", "description": "Shorter name for the tactic `reflexivity'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.smul_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.pointwise_smul_subset_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.pointwise_smul_le_pointwise_smul_iff\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.smul_mem_pointwise_smul_iff\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.subset_pointwise_smul_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.pointwise_mul_action", "description": " The action on a subsemiring corresponding to applying the action to every element.\n\nThis is available as an instance in the `pointwise` locale.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.mem_smul_pointwise_iff_exists", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.smul_mem_pointwise_smul_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.le_pointwise_smul_iff\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.mem_inv_pointwise_smul_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.pointwise_smul_le_iff\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.pointwise_central_scalar", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.smul_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.pointwise_smul_le_pointwise_smul_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.mem_pointwise_smul_iff_inv_smul_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.mem_pointwise_smul_iff_inv_smul_mem\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.mem_inv_pointwise_smul_iff\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.pointwise_smul_def", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.smul_mem_pointwise_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.pointwise_smul_to_add_submonoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.smul_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subsemiring.coe_pointwise_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo2008_q3", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2008_q3.p_lemma", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "is_cyclotomic_extension.discr_prime_pow", "description": " If `p` is a prime and `is_cyclotomic_extension {p ^ k} K L`, then the discriminant of\n`h\u03b6.power_basis K` is `(-1) ^ ((p ^ k).totient / 2) * p ^ (p ^ (k - 1) * ((p - 1) * k - 1))`\nif `irreducible (cyclotomic (p ^ k) K))`. Beware that in the cases `p ^ k = 1` and `p ^ k = 2`\nthe formula uses `1 / 2 = 0` and `0 - 1 = 0`. It is useful only to have a uniform result.\nSee also `is_cyclotomic_extension.discr_prime_pow_eq_unit_mul_pow`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_primitive_root.discr_zeta_eq_discr_zeta_sub_one", "description": " The discriminant of the power basis given by a primitive root of unity `\u03b6` is the same as the\ndiscriminant of the power basis given by `\u03b6 - 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_cyclotomic_extension.discr_prime_pow_ne_two'", "description": " If `p` is a prime and `is_cyclotomic_extension {p ^ (k + 1)} K L`, then the discriminant of\n`h\u03b6.power_basis K` is `(-1) ^ (p ^ k * (p - 1) / 2) * p ^ (p ^ k * ((p - 1) * (k + 1) - 1))`\nif `irreducible (cyclotomic (p ^ (k + 1)) K))`, and `p ^ (k + 1) \u2260 2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_cyclotomic_extension.discr_prime_pow_eq_unit_mul_pow", "description": " If `p` is a prime and `is_cyclotomic_extension {p ^ k} K L`, then there are `u : \u2124\u02e3` and\n`n : \u2115` such that the discriminant of `h\u03b6.power_basis K` is `u * p ^ n`. Often this is enough and\nless cumbersome to use than `is_cyclotomic_extension.discr_prime_pow`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_cyclotomic_extension.discr_prime_pow_ne_two", "description": " If `p` is a prime and `is_cyclotomic_extension {p ^ (k + 1)} K L`, then the discriminant of\n`h\u03b6.power_basis K` is `(-1) ^ ((p ^ (k + 1).totient) / 2) * p ^ (p ^ k * ((p - 1) * (k + 1) - 1))`\nif `irreducible (cyclotomic (p ^ (k + 1)) K))`, and `p ^ (k + 1) \u2260 2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_cyclotomic_extension.discr_odd_prime", "description": " If `p` is an odd prime and `is_cyclotomic_extension {p} K L`, then\n`discr K (h\u03b6.power_basis K).basis = (-1) ^ ((p - 1) / 2) * p ^ (p - 2)` if\n`irreducible (cyclotomic p K)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.bubble_sort_induction'", "description": " *Bubble sort induction*: Prove that the sorted version of `f` has some property `P`\nif `f` satsifies `P` and `P` is preserved on permutations of `f` when swapping two\nantitone values.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tuple.bubble_sort_induction", "description": " *Bubble sort induction*: Prove that the sorted version of `f` has some property `P`\nif `f` satsifies `P` and `P` is preserved when swapping two antitone values.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CompHaus.projective_ultrafilter", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CompHaus.category_theory.enough_projectives", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "CompHaus.projective_presentation", "description": "For any compact Hausdorff space `X`,\n the natural map `ultrafilter X \u2192 X` is a projective presentation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.to_nat_add_to_nat_neg_eq_nnnorm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.norm_coe_units", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.nnnorm_coe_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.nnnorm_coe_units", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.to_nat_add_to_nat_neg_eq_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo2011_q3", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "category_theory.over.creates_connected.raised_cone_lowers_to_original", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.over.creates_connected.raise_cone", "description": "(Impl) Given a cone in the base category, raise it to a cone in the over category. Note this is\nwhere the connected assumption is used.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.over.forget_creates_connected_limits", "description": "The forgetful functor from the over category creates any connected limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.over.creates_connected.raised_cone_is_limit", "description": "(Impl) Show that the raised cone is a limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.over.has_connected_limits", "description": "The over category has any connected limit which the original category has.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.over.creates_connected.raise_cone_\u03c0_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.over.creates_connected.nat_trans_in_over", "description": "(Impl) Given a diagram in the over category, produce a natural transformation from the\ndiagram legs to the specific object.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.over.creates_connected.raise_cone_X", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone_on.sum_le_integral_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone_on.integral_le_sum_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone_on.sum_le_integral", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone_on.sum_le_integral", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone_on.sum_le_integral_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone_on.integral_le_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone_on.integral_le_sum_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone_on.integral_le_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.X_pow_sub_X_sub_one_irreducible_rat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.X_pow_sub_X_sub_one_irreducible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.X_pow_sub_X_sub_one_irreducible_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_at_sign_of_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_at_sign_of_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_at_sign_of_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sign_type.topological_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sign_type.discrete_topology", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.is_scalar_tower_of_algebra_Module", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.linear_over_field", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.module_of_algebra_Module", "description": "Type synonym for considering a module over a `k`-algebra as a `k`-module.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_glb.exists_between_self_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_lub.exists_between_sub_self'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_lub.exists_between_sub_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_glb.exists_between_self_add'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expand_exists.parse_ctx_props", "description": "Data known when parsing the proposition (after parsing exists and pi expressions).\n\n`project_proof` projects a proof of the full proposition (eg `A \u2227 B \u2227 C`) to a specific proof (eg\n`B`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expand_exists.parse_ctx_props.to_parse_ctx_exists", "description": "Data known when parsing the proposition (after parsing exists and pi expressions).\n\n`project_proof` projects a proof of the full proposition (eg `A \u2227 B \u2227 C`) to a specific proof (eg\n`B`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expand_exists.parse_ctx_props.project_proof", "description": "Data known when parsing the proposition (after parsing exists and pi expressions).\n\n`project_proof` projects a proof of the full proposition (eg `A \u2227 B \u2227 C`) to a specific proof (eg\n`B`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expand_exists.parse_exists", "description": "Parses an `\u2203 a : \u03b1, p a`, and creates an associated definition with a value of `\u03b1`. When `p \u03b1` is\nnot an exists statement, it will call `parse_props`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expand_exists_attr", "description": "From a proof that (a) value(s) exist(s) with certain properties, constructs (an) instance(s)\nsatisfying those properties. For instance:\n\n```lean\n@[expand_exists nat_greater nat_greater_spec]\nlemma nat_greater_exists (n : \u2115) : \u2203 m : \u2115, n < m := ...\n\n#check nat_greater      -- nat_greater : \u2115 \u2192 \u2115\n#check nat_greater_spec -- nat_greater_spec : \u2200 (n : \u2115), n < nat_greater n\n```\n\nIt supports multiple witnesses:\n\n```lean\n@[expand_exists nat_greater_m nat_greater_l nat_greater_spec]\nlemma nat_greater_exists (n : \u2115) : \u2203 (m l : \u2115), n < m \u2227 m < l := ...\n\n#check nat_greater_m      -- nat_greater : \u2115 \u2192 \u2115\n#check nat_greater_l      -- nat_greater : \u2115 \u2192 \u2115\n#check nat_greater_spec-- nat_greater_spec : \u2200 (n : \u2115),\n  n < nat_greater_m n \u2227 nat_greater_m n < nat_greater_l n\n```\n\nIt also supports logical conjunctions:\n```lean\n@[expand_exists nat_greater nat_greater_lt nat_greater_nonzero]\nlemma nat_greater_exists (n : \u2115) : \u2203 m : \u2115, n < m \u2227 m \u2260 0 := ...\n\n#check nat_greater         -- nat_greater : \u2115 \u2192 \u2115\n#check nat_greater_lt      -- nat_greater_lt : \u2200 (n : \u2115), n < nat_greater n\n#check nat_greater_nonzero -- nat_greater_nonzero : \u2200 (n : \u2115), nat_greater n \u2260 0\n```\nNote that without the last argument `nat_greater_nonzero`, `nat_greater_lt` would be:\n```lean\n#check nat_greater_lt -- nat_greater_lt : \u2200 (n : \u2115), n < nat_greater n \u2227 nat_greater n \u2260 0\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expand_exists.parse_ctx", "description": "Data known when parsing pi expressions.\n\n`decl`'s arguments are: is_theorem, name, type, value.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expand_exists.parse_ctx.original_decl", "description": "Data known when parsing pi expressions.\n\n`decl`'s arguments are: is_theorem, name, type, value.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expand_exists.parse_ctx.decl", "description": "Data known when parsing pi expressions.\n\n`decl`'s arguments are: is_theorem, name, type, value.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expand_exists.parse_ctx.names", "description": "Data known when parsing pi expressions.\n\n`decl`'s arguments are: is_theorem, name, type, value.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expand_exists.parse_ctx.pis_depth", "description": "Data known when parsing pi expressions.\n\n`decl`'s arguments are: is_theorem, name, type, value.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expand_exists.parse_one_prop", "description": "Parses a proposition and creates the associated specification proof. Does not break down the\nproposition further.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expand_exists.parse_pis", "description": "Parses a `\u2200 (a : \u03b1), p a`. If `p` is not a pi expression, it will call `parse_exists`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expand_exists.instantiate_exists_decls", "description": "Replaces free variables with their exists declaration. For example, if:\n\n```lean\ndef n_value : \u2115 := ... -- generated by `expand_exists`\n```\n\nthen this function converts `#0` in `#0 = #0` from `\u2203 n : \u2115, n = n` to `n_value = n_value`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expand_exists.parse_props", "description": "Parses a proposition and decides if it should be broken down (eg `P \u2227 Q` -> `P` and `Q`) depending\non how many `names` are left. Then creates the associated specification proof(s).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.attribute.expand_exists", "description": "From a proof that (a) value(s) exist(s) with certain properties, constructs (an) instance(s)\nsatisfying those properties. For instance:\n\n```lean\n@[expand_exists nat_greater nat_greater_spec]\nlemma nat_greater_exists (n : \u2115) : \u2203 m : \u2115, n < m := ...\n\n#check nat_greater      -- nat_greater : \u2115 \u2192 \u2115\n#check nat_greater_spec -- nat_greater_spec : \u2200 (n : \u2115), n < nat_greater n\n```\n\nIt supports multiple witnesses:\n\n```lean\n@[expand_exists nat_greater_m nat_greater_l nat_greater_spec]\nlemma nat_greater_exists (n : \u2115) : \u2203 (m l : \u2115), n < m \u2227 m < l := ...\n\n#check nat_greater_m      -- nat_greater : \u2115 \u2192 \u2115\n#check nat_greater_l      -- nat_greater : \u2115 \u2192 \u2115\n#check nat_greater_spec-- nat_greater_spec : \u2200 (n : \u2115),\n  n < nat_greater_m n \u2227 nat_greater_m n < nat_greater_l n\n```\n\nIt also supports logical conjunctions:\n```lean\n@[expand_exists nat_greater nat_greater_lt nat_greater_nonzero]\nlemma nat_greater_exists (n : \u2115) : \u2203 m : \u2115, n < m \u2227 m \u2260 0 := ...\n\n#check nat_greater         -- nat_greater : \u2115 \u2192 \u2115\n#check nat_greater_lt      -- nat_greater_lt : \u2200 (n : \u2115), n < nat_greater n\n#check nat_greater_nonzero -- nat_greater_nonzero : \u2200 (n : \u2115), nat_greater n \u2260 0\n```\nNote that without the last argument `nat_greater_nonzero`, `nat_greater_lt` would be:\n```lean\n#check nat_greater_lt -- nat_greater_lt : \u2200 (n : \u2115), n < nat_greater n \u2227 nat_greater n \u2260 0\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expand_exists.parse_ctx_exists", "description": "Data known when parsing exists expressions (after parsing pi expressions).\n\n* `with_args` applies pi arguments to a term (eg `id` -> `id #2 #1 #0`).\n* `spec_chain` takes the form of `classical.some_spec^n (it_exists ...)`,\nwith `n` the depth of `\u2203` parsed.\n* `exists_decls` is a list of declarations containing the value(s) of witnesses.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expand_exists.parse_ctx_exists.to_parse_ctx", "description": "Data known when parsing exists expressions (after parsing pi expressions).\n\n* `with_args` applies pi arguments to a term (eg `id` -> `id #2 #1 #0`).\n* `spec_chain` takes the form of `classical.some_spec^n (it_exists ...)`,\nwith `n` the depth of `\u2203` parsed.\n* `exists_decls` is a list of declarations containing the value(s) of witnesses.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expand_exists.parse_ctx_exists.with_args", "description": "Data known when parsing exists expressions (after parsing pi expressions).\n\n* `with_args` applies pi arguments to a term (eg `id` -> `id #2 #1 #0`).\n* `spec_chain` takes the form of `classical.some_spec^n (it_exists ...)`,\nwith `n` the depth of `\u2203` parsed.\n* `exists_decls` is a list of declarations containing the value(s) of witnesses.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expand_exists.parse_ctx_exists.spec_chain", "description": "Data known when parsing exists expressions (after parsing pi expressions).\n\n* `with_args` applies pi arguments to a term (eg `id` -> `id #2 #1 #0`).\n* `spec_chain` takes the form of `classical.some_spec^n (it_exists ...)`,\nwith `n` the depth of `\u2203` parsed.\n* `exists_decls` is a list of declarations containing the value(s) of witnesses.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expand_exists.parse_ctx_exists.exists_decls", "description": "Data known when parsing exists expressions (after parsing pi expressions).\n\n* `with_args` applies pi arguments to a term (eg `id` -> `id #2 #1 #0`).\n* `spec_chain` takes the form of `classical.some_spec^n (it_exists ...)`,\nwith `n` the depth of `\u2203` parsed.\n* `exists_decls` is a list of declarations containing the value(s) of witnesses.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.finite_stable_under_composition", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.finite_respects_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.finite_stable_under_base_change", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.uniform_equicontinuous_of_continuity_modulus", "description": " For a family of functions between (pseudo) metric spaces, a convenient way to prove\nuniform equicontinuity is to show that all of the functions share a common *global* continuity\nmodulus.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.equicontinuous_at_iff", "description": "Characterization of equicontinuity for families of functions between (pseudo) metric spaces.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.uniform_equicontinuous_iff", "description": " Characterization of uniform equicontinuity for families of functions between\n(pseudo) metric spaces.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.equicontinuous_at_iff_right", "description": " Characterization of equicontinuity for families of functions taking values in a (pseudo) metric\nspace.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.equicontinuous_at_iff_pair", "description": " Reformulation of `equicontinuous_at_iff_pair` for families of functions taking values in a\n(pseudo) metric space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.equicontinuous_of_continuity_modulus", "description": " For a family of functions between (pseudo) metric spaces, a convenient way to prove\nequicontinuity is to show that all of the functions share a common *global* continuity modulus.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.equicontinuous_at_of_continuity_modulus", "description": " For a family of functions to a (pseudo) metric spaces, a convenient way to prove\nequicontinuity at a point is to show that all of the functions share a common *local* continuity\nmodulus.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.uniform_equicontinuous_iff_right", "description": " Characterization of uniform equicontinuity for families of functions taking values in a\n(pseudo) metric space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite.upper_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.finite.lower_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.infinite_of_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.infinite_of_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finsupp.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_on_pow", "description": " `x^n`, `n : \u2115` is convex on `[0, +\u221e)` for all `n`.\n\nWe give an elementary proof rather than using the second derivative test, since this lemma is\nneeded early in the analysis library.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_add_mul_self_le_rpow_one_add", "description": " **Bernoulli's inequality** for real exponents, non-strict version: for `1 \u2264 p` and `-1 \u2264 s`\nwe have `1 + p * s \u2264 (1 + s) ^ p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "one_add_mul_self_lt_rpow_one_add", "description": " **Bernoulli's inequality** for real exponents, strict version: for `1 < p` and `-1 \u2264 s`, with\n`s \u2260 0`, we have `1 + p * s < (1 + s) ^ p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_on_rpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_convex_on_exp", "description": " `exp` is strictly convex on the whole real line.\n\nWe give an elementary proof rather than using the second derivative test, since this lemma is\nneeded early in the analysis library.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_on_zpow", "description": " `x^m`, `m : \u2124` is convex on `(0, +\u221e)` for all `m`.\n\nWe give an elementary proof rather than using the second derivative test, since this lemma is\nneeded early in the analysis library.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_convex_on_rpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_concave_on_log_Iio", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex_on_exp", "description": "`exp` is convex on the whole real line.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "even.convex_on_pow", "description": " `x^n`, `n : \u2115` is convex on the whole real line whenever `n` is even.\n\nWe give an elementary proof rather than using the second derivative test, since this lemma is\nneeded early in the analysis library.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_concave_on_log_Ioi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.rewrite_search", "description": "Search for a chain of rewrites to prove an equation or iff statement.\n\nCollects rewrite rules, runs a graph search to find a chain of rewrites to prove the\ncurrent target, and generates a string explanation for it.\n\nTakes an optional list of rewrite rules specified in the same way as the `rw` tactic accepts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.rewrite_search", "description": "Search for a chain of rewrites to prove an equation or iff statement.\n\nCollects rewrite rules, runs a graph search to find a chain of rewrites to prove the\ncurrent target, and generates a string explanation for it.\n\nTakes an optional list of rewrite rules specified in the same way as the `rw` tactic accepts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conformal_map_complex_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conformal_map_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conformal_map.is_complex_or_conj_linear", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conformal_map_iff_is_complex_or_conj_linear", "description": "A real continuous linear map on the complex plane is conformal if and only if the map or its\n   conjugate is complex linear, and the map is nonvanishing.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_conformal_map_complex_linear_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_rees_algebra_iff_support", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monomial_mem_adjoin_monomial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rees_algebra.is_noetherian_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rees_algebra.monomial_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_rees_algebra_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rees_algebra.algebra.finite_type", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rees_algebra.fg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "adjoin_monomial_eq_rees_algebra", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rees_algebra", "description": " The Rees algebra of an ideal `I`, defined as the subalgebra of `R[X]` whose `i`-th coefficient\nfalls in `I ^ i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ore_localization.ore_set", "description": " A submonoid `S` of a monoid `R` is (right) Ore if common factors on the left can be turned\ninto common factors on the right, and if each pair of `r : R` and `s : S` admits an Ore numerator\n`v : R` and an Ore denominator `u : S` such that `r * u = s * v`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ore_localization.ore_set.ore_left_cancel", "description": " A submonoid `S` of a monoid `R` is (right) Ore if common factors on the left can be turned\ninto common factors on the right, and if each pair of `r : R` and `s : S` admits an Ore numerator\n`v : R` and an Ore denominator `u : S` such that `r * u = s * v`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ore_localization.ore_set.ore_num", "description": " A submonoid `S` of a monoid `R` is (right) Ore if common factors on the left can be turned\ninto common factors on the right, and if each pair of `r : R` and `s : S` admits an Ore numerator\n`v : R` and an Ore denominator `u : S` such that `r * u = s * v`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ore_localization.ore_set.ore_denom", "description": " A submonoid `S` of a monoid `R` is (right) Ore if common factors on the left can be turned\ninto common factors on the right, and if each pair of `r : R` and `s : S` admits an Ore numerator\n`v : R` and an Ore denominator `u : S` such that `r * u = s * v`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ore_localization.ore_set.ore_eq", "description": " A submonoid `S` of a monoid `R` is (right) Ore if common factors on the left can be turned\ninto common factors on the right, and if each pair of `r : R` and `s : S` admits an Ore numerator\n`v : R` and an Ore denominator `u : S` such that `r * u = s * v`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ore_localization.ore_set_of_no_zero_divisors", "description": " In rings without zero divisors, the first (cancellability) condition is always fulfilled,\nit suffices to give a proof for the Ore condition itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ore_localization.ore_eq", "description": "The Ore condition of a fraction, expressed in terms of `ore_num` and `ore_denom`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ore_localization.ore_num", "description": "The Ore numerator of a fraction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ore_localization.ore_set_bot", "description": "The trivial submonoid is an Ore set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ore_localization.ore_denom", "description": "The Ore denominator of a fraction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ore_localization.ore_set_comm", "description": "Every submonoid of a commutative monoid is an Ore set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ore_localization.ore_condition", "description": " The Ore condition bundled in a sigma type. This is useful in situations where we want to obtain\nboth witnesses and the condition for a given fraction.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ore_localization.ore_set_of_cancel_monoid_with_zero", "description": " Cancellability in monoids with zeros can act as a replacement for the `ore_left_cancel`\ncondition of an ore set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ore_localization.ore_left_cancel", "description": " Common factors on the left can be turned into common factors on the right, a weak form of\ncancellability.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.to_Types_is_sheaf", "description": "We show that the presheaf of functions to a type `T`\n(no continuity assumptions, just plain functions)\nform a sheaf.\n\nIn fact, the proof is identical when we do this for dependent functions to a type family `T`,\nso we do the more general case.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.to_Type_is_sheaf", "description": "The presheaf of not-necessarily-continuous functions to\na target type `T` satsifies the sheaf condition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf_to_Type", "description": "The sheaf of not-necessarily-continuous functions on `X` with values in a type `T`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf_to_Types", "description": "The sheaf of not-necessarily-continuous functions on `X` with values in type family\n`T : X \u2192 Type u`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dense_range_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_action.dense_orbit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_open.Union_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_empty_or_univ_of_vadd_invariant_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_open.Union_preimage_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.exists_finite_cover_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.dense_orbit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eq_empty_or_univ_of_smul_invariant_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_action.is_minimal", "description": " An action of an additive monoid `M` on a topological space is called *minimal* if the `M`-orbit\nof every point `x : \u03b1` is dense.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_action.is_minimal.dense_orbit", "description": " An action of an additive monoid `M` on a topological space is called *minimal* if the `M`-orbit\nof every point `x : \u03b1` is dense.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dense_of_nonempty_smul_invariant", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_compact.exists_finite_cover_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_open.Union_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_open.exists_vadd_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dense_range_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dense_of_nonempty_vadd_invariant", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_minimal_iff_closed_smul_invariant", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_action.is_minimal_of_pretransitive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.is_minimal_of_pretransitive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_minimal_iff_closed_vadd_invariant", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_open.exists_smul_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_open.Union_preimage_vadd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.is_minimal", "description": " An action of a monoid `M` on a topological space is called *minimal* if the `M`-orbit of every\npoint `x : \u03b1` is dense.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_action.is_minimal.dense_orbit", "description": " An action of a monoid `M` on a topological space is called *minimal* if the `M`-orbit of every\npoint `x : \u03b1` is dense.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char.linear_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char.of_nat_to_nat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_equiv.map_multiset_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_equiv.map_list_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_equiv.map_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_equiv.map_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_equiv.map_multiset_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_equiv.map_list_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_equiv.unop_map_list_prod", "description": "An isomorphism into the opposite ring acts on the product by acting on the reversed elements", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.quiver.is_thin", "description": "A small category with products is a thin category.\n\nin Lean, a preorder category is one where the morphisms are in Prop, which is weaker than the usual\nnotion of a preorder/thin category which says that each homset is subsingleton; we show the latter\nrather than providing a `preorder C` instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "div_eq_quo_add_rem_div_add_rem_div", "description": "Let R be an integral domain and f, g\u2081, g\u2082 \u2208 R[X]. Let g\u2081 and g\u2082 be monic and coprime.\nThen, \u2203 q, r\u2081, r\u2082 \u2208 R[X] such that f / g\u2081g\u2082 = q + r\u2081/g\u2081 + r\u2082/g\u2082 and deg(r\u2081) < deg(g\u2081) and\ndeg(r\u2082) < deg(g\u2082).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "div_eq_quo_add_sum_rem_div", "description": "Let R be an integral domain and f \u2208 R[X]. Let s be a finite index set.\nThen, a fraction of the form f / \u220f (g i) can be rewritten as q + \u2211 (r i) / (g i), where\ndeg(r i) < deg(g i), provided that the g i are monic and pairwise coprime.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.elim_var", "description": "Use two lists of linear combinations (one in which the resultant terms\n   include occurrences of the `m`th variable with positive coefficients,\n   and one with negative coefficients) and linearly combine them in every\n   possible way that eliminates the `m`th variable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.trisect", "description": "Divide linear combinations into three groups by the coefficient of the\n   `m`th variable in their resultant terms: negative, zero, or positive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.elim_var_aux", "description": "Use two linear combinations to obtain a third linear combination\n   whose resultant term does not include the `m`th variable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.find_neg_const", "description": "Search through a list of (linear combination \u00d7 resultant term) pairs,\n   find the first pair whose resultant term has a negative constant term,\n   and return its linear combination", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.find_scalars", "description": "Perform Fourier\u2013Motzkin elimination to find a contradictory\n   linear combination of input constraints.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "omega.find_scalars_core", "description": "First, eliminate all variables by Fourier\u2013Motzkin elimination.\n   When all variables have been eliminated, find and return the\n   linear combination which produces a constraint of the form\n   `0 < k + t` such that `k` is the constant term of the RHS and `k < 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.equivalent_one_zero_neg_one_weighted_sum_squared", "description": " **Sylvester's law of inertia**: A real quadratic form is equivalent to a weighted\nsum of squares with the weights being \u00b11 or 0.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.isometry_sign_weighted_sum_squares", "description": " The isometry between a weighted sum of squares with weights `u` on the\n(non-zero) real numbers and the weighted sum of squares with weights `sign \u2218 u`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quadratic_form.equivalent_one_neg_one_weighted_sum_squared", "description": " **Sylvester's law of inertia**: A nondegenerate real quadratic form is equivalent to a weighted\nsum of squares with the weights being \u00b11.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.nth_rewrite", "description": " `nth_rewrite n rules` performs only the `n`th possible rewrite using the `rules`.\nThe tactics `nth_rewrite_lhs` and `nth_rewrite_rhs` are variants\nthat operate on the left and right hand sides of an equation or iff.\n\nNote: `n` is zero-based, so `nth_rewrite 0 h`\nwill rewrite along `h` at the first possible location.\n\nIn more detail, given `rules = [h1, ..., hk]`,\nthis tactic will search for all possible locations\nwhere one of `h1, ..., hk` can be rewritten,\nand perform the `n`th occurrence.\n\nExample: Given a goal of the form `a + x = x + b`, and hypothesis `h : x = y`,\nthe tactic `nth_rewrite 1 h` will change the goal to `a + x = y + b`.\n\nThe core `rewrite` has a `occs` configuration setting intended to achieve a similar\npurpose, but this doesn't really work. (If a rule matches twice, but with different\nvalues of arguments, the second match will not be identified.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.nth_rewrite_lhs", "description": " `nth_rewrite n rules` performs only the `n`th possible rewrite using the `rules`.\nThe tactics `nth_rewrite_lhs` and `nth_rewrite_rhs` are variants\nthat operate on the left and right hand sides of an equation or iff.\n\nNote: `n` is zero-based, so `nth_rewrite 0 h`\nwill rewrite along `h` at the first possible location.\n\nIn more detail, given `rules = [h1, ..., hk]`,\nthis tactic will search for all possible locations\nwhere one of `h1, ..., hk` can be rewritten,\nand perform the `n`th occurrence.\n\nExample: Given a goal of the form `a + x = x + b`, and hypothesis `h : x = y`,\nthe tactic `nth_rewrite 1 h` will change the goal to `a + x = y + b`.\n\nThe core `rewrite` has a `occs` configuration setting intended to achieve a similar\npurpose, but this doesn't really work. (If a rule matches twice, but with different\nvalues of arguments, the second match will not be identified.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.target_or_hyp_type", "description": " Returns the target of the goal when passed `none`,\notherwise, return the type of `h` in `some h`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.nth_rewrite_core", "description": " Rewrite the `n`th occurrence of the rewrite rules `q` (optionally on a side)\nat all the locations `loc`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.nth_rewrite_rhs", "description": " `nth_rewrite n rules` performs only the `n`th possible rewrite using the `rules`.\nThe tactics `nth_rewrite_lhs` and `nth_rewrite_rhs` are variants\nthat operate on the left and right hand sides of an equation or iff.\n\nNote: `n` is zero-based, so `nth_rewrite 0 h`\nwill rewrite along `h` at the first possible location.\n\nIn more detail, given `rules = [h1, ..., hk]`,\nthis tactic will search for all possible locations\nwhere one of `h1, ..., hk` can be rewritten,\nand perform the `n`th occurrence.\n\nExample: Given a goal of the form `a + x = x + b`, and hypothesis `h : x = y`,\nthe tactic `nth_rewrite 1 h` will change the goal to `a + x = y + b`.\n\nThe core `rewrite` has a `occs` configuration setting intended to achieve a similar\npurpose, but this doesn't really work. (If a rule matches twice, but with different\nvalues of arguments, the second match will not be identified.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.replace_in_state", "description": " Replace the target, or a hypothesis, depending on whether `none` or `some h` is given as the\nfirst argument.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.nth_rewrite / nth_rewrite_lhs / nth_rewrite_rhs", "description": " `nth_rewrite n rules` performs only the `n`th possible rewrite using the `rules`.\nThe tactics `nth_rewrite_lhs` and `nth_rewrite_rhs` are variants\nthat operate on the left and right hand sides of an equation or iff.\n\nNote: `n` is zero-based, so `nth_rewrite 0 h`\nwill rewrite along `h` at the first possible location.\n\nIn more detail, given `rules = [h1, ..., hk]`,\nthis tactic will search for all possible locations\nwhere one of `h1, ..., hk` can be rewritten,\nand perform the `n`th occurrence.\n\nExample: Given a goal of the form `a + x = x + b`, and hypothesis `h : x = y`,\nthe tactic `nth_rewrite 1 h` will change the goal to `a + x = y + b`.\n\nThe core `rewrite` has a `occs` configuration setting intended to achieve a similar\npurpose, but this doesn't really work. (If a rule matches twice, but with different\nvalues of arguments, the second match will not be identified.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.get_nth_rewrite", "description": " Get the `n`th rewrite of rewrite rules `q` in expression `e`,\nor fail if there are not enough such rewrites.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.get_nth_rewrite_with_zoom", "description": " Rewrite the `n`th occurrence of the rewrite rules `q` of (optionally after zooming into) a\nhypothesis or target `h` which is an application of a relation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.is_coprime_of_dvd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd_monoid", "description": "Create a `gcd_monoid` whose `gcd_monoid.gcd` matches `euclidean_domain.gcd`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "right_div_gcd_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.gcd_is_unit_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gcd_ne_zero_of_right", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.dvd_or_coprime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "left_div_gcd_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_coprime_div_gcd_div_gcd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_domain.span_gcd", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gcd_ne_zero_of_left", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.End_monoidal.category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.End_monoidal", "description": "The endomorphisms of an object in a bicategory can be considered as a monoidal category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.End_monoidal.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.End_monoidal.monoidal_category", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.coe_neg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_top.linear_ordered_add_comm_group_with_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans", "description": "`nat_trans F G` represents a natural transformation between functors `F` and `G`.\n\nThe field `app` provides the components of the natural transformation.\n\nNaturality is expressed by `\u03b1.naturality_lemma`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.app", "description": "`nat_trans F G` represents a natural transformation between functors `F` and `G`.\n\nThe field `app` provides the components of the natural transformation.\n\nNaturality is expressed by `\u03b1.naturality_lemma`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.naturality'", "description": "`nat_trans F G` represents a natural transformation between functors `F` and `G`.\n\nThe field `app` provides the components of the natural transformation.\n\nNaturality is expressed by `\u03b1.naturality_lemma`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.naturality", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.congr_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.vcomp", "description": "`vcomp \u03b1 \u03b2` is the vertical compositions of natural transformations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.naturality_assoc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.id", "description": "`nat_trans.id F` is the identity natural transformation on a functor `F`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.ext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.vcomp_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.nat_trans.id_app'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.conj_Aut_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.conj_Aut_trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.conj_Aut_hom", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.trans_conj_Aut", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.conj", "description": " An isomorphism between two objects defines a monoid isomorphism between their\nmonoid of endomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.conj_id", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.hom_congr_trans", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.self_symm_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.conj_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.hom_congr", "description": " If `X` is isomorphic to `X\u2081` and `Y` is isomorphic to `Y\u2081`, then\nthere is a natural bijection between `X \u27f6 Y` and `X\u2081 \u27f6 Y\u2081`. See also `equiv.arrow_congr`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.symm_self_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.conj_Aut_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.refl_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.hom_congr_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.conj_comp", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.hom_congr_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.trans_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.hom_congr_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.conj_Aut_zpow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.conj_Aut_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.map_conj_Aut", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.hom_congr_refl", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.conj_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.conj_Aut", "description": "`conj` defines a group isomorphisms between groups of automorphisms", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.map_hom_congr", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.map_conj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.mul_dist_eq_mul_dist_of_cospherical_of_angle_eq_pi", "description": "**Intersecting Chords Theorem**.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.mul_dist_eq_mul_dist_of_cospherical", "description": " If `A`, `B`, `C`, `D` are cospherical and `P` is on both lines `AB` and `CD`, then\n`AP * BP = CP * DP`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_geometry.mul_norm_eq_abs_sub_sq_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.mul_dist_eq_abs_sub_sq_dist", "description": " If `P` is a point on the line `AB` and `Q` is equidistant from `A` and `B`, then\n`AP * BP = abs (BQ ^ 2 - PQ ^ 2)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "euclidean_geometry.mul_dist_eq_mul_dist_of_cospherical_of_angle_eq_zero", "description": "**Intersecting Secants Theorem**.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordering", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "ordering.lt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "ordering.eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "ordering.gt", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "ordering.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "ordering.has_repr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "ordering.swap_swap", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "cmp_using", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "cmp", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "ordering.swap", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "ordering.or_else", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_localization.order_embedding", "description": " If `S` is the localization of `R` at a submonoid, the ordering of ideals of `S` is\nembedded in the ordering of ideals of `R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.is_prime_of_is_prime_disjoint", "description": " If `R` is a ring, then prime ideals in the localization at `M`\ncorrespond to prime ideals in the original ring `R` that are disjoint from `M`.\nThis lemma gives the particular case for an ideal and its map,\nsee `le_rel_iso_of_prime` for the more general relation isomorphism, and the reverse implication", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.comap_map_of_is_prime_disjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.order_iso_of_prime", "description": " If `R` is a ring, then prime ideals in the localization at `M`\ncorrespond to prime ideals in the original ring `R` that are disjoint from `M`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.bot_lt_comap_prime", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.surjective_quotient_map_of_maximal_of_localization", "description": "`quotient_map` applied to maximal ideals of a localization is `surjective`.\n The quotient by a maximal ideal is a field, so inverses to elements already exist,\n and the localization necessarily maps the equivalence class of the inverse in the localization", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.is_prime_iff_is_prime_disjoint", "description": " If `R` is a ring, then prime ideals in the localization at `M`\ncorrespond to prime ideals in the original ring `R` that are disjoint from `M`.\nThis lemma gives the particular case for an ideal and its comap,\nsee `le_rel_iso_of_prime` for the more general relation isomorphism", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.mem_map_algebra_map_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.map_comap", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_bounded_zero_one_of_closed", "description": " Urysohns lemma: if `s` and `t` are two disjoint closed sets in a normal topological space `X`,\nthen there exists a continuous function `f : X \u2192 \u211d` such that\n\n* `f` equals zero on `s`;\n* `f` equals one on `t`;\n* `0 \u2264 f x \u2264 1` for all `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_bounded_mem_Icc_of_closed_of_le", "description": " Urysohns lemma: if `s` and `t` are two disjoint closed sets in a normal topological space `X`,\nand `a \u2264 b` are two real numbers, then there exists a continuous function `f : X \u2192 \u211d` such that\n\n* `f` equals `a` on `s`;\n* `f` equals `b` on `t`;\n* `a \u2264 f x \u2264 b` for all `x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.of.has_groupoid", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "structure_groupoid.local_invariant_prop.section_spec", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.of.charted_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "structure_groupoid.local_invariant_prop.sheaf", "description": " Let `P` be a `local_invariant_prop` for functions between spaces with the groupoids `G`, `G'`\nand let `M`, `M'` be charted spaces modelled on the model spaces of those groupoids.  Then there is\na sheaf of types on `M` which, to each open set `U` in `M`, associates the type of bundled\nfunctions from `U` to `M'` satisfying the lift of `P`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "structure_groupoid.local_invariant_prop.local_predicate", "description": " Let `P` be a `local_invariant_prop` for functions between spaces with the groupoids `G`, `G'`\nand let `M`, `M'` be charted spaces modelled on the model spaces of those groupoids.  Then there is\nan induced `local_predicate` on the functions from `M` to `M'`, given by `lift_prop P`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "structure_groupoid.local_invariant_prop.sheaf_has_coe_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.denominators_recurrence", "description": "Shows that `B\u2099 = b\u2099 * B\u2099\u208b\u2081 + a\u2099 * B\u2099\u208b\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.continuants_recurrence", "description": "Shows that `A\u2099 = b\u2099 * A\u2099\u208b\u2081 + a\u2099 * A\u2099\u208b\u2082` and `B\u2099 = b\u2099 * B\u2099\u208b\u2081 + a\u2099 * B\u2099\u208b\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.continuants_recurrence_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.continuants_aux_recurrence", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "generalized_continued_fraction.numerators_recurrence", "description": "Shows that `A\u2099 = b\u2099 * A\u2099\u208b\u2081 + a\u2099 * A\u2099\u208b\u2082`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "controlled_closure_range_of_complete", "description": " Given `f : normed_add_group_hom G H` for some complete `G`, if every element `x` of the image of\nan isometric immersion `j : normed_add_group_hom K H` has a preimage under `f` whose norm is at most\n`C*\u2016x\u2016` then the same holds for elements of the (topological) closure of this image with constant\n`C+\u03b5` instead of `C`, for any positive `\u03b5`.\nThis is useful in particular if `j` is the inclusion of a normed group into its completion\n(in this case the closure is the full target group).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "controlled_closure_of_complete", "description": " Given `f : normed_add_group_hom G H` for some complete `G` and a subgroup `K` of `H`, if every\nelement `x` of `K` has a preimage under `f` whose norm is at most `C*\u2016x\u2016` then the same holds for\nelements of the (topological) closure of `K` with constant `C+\u03b5` instead of `C`, for any\npositive `\u03b5`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.nth_rewrite.congr.rewrite_without_new_mvars", "description": " Helper function which just tries to rewrite `e` using the equality `r` without assigning any\nmetavariables in the tactic state, and without creating any metavariables which cannot be\ndischarged by `cfg.discharger` in the process.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.nth_rewrite.congr.all_rewrites", "description": " List of all rewrites of an expression `e` by `r : expr \u00d7 bool`.\nHere `r.1` is the substituting expression and `r.2` flags the direction of the rewrite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.nth_rewrite.congr.rewrite_at_lens", "description": " Function which tries to perform the rewrite associated to the equality `r : expr \u00d7 bool` (the\nbool indicates whether we should flip the equality first), at the position pointed to by\n`l : expr_lens`, by rewriting `e : expr`. If this succeeds, `rewrite_at_lens` computes (by unwinding\n`l`) a proof that the entire expression represented by `l.fill e` is equal to the entire expression\n`l.fill f`, where `f` is the `expr` which has replaced `e` due to the rewrite. It then returns the\nsingleton list containing this rewrite (and the tracking data needed to reconstruct it directly). If\nit fails, it just returns the empty list.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.nth_rewrite.congr.rewrite_is_of_entire", "description": " Returns true if the argument is a proof that the entire expression was rewritten.\n\nThis is a bit of a hack: we manually inspect the proof that `rewrite_core` produced, and deduce from\nthat whether or not the entire expression was rewritten.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.well_powered_AddCommGroup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.over.category_theory.limits.has_pullbacks", "description": "Make sure we can derive pullbacks in `over B`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.over.has_finite_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.over.has_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.over.category_theory.limits.has_equalizers", "description": "Make sure we can derive equalizers in `over B`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto_nhds_at_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto_nhds_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.nhds_at_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "filter.tendsto.nhds_at_top", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fgModule.category_theory.forget\u2082.category_theory.creates_limits_of_shape", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fgModule.category_theory.limits.pi_obj.finite_dimensional", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fgModule.forget\u2082_creates_limit", "description": "The forgetful functor from `fgModule k` to `Module k` creates all finite limits.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fgModule.category_theory.limits.limit.finite_dimensional", "description": " Finite limits of finite dimensional vectors spaces are finite dimensional,\nbecause we can realise them as subobjects of a finite product.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fgModule.category_theory.limits.has_finite_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fgModule.category_theory.forget\u2082.category_theory.limits.preserves_finite_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eigenvalue_nonneg_of_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.orthogonal_family_eigenspaces'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.eigenvalues", "description": " The sequence of real eigenvalues associated to the standard orthonormal basis of eigenvectors\nfor a self-adjoint operator `T` on `E`.\n\nTODO Postcompose with a permutation so that these eigenvalues are listed in increasing order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.apply_eigenvector_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.has_eigenvector_eigenvector_basis", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.diagonalization_basis_apply_self_apply", "description": " *Diagonalization theorem*, *spectral theorem*; version 2: A self-adjoint operator `T` on a\nfinite-dimensional inner product space `E` acts diagonally on the identification of `E` with\nEuclidean space induced by an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors of `T`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.diagonalization_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.orthogonal_supr_eigenspaces_invariant", "description": " The mutual orthogonal complement of the eigenspaces of a self-adjoint operator on an inner\nproduct space is an invariant subspace of the operator.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.orthogonal_supr_eigenspaces", "description": " The mutual orthogonal complement of the eigenspaces of a self-adjoint operator on an inner\nproduct space has no eigenvalues.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.orthogonal_supr_eigenspaces_eq_bot", "description": " The mutual orthogonal complement of the eigenspaces of a self-adjoint operator on a\nfinite-dimensional inner product space is trivial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.direct_sum_decompose_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_apply_eigenvector", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.invariant_orthogonal_eigenspace", "description": "A self-adjoint operator preserves orthogonal complements of its eigenspaces.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.direct_sum_is_internal", "description": " The eigenspaces of a self-adjoint operator on a finite-dimensional inner product space `E` gives\nan internal direct sum decomposition of `E`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.has_eigenvalue_eigenvalues", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.direct_sum_decomposition", "description": " The eigenspaces of a self-adjoint operator on a finite-dimensional inner product space `E` gives\nan internal direct sum decomposition of `E`.\n\nNote this takes `hT` as a `fact` to allow it to be an instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.conj_eigenvalue_eq_self", "description": "The eigenvalues of a self-adjoint operator are real.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.orthogonal_family_eigenspaces", "description": "The eigenspaces of a self-adjoint operator are mutually orthogonal.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "eigenvalue_pos_of_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.eigenvector_basis", "description": " A choice of orthonormal basis of eigenvectors for self-adjoint operator `T` on a\nfinite-dimensional inner product space `E`.\n\nTODO Postcompose with a permutation so that these eigenvectors are listed in increasing order of\neigenvalue.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.orthogonal_supr_eigenspaces_eq_bot'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.diagonalization", "description": " Isometry from an inner product space `E` to the direct sum of the eigenspaces of some\nself-adjoint operator `T` on `E`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_symmetric.diagonalization_apply_self_apply", "description": " *Diagonalization theorem*, *spectral theorem*; version 1: A self-adjoint operator `T` on a\nfinite-dimensional inner product space `E` acts diagonally on the decomposition of `E` into the\ndirect sum of the eigenspaces of `T`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.has_projective_separator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.has_injective_coseparator", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.projective_iff_preserves_epimorphisms_preadditive_coyoneda_obj'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.projective.projective_iff_preserves_epimorphisms_preadditive_coyoneda_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_det_X_add_C_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.leading_coeff_det_X_one_add_C", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.coeff_det_X_add_C_card", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.nat_degree_det_X_add_C_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.cardinal_mk_eq_max_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.cardinal_mk_le_max", "description": " The cardinality of the multivariate polynomial ring, `mv_polynomial \u03c3 R` is at most the maximum\nof `#R`, `#\u03c3` and `\u2135\u2080`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.cardinal_lift_mk_le_max", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.cardinal_mk_eq_lift", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.cardinal_mk_eq_max", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso.inv_hom_id_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.inv_hom_id_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.is_iso.hom_inv_id_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.iso.hom_inv_id_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_limits_of_shape_of_essentially_small", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_products_of_shape_of_small", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_coproducts_of_shape_of_small", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_colimits_of_shape_of_essentially_small", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.not_second_countable_topology_alexandroff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.not_first_countable_topology_alexandroff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.not_countably_generated_cocompact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.totally_disconnected_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.cocompact_inf_nhds_ne_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.interior_compact_eq_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.dense_compl_compact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.not_countably_generated_nhds_infty_alexandroff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_limit_cospan_of_has_limit_pair_of_has_limit_parallel_pair", "description": "If the product `X \u2a2f Y` and the equalizer of `\u03c0\u2081 \u226b f` and `\u03c0\u2082 \u226b g` exist, then the\n   pullback of `f` and `g` exists: It is given by composing the equalizer with the projections.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_pushouts_of_has_binary_coproducts_of_has_coequalizers", "description": "If a category has all binary coproducts and all coequalizers, then it also has all pushouts.\n   As usual, this is not an instance, since there may be a more direct way to construct\n   pushouts.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_colimit_span_of_has_colimit_pair_of_has_colimit_parallel_pair", "description": "If the coproduct `Y \u2a3f Z` and the coequalizer of `f \u226b \u03b9\u2081` and `g \u226b \u03b9\u2082` exist, then the\n   pushout of `f` and `g` exists: It is given by composing the inclusions with the coequalizer.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.limits.has_pullbacks_of_has_binary_products_of_has_equalizers", "description": "If a category has all binary products and all equalizers, then it also has all pullbacks.\n   As usual, this is not an instance, since there may be a more direct way to construct\n   pullbacks.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_bind", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_bind.bind", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_bind.seq", "description": "Identical to `has_bind.and_then`, but it is not inlined.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "has_bind.and_then", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad.to_applicative", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad.to_has_bind", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "return", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.tsum_eq_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.has_sum'", "description": "Applying a continuous linear map commutes with taking an (infinite) sum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.summable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.has_sum", "description": "Applying a continuous linear map commutes with taking an (infinite) sum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.map_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_sum.mapL", "description": "**Alias** of `continuous_linear_map.has_sum`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_sum.smul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "summable.mapL", "description": "**Alias** of `continuous_linear_map.summable`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "summable.smul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.has_sum", "description": "Applying a continuous linear map commutes with taking an (infinite) sum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.map_tsum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsum_smul_const", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.summable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_fiberwise_linear.locality_aux\u2082", "description": " Let `e` be a local homeomorphism of `B \u00d7 F` whose source is `U \u00d7\u02e2 univ`, for some set `U` in\n`B`, and which, at any point `x` in `U`, admits a neighbourhood `u` of `x` such that `e` is equal on\n`u \u00d7\u02e2 univ` to some bi-smooth fiberwise linear local homeomorphism.  Then `e` itself is equal to\nsome bi-smooth fiberwise linear local homeomorphism.\n\nThis is the key mathematical point of the `locality` condition in the construction of the\n`structure_groupoid` of bi-smooth fiberwise linear local homeomorphisms.  The proof is by gluing\ntogether the various bi-smooth fiberwise linear local homeomorphism which exist locally.\n\nThe `U` in the conclusion is the same `U` as in the hypothesis. We state it like this, because this\nis exactly what we need for `smooth_fiberwise_linear`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiberwise_linear.source_trans_local_homeomorph", "description": " Compute the source of the composition of two local homeomorphisms induced by fiberwise linear\nequivalences.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiberwise_linear.target_trans_local_homeomorph", "description": " Compute the target of the composition of two local homeomorphisms induced by fiberwise linear\nequivalences.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiberwise_linear.local_homeomorph", "description": " For `B` a topological space and `F` a `\ud835\udd5c`-normed space, a map from `U : set B` to `F \u2243L[\ud835\udd5c] F`\ndetermines a local homeomorphism from `B \u00d7 F` to itself by its action fiberwise.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_fiberwise_linear.locality_aux\u2081", "description": " Let `e` be a local homeomorphism of `B \u00d7 F`.  Suppose that at every point `p` in the source of\n`e`, there is some neighbourhood `s` of `p` on which `e` is equal to a bi-smooth fiberwise linear\nlocal homeomorphism.\nThen the source of `e` is of the form `U \u00d7\u02e2 univ`, for some set `U` in `B`, and, at any point `x` in\n`U`, admits a neighbourhood `u` of `x` such that `e` is equal on `u \u00d7\u02e2 univ` to some bi-smooth\nfiberwise linear local homeomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fiberwise_linear.trans_local_homeomorph_apply", "description": " Compute the composition of two local homeomorphisms induced by fiberwise linear\nequivalences.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_fiberwise_linear", "description": " For `B` a manifold and `F` a normed space, the groupoid on `B \u00d7 F` consisting of local\nhomeomorphisms which are bi-smooth and fiberwise linear, and induce the identity on `B`.\nWhen a (topological) vector bundle is smooth, then the composition of charts associated\nto the vector bundle belong to this groupoid.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mem_smooth_fiberwise_linear_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cartesian_closed_functor_of_left_adjoint_preserves_binary_products", "description": "If `F` is full and faithful, and has a left adjoint which preserves binary products, then it is\ncartesian closed.\n\nTODO: Show the converse, that if `F` is cartesian closed and its left adjoint preserves binary\nproducts, then it is full and faithful.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.frobenius_morphism_iso_of_preserves_binary_products", "description": "If `F` is full and faithful and has a left adjoint `L` which preserves binary products, then the\nFrobenius morphism is an isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exp_comparison_whisker_left", "description": "The exponential comparison map is natural in `A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cartesian_closed_functor", "description": "The functor `F` is cartesian closed (ie preserves exponentials) if each natural transformation\n`exp_comparison F A` is an isomorphism", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.cartesian_closed_functor.comparison_iso", "description": "The functor `F` is cartesian closed (ie preserves exponentials) if each natural transformation\n`exp_comparison F A` is an isomorphism", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.frobenius_morphism_mate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.frobenius_morphism", "description": "The Frobenius morphism for an adjunction `L \u22a3 F` at `A` is given by the morphism\n\n    L(FA \u2a2f B) \u27f6 LFA \u2a2f LB \u27f6 A \u2a2f LB\n\nnatural in `B`, where the first morphism is the product comparison and the latter uses the counit\nof the adjunction.\n\nWe will show that if `C` and `D` are cartesian closed, then this morphism is an isomorphism for all\n`A` iff `F` is a cartesian closed functor, i.e. it preserves exponentials.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coev_exp_comparison", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exp_comparison", "description": "The exponential comparison map.\n`F` is a cartesian closed functor if this is an iso for all `A`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exp_comparison_ev", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.uncurry_exp_comparison", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.frobenius_morphism_iso_of_exp_comparison_iso", "description": "If the exponential comparison transformation (at `A`) is an isomorphism, then the Frobenius morphism\nat `A` is an isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.exp_comparison_iso_of_frobenius_morphism_iso", "description": "If the Frobenius morphism at `A` is an isomorphism, then the exponential comparison transformation\n(at `A`) is an isomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.exists_mem_of_not_is_unit_aeval_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.map_pow_of_pos", "description": " A specialization of `spectrum.map_polynomial_aeval_of_nonempty` to monic monomials for\nconvenience.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.map_polynomial_aeval_of_nonempty", "description": " In this version of the spectral mapping theorem, we assume the spectrum\nis nonempty instead of assuming the degree of the polynomial is positive.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.map_pow_of_nonempty", "description": " A specialization of `spectrum.map_polynomial_aeval_of_nonempty` to monic monomials for\nconvenience.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.map_polynomial_aeval_of_degree_pos", "description": " The *spectral mapping theorem* for polynomials.  Note: the assumption `degree p > 0`\nis necessary in case `\u03c3 a = \u2205`, for then the left-hand side is `\u2205` and the right-hand side,\nassuming `[nontrivial A]`, is `{k}` where `p = polynomial.C k`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.subset_polynomial_aeval", "description": " Half of the spectral mapping theorem for polynomials. We prove it separately\nbecause it holds over any field, whereas `spectrum.map_polynomial_aeval_of_degree_pos` and\n`spectrum.map_polynomial_aeval_of_nonempty` need the field to be algebraically closed.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.pow_image_subset", "description": "A specialization of `spectrum.subset_polynomial_aeval` to monic monomials for convenience.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "spectrum.nonempty_of_is_alg_closed_of_finite_dimensional", "description": "Every element `a` in a nontrivial finite-dimensional algebra `A`\nover an algebraically closed field `\ud835\udd5c` has non-empty spectrum.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.card_le", "description": "A localization always has cardinality less than or equal to the base ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.card", "description": "If you do not localize at any zero-divisors, localization preserves cardinality.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_subgroup.nsmul_mem_zmultiples_iff_exists_sub_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quotient_add_group.zmultiples_nsmul_eq_nsmul_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "quotient_add_group.zmultiples_zsmul_eq_zsmul_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_subgroup.zsmul_mem_zmultiples_iff_exists_sub_div", "description": "`z \u2022 r` is a multiple of `p` iff `r` is `pk/z` above a multiple of `p`, where `0 \u2264 k < |z|`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_eq_prod_roots_charpoly", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.det_eq_prod_roots_charpoly_of_splits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.trace_eq_sum_roots_charpoly", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.trace_eq_sum_roots_charpoly_of_splits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.hasse_preconnected_of_pred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.hasse_dual_iso", "description": "`\u03b1\u1d52\u1d48` and `\u03b1` have the same Hasse diagram.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.path_graph_preconnected", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.path_graph", "description": "The path graph on `n` vertices.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.path_graph_connected", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.hasse_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.hasse_dual_iso_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.hasse", "description": "The Hasse diagram of an order as a simple graph. The graph of the covering relation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.hasse_adj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.hasse_preconnected_of_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.hasse_dual_iso_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.cos_lt_one_div_sqrt_sq_add_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.le_tan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.sin_gt_sub_cube", "description": " For 0 < x \u2264 1 we have x - x ^ 3 / 4 < sin x.\n\nThis is also true for x > 1, but it's nontrivial for x just above 1. This inequality is not\ntight; the tighter inequality is sin x > x - x ^ 3 / 6 for all x > 0, but this inequality has\na simpler proof.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.lt_tan", "description": " For all `0 < x < \u03c0/2` we have `x < tan x`.\n\nThis is proved by checking that the function `tan x - x` vanishes\nat zero and has non-negative derivative.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.deriv_tan_sub_id", "description": "The derivative of `tan x - x` is `1/(cos x)^2 - 1` away from the zeroes of cos.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.cos_le_one_div_sqrt_sq_add_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.sin_lt", "description": "For 0 < x, we have sin x < x.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_terminal_of_equivalence", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.has_initial_of_equivalence", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.equivalence.has_terminal_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.equivalence.has_initial_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.to_matrix_transpose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.to_lin_transpose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.mem_foldr_sup_support_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.support_sum_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.support_sum_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.support_sum_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.support_sum_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.mem_sup_map_support_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.mem_sup_support_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.support_sum_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "list.support_sum_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal_pregroupoid", "description": "The pregroupoid of conformal maps.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "conformal_groupoid", "description": "The groupoid of conformal maps.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "theorems_100.cubic_monic_eq_zero_iff", "description": "Roots of a monic cubic whose discriminant is nonzero.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.cubic_eq_zero_iff_of_p_eq_zero", "description": "the solution of the cubic equation when p equals zero.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.cube_root_of_unity_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.cubic_eq_zero_iff", "description": "**The Solution of Cubic**.\n The roots of a cubic polynomial whose discriminant is nonzero.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "theorems_100.cubic_basic_eq_zero_iff", "description": "The roots of a monic cubic whose quadratic term is zero and whose discriminant is nonzero.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "probability_theory.lintegral_mul_indicator_eq_lintegral_mul_lintegral_indicator", "description": "If a random variable `f` in `\u211d\u22650\u221e` is independent of an event `T`, then if you restrict the\n random variable to `T`, then `E[f * indicator T c 0]=E[f] * E[indicator T c 0]`. It is useful for\n `lintegral_mul_eq_lintegral_mul_lintegral_of_independent_measurable_space`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.indep_fun.integrable_left_of_integrable_mul", "description": " If the product of two independent real_valued random variables is integrable and\nthe second one is not almost everywhere zero, then the first one is integrable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.lintegral_mul_eq_lintegral_mul_lintegral_of_independent_measurable_space", "description": "If `f` and `g` are independent random variables with values in `\u211d\u22650\u221e`,\n  then `E[f * g] = E[f] * E[g]`. However, instead of directly using the independence\n  of the random variables, it uses the independence of measurable spaces for the\n  domains of `f` and `g`. This is similar to the sigma-algebra approach to\n  independence. See `lintegral_mul_eq_lintegral_mul_lintegral_of_independent_fn` for\n  a more common variant of the product of independent variables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.indep_fun.integrable_right_of_integrable_mul", "description": " If the product of two independent real_valued random variables is integrable and the\nfirst one is not almost everywhere zero, then the second one is integrable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.lintegral_mul_eq_lintegral_mul_lintegral_of_indep_fun", "description": "If `f` and `g` are independent random variables with values in `\u211d\u22650\u221e`,\n  then `E[f * g] = E[f] * E[g]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.indep_fun.integrable_mul", "description": "The product of two independent, integrable, real_valued random variables is integrable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.indep_fun.integral_mul", "description": "The (Bochner) integral of the product of two independent random\n variables is the product of their integrals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.indep_fun_iff_integral_comp_mul", "description": "Independence of functions `f` and `g` into arbitrary types is characterized by the relation\n `E[(\u03c6 \u2218 f) * (\u03c8 \u2218 g)] = E[\u03c6 \u2218 f] * E[\u03c8 \u2218 g]` for all measurable `\u03c6` and `\u03c8` with values in `\u211d`\n satisfying appropriate integrability conditions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.lintegral_mul_eq_lintegral_mul_lintegral_of_indep_fun''", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.lintegral_mul_eq_lintegral_mul_lintegral_of_indep_fun'", "description": "If `f` and `g` with values in `\u211d\u22650\u221e` are independent and almost everywhere measurable,\n  then `E[f * g] = E[f] * E[g]` (slightly generalizing\n  `lintegral_mul_eq_lintegral_mul_lintegral_of_indep_fun`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.indep_fun.integral_mul_of_nonneg", "description": "The (Bochner) integral of the product of two independent, nonnegative random\n variables is the product of their integrals. The proof is just plumbing around\n `lintegral_mul_eq_lintegral_mul_lintegral_of_indep_fun'`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.indep_fun.integral_mul_of_integrable", "description": "The (Bochner) integral of the product of two independent, integrable random\n variables is the product of their integrals. The proof is pedestrian decomposition\n into their positive and negative parts in order to apply `indep_fun.integral_mul_of_nonneg`\n four times.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "probability_theory.indep_fun.integral_mul'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.tsum_eq_tsum_fourier_integral_of_rpow_decay_of_summable", "description": " **Poisson's summation formula**, assuming that `f` decays as\n`|x| ^ (-b)` for some `1 < b` and its Fourier transform is summable.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.fourier_coeff_tsum_comp_add", "description": " The key lemma for Poisson summation: the `m`-th Fourier coefficient of the periodic function\n`\u2211' n : \u2124, f (x + n)` is the value at `m` of the Fourier transform of `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_O_norm_restrict_cocompact", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.tsum_eq_tsum_fourier_integral_of_rpow_decay", "description": " **Poisson's summation formula**, assuming that both `f` and its Fourier transform decay as\n`|x| ^ (-b)` for some `1 < b`. (This is the one-dimensional case of Corollary VII.2.6 of Stein and\nWeiss, *Introduction to Fourier analysis on Euclidean spaces*.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_O_norm_Icc_restrict_at_top", "description": " If `f` is `O(x ^ (-b))` at infinity, then so is the function\n`\u03bb x, \u2016f.restrict (Icc (x + R) (x + S))\u2016` for any fixed `R` and `S`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_O_norm_Icc_restrict_at_bot", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "schwartz_map.tsum_eq_tsum_fourier_integral", "description": "**Poisson's summation formula** for Schwartz functions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.tsum_eq_tsum_fourier_integral", "description": "**Poisson's summation formula**, most general form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.injective.injective_iff_preserves_epimorphisms_preadditive_yoneda_obj'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.injective.injective_iff_preserves_epimorphisms_preadditive_yoneda_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.forget.category_theory.creates_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.category_theory.limits.has_colimits_of_size", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.is_sheaf_of_is_limit", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.limit_is_sheaf", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.sheaf.category_theory.limits.has_limits_of_size", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.category_theory.limits.has_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mathlib_dir_locator", "description": "This is a dummy declaration that is used to determine the project folder of mathlib, using the\n tactic `tactic.decl_olean`. This is used in `tactic.get_mathlib_dir`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "localization.away_equiv_adjoin", "description": "The `R`-`alg_equiv` between the localization of `R` away from `r` and\n   `R` with an inverse of `r` adjoined.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.adjoin_inv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.away.finite_presentation", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.conj_cle_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.re_clm_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.re_clm_nnnorm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.conj_cle_nnorm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.det_conj_lie", "description": "The determinant of `conj_lie`, as a linear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.im_clm_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.of_real_clm_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.linear_equiv_det_conj_lie", "description": "The determinant of `conj_lie`, as a linear equiv.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.of_real_clm_nnnorm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.im_clm_nnnorm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.teichmuller_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.ghost_component_teichmuller", "description": "The `n`-th ghost component of `teichmuller p r` is `r ^ p ^ n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.teichmuller_fun", "description": "The underlying function of the monoid hom `witt_vector.teichmuller`.\nThe `0`-th coefficient of `teichmuller_fun p r` is `r`, and all others are `0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.teichmuller_coeff_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.map_teichmuller", "description": "`teichmuller` is a natural transformation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.teichmuller", "description": " The Teichm\u00fcller lift of an element of `R` to `\ud835\udd4e R`.\nThe `0`-th coefficient of `teichmuller p r` is `r`, and all others are `0`.\nThis is a monoid homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "witt_vector.teichmuller_coeff_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_ne_zero_mem_lattice_of_measure_mul_two_pow_lt_measure", "description": " The **Minkowksi Convex Body Theorem**. If `s` is a convex symmetric domain of `E` whose volume\nis large enough compared to the covolume of a lattice `L` of `E`, then it contains a non-zero\nlattice point of `L`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.exists_pair_mem_lattice_not_disjoint_vadd", "description": " **Blichfeldt's Theorem**. If the volume of the set `s` is larger than the covolume of the\ncountable subgroup `L` of `E`, then there exists two distincts points `x, y \u2208 L` such that `(x + s)`\nand `(y + s)` are not disjoint.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype_of_option_equiv", "description": "A type is a `fintype` if its successor (using `option`) is a `fintype`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.card_option", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite.induction_empty_option", "description": " An induction principle for finite types, analogous to `nat.rec`. It effectively says\nthat every `fintype` is either `empty` or `option \u03b1`, up to an `equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype_of_option", "description": "If `option \u03b1` is a `fintype` then so is `\u03b1`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.induction_empty_option", "description": " An induction principle for finite types, analogous to `nat.rec`. It effectively says\nthat every `fintype` is either `empty` or `option \u03b1`, up to an `equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.trunc_rec_empty_option", "description": " A recursor principle for finite types, analogous to `nat.rec`. It effectively says\nthat every `fintype` is either `empty` or `option \u03b1`, up to an `equiv`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "univ_option", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "option.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "graded_monoid.gmul_action", "description": "A graded version of `mul_action`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "graded_monoid.gmul_action.smul", "description": "A graded version of `mul_action`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "graded_monoid.gmul_action.one_smul", "description": "A graded version of `mul_action`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "graded_monoid.gmul_action.mul_smul", "description": "A graded version of `mul_action`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.has_graded_mul.to_has_graded_smul", "description": "Internally graded version of `has_mul.to_has_smul`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "graded_monoid.ghas_smul.to_has_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.has_graded_smul", "description": "A version of `graded_monoid.ghas_smul` for internally graded objects.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.has_graded_smul.smul_mem", "description": "A version of `graded_monoid.ghas_smul` for internally graded objects.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "graded_monoid.ghas_smul", "description": " A graded version of `has_smul`. Scalar multiplication combines grades additively, i.e.\nif `a \u2208 A i` and `m \u2208 M j`, then `a \u2022 b` must be in `M (i + j)`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "graded_monoid.ghas_smul.smul", "description": " A graded version of `has_smul`. Scalar multiplication combines grades additively, i.e.\nif `a \u2208 A i` and `m \u2208 M j`, then `a \u2022 b` must be in `M (i + j)`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.ghas_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "graded_monoid.mk_smul_mk", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "graded_monoid.ghas_mul.to_ghas_smul", "description": "A graded version of `has_mul.to_has_smul`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "graded_monoid.gmul_action.to_ghas_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.is_homogeneous.graded_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set_like.coe_ghas_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "graded_monoid.gmul_action.to_mul_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "graded_monoid.gmonoid.to_gmul_action", "description": "The graded version of `monoid.to_mul_action`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.grothendieck_topology.cover.is_cofiltered", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sites.pullback_pushforward_adjunction", "description": "The pushforward functor is left adjoint to the pullback functor.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sites.pushforward.limits.preserves_finite_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sites.pushforward_map_val_app", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sites.pushforward", "description": " The pushforward functor `Sheaf J A \u2964 Sheaf K A` associated to a functor `G : C \u2964 D` in the\nsame direction as `G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf_to_presheaf.creates_limits", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sites.pushforward_obj_val_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sites.pushforward_obj_val_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.locally_finite_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.uIcc_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.card_Icc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.card_Ioo", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.card_Iic", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.Icc_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.card_Ioc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.card_Ico", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.card_uIcc", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.is_succ_archimedean", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast_int_covby_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.is_pred_archimedean", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.covby_add_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.pred_eq_pred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.sub_one_covby", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.succ_iterate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.pred_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "covby.cast_int", "description": "**Alias** of the reverse direction of `nat.cast_int_covby_iff`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.pos_iff_one_le", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.succ_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.pred_iterate", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.covby_iff_succ_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.succ_eq_succ", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.volume_preserving_equiv_real_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.measurable_equiv_pi", "description": "Measurable equivalence between `\u2102` and `\u211d\u00b2 = fin 2 \u2192 \u211d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.measurable_equiv_real_prod", "description": "Measurable equivalence between `\u2102` and `\u211d \u00d7 \u211d`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.measure_space", "description": "Lebesgue measure on `\u2102`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.volume_preserving_equiv_pi", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_extension", "description": "A type alias for `\u03b1`, intended to extend a partial order on `\u03b1` to a linear order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "to_linear_extension", "description": "The embedding of `\u03b1` into `linear_extension \u03b1` as a relation homomorphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_extension.linear_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "extend_partial_order", "description": "Any partial order can be extended to a linear order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_extension.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "match_failed", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_fail_lift", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_fail", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "monad_fail.fail", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "encodable.Union_decode\u2082_cases", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "encodable.Union_decode\u2082_disjoint_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "encodable.supr_decode\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "encodable.Union_decode\u2082", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.linear_ordered_cancel_comm_monoid", "description": " Pullback a `linear_ordered_cancel_comm_monoid` under an injective map.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.ordered_cancel_add_comm_monoid", "description": "Pullback an `ordered_cancel_add_comm_monoid` under an injective map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.linear_ordered_cancel_add_comm_monoid", "description": "Pullback a `linear_ordered_cancel_add_comm_monoid` under an injective map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.ordered_cancel_comm_monoid", "description": " Pullback an `ordered_cancel_comm_monoid` under an injective map.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.restrict_scalars_linear_map", "description": "Restriction of scalars for linear maps between modules over a `k`-algebra is itself `k`-linear.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.restrict_scalars_linear_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.presheaf_to_Sheaf_additive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf.limits.has_finite_products", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.abelian", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Sheaf_is_abelian", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.product_of_mem_opens_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.eq_induced_by_maps_to_sierpinski", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.product_of_mem_opens_inducing", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.product_of_mem_opens", "description": "The continuous map from `X` to the product of copies of the Sierpinski space, (one copy for each\nopen subset `u` of `X`). The `u` coordinate of `product_of_mem_opens x` is given by `x \u2208 u`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_space.product_of_mem_opens_embedding", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.simp_cmd", "description": "The basic usage is `#simp e`, where `e` is an expression,\nwhich will print the simplified form of `e`.\n\nYou can specify additional simp lemmas as usual for example using\n`#simp [f, g] : e`, or `#simp with attr : e`.\n(The colon is optional, but helpful for the parser.)\n\n`#simp` understands local variables, so you can use them to\nintroduce parameters.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.simp_arg_type.replace_subexprs", "description": " `simp_arg_type.replace_subexprs` calls `expr.replace_subexprs` on the underlying `pexpr`, if\nthere is one, and then prepares the result for use by the simplifier.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.command.#simp", "description": "The basic usage is `#simp e`, where `e` is an expression,\nwhich will print the simplified form of `e`.\n\nYou can specify additional simp lemmas as usual for example using\n`#simp [f, g] : e`, or `#simp with attr : e`.\n(The colon is optional, but helpful for the parser.)\n\n`#simp` understands local variables, so you can use them to\nintroduce parameters.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.simp_arg_type.to_pexpr", "description": " `simp_arg_type.to_pexpr` retrieves the `pexpr` underlying the given `simp_arg_type`, if there is\none.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.module", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.smul_with_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.no_zero_smul_divisors", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.mul_action_with_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "counterexample.girard", "description": " **Girard's paradox**: there are no universes `u` such that `Type u : Type u`.\nSince we can't actually change the type of Lean's `\u03a0` operator, we assume the existence of\n`pi`, `lam`, `app` and the `beta` rule equivalent to the `\u03a0` and `app` constructors of type theory.", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "category_theory.skeleton.monoidal_category", "description": "The skeleton of a monoidal category has a monoidal structure itself, induced by the equivalence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.comm_monoid_of_skeletal_braided", "description": "If `C` is braided and skeletal, it is a commutative monoid.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.skeleton.monoid", "description": "The skeleton of a monoidal category can be viewed as a monoid, where the multiplication is given by\nthe tensor product, and satisfies the monoid axioms since it is a skeleton.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.monoid_of_skeletal_monoidal", "description": " If `C` is monoidal and skeletal, it is a monoid.\nSee note [reducible non-instances].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "setoid.trans", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "setoid.symm", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "setoid", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "setoid.r", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "setoid.iseqv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "setoid_has_equiv", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "setoid.refl", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "is_simp_eq", "description": " Checks whether two expressions are equal for the simplifier. That is,\nthey are reducibly-definitional equal, and they have the same head symbol.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "library_note.simp-normal form", "description": "This note gives you some tips to debug any errors that the simp-normal form linter raises.\n\nThe reason that a lemma was considered faulty is because its left-hand side is not in simp-normal\nform.\nThese lemmas are hence never used by the simplifier.\n\nThis linter gives you a list of other simp lemmas: look at them!\n\nHere are some tips depending on the error raised by the linter:\n\n  1. 'the left-hand side reduces to XYZ':\n     you should probably use XYZ as the left-hand side.\n\n  2. 'simp can prove this':\n     This typically means that lemma is a duplicate, or is shadowed by another lemma:\n\n     2a. Always put more general lemmas after specific ones:\n      ```lean\n      @[simp] lemma zero_add_zero : 0 + 0 = 0 := rfl\n      @[simp] lemma add_zero : x + 0 = x := rfl\n      ```\n\n      And not the other way around!  The simplifier always picks the last matching lemma.\n\n     2b. You can also use `@[priority]` instead of moving simp-lemmas around in the file.\n\n      Tip: the default priority is 1000.\n      Use `@[priority 1100]` instead of moving a lemma down,\n      and `@[priority 900]` instead of moving a lemma up.\n\n     2c. Conditional simp lemmas are tried last. If they are shadowed\n         just remove the `simp` attribute.\n\n     2d. If two lemmas are duplicates, the linter will complain about the first one.\n         Try to fix the second one instead!\n         (You can find it among the other simp lemmas the linter prints out!)\n\n  3. 'try_for tactic failed, timeout':\n     This typically means that there is a loop of simp lemmas.\n     Try to apply squeeze_simp to the right-hand side (removing this lemma from the simp set) to see\n     what lemmas might be causing the loop.\n\n     Another trick is to `set_option trace.simplify.rewrite true` and\n     then apply `try_for 10000 { simp }` to the right-hand side.  You will\n     see a periodic sequence of lemma applications in the trace message.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.simp_comm", "description": "A linter for commutativity lemmas that are marked simp.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.simp_var_head", "description": "A linter for simp lemmas whose lhs has a variable as head symbol,\nand which hence never fire.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linter.simp_nf", "description": "A linter for simp lemmas whose lhs is not in simp-normal form, and which hence never fire.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simp_nf_linter", "description": "Reports declarations that are simp lemmas whose left-hand side is not in simp-normal form.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_algebra.basis_free_monoid", "description": " The `free_monoid X` basis on the `free_algebra R X`,\nmapping `[x\u2081, x\u2082, ..., x\u2099]` to the \"monomial\" `1 \u2022 x\u2081 * x\u2082 * \u22ef * x\u2099`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_algebra.basis_free_monoid_repr_symm_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_algebra.rank_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "free_algebra.basis_free_monoid_repr_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.exists_is_internal_prime_power_torsion", "description": "A finitely generated torsion module over a Dedekind domain is an internal direct sum of its\n`p i ^ e i`-torsion submodules for some prime ideals `p i` and numbers `e i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_internal_prime_power_torsion", "description": "A finitely generated torsion module over a Dedekind domain is an internal direct sum of its\n`p i ^ e i`-torsion submodules where `p i` are factors of `(\u22a4 : submodule R M).annihilator` and\n`e i` are their multiplicities.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "submodule.is_internal_prime_power_torsion_of_is_torsion_by_ideal", "description": "Over a Dedekind domain, a `I`-torsion module is the internal direct sum of its `p i ^ e i`-\ntorsion submodules, where `I = \u220f i, p i ^ e i` is its unique decomposition in prime ideals.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.reflexivity", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.target_lhs_rhs", "description": "If the main target has the form `r lhs rhs`, then return `(r,lhs,rhs)`.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.symmetry", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.relation_lhs_rhs", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.subst_vars", "description": "Try to apply subst exhaustively", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.transitivity", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "well_order_extension.well_founded_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_order_extension.linear_order", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.well_order_extension", "description": " An arbitrary well order on `\u03b1` that extends `r`.\n\nThe construction maps `r` into two well-orders: the first map is `well_founded.rank`, which is not\nnecessarily injective but respects the order `r`; the other map is the identity (with an arbitrarily\nchosen well-order on `\u03b1`), which is injective but doesn't respect `r`.\n\nBy taking the lexicographic product of the two, we get both properties, so we can pull it back and\nget an well-order that extend our original order `r`. Another way to view this is that we choose an\narbitrary well-order to serve as a tiebreak between two elements of same rank.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "to_well_order_extension_strict_mono", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.exists_well_order_ge", "description": "Any well-founded relation can be extended to a well-ordering on that type.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_order_extension", "description": "A type alias for `\u03b1`, intended to extend a well-founded order on `\u03b1` to a well-order.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "to_well_order_extension", "description": "\"Identity\" equivalence between a well-founded order and its well-order extension.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_order_extension.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "well_founded.well_order_extension.is_well_founded_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unique_homset_of_initial_iso_terminal", "description": "If a cartesian closed category has an initial object which is isomorphic to the terminal object,\nthen each homset has exactly one element.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.equiv_punit", "description": "A cartesian closed category with a zero object is equivalent to the category with one object and\none morphism.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.unique_homset_of_zero", "description": "If a cartesian closed category has a zero object, each homset has exactly one element.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_commute.map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_semiconj_by.map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semiconj_by.map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "commute.map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.sqrt_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.exists_mul_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.sqrt", "description": " `sqrt z` is the square root of an integer `z`. If `z` is positive, it returns the largest\ninteger `r` such that `r * r \u2264 n`. If it is negative, it returns `0`. For example, `sqrt (-1) = 0`,\n`sqrt 1 = 1`, `sqrt 2 = 1`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.sqrt_nonneg", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_hom.is_central_scalar", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_hom.coe_smul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_hom.smul_comm_class", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_hom.is_scalar_tower", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_hom.smul_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_hom.distrib_mul_action", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_hom.module", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orientation.measure_eq_volume", "description": " In an oriented inner product space, the measure coming from the canonical volume form\nassociated to an orientation coincides with the volume.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orthonormal_basis.volume_parallelepiped", "description": " The volume measure in a finite-dimensional inner product space gives measure `1` to the\nparallelepiped spanned by any orthonormal basis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "orientation.measure_orthonormal_basis", "description": " The volume form coming from an orientation in an inner product space gives measure `1` to the\nparallelepiped associated to any orthonormal basis. This is a rephrasing of\n`abs_volume_form_apply_of_orthonormal` in terms of measures.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "embedding_like", "description": " The class `embedding_like F \u03b1 \u03b2` expresses that terms of type `F` have an\ninjective coercion to injective functions `\u03b1 \u21aa \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "embedding_like.coe", "description": " The class `embedding_like F \u03b1 \u03b2` expresses that terms of type `F` have an\ninjective coercion to injective functions `\u03b1 \u21aa \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "embedding_like.coe_injective'", "description": " The class `embedding_like F \u03b1 \u03b2` expresses that terms of type `F` have an\ninjective coercion to injective functions `\u03b1 \u21aa \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "embedding_like.injective'", "description": " The class `embedding_like F \u03b1 \u03b2` expresses that terms of type `F` have an\ninjective coercion to injective functions `\u03b1 \u21aa \u03b2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "embedding_like.to_fun_like", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "embedding_like.comp_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "embedding_like.injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "embedding_like.apply_eq_iff_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_differentiable_within_at_of_local_left_inverse_has_deriv_within_at_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "not_differentiable_at_of_local_left_inverse_has_deriv_at_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.tendsto_punctured_nhds", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_deriv_at.of_local_left_inverse", "description": " If `f (g y) = y` for `y` in some neighborhood of `a`, `g` is continuous at `a`, and `f` has an\ninvertible derivative `f'` at `g a` in the strict sense, then `g` has the derivative `f'\u207b\u00b9` at `a`\nin the strict sense.\n\nThis is one of the easy parts of the inverse function theorem: it assumes that we already have an\ninverse function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_homeomorph.has_strict_deriv_at_symm", "description": " If `f` is a local homeomorphism defined on a neighbourhood of `f.symm a`, and `f` has a\nnonzero derivative `f'` at `f.symm a` in the strict sense, then `f.symm` has the derivative `f'\u207b\u00b9`\nat `a` in the strict sense.\n\nThis is one of the easy parts of the inverse function theorem: it assumes that we already have\nan inverse function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "local_homeomorph.has_deriv_at_symm", "description": " If `f` is a local homeomorphism defined on a neighbourhood of `f.symm a`, and `f` has an\nnonzero derivative `f'` at `f.symm a`, then `f.symm` has the derivative `f'\u207b\u00b9` at `a`.\n\nThis is one of the easy parts of the inverse function theorem: it assumes that we already have\nan inverse function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_deriv_at.has_strict_fderiv_at_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.eventually_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.has_fderiv_at_equiv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.of_local_left_inverse", "description": " If `f (g y) = y` for `y` in some neighborhood of `a`, `g` is continuous at `a`, and `f` has an\ninvertible derivative `f'` at `g a`, then `g` has the derivative `f'\u207b\u00b9` at `a`.\n\nThis is one of the easy parts of the inverse function theorem: it assumes that we already have\nan inverse function.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.norm_volume_sub_integral_face_upper_sub_lower_smul_le", "description": "Auxiliary lemma for the divergence theorem.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.has_integral_GP_divergence_of_forall_has_deriv_within_at", "description": " Divergence theorem for a Henstock-Kurzweil style integral.\n\nIf `f : \u211d\u207f\u207a\u00b9 \u2192 E\u207f\u207a\u00b9` is differentiable on a closed rectangular box `I` with derivative `f'`, then\nthe divergence `\u2211 i, f' x (pi.single i 1) i` is Henstock-Kurzweil integrable with integral equal to\nthe sum of integrals of `f` over the faces of `I` taken with appropriate signs.\n\nMore precisely, we use a non-standard generalization of the Henstock-Kurzweil integral and\nwe allow `f` to be non-differentiable (but still continuous) at a countable set of points.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "box_integral.has_integral_GP_pderiv", "description": " If `f : \u211d\u207f\u207a\u00b9 \u2192 E` is differentiable on a closed rectangular box `I` with derivative `f'`, then\nthe partial derivative `\u03bb x, f' x (pi.single i 1)` is Henstock-Kurzweil integrable with integral\nequal to the difference of integrals of `f` over the faces `x i = I.upper i` and `x i = I.lower i`.\n\nMore precisely, we use a non-standard generalization of the Henstock-Kurzweil integral and\nwe allow `f` to be non-differentiable (but still continuous) at a countable set of points.\n\nTODO: If `n > 0`, then the condition at `x \u2208 s` can be replaced by a much weaker estimate but this\nrequires either better integrability theorems, or usage of a filter depending on the countable set\n`s` (we need to ensure that none of the faces of a partition contain a point from `s`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.fin_cases_at", "description": "`fin_cases_at with_list e` performs case analysis on `e : \u03b1`, where `\u03b1` is a fintype.\nThe optional list of expressions `with_list` provides descriptions for the cases of `e`,\nfor example, to display nats as `n.succ` instead of `n+1`.\nThese should be defeq to and in the same order as the terms in the enumeration of `\u03b1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.expr_list_to_list_expr", "description": "`expr_list_to_list_expr` converts an `expr` of type `list \u03b1`\nto a list of `expr`s each with type `\u03b1`.\n\nTODO: this should be moved, and possibly duplicates an existing definition.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.fin_cases", "description": "`fin_cases h` performs case analysis on a hypothesis of the form\n`h : A`, where `[fintype A]` is available, or\n`h : a \u2208 A`, where `A : finset X`, `A : multiset X` or `A : list X`.\n\n`fin_cases *` performs case analysis on all suitable hypotheses.\n\nAs an example, in\n```lean\nexample (f : \u2115 \u2192 Prop) (p : fin 3) (h0 : f 0) (h1 : f 1) (h2 : f 2) : f p.val :=\nbegin\n  fin_cases *; simp,\n  all_goals { assumption }\nend\n```\nafter `fin_cases p; simp`, there are three goals, `f 0`, `f 1`, and `f 2`.\n\n`fin_cases h with l` takes a list of descriptions for the cases of `h`.\nThese should be definitionally equal to and in the same order as the\ndefault enumeration of the cases.\n\nFor example,\n```lean\nexample (x y : \u2115) (h : x \u2208 [1, 2]) : x = y :=\nbegin\n  fin_cases h with [1, 1+1],\nend\n```\nproduces two cases: `1 = y` and `1 + 1 = y`.\n\nWhen using `fin_cases a` on data `a` defined with `let`,\nthe tactic will not be able to clear the variable `a`,\nand will instead produce hypotheses `this : a = ...`.\nThese hypotheses can be given a name using `fin_cases a using ha`.\n\nFor example,\n```lean\nexample (f : \u2115 \u2192 fin 3) : true :=\nbegin\n  let a := f 3,\n  fin_cases a using ha,\nend\n```\nproduces three goals with hypotheses\n`ha : a = 0`, `ha : a = 1`, and `ha : a = 2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.guard_mem_fin", "description": "Checks that the expression looks like `x \u2208 A` for `A : finset \u03b1`, `multiset \u03b1` or `A : list \u03b1`,\n   and returns the type \u03b1.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.fin_cases", "description": "`fin_cases h` performs case analysis on a hypothesis of the form\n`h : A`, where `[fintype A]` is available, or\n`h : a \u2208 A`, where `A : finset X`, `A : multiset X` or `A : list X`.\n\n`fin_cases *` performs case analysis on all suitable hypotheses.\n\nAs an example, in\n```lean\nexample (f : \u2115 \u2192 Prop) (p : fin 3) (h0 : f 0) (h1 : f 1) (h2 : f 2) : f p.val :=\nbegin\n  fin_cases *; simp,\n  all_goals { assumption }\nend\n```\nafter `fin_cases p; simp`, there are three goals, `f 0`, `f 1`, and `f 2`.\n\n`fin_cases h with l` takes a list of descriptions for the cases of `h`.\nThese should be definitionally equal to and in the same order as the\ndefault enumeration of the cases.\n\nFor example,\n```lean\nexample (x y : \u2115) (h : x \u2208 [1, 2]) : x = y :=\nbegin\n  fin_cases h with [1, 1+1],\nend\n```\nproduces two cases: `1 = y` and `1 + 1 = y`.\n\nWhen using `fin_cases a` on data `a` defined with `let`,\nthe tactic will not be able to clear the variable `a`,\nand will instead produce hypotheses `this : a = ...`.\nThese hypotheses can be given a name using `fin_cases a using ha`.\n\nFor example,\n```lean\nexample (f : \u2115 \u2192 fin 3) : true :=\nbegin\n  let a := f 3,\n  fin_cases a using ha,\nend\n```\nproduces three goals with hypotheses\n`ha : a = 0`, `ha : a = 1`, and `ha : a = 2`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_equiv_End_forget\u2082", "description": "An ingredient of Tannaka duality for rings:\nA ring `R` is equivalent to\nthe endomorphisms of the additive forgetful functor `Module R \u2964 AddCommGroup`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mdifferentiable.apply_eq_of_compact_space", "description": "A holomorphic function on a compact connected complex manifold is constant.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mdifferentiable.exists_eq_const_of_compact_space", "description": " A holomorphic function on a compact connected complex manifold is the constant function `f \u2261 v`,\nfor some value `v`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mdifferentiable.is_locally_constant", "description": "A holomorphic function on a compact complex manifold is locally constant.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.ring_of_integers.is_unit_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_unit_iff_norm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.obj.is_left_adjoint", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.limits.has_finite_products", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sort.cartesian_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.sort.limits.has_finite_products", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.cartesian_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.lists_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.mem_lists_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiset.lists", "description": "The `finset` of `l : list \u03b1` that, given `m : multiset \u03b1`, have the property `\u27e6l\u27e7 = m`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype_nodup_list", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_at.frequently_zero_iff_eventually_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fpower_series_at.locally_zero_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_sum.has_sum_at_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fpower_series_at.eq_pow_order_mul_iterate_dslope", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_on.eq_on_of_preconnected_of_frequently_eq", "description": " The *identity principle* for analytic functions, global version: if two functions are\nanalytic on a connected set `U` and coincide at points which accumulate to a point `z\u2080 \u2208 U`, then\nthey coincide globally in `U`.\nFor higher-dimensional versions requiring that the functions coincide in a neighborhood of `z\u2080`,\nsee `eq_on_of_preconnected_of_eventually_eq`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_at.eventually_eq_zero_or_eventually_ne_zero", "description": " The *principle of isolated zeros* for an analytic function, local version: if a function is\nanalytic at `z\u2080`, then either it is identically zero in a neighborhood of `z\u2080`, or it does not\nvanish in a punctured neighborhood of `z\u2080`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_sum.exists_has_sum_smul_of_apply_eq_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fpower_series_at.locally_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_on.eq_on_zero_of_preconnected_of_frequently_eq_zero", "description": " The *principle of isolated zeros* for an analytic function, global version: if a function is\nanalytic on a connected set `U` and vanishes in arbitrary neighborhoods of a point `z\u2080 \u2208 U`, then\nit is identically zero in `U`.\nFor higher-dimensional versions requiring that the function vanishes in a neighborhood of `z\u2080`,\nsee `eq_on_zero_of_preconnected_of_eventually_eq_zero`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fpower_series_at.has_fpower_series_iterate_dslope_fslope", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_at.eventually_eq_or_eventually_ne", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_on.eq_on_zero_of_preconnected_of_mem_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fpower_series_at.has_fpower_series_dslope_fslope", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_fpower_series_at.iterate_dslope_fslope_ne_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_at.frequently_eq_iff_eventually_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_on.eq_on_of_preconnected_of_mem_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "analytic_on.eq_of_frequently_eq", "description": " The *identity principle* for analytic functions, global version: if two functions on a normed\nfield `\ud835\udd5c` are analytic everywhere and coincide at points which accumulate to a point `z\u2080`, then\nthey coincide globally.\nFor higher-dimensional versions requiring that the functions coincide in a neighborhood of `z\u2080`,\nsee `eq_of_eventually_eq`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.swap_var", "description": "`swap_var [x y, P \u2194 Q]` swaps the names `x` and `y`, `P` and `Q`.\nSuch a swapping can be used as a weak `wlog` if the tactic proofs use the same names.\n\n```lean\nexample (P Q : Prop) (hp : P) (hq : Q) : P \u2227 Q :=\nbegin\n  split,\n  work_on_goal 1 { swap_var [P Q] },\n  all_goals { exact \u2039P\u203a }\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.swap_var", "description": "`swap_var [x y, P \u2194 Q]` swaps the names `x` and `y`, `P` and `Q`.\nSuch a swapping can be used as a weak `wlog` if the tactic proofs use the same names.\n\n```lean\nexample (P Q : Prop) (hp : P) (hq : Q) : P \u2227 Q :=\nbegin\n  split,\n  work_on_goal 1 { swap_var [P Q] },\n  all_goals { exact \u2039P\u203a }\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.ad_ker_eq_bot_of_semisimple", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.subsingleton_of_semisimple_lie_abelian", "description": "A semisimple Abelian Lie algebra is trivial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.is_semisimple_iff_no_solvable_ideals", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.abelian_radical_of_semisimple", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.is_semisimple_iff_no_abelian_ideals", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.is_irreducible", "description": "A Lie module is irreducible if it is zero or its only non-trivial Lie submodule is itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_module.is_irreducible.irreducible", "description": "A Lie module is irreducible if it is zero or its only non-trivial Lie submodule is itself.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.is_semisimple_of_is_simple", "description": "A simple Lie algebra is semisimple.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.abelian_radical_iff_solvable_is_abelian", "description": " The two properties shown to be equivalent here are possible definitions for a Lie algebra\nto be reductive.\n\nNote that there is absolutely [no agreement](https://mathoverflow.net/questions/284713/) on what\nthe label 'reductive' should mean when the coefficients are not a field of characteristic zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.is_semisimple", "description": " A semisimple Lie algebra is one with trivial radical.\n\nNote that the label 'semisimple' is apparently not universally agreed\n[upon](https://mathoverflow.net/questions/149391/on-radicals-of-a-lie-algebra#comment383669_149391)\nfor general coefficients. We are following [Seligman, page 15](seligman1967) and using the label\nfor the weakest of the various properties which are all equivalent over a field of characteristic\nzero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.is_semisimple.semisimple", "description": " A semisimple Lie algebra is one with trivial radical.\n\nNote that the label 'semisimple' is apparently not universally agreed\n[upon](https://mathoverflow.net/questions/149391/on-radicals-of-a-lie-algebra#comment383669_149391)\nfor general coefficients. We are following [Seligman, page 15](seligman1967) and using the label\nfor the weakest of the various properties which are all equivalent over a field of characteristic\nzero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.is_simple", "description": " A Lie algebra is simple if it is irreducible as a Lie module over itself via the adjoint\naction, and it is non-Abelian.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.is_simple.to_is_irreducible", "description": " A Lie algebra is simple if it is irreducible as a Lie module over itself via the adjoint\naction, and it is non-Abelian.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.is_simple.non_abelian", "description": " A Lie algebra is simple if it is irreducible as a Lie module over itself via the adjoint\naction, and it is non-Abelian.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lie_algebra.center_eq_bot_of_semisimple", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.unit_equiv_units_integer_symm_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.integer_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.unit_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.unit_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.integer_carrier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.coe_unit_equiv_units_integer_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.integer", "description": "The `R`-subalgebra of `S`-integers of `K`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.coe_inv_unit_equiv_units_integer_apply_coe", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.unit", "description": "The subgroup of `S`-units of `K\u02e3`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.unit_equiv_units_integer", "description": "The group of `S`-units is the group of units of the ring of `S`-integers.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.unit_valuation_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "set.integer_valuation_le_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "id_bind", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "id.monad", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "id.monad_run", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "inner_product_geometry.is_conformal_map.preserves_angle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "inner_product_geometry.conformal_at.preserves_angle", "description": "If a real differentiable map `f` is conformal at a point `x`,\n   then it preserves the angles at that point.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "szemeredi_regularity", "description": " Effective **Szemer\u00e9di Regularity Lemma**: For any sufficiently large graph, there is an\n`\u03b5`-uniform equipartition of bounded size (where the bound does not depend on the graph).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.frequently_at_top_modeq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.exists_prime_gt_modeq_one", "description": " For any positive `k : \u2115` there exists an arbitrarily large prime `p` such that\n`p \u2261 1 [MOD k]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.infinite_set_of_prime_modeq_one", "description": "For any positive `k : \u2115` there are infinitely many primes `p` such that `p \u2261 1 [MOD k]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_div_of_monotone_of_tendsto_div_floor_pow", "description": " If a monotone sequence `u` is such that `u \u230ac^n\u230b\u208a / \u230ac^n\u230b\u208a` converges to a limit `l` for all\n`c > 1`, then `u n / n` tends to `l`. It is even enough to have the assumption for a sequence of\n`c`s converging to `1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mul_pow_le_nat_floor_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tendsto_div_of_monotone_of_exists_subseq_tendsto_div", "description": " If a monotone sequence `u` is such that `u n / n` tends to a limit `l` along subsequences with\nexponential growth rate arbitrarily close to `1`, then `u n / n` tends to `l`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum_div_pow_sq_le_div_sq", "description": " The sum of `1/(c^i)^2` above a threshold `j` is comparable to `1/j^2`, up to a multiplicative\nconstant.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "sum_div_nat_floor_pow_sq_le_div_sq", "description": " The sum of `1/\u230ac^i\u230b\u208a^2` above a threshold `j` is comparable to `1/j^2`, up to a multiplicative\nconstant.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.prod.inverse_associator_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.prod.associator", "description": "The associator functor `(C \u00d7 D) \u00d7 E \u2964 C \u00d7 (D \u00d7 E)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.prod.inverse_associator", "description": "The inverse associator functor `C \u00d7 (D \u00d7 E) \u2964 (C \u00d7 D) \u00d7 E `.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.prod.inverse_associator_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.prod.inverse_associator_is_equivalence", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.prod.associativity", "description": "The equivalence of categories expressing associativity of products of categories.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.prod.associator_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.prod.associator_is_equivalence", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.prod.associator_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.ordered_ring", "description": "Pullback an `ordered_ring` under an injective map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.linear_ordered_comm_ring", "description": "Pullback a `linear_ordered_comm_ring` under an injective map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.strict_ordered_ring", "description": "Pullback a `strict_ordered_ring` under an injective map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.linear_ordered_comm_semiring", "description": "Pullback a `linear_ordered_semiring` under an injective map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.strict_ordered_comm_semiring", "description": "Pullback a `strict_ordered_comm_semiring` under an injective map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.strict_ordered_semiring", "description": "Pullback a `strict_ordered_semiring` under an injective map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.linear_ordered_semiring", "description": "Pullback a `linear_ordered_semiring` under an injective map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.linear_ordered_ring", "description": "Pullback a `linear_ordered_ring` under an injective map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.ordered_comm_semiring", "description": "Pullback an `ordered_comm_semiring` under an injective map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.ordered_comm_ring", "description": "Pullback an `ordered_comm_ring` under an injective map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.strict_ordered_comm_ring", "description": "Pullback a `strict_ordered_comm_ring` under an injective map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function.injective.ordered_semiring", "description": "Pullback an `ordered_semiring` under an injective map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.injective_as_module_of_injective_as_Ab", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.injective_of_injective_as_module", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.injective_of_divisible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.finite_type_respects_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.finite_type_stable_under_composition", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.finite_type_holds_for_localization_away", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.finite_type_is_local", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.finite_type_of_localization_span_target", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.is_iso_of_is_iso_of_is_iso_of_is_iso_of_is_iso", "description": " The five lemma. For names of objects and morphisms, refer to the following diagram:\n\n```lean\nA ---f--> B ---g--> C ---h--> D ---i--> E\n|         |         |         |         |\n\u03b1         \u03b2         \u03b3         \u03b4         \u03b5\n|         |         |         |         |\nv         v         v         v         v\nA' --f'-> B' --g'-> C' --h'-> D' --i'-> E'\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.mono_of_epi_of_mono_of_mono", "description": " The four lemma, mono version. For names of objects and morphisms, refer to the following\n    diagram:\n\n```lean\nA ---f--> B ---g--> C ---h--> D\n|         |         |         |\n\u03b1         \u03b2         \u03b3         \u03b4\n|         |         |         |\nv         v         v         v\nA' --f'-> B' --g'-> C' --h'-> D'\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.abelian.epi_of_epi_of_epi_of_mono", "description": " The four lemma, epi version. For names of objects and morphisms, refer to the following\n    diagram:\n\n```lean\nA ---f--> B ---g--> C ---h--> D\n|         |         |         |\n\u03b1         \u03b2         \u03b3         \u03b4\n|         |         |         |\nv         v         v         v\nA' --f'-> B' --g'-> C' --h'-> D'\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "assert_not_exists.linter", "description": "A linter for checking that the declarations marked `assert_not_exists` eventually exist.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "assert_not_exists", "description": "`assert_not_exists n` is a user command that asserts that a declaration named `n` *does not exist*\nin the current import scope.\n\nBe careful to use names (e.g. `rat`) rather than notations (e.g. `\u211a`).\n\nIt may be used (sparingly!) in mathlib to enforce plans that certain files\nare independent of each other.\n\nIf you encounter an error on an `assert_not_exists` command while developing mathlib,\nit is probably because you have introduced new import dependencies to a file.\n\nIn this case, you should refactor your work\n(for example by creating new files rather than adding imports to existing files).\nYou should *not* delete the `assert_not_exists` statement without careful discussion ahead of time.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "assert_exists", "description": "`assert_exists n` is a user command that asserts that a declaration named `n` exists\nin the current import scope.\n\nBe careful to use names (e.g. `rat`) rather than notations (e.g. `\u211a`).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "assert_no_instance.linter", "description": "A linter for checking that the declarations marked `assert_no_instance` eventually exist.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "assert_no_instance", "description": "`assert_no_instance e` is a user command that asserts that an instance `e` *is not available*\nin the current import scope.\n\nIt may be used (sparingly!) in mathlib to enforce plans that certain files\nare independent of each other.\n\nIf you encounter an error on an `assert_no_instance` command while developing mathlib,\nit is probably because you have introduced new import dependencies to a file.\n\nIn this case, you should refactor your work\n(for example by creating new files rather than adding imports to existing files).\nYou should *not* delete the `assert_no_instance` statement without careful discussion ahead of time.\n\nExample usage:\n```lean\nassert_no_instance linear_ordered_field \u211a\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "assert_instance", "description": "`assert_instance e` is a user command that asserts that an instance `e` is available\nin the current import scope.\n\nExample usage:\n```lean\nassert_instance semiring \u2115\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype.exists_of_subtype", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "subtype.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "subtype.eta", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "subtype.eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "subtype.tag_irrelevant", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "subtype.ne_of_val_ne", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "category_theory.Rel.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.rel", "description": "The category of types with binary relations as morphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.Rel", "description": "A type synonym for `Type`, which carries the category instance for which\n   morphisms are binary relations.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "riesz_lemma", "description": " Riesz's lemma, which usually states that it is possible to find a\nvector with norm 1 whose distance to a closed proper subspace is\narbitrarily close to 1. The statement here is in terms of multiples of\nnorms, since in general the existence of an element of norm exactly 1\nis not guaranteed. For a variant giving an element with norm in `[1, R]`, see\n`riesz_lemma_of_norm_lt`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "riesz_lemma_of_norm_lt", "description": "A version of Riesz lemma: given a strict closed subspace `F`, one may find an element of norm `\u2264 R`\nwhich is at distance  at least `1` of every element of `F`. Here, `R` is any given constant\nstrictly larger than the norm of an element of norm `> 1`. For a version without an `R`, see\n`riesz_lemma`.\n\nSince we are considering a general nontrivially normed field, there may be a gap in possible norms\n(for instance no element of norm in `(1,2)`). Hence, we can not allow `R` arbitrarily close to `1`,\nand require `R > \u2016c\u2016` for some `c : \ud835\udd5c` with norm `> 1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "metric.closed_ball_inf_dist_compl_subset_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.single_obj.preadditive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo2019_q1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "locally_constant.to_continuous_map_linear_map", "description": "The inclusion of locally-constant functions into continuous functions as a linear map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_constant.to_continuous_map_add_monoid_hom", "description": "The inclusion of locally-constant functions into continuous functions as an\nadditive monoid hom.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_constant.to_continuous_map_monoid_hom", "description": " The inclusion of locally-constant functions into continuous functions as a multiplicative\nmonoid hom.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_constant.to_continuous_map_alg_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_constant.to_continuous_map_linear_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_constant.to_continuous_map_alg_hom", "description": "The inclusion of locally-constant functions into continuous functions as an algebra map.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_constant.to_continuous_map_monoid_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "locally_constant.to_continuous_map_add_monoid_hom_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.Module_enough_projectives", "description": "The category of modules has enough projectives, since every module is a quotient of a free\n   module.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_projective.iff_projective", "description": "The categorical notion of projective object agrees with the explicit module-theoretic notion.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.projective_of_free", "description": "Modules that have a basis are projective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.is_closed_iff_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.enum_ord_is_normal_iff_is_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.is_open_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.topological_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.mem_closed_iff_bsup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.is_limit_of_mem_frontier", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.mem_closed_iff_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.is_normal_iff_strict_mono_and_continuous", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.order_topology", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.is_open_singleton_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.mem_closure_iff_sup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.mem_closure_iff_bsup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ordinal.is_closed_iff_bsup", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.denumerable", "description": "**Denumerability of the Rational Numbers**", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.infinite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cardinal.mk_rat", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function_field.class_number", "description": "The class number in a function field is the (finite) cardinality of the class group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function_field.class_number_eq_one_iff", "description": "The class number of a function field is `1` iff the ring of integers is a PID.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "function_field.ring_of_integers.class_group.fintype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_le_tsum_of_dist_le_of_tendsto", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cauchy_seq_of_dist_le_of_summable", "description": " If the distance between consecutive points of a sequence is estimated by a summable series,\nthen the original sequence is a Cauchy sequence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_le_tsum_of_dist_le_of_tendsto\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cauchy_seq_of_edist_le_of_summable", "description": " If the extended distance between consecutive points of a sequence is estimated\nby a summable series of `nnreal`s, then the original sequence is a Cauchy sequence.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_le_tsum_dist_of_tendsto", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "dist_le_tsum_dist_of_tendsto\u2080", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cauchy_seq_of_summable_dist", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.exists_C_coeff_not_mem", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.induction_on'", "description": "To prove something about polynomials,\nit suffices to show the condition is closed under taking sums,\nand it holds for monomials.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.induction_on", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.mem_span_C_coeff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.span_le_of_C_coeff_mem", "description": "  If the coefficients of a polynomial belong to an ideal, then that ideal contains\nthe ideal spanned by the coefficients of the polynomial.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.get_string_option", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.set_nat_option", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.set_string_option", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.get_nat_option", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.set_bool_option", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.get_bool_option", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.quotient_mk_comp_C_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.rank_mv_polynomial", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "subtype.decidable_eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "nat.pow_le_choose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.choose_le_pow", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.Instance cache tactics", "description": "For performance reasons, Lean does not automatically update its database\nof class instances during a proof. The group of tactics described below\nhelps to force such updates. For a simple (but very artificial) example,\nconsider the function `default` from the core library. It has type\n`\u03a0 (\u03b1 : Sort u) [inhabited \u03b1], \u03b1`, so one can use `default` only if Lean\ncan find a registered instance of `inhabited \u03b1`. Because the database of\nsuch instance is not automatically updated during a proof, the following\nattempt won't work (Lean will not pick up the instance from the local\ncontext):\n```lean\ndef my_id (\u03b1 : Type) : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b1 :=\nbegin\n  intro x,\n  have : inhabited \u03b1 := \u27e8x\u27e9,\n  exact default, -- Won't work!\nend\n```\nHowever, it will work, producing the identity function, if one replaces `have`\nby its variant `haveI` described below.\n\n* `resetI`: Reset the instance cache. This allows any instances\n  currently in the context to be used in typeclass inference.\n\n* `unfreezingI { tac }`: Unfreeze local instances while executing the tactic `tac`.\n\n* `introI`/`introsI`: `intro`/`intros` followed by `resetI`. Like\n  `intro`/`intros`, but uses the introduced variable in typeclass inference.\n\n* `casesI`: like `cases`, but can also be used with instance arguments.\n\n* `substI`: like `subst`, but can also substitute in type-class arguments\n\n* `haveI`/`letI`/`rsufficesI`: `have`/`let`/`rsuffices` followed by `resetI`. Used\n  to add typeclasses to the context so that they can be used in typeclass inference.\n\n* `exactI`: `resetI` followed by `exact`. Like `exact`, but uses all\n  variables in the context for typeclass inference.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "upper_half_plane.charted_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "upper_half_plane.mdifferentiable_coe", "description": "The inclusion map `\u210d \u2192 \u2102` is a differentiable map of manifolds.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "upper_half_plane.smooth_manifold_with_corners", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "upper_half_plane.smooth_coe", "description": "The inclusion map `\u210d \u2192 \u2102` is a smooth map of manifolds.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "with_zero.units_with_zero_equiv", "description": "Any group is isomorphic to the units of itself adjoined with `0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.flat.self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.flat", "description": " An `R`-module `M` is flat if for all finitely generated ideals `I` of `R`,\nthe canonical map `I \u2297 M \u2192\u2097 M` is injective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.flat.out", "description": " An `R`-module `M` is flat if for all finitely generated ideals `I` of `R`,\nthe canonical map `I \u2297 M \u2192\u2097 M` is injective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "counterexample.with_zero_unit_char_p_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.with_zero_unit_not_char_zero", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "counterexample.add_one_eq_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-counterexamples"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_inhabited_instance", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "list.qsort_cons", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "list.qsort", "description": "This is based on the minimalist Haskell \"quicksort\".\n\n  Remark: this is *not* really quicksort since it doesn't partition the elements in-place", "p": "core"}, {"name": "list.qsort.F", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "list.qsort_nil", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "add_comm_group.equiv_direct_sum_zmod_of_fintype", "description": " **Structure theorem of finite abelian groups** : Any finite abelian group is a direct sum of\nsome `zmod (p i ^ e i)` for some prime powers `p i ^ e i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_group.finite_of_fg_torsion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_comm_group.equiv_free_prod_direct_sum_zmod", "description": " **Structure theorem of finitely generated abelian groups** : Any finitely generated abelian\ngroup is the product of a power of `\u2124` and a direct sum of some `zmod (p i ^ e i)` for some\nprime powers `p i ^ e i`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.finite_of_fg_torsion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "comm_group.finite_of_fg_torsion", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.exists_eq_const_of_bounded", "description": "**Liouville's theorem**: a complex differentiable bounded function is a constant.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.norm_deriv_le_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.norm_deriv_le_of_forall_mem_sphere_norm_le", "description": " If `f` is complex differentiable on an open disc of radius `R > 0`, is continuous on its\nclosure, and its values on the boundary circle of this disc are bounded from above by `C`, then the\nnorm of its derivative at the center is at most `C / R`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.apply_eq_apply_of_bounded", "description": "**Liouville's theorem**: a complex differentiable bounded function `f : E \u2192 F` is a constant.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "differentiable.exists_const_forall_eq_of_bounded", "description": "**Liouville's theorem**: a complex differentiable bounded function is a constant.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.deriv_eq_smul_circle_integral", "description": " If `f` is complex differentiable on an open disc with center `c` and radius `R > 0` and is\ncontinuous on its closure, then `f' c` can be represented as an integral over the corresponding\ncircle.\n\nTODO: add a version for `w \u2208 metric.ball c R`.\n\nTODO: add a version for higher derivatives.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.liouville_theorem_aux", "description": "An auxiliary lemma for Liouville's theorem `differentiable.apply_eq_apply_of_bounded`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mathlib_linters", "description": "The default linters used in mathlib CI.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.command.linting commands", "description": "User commands to spot common mistakes in the code\n\n* `#lint`: check all declarations in the current file\n* `#lint_mathlib`: check all declarations in mathlib (so excluding core or other projects,\n  and also excluding the current file)\n* `#lint_all`: check all declarations in the environment (the current file and all\n  imported files)\n\nThe following linters are run by default:\n1. `unused_arguments` checks for unused arguments in declarations.\n2. `def_lemma` checks whether a declaration is incorrectly marked as a def/lemma.\n3. `dup_namespce` checks whether a namespace is duplicated in the name of a declaration.\n4. `ge_or_gt` checks whether \u2265/> is used in the declaration.\n5. `instance_priority` checks that instances that always apply have priority below default.\n6. `doc_blame` checks for missing doc strings on definitions and constants.\n7.  `has_nonempty_instance` checks whether every type has an associated `inhabited`, `unique`\n    or `nonempty` instance.\n8.  `impossible_instance` checks for instances that can never fire.\n9.  `incorrect_type_class_argument` checks for arguments in [square brackets] that are not classes.\n10. `dangerous_instance` checks for instances that generate type-class problems with metavariables.\n11. `fails_quickly` tests that type-class inference ends (relatively) quickly when applied to\n    variables.\n12. `has_coe_variable` tests that there are no instances of type `has_coe \u03b1 t` for a variable `\u03b1`.\n13. `inhabited_nonempty` checks for `inhabited` instance arguments that should be changed to\n    `nonempty`.\n14. `simp_nf` checks that the left-hand side of simp lemmas is in simp-normal form.\n15. `simp_var_head` checks that there are no variables as head symbol of left-hand sides of\n    simp lemmas.\n16. `simp_comm` checks that no commutativity lemmas (such as `add_comm`) are marked simp.\n17. `decidable_classical` checks for `decidable` hypotheses that are used in the proof of a\n    proposition but not in the statement, and could be removed using `classical`.\n    Theorems in the `decidable` namespace are exempt.\n18. `has_coe_to_fun` checks that every type that coerces to a function has a direct\n    `has_coe_to_fun` instance.\n19. `check_type` checks that the statement of a declaration is well-typed.\n20. `check_univs` checks that there are no bad `max u v` universe levels.\n21. `syn_taut` checks that declarations are not syntactic tautologies.\n22. `check_reducibility` checks whether non-instances with a class as type are reducible.\n23. `unprintable_interactive` checks that interactive tactics have parser documentation.\n24. `to_additive_doc` checks if additive versions of lemmas have documentation.\n\nThe following linters are not run by default:\n1. `doc_blame_thm`, checks for missing doc strings on lemmas and theorems.\n2. `explicit_vars_of_iff` checks if there are explicit variables used on both sides of an iff.\n\nThe command `#list_linters` prints a list of the names of all available linters.\n\nYou can append a `*` to any command (e.g. `#lint_mathlib*`) to omit the slow tests (4).\n\nYou can append a `-` to any command (e.g. `#lint_mathlib-`) to run a silent lint\nthat suppresses the output if all checks pass.\nA silent lint will fail if any test fails.\n\nYou can append a `+` to any command (e.g. `#lint_mathlib+`) to run a verbose lint\nthat reports the result of each linter, including  the successes.\n\nYou can append a sequence of linter names to any command to run extra tests, in addition to the\ndefault ones. e.g. `#lint doc_blame_thm` will run all default tests and `doc_blame_thm`.\n\nYou can append `only name1 name2 ...` to any command to run a subset of linters, e.g.\n`#lint only unused_arguments`\n\nYou can add custom linters by defining a term of type `linter` in the `linter` namespace.\nA linter defined with the name `linter.my_new_check` can be run with `#lint my_new_check`\nor `lint only my_new_check`.\nIf you add the attribute `@[linter]` to `linter.my_new_check` it will run by default.\n\nAdding the attribute `@[nolint doc_blame unused_arguments]` to a declaration\nomits it from only the specified linter checks.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_mem_name", "description": "makes the substructure axiom name from field name, by postfacing with `_mem`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.derive_field_subtype", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.subtype_instance", "description": " builds instances for algebraic substructures\n\nExample:\n```lean\nvariables {\u03b1 : Type*} [monoid \u03b1] {s : set \u03b1}\n\nclass is_submonoid (s : set \u03b1) : Prop :=\n(one_mem : (1:\u03b1) \u2208 s)\n(mul_mem {a b} : a \u2208 s \u2192 b \u2208 s \u2192 a * b \u2208 s)\n\ninstance subtype.monoid {s : set \u03b1} [is_submonoid s] : monoid s :=\nby subtype_instance\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.subtype_instance", "description": " builds instances for algebraic substructures\n\nExample:\n```lean\nvariables {\u03b1 : Type*} [monoid \u03b1] {s : set \u03b1}\n\nclass is_submonoid (s : set \u03b1) : Prop :=\n(one_mem : (1:\u03b1) \u2208 s)\n(mul_mem {a b} : a \u2208 s \u2192 b \u2208 s \u2192 a * b \u2208 s)\n\ninstance subtype.monoid {s : set \u03b1} [is_submonoid s] : monoid s :=\nby subtype_instance\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.surjective_stable_under_base_change", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.surjective_respects_iso", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.surjective_stable_under_composition", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ring_hom.surjective_of_localization_span", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zsqrtd.to_real", "description": " The image of `zsqrtd` in `\u211d`, using `real.sqrt` which takes the positive root of `d`.\n\nIf the negative root is desired, use `to_real h a.conj`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zsqrtd.to_real_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "zsqrtd.to_real_injective", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.dense_range_cast", "description": "Rational numbers are dense in a linear ordered archimedean field.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation.integers.integral_closure", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation.integers.mem_of_integral", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation.integers.integrally_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.strong_topology.locally_convex_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.locally_convex_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.simp_rw", "description": "`simp_rw` functions as a mix of `simp` and `rw`. Like `rw`, it applies each\nrewrite rule in the given order, but like `simp` it repeatedly applies these\nrules and also under binders like `\u2200 x, ...`, `\u2203 x, ...` and `\u03bb x, ...`.\n\nUsage:\n  - `simp_rw [lemma_1, ..., lemma_n]` will rewrite the goal by applying the\n    lemmas in that order. A lemma preceded by `\u2190` is applied in the reverse direction.\n  - `simp_rw [lemma_1, ..., lemma_n] at h\u2081 ... h\u2099` will rewrite the given hypotheses.\n  - `simp_rw [...] at \u22a2 h\u2081 ... h\u2099` rewrites the goal as well as the given hypotheses.\n  - `simp_rw [...] at *` rewrites in the whole context: all hypotheses and the goal.\n\nLemmas passed to `simp_rw` must be expressions that are valid arguments to `simp`.\n\nFor example, neither `simp` nor `rw` can solve the following, but `simp_rw` can:\n```lean\nexample {\u03b1 \u03b2 : Type} {f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2} {t : set \u03b2} :\n  (\u2200 s, f '' s \u2286 t) = \u2200 s : set \u03b1, \u2200 x \u2208 s, x \u2208 f \u207b\u00b9' t :=\nby simp_rw [set.image_subset_iff, set.subset_def]\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.simp_rw", "description": "`simp_rw` functions as a mix of `simp` and `rw`. Like `rw`, it applies each\nrewrite rule in the given order, but like `simp` it repeatedly applies these\nrules and also under binders like `\u2200 x, ...`, `\u2203 x, ...` and `\u03bb x, ...`.\n\nUsage:\n  - `simp_rw [lemma_1, ..., lemma_n]` will rewrite the goal by applying the\n    lemmas in that order. A lemma preceded by `\u2190` is applied in the reverse direction.\n  - `simp_rw [lemma_1, ..., lemma_n] at h\u2081 ... h\u2099` will rewrite the given hypotheses.\n  - `simp_rw [...] at \u22a2 h\u2081 ... h\u2099` rewrites the goal as well as the given hypotheses.\n  - `simp_rw [...] at *` rewrites in the whole context: all hypotheses and the goal.\n\nLemmas passed to `simp_rw` must be expressions that are valid arguments to `simp`.\n\nFor example, neither `simp` nor `rw` can solve the following, but `simp_rw` can:\n```lean\nexample {\u03b1 \u03b2 : Type} {f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2} {t : set \u03b2} :\n  (\u2200 s, f '' s \u2286 t) = \u2200 s : set \u03b1, \u2200 x \u2208 s, x \u2208 f \u207b\u00b9' t :=\nby simp_rw [set.image_subset_iff, set.subset_def]\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.card_fin_even", "description": " The cardinality of `fin (bit0 n)` is even, `fact` version.\nThis `fact` is needed as an instance by `matrix.special_linear_group.has_neg`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "fintype.is_square.decidable_pred", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_map.det", "description": " The determinant of a continuous linear map, mainly as a convenience device to be able to\nwrite `A.det` instead of `(A : M \u2192\u2097[R] M).det`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_linear_equiv.det_coe_symm", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.uniform_space", "description": "The uniform space structure coming from an absolute value.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.has_basis_uniformity", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.ordered_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "prod.ordered_add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_subgroup.cyclic_of_min", "description": " Given a subgroup `H` of a decidable linearly ordered archimedean abelian group `G`, if there\nexists a minimal element `a` of `H \u2229 G_{>0}` then `H` is generated by `a`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "int.subgroup_cyclic", "description": "Every subgroup of `\u2124` is cyclic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "units.charted_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "units.chart_at_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "units.chart_at_source", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "units.smooth_manifold_with_corners", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.dec_trivial", "description": " `dec_trivial` tries to use decidability to prove a goal\n(i.e., using `exact dec_trivial`).\nThe variant `dec_trivial!` will revert all hypotheses on which the target depends,\nbefore it tries `exact dec_trivial`.\n\nExample:\n```lean\nexample (n : \u2115) (h : n < 2) : n = 0 \u2228 n = 1 :=\nby dec_trivial!\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.dec_trivial", "description": " `dec_trivial` tries to use decidability to prove a goal\n(i.e., using `exact dec_trivial`).\nThe variant `dec_trivial!` will revert all hypotheses on which the target depends,\nbefore it tries `exact dec_trivial`.\n\nExample:\n```lean\nexample (n : \u2115) (h : n < 2) : n = 0 \u2228 n = 1 :=\nby dec_trivial!\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.abs_is_admissible", "description": "`abs : \u2124 \u2192 \u2124` is an admissible absolute value", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.is_admissible.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "absolute_value.exists_partition_int", "description": " We can partition a finite family into `partition_card \u03b5` sets, such that the remainders\nin each set are close together.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.uniform_continuous_ring_hom_eq_id_or_conj", "description": " Let `K` a subfield of `\u2102` and let `\u03c8 : K \u2192+* \u2102` a ring homomorphism. Assume that `\u03c8` is uniform\ncontinuous, then `\u03c8` is either the inclusion map or the composition of the inclusion map with the\ncomplex conjugation.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.subfield_eq_of_closed", "description": "The only closed subfields of `\u2102` are `\u211d` and `\u2102`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "contract_left_assoc_coevaluation", "description": "This lemma corresponds to one of the coherence laws for duals in rigid categories, see\n `category_theory.monoidal.rigid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "contract_left_assoc_coevaluation'", "description": "This lemma corresponds to one of the coherence laws for duals in rigid categories, see\n `category_theory.monoidal.rigid`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coevaluation_apply_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "coevaluation", "description": "The coevaluation map is a linear map from a field `K` to a finite dimensional\n vector space `V`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.reflects_isomorphisms_forget\u2082", "description": "A `forget\u2082 C D` forgetful functor between concrete categories `C` and `D`\nwhere `forget C` reflects isomorphisms, itself reflects isomorphisms.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.forget.reflects_isomorphisms", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_compact_support.deriv", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "support_deriv_subset", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.upto.gt", "description": "Lift the \"greater than\" relation on natural numbers to `nat.upto`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.upto", "description": " The subtype of natural numbers `i` which have the property that\nno `j` less than `i` satisfies `p`. This is an initial segment of the\nnatural numbers, up to and including the first value satisfying `p`.\n\nWe will be particularly interested in the case where there exists a value\nsatisfying `p`, because in this case the `>` relation is well-founded.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.upto.succ", "description": "The successor of `n` is in `nat.upto p` provided that `n` doesn't satisfy `p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.upto.zero", "description": "Zero is always a member of `nat.upto p` because it has no predecessors.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.upto.wf", "description": " The \"greater than\" relation on `upto p` is well founded if (and only if) there exists a value\nsatisfying `p`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.upto.has_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation.extend_to_localization_apply_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "valuation.extend_to_localization", "description": " We can extend a valuation `v` on a ring to a localization at a submonoid of\nthe complement of `v.supp`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "expr.rename_var", "description": "Rename bound variable `old` to `new` in an `expr`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rename_var_at_goal", "description": "Rename bound variable `old` to `new` in goal", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rename_var_at_hyp", "description": "Rename bound variable `old` to `new` in assumption `h`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.rename_var", "description": "`rename_var old new` renames all bound variables named `old` to `new` in the goal.\n`rename_var old new at h` does the same in hypothesis `h`.\nThis is meant for teaching bound variables only. Such a renaming should never be relevant to Lean.\n\n```lean\nexample (P : \u2115 \u2192  \u2115 \u2192 Prop) (h : \u2200 n, \u2203 m, P n m) : \u2200 l, \u2203 m, P l m :=\nbegin\n  rename_var n q at h, -- h is now \u2200 (q : \u2115), \u2203 (m : \u2115), P q m,\n  rename_var m n, -- goal is now \u2200 (l : \u2115), \u2203 (n : \u2115), P k n,\n  exact h -- Lean does not care about those bound variable names\nend\n```", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.rename_var", "description": "`rename_var old new` renames all bound variables named `old` to `new` in the goal.\n`rename_var old new at h` does the same in hypothesis `h`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.hom", "description": " `functor.hom` is the hom-pairing, sending `(X, Y)` to `X \u27f6 Y`, contravariant in `X` and\ncovariant in `Y`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.hom_obj", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.functor.hom_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.free.choose_basis_index.fintype", "description": "If a free module is finite, then any basis is finite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.finite.of_basis", "description": "A free module with a basis indexed by a `fintype` is finite.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.finite.matrix", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "rat.encodable", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "library_note.the algebraic hierarchy", "description": "# The algebraic hierarchy\n\nIn any theorem proving environment,\nthere are difficult decisions surrounding the design of the \"algebraic hierarchy\".\n\nThere is a danger of exponential explosion in the number of gadgets,\nespecially once interactions between algebraic and order/topological/etc structures are considered.\n\nIn mathlib, we try to avoid this by only introducing new algebraic typeclasses either\n1. when there is \"real mathematics\" to be done with them, or\n2. when there is a meaninful gain in simplicity by factoring out a common substructure.\n\n(As examples, at this point we don't have `loop`, or `unital_magma`,\nbut we do have `lie_submodule` and `topological_field`!\nWe also have `group_with_zero`, as an exemplar of point 2.)\n\nGenerally in mathlib we use the extension mechanism (so `comm_ring` extends `ring`)\nrather than mixins (e.g. with separate `ring` and `comm_mul` classes),\nin part because of the potential blow-up in term sizes described at\nhttps://www.ralfj.de/blog/2019/05/15/typeclasses-exponential-blowup.html\nHowever there is tension here, as it results in considerable duplication in the API,\nparticularly in the interaction with order structures.\n\nThis library note is not intended as a design document\njustifying and explaining the history of mathlib's algebraic hierarchy!\nInstead it is intended as a developer's guide, for contributors wanting to extend\n(either new leaves, or new intermediate classes) the algebraic hierarchy as it exists.\n\n(Ideally we would have both a tour guide to the existing hierarchy,\nand an account of the design choices.\nSee https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.09336 for an overview of mathlib as a whole,\nwith some attention to the algebraic hierarchy and\nhttps://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib-overview.html\nfor a summary of what is in mathlib today.)\n\n## Instances\n\nWhen adding a new typeclass `Z` to the algebraic hierarchy\none should attempt to add the following constructions and results,\nwhen applicable:\n\n* Instances transferred elementwise to products, like `prod.monoid`.\n  See `algebra.group.prod` for more examples.\n  ```lean\n  instance prod.Z [Z M] [Z N] : Z (M \u00d7 N) := ...\n  ```\n* Instances transferred elementwise to pi types, like `pi.monoid`.\n  See `algebra.group.pi` for more examples.\n  ```lean\n  instance pi.Z [\u2200 i, Z $ f i] : Z (\u03a0 i : I, f i) := ...\n  ```\n* Instances transferred to `mul_opposite M`, like `mul_opposite.monoid`.\n  See `algebra.opposites` for more examples.\n  ```lean\n  instance mul_opposite.Z [Z M] : Z (mul_opposite M) := ...\n  ```\n* Instances transferred to `ulift M`, like `ulift.monoid`.\n  See `algebra.group.ulift` for more examples.\n  ```lean\n  instance ulift.Z [Z M] : Z (ulift M) := ...\n  ```\n* Definitions for transferring the proof fields of instances along\n  injective or surjective functions that agree on the data fields,\n  like `function.injective.monoid` and `function.surjective.monoid`.\n  We make these definitions `@[reducible]`, see note [reducible non-instances].\n  See `algebra.group.inj_surj` for more examples.\n  ```lean\n  @[reducible]\n  def function.injective.Z [Z M\u2082] (f : M\u2081 \u2192 M\u2082) (hf : injective f)\n    (one : f 1 = 1) (mul : \u2200 x y, f (x * y) = f x * f y) : Z M\u2081 := ...\n\n  @[reducible]\n  def function.surjective.Z [Z M\u2081] (f : M\u2081 \u2192 M\u2082) (hf : surjective f)\n    (one : f 1 = 1) (mul : \u2200 x y, f (x * y) = f x * f y) : Z M\u2082 := ...\n  ```\n* Instances transferred elementwise to `finsupp`s, like `finsupp.semigroup`.\n  See `data.finsupp.pointwise` for more examples.\n  ```lean\n  instance finsupp.Z [Z \u03b2] : Z (\u03b1 \u2192\u2080 \u03b2) := ...\n  ```\n* Instances transferred elementwise to `set`s, like `set.monoid`.\n  See `algebra.pointwise` for more examples.\n  ```lean\n  instance set.Z [Z \u03b1] : Z (set \u03b1) := ...\n  ```\n* Definitions for transferring the entire structure across an equivalence, like `equiv.monoid`.\n  See `data.equiv.transfer_instance` for more examples. See also the `transport` tactic.\n  ```lean\n  def equiv.Z (e : \u03b1 \u2243 \u03b2) [Z \u03b2] : Z \u03b1 := ...\n  /- When there is a new notion of `Z`-equiv: -/\n  def equiv.Z_equiv (e : \u03b1 \u2243 \u03b2) [Z \u03b2] : by { letI := equiv.Z e, exact \u03b1 \u2243Z \u03b2 } := ...\n  ```\n\n## Subobjects\n\nWhen a new typeclass `Z` adds new data fields,\nyou should also create a new `sub_Z` `structure` with a `carrier` field.\n\nThis can be a lot of work; for now try to closely follow the existing examples\n(e.g. `submonoid`, `subring`, `subalgebra`).\nWe would very much like to provide some automation here, but a prerequisite will be making\nall the existing APIs more uniform.\n\nIf `Z` extends `Y`, then `sub_Z` should usually extend `sub_Y`.\n\nWhen `Z` adds only new proof fields to an existing structure `Y`,\nyou should provide instances transferring\n`Z \u03b1` to `Z (sub_Y \u03b1)`, like `submonoid.to_comm_monoid`.\nTypically this is done using the `function.injective.Z` definition mentioned above.\n```lean\ninstance sub_Y.to_Z [Z \u03b1] : Z (sub_Y \u03b1) :=\ncoe_injective.Z coe ...\n```\n\n## Morphisms and equivalences\n\n## Category theory\n\nFor many algebraic structures, particularly ones used in representation theory, algebraic geometry,\netc., we also define \"bundled\" versions, which carry `category` instances.\n\nThese bundled versions are usually named in camel case,\nso for example we have `AddCommGroup` as a bundled `add_comm_group`,\nand `TopCommRing` (which bundles together `comm_ring`, `topological_space`, and `topological_ring`).\n\nThese bundled versions have many appealing features:\n* a uniform notation for morphisms `X \u27f6 Y`\n* a uniform notation (and definition) for isomorphisms `X \u2245 Y`\n* a uniform API for subobjects, via the partial order `subobject X`\n* interoperability with unbundled structures, via coercions to `Type`\n  (so if `G : AddCommGroup`, you can treat `G` as a type,\n  and it automatically has an `add_comm_group` instance)\n  and lifting maps `AddCommGroup.of G`, when `G` is a type with an `add_comm_group` instance.\n\nIf, for example you do the work of proving that a typeclass `Z` has a good notion of tensor product,\nyou are strongly encouraged to provide the corresponding `monoidal_category` instance\non a bundled version.\nThis ensures that the API for tensor products is complete, and enables use of general machinery.\nSimilarly if you prove universal properties, or adjunctions, you are encouraged to state these\nusing categorical language!\n\nOne disadvantage of the bundled approach is that we can only speak of morphisms between\nobjects living in the same type-theoretic universe.\nIn practice this is rarely a problem.\n\n# Making a pull request\n\nWith so many moving parts, how do you actually go about changing the algebraic hierarchy?\n\nWe're still evolving how to handle this, but the current suggestion is:\n\n* If you're adding a new \"leaf\" class, the requirements are lower,\n  and an initial PR can just add whatever is immediately needed.\n* A new \"intermediate\" class, especially low down in the hierarchy,\n  needs to be careful about leaving gaps.\n\nIn a perfect world, there would be a group of simultaneous PRs that basically cover everything!\n(Or at least an expectation that PRs may not be merged immediately while waiting on other\nPRs that fill out the API.)\n\nHowever \"perfect is the enemy of good\", and it would also be completely reasonable\nto add a TODO list in the main module doc-string for the new class,\nbriefly listing the parts of the API which still need to be provided.\nHopefully this document makes it easy to assemble this list.\n\nAnother alternative to a TODO list in the doc-strings is adding github issues.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "library_note.reducible non-instances", "description": "Some definitions that define objects of a class cannot be instances, because they have an\nexplicit argument that does not occur in the conclusion. An example is `preorder.lift` that has a\nfunction `f : \u03b1 \u2192 \u03b2` as an explicit argument to lift a preorder on `\u03b2` to a preorder on `\u03b1`.\n\nIf these definitions are used to define instances of this class *and* this class is an argument to\nsome other type-class so that type-class inference will have to unfold these instances to check\nfor definitional equality, then these definitions should be marked `@[reducible]`.\n\nFor example, `preorder.lift` is used to define `units.preorder` and `partial_order.lift` is used\nto define `units.partial_order`. In some cases it is important that type-class inference can\nrecognize that `units.preorder` and `units.partial_order` give rise to the same `has_le` instance.\nFor example, you might have another class that takes `[has_le \u03b1]` as an argument, and this argument\nsometimes comes from `units.preorder` and sometimes from `units.partial_order`.\nTherefore, `preorder.lift` and `partial_order.lift` are marked `@[reducible]`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_localization.surj_of_gcd_domain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "gcd_monoid.to_is_integrally_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_monoid_algebra.mem_ideal_span_of'_image", "description": " If `x` belongs to the ideal generated by generators in `s`, then every element of the support of\n`x` factors additively through an element of `s`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monoid_algebra.mem_ideal_span_of_image", "description": " If `x` belongs to the ideal generated by generators in `s`, then every element of the support of\n`x` factors through an element of `s`.\n\nWe could spell `\u2203 d, m = d * m` as `mul_opposite.op m' \u2223 mul_opposite.op m` but this would be worse.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "num_derangements_tendsto_inv_e", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplex_category.to_Top", "description": "The functor associating the topological `n`-simplex to `[n] : simplex_category`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplex_category.to_Top_map_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplex_category.coe_to_Top_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplex_category.to_Top_obj.has_coe_to_fun", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplex_category.to_Top_map", "description": "A morphism in `simplex_category` induces a map on the associated topological spaces.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplex_category.to_Top_obj", "description": "The topological simplex associated to `x : simplex_category`.\n This is the object part of the functor `simplex_category.to_Top`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplex_category.continuous_to_Top_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplex_category.to_Top_obj_2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simplex_category.to_Top_obj.ext", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "emetric.paracompact_space", "description": " A `pseudo_emetric_space` is always a paracompact space. Formalization is based\non [MR0236876].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "emetric.normal_of_emetric", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.pretty_cases", "description": "Query the proof goal and print the skeleton of a proof by\ncases.\n\nFor example, let us consider the following proof:\n\n```lean\nexample {\u03b1} (xs ys : list \u03b1) (h : xs ~ ys) : true :=\nbegin\n  induction h,\n  pretty_cases,\n    -- Try this:\n    --   case list.perm.nil :\n    --   { admit },\n    --   case list.perm.cons : h_x h_l\u2081 h_l\u2082 h_a h_ih\n    --   { admit },\n    --   case list.perm.swap : h_x h_y h_l\n    --   { admit },\n    --   case list.perm.trans : h_l\u2081 h_l\u2082 h_l\u2083 h_a h_a_1 h_ih_a h_ih_a_1\n    --   { admit },\nend\n```\n\nThe output helps the user layout the cases and rename the\nintroduced variables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.pretty_cases", "description": "Query the proof goal and print the skeleton of a proof by\ncases.\n\nFor example, let us consider the following proof:\n\n```lean\nexample {\u03b1} (xs ys : list \u03b1) (h : xs ~ ys) : true :=\nbegin\n  induction h,\n  pretty_cases,\n    -- Try this:\n    --   case list.perm.nil :\n    --   { admit },\n    --   case list.perm.cons : h_x h_l\u2081 h_l\u2082 h_a h_ih\n    --   { admit },\n    --   case list.perm.swap : h_x h_y h_l\n    --   { admit },\n    --   case list.perm.trans : h_l\u2081 h_l\u2082 h_l\u2083 h_a h_a_1 h_ih_a h_ih_a_1\n    --   { admit },\nend\n```\n\nThe output helps the user layout the cases and rename the\nintroduced variables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.pretty_cases_advice", "description": "Query the proof goal and print the skeleton of a proof by cases.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.forget\u2082_AddCommGroup_is_equivalence", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.forget\u2082_AddCommGroup_ess_surj", "description": "The forgetful functor from `\u2124` modules to `AddCommGroup` is essentially surjective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Module.forget\u2082_AddCommGroup_full", "description": "The forgetful functor from `\u2124` modules to `AddCommGroup` is full.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_graph.end.is_empty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.prime.dvd_choose_add", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.prime.dvd_choose_self", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.prime.dvd_choose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_of_is_simple_module", "description": "A simple module is a simple object in the category of modules.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_iff_is_simple_module", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_of_finrank_eq_one", "description": "Any `k`-algebra module which is 1-dimensional over `k` is simple.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_simple_module_of_simple", "description": "A simple object in the category of modules is a simple module.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "simple_iff_is_simple_module'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "maximal_ideal_is_principal_of_is_dedekind_domain", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "discrete_valuation_ring.tfae", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exists_maximal_ideal_pow_eq_of_principal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "star_prod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.cardinal_mk_eq_max", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.cardinal_mk_le_max", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.wlog", "description": " `wlog h : P` will add an assumption `h : P` to the main goal,\nand add a side goal that requires showing that the case `h : \u00ac P` can be reduced to the case\nwhere `P` holds (typically by symmetry).\n\nThe side goal will be at the top of the stack. In this side goal, there will be two assumptions:\n- `h : \u00ac P`: the assumption that `P` does not hold\n- `this`: which is the statement that in the old context `P` suffices to prove the goal.\n  By default, the name `this` is used, but the idiom `with H` can be added to specify the name:\n  `wlog h : P with H`.\n\nTypically, it is useful to use the variant `wlog h : P generalizing x y`,\nto revert certain parts of the context before creating the new goal.\nIn this way, the wlog-claim `this` can be applied to `x` and `y` in different orders\n(exploiting symmetry, which is the typical use case).\n\nBy default, the entire context is reverted.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.wlog", "description": " `wlog h : P` will add an assumption `h : P` to the main goal,\nand add a side goal that requires showing that the case `h : \u00ac P` can be reduced to the case\nwhere `P` holds (typically by symmetry).\n\nThe side goal will be at the top of the stack. In this side goal, there will be two assumptions:\n- `h : \u00ac P`: the assumption that `P` does not hold\n- `this`: which is the statement that in the old context `P` suffices to prove the goal.\n  By default, the name `this` is used, but the idiom `with H` can be added to specify the name:\n  `wlog h : P with H`.\n\nTypically, it is useful to use the variant `wlog h : P generalizing x y`,\nto revert certain parts of the context before creating the new goal.\nIn this way, the wlog-claim `this` can be applied to `x` and `y` in different orders\n(exploiting symmetry, which is the typical use case).\n\nBy default, the entire context is reverted.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.take_pi_args", "description": "A helper function to retrieve the names of the first `n` arguments to a Pi-expression.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ex_of_psig", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "psigma.eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "sigma.eq", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.mk_has_reflect_instance", "description": "Solves a goal of the form `has_reflect \u03b1` where \u03b1 is an inductive type.\n   Needs to synthesize a `reflected` instance for each inductive parameter type of \u03b1\n   and for each constructor parameter of \u03b1.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "zmod.is_reduced", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_reduced_zmod", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "real.measurable_arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.arctan", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.induction_on_pi_max", "description": " Given a predicate on functions `\u03a0 i, finset (\u03b1 i)` defined on a finite type, it is true on all\nmaps provided that it is true on `\u03bb _, \u2205` and for any function `g : \u03a0 i, finset (\u03b1 i)`, an index\n`i : \u03b9`, and an element`x : \u03b1 i` that is strictly greater than all elements of `g i`, `p g` implies\n`p (update g i (insert x (g i)))`.\n\nThis lemma requires `linear_order` instances on all `\u03b1 i`. See also `finset.induction_on_pi` for a\nversion that `x \u2209 g i` instead of ` does not need `\u03a0 i, linear_order (\u03b1 i)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.induction_on_pi_min", "description": " Given a predicate on functions `\u03a0 i, finset (\u03b1 i)` defined on a finite type, it is true on all\nmaps provided that it is true on `\u03bb _, \u2205` and for any function `g : \u03a0 i, finset (\u03b1 i)`, an index\n`i : \u03b9`, and an element`x : \u03b1 i` that is strictly less than all elements of `g i`, `p g` implies\n`p (update g i (insert x (g i)))`.\n\nThis lemma requires `linear_order` instances on all `\u03b1 i`. See also `finset.induction_on_pi` for a\nversion that `x \u2209 g i` instead of ` does not need `\u03a0 i, linear_order (\u03b1 i)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.induction_on_pi", "description": " Given a predicate on functions `\u03a0 i, finset (\u03b1 i)` defined on a finite type, it is true on all\nmaps provided that it is true on `\u03bb _, \u2205` and for any function `g : \u03a0 i, finset (\u03b1 i)`, an index\n`i : \u03b9`, and `x \u2209 g i`, `p g` implies `p (update g i (insert x (g i)))`.\n\nSee also `finset.induction_on_pi_max` and `finset.induction_on_pi_min` for specialized versions\nthat require `\u03a0 i, linear_order (\u03b1 i)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.induction_on_pi_of_choice", "description": "General theorem for `finset.induction_on_pi`-style induction principles.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "additive.ordered_add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "additive.linear_ordered_add_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiplicative.ordered_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "multiplicative.linear_ordered_comm_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.add_choose_eq", "description": "Vandermonde's identity", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.quotient.is_noetherian_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv_within.star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_strict_deriv_at.star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_within_at.star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at_filter.star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_deriv_at.star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv.star'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "deriv.star", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "char_zero.infinite", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone_on.exists_antitone_extension", "description": " If a function is antitone and is bounded on a set `s`, then it admits an antitone extension to\nthe whole space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone_on.exists_monotone_extension", "description": " If a function is monotone and is bounded on a set `s`, then it admits a monotone extension to\nthe whole space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "topological_semiring_of_smooth", "description": " A smooth (semi)ring is a topological (semi)ring. This is not an instance for technical reasons,\nsee note [Design choices about smooth algebraic structures].", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "field_smooth_ring", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_ring.to_has_smooth_mul", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_ring.to_lie_add_group", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_ring", "description": " A smooth (semi)ring is a (semi)ring `R` where addition and multiplication are smooth.\nIf `R` is a ring, then negation is automatically smooth, as it is multiplication with `-1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_ring.to_has_smooth_add", "description": " A smooth (semi)ring is a (semi)ring `R` where addition and multiplication are smooth.\nIf `R` is a ring, then negation is automatically smooth, as it is multiplication with `-1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_ring.smooth_mul", "description": " A smooth (semi)ring is a (semi)ring `R` where addition and multiplication are smooth.\nIf `R` is a ring, then negation is automatically smooth, as it is multiplication with `-1`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.save_info_with_widgets", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "conv.interactive.apply_congr", "description": "Apply a congruence lemma inside `conv` mode.\n\nWhen called without an argument `apply_congr` will try applying all lemmas marked with `@[congr]`.\nOtherwise `apply_congr e` will apply the lemma `e`.\n\nRecall that a goal that appears as `\u2223 X` in `conv` mode\nrepresents a goal of `\u22a2 X = ?m`,\ni.e. an equation with a metavariable for the right hand side.\n\nTo successfully use `apply_congr e`, `e` will need to be an equation\n(possibly after function arguments),\nwhich can be unified with a goal of the form `X = ?m`.\nThe right hand side of `e` will then determine the metavariable,\nand `conv` will subsequently replace `X` with that right hand side.\n\nAs usual, `apply_congr` can create new goals;\nany of these which are _not_ equations with a metavariable on the right hand side\nwill be hard to deal with in `conv` mode.\nThus `apply_congr` automatically calls `intros` on any new goals,\nand fails if they are not then equations.\n\nIn particular it is useful for rewriting inside the operand of a `finset.sum`,\nas it provides an extra hypothesis asserting we are inside the domain.\n\nFor example:\n\n```lean\nexample (f g : \u2124 \u2192 \u2124) (S : finset \u2124) (h : \u2200 m \u2208 S, f m = g m) :\n  finset.sum S f = finset.sum S g :=\nbegin\n  conv_lhs\n  { -- If we just call `congr` here, in the second goal we're helpless,\n    -- because we are only given the opportunity to rewrite `f`.\n    -- However `apply_congr` uses the appropriate `@[congr]` lemma,\n    -- so we get to rewrite `f x`, in the presence of the crucial `H : x \u2208 S` hypothesis.\n    apply_congr,\n    skip,\n    simp [h, H], }\nend\n```\n\nIn the above example, when the `apply_congr` tactic is called it gives the hypothesis `H : x \u2208 S`\nwhich is then used to rewrite the `f x` to `g x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.apply_congr", "description": "Apply a congruence lemma inside `conv` mode.\n\nWhen called without an argument `apply_congr` will try applying all lemmas marked with `@[congr]`.\nOtherwise `apply_congr e` will apply the lemma `e`.\n\nRecall that a goal that appears as `\u2223 X` in `conv` mode\nrepresents a goal of `\u22a2 X = ?m`,\ni.e. an equation with a metavariable for the right hand side.\n\nTo successfully use `apply_congr e`, `e` will need to be an equation\n(possibly after function arguments),\nwhich can be unified with a goal of the form `X = ?m`.\nThe right hand side of `e` will then determine the metavariable,\nand `conv` will subsequently replace `X` with that right hand side.\n\nAs usual, `apply_congr` can create new goals;\nany of these which are _not_ equations with a metavariable on the right hand side\nwill be hard to deal with in `conv` mode.\nThus `apply_congr` automatically calls `intros` on any new goals,\nand fails if they are not then equations.\n\nIn particular it is useful for rewriting inside the operand of a `finset.sum`,\nas it provides an extra hypothesis asserting we are inside the domain.\n\nFor example:\n\n```lean\nexample (f g : \u2124 \u2192 \u2124) (S : finset \u2124) (h : \u2200 m \u2208 S, f m = g m) :\n  finset.sum S f = finset.sum S g :=\nbegin\n  conv_lhs\n  { -- If we just call `congr` here, in the second goal we're helpless,\n    -- because we are only given the opportunity to rewrite `f`.\n    -- However `apply_congr` uses the appropriate `@[congr]` lemma,\n    -- so we get to rewrite `f x`, in the presence of the crucial `H : x \u2208 S` hypothesis.\n    apply_congr,\n    skip,\n    simp [h, H], }\nend\n```\n\nIn the above example, when the `apply_congr` tactic is called it gives the hypothesis `H : x \u2208 S`\nwhich is then used to rewrite the `f x` to `g x`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra_map_monotone", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable.re", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable.inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.strongly_measurable.inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.ae_strongly_measurable.im", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast_choose_eq_pochhammer_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast_choose_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast_add_choose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.cast_choose", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "antitone_of_odd_of_monotone_on_nonneg", "description": " An odd function on a linear ordered additive commutative group is antitone on the whole group\nprovided that it is monotone on `set.Ici 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "monotone_of_odd_of_monotone_on_nonneg", "description": " An odd function on a linear ordered additive commutative group is monotone on the whole group\nprovided that it is monotone on `set.Ici 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_anti_of_odd_strict_anti_on_nonneg", "description": " An odd function on a linear ordered additive commutative group is strictly antitone on the whole\ngroup provided that it is strictly antitone on `set.Ici 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "strict_mono_of_odd_strict_mono_on_nonneg", "description": " An odd function on a linear ordered additive commutative group is strictly monotone on the whole\ngroup provided that it is strictly monotone on `set.Ici 0`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.add_haar_frontier", "description": "Haar measure of the frontier of a convex set is zero.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "convex.null_measurable_set", "description": " A convex set in a finite dimensional real vector space is null measurable with respect to an\nadditive Haar measure on this space.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "formal_multilinear_series.radius_eq_liminf", "description": " The radius of a formal multilinear series is equal to\n$\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[n]{\u2016p n\u2016}}$. The actual statement uses `\u211d\u22650` and some\ncoercions.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smul_eq_self_of_preimage_zpow_eq_self", "description": " Let `n : \u2124` and `s` a subset of a commutative group `G` that is invariant under preimage for\nthe map `x \u21a6 x^n`. Then `s` is invariant under the pointwise action of the subgroup of elements\n`g : G` such that `g^(n^j) = 1` for some `j : \u2115`. (This subgroup is called the Pr\u00fcfer subgroup when\n `G` is the `circle` and `n` is prime.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vadd_eq_self_of_preimage_zsmul_eq_self", "description": "Let `n : \u2124` and `s` a subset of an additive commutative group `G` that is invariant\nunder preimage for the map `x \u21a6 n \u2022 x`. Then `s` is invariant under the pointwise action of the\nadditive subgroup of elements `g : G` such that `(n^j) \u2022 g = 0` for some `j : \u2115`. (This additive\nsubgroup is called the Pr\u00fcfer subgroup when `G` is the `add_circle` and `n` is prime.)", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_at_hom_bundle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_on_continuous_linear_map_coord_change", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bundle.continuous_linear_map.vector_prebundle.is_smooth", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "smooth_vector_bundle.continuous_linear_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hom_chart", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "cont_mdiff_at_hom_bundle", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Profinite.exists_locally_constant", "description": " Any locally constant function from a cofiltered limit of profinite sets factors through\none of the components.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Profinite.exists_clopen_of_cofiltered", "description": "If `X` is a cofiltered limit of profinite sets, then any clopen subset of `X` arises from\na clopen set in one of the terms in the limit.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Profinite.exists_locally_constant_finite_aux", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Profinite.exists_locally_constant_fin_two", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Profinite.exists_locally_constant_finite_nonempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "category_theory.coyoneda_tensor_unit", "description": "`(\ud835\udfd9_ C \u27f6 -)` is a lax monoidal functor to `Type`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo2019_q4.upper_bound", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo2019_q4", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "finsupp.is_pwo", "description": " A version of **Dickson's lemma** any subset of functions `\u03c3 \u2192\u2080 \u03b1` is partially well\nordered, when `\u03c3` is `finite` and `\u03b1` is a linear well order.\nThis version uses finsupps on a finite type as it is intended for use with `mv_power_series`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tsub_div", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.tensor_product_apply'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.tensor_product_repr_tmul_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.tensor_product", "description": "If b : \u03b9 \u2192 M and c : \u03ba \u2192 N are bases then so is \u03bb i, b i.1 \u2297\u209c c i.2 : \u03b9 \u00d7 \u03ba \u2192 M \u2297 N.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "basis.tensor_product_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo2011_q5", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "bool.univ_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bool.compl_singleton", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "bool.range_eq", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.funext", "description": " Two multivariate polynomials over an infinite integral domain are equal\nif they are equal upon evaluating them on an arbitrary assignment of the variables.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mv_polynomial.funext_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finite_field.trace_to_zmod_nondegenerate", "description": "The trace map from a finite field to its prime field is nongedenerate.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.prod_factorial_dvd_factorial_sum", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "nat.prod_factorial_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.instance_tree", "description": "Tree structure representing a `testable` instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.instance_tree.node", "description": "Tree structure representing a `testable` instance.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.slim_check", "description": "`slim_check` considers a proof goal and tries to generate examples\nthat would contradict the statement.\n\nLet's consider the following proof goal.\n\n```lean\nxs : list \u2115,\nh : \u2203 (x : \u2115) (H : x \u2208 xs), x < 3\n\u22a2 \u2200 (y : \u2115), y \u2208 xs \u2192 y < 5\n```\n\nThe local constants will be reverted and an instance will be found for\n`testable (\u2200 (xs : list \u2115), (\u2203 x \u2208 xs, x < 3) \u2192 (\u2200 y \u2208 xs, y < 5))`.\nThe `testable` instance is supported by an instance of `sampleable (list \u2115)`,\n`decidable (x < 3)` and `decidable (y < 5)`.\n\nExamples will be created in ascending order of size (more or less)\n\nThe first counter-examples found will be printed and will result in an error:\n\n```lean\n===================\nFound problems!\n\nxs := [1, 28]\nx := 1\ny := 28\n-------------------\n```\n\nIf `slim_check` successfully tests 100 examples, it acts like\nadmit. If it gives up or finds a counter-example, it reports an error.\n\nFor more information on writing your own `sampleable` and `testable`\ninstances, see `testing.slim_check.testable`.\n\nOptional arguments given with `slim_check_cfg`\n* `num_inst` (default 100): number of examples to test properties with\n* `max_size` (default 100): final size argument\n* `enable_tracing` (default `ff`): enable the printing of discarded samples\n\nOptions:\n* `set_option trace.slim_check.decoration true`: print the proposition with quantifier annotations\n* `set_option trace.slim_check.discarded true`: print the examples discarded because they do not\n  satisfy assumptions\n* `set_option trace.slim_check.shrink.steps true`: trace the shrinking of counter-example\n* `set_option trace.slim_check.shrink.candidates true`: print the lists of candidates considered\n  when shrinking each variable\n* `set_option trace.slim_check.instance true`: print the instances of `testable` being used to test\n  the proposition\n* `set_option trace.slim_check.success true`: print the tested samples that satisfy a property", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.instance_tree.to_format", "description": "format a `instance_tree`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.summarize_instance", "description": " Gather information about a `testable` instance. Given\nan expression of type `testable ?p`, gather the\nname of the `testable` instances that it is built from\nand the proposition that they test.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.instance_tree.has_to_tactic_format", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pos_div_pow_pos", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "hofer", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_iff_is_sheaf_comp", "description": "If `G : C \u2964 D` is a functor which reflects isomorphisms and preserves limits\n(we assume all limits exist in both `C` and `D`),\nthen checking the sheaf condition for a presheaf `F : presheaf C X`\nis equivalent to checking the sheaf condition for `F \u22d9 G`.\n\nThe important special case is when\n`C` is a concrete category with a forgetful functor\nthat preserves limits and reflects isomorphisms.\nThen to check the sheaf condition it suffices to check it on the underlying sheaf of types.\n\nAnother useful example is the forgetful functor `TopCommRing \u2964 Top`.\n\nSee <https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0073>.\nIn fact we prove a stronger version with arbitrary complete target category.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.sheaf_condition.diagram_comp_preserves_limits", "description": "When `G` preserves limits, the sheaf condition diagram for `F` composed with `G` is\nnaturally isomorphic to the sheaf condition diagram for `F \u22d9 G`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.sheaf_condition.map_cone_fork", "description": "When `G` preserves limits, the image under `G` of the sheaf condition fork for `F`\nis the sheaf condition fork for `F \u22d9 G`,\npostcomposed with the inverse of the natural isomorphism `diagram_comp_preserves_limits`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.compact_space", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "weak_dual.character_space.norm_le_norm_one", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.uniform_add_group", "description": " The uniform space structure associated to an abelian group filter basis via the associated\ntopological abelian group structure is compatible with its group structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.cauchy_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_group_filter_basis.uniform_space", "description": " The uniform space structure associated to an abelian group filter basis via the associated\ntopological abelian group structure.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_algebra.graded_algebra.\u03b9_apply", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_algebra.graded_algebra", "description": "The tensor algebra is graded by the powers of the submodule `(tensor_algebra.\u03b9 R).range`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tensor_algebra.graded_algebra.\u03b9", "description": " A version of `tensor_algebra.\u03b9` that maps directly into the graded structure. This is\nprimarily an auxiliary construction used to provide `tensor_algebra.graded_algebra`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "linear_map.det_zero''", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali.vitali_family", "description": " Assume that around every point there are arbitrarily small scales at which the measure is\ndoubling. Then the set of closed sets `a` with nonempty interior contained in `closed_ball x r` and\ncovering a fixed proportion `1/C` of the ball `closed_ball x (3 * r)` forms a Vitali family.\nThis is essentially a restatement of the measurable Vitali theorem.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali.exists_disjoint_covering_ae", "description": " The measurable Vitali covering theorem. Assume one is given a family `t` of closed sets with\nnonempty interior, such that each `a \u2208 t` is included in a ball `B (x, r)` and covers a definite\nproportion of the ball `B (x, 3 r)` for a given measure `\u03bc` (think of the situation where `\u03bc` is\na doubling measure and `t` is a family of balls). Consider a (possibly non-measurable) set `s`\nat which the family is fine, i.e., every point of `s` belongs to arbitrarily small elements of `t`.\nThen one can extract from `t` a disjoint subfamily that covers almost all `s`.\n\nFor more flexibility, we give a statement with a parameterized family of sets.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali.exists_disjoint_subfamily_covering_enlargment", "description": " Vitali covering theorem: given a set `t` of subsets of a type, one may extract a disjoint\nsubfamily `u` such that the `\u03c4`-enlargment of this family covers all elements of `t`, where `\u03c4 > 1`\nis any fixed number.\n\nWhen `t` is a family of balls, the `\u03c4`-enlargment of `ball x r` is `ball x ((1+2\u03c4) r)`. In general,\nit is expressed in terms of a function `\u03b4` (think \"radius\" or \"diameter\"), positive and bounded on\nall elements of `t`. The condition is that every element `a` of `t` should intersect an\nelement `b` of `u` of size larger than that of `a` up to `\u03c4`, i.e., `\u03b4 b \u2265 \u03b4 a / \u03c4`.\n\nWe state the lemma slightly more generally, with an indexed family of sets `B a` for `a \u2208 t`, for\nwider applicability.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "vitali.exists_disjoint_subfamily_covering_enlargment_closed_ball", "description": " Vitali covering theorem, closed balls version: given a family `t` of closed balls, one can\nextract a disjoint subfamily `u \u2286 t` so that all balls in `t` are covered by the 5-times\ndilations of balls in `u`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "add_semigroup_pempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "semigroup_pempty", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_bracket", "description": "The has_bracket class has three intended uses:\n\n 1. for certain binary operations on structures, like the product `\u2045x, y\u2046` of two elements\n   `x`, `y` in a Lie algebra or the commutator of two elements `x` and `y` in a group.\n\n 2. for certain actions of one structure on another, like the action `\u2045x, m\u2046` of an element `x`\n   of a Lie algebra on an element `m` in one of its modules (analogous to `has_smul` in the\n   associative setting).\n\n 3. for binary operations on substructures, like the commutator `\u2045H, K\u2046` of two subgroups `H` and\n    `K` of a group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "has_bracket.bracket", "description": "The has_bracket class has three intended uses:\n\n 1. for certain binary operations on structures, like the product `\u2045x, y\u2046` of two elements\n   `x`, `y` in a Lie algebra or the commutator of two elements `x` and `y` in a group.\n\n 2. for certain actions of one structure on another, like the action `\u2045x, m\u2046` of an element `x`\n   of a Lie algebra on an element `m` in one of its modules (analogous to `has_smul` in the\n   associative setting).\n\n 3. for binary operations on substructures, like the commutator `\u2045H, K\u2046` of two subgroups `H` and\n    `K` of a group.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo1988_q6.constant_descent_vieta_jumping", "description": " Constant descent Vieta jumping.\n\nThis proof technique allows one to prove an arbitrary proposition `claim`,\nby running a descent argument on a hyperbola `H` in the first quadrant of the plane,\nunder the following conditions:\n\n* `h\u2080`     : There exists an integral point `(x,y)` on the hyperbola `H`.\n* `H_symm` : The hyperbola has a symmetry along the diagonal in the plane.\n* `H_zero` : If an integral point `(x,0)` lies on the hyperbola `H`, then `claim` is true.\n* `H_diag` : If an integral point `(x,x)` lies on the hyperbola `H`, then `claim` is true.\n* `H_desc` : If `(x,y)` is an integral point on the hyperbola `H`,\nwith `x < y` then there exists a \u201csmaller\u201d point on `H`: a point `(x',y')` with `x' < y' \u2264 x`.\n\nFor reasons of usability, the hyperbola `H` is implemented as an arbitrary predicate.\n(In question 6 of IMO1988, where this proof technique was first developped,\nthe predicate `claim` would be `\u2203 (d : \u2115), d ^ 2 = k` for some natural number `k`,\nand the predicate `H` would be `\u03bb a b, a * a + b * b = (a * b + 1) * k`.)\n\nTo ensure that the predicate `H` actually describes a hyperbola,\nthe user must provide arguments `B` and `C` that are used as coefficients for a quadratic equation.\nFinally, `H_quad` is the proof obligation that the quadratic equation\n  `(y:\u2124) * y - B x * y + C x = 0`\ndescribes the same hyperbola as the predicate `H`.\n\nFor extra flexibility, one must provide a predicate `base` on the integral points in the plane.\nIn the descent step `H_desc` this will give the user the additional assumption that\nthe point `(x,y)` does not lie in this base locus.\nThe user must provide a proof that the proposition `claim` is true\nif there exists an integral point `(x,y)` on the hyperbola `H` that lies in the base locus.\nIf such a base locus is not necessary, once can simply let it be `\u03bb x y, false`.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "imo1988_q6", "description": "Question 6 of IMO1988. If a and b are two natural numbers\nsuch that a*b+1 divides a^2 + b^2, show that their quotient is a perfect square.", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.async", "description": "Proves the first goal asynchronously as a separate lemma.", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.prove_goal_async", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "tactic.run_async", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "debugger.attr", "description": "", "p": "core"}, {"name": "with_zero.contravariant_class_mul_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.interactive.simpa", "description": "This is a \"finishing\" tactic modification of `simp`. It has two forms.\n\n* `simpa [rules, ...] using e` will simplify the goal and the type of\n  `e` using `rules`, then try to close the goal using `e`.\n\n  Simplifying the type of `e` makes it more likely to match the goal\n  (which has also been simplified). This construction also tends to be\n  more robust under changes to the simp lemma set.\n\n* `simpa [rules, ...]` will simplify the goal and the type of a\n  hypothesis `this` if present in the context, then try to close the goal using\n  the `assumption` tactic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic_doc.tactic.simpa", "description": "This is a \"finishing\" tactic modification of `simp`. It has two forms.\n\n* `simpa [rules, ...] using e` will simplify the goal and the type of\n  `e` using `rules`, then try to close the goal using `e`.\n\n  Simplifying the type of `e` makes it more likely to match the goal\n  (which has also been simplified). This construction also tends to be\n  more robust under changes to the simp lemma set.\n\n* `simpa [rules, ...]` will simplify the goal and the type of a\n  hypothesis `this` if present in the context, then try to close the goal using\n  the `assumption` tactic.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.eigenvalues.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.supr_generalized_eigenspace_eq_top", "description": "The generalized eigenvectors span the entire vector space (Lemma 8.21 of [axler2015]).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.End.exists_eigenvalue", "description": "Every linear operator on a vector space over an algebraically closed field has\n   an eigenvalue.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measurable.inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ae_measurable.inner", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_subset_mul_div", "description": "**Ruzsa's covering lemma**.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "finset.exists_subset_add_sub", "description": "**Ruzsa's covering lemma**", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_of_is_terminal_of_indiscrete", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_on_punit_of_is_terminal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_iff_is_terminal_of_indiscrete", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_sheaf_on_punit_iff_is_terminal", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo2001_q6", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.locally_surjective_iff_surjective_on_stalks", "description": " An equivalent condition for a map of presheaves to be locally surjective\nis for all the induced maps on stalks to be surjective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_locally_surjective_iff", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.presheaf.is_locally_surjective", "description": "A map of presheaves `T : \u2131 \u27f6 \ud835\udca2` is **locally surjective** if for any open set `U`,\nsection `t` over `U`, and `x \u2208 U`, there exists an open set `x \u2208 V \u2286 U` and a section `s` over `V`\nsuch that `$T_*(s_V) = t|_V$`.\n\nSee `is_locally_surjective_iff` below.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mdifferentiable_jacobi_theta", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvqpf.quotient_qpf", "description": " If `F` is a QPF then `G` is a QPF as well. Can be used to\nconstruct `mvqpf` instances by transporting them across\nsurjective functions", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvqpf.quot1.map", "description": "`map` of the `quot1` functor", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvqpf.rel_quot", "description": "`quot1` is a qpf", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvqpf.quot1.inhabited", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvqpf.quot1", "description": "Functorial quotient type", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "mvqpf.quot1.mvfunctor", "description": "`mvfunctor` instance for `quot1` with well-behaved `R`", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.intro_lst_fresh_reserved", "description": "`intro_lst_fresh_reserved ns reserved` introduces one hypothesis for every\nelement of `ns`. If the element is `sum.inl n`, the hypothesis receives the name\n`n` (which may or may not be fresh). If the element is `sum.inr ns'`, the\nhypothesis receives a fresh name from `ns`, excluding the names in `reserved`.\n`ns` must be nonempty. See `tactic.get_unused_name_reserved` for the full\nalgorithm.\n\nNote that the\norder of introductions matters:\n`intro_lst_fresh_reserved [sum.inr [`n], sum.inr [`n]]` will introduce\nhypotheses `n` and `n_1` (assuming that these names are otherwise unused and not\nreserved).", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.rename_fresh", "description": "`rename_fresh renames reserved`, given a map `renames` which associates the\nunique names of some hypotheses `h\u1d62` with either a name `n\u1d62` or a nonempty (!)\nname list `ns\u1d62`, renames each `h\u1d62` as follows:\n\n- If `h\u1d62` is associated with a name `n\u1d62`, it is renamed to `n\u1d62`. This may\n  introduce shadowing if there is another hypothesis `n\u1d62` in the context.\n- If `h\u1d62` is associated with a name list `ns\u1d62`, it is renamed to the first\n  unused name from `ns\u1d62`. If none of the names is unused, `h\u1d62` is renamed to a\n  fresh name based on the first name of `n\u1d62`. A name is considered used if it\n  appears in the context or in the environment or in `reserved`.\n\nSee `tactic.get_unused_name_reserved` for the full algorithm.\n\nThe hypotheses are renamed in context order, so hypotheses which occur earlier\nin the context are renamed before hypotheses that occur later in the context.\nThis is important because earlier renamings may 'steal' names from later\nrenamings.\n\n`rename_fresh` returns a list of pairs `(o\u1d62, n\u1d62)` where the `o\u1d62` are hypotheses\nfrom the context in which `rename_fresh` was called and the `n\u1d62` are the\ncorresponding hypotheses from the new context created by `rename_fresh`. The\npairs are returned in context order. Note that the returned list may contain\nhypotheses which do not appear in `renames` but had to be temporarily reverted\ndue to dependencies.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.intro_fresh_reserved", "description": "`intro_fresh_reserved ns reserved` introduces a hypothesis. The hypothesis\nreceives a fresh name from `ns`, excluding the names in `reserved`. `ns` must be\nnonempty. See `tactic.get_unused_name_reserved` for the full algorithm.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "tactic.get_unused_name_reserved", "description": "`get_unused_name_reserved ns reserved` returns the first name from `ns` that\noccurs neither in `reserved` nor in the environment. If there is no such name in\n`ns`, it returns a name of the form `n_i`, where `n` is the first name from `ns`\nand `i` is a natural number (like `tactic.get_unused_name`). If `ns` is empty,\nit returns an arbitrary name.\n\nFor example, assume we operate in the following context:\n\n```lean\nn n_1: \u2115\n```\n\nThen we have\n\n```lean\nget_unused_name_reserved [`n, `m, `list, `o] {m} = `o\n```\n\nsince `n occurs in the context, `m is reserved, `list occurs in the environment\nbut `o is unused. We also have\n\n```lean\nget_unused_name_reserved [`n, `m, `list] {m} = `n_2\n```\n\nsince all of the names from the list are taken and `n_2` is the first unused\nvariation of `n`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "continuous_affine_map.cont_diff", "description": "A continuous affine map between normed vector spaces is smooth.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "Top.is_topological_basis_cofiltered_limit", "description": "Given a *compatible* collection of topological bases for the factors in a cofiltered limit\nwhich contain `set.univ` and are closed under intersections, the induced *naive* collection\nof sets in the limit is, in fact, a topological basis.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "AddCommGroup.category_theory.preadditive", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.finite.multilinear_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "module.free.multilinear_map", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "matrix.square_of_invertible", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "integrable_of_is_O_exp_neg", "description": " If `f` is continuous on `[a, \u221e)`, and is `O (exp (-b * x))` at `\u221e` for some `b > 0`, then\n`f` is integrable on `(a, \u221e)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "exp_neg_integrable_on_Ioi", "description": "`exp (-b * x)` is integrable on `(a, \u221e)`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ax_grothendieck_of_locally_finite", "description": "Any injective polynomial map over an algebraic extension of a finite field is surjective.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.exists_root", "description": "**Fundamental theorem of algebra**: every non constant complex polynomial\n has a root", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.is_alg_closed", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "algebra.norm_localization", "description": " Let `S` be an extension of `R` and `R\u2098 S\u2098` be localizations at `M` of `R S` respectively.\nThen the norm of `a : S\u2098` over `R\u2098` is the norm of `a : S` over `R` if `S` is free as `R`-module.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_closed_fixed_points", "description": "The set of fixed points of a continuous map is a closed set.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "is_fixed_pt_of_tendsto_iterate", "description": " If the iterates `f^[n] x` converge to `y` and `f` is continuous at `y`,\nthen `y` is a fixed point for `f`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "oxford_invariants.week3_p1", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "is_conformal_map_iff", "description": " A map between two inner product spaces is a conformal map if and only if it preserves inner\nproducts up to a scalar factor, i.e., there exists a positive `c : \u211d` such that `\u27eaf u, f v\u27eb = c *\n\u27eau, v\u27eb` for all `u`, `v`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "lucas_primality", "description": "If `a^(p-1) = 1 mod p`, but `a^((p-1)/q) \u2260 1 mod p` for all prime factors `q` of `p-1`, then `p`\nis prime. This is true because `a` has order `p-1` in the multiplicative group mod `p`, so this\ngroup must itself have order `p-1`, which only happens when `p` is prime.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "comm_ring_strong_rank_condition", "description": "Any commutative ring satisfies the `strong_rank_condition`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.hermite_eq_deriv_gaussian'", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.deriv_gaussian_eq_hermite_mul_gaussian", "description": "`hermite n` is (up to sign) the factor appearing in `deriv^[n]` of a gaussian", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "polynomial.hermite_eq_deriv_gaussian", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "ideal.supr_infi_eq_top_iff_pairwise", "description": "A finite family of ideals is pairwise coprime (that is, any two of them generate the whole ring)\niff when taking all the possible intersections of all but one of these ideals, the resulting family\nof ideals still generate the whole ring.\n\nFor example with three ideals : `I \u2294 J = I \u2294 K = J \u2294 K = \u22a4 \u2194 (I \u2293 J) \u2294 (I \u2293 K) \u2294 (J \u2293 K) = \u22a4`.\n\nWhen ideals are all of the form `I i = R \u2219 s i`, this is equivalent to the\n`exists_sum_eq_one_iff_pairwise_coprime` lemma.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "complex.orientation", "description": "The standard orientation on `\u2102`.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "theorem_100.inverse_triangle_sum", "description": "**Sum of the Reciprocals of the Triangular Numbers**", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "linear_map.is_closed_or_dense_ker", "description": " The kernel of a linear map taking values in a simple module over the base ring is closed or\ndense. Applies, e.g., to the case when `R = N` is a division ring.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "measure_theory.hahn_decomposition", "description": "**Hahn decomposition theorem**", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "order_iso_Ioo_neg_one_one", "description": " In a linear ordered field, the whole field is order isomorphic to the open interval `(-1, 1)`.\nWe consider the actual implementation to be a \"black box\", so it is irreducible.", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "pi.exists_forall_pos_add_lt", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}, {"name": "imo2020_q2", "description": "", "p": "mathlib-archive"}, {"name": "dvd_sub_pow_of_dvd_sub", "description": "", "p": "mathlib"}]